1543	2026-06-05 01:55:33.784045+00	34014	M3		23	Desserts aren't just sweets, they're full of feelings and emotion in each plate. Although every culture has own desserts, each culture stands out for its symbolic tastes and styles. In particular, many cultures have developed their own dessert for hundred of years, they use many kind of feelings to make or create a dessert. Especially, Thai desserts have absorbing iconic for each dessert. Three of more fascinatind desserts in Thailand are Thong Yod, Kanom Chan and Mango with sticky rice.<br><br><br>One of fascinating dessert in Thailand is Thong Yod, a golden drop, made by egg yolks, sugar, jasmine-scented and rice flour. There're 5 steps to make Thong Yod. First, beat the egg yolks until smooth and fluffy. Second, make syrup by boiled sugar with jasmine-water. Third, drop small spoonfuls of egg mixture into the syrup. Then, let them cook until firm and golden. Last, remove and cool it in water before serving. The word "thong" mean golden, which represents the wealth and successful. The rounded-shape symbolize perfection. Thong Yod often served at wedding and blessings ceremonis to wish good luck and a bright future.<br><br><br>A second amazing dessert in Thailand is Khanom Chan, Thai layered dessert that made by Tapioka flour, rice flour, coconut milk, sugar and Pandan juice. First we need to mix all flour, sugar and coconut-milk until smooth. Then, seperated them into bowls. Then, stream them each layer by layer until every layer smooth and streamed. Khanom Chan traditionally made by 9 layers. The layer represent step by step process and growth. Also, number 9 is lucky in Thai culture, symbolizing moving forward in life. Khanom Chan often given on promotions or new beginning to wish success.<br><br><br>A third absorbing dessert in Thailand is Mango with sticky rice. It made from Glutinous rice, Mango, coconut milk, sugar and salt. First, we streamed glutinous rice until it soft. Then, mixed rice with sugar, salt and 