28836	2025-09-21	2025-09-21	2026	Term 1 Finals	My name is Namo K. You can call me Namo.<br><br>Oh, I'm from Bangkok, in Thailand. I have been since I'm born.<br><br>My favorite technology is the is phone because phone, it have multiple multiple options. Yeah. All right. So...<br><br>So, the one I I use the most is Line, because in Southeast Asia, we mostly use Line to communicate because it's easier and it's it's wide. So, like in everybody's phone, they have Line in it.<br><br>So, I really think that video calling is the best method for me because I am not the person that really like to tax, especially on computer. I am very bad at tapping in Thai and English.<br><br>So, in the first picture is a picture of a computer room. The computer is for each student. Each student we have one computer and the second picture is a teacher that using a big television to teach the students. These two have a very... sorry. So, these two from the computer is mostly used for coding lessons or computer lessons. The student will have each of their computer and they can do research in it. But in the second picture, the teacher will teach with the monitor. It really helps the student because in the olden days, teacher were really used to write on the whiteboards and it's it's hard for the student to lecture and remember it. So, they invented the television.<br><br>So, from the first technology that you see is playing games, playing online games together. I think it really made their friendship getting stronger because games are made to play solo or duo. So, if you play duo with your friends, you can go together and explore. And you make your friendship better because you communicate with each other and help each other. Do you have any other nice?<br><br>Yeah, it's great, but there are some other choice too, like sending voice message. Some people, like me, don't like to text a lot because we are not good at texting or we don't like it. So, we use voice messaging to tell what they want to what to tell and it would be more easier than just typing and much faster.<br><br>So, I think playing online games together is the most fun and feeling because what? Oh, sorry. So, I think it was playing online games together because there are many different types of games. For example, like horror games. I once played a horror game with my friends and it was very fun. We are escaping the ghost and it was very trilling. What about you?<br><br>So, I think it really helps because nowadays, internet are worldwide. Every nearly everyone in the in the world have internet and their phones. So, you can get a friend from across the world, for example, like in the USA. You can get many friends from internet and it's it's good for themselves because if you have more friends from other country, you can learn their culture and understand them more.<br><br>So, I think the positive is that they can talk in long distances because mostly teenagers are in school year. So, in the olden days, they can only talk with each other in school or by mailing. But internet helped it because you just only open your phone and text your friends because there is a satellite system. But there are also some bad thing too. So, if they are typing, they can't understand their full expression. If they are happy and they type, they they take very thing. So, it doesn't only have advantage. It also have a disadvantage.<br><br>	596	2	Namo K, you do a good job of getting your ideas across, especially when you talk about everyday things like your favourite technology or how you use social media. That's a great start! However, to speak English with more confidence, we need to focus on making your sentences more accurate. For instance, you said, "phone, it have multiple multiple options" (which should be "phones have many options") and "I have been since I'm born" (which would be clearer as "I have lived here since I was born"). Another common area to practise is using the correct form of verbs, like when you mentioned "teacher were really used to write" (which should be "teachers were used to writing"). Thai speakers sometimes make these kinds of small grammar mistakes because the Thai language doesn't always show plurals or change verb endings like English does. A good step for you would be to really concentrate on subject-verb agreement (making sure the verb matches the 'doer' of the action) and using the right verb tenses to clearly show *when* things happen. You could try keeping a small notebook to jot down new vocabulary and how it's used in full sentences. ✍️	4	You did a super job of speaking for extended periods, Namo K, and your contributions were always relevant to the conversation! You presented your ideas clearly and used linking words like "so" and "because" to connect your thoughts logically. For example, when you explained why you prefer video calling, you said, "So, I really think that video calling is the best method for me because I am not the person that really like to tax..." That's a great way to show clear thinking! You also managed to speak for a good length of time without much hesitation or repeating yourself, which is a key part of fluent speaking. To get even better, try to expand your range of linking words. Instead of always using "and" or "but," you could experiment with words like "however," "although," or "therefore" to show more complex relationships between your ideas. This will make your speaking flow even more smoothly! 🗣️	3	Namo K, you are very easy to understand, which is the most important thing! Your voice goes up and down quite naturally (we call this intonation), and this helps people follow what you're saying. Your biggest area for improvement is to work on making your speech flow a bit more smoothly, as sometimes it can sound a little bit staccato, like when you say each word separately instead of linking them together. This often happens because Thai speakers tend to keep word boundaries very clear. Also, you sometimes had trouble with the /tʃ/ sound, like in "coach" (which sounded more like /koʊʃ/), and you found it tricky to clearly pronounce the final /s/ and /z/ sounds in words like "that's" and "these." This is a very common challenge for Thai learners, as these sounds are not as prominent at the end of words in Thai. To work on this, try practising linking words together when you speak, like "that's interesting" instead of "that is interesting." Also, pay special attention to making those final /s/ and /z/ sounds clear and strong. You could even try humming the /z/ sound to feel how the vibration works! 🎶	4	Namo K, you were excellent at participating in the discussions! You confidently started new topics, responded thoughtfully to the interviewer and your partner, and did a great job of linking your comments to what others had said. For example, when Ken spoke, you responded with, "Yeah, it's great, but there are some other choice too, like sending voice message." This shows you were listening and adding to the conversation! You also helped to keep the interaction going by asking questions, like "Do you have any other nice?" This is fantastic, as it means you're actively involved in a two-way conversation. To reach the highest level in this area, you could try to develop your ideas even further by sometimes challenging your partner's opinions in a friendly way, or by explicitly working together to agree on an outcome. For instance, you could say, "I see your point, but perhaps we should also consider..." to add more depth to your interactions. Keep up the great communication! 👋	13.0	Namo K, it's clear you've been working hard on your English speaking, and you expressed your ideas thoughtfully and engaged very well in the discussion! 🎉 Your ability to keep the conversation going and share detailed thoughts on various topics is a real plus. To make your English even stronger, let's focus on making your sentences a little more precise and varied. Keep up your wonderful energy, and with a bit of focus on sentence structure, your communication will feel even more polished and natural. 🚀<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Progress:**<br>*   ✔️ Spend some time each week reviewing basic grammar rules, especially subject-verb agreement and correct verb tenses.<br>*   ✔️ When you speak or write, try to use different words to link your ideas, like "however," "although," or "furthermore," to add more variety to your sentences.<br>*   ✔️ Listen carefully to English podcasts or videos and try to notice how speakers naturally connect words and pronounce sounds like /tʃ/, /s/, and /z/ at the ends of words.<br>	B2	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
30642	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	Hello everyone, my name is [laugh] Nine. Today I'm going to talk about my favorite story name The Rabbit and The Turtle. <br>What would you do if you were a very slow turtle but needs to race with fast rabbit? You might be surprised to learn that slow animal can win a competition. <br>In this story, the main character is turtle that very slow but never stop walking and the rabbit that very fast but have too much confident. At first, rabbit run very fast, he was very far away from turtle but something unexpected happen. Rabbit stop running and sleep under the tree because he think that turtle cannot win this competition. Suddenly when rabbit wake up, he ... turtle was near the finish line already. Rabbit run as fast as he can but he can't catch up. <br>This story is perfect for someone that have too much confident and in this story teach us about never give up and don't have too much confident. Thank you.	168	3	Nine, your book review of the classic fable was very clear and easy to follow. Translating thoughts from Thai into English grammar can be difficult, but you were successful in explaining the plot. With a score of 3 out of 5, you show a good understanding of how to build a story using English. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨ <br>You used a variety of words to describe the characters like "fast," "slow," and "unexpected." Your use of past tense was often correct, and you tried to use relative clauses to describe people and things. This helped make your story feel more detailed and interesting to the listener. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀 <br>One thing to check is the difference between adjectives and nouns, such as using "confidence" instead of "confident" (/ˈkɒnfɪdə**nt**/ → /ˈkɒnfɪdə**ns**/). You also missed some small words like the verb "to be" when you said "turtle that very slow" instead of "the turtle is very slow." This is a common pattern for Thai speakers, so try to remember that every English sentence needs a verb. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀 <br><ul><li><b>Noun vs Adjective forms</b>: Practice using "confidence" as a noun for things you have and "confident" as a descriptor for how you feel.</li><li><b>Check for main verbs</b>: Ensure every sentence has a main verb like "is," "am," or "are."</li><li><b>Consistent past tense</b>: Record yourself and listen for the "-ed" sounds at the end of verbs to make sure the story stays in the past.</li></ul> <br>Sharing your ideas with others is a helpful skill! 🎯	3	Nine, you organized your talk very well and used a great hook to start. Connecting a long story can be challenging because it requires many different transition words to stay organized. With a score of 3 out of 5, you followed the structure and connected your ideas logically. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨ <br>Your rhetorical question at the beginning was an effective way to get the audience's attention. You followed the story structure well by setting the scene, explaining the problem, and giving us a resolution. The way you used words like "at first" and "suddenly" helped the listener understand the order of events clearly. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀 <br>You missed the name of the author, which was one of the requirements for this task. Your talk was also under the two-minute limit, so try to add more details about the setting or the characters' feelings to speak for longer. Providing a specific reason why you liked the story would also make your opinion section stronger and more detailed. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀 <br><ul><li><b>Check the task list</b>: Always make sure you mention the author's name at the beginning of your talk.</li><li><b>Expand your descriptions</b>: Talk more about the setting or how the rabbit felt when he realized he was losing the race.</li><li><b>Time your practice</b>: Use a stopwatch to ensure you are speaking for at least two minutes by adding more examples.</li></ul> <br>You f