, affection until he returned back to Nastenka, her old lover. And she didn't hesitate and returned back to him right away leaving our dreamer in dullness once again. <br>Well, a... [clears throat] I love how both of the characters were written since I found dreamer to drown in his fantasies and and take the idea of Nastenka not who she was into his own dreams or fantasies. And for Nastenka, I feel like her choice it may not satisfy the readers since it was like a bad ending. But honestly, I mean, how can you fall in love with someone you just met in four days? But the thing that I don't... however, the thing that I don't like about the story is that, uh, I found it a bit confusing due to its language and when it was written since it was in for so long the language wasn't as simple as it was today. Uh, well, if you enjoy, uh, sentimental love stories... I think, I think you'll enjoy this book. Uh, I hope you give it a try. That's all from me. Thank you.	539	5	Racing, you did a great job sharing this classic story today! Discussing Dostoevsky is quite impressive for a middle-school student. You showed a high level of control over your language throughout the talk.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a wonderful range of vocabulary like "mutual," "platonic," and "sentimental." Your grammar was mostly very accurate, especially when using complex sentences to explain the characters' feelings. You also managed past tense narrative forms very well to tell the story.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were a few small errors with singular and plural nouns, such as saying "any relative" instead of "any relatives." You also used the phrase "returned back," which is redundant because "return" already means to go back. Watch out for these little slips when you are explaining complicated plot points.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Plural forms</b>: Remember that after "any," we usually use a plural noun in negative sentences like "doesn'