ic relationship between two people but what I really dislike because they loving each other too much with mouth very more too much and I don't like it some time ... some time it's cute but some time it's disgusting [laugh] oh ... wait no [laugh] I recommend this book for someone who love gay ... um, [laugh] love healthy relationship like this two men thank you everyone for listening to me	481	4	Platwo, your book review was very entertaining and easy to follow! Using English to describe such a detailed story is a great achievement. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows you have a strong grasp of many useful words and sentences. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You used some great vocabulary like "rival," "accidentally," and "emergency" to make your story interesting. You also tried a complex opening with a "what would you do" question which is excellent for B1. Your meaning was always clear even when the story got complicated. You used expressive words like "healthy relationship" to explain your thoughts.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You sometimes used "more" with words that already show a comparison. For example, you said "very more too much" instead of just "too much." Also, try to be consistent with your verb endings. You said "tell" /tel/ instead of the past tense "told" /toʊl<b>d</b>/. You also dropped the final sound in "texts" /teks/ instead of /teks<b>ts</b>/, which is a common Thai speech pattern. Some phrases like "loving with mouth" were a bit awkward, so try to use more standard words like "kissing."<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Watch out for double comparatives:</b> Remember not to use "more" with words that are already strong. Just say "it was too much" or "it was better."</li><li><b>Practise past tense endings:</b> Work on your final consonants for past tense verbs. Practise saying words like "kissed" /kɪst/ and "told" /toʊld/ slowly to hear the final sound.</li><li><b>Learn synonyms for romance:</b> To sound more 