ive, such as "his uncle who is very mean." It was good to see you using the past tense correctly for most of the story.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Be careful with subject-verb agreement and plurals. You said "a boys" /bɔɪ<b>z</b>/ instead of "a boy" /bɔɪ/. You also said "Voldemort want" /wɒnt/ instead of "Voldemort wants" /wɒnt<b>s</b>/. Also, watch out for verb patterns after "don't" because you said "don't understanding" instead of "don't understand." These are common patterns for Thai learners where final sounds and endings can be tricky.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement</b>: Practice adding the /s/ or /z/ sound for he, she, and it in the present tense.</li><li><b>Plurals and Articles</b>: Check if a noun should be singular or plural before you speak.</li><li><b>Verb Patterns</b>: Review when to use the base form of a verb versus the ending with -ing.</li></ul><br>Your grammar foundations are very strong for this level! 🎯	4	Kathi, you followed the structure of the book review very well. Organising a summary of such a famous book can be difficult. You managed to keep the story logical and included all the necessary parts.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You included every part of the required task. You named the author, used a clear "Imagine" hook, and explained the climax and resolution clearly. Your use of transition words like "However" and "So after that" helped the story flow logically.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were a few moments where you repeated words while thinking, like "destroy... destroy." This happens when you are searching for the next idea. Try to use small pauses instead of repeating the same word. You also had some filler words like "um" which can make the talk sound a bit slower. These small hesitations are natural but can affect how smooth your speech sounds.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Reduce Repetition</b>: When you feel stuck, take a silent