's so happy to to celebrate with family or friends and and watching the the the end and I'm very so happy to get party.<br><br>I, I think a big party is very so good. But the big party we need to use the much money to to make a big party.<br><br>Yeah, it's very so important. We, because we need to family or celebrate when it's like the birthday. Yeah.<br><br>	445	3	Pun, you do a good job of using basic sentences correctly, especially when you talk about things you know well, like your favorite subjects and using your mobile phone. 👍 For example, you said, "My best favorite subject is art." That’s a lovely, clear sentence! You also use a good range of words for everyday topics. However, sometimes you miss out on some small but important grammatical words or endings. For instance, you said, "I, I thirteen years old" (which should be "I am thirteen years old") and "I going to say it" (which should be "I am going to say it"). Also, phrases like "make the eyes damage" would be more accurate as "damage people's eyes." This is very common for Thai learners, as your language structures are sometimes a bit different from English. To keep improving, try to remember the 'be' verbs (am, is, are) when you're talking about yourself or describing things, and also pay attention to making words plural with an 's' sound.	3	You manage to speak for a good amount of time, Pun, which is a great start! Your answers are always relevant to the questions, which is super. 😊 However, sometimes you pause a little, and you repeat some of your ideas. For instance, in your description of the family at the beach, you said "two of daughter" several times. To make your speech flow even better, try to use different connecting words and phrases beyond "because" and "so." Imagine you’re building a bridge between your ideas – you can use words like "also," "however," and "and then" to make the path smoother. This will help you keep your ideas organised and avoid repeating yourself too much, making your stories even more engaging!	3	Pun, good job! I could understand nearly everything you said, which is fantastic. Your biggest problem is that your intonation (the way your voice goes up and down) is a little flat, and you have a habit of overstressing final syllables in words. This is something that Thai speakers often do when they speak English because the tones in Thai are different from the intonation patterns in English. For example, you might pronounce "happy" as /hæˈpiː/ or "party" as /ˈpɑːrˌtiː/ with a strong stress on the end. 🗣️ If you can focus on making your voice more expressive and letting your natural English intonation come through, your speech will sound even more natural and easier to follow!	2	You’re good at starting conversations and answering questions, Pun! 🚀 When the examiner spoke, you responded appropriately. You also gave clear opinions with reasons, like when you talked about using your mobile phone. However, during the collaborative task, you found it a bit difficult to really build on Mathew's ideas or ask him for his thoughts. You tended to share your own ideas in a long turn, which is good for showing what you know, but it meant less back-and-forth conversation. To make your interactive communication even better, try to ask your partner questions like, "What do you think about that?" or "Do you agree?" This helps to keep the conversation flowing and shows you are really working together.	11.0	Hello Pun! 👋 It was a real pleasure to listen to you speak. You communicate well, and it's clear you have lots of interesting things to say. Keep up the good work! Your confidence is a real strength, but focusing on making sure your sentences are always grammatically correct will help you improve even further. You’re already doing well, and with a bit of practice, you’ll be communicating even more smoothly.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Speaking Practice:**<br>*   ✔️ When you’re talking about yourself, always remember to use "I am" before your age or when describing what you are doing, like "I am thirteen years old" or "I am drawing."<br>*   ✔️ Pay close attention to small words like 'a', 'an', and 'the', and also to plural endings like '-s' at the end of words like "shops" or "eyes," to make your sentences more accurate.<br>*   ✔️ Practice making your voice go up and down more naturally when you speak English, and try not to stress the very last sound of every word too much.	A2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
