"to" when explaining a reason, such as saying "for protect them" instead of "to protect them." I also noticed you said the ending was "very rush," but in English, we use the adjective "rushed" (/rʌʃ<b>t</b>/) to describe something finished too quickly. This is a common pattern for Thai learners who might miss the final consonant sounds in English adjectives.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice verb patterns:</b> Remember to use "to + verb" when explaining a purpose, like "to protect" or "to help."</li><li><b>Check plural nouns:</b> Try to pause for a second to think if you are talking about one person (child) or many people (children).</li><li><b>Use -ed adjectives:</b> When describing how a movie or book felt, remember to use the -ed ending, such as "rushed" or "excited."</li></ul><br>You are using a great range of words to tell exciting stories! 🎯	3	Best, you followed the structure of the book review very well! Organizing a long talk can be difficult, but you moved through the story and your opinion logically. You should feel good about how you organized your ideas.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used helpful transition phrases like "In the beginning" and "In the end" to guide the listener through the timeline of the manga. Your "Imagine" hook was very effective because it set the scene perfectly and drew the listener into the world behind the walls. You included all the required parts of the task, including the author and your own recommendation at the end.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were several long pauses and sighs during your talk which made the flow feel a bit broken. While it is okay to think, try to avoid stopping in the middle of a sentence too often as it makes it harder for the listener to follow. Your resolution and climax sections were also a little bit short, so try to add more details about the most exciting parts of the story next time.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Reduce pauses:</b> Try to practice your talk a few times before recording so you feel more comfortable with the flow.</li><li><b>Expand the ending:</b> Spend more time explaining the "climax" or the most exciting part of the book to build more tension.</li><li><b>Use more connectors:</b> Try to use words like "however" or "therefore" to link your sentences together more smoothly.</li></ul><br>You have a clear way of organizing your thoughts, so keep practicing your delivery! 🎯	3	Best, congratulations on finishing your book review despite being so nervous! You should be very proud of yourself for speaking in front of the class. With a score of 3 out of 5, you've shown you can communicate clearly even when you're feeling a bit shaky. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your volume was consistent and strong enough for everyone to hear you throughout the talk. Even though you were nervous, your speaking was mainly intelligible and easy to follow. Your individual sounds were generally clear and sounded correct to the listener.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>The main area to improve is your flow, as your speech was quite choppy with many hesitations. I also noticed a small slip where you pronounced the "s" in "island", making it sound like "iceland" (/ˈaɪlənd/ → /ˈaɪ<b>s</b>lənd/). This is a very common mistake for Thai students, so don't worry—just remember the "s" is silent!<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Manage Nervousness:</b> Try taking three deep breaths before you start to help your speaking sound smoother and less choppy.</li><li><b>Master Silent Letters:</b> Practice saying "island" (/ˈaɪlənd/) and "aisle" (/ˈaɪl/) to remember that we don't pronounce the "s" in these words.</li><li><b>Practice Word Linking:</b> Try connecting simple words together in pairs, like "it‿is" or "in‿the," to help reduce the number of pauses.</li></ul><br>You did a great job today, so keep practicing and your confidence will grow! 🎯	\N	\N	13.0	Best, you did a great job reviewing Attack on Titan! You followed the structure well, included the author, and used an excellent "Imagine" hook to start your talk. Your vocabulary was strong for a B1 level, though you should watch out for small grammar slips like using "to protect" instead of "for protect" and checking your plural nouns. Try to reduce the sighs and long pauses to make your speaking flow more naturally. You are communicating your ideas clearly, so keep up the effort! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
