ic use and recycling waste because recycling waste is just use the plastic bag, plastic bag or plastic bottle and throw it in the right bin. What do you think about recycling waste?<br><br>I think it's very important for school school to teach us about environment because it's a world we're living in and and we can help each other to make our world a better place to live.<br><br>I think technology help reduce pollution like technology can help us give the acknowledge to know how to reduce pollution. And sometimes if you want to know something, you need to drive to the library, but now you can use technology to search up the thing you want to know.<br><br>I think it's, I think since it become very overpopulate in Thailand, we need to cut down the forest to start making a house, a condominium, or apartment to live. And sometimes a very popular popular place like maybe in Chiang Mai, uh, it is a environment environment place, but a lot of tourist just don't respect the rule and leave a many trash behind.<br><br>	549	3	You do a good job of using simple sentences correctly when you talk about things you know well, like your schoolwork or what you do in your free time. For example, you said, "I usually walk to school." That’s perfect! You also tried to use some longer, more complex sentences to explain your thoughts, especially about environmental issues, which is fantastic ambition! ✨ For instance, when you explained why planting trees is enjoyable, you said, "I think the activity that might be more enjoyable is planting a tree because it actually help and affect to the world." That's a great attempt at a complex idea! To make it even better, you could say, "I think the activity that might be more enjoyable is planting a tree because it actually **helps** and **affects** the world." Adding the 's' for third-person singular verbs is a small but important detail. Another example is when you said, "tree can photosynthesis." This could be "trees can photosynthesise." Thinking about verb agreements and correct word forms will really tighten up your sentences. Finally, keep building your vocabulary; you used some great words like "safe zone," "photosynthesis," and "reduce plastic use," which shows you're on the right track!	3	Rome, you're good at speaking for longer periods and getting your message across, particularly when you described the pictures and discussed how young people can protect the environment. You gave detailed explanations and stayed on topic, which is excellent! For instance, when you compared planting trees and collecting trash, you offered a clear reason for your preference. You used connecting words like "because," "but," and "I think" to link your ideas together, which helps your listener follow your train of thought. To make your speech even smoother, try to minimise small hesitations and avoid repeating words like "school school." You could try pausing briefly to collect your thoughts instead of filling the silence with repeated words. Practising using a wider variety of linking phrases, such as "on the one hand," "however," or "in addition," will also help you create more sophisticated and flowing explanations. Keep pushing yourself to build on these good habits! 🚀	2	Rome, you are generally easy to understand, which is the most important thing! 👍 However, one area to work on is how you stress syllables in words. Sometimes, the stress falls on the wrong part of the word, like in "people" or "picture," which can make it a little harder for your listener. For example, in words like "collection" or "enjoyable," the stress should be on the second syllable: /kəˈlɛkʃən/ and /ɪnˈdʒɔɪəbəl/. Another thing to be mindful of is making sure you pronounce the final consonant sounds in words. For instance, when you say "about," try to clearly articulate the /t/ sound at the end, and for "hard," make sure the /d/ sound is heard. In Thai, final consonants can sometimes be softened or dropped, which might be why this happens in English. Practising words with clear final sounds, like "went" /wɛnt/ or "good" /ɡʊd/, will really help your words stand out clearly!	5	Rome, your ability to talk with others in a conversation is truly a strong point! 🌟 You were great at starting discussions and responding thoughtfully to In-sun and the interviewer. You showed that you were listening carefully by linking your comments to what others had said, and you actively encouraged more conversation by asking questions like, "What, what about you In-sun?" and "What do you think about recycling waste?" This helped keep the conversation moving and interesting! You also did a fantastic job of giving your opinions with reasons and even respectfully shared a different viewpoint, for example, when you said, "But I think sometimes saving electricity at home might be difficult since we use a lot of air conditioner to help us cool in the summer." This shows you're not afraid to engage deeply with the topics and work together towards an outcome. Keep using these wonderful skills in all your English conversations!	13.0	Rome, it was a pleasure to hear you speak! You show a solid ability to communicate your ideas in English, especially when discussing important topics like the environment. Keep up this excellent effort, and you'll continue to grow your confidence and skills. While your ability to interact with others is a real strength, focusing on your pronunciation and the accuracy of your grammar will help you express yourself even more clearly and effectively. I'm excited to see you continue to make progress!<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Rome:<br>✔️ Pay close attention to the end of your words. When you say words like "about" or "hard," try to make sure those final sounds (/t/ and /d/) are clear.<br>✔️ Practise stressing the important parts of words and sentences. For instance, in words like "people" or "picture," the stress usually falls on the first part, not the last.<br>✔️ When you're speaking, pause and think about how you're connecting your sentences, especially when you're explaining a reason or a consequence. Using linking words correctly will really help!	B2	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
