low your timeline.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For: 👀</b><br>You sometimes used the wrong pronoun, such as him mother instead of <b>his</b> mother. You also said be a elephant instead of <b>an elephant</b> (/ə/ → /ə<b>n</b>/). This happens because Thai speakers often skip the final consonants in words or sounds like /n/. You also used the wrong verb forms at times, saying he want instead of he <b>wanted</b>.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better: 🚀</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice Pronouns:</b> Record yourself telling the story and listen specifically for words like he, his, and him to make sure they match the person you are talking about.</li><li><b>Articles and Vowels:</b> Practice saying <b>an</b> before words that start with vowel sounds, like an elephant or an apple.</li><li><b>Past Tense Endings:</b> Focus on adding the -ed sound to past tense verbs like wanted or started so the story sounds finished.</li></ul><br>Great job using new vocabulary to tell a historical story! 📚	3	Ken, you followed the structure of the book review task very well from start to finish. Organizing a long talk is a big challenge, but you moved through the different parts of the story logically. You received a score of 3 out of 5 for this section.<br><br><b>What You Did Well: ✨</b><br>You used an excellent Imagine hook to start your talk and get the listeners interested in the elephant's life. Your use of transition words like After that, The turning point, and To sum up made it very easy to follow your ideas. You covered all the main parts of the story, including the climax and the final resolution.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For: 👀</b><br>You did not mention who wrote the book, which was a required part of the review task. There were also many pauses and filler words like yeah or um throughout your talk. This makes your story sound a bit choppy and stops the flow of your ideas. Sometimes you repeated words when you were thinking of what to say next.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better: 🚀</b><br><u