ample, you said "start to shaking" instead of "started to shake." You also said "many character" instead of "many characters" /ˈkærəktə**z**/. Sometimes you used the present tense instead of the past, like saying "become" /bɪˈkʌm/ instead of "became" /bɪˈkeɪ**m**/.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice past tense</b>: When telling a story, try to use past tense verbs like "became" or "began" consistently.</li><li><b>Check verb patterns</b>: Remember that "to" is followed by the base form, like "start to shake," instead of "shaking."</li><li><b>Plural nouns</b>: Watch out for words after "many" or "all," and make sure to add an "s" to the noun to show there is more than one.</li></ul><br>Your vocabulary choice shows you are ready for more complex topics! 🎯	3	Sun, you did a great job following the structure of the book review and keeping the story interesting. It can be tricky to organize a long talk, but you managed to cover the beginning, middle, and end. You are already communicating well, and now we can look at how to connect your ideas even better.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your "Imagine" hook was a very strong way to start your talk. You used simple connecting words like "so," "after that," and "because" to help the listener follow the plot. Your explanation of the main character's sacrifice gave the story a clear ending.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You missed the name of the author, which was one of the requirements for this task. There were also a few long pauses where you were searching for words, which can happen when you are thinking about Thai and English at the same time. Your conclusion was a bit fast, so it would be better to explain more about why you recommend the book.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Use a checklist</b>: Before you speak, make sure you have all the points like the book title and author written down so you don't forget them.</li><li><b>Expand your reasons</b>: Instead of saying you "vibe" with characters, explain one specific thing they did that you liked to give more detail.</li><li><b>Smooth transitions</b>: Practice using phrases like "In addition" or "However" to make your speech sound more professional and connected.</li></ul><br>You have a natural way of telling stories that keeps the audience listening! 🎯	2	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 2/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Sun, thank you for your book review! You have a good ear for intonation, which is a great foundation to build on. With a score of 2 out of 5, we have a clear path to help you sound more like a B1 speaker. 💪<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your intonation was strong! The pitch of your voice moved up and down, which made your speaking more engaging to listen to. You already understand the "melody" of English speaking.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>We need to focus on your consonant sounds. You often missed the endings of words, like /-d/, /-t/, /-ɪd/, /-s/, and /-v/. For example, in words like "lived" or "books," those final sounds are important for meaning. Also, your speaking was "choppy"—this means you put a gap between every single word (one. who. has.). This made your syllable stress sound a bit strange, like in "noVEL" where the end was too strong (/ˈnɒvl̩/ → /nɒ<b>V’vɛl</b>/). <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>The "Linking" game:</b> Practice saying pairs of words as if they were one word. Try "he-is" or "read-a." Think of it like a chain with no broken links!</li><li><b>Check your word endings:</b> Look at the last letter of every word in your script. Practice saying them out loud and "snap" those final consonants!</li><li><b>Listen to word stress:</b> Most English two-syllable words are stressed at the beginning. Practice saying <b>NO-vel</b>, not no-VEL.</li></ul><br>You've got the music down; let's fix the sounds! 🎯	\N	\N	11.0	Sun, your "Imagine" hook was a fantastic way to start your review and it really grabbed my attention! You told the story of Kim Dokja clearly and used some great vocabulary like "sacrifice" and "unexpected." To reach a Band 5 (B2), make sure to always include the author's name, work on using past tense verbs consistently, and try to reduce the pauses between your sentences. 🎯	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
