ount of time and kept your answers relevant to the questions, which shows you can manage your ideas well. You used connecting words like "because" and "so" to link your thoughts. To develop this further, try to reduce hesitations by thinking for a moment before you speak, and aim to use a wider variety of phrases to introduce your points. For instance, instead of always starting with "I think," you could say "In my opinion," or "I believe that." You covered all the points expected in the tasks, and your word count was excellent, showing your ability to produce extended spoken English.<br><br>**Pronunciation: 1/5**<br>Your intonation was generally appropriate, helping us understand your meaning. However, a key area for you to work on is making your sounds clearer, especially the /s/ sound. Words like "schools" or "virus" need a distinct, hissing /s/. Also, pay close attention to pronouncing final consonant sounds in words like "drink" /drɪŋk/, "worth" /wɜːθ/, and "it's" /ɪts/. Sometimes these endings were not clearly heard. This is a common challenge for Thai speakers because the Thai language often drops or softens final consonants and does not have the same sharp /s/ sound, but mastering these will make you much more easily understood. Practice these sounds consciously!<br><br>**Interactive Communication: 4/5**<br>You did a good job of talking with your partner, Tiw! You initiated discussions, responded thoughtfully, and linked your ideas to what Tiw was saying, which kept the conversation flowing naturally. To aim for a top score, try to push a bit more towards making clear decisions together, especially in the collaborative tasks. For example, after discussing different options, you could say something like, "So, are we both agreeing that playing online games is the most fun?" This helps to really negotiate and confirm an outcome as a pair.	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
