eed to have a try. What I mean is ... when you have a talent, but you don't have any energy to work out, you don't have any willpowers ... no, there will no be result. Thank you for listening and have a good day. [laugh]	415	3	Arther, you did a great job sharing this classic story with so much energy. Telling a story with a clear moral can be difficult, but you made your message very clear. Your score shows you have a good foundation in using English to express your ideas.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You used a variety of interesting words like "determined," "willpower," and "childish" to describe the story and its characters. It was great to see you attempt complex sentences, especially during your introduction and the final summary. Your use of past tense was mostly clear, which helped the listener follow the sequence of events in the race.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>Sometimes you mixed up your tenses by using present tense when describing things that happened in the past, such as "it teach us" instead of "it taught us." There were also a few small errors with verb patterns, such as "turtle was tell him" instead of "the turtle told him." Watch out for word forms too; you used "willpowers" (/ˈwɪl.paʊ.əz/) which is usually an uncountable noun, so just "willpower" (/**ˈwɪl.paʊ.ə**/) is better.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Master the Past Tense</b>: Practice telling short stories using only past tense verbs. Focus on irregular verbs like "ran," "fell," and "won."</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement</b>: Remember to add an "s" for third-person singular in the present tense (e.g., "the story teaches"). This is a common area for Thai learners to check.</li><li><b>Uncountable Nouns</b>: Look up nouns that do not need an "s" at the end. Words like "willpower," "advice," and "information" stay the same whether the amount is big or small.</li></ul><br>You have a real talent for storytelling, so keep practicing these grammar poi