nce structures effectively, especially when talking about things you like, such as music and friends. However, there were times when your grammar made it a little hard to understand your message fully. For example, you said, "the two pictures are friends groups that are they talk about uh, something of their work." A clearer way to say this would be, "the two pictures show groups of friends who are talking about their work." Another instance was "in the picture too, it's a he love when he do and he love..." which could be improved to, "in the second picture, he loves what he is doing." Also, try to be careful with subject-verb agreement, like "it's a guy that are relax" which should be "it's a guy who *is* relaxing." Focusing on these basic structures will help you to express yourself with much more accuracy. You used some good vocabulary, but sometimes a wider range of words would help you be more precise. For example, instead of repeating "team work," you could use "team effort" or "collaboration." Expanding your vocabulary will allow you to share your thoughts more specifically and avoid repetition. Keep trying to use new words you learn every day! 🚀	1	You made a good attempt to extend your answers beyond short phrases, Mirin, which is a positive step! You mostly stayed on topic, but sometimes your answers were a bit short, and you hesitated quite a lot, which can make it a little difficult for the listener to follow. To make your speech flow more smoothly, try to prepare a few extra details or examples in your mind when you answer questions. This will help you to speak for longer stretches without pausing too much. You used simple connecting words like "and" and "because," which is a good start. To improve, think about how you can use other words, like "but," "so," or "although," to link your sentences and show clearer relationships between your ideas. For instance, instead of saying two separate short sentences, try to combine them with a more complex linker. 📝	3	Mirin, your intonation, or the way your voice rises and falls, was generally good, which helped to make your speech sound natural! However, you spoke quite softly, and this sometimes made parts of what you said difficult to hear and understand. To make sure everyone can hear your clear ideas, try to project your voice a little more. You might also want to practice articulating your words more distinctly, ensuring that each sound is clear, even if you are speaking quickly. Sometimes Thai speakers might speak softly in English, but for effective communication in an international setting, it's important to be easily heard. Try reading English sentences aloud every day, focusing on speaking clearly and at a good volume. 🗣️	2	You did a good job of responding to questions and initiating some parts of the conversation, Mirin! You showed you were engaged with the task and your partner. However, there were times when you gave very short answers, like just "No," and needed the interviewer to ask you "Why?" to help you say more. To improve this, try to add a reason or an example immediately after giving a short answer. For instance, if you say "No," you could follow up with "because..." or "I think this is true since..." This will help you keep the conversation flowing and show that you can develop ideas more independently without much prompting. Keep practising asking your partner for their thoughts too, like "What do you think?" to make the interaction even more balanced and extended! 👍	7.0	Mirin, it was lovely to hear you speak in your Term 1 Finals! You made a good effort to communicate your ideas clearly and shared your opinions on a variety of topics. To help your English speaking truly grow, focusing on expanding your sentence structures and linking your ideas more smoothly will make a real difference. Keep practising, and you'll find your confidence and clarity continuing to develop! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Continued Progress:**<br>✔️ Try to extend your answers