an be tricky because it is so different from Thai. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows that you are doing a great job using different words and sentences. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a variety of words to describe the story, such as "investigating" and "criminal." You showed good control of the past tense most of the time when talking about the characters. Your sentences were clear and helped me understand the relationship between Ash and Eiji. You also used some nice complex phrases to explain the plot.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Sometimes the subject and verb did not match correctly. For example, you said "Ash don't know" instead of "Ash <b>didn't</b> know" or "he meet" instead of "he <b>met</b>." You also added an extra "s" to some words where it wasn't needed, like "one years" instead of "one <b>year</b>." Try to remember that past events always need past tense verbs.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice past tense verbs:</b> Make a list of common irregular verbs like "met," "won," and "died." Say them out loud in sentences to get used to the sound.</li><li><b>Check singular and plural:</b> Before you speak, think about if you are talking about one thing or many things. Remember that "one" always goes with a singular word like "one <b>year</b>."</li><li><b>Watch verb agreement:</b> Practice using "doesn't" and "don't" correctly. Since the story is in the past, focus on using "didn't" for all subjects.</li></ul>One encouraging sentence with emoji. 🎯	3	Florence, you did a great job organizing your thoughts for this manga review. It is brave to talk about such a sad story in another language! Your score of 3 out of 5 means you followed the task well, but we can make your flow even smoother. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You included almost all the required parts of the review, including the title and the author. You set the scene in New York clearly and explained the climax and the ending very well. It was also great to hear your specific opinion about why the ending made you feel sad.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You missed using one of the four specific hooks, such as an "Imagine" statement or a rhetorical question. There were also many pauses and filler words like "uh" and "like" while you were thinking. These hesitations made it a bit difficult to follow the exciting parts of the story and slowed down your pace.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Use a strong hook:</b> Start your next talk with a question or an "Imagine" sentence. This helps catch the attention of your listeners right away.</li><li><b>Reduce filler words:</b> Try to replace "uh" and "like" with a short silence. This will make you sound more confident and professional.</li><li><b>Plan your transitions:</b> Practice words like "first," "then," and "finally." These words help you move from one part of the story to the next without pausing too much.</li></ul>One encouraging sentence with emoji. 🎯	3	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 3/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Florence, thank you for your book review! Your intonation was good, and you made the story sound interesting. With a score of 3 out of 5, you've shown an understanding of English rhythm. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your intonation was a highlight! You used your voice well to convey the meaning of the story. You have an instinct for which words should be stressed to make your meaning clear to the listener.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>We need to watch out for those final consonants. You often missed the /d/ in "died" (/daɪ d/) and the /z/ sound in "was" (/wəz/). These little sounds are important for meaning. Also, there were moments of "choppiness" where you spoke word-by-word (he. was. a. leader.). This makes it very hard for the listener to keep up with your points.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Think in phrases:</b> Try to see your sentences as groups of words. Practice saying "he was a leader" as one smooth flow of sound without any gaps.</li><li><b>Exaggerate the endings:</b> Try to say your final /d/ and /s/ sounds loudly. This will help your brain remember to pronounce them!</li><li><b>Listen and Shadow:</b> Listen to a B1 level recording and try to mimic the "flow." Notice how the words stick together in strings.</li></ul><br>You have great rhythm! 🎯	\N	\N	13.0	Florence, you gave a clear and emotional review of Banana Fish with a good range of vocabulary and clear descriptions of the characters. 👍 To reach a Band 5 (B2), try to reduce the use of filler words like "like" and "uh" to make your speech flow more smoothly, and be sure to use one of the four specific hooks to start your talk. 🎯	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
