d I think so cooking too, so I think I should this one too. And I think a lot of students...<br><br>LIN-LIN: Um, I think I think it's important for people because if we know know what the food come from, so we can can choose the the we can choose the thing that more healthy and will be good for our body.<br><br>LIN-LIN: Um, I think yes because a lot of young people they like to eat the food that unhealthy because because it is yummy and we can find a lot of a lot of restaurant that unhealthy because it is really yummy for a lot of people like to eat it.<br><br>LIN-LIN: I think parents can can teach can teach their kid to eat the food that healthy because it will be more good for their body, so they will doesn't want to go to hospital to work.<br><br>LIN-LIN: Um, I think it's it's good because it is good but it doesn't... but it isn't important because we can buy it in the store, but I think if we learn about cooking it good because cooking by yourself is it's really happy.	582	2	Lin-Lin, you're doing a good job of expressing yourself on everyday topics! 👍 You can put simple sentences together to share your thoughts. For example, you said, "I really enjoy cooking because I like to cooking with my mom and I like to baking too." This clearly shows your enthusiasm! To make your sentences even more precise, remember that after "like," we usually say "cooking" or "to cook," not "to cooking." So, it would be "I really enjoy cooking because I like *cooking* with my mom and I like *baking* too." Another example is "my mom like to teach me to cooking," which should be "my mom *likes* to teach me to *cook*." Sometimes, like many Thai learners, you might mix up words that sound similar or use a verb in the wrong form. Focusing on these small but important grammar points, such as verb tenses and prepositions, will really help your sentences sound more accurate and natural. For instance, when you said "go to buying food in in market outside," practicing prepositions would help you say "go to *buy* food *at* a market *outside*."	2	You produce quite a lot of English, Lin-Lin, which is fantastic! 🎉 You always try to give longer answers, which is a great way to develop your speaking skills. Your contributions are almost always on topic, too! However, sometimes you repeat certain words or phrases, which can make your ideas a little less clear. For example, when you described the pictures, you said, "He he is very very he is very happy that I like to go to buying food in in market too." This shows you are working to extend your ideas, but repeating "he he is very very he is very happy" makes it a little harder to follow. To make your ideas flow more smoothly, try using different words to say the same thing, and think about using a wider range of connecting words, not just "and" and "because." This will help your longer sentences stay organised and easy for your listener to understand.	2	Lin-Lin, you are mostly understandable, which is the main goal of speaking! 🙌 However, your voice is quite flat, and it would be really helpful to work on using more intonation – that's the way your voice goes up and down when you speak. This will make your English sound more lively and natural, and much easier for listeners to follow. We also noticed that you often drop the final /t/ and /d/ sounds in words, for example, in "outside" /aʊtˈsaɪ/ and sometimes the /k/ sound in words like "think" /θɪn/. These small sound omissions can sometimes make it a little harder to hear exactly what you mean. Practising these final sounds and trying to add more expression to your voice will make a big difference!	3	You do a good job of starting conversations and responding to questions, Lin-Lin! 👍 You're able to keep the discussion going without needing much help, which is excellent. For instance, you clearly presented your idea for the school project, saying, "I think designing poster about healthy snack because..." You also give reasons for your opinions, which helps to develop the interaction. To improve even further, try to listen carefully to what your partner says and then try to link your ideas more directly to theirs, or even ask them for more details. Sometimes, you tend to agree with your partner rather than adding a new idea or building on what they'sve said. This is a great skill to develop for more engaging conversations!	9.0	Hello Lin-Lin! 👋 It was lovely to hear you speak. You're doing a wonderful job of getting your ideas across and participating in conversations. You have a good base for communicating in English! To really help your speaking become even clearer and more natural, let's focus on making sure your verbs are always in the correct form and thinking about how to connect your sentences more smoothly. Keep practising, you're doing well! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ Pay close attention to verb forms, especially when you use "like" (e.g., "I *like cooking*" or "I *like to cook*," not "I like to cooking").<br>✔️ Practise using a wider variety of connecting words like "however," "although," or "therefore" to make your ideas flow more smoothly between sentences.<br>✔️ Try to listen to how native speakers use intonation (the rise and fall of their voice) and experiment with adding more variety to your own voice when you speak.	B1	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
33169	2026-01-09	2026-01-09	2026	Book Review	Good morning everyone. Today I'm going to talk about a book called Percy Jackson and the Olympians the Lightning Thief. So ... uh written by Rick Riordan. \n<br>\nSo what would you do if the Greek gods were real and you were secretly one of their children? The story takes place in New York City in 21st century. The ... the main character is a 12 year old boy named Percy. At first Percy is just a boy who gets in trouble often in school but he later discovers that he is a demigod, a son of Poseidon. And so things get interesting when Percy got blamed for stealing Zeus's lightning bolt. And so he goes on a dangerous quest with his friends Annabeth and Grover to find the real thief and stop the war between the gods. In the end, Percy returns the lightning bolt to Zeus and stop the war but the ... the real thief is someone closer than he thought and this is just the beginning of their plan. \n<br>\nI really ... I really enjoy the fighting scene on like when they are on their quest because it's really easy to imagine due to the excellent explanation in the book. And however I found it a bit confusing of the ... some of the Greek mythology in the book because like it's my first time reading a book that has a Greek mythology in it but it's really interesting so that it keeps me reading till the end of the book. \n<br>\nTo sum up ... uh Percy Jackson and the Olympians the Lightning Thief is a fantasy story about adventure, friendship and self-discovery. It combines Greek mythology with the modern world which makes the story interesting, fun and easy to enjoy. [laugh] Thanks ... uh thanks for listening. That's all from me.	305	4	Milan, you spoke very clearly about Percy Jackson and showed great control over the story. English grammar can be tricky when describing book plots, but you handled it well. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows you are a strong B1 speaker.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a variety of useful vocabulary like "demigod," "secretly," and "self-discovery" which fit the topic perfectly. Your use of relative clauses like "a boy who gets in trouble" helped you combine ideas smoothly. You also used the phrase "due to," which is an effective way to explain your reasons using more advanced language.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You had a few small slips with noun types and verb consistency. For example, you said "has a Greek mythology," but mythology is usually an uncountable idea, so you should say "has Greek mythology." Also, remember to stay in the same tense when telling a story because you mixed past and present in "Percy returns the lightning bolt and stop the war."<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Verb Tense Consistency:</b> Choose either the past tense or the present tense and stick to it for the whole plot summary.</li><li><b>Countable vs. Uncountable:</b> Review words like "mythology" or "advice" that do not usually take "a" or an "s."</li><li><b>Refining Phrasing:</b> Instead of "confusing of the," try using "confused by the" to sound more natural.</li></ul><br>Your vocabulary choices really helped bring the story to life! 🎯	4	Milan, your book review followed a very logical path and was easy to follow. Organizing a long talk can be difficult, especially when you have so many plot details to share. You achieved a score of 4 out of 5 because you included every required part of the task.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a great range of linking words like "so," "at first," "however," and "to sum up." This made the transition between the story and your opinion very clear. Your rhetorical question hook at the start was an excellent way to grab the audience's attention immediately.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were some moments where you repeated words or had short hesitations while thinking of the next point. For example, you said "I really... I really enjoy" and "The... the main character." These small breaks affect your fluency, although they did not stop me from understanding your message.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice Fluency:</b> Try to pause silently instead of saying "uh" or repeating a word when you are thinking of what to say next.</li><li><b>Vary Your Connectors:</b> Instead of starting sentences with "and so," try using words like "consequently" or "eventually" to show more range.</li><li><b>Expand on Reasons:</b> You mentioned the fighting scenes were easy to imagine, so giving one specific example from the book would make your opinion even stronger.</li></ul><br>You followed the structure perfectly and told a very exciting story! 🎯	3	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 3/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Milan, you have a loud and confident speaking voice! This confidence made your book review easy to listen to. With a score of 3 out of 5, you are meeting the standard—let's look at how to reach the next level. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your confidence is your biggest strength! You spoke clearly and with good intonation. Your syllable stress was fine, and you made an effort to link your words together. This helped your speaking feel quite fluent.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>The main issue was that many important sounds were dropped or changed. For example, you missed the /s/ in "Percy," used /ʃ/ instead of /tʃ/ for "children" (/ˈtʃɪldrən/ → /<b>SH</b>ɪldrən/), and dropped the /c/ in "character." You also missed many final consonants like the /d/ in "end" (/end/ → /en/). These errors happened many times throughout your talk. Confident speaking is great, but these sounds are necessary for higher marks.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Practice 'ch' vs 'sh':</b> These are different sounds! Practice saying "cheap" vs "sheep" and notice how the /tʃ/ sound starts with a little "stop" of air in your mouth.</li><li><b>Final consonant check:</b> Focus especially on words ending in /d/ and /s/. Practice saying them clearly so they don't disappear!</li><li><b>Slow down for clarity:</b> Confidence is fine, but slowing down a little bit will give you time to pronounce every sound correctly.</li></ul><br>Great energy, Milan! 🎯	\N	\N	15.0	Milan, you delivered a very organized book review with a fantastic rhetorical question hook that really engaged the audience. 📚 You showed strong control over B1 grammar and used linking words effectively to tell Percy's story from beginning to end. To reach a Band 5 (B2), try to keep your verb tenses consistent throughout the plot summary, reduce small hesitations to improve your flow, and provide more specific details when giving your opinion. 🎯	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
