xcellent work!	B2	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
28758	2026-01-09	2026-01-09	2026	Book Review	Good morning guys, I am Mymind. Today ... oh, recently I read ... I read a story, a fascinating story about cooking, baking desserts ... yeah, something like that. And the ... the story called Min and Mina the Junior Bakery Chef. Yeah ... [laugh] So ...\n\nImagine you just a normal person who loves to baking as a hobby, then one day you got ... then you got leads to a big competition because you really good at it. So the main character name is Mina and she is a ... talented baker that loved to baking, yeah.\n\nSo the story set in a summer day, in a hurry the girl named Fahsai, hurry to meet her friend who is sick, see if she getting better and they have a conversation together after haven't meet each other for a long time. So after they talk together finished, the ... her friend who sick said she want to eat something nice to get more better after being sick for a long time in the hospital because she just eat like ... boring dishes, so want to eat something nice and sweet.\n\nSo that day Fahsai ... go back to her dorm that live ... that she lives with Mina, the girl who loves to ... good at baking. So Fahsai tell Mina what she ... what she should ... uh ... give her friend ... give a good ... good dessert for her friend who sick. A nice dish. So Mina have an idea so she decide to make a pancake for Fahsai to give to her friend. \n\nSo after that, the next day after Mina finish ... finished to made that pancake give to Fahsai, Fahsai go to the hospital and give her friend and she eat it. She like ... Wow, this taste is so wonderful, I have never tasted something like this before for a long time now. \n\nSo after she ... Mina have practice a lot and making a lot of baking, yeah she also have bakery, one day she got a message to go to competition so she ... yeah, got win the competition. \n<br>\nSo about this story the thing that I like is the ... it makes me feel like ... enjoy it, yeah. And I ... however I think they should like give something more about cooking, like more the ... the information about cooking ... yeah to give some let us know something like that, yeah. \n<br>\nSo I hope this book ... makes you guys enjoy it. Thank you. [clapping]	429	4	Mymind, you did a good job telling the story of Min and Mina in your book review. Talking about stories in English is a big step for any student. Your effort shows that you are becoming a more confident speaker. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You used many good words like "fascinating" and "competition" to describe the story. It was easy to understand the main points because you used past tense verbs correctly most of the time. Your use of words like "who" to describe characters made your sentences sound more advanced. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>Be careful with verb patterns after words like "finish" or "love." Instead of "finished to made," you should say "finished <b>making</b>." Also, remember that "better" already means more good, so you do not need to say "more better." Try to include words like "a" or "the" before nouns, for example saying "a competition" instead of just "competition." <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Master verb patterns</b>: Some verbs are followed by "-ing." Practice phrases like "enjoy baking" or "finish eating" to make your speech smoother.</li><li><b>Fix double comparatives</b>: Remember that short adjectives like "good" or "fast" change to "better" or "faster" without the word "more." Check your sentences for these small slips.</li><li><b>Article practice</b>: Try to use "a" when you mention something for the first time. Reading English stories aloud can help you hear where these small words belong.</li></ul><br>You are clearly working hard on your grammar and it is paying off. 🎯	3	Mymind, your book review followed the required structure very well. Sharing a summary and your opinion can be difficult, but you stayed on track. You reached a solid level today, and there are just a few steps to make your talk even better. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You used a great hook by asking the listener to "imagine" being a baker. This caught my attention right at the start. You linked your ideas together well using words like "so," "after that," and "however." Your description of the scene at the hospital and the dorm helped me follow the story clearly. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You missed one important detail which was the name of the author. Try to give more time to the climax and the ending of the story. You said "she got win the competition" very quickly, but it would be better to explain how she felt or what happened during the contest. Also, try to use fewer filler words like "yeah" or "something like that" when you are thinking of what to say next. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Use a checklist</b>: Before you speak, make a small list of the required points like "Author" and "Climax." This ensures you do not forget any parts of the task.</li><li><b>Expand the ending</b>: Spend more time describing the most exciting part of the book. Tell us about the tension of the competition to make your story more interesting.</li><li><b>Reduce fillers</b>: When you need to think, it is okay to be silent for a second. Try to avoid repeating "yeah" or "something like that" as it can distract the listener from your great ideas.</li></ul><br>You have a good sense of how to organize a talk, so keep practicing! 🎯	4	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 4/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Mymind, your pronunciation in this book review was strong! You showed real control over English sounds, and your speaking was easy to listen to. With a score of 4 out of 5, you are doing well—let's look at the details. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your intonation was effective and sounded natural. You also had no major problems with individual consonant sounds, which is a great foundation. Your syllable stress was fine. It is clear you practiced your speaking! <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>The main area to improve is your linking. You often spoke slowly, and your hesitations made your speech sound choppy. In natural English, words should flow together (like "and-then"). When there are too many gaps, it can be harder for people to follow your meaning. Also, watch out for overstressing the final syllables in words like "characTER" (/ˈkærəktər/ → /kæræk<b>Tɝː</b>/). This is a common Thai pattern, but we usually stress the first part: <b>CHA</b>-rac-ter. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Speed up slightly:</b> Try reading your transcript while listening to a metronome or a slow beat. This will help you keep a steady pace.</li><li><b>Practice linking:</b> Focus on small phrases where words connect. Instead of "I . like . it", try to say "I-like-it" as one smooth unit.</li><li><b>Check early stress:</b> For longer English words, the stress is often at the beginning. Listen to how native speakers say words like "character" and copy their rhythm!</li></ul><br>Your foundations are solid! 🎯	\N	\N	15.0	Mymind, you used the "Imagine" hook effectively and told the story of the bakery competition with good energy. 🍰 To reach a Band 5 (B2), make sure to include the author's name, expand on the climax of the story, and work on reducing filler words like "yeah" to improve your flow. 🎯	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
