b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Sometimes you swapped between the present and past tense in the same sentence. For example, you said "the hare run" instead of "the hare <b>ran</b> /ræn/." You also missed some articles like saying "win a race" instead of "win <b>the</b> race." These are common patterns because Thai doesn't use word endings for tense. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Tense Consistency:</b> Practice keeping all your story actions in the past tense. Practice saying "ran," "thought," and "started" out loud to make your storytelling smoother.</li><li><b>Article Usage:</b> Pay attention to words like "a" and "the." Use "the" when you are talking about a specific thing the listener already knows about.</li><li><b>Verb Patterns:</b> Practice using the passive form "written by" instead of "write by." This will help you sound more natural when introducing authors.</li></ul><br>You are doing a great job with your sentence structures! 👏	3	Yiwa, you organized your talk very well and followed all the required steps for the book review. This skill is challenging because it requires connecting many ideas together in order. You earned a 3 out of 5 for how you managed your speech. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a variety of linking words like "so," "when," and "finally" to move from one part of the story to the next. Your hook was effective because you asked a question that made the audience think. You included every part of the story from the beginning to the moral at the end. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were several long pauses during your talk where you stopped to think. These hesitations made the flow of your speech feel a bit broken. You also started many sentences with the word "and." Try to vary your transitions to make your speech sound more professional. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Reduce Hesitation:</b> Try recording yourself telling the story several times. Each time, try to speak without stopping for more than two seconds.</li><li><b>Use Diverse Linkers:</b> Instead of starting every sentence with "and," try using words like "however" or "unexpectedly." This will help your story feel more exciting for the listener.</li><li><b>Expand Your Climax:</b> Spend a little more time describing the feelings during the race. Adding details about how the hare felt when he saw the turtle near the finish line will make your talk more engaging.</li></ul><br>Your story was very easy to follow from start to finish! 🌟	4	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 4/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Yiwa, you have a lovely speaking voice and your intonation was strong! You did a great job making your book review sound interesting. With a score of 4 out of 5, you are performing at a high level—here is the breakdown. 🌟<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your intonation was a strength. You used the rise and fall of your voice well to keep the listener engaged. Your linking was also good, which helped your speech flow smoothly. Most of your individual sounds were clear and easy to understand.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>Watch out for past tense endings! For example, you missed the /-ɪd/ sound in words like "started" (/ˈstɑːrtɪd/). It is important to pronounce these final sounds clearly so your listener knows you are talking about the past. Also, the hesitations in your speech made it bit choppy. Because you were relying on memorized sentences, there were many gaps. This makes it harder for the listener to follow your story.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Focus on -ed endings:</b> Practice saying words like "started," "wanted," and "needed." Make sure we can hear that extra syllable at the end!</li><li><b>Use notes, not a script:</b> Instead of memorizing full sentences, try using keywords. This will make your speaking feel more natural and reduce those long hesitations.</li><li><b>Practice with 'connected' sounds:</b> Watch videos about "linking" in English. Try to make your words connect so there are no gaps between them.</li></ul><br>You have great natural ability! 🎯	\N	\N	15.0	Yiwa, you did a great job following the structure of the book review and using a clever rhetorical question as a hook to engage your audience. Your use of past tense was generally strong, and you successfully included the moral and a clear recommendation. To reach a Band 5 (B2), you should work on reducing the long pauses between your ideas to improve your fluency and flow. Focus on keeping your verb tenses consistent throughout the whole story and try using a wider range of transition words instead of starting every sentence with "and." 🎯	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
