example, we are we don't have any the healthy skill and we maybe eating everything, but school can teach us about what we're eating.<br><br>PEPTINE: I think the hardest challenge for me is uh, every people doesn't trust any, I don't trust other people, so they're doing everything that's eating fast food and not doing any exercises, playing games every day.	412	3	Peptine, you show a good understanding of how to put sentences together, especially when talking about familiar topics like your hobbies and where you live. For example, you said, "I usually play badminton with my father like every Sunday" which is clear and correct! 👍 You also attempted some more complex sentences, such as "if you sleep for six to eight hours above, you your mind will get rest," which is a good effort. However, sometimes these attempts had small grammatical errors or used slightly imprecise vocabulary that could make your meaning a little less clear. For instance, you said, "can make our feel relaxing" which should be "can make us feel relaxed," and "can give us a advice" which should be "can give us advice." Also, "every the part of body" would be more natural as "every part of the body." These are common learning points for Thai speakers, as the structure of English grammar, particularly with articles and countable/uncountable nouns, can differ from Thai. To help with this, try focusing on subject-verb agreement and the correct use of articles (a/an/the) and prepositions. 📖 You could also pay close attention to adjectives and adverbs to ensure you're using the correct form to describe actions or states, like using "relaxed" instead of "relaxing" when describing how you feel. ✨ Finally, when you learn new vocabulary, try to learn phrases or sentences where the words are used in context, rather than just individual words.	3	You did a good job of giving extended answers and keeping your contributions relevant to the topic! For instance, when describing the pictures in Part 2, you provided clear comparisons between relaxing with others and relaxing alone. You also used basic linking words like "and," "because," and "so" to connect your ideas. 😊 However, there were times when you hesitated a bit, and you occasionally repeated ideas or phrases, particularly when describing the pictures. This is a normal part of learning, but we can work on making your speech flow even more smoothly! To help with this, when you're giving extended answers, try to think about having a clear beginning, middle, and end to your response. 📝 You could also practice using a wider range of cohesive devices to link your sentences and paragraphs more smoothly, such as "furthermore," "in addition," or "however." This helps your ideas connect more logically. 🔗 Finally, to reduce repetition, try to vary your sentence structures and vocabulary as much as possible, perhaps by using synonyms or rephrasing ideas.	1	Peptine, your pronunciation often made your speech difficult to understand because of how frequently you drop final consonant sounds. For example, in words like "talking," the final /ŋ/ sound was almost completely absent, and the /t/ in "about" was often dropped. Similarly, when you said "each other," both the /tʃ/ and /ð/ sounds weren't very clear, which significantly affects intelligibility. Additionally, you sometimes overstressed final syllables, such as in "picture," which can make your speech sound less natural and disrupt the usual rhythm of English. In Thai, final consonants are often unreleased or dropped, and some English sounds like /ð/ and /θ/ do not exist, which can lead to these pronunciation patterns. To improve, try consciously over-articulating final consonant sounds when you practice speaking. 🗣️ Focus on words that end with /t/, /d/, and /ŋ/. You could also practice tongue twisters or specific sound exercises for sounds like /tʃ/ (as in "choose," "challenge") and /ð/ (as in "this," "other") to train your mouth and tongue for these new sounds. 👅 Recording yourself and listening back can be incredibly helpful to identify where you're dropping sounds and then working to correct them. 🎧	3	You did well at initiating conversations and responding appropriately to both the interviewer and your partner! You maintained the interaction by giving your opinions and reasons, such as when you discussed the benefits of team sports for improving health. 😊 This kept the conversation flowing. However, you could work on linking your contributions more consistently to what your partner had just said. Sometimes, your comments ran alongside your partner's ideas rather than explicitly building on them or asking for more details, which helps create a richer and more collaborative discussion. To make your interactions even stronger, try to acknowledge your partner's points before presenting your own. For example, you could say, "I agree with that point, and I'd also like to add..." or "That's an interesting perspective; what do you think about...?" 🤝 Asking open-ended questions can also encourage your partner to expand on their ideas, making the discussion more dynamic. 🤔 Remember that in a speaking test, the interaction with your partner is a key part of showing your English skills!	10.0	Peptine, your contributions in the speaking test were clear, and you showed a good ability to express your ideas on familiar topics! Your overall score for this speaking assessment is 10 out of 20, which maps to a B1 level on the CEFR scale. 👍 The most important area for you to focus on right now is making your pronunciation clearer, especially by ensuring all your consonant sounds are distinct, particularly at the ends of words. Keep practicing, and you'll find your confidence growing even more as your spoken English becomes easier for everyone to understand. 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ Practice enunciating final consonant sounds like /t/ and /d/ in words like "about" and "friend."<br>✔️ Work on differentiating similar consonant sounds, such as /tʃ/ (as in "cheese" or "each other") and /ð/ (as in "the" or "other").<br>✔️ Record yourself speaking and listen back to identify words where sounds might be "swallowed" or unclear.	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
