 friend like wanted to... okay. I really liked this book because when I was kid, I read it many times and, um, like one week I read it and read it in this book and so I recommend this book into many people to read it and thanks for everyone to listen my story, thank you.	431	4	Ikkyu, you did a great job sharing your love for Harry Potter! Speaking about such a famous book can be tricky because there is so much to say. You showed good control of your English and scored a 4 out of 5. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You used the past tense well to describe the story events. Your vocabulary was clear and you used words like "villains," "survived," and "magical" correctly. You also tried to use more complex grammar like the passive voice when talking about Harry being treated like a housemate or being chosen for his fame.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>Sometimes you missed small words like "was" or "to" when speaking. For example, you said "since you born" instead of "since you <b>were born</b>." You also said "listen my story" instead of "listen <b>to</b> my story." These are small details but fixing them will make your English sound more natural and accurate.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Preposition practice:</b> Focus on verbs that always need a preposition like "listen <b>to</b>" or "talk <b>to</b>."</li><li><b>Past participles:</b> Remember to use "was/were" with the word "born" when talking about the past.</li><li><b>Word choice:</b> Try to avoid using "like" too often as a filler word when you are thinking of what to say next.</li></ul><br>You are clearly comfortable using English to tell stories! 🎯	4	Ikkyu, your book review followed the required structure very well! It is often hard to organize a long story, but you kept your ideas in a clear order. You earned a 4 out of 5 for how you managed your talk. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You used a very good "Imagine" hook to start your talk. You covered all the important parts of the story, from the Dursley family to the school. Your use of words like "after that" and "at last" helped the listener follow the timeline easily without getting lost.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You used many filler words like "um," "yeah," and "like" while you were thinking. This makes your speech sound a bit slower and can break the flow of the story. You also repeated the phrase "at last" several times instead of using different words like "finally" or "eventually."<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Vary your connectors:</b> Instead of always saying "at last," try using words like "eventually," "finally," or "in the end."</li><li><b>Practice the hook:</b> Your "Imagine" hook was good, but try to answer the scenario you created to lead more smoothly into the story.</li><li><b>Reduce fillers:</b> Try to pause silently for a second when you think instead of saying "um" or "like" to sound more confident.</li></ul><br>You have a great sense of how to structure a presentation! 🎯	4	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 4/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Ikkyu, this was a major improvement from last time—well done! You have been practicing, and it shows in your clear sounds. With a score of 4 out of 5, you've reached a high standard. Let's look at what's next. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your individual sounds were clear! I could hear an effort to pronounce consonants and plurals clearly. For example, your final /t/ in words like "that" (/ðæt/) was sharp. This level of detail makes your speaking much easier to follow than before. Keep that same energy!<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>Watch out for word stress. Sometimes you put the emphasis on the wrong part of the word—for example, saying "WIZard" (/ˈwɪzərd/) is correct, but be careful not to flatten it too much. The main challenge remains "choppiness." There were many hesitations that broke the links between your words. In English, we try to slide from one word to the next without stopping. These gaps are the main thing blocking your flow.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>"Chunking" practice:</b> Instead of thinking word-by-word, try to think in groups of 3-4 words. Practice saying "I would like to tell you" as one continuous sound.</li><li><b>Listen to word rhythm:</b> Use a dictionary website to hear the stress on new words. Notice how the first syllable is often louder and longer (like <b>WIZ</b>-ard).</li><li><b>Shadowing:</b> Find a short English clip and try to speak at the exact same time as the speaker. This will help you find a natural rhythm.</li></ul><br>Massive progress, Ikkyu! 🎯	\N	\N	16.0	Ikkyu, you gave a clear and organized review of Harry Potter with a great "Imagine" hook! You used the past tense effectively to describe the plot and your vocabulary fit the magical theme well. To reach a Band 5 (B2), work on reducing filler words like "um" and "like" to sound more fluent, vary your linking words instead of repeating "at last," and check your prepositions in phrases like "listen to my story." 🎯	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
