p" or "In conclusion" to give your talk a smoother ending.</li><li><b>Reduce pauses</b>: Record yourself and listen for the "ums" so you can practice speaking more continuously without stopping.</li></ul><br>Your storytelling skills are improving every time you practice! 🎯	1	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 1/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Leo, thank you for sharing your book review with me! You have a good sense of rhythm, but we need to work on making your voice clearer. With a score of 1 out of 5, our goal is to help your listeners understand every word you say. 💪<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your intonation was fine! You understand how the "music" of English should sound, which is a great start. You clearly have a good ear for the pitch of the language.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>The biggest issue was that your voice was soft and many sounds were "muddy." This means your consonants (the sharp sounds like /p/, /t/, /k/, and /s/) were not clear. Because of this, it was difficult to understand what you were saying. Dropping these sounds is common, but it makes the speech hard to follow. We need you to speak up and emphasize those final sounds!<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Project your voice:</b> Imagine you are speaking to someone at the back of the room. Using more air will make your sounds much clearer.</li><li><b>The "Final Sound" challenge:</b> Practice saying words with strong endings like "cat," "dog," and "fish." Over-emphasize the final sound at first until it becomes natural.</li><li><b>Record and listen:</b> Record yourself on your phone and listen back. Ask yourself: "Could someone who doesn't know my story understand these words?"</li></ul><br>Let's make your voice heard next time! 🎯	\N	\N	11.0	Leo, you gave a very engaging review of Solo Leveling and used a great rhetorical question to start your talk. 👏 Your vocabulary was appropriate for the topic and you described the plot points in a logical order. To reach a Band 5 (B2), 