<br>For me, what can make this party successful, I think it it is activities because if we got many activities, they they don't get bored and they have something to do.<br><br>Thank you.<br><br>	377	2	Chopin, you've got a good handle on using everyday words and building simple sentences to share your ideas, like when you told us, "I like to play some games, such as computer games or go play football outside." That's really clear! 👍 However, sometimes when you talk about things that happened in the past, or when you're trying to put together a slightly longer sentence, a few small errors pop up. For instance, you said, "I go play football with my friend at this school" (which should be "I *went* to play football" or "I *played* football") and "I enjoy to go to my friend's birthday party" (which should be "I enjoy *going* to my friend's birthday party"). Also, remember that when you're talking about more than one friend, you need an 's' at the end, so "my friend" becomes "my friends." In Thai, sentences can sometimes be less strict with verb tenses and plural forms, so it's a common area for Thai learners to work on in English. To improve, try to consciously think about whether you're talking about now or the past when you speak, and double-check your 's' endings for plurals and when describing what 'he' or 'she' does. You're well on your way to building strong and correct sentences! 🌟	2	You do a good job of speaking in longer phrases and sentences, Chopin, which helps us understand your thoughts and feelings. For example, when you described the football match, you gave us some good details: "Uh, we played on the Saturday at 7:00 p.m. It was good but I didn't score any goal but I assist to my friend and he scored in the corner of the goal." That's clear and informative! However, sometimes there was a bit of repetition, and during the group activity about planning a party, you and your partner moved away from the main topic a little, focusing more on a personal birthday than the school's party. 🎉 To make your speaking even clearer and more organised, try to keep your answers directly related to the question, especially in group tasks. Also, thinking about one or two new points you want to make before you start speaking can help you extend your answers without repeating yourself. Keep practicing how to connect your ideas smoothly, and your conversations will flow beautifully! 🚀	4	Chopin, your English is very easy to understand, and that's a huge plus! Your voice goes up and down in a way that sounds natural, and most of your individual sounds are clear. That means your message gets across without any trouble. 👍 One little thing you could work on is how you stress syllables in words. Sometimes you tend to put a bit too much emphasis on the last part of words, which is a common pattern for Thai speakers. For example, words like "taller" /ˈtɔːləːr/, "comfy" /ˈkʌmfɪː/, and "picture" /ˈpɪktʃəːr/ might sound a bit more natural if the stress is placed a little differently. If you can practise letting the stress fall a bit more softly on those final syllables, your speech will sound even more smooth and natural! 🎶	3	You're great at jumping into the conversation and responding to questions, Chopin, both with the examiner and your partner! You asked some good questions during the party planning, like "Oh. Are we preparing some drinks or some food?" and "Who are we invited?". That really helps to keep the conversation going! 😄 However, during that party planning task, you and your partner started talking about a personal birthday instead of the school's party. While you kept the chat going, it meant you didn't quite manage to "develop the interaction and negotiate towards an outcome" for the *specific task* you were given. To improve, try to listen very carefully to the task instructions at the start of any group activity. If you can make sure your questions and comments always guide the conversation towards solving that specific task, you'll be interacting like a pro! 🤝	11.0	Hi Chopin! It was a real pleasure to listen to you speak. You communicate well, sharing your thoughts and experiences clearly, especially when talking about your favourite things to do and parties you've enjoyed! 🎉 To really step up your English, focusing on using the correct tenses for past events and making sure your sentences agree will help your message come across even more smoothly. Keep practising, and you'll soon find your English becoming even more precise and natural! 🚀<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Success:**<br><ul><li>✔️ When you're talking about something that happened in the past, try to remember to use the past tense form of verbs, like saying "I *went* to play football" instead of "I go play football."</li><li>✔️ Pay attention to your verb agreements, especially with "he/she/it." For example, remember to say "he *wants*" instead of "he want."</li><li>✔️ Before speaking in a group task, quickly check if you're answering the exact question the examiner asked. It's easy to get excited and talk about something else!</li></ul>	A2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
