fficult, but you did a great job sharing the plot. With a score of 4 out of 5, you have shown strong B1 skills in your grammar and vocabulary choices. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a wide range of vocabulary that fit the story perfectly, such as "Colossus," "mysteries," and "unexpected." Your ability to use relative clauses like "a boy named Eren" helped make your sentences more descriptive. It was also great to hear you use different tenses to describe the setting and the action. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Sometimes your verb tenses jumped between the past and the present, which can be a bit confusing for the listener. For example, you said "thing start to get interesting" instead of "things started to get interesting." You also had a small error with the phrase "was almost died," where it should simply be "almost died." Be careful with plural nouns too, like saying "all the Titan" instead of "all the Titans." <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Check your tenses:</b> When telling a story, try to stay in the past tense for all the events.</li><li><b>Practice plural 's':</b> Remember to add the /s/ sound to the end of words when there is more than one, like "Titans" or "friends."</li><li><b>Refine verb patterns:</b> Practice using verbs like "died" without adding "was" before them.</li></ul><br>You have a great way of telling stories, so keep practicing! 🎯	4	Arty, you did a wonderful job organizing your talk about this famous manga. Structuring a long talk can be a challenge, but you kept the story moving forward logically. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows that you can manage a long conversation well. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You followed the required structure perfectly by introducing yourself, naming the author, and using a great "Imagine" hook. Your story had a clear beginning, middle, and end, which made it very easy for the audience to follow. You also used helpful connecting words like "so," "then," and "but then" to link your ideas together. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You had some hesitations and used filler words like "uh" when you were thinking of what to say next. While your story was very relevant, you sometimes repeated the word "then" to start your sentences. Try to vary your transitions to make your speaking sound more natural. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Vary your transitions:</b> Instead of using "then" every time, try phrases like "After that," "Suddenly," or "Following the attack."</li><li><b>Reduce filler words:</b> Practice your talk a few times to get comfortable with the story so you do not need to pause as often.</li><li><b>Expand on your opinion:</b> You mentioned the mysteries are interesting, so try to give one specific example of a mystery you liked to add more detail.</li></ul><br>Your talk was very well-structured and kept me interested the whole time! 🎯	3	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 3/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Arty, thank you for sharing your book review about Attack on Titan! Your intonation was fine and your speaking was easy to follow. With a score of 3 out of 5, you have a solid foundation to build on. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your intonation was natural and kept the story moving forward. You understood what you were talking about, and that helped your listener stay engaged with your review. Your basic sounds were also quite clear.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>Watch out for your word stress! For example, you said "al-SO" (/<b>ɔːlˈsoʊ</b>/) but we usually stress the first syllable: <b>AL</b>-so. You also said "ti-TUN" (/taɪ**tʌn**/) instead of "<b>TI</b>-tan" (/ˈtaɪtn̩/). The main issue was choppiness. Because you were a bit nervous, you spoke word-by-word (he. also. got. a.), which stopped your words from linking together. English sounds best when the words flow smoothly.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Relax and link:</b> It's okay to be nervous! Take a deep breath and try to link your words. Think of them as "he-also-got-a" as a single unit of sound.</li><li><b>Dictionary practice:</b> Use a site like Cambridge Dictionary to hear where the stress / ' / is on words like "Titan." It's almost always on the first part!</li><li><b>Read aloud daily:</b> Spending just 5 minutes a day reading English aloud will help your mouth get used to connecting those sounds smoothly.</li></ul><br>A solid performance, Arty! 🎯	\N	\N	15.0	Arty, your Attack on Titan review was excellent because you used the "Imagine" hook effectively and provided a very clear summary of the plot. 👏 To reach a Band 5 (B2), focus on keeping your verb tenses consistent throughout the story, use a wider variety of linking words beyond "then" and "so," and try to reduce hesitations to make your speech flow more smoothly. 🎯	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
