ords like "uneventful," "mystery," and "dimension" were used very effectively. You also did a good job describing the setting so the listener could visualize the scary details of the town.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were a few small mistakes with how verbs are used after the word "is." For example, you said "the town is have the fog" instead of "the town <b>has</b> fog." You also said "the letter is tell James" when it should be "the letter <b>tells</b> James." Watch out for adding "the" when it is not needed, like in the phrase "from <b>the</b> your wife."<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Master Verb Forms</b>: Practice using simple present or past tense without adding "is" before the main action verb.</li><li><b>Review Articles</b>: Focus on when to use "the" and when to leave it out, especially before possessive words like "your."</li><li><b>Word Choice</b>: Try to find even more descriptive words for the horror genre to make your reviews even more exciting.</li></ul><br>You have a strong vocabulary—keep using those interesting words! 🎯	3	Vegas, your book review followed the required structure very clearly from start to finish. It is not always easy to organize a long talk about a complex plot, but you stayed on track. This score reflects your ability to connect your ideas using transition words.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a great variety of connecting words like "however," "at first," and "then" to move through the story. Your "Imagine" hook was very effective because it made the listener think about the main character's situation immediately. You also gave a clear opinion about why some people might find the start of the book a bit boring.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You had quite a few pauses where you said "I don't know how to say it" or used filler words like "um" many times. These hesitations made it a bit difficult to follow the flow of your story smoothly. Try to make the "climax" or most exciting part of the story more obvious so the listener knows exactly when the action is at its peak.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Preparation</b>: Before speaking, write down a few keywords for the climax so you do not have to search for words while talking.</li><li><b>Fluency Practice</b>: Try to pause silently instead of saying "like" or "um" when you are thinking of the next word.</li><li><b>Balance Your Sections</b>: Spend a little more time explaining the resolution of the story so the ending feels complete for the listener.</li></ul><br>Your structure is very logical, so now focus on making your delivery smoother. 🎯	2	<b>Pronunciation (Score: 2/5)</b> 🎤<br><br>Vegas, thank you for telling me about your book! You showed a lot of courage speaking even when you were nervous. With a score of 2 out of 5, we have some specific areas to work on to make your speaking clearer. 💪<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>I noticed how you became more confident as you went on! At the beginning you were nervous, but as you spoke more, your flow started to improve. This shows you have the ability to speak well once you relax.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>We have a few things to watch. First, syllable stress: you said "ma-RY" (/<b>məˈriː</b>/) but it should be <b>MA</b>-ry (/ˈmeri/). Also, you dropped many final consonants, such as the /n/ in "town" and the /d/ and /l/ in "Sunderland." Be careful with your /r/ and /l/ sounds too—you said "led" instead of "red." These errors, along with the "choppiness" (word-by-word speaking), make it harder for the listener to follow you.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>The 'L' and 'R' challenge:</b> Practice words like "red," "lead," "rice," and "lice." Notice where your tongue is! For /l/, the tongue touches the top teeth; for /r/, it pulls back.</li><li><b>Over-enunciate:</b> For now, try to say every final consonant (/n/, /d/, /t/) loudly. It will feel strange, but it will help your listener!</li><li><b>Record your start:</b> Since you are more nervous at the beginning, practice the first 30 seconds of your talk many times until you can say it smoothly.</li></ul><br>You improved as you went! 🎯	\N	\N	12.0	Vegas, you used the 'Imagine' hook effectively and described the spooky atmosphere of Silent Hill with some very impressive vocabulary like 'uneventful' and 'dimension.' 👍 To reach a Band 5 (B2), try to reduce the number of hesitations and filler words to make your speech flow more naturally, and work on using correct verb forms without adding extra 'is' helping verbs. 🎯	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
