son to get and there's have a hundred baht one left and fifty and twenty baht. So I put my hand and just bring the one out and I just get a hundred baht, the last one.<br><br><br>RICHTER: Friends.<br><br><br>RICHTER: Uh, when there's nobody not a lot for me. Sometimes my birthday last year, I don't have a friend but just my parent. Uh, and it was my birthday, I think.<br><br><br>RICHTER: Uh, when is my birthday last year, I don't have my friend. That's just my parent. Um, and it was my birthday, I think.<br><br><br>RICHTER: Uh, most of them, for some teenager want.	445	2	Richter, you did a good job of using appropriate words to talk about familiar topics like your hobbies and what you did last weekend. 👍 You have a clear vocabulary that helps you express your ideas. However, to help your English become even clearer and more natural, we need to focus on some key grammar points. For instance, when you talked about last weekend, you said, "Last weekend, I *go*... I *was* in... I *go* to Siam," but when talking about something that happened in the past, we need to use past tense verbs like "went" and "was." So, "Last weekend, I *went* to Siam." Also, when you mentioned Miss Atin, you said, "when someone *do* that things," but for a singular subject like 'someone', the verb needs an '-s', so it should be "when someone *does* those things." These are common challenges for Thai learners, as Thai grammar doesn't change verb endings for tense or subject like English does. Thinking about 'when' something happened (past, present, future) and 'who' is doing the action (one person or many) will really help your sentences become super accurate! ✍️🌟	3	You did well, Richter, in producing responses that were longer than just short answers, especially when you described the sports event and recounted your exciting Children's Day story at Assumption! 🗣️ It was easy to follow your ideas, and you managed to keep talking despite a few hesitations like "uh" or "um." These little pauses are completely normal when you're thinking, so don't worry about them too much. You also used basic connecting words like "and" and "because" to link your thoughts, which helps your listener understand you better. To make your stories even more engaging, you could try using a wider variety of connecting words like "however," "although," or "first, then, finally." Keep up the good work in extending your turns; it shows you're confident in speaking! ✨	3	Richter, your voice is nice and clear, and your intonation (the way your voice rises and falls) is generally good, which makes you mostly easy to understand – fantastic! 🎤 However, one area to work on is making sure all your consonant sounds are clearly pronounced, especially at the ends of words. You have a habit of sometimes dropping consonants, for instance, in words like 'friends' or 'gifts'. For example, 'friends' should clearly end with a /z/ sound, not just 'frien', and 'gifts' should have a distinct /ts/ sound at the end. This is often something Thai speakers do because Thai language pronunciation rules are different regarding final consonant sounds. If you practise really hearing and saying those final consonants, your speech will become even more precise and easy for everyone to understand. 👂🎯	3	You did a good job, Richter, of jumping into conversations and responding to what others said during the speaking test! 👋 You offered good ideas during the party planning discussion, suggesting "fun games" and "gifts," and you also responded positively to Tonnam's ideas, like when you agreed that "we will have fun and have some delicious food." This showed you were listening and participating well. To take your interactive communication to the next level, you could try not only agreeing but also expanding on your partner's ideas more, or perhaps asking them specific questions to draw out their opinions further. This will help you and your partner develop the conversation even more and work together towards a clear outcome. 🤝💬	11.0	Richter, you showed some lovely ideas and clear communication during your speaking test! 🌟 Your ability to talk about different topics was good, and it's clear you have interesting thoughts to share. Your grammar, especially your verb tenses and subject-verb agreement, is an area where a little bit of focus could make a huge difference to your overall fluency and accuracy. Keep practising, and you'll see fantastic progress!📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>* Focus on your verb tenses, especially when talking about past events. Before speaking, quickly think: "Did this happen yesterday? Then I need a past tense verb!" For example, try to say "I went to Siam" instead of "I go to Siam."<br>* Pay close attention to subject-verb agreement. If you're talking about 'someone' (singular), remember the '-s' on the verb (e.g., "someone does," not "someone do").<br>* Practise clear pronunciation of all consonants at the end of words. Try saying words like 'friends' or 'gifts' slowly and clearly to make sure you can hear every sound you make.	A2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
