ressed the final syllables in words, like "freedom" sounded like free-*dom* instead of *free*-dom, which affects the natural rhythm of English. To improve, try linking words together more smoothly, especially when a word ends with a consonant and the next starts with a vowel. Focus on clearly pronouncing all the final consonant sounds in words, and listen carefully to native English speakers to notice which part of a word they emphasize. Practice makes perfect! 🎧	\N	\N	8.0	Hello Boss!<br><br>It was lovely listening to your presentation about Harriet Tubman. You chose an inspiring hero and shared some great information with us. You're doing well, and with a bit more practice, you'll be speaking even more clearly and confidently. Focusing on making your sentences flow more smoothly and making sure your grammar is just right will really help. Keep practicing, and you'll see a wonderful improvement in your presentations! 🎉<br><br>📋 **Action Plan:**<br>*   ✅ Practice subject-verb agreement in sentences, especially for 'he', 'she', and 'it'.<br>*   ✅ Focus on linking words and clearly pronouncing word endings.<br>*   ✅ Expand your vocabulary related to social issues and historical events.<br><br>Here are some concrete steps you can take:<br>1.  Record yourself describing a recent event, like your last weekend, and really concentrate on using the correct past tense verbs for 'I', 'he', 'she', and 'they'. For example, "I *went*," "He *played*."<br>2.  Read a short paragraph aloud from an English book or article. Pay close attention to how you link words together and make sure you pronounce every 's' or 'ed' ending clearly, like in "help**ed**" or "friend**s**."<br>3.  Research and learn three new synonyms for "enslaved people" (like "slaves" or "bondspersons") and use each of them in a different sentence to describe Harriet Tubman's work.	A2+	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
