34059	2025-09-18	2025-09-18	2026	Term 1 Finals	Uh, my name is Onruethai Wongsnit. Uh, my nickname is Namunn. Uh, I live in the Rama II road, uh, Phuttha Bucha 25 soi. What do you enjoy doing when you're not at school? Uh, uh, I'm enjoy, uh, sometimes I will go to fitness to, uh, get my body ready to the game because, because I play ice hockey and and, uh, I'm usually go to practice on ice. Uh, the music that I like, uh, I love to listen, uh, many types like pop song, a K-pop, C-pop, uh, which is China song. Yeah. Uh, so, uh, you can see that this pict- first picture is the boys, uh, who are playing basketball at the park together and the second picture is the woman that, uh, stretching and do maybe yoga, I guess. Uh, and but she do only by herself and don't have friends to do against. And I think people must enjoy the basketball more than the stretching because because, uh, they will have a friends and and and talk together about basketball. But if she do, uh, if she do alone, she will lonely. Yeah. And I think the basketball will, uh... Uh, I, uh, to keeping fit, I do in the many ways. And the first one is I I often go to fitness to to stay fit and not try it, uh, entire game. And second way is I will practice on ice because it's keeping me like not try it but when when I'm reading on on ice. Uh, I think, I think getting enough sleep and eating healthy food is, uh, the, the is the good way to improve health for the young people because, uh, the young people, uh, need to rest, more rest and get enough rest. What about you? I see your point of view but I think, uh, doing team sport will improve, uh, the young people health more than learning a new skill because learning new skill, uh, not improve health but you can unlock the new skill, you know? And doing team sport is help you keep keeping fit and help you stronger. Yeah for... I think, uh, in my opinion, doing team sport is, uh, is the be- the best choice to improve to to improve health because because doing sport is will make your muscles stronger and and you will get healthy every every day. Uh, but doing team sport, uh, is for like some some people because some people don't like playing sport. And I think young people will choose spending time outdoor more than doing team sport. Uh, what do you think? Uh, I think in my opinion, I think, uh, it's very important to teach the young people to eat eating healthy because, uh, if you eating healthy, you will get the get healthy and you will get healthy and you will not sick, not fever. Uh, yeah, and for example, uh, yesterd- yesterday in the PE class, uh, the teacher teach us about how to get keeping fit by eating healthy food like vegetable and meat or something like carbohydrate. Yeah. Uh, I think technology will will make you stay healthy because, uh, every day we will slide on Tik Tok and we will see the people who are stay stay healthy and they like, uh, have the good body and they they muscle is showing us so, I think many people especially girl want to get healthy, more healthy because they want to be like them. Thank you.	589	2	Namunn, you've shown that you can put together simple sentences pretty well, especially when you're talking about things you know, like where you live or what you do for fun. For instance, you said, "I live in the Rama II road." That's a great clear statement! 👍 However, sometimes a few little errors creep in, which can make your message a little less clear. For example, you said, "I'm enjoy, uh, sometimes I will go to fitness," but when we talk about what we generally like, we should say "I **enjoy** sometimes going to fitness." Another example was "doing sport is will make your muscles stronger," which should be "doing sport **will** make your muscles stronger" or "doing sport **makes** your muscles stronger." 🤔 To help you improve, try to pay close attention to the small grammar rules like making sure your verbs agree with "I" (I enjoy, not I'm enjoy) and using the correct verb tense. This will make your English sound much more natural!	2	You're good at giving longer answers, Namunn, which is fantastic because it shows you can talk about a topic in more detail. You kept the conversation going and your contributions were always on topic. 🎉 However, sometimes there were pauses, and you repeated yourself a little, especially when you said, "because because." To make your speaking even better, try to use different linking words or phrases to connect your ideas more smoothly, like "also," "furthermore," or "in addition." For example, instead of just saying "because because," you could say, "...because... and another reason is..." This will help your ideas flow more clearly and keep your listener interested.	1	You were generally understood, Namunn, which is a great start! However, to make your English even clearer and more pleasant to listen to, we need to work on making your voice more lively. Your voice sometimes sounds a bit flat, and you speak quite slowly, which can make it hard for the listener to stay engaged. 🗣️ Also, you tend to drop consonant sounds at the end of words. For example, in "must," the /st/ sound wasn't always clear, in "think," the /k/ sound was often missing, and in "boys," the /s/ sound was sometimes hard to hear. For "relax," the /ks/ sound was sometimes hard to hear. Try practising making these sounds clearly at the end of words. You also pronounced "yoga" as /ˈjoʊkə/, where the /g/ sound should be voiced as /ˈjoʊɡə/. Many Thai speakers find it tricky to pronounce final consonants and voiced consonants, as they are less common in Thai, so it's a common area to focus on for improvement! There was also some fricative distortion of /s/ sounds.	3	Namunn, you did a good job of responding to questions and starting new parts of the conversation. You were able to keep the discussion moving along, especially when you shared your thoughts and reasons, like when you said, "I think in my opinion, I think, uh, it's very important to teach the young people to eat eating healthy because, uh, if you eating healthy, you will get the get healthy and you will get healthy and you will not sick, not fever." That's a good way to give your opinion! 👍 To really get to a higher score here, try to build more on what your partner says. Instead of just giving your opinion, you could ask them more questions to get more details or explain *why* you agree, perhaps adding a new point. For instance, you could say, "I agree with you because... and another reason is..." This shows you're not just listening, but you're also adding to the conversation in a deeper way!	8.0	Hi Namunn! It was lovely to hear you speak. You're doing a good job of getting your ideas across, and that's the most important thing in speaking English. Keep working on making your sentences a little more varied and clear, and you'll find your confidence growing even more. You're making progress, and with a bit of focus, you can continue to improve how smoothly and clearly you express yourself! <br><br> 📋 Action Plan: <br>✔️ Practice making your sentences a bit longer by using words like "because," "and," or "but" to connect your ideas. <br>✔️ Pay attention to the endings of your words, especially those 's' sounds for plurals (like "kilometres" instead of "kilometre") and verb endings. <br>✔️ Try to speak a little faster and use a wider range of tones in your voice to make your speech sound more natural and engaging.	B1	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
34912	2025-09-07	2025-09-07	2026	My Favourite Photograph	Good morning, good morning everyone. So great to be here. Please call me Hero. So, today, I'm really excited to get you through my presentation about a per, a personal photo of my baby brother, which is may seem simple to you guys, but he has tons of meaning to me because it represents happiness, innocence, and inspiration. And it also shows why personal photos are so important in our lives. And by the end of this presentation, I want you to understand my main message, which is innocent doesn't last forever. It change as we grow up.<br><br>Well, today, I'm going to do three things. Firstly, I'm going to tell you all about my photo. Secondly, I'm going to tell you why I selected this photo. Thirdly, I'm, I'm going to tell you why personal photos are so important.<br><br>All right, so let me tell you all about my photo. This photo was taken by me on my latest birthday and it shows my baby brother cuddling with his toy on the sofa in his own playroom, which he did this because he wanted attention from me and it look really cute.<br><br>So, I'm going to tell you why I chose this picture. Firstly, it is the most adorable photo in my view because he looks happy and full of life. Every time when I look at it, it just, I instantly get brings, it instantly brings me so much joy and I gain about 50% of my energy back.<br><br>Secondly, is a photo that inspires me the most because no other photos can make me feel the same way. When I look at this, in whenever I look at this, it'll make me feel like, oh, wow. When I look at other projects, feel like, oh, it, it seem very normal and it's just my numbing. It's not even make me feel better at all.<br><br>Thirdly, it's reminder of a peer of innocence because as we know, when you, when you're a child, you, you life is simple and full of wonder. You don't have to worry about education or work at all. And, for example, my baby brother can spend one, one hour playing with his toys and not even getting bored.<br><br>Now, we've reached the end of my presentation. To finish, I want to tell you why personal photos are so important. Firstly, personal photos preserve memories by capturing special moments we can look back on. It doesn't matter if you feel sad, bored, lonely, or other negative emotions.<br><br>Secondly, personal photos work as emotional connectors. It is evokes warmth and closeness feelings. For example, when I look at my baby brother smiling, I feel, I feel like I'm in that picture. I feel like he's standing in front of me and smiling at me. It feels really good and I feel like, yeah, I got this, I gained so much confident in my presentation because I look at this photo as you can see here.<br><br>Thirdly, it's personal photo give us inspiration and motivation. Because you gain so much confident, it's inspired and motivate you. For example, for example here, there's a picture of my baby brother in the hotel. It's another picture that inspires me and this picture, when I look at it, it's made me feel, feel, feel other way, not the same as the first one. It told me that it's waiting for me. It reminds me that it reminds love and beauty as a sibling.<br><br>In conclusion, today I've talk about three things. Firstly, I've, I tell you all about my favorite photo. Secondly, I tell you, I tell you why I selected this photo. Thirdly, I tell, I tell you why personal photos are so important in our lives. Thank you for listening, guys.<br><br>	625	3	You've got a good grasp of everyday English grammar, Hero, and you use a nice range of words to express your ideas, which is brilliant! 🎉 You made some good attempts at longer, more complex sentences. To help you move towards a higher score, let's look at a few common grammar points. For example, when you said, "It change as we grow up," you need to add an 's' to "change" for "it" in the present simple, so it should be "it changes" (/ˈtʃeɪndʒɪz/). Similarly, "it look really cute" should be "it looks" (/lʊks/). Also, when talking about past actions with "I've," you need the past participle, so "I've talk" should be "I've talked" (/tɔːkt/). These kinds of agreement and verb form errors are quite common for Thai speakers because Thai grammar doesn't always require these changes to verbs based on the subject or tense in the same way English does. Practising these will make your English sound even more polished! 💫	4	You did an excellent job of organising your presentation, Hero! 🌟 You spoke for a good amount of time and clearly guided us through your introduction, detailed descriptions of the photo, why you chose it, and the importance of personal photos, right up to your conclusion. You used phrases like "Firstly," "Secondly," and "Thirdly" wonderfully to show us exactly where you were in your talk. Your ideas were all very relevant to the topic, and you kept the flow going smoothly. This clear structure made your presentation very easy to follow and engaging. Well done on keeping your audience with you every step of the way! 👍	4	Your pronunciation is generally clear, and your intonation is really natural and engaging, which makes listening to you a pleasure! 😊 However, there are a couple of areas we can fine-tune. Sometimes, the stress in certain words was a little misplaced. For instance, in "excited," the stress should be on the second syllable (/ɪkˈsaɪtɪd/), not the first. The same goes for "selected" (/sɪˈlɛktɪd/). Another point to practice is distinguishing between the /tʃ/ sound (like in "cheese") and the /dʒ/ sound (like in "judge"), especially in words such as "change" (/tʃeɪndʒ/). Thai speakers often place stress differently in words compared to English, and the distinction between these two sounds can be tricky as Thai does not clearly differentiate between affricates and fricatives in the same way. Focusing on these areas will help your English sound even more like a native speaker! 🗣️	\N	\N	15.0	Hello Hero! 👋 It was a real pleasure to listen to your presentation about your baby brother's photo. Your enthusiasm and natural intonation made your talk very engaging – fantastic work! You had a clear message and guided your audience through your points with ease. To make your English even more precise, we can focus on tidying up those verb forms and making sure your word stress is always spot on. Keep up the great work; your passion for sharing your story really shines through! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   ✔️ Practice subject-verb agreement (e.g., "it changes" instead of "it change").<br>*   ✔️ Pay attention to correct verb forms and participles (e.g., "I've talked" instead of "I've talk").<br>*   ✔️ Record yourself saying words like "excited" and "selected" and check that the stress falls on the correct syllable.	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
34043	2025-09-07	2025-09-07	2026	My Hero	Today, I am delighted to tell you guys all about Seub Nakhasathien. If you are a Thai student, I'm pretty sure that you must know about him. He appear in the textbook for many times as a hero of Thailand. He is fight for wildlife and forest that can speak for its own so he want to raise awareness about forest conservation among the society.<br><br>The main message that really appreciate me is I speak for the voiceless of the wild because he stood up to fight for it. Even if he was alone, he would keep fighting for it.<br><br>And I will giving you a quick outline of today's topic. First, I will interview who is he? He is a conservation... uh conservationist with a strong and determined heart to protect the nature. Second, I will move on to what he does that very impact to Thailand. He willing to sacrifice himself to propose UNESCO to accept Huai Kha Khaeng Wildlife Sanctuary to be a World Heritage site of Thailand. And finally, I will wrap up why he is a hero of Thailand. He always fight for nature and wildlife so he... so he always try to speak that wildlife and nature in Thailand is in a danger so he want to raise awareness among the society.<br><br>And now I will talk about Seub Nakhasathien's early life and how he became a conservationist that have so much passionate about it. First, he was born in Prachinburi in a rural family so he was close to the nature since he was a child. And he graduated from Faculty of Forest in a Kasetsart University, but this is not a real beginning of his journey. In 1975, he have started his first government career in wildlife conservation and it exposed him to the problem of forest destruction and wildlife threaten, and he start to notice that he want to fix it correctly. The big influence on him is when he was emotionally affected by the death of the forest rangers. Uh, he... he that is motivated him to protect the forest with all his effort and dedication.<br><br>Uh, so and now I would like to talk about Seub Nakhasathien award. When he was alive, during his lifetime, he did not get so much recognition, recognition and documented, but after his sacrifice on one September, he the one September is a day that is a annually day that call us Seub Nakhasathien Day because on this day he is sacrificed himself to propose UNESCO to accept Huai Kha Khaeng Wildlife Sanctuary to be a World Nature Heritage site of Thailand.<br><br>Um, he have some problem because back then he had very support from anyone, not even from the government. He got conflict with government because his strong opposite policies and... and... project to the project of government that want to destroy the forest and he could not accept it. So that why he get a little support from them.<br><br>And now and finally I would like to show you guys how why he is a real hero not just a conservationist. First and most importantly, he is a... he is always fight for forest and wildlife. He... he always think for public benefit and he always try to call out for wildlife and forest till the end of his life. And finally, he always working so hard for it and he take the responsibility to speak for it because it can speak for its own.<br><br>And to sum up my presentation today, I have show you guys and talk all about Seub Nakhasathien. I show you why he is a hero, his early life, and his award. The most important thing that I have left you guys today is this sentence, I speak for the voiceless of the wild. This sentence very impact and meaningful to Thai people because he is the one who really fight for nature in Thailand and made Huai Kha Khaeng Wildlife Sanctuary as a World Heritage site of Thailand.<br><br>Thank you for listening me today. I hope you guys enjoy.	691	3	You're doing a great job using lots of different words to explain your ideas, which shows a good understanding of English vocabulary! However, sometimes when you talk about "he" or "she" in the present, we need to add an 's' to the verb, like "he *appears*" instead of "he appear". Also, after words like "will," the next verb should be in its simple form, so "I will *give*" not "I will giving." Don't worry, many Thai learners often miss these small endings because Thai grammar works a bit differently. Keep practicing, and you'll get the hang of it! 👍	4	You did a brilliant job structuring your presentation, Kathi! You introduced your hero, explained what he did, and gave us a clear conclusion, just like a good presentation should. Your ideas flowed well, and you used good connecting words to help us follow along. Sometimes you repeated a few points, which is a common way to emphasise in Thai, but in English, we often try to say new things or use different words to keep our listeners engaged. You're definitely on the right track! 🌟	3	You speak clearly, and we can understand what you're saying, which is the most important thing! 😊 One thing to listen out for is where you put the stress in words. For example, in "conservation," the stress is on the "va" part /ˌkɒnsərˈveɪʃən/, but sometimes it sounded like you stressed the last part too much. Also, with words like "accept," the stress is on the second part /əkˈsept/. This is super common for Thai speakers because English has a different rhythm than Thai. Another little thing to practice is making sure you pronounce the final consonant sounds in words, like the /s/ in "always" (so it sounds like "alway**s**") or the /d/ in "child" (so it sounds like "chil**d**"). These sounds can sometimes be quiet in Thai, but they're important in English for clarity! 🎶	\N	\N	13.0	What a thoughtful presentation you delivered about Seub Nakhasathien! It's clear you put a lot of effort into sharing his important story. While you're already doing well, focusing on those trickier verb endings and making sure your words have the right rhythm will really help your English flow beautifully. Keep up the fantastic work! ✨<br>📝 Action Plan<br>*   🤔 Pay close attention to verb endings, especially when you're talking about 'he', 'she', or 'it' in the present. Try to remember the 's' sound!<br>*   🗣️ Practice saying words like 'conservation' and 'accept' out loud, making sure you put the stress on the correct part of the word. You can use an online dictionary to check!<br>*   👂 Record yourself speaking and listen back for any missing 's' sounds at the end of plural words or other final consonant sounds.	B2	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
35239	2025-09-23	2025-09-23	2026	Term 1 Finals	ANA: My name is Ana.<br><br>ANA: Yeah, well, me too. I really enjoy playing video games. Right now, I'm really into Valorant right now. Uh, I really like it because it's like a way to connect on a deeper level with my friends, I think. It's really nice and right now I'm also really into watching movies. I find it really interesting. I actually try to watch one every day.<br><br>ANA: I think my music taste is kind of more diverse. I really just listen to everything nowadays. It just kind of depends on my mood. But right now, I kind of like metal. I'm really into metal. I like Metallica and about my friends, I think it's really important to connect with them. I always find time for them and I always try to speak to them at least once a week. Like every single one of my friends at least once a week.<br><br>ANA: Okay. So I think on the picture on the top, I think the boy might enjoy playing basketball because it's a way to uh, enjoy while also being with his friends. It's a group activity, so I think the teamwork, maybe he really enjoys that. I also kind of like playing basketball. I might not be as good as it, at as good at it, but, you know, I try. And the girl on the bottom, I think, I'm not exactly sure what she's doing. Maybe she's doing yoga, maybe Pilates. I've tried doing Pilates. It's that's really not for me though, but she might enjoy it because, you know, she will keep fit and have like a great body. So I think that's what she might enjoy about it. But personally, it's not for me. I personally like basketball more.<br><br>ANA: Okay, so I think my favorite one out of this is probably spending time outdoors. What about you?<br><br>ANA: Yeah, well, I, I agree with that too. I think eating healthy food is super important. But honestly for me, it's super hard to eat healthy food. I don't really enjoy eating healthy food, you know? Like especially fruits and vegetables. There's just not that enjoyable to me. So I think spending time outdoors for me is very important. I don't think, you know, being in your room or anything like that too much can be good for your health, you know, physically and mentally. What about you, what else? What about like getting enough sleep? What do you think about that?<br><br>ANA: Uh, I think learning a new skill. I mean, it's hard. It's very difficult for me. I don't really learn anything that much unless it's given to me. But let's go back to sleep. I think sleep is super important. Like I really try to have a good sleep schedule, but it's just super hard for me. Since I, like I have a lot of school work and stuff like that. It's just super hard to get enough sleep. And, you know, it's weird because sometimes I think that the less sleep I get, the more energetic I feel. And the more sleep I get, the less energetic I feel. I don't know why that is, but I know it's not good, so I need to improve on that. But what about team sports? How do you feel about that?<br><br>ANA: Okay, well, actually, okay. I think exercise for me personally is super important. I try to exercise at least five times a week, like after school. And then I just kind of give myself rest on weekends. I think exercise is super important. And it's also very fun for me. Like I find different ways to keep myself entertained, but sometimes I find, I find entertainment in exercise, you know? But like you said, I also think sleep is super important. I mean, I love sleeping. It it's so weird though. Like I love sleeping, but when it's time to actually go to sleep, I never want to go to sleep. But when I wake up, it's just I never, I never want to get up. Like I just want to keep sleeping. You know what I mean? It's like a love-hate relationship, sort of.<br><br>ANA: I think it's super important. I think it's very important to know what's healthy and what's not. I mean, some people might think that it's common knowledge, but some really just don't know. And I think it's important to stay educated on topics like that. Super important to, you know, kind of differentiate what's good and what's bad for your health. Me personally, I don't really know what's good and what's bad. And even if I do, I just kind of tend to lean more on what I enjoy, even if it's not good. Like one of my guilty pleasures have to be fast food. I love fast food. I love McDonald's. I love KFC. I admit, like, I eat it at least three or four times a week. Like, I know. Or even more than that. I'm not going to lie. It's very bad and I know it's bad and, you know, I try to kind of implement more healthy things into my diet, but it's very hard. I I just don't find it really enjoyable.<br><br>ANA: Um, I think by using the AI bots which are available to us, like, to everyone now. I think you can find more knowledge in that. You can kind of ask, you know, ask them questions or what's good and what's bad. Actually, I tried once, using ChatGPT to create like a diet for me, because I wanted to, you know, implement more healthy things. But, okay, I'm not going to lie. I didn't really stick to it. I still ate a lot of fast food, but I think that's one of the ways that you can kind of, you know, implement it into your life.<br><br>ANA: No. I think a lot of people tend to eat like unhealthy foods. I don't really know that many people that eat anything healthy really. And especially Thai food, like I don't think most of them are relatively healthy or, you know, good for your health. I think that they're missing some sort of supplements in them, but yeah, I think most people in Thailand just tend to eat a lot of fast food and just food that's just already there, you know?<br><br>ANA: Absolutely. I think they put way too much pressure on them. And I think even parents put their like too much pressure on their children to exceed in sports. But I think people should just do sports to relax. I think sports is just there to help you, you know, release stress from your day. And I think sports is a great activity. I think it's great for you, it's great for your health, and I don't think it there should be that much pressure on somebody just to exceed in sports. I think it should just be there for entertainment and for fun.<br><br>	1169	5	Ana, you showed a strong control over how you put sentences together, using both simple and more complex grammar to share your thoughts on many different topics. For example, your phrases like "it's like a way to connect on a deeper level with my friends" and "I find different ways to keep myself entertained" are excellent examples of varied and effective grammar. You also chose your words very well, making your discussions about hobbies, health, and even social pressures clear and easy to understand. To keep improving, try to notice new words and phrases when you're reading or listening to English, and then make a point of using them yourself! For instance, if you learn a new adjective, try to pair it with a new noun in your speaking.	5	You spoke at length without much hesitation, Ana, which made your contributions feel very natural and fluent. Your ideas were always relevant to the conversation, and you organised them clearly, which meant your listener could follow along easily. You used words and phrases like "Yeah, well, me too," "But personally," and "Absolutely" very effectively to connect your ideas and guide the conversation. This made your speaking very engaging! To make your explanations even clearer, you could try to use a wider variety of linking words (for example, "consequently," "furthermore," or "however") to show how your ideas relate to each other.	5	Ana, it was very easy to understand you, and your voice went up and down in a natural way that helped to convey your meaning clearly! You generally put the stress on the right parts of words and sentences, which is a sign of very clear speech. You sometimes swallowed the /t/ sound a little in words like "thought" /θɔːt/ and "important" /ɪmˈpɔːtənt/. This is a minor point, but if you focus on making sure all your consonant sounds are fully articulated, your speech will be even crisper and clearer. A good way to practise this is to read aloud and consciously exaggerate the 't' sounds, then gradually make them more natural.	5	Ana, your ability to interact with the interviewer and your partner was excellent! 🌟 You were great at starting conversations and responding thoughtfully, consistently linking what you said to what others were talking about. You did a fantastic job of keeping the discussion going and developing the ideas, for example, by asking questions like "What about you?" and "What do you think about that?" and building on your partner's points with phrases like "Yeah, well, I, I agree with that too." This really showed how well you can engage in a conversation and work with others to reach a conclusion! To continue excelling in this area, you could try to explicitly summarise a point your partner made before offering your own perspective, for example, "So, if I understand correctly, you're saying that... and I think..." This can deepen the sense of shared understanding in the conversation.	20.0	Ana, your speaking performance in the Term 1 Finals was truly excellent! You spoke with confidence and clarity, expressing your ideas beautifully and engaging with the tasks so well. While your performance was consistently very good, focusing on fully articulating those tricky 't' sounds will help you speak with even greater precision. Keep up the fantastic work; you're communicating at a very sophisticated level!<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Continued Progress:**<br>✔️ Pay close attention to fully pronouncing 't' sounds at the end of words or in the middle, like in "thought" or "important." Practice saying these words slowly to ensure each sound is clear.<br>✔️ Continue expanding your vocabulary, especially on less familiar topics, by reading English books and articles.<br>✔️ Keep up your wonderful habit of initiating and extending conversations; perhaps try to introduce slightly more complex sentence structures when doing so.	C1	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
