 grammar while telling a story. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a variety of tenses correctly to describe the plot and your opinions. I was impressed by your use of complex structures like relative clauses and contrast words such as "even though." Your vocabulary choices, like "unexpected" and "slice of life," were very appropriate for a book review context. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You sometimes forgot the final "s" on verbs when talking about a single person, like saying "the story take place" instead of "the story take<b>s</b>" /teɪk<b>s</b>/. There were also some small errors with word forms, for example, saying "feel relate" instead of "feel relat<b>ed</b>" /rɪˈleɪt<b>ɪd</b>/. Be careful with verb patterns after prepositions; it should be "sleeping" instead of "slept" in the phrase "ask him for sleep<b>ing</b>." <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Master subject-verb agreement:</b> When talking about one person (Itsuomi/Yuki) or one thing (the story), always add the "s" to the present tense verb.</li><li><b>Review adjective endings:</b> Remember to use "-ed" when describing how a person feels, such as feeling "relat<b>ed</b>" or "excit<b>ed</b>."</li><li><b>Practice verb patterns:</b> Many verbs and prepositions are followed by the "-ing" form, so try to group these patterns together when you study.</li></ul><br>You are becoming a very clear communicator, Naenae! 🌟	2	Naenae, you followed the required structure for your book review, but the flow of your talk was quite difficult to follow. You earned a score of 2 for discourse management because your speech was characterized by frequent hesitations and many restarts where you repeated whole phrases while searching for words. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a clear structure to guide your listeners through the story, moving from the beginning to the exciting climax and the happy ending. You also used some helpful connectors like "After that" and "The most exciting part" to label different sections of your talk. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your speech is very broken and contains a large number of long pauses and repetitions (e.g., repeating "This manga is write by..." and "I like this story because..."). This makes it hard for a listener to stay focused on your ideas. You also missed mentioning the author of the manga, which was a requirement.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Simplify Your Sentences:</b> When you feel stuck, try to use shorter, simpler sentences that are easier to say without pausing.</li><li><b>Build Consistency:</b> Practice telling your story multiple times until you can move from one idea to the next without needing to restart your sentences.</li><li><b>Use a task checklist:</b> Before you start speaking, quickly check that you have all the required details like the title and the author ready.</li></ul><br>Your organization is a great start—now focus on making your speech flow more smoothly! 🎯	2	You did a great job today sharing the story of your favourite book, Naenae. I really enjoyed hearing about the characters and the plot you described. Well done for speaking so clearly!<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You have a lovely natural intonation that makes your speech very pleasant to listen to. I also noticed that you attempted to use linkages between words, which is a fantastic skill to develop for more fluent speaking.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>I noticed a repetitive habit of overstressing final syllables in words like "character" /ˈkærəktə/ and "story" /ˈstɔːri/, which can be a bit intrusive for the listener. There were also a few moments where /v/ sounds were pronounced as /w/, such as saying "wery" instead of "very" /ˈveri/.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Lighten Word Endings:</b> Practice saying words like "character" and focus on making the last sound very soft and quick.</li><li><b>Master the /V/ Sound:</b> Practice the /v/ sound by biting your bottom lip slightly with your top teeth to ensure it doesn't sound like a /w/.</li><li><b>Shadowing Practice:</b> Try "shadowing" (repeating after) a native speaker to match their rhythm and see how they handle final syllables.</li></ul><br>Your intonation is a real strength, so keep working on these technical details to reach the next level! ✨	\N	\N	11.0	Naenae, this was a strong book review that showed your ability to tell a story and express opinions clearly. You used great connectors like "suddenly" and "even though," and your structure was very logical. To reach the next level, focus on small grammar details like subject-verb agreement and try to reduce the number of pauses during your speech. Don't forget to include the author next time, but your review was very engaging and easy to follow! 🌟	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
