</b><br>Your volume was consistent throughout the talk, ensuring everyone could hear your ideas. Your intonation was also a strength, as you used the rise and fall of your voice to emphasize the exciting parts of the story.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your speech was quite choppy and hesitant at times, which made it difficult to follow the flow of your sentences. You also missed some final consonants, such as the /d/ sound in "died" (/daɪd/), and you tended to overstress the final syllables in words like "character" (/ˈkærəktər/), "power" (/ˈpaʊər/), and "many" (/ˈmɛni/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Focus on Final Consonants</b>: Practice pronouncing the ending /d/ sound in words like "died" (/daɪd/) and "called" (/kɔːld/) to improve clarity.</li><li><b>Avoid Final Overstressing</b>: Work on placing the stress on the first syllable for words like "character" (/ˈkærəktər/) rather than the end of the word.</li><li><b>Improve Word Linking</b>: Try to connect your words together in short phrases to reduce the choppiness and make your speaking sound more natural.</li></ul><br>Your confidence in speaking is growing, so keep practicing these sounds! 🌟	\N	\N	9.0	Kao-Oat, you gave a clear and interesting review of your manga! Your "Imagine" hook was very effective and grabbed the attention of the audience immediately. You used some good vocabulary and relative clauses to describe the characters and the fantasy setting. To reach a higher score, you should try to speak for longer by adding more details about the author and the climax of the story. Watch out for small grammar points like subject-verb agreement and making negative sentences with "do not." You are doing a good job expressing your ideas clearly! 🌟	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
