ader... call... the leader name is Shigaraki. He's really jealous of... I don't know what he's jealous about, like, he's just mad from something about his grandma or something, and then he just came and just want to destroy the... this school, he just like, "Hey, hello, I'm here, I'm... I'm ready to destroy your school!"<br>So yeah, Deku have to fight it and then All Might was here because All Might was also a teacher in the school. He be like fight with Nomu and he's got punch in... it's drama and he's... come a hole, bro, it's a hole! Oh my god! Why did he...? Yeah, and then after that the story still continue about how... how Deku like... go fight with villain. Okay, and there's a highlight, after All Might can't fight anymore, Deku have... has to be... has to be the one who fight it. So he began to practice a lot and then just be... isolated, just let... let the friends behind his back because he don't want the friends to be in... like a danger, and then there's one day that Shigaraki came and then they have a big fight. <br>So... I won't tell that are they... who... who's gonna win, so yeah. Uh, I really like about the... something I really like about this book is about I like about their friendship, about between Deku and Bakugou and all the class. However, I don't like about at season one to three, I think it... it was too slow, like, oh my... it's so slow so I really bored and I just skip it. So, but I really recommend for someone who really like about friendship, about adventure, and just in the last part... it was really... there's many heartbreaking but also a... it make... feel you make you feel with happiness, so I really recommend you for that. Thank you everyone for listening.	708	4	Grace, your grammar and vocabulary choices made your review of My Hero Academia very exciting to listen to! Using English to describe complex plot points and character emotions can be tough, but you handled it with confidence. You earned a score of 4 out of 5 because you used a wide range of words and showed good control over your sentences. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a great variety of topic-specific vocabulary such as "quirkless," "isolated," and "heartbreaking" which really helped describe the story. Your use of the past tense was generally accurate when you were retelling the events of the manga. You also successfully used some complex structures like "someone who really likes adventure" and "if I was Deku I would do that."<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were some small errors when you used verbs after the word "did" or "to." For example, you said "what did you have to <b>did</b>" when it should be "what did you have to <b>do</b>." You also used the phrase "he be like fight," but in a B1 assessment, it is better to say "he was <b>fighting</b>" or "he <b>fought</b>." Try to remember that the word "finish" is usually followed by an "-ing" verb, like "finished <b>reading</b>."<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Master Verb Patterns:</b> Spend some time practicing which verbs take "to" and which take "-ing" so your sentences flow more accurately.</li><li><b>Refine Formal Language:</b> While your speaking was very natural, try to avoid using slang like "bro" or "sucks" during a formal school assessment.</li><li><b>Check Your Question Forms:</b> Practice forming questions in the past tense, making sure the main verb stays in the base form after "did."</li></ul><br>Your ability to express your feelings about the characters was a real highlight of your talk! 🎯	4	Grace, you did a wonderful job organizing your thoughts and leading the listener through the world of your favorite manga! It is easy to get lost when explaining a long story, but you kept your points logical and easy to follow. You achieved a score of 4 out of 5 for your clear structure and your ability to keep speaking for the full time. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You followed the required structure perfectly by including a great "imagine" hook and clearly naming the author. Your talk moved smoothly from the introduction to the climax and then to your personal opinion. You used many helpful connecting words like "however," "because," and "after that" to link your ideas together.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You used some filler words like "yeah" and "like" quite often while you were thinking of what to say next. While some pauses are normal, try to use more professional discourse markers like "actually" or "as a result" instead. Also, make sure that your opinion section explains exactly why you felt the first three seasons were "slow" to give more detail to your review.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Reduce Filler Words:</b> Try recording yourself speaking and see if you can explain a scene without using the word "like" too many times.</li><li><b>Vary Your Transitions:</b> Instead of always using "and then," try using words like "subsequently," "meanwhile," or "eventually" to show the order of events.</li><li><b>Expand Your Conclusion:</b> You gave a good recommendation, so try to add one more specific reason why a specific type of person would enjoy this book.</li></ul><br>You are a very talented storyteller who knows how to keep an audience engaged! 🎯	3	Grace, you delivered a very natural and engaging book review today. Your intonation was a highlight of your talk and helped bring the story to life. You should be very proud of how you handled such a complex manga plot.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your intonation was very natural, which made your storytelling feel authentic and interesting to listen to. You also used appropriate syllable stress across most of your vocabulary, helping the audience follow your points.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your volume was slightly low at times, making it a bit hard for everyone to hear your great ideas clearly. You also frequently dropped final consonant sounds, which reduced your overall intelligibility, such as the /t/ in "about" (/əˈbaʊt/), the /d/ in "middle" (/ˈmɪdəl/), and the /k/ in "shock" (/ʃɒk/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Increase Speaking Volume</b>: Try to raise your volume by about 10% to ensure your voice carries to the back of the room.</li><li><b>Pronounce Final Consonants</b>: Pay close attention to ending sounds like /t/, /d/, and /k/ in words such as "about" (/əˈbaʊt/), "bullied" (/bʊlid/), and "jump" (/dʒʌmp/).</li><li><b>Slow Down for Clarity</b>: Taking a breath between sentences will help you focus on finishing each word completely before starting the next one.</li></ul><br>You have a great natural rhythm to your speech! ✨	\N	\N	15.0	Grace, you delivered a very detailed and enthusiastic book review! You followed every part of the required structure, from the "imagine" hook to the author's name and the full plot exposition. Your vocabulary was a major strength, as you used specific words like "isolated" and "heartbreaking" to make your story more vivid. You also showed a good grasp of complex grammar and kept the story moving logically with effective transitions. To reach the next level, focus on using slightly more formal language by avoiding slang and filler words like "bro" or "yeah." You should also double-check your verb patterns, especially making sure to use the base form of the verb after "did." You have a natural talent for speaking, so keep up the great work! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
