e book. So overall I really love the book and also the show but in the book there's there's more detail and I love to read a details. Thank you for listening to my presentation.	415	4	In-Sun, you did a great job sharing your passion for this famous book series. Talking about such a complex story shows you have a lot of confidence in English. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows you are very close to the top level. <br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used advanced words like "savage" and "executed" to make your story more interesting. I liked how you compared George R. R. Martin to Harry Potter to explain the author's style. You also used relative clauses like "which is Jon Snow" very well to give extra details about the characters. <br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Watch out for the difference between adjectives ending in -ed and -ing. You said the book was "not excited," but it should be "not excit<b>ing</b>" because the book is the thing that causes the feeling. Also, be careful with subject-verb agreement, like saying "the author write" instead of "the author write<b>s</b>." These small slips are common for Thai speakers but important to fix to sound more natural. <br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Adjective patterns:</b> Practise the -ed vs -ing rule. Use -ing for things that cause a feeling and -ed for how people feel (/ɪkˈsaɪtɪŋ/ → /ɪkˈsaɪtɪ<b>d</b>/).</li><li><b>Third-person verbs:</b> When talking about the author or a character, always remember to add the "s" sound to your verbs in the present tense.</li><li><b>Plural nouns:</b> Check if you need an "s" on your nouns when talking about one of many things, such as saying "one of the successful show<b>s</b>."</li></ul>You are using very natural language—keep pushing! 🎯	4	In-Sun, your talk was very well organized and easy to follow. Discussing such a long story in just a few minutes is difficult, but you managed it well. This score of 4 out of 5 reflects your ability to link your ideas together smoothly. <br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You followed the required structure perfectly. You included a great hook, named the author, and explained the setting of the story clearly. Your use of words like "however" and "at first" helped the listener understand the flow of your ideas and the order of events. <br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You had some long pauses between your main ideas. While these help you think, try to use small filler phrases instead of silence. Also, try to give a bit more detail about the resolution of the first book so the story feels finished. Sometimes you repeated words like "which we will meet" when you were searching for your next thought. <br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Filler phrases:</b> Instead of long silences, try using phrases like "Let me think" or "Another interesting point is..." to keep your speech moving.</li><li><b>Summary skills:</b> Practise summarizing the ending of a story in just two sentences. This will help you finish your talk more strongly and clearly.</li><li><b>Vary your connectors:</b> You used "and also" quite often. Try to use other words like "moreover" or "in addition" to make your speech sound more varied and professional.</li></ul>You followed the structure of the talk very well! 🎯	4	In-Sun, you have made incredible progress with your speaking volume recently. You were perfectly audible throughout your interview, which is a huge improvement. Your intonation was also much more expressive, adding great 'color' to your storytelling.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You clearly tried very hard to pronounce difficult plural endings like /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/, which was wonderful to see. Your intonation massively improved, replacing your previously flat tone with a more dynamic and engaging voice.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your speech was a bit choppy at times, as you are still working on linking words smoothly together. I also noticed some dropped sounds at the ends of words, such as the /l/ in "successful" (/səkˈsɛsfəl/) and the final /s/ in "shows" (/ʃoʊz/) and "takes" (/teɪks/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Work on Word Linking</b>: Practice linking words in common phrases like "said the story will take us to" to make your speech flow more naturally.</li><li><b>Focus on Final /s/ and /l/</b>: Pay extra attention to the ending sounds in words like "successful" (/səkˈsɛsfəl/) and "takes" (/teɪks/).</li><li><b>Maintain Expressive Tone</b>: Keep practicing with your new dynamic voice to ensure your storytelling remains colorful and engaging.</li></ul><br>Your hard work is clearly paying off, so keep up the fantastic effort! 🌟	\N	\N	16.0	In-Sun, your review of A Game of Thrones was excellent and showed a high level of English! You used advanced vocabulary and relative clauses to describe the complex world of Westeros very clearly. You followed the task structure well, including a strong hook and an interesting comparison to other books. To reach the next level, focus on the -ed/-ing adjective rules and try to reduce the long pauses between your sentences. Great job sharing your thoughts on this famous series! 🌟	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
