 stronger.<br><br>	355	4	Dragon, you show a good understanding of how to put sentences together, especially when talking about familiar topics like your hobbies, food, and travel. You used some good vocabulary, such as 'private' when describing the restaurant and 'yummy' for your favourite food. You also tried to use more complex sentence structures, like 'I eat it since I was a kid' and 'because I want to prepare for a test next week,' which is excellent ambition! 👍 To reach an even higher score, you can continue to refine these complex forms for greater accuracy. For example, when you said, 'My, both of my parents are Chinese and half Thailand,' a more accurate way would be 'Both of my parents are Chinese and half Thai.' Another small point is using 'like' unnecessarily, as in 'My favorite kind of food is like chicken wings.' You could simply say, 'My favorite kind of food is chicken wings.' Finally, remember that 'Thailand' is the country, and 'Thai' is the adjective for people or things from Thailand. Paying attention to these small details will make your grammar even more precise! ✨	3	You do a great job of speaking in longer sentences rather than just short answers, Dragon, and that's fantastic for keeping the conversation going! Your contributions are usually very relevant to the topic, which shows you're listening and engaging well. For example, your description of the family in the restaurant was quite detailed. To improve this area, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas more smoothly and avoid repetition. For instance, instead of saying 'by myself and alone,' you could simply say 'by myself.' Also, instead of 'since I was a kid, same thing,' you could try 'I've played it since I was a kid, just like badminton.' Exploring different cohesive devices will help your speech flow more naturally and make your ideas even clearer for your listener! 🧩	2	Dragon, it's great that you are mostly understandable, and I can generally follow what you are saying. However, you have a very soft and flat speaking voice, and sometimes it's a little tricky to hear the ends of your words, which often affects clarity. This is frequently because, in Thai, consonant sounds at the end of words can be less pronounced or even dropped, but in English, they are really important for clarity. For example, when you say words like 'restaurant' or 'weekend', try to make sure those final /t/ and /d/ sounds are clear. You also have a tendency to swallow consonants, which means they aren't fully articulated. For instance, in words like 'playing' or 'something', make sure the /ŋ/ sound is distinct. These habits can sometimes make your speech sound a bit 'staccato' or abrupt. A little practice with pronouncing the final sounds of words and speaking with a bit more volume will make a big difference to how clear and fluent you sound! 🎤	3	You're good at starting conversations and answering questions, Dragon, which is a key part of speaking well with others! You also kept the conversation going nicely in the group discussion, offering your ideas about which club to join. Asking 'Can you recommend me which club is best for me?' and 'So, what about you?' are fantastic ways to involve your partners. This shows you are actively participating. To make your interactive communication even stronger, try to build more directly on what your partners say and extend your contributions to develop the conversation further. For instance, after someone shares an idea, you could say, 'That's an interesting point about the book club, but I think football would be more fun because...' This shows you're listening carefully and also adding your own thoughts to develop the conversation towards a shared outcome. Keep up the good work in chatting and sharing your ideas! 🗣️🤝	12.0	Hello Dragon! 👋 It was a real pleasure to listen to you speak in your English exam. You did a good job of communicating your ideas clearly and participating in the discussions, showing some good progress in your speaking abilities! To make your English even stronger, let's focus on making your voice a bit clearer and ensuring your speech flows even more smoothly. Keep up the great effort! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ **Speak with clarity:** Practice speaking a little louder and make a conscious effort to pronounce the consonant sounds at the ends of your words. Reading English stories or articles aloud every day can really help you develop this habit! 🗣️<br>✔️ **Expand your linking words:** When connecting your ideas, try to use a wider range of words and phrases beyond 'and' or 'because'. Think about using words like 'however', 'although', 'therefore', or 'in addition' to build more sophisticated sentences. 🌉<br>✔️ **Build on others' ideas:** In conversations, actively listen to your partners and try to extend what they've said. You could use phrases like 'That's an interesting point, and I also think...' or 'I agree with you, and I'd like to add that...' to keep the discussion lively and show great interaction skills! 💬	B1	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
33168	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	Hello, my name is Rome, and today I'm going to talk about the book I want to recommend. It is called The Phantom of the Opera by Gaston Leroux.<br>What would you do if you were an famous opera singer who want to marry your childhood friend but marrying your childhood friend would mean you have to end your own career? The story takes place in 1880s at the Palais Garnier opera theater in, in Paris. There was a little girl who's name is Christine Daaé. She was a ballet, ballet dancer who's become very popular after replacing the, the lead soprano because of her good teacher who she called the Angel of Music. And one day... she like performing like normal but her childhood friend, like, who she didn't see, like, for many years and, and, and want to marry her and but marry her would mean she have, she has to end her own career. So the, her teacher who's revealed to be Erik got very angry and kill innocent people then kidnap Christine to his lair below the, below the opera theater. So her childhood friend Raoul attempt to, attempt, attempt to rescue her but end up in a death trap. So Erik decided, if you want to save your childhood friend you have to choose me and if you choose Raoul, Raoul would die. So, so instead of choosing Raoul and kill him, she choose the phantom or the Erik. Then Erik got very, very, very confused, like, I never seen a person with, with this kindness before and he, like, wow, and then ... ... and then, like, disappear and let, and let Christine and Raoul go. <br>I really like this story because it, it was very complicated and, and the thing I like the most is when Raoul choose the strategy of, of Erik to kill a innocent people he cut the rope of the chandelier and let it boom, [laugh] and let it fall down. And, and the thing I don't like is in the end the, you know, human feeling is very complicated and I don't know why Erik choose to let them both go. Thank you.	365	4	Rome, you did a great job explaining the story of this classic book! Talking about complex plots and character motivations can be difficult in a second language, but you were very clear. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows that you have a strong grasp of B1 grammar and vocabulary. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used some excellent vocabulary to describe the plot, such as "innocent people," "kidnap," and "complicated." I was also impressed by your use of relative clauses to add detail, like when you spoke about the teacher "who she called the Angel of Music." You showed good control over tenses while telling the story, which helped the listener understand the sequence of events. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were a few small errors with articles, such as saying "an famous opera singer" instead of "<b>a</b> famous opera singer." I also noticed some issues with subject-verb agreement, for example saying "she have to" instead of "she <b>has</b> to." This is common for Thai speakers because Thai verbs do not change their endings. Finally, watch out for word order in phrases like "who she didn't see like for many years," which should be "who she <b>hadn't seen for many years</b>." <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Master Articles:</b> Practice choosing between "a" and "an" by looking at the first sound of the next word.</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> Create a checklist for your speaking practice to ensure "he," "she," and "it" always get an "s" on the verb in the present tense.</li><li><b>Verb Patterns:</b> Practice using the past perfect tense (had + past participle) when talking about things that happened before other events in your story.</li></ul><br>You are using a great range of words to tell exciting stories! 🎯	4	Rome, your presentation was very well-organized and easy to follow from start to finish! Organizing a book review with so many required parts is a big challenge, but you included almost everything. Your score of 4 out of 5 reflects your ability to speak at length and connect your ideas well. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your use of the rhetorical question hook was very effective because you actually answered it to set up the rest of your talk. You used a good variety of linking words like "so," "then," and "because" to show how one event led to another. You also provided a very clear exposition, covering the scene-setting and the climax in a way that kept the listener interested. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You used the word "like" as a filler many times while you were thinking about what to say next. There were also some moments where you repeated words like "and, and, and" or "ballet, ballet." While these are natural hesitations, they can sometimes make your speech feel a bit choppy. Try to use a short, silent pause instead of a filler word when you need a moment to think. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Reduce Filler Words:</b> Record yourself speaking for one minute and count how many times you say "like" or "um."</li><li><b>Vary Your Transitions:</b> Instead of always using "so" or "and," try using words like "however," "consequently," or "eventually" to move the story forward.</li><li><b>Stronger Conclusion:</b> You gave a good opinion, but try to end with a very clear recommendation sentence, such as "I recommend this book to anyone who loves a mystery."</li></ul><br>You have a natural way of telling stories that keeps people listening! 🌟	2	Rome, you did a great job following the structure of the book review and recommending a complex story. Your volume and intonation were both well-managed throughout your talk. You should be proud of how you handled such a difficult piece of literature.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your volume was consistent and your intonation helped to separate the different sections of your review. This made it easy for the audience to understand when you were moving from the plot to your opinion.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>The major block to your intelligibility was the repetitive dropping of final consonant sounds, which is a common challenge for Thai learners. I noticed this specifically with the /d/ sounds in "called" (/kɔːld/), "would" (/wʊd/), and "friend" (/frɛnd/), as well as /t/ sounds in "complicated" (/ˈkɒmplɪkeɪtɪd/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Focus on Final Consonants</b>: Practice saying words like "called," "would," and "friend" out loud, making sure the final /d/ is clearly heard.</li><li><b>Improve Sentence Stress</b>: Work on emphasizing the most important words in each sentence to help separate them from the smaller linking words.</li><li><b>Practice Word Linking</b>: Use a slow-paced recording to practice connecting your words without dropping the final sounds that join them.</li></ul><br>You have a strong understanding of story structure, so keep focusing on those final sounds! 🌟	\N	\N	13.0	Rome, you gave a very clear and engaging review of "The Phantom of the Opera"! You used an excellent rhetorical question hook and followed the story structure perfectly, making it easy for the listener to follow the plot. Your grammar and vocabulary are strong, especially when you use relative clauses to describe the characters. To improve, try to watch your articles (a/an) and subject-verb agreement. Also, working on reducing filler words like "like" will make your speaking sound even more professional. Well done on a successful book review! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
