our next review.</li></ul><br>You have a wonderful command of English vocabulary Venice! 🎯	5	Venice, your book review followed the required structure perfectly from start to finish. Organizing a long story into a short talk is a big challenge, but you kept everything in a logical order. Your score reflects how well you guided the listener through the plot. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You included every single part of the task, including the author and a great "Imagine" hook. Your use of transition words like "To begin" and "Along the way" helped the story flow smoothly. You also balanced the plot summary with your own opinions very well. You spoke for a good amount of time and gave enough detail for each section of the review.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Sometimes you use "and" or "so" to start many sentences in a row. Try to vary these connectors to make your speech sound more professional and less repetitive. You also had a few moments where you repeated words while thinking, which is common when telling an exciting story. Try to pause silently for a second instead of repeating the word "and" several times.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Vary your connectors:</b> Instead of starting with "so," try using words like "consequently," "as a result," or "furthermore."</li><li><b>Practice the climax:</b> Spend a little more time describing the feelings during the most exciting part of the story to build more tension.</li><li><b>Control fillers:</b> Record yourself speaking and listen for how many times you say "and... and." Practice replacing those with a short breath.</li></ul><br>You followed the structure like a professional reviewer! 🎯	3	Venice, your speaking volume was excellent today and your intonation was very engaging. You made a strong attempt to link your words together, which helped the overall flow of your talk. It was clear that you had practiced your transitions thoroughly.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You successfully avoided common Thai L1 patterns like overstressing final syllables, which made your speech sound very natural. Your use of intonation helped to highlight the drama and excitement in the story you were telling.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your main challenge today was with consonant clusters and final sounds, which were systematically dropped. This was noticeable in words like "criminal" (/ˈkrɪmɪnəl/), "got" (/ɡɒt/), "call" (/kɔːl/), and the /ks/ sound in "crucifix" (/ˈkruːsəfɪks/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Master Consonant Clusters</b>: Practice saying words with multiple consonants together, like /pr/ in "April" or /ks/ in "crucifix" (/ˈkruːsəfɪks/).</li><li><b>Pronounce Final /t/ and /l/</b>: Focus on finishing words like "got" (/ɡɒt/) and "call" (/kɔːl/) with their correct ending sounds.</li><li><b>Add More Heart to Speech</b>: Continue practicing your natural intonation to ensure your audience feels the emotion in your book reviews.</li></ul><br>Your natural rhythm is a huge asset to your speaking! 🌟	\N	\N	17.0	Venice, this was a brilliant book review that really captured the excitement of a thriller! You used impressive vocabulary like psychologist and autopsy and followed the required structure perfectly, including a great hook and clear exposition. Your grammar was strong, and you used helpful transition words to connect your story points logically. To improve even more, focus on using adjective forms like violent instead of nouns and try to vary your sentence starters so you do not rely on the word so too much. You explained the plot, the climax, and your opinion with great clarity and confidence. Excellent work on this speaking task! 🌟	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
