nse was mostly correct when describing Harry's background. You also used relative clauses well to give more information about the characters, such as describing the family he lived with.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Sometimes you missed the word 'is' or 'was' in your sentences. For example, you said 'he 11 years old' instead of 'he <b>was</b> 11 years old.' There were also a few times you used the wrong verb form after 'have to.' It should be 'he had to <b>face</b>' rather than 'facing.' Watch out for subject-verb agreement too, as 'he has' or 'he had' is better than 'he have.'<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Check your 'be' verbs:</b> Always make sure your sentences have a main verb, especially when talking about age or identity.</li><li><b>Practice 'have to' patterns:</b> Remember that 'have to' or 'had to' is always followed by the base form of the verb, like 'have to protect.'</li><li><b>Singular vs. Plural:</b> Spend a little time practicing the 's' ending for third-person singular verbs in the present tense to make your grammar even smoother.</li></ul><br>Your grammar is already helping you tell great stories! 🎯	5	Kaohom, you followed the structure of the book review perfectly! It can be hard to organize a long talk. You should be happy with this score because you stayed very organized.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used an excellent rhetorical question as a hook and answered it right away. The way you moved from the setting to the main problem was very clear. You used words like 'so,' 'at first,' and 'that's why' to help the listener follow your story without getting lost.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your explanation of the 'confusing point' was a little bit brief. You mentioned it was hard to imagine because it is fantasy. Giving a specific example of a confusing scene would make it even clearer. Also, try to avoid using 'you know, like' too often when you are thinking of what to say next as this can break