to do about how this movie is so exciting or how about movie is...<br><br>Um, teacher will give a movie choice like Harry Potter or some zombie movie to student to act like in the movie, have a group and act to teacher and people in the club look how do it that.<br><br>Um, in M one, they make student to make a club for their own and join friend other room to come in club. And I think should be like a M one because in M two they didn't, uh, give student to make a club. They bring the teacher to make a group of club like basketball, fut, soccer, and dancing club, movie, or something like history. And I think it's so boring sometimes because teacher didn't have a relax more and she, and they didn't give student to uh... don't give a opinion how it make something in a club.	520	2	Ping-Ping, you used a good range of words to describe things you enjoy, like "fried rice with egg" and the "shabu" in the photograph, which is excellent! You also made some good attempts at longer sentences, especially when describing the movie club activities. 👍 However, to make your sentences even stronger, let's focus on two key areas: verb tenses and how verbs agree with their subjects. For example, you said, "Last weekend I **go** to a movie," but because it happened in the past, it should be "Last weekend I **went** to a movie." Similarly, "it **make** easy and fast to eat" would be clearer as "it **makes** easy and fast to eat." Sometimes, Thai speakers might find these specific grammar points challenging because the rules are a bit different from Thai, where verb forms often don't change as much. Practising these little changes will make a big difference to how clear and natural your English sounds!	3	You did a fantastic job of speaking for longer periods, Ping-Ping, especially when you described the family dinner in the photograph! You shared many details and even expressed your feelings, which kept me very engaged. Your contributions were always relevant, and you used words like "and" and "because" to link your ideas together nicely. While there were a few moments where you paused to think, this is a very natural part of speaking, and you always managed to continue. You are well on your way to connecting your thoughts even more smoothly, like weaving a beautiful story with your words! 🧶	3	Ping-Ping, your speech was generally clear, and I could understand most of what you said, which is a great achievement! You made a good effort to control the rhythm and flow of your sentences. However, I noticed that you sometimes drop the sounds at the ends of words, for instance, pronouncing "shrimps" as if it were "shrimp" or "fried" without a clear /d/ sound at the end. Conversely, you sometimes overstressed the final syllables in words, which can make your speech sound a little less natural. For example, you might pronounce "picture" as /pɪkˈtʃɜːr/ instead of /ˈpɪktʃər/. This is quite common for Thai speakers because Thai often uses less emphasis on final consonants and has different stress patterns. Practising those final sounds and letting the natural rhythm of English guide you will help your pronunciation become even more fluent and clear! 🎶	3	You're a great conversationalist, Ping-Ping! You did an excellent job of starting discussions and responding to both the examiner and Pheem. You kept the conversation going by asking questions like "What do you think?" and "How about tennis?", which is a wonderful way to involve your partner and develop the interaction. It shows you were actively listening and keen to hear other opinions. You also clearly stated your own ideas, helping to move the discussion forward without needing much encouragement. Keep up this brilliant interaction; it makes talking to you very enjoyable! 👯‍♀️	11.0	Hello Ping-Ping! It was wonderful to hear you speak. You're doing a really good job of expressing your ideas and keeping conversations flowing. To take your English to the next level, let's focus on fine-tuning your grammar and practicing those tricky English sounds. Keep up your enthusiastic participation; your hard work is certainly paying off! ✨<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>• ✅ Master Your Tenses: Pay close attention to when actions happen! If you're talking about something you did "last weekend," remember to use the past tense, like "I **went** to a movie" instead of "I go." A quick check: does the action belong in the past, present, or future? 🤔<br>• ✅ Check Subject-Verb Agreement: When you use "it" or "my dad," make sure the verb matches! For example, "it **makes**" (with an 's') and "my dad **brings**" (with an 's') are correct. Practice saying these sentences out loud to get a feel for it. 🗣️<br>• ✅ Pronounce Final Sounds Clearly: English loves its final consonant sounds! Try to make sure you clearly pronounce the /s/ at the end of words like "shrimps" /ʃrɪmps/ or the /d/ at the end of "fried" /fraɪd/. This will make your speech even clearer and help avoid misunderstandings. 🔊	A2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
