 that' instead of 'how will you express that.' You also missed the '-s' on verbs for the writer or the boy, such as 'writer explain' instead of 'writer explains.' Using 'a one book' is also a bit unnatural because you only need to say 'one book' or 'a book.' <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> <br><ul><li><b>Word families:</b> Practice the difference between nouns like 'expression' and verbs like 'express.'</li><li><b>Subject-verb agreement:</b> Remember to add an '-s' to verbs when talking about a person in the third person like 'he says' or 'she reads.'</li><li><b>Article use:</b> Practice when to use 'a' or 'the' and avoid combining 'a' with the number 'one.'</li></ul><br>Keep building your vocabulary and you will feel even more confident! 🎯	3	Kaohom C, it was very interesting to hear about your book choice today. Organizing a book review with a hook and exposition can be tricky, but you followed the requirements. You reached a solid level for this task, so let's look at how you did. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> <br>You followed the required structure by introducing yourself and giving the book title and author. Your use of the 'Imagine' hook was very effective because it made the topic relatable for the audience. You used basic cohesive devices like 'so,' 'but,' and 'because' to connect your different ideas. You also provided a clear recommendation at the end which helped wrap up your talk. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> <br>There were several long pauses and filler words like 'um' and 'uh' throughout your speech. This made your talk sound a bit choppy and affected the flow of your story. The description of the story events was quite short, so we didn't hear much about the climax or how the boy's problem was solved. Try to spend a little more time developing the middle part of your review to keep the audience interested. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> <br><ul><li><b>Practice with notes:</b> Write down bullet points for each section and practice speaking until you have fewer hesitations.</li><li><b>Expand the climax:</b> Try to describe the most exciting or emotional part of the book in more detail.</li><li><b>Use silence:</b> When you need to think, it is better to have a short silence than to use filler words like 'yeah' or 'um.'</li></ul><br>You have a clear voice, so keep practicing your storytelling! 🎯	3	Kaohom C, you chose a very interesting book to talk about and your intonation showed a good understanding of the story's mood. You have a very polite and gentle speaking style that is pleasant to listen to. With more confidence, your book reviews will be even better.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your choice of vocabulary was appropriate for the story and you made a good attempt at using intonation to convey the 'feeling' of the book. You successfully followed the required structure for the presentation.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>The major issue today was your volume, as you were speaking so softly that it was sometimes hard to hear your words. You also tended to overstress final syllables in words like "writer" (/ˈraɪtər/) and "feeling" (/ˈfiːlɪŋ/), and the slow pace reduced your overall flow.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Increase Your Volume</b>: Try to speak significantly louder—about 20% more—to ensure everyone can hear your great ideas.</li><li><b>Correct Final Syllable Stress</b>: Practice stressing the first syllable in words like "writer" (/ˈraɪtər/) and "feeling" (/ˈfiːlɪŋ/).</li><li><b>Work on Fluency</b>: Try to speak in shorter phrases rather than word-by-word to help improve your overall speed and flow.</li></ul><br>You have high-quality ideas, so make sure we can hear them clearly! 🌟	\N	\N	12.0	Kaohom C, you gave a solid book review with a very effective 'Imagine' hook! You followed the structure well and shared your personal opinions clearly. Your grammar shows good control of simple forms, but you should practice the difference between nouns like 'expression' and verbs like 'express.' To improve your score, try to reduce the pauses and filler words to make your speech flow more naturally. Focus on adding more detail to the middle of your story and checking your verb endings! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
