rong verb form. For example, instead of "Greg very happy," you should say "Greg <b>was</b> /wɒz/ very happy." Also, watch out for present versus past tense when describing the ending; "he leave" should be "he <b>left</b>" /left/. In Thai, verbs do not change for the past, so this is a very common thing to check in English.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Use the verb "to be":</b> Remember to include "is," "am," or "are" (or "was/were" for the past) before adjectives like "happy" or "confused."</li><li><b>Past tense consistency:</b> When telling a story about something that already happened, try to keep all your verbs in the past form.</li><li><b>Recommend patterns:</b> Instead of saying "I very recommend it," try using "I <b>highly</b> recommend it" or "I recommend it <b>very much</b>."</li></ul><br>Your vocabulary choices were very appropriate for this book review. 🎯	3	Namunn, you followed the structure of the book review task well! Talking for a long time in English is a big challenge for many students. You managed to cover almost all the required parts of the presentation in a logical way.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a great "Imagine" hook to start your talk and get the audience interested in the party. Your exposition was clear because you set the scene in the United States and described Greg Heffley well. You also gave a clear opinion on why you liked the plot twist and provided a recommendation at the end.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You missed mentioning the <b>author</b> of the book, which was a required part of the task. Your talk was also a bit shorter than the two-minute goal. Try to add more details about the climax to make the story even more exciting for the listener and to meet the time limit.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Check the requirements:</b> Before you start, make sure you have the author's name written down so you do not forget it.</li><li><b>Expand the climax:</b> Spend more time describing the "shocking" part of the story to help the audience understand exactly what happened at the party.</li><li><b>Practice with a timer:</b> Aim to speak for the full two minutes by adding one or two more details to the beginning and the end.</li></ul><br>You followed the structure carefully and made the story easy to follow. 🎯	2	Namunn, you delivered your book review with good volume and appropriate intonation today. Your storytelling was clear and you followed all the required structural steps for the presentation. You should be pleased with how you communicated the main plot points.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your volume was perfect for the room and your intonation helped to make the story sound interesting. You were able to convey the main ideas of your book review effectively to your classmates.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>The main challenges were with sound drops and incorrect word stress. You systematically dropped final sounds in words like "states" (/steɪts/), "found" (/faʊnd/), and "end" (/ɛnd/), and you tended to overstress words like "party" (/ˈpɑːrti/) and "united" (/juːˈnaɪtɪd/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Focus on Final Consonants</b>: Practice pronouncing the ending sounds in "states" (/steɪts/), "found" (/faʊnd/), and "end" (/ɛnd/).</li><li><b>Improve Word Stress</b>: Focus on emphasizing the correct syllables in words like "party" (/ˈpɑːrti/), "character" (/ˈkærəktər/), and "united" (/juːˈnaɪtɪd/).</li><li><b>Strengthen Word Linking</b>: Work on connecting your words together smoothly without losing the important consonant sounds between them.</li></ul><br>You have a solid foundation, so keep focusing on these specific sounds! 🌟	\N	\N	11.0	Namunn, you gave a clear and engaging review of Diary of a Wimpy Kid! You used a strong Imagine hook and included great vocabulary like plot twist and uncomfortable. To improve, remember to mention the author and try to speak for the full two minutes by adding more details to the story. Watch out for small grammar points like using was before adjectives and keeping your verbs in the past tense. You are a confident speaker. 🌟	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
