illage that <b>lives</b>." You also used "got control by it" when "was <b>controlled</b> by it" /kənˈtrəʊld/ sounds more natural for this context. Watch out for phrases like "get action to them," which could be replaced with "took <b>action</b>."<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement</b>: Practice matching singular subjects with "s" verbs in the present tense to avoid dropping final sounds.</li><li><b>Passive Voice</b>: Learn how to use "was/were + past participle" for things happening to people, like "she was <b>possessed</b> by a ghost."</li><li><b>Prepositions and Collocations</b>: Read more English books to see how words fit together naturally, like "acting <b>strangely</b>" instead of "acting weird."</li></ul><br>Your storytelling is very engaging! 🎯	3	Boss, you followed the structure of the book review task very closely!<br>It is not easy to organize a long talk like this, but you kept the story moving forward.<br>You achieved a score of 3 because you covered most parts of the task well. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your hook was very effective because you used a rhetorical question to grab the listener's attention. You used helpful transition words like "in the beginning," "however," and "at the end" to guide us through the plot. The way you explained the climax and the sad resolution made the story easy to follow.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You missed one required part of the task, which was naming the <b>author</b> of the book. Also, your conclusion was a bit too short; simply saying "that's all" does not give the listener a strong finish. Try to avoid long pauses like "uh... ... ..." at the end of your talk to maintain a steady flow.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Check the Task List</b>: Always make sure you include the author's name at the start of your review.</li><li><b>Stronger Conclusion</b>: End with a recommendation, like "I recommend this book to anyone who loves horror stories."</li><li><b>Expand Your Reasons</b>: If you run out of things to say, try giving more details about why the book made you feel "interested" or "scared."</li></ul><br>You have a natural way of telling stories that keeps people listening! 🎯	4	You spoke with great energy and volume during your book presentation. It was very easy to follow your ideas because of your clear delivery and good pace. Your intonation helped highlight the most interesting parts of your review.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> You demonstrated a good ability to link sounds together in some phrases, which added a natural quality to your speech. Your volume was consistent, ensuring that your voice was heard clearly throughout the room.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> You systematically dropped some final consonant sounds /ˈkɒn.sə.nənt/, such as the "j" in "changed" /tʃeɪndʒd/ and the "s" in "that's" /ðæts/. There were also some slight issues with syllable stress /ˈsɪl.ə.bəl/ in longer words.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Focus on clearly pronouncing the final /d/ or /ɪd/ sounds in past tense verbs like "completed" /kəmˈpliː.tɪd/.<br>2. Practice the "s" sounds at the end of plural words like "challenges" /ˈtʃæl.ɪn.dʒɪz/ to ensure they are fully audible.<br><br>Keep up the great effort and focus on those final sounds, Boss! 🚀	\N	\N	15.0	Boss, this was a scary and exciting book review! You used a great rhetorical question as a hook and followed the plot of 'Tee-Yod' very clearly from the beginning to the tragic end. Your grammar is strong, especially your use of 'if' sentences and relative clauses. To improve, remember to mention the author and try to create a more formal conclusion instead of just saying 'that's all'. Working on your subject-verb agreement and avoiding long silences at the end will help you reach a higher band. You have a real talent for making a story sound interesting! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
