e end of words. Keep listening closely to native speakers and try to imitate how they finish their words and where they place the stress – it will make a big difference! 👂	2	You did a good job of responding to questions and clearly stating your opinions, Kao, which helps keep the conversation going! 😊 However, to score even higher, try to link your ideas more consistently to what your friends are saying. Instead of just giving your own viewpoint, you could ask follow-up questions or build directly on their points. For example, you could say, "I agree with [friend's name] because..." or "That's an interesting point, and I also think..." This shows you're not just participating but also actively engaging in a lively discussion! 🎉	10.0	Hello Kao,<br><br>You did a really good job of sharing your thoughts and participating in the discussions! You have a clear voice and some great ideas. To help you progress even further, focusing on polishing your pronunciation, especially those final sounds in words, and trying to build more on your friends' ideas in conversation will be beneficial. Keep speaking, Kao; your English is steadily improving!<br><br>📋 **Action Plan** <br><li>Practice finishing your words clearly and pay attention to word stress.</li><br><li>Work on linking your contributions more directly to what others say.</li><br><li>Actively listen to how native speakers pronounce consonants at the end of words.</li>	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
34189	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	My name is Fergie and today I'm going to talk about a book called Rise and Fall of the Third Reich by William Shirer.<br>So, imagine that you are a leader of a powerful nation. Everybody fear and respect you. How interesting would that be? The book set in the... ... is set in German 1933 when Hitler became the leader of the Nazi German. He have... he has a dream to make the economy of German great again. So, he start expanding his power across Europe. He invade France, Poland, and Norway. And he even invade the Soviet in 1941.<br>Things get very interesting when the brutal Soviet winter hit. German lose... lost at Stalingrad, this is a key point of turning the war. The German got pushed back from the Soviet border and the Allies that contains the USA, British, start pushing from the Western front and Soviet start advancing... from the Eastern front. This... stress Hitler because he cannot deal from two fronts at once. He start... getting mentally... disordered. So, in April... April 1945, he commit suicide in his bunker with his wife Elena. So, that mark the end of the most fearsome and most powerful leader... in history.<br>So, my favorite thing about this book is how they show the character development, the... that show us how Hitler start from just a normal painter to a dictator. But... I found it quite boring because it is a old book because... and there is no picture at all, just a pure text. But... I really recommend you guys if you guys like history, you should try it out. Thank you for paying attention to my presentation.	280	5	Fergie, your grammar and vocabulary were very effective during your book review. Learning to describe historical events in English is a big challenge for many students. You should feel good about how clearly you shared your ideas.<b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You used a wide range of vocabulary that was perfect for a history topic. Words like "invade," "dictator," and "mentally disordered" made your talk sound very professional. You also did a great job of correcting yourself when you made a small mistake, which shows you are thinking about your grammar as you speak.<b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You sometimes missed the final "s" or the past tense ending on verbs. For example, you said "he start" when it should be "he start<b>ed</b>" or "he start<b>s</b>." You also used the word "German" when you meant the country "Germa<b>ny</b>." This is a common pattern for Thai speakers because the words for countries and nationalities can be confusing.<b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Subject-verb agreement:</b> Practice saying sentences about a single person like Hitler or a leader using the correct verb endings.</li><li><b>Country vs Nationality:</b> Remember that Germa<b>ny</b> is the place and Germa<b>n</b> is the language or the person.</li><li><b>Verb patterns:</b> Try to use more patterns like "he decided to" or "he planned to" to add variety to your sentences.</li></ul>Your vocabulary choices were excellent and helped me understand the book well! 🎯	5	Fergie, you organized your presentation exactly as the task required. It is not easy to remember all the different parts of a talk, but you included everything. Your score shows that you are very good at planning your speech.<b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You followed the structure of the book review perfectly. Your "imagine" hook was a great way to start because it made the listener interested in the story right away. You also explained the climax and the end of the book very clearly, which helped the story make sense.<b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You had some long pauses between your ideas, especially when you were moving from the story to your opinion. Try to use small connecting words to fill these gaps instead of silence. Also, when you talk about your opinion, try to give more details about why the book was boring or why the character development was good.<b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Use transition phrases:</b> Practice phrases like "Next, let's look at my opinion" or "Finally, I recommend" to connect your sections.</li><li><b>Expand your reasons:</b> When you say a book is boring, explain if it is because of the words, the speed of the story, or the topic.</li><li><b>Practice the flow:</b> Record yourself speaking and try to reduce the silence between your sentences to make the talk smoother.</li></ul>You kept the listener interested from the beginning to the end! 🎯	4	Fergie, you have a very natural and pleasant intonation that makes your speaking easy to follow. You chose a great book and explained the story details with a lot of clarity and charm. Your overall delivery was very professional and well-paced.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your intonation was a real strength today, giving your book review a very natural and authentic feel. You used a good range of vocabulary to describe the events and characters in your story.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your volume was a little soft at times, so raising it slightly would help ensure everyone can hear you properly. I also noticed some dropped tense endings like "ended" (/ˈɛndɪd/) and "pushed" (/pʊʃt/), as well as challenges with the /pr/ cluster in "April" (/ˈeɪprəl/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Raise Your Volume</b>: Try to speak about 5% louder to ensure your voice is clear throughout the entire talk.</li><li><b>Master Tense Endings</b>: Practice the /ɪd/, /t/, and /d/ sounds at the ends of past tense verbs like "ended" (/ˈɛndɪd/) and "pushed" (/pʊʃt/).</li><li><b>Practice Consonant Clusters</b>: Work on the /pr/ sound in words like "April" (/ˈeɪprəl/) to make your pronunciation even clearer.</li></ul><br>You are making steady progress with your speaking skills, so keep it up! 🌟	\N	\N	19.0	Fergie, you did a great job explaining a complex history book clearly. You used the imagine hook effectively and followed every part of the required structure. Your vocabulary was very appropriate for the topic of war and history. To improve, focus on small grammar points like subject-verb agreement and the difference between the country name and the nationality. You are speaking at a very high level for your grade! 🌟	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
