ies" and "healthy recipes," which is excellent. Sometimes, like many Thai speakers, you might omit a small word like "is" or use the wrong pronoun, so try to listen for those little details to make your sentences even more precise!	4	You're doing a wonderful job of speaking at length, Kathi, and your ideas are usually very clear and easy to follow! 🎉 You hardly hesitate, and your contributions are always relevant to the discussion. You use connecting words like "so," "because," and "and" really well to link your thoughts together, which helps the listener understand your points. When you compared the two pictures, you organised your thoughts very logically, explaining what was happening in each one. Sometimes, you might repeat certain phrases, like "very enjoy" or "well well," so try to think of different ways to express the same idea to add variety to your speaking. Overall, you do a great job of keeping the conversation going and making your points clear!	3	Kathi, you are very easy to understand, which is the most important thing! 🙌 You have some control over how your words and sentences sound. To make your speech even smoother, try to link your words more often. For instance, in "teenager," the 'n' sound can flow into the 'a' sound rather than sounding like two separate words. I also noticed that you sometimes put a lot of emphasis on the last syllable of words or the last word in a phrase (like in "second," "very," and "cooking"). Practising a more natural rhythm and varying your stress can help your English sound even more natural and effortless.	5	You are a fantastic communicator, Kathi! 🌟 You initiate discussions and respond to others brilliantly, often building on what your partner says. During the healthy eating project discussion, you confidently proposed the "cooking club" and then involved Lin-Lin by asking, "How about you?" You also showed you were listening carefully to Lin-Lin when you said, "I think like Lin-Lin because if we know what where the food