instead of 'the alien <b>was</b> still alive.' This is a common pattern for Thai learners who might focus more on the main action word. You also used some awkward verb forms like 'turning people into become' instead of just 'turning people into' them. Watch out for mixing up 'doesn't' and 'didn't' when talking about a story that happened in the past.<b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice the verb 'to be':</b> Make sure every sentence has a main verb, especially when you are describing how something looks or feels.</li><li><b>Verb patterns:</b> Practice which verbs can be followed by 'ing' or prepositions like 'into' so your sentences flow more naturally.</li><li><b>Maintain past tense:</b> Since your story happens in 1985, try to use past tense verbs like 'didn't' and 'was' throughout your talk.</li></ul>Great job sharing this scary story! 🎯	3	Rico, you followed the book review structure very well from start to finish. Organizing a long talk is a big challenge for many students. Your score of 3 means you connected your ideas well enough for the listener to follow.<b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your 'Imagine' hook was very effective because it made the listener interested in the alien story immediately. You used helpful transition words like 'At first,' 'After that,' and 'In conclusion' to move between different parts of your talk. You covered all the important parts of the story, including the setting in Antarctica and the final resolution.<b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You forgot to mention the author of the book, which was a required part of the task. There were several long pauses and sighs where you seemed to lose your place or feel a bit nervous. This made the flow of your speech feel a bit disconnected at times, which happens when we search for the right words. Try to keep the story moving even if you forget a specific detail.<b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Use a checklist:</b> Before you speak, write down the book title and author 