ss your opinions clearly and engage in a natural chat is something to be truly proud of! 🎉 To make your speaking even clearer and more impactful, focusing on reducing your use of filler words like "like" and refining some of your grammatical structures will be a wonderful next step. Keep up the excellent work, and you'll continue to grow as a confident English speaker! 🌟<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Speaking Success:**<br>*   ✍️ Try to notice when you use filler words like "like" and pause instead, or replace them with short phrases like "I mean" or "you know."<br>*   📚 Practice using a wider variety of grammatical structures, especially when expressing preferences or explaining reasons, like using "I prefer..." instead of "I'm more prefer..."<br>*   🗣️ Focus on pronouncing words with multiple syllables, paying attention to which syllable naturally receives the main stress to help your rhythm sound even more natural.	B2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
33953	2025-09-18	2025-09-18	2026	Term 1 Finals	My name is Salila Larpkasetsukwong, S A L I L A. Or you can call me Kaohom for short.<br><br>KAOHOM: I'm from Samut Sakhon. This city is very famous for seafood and sea.<br><br>KAOHOM: Um, I use the internet for learning online or in clips in YouTube. It's make me more understand in the some subject that I learned in the class and I didn't understand that much.<br><br>KAOHOM: I think the picture number two is the easiest to use because many people use like in the picture, have apps like Instagram, Facebook, and they can text, and it's like faster to like join Zoom or send a link to join together.<br><br>KAOHOM: Okay, um, at the photo on the top, I think it's really good for education because nowadays it, um, technology become take place in education. Like in the picture, they have many computers, so they can use technology, AI for searching for the information in studying and education. And the photo below, um, it's in the classroom, so they can learn like, um, have many people, so they can improve the communication skills, and I think it's sometimes is easier to understand because they have the teacher to explaining the information for you.<br><br>KAOHOM: Yes, I see you points. And I think making video calls is connect to playing online games together because sometimes I also, um, in the video call with my friends and also playing online games together.<br><br>KAOHOM: Yes. Um, I think using social media is make me connect with my friends too because I'm always follow my friends in social media, so I can see what they are doing now and they like what activity that they do with friends or families.<br><br>KAOHOM: Yes, I think making video call is the most fun way to communicate with my friends because, like, you can share your idea, what you, what it's happened to you today. I, I use this way to talk with my friends, like sometimes every night, because I can share my idea what I want to do or decide activity to do something with it.<br><br>KAOHOM: I also think technology make we can have friends easier because, like, I can find some new friends in like sometimes in online because sometimes people can find friends in foreign country for in online to improve their English. Yes, but... yeah.<br><br>KAOHOM: Um, I think if we meet face-to-face, we can see the emotion of each other more easier because in online, sometimes we talk together, but not see the face. So we can understanding each other easier.<br><br>KAOHOM: I think the positive way is you can communicate each other all the time and everywhere. So it's easier to communicate when we have technology. And the negative way, I think sometimes people cannot separate their time, so they're spending many times on social media, technology, and didn't meet with their family, friends in real life.<br><br>KAOHOM: Um, from my view, I think yes, because nowadays technology become take place but, oh sorry. In education very much, like in our classroom, we also have like digital TV, so it's make our education become more easier. And if teenager or students know a lot about technology, so they can use it in the correct way, not to make you more lazy and don't think.	547	3	You show a good understanding of how to put sentences together, especially when talking about familiar topics like your hometown and how you use technology for learning. For example, you said, "This city is very famous for seafood and sea," which is a clear and well-structured sentence. 👍 You also used a good range of vocabulary appropriate for discussing technology and education. However, there were a few instances where refining your grammar could make your communication even clearer. For instance, you said, "It's make me more understand," which would be more accurate as "It **makes** me understand better." Also, "technology become take place" would be better phrased as "technology **has taken its** place" or "technology **is taking its** place." These minor corrections in verb forms and phrasing will help you express your ideas with greater precision. Focusing on consistent subject-verb agreement and choosing the most natural-sounding verb phrases will really elevate your grammar! 📚	3	You did a fantastic job of producing extended stretches of language and keeping your contributions relevant, with very little repetition! You used cohesive devices like "because" and "and" effectively to link your ideas, which helped your partner and the examiner follow your thoughts. For example, when comparing the photos, you explained, "Like in the picture, they have many computers, so they can use technology, AI for searching for the information in studying and education." This shows good organization. To move towards an even higher score, you could try using a greater variety of discourse markers and connectors. Instead of always using "and" or "so," try incorporating words like "furthermore," "however," "in addition," or "consequently" to add more sophistication to your spoken arguments and make your ideas flow even more smoothly. This will show you can manage your discourse with more advanced tools! 🧩	4	Your pronunciation is really clear, and you are nearly always intelligible, which means you are very easy to understand! 🌟 Your linking of sounds is good, which helps your speech sound natural, and it's excellent that you can pronounce plural endings accurately. You're very close to achieving the top score in this area! The main area for improvement is a mild tendency to overstress some final syllables, for example, in words like 'picture' or 'computer'. Try to let these final syllables relax a little more rather than giving them too much emphasis. This is a common pattern for Thai speakers, and focusing on the subtle rhythm and stress of English words can help your speech sound even more authentic and effortless. Keep listening to English speakers and try to mimic their natural intonation! 🎧	4	You were great at initiating and responding appropriately, and you actively maintained and developed the interaction with your partner! For example, when Arty shared an idea, you built on it by saying, "Yes, I see you points. And I think making video calls is connect to playing online games together..." This shows you were listening carefully and actively contributing to the discussion. You also clearly stated your opinions and reasons, helping the conversation move forward. To reach a perfect score, you could try to more consistently link your contributions *directly* to your partner's specific ideas, perhaps by explicitly referring back to their exact words or expanding on a particular detail they mentioned. This would demonstrate an even higher level of collaborative negotiation and development in your interaction! Keep up the brilliant teamwork! 🤝	14.0	Kaohom L, you did a wonderful job today in your speaking test, especially with your clear pronunciation and how you kept the conversation going! 🗣️ Your ability to connect your ideas and contribute to the discussion was really strong. To make your English even more precise, let's focus on refining some of your grammar, especially when you're forming more complex sentences. You're demonstrating a solid B2 level, and with a little more attention to detail, you'll be speaking with even greater accuracy! ✨<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Pay close attention to verb forms and subject-verb agreement, especially when using phrases like "it makes" or "it helps."<br>✔️ Practice using a wider range of cohesive devices and introductory phrases to vary how you link your ideas and sentences.<br>✔️ Continue to focus on the natural rhythm and stress of English words and sentences to refine your pronunciation further.	B2	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
28836	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	Hi everyone, good morning. I'm Namo K. Today I'm going to talk about my favorite book of all time. It's called Ramakien and it's written by King Rama One. So, picture this. You were given a task to kill a giant as a human.<br>So, this all started at heaven where the gods live. There was once a giant named Nonthok. He didn't have any powerful powers and work for the angels. But the angels didn't treat him light. They treated him like a trash. They bullied him, even stole his hair. So, that made Nonthok furious. He wish for a finger that can kill any god. When his... when his wish was granted, there was... there was many angels that died. So cause of that, a god was sent to kill him. But before he died, they made a promise that the god will reborn with Nonthok and kill him as a human. And Nonthok will have twenty hands and ten heads with powerful power.<br>Then when they are reborn, Nonthok was reborn as Tosakanth. He was training since he was born as a child. Throughout time, he conquer many cities and become the most powerful being in the earth. Now for the god, he born as a... as a human in a... in a town. He train himself too. He actually got a wife and they live peaceful together until one day they went into the jungles where the human go find foods for his wife. But suddenly, Tosakanth come out of nowhere and kidnap her. This make... this action started a huge war between the human and Tosakanth. It causes... it cause to a million loss of lives, but in the end, the human win again.<br>So, what I really like about this story is that they got a lot of characters because the characters have a such interesting details and are related together. But what I don't like is that they design some of the character personality to be bad. Example like Tosakanth. He was very lustful and have a thousands of wife. So, from this, I really recommend this book to you guys if you want to know about history about Thailand. Thank you. [applause]	370	4	Namo, you did a great job sharing the story of the Ramakien! Telling a long historical epic in a second language can be tricky, but your meaning was very clear. You should feel good about your performance today. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used some very effective words like "furious," "kidnap," and "lustful" to make your story more interesting. Your use of past tense was mostly consistent throughout the talk. You also correctly used relative clauses like "a finger that can kill any god" to join your ideas together. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were a few small slips with word forms and articles. For example, you said "treat him light" instead of "treat him <b>lightly</b>" and "like a trash" instead of just "like trash." Sometimes you forgot the /d/ or /t/ sounds at the end of past actions, like saying "he conquer" /kɒŋkə/ instead of "he <b>conquered</b>" /kɒŋkəd/. These are common patterns for Thai speakers, so paying attention to those final sounds will help a lot. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Focus on Adverbs:</b> Practice adding "-ly" to words that describe how someone does something, such as saying "lived peacefully" or "treated him badly."</li><li><b>Uncountable Nouns:</b> Remember that words like "trash" and "food" usually do not need "a" or an "s" at the end.</li><li><b>Past Tense Endings:</b> Record yourself speaking and listen specifically for the /t/ or /d/ sounds at the end of verbs to ensure they are clear.</li></ul><br>You have a great vocabulary for describing stories! 📖	5	Namo, you followed the structure of the book review task perfectly! Sharing such a famous Thai story in English is a big challenge, but you organized your ideas very well. Your score shows that you are a confident storyteller. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used an excellent variety of linking words like "Throughout time," "Suddenly," and "Until one day" to keep the story moving. The way you used the "Picture this" hook was very engaging and made the listener curious. You also made sure to include all the required parts, including the author and your own recommendation. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your storytelling was very logical, but sometimes the transitions between the background story and the main plot were quite fast. You mentioned the "human" character quite late in the talk. It is helpful to spend a little more time explaining who the main characters are at the very beginning so the listener doesn't get confused. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Character Introductions:</b> At the start of your talk, briefly list the main characters so the audience knows exactly who is participating in the story.</li><li><b>Signposting:</b> Use phrases like "The main problem is" or "In the end" to help your listener follow your structure even more easily.</li><li><b>Vary Your Connectors:</b> Instead of using the word "So" many times, try using other words like "Therefore" or "Because of this" to connect your sentences.</li></ul><br>Your narrative flow was easy to follow and very well organized! 🗣️	3	Your book review was very well-organized and presented with a good level of confidence. You managed to keep a steady pace throughout the recording, which made it easy to follow your ideas. Your volume was perfect, and I could hear every word you said quite clearly.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> You maintained a consistent volume and used syllable stress /ˈsɪl.ə.bəl.stres/ accurately in most words. This gave your speech a stable rhythm and helped me understand which words were the most important in each sentence.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> The most noticeable issue was the repetitive dropping of final consonant sounds /ˈkɒn.sə.nənt/. You often missed the "d" in "died" /daɪd/, the "n" in "human" /ˈhjuː.mən/, and the "n" in "town" /taʊn/, which can be intrusive for the listener.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Practice emphasizing the final consonant of every word to avoid dropping sounds systematically.<br>2. Focus on linking vowel sounds to the following consonants to create a more fluid, connected speech pattern.<br><br>You are making great progress with your clarity, Namo K! 🌟	\N	\N	16.0	Namo, this was a great book review of the Ramakien! You managed to explain a very complex story in a way that was easy to follow and very engaging for the listener. Your use of descriptive vocabulary really brought the characters to life, and you used a great range of linking words to move through the plot. To reach the next level, just keep an eye on your past tense endings and try to introduce your characters more clearly at the start. Your confidence in speaking is great to see! 🌟	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
28841	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	Good morning everyone, my name is Ie Ie, and today I am going to tell you about my favorite manga of all time, which is Made in Abyss by Akihito Tsukushi. He is an author of this story and also the artist of for this story too.<br>For the question, what would you do if someone suddenly come to you and tell you that your mother is waiting for you at the bottom of the giant pit called the Abyss? What would you do? For me, I would go down there without any hesitation. Not because that I want... only want to find my mother, but also because I want to explore the Abyss myself too, maybe with my companions, or maybe by myself alone.<br>So the setting of this story will be... at first will be at the above... above the Abyss called Orth City, and later on the setting of this story will mainly focus inside the Abyss instead. Our main character name is Rico. She was just a little girl who dreamed about explore the Abyss, and later on, she find out that her mother is waiting for her inside the Abyss, so she decided to run away with her friends, which is a cyborg boy named Reg, which she name it after her dead dog. Things got really interesting when... Rico and Reg reach the fourth layer of the Abyss, but sadly for our little Rico, she was attacked by the creature there and also affected by the curse there too, and so she almost died there. But luckily, there is a mysterious person came to them and decided to help Rico before she died. And that person name is Nanachi. Nanachi told Rico and Reg after Rico has finally recovered herself from the all the scars or wounds that there might be something else waiting for her at the deeper layers of the Abyss. But of course, Rico doesn't... didn't listen to Nanachi warnings and also still want... wanted to go down deeper. And Nanachi didn't mind about Rico's decision because it's her decision, it's herself. So in the end, Nanachi decided to join their team too, into the travel with... and Nanachi decided to travel with them into the deeper layers of the Abyss with her new friends.<br>What I like the most about this story is the world-building. The world-building of this story is very interesting and also fas... always fascinating me every time when I read it, even though it's not my first time anymore. But however, the main problem was that some of the character design are a little bit too inappropriate and also suggestive too. But if there any reason behind that, it's still understandable for me. If you like the book that are dark fantasy, adventure, yet family found, and heartwarming, you should try Made in Abyss, and you will won't be disappointed. And that's all for today. Thank you for listening. [applause] [laugh]	495	5	Ie Ie, you did an amazing job with this manga review! Talking about complex stories like Made in Abyss in a second language is a big challenge. Your score of 5 out of 5 shows that you have very strong control over your English grammar and vocabulary. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a wide range of impressive vocabulary words like "hesitation," "fascinating," "inappropriate," and "suggestive." Your use of complex sentences was also very good. You connected ideas naturally using phrases like "not only... but also" and relative clauses like "which she name it after her dead dog." Your vocabulary was perfectly suited for a dark fantasy book review.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You sometimes missed the past tense when telling the story. For example, you said "someone suddenly come" instead of "someone suddenly <b>came</b>." You also said "dreamed about explore" which should be "dreamed about <b>exploring</b>." Watch out for small slips like "you will won't be disappointed" where you used two helping verbs together by mistake. These are minor points that did not stop me from understanding your great ideas.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Verb patterns:</b> Remember that verbs following prepositions like "about" usually need the -ing form. Practice saying phrases like "dreamed about exploring" or "thought about going" until they feel natural.</li><li><b>Past tense consistency:</b> When you tell a story about events that happened, try to keep all your verbs in the past. Practice changing "find out" to "found out" and "name" to "named" in your head before you speak.</li><li><b>Subject-verb agreement:</b> Make sure your verbs match your subjects even when you are speaking quickly. Practice saying "that person's name <b>was</b>" instead of "that person name is."</li></ul><br>You are using very advanced words for your level, so keep it up! 🌟	5	Ie Ie, your presentation was very well-organized and easy to follow! It can be hard to summarize a long story in just a few minutes, but you managed it perfectly. With a score of 5 out of 5, you showed excellent skill in managing your talk from start to finish. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You followed the required structure perfectly. You included the author, used a great rhetorical question hook, and answered it well. Your story moved logically from the setting in Orth City to the climax at the fourth layer and then to a clear conclusion. You used a good range of transition words like "later on," "but luckily," and "however" to guide the listener through your points.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were a few moments where you repeated words like "above... above" or "want... wanted." These were very small hesitations that did not break the flow. Sometimes you used the word "which" in a way that made the sentence a bit too long. Try to break very long thoughts into two separate sentences to keep your message as clear as possible for the audience.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Vary your transitions:</b> You used "but" and "so" very well. Try adding more variety like "consequently," "furthermore," or "as a result" to sound even more professional in your future talks.</li><li><b>Expand on the 'why':</b> You mentioned the character designs were "suggestive." Adding one short sentence to explain why that was a problem for you would make your opinion section even more persuasive.</li><li><b>Practice the hook:</b> Your rhetorical question was a great choice! Practice saying it with a slightly longer pause after the question to let the audience think before you give your answer.</li></ul><br>Your storytelling flow is fantastic and you kept me interested the whole time! 🎯	2	It was wonderful to hear you speak so clearly and audibly during your book review today. You have made a significant improvement in your volume compared to previous assessments. This increased confidence really helped your message reach the listener.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> Your volume was much better than before, which is a major achievement. By speaking louder, you have made it much easier for everyone to hear your interesting ideas about the story.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> To improve your clarity /ɪnˌtel.ɪ.dʒəˈbɪl.ə.ti/, you need to slow down slightly and emphasize individual sounds. The "s" sound /s/ is consistently being dropped or not pronounced, which can create blockages in understanding for the listener.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Practice slowing your speech down to give yourself time to pronounce every consonant clearly.<br>2. Focus specifically on the "s" sound at the ends of words to ensure your plurals and possessives are clear.<br><br>This is a huge step forward in your speaking confidence, Ie Ie! 🚀	\N	\N	16.0	Ie Ie, this was a brilliant review of Made in Abyss! You used excellent vocabulary and organized your ideas very logically throughout the talk. Your use of the rhetorical question hook was very effective and drew me into the story immediately. To improve even further, just focus on keeping your past tense verbs consistent and double-checking your verb patterns like "dreamed about exploring." You have a natural talent for storytelling in English! 🌟	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
