h my friends or my old friends. And just talking and just open the songs that very cool for me.<br><br><br>KATI: Um, I think it's not important because if we celebrate, uh, this, the event we don't need to celebrate, uh, too much big. Uh, like my birthday, I usually just, uh, eating on my home with my family, just buy some food or mini cake, mini cake to eat together. It's not important to me.	326	3	Kati, you do a good job of using simple sentences correctly when you talk about everyday things, like your hobbies or what you do in your free time. For example, you said, "I always use it every day," which is a correct simple sentence. You also try to use more complex sentence structures, which is great. Be careful with your verb forms when talking about the past. For instance, you said, "last weekend I have went to Pattaya," which should be "last weekend I went to Pattaya." Also, "he he teach me" should be "he teaches me." These small changes will make your English sound more natural. Thai often has less tense marking than English, so practising verb endings and tense forms will help.	3	You manage to speak at good length, which is excellent for expressing your ideas. You keep your contributions relevant and use basic linking words like "and" and "because" to connect your thoughts. For example, when you described the beach picture, you started by talking about the people and then what they were doing, which helped the listener follow your ideas. Sometimes you repeat yourself or pause while thinking, such as "Because I think, I think, uh, they have not, not, they don't have very many times to be together." Planning your sentences a little more and using varied connectors will improve the flow and organisation of your speech.	4	Your voice is clear and very easy to understand. Your intonation generally sounds natural and your sentence and word stress are usually correct. These strengths make your speech easy to follow. There are a few small pronunciation points to check, for example ensuring final /s/ sounds are distinct in words like "musics" (when referring to music in general the correct word is "music"). Practising listening and repeating target sounds will help your clarity even more.	3	You start conversations and respond appropriately, and you keep the interaction going with little prompting. You show listening by agreeing with your partner, for example "I really agree with you, Praewwan." To improve further, try to ask your partner follow-up questions or add new information after agreeing, such as "That's a good idea, and we could also..." This will help you develop the interaction and show more complex conversational skills.	13.0	Kati, you have a lovely clear voice and it's easy to understand you, which is a big plus for communication! Keep up the good work in making your points clearly. While you're good at sharing your ideas, focusing a little more on how you connect your sentences and thoughts will really help your English flow even better. If you can do this, your speaking will become much more natural.<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Practice verb tenses: Pay close attention to using the correct past tense forms when talking about past events (for example, "I went" instead of "I have went").<br>✔️ Expand sentence connectors: Try using a wider variety of words to link your ideas, such as "however," "therefore," or "in addition," to make your speech more organised.<br>✔️ Engage more in discussions: When talking with a partner, try not only to agree but also to ask them questions or build on their ideas with phrases like "What do you think about...?" or "That's a good point, and I'd also add..."	B1	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
