. Rest in peace to him though. So of course, that make a mark, that make a mark on Jann heart and almost, he almost designed to quit racing. But he realized all the thing he lost, all the risk he take off. Okay.<br>All right, so to wrap it up, overall, this, uh, this book was really good for inspiring us, sharing us that don't we don't need to judge other dream. And thank you for your attention, hope you guys a wonderful, wonderful day.	450	4	Krewkehw, you did a clear job sharing the exciting story of Gran Turismo with us! Your ability to tell a complicated story about racing was easy to follow. Your score of 4 out of 5 for grammar and vocabulary shows you are comfortable with English. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a variety of racing and storytelling vocabulary like "manufacturer," "mechanical issue," and "spectator." Your use of past tense was mostly consistent which helped me understand the sequence of events. You also tried to use longer sentences with words like "because" and "where" to add more detail to your descriptions. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You had some small slips with verb patterns, such as saying "made you to become" instead of "made you become." There were also times when plural nouns were missing a final /s/ sound, like "his parent" instead of "his parent<b>s</b>" /'peə.rənt<b>s</b>/. Sometimes you used the wrong verb form after "to," like "to giving" instead of "to give." This dropping of final consonants is common for Thai speakers, so try to emphasize those ending sounds. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Verb Patterns</b> - Practice which verbs need "to" and which ones do not, especially after words like "make" and "let."</li><li><b>Plural Nouns</b> - Focus on adding the final /s/ sound to plural words like "parents," "others," or "spectators" to be more precise.</li><li><b>Word Forms</b> - Check if you need a noun or an adjective, such as using "guaranteed" instead of "guarantee" in your sentences.</li></ul><br>You are a very expressive speaker! 🏎️	4	KK, your book review was engaging and kept my interest from start to finish! Using the "imagine" hook was a smart way to get the audience to think about the story. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows you have a good grasp of how to organize a talk. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You followed the required structure and gave a lot of detail about the plot and the climax of the story. Your transitions like "so in the beginning" and "to wrap it up" helped the listener follow your ideas. You spoke for a good amount of time and reached the required length easily. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You did not mention the author of the book, which was one of the requirements for this task. Sometimes you repeated phrases when you were thinking, such as "one race, one race," which slows down your speed. While your ending was nice, you could explain more about why you recommend it to others. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Checklist Review</b> - Before you start, make sure you have all required parts like the author's name ready to go.</li><li><b>Reduce Repetition</b> - If you need to think, try to use a single "um" or a short pause instead of repeating words.</li><li><b>Expand Recommendations</b> - Tell us specifically who would like this book, like "fans of sports movies" or "people who like true stories."</li></ul><br>You have a great natural rhythm when you tell a story! 📚	4	You delivered a very engaging book review with great energy and volume. It was a pleasure to listen to your ideas, and your confidence really shone through in the recording. Your syllable stress was consistently accurate, which helped the listener follow your rhythm.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> Your volume was excellent throughout the talk, ensuring that your voice was heard clearly. You also made some successful attempts at word linking, which helped create a more natural and fluid flow to your sentences.<br><