und out," "tricked," and "rescued" to tell the plot. Your vocabulary was appropriate for the topic, and your use of relative clauses like "the part where the werewolf tricked the little girl" showed a good attempt at using more complex grammar.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You often missed the final <b>-s</b> on verbs in the present tense, such as saying "story take place" instead of "story take<b>s</b> place" (/teɪk/ → /teɪk<b>s</b>/). There were also some small errors with singular and plural nouns, like saying "a young men" instead of "a young man" (/men/ → /mæn/). Additionally, you used an adverb where an adjective was needed when you said "how bravely the men are" instead of "how brave" /breɪv/.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Master Third-Person S:</b> Practice speaking about characters in the present tense and remember to add the /s/ sound to the end of verbs.</li><li><b>Singular vs Plural:</b> Pay close attention to the difference between "man" /mæn/ and "men" /men/ to make your descriptions more accurate.</li><li><b>Adjectives for Descriptions:</b> Use adjectives like "brave" to describe people and adverbs like "bravely" to describe how they do an action.</li></ul><br>Your grammar foundations are getting stronger every day! 🎯	3	Gunner, your book review was very easy to follow because you organized your ideas well. It is common to feel a bit nervous when speaking for a long time, but you kept your energy up until the end. Your score reflects that you met the main requirements of the speaking task. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You followed the required structure by introducing yourself and using a rhetorical question as a hook to engage the audience. Your explanation of the story was clear, moving from the beginning to the exciting climax and the happy resolution. You also used good connecting words like "so," "and," and "finally" to link your sentences together logically.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>One detail to remember for next time is to include the name of the author of the book. You also had several moments where you repeated words, such as "when, when, when a girl," which can happen when you are searching for the next idea. Try to extend your conclusion a little more to give the audience a very clear ending to your talk.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Use a Checklist:</b> Before you start, write down the author's name and other required details to make sure you do not miss them.</li><li><b>Reduce Repetition:</b> When you feel stuck, take a small breath instead of repeating the last word you said.</li><li><b>Practice with a Timer:</b> Try to speak for the full two minutes by adding more details about why you liked specific parts of the story.</li></ul><br>You are a clear communicator with a great sense of structure! 🎯	3	You presented your book review with great enthusiasm and a very clear speaking voice. Your individual sounds were well-formed, and your intonation added a lot of character to your storytelling. I really enjoyed the energy you brought to the recording today.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> Your volume and basic sounds were very good, and you used intonation effectively to express emotion in your story. This kept me interested in your review of the plot and characters from beginning to end.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> The most significant area for improvement is syllable stress /ˈsɪl.ə.bəl.stres/. You have a frequent tendency to overstress the final syllable of words, such as "Gunner" /ɡʌnˈɜːr/, "grandmother" /ɡrændˌmʌðˈɜːr/, and "finally" /faɪ.nəlˈliː/, which can be quite intrusive for a listener.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Practice the "schwa" sound /ə/ for final syllables to avoid overstressing them (e.g., Gunner /ˈɡʌn.ər/).<br>2. Listen to recordings of native speakers and notice how they often make the final sounds of words like "sleeping" /ˈsliː.pɪŋ/ or "secretly" /ˈsiː.krət.li/ shorter and softer.<br><br>Your energy is fantastic, Gunner, so keep working on that syllable stress! 🚀	\N	\N	12.0	Gunner, this was a successful book review where you clearly explained the plot and shared your personal thoughts on the characters! 🌟 You followed the requested structure and used an effective hook to grab the audience's attention. Your use of the past tense was generally accurate, and you used helpful transition words to move through the story's events. To reach a higher level, try to include all required details like the author's name and focus on speaking more smoothly without repeating words. Working on subject-verb agreement and singular versus plural nouns will also help polish your speaking. You are a brave speaker with a clear voice, so keep practicing your storytelling! 🎯	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
