r other people to come and help you or rest of your life.<br><br>I think it's about friend because not everyone like support them or some of friend will give you, "Oh, drink soda, drink, uh, eat junk food." You have to confident, uh, up to be a healthy.<br><br>Yes because, uh, I see today trends, they are starting a healthy trend like reading a book to improve skill, eating a healthy food, I see on the social media.<br><br>I think if we play a just a little bit, not like a professional player. I think professional player is a really have a pressure but if we play with friends or with team in school, it's not that much pressure.<br><br>So, I think Thai food is more healthy because it's not a processed food like burgers or bread that are processed very, very, very processed. I feel like som tum is just a papaya, tomatoes, and some sauce, yeah.	524	2	Nine, you showed a good understanding of how to put simple sentences together, and you made efforts to use more complex structures. For example, when you spoke about the importance of friends, you said, "I think friend is a really important thing in everyone life because we have to think, have a conversation with someone you meet and friend can help you do work and make you relax." That's a good attempt at expressing several ideas in one sentence! 👍 However, for greater accuracy, 'everyone life' should be 'everyone's life,' and 'friend' should be 'friends' when talking in general. More importantly, I noticed some recurring issues with subject-verb agreement and noun usage. For instance, you said, "I think they **has** to be a teamwork." Here, 'has' should be 'have' to match 'they,' and 'teamwork' usually doesn't take 'a' before it; a better phrase would be "they **have** to **work as a team**." Another example was "if food **it** have many kind," which would be more accurate as "if food **has** many kinds." Also, when referring to a rapper's music, "his slick" isn't the usual phrase; "his music" or "his tracks" would be more precise. Many Thai learners find English grammar, especially subject-verb agreement and countable/uncountable nouns, a bit tricky because the rules are different from Thai. Focusing on these points will make your English much more accurate and easier to understand.	3	You managed to produce responses longer than just short phrases, Nine, which is a great step! You provided relevant contributions to the tasks, like when you described the pictures of people keeping fit. You used basic linking words like "because" and "and" to connect your ideas. To help your English flow even more smoothly, try to reduce some of the hesitations you had and aim for a more consistent pace. Sometimes, adding a little more detail or explanation to your points would also make your answers even more developed. For example, when asked what you do to keep fit, you could have expanded on your activities. Thinking about a few extra details before you start speaking can really help!	2	Nine, your speech was generally clear enough for me to understand you, which is the most important thing! 😊 However, I noticed that your intonation was sometimes a little flat, which means the rise and fall in your voice wasn't always very natural for English. Also, a key area to practice is making sure you pronounce the final consonant sounds in words. For instance, you often dropped the /k/ sound in "think," the /l/ sound in "goal," or the /d/ sound in "kind." These small sounds are important for clarity in English! For example, try saying "think" as /θɪŋk/ with a clear /k/ at the end, rather than /θɪŋ/. Thai speakers often don't emphasize final consonants in their language, so it's a common area for improvement in English. Actively practicing these sounds will make your speech much clearer and more natural.	4	You did a good job of starting and responding appropriately in the conversations, Nine! 🤝 You linked your ideas to what your partner said, especially during the discussion tasks, like when you built on Prize's point about sleep by bringing up healthy eating. You kept the conversation going by giving your opinions and reasons for them, and you even asked your partner to contribute, like when you said, "I first, can I?". This showed you were actively engaging with the task and your partner. To reach a perfect 5, you could try to develop your partner's ideas even further or occasionally offer a polite challenge to expand the discussion even more.	11.0	Hello Nine! 👋 It was a pleasure to hear you speak. You communicated your ideas well and contributed actively to the discussions. To help you progress even further, focusing on fine-tuning your grammar, making your pronunciation even clearer, and developing your ideas a little more will be excellent steps. Keep going; you're doing a fantastic job! <br><br>📋 **Action Plan:**<br>✔️ **Master subject-verb agreement:** Pay extra attention to ensuring your verbs match your subjects, especially with plural nouns and pronouns (e.g., "they have" not "they has").<br>✔️ **Practice final consonant sounds:** Consciously try to pronounce the ending sounds of words like /k/ in "think" or /l/ in "goal" to ensure every word is clear.<br>✔️ **Expand and elaborate:** Try to add a little more detail or explanation to your responses to make your ideas even richer and more convincing.	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
