mes, you paused a bit or repeated a phrase, like "I think eating... I think eating healthy food," and in longer explanations, it can be a little tricky to follow all your ideas. To make your speech even smoother and clearer, try to practise speaking for a minute on a topic without too many long pauses, and focus on connecting your ideas more seamlessly. This will help your listener follow your train of thought even more easily.	2	Mymind, you have a lovely, loud, and clear voice, which makes it generally easy to understand what you're saying. That's a real advantage! ✨ However, one area to focus on is the rhythm of your English. You tend to put a strong stress on the very last syllable of many words, like /ækˈtɪvɪˈtiː/ (activity), /ˈkæləˈriːz/ (calories), and /ˈpiːpəl/ (people). This creates a somewhat strained sound and can make it difficult for listeners to follow the natural flow of your speech. English often has stress earlier in words. For example, in 'activity', the stress is on the second syllable: /ækˈtɪvɪti/. Practising where to put the stress in longer words will help your English sound much more natural and easier on the ear! Listen to native speakers and try to imitate their stress patterns.	3	Mymind, you were great at starting conversations and responding thoughtfully, and you definitely kept the interaction going! 🌟 You often linked your contributions to what your partner had said, usually by agreeing with them, like when you said, "I agree about this because..." and "I agree with what you said." This helped to maintain a cooperative atmosphere! To make your interactions even stronger, try to develop your ideas further instead of just agreeing. For example, after agreeing, you could add a new point or a different perspective, or ask your partner for more details to expand the conversation beyond just your personal preferences. This will help you to maintain and develop the interaction more independently.	10.0	Well done, Mymind! You've shown that you can communicate your ideas clearly and take an active part in conversations. While there are a few areas where you can tidy up your grammar and keep your speaking flow a bit smoother, your ability to interact and get your message across is really strong. Keep practising, and you'll continue to grow your confidence and skill.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br> *   ✔️ Pay close attention to subject-verb agreement in your sentences, especially when using "people" or other plural nouns.<br> *   ✔️ Practise using infinitives correctly after verbs like "like" and "enjoy," for example, "I like *to listen* to music."<br> *   ✔️ Work on connecting your sentences more smoothly and speaking at a slightly quicker, more natural pace to improve your flow.	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
28863	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	Hello my name is Namo T and today I'm going to talk about a book called Phai Sa Nam by Saksit Thewabim.<br>What would you do if you found a ghost? If it was me, I would run away. It's set in countryside Thailand and it's about a boy called Tonnam who move... to move his house from capital city to countryside. At first he hate his new house because it was too boring and got nothing to do. There were just only trees and nature. Then something unexpected happens when he found a pond near his new house.<br>After that he invited his new friend to swim with him. Suddenly his friend got pulled down the water and the main problem is that when he went to help his friend and found that it was a ghost. The most exciting part is when the ghost gave Tonnam a special deal to help keep the pond clean without any garbage from human. Finally Tonnam finished the mission and became friend... friends with the ghost.<br>I really enjoyed when the ghost appear because it was really unexpected. However, I don't... I didn't really like when the story start because it was too boring and too hard to understand. This book is perfect for anyone who like ghost or horror story and overall, I really like this story because it's interesting and got happy ending. Hope you will give it a try. Thank you. [applause]	243	4	Namo, you did a great job sharing your book review with us! Telling a story in a second language can feel tricky, but you handled it very well. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows you have a strong command of B1 level English. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> <br>You used a variety of vocabulary like "unexpected," "garbage," and "mission" to make your story interesting for the listener. Your use of a conditional sentence at the start was a great way to introduce your thoughts. You also used different tenses to talk about the events in the book and your own opinions. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> <br>You had some small slips with subject-verb agreement and past tense endings. For example, you said "he move" instead of "he mov<b>ed</b>" (/muːv<b>d</b>/) and "he hate" instead of "he hat<b>ed</b>" (/heɪt<b>ɪd</b>/). This is common for Thai speakers because final consonant sounds can be difficult to pronounce. Also, remember the "s" for third-person subjects, such as in "anyone who like<b>s</b>" (/laɪk<b>s</b>/). <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> <br><ul><li><b>Practice past tense endings</b>: Focus on the /d/ or /t/ sounds at the end of regular verbs to show the action is finished.</li><li><b>Subject-verb agreement</b>: Remind yourself that "he," "she," and "it" always need an <b>s</b> on the verb in the present tense.</li><li><b>Word patterns</b>: Keep reading more books to see how words like "finish" or "start" are used naturally with other verbs.</li></ul> <br>You have a clear way of expressing yourself—well done! 🎯	4	Namo, the way you organized your talk was very clear and easy to follow! It is often hard to remember all the parts of a presentation, but you included every required section. This score of 4 out of 5 reflects how well you managed your ideas and followed the task instructions. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> <br>You used a fantastic variety of connecting words like "at first," "then," "after that," and "finally." These words acted like a map for the listener to follow the sequence of your story. Your hook was also very effective because you asked a rhetorical question and then answered it immediately to set the mood. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> <br>Your exposition was clear, but the ending of your talk felt a little bit faster than the beginning. You used the word "because" quite often to connect your ideas, which is okay, but it can make sentences very long. Sometimes it is better to finish a sentence and start a new one to keep your points sharp. Try to avoid using filler phrases like "got nothing to do" when more formal B1 vocabulary could be used. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> <br><ul><li><b>Vary your connectors</b>: Since you used "because" a lot, try using "as" or "since" to give reasons for your opinions.</li><li><b>Develop the climax</b>: Spend a little more time describing the "special deal" with the ghost to make that exciting part last longer.</li><li><b>Conclusion strength</b>: Your ending was good, but you can make it even stronger by summarizing the main lesson of the book in one final sentence.</li></ul> <br>Your presentation was very well-structured from start to finish. 🎯	1	Thank you for your effort in preparing and delivering your book review today. It was clear that you had put thought into the structure of your presentation and knew your story well. I appreciate the time you took to share your thoughts on the book with me.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> You followed the structure of the assignment well and included the key parts of the story. This shows a good understanding of how to organize a presentation in a logical way from start to finish.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> The major challenge in your recording was that your voice was too soft /sɒft/. I could only understand about one word in ten because of the low volume, which makes it very difficult to assess your speaking skills accurately.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Practice speaking with a much higher volume, making sure you are projecting your voice toward the recorder.<br>2. Focus on clear articulation of every word so that your interesting ideas can be heard and understood by your audience.<br><br>Raising your volume will make a huge difference in your next assessment, Namo T! 👍	\N	\N	12.0	Namo, you gave a fantastic book review that was very easy to follow! Your organization was excellent, especially the way you used a question to hook the audience and clear transitions like "after that" and "finally" to tell the story. You used a good range of vocabulary and even tried some complex grammar. To get even better, focus on your past tense endings like "moved" or "hated" and make sure your subjects and verbs match. You should be happy with this performance! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
