ases like "My name is Frame" and "I love spaghetti with creamy and a lot of tomato." You also tried to use more complex ideas, such as "because my parent will drive me into the place I want" and "I have ever played badminton before." This shows good ambition! 👍 To improve this area, let's pay close attention to some basic grammar points. You sometimes used incorrect verb forms or word choices, for instance, saying "I spent with my friend" when it should be "I **spend time** with my friend" or "I **spent my weekend** with my friend." Another example is "my parent will drive me," which should be "my **parents**" (plural). Also, saying "I didn't really good at football" should be "I wasn't really good at football" or "I'm not really good at football." These little adjustments will make your English much clearer and more accurate! ✨	2	You do produce responses that are longer than just short phrases, Frame, which is a good start! Your contributions are generally relevant to the topics being discussed. However, I noticed you hesitated quite a lot, and sometimes your ideas became a bit jumbled, making it hard to follow your full meaning. For example, when describing the photo, the sentence "From this photo, it's school time with the children that and there's a, they have a teacher, play football" was a bit difficult to understand. To make your speech clearer, try to take a moment to organise your thoughts before speaking. Use basic connecting words like "because," "and," and "but" to link your sentences, and try to avoid repeating words or phrases too often, like "I really, I really don't know." Planning your sentences will help your thoughts flow more smoothly and clearly for your listener! 🧠	2	Frame, while I could mostly understand what you were saying, your pronunciation could be clearer. You really need to work on your intonation – that's the way your voice goes up and down when you speak. Your speaking voice tended to be quite flat, which made it difficult to understand which parts of your message were most important. In English, intonation helps us understand feelings and emphasis. For example, when asking a question, your voice usually rises at the end. Thai speech patterns can sometimes influence how people use intonation in English, leading to a flatter delivery. Practising making your voice more expressive will help your listener follow your meaning much more easily. Try to listen to English speakers and imitate their intonation, and this will make a big difference to your overall clarity! 🎤	3	You did a good job of starting conversations and responding to questions, Frame, which is important for communicating effectively! You also kept the group discussion going without needing much help, offering suggestions like "I think you could try a badminton" and agreeing with your friends. You gave reasons for your choices, which is excellent. However, to get an even better score, try to link your comments more closely to what your partners have just said. Instead of just stating your own opinion, you could say, "I agree with you about football, and I also think it's good because..." or "That's a good idea about badminton, but I prefer football because..." This shows you're actively listening and building on the conversation, which makes for a richer and more engaging interaction! 🤝	10.0	Hello Frame! 👋 It was good to hear you speak during your English exam. You did a good job of sharing your ideas and participating in the discussions. To help your English progress even further, let's concentrate on making your sentences more accurate and your voice more expressive, especially with intonation. Keep working hard! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ **Sharpen your grammar basics:** Focus on getting your basic sentence structures right, especially verb tenses and plural forms. A good practice is to write down what you want to say first, then check it for correctness before speaking. ✍️<br>✔️ **Practice expressive speaking:** Work on making your voice go up and down naturally when you speak English. Listen to English songs or watch short videos and try to copy the way people speak – this will help your intonation and make your speech easier to follow! 🎶<br>✔️ **Organise your thoughts:** Before you speak at length, take a moment to quickly plan what you want to say. Think about one main idea per sentence and use clear connecting words to link them together, like "first," "next," or "finally." 🧩	A2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
28716	2026-02-24	2026-02-24	2026	2026 Final Speaking Exam	Hi, my name is Aunna.\n\nUm, 14 years old.\n\nI live in Nonthaburi.\n\nFor me, I travel occasionally because I think that travel to another places or another country would be very hectic. So I mostly stayed at my home and chilling with my family.\n\nI usually get to school by bus.\n\nOkay. According to this picture, I think they were traveling but by a guide tour at the London Bridge. I can see tons of people who are preparing to take picture of some buildings or something very fascinating. As, as I can see in this picture, there are on a boat or maybe a yacht and it was on the lake. And as I, as I can see on the right side, I can see tons of building and constructions along with the trees and nature. I think both of them when it's mixed, mixture together, it just get along very well. And also, I can see some of the transportation, for example, a car over here and maybe just a little bit of sailing boat along the lake. Next, I'll be describing the person.\n\n[laugh]\n\nI think going to the museum was also a viable option, but for me, for elementary kids or students, I think going to a theme park would be a lot better since I think that theme park was suitable for students because when I was a kid, I always like to go to a theme park or maybe something entertaining. So maybe going to the theme park would be a great option. How about you?\n\nFor me, I also prefer a car since as I, as I said that I don't really like a place that was very hectic. And for example, a train that was very crowded with people and a lot of strangers. So I think that if I sit at my own car with my parents or my friends, I would be a lot comfortable.\n\nI think it's very slightly important because imagine if you just have, you are, you didn't have any knowledge to go to that place and you just come up, imagine if you're going to a museum and you just stand up there and just thinking about it like where it came from or how, how is it built? I think, so I think that maybe doing researching before going to the places would be a lot more better.\n\nThe most interesting place for me is the temple. I mean, um, Wat Phra Kaew, um, I think you know it because the construction there is look pretty cool and majestic. And I also like to study about Thai mythic, mythic, mythology and also I like to go there too with my family and also to study about how it was built, how when it was built and how many important people in this world ever visit it.\n\nFor me, I like the best place that I like to go or went is, I think it's called ตึกลูกเต๋า. It was a scientific building full of science creation or maybe scientific method. I, I used to be struggled with science very, very much. So I think that if I maybe learn more science or maybe like new in, it could be my new interest also.	548	5	Aunna, you have a wonderful range of vocabulary! Your use of complex structures is natural and effective. To reach even higher, keep refining the precision of your word choices.	5	Your ability to develop ideas at length is exceptional, Aunna! You organize your thoughts logically and use a wide range of connectors. Your speech flows very naturally.	4	Aunna, Intonation very good, links sounds well. Drops final consonants. Focusing on these small details will make your already good communication even more precise. Keep up the great work!	5	Aunna, you are a very natural communicator! You lead the conversation and build on your partner's ideas effortlessly. Your interaction is proactive and engaging.	19.0	Aunna, your performance was outstanding! You communicate with confidence and show a sophisticated command of English. Keep using your skills and you will achieve great things! All the best, Ed	B2	Speaking	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N
28890	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	Morning everyone, my name is Kun and today I'd like to share one of my favorites novel so far. It's called Songlight by Mora Mefinnet.<br>Okay, so, imagine a world that certain people can connect telepathically. Beautiful, right? What can go wrong? The story is set in Byron Land, a island inspired by minimalist England. We follow Elsa, a girl who live in society terrified by Songlight. She's hiding her ability and in love with Bray, who share her gift. At first it was really Romeo and Juliet like, but then Bray got taken by military. This is where the story get good. Elsa left... was left heartbroken and her despair is so strong that it... spark a whole rebellion. <br>The story unfold by many role... soldier, rebel, those who in power... each other adding a layer to this intricate world. The ending was a cliffhanger. This story is perfect for anybody who like a engrossing story with love, adventure, and politic. The ending was a cliffhanger, so, that was very disappointed. What I like was the plot twist was very unexpected. In other hand, the ending was very disappointed because it was a cliffhanger. Thank you. [applause]	198	4	Kun, you used some very descriptive words to explain your book! This can be difficult when talking about fantasy stories, but you handled it well. Your score of 4 out of 5 shows that you have a strong grasp of B1 vocabulary. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used some very impressive words like 'telepathically,' 'minimalist,' and 'intricate.' These choices are perfect for a book review and show a high level of vocabulary. You also used relative clauses well, such as 'a girl who live in society.' Your ability to explain the plot using different tenses was successful and easy to follow for the listener.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You sometimes forgot to add the final /s/ sound to verbs and nouns. For example, you said 'the story get good' instead of 'the story get<b>s</b>' /ɡet/ → /ɡet<b>s</b>/. This is a common pattern for Thai speakers where the end of the word is dropped. You also used 'disappointed' to describe the ending, but you should use 'disappointing' /ˌdɪsəˈpɔɪntɪd/ → /ˌdɪsəˈpɔɪntɪ<b>ŋ</b>/. Remember that -ing describes the thing, and -ed describes your feeling.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Master -ed and -ing adjectives:</b> Practice the difference between feelings and descriptions. A book is 'boring' or 'exciting,' and you feel 'bored' or 'excited.'</li><li><b>Check your plurals:</b> When talking about one of many things, use 'one of my favorite novels.' Remember to add the /z/ sound at the end of the noun: /ˈnɒvəl/ → /ˈnɒvəl<b>z</b>/.</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> Pay attention to singular subjects like 'Elsa' or 'the military.' Try to remember the /s/ sound at the end of the verb: 'Elsa lives' or 'the military takes.'</li></ul><br>You have a wonderful vocabulary—keep using those big words! 🎯	4	Kun, your book review followed the required structure perfectly! Telling a story with a beginning, middle, and end is a big challenge in a second language. You reached a score of 4 out of 5 because your ideas were very easy to follow. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used your 'Imagine' hook perfectly to catch the audience's attention right at the start. Your transition words like 'at first,' 'but then,' and 'this is where' helped the story flow smoothly. You also did a good job of setting the scene in Byron Land before moving on to the main events. It was clear that you understood the book very well and wanted to share that with us.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You repeated the point about the cliffhanger ending a few times toward the end. Instead of saying 'the ending was a cliffhanger' three times, try to explain more about why that specific ending made you feel a certain way. Also, you used the phrase 'in other hand' which should be 'on the other hand.' Try to avoid repeating the same sentence and instead add new details to each part of your talk.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Vary your transitions:</b> Instead of repeating the same ideas, use words like 'furthermore' or 'however' to add more detail. This will make your speaking sound more advanced and natural.</li><li><b>Expand your opinions:</b> You mentioned the plot twist was unexpected. Tell us a bit more about how it made you feel or why it was so surprising to you as a reader!</li><li><b>Practice idioms correctly:</b> Make sure to use the full phrase 'on the other hand.' Using these idioms correctly will help you reach the highest bands in future tests.</li></ul><br>Your structure is excellent and you made the story sound very exciting. 🎯	2	Thank you for sharing your book review with me today. You started your presentation with a good level of volume and a clear structure. It was great to see you beginning your talk with such focus and preparation.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> Your volume was perfectly audible at the beginning of your speech, which made the introduction very clear. You also managed to organize your thoughts in a logical way that was easy to follow initially.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> As the speech went on, your voice became increasingly quiet and your intonation /ˈɪn.tə.neɪ.ʃən/ remained very flat. This was likely due to nerves, but it made it difficult to follow your points as the review progressed.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Practice maintaining your initial volume throughout the entire talk, even when you feel nervous.<br>2. Focus on varying your intonation to emphasize important words, which will help keep your listener engaged.<br><br>With more practice and confidence, your natural speaking voice will really shine, Kun! 🌟	\N	\N	13.0	Kun, you gave a very clear and interesting review of your novel! You used advanced words like 'intricate' and 'telepathically' to describe the setting and characters accurately. Your talk had a great flow because you used transition phrases to move between the different parts of the story. To reach the next level, try to be more careful with small grammar details like subject-verb agreement and noun plurals. You should also try to give more variety in your opinions so you do not repeat the same sentences. You made the book sound very exciting! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
31545	2025-09-18	2025-09-18	2026	Term 1 Finals	MEW: My name is Mew.<br><br>MEW: I live in Nakhon Pathom. I live in near the, uh, temple and, uh, the floating market called ... Wait, I forgot. ...... Never mind.<br><br>MEW: Come again?<br><br>MEW: At school, I prefer to play with my dog and sometimes play with my brother.<br><br>MEW: I like to listen to K-Pop music. I can dance and uh, relax with it.<br><br>MEW: Uh, I think spend time with friend can make us more knowing, know know together and have a time with with us together.<br><br>MEW: Okay. Well, the first picture, they are playing basketball together. I think they like to communicate and uh, exercise in one with one sport that is like a ball. And the the second picture is she is doing yoga, maybe. And she uh, her face look she so, well, so hurt or something. ...... All right. So Peptine, what what do you do to keep fit?<br><br>MEW: For a picture, I prefer the first one because I like to communicate with my friend and I like to share, uh, some thing with my friend and I don't want to be alone.<br><br>MEW: Okay. Uh, for me, in my opinion, getting enough sleep is the best way to improve your health because getting enough sleep make your brain, uh, more health and you can use your brain in 100% and you're not being tired when you do something or do your work. And uh, you know sleep make you relax and have more time with yourself.<br><br>MEW: Uh, in my opinion, spending time outdoors is the best way to improve health to teenager because spending time outdoor, you can have a time with your family or friend and, uh, you can learn many skill and, uh, share share information to your friend or family and, uh, improve your health with your, uh, activities.<br><br>MEW: Uh, yeah, is important because sometimes for me, I I usually eat everything I want, but I don't care how good or healthy is it. And I think if school teach us to choose the food, we can improve, uh, our deal.<br><br>MEW: Uh, in my opinion, the hardest challenge is do the exercise. I think the exercise need to have time and, uh, structuring, struct, struct, and you need to have time with yourself to do the exercise. And it's so hard because I already try it.	391	3	You show a good understanding of how to put sentences together, especially when talking about familiar topics like your hobbies and where you live. For example, you said, 'I live in Nakhon Pathom' and 'I like to listen to K-Pop music,' which are both clear and correct! 👍 You also tried to use some more complex structures, which is excellent. However, sometimes these attempts had small errors that could make your meaning a little less clear. For instance, you said, 'make us more knowing, know know together,' which could be phrased more naturally as 'help us get to know each other better' or 'help us understand each other more.' Another example was 'make your brain, uh, more health' which should be 'make your brain healthier.' Also, when you said 'we can improve, uh, our deal,' it sounds like you meant 'our health' or 'our well-being.' Thai learners sometimes transfer direct translations or less flexible grammatical structures from their first language, which can sometimes lead to slightly unnatural English phrasing. To help with this, try to learn common collocations (words that often go together) when you learn new vocabulary; for example, we usually say 'healthier' rather than 'more health' when describing a state. 🧠 You could also practice constructing sentences with relative clauses (e.g., 'the floating market *which is called*...') to add more detail and complexity to your sentences. ✍️ Finally, pay attention to using the correct form of words, like adjectives and adverbs, when describing things.	3	You did a good job of giving extended answers and keeping your contributions relevant to the topic! For example, when you discussed the ways to improve health, you gave a detailed opinion about getting enough sleep. You also used basic linking words like 'because' and 'in my opinion' to connect your ideas. 😊 However, there were times when you hesitated a bit or repeated words, like 'know know together' or 'struct, struct, struct' when you were explaining ideas. This is a common part of learning, but we can work on making your speech flow even more smoothly! To help with this, when you're comparing two things, try to use more varied linking words like 'whereas,' 'on the other hand,' or 'in contrast.' This helps to show clear differences between your ideas! ↔️ Also, to reduce repetition, try rephrasing your ideas using synonyms; for example, instead of repeating 'good,' you might use 'beneficial' or 'positive.' ✨ Finally, practicing speaking for longer stretches of time without interruption, perhaps by describing a photograph for a full minute, can help you develop a smoother flow of ideas. 🎤	1	Mew, your intonation, or the rise and fall of your voice, is generally acceptable, which helps people follow what you're saying. 👍 However, the main thing to focus on is making sure all your individual consonant sounds are really clear. Sometimes, words can sound a bit 'swallowed' because certain consonant sounds aren't pronounced strongly enough. For example, the /tʃ/ sound in words like 'each other' and the /θ/ sound in 'thing' sometimes aren't very distinct, and the /t/ sound at the end of words like 'about' can be dropped. On occasion, you might overstress final consonants, like in the word 'picture,' which can sound a little unnatural. In Thai, there aren't always strong final consonant releases, and some English sounds like /θ/ don't exist, so this can sometimes influence how you pronounce English words. To improve, try really exaggerating the final consonant sounds when you practice, especially /t/ and /d/. Try saying words like 'about' and 'friend' slowly and clearly. 🗣️ You could also practice words containing the /tʃ/ sound (like 'choose,' 'challenge') and the /θ/ sound (like 'think,' 'health') in front of a mirror to see your mouth movements. This helps to train your tongue and lips for these new sounds. 👅 Finally, record yourself speaking individual words and then short sentences, and then listen back carefully to identify any sounds that are difficult to hear, and try saying them again with more emphasis. 🎧	3	You did well at initiating conversations and responding to both the interviewer and your partner! You clearly stated your opinions and gave reasons, like when you said, 'I like to communicate with my friend' and 'in my opinion, getting enough sleep is the best way...' 😊 This kept the conversation going effectively. However, you could work on linking your contributions more consistently to what your partner, Peptine, had just said. Sometimes, your comments ran alongside his ideas rather than building on them or asking for more details, which helps to create a deeper discussion. To make your interactions even stronger, try to explicitly refer to what your partner has just said before giving your own opinion. For example, you could start with, 'I agree with Peptine that...' or 'That's an interesting point, and I'd also add that...' 🤝 Another helpful strategy is to ask follow-up questions to your partner to encourage them to elaborate; for instance, after your partner gives an opinion, you could ask, 'Why do you feel that way?' or 'Can you give an example?' 🤔 In discussions like Part 3 of the test, remember that you're working *with* your partner to reach a shared conclusion or decision, so think of it as building something together! 🏗️	10.0	Mew, your effort in the speaking test was clear, and you communicated your ideas well on many familiar topics! 👍 The most important area for you to focus on right now is making your pronunciation clearer, especially by ensuring all your consonant sounds are distinct. Keep practicing, and you'll find your confidence growing even more as your spoken English becomes easier for everyone to understand. 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br