ou have a solid foundation in English grammar. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used some very nice vocabulary like "material," "friendship," and "empathy" to describe the story. You also tried to use the past tense in many places, like when you said "pig three used the very good material." Using these specific words helped the listener understand your ideas clearly. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Sometimes you mixed up your tenses or used incorrect comparative forms. For example, you said "get more bad" instead of "got worse" or "became worse." You also said "they have build" instead of "they built." This is a common pattern for Thai learners, but try to keep your tenses consistent when telling a story. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Master irregular past tenses:</b> Practice verbs like "build" (/bɪlt/) and "take" (/tʊk/) so they come naturally when you tell stories.</li><li><b>Learn irregular comparatives:</b> Remember that "bad" becomes "worse" and "good" becomes "better" instead of using the word "more."</li><li><b>Practice verb patterns:</b> Practice using "want to" followed by the base verb, like "want to eat" instead of repeating "want to" multiple times.</li></ul><br>Your vocabulary choices are helping you express very clear ideas! 🌟	3	LinLin, you followed the structure of the book review very well from start to finish. Organizing a long talk can be difficult, but you kept the story moving forward logically. With a score of 3 out of 5, you have met the B1 standard for organizing your ideas. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used clear markers to help the listener follow the story, such as "in the beginning" and "in the end." You successfully covered the scene, the main events, and the lesson learned by the pigs. Your final recommendation was also strong because it gave a clear opinion to the audience. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You missed two important parts of the task: naming the author and using