tition and that your contributions are consistently relevant and well-developed.</p>	2	<p>Mew, your pronunciation was mostly intelligible, which is important for your audience to understand you. However, there were some areas where you can improve to make your speech even clearer.<br><br>One pattern observed was the repetitive overstressing of final syllables, for example, in words like "painter" and "presentation." This is a common pattern for Thai speakers because Thai is a syllable-timed language, where each syllable tends to have equal stress. In English, however, word stress is variable and can change the meaning or natural rhythm of a word. To improve this, try listening carefully to how native speakers stress words and practice identifying the stressed syllable. You can mark the stress on new vocabulary items to help you remember.<br><br>You also tended to drop consonant clusters at the ends of words, such as in "child" (which sounded more like "chai"). English has many consonant clusters that Thai does not, and Thai speakers sometimes insert a vowel or drop one of the consonants to make it easier to pronounce. Practice saying words with final consonant clusters slowly and clearly, making sure to articulate each sound: for example, /ˈtʃaɪld/.<br><br>Additionally, you occasionally mixed up the /ʃ/ sound (as in "she") and the /h/ sound (as in "he"). This can sometimes lead to confusion. The /ʃ/ sound requires rounding your lips and pushing air through a wide tongue, while the /h/ sound is a simple aspiration of air from the throat. Pay attention to minimal pairs like "ship" /ʃɪp/ and "hip" /hɪp/, and practice articulating these sounds distinctly.<br><br>To improve your pronunciation, focus on listening practice to identify English stress patterns and minimal pair exercises for challenging sounds like /l/ vs /r/, /θ/ vs /t/ or /d/, and /ʃ/ vs /h/. Regular practice with these specific sounds will greatly enhance your intelligibility and fluency.</p>	\N	\N	11.0	<p