l the necessary parts of the review, including the author name and your own opinion. Using words like "finally" and "because" helped connect your ideas together and made the story easy to follow.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You repeated the phrase "British in the Japanese land" many times, which made the middle part of your talk feel a bit repetitive. Try to use different words like "foreigner" or "Englishman" to keep the listener interested. Some of your sentences were a bit short, and using more transition words like "however" or "consequently" would make your story flow even better.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Use synonyms:</b> When you have to mention a person or place many times, try to find different ways to say it to avoid repetition.</li><li><b>Expand the resolution:</b> Spend a little more time explaining how the story ends so the listener does not feel the conclusion is rushed.</li><li><b>Practice with a timer:</b> Try to speak for the full two minutes by adding more details about the setting and how the characters felt.</li></ul><br>You followed the structure clearly and told a great story! 🎯	3	You delivered an exceptionally energetic and well-paced book review today. Your voice was loud and clear, and your excellent use of intonation made your talk very engaging. It was a pleasure to hear how much passion you put into your presentation.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> Your volume and intonation were outstanding. You used your voice as a dynamic tool to highlight the most important parts of your story, which kept the listener fully involved in your review from start to finish.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> There were patches of unintelligibility /ʌn.ɪnˌtel.ɪ.dʒəˈbɪl.ə.ti/ due to the repetitive dropping of consonant sounds. You missed sounds in words like "think" /θɪŋk/, "five" /faɪv/, "Netherlands" /ˈneð.ə.ləndz/, and "England" /ˈɪŋ.ɡlənd/. There was also a tendency to overstress final syllables in words lik