e vitamins, or did they give proteins, carbohydrates. So they can like calculate their weight to eat like should they eat more or eat less.<br><br>So, I think a lot of teenagers go eat like in fast food, eat fast food because I think it, it's fast, and it tastes great, but they don't know like, uh, what did they put in there? So, like, I think, but, uh, like a small group also not prefers fast food because they think that it will destroy their health, and they, I think they would prefer their like mom's cooking because it reminds them of childhood, and I think they cook, their mother mom cooking is also more taste well.<br><br>So, I think they should, um, uh, at first, they should like understand what is their problem, like their children child problem. Like is it health or just like they got any like or did they got like cancer or something? And then they would like talk to them probably, not just like shout or like say that you should not, you should not eat like that. You should eat like this, like this. So, the children will be like, uh, upset like why would they just got shout because they don't eat things that they didn't want to.	950	4	Grace, you show a good understanding of how to put sentences together, especially when talking about familiar topics like your hobbies and food. For example, you said, "I really enjoy cooking because it makes me feel like relaxed," which is a lovely start to explaining your feelings! 👍 To make your sentences even more precise, you could try to remove unnecessary words; in that sentence, for instance, "like" wasn't needed, and you could say, "it makes me feel relaxed." Another good example is "when I'm go out with my friends." Here, we need to remember that after "I'm" (which is short for "I am"), we usually use a verb ending in -ing, or "I go out" if it's a habitual action, so it would be "when I go out with my friends." Lastly, when describing taste, you said, "mom cooking is also more taste well." A helpful way to say this is