etition.	1	Ie Ie, you made some attempts at linking words, like in "happiness of," which shows you're thinking about natural English speech rhythms. However, the main area to focus on is projecting your voice much more loudly so everyone can hear your important points, and also incorporating more deliberate pauses to emphasise your ideas. Your voice was very quiet, making it difficult for the listener to understand many of your words, which is a significant barrier to intelligibility 😔. This meant that even though you used good vocabulary, it was hard to hear. Thai speakers sometimes speak more softly in English, and ensuring your voice is clear and audible is a key step to sounding more confident and getting your message across. Focus on articulating final consonants more clearly, as these were often dropped (e.g., "wha" instead of "what" or "ba" instead of "bag" – a common pattern for Thai speakers who tend to omit or weaken final consonants), and try to differentiate vowel lengths more consistently, as confusion between sounds like /ɪ/ (as in 'ship') and /iː/ (as in 'sheep') can sometimes make words harder to distinguish.	\N	\N	12.0	Ie Ie, you put together a truly comprehensive and thoughtful presentation about King Rama V! Your main challenge to consider for next time is making sure your voice carries so all your wonderful ideas can be heard clearly. Keep up the fantastic effort in structuring your thoughts and exploring your topics in depth!<br><br>**Action Plan**<br>*   🎙️ Record yourself speaking and try to project your voice more, imagining you're speaking to someone at the back of a large room.<br>*   🤔 Pay close attention to verb endings and agreement when you practice, especially with 'he/she/it' and past tense verbs.<br>*   🗣️ Practice saying words with clear final consonant sounds, as this will help listeners understand you easily.	B1+	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
