o, he got called this from... like a reporter or fan or his members.<br><br><br>Um... even he is a famous artist now, he also got a rude coming from some mean people. I don't know why. You know, like some... people doesn't have the reason to be rude to someone. But... he went through this his members. Why is he a hero? Oh, yeah, the first one is I told you before, donate money to the foundation, get a role model. Um, now it's going to be so over because Stray Kids now like... so popular in Asia or western or anywhere.<br><br><br>So, someone that went to Stray Kids' concert, I think they also become like an idol or role model too. And so influence. And last, the comfort zone. Yeah, I told you like he have empathy and good spirit to others in a way. He always the place that members went to him to get a comfort. Yeah. So, this quote, "Doesn't matter what you are or what you choose to be, you deserve love." This is his quote like a few years ago. Um... this means like everyone deserves love, but just... uh, like anyone doesn't deserve the hate or rude. Thank you.	575	3	<p>You show a good grasp of simple grammatical forms, and you tried to use some more complex structures, which is fantastic! For example, you used phrases like "He was born in Sydney, Australia in 1997" and "he decided to join JYP Entertainment in 2010" correctly. However, there were a few instances where your grammar could be more accurate, which sometimes made your meaning a little less clear. For example, you said "he was <b>choose</b> to be a leader" instead of "he was <b>chosen</b> to be a leader." Also, "he keep learning and practicing" should be "he <b>kept</b> learning and practicing" or "he <b>keeps</b> learning and practicing" depending on the tense. To achieve a score of 5, focus on consistent accuracy with past tense verbs and participle forms. Try to vary your vocabulary more too; while "famous artist" and "best leader" are good, using synonyms or more descriptive adjectives would make your language richer. For instance, instead of "a rude coming from some mean people," you could say "faced rudeness from some unkind people."</p>	3	<p>You produced an extended piece of language, which is excellent, and you covered all the expected parts of the presentation structure! Your contributions were mostly relevant, and you used some cohesive devices to link your ideas. For instance, you used "So, if you wonder..." to transition to a new point and "And then he got like a reward..." to sequence information. You did have some hesitation, which is natural, but sometimes it felt a bit frequent and broke the flow. For example, "And... he was choose to be a leader because of his spirit" or "Um... even he is a famous artist now...". Your presentation was well-structured overall, covering his early life, achievements, and why he is a hero. To reach a score of 5, aim for even smoother transitions between your points and try to reduce hesitation to maintain a consistent flow. You can practice using more varied discourse markers like "Furthermore," "In addition," or "Despite this" to make your arguments even more connected and persuasive. Keep practicing extending your ideas with minimal pauses!</p>	3	<p>Your pronunciation is generally intelligible, which is great! 👍 You demonstrated nice linking of sounds and good intonation patterns. Your individual sounds are also generally articulated clearly. However, as noted, your hesitation was quite intrusive. To improve, focus on practicing fluency exercises to reduce these pauses. For example, try reading texts aloud to build confidence and rhythm. Also, be mindful of specific sounds that Thai speakers sometimes find challenging, such as the /θ/ and /ð/ sounds (like in "think" or "this"), which can sometimes be pronounced as /t/, /d/, or /s/. The distinction between /l/ and /r/ sounds, as in "leader" versus a potential mispronunciation, is also important. Continuing to focus on these areas will help your pronunciation become even clearer and more natural!</p>	\N	\N	13.0	<p>Hi Kaohom C! 👋 Your presentation about your hero, Bang Chan, was very engaging and you provided some great details about his life and achievements. It's clear you have a strong connection to your topic! You managed to speak for a good length of time, which is a real positive! Keep up the great work in expanding your grammar and vocabulary to express your ideas even more precisely. Your ability to connect with your audience is definitely a strength!</p>	B2	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
