eat healthy food?" And he told me, "I love eating junk food and McDonald's. Big man is my favorite food." And I don't know what to say.<br><br>NINE: Yes. Because some students don't know don't know how to eat healthy food and improving your body shape. Okay. What do you think, Mr. Nine? I think it's very important because in my in my house, uh... because my friend in he didn't know like what five thing that he needs to eat and he like very fat and now he passed away.<br><br>NINE: Eating a vegetable because it in my opinion, I I like to eat because it healthy and I like the taste, but for some people, it didn't good for them.<br><br>NINE: Um, I think technology it have one thing that I hear is it can get a fat out of our body and I think it help.<br><br>NINE: No. Because in Thailand, we many people didn't eat healthy food. They eat like Som Tum and it's not very healthy. It very salty and sometimes they have Pla ra and it didn't like fresh not fresh.<br><br>NINE: I think some sport is because like when they have a competition or something like their parents want to want their kid to go and tell them to like go practice, go practice, and didn't have time to relax. Okay. Thank you very much, Platwo. Thank you, Nine.No putput	592	1	Nine, you showed that you can use some simple sentences to talk about everyday things, which is a fantastic start! For instance, saying "I enjoy playing games and reading books" is perfectly clear. 😊 However, when you tried to make longer or more complex sentences, there were quite a few grammatical errors that sometimes made it hard to understand your message. For example, you said, "I like K-pop same like Platwo that it like rock it a little bit not mind," which was a bit tricky to follow. Also, remember that when we talk about many things, we usually add an 's' to the end, like "vegetable*s*" instead of "vegetable." Thai speakers sometimes leave out small words like "is" or "are" in sentences, which can make a big difference in English. To improve, try to build your sentences step by step, focusing on getting the basic grammar right before adding more detail.	2	You did well to keep talking for quite a while, Nine, especially when you were comparing the pictures and discussing ways to improve health. Your comments were usually on topic, and you managed to produce some longer sentences. However, I noticed you hesitated quite a bit, and sometimes repeated yourself or had to restart your thoughts. For example, you said, "don't know don't know" when explaining about healthy food. 🤔 To help your speaking flow better, try to think about how your sentences connect to each other. You used "because" and "and" often, which is good, but you could also try other linking words to make your ideas clearer, like "firstly," "next," or "in my opinion." If you can reduce the repetition and hesitation, your overall message will be much smoother and easier for your listener to follow.	3	Nine, your speech was generally easy to understand, which is excellent! 👍 I could follow what you were saying for the most part. However, I noticed two main things that you could work on. Firstly, you often dropped the final consonant sounds of words, so words might sound incomplete. For example, "friend" might sound like "frien." Secondly, you sometimes put too much stress on the last syllable of words, like when you said "people" as /ˈpiː.pɔːlər/ or "our" as /ˈaʊ.wər/. This can make your speech sound a little less natural and more choppy. Thai speakers often have these habits because the rhythm and final sounds in Thai are different from English. To improve, try to pay close attention to the ends of words and practise saying them clearly. Listen to how native English speakers pronounce words and try to copy their rhythm and stress patterns.	2	You were good at starting conversations and responding when asked, Nine, and you definitely kept the interaction going! For example, you asked Platwo, "What about you, Platwo?" which is a good way to involve your partner. You also shared your own ideas and opinions, especially about healthy eating. 😊 However, I noticed that sometimes your contributions were a bit short, and you tended to focus on giving your personal views rather than really building on what Platwo had said or trying to reach a shared agreement. For instance, when discussing the most enjoyable way to improve health, you told a personal story, which was interesting, but it didn't really move the discussion forward collaboratively. To improve, try to link your ideas more directly to your partner's comments and suggest ways to come to a joint conclusion. This will help you have even richer and more engaging conversations!	8.0	Nine, you did a good job of keeping the conversation going in your Term 1 Finals speaking! It was great to hear you share your thoughts and opinions on a variety of topics. To make your English even clearer and stronger, let's work on making sure your sentences are always grammatically correct and that your speech flows smoothly without dropping those important ending sounds. Keep practising, and you'll notice a significant improvement! 👍<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ **Focus on basic sentence structure:** Pay close attention to subject-verb agreement (e.g., "your body *needs*," not "you body need") and remember to include auxiliary verbs (e.g., "he *is* sitting alone," not "he sitting alone").<br>✔️ **Practice using a wider range of linking words:** Instead of using "like" frequently as a filler, try out other words to connect your ideas, such as "however," "therefore," or "for example."<br>✔️ **Pronounce final consonants clearly:** Make sure you don't drop the sounds at the end of words. Listen carefully to English speakers and try to imitate how they pronounce words like "friends," "books," and "meat."	B1	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
35239	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	Okay hi everyone so today I'm going to be talking about my favorite book it's called You written by Caroline Kepnes. So have you guys ever wondered what it would be like to be in the mind of a stalker? Well this book goes into detail about a psychotic stalker. And this all takes place in 2018 in New York City. This story is about a man called Joe Goldberg and he is a psychotic stalker.<br>Okay so at first Joe Goldberg was a regular guy working at a book shop who took a deep interest in books and found comfort in them ever since he was a kid. But one day a girl named Beck walked into his store and from that day on he began obsessively stalking her and the people around her. And he even went as far as to lock the people around her up in cages and eventually murdering them. So in the end Beck did find out because she found a jar of human teeth and among other things that he took from her without her permission. And eventually he did get away without any consequences.<br>And one of the my favorite things about this book is that I really like how you can tell what he was thinking and you know what his thought process was what possessed him to do the things that he does. But I really dislike the fact that he did get away scot-free I found it a little bit upsetting and it kind of left me on a cliffhanger. So I really recommend this book if you like thrillers and I hope that you'll give it a chance thank you.	282	5	Ana, you spoke with a lot of clarity and used excellent words during your book review! Talking about a dark story like this can be a challenge, but you handled it very well. Your score shows that you have a strong command of English grammar and vocabulary for a middle-school student. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You used very specific and impressive vocabulary like "psychotic," "obsessively," and "consequences." These words fit the thriller theme perfectly. I also noticed how well you used relative clauses to describe the characters and the plot. Your use of the phrase "got away scot-free" was a highlight of your talk because it showed a high level of vocabulary range. Your past tense verbs were consistent throughout the story.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>There were only a few minor slips to look at. For example, you said "one of the my favorite things," which should just be "one of my favorite things." You also said "what possessed him to do the things that he does," where "did" would match the past tense narrative better. Sometimes a "the" was missing before nouns like "book shop." These small errors did not make you hard to understand, but fixing them will make your English even smoother.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Focus on articles</b>: Pay attention to when you need "the" or "a" before places like "the bookstore" or "the book."</li><li><b>Refine possessive phrases</b>: Practice saying "one of my [noun]s" to avoid adding extra words like "the" in the middle.</li><li><b>Tense consistency</b>: When telling a story in the past, try to keep all verbs in the past tense even when talking about the characters' feelings.</li></ul><br>You are using very sophisticated language for your level! 🎯	4	Ana, your organization of this book review was very logical and easy to follow! Preparing a long speech with many requirements is difficult, but you stayed on track throughout the entire talk. You clearly understood the structure needed for a successful presentation. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You followed the required structure from the introduction all the way to the conclusion. Your hook was excellent because you asked a rhetorical question and then answered it to lead directly into your story. You used good connecting words like "eventually" and "first" to help the listener understand the timeline of the book. Each part of your talk felt logical and you gave enough detail about the plot without giving too much away. Your recommendation was clear and fit the genre of the book.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>You covered the plot well, but you could add a bit more variety to your transitions. You used the word "and" quite frequently to start new sentences. While this helps the flow, using different words can make your speech sound more professional. Also, make sure you spend a little more time describing the climax to build more tension for the listener. Your ending was good, but it could be even stronger with a final closing sentence that summarizes your feelings.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Vary your transitions</b>: Instead of starting sentences with "and," try using words like "furthermore," "however," or "in addition."</li><li><b>Build tension</b>: When describing the climax, use more descriptive adjectives to make the listener feel the excitement of the discovery.</li><li><b>Expand on opinions</b>: You gave good reasons for your likes and dislikes, but you could try to explain how the "cliffhanger" made you feel as a reader.</li></ul><br>Your storytelling skills are very strong and kept me interested! 🎯	4	Your book review was exceptionally well-structured and delivered with a high level of confidence. Your pronunciation of individual sounds was very accurate, and your vocabulary was perfectly suited for the task. You followed the structure of the presentation logically from beginning to end.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> Almost everything about your phonetic delivery was excellent; your individual sounds and word stressing were very precise. This made your speech very easy to follow and gave your presentation a professional feel.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> The only significant area for improvement is your intonation /ˈɪn.tə.neɪ.ʃən/, which was quite flat throughout the talk. If you can vary your pitch more, your speech will sound much more natural and engaging for your audience.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Practice using more rising and falling pitch patterns to emphasize key information and show emotion in your storytelling.<br>2. Record yourself reading and focus specifically on making the most important words sound a little higher or lower in pitch.<br><br>Fixing that flat intonation will make a huge difference in your overall delivery, Ana! 🚀	\N	\N	17.0	Ana, your book review was impressive and showed a high level of English ability! You chose very effective vocabulary for a thriller and organized your story in a logical way. Your rhetorical question was a great way to start, and you successfully included all the required parts of the task. To improve, try to reduce your use of the word "and" when starting new sentences and keep a close eye on your use of articles. You are speaking with a lot of confidence and clarity! 🌟	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
