t, and you keep your contributions relevant to the topic without repeating yourself too much! You also use connecting words to link your ideas. For instance, in your description of the wedding, you moved logically from the couple to the parents and then to other details. You also explained your reasoning well in the collaborative task, giving good arguments for your choices. 👏 While you sometimes paused to find the right word, you always managed to keep the conversation going. To make your speech even smoother, try to use a wider variety of cohesive devices and discourse markers. Instead of always using "and," you could try "in addition," "furthermore," or "however" to show different relationships between your ideas. This will help you organise your thoughts even more effectively and sound even more fluent! 🚀	5	Yayar, your pronunciation is truly excellent! You are incredibly clear, and it was easy to understand every single word you said. Your intonation, which is the way your voice rises and falls, is very natural, making your speech sound lovely and engaging. You also place the stress on words and sentences perfectly, and you articulate individual sounds with great clarity. This means that your control over the sounds of English is outstanding for this level. Keep up the fantastic work! You're doing a brilliant job! ✨🗣️	4	You're very good at starting conversations and responding appropriately, Yayar, and you actively keep the interaction going with your partner, Elle. You linked your ideas well to what Elle said, and you clearly tried to lead the discussion towards an outcome in the collaborative task. For example, you asked Elle, "What do you think?" which is a great way to involve your partner. You also developed your points, explaining why you thought tour guides were a good option. 🤝 To move from good to truly outstanding, try to challenge your partner's ideas a little more constructively, or ask for more detail from them. While you encourage interaction, sometimes your questions are a little general. For instance, instead of just "What do you think?", you could ask, "That's an interesting idea about buying a map, but do you think it might be difficult to navigate a new city without a local guide?" This helps to deepen the conversation and explore different perspectives! 💬	16.0	Hi Yayar! It was wonderful to listen to you speak in your Term 1 Finals! You express yourself very clearly and can talk about lots of different topics with confidence. You're particularly good at making sure your listeners can understand you, and you keep conversations flowing smoothly. We'll work on making your sentences even more accurate and help you link your ideas even better when discussing with others. With a bit of attention to these areas, your English will feel even more polished! 🌟<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Yayar:**<br>*   ✍️ When you talk about past experiences, think carefully about which verb tense you need. For instance, if you're talking about something that started in the past and continues to now, use the present perfect tense, like "I **have studied** English" instead of "I **am study** English."<br>*   🗣️ To make your explanations even clearer, try to use more varied connecting words beyond "and" or "because." Words like "however," "therefore," "for instance," or "in addition" can help you show different relationships between your ideas.<br>*   💬 When collaborating with a partner, remember to ask them questions like "What do you think about that?" or "Do you agree with me?" to make sure you're both building the conversation together and exploring different viewpoints.	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
