>You often missed the '-s' for third-person subjects, like saying 'he discover' /dɪˈskʌv.ə/ instead of 'he discover<b>s</b>' /dɪˈskʌv.ə<b>z</b>/. Sometimes you left out the verb 'to be' in sentences like 'the story trying to explain' instead of 'the story <b>is</b> trying to explain.' At the end, you said 'You won’t disappoint' when you meant 'You won’t <b>be</b> disappoint<b>ed</b>' /ˌdɪs.əˈpɔɪn.tɪ<b>d</b>/.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Third-person singular</b>: Remember to add '-s' when talking about Harry or the author. It is a common Thai pattern to drop final sounds, so focus on the 's' in words like 'thinks' or 'faces.'</li><li><b>The verb 'to be'</b>: When using '-ing' words to describe an action, always include 'is' or 'are.' For example, say 'the story <b>is</b> trying' instead of 'the story trying.'</li><li><b>Passive feelings</b>: Use '-ed' for your feelings or the reader's feelings. Instead of 'You won’t disappoint,' use 'You won’t <b>be</b> disappoint<b>ed</b>.'</li></ul><br>You have a strong foundation in grammar, so focus on these small details! 🎯	4	Nut, your book review followed the required structure perfectly! Moving between different parts of a long talk is a big challenge for many Thai learners. You reached a score of 4 because your story was easy to follow from start to finish. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You included every part of the task, from the 'Imagine' hook to the final recommendation. You used transition words like 'After that' and 'In the beginning' to help the listener follow the timeline. Your explanation of why you found some parts boring was very clear and well-reasoned.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You had several long pauses and sighs where you seemed to be searching for the next idea. This made the flow of your talk stop and start a few times. You also repeated the phrase 'the most exciting parts for me' twice, which made the middle section feel a bit repetitive.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Record your hook</b>: Record your 'Imagine' opening until you can say it without any pauses. This will help you start the rest of the talk with more confidence.</li><li><b>Avoid repetitive phrases</b>: Instead of saying 'the most exciting part' again, try using 'I also loved when...' or 'Another great moment was...'</li><li><b>Connect your ideas</b>: Work on linking your sentences together so there are fewer gaps. Try to keep your breath moving as you transition from one event to the next.</li></ul><br>You are making great progress in your speaking! 🎯	5	Nut, your pronunciation in this book review was excellent and very easy to understand! You reached the top score of 5 out of 5, which is a fantastic achievement. You spoke with confidence and clarity from beginning to end. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You were highly intelligible throughout your entire presentation, making it a pleasure to listen to your ideas. Your intonation was natural and helped to emphasize the most important parts of your story. Your individual sounds were almost perfect, showing that you have a strong foundation in English pronunciation.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>The only very minor thing to watch out for is the "schwa" sound in the unstressed syllables of some words. For example, in words like "Scotland" (/ˈskɒt.lənd/) or "wizard" (/ˈwɪz.ərd/), the second part should be very weak and short. Sometimes you gave these lazy sounds a bit too much strength. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Practice the Schwa:</b> Listen to how native speakers say "wizard" (/ˈwɪz.ərd/) and copy the short, lazy sound at the end of the word.</li><li><b>Focus on Unstressed Syllables:</b> Try to make the non-important parts of words sound much shorter and "smaller" than the main stress.</li><li><b>Keep Modeling:</b> Continue listening to English podcasts or movies to maintain your natural-sounding rhythm and intonation.</li></ul><br>You are 