imes forgot to add the correct endings to verbs when talking about the past. For example, you said "write by" instead of "written by" and "boy name Ichigo" instead of "boy name<b>d</b>" /neɪm<b>d</b>/. There were also some extra words like "the Japan" where we usually do not use an article. These are small slips, but fixing them will make your English sound much more natural.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice Past Participles:</b> Focus on irregular verbs like "written" or "seen" when describing who created a book.</li><li><b>Noun endings:</b> Practice saying "named" and "called" with a clear final /d/ or /t/ sound so the past tense is obvious to the listener.</li><li><b>Article rules:</b> Remember that we usually do not use "the" before names of most countries like Japan, Thailand, or England.</li></ul><br>Your vocabulary and grammar choices made the story very easy to follow! 🎯	4	Peptine, your presentation followed the book review structure perfectly from start to finish! It was very easy to follow your story because you organized your ideas so logically. A score of 4 shows that you have a strong grasp of how to give a long talk in English.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You included every required part of the task, including the author's name and a clear recommendation at the end. Your use of transition words like "In the beginning," "Suddenly," and "But" helped move the story along smoothly. You also gave a very honest opinion about the parts of the manga you found boring, which made your review feel more personal.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were a few moments where you paused or repeated words while searching for the next idea. For instance, you said "a... a boy" and "help... help Rukia" which broke the flow of your sentence. Many Thai speakers do this when they are thinking, but try to use a short silence instead of repeating the word. This will help your speech sound smoother and more confident.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Use story markers:</b> Practice using phrases like "After that" or "Eventually" to connect different parts of your plot.</li><li><b>Expand your opinion:</b> When you say a part is "boring," try to explain exactly why, such as "the pacing was too slow" or "the characters did not talk much."</li><li><b>Record and listen:</b> Record yourself speaking for two minutes and listen for where you repeat words, then try to say that specific sentence again without any repeats.</li></ul><br>You followed the structure perfectly and told a very exciting story! 🎯	3	Peptine, well done on delivering a very professional and well-paced book review! You have many great habits in your speaking, and you earned a solid score of 3 out of 5. You should be very happy with your performance. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your volume, intonation, and pacing were all excellent! This made your talk very pleasant to listen to and easy to follow as you moved through the plot. You have a very natural-sounding voice when you are speaking English, which is a great asset.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>The main area to improve is your final consonant sounds, which were occasionally missing. I noticed this happening with the "n" in "imagine" (<b>/ɪˈmædʒ.ɪn/</b> → /ɪˈmædʒ.ɪ/), "l" in "school" (<b>/skuːl/</b>), and "f" in "life" (<b>/laɪf/</b>). I also noticed the "id" sound was dropped in "respected" (<b>/rɪˈspek.tɪd/</b> → /rɪˈspekt/). When these sounds are missing, it can sometimes change the meaning of your words.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Finish the Word:</b> Practice saying "imagine" (<b>/ɪˈmædʒ.ɪn/</b>) and "school" (<b>/skuːl/</b>) out loud, focusing on making the very last sound clearly audible.</li><li><b>Focus on -ed Endings:</b> When a word ends with "t" or "d" and we add -ed, like "respected" (<b>/rɪˈspek.tɪd/</b>), remember that we need to add an extra syllable.</li><li><b>Slow Down for Linking:</b> Try connecting words like "life is" or "school was" so you can practice the ending sounds while moving to the next word.</li></ul><br>You have a natural flow to your speech—keep focusing on those final sounds! 🎯	\N	\N	15.0	Peptine, you gave a fantastic review of Bleach that was very easy to follow! You used all the required parts of the structure, including the hook and the author's name, which made your talk complete. Your vocabulary was well-chosen for this topic, and you used clear transition words to tell the story of Ichigo. To improve, focus on your past tense endings like "named" and "written," and try to reduce word repetitions to make your speaking even smoother. You are a confident speaker who knows how to keep an audience interested! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
