erb endings (like the '-s' for 'he/she/it', as in "one boy **wears** a yellow shirt" instead of "one boy wear yellow shirt") and make sure your adjectives are the right form (describing a situation as 'tiring' rather than 'tired'). Also, sometimes articles ('a', 'an', 'the') were missed; for example, "I'm in part of badminton club" would be better as "I'm **part** of **the** badminton club." Many Thai learners find these aspects tricky because Thai grammar doesn't change verbs for tense or subject, and articles aren't used in the same way, so keep practising these little but important differences! 👍	4	You're really good at speaking for extended periods, Hero, and you do it with very little hesitation, which is fantastic! 🎉 Your answers are always relevant to the questions, and you generally organise your thoughts clearly. You also use connecting words like 'because' often to explain your reasons, and phrases like "um, so" to help move your ideas along. For instance, when you described your favourite food, Tom Yum Goong, you naturally explained, "...because, um, it tastes sour and spicy and I don't know but it's kind of like, it just melt in my, my, my mouth right away." While your communication is clear, to reach an even higher score, you could try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of always using "because," you could sometimes say "consequently," "therefore," or "in addition" to make your speech more varied and sophisticated. Thinking about a clear structure before you speak, moving from a general idea to specific details, can also help make your extended answers even more organised.	2	Hero, it's great that you're mostly intelligible, meaning that I could understand what you were saying most of the time! However, there are a couple of areas that, with practice, will make your speech even clearer. You sometimes have problems linking sounds together, and you frequently drop the endings of words. For example, when you said "clubs," it sounded more like /klʌb/ instead of the full /klʌbz/, and "played" sometimes lost its final /d/ sound, becoming /pleɪ/. When you say "picked me up," sometimes the /t/ sound in "picked" can almost disappear, making it sound less distinct. Try to really exaggerate those final sounds when you practise, and then work on gently connecting words. For instance, when a word ends with a consonant and the next one starts with a vowel, like "picke**d** **u**p," try to blend them together smoothly, almost like "pick-**dup**." Thai learners sometimes find linking sounds and pronouncing final consonants challenging because Thai words are often pronounced with clear breaks between them, and final consonant sounds are often often simplified or not pronounced as strongly as in English. Keep practising these sounds, and your pronunciation will become even more natural! 🗣️👂	4	You did a very good job of interacting in the conversation, Hero! You initiated discussions and responded appropriately to the examiner and your partner, Atom. For example, when you suggested joining the swimming club and then later agreed on badminton, you were helping to move the discussion forward, and when you asked Atom if he agreed with you, you kept the interaction going smoothly. You worked well with your partner to negotiate towards an outcome, which is a very important skill in these tests! To make your interactive communication even stronger, remember to explicitly ask your partner for their thoughts more often. Phrases like "What do you think, Atom?" or "How about you?" are wonderful ways to invite them into the conversation and show you value their opinion. You could also try to build on their ideas more often, perhaps saying something like, "That's a good point, and I also think..." This would demonstrate even greater collaboration and development of the interaction. Keep up the good work! 🤝	13.0	Hero, you've done a very good job in your speaking assessment, communicating your ideas clearly and engaging well in the conversation! Your ability to keep the conversation flowing and to respond thoughtfully is a real strength. However, paying a little extra attention to your grammar and pronunciation will make your English sound even clearer and more natural. With a bit of focus on these areas, your English will truly flourish! 🌟<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   Remember to check your verb endings carefully, especially the '-s' for 'he/she/it' and '-ed' for past tenses.<br>*   Practice linking words together smoothly to help your speech sound more connected, rather than separate.<br>*   Make sure to clearly pronounce the final sounds of your words so that nothing gets missed by your listener.	B1	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
