ou.	353	1	<p>Tatar, you used some good vocabulary when talking about familiar topics, which is a wonderful start! 🎉 To really boost your score to a 5, try to get a stronger grip on basic grammar rules and challenge yourself to use more complex sentences accurately. For example, instead of saying "he born in Lincolnshire," you could say "he <em>was</em> born in Lincolnshire." And when you say "he discover light, motion and gravity," a small change to "he <em>discovered</em> light, motion, and gravity" makes a big difference! Focusing on your verb tenses and making sure your subjects and verbs match will make your speech much clearer and more natural. Keep working on these details, and your English will become super precise! 🚀</p>	2	<p>You did a great job of speaking for extended periods, Tatar, which is a real strength! 💪 You introduced your hero well and made sure to cover most of the important parts of your talk, showing you can organize your thoughts. There were a few moments where you hesitated, like when you paused before saying "1665 to 1666," which can sometimes make your speech feel a little less smooth. To reach a score of 5, aim to connect your ideas even more seamlessly and try to avoid repeating yourself too much.</p><br><p>Also, your presentation had 353 words. While that's a good effort, the aim was to speak for at least 400 words. Reaching that target helps you share even more detailed thoughts and show off a wider range of language. Don't worry, this is a great area to improve! Try to add more details or examples to your points; for instance, you could have told us more about <em>why</em> people found Newton "sensitive and don't friendly." 🌟 Expanding your ideas will naturally help you hit that word count!</p>	2	<p>Tatar, sometimes your pronunciation made it a little tricky to understand everything you said. Your voice was quite flat, which can make it harder for listeners to follow along. Try to vary the 'tune' of your voice (intonation) more, making some parts higher and some lower, just like music! 🎶</p><br><p>Also, you tended to separate words too much. For instance, "was a good student" sounded like individual words instead of flowing smoothly together. English often links words, especially short ones like "was" and "a," so practicing connecting them will make your speech sound much more natural.</p><br><p>It's common for English learners to overstress the last part of words, like in "England" /ˈɪŋ.ɡlənd/ or "information" /ɪn.fəˈmeɪ.ʃən/. In English, one part of a word usually sounds stronger than the others.</p><br><p>We noticed a few specific sounds that could use some extra practice! For "discover," the vowel sound in the middle should be like the 'u' in 'up' /dɪsˈkʌvər/, rather than an 'aw' sound. Also, a common habit is to drop the ends of words, especially in past tense verbs like "started" (make sure to say the /ɪd/ sound clearly!). Sometimes, you also missed the very last sound of words, like the /f/ in "life" or the /z/ in "was." Practicing these tricky sounds and really focusing on saying every sound at the end of words will help a lot. Listening carefully to English speakers and trying to copy exactly how they say things can be a fun way to improve! 🗣️👂</p>	\N	NULL	10.0	<p>Hello Tatar! 👋 You did a really good job introducing Sir Isaac Newton and setting out what you were going to talk about. It's clear you put thought into structuring your presentation, and you covered most of the important points about your hero! ✨ That's fantastic effort!</p><br><p>To help you get even better, let's focus on a few things. You can make your sentences smoother by paying close attention to grammar, and by trying out new words to make your descriptions even more exciting! Also, practicing how you say words, especially endings, will really help everyone understand your great ideas even more clearly. Keep practicing, and you'll see amazing progress! 👍</p>	A2+	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
