ice with a timer:</b> Use a stopwatch while practicing at home to make sure you can speak comfortably for at least two minutes.</li></ul>You have a great start with your hooks—now work on adding more detail to the story! 🎯	3	You did a great job sharing your thoughts on the movie K-Pop: Demon Hunter today, Tatar. It was very interesting to hear your perspective on the action and the characters. Thank you for putting so much effort into your speaking assessment!<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your individual sounds are quite clear, and I noticed that you successfully used some linkages to connect your words smoothly in several places. This shows that you are developing a good foundational awareness of how English sounds interact.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>The most significant challenge currently is a very flat intonation, which can make it harder for the listener to follow your meaning. I also noticed quite a bit of hesitation and some overstressing of final syllables in words like "hunter" /ˈhʌntə/, "crazy" /ˈkreɪzi/, and "energy" /ˈenədʒi/. Additionally, you occasionally dropped final consonant clusters, such as the /st/ in "first" /fɜːst/. Your voice was also extremely quiet, so it was hard to hear in some places.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice Sentence Stress:</b> Try to identify the most important words in a sentence and give them more "life" by slightly increasing your volume and pitch on them.</li><li><b>Smooth Out Hesitations:</b> Practice reading short paragraphs aloud multiple times to build "muscle memory" for common phrases, which will help reduce pauses.</li><li><b>Focus on Final Consonants:</b> Slow down slightly when you reach the end of a word to ensure sounds like /t/, /d/, or /st/ are fully voiced.</li></ul><br>Keep practicing your projection and intonation, and you will become a much more confident speaker! 🌟	\N	\N	11.0	Tatar, you gave a clear introduction and used a very creative hook to start your review! You used good vocabulary to describe the action and the setting in South Korea. To improve, remember to include the author's name and spend more time explaining the most exciting parts of the plot. Focus on your subject-verb agreement and making sure plural words end with an "s" sound. Speaking for a bit longer and adding more detail to your opinion will help you reach a higher B1 level! 🌟	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
