irt because it's easy to wear and we can style it anyway. Maybe, maybe I will add some accessory like necklace.<br><br>	391	2	You show a good understanding of how to put simple sentences together, Elle, especially when talking about familiar topics like your hobbies and what you did last night. You used a good range of everyday words, which is fantastic! 👍 To help your English grow even stronger, let's focus on a few things. Sometimes, when you speak, there are small grammatical errors that can make your message a little harder to understand. For instance, you said, "I see a boy **are** very happy," but it should be "I see a boy **is** very happy" because 'boy' is singular. Another example is "I like to **drawing**," which should be "I like to **draw**." These are common challenges for Thai learners, as the structure of sentences and verb forms in English can be quite different from Thai. To improve, try to pay close attention to verb agreement (matching your verb to your subject) and the correct form of verbs after "to" (we use the base form, like "to draw," not "to drawing"). Practising these will make your English even clearer! 🚀	3	You do a great job of speaking in longer sentences, Elle, which is a fantastic step beyond just short answers! You always try to keep the conversation going and your contributions are relevant to the topic. 🎉 However, sometimes there's a little bit of hesitation, and your ideas could be connected even more smoothly. For example, when you were describing the birthday party, you jumped between different details a bit. To make your English flow even better, try to use more connecting words and phrases like "firstly," "next," "after that," or "on the other hand." This helps your listener follow your thoughts more easily. Also, try to expand on your ideas a bit more. Instead of just stating an opinion, you could add another sentence or two to explain why you feel that way. This will help you produce even more organised and coherent responses! 🧩	4	Elle, your pronunciation is very clear, and I could understand almost everything you said, which is a big achievement! Your voice goes up and down in a natural way when you speak, making it easy to listen to you. ✨ We noticed some very minor instances where you slightly overemphasised the final sounds of words. This is a common habit for Thai speakers, as the stress patterns in Thai can be different from English. For example, you might say "teach**er**" with a slightly stronger stress on the '-er' sound than a native English speaker would. To refine this, try to listen carefully to how native speakers pronounce words and aim to blend those final sounds a little more gently. With this small adjustment, your speech will sound even more natural! 🗣️	3	You're good at starting to speak and responding when it's your turn, Elle, and you manage to keep the conversation going with your partner, Yaya. For instance, you agreed with her idea about safety and added your own suggestions about buying a city guide or a map. That's excellent! 🤝 To make your interactive communication even stronger, try to link your contributions more closely to what your partner has just said. Sometimes your comments, while relevant, were a bit more about your own thoughts without building directly on Yaya's last point. For example, you could say, "That's a good point about tours being safe, and **in addition to that**, I think buying a book would also give us...". Also, try to ask your partner more questions to get their opinion and encourage them to say more. This will help you both develop the conversation more deeply and reach conclusions together! 💬	12.0	Hello Elle! It was a pleasure listening to you speak in your Term 1 Finals. You communicated well and showed good confidence in expressing your thoughts, especially regarding your interests and daily life. To make your English even clearer and more natural, we'll focus on making sure your sentences are always grammatically correct and that