nsonant clusters, like the "st" in "forest" (/ˈfɒr.ɪst/ → /ˈfɒr.ɪ/<b>s</b>) and the "ch" in "witch" (/wɪtʃ/). Occasionally, you put too much stress on the final syllables of words like "Rapunzel" (/rəˈpʌn.zəl/ → /rə.pʌnˈzəl/) or "probLEM" instead of "PROBlem" (/ˈprɒb.ləm/). <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Project Your Voice:</b> Try to imagine you are speaking to someone at the very back of the room to help increase your volume by about 10%.</li><li><b>Practice Clusters:</b> Say words like "forest" (/ˈfɒr.ɪst/) slowly to make sure both the "s" and the "t" are heard clearly at the end.</li><li><b>Check Early Stress:</b> Remember that most English words are stressed at the beginning. Practice saying "<b>PROB</b>-lem" (/ˈprɒb.ləm/) and copy the rhythm of a native speaker.</li></ul><br>You are a very expressive speaker—keep up the great work! 🎯	\N	\N	16.0	Bona, you gave a very clear and organized review of Tangled! You followed the required structure perfectly, including the author and an engaging rhetorical question at the start. Your vocabulary was impressive, and you used many linking words to connect your ideas logically. To improve, try to be more careful with your verb endings like "s" and "ed," and work on reducing long pauses to make your speech sound even smoother. You have a great foundation for storytelling! 🌟	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
