e didn't deserve to to be recognized.<br><br>And next, why is she a hero in my opinion? She was really brave and willing to sacrifice her life for her people that most celebrity won't do that.<br><br>And this is the last one. Now I'm going to conclude you what lesson do we learn? So I'm talk to you about her early life, uh why why did she become hero and what she did. And I think she deserves our respect from all us. And the last main message that I'm going to give you is bravery is everything. Thank you and wish you guys have a nice day.	460	3	You did a good job using familiar words to describe Grandma Mo's story, which is excellent! 😊 You also tried to use some more advanced sentence structures, which is fantastic. To get an even better score, try to be super careful with your verbs, especially when talking about things that happened in the past. For example, instead of 'it just come,' we usually say 'it just *comes*' or 'it just *came*.' Also, remember that after 'did' or 'didn't,' the main verb stays in its base form, so 'what did she did' should be 'what did she *do*.' Thai learners sometimes mix up verb forms because Thai grammar doesn't change verbs in the same way English does for tenses or singular/plural subjects. Keep an eye on those small but important changes! 👀	3	You organised your presentation clearly, which made it easy to follow Grandma Mo's story from her early life to why she is your hero. That's a real strength! 👍 Your introduction and conclusion were clear, and you kept your points relevant to the topic. To make your presentation flow even better and earn a higher score, try to link your ideas more smoothly and avoid repeating phrases too often. Sometimes, using rhetorical questions can make your speech sound a little bit abrupt, so try to introduce new points more directly or with different transition words to keep your listener engaged without any sudden changes in tone. Think about how you can vary your sentence structures too, so your speech