med after a album in 2011. And the next, she have help other... a lot of people over a 1,500 people. And by this, she got a National Council for Behavioral Health Champion Award.<br><br><br>And of course, a person cannot gain a universal love. She get accused of some report said that a foundation money spend a large amount on legal fee. Lady Gaga and her mother deny all the accusation and said that the foundation does its own work rather than giving other people grant. The money spend on publicity and consultation to help other people.<br><br><br>And why she not just a celebrity? In 2011, on March 11, at 2:46 p.m., a 8.9 to 9 magnitude earthquake have have struck in the coast of Japan. This make over a hundred person have died, so she want to relieve the victim. So, she made the "We pray for Japan" wristband. This raised for 250,000 dollars and help to a lot of people in Japan. And the next, she made a foundation to help other people face bullying and mental health, and so she want to make a braver and kinder world.<br><br><br>"You have to be unique and defend and shine in your own way." This phrase means the person should embrace their individuality and not conform to others. Is... is celebrate what... celebrate what make one unique and confidently expressing it. Authenticity... courage despite societal pressure and... and by shining in your own way, a person can inspire other people by their originality and and their self-acceptant and self-expression. And this is why Lady Gaga is my hero. Thank you.	589	3	<p>Rome, you show good control of simple sentences, like when you said, "She is a famous singer" and "Her name is Stefani Joanne Angelina Germanotta." This is a strong foundation! 👍 You also tried to use more complex ideas, for example, "This make her want to prove the bully wrong that she can become famous." However, sometimes small mistakes in grammar can make your message a little less clear. For instance, you said "what she do" instead of "what she does," and "a 8.9 to 9 magnitude earthquake have have struck" which had a repeated word and an incorrect verb form.<br><br>Your vocabulary is good for talking about topics you know well, like Lady Gaga's life. However, to get a higher score, try to use more exact words. For example, you said "famous from her album" but "famous for her albums" would be more accurate. To reach a score of 5, focus on making sure your grammar is correct all the time, especially when you try new, longer sentences. Also, try to learn a wider variety of words so you can express your ideas with more precision! 🎯</p>	2	<p>You spoke for a good amount of time, Rome, which is fantastic! You clearly organized your ideas about Lady Gaga, and everything you said was relevant to the topic. You used some useful linking words like "And the next" and "In her early life" to help guide the listener through your presentation.<br><br>However, during your presentation, it sounded like you were reading from notes. In a presentation, it's really important to speak naturally and connect with your audience. Reading too much can make your talk sound less like a conversation and more like a script. This is why your score for Discourse Management was reduced by one point. For a score of 5, you need to produce long stretches of language with very little hesitation and show a clear organization of ideas without relying on reading. Try practicing your presentation without notes, maybe just using a few keywords to remember your main points. This will help you speak more smoothly and engage your audience better! 😊</p>	1	<p>Rome, it's great that you're focusing on your pronunciation! I noticed that you sometimes overstressed the final syllables in words, like "singer." In English, the stress usually falls on a different syllable, and practicing this can make your words sound more natural.<br><br>It's also important to pay attention to the end of words. I noticed you regularly dropped final consonants and plural /s/ sounds. For 