28900	2025-09-01	2025-09-01	2026	My Hero	Hi everyone, my name is Bona and today I am going to tell you all about King Taksin. As you may know, King Taksin was an important figure in Thai history.<br><br><br>Let me tell you an overview of my presentation today. First, I am going to tell you about who King Taksin was. Next, I will discuss what he did, and finally, why he is the hero of Thailand.<br><br><br>Let's start with his early life. King Taksin was born in 1734 in Ayutthaya. His father was a Chinese immigrant and his mother was a Thai. When he was young, he noticed the importance of leadership and military skill. And he served under King Ekkathat.<br><br><br>In 1767, after Ayutthaya fell to the Burmese, he protected the people in Thailand and recovered the country. In 1768, he declared independence and saved the people in Thailand and he rebuilt Thonburi as a new capital.<br><br><br>Not everyone liked King Taksin because some nobles thought he was too powerful and too expressive.<br><br><br>Let me tell you why he is a real hero. He saved the nation and he protected the people in Thailand and he gave hope to them. And he united the land and rebuilt Thonburi as a new capital.<br><br><br>I have read a strong message which is the man who defeated the fall of Ayutthaya is very important for young people in Thailand because it can help us to be brave and it can help us to never give up in difficult times. Thank you.	259	2	What I noticed (student-friendly): You use simple past and simple sentences correctly at times (e.g., “He saved the nation”), but many sentences are incomplete or have mistakes: “King Taksin was an important in Thai history.” — should be “King Taksin was an important figure in Thai history.”; “In 1768, he independent and saved the people…” — should be “In 1768, he became independent / he declared independence and saved the people…”. You also repeat words (“of of”) and use limited vocabulary to explain events.<br><br>Examples from your transcript: Error: “King Taksin was an important in Thai history.” — should be “King Taksin was an important figure in Thai history.”<br><br>Error: “In 1768, he independent and saved the people…” — should be “In 1768, he became independent / he declared independence and saved the people…”<br><br>Weak collocation: “gave the hope for them” → “gave them hope” or “gave hope to the people.”<br><br>Communication effect: These errors rarely make your meaning impossible to understand, but they reduce precision and naturalness.<br><br>How to reach 5: Learn and practise key collocations and fixed phrases (e.g., “give hope to”, “declared independence”, “rebuild the capital”). Build sentences with one complex structure per paragraph (e.g., use a relative clause: “The king, who had a Chinese father, learned military skills early.”). Self-check: after writing a sentence, ask if it sounds natural. If not, simplify and then add a single complex element.	1	You attempt the expected structure (intro → early life → achievements → why hero → conclusion) and use signposting phrases. However, the presentation is too short and lacks development and specific supporting details (dates are given but stories and explanations are thin). There are many hesitations and repetitions which interrupt flow.<br><br>Good signposting: “First… Next… Finally.” ✔️<br><br>Missing development: “When he was young, he noticed the importance of leadership and military skill.” — you don’t give a specific example of an event that shaped him.<br><br>Lack of specific evidence: “He saved the nation” — but no explicit story or details of actions (who, when, how).<br><br>Effect of word count: The task required a minimum of 400 words. You produced 259 words; because this is significantly below the 350 threshold, I applied a strong penalty to Discourse. This is the main reason your overall score is low.<br><br>How to reach 5: Follow the given structure and add at least 2–3 supporting details or short examples in each section (e.g., a short story from his early life, one clear action with date and impact, and one quote or public response). Practice planning: spend 3–4 minutes before speaking to note 3 bullet points for each section (Intro, Early life, Actions, Reasons, Conclusion). Use linking devices to connect ideas: “Because of this… As a result… For example…”	2	What I noticed (from your notes and transcript): Frequent final-syllable stress (e.g., “histo'ree” vs. correct /ˈhɪstəri/), weak linking between words (staccato style), and stress‑timing issues that interfere with natural rhythm and sometimes intelligibility.<br><br>Phonemic examples & targets: “history” — correct: /ˈhɪstəri/ (stress on first syllable). Avoid placing primary stress on final syllable. “presentation” — correct: /ˌprɛzənˈteɪʃən/ (primary stress on /teɪ/). Link: “the impact of” is pronounced /ði ɪmˈpækt əv/ in connected speech — try connecting “impact” and “of” so it sounds like /ˈɪmpæktəv/ rather than three separate sharp words.<br><br>Relevant Thai interference patterns (brief): Stress pattern and syllable-timing differences → Thai tends to stress syllables more evenly; English needs stress on specific syllables and reduced unstressed syllables (schwa /ə/). This is the most serious intelligibility issue here. Linking and reduction problems → Thai speakers may not link words or reduce vowels, causing a choppy rhythm.<br><br>Exercises to improve: Practice stress with 2‑syllable and 3‑syllable words: mark the primary stress and say the word slowly, then faster. Use /ˈhɪstəri/, /prɪˌzɛnˈteɪʃən/, /ɪmˈpækt/. Sentence chunking: practice the sentence “He saved the nation and gave hope to the people.” First mark chunks: [He saved the nation] [and gave hope] [to the people]. Say each chunk smoothly and connect final and initial sounds. Shadowing drills: listen to a short spoken model (10–20 seconds) and try to imitate stress and linking exactly. Focus on schwa /ə/ for unstressed syllables (e.g., “the” /ðə/ or /ði/ depending on context; “of” reduced to /əv/).<br><br>How to reach 5: Work specifically on stress placement and connected speech. Record yourself and compare with a native model. Practise two short sentences every day, focusing on stress and linking.	\N		7.0	Hi Bona 👋 — good work on your presentation about King Taksin. You make your main message clear: he saved and reunited Thailand, and you tried to follow the presentation structure. 👍<br><br>Clear overall message: you repeatedly say that King Taksin saved and united the country — this helps listeners understand your point. ✅<br><br>Basic organisation: you attempt an introduction, early life, what he did, and a conclusion, with signposting like “First… Next… Finally.” 🗂️<br><br>Your presentation is short (259 words) and many expected details and examples are missing. The task asked for at least 400 words; this affected your Discourse score. ⚠️<br><br>You use mostly simple grammar correctly, but there are frequent errors and limited variety in vocabulary (e.g., “he independent”, “important in Thai history”). Try to practise more complex sentences. 🛠️<br><br>Your stress patterns and lack of linking make parts hard to follow (you often stress final syllables and speak in a staccato rhythm). This reduces clarity. 🔉<br><br>You have good ideas and the right structure. If you add more supporting detail, check grammar, and practise natural stress and linking, your score will improve. Keep going — you’re on the right track! 💪😊	A2+	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
