ot;a research.&quot; In English, &quot;research&quot; is usually an uncountable noun, so we just say &quot;do research&quot; without &quot;a.&quot; Lastly, some word choices like &quot;stomach aid&quot; made it a bit unclear. We usually say &quot;stomach ache&quot; or &quot;stomach ulcer&quot; for this. Paying close attention to verb tenses, especially for past events, and learning when to use &quot;a&quot; or &quot;an&quot; with nouns will really help. Also, building your vocabulary for specific topics will make your explanations much clearer. In Thai, verb tenses are often understood from context rather than changing the verb itself, which can make English verb forms challenging. Also, the concept of countable and uncountable nouns is different, leading to errors with articles.	2	You did a great job following the structure for your presentation! You introduced your hero, talked about his early life, his actions, and then concluded with why his story matters. That shows good planning! 👍 However, your presentation was quite short (287 words), and it felt a little bit rushed with many pauses, which made it hard to develop your ideas fully. You tended to use short phrases instead of longer, more detailed sentences. Try to speak more continuously and give more examples or details to explain your points. Imagine you're telling the story to someone who doesn't know anything about Barry Marshall. Practising speaking for a longer time without stopping will help you extend your ideas. Thai communication often relies on implied meaning and shorter utterances, which can make developing extended discourse in English more challenging. The hesitations might also come from thinking about sentence structure or vocabulary.	1	You were generally understandable, and your effort to present in English is clear. The biggest challenge was intelligibility. You tended to put too much stress on the last part of words (like &quot;doc-<i>tor</i>&quot; instead of &quot;<i>doc</i>-tor&quot; 😬), and your voice stayed quite flat throughout the sentences. This makes it difficult for listeners to understand the rhythm and emphasis in English. Specifically, issues with word stress and intonation contributed significantly. For example, in English, stressed syllables are usually longer, louder, and have a higher pitch. Focus on listening to native speakers and copying their word stress and intonation patterns. Try to vary the pitch of your voice more, especially on important words in a sentence. Thai is a syllable-timed language, which means each syllable gets roughly the same amount of time. English, however, is stress-timed, meaning stressed syllables are longer and unstressed syllables are often reduced (like the /ə/ sound in &quot;doctor&quot;). This difference causes many Thai speakers to overstress all syllables or place stress incorrectly. 🇹🇭➡️🇬🇧	\N	\N	7.0	Kun, your presentation about Barry Marshall showed you can organise your ideas and tell a story! 🎉 The main challenge for you right now is to speak more clearly and develop your ideas further. Keep practising, and you'll definitely see your English communication grow. 🌱	A2+	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
29034	2026-01-12	2026-01-12	2026	Book Review	Um, hello everybody, hi, wow, hi, good morning, good morning. My name is Anu, you probably know me already. So, today I'm going to talk about a very interesting and complex novel called A Good Girl's Guide to Murder, written by Holly Jackson.<br>So, let's start off with a hook, shall we? ... ... Imagine this. Your friend was framed for murder, but he couldn't prove his innocence because he committed suicide before he went to court. So, you decide to use your final school project as an excuse to investigate the murder of his victim, Andie Bell, to uncover the truth because you know, you knew he was innocent, but you don't have proof to prove it.<br>So, the stories takes place in a small, small town called Kilton, in the year 2017. The main character is a girl called Pip, Pippa Fitz-Amobi. That's quite hard to pronounce. Basically, she knew, she knew that her friend Sal Singh did not murder Andie Bell five years ago, but she couldn't prove it. So she's using her final school project as an excuse to investigate the murder of Andie Bell. Of course, along the way she discovers clues and Pip partners up with Sal's little brother, Ravi Singh.<br>Of course, something unexpected happens like in every story. Now, the thing that is most disturbing about Andie Bell is that she was a drug dealer. She used to deal drugs at house parties. So, Pippa actually managed to track her supplier, who apparently was a man called Howie Bowers. Now, Howie actually gave very relevant information about Andie. She had a planner and a secret phone. A secret second phone, that is.<br>Now, let's talk about the most exciting part. The most exciting part on the book is obviously the climax, right? Now, in this case, I would have to say climaxes because there's two. Basically, the first one, I'm going to explain the first one very quickly. The killer actually threatens Andie, and I mean Pippa, there's too many characters... ... Pippa. Basically by kidnapping her dog and threatening Pippa that they will kill her dog if she does not burn all the evidence that she has gathered. So she does that, but later she finds her dog dead. And then the second climax is when she finds out who the killer actually is, Mr. Ward, a history teacher, or in this case, a pedophile. Yes, I said it ladies and gentlemen, a pedophile. He was the one sleeping with Andie Bell and who murdered her, and Sal Singh to cover up evidence. Yes, absolutely disgusting.<br>Now then, let's talk about what I like about the book. I don't think everybody will agree with this, but I really like that not all of the suspects are innocent. I mean, even if they are not the killer, they are still not innocent. There's a drug dealer and a rapist for example. But what I disliked about the book is that there are too many characters because it makes my brain go blank every time I read a page with a character I don't recognize because there's too many.<br>Now, to conclude this, I recommend this book to anybody who likes gore violence and is not bothered by mentions of pedophile, gore violence, kidnapping, and murder. And to conclude this, I hope you enjoy this book and at least watch the movie. Thank you very much. [applause]	574	5	Anu, you did a great job with this book review! It is not easy to talk about such a dark and complex story in a second language. You should feel very proud of how clearly you spoke today. <br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used many impressive words like "innocence," "investigate," and "apparently." Your sentences were long and well-structured, especially when you explained the past events. You showed that you can use both simple and more difficult grammar very naturally.<br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>There were only a few small mistakes to notice. Sometimes the subject and the verb did not match, like when you said "the stories takes place" /teɪks/ instead of "the story takes /<b>teɪks</b>/ place" or "stories take /<b>teɪk</b>/ place." You also used the wrong preposition when saying "on the book" /ɒn/ instead of "in /<b>ɪn</b>/ the book." These are tiny slips, but fixing them will make your English even smoother.<br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement</b>: Pay close attention to singular and plural subjects. Remember to add "s" to verbs only when the subject is "he," "she," or "it" in the present tense.</li><li><b>Preposition Practice</b>: We usually say "in a book" or "in a story" rather than "on." Try to read more English books to see how these small words are used.</li><li><b>Expand Your Vocabulary</b>: You already have great words, so try to learn more synonyms for "interesting" or "exciting." This will help you describe your feelings about books in more detail.</li></ul><br>Your English skills are very strong—keep reading and speaking! 🎯	5	Anu, your organization in this talk was excellent and very easy to follow! Sharing a long story can be tricky, but you kept everything in the right order. Your score shows that you have a great handle on how to structure a presentation.<br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You followed the required structure perfectly and gave all the information about the author and title. Your hook was very effective and made the listener want to know more about the mystery. You also used great linking words like "basically," "of course," and "to conclude" to move from one idea to the next.<br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You had a few moments where you hesitated or repeated words while thinking of what to say next. For example, you said "she knew, she knew" and "small, small town." You also mixed up two characters for a moment, but you corrected yourself very well! It is normal to feel a bit nervous during a long talk, but try to keep your flow steady.<br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Use Fillers Naturally</b>: Instead of repeating words, try using phrases like "Let me see" or "How can I explain this?" This sounds more natural than repeating a name or a verb.</li><li><b>Practice the Climax</b>: Your explanation of the two climaxes was good, but it was a bit fast. Try to slow down during the most exciting parts to build more tension for your audience.</li><li><b>Deepen Your Opinions</b>: You mentioned the number of characters made your brain go blank. Try to explain why that was a problem—for example, did it make the mystery harder to solve?</li></ul><br>You are a very talented storyteller with a clear voice! 🎯	4	Thank you for such a detailed and well-structured presentation today, Anu. Your preparation really showed, and you did a fantastic job explaining the book. It was a very impressive performance!<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your individual sounds are very clear and accurate, which is a great foundation for your speaking. Your intonation is also excellent and shows that you have a very strong grasp of English rhythm.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>I noticed that your intonation can sometimes become a bit "sing-songy," which might happen because of nervousness. It is also important to project your voice more, as it was a bit quiet and hard to hear in some parts of the recording.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Project Your Voice:</b> Practice speaking from your chest rather than your throat to give your voice more power and clarity, even when you feel nervous.</li><li><b>Ground Your Intonation:</b> Try to keep your voice a bit more level and steady to avoid the sing-song pattern, which will make you sound more professional and confident.</li><li><b>Breathe and Relax:</b> Take deep breaths before you start speaking to help calm your nerves and keep your volume consistent throughout.</li></ul><br>You have all the right skills, so just focusing on your volume and tone will make you a truly stellar speaker! 🌠	\N	\N	19.0	Anu, this was a fantastic book review! You used an excellent "Imagine" hook that really grabbed my attention, and you followed the structure of the task perfectly. Your vocabulary was very advanced for a B1 student, with words like "framed," "investigate," and "evidence." You also used great linking words to connect your ideas smoothly. To get even better, watch out for small grammar slips like subject-verb agreement and prepositions. Your ability to explain such a complex mystery was very impressive! 🌟	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
