 to know that, uh, and remind of the main message, which is science discover is on time. Uh, thank you, everyone.	403	3	Nene, you used a good range of words to talk about your favourite photo, which made your presentation interesting and easy to follow! You managed to use simple sentences very well, and even tried out some longer, more complex sentences. However, we noticed a few places where your grammar could be even stronger. For example, sometimes you missed small but important words, like when you said "he the first person" instead of "he *was* the first person." Also, when you talk about more than one thing, make sure your nouns agree, like "three main topics" instead of "three main topic," and "many things" instead of "a many thing." Lastly, remember that when you're talking about 'it' in the present tense, the verb often needs an 's' at the end, so "it tells" instead of "it tell." Thai speakers sometimes omit these small words or verb endings because the grammatical structures in Thai can be quite different from English, where these elements are very important for clarity. Keep an eye on these details, and your English will become much more precise! 👀	3	You did a super job of making sure your talk followed a clear plan, Nene! You introduced your topic, described the photo, explained why you chose it, and then discussed the importance of historic photos, which was exactly what we were looking for. 👍 You also used some good linking words to connect your ideas. To get to a 5, try to work on speaking more smoothly. We noticed you hesitated quite a bit and sometimes repeated words or phrases, like "the, uh, the, the photo" or "start, start the, uh, uh, the experiment." Practising speaking continuously about a topic without stopping and starting will really help your ideas flow better and make your talk even more polished. This is something many learners do when they are thinking of the next point, but with practice, you can reduce those pauses! 🗣️	5	Your pronunciation, Nene, was truly excellent! You spoke so clearly that it was easy to understand every word you said. Your intonation (the rise and fall of your voice) was appropriate, and you generally put the stress on the right parts of words and sentences. We also noticed how well you linked words together, which made your speech sound very natural. It's fantastic that even though you were a bit nervous and laughed sometimes, it didn't stop us from understanding you. The only very small point was a slight overstressing of the auxiliary verb "was," which is a minor detail. Overall, your sound production is brilliant! Keep up the great work! 🌟	\N	\N	15.0	Nene presented a monologue about her favourite photograph, "The Man on the Moon," showing strong pronunciation skills despite some nervousness.<br>Her grammar and vocabulary allowed for clear communication, though there were some recurring errors in agreement and sentence structure.<br>Her discourse management demonstrated an ability to structure a talk, but was impacted by noticeable hesitation and repetition.	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
