gh score because your message was very clear to the listener. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used a wide range of vocabulary related to the fantasy genre like "magic kingdom," "main character," and "unexpected." Your description of the "Grimoire" and Asta's journey was easy to follow. You also used a variety of connecting words like "so," "at first," and "anyway" to help the listener track the story. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You had some small slips with your verb forms, such as saying "he don't have" instead of "he doesn't have." There were also a few times when you used the passive voice incorrectly, for example saying "he was grow up" instead of "he grew up." In English, "grow up" is something we do ourselves, so we do not use the word "was" with it. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice third-person 's':</b> Remember that "he," "she," and "it" always need an "s" on the verb in the present tense like "he has" or "he lives."</li><li><b>Active vs Passive:</b> Review when to use "was." Usually, you only need it if someone else did the action to the person, such as "he was born."</li><li><b>Expand your opinion:</b> You mentioned the book relates to your childhood dreams, which is a great detail. Try to explain one specific dream you had so the listener can understand your feelings even better.</li></ul><br>Keep reading your favorite manga because it is helping your English vocabulary grow! 🎯	4	Kris, you followed the structure of the book review task perfectly. Talking about plots and characters can be challenging, especially when a story has many episodes. Your score reflects how well you organized your ideas from the start to the end. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You included every required part of the task, including the author's name and a very exciting "imagine" hook. Your transition from the setting to the main character's problem was smooth and logical. You successfully explained the climax and the resolution, which helped the story feel complete. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>While your ideas were clear, there were several moments where you paused to find the right words. These small breaks in your speech make the rhythm sound a bit less natural. Try to avoid using "yeah" or "you know" as filler words too often during your talk. <br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Reduce filler words:</b> Try to record yourself and count how many times you say "yeah." Practicing without these words will make your speaking sound much smoother.</li><li><b>Bridge the gaps:</b> Use phrases like "another interesting point is" to fill the silence while you think of your next idea.</li><li><b>Build the tension:</b> When you talk about the "biggest demon," try to use more descriptive words to make the climax sound even more exciting for the listener.</li></ul><br>You are a very talented storyteller, so keep sharing your favorite stories with us! 🎯	4	Kris, your pronunciation in this book review was very strong and confident! You reached a solid score of 4 out of 5, showing that you have excellent control over many parts of English speaking. Well done! 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your volume was perfect, and you used syllable stress and word linking very effectively to make your speech flow naturally. This made your talk very easy to follow and pleasant to listen to. You have a good sense of how English words should connect together in sentences.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>The main area to polish is your final consonant sounds, which you sometimes drop. I noticed this happening with the "s" in "introduce" (<b>/ˌɪn.trəˈdjuːs/</b> → /ˌɪn.trəˈdjuː/), "d" in "called" and "world", and "k" in "book" (<b>/bʊk/</b> → /bʊ/). Even though this didn't block comprehension today, fixing it will make your speech even clearer.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Focus on Endings:</b> Practice saying the final "s" in "introduce" (<b>/ˌ