30773	2025-09-03	2025-09-03	2026	My Hero	<p>Hello everyone, I'm Nene. Today I'm going to tell you all about Queen Victoria, the woman who full of passion. Go back to 19th century. As you may know, Queen Victoria, she is a queen in England. This name used to call the era to name Victorian era. And England was called the title of the empire on which the sun never says.<br><br>Now, I will give you an overview of my talk today. First, I will talk who is Queen Victoria. Second, what she did. And the last one, why she is a hero.<br><br>First, I will talk Queen Victoria early life and how she became a queen. In 1819, she was born in Germany, but her family moved to England. A few years later, her father died and she was raised by her mom at Kensington Palace. When her mom know Queen Victoria have to be a queen in the future, so she raised her daughter in Kensington system. She have to learn six to seven hours per day. Whenever she go, she will have a chaperone, follow her step. So it's make her uncomfortable. In 1837, when she 18 years old, she become a queen after the King William IV dead.<br><br>In 1851, she improving social life like condition, health and education for children.<br><br>So, I will talk the work of Queen Victoria. She create campaign name The Great Exhibition in 1851. They show new invention or successful machine during the industrial revolution. And it become a global with over six million visitor in five months. So this exhibition helped people like give people more job and money from the exhibition used to create art and science school. But at first, have some people worry about exhibition because this exhibition... exhibition built in High Park, is a central park in London. So have people worry that exhibition will damage High Park and environment. So she find the way, so she use green house to cover High Park and it didn't damage any environment or environment... or any plant.<br><br>However, not everyone love Queen Victoria. In 1861, is an annus horribilis of Queen Victoria because at first, her mother died, and a few months later, her husband died too, so it's make... make she very sad. And she avoid society for long time. So people strongly disagree with her action because people think she abandon people and she didn't do any work. But I agree with people because she abandoned... she didn't do work for a long time like 13 years. So people think she could not do like this. And have people put sign on in front of Buckingham Palace, the sign write about for rent or sell because owner didn't care anything.<br><br>So, I will tell you why she's a hero because she create the elementary education act in 1870. In 1848, have a lot of slum in London. People don't have house to live. So at night when they sleep, they have to pose and have bed robe between and they sleep on robe. Like this picture. This picture name two penny hangover. You will see people sleep on love and in the morning, have people cut the robe and they will fall down and wake up. So this is worse because it's see people don't have enough money. So, and she see have a lot of children. So this children should go to school. So she create the elementary education act for children to go to school to not be a child labor and it's a pattern for a lot country.<br><br>Okay, when we wrap up all my presentation. Today I talk about Queen Victoria early life, important action, why she is a hero. I will leave a strong message, the woman who full of passion. This is a meaningful and important message because she create the elementary education act. So it's a pattern for Thailand to have primary school. So maybe if we not have her create, maybe we will not have primary school like today. Thank you.</p>	516	2	<p>Nene, you used a good range of vocabulary related to your historical topic, which is excellent! For example, you talked about "industrial revolution," "annus horribilis," and "elementary education act." However, there were frequent grammatical errors, especially with verb tenses and articles, which sometimes made your meaning unclear. For instance, you said "the woman who full of passion" instead of "the woman who *was* full of passion," and "She create campaign" instead of "She *created a* campaign." Also, phrases like "Kensington system" and "people sleep on robe" were a bit difficult to understand. To get a score of 5, focus on checking your verb tenses (past simple is often needed when talking about history!), using articles like 'a' and 'the' correctly, and choosing the most precise vocabulary. Try to think about how different words connect and make sure your sentences are clear and complete. Practise making sure your sentences are grammatically correct when you speak.</p>	3	<p>Nene, you did a fantastic job of producing extended stretches of language, which means you spoke for good periods without stopping. Your presentation was well-organised, following a clear structure with an introduction, body, and conclusion, and you used good linking words like "First," "Second," "However," and "So." You also effectively managed to speak 516 words, which is well within our expected range – great job achieving that! 🎉 While your ideas were mostly relevant, sometimes the clarity of your explanations was affected by grammatical errors, especially when describing complex ideas like the "two penny hangover." To achieve a score of 5, aim for even smoother delivery with fewer hesitations and ensure all your ideas are expressed with crystal-clear language so your audience can follow every detail effortlessly.</p>	1	<p>Nene, your pronunciation needs quite a bit of attention as it significantly affected how clear your message was. For example, you had difficulty distinguishing between words like "error" /eərər/ and "era" /ˈɪərə/, which can confuse your listener. You also mispronounced "Victoria" /vɪkˈtɔːriə/ as "wiktor," which indicates issues with word stress and potentially the /v/ sound. Furthermore, words like "six" /sɪks/ were pronounced as "sik" /sɪk/, and "seven" /ˈsɛvən/ as "sewen," showing you are either dropping final consonants or substituting consonant sounds like /v/ with /w/. Similarly, "old" /əʊld/ was pronounced as "ol," indicating you dropped the final /d/ sound. In Thai, the sounds /l/ and /r/ are often confused, and final consonants are frequently weakened or omitted, which can lead to these types of errors. To improve, focus on clearly articulating final consonants like /s/ and /d/. Practice distinguishing between similar vowel sounds and ensure you are placing stress on the correct syllable in English words. Also, pay special attention to the /v/ sound, ensuring it's distinct from /w/. A great exercise is to listen carefully to native speakers and try to imitate their pronunciation exactly, paying attention to sounds like /s/, /d/, /v/, and the /əʊ/ diphthong.</p>	\N	\N	8.0	<p>Hi Nene! 👋 It's great to see your presentation on Queen Victoria. You did a good job structuring your talk and presenting a lot of interesting information about her life and achievements. You also managed to speak for a good length of time! Keep up the great effort. 👍 To really shine even more brightly, let's focus on making your grammar more accurate and ensuring your pronunciation is super clear so that every word comes across perfectly.</p>	B1	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
