g! 👂 You're on your way to a score of 5, but there are a couple of things you can work on to make your pronunciation even clearer and more natural. You tend to put a little too much stress on the final part of some words, for example, saying 'pictu**re**' instead of 'picture' and 'activi**ty**' instead of 'activity'. Try to listen to how native speakers stress words in sentences. Also, try to make sure you always pronounce the very end of your words, especially sounds like /s/ in 'sometimes', /z/ in 'because', and /k/ in 'think'. Sometimes, in Thai, the final consonant sounds can be very light or even dropped, so focusing on making those English final sounds clear will make a big difference to how easily others understand you.	2	You do a good job of responding to questions and taking your turn in the conversation, Nut, which is really important for good interactive communication! 🗣️ You initiated some responses appropriately, and you kept the conversation going. To improve your score towards a 5, try to explicitly link your comments to what your partner has just said. For example, instead of just starting a new point, you could say, "I agree with Kris about that, and I'd also add..." or "That's an interesting point, Kris, but I think..." This shows you're actively listening and building on the conversation. Developing your partner's ideas more, rather than just giving your own opinion, will make your interaction even richer and more engaging!	11.0	Hello Nut! 👋 It was a real pleasure listening to you speak in your Term 1 Finals! You did a great job of expressing your ideas clearly on familiar topics, and your answers were always relevant to the questions. Keep working on expanding your sentences and connecting your ideas more smoothly, and you'll find your English flowing even more naturally. You've made a fantastic start, and with a bit more practice, you'll be communicating with even greater confidence!<br><br>📝 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   ✅ **Expand your ideas:** When you give an opinion, try to add two or three more sentences explaining *why* you think that, or *how* it makes you feel.<br>*   ✅ **Practise linking words:** Experiment with more words like "however," "therefore," "in addition," or "on the other hand" to connect your sentences and make your speech flow.<br>*   ✅ **Focus on final sounds:** Pay close attention to the ends of your words. Try exaggerating the final /s/, /z/, /t/, and /k/ sounds when you practise, just to make sure they're always clear.	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
