ail about the characters. It was also good to see you using "because" to explain your reasons for liking or disliking certain parts of the book.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>One thing to watch for is mixing up "he" and "she," which is a common slip for Thai speakers because of how Thai pronouns work. For example, you said "he has... jealous stepmother" when you meant "she." Also, try to use the past tense more consistently for stories, such as saying "asked" instead of "ask" /æsk/ → /æs<b>kt</b>/. You also used some extra words like in the phrase "love it book" instead of just saying "love this book."<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li>1. <b>Pronoun Practice:</b> Try recording yourself and listen specifically for when you say "he" or "she" to make sure they match the gender of your character.</li><li>2. <b>Past Tense Verbs:</b> Make a list of common story verbs like "said," "went," and "asked" to practice before you speak.</li><li>3. <b>Word Patterns:</b> Remember that verbs like "love" or "like" usually point directly to the object, so you can just say "I love this story" or "I love it."</li></ul><br>You are building a strong foundation in English grammar! 🎯	3	Mew, you followed the story of Snow White from the beginning all the way to the end very well. Organizing a long talk is a big challenge, but you kept the events in the right order. Your presentation was easy to follow and you reached the conclusion clearly. <br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You used several connecting words like "so," "but," and "then" to link your ideas together smoothly. You included all the main parts of the plot, from the stepmother's jealousy to the magic of the prince's kiss. Your conclusion was clear, and you did a good job inviting your friends to read the book for themselves. <br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You missed two specific requirements for this task: the name of the author and using a specific hook like a rhetorical question or "Imagine." There were also some long pauses where you were searching for words, which made the flow of the story a bit choppy in the middle. Try to avoid repetitive phrases like "and then" too often by using other words like "after that" or "next." These small changes will make your speaking feel more natural and professional.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li>1. <b>Use a Checklist:</b> Before you speak, check that you have the author's name and your chosen hook written down in your notes.</li><li>2. <b>Practice the Hook:</b> Try starting with an "Imagine" hook, like "Imagine sleeping for a hundred years," to grab the audience's attention right away.</li><li>3. <b>Smooth Transitions:</b> Work on saying linking phrases without pausing to help your story flow from one event to the next.</li></ul><br>Your storytelling skills are getting better with every practice! 🎯	3	Mew, thank you for sharing your book review with us! You have a very clear way of telling a story, and with a score of 3 out of 5, you are doing well. Let's look at a few details that will help you reach even higher scores. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>You showed a good understanding of the story's timeline, and your speaking was generally intelligible throughout. Your intonation helped to separate the different characters in your story, which was very helpful for the listener. You clearly put a lot of effort into your preparation.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>A few sounds were a bit intrusive today, such as dropping the final "t" in "white" (<b>/waɪt/</b> → /waɪ/) and "d" in "found" (<b>/faʊnd/</b> → /faʊn/). I also noticed you added an extra sound to "snow," making it "suhnow." Be careful not to overstress the final syllables of words like "character" (<b>/ˈkær.ək.tər/</b> → /kær.ək<b>ˈtɜːr</b>/) or "mother" (<b>/ˈmʌð.ər/</b> → /mʌð<b>ˈɜːr</b>/). Lastly, watch the "r" and "l" difference—you pronounced "mirror" like "miller" (<b>/ˈmɪr.ər/</b> → /ˈmɪl.ər/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Speak Up:</b> Try to increase your volume by about 10% to ensure your voice carries to everyone in the room.</li><li><b>Master Consonant Clusters:</b> Practice saying "snow" (<b>/snəʊ/</b>) as one smooth sound without adding a "suh" at the beginning.</li><li><b>Practice Final Consonants:</b> Say "white" (<b>/waɪt/</b>) and "found" (<b>/faʊnd/</b>) slowly, making sure you can hear the sharp "t" or "d" sound ending the word.</li></ul><br>You have a great foundation, Mew—just focus on these small sounds and you will be even clearer! 🎯	\N	\N	12.0	Mew, you gave a clear and organized review of Snow White! You used some great descriptive words and followed the plot perfectly from start to finish. To improve, remember to check your pronouns so you don't swap "he" and "she" by mistake. It is also important to include the author's name and use a specific opening hook to grab your audience's attention. Focusing on past tense verbs like "asked" and "told" will make your storytelling more accurate. Speaking with fewer pauses will help your story flow even better and keep your listeners interested. You are making good progress with your speaking skills! 🌟	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
