bles are stressed and others are not. Focusing on clearly articulating those final consonants and paying attention to which part of a word is stressed will make a big difference to how clear and natural your English sounds! For instance, try to really feel the /d/ sound in "and" and the /k/ sound in "talk."	\N	\N	11.0	Hello Richter, I'm here to give you some feedback on your presentation about your favourite painting. Overall, you did a good job of organising your ideas clearly and keeping your presentation structured. However, we can definitely work on making your sentences a bit smoother and clearer, and polishing up some of those tricky English sounds. Keep up the great effort, and you'll be speaking with even more confidence in no time! 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br>Here are three concrete steps you can take to improve:<br>• ✅ Practice describing your favourite things (like a painting or a photo) out loud. Focus on using a variety of adjectives and connecting your ideas smoothly with words like "because," "however," and "therefore."<br>• ✅ Record yourself speaking and then listen back to it. Pay special attention to the ends of your words, making sure all the consonant sounds are clear. For example, check if you are saying "what" or "wha," and "bag" or "ba."<br>• ✅ When you learn new words, try to find out which syllable is stressed. You can often do this by listening to how native speakers say the word. For example, say "finally" with the stress on the first part, not the last part.	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
