nd accurate sentences.</p><p>To get a score of 5, aim for more control over complex grammatical forms and ensure your vocabulary is always used precisely. Practicing sentence structure and expanding your vocabulary with synonyms will help a lot! For example, instead of repeating "activity," you could use "event," "campaign," or "movement."</p>	3	<p>You did a good job of producing extended language and keeping your presentation relevant to Yok Thanalop. Your introduction, outline, and sections about Yok's life and actions showed a clear structure. 🎉 You also met the expected word count with 460 words, which is excellent!</p><p>However, there was some hesitation ("uh," "um") and occasional repetition which sometimes interrupted the flow of your ideas. For instance, you used "bad, bad thing" when one "bad" would be sufficient, or "straight... straight action" could be improved to "direct action." Improving your sentence structure and using a wider range of cohesive devices (words and phrases that link ideas, like "furthermore," "consequently," or "in addition") will help your ideas connect even more smoothly and reduce repetition. This will make your presentation sound more natural and confident.</p><p>To achieve a score of 5, try to produce even longer stretches of language with very little hesitation, ensuring your ideas are clearly organized and linked with a variety of cohesive devices.</p>	1	<p>Nam, your pronunciation had some challenges that impacted how clearly you communicated your ideas. I noticed large gaps in intelligibility, meaning it was sometimes difficult to understand what you were saying. 🗣️ This was partly due to very little linking of sounds between words.</p><p>A common pattern for Thai speakers, which was noticeable in your talk, is dropping final consonants. For example, words like "presentation" /prɛzənˈteɪʃən/, "outline" /ˈaʊtlaɪn/, "life" /laɪf/, "interested" /ˈɪntrəstɪd/, "hairstyle" /ˈhɛəstaɪl/, and "called" /kɔːld/ often had their final sounds missing. In English, these final sounds are very important for clarity.</p><p>Another area to focus on is word stress. English has variable stress, unlike Thai. I noticed you sometimes overstressed final syllables, for example, in the word "activity" /ækˈtɪv.ɪ.ti/, the stress should be on the second syllable, not the last.</p><p>To improve your pronunciation, focus on clearly articulating final consonants. You can practice this by exaggerating the final sounds of words. Also, pay attention to English word stress patterns; try listening to native speakers and mimicking their pronunciation. Learning about stress-timed rhythm in English will also help you link sounds more naturally. Keep practicing, and you will see improvement! ✨</p>	\N	\N	9.0	<p>Hello Nam! 👋 Great job on your presentation about Yok Thanalop! It's clear you put thought into choosing a hero who means a lot to you. You shared some really interesting details about her activism and the challenges she faced. Keep up the good work!</p><p>To make your next presentation even stronger, focus on making sure your sentences flow smoothly and that you pronounce all the sounds at the ends of words. Don't worry, with a little practice, you'll see a big improvement! 💪</p>	B1	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
