ike "Suddenly" and "However" to move the story along. You covered the setting, the characters, and your own opinion very effectively, making it easy to identify the different parts of your review.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You have a tendency to restart your sentences or pause for a long time mid-thought (e.g., "Tao... ... Tao took her mission"). These breaks make your speech feel a bit disjointed. You also missed mentioning the author of the manga, which was part of the task requirements.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice Linking Thoughts:</b> Try to think of your whole sentence before you start speaking to reduce the number of mid-sentence pauses.</li><li><b>Complete Your Phrases:</b> Focus on finishing a thought without going back to restart the sentence, even if you make a small mistake.</li><li><b>Use a checklist:</b> Always have a quick list of required points like the author's name to ensure you include everything while you speak.</li></ul><br>You are a natural storyteller with great ideas! 🎯	3	That was a fantastic presentation about your book today, Lookpear. You were very clear and organized in the way you told the story. It was very easy to follow your ideas!<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>I was extremely impressed by how well you pronounced plural sounds, like in the word "secrets" /ˈsiːkrɪts/. You also have excellent intonation that really brings your speaking to life and shows your confidence.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You still have a tendency to drop final consonant sounds, such as in words like "strange" /streɪndʒ/ or "fought" /fɔːt/. I also noticed that some past tense endings, like the /ɪd/ in "unexpected" /ˌʌnɪkˈspektɪd/, were dropped, which can change the meaning of your sentences.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Focus on Past Tense Endings:</b> Pay extra attention to /ɪd/ endings when talking about the past; even if it is just a small /t/ or /d/ sound, it makes a big