ng silent when you think, use phrases like "Let me think" or "How can I say this."</li><li><b>Practice your hook:</b> Rehearse the first few sentences many times so you can start your talk with lots of confidence.</li><li><b>Expand on your reasons:</b> You mentioned the spider demon part was exciting, so you could add one more sentence about how the characters felt during that fight.</li></ul><br>You are a very clear communicator! 🎯	2	Thank you for sharing your book with me today, ThatThat. It was great to hear you describe the story you chose. You did a good job completing the task despite feeling a bit nervous!<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your speech is mostly understandable, and you managed to convey the main points of the story clearly. Your effort to use the English you know to describe the plot was very evident.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your speech currently feels a bit disjointed and "choppy" because of hesitation and a lack of linking between words. This, combined with a flat intonation, can make it more difficult for a listener to follow the flow of your ideas.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice Linking Words:</b> Try to "push" words together rather than speaking one word at a time; for example, practice saying "this is" as if it were one word: /ðɪsɪz/.</li><li><b>Build Fluency with Repetition:</b> Read the same short story aloud several times until you can say it smoothly without many pauses or hesitations.</li><li><b>Add Dynamic Tone:</b> Try to vary the pitch of your voice more so it doesn't sound flat; this will help your listeners stay more engaged with what you are saying.</li></ul><br>Becoming more fluent takes time and practice, so keep going and your confidence will definitely grow! 💪	\N	\N	13.0	ThatThat, you gave a very clear and organized review of Demon Slayer! You covered all the required points, including a good hook and a clear recommendation at the end. Your grammar was strong, especially when