rectly. It's fantastic that you're also trying out more complicated sentence structures to express detailed ideas! 😊 Sometimes, these more complex sentences had a few slips, like saying "people doesn't realize" instead of "people don't realize," or using phrases like "pull off some weight" when "lose weight" would be more natural. Thai learners sometimes make these kinds of grammar mistakes because the sentence structure and how we say things in English can be quite different from Thai, especially with subject-verb agreement and using exact English idioms. To improve, try to consciously check your subject-verb agreement, pay attention to common English idioms, and aim for precise vocabulary choices when explaining your points.	4	You did a super job of speaking for extended periods, Arther, which really showed off your ability to communicate in detail! You also kept your ideas relevant to the topic and used good linking words like "because" and "but" to help your listener follow along. 👍 While you sometimes paused with an "um" or "uh" and repeated a few phrases, this is a very normal part of speaking a second language and didn't stop people from understanding you. Developing fluency means sometimes searching for the right words, and it's a step on the way to speaking even more smoothly. To keep improving, try to consciously reduce those hesitations and repetitions, and focus on making your longer sentences even clearer and more direct.	2	Your intonation was generally good, Arther, and it was easy to understand most of what you said! 👂 One thing to really focus on is pronouncing the final consonant sounds in words. For example, in words like "sports" /spɔːts/, "weights" /weɪts/, and "wake" /weɪk/, you often dropped the final sounds. Also, work on stressing the correct syllable in longer words, like /əˈpɪnjən/ for "opinion" instead of stressing the first part. This is a common area for Thai speakers to work on because Thai language often doesn't have strong final conso