e, such as when you mentioned 'people who like sci-fi.'<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>One area to check is subject-verb agreement in your sentences. For example, you said 'he sell' instead of 'he sold' or 'he sells.' You also used 'people' when you meant just one 'person,' saying 'Tony Stark is the normal people.' This is a common pattern for Thai speakers because the Thai language often uses the same word for both. Also, watch out for past tense endings; you said 'he start build' instead of 'he starte<b>d</b> building' (<b>/ˈstɑːrt.ɪd/</b>).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice subject-verb agreement:</b> Try writing sentences about characters using the present and past tense to make sure the verb matches the subject.</li><li><b>Learn singular vs plural nouns:</b> Remember that 'person' is for one and 'people' is for many. You can practice this by describing photos of individuals versus groups.</li><li><b>Check your verb patterns:</b> After the word 'start,' you can use 'building' or 'to build.' Recording yourself and listening back for these small endings is a great way to improve.</li></ul><br>Your storytelling skills are developing well! 🎯	4	Nine, you did a great job organizing your book review from start to finish. Following a clear structure makes it much easier for your audience to understand your story. Your score reflects how well you connected your ideas today.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your use of a rhetorical question at the start was a very effective hook. You followed the required structure well, moving logically from the setting in Afghanistan to the climax and resolution. You used basic cohesive devices like 'so' and 'but' to keep the story moving forward without stopping.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You missed mentioning the author or creator of the comic, which was one of the task requirements. There were a few moments where you repeated phrases, like saying 'the thing...' several times while 