forehand can also help you feel more confident and speak more smoothly. This is something many Thai learners work on, as English often links ideas together in longer stretches of speech. 🔗	2	You spoke clearly enough for us to understand most of what you said, which is a great start! You tried hard to get your message across. 😊<br>However, you sometimes rushed your words, which meant it was hard to hear every sound. For example, when you said words like "life," "what," or "nine," the sound at the very end sometimes disappeared! Also, with words starting with /k/ and /g/ (like "case"), you sometimes added a little "catch" in your throat just before the sound, which we call <b>glottalization</b>. In English, we usually let the air flow smoothly for these sounds. Another thing to watch out for is putting too much stress on the <i>last</i> part of some words, like "later" or "university," when the main stress is actually earlier in the word. Lastly, don't forget the 's' sound at the end of plural words like "areas"! 🤫<br>To help with this, try to slow down just a little bit. Really focus on pronouncing <i>all</i> the sounds in a word, especially the ones at the end. For those /k/ and /g/ sounds, imagine letting the air out from your mouth without stopping it in your throat first. Listening carefully to how native speakers say these words and trying to copy them can be very helpful. Thai speakers often don't have these final consonant sounds or the same stress patterns, so it takes practice to get them just right in English. 👂<br><br><b>✨ Action plan:</b><br><li><b>Focus on verb tenses:</b> Before you speak, quickly think: "Is this happening now, or did it happen in the past?" Pay close attention to adding "-s" for "he/she/it" in the present, and using the correct past tense form.</li><br><li><b>Practice final consonants and plurals:</b> When you learn new words, always check how they end and how to make them plural. Try exaggerating the final sounds when you practice speaking.</li><br><li><b>Read aloud slowly:</b> Choose an English text and read it aloud, concentrating on clear pronunciation of every sound and natural sentence stress. This will help you get used to the rhythm of English speech.</li>	\N	\N	10.0	Hello Vegas! 👋<br>It was great to hear your presentation about Toon Bodyslam, your hero! You shared some really interesting details about his life and why he's so inspiring. Keep up the good work! Your presentation was clear and well-organised, but working on making your sentences sound more natural will help your ideas come across even better. You're doing a great job, and with a bit more practice, your English will sound even smoother!	B1	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
