 It's mean uh you doing good is hard and may take a long time, but even it's hard, you... you should never get up doing that. Thank you.	392	3	Your presentation was very well organised, Mirin! 👏 You guided your audience through your topic with a clear introduction, covered all the important parts about King Rama IX's life and his heroic actions, and ended with a good conclusion. Using phrases like "First is," "Second is," and "For example" really helped to link your ideas together smoothly. This shows you can manage your talk effectively and keep your audience engaged, even with a little natural hesitation, which is perfectly normal.	4	You're doing a really good job of using simple sentences correctly, and you even tried some more complex ideas, which is fantastic! 👍 Your vocabulary was appropriate and helped explain your ideas clearly. However, one area to watch out for is making sure your verbs match your subjects. For example, you said "some royal project use" when it should be "some royal **projects** use," because "projects" is plural and the verb *use* must agree with it. Also, when talking about "he" or "she," remember to add an '-s' to the verb, like "he **teaches** us" instead of "he teach us." Thai speakers sometimes don't focus as much on these small endings, but in English, they are important for clear communication!	3	You have a lovely, natural way of speaking, Mirin, and your intonation generally sounds just right. 🎵 It was particularly impressive how you managed the /ks/ sound in "six"! However, you sometimes spoke a little quickly, which led to you dropping the final consonant sounds in words. For example, in words like "about" /əˈbaʊt/ (often heard as /əˈbaʊ/) or "path" /pɑːθ/ (often heard as /pɑː/), those final /t/ and /θ/ sounds need to be clear. Also, try not to put too much stress on the very last part of a word, like in "conclusion" /kənˈkluːʒən/ – the stress should be on the second syllable, /kluː/. Many Thai speakers find these final consonant sounds and stress patterns challenging because Thai has a different rhythm and doesn't always have these distinct final sounds. Keep practising them, and your clarity will really improve! ✨	\N	\N	13.0	Hello Mirin! 👋 It was a pleasure listening to your presentation about King Rama IX, your personal hero. You shared many thoughtful details about his life and contributions, and your clear presentation structure made it very easy to follow along. Keep working on those tricky word endings and verb agreements, and you'll be speaking with even more confidence! 🚀<br><br>**Action Plan** 📝<br>*   Practice pronouncing the final sounds of words, especially those tricky consonants!<br>*   Focus on making sure your verbs "agree" with their subjects (e.g., "he teaches" instead of "he teach").<br>*   Record yourself speaking and listen back to identify words where you might be dropping sounds or stressing the wrong part.	B2	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
