cause if you're not interested in the healthy food, you will not buy it. So I think designing the poster about healthy snack is the best way because we all love snacks. So just have some healthy snack to present them is the good way.<br><br>Oh, yes. If they have free food, I think they will eat it.<br><br>This is for the healthy day.<br><br>Maybe organizing a healthy food fair?<br><br>It's fun to walk in the fair and look for some delicious thing.<br><br>Oh, yes, true.<br><br>Yes, I think it's not as much interesting.<br><br>I think it's good to know what where your food come from because you will know you will uh, know the cost of them and you will eat them like knowing how hard they come from. But I think it's not important about knowing what parts of the animal the food comes from because we still eat it because it's just transformed into food we eat.<br><br>Um, I think yes. Young people eat them too often, but I understand because it's fast and it's easy and it's not much expensive for our life now that it's very rush.<br><br>Yes, I think because our food in school is not much healthy. There are many fast and carbohydrate. I think it's good if you if we change some food in our school to be more healthier for the student to buy. But I think it will hard because many student in school don't like the healthy food. If they have many healthy food store, the income will decrease.<br><br>	600	3	Nam, you used a good range of everyday words and were able to talk about familiar topics like food and school projects. That's really helpful for getting your message across! You generally use simple sentences well. However, sometimes your more complex sentences can be a little tricky, like when you said "I don't good in cooking" instead of "I'm not good at cooking," or "activity in the days" instead of "activities during the day." Working on making these longer sentences flow more smoothly will help your English shine. Keep listening to how native speakers phrase things and try to copy them! 😊 Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai, which can lead to these sorts of small grammatical slips.	3	You did well at keeping the conversation going, Nam, and your answers were mostly on topic! You extended your responses beyond just short answers, which is a great step. You also used basic linking words like "because" and "and" to connect your ideas. To improve further, try to make your ideas even more organised and avoid repeating yourself, for example, when you said "you not you not not using the chemical." Thinking about the full arc of your answer before you start can help ensure everything is super clear from beginning to end. 💡 Sometimes, learners might repeat themselves a little when they are thinking, which is a normal part of learning!	2	Nam, your intonation was generally good, which helped with the rhythm of your speech! 👍 However, some individual sounds and word endings were a bit unclear, which sometimes made it difficult to understand certain words. For example, you sometimes dropped sounds like the /tʃ/ in "kitchen," the /t/ at the end of words like "restaurant" and "at" (/æt/), and the /m/ in "home." Also, words like "vegetable" were pronounced with each syllable stressed equally, rather than the natural English way. In English, we often reduce unstressed syllables, and consonant sounds at the end of words are very important for clarity. Thai speakers often omit or soften final consonants due to differences in syllable structure. Practising these tricky sounds will make a big difference! 🗣️	3	You were good at responding to questions and giving your opinion, Nam, which kept the conversation moving along! You also responded to your partner's ideas, like when you talked about the healthy food fair. To get an even higher score, try to really build on your partner's ideas and ask them more questions to encourage a deeper discussion, instead of just agreeing. Imagine you're both building a house of ideas together! 🏗️ This shows