mes, Thai speakers find these rules tricky because the grammar in Thai works a bit differently, so it just takes extra practice to get used to the English way!	1	You did a super job of organising your presentation with a clear introduction, main points, and a conclusion! Your ideas were definitely relevant to the topic of your favourite photograph. To really develop your speaking, try to share your thoughts more naturally, almost like you're having a chat, instead of reading directly from your slides. This will help your talk sound more spontaneous and engaging, and you'll find yourself repeating information less. Also, try to give a bit more detail and expand on your points so your presentation is longer and shows off more of your English. You see, when we read from notes, it can sometimes make our speech sound a little less fluid, and expanding on ideas is a skill that takes time to develop, especially when you're also focusing on all the other parts of speaking English well.	2	Your intonation was generally appropriate, which means your voice went up and down in a way that made you easy to understand – that's excellent! 👍 One area to really focus on is making sure you pronounce the very end of your words clearly. For example, in words like "man" /mæn/ or "style" /staɪl/, sometimes the final /n/ or /l/ sound was dropped. Practising these final consonant sounds will make your speech much clearer. A great way to practise is to record yourself speaking and then listen back to see if you can hear those endings clearly. This is a common challenge for Thai speakers because in Thai, final consonant sounds are often much softer or sometimes not pronounced at all, which is different from English where they are quite important for clarity.	\N	\N	7.0	You shared some interesting ideas, and with a little more practice, you'll be communicating with even greater clarity and confidence. Your overall score for this speaking assessment is 7 out of 20, which puts you at an A1+ level 