em relax and fun. What about you?<br><br>ARTY: Uh, I think technology makes, uh, it's easier to make friends, and it's because technology, you can, like, do lots of things. For example, like playing games, and you can find lots of people there, and also, like, there's a lots of application that you can use to talk with with other people. So, I think technology make, uh, it's make make making friends easier.<br><br>ARTY: Oh, I also think the same as Kaohom. It's easier to tell what they are thinking. You can see their face and interacting to each other.<br><br>ARTY: Okay. So firstly, uh, I think the positive way on online technologies, it's like, um, you can, like, connect to each other easily, and you can do lots of things faster and easier. And next, the negative way on when you are, like, playing on your phone for a lot of time, it's affect your eyesight, and it's make you, like, lazy, wants to plays all day.	527	3	You show a good understanding of how to put sentences together, especially when talking about familiar topics like where you live and your favourite technology. For example, you said, "I am from Nakhon Pathom. And it is a city that's known for its temple. It is very famous." That's excellent! 👍 You also tried to use some more complex sentences, like when you explained why you like mobile phones: "because you can always take it with you and you can do lots of things, like, uh, message your friend or family in the application in your phone." To improve further, try to use a wider range of connectors when joining ideas, such as "although," "however," or "therefore," to make your sentences more varied and sophisticated. You're doing well at using words that fit the topic, and expanding your range of more descriptive adjectives would also be a great step! 📖	2	You manage to speak for extended periods, which is fantastic! However, sometimes your speech was a little slow, and you had some noticeable pauses. For instance, you said, "I think technology makes, uh, it's easier to make friends, and it's because technology, you can, like, do lots of things." This shows you are thinking carefully, but it can make your speech sound less fluent. To help with this, you could try practicing expressing your ideas with a few different linking words to avoid repeating "and" or "it's because" too often. For example, instead of saying "It's because most teenagers love to play games, and their favorite games," you could say, "Because most teenagers love playing their favourite games, which helps them relax and have fun." This will make your speaking flow more smoothly! 🌊 Sometimes, in Thai, it's common to pause for thought, but in English, a steady pace helps the listener follow your ideas more easily.	2	Your speaking is generally clear, and people can understand what you're saying, which is the most important thing! 🙌 You did well at correcting yourself when you noticed you weren't pronouncing plural sounds like /s/, /z/, or /ɪz/ correctly, which shows great awareness. However, sometimes you tended to overstress the final consonant sounds in words, making them sound a bit abrupt, like in 'picture' and 'application'. For example, try to let the final consonants soften a little more. You might also find that working on the rhythm of your sentences helps. Sometimes Thai learners might carry over sentence rhythms from their first language, which can sometimes make English sound a little "staccato." Practising listening to English speakers and imitating their natural flow can really help you sound more natural. 🗣️	3	You did a good job of starting and responding in the conversations! For example, when you asked Kaohom, "What about you?" you showed that you wanted to involve your partner in the discussion. You were also good at giving your opinions and reasons, like when you said, "I think it's playing online games together. It's because most teenagers love to play games." That's a solid way to contribute! 👍 To make your interactions even stronger, try to build more directly on what your partner says. For instance, if Kaohom expresses an idea, you could say, "I agree with Kaohom, and I'd add that..." or "That's an interesting point, but I also think..." This shows you're not just giving your own ideas, but actively engaging with theirs. Keep up the good work in connecting with your partner! 🤝	10.0	Arty, you did a great job today in your speaking test, especially with your clear communication in Part 1! 👍 You kept the conversation going well in the group task and expressed your ideas clearly. To make your English even stronger, let's focus on speaking a little more smoothly and using a wider variety of ways to connect your sentences. You're well on your way to becoming a confident English speaker! ✨<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Practice linking your sentences with different words and phrases to make your speech flow more naturally.<br>✔️ Work on speaking at a steady pace, trying to reduce hesitations by taking a moment to plan your thoughts before speaking.<br>✔️ Experiment with varying your intonation and stress on words to make your speech sound more lively and natural.	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
