etimes tricky to hear the final /d/ in "food," the final /p/ in "people," or the initial /θ/ (the 'th' sound, like in 'thing') in words like "healthy" (which sometimes sounded like 'wealthy' due to the dropped 'th' sound, or the /n/ in "lonely"). Many Thai learners sometimes soften or drop these sounds, which can occasionally make it harder for English speakers to catch every word. Practising these sounds distinctly will make your speech even clearer and more precise! 🗣️	3	Florence, you initiate and respond appropriately in the conversation, and you do a good job of keeping the interaction going without needing much prompting from the examiner or Grace. For example, you readily answered questions about your preferences, like when you said, "Um, for me, I like eating out because like it's easy to do." You maintain the discussion by giving your opinions with reasons. To really step up your interactive communication, try to do more than just agree with your partner. You could challenge an idea gently, ask for more details, or expand on what they've said with your own new thoughts. This will help you develop the conversation more deeply and show an even stronger ability to work with your partner to reach outcomes! 🤝	12.0	Hello Florence! You've shown a good foundation in your English speaking and can communicate your ideas well, especially on topics you know. To really see your communication become even clearer and more flowing, a great next step will be to focus on tidying up some of your sentence structures and making sure all your consonant sounds are clearly heard. You're certainly on the right track! 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Florence:**<br>*   ✔️ When using "there is" or "there are," practice making sure the verb agrees with the noun (e.g., "there are many people," not "there's is a market").<br>*   ✔️ Consciously practice pronouncing the final /d/ sound in words like 'food,' and the final /p/ sound in 'people' to make your words sharper.<br>*   ✔️ Experiment with different linking words like "however," "although," or "therefore" to make your extended responses more varied and connected.	B2	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
