segregated bus in Montgomery. This event launched him into the national spotlight as an advocate for racial equality.<br><br>So, Martin Luther King did many good things and important things, but the most important thing that he did is lead the civil rights movement. So Martin Luther King led the civil rights movement from 1955 up until his assassination in 1968. Uh, using non-violent resistance inspired by Mahatma Gandhi, he organized peaceful protests against racial segregation, which helped raise awareness to the black community... He then went on to receive multiple awards, one of which being the infamous Nobel Peace Prize in 1964 for his leadership. He was only 35 at the time making him the youngest recipient at that time.<br><br>With success, always comes criticisms and problems. He faced many obstacles in his lifetime. Um, the most intense one being imprisoned 30 times for civil disobedience. And you see Martin Luther King did many good things, but people still disagreed with him. Most people in America viewed him as a militant figure especially among white Americans. And even the media, they labeled him as an extremist and labeled his protest as useless.<br><br>And you might ask, why is Martin Luther King a hero? So here's my take. So I think that what Martin Luther King did really made a huge impact on the world. He brought peace and equality into the world and helped the black community because without Martin Luther King, the black community would still most likely face racial segregation and he helped bring peace to them.<br><br>So to summarize, Martin Luther King was a great civil rights leader who led the civil rights movement for many, many years and helped to bring peace into the world, peace and equality. And the message that I want to leave you with is the time is always right to do the right thing. Like I said before, this means that it is never really truly a right time to do the right thing. And one major lesson that we can learn from Martin Luther King is to always stand up for what you believe in. Thank you.	703	3	You've shown a good command of everyday English grammar, and you used a range of good words to explain your points. That's really commendable! 👍 However, to score even higher, we can work on a few specific things. For instance, when you talked about Mahatma Gandhi, you said, 'which was Martin Luther King's biggest influences.' For people, we usually use 'who,' so it would be better to say, 'who was one of Martin Luther King's biggest influences.' 🧐 Also, starting many sentences with 'So' can sometimes make your presentation feel a little less formal and can interrupt the flow. Finally, phrases like 'gave peace to the black community' could be made even stronger with words like 'brought peace to' or 'fostered peace for.' Focusing on these areas will help your language become even more precise and natural. 🎯	4	You did a super job of speaking for an extended period, and your presentation was very well-organised, following the structure we discussed with a clear introduction, details about your hero's life and actions, and a thoughtful conclusion. This shows you can present ideas in a logical way! 🧠 To get to a 5, try to reduce those small hesitations and avoid repeating information, especially in your introduction and conclusion. Planning a wider range of linking words and phrases can also help your ideas connect even more seamlessly and smoothly. 🔗	5	Your pronunciation was excellent, Ana! You were very clear and easy to understand throughout your presentation, which is the most important thing. 👂 Your individual sounds were well-articulated, and your word stress was mostly accurate. The only minor point was that your intonation felt a little flat at times, meaning your voice didn't always go up and down as much as it might in English. This is quite common for Thai speakers because Thai is a syllable-timed language, where each syllable tends to have similar stress, unlike English, which is stress-timed. To really nail that 5, try listening to native English speakers and mimic how their voices rise and fall when they express excitement or make a strong point. 🎶 Practising exaggerating the stress on important words can also make your speech sound more dynamic and engaging, helping you sound even more natural. 🗣️	\N	\N	16.0	Ana: Your presentation about Martin Luther King was compelling and well-structured. Focusing on refining your grammar for even greater accuracy and naturalness will help you express your ideas with even more precision. Keep up the excellent work, and you'll continue to progress wonderfully! ✨	B2+	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
