her, try to reduce some of your hesitations and use a wider range of connecting words to show how your ideas relate to each other, such as "however," "therefore," or "in addition." This will help your conversation flow even more naturally.	2	Kaohom, your English is mostly easy to understand, and your intonation generally sounds natural. However, there are times when some sounds are a bit unclear. For example, you sometimes understress consonant sounds, like the /k/ in "talking" or the /s/ in "faces." This can make it a little difficult for your listener to catch every word. You also sometimes overstress the final parts of words, like in "application." To help with this, try to make sure your consonant sounds are clear and distinct, and listen to how native speakers stress different syllables in words. Practising these sounds will help you speak with even more clarity! 🗣️	3	Kaohom, you participate well in the conversations and respond appropriately to the questions and your partner. You initiated the discussion on voice messages by saying, "Um, I think sending a voice message is useful because..." and agreed with your partner with "Oh, okay. I think so because it's so funny like seeing their face when they're doing..." This shows you can keep the conversation going! 👍 To further develop your interactive skills, try to build on your partner's ideas more often. Instead of just agreeing, you could add more detail or offer a slightly different perspective to extend the discussion. For example, you could say, "I agree with you, and also..." This will help you to show a deeper engagement with the conversation.	11.0	Kaohom, you showed a good understanding of how to communicate your ideas clearly and participate well in the discussions. You were able to respond to questions and interact with your partner effectively. To keep improving, focus on expanding your range of grammatical structures and ensuring your pronunciation of consonant sounds is always clear. Keep up the grea