ar. And what your, what's your opinion?<br><br>I think that's good, but I think many, many of, um... many project is useful to do and and I have a more idea to do a new project that not have in this, this paper.<br><br>Um, in the opinion, we think a school vegetable garden is the most useful, useful to make an student can, um, can, can practice to be a farmer, and they can plant a veg, practice a plant a vegetable by themselves, and they will, they will be fun when they plant and make a food by themselves.<br><br>I think everybody want to know that what the ingredient of in their food come from because they want to know if it clean or it dirty or something, and they can know how it made by.<br><br>Think fast food restaurant is good for someone that need to go to work because it can easy to eat, and it make them full, but sometimes the fast food is have a lot of carrier and have many fat that make people overweight.<br><br>I think they can make a food in a shape and shape like a circle or or a cartoon they like and give it to them, then will think it's a cartoon they like, and they will eat it.<br><br>I think that school can make a more healthy option. They can sell a food that made by an healthy vegetable of fresh, fresh vegetable that good than a fast food in many school.<br><br>I agree with that because the cooking is a basic skill that people should know for live and for them because, um, in the future, some people need to live for themselves and alone, they and they can make a food for themselves.	670	2	Sun, you've got a good grasp of how to use simple sentences to talk about everyday things, like making desserts with your sister or enjoying Thai food. That's a strong foundation! 👍 For example, you said, "I love to make a dessert with my sister every week." That's a clear sentence (but remember "dessert" can be plural if you make more than one!). To take your English to the next level, let's work on making sure all those small but important words are always there, like "a" and "the," and remembering to add the 's' for plurals. For instance, when you said "group of friend" it should be "group of *friends*." Also, try to think about how English expresses feelings – instead of "I never bored it," we would usually say, "I never get bored of it." Sometimes, in Thai, you might not use articles or plural markers in the same way, so it's a common point for learners to focus on in English. Keep an ear out for these little differences!	2	Sun, you're doing well at giving longer answers, which is fantastic! You clearly have lots of ideas to share, like when you talked about enjoying food with friends or family. 💡 However, to make your English even clearer and more engaging, we can focus on making your ideas flow more smoothly without too many pauses or repetitions. For example, when you were describing the pictures, you repeated "useful" and "can" quite a few times. Try to vary your sentence beginnings and use different connecting words beyond "and" or "because." Think about words like "furthermore," "in addition," or "on the other hand" to show how your ideas connect. This will help your listener follow your thoughts more easily and show off your great ideas even more!	2	Your speaking is generally easy to understand, Sun, which is the most important thing! 😊 You have a good rhythm when you speak. A key area for you to work on is making sure all the sounds at the ends of words are clear. For example, you sometimes drop the /k/ sound in words like "think" or the /d/ sound in "friend." Also, pay close attention to the 's' sound at the end of plural words. For instance, "friends" should have a clear /z/ sound. Many Thai words don't have these strong final consonant sounds, so it's a common habit to carry over into English. Practising words with these ending sounds slowly and carefully will make a big difference to how clear and natural your English sounds! 🗣️	3	Sun, you're a really good communicator in a conversation! 🌟 You were great at jumping into discussions and responding to the interviewer and Vegas. You also did a good job of keeping the conversation going and sharing your thoughts, especially when you asked Vegas, "And what your, what's your opinion?" This shows you're thinking about your partner and the task. There was just one small moment where you said "No, no, no. Just by yourself," which was a little misunderstanding of the task, but generally, you maintained the interaction well. Keep practicing linking your ideas even more directly to what your partner has just said; for instance, you could start with "That's an interesting point, and I also think..." This will make your conversations even richer and more collaborative.	9.0	Sun, you've made a really good effort in your speaking assessment! You're clearly able to get your ideas across, and you show a thoughtful approach to topics. To take your English speaking to the next level, a key area where you can really strengthen your English is by focusing on the smaller sounds in your words, especially those at the end, and working on making your sentences flow a little more smoothly. Keep up the enthusiasm for communicating in English, and you'll see your confidence and clarity grow even more! 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan:** Here are three things you can do to really help your English progress:<br>*   ✅ Practice really listening for and saying those tricky 's', 'k', and 'd' sounds at the end of words. Try saying words like "friends" or "think" slowly, making sure every sound is clear.<br>*   ✅ When you're speaking, try to take a moment to connect your ideas using different linking words, like "however" or "although," not just "and" or "because."<br>*   ✅ Read your own sentences aloud to yourself and see if they sound complete and natural. Sometimes just hearing them helps you spot if a little word like "a" or "the" is missing!	B1	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
