u can practice by reading your sentences aloud to check if they sound right. You could also try to expand your vocabulary related to specific topics to avoid using similar-sounding words incorrectly. For example, knowing the difference between "concert" (a musical performance) and "conserve" (to protect something) is important.</p>	3	<p>Grace, you did a good job of speaking for an extended time, which means you kept talking and explaining your ideas. You followed the structure for the presentation very well, from introducing your hero to explaining why he is important. This made your talk easy to follow! You also used some good linking words to move from one idea to the next, like "first one is" and "the second one is" and "the last one is." 👍</p><p>You successfully met the minimum word count of 400 words with 412 words, which is a great achievement and shows you can develop your ideas sufficiently. Well done! 🎉</p><p>Sometimes you hesitated a little, and you repeated some words, for example, "the trees and... and the trees" or "he shot his... he shot... he shot himself." To get a score of 5, try to make your speech smoother by planning what you want to say next in your head, or by using different linking words and phrases. Practicing your presentation multiple times can help reduce hesitation and repetition, making your flow even better!</p>	3	<p>Grace, your intonation was generally appropriate, which means the rise and fall of your voice helped listeners understand your meaning. That's a good strength! However, you would benefit from improving your linking of words and slowing down a bit. Speaking at a slightly slower pace would allow you to emphasize important words and make your speech clearer for your audience.</p><p>A common pattern in Thai English is difficulty with consonant clusters, and this was observed in your speech (e.g., "wants" might be pronounced as "wan"). Thai does not allow many consonant clusters, which can lead to extra vowel sounds being inserted or clusters being simplified. To improve, practice saying words with consonant clusters slowly and clearly, making sure all the sounds are present. For example, for "wants," ensure you pronounce both the /n/ and /ts/ sounds clearly. You can also work on your /l/ and /r/ sounds, as Thai speakers often confuse these; the /l/ sound requires your tongue to touch the roof of your mouth, while for /r/ your tongue curls back slightly. Another area to focus on is the 'th' sounds. English 'th' sounds like in "thing" /θ/ and "this" /ð/ require you to place your tongue between your teeth and blow air, which is a sound not present in Thai. Practicing these sounds will really help make your pronunciation shine! ✨</p>	\N	NULL	12.0	<p>Hi Grace! 👋 You did a great job talking about Sueb Nakhasathien as your hero. Your presentation was well-structured, and you explained why he is so important. Keep practicing your grammar and pronunciation to make your English even clearer. You're doing really well, and with a little more practice, your speaking will be fantastic! 😊</p>	B2	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
