 or "she had a message." <br>Also, be careful with articles – instead of "a one of the best scientists," try "one of the best scientists." <br>Pay attention to verb tenses too, like in the phrase "what did she did," which should be "what did she do?". <br>To improve, focus on mastering basic grammar rules, such as article usage, verb tenses, and prepositions. <br>Expand your vocabulary by reading more about scientific achievements and biographical details. 🤓</p>	1	<p>Your presentation jumps between ideas without clear connections, making it difficult to follow. For instance, the shift from Marie Curie's early life to her achievements feels abrupt. <br>Use cohesive devices like "therefore," "however," and "in addition" to link your thoughts smoothly. <br>The introduction is present but doesn't fully capture the audience's attention, and the conclusion could be stronger by summarizing your main points more effectively. <br>Your word count was below the required minimum of 400 words, limiting the depth and coherence of your presentation. <br>To get a better score, plan your structure carefully, use transition words, and aim to speak for the required duration to fully develop your ideas! 🌟</p>	2	<p>Your intonation is generally good! <br>However, your speech can sound a bit staccato, meaning the sounds are not linked together smoothly. <br>You also drop consonants from words; for example, the /l/ in "popular" and the /z/ in "prize" are often missing. <br>You also dropped the final /ld/ sound in 'world'. <br>Also, remember to pronounce years as "eighteen ninety-one" instead of "one eight nine one." <br>Practice linking words and focusing on pronouncing final consonants clearly. For example, try practicing minimal pairs such as ship /ʃɪp/ and sheep /ʃiːp/ to hear the difference in vowel length. <br>Focus on clear enunciation to improve your pronunciation. 🗣️</p>	\N	\N	7.0	<p>Hey Sun! 😊 You chose Marie Curie as your hero, which is awesome! You have some good ideas, but the 