ur friend start to worry" – remember that for "friend" (singular), we need to say "your friend *starts* to worry." Another example was "they trying" instead of "they *are* trying." Thai learners sometimes miss these small grammatical words because they might not be as prominent in Thai sentence structure. To improve, try to pause sometimes and mentally check your verbs to make sure they match who or what you are talking about. You also have a good range of words, like "exhausted" and "confident," but do make sure you choose the most precise word, such as saying "improving your *health*" instead of "hills."	3	You did a good job of speaking for longer periods, Platwo, especially when you were comparing the pictures and discussing ways to improve health. Your contributions were always relevant to the topic, and it was easy to follow your ideas. For instance, when comparing basketball and yoga, you clearly distinguished between energy use and social connection. 😊 To help your ideas flow even more smoothly, try to use a wider range of linking words and phrases. While you used "but," "because," and "and" effectively, you could also experiment with phrases like "on the other hand," "furthermore," or "in addition" to connect your sentences more elegantly. Sometimes there was a little repetition, but overall, you managed your thoughts well.	2	Platwo, I could understand nearly everything you said, which is a great start! However, I noticed you have a habit of putting a lot of emphasis on the ends of your words, particularly on the last consonant sounds. This can make your speech sound a bit "staccato" – a bit like individual notes played separately, rather than flowing smoothly. For example, when you say words like "picture" /ˈpɪktʃəːr/ or "different" /ˈdɪfərənt/, try to let the sounds flow into each other a bit more, rather than making the final sound really strong. Thai speakers sometimes do this because Thai words don't typically have the same kind of consonant clusters at the end that English does, or the final sounds are pronounced more sharply. To sound more natural, try listening to English speakers and noticing how they often soften or connect the ends of words. Practice saying words and sentences in a more connected way. 🎶	3	You were good at starting conversations and responding when asked, Platwo! You kept the interaction going by sharing your opinions and giving reasons, especially during the discussion about improving health. For example, you asked Nine, "So, Nine, what do you think of way to improving hill in the picture?" which is a good way to involve your partner. 👍 You also gave your own reasons, like when you spoke about how spending time outdoors helps with confidence. While you responded well, try to build more directly on your partner's ideas. For instance, after Nine shares an opinion, you could say something like, "That's an interesting point, Nine, and I agree, but I also think..." This shows you're not just giving your opinion, but you're actively engaging with what your partner has said. This will help you negotiate and develop discussions even more effectively!	11.0	Well done, Platwo! You communicated your ideas clearly in the Term 1 Finals, showing that you can talk about a good range of topics. Your speaking was easy to follow, and you did a fine job of keeping the conversation going. To improve even further, try to focus on making your sentence structures a bit more precise, especially with verbs and pronouns, and work on making your speech flow a little more smoothly. Keep up the great effort, and you'll see even more improvement! 👍<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ **Review basic grammar rules:** Pay special attention to subject-verb agreement (e.g., "your friend *starts* to worry," not "start") and the correct use of auxiliary verbs (e.g., "they *are* trying," not "they trying").<br>✔️ **Practice using linking words and phrases:** Try to use a wider variety of cohesive devices to connect yo