nk your ideas together, which helps people follow your thoughts. For example, when you explained why you liked the computer room, you said, "because it's change the atmosphere of the class to help student be fun with it." That’s a good use of a cohesive device! While you spoke for a good length of time, sometimes there were pauses or slight repetitions that made your speech a little less smooth. To make your speech flow even better, try to expand your range of linking words beyond just "and" and "because." You could try using words like "however," "although," "therefore," or "in addition" to connect your ideas in more varied and sophisticated ways. This will make your speaking sound more polished and organised. 🗣️	2	Ken, it’s great that you are mostly intelligible, meaning people can generally understand what you're saying! However, there's a really noticeable pattern in your speech where you overstress the final parts of words, which can make your English sound a bit "choppy" or staccato. For instance, words like "picture" /ˈpɪktʃəːr/, "talking" /ˈtɔːkɪŋ/, and "computer" /kəmˈpjuːtəːr/ sound like the end is pushed too hard. This is a very common influence from Thai, where word stress patterns are different. You also found it challenging to clearly pronounce the plural /s/ and /z/ sounds at the end of words like "pictures" and "friends." To work on this, try to listen carefully to how native English speakers let the stress fall naturally on certain syllables within words, and how they soften the ending sounds. Practise saying words and sentences while focusing on *not* pushing the final syllable so hard. You can also work on making those final /s/ and /z/ sounds clear by really feeling the air or vibration as you say them! 🌬️	3	You did a good job of getting involved in the conversation, Ken! You initiated topics and responded appropriately to the interviewer and your partner, which is a great skill to have. You also managed to keep the conversation going without needing much help, and you linked your contributions by agreeing with your partner, for example, when you said, "Yeah, I agree with you because today, people don't want to typing..." That’s a clear way to show you were listening and responding! To develop this further, try to do more than just agree. You could try asking follow-up questions to your partner to encourage them to say more, or even politely offer a different point of view to spark more discussion. For instance, you could say, "I agree with that, but what about...?" This will help you to maintain and develop the interaction even more effectively. Keep up the good work engaging with others! 👋	10.0	Ken, it's great to see your enthusiasm for speaking English and how well you participated in the discussion! 🎉 You shared your ideas and opinions clearly, and you're good at keeping the conversation moving. To make your English sound even more natural and fluent, let's concentrate on smoothing out your pronunciation and making your sentences a bit more varied. Keep practising, and you'll notice a big improvement in how easily you can express yourself! 🌟<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Progress:**<br>*   ✔️ Practice "linking" your words when you speak, like saying "an-apple" instead of "an apple" with a pause, to help your speech flow better.<br>*   ✔️ Pay close attention to the small grammar rules, like using "a lot of" instead of "a more," and making sure your verbs match your subjects.<br>*   ✔️ Listen to native English speakers and try to copy their intonation and how they stress words, especially avoiding overstressing the very last part of words.<br>	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
