For instance, instead of just saying "My father is...", you could add, "My father is a kind person, and he works very hard." This helps you share more of your thoughts!<br>• ✅ Listen and Respond to Others: In conversations, try to connect your ideas to what your partner has just said. You could start with phrases like "That's a good idea, and I also think..." or "I agree with you, and another point is..." This shows you’re actively listening and can help the conversation flow more naturally.	A1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
28960	2025-09-02	2025-09-02	2026	My Hero	<p>Hello everyone, my name is Ant. So today I'm going to talk about Lady Mo who is the girl who is helped Nakhon Ratchasima.</p><br><br><p>So let me... let me talk... or let me talk, oh... who is she. Next, what she did. And finally, why she is a hero.</p><br><br><p>Her early life. In 1771, she was born in Nakhon Ratchasima. And... and... and she was married with Deputy Governor of Nakhon Ratchasima too. But when 18... 1826, Chai Anuwong army invade Nakhon Ratchasima and kidnapped Lady Mo and people in Nakhon Ratchasima, but finally, Lady Mo was protect everyone and she help people in Nakhon Ratchasima by giving intelligent plan.</p><br><br><p>So her achieve is she can protect Nakhon Ratchasima, and then when people respect her and she show that she have a strong brave, so she give a... so they give a second name of her that Thao Suranari.</p><br><br><p>But not everyone like her because they... I don't know... uh... like some men or Lao people side. For example, some men told that Lady Mo have to... have to do a work house or take care of men or her her wife, but she... she... I don't know. And and Lao people side, they they thought that Lady Mo is a bad guy because they bomb... bomb their house. Okay.</p><br><br><p>Okay, so why she is a hero? Because she protect the... she protect people in Nakhon Ratchasima and she was the first woman that she have a strong brave... Yeah.</p><br><br><p>Okay, I have left a strong... what's strong... strong message. Uh... the girl who is helped Nakhon Ratchasima. Thank you.</p>	276	1	<p>Ant, your presentation shows that you understand the task, but there are some areas in grammar and vocabulary that need attention. For example, you said "who is helped Nakhon Ratchasima" instead of "who helped Nakhon Ratchasima." Also, phrases like "strong brave" could be improved to "bravery" or "strong and brave." To get a score of 5, you need to show more control over simple grammatical forms, like correct verb tenses and sentence structure. Try to use a wider range of vocabulary and practice forming more complex sentences accurately. For instance, instead of "she was protect everyone," you could say, "she protected everyone." Focusing on accurate sentence construction will make your message much clearer!</p>	1	<p>One of the biggest areas for improvement here is the length of your talk, Ant. You produced 276 words, which is significantly below the minimum of 400 words expected for this assessment. This has had a noticeable effect on your score. While you attempted to follow the presentation structure, your ideas were not always fully developed. For example, in the "Hero's early life" section, you mentioned Lady Mo's birth and marriage but didn't extend on events that shaped her life or early influences. To achieve a score of 5, you need to produce longer stretches of language with fewer hesitations and ensure your contributions are well-organized and relevant, without too much repetition. Try to elaborate on each point of your outline with more detail and examples. For instance, when talking about what Lady Mo did, you could give more specific examples of her "intelligent plan."</p>	2	<p>Your pronunciation is mostly clear and understandable, which is good! However, there are a couple of points to work on. You tend to overstress final syllables, like in "goING," "finaLLY," and "heRO." This is a common pattern for Thai speakers because English has different stress patterns than Thai. Try to listen carefully to how native speakers stress words and sentences. Also, your speech has a slightly 'staccato' effect due to minimal linking between words. In English, we often link words together, for example, "what she did" can flow more smoothly like /wɒtʃi dɪd/. To improve, focus on sentence stress and intonation patterns. Practicing linking words and reducing the stress on unstressed syllables can make your speech sound more natural and fluent. For example, try to reduce vowels in unstressed syllables to a schwa /ə/ sound.</p>	\N	\N	5.0	<p>Hi Ant! 👋 It's great to see your effort in talking about Lady Mo as your hero. You did a good job of introducing your hero and outlining your talk, which is a great start! However, to really shine in your presentations, you'll need to work on expanding your ideas and making sure your sentences are grammatically correct. Keep practicing, and you'll definitely improve! ✨</p>	A2+	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
29623	2025-09-23	2025-09-23	2026	Term 1 Finals	SIRI: And my name is Siri. Siriwanrat Sawangyen and you can call my nickname is Siri.<br><br>SIRI: Yeah, I study English at school with teacher Ed. I study in Assumption College Thonburi. Uh, you can call ACT.<br><br>SIRI: I think it should be useful because English is, um, the main, uh, uh, is this, is this one of, that's language that is have many people will speak it. That's language around the world more than China. So, I think when I grow up in, in my job or in the future, I think I need to use English.<br><br>SIRI: In my free time, I'm just sleep because because I think it's have have to, uh, it's like I need to sometime I need to relax, uh, take a break. But because I like to watch TV at some time, and I need to rest my eye.<br><br>SIRI: I, I see, uh, man and a woman hug each other and at the back, I think is, is she mom's. She hold the presents. Maybe it's her, girl who wearing the dress, white, white, white dress. And, uh, at the back, it's also has a white car. I think the boy buy it and it's new. I think they are celebrate, um, their wedding. ... Uh, at the street. And people are...<br><br>SIRI: I think you can go to China because it's have, um, many culture in there because this is like a, it's have, this is a big country and...<br><br>SIRI: Oh, this picture? Okay. Um...<br><br>SIRI: Uh, I think the bus is sightseeing tour. Talk together? I think the bus is sightseeing tour because, um, you know, if I go there, I can go, uh, at the place of them because...<br><br>SIRI: Um... The special occasion I enjoyed, I think I go to Dream World because I think it's so much fun. It's have a, it's not a real snow, but it's also very cold there. It's minus four, I think. And I also remember that day I cried because I, I can't find my parents. And Dream World also have a like, uh, have... rollers...<br><br>SIRI: Um, two years ago, I went to, um, my aunt's friend birthday, uh, wedding. Um, in Kanchanaburi. My aunt brought there. And it's, it's in the garden, but, uh, many flowers. I take a pic, I take a some of picture of them.<br><br>	400	1	Siri, you use a good range of everyday words when talking about familiar things like your hobbies and family, which is fantastic! You clearly know how to talk about things you enjoy, like "delicious food" at the "water park." However, we noticed a few places where some small grammar adjustments could make your message even clearer. For example, instead of saying "I learned English every week, like three ones," we could say, "I learned English three times a week." Also, when talking about something for a purpose, we often use 'to' with the base verb, so "for communicate" would become "to communicate with other people." Another common point to remember is that when you're talking about a singular thing, like "his family," the verb that follows needs an 's' at the end – so "his family that give to him" would be "his family who gives to him." Focusing on these small but important grammar points will help you build stronger sentences and express yourself with even greater precision.	2	You do a good job of moving beyond just short answers, Siri, and your contributions are always on the topic, which is excellent! You use basic connecting words like "because" and "and" to link your ideas, which helps your listener follow along. However, sometimes you pause or hesitate a little bit, like "Um..." or "... Uh...," especially when you're telling a longer story or describing a picture. If you can practice speaking for slightly longer without these pauses, perhaps by thinking a sentence ahead, your speech will flow even more smoothly. For instance, when you were describing the photograph, trying to link your sentences more directly could make your description feel more complete and detailed. You could also try to introduce a wider variety of linking words, like "firstly," "next," or "in addition," to help organise your thoughts even better.	4	Siri, you are generally easy to understand, which is the most important thing! Your voice goes up and down in a way that sounds natural, and most of your individual sounds are clear. However, one area to practise is making sure you really emphasise the final sounds of your words. Sometimes, the end of a word might not be as clear as it could be, which is a common feature in Thai speech where final consonants can be unreleased or silent. For example, make sure we can clearly hear the /t/ sound in "present" /ˈprezənt/, the /d/ in "liked" /laɪkt/, or the /s/ in "slides" /slaɪdz/. Working on this will help all your words sound even more distinct and make your overall speech flow with greater clarity. Keep listening carefully to English speakers and try to copy how they finish their words!👂	3	You did well initiating conversations and responding to questions, Siri, and you kept the conversation going with your partner. For example, when your partner suggested the bus tour, you agreed and added your own reason, saying, "I think it too because, uh, the guide can tell us the information..." This shows you can support an interaction. To develop this even further, try to build on your partner's ideas more directly. Instead of just agreeing, you could say something like, "Yes, I agree with that, and also, we could..." or ask your partner for more details, like "That's a good point, what do you think about...?" Sometimes, in Thai culture, it's polite to agree, but in English speaking exams, developing the conversation by adding new ideas or even politely disagreeing can show a wider range of communication skills. Try to make those links between your comments and your partner's even stronger to create a more dynamic chat! 💬	10.0	Hi Siri, it was lovely to hear you speak English! You shared some interesting ideas and were able to communicate quite well during our chat. ✨ To help your English speaking become even clearer and more confident, let's focus a little more on making sure our grammar is super accurate, especially with those sentence endings and verb choices. Keep up the great effort, as every bit of practice makes a difference! 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Speaking:**<br>✅ **Grammar Check-Up:** When you practice speaking, try to mentally (or even physically!) check your sentences for common grammar points like making sure your verbs agree with "he/she/it" (e.g., "he likes," "it has") and using the correct form of a verb after words like "for" or "to." For instance, instead of "for communicate," think "for communicating" or "to communicate."<br>✅ **Extend Your Ideas:** Challenge yourself to add one or two more sentences to your answers, even if they're simple. This will help you produce longer stretches of language and feel more comfortable expanding on your thoughts.<br>✅ **Final Consonant Focus:** Practice really sounding out the ends of your words, especially those consonants like /t/, /d/, /s/, and /k/. You could try exaggerating them a little at first to build the muscle memory!	A2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
