plicated sentence structures, which is fantastic! Sometimes, you try out these harder sentences, but they don't quite come out perfectly. For instance, you said, "I always drawing" when it should be "I am always drawing," and "I do, I do a, a drawing that that a very great and give it, draw a eyes and give it to teacher" could be "I did a drawing that was very good and gave it to the teacher." This is a common step in learning, and it shows you are brave enough to try new things! To keep improving, try to check that your verbs match the timing of your sentences (like past tense for past actions). Many Thai learners sometimes miss small words like 'am' or 'is' in sentences, so keep an ear out for those!	3	You manage to speak for a good amount of time, Mathew, which is a great start! Your answers are always relevant to the questions, which is super. 😊 However, sometimes you pause a little, and you repeat some of your ideas, particularly when you were talking about what to do for the party. For instance, you used "we can bring them to..." several times. To make your speech flow even better, try to use different connecting words and phrases beyond "because" and "so." Imagine you’re building a bridge between your ideas – you can use words like "also," "however," and "and then" to make the path smoother. This will help you keep your ideas organised and avoid repeating yourself too much.	3	Mathew, good job! I could understand nearly everything you said, which is fantastic. Your biggest problem is that you have a tendency for flat intonation, which means your voice doesn't go up and down as much as it could. This is something that Thai speakers often do when they speak English because the tones in Thai are different from the intonation patterns in English. You also tend to drop a lot of consonant sounds, especially at the ends of words, which can sometimes make your speech a little less clear. For example, you might pronounce "friends" as /frɛn/ or "last" as /lɑːs/. 🗣️ If you can focus on making your voice more expressive and clearly pronouncing all your consonant sounds, your speech will sound even more natural and easier to follow!	2	You’re good at starting conversations and answering questions, Mathew! 🚀 When the examiner or Pun spoke, you responded appropriately. However, during the collaborative task, you found it a bit difficult to really build on Pun's ideas without some support. You tried to jump in with "I'm sorry to interrupt. We need to..." which was a good effort to engage, but then you went back to listing things rather than discussing them together. To make your interactive communication even better, try to ask your partner questions about their ideas, or say things like, "That's a good idea, and also, we could..." This helps to keep the conversation flowing back and forth and shows you are really working with your partner to develop the discussion.	11.0	Hello Mathew! 👋 It was a real pleasure to listen to you speak. You communicate well, and it's clear you have lots of interesting things to say. Keep up the good work! Your confidence is a real strength, but focusing on how you connect your ideas and use a wider variety of words will help you improve even further. You’re already doing well, and with a bit of practice, you’ll be communicating even more smoothly.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Speaking Practice:**<br>*   ✔️ When you’re practicing speaking, try to link your sentences together using different words like "however," "therefore," or "in addition" to make your ideas flow more smoothly.<br>*   ✔️ After you say something, challenge yourself to add one more different idea or explanation to make your answers a bit longer and more detailed.<br>*   ✔️ Pay attention to the endings of your words when you speak, especially 's' and 't' sounds, to make sure every word is clear!	A2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N
