n black people, make them black people wanted to live anymore for them. Yeah.<br><br>Now, on the famous words in what did she tell you. The only thing tired I was, was tired of giving in. Yeah, thank you.	401	1	Arther, you used some good words to describe Rosa Parks, and you got your main message across! 👍 To improve, try to pay close attention to how you use past tense verbs. For example, when you use "did," the next verb should be its base form, like "What did she <i>do</i>?" not "What did she <i>did</i>?" This is a common point to practise, as in Thai, you don't always change the verb in the same way. Also, when you talk about "she," remember to add an '-s' to verbs in the present tense (e.g., "she <i>attends</i>") or use the correct past tense form (e.g., "she <i>attended</i>," or "she <i>was awarded</i>"). These small changes will make your sentences sound much more natural and correct! Keep practising how verbs change in English, especially with "did" and when you're talking about one person.	1	You did well to organise your presentation with an introduction and sections about Rosa Parks' life and actions, which is a good structure! 👏 Sometimes you paused quite a bit or repeated words, like "refuse refuse" or "she she." This can make it a little harder for your listener to follow along smoothly. To get to a 5, try to link your ideas more clearly and use different words to avoid repetition. Planning out some connecting words before you speak can really help your presentation flow more smoothly, just like building a bridge between your ideas.	2	Arther, you were generally understandable, which is the most important thing! 🙌 However, I noticed a couple of things that made it a bit tricky to follow sometimes. You often dropped the /s/ sound at the end of words, for example, saying "Rosa Park" instead of "Rosa Park<b>s</b>" or "refuse" instead of "refuse<b>d</b>." This is a common pattern for Thai speakers because final consonants in English often get dropped or weakened in Thai. Also, the /v/ sound sometimes became a /w/ sound, so "very" sounded like /wɛri/. This is also a typical pattern because the /v/ sound (where your top teeth touch your bottom lip) isn't present in Thai. To score a 5, you need to make sure all your individual sounds are clear and that your word endings are pronounced. Practising these specific sounds will make a big difference!<br><br>🗣️ <b>Action plan:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practise verb forms after "did"</b>: Always use the base form of the verb after "did" (e.g., "did she <i>go</i>?", "did she <i>eat</i>?").</li><li><b>Focus on final /s/ and /d/ sounds</b>: Pay special attention to pronouncing the 's' at the end of plural nouns (e.g., "Parks," "boys") and the 'ed' sound in past tense verbs (e.g., "refused," "walked").</li><li><b>Distinguish /v/ from /w/</b>: Practise making the /v/ sound by gently biting your bottom lip with your top teeth and letting air out, contrasting it with the /w/ sound where your lips are rounded.</li></ul>	\N	\N	5.0	Hello Arther! It was great listening to your presentation about Rosa Parks. You picked a really important historical figure and managed to talk about her for a good amount of time, which is a fantastic start! 😊 Your enthusiasm for the topic came through, and it's clear you tried your best to share her story. To help you get even better, let's look at a few areas where you can focus your practice.	A2	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
