llow and understand, which is a fantastic speaking skill! ✨	4	You are very easy to understand, Kati, and you pronounce most individual sounds clearly! That's excellent. 😊 To make your speaking even more natural and impactful, let's work on two key areas. Firstly, try to make your voice a bit louder and more expressive; sometimes your intonation (the rise and fall of your voice) was a little flat, which can make it sound like you're reciting rather than really sharing. Think about emphasising important words more! Secondly, pay attention to pronouncing the final /s/ sound in words. For example, when you said "photos" or "friends," the final /s/ sound was sometimes dropped or very quiet, sounding more like "photo" or "friend". This is a common pattern for Thai speakers because Thai often omits or weakens final consonants. Practicing words like "picture**s**" /pɪktʃəz/ and "friend**s**" /frendz/ with a clear final /s/ sound will make a big difference! 🗣️	\N	\N	16.0	Your presentation about your favourite photograph was a real delight to listen to! You did such a wonderful job of sharing a personal story and making it interesting for your audience. Keep working on those trickier grammar points and making your voice heard, and you'll be speaking with even more clarity and confidence. You shared your story beautifully, and it was lovely to hear about your friendships! 📸💖<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   ✔️ Practice making sure your verbs agree with "it" and "he/she/it" (third person singular), and remember to use the correct forms of verbs after "have" (like "I have *talked*" instead of "I have *talk*").<br>*   ✔️ Focus on making your voice a bit louder and varying the sound of your voice more, especially when you want to make an important point.<br>*   ✔️ Pay close attention to pronouncing the final /s/ sound at the end of words like 'friends' and 'photos'.	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
