an, there's you need to do the prop, the backgrounds and there's like people who's actors and who are not. Yeah, I think that will work a lot more, you know.\n\nYeah, I I agree with you. But I think computer science might be the least popular.\n\nNot a lot of people like to do coding. That's just fun.\n\nUh, yes, I think it's important to have a hobby since it not only that does it kill the time, it I feel like if you pick a good hobby, it would develop into a great skill that you can, uh, maybe turn it into a job. It would be better than not doing anything at all.\n\nUh, I agree with you. I think that doing it by yourself is better since I feel like if you, uh, do hobbies with someone else, it wouldn't really be considered like, you know, uh, friendly. I feel like it's more like a little bit of a competition, whether who's better and who's worse.\n\nI think I'd really like to start taking photography lessons since I really like, uh, art and I think that art requires a lot of knowledge about composition and I like to focus on that more and I think photography helps that a lot considering their their whole thing is basically just taking pictures with good composition.	612	5	Racing, your vocabulary is quite impressive. The range of words you used, like "composition" and "intellectually," made your descriptions very vivid and detailed. It shows you're comfortable expressing complex ideas. To push yourself further, try to focus on using more accurate grammatical structures when you're speaking quickly. For example, instead of saying "Not a lot of people like to do coding. That's just fun," it would be better to say "Not a lot of people like to do coding. It's just not fun."	4	Racing, you did a wonderful job organizing your thoughts and expressing them clearly. You spoke at length about the picture of the chess players, describing their expressions and the overall scene in detail. The listener can easily follow your descriptions. To improve further, focus on reducing filler words like "uh" and "um" by pausing to think before you speak. For instance, instead of saying "Uh, in the picture, it seems to...", try pausing briefly, then starting with "In the picture, it seems that...".	4	Racing, your intonation and syllable stress are excellent, which makes you very easy to understand! I noticed that you sometimes drop the final plosive consonants in words like 'pick', 'white', and 'opponent'. For example, 'pick' might sound like /pɪ/ instead of /pɪk/, and 'white' might sound like /waɪ/ instead of /waɪt/. Focusing on making those final 'k', 't', and 'nt' sounds crisp will make your already great speech even more precise. Keep up the wonderful work!	5	Racing, you're great at keeping the conversation flowing! For example, you responded to Rome's ideas in the collaborative task with phrases like "I get what you mean," which really helped to build on each other's thoughts. This makes the conversation feel natural and engaging for everyone. To make it even better, try to ask more follow-up questions to show deeper interest. For instance, after Rome said he wanted to learn chess, you could have asked, "Oh, that's cool! What specifically interests you about chess?"	18.0	Racing, you demonstrated strong B1 skills and even showed some B2 potential in this speaking test! Your vocabulary and ability to express complex ideas are particularly impressive. Focus on refining your grammar and reducing filler words to reach the next level. You communicate clearly and engage well with your partner. Keep practicing, and you'll continue to improve! All the best, Ed	B2	Speaking	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N
