s/, and /z/. Thai speakers sometimes drop these sounds, which can make it hard for listeners to catch every word.<br>*   🎤 **Record yourself:** Use your phone to record your presentations. Listen back to them and notice where you can slow down, articulate more clearly, and add more expression to your voice.	A2	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
34931	2025-09-07	2025-09-07	2026	My Favourite Photograph	Good afternoon, everyone. My name is Frame, and today I'm going to tell you all about my photo. Which as you can see here, is 911. A plane driving to the towers, then boom. So, by the end of this presentation, I want you to understand the main message of my photo, which is disaster can happen at any time, so we must stay united.<br><br>So now I'm going to tell you three things. The first, I'm going to describe about my photo. Next, I will explain why is it important. And finally, I'm, I'm going to describe about why is history called photos are important.<br><br>So now I'm going to tell you all about the photo. The photo was taken on September 11, 2001. It shows the ordinary people in New York City. The airplane used by terrorists. And it captured the tragic moment when a plane crashed into the twin towers of the World Trade Center in Pentagon. The attack was carried out by terrorists who hijack passenger airplane to spread fear and make a political statement.<br><br>Moving on to this part, I'm going to tell you why I chose this photo. So first, it captures the moment that changed the world. Because 911 affected not only the United States, but also the whole world. Second, it's a very sad photo because many innocent people lost their lives and others could only hug each other for comfort. Third, life is full of danger because accidents or disasters can happen anytime, just like a car crash on the street.<br><br>So now we have reached the end of my presentation. To finish, I'm going to tell you why historic photos are important. So first, it affects our life, because it shows a, it captured some moment that changed people's lives. Next, they shape our world, because many photos are captured a moment that influenced history and society. And finally, we can learn from past by looking at this photo and remembering what happened, so we can, so we can make a better choices in the future.<br><br>So in conclusion, I have talk about a photo. I explained why is it important. And I explained why historic call photos are important. So I hope you guys remember my main message. That is, disaster can happen at any time, so we must stay united. Thank you.	389	3	You've got a good range of words to talk about important topics, Frame, which is really impressive! You chose words like "tragic moment," "terrorists," and "innocent people" that helped describe your photo effectively. However, sometimes there were small slips in grammar that can make your sentences a little less clear. For instance, you said, "Next, I will explain why is it important" – in English, when you have a question inside a statement, the word order changes to "why it is important." Another example was "I have talk about a photo"; the correct form here would be "I have talked." Also, when you mentioned "a better choices," because "choices" is plural, we'd say "better choices" without "a." Thai learners sometimes make these kinds of mistakes because the rules for sentence structure and verb forms can be quite different from Thai, especially when it comes to tenses and how questions are formed within sentences. Keep practicing these structures, and your English will become even more precise! 🌟	5	Frame, you did a brilliant job here! 👏 Your presentation was incredibly well-organised and easy to follow. You started with a clear introduction, moved logically through your detailed descriptions, explanations of your choice, and the importance of historic photos, and then rounded it off with a strong conclusion that reminded us of your main message. You used excellent linking words like "The first," "Next," "Moving on to this part," and "To finish" which helped your talk flow beautifully. You produced extended parts of your talk and everything you said was relevant to your topic. This is a real strength in your speaking! 😊	3	You were mostly easy to understand, Frame, and your intonation generally sounded good! 👍 However, one area where you could improve is how you link words together. Sometimes your speech felt a bit "staccato," meaning each word was pronounced separately rather than flowing smoothly into the next. For example, the auxiliary verb "was" should often be pronounced with a soft /ə/ sound, like /wəz/, but it sounded a bit overstressed, making it harder for your speech to sound natural. Also, sometimes the endings of words were dropped, like final consonants, which can make it a little tricky for listeners to catch every word. For example, "captured" /kæptʃəd/ or "changed" /tʃeɪndʒd/. Thai speakers often have these issues because Thai doesn't always have strong final consonants, and it's a syllable-timed language where each syllable tends to get equal stress, unlike English, which stresses certain words more than others and often reduces unstressed vowels to the /ə/ sound. Practising linking words and making sure those word endings are clear will make a big difference to your fluency and intelligibility! 🗣️	\N	\N	15.0	Hi Frame! 👋 Your presentation on the 9/11 photograph was really well-organised and your message about unity in the face of disaster was very powerful. You clearly put a lot of thought into structuring your ideas, and that came through wonderfully! To really take your English to the next level, focusing on getting those tricky grammar points just right will make your message even clearer and more impactful. Keep up the fantastic work; your ability to connect with your audience is a real strength! ✨<br>📋 **Action Plan**<br>Here are three things you can focus on to help your English speaking improve even more:<br>•   ✅ When you're asking an indirect question, like "I will explain why it is important," try to remember the order of the words should be like a statement (subject then verb) rather than a direct question.<br>•   ✅ Practice pronouncing word endings, especially /s/, /ed/, and /t/ sounds, for example, "crashed" /kræʃt/ and "affected" /əˈfektɪd/.<br>•   ✅ Read a short paragraph aloud and try to link words together smoothly, reducing the stress on small words like "was" /wəz/ so your speech flows more naturally. <br>Your presentation on the 9/11 photograph was really well-organised and your message about unity in the face of disaster was very powerful. You clearly put a lot of thought into structuring your ideas, and that came through wonderfully! To really take your English to the next level, focusing on getting those tricky grammar points just right will make your message even clearer and more impactful. Keep up the fantastic work; your ability to connect with your audience is a real strength! ✨	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	\N	\N
