g to the slides.	B1+	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
34080	2025-09-02	2025-09-02	2026	My Hero	Hello everyone, I'm ThatThat and today I will tell you all about Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng.<br><br><br>As you may know, Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng is a king of Sukhothai that help people in many ways. So next, I will give you an overview of my presentation today. So first, I will tell you who Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng is, and next, I will discuss what he did, and finally I am talking about why he is a hero.<br><br><br>Let's me start by discussing Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng early lives. Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng born in Sukhothai in 1239 and his father name is Pho Khun Sri Intharthit and his mother name is Nang Sueng. He is the youngest children in his family. He have one brother and one sister. In 1279, he become a the third king of Sukhothai and he want to find a way to give people in Sukhothai have a better live life. So, he see people in Sukhothai is saying two language which is Khom and Mon language. For example, in northeast of Sukhothai, people saying Khom language, but in the western of Sukhothai, people saying Mon language. So, he want to fit it all or do it better than now.<br><br><br>Next, I will talking about what Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng did and why we call him a hero. And the most important, he created the first Thai letter which is calling Lai Sue Thai and and it make people have their own language to speak and it's helped to easier to understand each other when they talking or when they trading something.<br><br><br>And now I want to point out that even hero got criticized a lot for many month in Sukhothai think he cannot do it because people in Sukhothai, they was saying Mon and Khom language when they was born until now and they think it's hard to study a new language that Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng make. So, but however, Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng make the language and it easy to understand and all people can speak it.<br><br><br>And now... I want to tell you why Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng is a real hero, not just a king, because he create the first Thai letter that give many Thai people can speak and he changed the language in Thailand. From in the past, there are two language in Sukhothai. There are Mon and Khom language, but he can change in one language that is Thai language.<br><br><br>Okay, it's time to conclude. Today I have told you all about Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng. I explain who Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng is, what he did, and why he is a hero. So, and the importantly, I have left you the message with is the man... the man who changed Thai language and this message is very important and really for Thai children our age because it tell us to know who create Thai letter and if we don't have Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng, we will don't have Thai letter today.<br><br><br>Thank you.	403	2	<p>ThatThat, you used a good range of vocabulary to talk about your hero, which is a strong point. However, to achieve a higher score, focus on improving your control over basic grammatical forms and expanding your vocabulary use.</p><br><p>Here are some examples from your transcript to help you understand:<ul><li>Instead of "Let's me start," you could say "Let me start..." or "I'd like to start..."</li><li>When talking about the past, make sure to use past tense verbs. For example, "Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng <b>born</b> in Sukhothai" should be "Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng <b>was born</b> in Sukhothai." Similarly, "he <b>become</b> a the third king" should be "he <b>became</b> the third king."</li><li>Pay attention to subject-verb agreement, especially with "have" and "has." You said, "He <b>have</b> one brother," but it should be "He <b>has</b> one brother."</li><li>Try to use more precise language. For instance, instead of "people <b>saying</b> two language," it's more natural to say "people <b>speaking</b> two languages."</li><li>Some of your sentences became a bit unclear due to grammar, like "which is calling Lai Sue Thai and and it make people have their own language to speak and it's helped to easier to understand." A clearer way could be: "He created the first Thai script, called Lai Sue Thai, which gave people their own language to speak and made it easier for them to understand each other."</li><li>Work on your conditional sentences. You said, "if we don't have Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng, we will don't have Thai letter today." A more accurate way to express this would be: "If we <b>didn't</b> have Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng, we <b>wouldn't</b> have Thai letters today."</li></ul>Practicing these basic grammar points will make your ideas much clearer and help you reach a score of 5!</p>	3	<p>You did a good job of producing extended parts of your presentation, and your contributions were always relevant to the topic of Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng as your hero. You also successfully met the word count expectation, producing 403 words! 🎉 This shows you can develop your ideas well.</p><br><p>You used some basic cohesive devices like "So next," "So first," "and next," and "finally" to guide your audience through your talk. To improve and aim for a score of 5, try to vary your cohesive devices more. Instead of repeating similar phrases, you could use transition words like "Furthermore," "In addition," "Moreover," "However," or "Therefore" to connect your ideas more smoothly and show clearer logical relationships between them. For example, you said, "And now I want to point out..." and then later, "And now... I want to tell you..." Using different phrases here could make your discourse more sophisticated.</p>	1	<p>ThatThat, you have some areas in pronunciation that, with practice, will significantly improve your intelligibility and make your speech sound more natural.</p><br><ul><li><b>Confusing /w/ and /v/ sounds:</b> You had major problems distinguishing between /w/ and /v/, particularly evident in words like "overview." In English, the /w/ sound, as in 'water' /wɔːtər/, is made with rounded lips, while the /v/ sound, as in 'video' /vɪdiəʊ/, is made by touching your upper teeth to your lower lip and letting air out. Many Thai speakers face this challenge because Thai does not have the /v/ sound. Practicing minimal pairs like 'wet' vs. 'vet' can be very helpful!</li><li><b>Lack of linking sounds:</b> Your speech sometimes sounded a bit "staccato" because you didn't always link words together smoothly, for example, "he is." In English, when a word ends with a consonant and the next word starts with a vowel, we often link them (e.g., "he_is" /hiːɪz/). This natural flow makes speech sound more fluent and easier to understand. Try to practice connected speech, especially when a word ends in a consonant and the next begins with a vowel.</li><li><b>Difficulty with 'tr' cluster:</b> You had a noticeable problem with consonant clusters, specifically the 'tr' sound, as in "travel." Sometimes, the /r/ sound was missing or replaced with a /w/ or /v/ sound. English has many consonant clusters that are not common in Thai. To improve, practice saying words like 'train' /treɪn/, 'tree' /triː/, and 'strong' /strɒŋ/ slowly, making sure to articulate both consonants clearly without adding an extra vowel sound in between.</li><li><b>Dropped final consonants:</b> You frequently dropped consonant endings, for example, in the word "main." In English, final consonants are very important for meaning and clarity. Thai often emits or weakens final consonants, which can make it hard for English listeners to understand you. Practice exaggerating the final consonant sound in words like 'cat' /kæt/, 'book' /bʊk/, and 'main' /meɪn/ until it feels natural.</li></ul><p>Focusing on these specific areas will greatly enhance how clearly you speak English!</p>	\N	NULL	8.0	<p>Hello ThatThat! 👋 You did a great job organizing your presentation about Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng and sharing lots of interesting information about why he is your hero. Your talk followed a clear structure, which made it easy to follow your ideas. Keep working on making your sentences more accurate and clearer, especially with your grammar, and pay special attention to pronouncing all your sounds clearly. You have a lot of potential, and with consistent practice, your English will shine even more! ✨</p>	B1	Speaking	CA	FIRST	\N	\N
