n this context is usually uncountable, or "works" refers to creations. Also, remember to use "these" when referring to plural items, like "both of these pictures" instead of "both of this pictures." Sometimes, learners in Thailand might accidentally drop the "the" in phrases like "some of the students" because the structure is slightly different in Thai. Keeping an eye on these small details will make a big difference!	2	You do a good job of speaking in longer turns and keeping your contributions relevant to the topic, which is a big step towards fluent communication! 😊 However, sometimes you pause quite a bit and use filler words like "um" and "uh." While this is natural for all speakers, it was a little disruptive during your longer answers. To improve, try to slow down slightly and gather your thoughts before you speak, or use shorter pauses more effectively. This will help your ideas flow more smoothly and make it easier for your listener to follow your thoughts. Thai learners sometimes use more hesitation when speaking English as they are translating ideas in their head or searching for the right vocabulary, so don't worry, this is a common step in the learning process!	2	Venice, it's great that you're speaking English and trying out new words! 💪 To help people understand you even better, let's work on a few things. Sometimes, your words don't link together very smoothly, and you tend to shorten long vowel sounds. For example, "tree" /triː/ sometimes sounded like "tri" /trɪ/. Also, pay close attention to the difference between the /v/ sound, as in "very" /veri/, and the /w/ sound, as in "wery" /wɛri/. These sounds can be tricky for Thai speakers because the /v/ sound is not common in Thai, leading to substitutions with /w/ or other similar sounds. Practicing tongue twisters and carefully listening to how native speakers pronounce these sounds will help a lot!	4	You were really good at talking with your partner and the examiner, Venice! You asked and answered questions well, and you helped keep the conversation going, especially during the discussion parts of the test. You also tried to make decisions together, which is a key part of speaking interactively. It's a fantastic skill to have, and it shows you're comfortable communicating with others in English! 👍	11.0	Hello Venice! 👋 It was a pleasure listening to your speaking test. You did a great job participating in the discussions and sharing your thoughts, especially when talking about how technology can help the environment! 🌿 Your ability to keep the conversation flowing is a real strength, and with a bit of focus on smoothing out your speech and tidying up some grammar points, you'll be communicating with even more clarity and confidence. Keep up the excellent effort! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Venice**<br>Here are three concrete steps you can take to help your English speaking grow:<br>*   ✅ **Practice linking words**: Try recording yourself saying sentences like "clean cleaning the beach" or "if you got enough" and focus on making the words flow together more naturally, like "cleaning the beach" and "if you've got enough."<br>*   ✅ **Mind your vowel sounds**: When you practice words with long vowel sounds, like "tree" /triː/, try to hold the sound a little longer. Pay special attention to the difference between "very" /veri/ and "wery" /wɛri/, ensuring your /v/ sound is clear.<br>*   ✅ **Focus on subject-verb agreement**: When you're talking, try to match your verbs to your subjects, for example, "a lot of work" instead of "a lot of works," or "these pictures" instead of "this pictures."	B1+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
