 few small mistakes can make it a little tricky for your listener to understand exactly what you mean. For instance, you said, "I last midterm test is very pretty poor," which could be clearer if you said, "My last midterm test was very poor." Also, "Money is a live of thing" would be much clearer as "Money is a way of life." Another example is "A lot of water of pond in Thailand is full of a waste or trash," which would be more accurate as "A lot of water in ponds in Thailand is full of waste or trash." 🤔 To help with this, try to pay extra attention to how your verbs match your subjects (for example, "I *am*" not "I *is*") and practice using articles like "a," "an," and "the" correctly. Thai grammar doesn't always use articles in the same way English does, so this is a common area for Thai learners to work on! Reading and listening to English often can help you pick up on these patterns naturally. 📚	2	You do a good job of giving longer answers, Rico, which is a big step up from just short responses! 👍 Your contributions are usually relevant to the topic, which shows you’re listening and thinking about what’s being asked. However, sometimes you pause a fair bit or repeat words like "Okay, I'll go first. Okay, I'll go first." This can make your speech a little less smooth. To make your ideas even clearer and easier to follow, try to think about connecting your sentences with words like "because," "so," "however," or "in addition." For example, instead of just repeating, you could say, "I'll go first because I have an idea about recycling." This helps your listener follow your train of thought more easily! It also helps to plan what you want to say in your head for a second before speaking, even just a tiny pause, rather than pausing within sentences. 😊	1	Rico, you are generally understood, which is the most important thing! 🙌 Your intonation, the way your voice goes up and down, is often quite good, which helps make your speech sound more natural. However, sometimes it can be a bit hard to catch all your words clearly because you tend to drop some consonant sounds. For instance, in words like "badminton," the /d/ and /t/ sounds at the end can be missed, and the "st" sound in "forest" can be tricky. Also, the 'th' sound in words like "the" /ðə/ often needs a bit more attention. You also sometimes mix up the 'sh' /ʃ/ and 'ch' /tʃ/ sounds, like saying "sheapest" instead of "cheapest" /tʃiːpɪst/. These small changes can make a big difference in how clearly others understand you. A good idea is to practice words that end with consonants very carefully, making sure you make the sound. For example, try "badmint-**ON**" and "fores-**T**." Sometimes, in Thai, final consonant sounds are softened or dropped, which can carry over into English, so being extra aware of these sounds will really help! 🗣️	1	You did well at responding to the interviewer and your partner, Rico, which is a great start for interacting in English! 👍 You participated in the discussions and shared your opinions when asked. However, to make your conversations even more engaging, try to do a bit more than just agree. For instance, after saying "Yeah," you could add, "Yeah, and I think it's important because..." or "Yeah, I agree, but have you thought about...?" This helps to keep the conversation flowing and shows you're really listening and building on what others say. Sometimes, you waited a little while before responding, like when asked about "Participating" in clean-up events. Trying to jump in a little more quickly, even with a short phrase to show you're thinking, can make the interaction feel more natural and dynamic! Practice initiating new ideas or questions to your partner to extend the conversation further. 💬	6.0	Hi Rico! 👋 It's wonderful to see your effort in the speaking test today. You’re definitely getting better at expressing your ideas and participating in conversations. You did a good job of trying to explain your thoughts and engaging with the topics! Keep up the great work in developing your confidence. 😊 However, to really help your listeners understand your excellent ideas, focusing on how clearly you pronounce your words will be a big step forward. With a bit of focused practice, you’ll find it much easier to share all your great thoughts. 👍 You're on a solid path, and with a little extra practice, you'll feel even more comfortable and clear when speaking English! <br>📋 Action Plan for Rico: <br>• Make a list of words you often use (like "the," "badminton," "forest") and practice saying them slowly, making sure to hit all the consonant sounds at the beginning, middle, and end. You could even record yourself and listen back! ✅ <br>• Try some tongue twisters or minimal pair exercises focusing on similar sounds like /ʃ/ (sh) and /tʃ/ (ch). This will help you distinguish and produce them more clearly. For example, "She sells seashells" for /ʃ/ and "Cheetahs choose cheese" for /tʃ/. ✅ <br>• When speaking, remember to pause briefly after each sentence or phrase to give yourself a moment to collect your thoughts and ensure you're articulating each word fully. This can help with both clarity and fluency. ✅	A2+	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N
