mation, for example, when you said "In Thailand, I think football because like when I go anywhere, I see people play football and when I ask there, they say like this is my favorite hobby." To make your answers even smoother, try using connecting words like 'because' or 'so' more often. For instance, you could have said, "Acting can be my new hobby because I can enjoy it."	3	Nine, your pronunciation was clear overall, and I could understand you easily! However, I did notice a few times where the stress on certain syllables could be improved. For example, when you said "favorite" (/ˈfeɪvərɪt/), try to stress the first syllable more: /ˈfeɪvərɪt/. Also, pay attention to linking words between sounds to make it smoother. For instance, instead of pausing between 'riding' and 'horse' when you said 'when I riding horse', try to blend them together slightly.	3	Nine, you were very good at interacting with me and sharing your opinions. You asked me questions such as "What about you? Do you like cooking?" which kept the conversation flowing. That's exactly what we want to see! To make your contributions even more impactful, try to elaborate a bit more on your answers. For example, when I asked if it's important to have a hobby, you could have added a specific example of how a hobby has helped you or someone you know.	12.0	Nine, you showed solid B1 level skills in our speaking test. Your grammar and vocabulary are developing well, and you are able to express your ideas clearly. You interacted well and kept the conversation going. Keep practicing your pronunciation and focusing on using connecting words to make your speech even smoother. All the best, Ed	B1	Speaking	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N
29528	2026-06-24	2026-06-24	2027	Are Gen Z Thais more progressive or traditional than the older generations?	It seems to me that gender roles are very important things in our life. And in the past, I saw that males are above the female. But nowadays, in Thailand, we have gender equality, like, men and women are equal to each other. And ... last year, I have a classmate that was, uh, lgbtq. She was a lesbian, and ... we don't judge her anymore. ... \n\nUh, ...... And I think being, uh, lgbtq genders are, uh, not very. ... \n\nOh, ......\nNot. Not bad at all. Just be like, whatever you want to be. Yes.	92	3	At Band 5, a speaker uses a good range of grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes. You're at 3/5 because your vocabulary is everyday and generally appropriate, and your simple sentences are mostly correct. You said *'males are above the female'* and *'men and women are equal to each other'*: these are clear and easy to understand. You also used *'we don't judge her anymore'* — that's a nice, natural phrase!\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Fix your verb tenses. You said *'I have a classmate'* when talking about something that happened last year: use 'I had a classmate' (past tense). Practice checking if your story is about now or the past.\n\n- Choose the right words. You said *'lgbtq genders'*: the correct phrase is 'LGBTQ+ people' or 'LGBTQ+ identities'. Try to learn common word pairs for this topic.\n\n- Finish your sentences. You said *'being, uh, lgbtq genders are, uh, not very...'* and then stopped. Try to complete your thought, even if it's simple. For example: 'being LGBTQ+ is not very accepted by some people.'\n\nKeep going! Your ideas are clear and you have a good opinion. With better grammar and word choice, your speaking will be even stronger.	2	At Band 5, a speaker produces long, clear answers with a beginning, middle, and end. They use words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example' to connect their ideas smoothly.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your answer is short—83 seconds instead of 2 minutes—and you have long pauses that break the flow. You said *'And ... last year, I have a classmate'*: that pause makes it hard to follow. Later, you said *'Uh, ...... And I think being, uh, lgbtq genders are, uh, not very. ... Oh, ......'*: these long silences show you lost your train of thought. You also use filler words like *'uh'* and *'like'* many times.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Speak for longer. Your answer is only 83 seconds. Try to add more details. For example, instead of just saying 'men and women are equal', you could say: 'In the past, men were seen as the leaders, but now women can be leaders too. For example, my aunt is a manager at her company.' This makes your answer longer and more interesting.\n\n- Reduce long pauses. When you don't know what to say next, use a simple phrase like 'Let me think about that' or 'Another example is...' instead of staying silent. This keeps the conversation going smoothly.\n\nYou have a good topic! With more practice, you can speak for longer and with fewer pauses.	4	Your speaking is clear and your sounds are good. You speak a little slowly, but that is okay. If you speed up just a bit, your words will be easier to understand.\n\nOne thing to try is adding more feeling to your voice. Right now, your tone is quite flat. Try to show more emotion when you speak — for example, make your voice go up and down a little more. This will make your speaking sound more lively and interesting.\n\nYou are doing well. Keep practicing and you will get even better!	\N	\N	12	Hi Tonnam,\n\nYou're working at an B1 level in speaking. I really like that you shared a personal example: you talked about your classmate who is a lesbian and said *'we don't judge her anymore'*. That's a great way to show your point! You also used some good vocabulary like *'equality'* and *'progressive'*.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Speak for longer. Try to add more details and examples to reach 2 minutes.\n\n- Practice using the past tense correctly when you tell stories. For example, say 'I had a classmate' instead of 'I have a classmate'.\n\nYou have good ideas about gender equality. Keep practicing!	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	83	https://drive.google.com/file/d/104lLP_yZ8lz-QAixckIXa4pesTx4Zj6q/view
