p words or leave sentences unfinished.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Choose the right words. You said *'the society that they're in for'*: this should be 'the society they are in' or 'the society they belong to'. Practice using common word pairs (collocations) like 'in a society', 'grow up in', 'be part of'.\n\n- Finish your sentences clearly. You said *'you will see such topics as like, such as misogyn'*: this is incomplete. Try to say 'you will see topics like misogyny' or 'you will see topics such as misogyny'. Practice saying one complete idea before moving to the next.\n\n- Use correct word forms. You used 'misogyn' instead of the correct noun 'misogyny' or adjective 'misogynistic'. Learning the noun and adjective forms of new words will help you sound more natural.\n\nKeep going! Your ideas are interesting, and with better word choice, your speaking will be much clearer.	3	At Band 5, a speaker produces long, well-organised speech with clear connections between ideas.\n\nYou're at 3/5 because you produce extended speech with some organisation, but there is noticeable hesitation and repetition. You said *'Okay, I'm gonna start again'* and then restarted your first point. You also repeated phrases like *'makes like, helps like influence'* and *'we'd always be like, that's very rude. But to them it seems normal. But to us it just seems very weird'*: this shows you are circling back to the same idea. You use basic connectors like 'so', 'furthermore', and 'and', but your speech feels a bit list-like and sometimes loses focus.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Reduce hesitation and restarts. You said *'As I see it, the Gen Z isolations... Okay, I'm gonna start again'*: this breaks your flow. Try to plan your first sentence before speaking. For example, say 'As I see it, Gen Z adolescents have a more progressive view on gender roles than older generations' and then continue without restarting.\n\n- Use a clearer structure. You have a first point ('in an international society') and a second point ('in a Thai society'), but the connection between them is not clear. Try using signposting words like 'first of all' and 'secondly' to help the listener follow your ideas. Also, finish each point before moving to the next.\n\nYou have good ideas about different societies. With better organisation, your speaking will be much easier to follow!	5	Your pronunciation is already very strong, and you should feel proud of how clearly you express yourself. There are just a couple of very small areas to refine as you continue improving.\n\nFirst, you occasionally mispronounce complex words like *misogyny* and *misogynistic*. These are tricky words for many learners, so this is completely understandable. To improve, try breaking them down into syllables and practicing them slowly until the rhythm feels natural.\n\nSecond, I noticed a very slight tendency to drop the final /t/ sound in words like *point*. This is a common habit, but it can affect clarity in fast speech. Try to make a conscious effort to release that final /t/ — even just a light touch at the end of the word will make a difference.\n\nOverall, your speaking is clear and confident. With a little focused practice on these two points, you'll sound even more polished. Keep up the great work!	\N	\N	15	Hi Elenna,\n\nYou're working at a solid B1+++ level in speaking. I really like that you have a clear opinion and you try to explain it with examples from different societies. You said *'we all came from different societies and we all had different backgrounds, we all thought differently'*: this shows you can develop an idea with a personal story. That's great! You also used some good vocabulary like *'progressive'* and *'backgrounds'*.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Choose your words more carefully. For example, use 'adolescents' instead of 'isolations'.\n\n- Plan your speech before you start so you don't need to restart. Try to use word