ows you have good ideas.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Fix your subject-verb agreement. You said *'Thai teenager is'* but you mean many teenagers: it should be **'Thai teenagers are really progressive'**. Practice checking if your subject is singular or plural before you choose the verb.\n\n- Use clearer vocabulary. You said *'trend wife'* and *'traditional mind'* — these phrases are not correct. Try **'stay-at-home wife'** or **'traditional wife'** and **'traditional mindset'** or **'traditional thinking'**. Learning common word pairs will help your meaning be clearer.\n\n- Remove extra filler words from your sentences. You said *'like um'* and *'really like'* many times. Try saying your ideas directly without these words.\n\nKeep trying! Your ideas about gender roles are interesting, and with better grammar and word choice, your speaking will be much easier to understand.	3	At Band 3, a speaker produces long, well-organised speech with a clear structure and smooth connections between ideas. They use words like 'however', 'for example', and 'in addition' to link their points.\n\nYou're at 3/5 because your response is short and has many long pauses that break the flow. You said *'So ... they never have like good life'* — the pause here makes it hard to follow. You also repeat the same ideas, like saying *'traditional minds'* and *'progressive minds'* many times without adding new details. Your response has a start and an end, but the middle is not developed.\n\nTo reach Band 3, work on these two things:\n\n- Reduce your pauses. You used *'um'*, *'like'*, and *'uh'* many times as fillers. Try pausing silently instead, or use a phrase like **'Let me think...'** once. This will make your speech sound smoother.\n\n- Connect your ideas better. You jumped from Australian kids to your friends without a clear link. Try using **'For example'** or **'In contrast'** to show how your ideas relate. This will help your listener follow your argument.\n\nYou 