ge and mostly succeeding.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Fix 'is exist' constructions. Instead of *'is still exist'*, say **'still exists'**. A common pattern: if the verb already carries the meaning (exist = 'to be'), don't add 'is' before it.\n\n- Check your verb endings for past forms. Instead of *'could have adapt'*, say **'could have adapted'**. After 'have', always use the past participle form.\n\nYour vocabulary is strong and your ideas are interesting. With just a few grammar fixes, you'll be at Band 5!	4	At Band 5, a speaker produces extended speech with a clear structure, using a range of cohesive devices to guide the listener through their ideas.\n\nYou're at 4/5 because your speech is well organised and not excessively disrupted by hesitations. You start with your opinion: *'Gen Z is definitely more progressive'*, then address a counterargument, move to a specific example about Thailand's past, and finish with a clear conclusion. This is a logical structure. You use cohesive devices like *'While others argue'*, *'You can see'*, *'Let's talk about'*, and *'in conclusion'* to guide the listener. Your hesitations (*'um'*, *'uh'*) do occur but they are mostly between phrases rather than mid-sentence, so the coherence is maintained. The listener can follow your argument without effort.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Develop each point more fully. You introduced the past gender roles in Thailand but moved quickly to the present. Try adding a detail or example for each time period: **'In the past, men were the breadwinners and women stayed home. But now, both can work and share responsibilities.'**\n\n- Connect your examples more explicitly. You mentioned TikTok and X, then shifted to the past. Add a linking sentence like **'Social media is one reason for this change. Now let me explain how things were different in the past.'**\n\nYour speech is already well structured. With fuller development of each point, you'll reach 