ey didn't used to accept'*, try **'they didn't use to accept'** or **'they used to not accept'**. Practice keeping one tense per sentence.\n\n- Use 'a', 'an', and 'the' correctly. You said *'in traditional gender role'* — this should be **'in traditional gender roles'** (no article needed for plural general nouns) or **'in a traditional gender role'** (if talking about one).\n\nYour ideas are clear and your vocabulary is at B1 level. With better sentence control, you'll improve quickly!	2	At Band 5, a speaker produces long, clear answers with a beginning, middle, and end. They use words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example' to connect their ideas smoothly.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your answers are quite short and it's hard to follow the connections between your ideas. I really like that you shared your opinion on gender roles — you said *'people will forget about gender role'*, which shows you're thinking about the topic. You also tried to give reasons, like *'women have become more loud mind'*, and that's a good start.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on these two things:\n\n- Plan your answer before you speak. Think of 2-3 clear points. For example: **'First, women have more job opportunities now. Second, young people don't care about old rules. Third, my friends never talk about gender roles.'** This makes your answer easy to follow.\n\n- Use simple linking words. Instead of jumping from one idea to the next, say **'Also...'** or **'For example...'** or **'Another thing is...'**. This helps your listener understand how your ideas connect.\n\nYou have good ideas about gender roles. With a simple plan and linking words, your speaking will be much clearer!	2	You have a nice, steady voice, and it is easy to understand you. One thing to work on is making your voice sound more lively. Right now, it is very flat. Try to go up and down a little when you speak. This will help you sound more interested and natural.\n\nYou also push too hard on the last part of many words. When you do this, your words can sound a bit strange. Try to keep the same energy from the beginning to the end of each word. Do not make the end louder or longer.\n\nBe careful with the word *mother* /ˈmʌðə/. In your recording, it sounds like *mutter* /ˈmʌtə/. The middle sound should be soft, like the "th" in "this". Practise saying it slowly.\n\nFinally, watch the sounds at the end of words like *roles* /rəʊlz/. You are missing the final /z/ sound. This sound is important because it shows the word is plural. Try to add a little buzz at the end. Keep practising, and you will get better. You are doing a good job	\N	\N	9	Hi Ploy,\n\nYou're working at an A2 level in speaking. I like that you have a clear view — you said *'Thai talent has become more progressive'* and you tried to explain how things have changed. Your vocabulary is good for this topic, using words like *'progressive'* and *'gender role'*.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things: first, keep one verb tense per sentence instead of mixing past and present. Second, practice using 'a', 'an', and 'the' correctly.\n\nYour ideas come through clearly. Keep practicing!	A2	Speaking	CA	PET	86	https://drive.google.com/file/d/1TP2IugPGGYx8Y2iL4xXKfk5rgb-sh6af/view
30055	2026-06-24	2026-06-24	2027	Are Gen Z Thais more progressive or traditional than the older generations?	So uh, I, so I recently watched a BTN news report about uh, Gen Z adolences in Australia being holding more traditional values than their earlier generations. But I don't think that's the case for Thailand because uh, for one uh, the Gen Zs here still hold a really modern views on gender roles similar uh, to their parents. So uh, for our beliefs here is that both men and women should work. There's no divide that who should stay home. Look at the kids. \n\nUh, for us, we all, we split, we balance our roles. Unlike uh, the news reports statistic from Australia which shows uh, uh, the teens there, they divide clearly that women should stay home and men should work. But here's the difference and secondly is that uh, we have been this way for years. So it's not just like we just suddenly change like Australia, we have been organized uh, like this for quite a few generations and it has proved to be really useful for everyday life because we don't have to be sitting and arguing about like who, who should go to work today, who should sit and look at the kids today. We, we divide our roles. Clearly \n\nwe help each other in the family. So like it's really easy to you know, to work because we don't have to argue every day about some uh, nonsense stuff, you know. So in conclusion, pretty much that uh, is that, well we still hold a really good, good traditional uh, gender values compared to Australian Gen Z adolences.	273	3	At Band 5, a speaker uses a good range of grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes. They can use complex sentences and specific words naturally.\n\nYou're at 3/5 because your vocabulary is everyday and generally appropriate, but you have some noticeable errors. For example, you said *'Gen Z adolences'* (should be 'adolescents'), *'being holding'* (should be 'are holding' or 'hold'), and *'we have been organized like this'* (should be 'we have been organised like this' or 'we have organised things like this'). These errors don't stop me from understanding you, but they are clear. On the positive side, you used some nice phrases like *'traditional values'* and *'balance our roles'* — good work!\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Fix your verb forms. You said *'the Gen Zs here still hold a really modern views'*: this should be 'Gen Z here still holds really modern views' (singular 'holds' for 'Gen Z', and no 'a' before plural 'views'). Practice checking if your subject and verb match.\n\n- Choose the right words. You said *'adolences'* and *'nonsense stuff'*: try 'adolescents' and 'unnecessary arguments' or 'small disagreements'. Learning more specific words will make your speaking clearer.\n\n- Check your word order in complex sentences. You said *'There's no divide that who should stay home'*: this should be 'There's no divide about who should stay home'. Removing the extra word 'that' makes the sentence correct.\n\nKeep going! Your ideas are interesting and you can already communicate well. A few small changes will make a big difference.	3	At Band 5, a speaker produces long, well-organised speech with a clear structure and a range of linking words. The listener can follow easily without any effort.\n\nYou're at 3/5 because you produce extended speech with a clear beginning, middle, and end. You compare Thailand and Australia, and you give examples like *'we split, we balance our roles'*. You also use some good linking words like 'because', 'unlike', and 'in conclusion'. That's a great start! However, your speech has many hesitations and filler words like *'uh'*, *'you know'*, and *'like'* that break the flow. You also repeat yourself a bit — for example, you say *'we divide our roles'* and later *'we split, we balance our roles'*, which are the same idea said twice.\n\nTo improve, work on these two things:\n\n- Reduce filler words. You said *'uh'* about 15 times and *'you know'* twice. Instead of saying *'uh'*, try pausing silently for one second to think. Practice your answer before you speak so you feel more confident.\n\n- Avoid repeating the same idea. You said *'we divide our roles'* and later *'we split, we balance our roles'* — these all mean the same thing. Try to add new information instead. For example, say: 'We divide our roles equally, which means both partners share housework and childcare.' This adds detail instead of repeating.\n\nYou have a good structure and clear opinions. Keep practicing to make your speech smoother — you're on the right track!	5	You’re doing a great job. Your pronunciation is clear and easy to follow throughout, and your intonation sounds natural and well-controlled. There are no specific problem areas to highlight — your sounds are accurate, and your speech flows smoothly from start to finish.\n\nKeep up the good work. You’ve built a solid foundation, and your clarity makes your ideas easy to understand.	\N	\N	15	Hi Jomtub,\n\nYou're working at a strong B1+++ level in speaking. I really like that you have a clear opinion and you compare Thailand and Australia — that shows good thinking! You used some nice vocabulary like *'traditional values'* and *'balance our roles'*, which are great phrases.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your grammar carefully. Make sure your verbs match your subjects (like 'Gen Z holds', not 'Gen Z hold a views').\n\n- Try to speak more smoothly by reducing filler words like 'uh' and 'you know'. Practice pausing silently instead.\n\nYou have good ideas and a clear structure. Keep practicing — you're doing really well!	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	117	https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XRi6eD0TyMhX16u9UO8d8f8eqwdr0JMK/view
30584	2026-06-26	2026-06-26	2027	What makes you stressed?	One thing that really stressing me out is math subject. The first reason is that difficult and boring, and the second reason is that hard to understand. For example, last week at your school, my teacher teach me about math, like how to divide, how to find the answer, but I don't understand anything about math because my teacher speak so fast, so I don't understand what she need to explain to me.\n\n<br><br>\nAnd let me ...... I tell you another story. ... One time I want to sleep because the lesson is so boring. ... That's it.	100	2	You're at 2/5 because your sentences have mistakes that make the meaning a little unclear. You used some good words like *'stressing'* and *'boring'* — these are useful words for your topic.\n\nTo get a higher score, work on these three things:\n\n- Fix your verb tenses. You said *'my teacher teach me'* — this should be 'my teacher taught me' because it happened in the past. You also said *'I don't understand'* — this should be 'I didn't understand'.\n\n- Use the right word forms. You said *'One thing that really stressing me out'* — this needs 'is' before 'stressing'. The correct sentence is 'One thing that is really stressing me out'.\n\n- Use 'a', 'an', or 'the' correctly. You said *'math subject'* — try saying 'the math subject' or 'math class'. You also said *'at your school'* — this should be 'at my school'.\n\nKeep trying! Your ideas are clear. With better grammar, your speaking will be much stronger.	2	You're at 2/5 because your answer is short (44 seconds instead of 60 seconds) and you have long pauses. I can see you have two ideas: you talk about a difficult lesson and a boring lesson. That's good! You said *'One time I want to sleep because the lesson is so boring'* — this is a clear reason.\n\nBut the pauses make it hard to follow. You had a very long pause between *'let me'* and *'I tell you'*. That pause was more than 7 seconds. You also ended very quickly with *'That's it'*.\n\nTo get a higher score, try these two things:\n\n- Speak for longer. You spoke for 44 seconds. Try to speak for 60 seconds. After you say the lesson is boring, add more details. For example: 'The teacher talked in a slow voice. I felt very tired. I tried to stay awake, but it was hard.'\n- Reduce long pauses. If you need to think, say a short word like 'well' or 'let me think' instead of stopping. This will make your speaking smoother.\n\nYou have good ideas. Keep practicing to speak longer and with fewer pauses!	3	You have a nice voice, and it is easy to understand you. You are doing a good job with your speaking. There are just a few small things to work on.\n\nFirst, watch your final consonant sounds. In the word first /fɜːst/, the /st/ sound at the end needs to be clear. Also, in understand /ˌʌndəˈstænd/ and divide /dɪˈvaɪd/, make sure the last sound is strong. Try to say these words slowly and finish each one clearly.\n\nSecond, you sometimes push too hard on the last part of words. This happens with answer /ˈɑːnsə/, teacher /ˈtiːtʃə/, another /əˈnʌðə/, and story /ˈstɔːri/. Try to keep your voice softer at the end of these words. Let the word end gently.\n\nYou are making good progress. Keep practising, and your speaking will sound even better!	\N	\N	9	Hi Mita,\n\nYou're working at an A2 level in speaking. I like that you gave two clear reasons why math stresses you out: you said *'difficult and boring'* and *'hard to understand'*. That's a good start! You also used the word *'stressing'*, which is a good word to describe your feelings.\n\nTo get a higher score, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your verb tenses: decide if you're talking about the past or present, and use the correct form. For example, change 'teach' to 'taught' and 'don't understand' to 'didn't understand'.\n\n- Try to speak for the full time and reduce long pauses. Practice adding more details to your answers. If you need to think, say 'well' or 'let me think' instead of stopping.\n\nYou have good ideas. Keep practicing and you will improve!	A2	Speaking	CA	PET	44	\N
30858	2026-06-24	2026-06-24	2027	Are Gen Z Thais more progressive or traditional than the older generations?	In my opinion. It seems to me that a lot of Australian kids tend to have more traditional thinking because I found you made me watch a study from King's College that showed that a lot of men still think that thirty three percent of women should still be housewives and nineteen percent of girls still think that they should be housewives, which is very anti feminism. It's twenty twenty six. We should not be thinking like this. Overall, even though Australia has a lot of online exposure, the thinking is still not progressive enough ... to be for this era. \n\nIt's very traditional, very back. Feminism started in the nineteen fifties. It shouldn't still be a problem. ... \n\nWell, in Thailand, a lot of people are, um, very feminist and they believe that people all around, everybody is equal, we get paid equally, Everything is equal. You can go in certain places ... and you don't get charged more just because you're a woman. ... And my conclusion is that Thai, uh, people have much more progressive thinking than Australians in many ways, and that's all.	190	4	At Band 5, a speaker uses a good range of grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes. You're at 4/5 because you use a nice mix of simple and more complex structures, and your vocabulary is often specific and effective. You said *'anti feminism'*, *'progressive enough'*, and *'online exposure'* — these are great words that go beyond basic vocabulary. You also used a complex sentence: *'I found you made me watch a study from King's College that showed that...'* — this shows you can build longer sentences. Well done!\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Check how you say years. You said *'It's twenty twenty six'*: in formal speaking, say 'It's 2026' or 'It's the year 2026'. Practice saying years clearly.\n\n- Choose clearer vocabulary. You said *'very back'* — this is a bit confusing. Try 'very backward' or 'very traditional' instead. This will make your meaning stronger.\n\n- Watch out for repetition. You said *'we get paid equally, Everything is equal'* — using 'equal' twice in a row sounds a little basic. Try using synonyms like 'fair', 'the same', or 'just' to vary your language.\n\nYou have a confident voice and good ideas — keep building your vocabulary and checking your grammar, and you'll reach Band 5 soon!	3	You're at 3/5 because your response has a clear beginning, middle, and end, and you stay on topic. You start with *'In my opinion'*, then give evidence from a study, and finish with a conclusion comparing Australia and Thailand — that's good organisation. However, your speech is a bit short (89 seconds instead of the expected 120 seconds), which means you didn't fully develop your ideas. I also noticed some pauses, like before *'... to be for this era'* and *'...'* before *'Well, in Thailand'*. These make your speech feel a little choppy. You use basic linking words like *'because'* and *'Overall'*, but you could add more variety like 'however', 'for example', or 'in addition'.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Speak for longer. Your response was 89 seconds, but the task expects about 120 seconds. Try adding more details or examples. For example, after saying *'a lot of men still think that thirty three percent of women should still be housewives'*, you could add: 'For example, this shows that traditional ideas are still strong in some families.'\n- Reduce your pauses. Instead of pausing before *'Well, in Thailand'*, try using a linking phrase like 'On the other hand, in Thailand...' or 'In contrast, Thailand is different because...' This will make your speech flow more smoothly.\n\nYou have a clear opinion and good structure — with more development and smoother transitions, you'll be at Band 5!	5	You have a very natural, easy-to-listen-to voice, which is a real strength. Your intonation is good, and you show a nice capacity to emphasise the right words, which helps your meaning come through clearly. Your linking between words is developing well, and for the most part, your sounds are clear and easy to follow.\n\nOne small area to focus on is that you sometimes overstress the final syllables of words. This can make the ends of words sound a little too heavy or forced. Try to soften your word endings and keep the stress more even across the whole word. This will help your speech sound even more natural and fluent.\n\nYou're doing a great job — keep practising, and these small adjustments will make a big difference.	\N	\N	16	Hi Katie,\n\nYou're working at a solid B2 level in speaking. I really like that you have a clear opinion and you support it with evidence from a study — you said *'a study from King's College that showed that a lot of men still think that thirty three percent of women should still be housewives'*. That's a great way to make your argument stronger! You also used some good vocabulary like *'progressive'* and *'online exposure'*.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Speak for the full time (about 120 seconds) by adding more examples or explanations.\n\n- Reduce your pauses by using linking phrases like 'on the other hand' or 'in contrast' instead of stopping and starting again.\n\nYou have confident ideas. Keep practicing, and you'll get to Band 5!	B2	Speaking	CA	PET	89	https://drive.google.com/file/d/1qiDrt_YNg11VVzZcWSsbRhM6MefJLiMJ/view
