\nKeep going! Your ideas are clear and you're using some good vocabulary.	3	At Band 5, a speaker produces extended speech with a clear structure and uses a range of linking words. The response flows smoothly and ideas are well developed.\n\nYou're at 3/5 because your response is mostly relevant and has some organisation, but it's quite short and has some hesitation. I like how you organised your ideas: you gave your opinion, then a counterpoint, then a conclusion. You also used linking words like *'because'*, *'but'*, and *'in conclusion'* — that's a good start! You also used a nice phrase: *'things men are expected to do'* — that's a good way to talk about roles.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Reduce your filler words. You said *'um'* three times and *'uh'* twice, which breaks your flow. Try pausing silently instead of saying 'um'.\n\n- Develop your ideas more. You mentioned a statistic but didn't explain it. Instead of repeating *'I think Gen Z adolescents hold more progressive views'*, try adding a new idea like: 'For example, many young people now share household chores equally, which shows a shift in thinking.' This will make your speech longer and more interesting.\n\nYou have a clear opinion and good structure. Keep practicing to speak for the full time!	4	You have a nice voice, and it is easy to listen to you. Your English is clear, and people can understand your ideas. There are a few small things you can work on to sound even better.\n\nFirst, try to make your voice a little louder. When you speak more quietly, it can sound a bit flat. Also, remember to add short pauses between your ideas. This helps your listener follow what you are saying.\n\nSometimes you push too hard on the last part of a word. For example, in the word exposure /ɪkˈspoʊʒə/, you stress the ending too much. Try to keep the sound the same from the start to the end of the word.\n\nYou also drop some sounds at the end of words. In your recording, the final /t/ in about /əˈbaʊt/ is missing, and the final /s/ in statistics /stəˈtɪstɪks/ is not there. Practise saying these words slowly, and make sure you say every sound clearly.\n\nYou are doing a good job. Keep practising these small points, and your speaking will get even better!	\N	\N	13	Hi Lily,\n\nYou're working at a strong B1 level in speaking. I really like that you have a clear opinion and you tried to explain it with a statistic — that shows you can think about your ideas! You also used some good vocabulary like *'progressive views'* and *'breadwinner'*.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your grammar, especially making sure your subjects and verbs match. For example, say 'adolescents hold', not 'adolescents holds'.\n\n- Try to speak for longer and use words like 'however' or 'for example' to connect your ideas better.\n\nYou have good ideas. Keep practicing!	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	80	https://drive.google.com/file/d/1NaD0KWwCyjVxKRjcfc8hGqB5ayxtgh5x/view
32376	2026-06-26	2026-06-26	2027	What makes you stressed?	Hello, my name is Mon. One thing that really stressed me out is mathematics homework that you give us a lot. Our teacher named Ms. Tan, she gave us a lot of homework for no reason, and everyone asked her, she just said, "Just do it, don't ask." ... She just gave us yesterday a lot of homework for no reason. She gave us, uh, she gave us homework. ...... She gave us in the classroom that we normally study, and ... the reason that it very stress me out is because, uh, it have a lot of homework, and sometimes I have to practice football, ... and I cannot do it, and it take a lot of time to do.\n\n<br><br>\n\nSecondly, reason is ... she is very angry, and sometimes she cheat us. We didn't do anything wrong, but she just angry and gave us a lot of homework. ...... All right.	151	2	At Band 5, a speaker uses a mix of simple and more difficult grammar with only small mistakes. They also use some interesting words that go beyond everyday topics.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your sentences often have mistakes that make the meaning unclear. For example, you said *'it have a lot of homework'*: the subject 'it' needs the verb 'has', not 'have'. You also said *'she just angry'*: this is missing the verb 'is'. You said *'it very stress me out'*: this should be 'it stresses me out a lot'. These mistakes happen on almost every sentence.\n\nHere are two things to work on:\n\n- Fix your subject-verb agreement. You said *'it have a lot of homework'*: this should be 'it has a lot of homework'. Practice matching 'he/she/it' with 'has' and 'they/we' with 'have'.\n\n- Add the verb 'to be' when needed. You said *'she just angry'*: this should be 'she is just angry'. Remember that in English, you need 'is', 'am', or 'are' before words like 'angry', 'stressed', or 'tired'.\n\nKeep going! Your ideas are clear, and with better grammar, your speaking will be much stronger.	2	At Band 5, a speaker talks for longer and connects their ideas clearly. They use words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example'. The listener can follow their story easily.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your answer is very short and you stop a lot. You spoke for 66 seconds, but the speech feels broken. You had a long pause (......) in the middle and many shorter pauses (...). You also said the same thing many times: you said *'she gave us a lot of homework'* three times. Your linking words are only 'and' and 'because'.\n\nHere are two things to work on:\n\n- Reduce your pauses. You had a long silence (......) after *'She gave us homework'*. Try to plan your next sentence while you finish the current one. Practice speaking without stopping for more than 2 seconds.\n\n- Develop your ideas instead of repeating. You said *'she gave us a lot of homework'* three times. Instead, try to add a new detail: 'She gave us a lot of homework, and this is stressful because I also have football practice.' This adds new information and moves the story forward.\n\nYou have a clear topic and a good reason for being stressed. With more practice, your speaking will flow much better!	2	You're doing a great job with your speaking. Your voice is clear and you are trying hard. There is one big thing to work on that will help people understand you better.\n\nYou are adding an extra sound at the end of some words. This happens with words like mathematics /ˌmæθəˈmætɪks/, gave /ɡeɪv/, us /ʌs/, and it /ɪt/. When you say these words, you put a strong, extra sound on the final consonant. This makes your speech hard to follow.\n\nTry to say the last sound of each word very softly. For example, in "gave," just stop after the /v/ sound. Don't push it. In "it," make the /t/ very short and light. Practice saying these words slowly and gently at the end. Your ideas are good, and this small change will make a big difference. Keep going!	\N	\N	8	Hi Boss,\n\nYou're working at an A2 level in speaking. I can see you have a clear opinion: you said *'mathematics homework stressed me out'* and you gave two reasons. That's a good start!\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things. First, check your grammar: make sure your subjects and verbs match (like 'it has', not 'it have'). Second, try to speak without long pauses and develop your ideas instead of repeating them. For example, instead of saying 'she gave us homework' three times, add a new detail each time.\n\nYou have good ideas. Keep practicing!	A2	Speaking	CA	PET	66	\N
32217	2026-06-24	2026-06-24	2027	Are Gen Z Thais more progressive or traditional than the older generations?	Some people say that Thai generations see ... views ... gender roles more traditionally, but I would argue with that. I see that Thai Tennessee adolescents views generals more progressive. And here's why. ... \n\nAccording to the research in Australia, the gender pay gap is still a big problem. Thirteen percent of women still get uh, paid less than men when working in the same job. ... \n\nFor another example, to support my ideas that ... Thai people use gender roles more aggressively. ... \n\nBack in nineteen, ... um, eighty, my grandma and my grandfather, they each have a job. They had a job that is that was making different amount of money. ... \n\nBut today my mom and my dad, they are working different job but still get pay the same as other gender. ... \n\nAnd for conclusion, I think Thai Gen Z adolescence, uh, would mute the gender role more progressively. Thank you.	147	2	At Band 5, a speaker uses a good range of grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes. They can use complex sentences and specific words naturally.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your sentences often have errors that make the meaning unclear. For example, you said *'Thai Tennessee adolescents views generals more progressive'*: the word 'Tennessee' doesn't fit here, and 'views generals' is confusing. You also said *'would mute the gender role more progressively'*: the word 'mute' doesn't mean what you want here.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Fix your subject-verb agreement. You said *'adolescents views'*: this should be 'adolescents view' or 'adolescents have views'. Practice checking if your subject and verb match in number.\n\n- Choose the right words. You said *'mute the gender role'*: the correct phrase is 'change gender roles' or 'see gender roles differently'. Try to learn common word pairs (collocations) like 'change roles', 'have views', 'see things differently'.\n\nKeep practicing! Your ideas are interesting, and with better grammar and word choice, your speaking will be much clearer.	2	At Band 5, a speaker produces long, clear speech with a logical structure. They use words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example' to connect their ideas smoothly.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your speech is short and has many pauses. You spoke for 125 seconds, which is okay, but your ideas are not well connected. You said *'And here's why'* which is a great start, but then you didn't explain your reason clearly. You also had many long pauses (like `...` and `......`) that make it hard to follow your thinking.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Use linking words to connect your ideas. You said *'For another example'* but then you didn't finish the example clearly. Try saying: 'For example, in 1980 my grandparents earned different amounts of money, but today my parents earn the same amount.' This connects the two ideas.\n\n- Reduce your pauses. You said *'Back in nineteen, ... um, eighty'*: this pause makes your speech sound broken. Practice saying the full date smoothly: 'Back in nineteen eighty' without stopping. Try to practice your talk out loud before the test.\n\nYou have good ideas about generations and gender roles. With more practice, your speech will flow much better!	5	You started a little slowly and it was a bit hard to follow at first. But as you kept going, you got much better. Your speaking became smoother and more confident.\n\nIt sounds like you were trying to remember what to say. This can make your speech a little choppy. Try to focus more on the ideas you want to share, and less on saying the exact words. This will help you sound more natural.\n\nYour pronunciation is really good. Your sounds are clear, and you put the stress on the right parts of words. Keep practicing, and you will get even better.	\N	\N	12	Hi Poopup,\n\nYou're working at an B1 level in speaking. I really like that you have a clear opinion: you said *'I would argue with that'* and you tried to give examples from your family. That's a great start! You also used the phrase *'more progressive'* which shows you understand the topic well.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your grammar: make sure your subjects and verbs match (like 'adolescents view', not 'adolescents views').\n\n- Practice speaking without long pauses. Try to connect your ideas with words like 'because' or 'for example'.\n\nYou have good ideas about generations and gender roles. Keep practicing!	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	125	https://drive.google.com/file/d/13H-8MOVVjpwtjyfOm6v1QYhe4lG27jT-/view
33266	2026-06-24	2026-06-24	2027	Are Gen Z Thais more progressive or traditional than the older generations?	Personally, I would say that, um, Gen Z adolescents hold a more progressive way of thinking if you compare it to the older generation. Um, some people say that Gen Z students are very traditional and old fashioned, but I disagree with them. Um, well, there is a study in the University of Melbourne that shows Gen Z students, um, displaying a more traditional way of thinking compared to, um, uh, older eras. Um, you, you could see that that study was made in Australia and you can't use it to define, um, any thinking traits or any behavioral traits in Thailand because, um, the culture in there and here is quite different. I mean, you, um, could see that, uh, the difference in Australia and Thailand. \n\nUh, in Australia, during the nineteen eighties and nineteen nineties War, women were very independent. They fought for equality and for women's rights. Um, but in Thailand during the nineteen eighties and nineteen nineties, um, men were usually the breadwinner. They, um, went out, they make money, um, they had to provide for their family. But women, uh, had to stay home. \n\nThey would have to cook, clean, take care of their children. Uh, they wouldn't get the opportunity to go out and actually work like men as much as we do now. So you can actually see how much that it has progressed. Um, during the nineteen eighties until now, um, you could really see the difference because right now, um, both women and men are allowed to work. Um, it's different. \n\nUh, we all have equal rights. LGBTQ is now accepted. Um, uh, we could all, uh,	280	4	At Band 5, a speaker uses a good range of grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes.\n\nYou're currently at 4/5 because you use a nice mix of simple and more complex grammar, and your vocabulary goes beyond basic words. For example, you said *'a more progressive way of thinking'*, *'fought for equality'*, *'the breadwinner'*, and *'provide for their family'*. These are great, specific words for B1 level. You also tried some complex forms like *'if you compare it to'* and *'you could see that... because...'*. Most of your sentences are accurate and easy to follow.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Check your verb tenses when talking about the past. You said *'they make money'* in a story about the past — the correct form is 'they made money' or 'they would make money'. Practice matching your tense to the time you are talking about.\n\n- Expand your vocabulary range. You used some great words, but you repeated 'difference' and 'see' several times. Try using more precise words like 'change', 'shift', or 'progress' instead of always saying 'difference'.\n\n- Watch your word order in comparisons. You said *'if you compare it to the older generation'* — this is fine, but a more natural way is 'compared to the older generation' or 'when you compare them to the older generation'. Try to keep the two things you are comparing clear.\n\nYou have a strong vocabulary and good control of grammar. Keep practicing those small details — you're almost there!	3	At Band 5, a speaker produces long, well-organised speech with a clear structure and a range of linking words.\n\nYou're currently at 3/5 because you produce extended speech and your ideas are mostly relevant, but your response feels a bit list-like and has some hesitation. You have a clear structure: you give your opinion, talk about a study, and then compare Australia and Thailand. You use some good linking words like *'if you compare it'*, *'because'*, *'but'*, and *'so'*. However, you use the filler word *'um'* very often (I counted 15 times), and you sometimes repeat yourself, like *'you could see that... you could see that'* and *'you could really see the difference because right now... it's different'*. This makes the speech feel a bit choppy.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on these two things:\n\n- Reduce your use of 'um'. Try pausing silently instead of saying *'um'*. For example, instead of *'Um, some people say...'*, just pause for a second and then say 'Some people say...'. Practice recording yourself and counting your 'um's.\n\n- Avoid repeating the same idea. You said *'you could see the difference'* twice in the same paragraph. Instead, try to develop the idea: 'You can see how much things have changed. For example, now both men and women can work, which is a big difference from the past.'\n\nYour ideas are interesting and you have a good structure. With less hesitation and more development, your speaking will be much smoother!	5	You have a very natural, fluent delivery — your voice comes across as confident and easy to follow. There are just a few small areas to focus on to polish your pronunciation further.\n\nOne thing to watch is the s and z sounds. In the word use /juːz/, the final sound should be voiced — it should feel like a gentle buzz, not a hiss. Practise making that z sound clear at the end.\n\nYou also dropped the final /t/ in traits /treɪts/. Make sure that last consonant is fully pronounced — it should be crisp and audible, not swallowed.\n\nAnd finally, you pronounced women as if it were the singular woman /ˈwʊmən/. The correct pronunciation for the plural is women /ˈwɪmɪn/, with a short i sound in the first syllable, like the i in "win". Try saying it slowly a few times to get the vowel difference.\n\nYou're doing really well — your speech is natural and easy to listen to. A bit of focused practice on these points will make it even stronger.	\N	\N	16	Hi Taia, you're working at a solid B2 level in speaking. I really like your vocabulary: words like *'progressive'*, *'breadwinner'*, and *'fought for equality'* show you can express your ideas clearly. Your comparison between Australia and Thailand is interesting and well-structured.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Reduce your use of 'um' by pausing silently instead.\n\n- Develop your ideas more instead of repeating the same point. For example, after saying things are different now, you could add a specific example.\n\nYou have great ideas and good language. Keep practicing!	B2	Speaking	CA	PET	121	https://drive.google.com/file/d/17FNiQYDDQArphcTjdhqT4GNMGo7S8tp7/view
33919	2026-06-24	2026-06-24	2027	Are Gen Z Thais more progressive or traditional than the older generations?	In the past Thai people always believed that uh men should be a leader and women should follow it. But now the things are changing As I see it um Gen Z right now have a phone so they can know their freedom or equality and ah they are supported marriage equality so they know their freedom like they can be what they want to be. They can the job the tenor cannot decide a job and cannot decide the way you live. So um Gency. ...... \n\nLast year I go to the Siamscape and I saw a boot that student was making. It's made a uh gentle dress that everyone can wear what what they want to wear and they can show it by themselves it show that you can be themselves and is this true that all old generation thinks that agenda was wrong uh other genders was wrong But I think if agency chaining a dang mindset it can be great. That's his teacher.	168	2	At Band 5, a speaker uses a good range of grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes. They can use complex sentences and specific words naturally.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your sentences often have errors that make the meaning unclear. For example, you said *'men should be a leader and women should follow it'* — the word 'it' doesn't match 'women'. You also said *'They can the job the tenor cannot decide a job'* — this sentence is very hard to follow. On the positive side, you used *'equality'* and *'freedom'* correctly, which shows you have the right topic words.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Check your verb tenses. You said *'Last year I go to the Siamscape'* — this should be 'Last year I went to Siamscape'. Practice using past tense for finished actions.\n\n- Choose the right words. You said *'a boot'* — you probably mean 'a booth' or 'a stall'. Also, *'agenda'* was used instead of 'gender'. Try to learn the exact words for your ideas.\n\n- Remove extra words from your sentences. You said *'They can the job the tenor cannot decide a job'* — try simplifying to 'They can't decide a job' or 'They cannot choose a job'.\n\nKeep going! Your ideas about equality are interesting, and with better grammar and word choice, your speaking will be much clearer.	2	At Band 5, a speaker produces long, well-organised speech with clear connections between ideas. They use words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example' to link their points.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your response is a bit short — about 73 seconds instead of the expected 2 minutes — and your ideas are sometimes hard to follow. You had a long pause after *'So um Gency'*, which shows you were searching for what to say next. I also noticed you jumped from Gen Z having phones to a booth at Siamscape without explaining the link, and you repeated the same idea about freedom and equality a few times. One thing you did well: you used *'but now the things are changing'* to show a contrast, which is a good start with linking words.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Speak for longer. Your recording was only 73 seconds, but the task asks for about 2 minutes. Try to add more details: 'Last year I went to Siamscape and saw a booth where students were making gender-neutral clothes. This shows that young people support equality.'\n- Use linking words to connect your ideas. You said *'But now the things are changing'* and then jumped to Gen Z. Try: 'However, things are changing because Gen Z has access to information on their phones.' This makes the connection clear.\n\nYou have good ideas about equality. Try to explain each idea more fully and connect them with simple words like 'because' and 'so'.	4	Your pronunciation is coming along really well. Your linking between words sounds good, and your syllable stress is generally fine too — that's great progress.\n\nOne thing to focus on now is making your consonant sounds stronger. Some of them are a bit too soft, and they need to be clearer. For example, the /t/ in support /səˈpɔːt/ should be more noticeable. The final /d/ in decide /dɪˈsaɪd/ also needs to be heard clearly. The /p/ in escape /ɪˈskeɪp/ and the /k/ in think /θɪŋk/ are two more sounds that you should push a little harder.\n\nTry saying these words slowly at first. Make sure every consonant comes through. You're doing a good job — keep practising and you'll get even better!	\N	\N	11	Hi Fuji,\n\nYou're working at an A2+++ level in speaking. I really like that you have a clear opinion about equality and freedom: you said *'they can be what they want to be'* and that's a great message! You also used the word *'equality'* correctly, which shows you're thinking about important topics.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Speak for the full 2 minutes by adding more details and examples.\n\n- Check your grammar, especially past tense (like 'I went' not 'I go').\n\nYou have interesting ideas. Keep practicing!	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	73	https://drive.google.com/file/d/1GqX1oL_FJY0PhUEQnkmeoAuPJkt3dhJu/view
34174	2026-06-24	2026-06-24	2027	Are Gen Z Thais more progressive or traditional than the older generations?	Uh, why Gen X people are more progressive in Thailand. It seems to be like Gen Z people are more progressive because uh, they have uh. Have a more open minded about traditional or what adult thinking. ... \n\nFirst point is gently people in Thailand to have um. M. Um. ......\nI forgot teacher just talk about your friend. Um, example like my friend, uh, who's like argue with his dad like his dad say ah school should still have a ah uniform because they easy to identify what school they are. Um, and it's formal, it's polite. But my friend said um, students should have their ... own shirt to be a school like something like they should wear whatever they want like that. I think I agree with my friend. \n\nUltimately. Um, I think ... my friend do do have a point with this.	145	2	You're at 2/5 because your sentences often have mistakes that make the meaning unclear. For example, you said *'Gen Z people are more progressive because uh, they have uh. Have a more open minded'* — the word 'have' is used twice and the phrase 'more open minded' is missing a word like 'are'. You also said *'gently people in Thailand to have'* — the word 'gently' doesn't fit here, and the sentence is not complete. But you did use *'progressive'* which is a good word for this topic, and your example about your friend shows you can share a real idea.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Fix your verb forms. You said *'my friend do do have a point'* — try saying **'my friend has a point'** or **'my friend does have a point'**.\n\n- Choose the right words. You said *'gently people'* — the correct word is probably **'older people'** or **'elderly people'**. Try to learn common word groups like 'older generation' or 'young people'.\n\n- Complete your sentences. You said *'they have uh. Have a more open minded'* — try to finish the idea: 'they have a more open mind' or 'they are more open-minded'.\n\nKeep practicing! Your ideas are interesting, and with better grammar and word choice, your speaking will be much clearer.	1	At Band 5, a speaker answers the question directly, with ideas that match the topic and are clearly connected.\n\nYou're at 1/5 because your answer was off-topic. The question asked whether Gen Z Thais are more **progressive or traditional than the older generations** specifically about **gender roles and attitudes**. Instead, you talked about school uniforms and general values. You said *'my friend argue with his dad about school uniform'* and *'students should wear whatever they want'* — this is about school rules, not about gender attitudes or progressive vs traditional views on gender.\n\nTo reach a higher score, work on these two things:\n\n- Read the question closely. The key words were **'gender roles'** and **'progressive or traditional'**. Before you speak, ask: 'What kind of examples fit this?' For gender attitudes, you could have said: **'Gen Z Thais are more progressive because they support equal marriage and LGBTQ+ rights, while older generations think men should be the breadwinner.'**\n\n- Stick to one topic. You started with a general idea about progressive vs traditional, but then switched to school uniforms. Keep your examples focused on the exact question. If the question is about gender, every example should be about gender.\n\nYou tried to give a personal story, which is a good technique — but make sure it matches the question next time!	4	Your voice is clear and easy to follow, which is great. To make your speaking even better, try to link your words together more when you talk. This means not pausing too much between words, so your sentences flow smoothly.\n\nAlso, you need to speak a little louder. It was sometimes hard to hear you. Try to raise your volume so your words are strong and easy to catch.\n\nKeep practicing these two things, and your speaking will sound even more natural and confident. You're doing well!	\N	\N	9	Hi Krabi,\n\nYou're working at an A2 level in speaking. You tried to give a personal example, which is a good technique, but your answer was off-topic. The question asked about **gender roles and attitudes**, but your example was about school uniforms. That's why your discourse score is low.\n\nTo improve, focus on two things:\n\n- Read the question carefully. If it asks about gender roles, make sure your examples are about gender — not school rules or general values.\n\n- Reduce your 'um' and 'uh' fillers. Try pausing silently instead.\n\nNext time, check that your example matches the question before you speak!	A2	Speaking	CA	PET	120	https://drive.google.com/file/d/1-NNLSCihie0VyQzMDCZq3F17ESnKrvui/view
35995	2026-06-26	2026-06-26	2027	What makes you stressed?	One thing that really stressed me out is studying because ... if the teacher was strict and in a strict environment, ... classroom could be like really, really like no fun and no emotion.\n\n<br><br>\n\nThe reason is, uh, ...... The reason is, uh, if the teacher came and like, ... example, if the math teacher is restrict the classroom	57	2	A good speaker at Band 5 uses different grammar and words with only small mistakes. They can use different tenses and sentence types naturally. You are at 2/5 because your sentences often have mistakes that make the meaning unclear. For example, you said *'if the teacher was strict and in a strict environment'*: this mixes 'was' (past) with 'is' (present) in the same sentence. You also said *'if the math teacher is restrict the classroom'*: this has two verbs ('is' and 'restrict') together without the right form.\n\nTo get a better score, try these two things:\n\n- Fix your verb forms. You said *'if the teacher came and like, example'*: this should be **'if the teacher came and, for example'** or **'if the teacher comes and, for example'**. Practice using one time (past or present) in each sentence.\n\n- Choose the right words. You said *'classroom could be like, really, really like no fun'*: this should be **'the classroom could be really boring and unemotional'**. Try to use specific words instead of repeating 'like'.\n\nKeep trying! Your idea about strict teachers is clear, and with better grammar your speaking will be much stronger.	2	A good answer at Band 5 is long and clear. It has a beginning, middle, and end. The speaker uses words like 'because', 'for example', and 'however' to join their ideas. You are at 2/5 because your answer is very short and has many pauses. You said *'One thing that really stressed me out is studying'* and then you said *'The reason is, uh'* two times with a long silence. You did not finish your second sentence. The listener has to guess what you mean.\n\nTo get a better score, try these two things:\n\n- Speak for longer. You said about 57 words in 61 seconds. That is very short. Try to give two or three reasons. For example, after *'if the teacher was strict'*, you could say: **'because strict teachers don't let students talk or ask questions, so the class feels boring'**.\n\n- Use fewer pauses. You said *'uh'* and *'like'* many times. You also had a long silence (......) in the middle. Instead of saying *'uh'*, try to stop, take a breath, and then continue with your idea. Practice saying: **'The reason is that if the teacher is very strict, the classroom has no fun and no emotion.'**\n\nYou have a good start! With more practice, your answers will be longer and easier to understand.	4	Your pronunciation is really good overall. You speak clearly and it's easy to understand you.\n\nOne thing to work on is the word *emotion* /ɪˈməʊʃən/. You put a little too much stress on the last part of the word. Try to keep the stress more on the middle of the word, and let the ending be softer.\n\nYou're doing a great job. Keep practicing, and this small change will help your speech sound even more natural.	\N	\N	11	Hi Porsche,\n\nYou're working at an A2+ level in speaking. I like that you have a clear idea: you said *'strict teachers make the classroom no fun'* and that's a good start!\n\nTo get to Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your grammar. Make sure your verbs match the time (like 'the teacher is strict', not 'the teacher is restrict').\n\n- Try to speak for longer. Give two or three reasons and use words like 'because' and 'for example' to connect them.\n\nYou have good ideas. Keep practicing!	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	61	\N
30321	2026-06-26	2026-06-26	2027	What makes you stressed?	One thing that really stress me out is ... when I have to do too much homework because first, um, ... a lot of homework is give me a headache and it's make me so tired.\n\n<br><br>\nFor example, when teacher give me some homework, then will have another teacher give me too, so I will so lazy to do ... my homework, but sometime I will do it myself, but sometime I will copy my friends.	76	2	At Band 5, a speaker uses good grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes. You're at 2/5 because your sentences often have errors that make it hard to understand. For example, you said *'a lot of homework is give me a headache'* — this mixes the words 'is' and 'give' together. You also said *'it's make me so tired'* — this should be 'it makes me' or 'it makes me feel'. Another example is *'then will have another teacher give me too'* — the subject 'I' or 'we' is missing here.\n\nTo get a higher score, work on these two things:\n\n- Fix your verb forms. You said *'is give me a headache'* — this should be **'gives me a headache'** or **'causes me to have a headache'**. Practice using one verb at a time.\n\n- Check your subject-verb agreement. You said *'One thing that really stress me out'* — this should be **'One thing that really stresses me out'** because 'thing' is singular. Also *'teacher give me'* should be **'a teacher gives me'**.\n\nKeep trying! Your ideas are clear, and with better grammar, your speaking will be much stronger.	2	At Band 5, a speaker gives long, clear answers with good linking words like 'because', 'for example', and 'also'. You're at 2/5 because your answer is short and your ideas don't connect well. You spoke for 47 seconds — that's less than the 60 seconds we expect. You said *'One thing that really stress me out is when I have to do too much homework'* and then gave one example, but you didn't add more ideas. You used 'because' and 'for example', which is good, but the second part of your answer feels separate from the first part.\n\nTo get a higher score, work on these two things:\n\n- Speak for longer. You stopped after 47 seconds. Try to add more details. For example, after you say you copy your friends, you could say **'I know this is not good, but I feel I have no choice because I am too tired.'** This would make your answer longer and more complete.\n\n- Use more linking words. You used 'because' and 'for example', which is a good start. Try adding words like **'also'** or **'another problem is'** to connect your ideas better. For example: **'Too much homework gives me a headache. Also, it makes me feel very tired, so I sometimes copy my friends.'**\n\nYou have a clear main idea. Keep practicing to make your answers longer and better connected!	2	Your speaking is clear and easy to follow. You try to link your words together, which is good. Keep practising this — it will help your speech flow better.\n\nOne thing to work on is your word endings. You often push too hard on the last part of words. For example, in the word *copy* /'kɒpi/, the stress should be even, not too strong at the end. Try to make the whole word feel more relaxed.\n\nYou also have trouble with the *st* sound together. In *first* /fɜːst/, make sure you say both the /s/ and the /t/ clearly. Practise saying it slowly: *first*.\n\nFinally, you drop the last consonant in some words. For example, in *headache* /'hedeɪk/ and *tired* /taɪəd/, the final /k/ and /d/ sounds are missing. Try to finish each word fully.\n\nYou are doing well. Keep working on these small points, and your speaking will sound even better.	\N	\N	8	Hi Alin,\n\nYou're working at an A2 level in speaking. I like that you have a clear main idea: you said *'too much homework'* stresses you out, and you gave an example. That's a good start! You also used the word 'headache', which is a good word to describe how you feel.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your grammar. Make sure your verbs match your subjects (like 'it makes', not 'it make').\n\n- Try to speak for longer and use words like 'also' or 'another problem is' to connect your ideas.\n\nYou have good ideas. Keep practicing!	A2	Speaking	CA	PET	47	\N
