ulary, your speaking will be much stronger.	2	At Band 5, a speaker talks for a long time and organises their ideas well. They use words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example' to connect their ideas.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your answer is short (68 seconds) and you say the same things again and again. You said *'my math homework is very stressful'* and later *'it's very stressful for me'* — this is the same idea. You also have many 'uh' sounds and long pauses like '...' which make your speech hard to follow. You tried to give an example (*'for example, when I get my math homework'*), but you didn't tell us more about it.\n\nHere are two things to work on:\n\n- Plan your answer before you speak. Think of 2-3 different points. For example: 'First, the homework is hard. Second, I have to do it every day. Third, the teacher gets angry if I don't finish.' This will help you avoid repeating yourself.\n\n- Reduce your 'uh' sounds and long pauses. Try pausing silently instead of saying 'uh'. Practice talking about a topic for 1 minute without stopping. Record yourself and listen for the 'uh' sounds.\n\nYou have a clear topic, and with more structure and fewer hesitations, your speaking will be much easier to follow!	3	You're doing a good job with your speaking. Your voice is clear and easy to follow. There are a few small things to work on that will help you sound even better.\n\nSometimes you drop the /d/ sound in the word *understand* /ˌʌndəˈstænd/. Make sure you say that final /d/ clearly at the end. Also, watch the final /k/ sound in words like *homework* /ˈhoʊmwɜːrk/. Try to keep that /k/ sound strong so it doesn't disappear.\n\nAnother thing is that you push too hard on the last part of some words. For example, you overstress the final syllable in *immediately* /ɪˈmiːdiətli/ and *teacher* /ˈtiːtʃər/. Instead of making the end of the word very strong, try to keep the stress more even across the whole word. This will make your speech sound more relaxed and natural