 make me hurt'*. You could add more: *'He gets angry when he loses, and then he hits me. This makes me feel stressed because I don't feel safe.'*\n\nYour ideas are clear and interesting. Keep practicing to make your speech smoother!	3	You’re doing really well — your voice is clear and easy to understand. There are just a couple of things to work on to sound even more natural.\n\nYou often push too hard on the last part of words. This happens a lot with words like brother /ˈbrʌðə/, reason /ˈriːzən/, angry /ˈæŋgri/, and example /ɪɡˈzɑːmpəl/. Try to keep the stress more even across the whole word. Don’t push the ending so much — let it be softer.\n\nAlso, watch the “th” sound. In your recording, the word thing sounds like “ting.” The “th” sound needs a little more air between your tongue and your top teeth. Try saying thing /θɪŋ/ slowly, and feel the air move.\n\nYou’re making good progress — keep practicing, and these small changes will make a big difference!	\N	\N	12	Hi Tee,\n\nYou're doing a good job in speaking! I really like that you gave two clear reasons and an example to explain why your brother stresses you out. You said *'he likes to talk a lot about his friend'* — that's a nice, clear sentence!\n\nTo get a higher score, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your grammar: remember to use the verb 'to be' (like 'he is angry', not 'he angry') and use articles like 'a' and 'the'.\n\n- Try to speak more smoothly by reducing filler words like 'uh' and 'like'. Take a quiet breath instead.\n\nYou have a good story to tell. Keep practicing and you will get even better!	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	52	\N
