our speaking will be much stronger.	3	You have a clear structure in your talk. You started with your main idea – *'one thing that keep me stressed is homework'* – then gave two reasons, and finished with an example. That's good organisation! You also used basic linking words like 'and', 'but', and 'for example'. This helps the listener follow your ideas. However, you used filler words like *'um'* (4 times) and *'uh'* (once), and you had a short pause before *'when teacher give me too much homework'*. These make your speech sound less smooth.\n\nTo make your speech smoother, try these two things:\n\n- Reduce filler words. Instead of saying *'um'* or *'uh'*, try pausing silently for a moment to think. For example, instead of *'Um, one thing that...'*, you could say **'One thing that keeps me stressed is...'** and then pause briefly before continuing.\n\n- Use more linking words to connect your ideas. You said *'And the second reason is...'*: try using **'The second reason is that...'** or **'Another reason is...'** to vary your language. You could also add a conclusion, like **'So, that's why homework makes me stressed.'**\n\nYou have a clear story to tell. Keep practicing to make your speech smoother!	3	Your pronunciation is coming along really well. You make a good effort to connect your words when you speak, which helps your English flow more smoothly.\n\nOne thing to work on is how you say the ends of words. You often say the final syllable too strongly, like in **teacher** /ˈtiːtʃə/ and **lazy** /ˈleɪzi/. Try to make the last part of these words softer and quieter. Think of it like the sound just fades away at the end.\n\nKeep practicing, and you will sound more relaxed and natural. You are doing a great job!	\N	\N	11	Hi Tul,\n\nYou're working hard and it shows! I really like that you gave a clear example from your own life: you said *'2 days ago I'm going to play live music, so I didn't have time to do my homework'*. That makes your story real and easy to understand! You also used the word *'literally'* which is a good, strong word.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your grammar. Make sure your subjects and verbs match (like 'the teacher gives', not 'teacher give').\n\n- Try to reduce filler words like 'um' and 'uh' by pausing silently instead. This will make your speech sound smoother.\n\nYou have good ideas and a clear structure. Keep practicing!	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	59	\N
