try **'When I study...'** or **'After I study...'**. This sounds more natural.\n\n- Add more detail to your ideas. You said *'I feel very sleepy'* but didn't say why. Try: **'I feel very sleepy because I study late at night'** or **'I feel very sleepy because the subjects are boring'**.\n\nYour story is easy to follow. With more linking words, it will sound even better!	3	You're doing a really nice job with your speaking. Your voice is clear and easy to understand. There are a few things you can work on to sound even better.\n\nFirst, you often drop the last consonant sound in words. For example, in words like *exams*, *get*, and *good*, the final sound doesn't come through. Try to say the last sound clearly, even if it feels small.\n\nSecond, you don't link your words together very much yet. Linking means connecting the end of one word to the start of the next word, like "get up" sounding like "ge-tup". Practise saying short phrases slowly and smoothly.\n\nThird, you push too hard on the final part of some words. This happens with words like *remember*, *sleepy*, *study*, *happy*, and *excited*. Instead of making the end of the word strong, try to keep the whole word even and soft at the end.\n\nYou're making good progress. Keep practising these small points, and your speaking will sound more relaxed and clear.	\N	\N	11	Hi Poch,\n\nYou're working at an A2+ level in speaking. I really like that you told a story with a clear beginning, middle, and end. You said *'Let me tell you a story'* and then explained what happened. That's a great way to organise your speech! You also used good words like *'stressed'* and *'happy'* to describe your feelings.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your verb forms. Say **'studying for exams'** instead of 'study for exam'.\n\n- Try to use more linking words like 'because' or 'so' instead of always saying 'and'.\n\nYou have good ideas. Keep practicing!	A2+	Speaking	CA	PET	71	\N
36018	2026-06-26	2026-06-26	2027	What makes you stressed?	One— the one thing that really stresses me out is ... my younger brother. ... Uh, first reason is he likes to make a lot of weird and strange noises at home. Or another reason is he doesn't let me concentrate on my homework or my online classes when I'm at home.\n\n<br><br>\nFor example, last week when I came home ... He used to, um, he annoyed me by making loud and great noises. And when I went up to my room, I was doing my, I was doing my homework, and he was, um, annoying me, like not letting me concentrate on my homework.	106	4	At Band 5, a speaker uses a good range of grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes.\n\nYou're at 4/5 because your grammar is mostly accurate and you use some good vocabulary. You said *'he likes to make a lot of weird and strange noises'* — this is clear and correct. You also used *'he doesn't let me concentrate'* and *'he annoyed me'*, which show good control of present and past tenses. Your use of *'stresses me out'* is a nice natural phrase.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these three things:\n\n- Check your word choice. You said *'loud and great noises'*. 'Great' doesn't fit here because it has a positive meaning. Try **'loud and strange noises'** or **'loud and annoying noises'** instead.\n\n- Expand your vocabulary range. You repeated *'annoyed'* and *'annoying'* several times. Try using other words like 'irritating', 'frustrating', or 'distracting' to add variety.\n\n- Watch your verb forms when mixing past structures. You said *'He used to, um, he annoyed me'*. This mixes two different past forms. Choose one: **'He used to annoy me'** (habit in the past) or **'He annoyed me'** (a specific time).\n\nYou're doing well — your meaning is always clear, and with small fixes your English will sound even more natural.	3	At Band 5, a speaker produces extended speech with a clear structure and uses linking words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example' to connect ideas smoothly.\n\nYou're at 3/5 because your response is relevant and has a clear main idea — your younger brother causes you stress. You made a good start with *'One thing that really stresses me out is my younger brother'*. You also used helpful phrases like *'first reason is'* and *'or another reason is'* to organise your ideas. However, the speech is quite short and you repeated the same point: you said *'he annoyed me by making loud noises'* and then *'annoying me, like not letting me concentrate'* — these are very similar. You also used filler words like *'um'* and *'like'* several times, which made your speech sound less confident.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on these two things:\n\n- Develop your ideas with more detail. Instead of saying 'he annoyed me' twice, try adding a new piece of information: **'He was making loud noises while I was trying to do my homework. This made it really hard for me to focus on my work.'** This adds a specific context and explains the result.\n\n- Reduce filler words like 'um' and 'like' by pausing silently. For example, instead of *'He was, um, annoying me, like not letting me concentrate'*, try: **'He was annoying me. For example, he wouldn't let me concentrate on my homework.'** This sounds more polished and confident.\n\nYou have a clear topic and good ideas. Keep practicing to make your speech longer and smoother!	4	Your delivery is already very good — you speak with a clear, confident tone that makes it easy to follow your ideas. To take it to the next level, try incorporating a few more pauses into your speech. Pausing not only gives you a moment to gather your thoughts, but it also helps your listener absorb what you've just said, especially before an important point or after a key idea.\n\nYou could also work on using your intonation more expressively. Right now, your voice stays fairly steady throughout, but by varying your pitch more — rising a little to show surprise or interest, and dropping to signal certainty or seriousness — you can convey much more emotion and meaning. Think of your voice as a tool for highlighting the feeling behind your words, not just the words themselves. A small lift on a key word or a slight slowdown before a strong opinion can make your message much more engaging. You're on the right track — just a few adjustments will make your speaking feel more natural and connected to your listener.	\N	\N	15	Hi Pooh,\n\nYou're working at a solid B1++ level in speaking. I really like that you chose a clear topic and gave a specific example: you said *'last week when I came home... he annoyed me by making loud noises'*. That's a great way to make your story feel real and personal! You also used some good vocabulary like *'stresses me out'* and *'weird and strange noises'*.\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Add more details to your examples so your speech is longer and more interesting. You repeated the same idea about your brother being annoying, but you could have explained *why* it was hard to concentrate or *what* you were trying to do.\n\n- Reduce filler words like 'um' and 'like' by pausing silently instead. This will make you sound more confident and fluent.\n\nYou have good ideas and your English is clear. Keep practicing!	B1+	Speaking	CA	PET	51	\N
