your speaking will be even stronger.	3	At Band 5, a speaker talks for a long time with clear organisation, uses different linking words, and is easy to follow.\n\nYou're at 3/5 because you talked for a long time and stayed on topic. You used good linking words like *'because'*, *'and'*, and *'another reason is'*. But you said the same idea (not having time for homework) three times without adding new details. The two pauses in your speech (marked `...`) show you were thinking, but they didn't stop the flow.\n\nTo reach Band 5, work on these two things:\n\n- Use more linking words like 'however', 'for example', or 'in addition' instead of just 'and'. For example: 'In addition to homework, I also have chores like walking my dog.'\n- Add more details to your ideas. Instead of saying 'I don't have time' three times, tell one story with more information. For example: 'One time, I had to finish a project but I also had to walk my dog. I felt very stressed because I couldn't do both well.'\n\nYou made a good start with your restaurant story. Keep practicing to make your speaking more organised!	3	Your speaking is clear and easy to follow. You have a good, steady voice. One thing to work on is how you connect your words. Right now, your speech sounds a little choppy, like each word is separate. Try to link your words together more, so your sentences flow smoothly.\n\nAnother small point is the word *eating*. You put a little too much stress on the end of it. Try to keep the stress even across the whole word. This is not a big problem, so don't worry too much.\n\nYou are doing a good job. Keep practising, and your speaking will sound more and more natural.	\N	\N	12	Hi Benz,\n\nYou're doing a good job at B1 level in speaking. I really like that you gave a clear example about the restaurant with your dad: *'when we finished eating, it's already midnight'*. That story makes your point about being stressed very clear!\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Check your verb tenses when you tell a story (use past tense all the way through).\n\n- Try to use more linking words like 'however' or 'for example' to connect your ideas.\n\nYou have good ideas and you communicate well. Keep practicing!	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	67	\N
36111	2026-06-26	2026-06-26	2027	What makes you stressed?	The one thing that got me stressed is math because the teacher is so strict. And the second reason is when, when I don't do my homework, the teacher give me a minus point. And when I forgot to do a lot of homework, teacher give me a lot of minus point. And sometimes when I forgot to do the work in class and I don't understand something, teacher sometimes don't teach me.\n\n<br><br>\n\nAnd in the ...... Recently, of my last one, ...... uh, exam always last year, I forgot to do homework and last week I got a lot of money for	106	2	At Band 5, a speaker uses a good range of grammar and vocabulary with only small mistakes. They can use different tenses and sentence types, and choose words that fit the topic well.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your sentences often have mistakes that make the meaning unclear. For example, you said *'the teacher give me a minus point'*: the verb 'give' should be 'gives' because 'teacher' is one person. You also said *'teacher sometimes don't teach me'*: this should be 'doesn't teach me'. Your vocabulary is very simple — you use words like 'strict', 'homework', and 'minus point' many times.\n\nHere are two things to work on:\n\n- Fix your subject-verb agreement. You said *'teacher give me a lot of minus point'*: this should be 'the teacher gives me a lot of minus points'. Practice matching singular subjects (he, she, it) with 's' on the verb.\n\n- Use more specific words. Instead of saying *'a lot of minus point'* every time, try 'many penalty points' or 'several deductions'. This will make your speaking sound more natural and interesting.\n\nKeep going! Your ideas about school stress are clear, and with better grammar and word choice, you can express them much more strongly.	2	At Band 5, a speaker talks for a long time and organises their ideas well. They use words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example' to connect their ideas smoothly.\n\nYou're at 2/5 because your answer is very short — it feels like a list. You said *'The one thing that got me stressed is math... And the second reason is... And when I forgot...'*: this is just listing things, not explaining them. You also had a long pause (marked as ......) and then you suddenly changed topic to *'exam always last year'* and *'I got a lot of money'*. This is confusing because your ideas don't connect clearly.\n\nHere are two things to work on:\n\n- Plan your answer before you speak. Think of 2-3 points and explain each one with an example. For example: 'Math stresses me because the teacher is very strict. For example, she gives minus points for late homework. This makes me feel worried.'\n\n- Use words to connect your ideas. Instead of just saying 'And... And... And...', try 'The first reason is... Another reason is... Also...' or 'Because of this...' This helps your listener follow your thinking.\n\nYou have good ideas about what stresses you. With a little planning, you can turn this into a clear answer!	3	Your voice is clear and you say each sound quite well. That's a good start. The main thing to work on is making your speech sound more lively and connected.\n\nRight now, your voice sounds very flat. Try to change your pitch a little when you speak. Go up and down a bit, like you are telling a story. This will make you sound more interested and interesting.\n\nYou also do very little linking between words. Each word sounds separate. In English, words often join together. For example, "get up" sounds like "ge-tup". Try to connect the end of one word to the start of the next word. This will help your speech flow better.\n\nKeep practising with your clear sounds. You are on the right track. Just add more feeling and more connections between your words.	\N	\N	9	Hi Wan,\n\nYou're working at an A2 level in speaking. I can see you have clear ideas about what stresses you at school, like math and homework. That's a good start!\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things:\n\n- Make sure your subjects and verbs match. For example, say 'the teacher gives', not 'the teacher give'.\n\n- Plan your answer so it has a clear beginning, middle, and end. Try to explain each point with an example.\n\nYou have good things to say. Keep practicing and you will improve quickly!	A2	Speaking	CA	PET	60	\N
