er.\n\n- Organise your ideas better. You listed problems like 'study a lot' and 'parent pressure'. Try using words like **'first'**, **'also'**, and **'finally'** to make your points clearer. For example: **'First, I have to study a lot. Also, my parents put pressure on me.'**\n\nYour topic is clear and you have good examples. Keep practicing!	3	Your voice is very flat, which makes it hard for the listener to follow what you are saying. Your sounds and stress are okay, but the lack of feeling in your voice blocks the message.\n\nTry to add more life to your speaking. When you say a sentence, make your voice go up and down a little. For example, go higher on the most important word. This will help your listener understand your main idea.\n\nYou can practise by reading a short sentence out loud. First, say it in a flat way. Then, say it again and push your voice up on one word. Listen to the difference. Keep trying this, and your speaking will become clearer and easier to understand. You are on the right track!	\N	\N	12	Hi Yada!\n\nYou did a great job talking about stress. I really liked your example from last year: *'when the grades came out, I got a very good grades'* — this made your answer personal and easy to understand. You also used the word *'pressure'* which is a good word for this topic.\n\nTo get a higher score, focus on two things:\n\n- Use fewer filler words like 'uh' and 'um' by pausing silently instead.\n\n- Check your grammar for small errors like 'a time' (just say 'time') and 'to reading' (say 'to read').\n\nYou have good ideas and a clear voice. Keep practicing!	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	63	\N
