iend Pete are clear and interesting.	2	At Band 5, a speaker produces extended speech with a clear structure, uses a range of linking words, and the listener can follow easily without effort.\n\nYou're currently at 2/5 because your response is quite short (83 seconds) and has many hesitations that break the flow. You said *'So it's supposed to be like, um, so they, ... they are like, um, ... so sorry'*: this shows you lost your train of thought and couldn't continue. The word 'like' is used as a filler many times, which makes your speech sound choppy. You also repeat yourself: *'They have, they have many creative views'*.\n\nHere are two things to work on:\n\n- Reduce filler words. You used 'like' and 'um' many times. Try pausing silently instead of saying 'um' or 'like'. Practice by recording yourself and counting your fillers.\n\n- Plan your ideas before you speak. You had a good start (Gen Z views on gender, creativity) and a good example (Pete), but you stopped in the middle. Try writing 3 short bullet points before you speak: idea 1, idea 2, example. This will help you keep going.\n\nYou have good content! With more practice, your speech will flow much better.	4	Here is the rewritten version, following your instructions for a warm, B1-level tone, prose paragraphs, and no added examples.\n\nYou're doing a really nice job with your speaking. Your intonation is good, which means you sound interested and natural when you talk. That's a great start. Just one small thing: try to speak a little louder. When you speak more quietly, your voice isn't as clear, and it can be harder to follow your ideas.\n\nAnother thing to work on is the final sound at the end of words, especially the s or z sound. In the word "perspectives," that ending sound is a bit soft and gets lost. You want to make sure people can hear it clearly. Try saying the word slowly: per-SPEC-tives, and really push that /z/ sound at the end. Keep practicing, and your speaking will get clearer and clearer. You're doing well!	\N	\N	12	Hi Yaya,\n\nYou're working at a B1 level in speaking. I really like that you have clear opinions: you said *'Thai adolescents accept other people's sexual orientation'* and you gave a real example with your friend Pete. That's great!\n\nTo reach Band 5, focus on two things: first, reduce filler words like 'um' and 'like' — try pausing silently instead. Second, plan your ideas before you speak so you don't get stuck in the middle. You have good ideas, and with smoother delivery, your speaking will be much stronger.\n\nKeep practicing!	B1	Speaking	CA	PET	83	\N
