ut different generations are interesting, and with better word choice, your speaking will be much clearer.	2	At Band 5, a speaker produces extended speech with a clear structure and uses words like 'because', 'however', and 'for example' to connect ideas smoothly.\n\nYou're currently at 2/5 because your response is short (79 seconds) and has many hesitations. You said *'I think, um, in, in this generation, um, maybe Gen Z, uh, in my generation is Gen Z'*: this has a lot of filler words like 'um' and 'uh' that break the flow. You also repeated *'I think'* many times. Your ideas are there but they feel disconnected: you talk about LGBTQ, then about a paper, but the link is not clear.\n\nHere are two things to work on:\n\n- Reduce filler words. You used 'um', 'uh', and 'like' many times, for example *'and like, um, our mindset is'*. Try to pause silently instead of saying 'um'. Practice by recording yourself and counting your fillers.\n\n- Connect your ideas better. You said *'I think it's a bit of different from the past, I think. And like, um, our mindset is so different'*: this jumps between topics. Use a linking word like **'For example'** or **'Also'** to show how your points are related.\n\nYou have good examples from your life. With fewer fillers and clearer connections, your speaking will be much stronger!	4	You're doing a really good job with your speaking. Your intonation sounds natural, and your word stress is okay. You also link some words together well, which helps your speech flow nicely.\n\nThere are a few small things you can work on. Sometimes you drop the last sound of a word. For example, the /t/ in point /pɔɪnt/ is not clear. The /s/ in difference /'dɪfərəns/ is missing, and the /d/ in upload /ʌp'ləʊd/ is not there. Also, the /z/ in says /sez/ needs to be heard. Try saying these words slowly and make sure you finish each sound.\n\nYou also make some long vowel sounds too short. Listen to your recording again and try to hold the long sounds a little 