smoothly between sentences and paragraphs. Words like "furthermore," "however," or "consequently" can really help your essay flow.	Kun, you have done a good job of addressing the essay question, discussing the influence of friends and parents on choices related to clothes, fashion, and studying. You even added your own thoughtful idea about career choices, which is excellent. 👍 To elevate your score, think about adding more depth to your arguments. For example, instead of just stating that parents might be upset by clothes, you could explain *why* they might feel that way, or give a specific scenario. Adding more specific details and examples would help the reader feel fully informed and truly understand your perspective.	You've made a good attempt to write an essay, which is the correct format for this task, and you've communicated your main ideas clearly. 😊 To make your writing even more effective, consider refining your tone to be consistently more formal. An essay at this level often benefits from a more academic style of language, rather than conversational phrases. For instance, instead of starting with "well, I'll be writing about...", you could use a phrase like "This essay will explore..." or "This report will examine..." This helps to set a more serious and appropriate tone for your reader.	Your essay has a clear structure, which is a great foundation. You have an introduction and separate paragraphs for each of your points, which helps the reader follow your arguments. 👏 To improve further, focus on making the connections between your ideas and paragraphs even smoother. While you use basic linking words like "first" and "then," try incorporating a greater variety of cohesive devices. For example, when moving from discussing fashion to studying, you could use a phrase such as "Turning our attention to academics..." or "In addition to fashion choices, another significant area of influence is..." This helps to guide the reader seamlessly through your essay. Sometimes, Thai learners might be a little too direct in their transitions, so thinking about how each paragraph relates to the previous one can be very helpful.	You use everyday words well, and your meaning generally comes across, which is very important. However, a key area for you to concentrate on is improving the accuracy of your grammar and spelling. For instance, you wrote "Teenager makes good and bad choices," but "Teenagers make" or "A teenager makes" would be grammatically correct. Also, be careful with common spelling mistakes like "writting" instead of "writing," and distinguishing between "affect" and "effect." Remember, "affect" is usually a verb (to influence), and "effect" is usually a noun (the result). Another common area to review is words that sound similar but have different spellings and meanings, like "their," "there," and "they're." For example, you wrote "went their has more effect on there study," which should be "when *friends* have more *effect* on *their* study." Many Thai learners sometimes make these kinds of errors because English pronunciation does not always clearly differentiate between homophones, so extra practice with spelling rules and common confusing words will be very beneficial. Keep working on these details, and your writing will become much more precise and impressive. ✨	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
