1091	35309	2025-08-21 15:44:29.390872+00	Social Issue	in my view, I strongly agree that students should be able to use mobile phones in classrooms. Firstly, our technology is way ahead of what we had before. According to my dad, who's an engineer, cellular devices weren't a thing before my generation, and he also told me that it's quite challenging to even find how to draw a car without searching online. Next, kids need online assistant on quite lot of stuff such as reading, listening, information gathering and overall entertainment. I couldn't survive a day without my phone, it helps me research informations on those difficult subjects like history, science etc. and also with AI assistant peoples work are always in the best qaulity. Lastly, the statement which, I saw online. According to World's Technology Association (WTA), kids with the access of cellular devices or online, tend to have a better discipline than the one that doesn't, because of how the phone's algorithm taught us to be discipline using notifications and more. Ultimately I think kids should be able to access to mobile phones in classes so it would be more compelling to attend classes and to do works.	191	14.0	<b>📝 Dear Atom</b><br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Social Issue'. You wrote 191 words and achieved B1. You presented a clear opinion with supporting ideas, showing a good grasp of English grammar and an ambition to use more complex structures.<br><br>	You presented a clear opinion and provided supporting ideas. The target reader is generally well-informed, but strengthening the direct connection between your reasons and your main opinion would make your arguments even more convincing.<br><b>Problem:</b> Your first supporting idea about your dad's generation and finding how to draw a car does not directly support why mobile phones should be allowed in classrooms.<br><b>Correction:</b> For example, you could write: "Firstly, the rapid advancement of technology means that mobile phones are now essential learning tools. My father, an engineer, often highlights how much more challenging it would have been to access information for his studies without modern cellular devices, demonstrating their value in today's educational environment."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Ensure your reasons directly answer or explain your main opinion. In English academic writing, each supporting point should clearly link back to your main argument.	You used the conventions of an academic paragraph to communicate your ideas. However, some of your supporting reasons didn't fully or clearly support your main opinion, which made your argument less impactful for the reader. This is a common point for B1 level writers to develop.<br><b>Problem:</b> In your second reason, you state, "Next, kids need online assistant on quite lot of stuff such as reading, listening, information gathering and overall entertainment. I couldn't survive a day without my phone, it helps me research informations on those difficult subjects like history, science etc." While it speaks to the usefulness of phones, the personal tone and the phrase "I couldn't survive a day without my phone" are too informal for an academic paragraph.<br><b>Correction:</b> You could revise this to be more formal and directly connected to classroom use: "Furthermore, mobile phones offer indispensable online assistance for students. They provide immediate access to resources for reading, listening, and information gathering, which is crucial for researching challenging subjects like history and science. The integration of AI assistants also ensures that students can produce work of the highest quality."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Academic writing in English typically requires a more formal and objective tone. Personal anecdotes and informal language can weaken the impact of your arguments. Focus on presenting information and arguments in a way that is suitable for a formal essay or report.	Your paragraph is connected and coherent, and you used basic linking words like "Firstly," "Next," and "Lastly" to guide the reader through your points. This is a good foundation. To achieve a higher score, try to use a wider variety of linking words and more complex cohesive devices to create an even smoother flow between your ideas.<br><b>Problem:</b> While you use good basic linking words, there are instances where sentences could be connected more smoothly for a richer flow, e.g., "Lastly, the statement which, I saw online. According to World's Technology Association (WTA)..."<br><b>Correction:</b> You could combine these thoughts with a more sophisticated linker: "Lastly, there is external validation for the benefits of mobile device access. For instance, according to the World's Technology Association (WTA), students with access to cellular devices or online resources tend to exhibit better discipline..."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, using a variety of cohesive devices (like conjunctions, transitional phrases, and pronouns) helps to create a more sophisticated and fluent text. It shows the relationship between ideas clearly and avoids a choppy feeling.	You used a range of everyday vocabulary appropriately, and you showed ambition in using both simple and some complex grammatical forms with good control. Your errors did not prevent the reader from understanding your message. This is a strong aspect of your writing. To further improve, pay close attention to minor errors, especially those common for Thai speakers.<br><b>Problem:</b> "in my view, I strongly agree that students should be able to use mobile phones in classrooms. Firstly, our technology is way ahead of what we had before." The sentence starts with a lowercase 'i'.<br><b>Correction:</b> "In my view, I strongly agree that students should be able to use mobile phones in classrooms. Firstly, our technology is way ahead of what we had before."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, every sentence must begin with a capital letter. This is a fundamental rule of punctuation.	4.0	3.0	3.0	4.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
