ongly about how wonderful pets can be. It's great to see your enthusiasm, and with a few adjustments, your writing can become even clearer and more impactful. Keep working on those writing skills, and you will certainly see improvement. 📋 Your action plan for improving your writing includes:<br>•\t✅ Carefully reading the whole question to make sure you have covered every part of the task.<br>•\t✅ Taking some time to check your spelling and punctuation after you have finished writing.<br>•\t✅ Practising writing with different sentence structures to make your ideas flow smoothly.	You've made a good start by sharing your strong opinion that owning a pet is a great idea, and your reasons for this are clear and interesting. However, the task asked you to consider both the advantages and disadvantages of having pets. You focused mostly on the positive aspects, which is understandable given your enthusiasm. Next time, try to remember that a balanced article for a magazine like this should explore both sides of the argument, even if you ultimately lean towards one. For example, you could have briefly mentioned things like the cost of food, the time needed for walks, or the responsibility of looking after them, even if it was just to say you think the advantages outweigh these things. This would ensure your article fully informs the reader, as mentioned in the B2 marking guide, "Minor irrelevances and/or omissions may be present. Target reader is on the whole informed."	You've certainly managed to keep the reader's attention, Fergie, especially with your wonderful story about Jean the golden retriever. It's a really touching and memorable part of your article, and those kinds of personal touches make your writing engaging and lively. 🎉 Your use of a question at the start also helps to draw the reader in. You've successfully communicated your ideas in a way that is easy to follow. To get an even higher score, you could work on refining the overall tone and style to make sure it consistently fits the expectations of an article for a school magazine. For instance, sometimes the language feels a little informal. The B2 marking guide notes, "Uses the conventions of the communicative task to hold the target reader's attention and communicate straightforward ideas."	Your article has a clear structure, which is a great foundation. You've included an introduction, body paragraphs to explain your points, and a conclusion, which helps the reader follow your arguments. You also used some linking words like "Firstly," "Besides," and "Lastly," which are helpful signposts for the reader. 👍 To improve, try to make sure your paragraphs are not too long, as very long paragraphs can sometimes make it harder for the reader to absorb your ideas. You could also experiment with a wider range of linking words and phrases to create an even smoother flow between your sentences and paragraphs. Thinking about how one idea naturally leads to the next will help make your article even more connected and coherent.	You use everyday vocabulary quite well to express your points, which is important for getting your message across. However, there are quite a few issues with spelling and grammar that sometimes make your ideas a little harder to understand. For instance, words like "pete" (which should be 'pet'), "Entertain" (should be 'entertain' and lowercase), and "hallarious" (should be 'hilarious') need a closer look. 🧐 It's really common for English learners, especially Thai learners, to sometimes misspell words that sound similar or to get their capitalisation a little mixed up, so you are not alone in this. Also, try to break down some of your longer sentences, as they can sometimes run on a bit, making them tricky to read. For example, instead of "all pets are a Great companion here's why because they will always keep you Entertain," you could write, "All pets are great companions because they will always keep you entertained." Pay close attention to your punctuation, too, as this helps to separate your thoughts and make your sentences clearer. A good tip is to read your work aloud; often, you can hear where a sentence needs a break or a word sounds a bit off. The B2 marking guide states that at this level, "While errors are noticeable, meaning can still be determined," but aiming to reduce these will definitely help your score.	2.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
