3094	29832	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	Hi Maya, I have reading about your email already and I recommend you to go to stay hostel because the price didn't cost much as the hotel and it near the school too. If you want to learn about Thai history you should go to the museum intest. It abouts 30 min. walk from school. You can ask diretion with people outthere. In the museum has many historic storys in there maybe you will know what your want in there too! And last I will give you about body-language tips. First when you come to my school you should "wa-num-mee" to say hello. And when you want to ask something plese raise your hand. I hope you will have a good memory in there.<br><br>See you,<br><br>Nene	125	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2	2.28	Hi Nene! I really enjoyed reading your advice for Maya. You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used excellent topic-specific words like "hostel", "recommend", "historic", and "museum" to give very clear advice.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> You successfully attempted a complex conditional sentence: "If you want to learn about Thai history you should go...", which shows you are trying to use more advanced structures!</li>  <li>💪 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You used several connectors like "because", "and", and "too" to join your ideas together into longer sentences.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Using "There is/There are" instead of "Have"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"In the museum has many historic storys in there maybe you will know what your want in there too!"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"<b>There are</b> many historic <b>stories in the museum</b>; maybe you will <b>find</b> what you want there too!"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, we use the word "มี" for everything. You might think "In the museum has..." because in Thai we say "ในพิพิธภัณฑ์มี" (nai pipitapan mee). But in English, if you are saying something exists in a place, we use <b>"There is"</b> or <b>"There are"</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Missing the "is/am/are" (BE verb)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It abouts 30 min. walk from school."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It <b>is about a 30-minute</b> walk from school."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can describe something directly, like "มันไกล" (man glai). You don't need a connecting verb. In English, every sentence needs a main verb. When you describe a distance or a location, you must include <b>"is"</b>. Also, remember that "about" is not a verb, so we don't add "s" to it!</li><li><b>📝 Spelling Patterns and Plurals</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"If you want to learn about Thai history you should go to the museum intest."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"If you want to learn about Thai history, you should go to the museum <b>instead</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> English spelling can be tricky! You also wrote "storys" and "diretion". For words ending in -y like "story", when we make them plural, we usually change the -y to <b>-ies</b> ("stories"). Keep an eye on those endings!</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! You have a great vocabulary, so once we fix these small patterns, your writing will be excellent. 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>6</b> sentences in this section, and most of them helped communicate your ideas clearly.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 3 instances (e.g., "intest", "diretion")</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 3 instances (e.g., "have reading", "abouts")</li><li><b>Preposition Errors:</b> 2 instances (e.g., "ask... with people")</li><li><b>Missing Verbs:</b> 1 instance (missing "is")</li><li><b>Noun Number (Plurals):</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
