xhibitions</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, the noun doesn't change when there is more than one thing (เช่น "พิพิธภัณฑ์หลายแห่ง"). In English, if you are talking about more than one, you must add an "-s" to the end of the noun. You also did this with "stranger" later in the text—it should be "strangers."</li><li><b>📝 Natural Verb Patterns (recommend/suggest)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"First of all, I recommend you to stay at a hostel since it's quite cheap and convinince because ites close to grocery shop."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"First of all, I recommend <b>staying</b> at a hostel since it's quite cheap and <b>convenient</b> because <b>it is</b> close to <b>a</b> grocery shop."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You used "recommend you to," which is a direct translation of "แนะนำให้คุณ...". However, in English, after the verbs "recommend" or "suggest," we usually use the <b>-ing</b> form of the next verb (recommend <b>staying</b> / suggest <b>going</b>). Also, notice that "convenient" is the adjective form needed to describe the hostel, not the noun "convenience" (ความสะดวกสบาย).</li><li><b>📝 Prepositions after Verbs</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Ultimately, There are fue tips on what you should do and shouldn't do, such as you shouldn't point your finger at stranger or someone who is older than you because they will misunderstand and assume that you are gossiping them!"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Finally, there are <b>a few</b> tips on what you should and shouldn't do. For example, you shouldn't point your finger at <b>strangers</b> or someone who is older than you because they might misunderstand and assume that you are gossiping <b>about</b> them!"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, we say "นินทาเขา," but in English, the verb "gossip" needs the preposition <b>"about"</b> before the person you are talking about. Think of it as "gossip <b>about</b> someone."</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>8</b> sentences, and <b>4</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 4 instances (like "convinince", "Ayuthaya", "encloat", and "fue")</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Plural Noun Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Capitalization Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Missing Prepositions:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	B1	2026
