1794	28758	2025-09-13 15:50:33.179308+00	Term 1 Exam Question 2	Having pets - a good or bad idea?, having pets it's a good idea and also a bad idea on the time, so there are the typecal things for having pets is a good idea or a bad idea, so there are like these quation like "Do you have time to take care them", "Do you have enough money for petting them" and "Do you treat them well."<br><br><br>Firstly, before you are going to adopt a pet you should made or prepair for the time for to take care them for sure, so do you have time to take care them. It would be a really good idea if you have time to take care them like playing with them to be realax or take them when they are sick, so if you do not have time to take care them so do not, because that would be a really bad idea a lot.<br><br><br>Beside, do you have enough money for petting them, so if you have and you sure that you can take care them, it would be a really good idea because you are ready to pay the food for them, toys and a lot of other things else like about their health too, to take care them you have to pay the treatment that the doctor gave them too, that was a lot of money, so you need to be sure and ready to adopt them, so if you are not ready do not adopt that would be a really bad idea.<br><br><br>And the last, when you adopted them you should treat them well like, where to eat, where to sleep and how to act like those tricks to let them be nice, it would be such a good idea but if you can not so don't adopt one.<br><br><br>Over all, I will disadvantages for having a pet, because I am not ready for taking care of them and also have a lot of things to do too, so I don't have time excally.	341	9.0	Hi Mymind, it's lovely to see your effort in tackling this writing task. You have some really interesting ideas about pet ownership, and it's clear you've thought about the responsibilities involved. To really make your writing clear and easy to understand, focusing on improving your grammar and sentence structure will be a big step forward. Keep up the good work, and with a bit more practice, your writing will truly flourish. 🥳<br><br>📝 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ When you finish writing, read through your work slowly, sentence by sentence, and listen to how it sounds. Are there any parts that are difficult to understand?<br>✔️ Pay close attention to the beginning and end of your sentences. Make sure your capital letters are in the right places and that you use full stops correctly.<br>✔️ Try to learn a few more linking words and phrases, like "however," "in addition," or "for example," to help connect your ideas smoothly.	You've made a good start on the topic of having pets by discussing the important responsibilities involved, like time and money. It's clear you've considered what it takes to look after an animal, and that's a very thoughtful approach. 😊 To get an even higher score in this area, you could try to directly address both the 'good ideas' (advantages) and 'bad ideas' of pet ownership more explicitly, as the question asked. For instance, you could talk about the joy and companionship pets bring as an advantage, or perhaps the limitations they can place on travel as a disadvantage. This would help inform the reader more fully about the different facets of having a pet, ensuring all aspects of the task are explored.	You've tried to present your ideas in a way that resembles an article, which is commendable. You've used paragraphs to separate your points and ended with a personal conclusion. 👍 To truly grab and keep your reader's attention, and to communicate your ideas more clearly, we need to work on making your sentences flow a little better and ensuring your grammar is more accurate. Sometimes, errors can make it a bit difficult for the reader to follow your thoughts. Think about the reader's experience – is every sentence perfectly clear? For example, instead of "having pets it's a good idea and also a bad idea on the time," you could try "Having pets can be both a good and a bad idea, depending on the situation." This makes the sentence clearer and more engaging for your reader.	Your article has a basic structure, which is a good foundation. You’ve included an introduction, separate sections for different points like time and money, and a conclusion. This helps to guide the reader through your thoughts. 👏 You've also used some basic linking words, such as "Firstly" and "Beside" (which should be "Besides"). To improve even further, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just starting a new paragraph, you could use phrases like "In addition to this," or "On the other hand," to show how your ideas are connected. This makes your writing more sophisticated and easier to follow. Thai learners sometimes focus on conveying information but can overlook these subtle connections that really make writing coherent.	Mymind, you've used some everyday words to talk about pets, which is a great start. However, this is an area where we can really make a lot of progress together. There are quite a few spelling errors, like "typecal" (should be typical), "quation" (question), "prepair" (prepare), "realax" (relax), and "excally" (exactly). I know English spelling can be tricky. 😬 Also, we need to work on making your sentences grammatically correct. For example, instead of "Do you have time to take care them," it should be "Do you have time to take care *of* them." Another example is "Beside, do you have enough money for petting them," which should be "Besides, do you have enough money *for* them" or "to *keep* them." We also see some issues with articles, like "on the time" instead of "at the time." These small changes will make a huge difference to how clear and precise your writing is. Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai, which can lead to these sorts of grammatical patterns that are not standard in English. Let's work on practicing these common errors.	