3087	29579	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	Hello Maya, I got your email and I'll recommend a place to stay for you. There is a hotel that near my school, name's Sai 4 hotel. The price are very cheap but the room is clean and have breakfast for you all. Also, a place to learn more about Thai communication history. There is a Thai history museum that opens at 9 a.m. to 8 p.m. You can go before you come to school. The body language tips are important. First, you can Whai when you meet someone. Second, low your head down when you walk by people.<br><br>See you soon,<br><br>Ka-nhom	101	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2	2.28	Hi Ka-nhom! It was great to read your descriptions of the hotel and the museum—you gave some very useful information for visitors! br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used specific topic words like "communication history," "museum," and "breakfast," which help the reader understand exactly what you are describing.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You successfully used connectors like "but," "also," and "second" to organize your thoughts and move from one idea to the next.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> I like that you attempted to use relative clauses (using "that") to add more detail to your sentences, such as describing the hotel and the museum.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Missing the "BE" Verb (is/are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"There is a hotel that near my school, name's Sai 4 hotel."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"There is a hotel near my school <b>called the</b> Sai 4 Hotel."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can sometimes describe a location or a state without a linking verb (เช่น "โรงแรมที่ใกล้โรงเรียน"). But in English, if you use a describing word or a relative clause, you usually need a verb like "is." Also, for names, we use "called" rather than just "name's."</li><li><b>📝 Subject-Verb Agreement</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The price are very cheap but the room is clean and have breakfast for you all."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The <b>rooms are</b> very cheap, but <b>they are</b> clean and <b>breakfast is included</b> for everyone."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, verbs and nouns don't change their form (เช่น "ราคา" doesn't change if there is one or many). In English, if you use "are," the subject must be plural (rooms). Also, instead of saying "have breakfast" (which sounds like the room itself is eating!), we say "breakfast is included" or "there is breakfast." This comes from the Thai word "มี" which can mean both "to possess" and "there is."</li><li><b>📝 Using "There is" to start ideas</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Also, a place to learn more about Thai communication history."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Also, <b>there is</b> a place to learn more about Thai communication history."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you might start a thought with "นอกจากนี้ยังมี..." and skip the formal subject. In English, every sentence needs a subject and a verb. When you want to say something exists, you must use <b>"There is"</b> or <b>"There are."</b></li><li><b>📝 Word Forms (Adjective vs. Verb)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Second, low your head down when you walk by people."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Second, <b>lower</b> your head when you walk by people."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> "Low" is an adjective (ต่ำ), but to describe the ACTION of moving your head, you need the verb <b>"lower"</b> (ก้ม/ลดลง).</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>8</b> sentences, and <b>3</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Preposition Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Determiner/Article Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Other Grammatical Errors:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
