3089	29599	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	That sound interesting, in my view I recommend you to stay at the hotel, there is a hotel near my school which is cheap and it's so private, the location is 32 road and it also clean too it's pretty good rigth ? Oh there is a libary near 32 road, i suggest you to go there because it's open very early so after you visit there you also have more much time to spend on other things, i think it's open at 8:00 in the morning, and don't forget this at the libary there is a rule that you need to be polite and quiet, if you have to cough cover your mouth and you need to turn off your phone notification alert.<br><br>see you soon,<br><br>Praewwan	125	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2	2.28	Hi Praewwan! You have written a very helpful and detailed message with some great advice for your friend!<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used some excellent topic-specific words like "private", "polite", and "notification alert" which made your writing very clear.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> I was impressed to see you attempting complex structures, like using a relative clause ("which is cheap") and a conditional sentence ("if you have to cough...").</li>  <li>💪 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You used a good variety of connectors like "because", "so", and "if" to join your ideas together logically.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Ending your sentences (Run-on sentences)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"That sound interesting, in my view I recommend you to stay at the hotel, there is a hotel near my school which is cheap and it's so private, the location is 32 road and it also clean too it's pretty good rigth ?"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"That <b>sounds</b> interesting. I recommend <b>staying</b> at <b>the</b> hotel near my school; it's cheap, private, and <b>is also clean</b>. It's quite good, <b>isn't it</b>?"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In English, we cannot keep joining main ideas with only a comma. This is called a "comma splice." When you finish one complete thought, you must use a full stop ( . ) before starting the next one. Otherwise, the reader gets "breathless" trying to read it all at once!</li><li><b>📝 Missing the verb "to be" (is/am/are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"the location is 32 road and it also clean too it's pretty good rigth ?"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The location is Road 32 and it <b>is</b> also clean."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is a very common mistake for Thai learners! In Thai, you can say "มันสะอาด" (subject + adjective) without using a linking verb. However, in English, every sentence MUST have a verb. When you describe something, you must use <b>is</b>, <b>am</b>, or <b>are</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Capital Letters for "I" and Sentences</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Oh there is a libary near 32 road, i suggest you to go there because it's open very early so after you visit there you also have more much time to spend on other things, i think it's open at 8:00 in the morning, and don't forget this at the libary there is a rule that you need to be polite and quiet, if you have to cough cover your mouth and you need to turn off your phone notification alert."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Oh, there is a library near Road 32. <b>I</b> suggest <b>going</b> there because it <b>opens</b> very early... <b>I</b> think it opens at 8:00 in the morning."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Thai script does not have "ตัวพิมพ์ใหญ่" (capital letters), but in English, they are very important. You must always use a capital letter for the word <b>I</b> (ฉัน) and at the very beginning of every new sentence.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>6</b> sentences, and <b>1</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Punctuation Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Capitalisation Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Verb Form/Tense Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Missing Verbs:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
