nts. However, parents still do have effect on them too but less than for many reasons and one of these reasons is their spending time. I strongly believe that friends play an important role on the choices teenagers make.	365	12.0	Hello Ie Ie! It is clear you have some thoughtful ideas on how friends influence teenagers, and you structured your essay in a logical way. Your ability to connect your own ideas, like hobbies, to the main question shows creativity. To make your writing even stronger, a key area to focus on will be refining your language use and ensuring your essay stays within the suggested word count. Keep up the good work; with a bit of focus on these areas, your writing will truly flourish.<br><br>📋 Action Plan:<br>*   ✅ Pay close attention to subject-verb agreement in your sentences.<br>*   ✅ Practice using a wider range of vocabulary to express your ideas with more variety.<br>*   ✅ Work on being concise and writing to the specified word length for the task.	Ie Ie, you did a good job of covering the points about clothes and fashion, and studying. It was also clever of you to include your own idea about hobbies, which fit well with the exam question. Your essay showed that you understood the question and kept the reader informed, which is an important part of a good essay. However, for a Band 3 in Content, "minor irrelevances and/or omissions may be present. Target reader is on the whole informed." While your points were relevant, your essay was quite a bit longer than the suggested word count. For future tasks, try to express your ideas more concisely. For example, if you have a lot to say on one point, try to pick the most important details rather than including every idea that comes to mind. This helps you to stay within the word limits and keeps your essay focused.	You certainly used the conventions of an essay, Ie Ie, which is a great starting point for this type of writing. You had a clear introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion, which helps to communicate your straightforward ideas. This aligns with a Band 3, where the writing "uses the conventions of the communicative task in generally appropriate ways to communicate straightforward ideas." To make your writing even more engaging for your reader, try to vary your sentence structures a little more. Sometimes, repeating similar sentence patterns can make the text feel a bit drawn out. For instance, instead of always starting sentences with "Friends have...", you could try rephrasing to "The influence of friends often extends to..." or "One way friends impact choices is...". This helps to keep the reader interested from beginning to end.	Your essay was generally well-organised, Ie Ie, and it was easy to follow your points thanks to your clear paragraphing. You used basic linking words like "First," "Besides," and "Lastly" to connect your ideas, which helps create coherence in your writing. This is characteristic of a Band 3, where the "text is connected and coherent, using basic linking words and a limited number of cohesive devices." To make your writing flow even more smoothly, you could try using a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just "Besides," you might use "Furthermore," "In addition," or "Moreover." Also, some of your sentences were quite long. Breaking these down into two shorter sentences could sometimes make your ideas clearer and help the reader move through your text more easily. Thai learners sometimes focus heavily on getting all their ideas down, which can sometimes lead to longer sentences that could benefit from being broken up.	You used a good range of everyday vocabulary in your essay, Ie Ie, and mostly in an appropriate way. Your use of simple grammatical forms also showed a good degree of control. While there were some noticeable errors, the meaning of your message was still clear, which puts you at a Band 3 in Language: "Uses everyday vocabulary generally appropriately, while occasionally overusing certain lexis. Uses simple grammatical forms with a good degree of control. While errors are noticeable, meaning can still be determined." Let's look at three areas where you can make some excellent improvements. Firstly, be mindful of subject-verb agreement. For example, you wrote "friends has more effect," but since "friends" is plural, it should be "friends **have** more effect." Similarly, "Everyone have their own favorite hobbies" should be "Everyone **has** their own favorite hobbies" because "everyone" takes a singular verb. Secondly, pay close attention to word choice and common pairings of words. You used "many effect" which should be "much effect" or "many effects," and "adsorb" when you likely meant "absorb" (to take in). English has many specific word combinations, and learning these can make your writing sound more natural. Lastly, review your spelling and word forms, such as "frequnetly" which should be "frequently." Taking a moment to proofread for these common slips can make a big difference. These kinds of errors are sometimes common for Thai learners, as direct translations or a focus on conveying the main idea can sometimes lead to overlooking these smaller, but important, grammatical details.	3.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
