ting ("Hello Corey") and ended with an appropriate closing ("Bye, Natee"). This shows you understand the basic format for an informal email. To really make your email easier for the reader to follow and to achieve a higher score, try to arrange your information into separate paragraphs. For instance, you could have one paragraph for your response about the tickets and meeting arrangements, another for your thoughts on the robot activity, and a final one for your suggestion about what to bring. This helps your ideas flow more logically and keeps the reader engaged.	Your email is connected and the ideas are generally coherent, meaning they make sense together. You use some basic linking words, like "because" and "but," which help tie your sentences together. However, to move towards a score of 5, you could use a wider variety of linking words and phrases to create smoother connections between your sentences and ideas. For example, instead of just using "because," you could also use "since," "so," or "also" to introduce new points more smoothly. Thai learners sometimes write very directly, but adding a few more linking phrases can help create a more natural flow in English.	You used everyday vocabulary, which is appropriate for an email to a friend, and your message was generally understandable. However, there were a few grammatical areas that could be improved to make your writing even clearer and more accurate. For example, in your opening, "It's very good thing that you just get the tickets for me" could be improved to "It's very good news that you got the tickets for me." The word "thing" is a little too general, and "just get" sounds a bit awkward; "got" or "have gotten" would be more suitable. Another area to watch is how you connect ideas; "because so it will be fast" could be simplified to "because it will be fast" or "so it will be fast" to avoid repeating the conjunction. Also, with phrases like "pocket of money" and "water bottle," it's good to remember to use "pocket money" and "a water bottle" or "some water bottles." Paying close attention to these smaller details in word choice and grammar will greatly improve your overall language control.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
