l improvement in your writing.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br>• ✅ When you're planning your essay, think about who you're writing for and what kind of language they expect. For academic essays, try to use more formal words instead of everyday spoken phrases.<br>• ✅ After you write a paragraph, go back and check if all your verbs agree with their subjects (e.g., "friends *have*", not "friends *has*"). This is a common area to improve for many Thai learners.<br>• ✅ Try to replace very common words with more interesting ones. For example, instead of using "very" all the time, explore words like "significant," "substantial," or "profound."	Namo K, you've done a good job of covering all the points in the exam question, including your own interesting idea about "understanding each other." That shows you're thinking carefully about the prompt, which is excellent. 👍 To make your content even stronger, try to explain your ideas with a bit more detail and specific examples. For instance, when you discuss how friends affect choices in fashion, you could give a brief example of a popular trend among friends and how it contrasts with family expectations. This helps the reader fully grasp your points and makes your arguments more persuasive.	You've started to use some of the features of an essay, which is a good foundation. However, to make your writing more suitable for academic tasks, we need to think about the 'tone' or 'register.' Words and phrases like "I'll start off by" or "I'll finish off with," and the repeated use of "hang out" make the essay feel quite informal, almost like you're chatting with a friend. While this can be good in some situations, for an essay, we need to use more formal language. For instance, instead of "hang out," you could use "spend time together" or "socialise." Focusing on a more consistent formal style will help you hold your reader's attention more effectively in this type of writing.	Your essay has a clear flow with an introduction, body paragrap