re to cover everything, leaving Corey completely informed.	Your email is perfectly structured for writing to a friend, Yaya 📧. You used a friendly greeting at the start and a polite closing at the end, which are just right for an informal email. Breaking your ideas into clear paragraphs also helped make your message very easy and pleasant to read. You communicated all your ideas simply and clearly, ensuring Corey would understand everything without any trouble.	The way you organised your email is very clear and easy to follow, Yaya 👍. Each paragraph deals with a different part of Corey's email, which makes your response logical and coherent. You used connecting phrases like "Also" and "But if you really want to watch" to link your ideas smoothly, helping the reader move from one point to the next effortlessly. This careful organisation helps to ensure your message flows naturally.	You used a good range of everyday words that were very suitable for your email, Yaya. Your grammar was also mostly controlled, allowing you to express your ideas clearly without confusing the reader. For instance, when you wrote "I don't really feel like watching film," adding the article 'a' would make it "watching a film" to be grammatically complete. Similarly, "incase" should be written as two words, "in case," as it is a phrase. When suggesting items like "phone, powerbank," try to weave them into a sentence more fully, for example, "I also think we should bring our phones and powerbanks." Thai learners sometimes omit articles because they are not used in a similar way in Thai, and compound words can also be tricky. Paying attention to these small details will help your writing become even more precise and natural for an English speaker to read.	5.0	5.0	5.0	4.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
