hatThat, you did well to include all the topics asked for: clothes and fashion, studying, and your own idea about free time activities. That shows you can read the prompt carefully! However, the most important part of the question was about *who has more effect* on teenagers' choices – friends or parents? Your essay talked more about the *differences* in choices between teenagers and parents, rather than which group influences those choices more. For example, instead of just saying "Teenagers like street wear... But parent like to wear something that easy and good," you could have explored *why* teenagers choose street wear – is it because their friends wear it, or because their parents bought it for them? To score a 5 here, you need to make sure your essay always, always answers the exact question asked, not just the general topic.	You tried to write an essay, and you used some formal words, which is a good effort! Your goal with this essay was to argue *who* has more influence, but because you focused more on *what* the differences were, the reader didn't get the clear argument they expected. 🤔 Think about your audience – your teacher expects a clear point of view in an essay. For instance, if you believe friends have more influence, make that clear in your introduction and then support it with examples in your body paragraphs. To improve, try to keep a consistent formal tone throughout your writing and make sure your main message (your argument) is very clear from beginning to end.	You structured your essay with an introduction, separate paragraphs for each point, and a conclusion, which is a really good start! 👍 However, the way you connected your ideas could be clearer. You used phrases like "The first is..." and "The finally is..." which are simple ways to link paragraphs, but for an essay, we need to build a stronger argument. For example, you could use transition words that show comparison or contrast if you are talking about differences, or words that show cause and effect if you are discussing influence. Sometimes, your paragraphs felt a little disconnected from the main question. To score higher, try to use a wider range of linking words and phrases to show how your ideas relate to each other and, crucially, how they all support your main argument.	You used everyday words to talk about clothes, studying, and free time, which is understandable. 😊 However, there were quite a few errors in your grammar and spelling that sometimes made your sentences a bit tricky to understand. For instance, you wrote "choths" instead of "clothes" and "parent like" instead of "parents like" or "a parent likes." You also used "to learning" and "to playing" when it should be "to learn" and "to play." Thai learners often make these kinds of mistakes because the rules for plural nouns, subject-verb agreement, and verb forms can be different in Thai. For example, in English, we always need to make sure the verb matches if the subject is singular or plural (e.g., "parents *like*", but "a parent *likes*"). Also, after "to" we usually use the base form of the verb, not the -ing form (e.g., "easier *to learn*", not "easier *to learning*"). To make your writing clearer, focus on getting these basic grammar rules right. Try checking your sentences for these common errors as you write.	2.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
