r><br>	You answered the task by naming two activities (hanging out and playing sports) and giving reasons for both. Your ideas are relevant and generally connected to the question, but many points are repeated and lack specific examples or development (e.g., where or when these activities happen, or a clear personal example).<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Add specific details or a short example for each activity to make your reasons more convincing (e.g., a place, a typical activity, or a real event).<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "It seems like Thailand doesn't have anything to make teenagers feel more relax."<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "It may seem that teenagers in Thailand have few places to relax, but small activities such as meeting friends at a café or joining a weekend football game can help them relax."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved sentence gives concrete examples (a café, a weekend football game) which make your point clearer and more persuasive. Specific details help the reader understand and believe your reasons.<br><br>	Your tone is appropriate for an essay and your purpose (persuading the reader to try these activities) is clear, but the register and phrasing are sometimes too informal or unclear for a First exam essay.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Use slightly more formal language and avoid conversational fillers; be careful with sentence forms so your ideas sound confident and clear.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "We'll be really enjoy with it while we're playing it."<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "I really enjoy playing sports because they help me forget my stress."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved phrase is grammatically correct and more appropriate for written English. In Thai spoken English, learners sometimes keep very casual spoken patterns; in formal writing you should use complete, accurate sentences to show control of register.<br><br>	Your essay follows the required four-paragraph structure: an introduction with a thesis, two body paragraphs each with a main idea, and a conclusion with a recommendation. This gives the reader a clear map of your ideas.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Strengthen topic sentences and add one linking phrase at the start or end of each paragraph to improve flow (e.g., "Firstly," "In addition," "Finally,").<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "Another free time activity that I suggest is play sports with your friends."<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Another effective free-time activity is playing sports with friends, because physical activity reduces stress and helps you meet new people."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved topic sentence is grammatically correct and gives the paragraph a clear direction. A strong topic sentence helps the reader know what to expect and improves paragraph cohesion.<br><br>	You use everyday vocabulary and simple grammar well enough to be understood, but there are frequent errors (spelling, verb forms, determiners, prepositions) and the task asked for relative clauses and passive forms, which you did not use. The main challenges are with a few key grammar and word-choice rules.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Nowsaday, everyone needs a free time activity to be happy."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Nowadays, everyone needs a free-time activity to be happy."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai learners sometimes write English words the way they sound in Thai. This is related to the B2 pattern "word_form" (Thai uses flexible forms; English requires precise morphology and standard spelling). Checking common spellings (e.g., "nowadays") will help your essay look more accurate and professional.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Even teenagers, adults or old people are also need these kind of activities."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Even teenagers, adults and older people also need these kinds of activities."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This error involves plural agreement and word choice. The B1 pattern "plural_s and count/uncount" explains this: Thai does not mark plural nouns like English does, so learners often forget to make nouns plural ("kinds") or use the correct form. Also, the extra "are" should be removed—English uses "also need" here, not "are also need." Focus on subject–verb patterns (we need / she needs) and plural forms when using "these/these kinds."<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "From my perspective, hang out with friends influences a lot of consequences to our feely when we're stressed."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "From my perspective, hanging out with friends has many positive consequences for our feelings when we're stressed."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> There are two issues here: the verb form ("hanging out") and the preposition ("consequences for" rather than "consequences to"). The B1 pattern "prepositions of time/place" (Thai often transfers prepositions directly) explains why prepositions are tricky—Thai uses different particles and often omits prepositions, so choosing "for" or "of" correctly is important in English. Also, "feely" is a misspelling of "feelings"—check noun forms and spelling.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Spend 15 minutes after writing to check common spellings and noun forms (make a short list of 10 frequently misspelt words).<br>2. Do 3 focused exercises this week on prepositions and plural forms (use an online grammar practice or workbook).<br>3. Rewrite the essay shortening it to 160–180 words and add one concrete example for each activity (place/time/person) to improve Content and maintain the required word limit.<br><br>	4.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
