ing pets is a good idea and gave us reasons why, like how they help us relax after a long day. 🐕 You also thought about the other side of the argument, mentioning why some people might be worried about owning pets and the responsibilities involved. To improve to a perfect 5, think about developing your points a little more deeply. For instance, when you say pets make you relax, you could add a specific example of what a pet does that helps you feel calm, rather than just stating the general idea. This would give your reader an even clearer picture of your point.	You've made a good attempt at writing an article, Sun, which is a great start. You've tried to organise your ideas with an introduction and a conclusion, which helps the reader follow your thoughts. 😊 However, sometimes your ideas don't quite connect as smoothly as they could, and some of your sentences are a little tricky to understand. This can make it hard for the reader to stay focused on your message. To make your writing more engaging, try to simplify your sentences a bit and ensure each idea leads naturally to the next. For example, instead of a very long sentence, try breaking it into two shorter ones to make your point clearer.	Your article has a basic structure, which is a positive start, Sun. You've used paragraphs to separate your introduction, the main body of your argument, and your conclusion, which is an important step in organised writing. 👍 However, sometimes the way your sentences and ideas are connected within these paragraphs could be stronger. For instance, when you move from talking about the benefits of pets to the worries some people have, a clear linking phrase would help the reader transition more smoothly between these different ideas. Thai learners sometimes focus more on getting all their ideas down, and less on the connecting words that help those ideas flow. Practise using simple linking words like "however," "on the other hand," or "for example" to guide your reader through your thoughts.	Sun, you've used some good everyday words to talk about pets, which is a great foundation. 📚 However, there are quite a few mistakes with your grammar and spelling that make your meaning a bit unclear at times. For example, you wrote "Most people has an question" instead of "Most people have a question." Remember that "people" is plural, so it needs a plural verb like "have." Another common mistake was with spelling, like "belief" for "believe," "fell" for "feel," and "forniture" for "furniture." These small errors can add up and distract the reader from your message. Thai learners often mix up singular and plural verb forms, and also struggle with spelling words that sound similar or have silent letters. A good way to improve is to double-check your subject and verb agreement in every sentence, and to keep a personal list of words you often misspell so you can review them. Also, a complex sentence like "some if you not pay attention to them enough they can be agressive and not listen to you to weeck to have a lot of responbilitie and too feed them and play with it will make them respect and rayalty to you" is difficult to follow. Try to break down long sentences like this into smaller, clearer ones, for instance: "Some pets can become aggressive if you don't give them enough attention. You also need to feed them and play with them regularly to earn their respect and loyalty." Focusing on these areas will make your writing much clearer and more professional.	4.0	2.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
