3216	29031	2026-01-12 12:10:44.278954+00	Book Review	Good morning everyone. Today I want to share a good book with you that I just read yesterday. It's called White Nights and it's written by Fyodor Dostoevsky. [laugh]<br>Well, it's a story about sentimental love or old school love but it's actually not about that at all. Have you ever fallen in love before? Well, I bet you have whether it's mutual or not. Well, the story begins... ...no, the story takes place in the 19th century of St. Petersburg, Russia. And it's about a an unnamed dreamer. Well, we never learned about his name throughout the story at all. It was a lonely man who doesn't have any friends or any relative at all. Well, he was... he has been living in St. Petersburg for a while now and he decided to go on walks during the night. And he found himself talking to a young woman named Nastenka. She's... um, they both shared their experience and she shared about the stories about her life. And they decided to plan... and they they planned that in the following night they would meet up here again. <br>When the following night comes, Nastenka and... and dreamer, they share more experience of of each other to one another. And, um, when he returned to his home, he realized that he had fallen for Nastenka although they just met in the in two nights. Well, the following night Nastenka told him that he... ...that she already belonged to somebody else and she warned him several times that he shouldn't fall in love with her and their relationship was purely platonic and brotherly like. [laugh] And well, as he... as dreamer, he begins to hesitate about his feelings towards her... not until the the next night. <br>She expressed her boredom towards her lover and about how he was... [clears throat] bored of waiting for him to return back to her. And that's when he finally realized that he couldn't take it anymore and he decided to confess his love towards Nastenka. She was, uh, surprised but she was about to give in to his, uh, affection until he returned back to Nastenka, her old lover. And she didn't hesitate and returned back to him right away leaving our dreamer in dullness once again. <br>Well, a... [clears throat] I love how both of the characters were written since I found dreamer to drown in his fantasies and and take the idea of Nastenka not who she was into his own dreams or fantasies. And for Nastenka, I feel like her choice it may not satisfy the readers since it was like a bad ending. But honestly, I mean, how can you fall in love with someone you just met in four days? But the thing that I don't... however, the thing that I don't like about the story is that, uh, I found it a bit confusing due to its language and when it was written since it was in for so long the language wasn't as simple as it was today. Uh, well, if you enjoy, uh, sentimental love stories... I think, I think you'll enjoy this book. Uh, I hope you give it a try. That's all from me. Thank you.	539	19.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	PET	Racing, you have a very strong and confident speaking voice. Your volume and intonation were both excellent throughout the entire presentation. You did a fantastic job keeping the audience's attention from beginning to end.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your volume was perfect, and your intonation really helped to highlight the most important parts of your book review. This made your talk very easy to follow and highly engaging for your classmates.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You tend to speak a bit too fast, which occasionally makes it difficult to understand your word linking. I also noticed some dropped final sounds in words like "childhood" (/ˈtʃaɪldhʊd/), "got" (/ɡɒt/), and "park" (/pɑːrk/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Slow Down the Pace</b>: Try to speak about 10% slower to give yourself more time to link your words together smoothly.</li><li><b>Master Ending Consonants</b>: Practice the final /d/, /t/, and /k/ sounds in words like "childhood" (/ˈtʃaɪldhʊd/) and "got" (/ɡɒt/) to improve your clarity.</li><li><b>Practice Plural Sounds</b>: Focus on pronouncing the final /s/ or /z/ sounds in plural words like "guys" (/ɡaɪz/) to help your listener follow your meaning.</li></ul><br>You are a very talented speaker, so keep focusing on those final sounds! 🚀	Racing, you provided a sophisticated and well-structured review of a challenging book! Your vocabulary was impressive, and you handled the complex narrative of White Nights with clarity. You used a rhetorical question effectively and followed the required structure perfectly. To improve, work on reducing redundant phrases and minor noun errors while focusing on a smoother flow with fewer fillers. You are performing at a very strong B1 level. 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1nuXLkyZgiWXUQVFKnklUKyxFV7qAEVJ7?usp=sharing	B2	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
