e time Activities'. You wrote 279 words and achieved B1. You have some good ideas about why free time activities are important for teenagers.<br><br>	You successfully identified three free time activities (sport, games, and reading) and explained why they are important for teenagers. This shows you understood the task well.<br>👉 <b>To reach a 5/5:</b> Make sure to explicitly address all parts of the prompt. If asked for two activities, focus your detail on those two, providing more in-depth explanations and convincing examples for each.<br><br>	You communicated your ideas clearly, and the reader could understand your main points. However, the tone of your essay was quite informal for an academic essay.<br>To improve, remember that academic essays in English usually require a more formal tone. This means avoiding very casual language or expressions you might use when speaking to friends. Think about the difference between informal spoken English and more formal written English when you are writing.<br><br>	You used paragraphs to separate your ideas, which helps the reader follow along. However, to achieve a higher score, focus on making your introduction clearly present the main purpose of your essay. Also, each body paragraph should have one main idea that is clearly explained. Your conclusion should then summarise your main points and bring your essay to a clear end, avoiding repetition and informal language.<br><br>	You used a good range of vocabulary related to free time activities, and you attempted to use some more complex sentences. The main challenges are with a few key grammar rules, often influenced by how you would say things in Thai.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Thai culture **are** differance from other..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Thai culture **is** different from other..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, verbs don't change form based on the subject. In English, a singular subject like 'culture' needs a singular verb like 'is'.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "thai is made many amazing **book**"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Thais have written many amazing **books**"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, you don't always add a plural marker. In English, we usually add '-s' to count nouns when there is more than one.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Most of the teenager in the world not really use **they** free time widely."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Most of the teenagers in the world do not really use **their** free time widely."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, we use possessive adjectives like 'their' to show who something belongs to. In Thai, this is often expressed differently.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "most of the time **is drain** by school"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "most of their time **is drained** by school"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, when something *is done to* an object, we use the passive voice, which needs the 'be' verb + the past participle (e.g., 'is drained').<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Review subject-verb agreement rules, especially for 'he/she/it' and singular/plural nouns, and practice writing 5-7 sentences correctly using these rules.<br>2. Before writing, create a short outline for your essay, making sure your introduction clearly presents the main purpose of your essay and each body paragraph focuses on one main idea.<br>3. When writing, pay close attention to using possessive adjectives (my, your, his, her, its, our, their) correctly, and practice forming passive voice sentences (e.g., 'My time was drained by school').<br><br>	3.0	3.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
962	34080	2025-07-23 14:53:47.491618+00	A Place I Love	A place I love is Beijing, a mega city, which is in north of China. There are many delicious food you can eat and many beautiful place you can go to visit. Also, Beijing is a city that full of history.<br><br><br>I had been to Beijing when I was young. There have many delicious such as Peking duck and Peking noodle. It taste really good. Second, there have many beautiful and interesting place such as The great wall of China and The forbidden city. You can see the old wall that is over 2,300 year and walk on the wall, which is the longest wall in the world. In the forbidden city you can know about the life style of the king in the past. There also have beautiful buliding, which is over 600 year that you can visit.<br><br><br>In conclusion, Beijing is a city that have famous foods and places. It is a really good city that you can go to visit.	163	12.0	ThatThat, you wrote a good article about Beijing as a place you love. You did well introducing the city and giving specific examples of delicious food and interesting places like the Great Wall and the Forbidden City. To make your writing even better, you can focus on making your sentences flow more smoothly and correcting some small grammar mistakes, especially with how you use "there have." Keep up the good work!	ThatThat, you did a good job of telling us about Beijing and why it's a place you love. You mentioned delicious food, beautiful places, and its history, which directly answers the prompt. You gave good examples like Peking duck and the Great Wall, helping the reader understand why you find Beijing special.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "A place I love is Beijing, a mega city, which is in north of China. There are many delicious food you can eat and many beautiful place you can go to visit. Also, Beijing is a city that full of history."\n    Problem: While you introduce Beijing well, you could add a little more personal feeling right at the beginning to really show *why* you love it, rather than just stating facts.\n    How to fix it: You could add a sentence like, "From the moment I first visited Beijing, I knew it was a place I would always carry in my heart." This immediately tells the reader about your strong personal connection.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "In conclusion, Beijing is a city that have famous foods and places. It is a really good city that you can go to visit."\n    Problem: Your conclusion is clear, but it could be a bit more powerful or memorable. It repeats ideas you already shared.\n    How to fix it: To make your conclusion stronger, you could try to summarize your main points while also leaving the reader with a lasting impression or a clear call to action. For example, "In short, Beijing offers a perfect blend of ancient wonders and modern delights, making it a truly unforgettable city. I encourage everyone to experience its unique charm and discover why it holds such a special place in my heart."	You wrote an article that is mostly appropriate for a travel website, and you communicated your ideas clearly. You shared information about Beijing that would interest a reader. However, some phrases were a bit repetitive or informal for an article.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "There are many delicious food you can eat and many beautiful place you can go to visit." and later "There have many delicious such as Peking duck and Peking noodle. It taste really good. Second, there have many beautiful and interesting place such as The great wall of China and The forbidden city."\n    Problem: You repeat the phrases "many delicious food" and "many beautiful place," which makes the writing less engaging. Also, "It taste really good" is a bit simple for an article.\n    How to fix it: Vary your language to keep the reader interested. Instead of repeating "many delicious food," you could say "a wide variety of delectable dishes" or "an array of mouth-watering cuisine." For "It taste really good," you could use "Its flavour is truly exquisite" or "The taste is unforgettable."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "You can see the old wall that is over 2,300 year and walk on the wall, which is the longest wall in the world."\n    Problem: The phrasing "you can see... and walk on the wall" is a bit direct and less descriptive for an article that aims to evoke a sense of wonder.\n    How to fix it: You could make the language more vivid. For instance, "Imagine gazing upon the ancient stones of the Great Wall, a structure over 2,300 years old, and walking along its majestic length – the longest wall in the world." This helps the reader visualize and feel the experience.	Your article has a clear three-paragraph structure, with an introduction, a body, and a conclusion. This helps the reader follow your main points. You used some basic linking words, but you could use a greater variety to make your sentences and ideas connect more smoothly within your paragraphs.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "There have many delicious such as Peking duck and Peking noodle. It taste really good. Second, there have many beautiful and interesting place such as The great wall of China and The forbidden city."\n    Problem: The transition from food to places is a bit abrupt. While "Second" helps, it's a very simple connector.\n    How to fix it: You could use a stronger transition to smoothly move from one topic to the next. For example, "Beyond the culinary delights, Beijing also boasts a wealth of historical and beautiful places to explore." This makes the link between the two main points clearer.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "In the forbidden city you can know about the life style of the king in the past. There also have beautiful buliding, which is over 600 year that you can visit."\n    Problem: The sentence about knowing the king's lifestyle and then immediately talking about beautiful buildings feels a little disconnected.\n    How to fix it: You could combine these ideas more effectively or add a word that shows the connection. For instance, "Inside the Forbidden City, you can learn about the fascinating lifestyle of past emperors and admire the stunning architecture of its buildings, some of which are over 600 years old." This creates a more cohesive description.	You used everyday words to describe Beijing, and your message was generally understandable. However, there were a number of repeated grammar mistakes, especially with verbs, and some small spelling errors that made the writing less clear.\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement:\n    Example: "It **have** a very beatiful beach and sea." (from previous essay, similar error here "Beijing is a city that **have** famous foods") and "There **have** many delicious such as Peking duck..." and "There also **have** beautiful buliding..."\n    Problem: You consistently used "have" when you should use "has" (for singular subjects like "It" or "Beijing") or "are" (for "There" when talking about plural things).\n    How to fix it: Remember that for singular subjects like "it" or "Beijing," you should use "has" (e.g., "Beijing **has** famous foods"). When you talk about multiple things existing, use "there are" (e.g., "There **are** many delicious foods") or "there were" if you're talking about the past (e.g., "There **were** many delicious foods").\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "beautiful **place** you can go to visit." (should be "places") and "beautiful **buliding**" (should be "building")\n    Problem: You missed an "s" for plural nouns and misspelled "building."\n    How to fix it: Always proofread your work carefully for spelling mistakes. Think about whether you are talking about one thing or many things to decide if you need an "-s" at the end of a noun.\n\n*   Word Choice and Phrasing:\n    Example: "You can see the old wall that is over 2,300 year"\n    Problem: "2,300 year" should be "2,300 years old" or "2,300 years."\n    How to fix it: Pay attention to common phrases and how numbers are used with measurements of time. "Over 2,300 years old" is the correct way to say this.	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
3222	33961	2026-01-12 12:10:44.278954+00	Book Review	Hi everyone, my name is Dejung and today I'm going to tell you about my favorite manga... anime and anime called Detective Conan by Gosho Aoyama. Okay, so I'm pretty sure that you guys might already have known what Detective Conan is since it's one of the biggest childhood anime for a Thai person, but... but I'm pretty sure that you guys are not probably detailed yet, so just imagine if one day you got turned into a seven-year-old child and everybody thinks that the real you is already dead.<br>So this whole story is set in... is mostly set in Tokyo, Japan, 1990s. It begins with our main character, Kudo Shinichi. He is a high schooler detective who is very famous in Japan and known for his intelligence. And one day he had a promise to go on a date with his crush, Mori Ran, and he go on a date at the amusement park, and he ride on a roller coaster and then he saw these two guy who dressed in all black and he think that those two guy look really suspicious. So he decided to follow them and without noticing that one of those guy is actually behind his back. So the guy behind his back hit him from the back and he got fainted. And they feed him a weird looking medicine on a purpose to poisoning... to poison him to the death with no evidence left because that is the... that because that's what the medicine supposed to do. But instead, the medicine turn him into a seven-year-old child.<br>After that whole chaotic scene happened, Shinichi change his name into Edogawa Conan and live with Mori Ran's and his dad who is also a detective called Mori Kogoro. And things started to get more excited when he starting to solve case by case by using Mori Kogoro as his mask while solving cases. And... and he started to find lots and lots more evidence about the black organization, the organization that turn him into a child and even got into a fight with them and he start to tag team and help the FBI agent to help... help arresting the black organization. And the things that I really love about this story is that how smart the main character is and how Conan being able to... to solve every cases and explain every cases. And... and the strategy that the criminal use in every case is so surprising and unexpected. But the things that I don't like about this story is that it's way too long, like I've watched like thousands and two hundred episodes in anime and it way... it is way far from the end. Like I think it would take like 20 years to the end. And also the fandom is like a prison because... like lots of people thinks that Haibara should have been the... that Haibara should have been the female lead instead of Ran. But so that's is very annoying to me. And I'm pre... but I'm pretty sure that if you guys like detective or something like that I think you should give it a try for like a few episode and I promise you'll love it. Thank you.	542	17.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	PET	Gut-Jung, you are a very fluent speaker with a lot of potential to reach the highest scores. Your intonation and volume were both excellent, making your book review a standout performance. You handled the material with great confidence and style.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>Your intonation was highly engaging and your volume was perfect for a large audience. This made your storytelling feel very professional and ensured that everyone was interested in what you had to say.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>You tend to speak very quickly, which sometimes reduces your overall intelligibility and breaks your linking. I also noticed some missed ending sounds in words like "childhood" (/ˈtʃaɪldhʊd/), "got" (/ɡɒt/), "park" (/pɑːrk/), and "guys" (/ɡaɪz/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Slow Down for Precision</b>: Try to reduce your speed by about 10% to allow for clearer word linking and better understanding.</li><li><b>Focus on Final /d/ and /t/</b>: Practice your ending sounds in wo