 the plot and sharing your opinions. Your volume was perfect, and you made a noticeable effort to link your words together smoothly.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> Despite the linking attempts, clarity was sometimes lost because you dropped final consonants in words like "just" /dʒʌst/, "England" /ˈɪŋ.ɡlənd/, and "survive" /səˈvaɪv/. There was also a tendency to overstress final syllables in words such as "power" /paʊˈɜː/, "Potter" /pɒtˈɜː/, and "harder" /hɑːdˈɜː/.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Focus on the final consonant sounds of every word to avoid dropping important /st/, /v/, and /d/ sounds.<br>2. Practice relaxing the second syllable of two-syllable words and using the "schwa" sound /ə/ instead (e.g., Potter /ˈpɒt.ər/).<br><br>You have a great foundation for speaking, Kathi—keep up the good work! 🌟	Kathi, you delivered a clear and well-structured review of Harry Potter! 📚 You followed the task requirements by naming the author and using a creative hook to start your talk. Your grammar was effective, especially your use of relative clauses to describe the characters. While you had some small slips with plurals and subject-verb agreement, your meaning was always easy for the listener to understand. Try to work on reducing word repetitions to make your speaking flow even better. This was a strong B1 performance that showed you can handle a long monologue well. 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/149T-QIza1cCqeNpsUNQzEvHxMwqCJN4X?usp=sharing	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
2012	29710	2025-09-21 16:58:08.231306+00	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! My school has new swimmingpool, and new classroom We are exited to use it. But we need to studies first. After the class, we went to new classroom teacher said This is our new classroom that was great! There are many new desk and chair. that so nice! to morrow we will use this classrom. after that... we went to new swimming pool. This swimming pool is so big. tomorrow we have PE class, I think we can use this swimming pool in PE class. But to day we need to go home now. The end.<br><br>	110	12.0	It's wonderful to see your enthusiasm for writing and how you've thought about telling a story about your school. You've done a good job of getting your ideas down and communicating the excitement of new facilities. To help your story come alive even more for your readers, focusing on developing your descriptive language and grammar will make a real difference. Keep exploring different ways to connect your sentences and choose your words, and your writing will continue to develop wonderfully.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for your next writing task:**<br>✔️ Pay close attention to your spelling, especially for words you use often, like "tomorrow" and "classroom."<br>✔️ Practice using prepositions and articles (a, an, the) accurately, as they help make your sentences clearer.<br>✔️ Try to combine some of your shorter sentences using words like "and," "but," or "because" to make your writing flow more smoothly.	Ping Ping, you've done well to include all the important parts of the story task. You started with the given sentence, and you clearly told us about the new swimming pool and classroom, which was your "something incredible." You also explained what happened next, how excited you and your friend felt, and when you expect to use these new facilities, finishing the story nicely. To get an even higher score here, try adding a little more detail about why the new facilities were "incredible" – perhaps describing what they looked like, or how you felt when you first saw them. For instance, instead of just saying "This swimming pool is so big," you could say, "The swimming pool was sparkling blue and looked enormous, much bigger than I expected!" This helps your reader really picture what you saw.	You've managed to tell a clear story with a beginning, middle, and end, which is a key part of writing a good narrative. You also communicated straightforward ideas well, making it easy for the reader to follow what was happening. To improve in this area, think about how you can make your story more engaging for the reader. While you mention being "exited," try to show that excitement through more descriptive words or actions. For example, instead of "We are exited to use it," you could write, "We were so excited we could barely stand still, imagining all the fun we'd have there." Adding these kinds of details helps to hold the reader's attention more strongly, making them feel like they are right there with you.	Your story has a clear structure, moving from arriving at school to seeing the new classroom and then the swimming pool, and you've used some linking words like "After the class" and "after that..." which is good. This helps the reader follow the sequence of events. To make your writing even more organised and coherent, try to use a wider range of linking words to connect your sentences and ideas more smoothly. For instance, when you talk about the new classroom and then the new swimming pool, you could use a phrase like "In addition to the classroom, we also saw..." or "Next, our attention turned to the new swimming pool." Also, ensure that each distinct idea gets its own paragraph to make the story easier to read and understand, allowing your ideas to flow more naturally from one to the next.	Ping Ping, you've used a good amount of everyday vocabulary to describe your school experience, and your simple sentences generally make your meaning clear. For example, "This swimming pool is so big" is easy to understand. However, there are a few areas where focusing on accuracy will make a significant improvement to your writing. Firstly, be careful with your spelling, especially common words. You wrote "swimmingpool," "exited," "studies," "to morrow," "classrom," and "to day" where they should be "swimming pool," "excited," "study," "tomorrow," "classroom," and "today." Remembering that words like "swimming pool" are two separate words, and "tomorrow" is one word, will help a lot. Secondly, pay attention to articles (like 'a' or 'the') and how you connect phrases. For example, "My school has new swimmingpool, and new classroom" would be clearer as "My school has a new swimming pool and a new classroom." Similarly, "teacher said This is our new classroom" would be better as "the teacher said, 'This is our new classroom.'" These small changes make your writing sound more natural and correct. Many Thai learners sometimes omit articles or struggle with combining sentences because it's different from how these concepts are expressed in Thai. Practising these small grammatical points will help your English become much more precise.	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
