y. There's so happy to to celebrate with family or friends and and watching the the the end and I'm very so happy to get party.<br><br>I, I think a big party is very so good. But the big party we need to use the much money to to make a big party.<br><br>Yeah, it's very so important. We, because we need to family or celebrate when it's like the birthday. Yeah.<br><br>	445	11.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	Pun, good job! I could understand nearly everything you said, which is fantastic. Your biggest problem is that your intonation (the way your voice goes up and down) is a little flat, and you have a habit of overstressing final syllables in words. This is something that Thai speakers often do when they speak English because the tones in Thai are different from the intonation patterns in English. For example, you might pronounce "happy" as /hæˈpiː/ or "party" as /ˈpɑːrˌtiː/ with a strong stress on the end. 🗣️ If you can focus on making your voice more expressive and letting your natural English intonation come through, your speech will sound even more natural and easier to follow!	Hello Pun! 👋 It was a real pleasure to listen to you speak. You communicate well, and it's clear you have lots of interesting things to say. Keep up the good work! Your confidence is a real strength, but focusing on making sure your sentences are always grammatically correct will help you improve even further. You’re already doing well, and with a bit of practice, you’ll be communicating even more smoothly.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Speaking Practice:**<br>*   ✔️ When you’re talking about yourself, always remember to use "I am" before your age or when describing what you are doing, like "I am thirteen years old" or "I am drawing."<br>*   ✔️ Pay close attention to small words like 'a', 'an', and 'the', and also to plural endings like '-s' at the end of words like "shops" or "eyes," to make your sentences more accurate.<br>*   ✔️ Practice making your voice go up and d