p where you pronounced the "s" in "island", making it sound like "iceland" (/ˈaɪlənd/ → /ˈaɪ<b>s</b>lənd/). This is a very common mistake for Thai students, so don't worry—just remember the "s" is silent!<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Manage Nervousness:</b> Try taking three deep breaths before you start to help your speaking sound smoother and less choppy.</li><li><b>Master Silent Letters:</b> Practice saying "island" (/ˈaɪlənd/) and "aisle" (/ˈaɪl/) to remember that we don't pronounce the "s" in these words.</li><li><b>Practice Word Linking:</b> Try connecting simple words together in pairs, like "it‿is" or "in‿the," to help reduce the number of pauses.</li></ul><br>You did a great job today, so keep practicing and your confidence will grow! 🎯	Best, you did a great job reviewing Attack on Titan! You followed the structure well, included the author, and used an excellent "Imagine" hook to start your talk. Your vocabulary was strong for a B1 level, though you should watch out for small grammar slips like using "to protect" instead of "for protect" and checking your plural nouns. Try to reduce the sighs and long pauses to make your speaking flow more naturally. You are communicating your ideas clearly, so keep up the effort! 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
