ys don't know anything<b>,</b> you can just ask some Thai people <b>because</b> they are very kind!"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai writing, you often use spaces to separate ideas rather than specific marks like colons or commas. ในภาษาไทยเรามักจะใช้การเว้นวรรคแทนเครื่องหมายวรรคตอน. In English, we use a colon (<b>:</b>) to introduce a specific tip and a comma (<b>,</b>) after an "if" phrase to give the reader a small breather.</li></ol><br><br>This is a helpful little guide for your friends. Keep an eye on those extra words and the punctuation marks, and you'll be set.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>5</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Extra Verbs:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Noun-related Punctuation:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Other Punctuation/Structure Errors:</b> 2 instances</li></ol>	B1+	2026
3184	30942	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hi! I would recommend you for a place to go when you come to Thailand. The first place that you should visit is Wat Arrun. It has many beautiful and fantastic sculpture that you should see once in your life. Many tourists always go to this place. Also, you can rent and Thai dress, if you wanna take a picture. And, one tip for you is about to mind your manner. It's very essential for Thai people.	77	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	3	2.53	Hi Praewa! Good to see you sharing advice about visiting Bangkok. You've given some solid tips for a tourist here.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used some good topic-specific words like "recommend", "sculpture", and "essential" to explain why Wat Arun is worth a visit.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You did a good job connecting your ideas with words like "when", "if", and "also", which helps the reader follow your suggestions.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> I noticed you attempted some more complex structures, like using "would recommend" and "should visit" to give advice.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It has many beautiful and fantastic sculpture that you should see once in your life."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It has many beautiful and fantastic <b>sculptures</b> that you should see once in your life."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, nouns don't change their form to show there is more than one. You might use a word like "หลาย" (laai) but the noun "รูปปั้น" stays the same. In English, when you use "many", you must almost always add an <b>-s</b> to the end of the noun. You also did this with "manner" at the end of your writing, which should be "manners".</li><li><b>📝 Word Order with "Recommend"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Hi! I would recommend you for a place to go when you come to Thailand."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Hi! I would recommend <b>a place for you</b> to go when you come to Thailand."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is a bit of a mix-up with how the verb "recommend" works. In English, we recommend a <b>thing</b> (the place) to a <b>person</b> (you). Think of it as "Recommend + [thing] + for/to + [person]".</li><li><b>📝 Using Articles (a/an/the)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Also, you can rent and Thai dress, if you wanna take a picture."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Also, you can rent <b>a</b> Thai dress if you wanna take a picture."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> It looks like a small slip here using "and" instead of "a". Because "Thai dress" is a singular noun you can count, it needs an article like "a". In Thai, you don't need these little words before a noun, so it's easy to pick the wrong one or use a word that sounds similar.</li></ol><br><br>Wat Arun is a great choice to recommend - the Thai dress rentals are very popular there at the moment. Keep at it!<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>7</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Noun Form and Spelling:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Word Order Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Plural Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Unnecessary Adjectives/Prepositions:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Other Slips (articles):</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	B1	2026
3185	34034	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hello Teacher Edward. I remember that you're coming to Thailand to stay for a while. Hello, Teacher Edward. There are some places i would definately recommend you to go. But if you're looking for somewhere to stay, that will be Lumphini Hotel. It's a perfect place to stay for a while like a couples of months. The hotel also have a swimming pool, fitness, and a small market. But if you want to buy or walk, there are also Big C on the other side of the road. But if you like it, you can also stay there for years too. That's all If you have a question about Thailand or Thai people, you can ask me by email once. That's all.<br>\nTake care,\nSingto	125	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	3	2.53	Hi Singto! Good on you for putting this together. It's a solid start at writing a travel recommendation, and you've included some helpful details for your teacher.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used specific words for hotel facilities like "swimming pool" and "market," and you tried a good verb like "recommend."</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You used "if" sentences well to show different choices, like when you talked about looking for a place to stay or wanting to go shopping.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> You had a go at using the future tense with "will" and "can," showing you are thinking about how to give advice for the future.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Subject-Verb Agreement</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The hotel also have a swimming pool, fitness, and a small market."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The hotel also <b>has</b> a swimming pool, <b>a fitness center</b>, and a small market."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, the word "มี" doesn't change based on who or what you are talking about. In English, we have to match the verb to the subject. Because "The hotel" is just one thing (it), you must use "has." Also, in English, "fitness" is an adjective or a concept; the physical place is a "fitness center" or a "gym." In Thai, you might just say "ไปฟิตเนส," but we need the full name of the place in English.</li><li><b>📝 Countable Nouns and "There is/are"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"But if you want to buy or walk, there are also Big C on the other side of the road."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"But if you want to buy <b>something</b> or walk, there <b>is</b> also <b>a</b> Big C on the other side of the road."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You used "are" because you're thinking about Big C being a big place, but since it's just one shop, we use "is." Also, remember that names of shops usually need "a" or "the" before them. In Thai, you don't need these little words (articles), so it's a common thing for Thai learners to skip them.</li><li><b>📝 Phrasing for invitations</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"That's all If you have a question about Thailand or Thai people, you can ask me by email once."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"That's all. If you have a question about Thailand or Thai people, you can ask me by email <b>anytime</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Using the word "once" sounds a bit like you only want him to email you one time! You might be thinking of "ที" or "สักครั้ง" as a polite way to end a sentence, but in English, if you want to be helpful, "anytime" is the way to go. It shows the teacher he is welcome to contact you whenever he needs help.</li></ol><br><br>You've got the right idea with your directions and suggestions. Just keep an eye on those singular and plural verbs and you'll be fine.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>10</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Nouns and Plurals:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Missing Articles (a/the):</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Spelling and Capitalisation:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Verb Tense:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	B1	2026
1182	28806	2025-09-02 13:17:11.280916+00	My Hero	Hello everyone, my name is Nut and today I will tell you about my hero. Uh, uh, I probably you don't know who he is. Edward Jenner, the man who saved a million lives.<br><br><br>I will tell you about overview of my presentation. First... first, I will tell you about who Edward Jenner is, and next, I will tell you about what he did. And the last important... and... and the last thing is why he is a hero.<br><br><br>Let's start with his early life. Edward Jenner... Edward Jenner born in, uh, the small cit... the small city named Berkeley in England, uh, 1749. And he's born into a large family and he is the youngest brother in of the nine children. He's... his father is clergyman, so family values education, uh, helped him to study a medicine. When... as when he was young, he... he like... he loved, uh, science and nature, or in... or in nowadays we call a biology. He learned from, uh, local doctor and milkmaid. Milkmaid who was sick from the cowpox, but when the smallpox come, uh, the milkmaid didn't get sick from the smallpox. This is an idea to... this is an idea to basis his first vaccines.<br><br><br>Now I will tell you about his activity. Uh, in 1796, he injected the mate... the cowpox materials into the boy's name James Phipps. Uh... James Phipps didn't get sick from the smallpox, uh, after he get a vaccine from Edward Jenner. The boy immune to smallpox and... but, uh, then his work proved vaccination work. Uh, the experience that proved the... his vaccination... his vaccine can use with the smallpox and save million of life. He honored by scientific society and government in Europe.<br><br><br>And now I will tell you a interesting thing why Edward Jenner got criticized from people. As first, people don't trust in vaccine because vaccine is a virus. And... they used to in the body, uh, human... human body... The... in that time, lo... local doctor, doctor, and clergymen don't... don't trust he because, uh, because his vaccine. And some people made a cartoon to mocking and joking him, like, uh, people have a part of cow, like cow ears or tails.<br><br><br>But, but then his work was accept and get a award.<br><br><br>Uh, his award, uh... [laughs] he get award but he don't get a Nobel Prize because Nobel Prize came in 1906.<br><br><br>Okay, I will tell you why he is a hero. He recovered vaccination and he saved millions of life. He changed... uh, he changed medicine forever with his work, vaccine.<br><br><br>Edward Jenner, the man who saved a million of life. Thank you.	445	13.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	FIRST	<p>Nut, you have a very clear speaking voice and excellent intonation, which makes your presentation easy to understand! Keep up the great work here. 👍</p><br><p>However, there were a few areas for improvement. You sometimes overstressed final syllables in words, for instance, in "ci**ty**," "Jen**ner**," and "fami**ly**." In English, stress usually falls on a different syllable in these words, such as /ˈsɪt.i/, /ˈdʒɛn.ər/, and /ˈfæm.li/.</p><br><p>You also had some issues with the final /s/ sound, as in "cowpox" which was pronounced more like "cow pok." Remember to articulate that final /s/ sound clearly. To improve, try practicing words with final /s/ and focusing on the correct stress patterns of English words. Listening to native speakers and mimicking their pronunciation can be very helpful! 🎧</p>	<p>Hi Nut! 👋 Overall, you did a great job with your "My Hero" presentation on Edward Jenner. You shared a lot of interesting information clearly and with good flow. You have a very clear speaking voice, which is a big strength! Keep practicing your presentation skills, and focus on expanding your vocabulary and varying your sentence structures to make your speech even more dynamic. You're well on your way to becoming an excellent speaker! 💪</p>	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1+	\N	\N