he costs, space requirements, and extra work involved. This means your article fully informed the reader, which is exactly what the task asked for. You didn't miss any parts of the question, and your ideas were well-chosen. That's a top score for content! 🎯	Peptine, you've successfully communicated your ideas about pets in an article format, which is a great achievement! 🌟 You’ve used an appropriate style for an article, and your main points come across clearly. To move closer to a perfect score, consider making your writing a little more engaging for your target audience, which is teenagers reading a magazine online. For example, you could try using more varied sentence structures or perhaps asking a rhetorical question to draw the reader in. While your ideas are straightforward and easy to understand, consistently holding the reader’s attention with a more dynamic and less direct style would elevate your article further. Thai learners sometimes lean towards a more direct style of writing, but experimenting with different ways to present your ideas can make your writing much more lively and captivating. Keep practising, and you'll soon find your voice becomes even more compelling! ✍️	Peptine, your article has a clear structure with an introduction, separate paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages, and a conclusion. That's fantastic! 📝 You've used linking words like 'Firstly,' 'Secondly,' 'In the other hand,' and 'In conclusion' to guide your reader through your ideas. To make your writing even smoother, try to vary your linking words a bit more. For instance, instead of always starting a new point with 'Secondly,' you could use 'Furthermore' or 'Moreover.' Also, think about how you can connect your sentences more tightly within each paragraph so that the ideas flow from one to the next without feeling too abrupt. For example, after mentioning pitbulls protecting us, you could link that idea more explicitly to why they are a good example. Thai learners sometimes