d. However, remember to be very precise with your email conventions. For instance, in your greeting, "Dear, corey" should ideally be "Dear Corey," with a comma after the name and no comma after "Dear". Paying attention to these small details will make your emails even more natural and effective.	Your email is well-organised and easy to follow. You connected your ideas logically, moving from acknowledging the tickets to discussing meeting arrangements, then activity preferences, and finally, your suggestion for what to bring. You used basic linking words like "because" and "if" effectively to show the connections between your sentences. To make your writing flow even more smoothly, you could try using a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of always starting a new idea, you could use phrases like "Furthermore" or "In addition" to build on previous points. Thai learners sometimes focus more on conveying meaning than on explicitly showing how ideas are linked, so practising different linking words can really help.	You used everyday words that were appropriate for your email, and your meaning was generally clear. However, there were a few grammatical areas where you could improve. For example, you wrote "for get a ticket" when "for getting a ticket" or "to get a ticket" would be more accurate. Another example is "where is next to my house," which should be "which is next to my house." Also, "That's will be educational" should be "That will be educational," and "on last year" should simply be "last year." Many Thai learners find prepositions and auxiliary verbs a bit tricky, and it's a very common area for improvement. Practising these specific grammar points will help you gain more control and make your writing even clearer and more precise.	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
