ed with even more precision.<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement<br>✅ Please improve your handwriting. It is verty small and hard to read. I had to make many corrections to the transcript and many times I could not read your words. Neither could the AI transcriber, which usually has no problems at all. WRITE LARGER.<br>✅ Before submitting your writing, read it aloud to yourself. This can often help you spot missing words or awkward phrases that your eyes might miss.<br>✅ Keep a small notebook for new vocabulary and common errors. When you learn a new word, write it down with an example sentence. For tricky grammar points like subject-verb agreement, write down a few correct examples to help you remember.<br>✅ When you've finished writing, take a moment to specifically check for spelling. You could even use a simple online spell-checker for a quick review before your final draft.	Kris, you've done a very good job of directly addressing the question about the advantages and disadvantages of having pets 🐾. You clearly talk about how pets can help with stress and emotions, which is a lovely point, and you also thoughtfully discuss the financial challenges, especially when a pet becomes sick. You've made sure the reader is well-informed about your opinions, which is exactly what the task asked for. While your article was quite a bit longer than the suggested word count, you still managed to include all the important details. Well done for covering all bases.	Your article communicates your ideas in a clear and understandable way, Kris 👍. You've generally used the conventions we expect for an article, by introducing your topic and then moving on to discuss specific points. You manage to keep the reader's attention with your examples, like comparing playing with a pet to charging a mobile phone. To make your communicative achievement even stronger, think about how each part of your article works together. For instance, your conclusion's point about pets causing problems for neighbours, while valid, feels a little separate from the main discussion of personal advantages and disadvantages. Perhaps you could integrate such a point more smoothly, or ensure it flows directly from a preceding point you've made.	The way you've organised your article is generally clear and logical, Kris. You've used paragraphs effectively to separate your introduction, the advantages of pets, the disadvantages, and your conclusion, which makes it easy to follow your thoughts 📖. You've also used some good linking words like "Even though," "For instance," and "In conclusion" to help connect your ideas. To improve your organisation further, you could try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases to create even smoother transitions between sentences and paragraphs. For example, instead of just saying "On the other hands," you might try "However, it's also important to consider" to show a more nuanced connection between opposing ideas. Thai learners sometimes focus on getting their ideas down, and don't always use enough variety in their linking phrases, so practising a few different ones can be really helpful.	Kris, you use a good range of everyday words that are appropriate for your topic, which is excellent. Your ideas are easy to understand despite some noticeable errors. Let's focus on a few key areas for improvement. Firstly, watch out for spelling 📝. Words like "dailylife" (should be "daily life"), "freetimes" (should be "free time"), "disadventages" (should be "disadvantages"), "emoutions" (should be "emotions"), "exhousted" (should be "exhausted"), "expenssive" (should be "expensive"), "happened" (should be "happened"), "Howerver" (should be "However"), and "neighbur" (should be "neighbour") popped up a few times. Checking your spelling carefully will make your writing much clearer. Secondly, let's work on getting your grammar just right, especially with subject-verb agreement and prepositions. For example, you wrote "that cause" when it should be "that causes," and "affect to our emotions" when "affect our emotions" would be more natural. Also, phrases like "simillar to a mobile phone when it is charged" are understandable, but could be rephrased for greater clarity, perhaps "Playing with our pet recharges our feelings, much like charging a mobile phone." Thai learners sometimes struggle with these smaller grammatical details and prepositions because their first language structures sentences differently, so paying close attention to English sentence patterns will really help you improve. Keep practising, and you'll see great progress.	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
