 focus on for improvement is organising your ideas into clear paragraphs and working on grammatical accuracy. If you work on these areas, your writing will become even clearer and more impactful for your readers. You're doing great, and with a bit more attention to structure and grammar, your writing will truly shine! ✨<br>📝 Organise your thoughts into distinct paragraphs before you start writing. Think of each paragraph as holding one main idea.<br>✅ When you finish writing, read through your work specifically looking for common grammar mistakes like verb agreement and word choice.<br>✅ Practise using a variety of linking words and phrases at the beginning of your paragraphs to guide your reader smoothly from one idea to the next.	You've done a good job of tackling the task, Aunna, by sharing your personal opinion about pets and discussing both their good points and some of the challenges. For example, you beautifully highlight how "a pet could easily cheer you up" when you're sad, and you also thoughtfully mention the "responsibility to take care of another lives." These are excellent points! To get even closer to a score of 5, try to expand on your ideas with a few more specific details or examples. For instance, when you say pets can "help you manage the food schedule and improve your managing skills," you could add something like, "For example, remembering to feed your cat at the same time every day teaches you to be organised." This makes your ideas super clear for the reader. Also, while it's great to have lots to say, try to keep your article within the word count (140-190 words) as this shows good control over your writing. Focusing your writing to hit the word target while still including all your fantastic points will make your articles even stronger.	You've made a good start at writing an article for teenagers, Aunna, by using questions to grab the reader's attention and ending with a recommendation. That's a great way to engage your audience! For example, starting with "Have you ever wanted a little cute creature..." is very inviting. To make your articles even more effective and hold your reader's attention throughout, think about breaking your text into clear paragraphs. Imagine your article as having an introduction, a section for advantages, a section for disadvantages, and a conclusion. Each of these parts would get its own paragraph. This helps your readers follow your ideas more easily and makes your writing feel more polished, just like a professional magazine article. Using distinct paragraphs helps to communicate both straightforward and more complex ideas much more clearly to your target audience.	You have a logical flow to your ideas, Aunna, starting with an introduction, moving to the advantages and disadvantages, and then concluding with a strong recommendation. You also use some useful linking words like "first of all," "on the other hand," and "but overall," which help to connect your thoughts. However, the biggest area for you to focus on here is using paragraphs. At the moment, your article is one long block of text. For instance, after you discuss the advantages of pets, you could start a new paragraph with "On the other hand, having a pet could be challenging for someone..." This creates a visual break and helps the reader understand that you are moving on to a new idea. In Thai, sometimes writers don't use as many paragraph breaks as we do in English, which can lead to a long text. In English writing, especially for articles, clear paragraphing is really important because it makes your text much easier to read and understand. Adding these breaks will make your writing feel much more organised and coherent! 📚	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit your topic well, Aunna, like "emotional support," "cheer you up," and "memorable moment." This shows you have a good vocabulary for discussing pets! However, there are a few places where your grammar could be a bit more precise to make your writing even clearer. For example, when you wrote "let you pet or being or having one," a clearer way to say this might be "let you pet them or have them as emotional support." Similarly, "another lives" should be "another life" or "other lives," and "livingthing" is usually written as "living thing" (two words). Another good example is "alot better," which should always be "a lot better." 😊 Sometimes, in Thai, the way we phrase things might be more direct, but in English, we need to pay close attention to plural forms, verb agreements, and compound words. Focusing on these small but important grammar points will make your sentences smoother and your meaning absolutely clear to your reader. Keep practicing those small details, and your language control will grow beautifully!	3.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
