m'. *Self-Correction: Ensure I reference the correct text!*). <br><br>Let me re-read Ken's text properly.<br><i>Text: Som/Sam janitor at Mall. Smell of bathroom bad. 3D prototype fail. Find chip. Fix it. World class product.</i><br>Okay, correction made.<br><br>Hello Ken, 👋<br><br>You have written a very inspiring story about a janitor who solves a big problem! 🌟 I liked how you described the specific problem (the bad smell) and how he worked hard to fix his invention. The quote at the end is a great lesson!<br><br>I have a few tips to help you improve your writing. 📋<br><br>1. <b>Verb Forms!</b> 🛠️ Watch out for 'it have' -> 'it has'.<br>2. <b>Adjectives!</b> 🎨 'He suspicious' -> 'He was suspicious'.<br>3. <b>Clarity!</b> 💡 Some sentences are a bit long. Try to keep them simple so the reader can follow your great ideas.	You followed the narrative arc well! 📚 You had a clear character (Som the janitor), a problem (the invention failed), and a success at the end.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>The Beginning!</b> 🏁 The prompt asked to start with 'Sam finished his invention...'. You wrote 'Som', which is fine, but make sure to copy the starter sentence exactly next time!</li><li><b>Explain the Invention!</b> 🧪 You mentioned a 'chip' and a 'program'. It would be cool to describe <i>how</i> the chip fixed the smell problem. Was it magic? Was it chemistry?</li>	You have some great ideas, and the story is encouraging. 🌈 The ending quote shows you want to communicate a strong message.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ Some phrases like 'the challenge way that he had to face' are a bit confusing. It's better to say 'the challenge he faced'. Keep it simple!</li><li><b>Consistency!</b> 🔄 You switched between 'Som' and 'He'. Make sure the reader knows who you are talking about.</li>	Your story is organised into paragraphs, which is excellent! 👏<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Linking!</b> 🔗 You used 'After that', 'Then', and 'Finally'. These are good, but try to use them to connect *sentences* too, not just paragraphs.</li><li><b>Sentence Structure!</b> 🏗️ 'His job was janitor... can go quickly to work it.' This runs two ideas together. Try: 'His job was a janitor. He lived near the bus station so he could get to work quickly.'</li>	You used some good technical words like 'prototype', 'absorb', and 'professional'. 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement!</b> ⚙️ 'It have some problem' -> 'It <b>had</b> some problem' (past tense). 'He know' -> 'He <b>knew</b>'.</li><li><b>Word Forms!</b> 🔄 'Successful to built' -> 'Successful <b>in building</b>'. 'Fail immediatly' -> 'Failed immediately'.</li><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Specail' -> '<b>special</b>'. 'Immediatly' -> '<b>immediately</b>'.</li><li><b>Articles!</b> 🅰️ 'Smell of bathroom' -> 'The smell of <b>the</b> bathroom'.</li>	5.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
