re it is played) and a specific reason (teamwork and fitness), which makes your idea more convincing and informative for the reader.<br><br>	You use an appropriate essay tone and follow the expected structure, so the reader understands your purpose. However, some sentences are informal or unclear, which reduces the overall effectiveness.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Use slightly more formal, precise phrasing in place of short spoken-style sentences and make sure each paragraph starts with a clear topic sentence linked to your thesis.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "That's very famous for teenagers in Thailand."<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "These activities are very popular among teenagers in Thailand."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved version uses a more formal written-English structure ("popular among") rather than the conversational "That's very famous," which fits essay writing better.<br><br>	Your essay is paragraphed into four clear parts (intro, two bodies, conclusion) and your introduction gives a thesis naming the two activities — this is a strong structural feature. Paragraphs generally focus on one activity each.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Begin each body paragraph with a clear topic sentence that links directly to the thesis, and use one linking phrase inside each paragraph to improve cohesion (e.g., "For example," "In addition," "Consequently").<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "One important type of activities is board games."<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "One important free-time activity for teens is board games, which they often play with friends at home or school."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved sentence is a clearer topic sentence: it labels the paragraph focus ("one important free-time activity") and signals what the paragraph will explain (where/how it is played), helping the reader follow your structure.<br><br>	You use simple vocabulary and many understandable sentences. The main challenges are with a few key grammar and spelling rules.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Board games are have a lot of games for examples chest, uno, were wolf and bingo."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Board games include many games, for example chess, UNO, Werewolf and bingo."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This error mixes extra verbs and wrong word forms. Thai learners often add or keep extra verbs because Thai verb morphology is more flexible. <br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "for all teenager"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "for all teenagers"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The noun should be plural. Thai does not always mark plural nouns the same way English does, so Thai learners sometimes omit the -s ending in English. (See Thai interference pattern: "plural_s" — plural -s and count/uncount differences.)<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Will shoot the ball in their own bucket."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "They will shoot the ball into their own basket."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This is a sentence fragment/missing subject and a preposition choice issue. In Thai it is common in spoken phrases to omit the subject or use different clause linking, which can lead to English fragments or incorrect clause boundaries. (See Thai interference pattern: "run_ons" — Thai allows different clause structures; English needs clear clause boundaries and explicit subjects.)<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Do 15 minutes of targeted grammar practice, 4 times a week: focus on subject–verb agreement and correct verb forms (use short online exercises and check answers).<br>2. Learn and practise 5 compound or commonly misspelled game/sport words each week (e.g., chess, Werewolf, UNO, basket, teammate) and test yourself with flashcards.<br>3. Each week write one short paragraph (70–90 words) using a relative clause and one passive sentence; get feedback on whether your subject is clear and sentences are complete.<br><br>	4.0	3.0	4.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	202