er build a robot because I enjoy hands-on activities." Also, you missed the chance to suggest one more item to bring and explain why. For example, you might have said, "We should also bring some snacks, as we'll probably get hungry." Adding these small details helps your reader feel fully informed and shows you've thought carefully about all parts of the task. Thai learners sometimes focus so much on direct answers that they forget to add these helpful explanations.	Your email uses the right tone for writing to a friend, which is super important 👍. You started with "Dear Corey" and ended with "Good luck, Pheem," which are great informal greetings and closings. Your ideas are easy to follow, and the email holds the reader's attention well. To reach a score of 5, you could aim for even greater control over your language, so your message comes across without any minor slips. Sometimes, small grammatical issues can slightly distract the reader, even if they understand your message perfectly.	Your email is well organised, and your ideas are grouped into separate paragraphs, which makes it easy to read and understand 📚. For example, you put your meeting preference in one paragraph and your activity choice in another. You also used some linking words like "But if you want to watch..." to connect your ideas. To improve to a 5, you could try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases to create even smoother transitions between your sentences and paragraphs. This helps your ideas flow together more naturally and makes your writing even more sophisticated.	You used some everyday words that fit the topic well, and your simple sentences are generally clear. However, there were a few grammatical slips that made your writing a little less precise, and this is where you can really improve 💪. For instance, you wrote "I already read your email" instead of "I have already read your email" or "I read your email." Another example is "that great," which should be "that's great." Also, when you mentioned the film, "was look bored" should be "looked boring." Finally, "we doesn't need to use" should be "we don't need to use." These types of errors are common for Thai learners as English verb tenses and subject-verb agreement can be quite different from Thai. A good way to improve is to double-check your sentences for correct verb forms and make sure your verbs agree with your subjects. Focusing on these common errors will make your writing much more accurate and fluent.	3.0	4.0	4.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
