nt, you've shared some really good reasons why you love Tokyo, like the friendly people, the interesting shops, and the delicious food! To make your writing even stronger, try to organize your ideas into clearer paragraphs. This will help the reader follow your points more easily. Also, focus on improving your grammar and making your sentences flow more smoothly. Keep up the great effort!	Ant, you have addressed the task by describing Tokyo as a place you love and providing several reasons why, such as friendly people, interesting shops, and delicious food. You included specific examples like the helpful person on the train, the gachapong machines, and the ramen restaurant. However, your essay is quite long at 373 words, significantly exceeding the expected 140-190 word range for a B2 exam. This means that while your content is relevant, it is not always concise, and there's a lot of detail that could be condensed. For example, some of the lengthy explanations about the train experience or the gachapong machines could be shortened. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to present all relevant content more effectively and efficiently, ensuring the target reader is fully informed within the typical word count expectations.	You communicate straightforward ideas about your positive impressions of Tokyo, and the purpose of the essay is clear. You convey your enthusiasm well, for example, \\"my eyes sparking\\" and \\"I can't stop to eating.\\" However, the continuous flow of information, without proper paragraphing, sometimes makes it difficult to maintain the target reader's attention throughout the long response. The essay feels more like a stream of thoughts rather than a structured piece. To improve to a 5, you would need to use the conventions of an essay more effectively, such as varying your sentence structure and using more sophisticated language to communicate ideas, which would help to consistently hold the reader's attention.	The text attempts to organize ideas by introducing different \\"impressions\\" (friendly people, shops, food), and there's a clear introduction and conclusion. You also use some basic linking phrases like \\"for example\\" and \\"In conclusion.\\" However, the significant lack of clear paragraph breaks makes the essay very difficult to read and follow. Most of the essay appears as one continuous block, which hinders coherence and the logical progression of ideas. For example, the detailed stories about the train or gachapong machines would benefit greatly from being in their own paragraphs. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to organise your text into well-structured paragraphs, using a wider variety of cohesive devices and organisational patterns to create a more fluent and easily digestible piece of writing.	You use everyday vocabulary generally appropriately to describe your experiences in Tokyo, such as \\"food,\\" \\"souvernies,\\" \\"special places,\\" \\"friendly,\\" \\"shops,\\" \\"restaurant,\\" and \\"delicious.\\" However, there's noticeable overuse of certain words like \\"very\\" and \\"impression.\\" You use simple grammatical forms with some control, primarily the past tense for narration. However, there are frequent and noticeable errors in grammar, spelling, and word choice that sometimes impede communication. For example: \\"best place ever I had visited\\" (should be \\"best place I have ever visited\\"), \\"There's many food\\" (should be \\"There are many foods\\"), \\"people hard to visited\\" (should be \\"hard for people to visit\\"), \\"taxi in Tokyo are very expensive\\" (should be \\"taxis in Tokyo are\\"), \\"tried trian\\" (should be \\"tried the train\\"), \\"map to go our trian\\" (should be \\"map to find my train\\"), \\"someone show up\\" (should be \\"someone showed up\\"), \\"gachapong machince\\" (should be \\"gashapon machine\\"), \\"gachapong hauls\\" (awkward phrasing), \\"which next my hotel\\" (should be \\"which was next to my hotel\\"), \\"smells is so good\\" (should be \\"the smell was so good\\"), \\"it's make my eyes sparking\\" (should be \\"it made my eyes sparkle\\"), \\"good tase\\" (should be \\"good taste\\"), and \\"I support you all to go to The Tokyo.\\" These errors, while not always preventing understanding, are numerous and affect the overall clarity and quality of the language. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to use a wider range of vocabulary more precisely, including less common lexis, and demonstrate greater control and flexibility with complex grammatical forms.	3.0	3.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	a2e775c4-e795-45e6-b042-7e908b05f577	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
