2002	34933	2025-09-21 16:01:34.862858+00	Term 1 Writing Task 2	Okay, I'll telling you about fastival that my family and I have go to. My family useally go to the songkram fastival every 13-15 May! we like to go at Kaowsan rodt, because that have many people it there. I like songkran fastival because we have to sprash the water to another people and that's why is fun, a water boliones, a cold water is the best! but in this years I didn't go to play with each other because My family said I have to study more so... I didn't play a water, I very sad but we have Songkran ever years so I can wait	107	12.0	Well done, Grace, for writing about the vibrant Songkran festival. It is clear that you have some exciting experiences to share about this special time. You have a good handle on describing your personal enjoyment of the festival, which is a lovely touch for your readers. To help your writing develop even further, the most significant area for you to focus on is organising your ideas into clearer paragraphs and paying close attention to accurate spelling and grammar. Keep up your enthusiasm for writing, and with a little more practice in these areas, your articles will truly engage your audience.<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Before you begin writing, quickly jot down your main ideas and decide how many paragraphs you will need. Each new main idea should start a new paragraph.<br>✔️ Read through your writing carefully, specifically looking for common spelling mistakes in words you use often. Using a dictionary or an online spell-checker can be very helpful.<br>✔️ Practise using correct verb forms and making sure your sentences are complete and grammatically sound. A good way to do this is to read your sentences aloud to yourself.	Grace, you have done a very good job of telling us all about the Songkran festival 🎉. You clearly named the festival, explained when your family usually celebrates it, and described fun activities like splashing water with water balloons. You also shared why you enjoy it so much, which helps your readers understand your personal connection to the festival. To elevate your score to a 5, make sure all your details are completely accurate. For example, Songkran typically falls in April, but you mentioned May. Being precise with details like this helps your readers feel fully informed and confident in the information you are sharing.	You've managed to communicate your straightforward ideas about Songkran with a lovely enthusiastic tone that is very suitable for young readers 🗣️. It is clear you are excited about the festival, and this comes across in your writing. To make your article even more effective and to truly capture your readers' attention, try to organise your writing into clear paragraphs. Imagine your article is being published on a website; clear paragraph breaks help the reader follow your ideas easily. For instance, you could have an opening paragraph introducing Songkran, a middle paragraph describing the activities, and a final paragraph for your personal feelings and the anecdote about not going this year. This helps to guide your reader through your thoughts without them feeling overwhelmed by a long block of text.	Your article has a clear flow of ideas, Grace, moving from introducing Songkran to describing the fun activities and then sharing your personal feelings and an anecdote 📝. You use basic linking words like "because" and "but" to connect your thoughts. To make your article even better organised and more coherent, consider using paragraph breaks to separate different ideas. For example, when you switch from describing what happens at Songkran to explaining why you like it, that is a great place to start a new paragraph. Similarly, your story about not going this year could form its own short paragraph at the end. Breaking your text into smaller, more focused paragraphs helps your reader understand the structure of your ideas and makes your writing much easier to read and enjoy.	You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately to describe the festival and your experiences, which is a great start 👍. However, to improve your score in language, it would be really helpful to focus on accuracy in both spelling and grammar. For example, remember to spell "festival" correctly and pay attention to verb forms, such as saying "I'm telling you" instead of "I'll telling you" or "I went" instead of "I have go to". Another important area is ensuring subject-verb agreement and correct prepositions, for instance, "many people *are* there" instead of "many people *it* there". Thai speakers sometimes face challenges with pluralisation in English because the concept is handled differently in Thai, which might lead to phrases like "fastival" instead of "festivals" or "ever years" instead of "every year". Also, paying attention to the articles "a" and "the" is important, as these are often not used in the same way in Thai. Focusing on these areas will make your writing much clearer and more professional. Keep practising, and your language will certainly become more controlled and precise.	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
558	28887	2025-07-11 03:18:00.899087+00	My favorite city	My favorite place is Shang hai which is a a big city China, in south asia. Shanghai is famouse for its spicy food and beutiful city, where there are pop mart and other famous stores in the city. One thing that I notice about Shang hai. there wasn't a single house it just condos did people live like this? There was a lot of weird foods to like rabits, turtle and bats they were really disturbing. There's a lot of place to go there in Shang hai , but our goal there is to win the rock climbing compitition. So we didn't do anything much. People there wasn't friendly but it's famouse about the food that's called Mala it was really delicouse That said, Shang hai is High tech city mix with China	133	6.0	Hello Leo, You've chosen an interesting city, Shanghai, and shared some unique observations about it, like the prevalence of condos and certain foods. It's good that you attempted to describe your personal experiences and what you noticed there. The most important areas for you to improve are the length of your writing, as your essay is significantly shorter than the required 190 words, and the accuracy of your language, especially spelling, capitalization, and grammatical control. These issues often make your writing difficult to understand and distract the reader. Keep practicing, Leo! Focusing on expanding your ideas and carefully checking your grammar and spelling will make a big difference in the clarity and quality of your writing.	Leo, you introduce Shanghai as your favorite city and mention some aspects like food and stores. You also include personal observations about condos and "weird foods." However, the content feels underdeveloped, and some points are tangential or confusing, such as the sudden mention of a "rock climbing compitition." To achieve a higher score like a 5, you would need to fully address the prompt by providing more relevant and well-developed ideas about *why* Shanghai is your favorite city, perhaps with more positive or detailed descriptions of its appeal, and fewer irrelevancies. The essay is also significantly below the expected word count, which limits the development of content.	You attempt to communicate straightforward ideas, but the effectiveness in holding the target reader's attention is limited. The text struggles with conveying its purpose clearly due to abrupt shifts in ideas and awkward phrasing. For instance, the question "did people live like this?" and the comment about people not being friendly feel out of place in a description of a favorite city. To improve to a 5, you would need to use the conventions of a descriptive essay more effectively, maintaining a consistent tone and purpose, and employing more engaging language and sentence structures to communicate both straightforward and more complex ideas appropriately.	The text is divided into paragraphs, but the coherence is weak, and the connections between ideas are very basic or absent. There is a lack of logical flow, with new ideas introduced abruptly. For example, "One thing that I notice about Shang hai. there wasn't a single house..." introduces a new thought without clear linkage to the preceding paragraph. The final sentence, "That said, Shang hai is High tech city mix with China," also feels disconnected from the rest of the essay. To reach a score of 5, you would need to organize your text in a more coherent way, using a variety of cohesive devices and organizational patterns to link ideas smoothly across and within paragraphs, ensuring a logical progression throughout the essay.	Leo, your essay uses some everyday vocabulary, but there are frequent and noticeable errors in spelling, capitalization, and grammar, which sometimes impede communication. For example, "Shang hai" should be "Shanghai," "famouse" should be "famous," "beutiful" should be "beautiful," "rabits" should be "rabbits," "compitition" should be "competition," and "delicouse" should be "delicious." Capitalization is inconsistent (e.g., "China" vs. "china," "south asia" vs. "South Asia"). Grammatical errors include issues with article usage ("a a big city"), subject-verb agreement ("there wasn't a single house it just condos" instead of "there *were* just condos"), and awkward phrasing like "High tech city mix with China." While meaning can sometimes be determined, the accumulation of errors makes the text difficult to read and understand. To improve to a 5, you would need to demonstrate a much higher degree of control over a range of vocabulary and grammatical forms, with errors being only occasional and non-impeding.	2.0	1.0	1.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
