explicitly linking the reasons for choosing these activities more directly to your *personal* preference, rather than just general observations about Thai teens.<br><br>	<b>COMMUNICATIVE ACHIEVEMENT: 3/5</b><br>You made a good effort to maintain an essay-appropriate tone, which is important for this assessment. However, sometimes your sentences were joined in a way that felt more like spoken Thai than formal written English, often using comma splices. This can make your writing less engaging and harder for the reader to follow. Remember that in English, separate complete thoughts usually need stronger connections than just a comma.<br><br>	<b>ORGANISATION: 3/5</b><br>You structured your essay clearly with an introduction, two body paragraphs, and a conclusion, which is a great start for an essay. Your paragraphs had clear topics. However, the abrupt ending of your conclusion meant your essay didn't feel fully wrapped up. Also, the frequent comma splices sometimes made the flow between ideas less smooth, affecting the overall coherence of your arguments.<br><br>	<b>LANGUAGE: 3/5</b><br>You used some excellent vocabulary, such as "inestimable value" and "digital creativity," which shows a good range of words! The main challenges in your writing were with grammar rules like subject-verb agreement, article use (a/an/the), plural nouns, and spelling ("exciment" instead of "excitement," "freetime" instead of "free time," "outgoing" instead of "going out"). Additionally, you often joined independent clauses with only a comma (comma splices), which is a common pattern in Thai when connecting ideas, but in English, this usually requires a conjunction (like 'and', 'but', 'so') or a stronger punctuation mark (like a semicolon or a full stop).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Freetime is a time where everyone usually do the things they like, although every country have freetime, but Thailand actually have less freetime than anyone else, so in Thailand freetime is inestimable value that Thai people yearn to have, three of the most famous and full of exciment activities that Thai people mostly choose are, travelling with friends and playing video games."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Free time is a period when everyone usually *does* the things they like. Although every country *has* free time, Thailand actually *has* less than many others, so in Thailand, free time is an inestimable value that Thai people yearn to have. Three of the most famous and exciting activities that Thai people mostly choose are travelling with friends and playing video games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, it's common to string many clauses together with commas. In English, each complete thought often needs its own sentence or a stronger connection (like a conjunction or semicolon). Also, we need subject-verb agreement (everyone *does*, country *has*) and correct articles (an *inestimable* value).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "One of the most successful hobbies that Thai teens often choose is travelling with friends, so you see, during the school time most Thai students are mostly concentrating in their school work or school project, which means that they don't usually talk together that much, so one of the first things to interact more with other friends is by outgoing with them, so to include interacting with friends and doing things teenager like during freetime, going out or travelling with friends would be the best option for it."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "One of the most popular hobbies that Thai teens often choose is travelling with friends. During school time, most Thai students are usually concentrating on their school work or school projects, which means they don't typically talk together much. Therefore, one of the best ways to interact more with friends and engage in activities teenagers enjoy during free time is by going out or travelling with them."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Again, the original sentence was a run-on. Breaking it into shorter, more distinct sentences improves clarity. "Outgoing" is not typically used as a verb in this context; "going out" or "socialising" would be more appropriate. "Teenager" should be pluralized if referring to a general group.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Video game is a game which is full of digital creativity and can fulfill most childhood time, same as in Thailand where people often on their electric devices such as Ipad, phone, computer which all of this devices are full of video games, so whenever they have their freetime one of the activities they always do is open their devices and play their video game."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Video games are a form of entertainment full of digital creativity and can enrich most childhoods. Similarly, in Thailand, people are often on their electronic devices, such as iPads, phones, and computers, all of which are full of video games. So, whenever they have free time, one of the activities they always do is open their devices and play their video games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> "Video game" should be plural if referring to the general concept, and "childhood time" is better as "childhoods." "Electric devices" is more accurately "electronic devices." The use of "this" with plural "devices" is incorrect; "these devices" or "all of which" is needed. The long sentence again demonstrates the comma splice pattern, which is clarified by breaking it into more manageable parts.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1.  **Practice Sentence Construction:** Focus on writing clear, complete sentences and use conjunctions (and, but, because, so, therefore) or semicolons to correctly join independent clauses, rather than just commas. Review how to avoid "run-on sentences" and "comma splices."<br>2.  **Review Subject-Verb Agreement & Plurals:** Pay close attention to making sure your verbs agree with your subjects (e.g., "everyone *does*", "country *has*") and correctly forming plural nouns (e.g., "activities," "devices," "teenagers").<br>3.  **Refine Word Choice:** Work on distinguishing between similar words (e.g., "electric" vs. "electronic," "outgoing" as an adjective vs. "going out" as a verb phrase). Make a list of new vocabulary and practice using it in different sentences.<br><br>	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
2068	29599	2025-09-23 08:35:48.474865+00	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and i arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible which is the diy solar system that someone is holding it while walking throgh the school hall way, that make everyone that saw it was in shock and jaw droped, and guess what me and my friend see!, We have seen the way teacher react to that diy solar system, teacher was so proud of her and she said that this is the most incredible work that all student she teach used to did for this sci homework, even the girl who did this for her group project was so proud of her self too!, even it's not us but we happy and glad to see other people have a positive feelings. and then when it's our turn to do that project, we already try our best but we still can't do the same or better than that girl did, but nevermind we still pass that project	161	13.0	Hello Praewwan,<br><br>It is wonderful to see your enthusiasm for storytelling in this piece. You have a lively way of describing events and emotions that really draws the reader in. Your story about the DIY solar system was engaging and it was clear you were excited to share this "incredible" moment. To make your stories even clearer and more polished, focusing on how you connect your sentences and make sure each one is perfectly formed will really help your writing soar. Keep up your imaginative storytelling. ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   ✍️ Practice breaking longer sentences into shorter, clearer ones to help your reader follow your ideas more easily.<br>*   🧐 Pay close attention to verb tenses, especially when telling a story about past events, to make sure they are consistent.<br>*    punctuation 🛑 Use full stops and capital letters more regularly to clearly show where one sentence ends and another begins.	Praewwan, you did a good job of starting your story exactly as the prompt asked and immediately introduced an exciting event – seeing an incredible DIY solar system at school. You also showed us how everyone, including your teacher, reacted, which made the story feel very real. To help your reader understand the story even better, think about how all parts of your story fit together. For example, after describing the amazing solar system, you could have focused more on how that specific event impacted you and your friend *at that moment* or what directly happened next because of seeing it. The part about your own project felt a little separate from the main "incredible" event. Try to keep all the details closely connected to the central idea of your story, so everything feels like one smooth journey for the reader. 🚀	You have a lovely, engaging voice in your writing, Praewwan. Phrases like "guess what me and my friend see!" truly make the reader feel involved in your experience. You clearly wanted to share an exciting story, and you largely succeeded in capturing that enthusiasm, which is fantastic for a story written for young people. To make your communication even more effective, work on smoothing out some of the grammatical rough edges. For instance, if you said, "Everyone who saw it was in shock and their jaws dropped," instead of "everyone that saw it was in shock and jaw droped," the flow would be even better, and your message would be super clear. Focusing on these little details will help your story captivate your readers even more. 🗣️🌟	Your story follows a logical order, starting with the incredible sight and moving through the reactions to your own project. This shows you have a good sense of how a story progresses. However, your writing tends to connect many ideas with "and" or "which is," creating very long sentences. Imagine your story as a path; sometimes, using too many connecting words makes the path very long without clear resting spots. To improve this, try using more full stops to create shorter, distinct sentences. For example, instead of writing "we saw something incredible which is the diy solar system that someone is holding it while walking throgh the school hall way, that make everyone that saw it was in shock and jaw droped," you could write: "We saw something incredible. Someone was walking through the school hallway, holding a DIY solar system. Everyone who saw it was in shock and their jaws dropped." See how much clearer that is? This helps your readers follow your thoughts more easily and gives your story a nicer rhythm. 🏞️📝	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit your story perfectly, Praewwan. Describing the "diy solar system" and people being "in shock and jaw droped" (which should be 'their jaws dropped') shows you can choose words that bring your story to life. You also attempted to use more complex sentence structures, which is a commendable step. However, there are some areas where your grammar could be more accurate, particularly with subject-verb agreement and consistent verb tenses. For instance, you wrote "that make everyone" instead of "that made everyone" or "that makes everyone." Also, "all student she teach used to did" should be something like "all the students she teaches used to do." These small changes make a big difference in how clearly your ideas come across. In Thai, verb tenses are often understood from context, but in English, using the correct tense is very important for showing *when* things happened. Practising these grammatical points will help your writing become much stronger and more precise. Keep up the great effort. 🚀📚	3.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
