ice <b>is</b> about 400 <b>Baht</b> per night."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can describe something by saying "มันดี" (It good) without a linking verb. However, in English, a sentence always needs a main verb. When you are describing a person, place, or thing, you must use <b>is/am/are</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Spelling and L1 Interference</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It not to fat from school, The museum is usually opens at 10 am."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It <b>is not too far</b> from <b>the</b> school, and the museum usually <b>opens</b> at 10 am."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You likely meant "far" (ไกล) but wrote "fat" (อ้วน). Because these words sound similar to some learners, it is easy to swap them! Also, remember that in English, we say "far from here" or "10 minutes away" rather than just "far" by itself.</li><li><b>📝 Capital Letters for Names and Sentences</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Dear maya, I'm so happy to know your class gonna visit my school next month."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Dear <b>M</b>aya, I'm so happy to know your class <b>is going to</b> visit my school next month."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Thai script does not have capital letters, so many students forget to use them in English. In English, you must ALWAYS use a capital letter for people's names (Maya), the word "I", and at the very beginning of every sentence.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>8</b> sentences, and <b>2</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Capitalisation Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Missing Verbs:</b> 2 instances</l