1052	28841	2025-08-16 21:27:58.395903+00	Thai Food	One of everyone's major needs is food. Although every culture has food, each culture has its own unique method to make food. These cooking methods have been passed down from generation to another generation until nowaday. In Thailand, there is a region called Issan also known as Northeast, which has a lot of amazing food and most of Thai people have tried it once in their lives. There are three famous food from Issan which are Som tum, Larb and Prala.<br><br><br>The most well-known Issan food is Som tum. People use a papaya, chilli, lime, and sometimes add crab too, which up to what kind of Som tum it is. The way to make Som tum is to put all ingredients into a mortar, a bowl-like thing that usually made from wood or sometimes stone and smash it repeatedly but not too hard with a pestle then pour a little bit of Prala's sap for salty taste. In the past, there was no refrigerator to keep Som tum from rotting so they added chilli into Som tum to slow down the rotting process and Som tum turned out to become even more delicious with chilli in it which is why Som tum has become so famous that many tourists want to try it. Originally, chilli wasn't grown in Thailand in the first place but Protuguese and later, chilli was brought to Thailand with King Rama IV's approve.<br><br><br>Besides Som tum, Larb is popular among Thai people and tourists too. Its ingredients included grinded pork, vegetables, lime, chilli, and beetroot. Sometimes people add powdered bean into Larb is very similar to Som tum excepted for grinded pork, which need to be cooked first, and a beetroot, which need to be chopped before adding into a mortar. It doesn't need to add Prala's sap like Som tum but people rarely add it too if the taste is very terrible. The pork in Larb shows us that in the olden days, framers did pig farm for its delicious meat, and people used it for making Larb.<br><br><br>The final famous food from Issan is Prala, which is a food that's known for a very strong horrible smell. To make Prala, you only need a bowl, water, some salt, and fish in order to make it. You have to chop fish into small pieces then put into a bowl, which was filled with water mixed with salt, and close the top of the bowl with a cloth to prevent flies or other insects. After all this you have to leave that bowl for around 1 week to 1 month. During this process, you should also have it outside of your house or somewhere that people visit to avoid a pretty terrible smell. When the time, you can take off a cloth and enjoy what is inside. People in the past use these method to perceive fish from rotting, just like Som tum but with no chilli.<br><br><br>In conclusion, Thailand or other countries have their own signature food menu, which sometimes similar to another culture or completely different. Food can also represent lifestyle and environment of the past. For these three foods from Issan, Som tum Larb, and Prala can represent Thai culture very well and attract people for around the world, who want to try because of unique taste or from its background before becoming what we know in nowaday.	565	\N	Overall, your essay is well-structured and clearly addresses the topic of Thai food, specifically from the Issan region. You successfully used the required linking phrase "Besides..." at the start of your second body paragraph. You also included some interesting historical and cultural details about the food, which is a great strength. To improve further, focus on refining your sentence structures, particularly by practicing relative clauses and passive voice, and paying close attention to word choice and common spelling errors.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Writing	Formative	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026
