3093	29720	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	To Maya,<br><br>That's so lovely to hear that you are visiting my school next month. I'm so excited to see you! So, if you want a hostel to stay near the school I recommend you the "Assumption's Hostel". You can find it easily because it's really close to the school and it's also very cheap! Also if you want to learn more about Thai communication history. There's 2 museum far away but you can use google map to find it. I visited it before with my family and it's absolutely beautiful. Also, when you visit my school, you shouldn't point at other people because it's disrespectful. It might seem strange in your culture but people here doesn't likes it.<br><br>See you soon,<br><br>Dragon	120	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2	3	2.28	Hi Dragon! You've done a great job sharing information about local history and culture in this writing.<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You used connectors like "if" and "but" to join your thoughts together, which helps the reader follow your ideas easily.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: You attempted a complex "if" sentence to provide more options to the reader, showing a great effort to move beyond simple sentences.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used precise topic-related words like "communication history" and "culture" correctly to explain your points.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 "There is/There are" and Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"There's 2 museum far away but you can use google map to find it."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"There <b>are</b> 2 <b>museums</b> far away, but you can use <b>Google Maps</b> to find <b>them</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, we use "มี" for everything. In English, we use "There is" for one thing and "There are" for two or more. Because you said "2", you must use "are". Also, Thai nouns don't change for plurals, but in English, you must add "-s" to "museum" when there is more than one.</li><br><li><b>📝 Subject-Verb Agreement with "People"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It might seem strange in your culture but people here doesn't likes it."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It might seem strange in your culture, but people here <b>don't like</b> it."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, verbs don't change based on the subject (คนไม่ชอบ, เขาไม่ชอบ). However, in English, "people" is treated as a plural group. This means we must use "don't" instead of "doesn't". Also, remember that after "do/does/don't/doesn't", we never add an "s" to the main verb like "likes".</li><br><li><b>📝 Completing your "If" thoughts</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Also if you want to learn more about Thai communication history."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Also, if you want to learn more about Thai communication history, <b>you should visit the museum</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> An "if" sentence usually has two parts: the condition and the result. In Thai, sometimes we leave the result out if we think the listener understands. In English, a sentence starting with "If" feels unfinished without a second part telling the reader what to do or what will happen.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>9</b> sentences, and <b>5</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Noun Plural Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Capitalization Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Punctuation Errors:</b> 1 instance</li></ol><br>	B1	2026
