1691	28997	2025-09-12 11:04:52.993227+00	Term 1 Exam Question 1	Nowadays, many teenagers did not choose the choices by themself, but they make choices because of their friends. They think that their friends are following trends and they think that is cool. These are three choices that teenagers get effect by their friends.<br><br>First of all, clothes and fashion. Many people including adults also like to following trends because it will look stunning, beautiful, gorgous and other. So if their friends choose a clothes or other things that is about fashion, they would think that they fit in any society, such as school, workplace or anywhere.<br><br>Besides clothes and fashion, they also studying because of their friends too. If they want to go to the same school, they need to really focus about studying. So, that encourage them to study like their other friends. And when you choose to study together, you can make a group to go study somewhere that is not in the house. It will make the study more fun and more fasinating.<br><br>The last one is their society. Many teens that choose the wrong path, it is because of their friends that persuade them to do something really bad such as drinking an alcohol, do something illegal. furthermore, have a illegal stuff. If you have a good friends, that is really lucky for you.<br><br>To summarize, many teenagers choose who they want to be, what to wear, what to think by get suggest from their friends because they think it is the best for them. Nothing wrong with that but, I think we also need to make choice by themself not only from their friends.	265	12.0	Well done, Grace! It's clear you put a lot of thought into your essay about how friends influence teenagers. You've certainly shown that you can put your ideas down on paper. While your essay has some good points, the biggest thing to work on is making sure you directly answer the main question throughout your whole piece, and polishing up some of your grammar. Keep practising, and you'll see your writing grow stronger! 📋<br><br>#### Action Plan<br>* Make sure you understand *all* parts of the question, especially the main point, and refer back to it as you write. ✅<br>* Read your essay aloud after you finish. This can help you spot awkward sentences or missing words. ✅<br>* Pay close attention to verb tenses, especially when talking about past events, and how pronouns like 'themself' should be 'themselves'. ✅	You've done a good job, Grace, of including the points about clothes and fashion, and studying, and even adding your own interesting idea about how friends influence "society" – which is a great way to talk about social groups and how we behave. 👍 However, the main question asked whether friends or parents have *more* influence, and your essay focused mostly on friends. Next time, try to remember the core question as you write each part of your essay. For instance, after discussing how friends influence clothing choices, you could add a sentence or two about how parents might also play a role, or how a teenager might balance both influences. This helps show that you're directly answering *all* parts of the prompt, making your argument even more persuasive. 😊	You've managed to get your message across quite well, Grace, which is the most important thing! Your writing is generally easy to follow. To make your essays even stronger, especially for a more formal piece like this, try to make your opening and closing paragraphs sound a bit more serious and academic. For example, instead of "These are three choices that teenagers get effect by their friends," you could try something like, "Teenagers' choices are often significantly shaped by their friends, particularly in three key areas." Also, keeping your language a little more formal throughout, rather than conversational, will really help your essays shine. ⭐ Thai learners sometimes find it tricky to switch between informal everyday English and more formal academic writing, so it's a super important skill to keep working on!	Your essay is well-structured, Grace, with clear paragraphs for each of your ideas, which makes it easy for your reader to follow your thoughts. You've also used connecting words like "First of all," "Besides," and "To summarize" to guide the reader, which is excellent. Perhaps next time, you could try to vary these linking phrases a little more to make your writing even more sophisticated and natural. Instead of always starting with "The last one is," you might consider phrases like "Finally," or "In addition to these points..." Good organisation is a fantastic foundation for any piece of writing, and you're doing a great job here! ✨	This is an area where a little extra practice will make a big difference, Grace. You've got some good vocabulary, but there are a few places where the grammar could be tightened up to make your meaning clearer and more precise. For example, in your first paragraph, "many teenagers did not choose the choices by themself" could be "many teenagers do not make their own choices," and "they get effect by their friends" should be "they are affected by their friends." Also, in the second paragraph, "like to following trends" should be "like to follow trends." Another common area to watch out for is spelling; for example, "gorgous" should be "gorgeous" and "fasinating" should be "fascinating." Many Thai learners find English verb tenses and pronoun agreement a bit tricky because their own language works differently. Don't worry, with consistent practice, especially focusing on these common mistakes, your language accuracy will improve heaps! Keep a notebook of common errors and try to correct them as you write. 📝	3.0	3.0	4.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
3095	31416	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	I think hostel is better than hotel. It's near our school so you won't be tried. A price are cheap and in the room are very clean. I believe that you will love it. When you drive from school 30 minute you will see a museum. There're a lot of visitor. It's opean 8:00 am - 18:30 pm. In the museum has many interest thing and you will see an old collect things in thailand. My school don't has many rules. But you should wai to our teacher. Another polite tips is don't talk when other people talking or explain something. You shouldn't point at people because thai people are very sensitive.<br><br>see you soon,<br><br>Tonnam	113	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2	2.28	Hi Tonnam! You did a great job describing your school and the museum nearby!<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You used connecting words like "When" and "and" to combine your ideas into longer sentences.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used specific words related to the topic like "museum", "rules", and "collect".</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> You successfully attempted a time-clause structure ("When you drive... you will see") which is a great way to give directions.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Subject-Verb Agreement (is vs are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"A price are cheap and in the room are very clean."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The price <b>is</b> cheap and the <b>rooms are</b> very clean."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, describing words like <b>"ถูก"</b> or <b>"สะอาด"</b> don't need a helping verb that changes. In English, we must match the subject. One <b>"price"</b> (ราคา) uses <b>"is"</b>, but many <b>"rooms"</b> (ห้อง) use <b>"are"</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"When you drive from school 30 minute you will see a museum."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"When you drive from school for 30 <b>minutes</b>, you will see a museum."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, we use the same word for singular and plural: <b>"นาที"</b>. But in English, if the number is more than one, we MUST add an <b>"s"</b>. Don't forget to add <b>"s"</b> to <b>"thing"</b> too!</li><li><b>📝 Negative Verbs and "Have"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"My school don't has many rules."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"My school <b>doesn't have</b> many rules."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is a common mistake because in Thai we use <b>"ไม่มี"</b> for everything. In English, for a singular subject like <b>"school"</b>, we use <b>"doesn't"</b>. Also, after <b>"doesn't"</b>, the verb always goes back to the base form: <b>"have"</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Capital Letters and Punctuation</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"see you soon, Tonnam"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"<b>S</b>ee you soon, Tonnam<b>.</b>"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Since Thai script doesn't have <b>ตัวพิมพ์ใหญ่</b>, it is easy to forget them. Remember that EVERY English sentence must start with a capital letter and end with a full stop.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>10</b> sentences, and <b>4</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Verb Agreement and Form Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Missing or Incorrect Articles (a/the):</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Capitalisation Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Plural Noun Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
