3096	33896	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	Dear, Maya<br><br>Hi Maya, your class is going to visit my school next month. Let me give you a recommend hotel to stay. "Sky hotel, which is the hotel that next to my school. I think it should be the good choice for you and your friends because the price it's very cheap. It just 500 $ per day. There's also has a museum near the school which is "Thai museum". There's has a lot of exhibition about Thai communication history. On week days, it's open at 7 o'clock in the morning and close at 8 o'clock in the evening. But weekend, it'll closed. Let me give you some polite body language. Firstly, if you want to order something in the shop, you should point at the thing that you need, don't point at the people, it will be rude. Secondly, you should smile at people so, they will smile at you back.<br><br>Good luck,<br><br>Kati	152	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2	2.28	Hi Kati! I really enjoyed reading your hotel and museum recommendations. You did a great job sharing helpful information!<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You used connectors like "because" to explain your reasons and "which" to give extra information about the hotel and museum.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used specific words like "exhibition" and "communication history," which made your writing more interesting.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: I noticed you tried to use relative clauses (sentences with "which") to add more detail to your descriptions.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Choosing between "There is" and "Have"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"There's has a lot of exhibition about Thai communication history."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"There <b>are</b> a lot of exhibition<b>s</b> about Thai communication history."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is a very common mistake for Thai learners because in Thai, we use the word "มี" for both "have" and "there is". However, in English, we never say "There has". We use <b>"There is"</b> or <b>"There are"</b> to show that something exists. Also, because you said "a lot of", you need to add an "-s" to "exhibition".</li><li><b>📝 Missing the "BE" verb (is/am/are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It just 500 $ per day."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It <b>is</b> just <b>$500</b> per day."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can say "มันแค่ 500" without using a linking verb. But in English, every sentence needs a main verb. When you are describing a price or a state, you must use <b>"is"</b>. You also did this in the sentence about the hotel <i>"that <b>is</b> next to my school."</i></li><li><b>📝 Adjectives ending in -ed</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Let me give you a recommend hotel to stay."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Let me give you a <b>recommended</b> hotel to stay <b>at</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> When we use a verb to describe a noun (like a hotel that has been suggested by someone), we often need to turn it into an adjective by adding <b>"-ed"</b>.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>10</b> sentences, and <b>4</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Missing Verbs:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Article and Determiner Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Noun Number (singular/plural) Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Preposition Errors:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
