 as your friend. Yeah.<br><br>IE IE: Um, maybe I think in future people will start to maybe starting to, um, communicate less as as said face to face and more like sending message instead, like sending message or talking communicate with on online instead.	768	11.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Your intonation was generally appropriate, helping us understand your meaning. However, a key area for you to work on is making your sounds clearer, especially the /s/ sound. Words like "schools" or "virus" need a distinct, hissing /s/. Also, pay close attention to pronouncing final consonant sounds in words like "drink" /drɪŋk/, "worth" /wɜːθ/, and "it's" /ɪts/. Sometimes these endings were not clearly heard. This is a common challenge for Thai speakers because the Thai language often drops or softens final consonants and does not have the same sharp /s/ sound, but mastering these will make you much more easily understood. Practice these sounds consciously!	Ie Ie, you did a really good job in your speaking test today! You managed to communicate your ideas well and engage with your partner. Keep up the fantastic effort in your English studies, and you'll continue to see improvements. 🌟<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br>• Focus on pronouncing final consonant sounds and clear /s/ sounds.<br>• Practice using a wider range of vocabulary and more accurate grammar when talking about the past.<br>• Work on smoothly guiding group discussions to a clear decision.<br><br>**Grammar and Vocabulary: 3/5**<br>You showed good control of simple sentences and tried to use more complex ones, which is great! You also used appropriate words for the topics. However, sometimes your grammar could be a bit more precise, especially with verb forms like "it make our life easier" which should be "it *makes* our life easier," or "doesn't have the same interesting" which would be better as "doesn't have the same *interests*." To help you improve, try actively noticing and correcting these small grammar points when you speak and write. Thai speakers sometimes use slightly different sentence structures or verb forms which can lead to these kinds of errors in English.<br><br>**Discourse Management: 3/5**<br>You spoke for a good amount of time and kept your answers relevant to the questions, which shows you can manage your ideas well. You used connecting words like "because" and "so" to link your thoughts. To develop this further, try to reduce hesitations by thinking for a moment before you speak, and aim to use a wider variety of phrases to introduce your points. For instance, instead of always starting with "I think," you could say "In my opinion," or "I believe that." You covered all the points expected in the tasks, and your word count was excellent, showing your ability to produce extended spoken English.<br><br>**Pronunciation: 1/5**<br>Your intonation was generally appropriate, helping us understand your meaning. However, a key area for you to work on is making your sounds clearer, especially the /s/ sound. Words like "schools" or "virus" need a distinct, hissing /s/. Also, pay close attention to pronouncing final consonant sounds in words like "drink" /drɪŋk/, "worth" /wɜːθ/, and "it's" /ɪts/. Sometimes these endings were not clearly heard. This is a common challenge for Thai speakers because the Thai language often drops or softens final consonants and does not have the same sharp /s/ sound, but mastering these will make you much more easily understood. Practice these sounds consciously!<br><br>**Interactive Communication: 4/5**<br>You did a good job of talking with your partner, Tiw! You initiated discussions, responded thoughtfully, and linked your ideas to what Tiw was saying, which kept the conversation flowing naturally. To aim for a top score, try to push a bit more towards making clear decisions together, especially in the collaborative tasks. For example, after discussing different options, you could say something like, "So, are we both agreeing that playing online games is the most fun?" This helps to really negotiate and confirm an outcome as a pair.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1yqGR5ip5XtVuoI2jPIboJbuDxmsnlN7a?usp=drive_link	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
