 you've addressed every single note from the task. Thai learners sometimes focus on brevity, but providing enough detail is very important for good communication.	You've made a good start here, Siri. You used a friendly greeting like "Hi" and a closing like "Bye, Siri," which is just right for an informal email to a friend. Using paragraph breaks also helps to organise your thoughts, making your email easier to read. 😊 To make your writing even more effective and hold the reader's attention, you could try to vary your sentence structures a little more and perhaps add a few more details to make your responses more engaging. For instance, instead of just saying "Let's go there," you could add a phrase like "I'm really looking forward to it."	It's commendable that you used paragraphs in your email, Siri, as this helps to separate different ideas. However, sometimes your ideas jump a little between paragraphs, and this can make it a bit difficult for the reader to follow. You also repeated some phrases, like "buid a robot we can watch movie next time," which can make your writing feel a little less organised. To improve, think about how each sentence connects to the one before it, and try to use linking words to guide your reader smoothly from one idea to the next. For example, you could use words like 'because,' 'also,' or 'but' to link your thoughts more clearly. Some Thai learners sometimes write shorter sentences without explicitly connecting them, which can impact the overall flow.	Siri, you've used some basic vocabulary that is appropriate for the topic, which is a good foundation. However, to really strengthen your writing, we need to focus on improving accuracy in your spelling and grammar. For example, I noticed a few spelling mistakes, such as "buid" instead of "build" and "flim" instead of "film." A good habit is to always proofread your work carefully, and if you're unsure of a spelling, a dictionary can be a valuable friend. Another area to focus on is correct verb forms and prepositions. For instance, you wrote "we going to eat breakfast" instead of "we *are* going to eat breakfast," and "bring you jacket" instead of "bring *your* jacket." These small changes can make a big difference to how clear and natural your writing sounds. Thai learners often face challenges with English verb tenses and prepositions because their native language has different grammatical structures, so a bit of focused practice here will be very beneficial.	1.0	3.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A1+	\N	\N	\N	A1+	2026
