ich is a good start. Keep practicing, and you will see improvement. 😊	You clearly state your opinion that mobile phones should be allowed in the classroom and provide several reasons to support it. You have many ideas, like using phones for information and fun learning games. However, your paragraph is quite long, and sometimes it's hard to follow all your points because of the length.<br><b>Problem:</b> You have many good ideas, but the paragraph is very long, making it hard for the reader to follow all your points and main arguments.<br><b>Correction:</b> You could split your writing into smaller paragraphs, with each paragraph focusing on one main idea. For example, you could have one paragraph about phones for information and another about phones for learning games. This makes your ideas clearer.<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English academic writing, paragraphs usually focus on one main idea. Thai writing sometimes allows for longer sections of text, but in English, breaking down ideas helps the reader understand your argument step-by-step.	You communicate your ideas directly, and your tone is quite engaging. However, for an academic paragraph about a social issue, the language sometimes feels too informal, like when you say "phone help us so much." This makes it less suitable for a formal essay. You successfully communicate your main message, but a more formal style would be better for this type of writing.<br><b>Problem:</b> Your writing uses informal language that is not typical for an academic essay, such as "phone help us so much in many things."<br><b>Correction:</b> Instead of "phone help us so much in many things," you could write: "Mobile phones significantly assist us in various aspects" or "Mobile phones provide substantial assistance in numerous ways."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, there might be less distinction in formality for certain phrases. In English, academic writing requires more formal vocabulary and sentence structures to sound appropriate and credible. Choosing words like "significantly assist" instead of "help so much" makes your writing more suitable for an academic context.	You try to connect your ideas using words like "first of all" and "because," which is a good start. However, your sentences are often very long and combine many different thoughts without clear breaks. This makes it difficult for the reader to understand the connection between your ideas and follow your argument smoothly. It's like one very long sentence that keeps going and going.<br><b>Problem:</b> Your sentences are often very long and contain many ideas, making them difficult to read and understand, for example: "In my view, mobile phones should be allowed to get use in classroom because phone help us so much in many things, first of all phone make people can find any information they want so much easier than the past, because if you use your phone you just go ahead to google and type the quition or things you wanted to know and enter, in less than one second you can get the information you want, if you need more you can enter the website to find more if you want to..."<br><b>Correction:</b> You could break this into shorter, clearer sentences: "In my view, mobile phones should be allowed in the classroom. They help us in many ways. First of all, phones make it much easier to find information than in the past. If you use your phone, you can simply go to Google, type your question, and get the information you want in less than a second."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Thai sentence structure often allows for longer, more continuous clauses. In English, it's common to break down complex ideas into shorter, separate sentences, or to use precise punctuation (like periods) to create clear boundaries between distinct thoughts. This improves readability and coherence for the English reader.	You use everyday words that are generally understandable. However, there are many noticeable errors in basic grammar, like using "phone" instead of "phones" for plural, or "it have" instead of "it has." These mistakes, especially when sentences are very long, can sometimes make your meaning unclear. Focusing on these basic grammar points will make a big difference.<br><b>Problem:</b> You sometimes forget to add '-s' for plural nouns, for example, "phone help us" instead of "phones help us."<br><b>Correction:</b> You should write "phones help us" because you are talking about more than one phone.<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, we don't usually add an 's' to show that something is plural. In English, we add '-s' to most nouns when there is more than one. This is a common difference between Thai and English grammar.<br><br><b>Problem:</b> You use "it have" instead of "it has," for example, "it have more application."<br><b>Correction:</b> The correct form is "it has more applications."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, when you use 'he', 'she', or 'it', the verb changes. For 'have', it changes to 'has'. This is called subject-verb agreement. Thai verbs do not change like this, which can be confusing.<br><br><b>Problem:</b> You sometimes use the wrong tense, especially when talking about the past, for example, "in the past if you want to have fun."<br><b>Correction:</b> It should be "in the past if you wanted to have fun" or "in the past if you wanted to had fun."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, we change the verb form to show when something happened (past, present, future). In Thai, we often use time words (like 'yesterday') to show the time, and the verb itself does not change. So, when you talk about something "in the past," the verb "want" needs to change to "wanted."	3.0	3.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
