Thai, nouns don't change to show there are many things (e.g., "5 เตียง" doesn't add an "s"). In English, if there is more than one, you must add "s" to the noun (5 <b>beds</b>). Also, for a single subject like "one room", the verb "have" must change to <b>has</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Run-on Sentences and Punctuation</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"There's a hotel called "Hamilton Hotel" around 5 km from my school it got 5 stars review on google which costs around 50 $ per night."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"There's a hotel called "Hamilton Hotel" about 5 km from my school. It <b>has a 5-star</b> review on Google <b>and</b> costs around $50 per night."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You had three separate thoughts here! In English, we cannot just keep adding information without using a full stop (.) or a connector like "and". In Thai, we often string phrases together, but in English, a sentence becomes too long and "run-on" if we don't break it up.</li><li><b>📝 Verb Agreement with Plural Subjects</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"They got many fossils and they also explains about solar system."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"They <b>have</b> many fossils, and they also <b>explain</b> about <b>the</b> solar system."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, the verb "อธิบาย" stays the same no matter who is speaking. In English, we only add "s" to the verb when the subject is one person (he/she/it). When the subject is "they", we use the base verb: <b>explain</b>.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! Your vocabulary is already very strong. 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>7</b> sentences, and <b>2</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 2 instances (e.g., "musaim")</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 2 instances (e.g., "it got" vs "it has")</li><li><b>Punctuation Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Missing Articles:</b> 1 instance (e.g., "the" solar system)</li><li><b>Noun Number (Plurals):</b> 1 instance (e.g., "5 bed" vs "5 beds")</li></ol>	B1	2026
3100	34931	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	Hello, Maya<br><br>I would like to help you on your questions. I see that you're searching for good place to stay near the school and, also a place to learn more about Thai communicate history. For a good place to stay. In my opinions, you should stay in the hostel. It's cheap and near the school.<br><br>Then, a place to learn more about Thai communication history. I would recommend a history museum. It's far away from the hostel 1 kilometers in the center of Nakon Pathom. It's opens at 8 am. and closes at 9 pm.<br><br>Aslo, I would like to give you a polite body-language tips. In Thailand, we usually cover our hands and say "Sawadee kab" instead of shakehands and smile. These are polite body-language you can use in Thailand.<br><br>Good luck,<br><br>Frame	130	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	3	2.28	Hi Frame! Great job explaining where to stay and giving helpful advice about Thai culture! You put a lot of thought into this.<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used excellent topic-specific words like "communication history", "body-language", and "searching for".</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You successfully connected your ideas using words like "and also", "then", and "instead of" to guide the reader.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> You attempted complex structures like "I would like to give you..." and the use of "instead of" to compare different cultural actions.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Describing Distance</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It's far away from the hostel 1 kilometers in the center of Nakon Pathom."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It's <b>1 kilometer away from</b> the hostel, in the center of Nakhon Pathom."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This happens because of the Thai phrase <b>"ไกลจาก"</b>. In English, when we give a specific measurement like "1 kilometer", we put the number BEFORE the words "away from". Also, because "1" is a single number, we do not need the "s" on "kilometer".</li><li><b>📝 Articles and Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Aslo, I would like to give you a polite body-language tips."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Also, I would like to give you <b>some</b> polite body language <b>tips</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, <b>"ทิปส์"</b> or <b>"คำแนะนำ"</b> doesn't change its form if there are many. In English, <b>"a"</b> is only used for ONE thing. Because "tips" is plural (it has an "s"), you should use <b>"some"</b> or no article at all.</li><li><b>📝 Natural Phrasing for Actions</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"In Thailand, we usually cover our hands and say "Sawadee kab" instead of shakehands and smile."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"In Thailand, we usually <b>put our hands together</b> and say "Sawadee krab" instead of <b>shaking</b> hands and <b>smiling</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You translated the action of <b>"พนมมือ"</b> literally. In natural English, we say "put our hands together". Additionally, after the phrase "instead of", English requires the <b>"-ing"</b> form of the verb (shaking and smiling).</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>9</b> sentences, and <b>5</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Articles and Determiners (a/the/some):</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Noun Number (singular vs plural):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Punctuation:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Other Word Form Errors:</b> 1 instance</li></ol><br>	B1	2026
3101	34936	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	Hellow maya you coming next month right? There is a good place to stay near the school is "fang dei". The hostel is a part place to stay a bit will. Is cost 2,000 $ for room there. the location at Bangkok, Dususi. For place to learn more about Thai communication history is base to be Gungpiqulya is a place were the old building there. When you visiting you should getting in Thai way just put your hand to geter and bow and say "sawaddi" is Hello in Thai you shouldn't be so much rude and eat in the public that for all your question. So see you next moth when you visit<br><br>see you.<br><br>R	113	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2	1	2.28	Hi Richter! You've put a lot of effort into sharing these helpful recommendations!<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used some great topic-specific words like "hostel", "location", and "communication history" to describe the places.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> I like how you attempted to use a "When" clause ("When you visiting...") to give advice to your friend.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You successfully used "and" to connect different actions, like "bow and say sawaddi".</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Missing the "BE" Verb (is/am/are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Hellow maya you coming next month right?"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Hello Maya, <b>are</b> you <b>coming</b> next month, right?"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can say "คุณมา" (khun maa) without a linking verb. But in English, when we talk about things happening now or in the future using "-ing", we ALWAYS need "is", "am", or "are". You also did this in "the location at Bangkok" which should be "The location <b>is in</b> Bangkok".</li><li><b>📝 Using "It" as a Subject</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Is cost 2,000 $ for room there."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"<b>It costs $2,000</b> for <b>a</b> room there."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, we often skip the subject and just say "ราคา..." (raakaa...). However, every English sentence needs a subject. When talking about prices or weather, we use "It". Also, remember that in English, the currency symbol ($) comes before the number!</li><li><b>📝 "Should" + Base Verb</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"When you visiting you should getting in Thai way"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"When visiting, you should <b>greet people</b> in the <b>Thai way</b>"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, verbs don't change their form. In English, after the word "should", we must use the base form of the verb (greet), not the "-ing" form (getting).</li><li><b>📝 Existence (There is/There are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"There is a good place to stay near the school is "fang dei"."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"There is a good place to stay near the school <b>called</b> "Fang Dei"."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You tried to use "There is" (มี) which is great! But you added a second "is" later in the sentence. In English, we only need one main verb per clause. To give a name to a place, we use the word "called".</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>7</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 5 instances</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Capitalisation Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Other Grammatical Errors:</b> 3 instances</li></ol>	A2	2026
