 be given equal time, unlike English, which is "stress-timed" and has some syllables that are longer and louder than others. To help with this, try listening to how native English speakers say things and imitate the "music" of their sentences. Recording yourself and comparing it to a model can be a super helpful way to hear where you can add more "tune" to your words! 🎤	Hi Ka-nhom! 👋 It was lovely to listen to your presentation about your favourite photograph. You made a good effort to organise your ideas and communicate your message clearly, which is a fantastic start! To do even better, focusing on extending your ideas and making your voice's rhythm more varied will really help you impress your listeners. Keep up the great work! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Ka-nhom:**<br>*   💡 **Practice expanding your points:** When you talk about something, try to add one or two more sentences to explain *why* or give an *example*. This makes your ideas much richer!<br>*   🗣️ **Work on your voice's music:** English has a special rhythm and tunes (intonation). Listen to English speakers and try to copy how their voices go up and down. This will make your speech sound more natural and engaging!<br>*   📝 **Review basic grammar rules:** Pay special attention to small but important words like 'a', 'an', 'the', and making sure you use words like 'am', 'is', 'are' when you need them, especially with 'going to'.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1239oN5PHBcxcQdDOcIWNI9TnYZoznqGS?usp=sharing	B1	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
