de my school is cost about 500 bath per day, it <b>have</b> an buffet breakfast too."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The price is a little bit cheaper than the hostel, and the location is good because it is beside my school. It costs about 500 Baht per day, and it <b>has a</b> buffet breakfast too."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, we use the word "มี" for everything. But in English, if the subject is "it," we must use "has." Also, when we want to say something exists, we use "There is" or "There are." In your sentence about the temple, instead of "it have lots of history," it is more natural to say "<b>there is</b> a lot of history there."</li><li><b>📝 Missing the "BE" verb (is/am/are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"And the place you could visit to learn more about Thai history is might be Wat Pra Keaw temple because in there it have lots of Thai history and also you can go to the near museum too I <b>don't sure</b> what the name is it, you can ask people there, thai people are friendly."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The place you could visit to learn more about Thai history might be Wat Phra Kaew temple because there is a lot of Thai history there. You can also go to the nearby museum. I <b>am not sure</b> what its name is, but you can ask people there. Thai people are friendly."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can say "ฉันไม่ชัวร์" without a linking verb. However, in English, "sure" is an adjective, so it always needs the verb "to be." We say "I <b>am</b> sure" or "I <b>am not</b> sure." We only use "don't" with action verbs like "don't know" (ไม่รู้).</li><li><b>📝 Capital Letters for Proper Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"the tips about the body language can be the bow and wai it's very polite if you do it."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"<b>A tip</b> about body language is the bow and wai. It's very polite if you do it."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Since Thai script doesn't use capital letters, it's easy to forget them in English! Remember that every new sentence must start with a capital letter, and specific names (like <b>T</b>hai, <b>W</b>at <b>P</b>hra <b>K</b>aew, or <b>B</b>aht) also need them.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>7</b> sentences, and <b>2</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Capitalization (Casing):</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Article/Determiner Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
