3103	35309	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	I would recommend staying in a hostel since your whole class is coming it will cost you a fortune to stay in a hotel. There's one hostel I've in mind, I always go there near the riverbank, the hygiene is great and the price is incredible only 3 pounds It's even located near one of Thai communication History musuem. While you're there don't point finger at people especially in the face it's ^considered rude and don't touch stuff without permission here it is forbidden to do so.<br><br>See you,<br><br>Fidel	88	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	3	2.28	Hi Atom! You put a lot of effort into this travel advice and it was very clear to read!<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used great topic-specific words and phrases like "cost you a fortune", "hygiene", and "forbidden" to give strong advice.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> You attempted complex structures using conjunctions like "since" and subordinate clauses like "While you're there", which shows a high level of ability.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You successfully used connectors like "while" and "and" to show the relationship between your ideas.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Ending Sentences with Full Stops (Punctuation)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"I would recommend staying in a hostel since your whole class is coming it will cost you a fortune to stay in a hotel."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"I would recommend staying in a hostel since your whole class is coming<b>. It would</b> cost you a fortune to stay in a hotel."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, we don't use full stops to end a sentence; we often use spaces instead. In English, when you have two complete thoughts (your class is coming + it will cost a fortune), you MUST separate them with a full stop. Otherwise, it becomes a "run-on sentence."</li><li><b>📝 Plural Nouns with "One of"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It's even located near one of Thai communication History musuem."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It's even located near one of <b>the</b> Thai <b>C</b>ommunication History <b>museums</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, nouns don't change their form to show there are many (e.g., "พิพิธภัณฑ์"). However, in English, the phrase "one of" implies you are picking one from a group of many. Therefore, the noun must be plural: "museums". Also, don't forget that specific names of places need a capital letter!</li><li><b>📝 Using "Your" with Body Parts</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"While you're there don't point finger at people especially in the face it's considered rude..."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"While you're there, don't point <b>your</b> finger at people, especially <b>at their</b> face—it's considered rude..."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, we can say "ชี้" or "ชี้นิ้ว" without needing to say who the finger belongs to. In English, we almost always use a possessive word like <b>"your"</b> or <b>"their"</b> when talking about parts of the body.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>4</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Punctuation Errors (Missing full stops/commas):</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Missing or Incorrect Articles (a/the):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Noun Number (Plural vs Singular):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Verb Tense:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Spelling:</b> 1 instance</li></ol><br><br>	B1	2026
