ically check your verbs! Make sure they agree with their subjects (e.g., if you're talking about "friends" – plural – the verb should also be plural, like "friends *have*").<br>*   🧐 Take a moment to proofread for spelling errors; a quick check can catch words like 'interests' or 'basically'. Using a dictionary or an online spell-checker can be really helpful here.<br>*   🤔 Pay close attention to small words like 'a', 'an', and 'the'. These "articles" are tricky, but getting them right makes your sentences sound much more natural.	You've done a very good job, Venice, addressing all parts of the essay question! You talked about how friends and parents influence choices regarding clothes and fashion, studying, and you also added your own interesting idea about how teenagers choose to spend their time. That's exactly what the question asked for! 🎉 However, to make your essay even stronger, try to develop all your points with similar detail. For example, while you spent a lot of time on friends' influence, you could expand a bit more on how parents' influence might play out, perhaps with more specific examples. It's also a good idea to try and keep your writing within the suggested word count, which was 140-190 words for this task; this helps you focus on expressing your ideas clearly and concisely.	You've made a great effort to write in a formal style suitable for an academic essay, and you've definitely communicated your main point very clearly – that friends often have more influence than parents. Well done! 🗣️ To push this score higher, try to maintain that formal tone throughout your essay. Sometimes, a phrase like "Nothing compe-cated" might feel a little too casual for an essay. Remember, in formal writing, we aim for a more consistent and serious approach to language. For instance, instead of "Nothing compe-cated," you might say "This is a straightforward point." This helps the reader take your academic argument seriously.	Your essay is well-organised, Venice, with a clear introduction, separate paragraphs for each of your points, and a conclusion. This makes it easy for the reader to follow your argument. 👍 You also use connecting words like "Then" and "For the last part" to guide the reader. To make your writing flow even more smoothly, think about using a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just "Then," you could use "Furthermore" or "In addition." This adds sophistication and helps your ideas connect more seamlessly from one sentence or paragraph to the next. You might also try using more sophisticated ways to refer back to ideas you've already introduced, making the whole text feel more unified.	You've used a good range of words and tried out some complex sentence structures, which is fantastic! That shows you're taking risks and expanding your English. ✨ However, there are quite a few small grammar and spelling mistakes that sometimes make your ideas a little harder to understand. Let's look at three important areas:<br><br>1.  **Subject-verb agreement:** This is when the verb in a sentence doesn't match whether the subject is singular or plural. For example, you wrote "friends has" when it should be "friends *have*." Another example is "Teenages is something..." which should be "Teenagers *are* something...". This is a very common mistake for learners of English, as the rules can be different from Thai, where verbs don't change form as much. A good tip is to identify the subject and then choose the correct verb form.<br>2.  **Spelling:** There were a few words that needed a quick check, such as "intrests" (should be 'interests'), "compe-cated" (should be 'complicated'), "Basiclly" (should be 'Basically'), "strick" (should be 'strict'), and "alwayls" (should be 'always'). Proofreading carefully for spelling can make a huge difference to how clear and polished your writing looks! Sometimes, Thai learners might focus more on getting the sounds right, and the exact letter combinations can be tricky.<br>3.  **Article usage:** You sometimes missed small words like 'a', 'an', or 'the', or used them incorrectly. For example, "a strick parents" should be "a *strict* parent" or "strict parents" (without 'a' if plural). Articles are notorious for being difficult in English because there isn't a direct equivalent in Thai, so it takes a lot of practice to get them right!<br><br>By focusing on these areas, you'll see your sentences become much clearer and more natural-sounding. Keep up the great work!	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
