ke, \\"Seoul was once called Hun-ou, but after World War 2, its name was changed to Seoul.\\" Also, the task asked you to describe what you love and why, with reasons and examples. You gave some reasons, but developing your examples more would help the reader.	You tried to share your ideas about Seoul, but sometimes the way you wrote made it hard for the reader to stay interested. For example, you wrote, \\"I will draw it so you can see how it look like.\\" In an article for a travel website, readers expect to learn from the words, not from a drawing you might add later. You could try describing the \\"honey river\\" more vividly with words instead. This will help you hold the reader's attention and communicate your ideas in a way that fits a written article.	You used paragraphs to separate your ideas, which is good. You started with an introduction and then moved to history, food, and K-pop. However, the connections between your paragraphs and sentences could be stronger. For example, when you move from history to food, you simply start a new paragraph with \\"food that you must try when you visit seoul.\\" It would be helpful to use linking words to make the transition smoother, like \\"Moving on to another reason I love Seoul, the food is amazing.\\" Also, the task asked for a conclusion, but your essay ends abruptly. Adding a concluding sentence or paragraph would help to summarize your points and give the reader a sense of completeness.	You used everyday words to talk about Seoul, but there are some mistakes in grammar and spelling that make your writing hard to read at times. For example, you wrote \\"seoul\\" instead of \\"Seoul.\\" Remember to always capitalize proper nouns like city names. Also, sentences like \\"seoul use to call Hun-ou\\" and \\"they changing Hun-ou to seoul\\" have grammatical errors. You could say, \\"Seoul used to be called Hun-ou, but they changed the name to Seoul after World War 2.\\" Improving your capitalization and grammar will make your writing much clearer and more professional.	3.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3104	27396	2026-01-06 15:12:39.74797+00	Politeness	Yes because I think that older people will have growth mindset than younger people.<br>\n\nFor example, the older people know that they have to greeting and respect to the people that older than their.	34	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2	2.0	Hi Nich! You put a lot of effort into sharing your opinions and your ideas are very clear!<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used great topic-specific words like "growth mindset" and "respect" to explain your thoughts.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You successfully used connectors like "because" and "For example" to organize your arguments.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: You attempted complex structures, including a comparative ("than younger people") and a relative clause ("that older than..."), which shows great progress!</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Verb Forms after "to" (Infinitives)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"For example, the older people know that they have to greeting and respect to the people that older than their."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"For example, older people know that they have to <b>greet</b> and respect people who are older than them."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In English, when we use "to" to show an action you must do, we always use the base form of the verb. In Thai, the verb "ทักทาย" doesn't change, but in English, we cannot use "greeting" after "to". Use <b>to greet</b> instead.</li><br><li><b>📝 Missing "BE" verb (is/am/are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"For example, the older people know that they have to greeting and respect to the people that older than their."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"For example, older people know that they have to greet and respect people <b>who are</b> older than them."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can describe someone as "แก่กว่า" without needing a linking verb. However, in English, descriptions and relative clauses usually need a "BE" verb (is/am/are) to be complete. Always remember to add <b>are</b> before "older".</li><br><li><b>📝 Object Pronouns vs. Possessive Adjectives</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"For example, the older people know that they have to greeting and respect to the people that older than their."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"For example, older people know that they have to greet and respect people who are older than <b>them</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You used "their", which in Thai is "ของพวกเขา" (possession). But here, you are comparing people to "them" (พวกเขา) as an object. When the person is the object of the sentence or comparison, we use <b>them</b>.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>2</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Punctuation & Pronoun Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Verb Tense (unnecessary "will"):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Missing Words (articles or comparatives):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Unnecessary Determiners ("the"):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Unnecessary Prepositions:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
