1789	33169	2025-09-13 15:29:29.049886+00	Term 1 Exam Question 2	In my perspective, having pets is a good idea. Not only because they can be your friends, having pets can also teach you responsibility and how to take care of them. Further more, having pet can help you do something else instead of playing video games all day and reduce stress. For instance, when you have a pet, which is mostly a dog or a cat, you have feed them everyday and also clean the mess they made like their poo or dead animals they found. But they're still one of the best friend you could have. <br><br> Pets are good friends, although we can't understand them and they can't understand us neither, they can feel the tension and our emotion by our voices. If our voices are high, smooth, and slow, they will think that we are no harm and friendly. Pets like cat or dog can be demaning for attention sometimes when they're lonely or hungry. They'll wake us up four in the morning because their food is run out, but for me, that's a good thing. They are like a alarm clock every four or five in the morning, they'll be in front of my door and crying untill some give them their food, which I like because every times they cried in front of my door that's mean I got about one to two hours more to sleep before I got to get up and go to school. <br><br> In the other hand, having pets can be troblesome. The paycheck for their medicine is really expensive and you got to take care of them. Pets like cats likes to sneak out and disappear for days, that's will make you worry like hell. I have experience it before. And if you have a dog, they'll pee everywhere and dig your garden into a whole bunch of holes, which of couse you have to clean the mess up. But when they know that they're wrong, they can express their guilt expression which is very cute, but some of them just look at the mess they made.	348	12.0	It was lovely to read your article about pets. You shared some really interesting thoughts and it's clear you have a strong personal connection to the topic. Your writing shows a good understanding of how to put your ideas across, and with a little more focus on balancing your arguments and refining your word choices, your articles will be even clearer for your readers. Keep up the great effort, and remember that every piece of writing is a chance to grow your skills. <br> 📋 Action Plan for Improvement <br> Here are three concrete steps you can take to make your writing even stronger: <br> ✅ When you're planning your articles, try to list an equal number of points for and against an idea, so your discussion feels balanced for the reader. <br> ✅ After writing, read through your work specifically looking for words that might be plural or singular. For example, check if you mean 'a pet' or 'pets' in general, and make sure your verbs match. <br> ✅ Take some time to proofread your work carefully for common spelling mistakes. Reading your work aloud can sometimes help you catch these little slips.	You did a good job of exploring the topic of pets and providing some thoughtful reasons why they are a good idea. You included some lovely personal touches, which made your writing feel genuine. 🥰 To develop your content further for an article, try to ensure that both sides of your argument – the advantages and disadvantages – are presented with equal detail and explanation. Sometimes, it felt like the positive aspects were discussed more fully than the challenges, so think about giving those 'troublesome' aspects a bit more elaboration too. For instance, when you talk about the cost of medicine, perhaps you could expand on how that impacts pet owners, or suggest how people manage these costs. Thai learners sometimes use personal anecdotes very effectively, but for an article, it's good to balance these with broader observations that apply to most readers.	You certainly managed to communicate your ideas clearly, and you kept me engaged as a reader. It's evident that you have a good grasp of expressing your views. 👍 To enhance your communicative achievement, think about the overall 'feel' of an article for a magazine. While personal stories are charming, an article often aims for a slightly more general and persuasive tone that would appeal to a wide audience. For example, instead of focusing heavily on just your own experience with an alarm clock pet, you could frame it as a common experience for many pet owners, making it more relatable for the target reader of a magazine. Practising writing for different purposes, like a formal report versus a friendly email, can help you adapt your writing style more easily.	Your article has a clear beginning where you introduce your perspective, and you've used some connecting words like 'furthermore' and 'in the other hand' to link your ideas. This helps the reader follow along. ✨ To make your organisation even better, you could work on varying the types of linking words and phrases you use. Instead of just 'but' or 'and,' explore words like 'however,' 'consequently,' or 'in addition.' Also, try to ensure that each paragraph has a very clear main idea, and that all the sentences within that paragraph directly support it. Sometimes, paragraphs began with a general point but then shifted quickly to a personal example. Making sure your paragraphs flow smoothly from one to the next, with clear topic sentences, will create a more unified text.	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit the topic well, and you generally control simple sentences effectively. I could understand your meaning, which is the most important thing. However, there are a few areas where we could strengthen your control over language. For example, paying close attention to whether a noun should be singular or plural will help your accuracy; you sometimes wrote 'having pet' when you meant 'having pets,' or 'Pets like cat or dog' instead of 'Pets like cats or dogs.' 🐾 Another common area for improvement is with small words like 'a' and 'an,' known as determiners; for instance, you used 'a alarm clock' when it should be 'an alarm clock.' Finally, a careful check for spelling before you finish writing will catch words like 'Further more' (which should be 'Furthermore'), 'demaning' (should be 'demanding'), and 'troblesome' (should be 'troublesome'). Thai learners sometimes struggle with these smaller grammatical points because the Thai language doesn't always distinguish between singular and plural or use articles in the same way as English, so it takes a bit of extra practice. Keep working on these, and your writing will become even more precise.	3.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
