 is you will not feel like that again.<br><br>I say that because I think if we make this scenario again, it will not feel like the first time we did it.<br><br>So in a, in conclusion, I have speak about my photo, why it's special, and why personal photo are important.<br><br>So, I wanted you to deeply understand my main message that every memory has a story and this photo tells mine.<br><br>Thank you.<br><br>[clapping]	401	13.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	PET	Your pronunciation made your presentation mostly clear and understandable, and your intonation generally sounded quite natural. That’s a great foundation! 😊 One area to really focus on for improvement is making sure you pronounce all the sounds at the end of words. For example, in words like "present" (/prɪˈzent/), "describe" (/dɪˈskraɪb/), and even "photo" (/ˈfəʊtəʊ/) – sometimes the final sounds were a little hard to hear because you were speaking quite quickly. This is a common challenge for Thai speakers because in Thai, many final consonants are either not pronounced or are softened, which is different from English where final consonants are often very important for clarity. Try to slow down just a little bit when you speak and really focus on articulating those final sounds clearly. It will make a big difference to how easily others understand every word you say! 🗣️	Hi Yayar! 👋 It was great to listen to your presentation about your favourite photograph! You clearly put a lot of thought into your topic, and you shared a lovely message about memories. Keep up the enthusiasm for speaking English! Your talk was easy to follow, and you shared some really interesting stories. To take your English speaking to the next level, focus on polishing those verb forms and making sure every word, especially those tricky endings, is clear. You've got some solid foundations, and with a little more attention to detail, you'll be communicating even more precisely! <br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   