3811	28858	2026-02-23 14:12:47.817065+00	Question 3 (Story)	Question 3\n\nLois smiled as she put the tickets in her pocket and walked out of her house. Lois, adopted kid who have a dream of going to bruno mars concert. Lois always use all her money buying useless toy she think that her mom is selfish and always giving her small amount of money. One day she complains to her mom about getting small amount of money and not enough to save up for her dream concert ticket. Her mom told her that even if I gave you more money it still won't be enough for you because you always spent them on useless thing and never learn how to save money. Her daughter Lois started thinking about it and realized she never save any money in her life. Her mom suggested her to stop buying useless thing and learn to save up for her dream. She start taking her mom suggestion and managed to save up enough money, and finally she learned how to spent money wisely.	178	16.0	Hi Gunner! 🌟 You’ve written a really heart-warming story about Lois learning a valuable life lesson to achieve her dream. You did a fantastic job of taking the required opening sentence and building a meaningful history behind it. Your biggest 'homework' for next time is to focus on subject-verb agreement (adding that 's' to verbs for 'he/she/it') and using punctuation for dialogue to make your writing even clearer. Keep up the great work! 🚀 All the best, Ed	It was great to see how you developed the backstory with 'Lois, adopted kid who have a dream of going to bruno mars concert.' This choice gives the reader a clear motivation for the character and makes the ending with the tickets feel very satisfying. To make your next story even stronger, try to describe the physical feeling of the tickets in her pocket to really bring that opening sentence to life.	When you wrote 'Her mom told her that even if I gave you more money it still won't be enough,' you effectively moved the plot forward through dialogue. However, switching between the third person ('her mom') and first person ('I') within the same sentence can be a bit like a sudden gear change in a car, which might startle your reader. For your next piece, try using quotation marks for direct speech to clearly separate the narrator's voice from the character's voice.	Your use of 'One day' to transition into the conflict helps the story move forward in a very logical, chronological way. This acts like a clear map for the reader to follow Lois’s growth from being a spender to becoming a saver. To reach a top score, consider using a paragraph break when the story shifts from the argument to Lois's realization, as this gives the reader a 'breathing space' between different ideas.	I see that you included some great vocabulary like 'complains,' 'realized,' and 'wisely,' which shows you have a strong range of words. I noticed 'Lois always use all her money,' where the missing 's' on the verb acts like a small hiccup in the rhythm of your writing. My tip for you is to do a quick 's-check' on your verbs when writing about one person (she uses, she thinks, she saves) to make your grammar as smooth as your storytelling.	5.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
