1772	28858	2025-09-13 10:54:05.680654+00	Term 1 Exam Question 1	Nowsaday people don't really believe on their self or be them self because they are scared of getting punishment by their parents, or scared they will get bullied by their friends. And the big 3 things they are forced to do or need to wear is their clothes and fashion secoundly Studying lastly free time.<br><br><br>One thing people are forced to do is clothes and fashion. people thinks that if you wear old thing not following what their friends wear. you will can't join ur friend groups and no one will care about you and that makes people think that they have to follow what their friends are wearing to not get bullied.<br><br><br>The next thing people believe they need to do is studying. We all get forced to study because of the law and our parents. They believe that if we study hard and we will get paid alot but thats wrong for some people. because they want to do something else. For example a boy wants to be a singer but they are forced to study because his parents say that it will never be true that you will be a singer.<br><br><br>Lastly their free time. when we have freetime some people might get forced to read books do chores grocery and more by our parent even if we want to spend time with other things.<br><br><br>in conclusion teenagers need to follow their parents and do something as other friends do to not get bullied and be in their groups even they want to be themself believe in their self its hard for them.	270	10.0	Hello Gunner, it's really good to see your effort in tackling this essay question. You've clearly put some thought into the topic, and that's a great start. To really make your writing stand out, the main thing to focus on is directly answering the question being asked and building a more formal and academic style. Keep practicing, and you'll certainly see your writing skills flourish! 🚀<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   ✅ Read the essay question carefully and underline the key parts before you start writing to make sure you address everything asked.<br>*   ✅ Practice writing topic sentences that clearly state the main idea of each paragraph and link back to your overall argument.<br>*   ✅ Focus on making sure your sentences are complete and grammatically correct, especially with subject-verb agreement and using the right words in the right places.	Gunner, you've done a good job of covering the points given in the question – clothes and fashion, and studying – and you even added your own idea about free time, which is excellent. 👍 However, to get an even better score, it’s really important to directly answer the main question. The prompt asked, "Who has more effect on the choices teenagers make - friends or parents?", but your essay talked more about what teenagers are "forced to do" in general. For example, when discussing clothes, you focused on the pressure to follow friends, but didn't directly compare that to parental influence on fashion choices. Next time, try to explicitly weigh up both sides: how much do friends influence, and how much do parents influence? Then, make sure your conclusion clearly states which group you think has a stronger effect, based on what you've written in your body paragraphs. Thai learners sometimes focus broadly on a topic rather than directly addressing the specific nuances of the question.	Your essay communicates your ideas, but for a B2 First exam, it needs to adopt a more academic and formal tone. It felt a little like you were chatting with a friend rather than writing for an exam assessor. For instance, phrases like "Nowsaday people don't really believe on their self" could be rephrased to sound more formal, like "In contemporary society, individuals often lack self-belief." To improve, try to use more formal vocabulary and avoid overly simple or conversational language. Think about your opening and closing sentences for each paragraph and for the whole essay: do they sound serious and clear? 🤔 Developing a stronger, more consistent formal register throughout your writing will help you hold the reader's attention and communicate more sophisticated ideas.	You have a basic structure with an introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion, which is a solid foundation. You used some simple linking words like "One thing" and "The next thing," which helps the reader follow your ideas. To improve your organisation and make your writing flow more smoothly, try using a wider variety of linking words and phrases, such as "Furthermore," "In addition," "However," or "Consequently." You could also work on making your topic sentences clearer. For instance, instead of starting a paragraph with "One thing people are forced to do is clothes and fashion," you could try something like, "Regarding personal style, the influence of peers often dictates teenagers' clothing choices." This helps to explicitly state the paragraph's focus and connect it back to the overall essay question. Thai learners sometimes use more repetitive linking or a less varied range of cohesive devices.	Gunner, it’s clear you have many ideas to express, but there are frequent grammatical errors that sometimes make your meaning a little unclear. Let's look at three key areas for improvement. First, **subject-verb agreement** is important; for example, you wrote "people thinks" when it should be "people think." Remember that "people" is a plural noun, so it needs a plural verb. ✍️ Second, watch out for your use of **articles and prepositions**. You wrote "believe on their self" when "believe in themselves" would be more accurate. Similarly, "scared of getting punishment by their parents" could be improved to "scared of being punished by their parents." Finally, your **sentence structure** could be more precise and formal. For example, "you will can't join ur friend groups" could be rewritten as "you may not be able to join your friend groups." Focusing on these areas will make your writing much clearer and more accurate. Thai learners sometimes struggle with these specific grammatical points due to differences in sentence structure and grammatical rules between English and Thai.	3.0	2.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
