3812	28863	2026-02-23 14:12:47.817065+00	Question 3 (Story)	Lois smiled as she put the tickets in her pocket and walked out of her house. One day Lois, a 14 years old girl, got invited by her friends to watch a movie. It was Saturday evening and Lois and her friends left the house after they got allowed by their parents. After few minutes of travelling by bus, they arrived at the cinema. Lois and her friends waited there for the movie to start in an hour. Suddenly Lois found that she lost the tickets that she have kept in her pocket. After minutes of searching the tickets they found them at the entrance of the cinema. Lois was lucky enough to watch the movie that day. Finally Lois and her friends finished watching the movie succesfully. This taught Lois to be more carefull with essential things in her pocket.	151	14.0	Hi Namo T! You've written a very logical story that perfectly captures the drama of losing something important. 🌟 It was great to see a clear ending where Lois learned a lesson. For your next piece of writing, my most important 'homework' for you is to practice using paragraphs to organize your story and to keep your timeline moving forward smoothly. Keep writing! 🚀 All the best, Ed	Concluding your story with the idea that the experience 'taught Lois to be more carefull' gave the narrative a very clear and satisfying purpose. This makes the ending feel complete because it provides a resolution that goes beyond just finding the tickets, showing growth in your character. To make your next story even more engaging, try to describe one specific detail about the movie they watched! 🌟	It was great to see how you established the characters by writing 'Lois, a 14 years old girl, got invited by her friends,' which sets the scene effectively. However, the story starts with Lois already outside, but then jumps back to 'One day...' which is a bit confusing for the reader. For your next story, try to keep the timeline moving forward from the first sentence to keep your reader on track! 🚀	Your use of time-related signposts like 'Suddenly' and 'After few minutes' helps the reader navigate the sequence of events. However, because the entire story is in one big paragraph, it can be a bit tiring for the reader to follow everything. Next time, try to use separate paragraphs for each main part of your story (like the journey, the problem, and the ending) to give your reader some 'breathing room'! 📝	I see that you included more complex structures like 'lucky enough to watch,' which shows a great effort to use a B1-level repertoire. While your meaning is always 100% clear, small mistakes like 'she have kept' instead of 'she had kept' can take the reader's attention away from your lovely story. A helpful tip for next time is to double-check your past tense forms! ✨	5.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
