3086	29561	2026-01-04 05:36:03.473315+00	Midterm Exam Part 1	sure you could visit me also I can help you about your stories also I recommend a hotel for you to stay the price just cost only 1$(Dollar) and also I suggest you a place like museum. The time that museum open is 10:00 am because It can help you find about Thai communication History also for tips to talk with thai people just use google translate and write what you gonna talk with them or ask any thing that you wana ask also dont be bad with them too because Thai people are friendly also to be sure be quiet to them too.	104	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A1+	2	2.28	Hi Mathew! 😊 You did a great job explaining your ideas and giving helpful advice to your friend!<br><br>You tried really hard with this and I noticed some really positive things about your work:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used some great topic-specific words like "recommend", "suggest", and "communication history" to describe the museum.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You used "because" effectively to explain your reasons, such as why the museum is helpful and why people should be nice to others.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: You attempted complex structures like relative clauses ("The time that museum open...") and modal verbs like "could" and "can" to make suggestions.</li></ul><br><br>However, there are some grammar patterns we need to work on together. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can fix them:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Ending sentences with Full Stops</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"sure you could visit me also I can help you about your stories also I recommend a hotel for you to stay the price just cost only 1$(Dollar) and also I suggest you a place like museum."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"<b>S</b>ure, you <b>can</b> visit me. I can <b>also</b> help you <b>with</b> your stories. I recommend a hotel for you to stay <b>at; it only costs one dollar.</b>"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai script, we don't use full stops (.) to show where a sentence ends. However, in English, we must use them to separate different ideas. Without them, your writing becomes a "run-on sentence," which is hard to read. Try to give each new idea its own sentence.</li><li><b>📝 Subject-Verb Agreement</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The time that museum open is 10:00 am because It can help you find about Thai communication History also for tips to talk with thai people just use google translate and write what you gonna talk with them or ask any thing that you wana ask also dont be bad with them too because Thai people are friendly also to be sure be quiet to them too."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The museum <b>opens</b> at 10:00 am. It <b>is a great place to learn</b> about Thai communication history."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, verbs like "เปิด" do not change. But in English, if the subject is singular (like "the museum"), we must add an 's' to the verb: "it open<b>s</b>". Also, remember to use capital letters for names of countries or languages like "<b>T</b>hai".</li><li><b>📝 Natural Phrasing for Etiquette</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"also dont be bad with them too because Thai people are friendly also to be sure be quiet to them too."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Also, <b>don't be rude to</b> them because Thai people are friendly. To be <b>respectful</b>, you should <b>also be quiet around</b> them."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You used "bad with" which sounds like a translation of "นิสัยไม่ดีกับ". In English, when talking about behavior towards people, we say "rude <b>to</b>" or "polite <b>to</b>". Similarly, "quiet <b>to</b> them" sounds like you are not speaking; "quiet <b>around</b> them" is the natural way to say you should be peaceful in their presence.</li></ol><br><br>Keep practising these patterns and you'll improve quickly! 🌟<br><br><hr><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>2</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Punctuation Errors:</b> 5 instances</li><li><b>Capitalisation Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Preposition Errors:</b> 2 instances</li></ol>	A2+	2026
