 moment to double-check your spelling for common words, perhaps by keeping a list of words you often find tricky.<br> *   ✅ After writing a sentence, read it aloud to yourself. If you find yourself taking a very long breath or getting lost, it's a good sign that you might need to break it into two or three shorter sentences and add some punctuation like full stops or commas.<br> *   ✅ Think about expanding on your ideas with more specific details and examples, ensuring that every point you make is fully clear to your reader.	You have made a good start by addressing both the good and bad aspects of having pets, which is exactly what the task asked for! 👍 You discuss how pets can be challenging to care for, and also mention the joy they bring to children and how they can offer emotional support. To improve towards a score of 5, try to expand on each point with more specific details and examples. For instance, when you talk about challenges, you could mention the time commitment for walks or training, or the financial cost of food and vet visits. Also, make sure all your ideas are expressed with complete clarity, as some phrases like "without a mad" were a little difficult to understand. Many Thai learners sometimes make points briefly, assuming the reader will fill in the gaps, so focusing on explaining your thoughts thoroughly will be a big help.	You've successfully attempted to write an article with a clear introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion, which is a great step in using the correct format for this task. 📝 Your ideas about pets being good companions and sometimes causing allergies are straightforward and easy to grasp. However, to really hold the reader's attention and communicate more effectively, focus on making your sentences smoother and more accurate. Sometimes, grammatical errors made it a bit challenging to follow your train of thought, which can distract the reader. Reading other articles for teenagers can give you some great examples of how to keep your audience engaged and express both simple and more detailed ideas clearly. Thai learners sometimes focus more on conveying the main message than on polishing the language and style, which can impact how well the article 'speaks' to its reader.	Your article is generally well-organised, and you use paragraphs to separate your ideas, which makes it easier to read. You also used clear linking words such as "First," "second," and "In the conclusion," which helps guide the reader through your arguments. This is a solid foundation! To enhance your organisation further, try to vary your linking words more to create an even smoother flow between sentences and paragraphs. For example, instead of always starting with "second," you could try "Furthermore," or "On the other hand" if you're introducing a contrasting idea. Experiment with different ways to connect your thoughts. Thai learners often use basic linking words effectively, but expanding this range can make your writing feel more sophisticated and connected.	You used some basic vocabulary related to pets that was appropriate, like "family," "house," and "play." This is a good starting point for discussing the topic. However, there were quite a few errors in your spelling and grammar that sometimes made it difficult to fully understand your meaning. Let's look at three important areas for improvement:<br><br>1.  **Spelling:** Words like "familys" should be "families," "challengs" should be "challenges," and "quitely" should be "quietly." A simple trick is to always use a dictionary or an online spell-check tool when you are unsure of how to spell a word. Keep a list of words you often misspell and review them regularly. 📚<br>2.  **Subject-Verb Agreement:** You wrote "Many animals wants to stay..." but because "animals" is plural, the verb should be "want." So, it should be "Many animals want to stay..." This is a common error, and focusing on whether your subject is singular or plural will help you choose the correct verb form.<br>3.  **Pronoun Usage and Clarity:** Phrases like "take care its" and "allergic them" were a bit unclear. For "take care its," you likely meant "take care of them" or "take care of their pets." Similarly, "allergic them" should be "allergic to them." Ensuring your pronouns clearly refer to the correct nouns and using the right prepositions will make your sentences much clearer.<br><br>Many Thai learners find these areas challenging because English spelling is not always phonetic, and grammatical rules like subject-verb agreement and precise pronoun use are very different from Thai. Keep practising, and these improvements will make a big difference to your writing! 💪	3.0	2.0	3.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
