adly about the topic. 👍 To achieve a higher score in content, remember that the essay question asks you to consider *who has more effect* – friends or parents. Your response describes influences, but it doesn't clearly state your opinion on which group has the stronger impact, nor does it compare their effects directly. For example, after discussing friends' influence on fashion, you could have explicitly contrasted it with parental influence, or lack thereof. Make sure to directly address every part of the prompt in your next essay.	You've shown a good basic ability to communicate straightforward ideas, Mirin, by describing different influences. 😊 However, for an essay at this level, the goal is to develop a clear argument that keeps the reader engaged. Because the central question about *who has more effect* wasn't explicitly answered, the essay doesn't fully achieve its communicative purpose of presenting a well-supported opinion. Think about starting with a clear statement of your main point or opinion in an introductory sentence. This will help you guide your reader and make your overall message much more impactful.	Mirin, you've shown you have distinct ideas, signalled by your labels like "Clothes and fashion," but presenting your entire response as one long, continuous block of text makes it very difficult for the reader to understand your organisation. There are no paragraph breaks at all, which means the text lacks basic coherence and makes it a challenge to follow your different points. 😔 Imagine reading a book without any spaces between the chapters. To improve this significantly, you must use paragraph breaks to separate each main idea. For instance, when you move from talking about "Clothes and fashion" to "Studying," start a brand-new paragraph. This is a fundamental step in making your writing clear and easy to read. Thai learners sometimes present ideas continuously, but in English academic writing, clear paragraphing is essential.	You've used some everyday words that relate to the topics, Mirin, and it's clear you have ideas you want to share. However, the frequent spelling and grammatical errors make it quite difficult for the reader to fully grasp what you are trying to say. 😟 For instance, "My friends has a influence" should be "My friends **have an** influence," and "she always dresses beatifully" needs to be "she always dresses **beautifully**." Another example is "it make me cares more about what I wear today," which should correctly be "it **makes** me **care** more about what I wear today." You also had several spelling mistakes like "scuvess" (success), "invivite" (invite), "exercies" (exercises), "frist" (first), "stord" (strong), and "healty" (healthy). These types of errors are common for Thai learners who might sometimes translate ideas directly or not yet be familiar with all English spelling and grammar rules. To improve, focus on checking your sentences for correct verb forms and subject-verb agreement, and always proofread for spelling before you finish writing.	3.0	3.0	0.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
