ing' form, like "screaming" instead of "scream."<br>✔️ Practise using phrases that show cause and effect or contrast. This will help your article flow even better, just like you started to do with "However" and "therefore."	You did a fantastic job of directly answering the question, In-Sun. You presented a clear opinion right from the start and then carefully explained both the benefits and the downsides of having pets. You covered all the important points, discussing how pets can help with mental well-being and companionship, as well as the responsibilities involved like cleaning up. This means your reader is completely informed about your viewpoint, which is exactly what a high-scoring article does. Your article showed that you understood the task perfectly.	You successfully managed to hold the reader's attention throughout your article, In-Sun. The tone was appropriate for an article sharing opinions, and you communicated your ideas in a way that was easy to understand. For instance, when you talked about pets healing mental health or being like children, you used language that made your points clear. To move towards a score of 5, you could try to develop some of your ideas a little more deeply, perhaps by giving a specific small example or elaborating on a feeling, which shows you can handle more complex ideas with confidence.	Your article was well-structured, In-Sun, which made it easy to follow your ideas. You started with a clear introduction, moved into separate paragraphs for the advantages, then discussed the disadvantages, and wrapped it up with a good conclusion. You used some helpful linking words like "Most importantly," "Secondly," "However," and "In conclusion," which guide the reader smoothly from one idea to the next. To make your organisation even stronger, try to add a few more phrases that connect the ideas within your paragraphs, ensuring every sentence feels naturally joined to the one before it. For example, instead of just starting a new sentence, you could use a phrase like "In addition to this..." or "Despite these benefits..." when you introduce a new point.	You used a good range of everyday words to talk about pets, In-Sun, and your meaning was generally clear despite some errors. However, there were a few places where the grammar could be more precise. For example, you wrote "reasnes" instead of "reasons" for plurals. Another area to watch is prepositional phrases, like "take care their health" which should be "take care *of* their health." Also, when you mentioned how pets "scream," it might be more natural to say "screaming" if you are describing a general behaviour. Finally, phrases like "take these responsibility" should be "take *this* responsibility" or "take *these* responsibilities" to match the number correctly. Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from their first language, or might overlook small grammatical endings that are very important in English for showing plurals or specific relationships between words. Focusing on these small but important details will really help your writing become more accurate and polished.	5.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2+	2026
