e is making sure you fully follow all instructions. For example, if the prompt asks you to disagree with an idea, it is important to express that disagreement clearly (you were supposed to disagree with Corey, not agree, about watching the film). Perhaps you could underline keywords in the task like "agree" or "disagree" before you start writing to help you remember. Thai learners sometimes focus more on conveying general meaning than on precise instruction following, so paying extra attention to those specific details will truly help your score grow.	You've successfully used the basic structure of an email, Richter, which is wonderful! You started with a greeting ("Hi! Corey") and ended with a closing ("see you Corey, R"), just as you should for an email to a friend. You also remembered to separate your ideas into different paragraphs, which makes the email easier to read and follow 😊. This shows you understand how to use the conventions of email writing to get your message across. To improve further, try to make sure your sentences are always very clear. Sometimes, a few grammatical errors made it a little difficult to understand your ideas fully. Practising clear and simple sentences will make your communication even more effective.	Your email has a good basic structure, Richter, with your thoughts generally grouped into separate paragraphs, which helps the reader understand your different points. You also used some straightforward connecting words like 'and' and 'so' to link ideas. This is a good foundation for organised writing. To make your writing flow even more smoothly, think about how each sentence connects to the one before it. Sometimes, parts of your sentences were a little unclear, which made it harder to follow your ideas. For instance, instead of combining too many ideas, try to use shorter, clearer sentences. Thai learners sometimes use longer sentences with many clauses, which can make it challenging to maintain perfect clarity and coherence in English.	Richter, you've used some everyday words that fit the topic of the email, which is a fine start. However, there are quite a few spots where the grammar and spelling could use some careful attention 🤔. For example, remember to always include the correct form of the verb "to be", such as "It **is** a great time" instead of "It a great time." Also, words like "early" (you wrote "erly"), "dinosaurs" (you wrote "Dinosaurre"), and "camera" (you wrote "camemer") need correct spelling. Many Thai learners sometimes struggle with articles (like 'a' or 'the') and prepositions (like 'for' or 'to'), and these small words are very important in English for making your meaning clear. For instance, you wrote "thanks you for the tickets to me"; it should be "thank you for the tickets." Focusing on these basic grammar patterns and checking your spelling will make a substantial difference to how clearly you communicate your ideas.	2.0	3.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2	2026
