1984	28771	2025-09-21 11:23:42.482413+00	Term 1 Exam Question 1	Teenagers are often influenced by both their parents and friends, but in different parts of life, the question that you give is "who has more effect on the choices teenagers make - friends or parents?<br><br><br>First, about clothes and fasion. Many teenagers want to be similar to their group, so they often wear same style as their friends. Parents may give advice but most of teenagers prefer to follow what their friends think it looks good.<br><br><br>Second, about studying. In my opinion parents have more power, they tell their children to do homework or study for the exams and tell them to think about the future. Friends can support each other but parents are the one who set rules and want to see a good results at school.<br><br><br>Finally, about freetime activities, friends are influence teenagers more.<br><br><br>They want to join hobbies, sports or games that their friends are also enjoys because they can spend time together.<br><br><br>In conclusion, parents has more effect about studying but in clothes and fasion teenagers has more effect for this choices.	172	13.0	Yiwa, you've made a really thoughtful attempt at your essay, clearly engaging with the question and presenting your ideas well. A key area to focus on for your next piece of writing is how you connect your ideas and form complete sentences, especially when starting new paragraphs. With a little attention to these structural details, your writing will become even clearer and more impactful. ✨<br><br>📝 **Action Plan:**<br> *   ✅ When starting a new paragraph, make sure your first sentence is a complete thought that introduces the paragraph's main idea. For example, instead of "First, about clothes and fashion," try "Firstly, when it comes to clothes and fashion, teenagers are often guided by their friends."<br> *   ✅ Read your essay aloud to yourself. This can help you hear where sentences might be incomplete or where a different linking word could make your ideas flow more smoothly.<br> *   ✅ Keep a small notebook of common words you misspell, like 'fashion,' and practice writing them correctly a few times each day.	You did a good job of covering all the points in the task, including your own idea about freetime activities. You clearly addressed the central question about who influences teenagers more, and your essay provides the reader with a good understanding of your perspective. You also remembered to include your own idea, which is a key part of the task. To get an even higher score, try to provide a more balanced discussion for each point. For instance, for "clothes and fashion," you could briefly mention parental influence before expanding on friends' influence. This shows a deeper engagement with the complexity of the question. You could say something like, "While parents might offer practical advice on clothes, it is often friends who dictate current trends and styles." This will help to present a more nuanced argument in your writing.	You successfully used the general structure of an essay, which is important for this type of writing. You got your main ideas across clearly, and the reader could easily follow your general argument from beginning to end. You kept the reader's attention by presenting clear points in different paragraphs. For this type of essay, the expectation is a formal style, like something you might write for a teacher or for a school publication. Sometimes your language felt a bit informal, for example, when you said, "parents have more power." To make it more formal, you could say something like, "parents exert greater authority" or "parents hold significant influence." Try to use more academic vocabulary and sentence structures to maintain a consistent formal tone throughout your essay. This helps to achieve the purpose of the task effectively. ✍️	You did well in organising your essay with clear paragraphs for each main idea and using good linking words like "First," "Second," "Finally," and "In conclusion." This makes your writing easy to follow. Your ideas flow logically from one point to the next, which is a great foundation. 📚 While your use of linking words is good, some of your paragraph beginnings are quite short and act as sentence fragments. For example, "First, about clothes and fasion." To make your essay even more coherent and polished, try to expand these into full, introductory sentences that clearly state the main point of the paragraph. For instance, you could write, "Firstly, let us consider the area of clothes and fashion" or "Turning to the realm of clothes and fashion, teenage choices are often shaped by..." This will make your transitions smoother and your essay feel more connected.	You used a good range of everyday words that were suitable for your essay topic, and I could always understand what you meant. You also used some simple grammatical structures accurately, which is a solid start. Your vocabulary, like "influenced," "similar to their group," and "support each other," shows you have a good grasp of words related to the topic. 👍 One important area to focus on is sentence structure, especially at the start of your paragraphs. Phrases like "First, about clothes and fasion" are sentence fragments and need to be expanded into full sentences. For example, you could write, "Firstly, regarding clothes and fashion, teenagers often seek to emulate their peer group." Also, pay close attention to subject-verb agreement. You wrote "parents has more effect" when it should be "parents *have* more effect." Similarly, "friends are influence teenagers more" should be "friends *influence* teenagers more" or "friends *are influencing* teenagers more." Finally, double-check your spelling for common words like "fasion" (which should be "fashion"). Focusing on constructing complete sentences with correct subject-verb agreement will greatly improve the clarity and accuracy of your writing. Sometimes, in Thai, sentence structure can be less rigid about explicit subjects and verbs in every clause, which might lead to these kinds of English errors.	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
