br><br>Action Plan for Improvement<br>• ✅ Practice writing complete sentences and connecting them smoothly with appropriate linking words.<br>• ✅ Pay close attention to spelling and common grammatical patterns, especially verb forms and plural nouns.<br>• ✅ When writing a formal essay, try to avoid very casual language and focus on presenting your ideas in a clear, structured way.	Rico, it is great that you have included all the points from the task, including your own idea about gaming. You have made an effort to give examples, like the "Water elephant pajama" in fashion, which helps to illustrate your points. 👍 To improve, try to make sure each paragraph clearly links back to the main question about who has more effect, friends or parents. For instance, in your section on studying, while you mention parents influence, think about how you can explicitly argue whether this influence is *more* or *less* than friends. Remember, an essay usually requires you to argue a point directly, not just describe a situation.	You have communicated your ideas in a straightforward way, Rico, which is a good start. 😊 However, for a formal essay like this, it is important to use language that is suitable for an academic audience. Starting with "Hello teacher, its me Rico" is very friendly, but it is not what we expect in an essay response. Try to imagine you are writing for someone you do not know personally, and aim for a more objective and formal tone. For example, instead of "its me Rico," you could begin with a general statement about the topic. Focusing on a consistent formal register throughout will help you achieve a higher score in this area.	Your essay has a basic structure with an introduction and separate paragraphs for each point, which is helpful for the reader to follow your ideas. You have used simple linking words like "Firt," "Move on to studies," and "Lastly." That is a good start. To make your essay even better, try to use a wider range of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas more smoothly. 🤔 For example, instead of just "Firt," you could say "Firstly, regarding fashion and clothes..." Also, make sure each paragraph has a clear topic sentence that tells the reader what that paragraph will be about, making the flow between your ideas much clearer. Thai learners sometimes use very direct transitions, but in English, we often use more varied phrases to guide the reader.	Rico, you have used everyday words to express your thoughts, which is important for getting your message across. However, there are quite a few noticeable errors in spelling, grammar, and sentence structure that sometimes make it a little difficult to understand your exact meaning. For instance, words like "unsterstan," "chouse," "becaus," "on tren," "studiet," and "relation chip" need careful attention. ✍️ Also, there are some issues with subject-verb agreement and plural forms, such as "teenagers usually wear a on tren clothes" instead of "teenagers usually wear trendy clothes." To improve, focus on basic sentence construction and common spelling patterns. Thai learners sometimes struggle with plural 's' and specific verb endings because these concepts are different in their native language. Practising these regularly will truly help your writing become much more accurate and clear.	3.0	2.0	2.0	1.0	writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3174	29508	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Greetings,\nI would like to recommend you the places you should visit. Ayutthaya, a major province in Thailand that famous of amazing monuments. The food there is wonderful, you can have Thai noodles for only 40-50 baht. The next place is Wat Phra Kaew, Thai temple that is known for stunning structure. Wat Phra Kaew is surround by Jayde (mini version of temple), makes Wat Phra Kaew a tourist attraction.<br>\nMy only tip for the trip is to be kind. Thai people mostly are friendly, if you act polite and respect.<br>\nBest wish,\nSatang	94	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	3	2.53	Hi Satang! Good to see you putting in the effort to describe these famous spots in Thailand. You've clearly got a good handle on the topic.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used some solid, topic-specific words like "monuments," "stunning structure," and "tourist attraction" to make your descriptions more interesting.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: I noticed you tried some more difficult structures, like using an appositive to explain what Ayutthaya is and a conditional sentence starting with "if."</li>  <li>💪 <b>Punctuation and Capitalisation</b>: Your use of capital letters for names like "Ayutthaya" and "Wat Phra Kaew" was spot on, and you used full stops correctly throughout.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Missing "BE" Verbs (is/am/are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Ayutthaya, a major province in Thailand that famous of amazing monuments."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Ayutthaya <b>is</b> a major province in Thailand that <b>is</b> famous <b>for its</b> amazing monuments."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can sometimes skip the linking verb, especially with adjectives. For example, you might say "มันเก่า" (it old). But in English, every sentence needs a main verb, and adjectives usually need "is," "am," or "are" to link them to the subject.</li><li><b>📝 Missing Articles and Determiners</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The next place is Wat Phra Kaew, Thai temple that is known for stunning structure."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The next place is Wat Phra Kaew, <b>a</b> Thai temple known for <b>its</b> stunning structure."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Thai doesn't use words like "a," "an," or "the" before nouns. In English, we need "a" because you are talking about one temple, and "its" to show that the structure belongs to that specific temple.</li><li><b>📝 Word Form (Nouns vs Adjectives)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Thai people mostly are friendly, if you act polite and respect."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Thai people <b>are mostly</b> friendly if you act polite and <b>respectful</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You used the word "respect," which is a noun (ความเคารพ) or a verb (เคารพ). However, because you are describing a person, you need the adjective "respectful" (ที่มีความเคารพ) to match "polite." I also adjusted the word order to make it sound more natural.</li><li><b>📝 Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Wat Phra Kaew is surround by Jayde (mini version of temple), makes Wat Phra Kaew a tourist attraction."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Wat Phra Kaew is <b>surrounded</b> by <b>Chedis</b> (mini <b>versions</b> of <b>temples</b>), <b>which</b> makes Wat Phra Kaew a tourist attraction."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, nouns don't change if there is more than one. You might say "เจดีย์หลายองค์" without changing the word "เจดีย์." In English, when you speak about things in general or in groups, you usually need to add an "s." I also fixed "surrounded" because it is a passive action.</li></ol><br><br>Your advice about being kind and respectful is a nice touch to wrap up your travel tips. Keep up the writing!<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>8</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Missing Determiners (a/the/its):</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation and extra words:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Noun errors (Plurals/Choice):</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Missing Verbs:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Preposition Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Word Order Errors:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	B1	2026
