 Corey asked about in the email. You made it clear that you were happy about the tickets 🥳, stated exactly how you wanted to get to the festival, and even gave a good reason why you'd prefer to build a robot over watching the film. You also remembered to suggest bringing a fan because of the hot weather. This meant Corey was very well-informed by your email. To get that top score of 5, try to make your reasons even more directly about your preference rather than solely about why you dislike the other option. For instance, instead of just saying "the film was made in 2014 and it really bad too," you could add a phrase like "I'd rather build a robot because I enjoy hands-on activities."	Your email had a lovely, friendly tone, just right for writing to a friend like Corey. You started with a clear "Hi" and ended with "Bye, Satang," which are perfect for an informal email. Using separate paragraphs also made your email easy to read and helped keep Corey's attention, so he could follow all your ideas without getting lost. To reach a score of 5, ensure that all your sentences are grammatically accurate. Although the message was clear, some minor language errors occasionally made the reader pause, which can slightly reduce the smooth flow of communication.	You did a fine job of organising your thoughts in a logical way, Satang. Your email flowed well from one point to the next, responding to Corey's questions in a clear order. You used phrases like "About the Way there" and "For the two main activities" to help guide the reader through your response, which is a clever way to keep your email coherent. Perhaps you could think about adding a few more linking words within sentences, for example, connecting two ideas more smoothly. Thai learners sometimes present ideas quite directly, but in English, using a wider range of linking words can create an even smoother reading experience.	You used a good range of everyday words that were suitable for your email, Satang. Most of your sentences were easy to understand, and you controlled your grammar well for many of your ideas. However, there were a few spots where the grammar could be tightened up. For example, instead of "thank a lot," it would be "thanks a lot" or "thank you a lot." Also, when you said "build a robot sound good," it should be "building a robot sounds good" because "building a robot" acts as a single idea, and the verb "sound" needs an 's' for agreement. Another example is "it really bad too," which should be "it's really bad too." These sorts of errors, especially with verb forms and missing words like 'is' or 'are,' are common for Thai learners as the grammatical structures in Thai can be quite different. Focusing on these small but important details will really help your sentences become more accurate and polished.	4.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
