ye, Eri", which are perfect for an informal email. You also used paragraphs, which makes your email easy to read. However, when writing for an exam, it's generally best to avoid exclamation marks, even in informal writing, as they can sometimes be seen as a little too casual or emotional. Additionally, while "Hi" is a good start, adding the recipient's name, like "Hi Corey," makes the greeting a little more complete and personal, which is always a nice touch in an email. Thinking about these small details can help you achieve an even higher score next time. 📧	Your email is well-organised and easy to follow, Eri, with your ideas clearly separated into paragraphs. This makes it very simple for the person reading your email to understand your message. You used some good connecting phrases like "it's because" and "In my opinion," which help link your thoughts together. To make your writing even more polished, try to use a wider range of words and phrases to connect your sentences and paragraphs. For instance, instead of always starting a new idea directly, you could use phrases like "Furthermore," "On the other hand," or "Moving on to..." This adds more variety and makes your writing flow even more naturally. Sometimes in Thai, direct translations can lead to simpler sentence structures, so practicing these more varied linking words in English can be very helpful. ✍️	You used a good range of everyday words that are appropriate for the topic, Eri, and your grammar was mostly very good. Words like "Science festival," "pick me up," "build a robot," and "pocket money" were all used correctly. There were a few small errors, such as "cousic" instead of "cousin," "it fix it and clean it" instead of "to fix and clean it," and "activite" instead of "activity." These are minor points, and despite them, your message was perfectly clear. No one would struggle to understand what you meant, which is the most important thing. Focusing on careful proofreading for these small spelling and grammar points will truly elevate your writing. Thai speakers sometimes omit articles or have slight difficulties with verb agreement in English; being aware of these common areas can help you fine-tune your accuracy. ✨	5.0	4.0	4.0	4.0	\N	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026
3175	29576	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	This is reason why you should visit Thailand. First, because Thai people are very friendly They always smile to a foreign like you. If you have a problem you can ask them to help. Second, Thailand has many interesting places.	40	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2	2.53	Hi Dee! Good on you for putting this together. You've clearly thought about how to welcome visitors to Thailand and organized your ideas well.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You used "First", "Second", and "If" to lead the reader through your points. This makes your writing easy to follow.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> You attempted a conditional sentence ("If you have a problem..."). This is a good way to add variety to your writing.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You chose appropriate words for the topic, like "friendly", "interesting", and "foreigners".</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Preposition Choices (Collocations)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"First, because Thai people are very friendly They always smile to a foreign like you."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"First, Thai people are very friendly <b>and</b> they always smile <b>at foreigners</b> like you."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you might say "ยิ้มให้" which sounds like "smile to". However, in English, we usually "smile at" someone. I also added "and" because English sentences need a connector to join two complete thoughts together.</li><li><b>📝 Nouns vs. Adjectives</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"First, because Thai people are very friendly They always smile to a foreign like you."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"First, Thai people are very friendly and they always smile at <b>foreigners</b> like you."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, "คนต่างชาติ" or "ชาวต่างชาติ" can be used in many ways without changing the word. In English, "foreign" is an adjective (a description), but "foreigner" is the noun (the person). Since you are talking about people, you need the noun "foreigner".</li><li><b>📝 Asking for things</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"If you have a problem you can ask them to help."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"If you have a problem, you can ask them <b>for</b> help."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> When we want to receive something in English, we use "ask for". While you can say "ask them to help" (using "help" as a verb), it sounds much more natural to "ask for help" (using "help" as a noun) when someone is in trouble. Also, remember to put a comma after your "If" phrase!</li><li><b>📝 Missing "The"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"This is reason why you should visit Thailand."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"This is <b>the</b> reason why you should visit Thailand."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Thai doesn't use articles like "a", "an", or "the". In English, when you are talking about one specific reason that you are about to explain, you need to use "the".</li></ol><br><br>You've got a good handle on describing things, Dee. Keep working on those small connecting words and you'll be set.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>4</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Preposition and Particle Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Determiner/Article Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Word Form (Morphology) Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Punctuation and Capitalisation:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Missing Conjunctions:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
