1968	34288	2025-09-20 19:54:10.593136+00	Term 1 Exam Question 2	Hi,<br><br>I think we should do something that make people in our school like it or it trending in online. So I think we use to do something easy and get alot of money. Yesterday I saw a TikTok trend, boys start to go to gym to get a massive muscle. We use to start a charity people who go to school gym have to donate 1$ to school and tell them that the money will use for sports equipment that every one can use it. To organize events is very easy we will start with tell everyone about this but we will said "it very secret don't tell anyone" but they will tell their best friend by the way but that what I want everyone will go to school gym to make sure and that how we raise the money easily.<br><br>Nine,	143	11.0	Hi Nine! 👋 It's great to see your thoughtful ideas for Lucy's fundraising event! You've clearly put some creative energy into helping your friend. Your main area for improvement is to focus on making your ideas super clear and easy to follow, especially when it comes to organising your sentences and using correct grammar. Keep up the fantastic effort, and remember that practice makes progress! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Nine:**<br>*   ✔️ **Break it down!** When you have a few ideas, try to give each one its own paragraph to make your writing easier to read.<br>*   ✔️ **Read it aloud!** After you write something, you can read it out loud to yourself. This can help you spot long, confusing sentences or places where a word might be missing.<br>*   ✔️ **Practice verb forms!** Pay special attention to your verb endings and whether you need an '-s' for 'he/she/it' in the present tense (e.g., 'it makes' instead of 'it make').	You've done a really good job of responding to Lucy's request and giving her some exciting ideas! 🎉 You suggested a clever fundraising activity by linking it to a popular TikTok trend at the gym, and you explained how people could donate money for sports equipment. You also gave a unique idea for organising the event by creating a "secret" buzz. To get a perfect score here, try to give a couple of different fundraising ideas, maybe one that's a bit more traditional and one that's as creative as your gym idea! That way, Lucy has more options to choose from. For instance, you could also suggest "a bake sale where everyone brings their favourite treats" or "a fun sports day with small entry fees." Expanding on the advice for organising events with more practical steps, like making posters or getting teachers involved, would also be a good step!	You certainly managed to communicate your ideas to Lucy, and your friendly tone is just right for writing to a friend! 😊 You used informal language that sounds natural, which is perfect for this task. However, sometimes your ideas were a little tricky to follow because of how the sentences were put together. For example, your explanation of the "secret" marketing strategy, while creative, got a bit lost in one very long sentence. To make your communication even stronger, try to use shorter sentences when you're explaining a complex idea. You could also ask yourself: "If Lucy read this, would she understand exactly what to do?" Making sure your instructions are super clear will really help your friend! 💬	You started your letter with "Hi," and ended it with "Nine," which is great for an informal email! 👍 However, the main part of your message was written as one long block of text. This makes it hard for your reader, Lucy, to see where one idea ends and another begins. Imagine your writing as a neat bookshelf, where each main idea gets its own shelf (or paragraph!). For example, you could have one paragraph for your fundraising idea and another for your organisational advice. When you said, "To organize events is very easy we will start with tell everyone about this..." that would be a perfect place to start a new paragraph! Using connecting words like "Firstly," "Next," or "Also," can help your ideas flow smoothly from one paragraph to the next. Think of these as signposts for your reader. 🗺️ In Thai, ideas are sometimes linked more subtly, but in English, clear paragraphing helps the reader follow your logical steps.	You used a lot of everyday words that are suitable for talking to a friend, which is great! 😊 Your message is understandable, which is the most important thing. However, there were quite a few places where the grammar could be improved, especially with verb forms and making sure your sentences were complete. For example, you wrote "something that make people" which should be "something that **makes** people" (because 'something' is singular). Another example is "the money will use for sports equipment," which needs to be in the passive voice: "the money will **be used** for sports equipment." Also, "we use to do" often suggests something you *used to do* in the past, but for suggestions, you could say "we **could** do" or "we **should** do." Trying to use accurate articles like "the" before "gym" (e.g., "go to **the** gym") would also help. These small changes will make your writing much clearer and more natural for English speakers to read! Keep practicing, and you'll see a big improvement. 🚀	4.0	3.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
3176	29584	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Greetings,\nI heard you are going to visit Thailand next week. Thailand is full of stunning places, temples here are fabulous. Thai people are friendly too, they give us a warm welcome. Also, Thai food is the best. Their food are fresh, clean, and various. Thailand is such a magnificent country. Their are many goods in the country. For your information, Thai people are very serious about manners. It's significant to be nice to other.<br>\nHave a nice visit,\nYaya	80	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	3	2.53	Hi Yaya (Nadi)! Good on you for putting this piece together. You've done a clear job of describing why Thailand is a great place to visit.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used some strong descriptive words like "stunning", "fabulous", and "magnificent" which make your writing much more engaging for the reader.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking</b>: I noticed you used connectors like "Also" and "For your information" to help transition between different topics.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: You attempted a more complex structure with "It's significant to...", which shows you're trying to move beyond basic sentence patterns.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Spelling and Word Choice (There vs Their)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Their are many goods in the country."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"<b>There</b> are many <b>good things</b> in the country."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> These two words sound the same, so it's easy to mix them up. In Thai, "มี" is used to say something exists, but in English, we use "There are" for this. "Their" means "ของพวกเขา". Also, we usually say "good things" or "great products" rather than just "goods" when speaking generally.</li><li><b>📝 Subject-Verb Agreement with Uncountable Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Their food are fresh, clean, and various."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Their food <b>is</b> fresh, clean, and <b>varied</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, a word like "สด" doesn't change regardless of the subject. In English, "food" is considered one group (uncountable), so it needs the singular verb <b>is</b>. I also changed "various" to "varied" because it sounds more natural when describing a selection.</li><li><b>📝 Connecting Complete Ideas</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Thailand is full of stunning places, temples here are fabulous."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Thailand is full of stunning places, <b>and the</b> temples here are fabulous."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You've written two complete ideas here. In English, we can't just put a comma between them; we need a word like "and" to glue them together. We also need "the" because you're talking about specific temples in Thailand.</li></ol><br><br>You've got a good handle on descriptive vocabulary, so just keep an eye on those small connecting words and singular/plural rules. Keep at it.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>10</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement (is/are):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Spelling and Homophones (There/Their):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Noun forms and Plurals:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation and Sentence Structure:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Missing Articles or Conjunctions:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Verb Usage:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	B1	2026
3177	29710	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hi! I would recommend you the place that you should go. The first place is Ayuttaya because this place has Thai communication history. The second place is Phuket the most famost beach in Thailand. Also, for the tips to greet with Thai people just say "Sawad dee ka" if you want to say sorry just say "kor tod ka". You should say it because in Thai manner is very important.	70	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A1+	2	2.53	Hi PingPing! Good on you for putting this travel guide together. It is a helpful list for anyone visiting Thailand for the first time.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Grammatical Range</b>: You attempted some complex structures, like using a relative clause with "the place that you should go" and an appositive to describe Phuket. It is good to see you trying these longer sentence patterns.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You used "because" and "if" correctly to connect your ideas and explain the reasons behind your recommendations.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used specific topic words like "recommend," "history," and "manner" to give your writing more variety.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Noun Forms and Subject-Verb Agreement</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"You should say it because in Thai manner is very important."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"You should say it because in <b>Thailand, manners are</b> very important."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> There are two things here. First, in Thai script, you often use <b>ไทย</b> for both the country and the description. In English, we must use "Thailand" for the location. Second, Thai does not use plural markers, but in English, when we talk about social rules in general, we use the plural <b>มารยาท</b> (manners). Because "manners" is plural, the verb must change from "is" to <b>are</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Punctuation and Sentence Structure</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Also, for the tips to greet with Thai people just say "Sawad dee ka" if you want to say sorry just say "kor tod ka"."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Also, for <b>specific</b> tips <b>on how</b> to greet Thai people, just say "Sawad dee ka", <b>and</b> if you want to say sorry, just say "kor tod ka"."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is a "run-on" sentence. In Thai, you can often string ideas together without much punctuation. In English, we need commas and words like "and" to help the reader breathe and understand where one idea ends and the next begins. I also removed "with" because in English we just "greet people," we don't "greet with people."</li><li><b>📝 Spelling and Prepositions</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The second place is Phuket the most famost beach in Thailand."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The second place is Phuket<b>,</b> the most <b>famous</b> beach in Thailand."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Be careful with the spelling of "famous." Also, remember that when you talk about going somewhere, like in your first sentence, you need the word <b>ไปที่</b> (go to). You wrote "the place that you should go," but it should be "the place that you should go <b>to</b>."</li></ol><br><br>Your advice about using <b>สวัสดีค่ะ</b> and <b>ขอโทษค่ะ</b> is very practical for tourists. Keep practicing your spelling and remember to use those commas to break up your long sentences.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>5</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation and Conjunction Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Noun and Pronoun Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Preposition and Article Errors:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Verb Form and Agreement Errors:</b> 2 instances</li></ol>	A2+	2026
1257	29689	2025-09-07 09:31:59.469616+00	My Favourite Photograph	Good morning everyone. My name is Pun. I am very so happy to see you all today. So today I going to talk about my this pictures is about my sister and me me on the Christmas holiday. So today I going to talk about the three topics. What is about the the photos? Three reasons why I should this picture and my main message. So, let's go.<br><br><br>This picture is about me and sister when I was celebrate with Christmas. I was... she was very so cute and and I have dinner with her and we, we always sharing the present together. And this picture is took it by my sister on the the 20, 20 December for the last year.<br><br><br>Why I, why I should this pictures? First reasons, she is so cute. Did you know when I was looking her, I was very like, oh my gosh, what, how cute of her, like the cute beautiful flowers.<br><br><br>Seconds, the seconds I very so happy to see her emotions like joy, angry or versus in this. And third reasons is remind me when I was celebrate and happy with my sister.<br><br><br>For for today, I want you to know, I want you to know about my sister I talk about about three reasons why I should this picture and and the story of the photo. So the main reasons I it was, I very so love my sister. I will never, I will never care who who who or looking my sister or look, I will never care who looking my sister very so ugly but I think she was very so adorable. So, thank you for listening.	280	13.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	PET	Your pronunciation, Pun, is really clear and easy to understand! 👍 Your intonation was generally appropriate, and it's great that you clearly pronounced the final /s/ sounds. This helps people understand you easily. One thing to focus on is making sure you pronounce the ends of all words, especially consonants. Sometimes, words like 'what' or 'loved' might lose their final sounds a little. Also, be mindful of where the stress falls in words, especially in longer words like 'twenty' /ˈtwen.ti/ or 'picture' /ˈpɪk.tʃər/, as sometimes the stress fell on the wrong syllable. This is a common area for Thai speakers to work on because Thai is a syllable-timed language, where each syllable tends to have similar stress, whereas English is stress-timed, meaning some syllables are much stronger than others. Keep up the great work on making your sounds clear!	Pun, you did a great job presenting your favourite photograph, showing how much you love your sister! Keep practising your grammar and making sure your ideas are fully developed, and you'll keep getting better. You speak with clear pronunciation, which is fantastic!<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br>*   Practise using auxiliary verbs like 'am', 'is', 'are' before verbs ending in '-ing'. For example, "I *am* going" instead of "I going."<br>*   Work on making sure your sentences connect smoothly. Try to use words like 'because', 'although', or 'however' to link your ideas more clearly.<br>*   Record yourself speaking and listen back. Pay attention to the endings of words and where you put the stress in longer words.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1239oN5PHBcxcQdDOcIWNI9TnYZoznqGS?usp=sharing	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3178	29727	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hello Dee!\nI recommend you to visit fantastic tourist attraction in Thailand. Firstly, it's a great experience I have high hope for this place. Lastly, if you meet Thai people, do not point at people, when you're in Thailand. It can be seen as rude, impolite and disrespectful. They're really friendly. Hope you'll have a great time!	57	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	3	2.53	Hi Mark! Good to see you getting stuck into this writing task and sharing some advice about Thailand.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You've used some high-level words like "tourist attraction", "impolite", and "disrespectful" to explain your points clearly.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> I like how you've organized your ideas using "Firstly" and "Lastly", as well as using "if" to create a clear advice structure.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> You made a good attempt at the passive voice with "It can be seen as", which is a sophisticated way to describe social rules.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Hello Dee! I recommend you to visit fantastic tourist attraction in Thailand."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Hello Dee! I recommend you visit some fantastic tourist <b>attractions</b> in Thailand."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, nouns don't change their form to show there is more than one. You use the same word "สถานที่ท่องเที่ยว" whether it's one or many. In English, if you are talking about things in general or more than one, you usually need to add an 's'. You did this again with "high hope" instead of <b>"high hopes"</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Verb Patterns after "Recommend"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Hello Dee! I recommend you to visit fantastic tourist attraction in Thailand."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Hello Dee! I recommend you <b>visit</b> some fantastic tourist attractions in Thailand."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is a tricky bit of English grammar. In Thai, you might say "แนะนำให้" (nam-nae hai), which makes you want to use "to" in English. However, the verb "recommend" is usually followed directly by the person and the base action, without the "to".</li><li><b>📝 Connecting Complete Thoughts</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Firstly, it's a great experience I have high hope for this place."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Firstly, it's a great experience <b>and</b> I have high <b>hopes</b> for this place."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You've put two separate ideas into one sentence without a bridge. In English, we call this a run-on sentence. You need a conjunction like "and" to join them together so the reader knows how the ideas relate.</li><li><b>📝 Using Pronouns to Avoid Repetition</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Lastly, if you meet Thai people, do not point at people, when you're in Thailand."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Lastly, if you meet Thai people, do not point at <b>them</b> when you're in Thailand."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, it's very common to repeat the noun "คน" (khon) or "คนไทย". In English, once we know who we are talking about (Thai people), we prefer to use a pronoun like <b>"them"</b>. This makes your writing sound much more natural.</li></ol><br><br>You've given some really practical advice for anyone visiting the country. Keep practicing those plural 's' endings!<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>6</b> sentences, and <b>2</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Noun Number (Plurals):</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Missing Conjunctions:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Missing Articles or Determiners:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Punctuation and Pronoun choice:</b> 2 instances</li></ol>	B1	2026
2082	34926	2025-09-23 10:43:45.842072+00	Term 1 Writing Task 2	last year, me and my friends went to celebrate new year. which is my favarit festival.<br><br><br>We went to Pattaya we have so much fun playing at the beaches one of my friend built a sand castle However, another on of my friends his name is frame he threw the ball at Atom's sand castle then Atom get really mad because it took him 30 minute's for him to build that castle. My favourite part is that Atom doesn't give up he built a new one but this time the dog destroyed it but Atom doesn't get mad because he finds that the dog is really cute.<br><br><br>with all that considered, I think its really important to always be happy	118	7.0	Hi Chopin,<br><br>It's lovely to see your writing, and you clearly have some interesting experiences to share. You show good enthusiasm for your topic, and that's a fantastic start. To help your writing become even clearer and more engaging, we need to focus on ensuring your articles fully answer the questions asked in the task and are well-organised for your readers. Let's work on making sure your ideas are presented in a way that truly informs and captivates your audience.<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>*   Remember to read the task carefully and make sure you answer all the specific questions about the festival you are describing.<br>*   Practice organising your ideas into clear paragraphs, with each paragraph introducing a new main point.<br>*   Focus on improving your grammar and spelling, especially with verb tenses and common words.	Chopin, you've shared a lovely story about your New Year's celebration, and it's great that you included a festival you enjoy. However, for an article about a festival, it’s really important to tell your readers more about the festival itself! For example, the task asked about *when* the festival takes place and *what happens* during it, such as special traditions or activities that your family participates in. Your article focused mostly on a fun trip with friends, which is great, but it meant we didn't learn much about the New Year festival. Try to keep the main focus on the festival details mentioned in the prompt. Next time, try to clearly name the festival, explain when it happens, and describe two or more specific things people do during the celebration, giving some good details. This will help your readers understand the festival better. For instance, you could talk about special foods, decorations, or family gatherings specific to New Year's. Think about what someone who doesn't know about New Year's would need to learn from your writing.	You do a good job of communicating straightforward ideas, and it’s clear you had fun celebrating. To make your article even more effective, let's think about how an article is usually structured for young people reading an international website. An article about a festival should probably start by introducing the festival clearly, then describe its traditions, and finish with your personal feelings about it. Your article shifted quite a bit to your personal story with friends, which, while interesting, meant it didn't quite fit the expectations for an informative article about a festival. To help your readers stay engaged, try to keep your writing focused on the main topic throughout. You could try using a friendly, informative tone and making sure your content explains any cultural points that someone from another country might not know. Your reader will enjoy learning about the festival if you present it clearly. 😊	Your writing has some good connections between sentences, and you've used paragraph breaks, which is a solid start. To really improve the organisation of your article, consider how you can arrange your ideas so they flow logically from one point to the next. For example, you could have an opening paragraph that introduces the festival, a middle paragraph that talks about what people do during the festival, and a concluding paragraph that shares what you love most about it. Right now, your story about Atom and the sandcastle, while entertaining, took up a lot of space that could have been used to describe the New Year festival more fully. Using linking words more often, like "First," "Next," "Also," or "Finally," can help your reader move smoothly through your ideas. Think of it like a journey for your reader – you want to guide them clearly from the beginning of your article to the end. 🗺️	You’ve used some everyday vocabulary to talk about your experience, which is good. We also see some attempts at using simple grammatical forms. To make your writing clearer and more accurate, let's focus on a few common areas. Firstly, pay close attention to subject-verb agreement. For example, you wrote "me and my friends went" and "Atom get really mad." It should be "my friends and I went" and "Atom **gets** really mad." Secondly, be careful with plural forms, like "30 minute's" instead of "30 **minutes**," and "favarit festival" instead of "favourite festival." Also, practice using the correct verb tenses. For example, "we have so much fun playing" could be "we **had** so much fun playing" if you're talking about a past event, or "we **have** so much fun playing" if it's something you do regularly. Thai learners sometimes face challenges with these aspects because the Thai language doesn't always mark plurals or verb tenses in the same way English does. A good step for you would be to double-check your sentences for these kinds of errors after you've written your first draft. This will make your meaning much clearer for your readers. Keep practicing, and your language control will grow. ✨	1.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2	\N	\N	\N	A1+	2026
3179	29745	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hello there!\nIf you're looking for a perfect country for a trip, let me give you some advices. As a Thai people, I suggest that you should visit our country for once in your life! First, in Thailand, people are very friendly and easygoing. Almost every places you go, people will look happy. Second, in our country, you can learn about our history and also going to the beautiful and peaceful temples that could	74	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	3	2.53	Hi Pheem! Good to see you putting in the effort here. You've got some solid points about why people should visit Thailand.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Sentence Linking</b>: Using connectors like "First" and "Second" helps the reader follow your points easily.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used some good descriptive words like "easygoing" and "peaceful" which fit the topic well.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: I like that you tried to use a conditional sentence ("If you're looking for...") to start your writing.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Countable vs Uncountable Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Hello there! If you're looking for a perfect country for a trip, let me give you some advices."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Hello there! If you're looking for <b>the</b> perfect country for a trip, let me give you some <b>advice</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In English, "advice" is uncountable. We never add an 's' to it. You might be thinking of the Thai phrase "คำแนะนำหลายอย่าง", but in English, whether it is one tip or ten, it is always just "advice".</li><li><b>📝 Singular vs Plural Subjects</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"As a Thai people, I suggest that you should visit our country for once in your life!"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"As a Thai <b>person</b>, I suggest that you should visit our country <b>once</b> in your life!"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> "People" refers to a group (คนหลายคน). Since you are talking about yourself, you are one "person". Also, we don't need the word "for" before "once". This is a common direct translation error from Thai.</li><li><b>📝 Parallel Verb Structures</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Second, in our country, you can learn about our history and also going to the beautiful and peaceful temples that could"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Second, in our country, you can learn about our history and also <b>go</b> to the beautiful and peaceful temples."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> When you use the word "can", it applies to both things you want to do. You wrote "can learn", so the second part must be "can <b>go</b>". You don't need to change it to "going". I also removed "that could" because the sentence ended before you finished the thought.</li><li><b>📝 Expressing "Everywhere"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Almost every places you go, people will look happy."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Almost <b>everywhere</b> you go, people will look happy."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You are translating "ทุกที่" directly as "every places". In English, when we want to say "all the places", we use the single word "everywhere".</li></ol><br><br>Keep working on these plural and singular forms, Pheem. You've got a good eye for vocabulary, so just focus on these grammar patterns next time.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>5</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Word Choice and Phrasing:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Incorrect Determiners (a/the):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Unnecessary Prepositions:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Punctuation and Noun forms:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Incomplete Phrases:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	B1	2026
