Correct version:</b> <i>"I'll give you two <b>reasons</b> why. <b>First</b> of all, you should visit Dream <b>World</b>"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai script, you don't use capital letters, so it's easy to forget them in English! Just remember that we ONLY use capitals for the start of a sentence, the word "I", and proper names like <b>D</b>ream <b>W</b>orld. Common nouns like "reasons" should stay in lowercase.</li><li><b>📝 Natural Prepositions</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"it has many zones to play with."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"it has many zones to play <b>in</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You might be thinking of the Thai word "กับ" (gap), which often translates to "with". While you play "with" a toy, you play "in" an area or a zone. Using the right preposition makes your English sound much more natural to a native speaker.</li></ol><br><br>Check those capital letters and make sure you finish your thoughts in the last sentence next time. You've got the right idea, just needs a bit of a polish.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>3</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Noun and Name Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation and Preposition Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Capitalisation Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Unnecessary Words:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Missing Words:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
3181	29769	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hey Jake, this is a email recommend a tourist attraction in Thailand. The place I recommend is night market, it a market that open in night time and close in the morning. It got alot of things like foods toys hats and clothes. Also, for price, it got very cheap price compare to other. I also have a tip when you go to a night market, make sure to go to popular store, if you have time go watch recommend video to know which store to look for.	88	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2	2.53	Hi Posh! Good on you for putting this email together for Jake. You've given him some practical advice on where to go and what to look for.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range:</b> You used some good, natural phrases like "tourist attraction," "popular store," and "look for," which help the reader understand exactly what you are describing.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You successfully used connectors like "if" and "when" to explain the best time to visit and what to do if you have extra time.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> You attempted to use relative clauses with "that" to give more information about the market, which is a good way to build more complex sentences.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Missing the "BE" verb (is/am/are)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The place I recommend is night market, it a market that open in night time and close in the morning."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The place I recommend is <b>the</b> night market; it <b>is</b> a market that <b>opens at</b> night and <b>closes</b> in the morning."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can describe something by putting the subject and the description together, like "มันแพง". However, in English, you always need a "linking verb" like <b>is</b>. You also need to add an 's' to verbs like <b>opens</b> or <b>closes</b> when talking about a single thing (the market) that happens regularly.</li><li><b>📝 Uncountable Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It got alot of things like foods toys hats and clothes."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It <b>has a</b> lot of things like <b>food</b>, toys, hats, and clothes."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, the word "อาหาร" can be used for one dish or all food in general. In English, <b>food</b> is usually "uncountable" when we talk about it generally, so we don't add an 's'. Also, try to use "has" instead of "got" in your writing, as it sounds more natural here.</li><li><b>📝 Using Articles (a/an/the)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Hey Jake, this is a email recommend a tourist attraction in Thailand."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Hey Jake, this is <b>an</b> email <b>recommending</b> a tourist attraction in Thailand."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Thai doesn't use articles like <b>a</b> or <b>an</b>. In English, we use <b>an</b> if the next word starts with a vowel sound (a, e, i, o, u). Because <b>email</b> starts with 'e', we must use <b>an</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Singular vs Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Also, for price, it got very cheap price compare to other."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Also, <b>regarding the</b> price, it <b>is</b> very cheap <b>compared</b> to <b>others</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> When you are comparing the night market to other places, you need to make "other" plural (<b>others</b>) because you are talking about many different places, not just one. Thai doesn't change the ending of words for plurals, so you have to be extra careful with that 's' in English.</li></ol><br><br>I hope Jake takes your advice and checks out a night market; they really are the best places to find a bargain.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>5</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Missing Articles (a/the):</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Verb Form and Tense Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Noun and Plural Errors:</b> 5 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation and Spelling:</b> 5 instances</li><li><b>Preposition Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Missing Verbs:</b> 1 instance</li></ol><br><br>	A2+	2026
1800	33955	2025-09-13 16:12:10.820601+00	Term 1 Exam Question 2	Most people love pets. However, there are some that hate it. In particular, there are advantages and disadvantages of having pets.<br><br><br>Firstly, there are lots of advantages of having pets. Pets make you feel relax when you are with them. It is because they are cute. Furthermore, pets can be your friend when you are lonely.<br><br><br>Besides having lots of advantages, pets also have lots of disadvantages. First, if they are not friendly they will attack you. Moreover, you need to spend massive amount of money to take care of them.<br><br><br>In conclusion, pets have a lot of advantages and disadvantages. But having lots of disadvantages do not mean pets are bad. Pets are a wonderful friend who will always be there for you. So, make sure to take a good care of your friends and do not leave them alone.	144	13.0	Arty, your article about pets is a thoughtful piece that clearly presents your ideas on the advantages and disadvantages of having animal companions. Your writing is well-structured and easy to follow, making it a pleasant read. To truly make your articles stand out, a bit more attention to varying your vocabulary and refining your grammar will help your messages come through even more clearly. Keep up the great effort, and remember that every piece of writing is an opportunity to improve.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ When you finish writing, read through your work and try to spot places where you have used the same words or phrases multiple times. Can you think of different ways to express those ideas?<br>✔️ Focus on verb forms and agreement. For example, check if your verbs match your subjects, and if you are using the correct tense or form, such as 'relaxed' instead of 'relax' when describing a feeling.<br>✔️ Practise using a wider range of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas, such as 'in addition,' 'however,' or 'consequently,' to make your arguments flow even more smoothly.	You've done a really good job of addressing the task, Arty, by clearly discussing both the good things and the not-so-good things about having pets. You made sure to include everything the question asked for, and anyone reading your article would be fully informed about your opinions. That's a strong start. 👍 It is always important to make sure you answer all parts of the question, and you certainly did that here.	Your article successfully communicates your straightforward ideas, and it definitely holds the reader's attention. You've used the basic conventions of an article well, which is important for this type of writing. To make your writing even more engaging, try to develop your ideas a little more fully. For instance, instead of just saying pets are 'cute,' you could describe *how* they are cute or what specific actions make them endearing. This helps to bring your writing to life. ✨	The way you've organised your article is logical and clear, Arty. You've used some good basic linking words like 'Firstly' and 'In conclusion,' which help the reader follow your points. You have a clear introduction, body paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages, and a conclusion, which is an excellent structure. To improve even further, try to include a greater variety of linking words and phrases to connect your sentences and paragraphs. For example, instead of just starting a new paragraph, you could use phrases like 'On the other hand' or 'Furthermore' to build a stronger bridge between your ideas. Thai learners sometimes focus on getting the main points down, and can forget these little connectors that really help the reader. 🌉	Your control over simple grammatical forms is good, Arty, and even with a few errors, your message is very clear, which is the main goal. You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, but you did repeat phrases like 'lots of advantages' a few times. Trying to use different words like 'numerous benefits' or 'several drawbacks' can make your writing more interesting. For example, you wrote 'Pets make you feel relax,' and it would be more grammatically accurate to say 'Pets make you feel **relaxed**.' Another example is 'But having lots of disadvantages do not mean pets are bad,' which could be improved by saying 'But having lots of disadvantages **does** not mean **that** pets are bad.' These are common areas where learners sometimes make mistakes. A great way to improve is to actively look for different ways to express similar ideas and to pay close attention to verb endings when describing feelings or states. Keep practising, and your language will become even more precise. ✍️	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
3186	34091	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hey, I think you should visit Iconsiam. It is a very famous places that many tourist visited. It's a very big mall,That is beside with Jao Pra ya river, So they will be boat that you can try and also a fountain show with amazing light and sound which is one of the show, Their are also fall foutain that drop water in different picture you can watch. And you can go shopping too they have alot of brand you can chose. I reccomed to travel by BTS go to Krug Thonburi station then connect to Iconsiam.	97	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2	2.53	Hi Tanya! You've done a solid job describing a place you clearly know well. Your directions for the BTS are helpful for anyone trying to find the mall.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used good topic-specific words like "fountain show", "brand", and "amazing light and sound" to describe the atmosphere.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: You attempted some complex structures using relative clauses, such as "that many tourist visited" and "which is one of the show". It's good to see you trying these.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You used words like "so", "also", and "then" to connect your ideas and give the reader more detail.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It is a very famous places that many tourist visited."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It is a very famous <b>place</b> that many <b>tourists visit</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, nouns don't change their form when there is more than one of something. For example, you use the same word for "one person" and "many people". In English, if you are talking about "many", you must add an <b>s</b> to the noun (tourist<b>s</b>). Also, because you are describing the mall right now, you should use the present tense "visit" instead of the past tense "visited".</li><li><b>📝 Existential "There is / There are"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"So they will be boat that you can try and also a fountain show... Their are also fall foutain that drop water in different picture you can watch."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"So <b>there</b> will be <b>boats</b> that you can try and also a fountain show... <b>There</b> are also <b>water fountains</b> that drop water in different <b>shapes</b> you can watch."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is a very common mix-up for Thai speakers because you use the word มี to mean both "to have" and "there is". In English, we use "There are" to say something exists. You used "they" and "their", which sound a bit like "there", but they have different meanings. Also, remember to add that <b>s</b> for plural things like boats and fountains!</li><li><b>📝 Sentence Punctuation and Run-ons</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"It's a very big mall,That is beside with Jao Pra ya river, So they will be boat..."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"It's a very big mall <b>that</b> is beside <b>the Chao Phraya</b> river, <b>so there</b> will be <b>boats</b>..."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai script, you don't use capital letters or full stops to end sentences. In English, we only use a capital letter at the very start of a sentence. You used capital letters after commas (mall,That), which we don't do. I also corrected the spelling of <b>เจ้าพระยา</b> to the standard English version: <b>Chao Phraya</b>.</li></ol><br><br>You've got a good eye for detail when describing things, Tanya. Just keep an eye on those plural 's' endings and your sentence endings next time.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>5</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Noun Plural/Singular Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Spelling Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Noun Choice Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation and Capitalisation Errors:</b> 5 instances</li><li><b>Verb Tense and Form Errors:</b> 4 instances</li><li><b>Pronoun Errors:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Missing or Unnecessary Words:</b> 4 instances</li></ol>	A2+	2026
2444	33171	2025-12-01 12:47:07.087453+00	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. However before that happended Sam was just a guy who lived together with his wife in America. He also worked in a company as an inventor but he got bullied by his coworker, who he didn't like. So he invented an invention, a gun that can erase people and ether will forget about them.<br><br><br>Initially he tested it at his company and it worked very well. He was very happy about it and like that he decide to tell his wife. When he got home his wife wasn't there yet so he just got out to buy some food for his success and left the gun on the sofa. When he came back the light was on, so he thought that his wife was in there but when he got inside the house were empthy and then he realise his wife got erase by the gun. Sam tried everything to get his wife back but it took year to get her back and when he got his wife back, she lost all her memory but in the end his wife got her memory back and they lived happily ever after.	201	16.0	Hello Florence, 👋<br><br>This is a clever story with a funny (but sad!) twist. The idea of an 'eraser gun' is very creative, and the accidental erasing of his wife was a surprising turn of events. I liked the happy ending where she got her memory back.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you polish your writing even further. 📋<br><li><b>Spelling Check!</b> 📝 Watch out for 'ether' (others), 'empthy' (empty), and 'happended' (happened).</li><li><b>Verb Tenses!</b> ⏳ Ensure all verbs are in the past. 'Decide' should be 'decided'.</li><li><b>Passive Voice!</b> 🛡️ 'Got erase' should be 'got <b>erased</b>'.</li>	All content is relevant. The narrative is complete and the reader is informed about the invention's unintended side effects ('erase people and ether will forget about them').	The conventions of a story are used to communicate straightforward ideas clearly ('Sam tried everything to get his wife back'). To improve, ensure clarity in word choice and avoid potentially confusing phrases.	The text is generally well organised. Linking words are used effectively ('However', 'Initially', 'So', 'but'). To improve, consider breaking longer paragraphs to enhance readability, especially the second paragraph.	Everyday vocabulary is used appropriately. There are some errors in spelling and grammar ('ether' for others/either?, 'house were empthy', 'got erase', 'happend') but meaning is clear. To improve, focus on correcting common spelling mistakes and ensuring correct passive voice usage.	5.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
1032	29031	2025-08-14 13:42:46.323372+00	Thai Food	Food is an important aspect in every culture. Although all food gives you energy, they all vary in different cultures. In particular, desserts exists in all cultures, specifically Thailand. The sweet taste has been relevant since the old period. Three of the most popular desserts are Mango Sticky Rice, Bua loi and Kanom chan.<br><br><br>One of the most known desserts in Thailand is Mango Sticky Rice. This cuisine consists of mango and sticky rice, usually paired with Kathi (coconut milk). The simplest method is by peeling the outer mango skin and steam the rice with flour in order to make sticky rice. Mango Sticky Rice is extremely popular around the world. This symbolizes how much impact it had in the food industry due to its easy recipe and local ingredients. This reflects Thailand's civilians of how they behave towards certain things by trying to make the best use of things around their area, instead of buying globalized food.<br><br><br>Besides Mango Sticky Rice, Bua Loi is another beloved dish. There are various reciepes and different flavours, but using corn strach and Kathi (coconut milk) is recommended. You can start off by boiling the corn strach then add the food coloring into the pot to add the taste and color. Finally, shape them into a mold then add them to Kathi. Bua loi displays how creativity can be shown through cooking, such as, shaping the mold into a star shape. This dessert freely express one's creativity, especially for people in Thailand.<br><br><br>Finally, Kanom Chan is another popular dessert among Thai individuals. It too has a simple recipe, inculding Baitocy and gelatin. Firstly, stir the Baitocy and then add some additional ingredients or accessory up to one's personal preference. Then add the gelatin in. A lot of classic reciepes mostly make them into a squared shape, so it all could stack up, hence the name, Kanom Chan. This dessert overall expresses how Thais', since from the old times, had always make up the most and best use of the enviroment around them.<br><br><br>To summarize, desserts in Thailand all have unique reciepes and taste, but one thing they all have in common is their local ingredients that is easily found in their own homes. In addition, Thai people is delicate about using their abudant enviroment to their best potential, specifically through desserts.	388	\N	You have written a well-structured essay about Thai desserts, demonstrating a good understanding of the topic and effectively communicating your ideas. You successfully commented on the symbolic and cultural value of the food in your body paragraphs, which was a key requirement. To improve further, focus on refining your spelling and consistently using relative clauses and passive structures to add variety to your writing. Your vocabulary use is appropriate and shows flexibility.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Writing	Formative	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026
3187	34927	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hello Baicha! Today I'm recommending Thai tourist attractions for you, their will be two places that I will recommed for you. The first place is down South, the place is called "The bunnies village". This place is mostly about bunnies so if you love bunnies this place is just for you. The second place is called "Alya's hotel and diner". This place is also down south near the beach, so they mostly sell seafood dishes. And the hotel room is very nice. Many tourists like to stay here so you might wanna book the room first.\nTalk soon,\nEri	99	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	3	2.53	Hi Eri! Good to see you putting in the effort to describe these spots in Thailand. You've got the right idea with your descriptions.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You used connectors like "so" and "if" effectively to show the relationship between your ideas, such as why someone should visit the bunny village.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: I noticed you tried some more natural structures like "you might wanna book," which shows you're thinking about how people actually speak.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used specific terms like "tourist attractions" and "seafood dishes" rather than just using basic words.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Choosing "There" vs "Their"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Hello Baicha! Today I'm recommending Thai tourist attractions for you, their will be two places that I will recommed for you."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Hello Baicha! Today I'm recommending Thai tourist attractions for you; <b>there</b> will be two places that I will <b>recommend to</b> you."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> These words sound the same, but have different jobs. In Thai, you might be thinking of "ที่นั่น" (there/place) or "ของพวกเขา" (their/belonging). When you want to say something exists, like "มี" in Thai, we use <b>"there"</b> in English. Also, we "recommend" something <b>"to"</b> a person, not "for" them.</li><li><b>📝 Joining Sentences (Comma Splices)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The first place is down South, the place is called "The bunnies village"."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The first place is down South<b>;</b> the place is called "The <b>Bunnies Village</b>"."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai writing, you don't use full stops or semicolons often. In English, you can't just join two complete sentences with a comma. You need a semicolon (;) or a full stop (.) to separate them properly.</li><li><b>📝 Capitalising Names of Places</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The second place is called "Alya's hotel and diner"."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The second place is called "Alya's <b>Hotel</b> and <b>Diner</b>"."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> Thai script doesn't have capital letters, so it's a common habit to skip them. However, in English, the names of businesses and specific places are <b>ชื่อเฉพาะ</b> (proper nouns) and every main word must start with a capital letter.</li></ol><br><br>Both of those spots sound like a good day out, especially the one near the beach. Keep at it.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>8</b> sentences, and <b>4</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Capitalisation and Spelling:</b> 5 instances</li><li><b>Punctuation (Joining sentences):</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Prepositions:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	B1	2026
3188	34929	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hi! I heard that you are visiting Thailand. That's great! First reason you should visit Thailand is the product here is very cheap and good too. The second reason is people here in Thailand is very kind and nice if you ask for help they would help you. Lastly I will give you one tip for your trip to Thailand, you should be nice to people here. Because if you don't they won't be nice to you either. And that's all I can help. Bye	85	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2	2.53	Hi Saint! Good on you for putting this together. You've given some clear reasons why someone should visit Thailand, and your advice is quite practical.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Sentence Linking:</b> You did a good job using signposting words like "First", "The second reason", and "Lastly" to guide the reader through your ideas.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Punctuation and Capitalisation:</b> You've got the hang of using capital letters for proper nouns like "Thailand" and starting your sentences correctly.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Grammatical Range:</b> I noticed you tried to use a conditional "if" structure to explain how people react to kindness. It's a good habit to try these more complex sentences.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Plurals and Subject-Verb Agreement</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"First reason you should visit Thailand is the product here is very cheap and good too."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"<b>The</b> first reason you should visit Thailand is <b>that</b> the <b>products</b> here <b>are</b> very cheap and good too."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, nouns like สินค้า don't change if there's one or many. In English, when you're talking about things in general, you usually need a plural 's' (products). Because "products" is plural, the verb must also change from "is" to "are".</li><li><b>📝 Run-on Sentences</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"The second reason is people here in Thailand is very kind and nice if you ask for help they would help you."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"The second reason is <b>that</b> people here in Thailand <b>are</b> very kind and nice<b>;</b> if you ask for help, they <b>will</b> help you."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is what we call a run-on sentence. In English, you can't just keep adding ideas without a clear break. You need a semicolon (;) or a full stop to separate the description of the people from the "if" sentence. It gives the reader a chance to breathe.</li><li><b>📝 Verb Prepositions</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"And that's all I can help. Bye"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"And that's all I can help <b>with</b>. Bye<b>.</b>"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can end a sentence with ช่วย (chuay), but in English, the verb "help" often needs the word "with" when you are referring back to a task or a situation you've been talking about.</li></ol><br><br>Cheers for the travel tips, Saint. Your points about the products being cheap and the people being kind are spot on.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>6</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Punctuation and Spelling:</b> 6 instances</li><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Missing Articles (the/a):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Verb Tense and Form:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Noun Plurals:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Missing Connector "that":</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
