3189	34932	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	Hello, my name is Natee. And today is 5th of January 2026. In the afternoon I will be talking about tourist attraction in Thailand, so in the Thailand there are many bad places to go, and I don't recommend yet, because the country is dirty and full of terrible people. I recommend you to go to Finland or Sweden, because these countries are very beautiful with a lot of buildings and good companies.	73	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2	2.53	Hi Natee! Good on you for getting this draft finished. You've clearly got some strong opinions about travel, and you've managed to get your message across.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You used connectors like "so", "and", and "because" to join your ideas together into longer sentences rather than just using short bursts.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: I noticed you had a go at using the future continuous tense with "I will be talking," which is a good step up from basic English.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used some good topic-specific words like "tourist attraction" and "companies" to explain your thoughts.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"In the afternoon I will be talking about tourist attraction in Thailand, so in the Thailand there are many bad places to go, and I don't recommend yet, because the country is dirty and full of terrible people."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"In the afternoon I will be talking about tourist <b>attractions</b> in Thailand..."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, nouns don't change when there is more than one of something. You might use a word like "หลาย" (lai) to show plural, but the noun stays the same. In English, we almost always need to add an "s" to the end of the word when talking about things in general.</li><li><b>📝 Articles with Country Names</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"In the afternoon I will be talking about tourist attraction in Thailand, so in the Thailand there are many bad places to go, and I don't recommend yet, because the country is dirty and full of terrible people."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"...so in <b>Thailand</b>, there are many bad places to go..."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> You added "the" before Thailand. In English, most country names don't need an article. We just say "Thailand" or "Finland". You don't need to add anything extra like you might do in Thai when referring to a specific place or "ที่" (tee).</li><li><b>📝 Verb Patterns with "Recommend"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"I recommend you to go to Finland or Sweden, because these countries are very beautiful with a lot of buildings and good companies."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"I recommend you <b>go</b> to Finland or Sweden..."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This is a very common mistake. You are likely thinking of the Thai structure "แนะนำให้คุณไป" (nae-num hai khun pai). While we use "to" with verbs like "want" or "tell", the verb "recommend" usually takes a different structure in natural English. Just keep it simple: recommend + person + base verb.</li><li><b>📝 Missing Object Pronouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"In the afternoon I will be talking about tourist attraction in Thailand, so in the Thailand there are many bad places to go, and I don't recommend yet, because the country is dirty and full of terrible people."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"...and I don't recommend <b>it</b> yet..."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, if the person you're talking to knows what you're talking about, you can often leave out the object (the "it"). In English, verbs like "recommend" almost always need an object after them to sound complete.</li></ol><br><br>It's interesting to hear your take on different countries, though I hope you find some better spots in Thailand soon! Keep practicing these plural and article rules.<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>3</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Noun Usage and Punctuation:</b> 2 instances</li><li><b>Missing Articles (the):</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Unnecessary Articles:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Plural Noun Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Unnecessary Adverbs:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Missing Pronouns:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Verb Form Errors:</b> 1 instance</li><li><b>Other Punctuation/Linking errors:</b> 1 instance</li></ol>	A2+	2026
