3190	34933	2026-01-08 08:51:00.907984+00	Email to Friend	I've know you're going to Thailand soon. If it true, I want to give you some amazing spot you have to go. I think you have to go to Khoa Yai and Siam parakron. I know it's not stay near but I think you should go to that. And don't be rude, Thai people quite sensitive, I think. If you nervose about Thai people, don't be worry. Thai People is very kind!	72	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Use of English	Formative	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2	2.53	Hi Grace! Good on you for putting these travel tips together for your friend. It's a kind thing to do, and your advice about being polite is spot on.<br><br>Here's what you did well:<br><br><ul>  <li>✨ <b>Vocabulary Range</b>: You used specific names for places like "Khao Yai" and "Siam Paragon", and you chose good descriptive words like "sensitive" and "kind" to describe people.</li>  <li>🎯 <b>Grammatical Range</b>: You attempted several conditional sentences using "if", such as "If it is true" and "If you are nervous". This shows you are trying to use more complex structures to explain your ideas.</li>  <li>💪 <b>Sentence Linking</b>: You successfully used connectors like "and" and "but" to join your thoughts together, which helps the writing flow better.</li></ul><br><br>Now for some areas to work on. I'll explain WHY you're making these mistakes so you can avoid them next time:<br><br><ol><li><b>📝 Missing "is/are" (The BE verb)</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"If it true, I want to give you some amazing spot you have to go."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"If it <b>is</b> true, I want to give you some amazing <b>spots</b> you have to go <b>to</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, you can often say a description right after the subject, like "มันจริง". But in English, you must always have a verb to link them. We use <b>"is"</b>, <b>"am"</b>, or <b>"are"</b> (<b>เป็น/อยู่/คือ</b>). You also did this with "Thai people <b>are</b> quite sensitive."</li><li><b>📝 Natural Phrasing for Locations</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"I know it's not stay near but I think you should go to that."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"I know <b>they are not near each other</b>, but I think you should go to <b>them</b>."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> This comes from a direct translation of "อยู่ใกล้". In English, we don't use the word "stay" for the location of a building or a city. "Stay" is for people living somewhere for a short time. For places, we just say they <b>"are near"</b> or <b>"are far"</b>.</li><li><b>📝 Plural Nouns</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"If it true, I want to give you some amazing spot you have to go."</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"If it is true, I want to give you some amazing <b>spots</b> you have to go to."</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> In Thai, the word for a place doesn't change if there is more than one. In English, if you say "some" or "many", you must add an <b>"s"</b> to the end of the noun. Since you mentioned two places (Khao Yai and Siam Paragon), you need to use the plural "spots".</li><li><b>📝 Subject-Verb Agreement with "People"</b><br><b>What you wrote:</b> <i>"Thai People is very kind!"</i><br><b>Correct version:</b> <i>"Thai people <b>are</b> very kind!"</i><br><b>Why this happens:</b> The word "people" is always plural in English (more than one person). Even though it doesn't end in "s", you must use <b>"are"</b> instead of <b>"is"</b>. Also, "people" doesn't need a capital letter in the middle of a sentence!</li></ol><br><br>I reckon your friend will find these suggestions very helpful for their trip to Thailand. Keep practicing those "is" and "are" links!<br><br><b>📊 Your Grammar Analysis</b><br><br>I analysed your grammar using Cambridge University's <b>ERRANT</b> script (ERRor ANnotation Toolkit), which is a tool that compares what you wrote against a corrected version to identify grammatical errors. It shows that you wrote <b>7</b> sentences, and <b>0</b> of them had no errors.<br><br><b>Your error breakdown:</b><br><ol><li><b>Verb Usage Errors:</b> 7 instances</li><li><b>Phrasing and Word Choice:</b> 6 instances</li><li><b>Spelling and Capitalisation:</b> 3 instances</li><li><b>Noun and Preposition usage:</b> 2 instances</li></ol>	A2+	2026
