2471	29929	2025-12-02 09:53:40.087331+00	Creative Writing	Sam finish his invention and pressed the button to start it. Sam was the genious inventor. He was attending at Oxford University and made engineering classes. He was working at this invention for one year and it already done. When he pressed the button the machine couldn't refill his pen ink for infinity because the machine didn't have some ingredient that would make him ink for infinity. He was really upset and he went to the museum to relax, but he saw the ingredient that could refill his ink for infinity. It called the Ancient Ink Stone. So it would made the ink for infinity and it's really expensive.<br><br><br>After that moment he make the plan to steal that stone. So he plan it for 3 days before he do the steal. So in the steal day he went into<br><br><br>the museum and he do the plan. he evade the staff again and again and he use his hammer to break the glass and take the stone. At that time all staff are alert and went to catch Sam. Sam use his plan to evade into the air condition way and he can take that stone back to home.<br><br><br>After that moment he went to hide the police at the country side and he use that stone and the machine. After that he Open the pen factory and become a millionere.	228	15.0	Hello Tiw, 👋<br><br>This is a very exciting story! 🕵️‍♂️ It reads like a heist movie—'Mission Impossible' starring Sam! I really liked the details about the 'Ancient Ink Stone' and how Sam used his engineering skills to escape the museum. It was a fun adventure from start to finish.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you improve your writing score. 📋<br><br>1. <b>Verb Tenses!</b> ⏳ This is the big one for you. Remember to use the past tense for stories ('finished', not 'finish').<br>2. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 Watch out for 'genious' (genius) and 'millionere' (millionaire).<br>3. <b>Linking Words!</b> 🔗 You used 'So' and 'After that' a lot. Try using different words to connect your sentences.	You have a great narrative arc! 🌟 The transition from being an inventor to a thief was a surprising twist.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>The Ending!</b> 💰 He becomes a millionaire, but does he feel guilty about stealing? Adding a sentence about his feelings would give the story more depth.</li><li><b>The Invention!</b> 🖊️ Explain <i>why</i> an infinite ink pen is so important. Why did he need it so badly that he became a thief?</li>	You told the story clearly and kept the action moving. 🏃‍♂️ The museum scene was particularly exciting.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Descriptive Language!</b> 🎨 Instead of 'he evade the staff', you could say 'he <i>sneaked past</i> the guards' or 'he <i>crawled through</i> the vents'. Stronger verbs make the action better.</li><li><b>Repetition!</b> 🔄 You repeated 'refill his ink for infinity' three times. Try to vary your words (e.g., 'never-ending supply', 'limitless ink').</li>	You used paragraphs to separate the introduction, the heist, and the ending. Good job! 📚<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Linking Words!</b> 🔗 You started three sentences with 'So' or 'After that'. Try using words like 'Consequently', 'Therefore', or 'Suddenly' to make your writing flow better.</li><li><b>Sentence Structure!</b> 🏗️ 'It called the Ancient Ink Stone.' -> 'It <b>was</b> called...'. Make sure you include the verb 'to be'.</li>	You used some good vocabulary like 'evade', 'alert', and 'ingredient'. 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Genious' -> '<b>genius</b>'. 'Millionere' -> '<b>millionaire</b>'.</li><li><b>Verb Forms!</b> ⚙️ 'Sam finish' -> 'Sam <b>finished</b>'. 'It already done' -> 'It <b>was</b> already done'. 'He do the plan' -> 'He <b>carried out</b> the plan'.</li><li><b>Modals!</b> 🚦 'It would made' -> 'It would <b>make</b>' (after 'would', use the base verb).</li>	5.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
2019	34929	2025-09-21 17:37:05.945177+00	Term 1 Email	Hi. I would love to come to the science festival this saturday. we can meet up at the front entrance Because it will be easier for you to come too that would be the best for me. No I don't think watching the Discovering Dinosaurs will be more interesting. Because if we go build a robot it will be more fun and creative. And I don't really like watching films too. okay I won't forget to bring a pocket money. But I thinke we should brought a fan too Because you said that it will be all days so I think that it gonna be hot too.<br><br><br>Bye<br><br><br>Saint	111	14.0	Your email to Corey is a lovely piece of writing, and it’s clear you understood the task and shared all the important information. You've done a very good job of responding to each part of Corey's email with your ideas and preferences. Your next big step in writing will be to organise your thoughts into clear paragraphs, which will make your messages even easier for your friends to read and understand. Keep up the good work; you are certainly on the right path to expressing yourself well in English. 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✅ Start a new paragraph each time you introduce a new main idea or topic in your email.<br>✅ Remember to begin each new sentence with a capital letter and to use correct verb forms.<br>✅ Pay close attention to whether nouns are countable or uncountable, and use words like 'a' or 'some' carefully.	You've done an excellent job of including all the information needed in your email to Corey, which is fantastic! You clearly told Corey you were excited about the festival, where you'd prefer to meet, and why you'd rather build a robot than watch a film. It was also clever of you to suggest bringing a fan and explain why it would be useful. This shows you're very good at making sure your reader has all the details they need. Keep thinking about what information the reader needs and how to include it clearly, just as you did here. 🥳	You certainly used a friendly tone for your email, and your "Hi" and "Bye" at the beginning and end were perfect for writing to a friend. This helps the reader feel like they are having a proper conversation with you. However, to make your emails even clearer and more engaging, try to arrange your ideas into different paragraphs. For example, you could have one paragraph where you talk about the tickets and where to meet, and then a new paragraph for discussing the activities. This helps your reader follow your thoughts more easily and makes your email much more structured. Remember, new topic, new paragraph. 🤔	Your ideas in this email are connected and flow quite well, which is important for any piece of writing. You've used words like "Because" to link your sentences and explain your choices, which helps the reader understand your reasoning. To take your organisation to the next level, focus on using paragraphs to separate your different points. Imagine each main idea needs its own little "box" of text, and that's what a paragraph does. For instance, after you talk about meeting Corey, you could start a new paragraph to discuss the robot or film activity. This way, your writing will be even clearer and more enjoyable for your reader to follow, showing off your ideas in a really polished way. ✨	You've used many everyday words appropriately in your email, and it's easy to understand what you mean, which is the main goal of communication! You expressed your ideas clearly, even with some noticeable errors. To make your English even more precise, let's look at a few things. Firstly, remember to always start a new sentence with a capital letter, even after a short phrase like "No." or "okay." For example, "we can meet..." should be "We can meet..." and "Because it will be..." should be "Because it will be...". Secondly, be careful with your verb forms; you wrote "if we go build a robot" which should be "if we **build** a robot," and "we should brought a fan" which should be "we should **bring** a fan." Sometimes, in Thai, you might not change the verb form for tense, but in English, these changes are quite important. Lastly, pay attention to words like "a" and "all days." "Pocket money" is usually uncountable in English, so we just say "pocket money" without "a." Also, for duration, we often say "all **day**" instead of "all days." Focusing on these small but significant details will help your writing sound more natural and accurate. You're already communicating well, so these refinements will truly enhance your command of English. 🚀	5.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
1094	29561	2025-08-22 10:18:03.711726+00	Social Issue	I beliveve that people around the world got bully first of all when people was a childrens there have a group that like to bully people bully new people at school or annoying them and it maybe make childrens Kill them self because they got bully for years or maybe childrens can greif	44	5.0	<b>📝 Dear 29561</b><br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Social Issue'. You wrote 44 words and achieved A1. This is a good start, and with some focus, you can improve your writing skills.😊<br><br>	Your paragraph presents an opinion on bullying, but it is too short and lacks detailed supporting ideas or evidence.<br><b>Problem:</b> Your writing did not meet the expected word count and needed more development for your ideas. For example, you wrote: "I beliveve that people around the world got bully first of all when people was a childrens there have a group that like to bully people bully new people at school or annoying them and it maybe make childrens Kill them self because they got bully for years or maybe childrens can greif"<br><b>Correction:</b> To meet the word count and develop your ideas, you could expand on how bullying starts and why it's a social issue. For example, "I believe that bullying is a serious problem around the world. It often starts when people are children, as groups sometimes bully new students at school or annoy them. This can unfortunately make children feel very sad and even lead them to harm themselves, or experience deep grief, because they have been bullied for many years. It's important to understand how these experiences affect young people."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In academic writing, it is important to provide enough detail and explanation to fully inform the reader about your opinion and supporting points. Meeting the word count helps you do this.	The text communicates simple ideas, but the informal style and errors make it hard for the reader to follow and engage.<br><b>Problem:</b> The language used is quite informal, and the many errors distract the reader, such as: "...there have a group that like to bully people bully new people at school or annoying them and it maybe make childrens Kill them self because they got bully for years or maybe childrens can greif"<br><b>Correction:</b> For an academic paragraph, you need to use more formal language and clear sentence structures. For example: "...there is often a group that bullies new students at school or annoys them. This can sadly lead children to consider harming themselves or to experience profound grief because of prolonged bullying."<br><b>Explanation:</b> For a formal paragraph, try to avoid slang or very direct, conversational language. Focus on writing clearly and precisely, which helps the reader understand your message without distraction.	The text lacks clear organization and coherent flow due to many errors and a lack of proper sentence structure.<br><b>Problem:</b> The sentences run together, and punctuation is missing, making it difficult to read. For example: "...annoying them and it maybe make childrens Kill them self..."<br><b>Correction:</b> You need to use proper punctuation, like commas and periods, to separate your ideas clearly. For example: "...annoying them, and it may make children kill themselves..."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, each sentence should have clear start and end points using capital letters and periods. This is different from Thai, where long clauses are common, so it's easy to connect too many ideas without clear sentence breaks in English.	Basic vocabulary is used, but frequent spelling and grammatical errors often hinder communication.<br><b>Problem:</b> You have several spelling errors and issues with plural nouns. For example: "I beliveve that people around the world got bully first of all when people was a childrens there have a group..."<br><b>Correction:</b> It should be "I believe that people around the world get bullied... when people are children, there is often a group..."<br><b>Explanation:</b>\n*   **Spelling:** Double-check common words like "believe" and "themselves".\n*   **Plural -s:** In English, we add '-s' to most nouns to show there is more than one (e.g., "children" is already plural, not "childrens"). Accordingly, Thai writers often forget to use plurals in English, or use them the wrong way.\n*   **Verb agreement:** Make sure your verbs agree with their subjects (e.g., "it makes" instead of "it make"). This is also a common L1 interference point.<br>	2.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A1+	\N	\N	\N	A1+	2026
