attempt to communicate straightforward ideas about why Chiang Rai is a place you love, and your enthusiasm for the city is evident. However, the conventions of an essay are not used effectively to hold the target reader's attention or communicate ideas clearly. The register shifts between formal ("Hello everyone. Today I will talk about...") and informal ("I gonna talk about," "I promise"). The short, simple sentences, especially in the introduction and conclusion, and the somewhat abrupt transitions, make the overall piece feel less sophisticated and cohesive. For example, "Chaing Rai, is my special place because my mother" is a very basic opening. To improve to a 5, you would need to use essay conventions more consistently and effectively, including a more appropriate register and more varied sentence structures to communicate ideas clearly and engage the reader throughout.	The text has a basic overall structure with an introduction, a body discussing food, another discussing special places, and a conclusion. You use basic linking words like "Next," "First," and "Second" to signpost ideas. However, the paragraphing is not consistently well-managed. The first paragraph combines introductory statements with details about food, which makes it less clear as an introduction. Ideas within paragraphs could also be grouped more logically to improve coherence. For example, the sentence "About nature is really good too" in the conclusion feels quite tacked on. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to organise your text more coherently with distinct paragraphs, each focusing on a main point, and use a wider variety of cohesive devices and organisational patterns to create a seamless flow of ideas.	You use everyday vocabulary generally appropriately, covering topics like food, places, and art. However, there is some overuse of simple linking phrases and basic vocabulary. The range of grammatical forms is primarily simple, and while you attempt some more complex structures (e.g., relative clauses like "who is the most famous, Thai artist"), the control over these is inconsistent. Errors are frequent and noticeable, sometimes affecting the flow and clarity. Examples include: "I been there" (should be "I've been there"), "Chaing Rai" (spelling error), "Night Barzzar" (spelling error), "alot of" (should be "a lot of"), "gilled sausage" (should be "grilled sausage"), "It a little bit spicy" (should be "It is a little bit spicy"), "You can watched" (should be "You can watch"), "who is the most famous, Thai artist" (should be "who is the most famous Thai artist"), "It all white" (should be "It is all white"), "Baan Dam musium" (spelling error), and "It all Black" (should be "It is all black"). The prevalence of simple sentences and these errors detract from the overall effectiveness. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to use a wider range of vocabulary more precisely, including less common lexis, and demonstrate greater control and flexibility over both simple and complex grammatical forms, with only occasional errors that do not impede communication.	4.0	2.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
