al to a native ear. Practicing the natural rhythm and stress patterns of English words and sentences, perhaps by listening to native speakers and trying to imitate their rhythm, will help a lot. For example, try saying "family" focusing the stress on the first syllable, /'fæm.ə.li/, rather than the last.	Hello Prize! 👋 It was a pleasure to hear you speak. You managed to communicate your ideas clearly and engaged really well in the discussions. To make your English even better, focusing on getting your grammar just right and fine-tuning some pronunciation details will be a great help. Keep up the excellent work! You've got a solid foundation to build upon.<br><br>📋 Action Plan:<br>✔️ **Double-check your grammar:** Before speaking, even for a moment, think about the correct verb forms, especially when talking about ongoing actions (e.g., "I am doing" instead of "I doing").<br>✔️ **Listen for natural stress:** Pay attention to where native speakers place emphasis in words and sentences, particularly for words like "family," "people," and "café." This will make your speech flow more naturally.<br>✔️ **Expand your vocabulary for precision:** Try to find more exact words to describe things, moving beyond general terms to express yourself more specifically (e.g., "looks enjoyable" instead of "look enjoys").	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1yqGR5ip5XtVuoI2jPIboJbuDxmsnlN7a?usp=drive_link	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
