ring your book review about Attack on Titan! Your intonation was fine and your speaking was easy to follow. With a score of 3 out of 5, you have a solid foundation to build on. 👏<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b> ✨<br>Your intonation was natural and kept the story moving forward. You understood what you were talking about, and that helped your listener stay engaged with your review. Your basic sounds were also quite clear.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>Watch out for your word stress! For example, you said "al-SO" (/<b>ɔːlˈsoʊ</b>/) but we usually stress the first syllable: <b>AL</b>-so. You also said "ti-TUN" (/taɪ**tʌn**/) instead of "<b>TI</b>-tan" (/ˈtaɪtn̩/). The main issue was choppiness. Because you were a bit nervous, you spoke word-by-word (he. also. got. a.), which stopped your words from linking together. English sounds best when the words flow smoothly.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Relax and link:</b> It's okay to be nervous! Take a deep breath and try to link your words. Think of them as "he-also-got-a" as a single unit of sound.</li><li><b>Dictionary practice:</b> Use a site like Cambridge Dictionary to hear where the stress / ' / is on words like "Titan." It's almost always on the first part!</li><li><b>Read aloud daily:</b> Spending just 5 minutes a day reading English aloud will help your mouth get used to connecting those sounds smoothly.</li></ul><br>A solid performance, Arty! 🎯	Arty, your Attack on Titan review was excellent because you used the "Imagine" hook effectively and provided a very clear summary of the plot. 👏 To reach a Band 5 (B2), focus on keeping your verb tenses consistent throughout the story, use a wider variety of linking words beyond "then" and "so," and try to reduce hesitations to make your speech flow more smoothly. 🎯	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1nuXLkyZgiWXUQVFKnklUKyxFV7qAEVJ7?usp=sharing	B2	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
