nfident as you went on! At the beginning you were nervous, but as you spoke more, your flow started to improve. This shows you have the ability to speak well once you relax.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> 👀<br>We have a few things to watch. First, syllable stress: you said "ma-RY" (/<b>məˈriː</b>/) but it should be <b>MA</b>-ry (/ˈmeri/). Also, you dropped many final consonants, such as the /n/ in "town" and the /d/ and /l/ in "Sunderland." Be careful with your /r/ and /l/ sounds too—you said "led" instead of "red." These errors, along with the "choppiness" (word-by-word speaking), make it harder for the listener to follow you.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>The 'L' and 'R' challenge:</b> Practice words like "red," "lead," "rice," and "lice." Notice where your tongue is! For /l/, the tongue touches the top teeth; for /r/, it pulls back.</li><li><b>Over-enunciate:</b> For now, try to say every final consonant (/n/, /d/, /t/) loudly. It will feel strange, but it will help your listener!</li><li><b>Record your start:</b> Since you are more nervous at the beginning, practice the first 30 seconds of your talk many times until you can say it smoothly.</li></ul><br>You improved as you went! 🎯	Vegas, you used the 'Imagine' hook effectively and described the spooky atmosphere of Silent Hill with some very impressive vocabulary like 'uneventful' and 'dimension.' 👍 To reach a Band 5 (B2), try to reduce the number of hesitations and filler words to make your speech flow more naturally, and work on using correct verb forms without adding extra 'is' helping verbs. 🎯	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1nuXLkyZgiWXUQVFKnklUKyxFV7qAEVJ7?usp=sharing	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
