981	29031	2025-07-24 15:32:06.58681+00	A Place I Love	A place I love is Pattaya which is located around the central south of Thailand. I'm really fonded of this place because of the memories I've made while I was staying there. Pattaya is greatly known for its sandy beach and vivid night life. Furthermore, it's also incredibly popular among tourists who visited Thailand. It's a place that you need to experience yourself at least once.<br><br><br>There are several reasons why Pattaya is packed with tourists almost everyday, and why I personally think that it deserves the hype. Firstly, Pattaya has a great mix of city and nature all together. It's a seaside filled with loads of people enjoying must-do activities, such as skiing, swimming or maybe even go on boat tours. Although it may seem not so relaxing, then I recommend enjoying the sunset at dawn. I have been admiring the scenery ever since, and never got bored of it. Next, the night life is something you can't miss. People never sleeps at night in Pattaya, since they'd be going to the night market to buy the local fresh seafood or they'd be at the bar district that is filled with people everyday. I've been to both of the districts, and I'm sure that you'll regret not visiting both of them.<br><br><br>All in all, Pattaya is a unique and an incredible place to visit during anytime' of the year, especially New Years, since they are extremely precise with the celebration. I've loved going to this place since I was young. Which is why you 'can't miss out.	258	14.0	Overall, Racing, you wrote an interesting article about Pattaya. You provided good details about why it's a popular place. To make your writing even better, try to focus more on your personal feelings and experiences when describing "a place you love." Also, pay close attention to little grammar points like subject-verb agreement and word choice, and make sure your punctuation is just right. Keep up the great work!	Racing did a good job of introducing Pattaya as a place they love and supported it with relevant details like the beach, nightlife, and tourist popularity. Racing also included reasons why they personally like it, such as the mix of city and nature, and specific activities. However, sometimes the essay reads more like a general description of Pattaya for tourists rather than a deep, personal reflection on *why* Racing loves it so much, beyond just the memories.\n\nExample: "Pattaya is greatly known for its sandy beach and vivid night life. Furthermore, it's also incredibly popular among tourists who visited Thailand."\nProblem: This sentence describes Pattaya from a general tourist perspective, not necessarily from a personal one about why *you* love it.\nHow to fix it: You could add more personal feelings or specific experiences that show your love. For example, instead of just saying it's popular among tourists, you might say, "I love seeing how happy the tourists are in Pattaya; their excitement makes the place even more special to me."\n\nExample: "There are several reasons why Pattaya is packed with tourists almost everyday, and why I personally think that it deserves the hype."\nProblem: Again, this sounds like you are trying to convince a general reader about Pattaya's worth, rather than sharing your personal connection and feelings that make it *a place you love*.\nHow to fix it: You could rephrase this to be more about your personal reasons. For example, "I think Pattaya deserves its popularity, and for me, it's special because of [your personal reason]."	The essay communicates straightforward ideas and generally holds the reader's attention. Racing uses an appropriate style for an article, trying to engage the reader with phrases like "It's a place that you need to experience yourself at least once" and "I'm sure that you'll regret not visiting both of them." However, the essay doesn't consistently communicate more complex personal feelings or reflections effectively, often leaning more towards a descriptive, informative tone for a general audience rather than a deeply personal one about "a place I love."\n\nExample: "It's a place that you need to experience yourself at least once."\nProblem: This is a common phrase used to recommend a place to others, but it doesn't clearly show your personal feelings or connection to the place.\nHow to fix it: You could phrase it to show your personal feeling more directly. For example, "It's a place that I feel everyone should experience at least once, because it holds so many special memories for me."\n\nExample: "People never sleeps at night in Pattaya, since they'd be going to the night market to buy the local fresh seafood or they'd be at the bar district that is filled with people everyday."\nProblem: This sentence clearly communicates information, but it doesn't really try to get the reader to feel your personal excitement or connection to the nightlife.\nHow to fix it: You could add a personal touch to make it more engaging. For example, "The nightlife is something you can't miss; I love how the energy of the city never sleeps, whether people are enjoying fresh seafood at the market or the lively atmosphere in the bar district."	The essay is generally well-organised with a clear introduction, body paragraphs that discuss different aspects of Pattaya, and a conclusion. Racing uses a variety of linking words and phrases such as "Furthermore," "Firstly," "Next," and "All in all" to connect ideas. The flow is generally logical and easy to follow.\n\nExample: "Although it may seem not so relaxing, then I recommend enjoying the sunset at dawn. I have been admiring the scenery ever since, and never got bored of it."\nProblem: The word "then" here isn't quite right. It makes the sentence a little confusing.\nHow to fix it: You could remove "then" or use a different linking word. For example, "Although it may seem not so relaxing, I recommend enjoying the sunset at dawn." or "However, if you're looking for relaxation, I recommend enjoying the sunset at dawn."\n\nExample: "All in all, Pattaya is a unique and an incredible place to visit during anytime' of the year, especially New Years, since they are extremely precise with the celebration. I've loved going to this place since I was young. Which is why you 'can't miss out."\nProblem: The punctuation is a little off with the apostrophe in "anytime'" and "Which is why" starts a new sentence without proper connection.\nHow to fix it: You could remove the apostrophe from "anytime" and connect the last two sentences more smoothly. For example, "All in all, Pattaya is a unique and incredible place to visit any time of the year, especially New Years, since they are extremely precise with the celebration; I've loved going to this place since I was young, which is why you can't miss out."	Racing uses a range of everyday vocabulary appropriately, such as "sandy beach," "vivid nightlife," "packed with tourists," "must-do activities," and "fresh seafood." There is also an attempt at some more varied vocabulary like "fonded of" and "deserves the hype," though "fonded of" is not a correct word. The essay uses a range of simple and some complex grammatical forms with a good degree of control. There are occasional errors, but they generally do not impede communication.\n\nExample: "I'm really fonded of this place because of the memories I've made while I was staying there."\nProblem: "Fonded of" is not the correct word. The correct phrase is "fond of."\nHow to fix it: You should say, "I'm really fond of this place..."\n\nExample: "People never sleeps at night in Pattaya, since they'd be going to the night market to buy the local fresh seafood or they'd be at the bar district that is filled with people everyday."\nProblem: "People" is a plural noun, so the verb should be "sleep," not "sleeps." Also, "everyday" should be "every day" when referring to "each day."\nHow to fix it: You should say, "People never sleep at night in Pattaya, since they'd be going to the night market to buy the local fresh seafood or they'd be at the bar district that is filled with people every day."\n\nExample: "I've been to both of the districts, and I'm sure that you'll regret not visiting both of them."\nProblem: While understandable, this sentence is a little strong and could be phrased more smoothly.\nHow to fix it: You could say, "I've been to both districts, and I'm sure you'll be glad you visited them." or "I've visited both districts, and I highly recommend experiencing them."	4.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
