tion head-on by discussing both the good things and the not-so-good things about having pets. You've clearly told your reader what you think, and you even included a lovely personal example about your cats which makes your writing feel very authentic and interesting. 😻 This shows you've understood the task well and have informed your reader thoroughly. To get full marks here, just be sure that every detail you include is perfectly clear and supports your main points directly.	Your article communicates your straightforward ideas about pet ownership quite effectively, Florence. You've clearly stated your opinion, and you've tried to engage the reader with questions at the end. 😊 However, to really grab and hold your reader's attention at this level, think about making your language a little more sophisticated and varied. For example, instead of asking "Do you think is bad or good," you could try, "What is your perspective: do the benefits outweigh the drawbacks, or vice-versa?" This adds a touch more formality and depth, which is what we look for in an article for this exam.	You've organised your ideas in a way that is easy to follow, Florence, starting with an introduction, moving to disadvantages, then advantages, and finishing with a conclusion. This logical structure is a great foundation for your writing. 👍 You've also used some basic linking words to connect your sentences, which helps the article flow. To improve, try using a wider range of linking phrases to make your connections even smoother. For instance, instead of just "But even," you could say "However, despite these drawbacks" or "On the other hand." Practising various transition words will help guide your reader through your thoughts with more elegance.	This is an area where we can really make some progress, Florence. You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately for your topic, which is good. However, there are a number of grammatical points we need to work on. For example, in sentences like "Having pets have many disadvantages," the verb "have" needs to agree with "Having pets" (which is a singular idea), so it should be "Having pets *has* many disadvantages." Also, be careful with articles like "a," "an," and "the." For instance, "want to have a pets" should be "want to have pets" or "want to have a pet." Lastly, watch your verb tenses and forms; "that make me felt so good" should be "that *made* me *feel* so good." Thai learners often find these aspects of English grammar tricky because your language handles subject-verb agreement, articles, and verb tenses differently, so it's a very common hurdle. Regular practice and careful proofreading will really help you overcome these common errors. Keep a notebook of common errors you make and try to correct them in future writing. ✏️	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
2924	29079	2025-12-14 09:35:14.029347+00	Term 2 Midterm M2A Part 2	I looked out of the window and couldn't believe my eyes. I am Ikkyu, a student who very lazy and sleep all the time in the classroom. But one day when I woke up. I saw an big-ugly alien, looked like a human but with a green shave, looked on me. I stunned and screamed but suddenly when my English teacher came in, it was disappeared. "Do you see it?" I asked, "What do you mean you see?" Everyone tooked at me which seemed no one see it. But then it come to the classroom and said, "Happy Halloween, everyone." I stunned. His costume was very real and very scared. Everyone clapped their hands.	114	14.0	Hello Ikkyu! 👋<br><br>This was a very funny and surprising story! 👽 I loved the twist about the alien actually being your English teacher for Halloween. You did a great job building up the mystery and then delivering a clever punchline.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you make your stories even more engaging! 📋<br>1. <b>Paragraphs!</b> 📝 Breaking your story into smaller paragraphs makes it much easier to read and follow the action.<br>2. <b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ Watch out for confusing phrases like 'green shave' (did you mean 'green skin' or 'green face'?).<br>3. <b>Verb Forms!</b> 🛠️ Remember 'disappeared' (not 'was disappeared') and 'looked' (not 'tooked').	You started your story perfectly with the given sentence and developed a very creative plot! 🌟 The Halloween twist was excellent and made the story very enjoyable.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Pacing the Reveal!</b> 🎭 The reveal that it's a costume could be even more impactful if the teacher's arrival and the alien's disappearance were described with a bit more tension, before the final funny reveal.</li>	You successfully communicated a simple narrative with a clear twist. ✅ Your story is very easy to understand.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Emotional Arc!</b> 🎢 While the twist is great, the sudden shift from fear ('stunned and screamed') to the teacher's entry and then clapping feels a bit abrupt. Try to smooth out these emotional changes for the reader.</li>	You used linking words like 'But one day' and 'But then' to connect your ideas. 🔗<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Paragraphs!</b> 🧱 The story is currently one long block of text. Breaking it into paragraphs (e.g., one for seeing the alien, one for the teacher's arrival, one for the reveal) would greatly improve readability.</li><li><b>Sentence Structure!</b> 🏗️ Some sentences are quite long. Breaking them into shorter, clearer sentences would enhance the flow.</li>	You used good everyday vocabulary like 'alien', 'costume', and 'screamed'. 📚<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Green shave' is unclear. Did you mean 'green skin' or 'green face'?</li><li><b>Passive Voice!</b> 🛠️ 'It was disappeared' should just be 'It <b>disappeared</b>'.</li><li><b>Past Tense!</b> ⏳ 'Tooked at me' should be '<b>looked</b> at me'.</li><li><b>Adjectives!</b> 🅰️ 'Very scared' should be 'very <b>scary</b>' when describing the costume.</li>	5.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
