e /s/ in "ghosts" /ɡəʊsts/ and the /l/ in "canal" /kəˈnæl/. These final sounds are very important for clarity.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Focus on Word Endings:</b> Try to slow down slightly at the end of words to make sure you fully pronounce every consonant, especially in clusters like /sts/.</li><li><b>Final Consonant Drills:</b> Practice lists of words that end in /s/, /l/, /t/, and /d/ to build the habit of finishing every word completely.</li><li><b>Exaggerate for Practice:</b> When you practice at home, try to exaggerate the final sounds a little bit more than usual to help your mouth get used to them.</li></ul><br>Your flow is already developing nicely, so keep working on those final sounds to become even easier to understand! 🚀	Ant, you told the story of Mae Nak very effectively and kept the listener interested throughout! Your use of connecting words like "suddenly" and "after that" helped the plot flow naturally and logically. You also used some great vocabulary related to the story like "pregnant" and "monk." To improve, try to be more consistent with your past tense verbs (like using "died" instead of "was die") and work on using a wider variety of transition words instead of repeating "so." Your confidence is growing and you are doing a great job communicating your ideas clearly. 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
