early!<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You have a lovely natural intonation that makes your speech very pleasant to listen to. I also noticed that you attempted to use linkages between words, which is a fantastic skill to develop for more fluent speaking.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>I noticed a repetitive habit of overstressing final syllables in words like "character" /ˈkærəktə/ and "story" /ˈstɔːri/, which can be a bit intrusive for the listener. There were also a few moments where /v/ sounds were pronounced as /w/, such as saying "wery" instead of "very" /ˈveri/.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Lighten Word Endings:</b> Practice saying words like "character" and focus on making the last sound very soft and quick.</li><li><b>Master the /V/ Sound:</b> Practice the /v/ sound by biting your bottom lip slightly with your top teeth to ensure it doesn't sound like a /w/.</li><li><b>Shadowing Practice:</b> Try "shadowing" (repeating after) a native speaker to match their rhythm and see how they handle final syllables.</li></ul><br>Your intonation is a real strength, so keep working on these technical details to reach the next level! ✨	Naenae, this was a strong book review that showed your ability to tell a story and express opinions clearly. You used great connectors like "suddenly" and "even though," and your structure was very logical. To reach the next level, focus on small grammar details like subject-verb agreement and try to reduce the number of pauses during your speech. Don't forget to include the author next time, but your review was very engaging and easy to follow! 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
2926	28887	2025-12-14 09:35:14.23633+00	Term 2 Midterm M2A Part 2	I do prefer shopping online because It's convinient and sometimes It's cheaper. Shopping online a lot because you can find things that you want easily. Shopping online stores sometimes I can't find what I want and waste my just for one thing and I get lost pretty easily to.<br><br>The best that I ever bought is a badmintan rocket ASTROX 99 Gen 3 pro. It's super rare and use it everytime one of my best racket that I ever bought. There are a lots of better rackets but finding one that suits your play style is very hard to find that's why it's my best thing that ever bought.	109	16.0	Hello Leo! 👋<br><br>You wrote a very practical and focused article about your shopping habits and an impressive best purchase! 🏸 Your reasons for online shopping are clear, and a 'badmintan rocket ASTROX 99 Gen 3 pro' sounds like a serious piece of equipment!<br><br>I have a few tips to make your writing even more powerful! 📋<br>1. <b>Word Forms!</b> 🗣️ 'Badmintan rocket' should be 'badminton <b>racket</b>'.<br>2. <b>Punctuation!</b> 🛑 You have some long sentences that need full stops. Break them up!<br>3. <b>Elaborate!</b> 📝 Tell us more about *why* that specific racket suits your play style so well.	You fully addressed both parts of the task with clear reasoning and a specific example for your best purchase. ✅ Your explanation for choosing a racket is well-detailed.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Online Cons!</b> 🛒 You mentioned getting lost easily in stores. Could you also add one small disadvantage of online shopping to balance your argument?</li><li><b>Racket Choice!</b> 🏸 You said 'finding one that suits your play style is very hard'. Could you briefly explain what your play style is and how this racket helps it?</li>	You clearly communicate your preferences and reasons. 👍<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Opening/Closing!</b> 🎣 Add a short introductory and concluding sentence to frame your article more formally.</li>	Your paragraph break clearly separates your shopping habits from your best purchase, which is great. 🧱<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Sentence Flow!</b> 🌊 Some of your sentences flow into each other without clear punctuation. Use periods (.) to separate complete thoughts.</li><li><b>Linkers!</b> 🔗 'Shopping online a lot because you can find...' is a bit direct. Try starting with 'I shop online a lot because...'</li>	You used good technical vocabulary like 'convenient', 'rare', and 'suits your play style'. 📚<br><br>Here are some corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Badmintan rocket' -> 'badminton <b>racket</b>'.</li><li><b>Homophones!</b> 🗣️ 'Get lost pretty easily to' -> 'get lost pretty easily <b>too</b>'.</li><li><b>Sentence Structure!</b> 🏗️ 'Shopping online stores sometimes I can't find...' -> 'Sometimes, in online stores, I can't find...'.</li><li><b>Agreement!</b> ⚙️ 'There are a lots of better rackets' -> 'There <b>are many</b> better rackets' or 'There <b>are a lot</b> of better rackets'.</li>	5.0	3.0	4.0	4.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
2940	30968	2025-12-14 09:35:16.614087+00	Term 2 Midterm M2A Part 2	I looked out of the window and couldn't believe my eyes.<br><br>At the beginning I just a college student that love to have a insect experiment so I try to make a vacine that can make insect even bigger to make my self easier to do a research of it. few month pass away but I still couldn't make it. so I gave up. but a prototype vacine that I press it on desk it fall and crash in piece, so I clean it but I doesn't realize that there a little ant over there. in the next morning when I wake up and go to open a window like usual I saw a giant ant that size similiar to a building overthere I couldn't believe my eyes and I really panic. I bring back my sense and try to do something but it too late the ant target is change to my apartment it walk so fast and went the ant try to ate me, I just woke up and realize that it was a dream I walk to the window and nothing is strange is usual.	188	14.0	Hello Sun! 👋<br><br>This is a wonderfully imaginative and terrifying story about a giant ant! 🐜 Your idea of a vaccine experiment going wrong and creating a building-sized insect is very creative and perfectly fits a sci-fi horror theme. The detail about the prototype crashing was a great touch.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you perfect this monstrous tale! 📋<br>1. <b>Paragraphs and Punctuation!</b> 📝 Your story is one long sentence! Breaking it into clear sentences and paragraphs would make it much easier to read and create more impact.<br>2. <b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Crash in piece' should be 'crash in <b>pieces</b>'. 'Ate me' should be '<b>eat</b> me'.<br>3. <b>Clarity!</b> 💡 'A prototype vacine that I press it on desk it fall and crash...' is quite long. Break it down!	You created a very original and compelling plot about a scientific experiment gone wrong. 🌟 The scale of the giant ant was well-conveyed, making the threat feel immediate and real.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Pacing the Experiment!</b> 🧪 You mentioned 'few month pass away'. Expanding a little on your efforts during that time, or the moment of giving up, could add more depth to your character's journey.</li><li><b>The Ant's Appearance!</b> 🐜 You said it was 'giant'. Could you add a few more details? What color was it? How many legs did it have? Was it hairy?</li>	You successfully communicated a very creative and frightening scenario. 😱 The sudden realization that it was a dream provided a clever twist.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Readability!</b> 💡 The lack of punctuation and paragraph breaks makes your story very difficult to read. Focusing on these basic structures will allow your excellent ideas to shine through much more clearly.</li>	You have a clear chronological sequence of events, from the experiment to the giant ant and the dream twist. ⏳<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Paragraphing is CRITICAL!</b> 🧱 Break your story into paragraphs each time the scene or main idea changes (e.g., experiment, discovery, panic, dream).</li><li><b>Sentence Breaks!</b> 🛑 Use full stops (.) to separate your sentences. This will make your story much, much easier to read and understand.</li>	You used good scientific vocabulary like 'experiment', 'vaccine', and 'prototype'. 📚<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Punctuation!</b> 🛑 Your story is currently one very long sentence. You need to use full stops (.) to end sentences.</li><li><b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Crash in piece' -> 'crash in <b>pieces</b>'.</li><li><b>Infinitives!</b> 🛠️ 'Try to do something but it too late the ant target is change to my apartment it walk so fast and went the ant try to ate me' -> 'try to do something but it was too late. The ant's target changed to my apartment. It walked so fast and when the ant tried to <b>eat</b> me'.</li><li><b>Verb Forms!</b> ⏳ 'I press it on desk it fall' -> 'I <b>pressed</b> it on the desk. It <b>fell</b>'.</li>	5.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
971	34043	2025-07-24 07:53:55.349292+00	A Place I Love	Hi everyone! Today I want to introduce you guys about the city that is have a very unique characters, Seoul. Their food, traditional and history is very iconic and don't forget about the most famous part of culture of Korea which is entertainment K-pop and K-drama.<br><br><br>We will begin with the history of Seoul it have a very very interesting story of history. First they had create there own Korean letter called Hanguel. They also had Korean war between North Korea and South Korea it had a lot of violence and many people died. Nowadays, Korea still seperated which is north Korea and south Korea at 38th parallel. This is a very short timeline about history of Korea. Next traditional Korea foods. I'm really like one dish of Korea food is called Tteokbokki I thought that everyone may know it because it is very famous in my country. Tteokbokki have a very unique tastes and texture is made from rice cake so it have a very chewy texture but it not have flavor so they put sauce in it and make it have full of flavored. Tteokbokki tasted a bit of spicy and sweet sometimes they're putting rice fish cake, cheese, cheese to make it more delicious delicious and more of nutrient. And I hope that you can go to Lotte world once in your life I have been there when I was a kid is very beautiful and fun so don't mis it you really should go there and give it a try. Also with entertainment of Korean you know it right? like K-pop and K-drama if you have watched some K-dramas I bet you know about Han-river. Han-river is very beautiful and have a fantastic vibe like it romantic, relaxing, calm and refreshing.<br><br><br>In conclution	297	8.0	Kathi, you started to write about Seoul, highlighting its food, traditions, history, and entertainment like K-pop and K-drama, which are all great ideas. You gave some good examples like Tteokbokki and Han River. To make your writing much stronger, you need to remember to write a complete conclusion for your article. Also, when you write a lot, it's very important to use separate paragraphs for each main idea. This will make your fantastic information much clearer for your readers. You should also work on making your language more formal for a website and correcting your grammar, spelling, and how you use connecting words.	Kathi, you introduced Seoul by mentioning its unique characteristics like food, traditions, history, and K-pop/K-drama. You gave several specific examples, which is good. However, your essay tried to cover too many topics in a short space, so each point felt very brief and wasn't fully developed. Also, your essay ended suddenly without a complete conclusion, which means the task was not fully completed.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "We will begin with the history of Seoul it have a very very interesting story of history. First they had create there own Korean letter called Hanguel. They also had Korean war between North Korea and South Korea it had a lot of violence and many people died. Nowadays, Korea still seperated which is north Korea and south Korea at 38th parallel. This is a very short timeline about history of Korea."\n    Problem: You included many historical facts in a very brief way. While interesting, for an article about a place you *love*, these facts don't really explain *why* they make you love Seoul or how they connect to your personal experience.\n    How to fix it: Choose only the most important historical facts that truly connect to your personal feelings about Seoul. Instead of just listing dates or events, explain *how* that history makes Seoul special to you. For example, "Seoul's rich history is fascinating; learning about the creation of Hangul makes me appreciate the unique culture of Korea even more."\n\n*   Example: Your essay ended with "In conclution" without a full conclusion paragraph.\n    Problem: A complete essay needs a conclusion to summarize your main points and give the reader a final thought. Ending abruptly makes your article feel unfinished.\n    How to fix it: Always plan to write a full conclusion. In it, you should briefly summarize why Seoul is a place you love and perhaps invite the reader to visit. For example, "In conclusion, Seoul offers a unique blend of rich history, delicious food, and vibrant entertainment that makes it a truly special place for me. I hope you consider visiting this dynamic city soon!"	You tried to introduce Seoul and encourage readers to visit. However, your language was very informal and conversational, which is not usually suitable for an article on a travel website. Many errors and awkward phrases made it hard for your ideas to be clearly understood and to keep the reader interested throughout the article.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "Hi everyone! Today I want to introduce you guys about the city that is have a very unique characters, Seoul."\n    Problem: This opening is very informal ("Hi everyone!", "you guys") and has grammatical errors ("that is have"). It doesn't set a professional tone for a travel article.\n    How to fix it: Use more formal and appropriate language for an article. For example, "Seoul is a city with a truly unique character that I would love to introduce to you."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Also with entertainment of Korean you know it right? like K-pop and K-drama if you have watched some K-dramas I bet you know about Han-river."\n    Problem: This section is very informal and sounds like you are talking directly to a friend ("you know it right?", "I bet you know").\n    How to fix it: Remove these informal conversational phrases. For instance, "Korean entertainment, like K-pop and K-drama, is also a significant part of its culture. If you have watched any K-dramas, you are likely familiar with the beautiful Han River."	You made an attempt to organize your essay by talking about different topics like history, food, and entertainment. However, your main body is written as one very long and dense paragraph, which includes too many different ideas. **It's perfectly fine to write a longer essay if you have a lot to say, but if you do, you must divide your ideas into separate, focused paragraphs.** This will make your article much clearer and easier for the reader to follow. The abrupt ending without a conclusion also made your essay feel unorganized.\n\n*   Example: Your second paragraph covers history (Hangul, Korean War), food (Tteokbokki description), Lotte World, and then jumps to K-pop/K-drama and Han River.\n    Problem: This single, very long paragraph is overwhelming and makes it hard to distinguish between your different points. The transitions are not smooth.\n    How to fix it: **Break this long paragraph into several shorter paragraphs.** For example:\n    *   One paragraph dedicated to Seoul's history.\n    *   A separate paragraph for Korean food, focusing on Tteokbokki.\n    *   Another paragraph for attractions like Lotte World.\n    *   And perhaps another for the entertainment culture and Han River.\n    This creates a logical flow and makes your ideas much easier to understand.\n\n*   Example: You used "First" and "Next" as transitions.\n    Problem: While these are transitions, they are very basic and can make your writing sound like a list.\n    How to fix it: Use a wider variety of more sophisticated transition words and phrases to connect your ideas smoothly. For example, instead of "Next traditional Korea foods," you could say, "Moving on to traditional Korean foods..." or "Another highlight of Seoul is its delicious cuisine."	You used some everyday vocabulary to describe Seoul and its features. You also tried to use some slightly more complex sentences, but there were many very noticeable and frequent mistakes in spelling, grammar (including verb forms, subject-verb agreement, and sentence structure), and punctuation. These errors often made it difficult to understand your intended meaning. Your vocabulary was also quite limited in places, leading to repetition.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "characters," "traditional," "conclution," "seperated," "faublous," "mis," "nutritent," "Han-river"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled or incorrect. They should be "characteristics," "traditions," "conclusion," "separated," "fabulous," "miss," "nutrient," "Han River" (Han should be capitalized, river is common noun). "Their" for "Their food" should be "Its food."\n    How to fix it: Proofread your writing very carefully for spelling errors. Use a dictionary or spell checker. Be mindful of words that look or sound similar but have different meanings or spellings.\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement, Verb Forms, and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "the city that **is have** a very unique characters," "it **have** a very very interesting story," "they had **create**," "Korea still **seperated**," "Tteokbokki **have** a very unique tastes," "it **not have** flavor," "they're **putting** rice fish cake" (should be "they put").\n    Problem: You consistently used incorrect verb forms (e.g., "is have" should be "has," "it have" should be "it has," "had create" should be "created"). There are issues with subject-verb agreement and sentence structure throughout, making many sentences ungrammatical and hard to follow.\n    How to fix it: Pay close attention to subject-verb agreement (singular subjects like "city" and "it" need "has," not "have"). Practice using correct past tense forms of verbs. Focus on building clear, complete sentences with a subject and a verb.\n\n*   Punctuation and Capitalization:\n    Example: "Seoul it have a very very interesting story of history. First they had create there own Korean letter called Hanguel." (run-on sentences), "This is a very short timeline about history of Korea. Next traditional Korea foods." (no period after Korea).\n    Problem: You often string sentences together without proper punctuation like periods or commas. Capitalization for "Korean" and "Han River" is inconsistent.\n    How to fix it: Use periods to clearly separate your sentences. Capitalize all proper nouns (names of specific languages, places, or rivers, e.g., "Korean," "Han River").	3.0	2.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
