 can get stronger.<br><br>	355	12.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	Dragon, it's great that you are mostly understandable, and I can generally follow what you are saying. However, you have a very soft and flat speaking voice, and sometimes it's a little tricky to hear the ends of your words, which often affects clarity. This is frequently because, in Thai, consonant sounds at the end of words can be less pronounced or even dropped, but in English, they are really important for clarity. For example, when you say words like 'restaurant' or 'weekend', try to make sure those final /t/ and /d/ sounds are clear. You also have a tendency to swallow consonants, which means they aren't fully articulated. For instance, in words like 'playing' or 'something', make sure the /ŋ/ sound is distinct. These habits can sometimes make your speech sound a bit 'staccato' or abrupt. A little practice with pronouncing the final sounds of words and speaking with a bit more volume will make a big difference to how clear and fluent you sound! 🎤	Hello Dragon! 👋 It was a real pleasure to listen to you speak in your English exam. You did a good job of communicating your ideas clearly and participating in the discussions, showing some good progress in your speaking abilities! To make your English even stronger, let's focus on making your voice a bit clearer and ensuring your speech flows even more smoothly. Keep up the great effort! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>✔️ **Speak with clarity:** Practice speaking a little louder and make a conscious effort to pronounce the consonant sounds at the ends of your words. Reading English stories or articles aloud every day can really help you develop this habit! 🗣️<br>✔️ **Expand your linking words:** When connecting your ideas, try to use a wider range of words and phrases beyond 'and' or 'because'. Think about using words like 'however', 'although', 'therefore', or 'in addition' to build more sophisticated sentences. 