g. Like this book give me a heart attack and a jaw-drop. And I couldn't breathe properly. And um, but however what I found it a bit violence, but of course it's a crime thriller book. It... it can't avoid being violence, right? And so overall uh, if you like crime thriller book with a heartbreaking plot twist, I recommend this book. You won't be disappointed and I hope you guys give it a try. Thank you for all your attention.	424	17.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	PET	Venice, your speaking volume was excellent today and your intonation was very engaging. You made a strong attempt to link your words together, which helped the overall flow of your talk. It was clear that you had practiced your transitions thoroughly.<br><br><b>What You Did Well:</b><br>You successfully avoided common Thai L1 patterns like overstressing final syllables, which made your speech sound very natural. Your use of intonation helped to highlight the drama and excitement in the story you were telling.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b><br>Your main challenge today was with consonant clusters and final sounds, which were systematically dropped. This was noticeable in words like "criminal" (/ˈkrɪmɪnəl/), "got" (/ɡɒt/), "call" (/kɔːl/), and the /ks/ sound in "crucifix" (/ˈkruːsəfɪks/).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Master Consonant Clusters</b>: Practice saying words with multiple consonants together, like /pr/ in "April" or /ks/ in "crucifix" (/ˈkruːsəfɪks/).</li><li><b>Pronounce Final /t/ and /l/</b>: Focus on finishing words like "got" (/ɡɒt/) and "call" (/kɔːl/) with their correct ending sounds.</li><li><b>Add More Heart to Speech</b>: Continue practicing your natural intonation to ensure your audience feels the emotion in your book reviews.</li></ul><br>Your natural rhythm is a huge asset to your speaking! 🌟	Venice, this was a brilliant book review that really captured the excitement of a thriller! You used impressive vocabulary like psychologist and autopsy and followed the required structure perfectly, including a great hook and clear exposition. Your grammar was strong, and you used helpful transition words to connect your story points logically. To improve even more, focus on using adjective forms like violent instead of nouns and try to vary your sentence starters so you do not rely on the word so too much. You explained the plot, the climax, and your opinion with great clarity and confidence. Excellent work on this speaking task! 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1nuXLkyZgiWXUQVFKnklUKyxFV7qAEVJ7?usp=sharing	B2	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
