u have some genuinely interesting ideas about the bond between humans and animals. To make your writing even clearer and more engaging, especially for a magazine article, focusing on structuring your arguments and refining your grammar will be a fantastic step forward. Keep up the great work! <br><br>Action Plan 📋<br>✅ Plan your article with clear paragraphs for each main idea (introduction, advantages, disadvantages, conclusion).<br>✅ Proofread carefully for common grammar errors like subject-verb agreement and pluralisation.<br>✅ Focus on writing to the suggested word count, aiming for quality over quantity in your explanations.	You’ve got some lovely thoughts about why pets are so important, like how they teach us responsibility and can be wonderful companions. It's great that you mentioned both the good parts and some of the harder parts, like the time and money involved. For instance, when you wrote 'Pets also heal your lonelyness,' that's a really good point! However, to make your article even clearer for a reader, try to present your arguments more directly. Instead of including all your thoughts in one big section, you could have a dedicated paragraph for advantages and another for disadvantages, with clear examples for each. This helps the reader follow your ideas easily. Also, remember that a good article should be concise; while it's great to have many ideas, choosing the strongest ones and explaining them thoroughly within the word limit is key. 🐾	You’re starting to get the hang of writing an article, especially by sharing your personal opinion on pets. It's clear you want to connect with your audience. For example, your personal story about your cat helping with loneliness is very relatable! To really draw in a teenage audience for an international magazine, think about how to keep their attention from start to finish. You could try using a catchy title, and then making sure each paragraph has a strong topic sentence that tells the reader what it will be about. This helps make sure your interesting ideas don't get lost, and your article flows smoothly from one point to the next.	You’ve made a start at connecting your ideas, which is a good foundation. However, to make your article much easier for your readers to follow, it's really helpful to organise your thoughts into distinct paragraphs. Right now, your article tends to jump between ideas, which can be a bit tricky for the reader to keep up with. For instance, you could group all your 'advantages' together in one paragraph and all your 'disadvantages' in another, using linking words like 'Firstly,' 'Additionally,' 'On the other hand,' or 'However,' to guide the reader. A clear introduction and a concluding paragraph that summarises your main points would also make a big difference, giving your article a strong, coherent structure.	You use a lot of everyday words that are appropriate for talking about pets, which is excellent! However, there are a few areas in your grammar that we can work on to make your meaning clearer. Let's look at three important points: <br><br>1. **Plurals and spelling:** Sometimes you forget to add 's' for plural nouns, or misspell common words. For example, you wrote 'Having Pets teaches us to have maner and responsibility' where 'Pets' should be 'pets' (unless it's a specific name) and 'maner' should be 'manners', and 'lonelyness' should be 'loneliness'. Another example is 'confable', which should be 'comfortable'. Practising your plurals and regularly checking your spelling will make your writing much more accurate. <br>2. **Subject-Verb Agreement:** There are times when your verbs don't quite match your subjects. For instance, 'Pet also heal your lonelyness' should be 'Pets also *heal* your loneliness' if you're talking about multiple pets, or 'A pet also *heals* your loneliness' if you're talking about one. Also, 'she are playful' should be 'she *is* playful'. Paying attention to whether your subject is singular or plural will help you choose the correct verb form. <br>3. **Sentence Structure and Clarity:** Some of your sentences are quite long and combine many ideas, which can make them hard to understand. For example, 'Pets are more unlike humans, people day are selfish, from my point of view, I think it is true, which was sad tho, unlike pets that protect us from Intruders during night, they would sacrifice their life to owner hoping to repay his Emphaty and kindness it just being true for example.' This sentence tries to do too much! Breaking it into shorter, clearer sentences would help your reader follow your thoughts. For example, you could say: 'Pets are often quite different from humans. I believe people today can be selfish, which is sad. Pets, however, protect us from intruders during the night. They might even sacrifice their lives for their owners, hoping to repay their empathy and kindness.' Thai learners sometimes combine many ideas into one sentence in English, similar to how they might structure sentences in Thai. Thinking about one clear idea per sentence in English can help with clarity.	3.0	2.0	1.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
