the next. Also, paying attention to some grammar points will really help your ideas shine! You have a great understanding of the topic, so keep practicing these fundamental areas.	Kaohom L, you have effectively addressed the task by describing Chiang Rai as a place you love and providing several reasons for this, covering nature, architecture, food (Kao Soi), and art (Baan Dum museum). You clearly convey why the mix of city and nature, along with the kind people, makes it special to you. The target reader is generally well-informed. To achieve a score of 5, you could develop some points further with more specific examples or sensory details, ensuring all content is fully elaborated to fully inform the target reader.	You attempt to use the conventions of an essay with a clear introduction and conclusion. You communicate straightforward ideas, and while your enthusiasm for Chiang Rai is clear, the overall effectiveness in holding the target reader's attention is hampered by the numerous language errors. The use of phrases like \\"trust me this is so delicious\\" is slightly informal for an essay, and combined with the intrusive spelling mistakes, it affects the overall communicative impact. To improve to a 5, you should consistently use conventions of an essay, maintaining a more formal and objective tone, and communicate both straightforward and complex ideas in a way that is consistently engaging and appropriate for the task, with minimal distracting errors.	Your essay is generally well organised with a clear opening, body, and conclusion. You use some linking words like \\"Another thing that make me,\\" \\"Fristly,\\" \\"Also,\\" \\"However,\\" and \\"There are several reason why I love this city.\\" However, there are some sentences that feel a bit disconnected within paragraphs, and the overall flow could be smoother. For example, the transition between discussing food/art and then nature within the second paragraph could be more fluid. To achieve a score of 5, you should use a wider variety of cohesive devices and organisational patterns more consistently to ensure the text is highly coherent and easy to follow throughout.	You use everyday vocabulary generally appropriately, relevant to the topic of travel and places (e.g., \\"traditional,\\" \\"nature,\\" \\"relax,\\" \\"signature food,\\" \\"delicious,\\" \\"fantastic,\\" \\"museum,\\" \\"mountains,\\" \\"friendly people\\"). You use simple grammatical forms with a good degree of control. However, errors are noticeable and often impede communication, particularly due to frequent and intrusive spelling mistakes. Specific examples include: \\"it a city\\" (should be \\"it is a city\\"), \\"algritechtures\\" (architecture, repeated), \\"fristly\\" (firstly), \\"recomend\\" (recommend), \\"coco nut\\" (coconut), \\"which is call\\" (which is called), \\"I was really impress\\" (I was really impressed), \\"it not just city\\" (it is not just a city), \\"suround\\" (surrounded), \\"Every times\\" (Every time), \\"possitive\\" (positive), \\"sooting\\" (soothing), and \\"thrue\\" (through\\"). These spelling errors, along with other grammatical inaccuracies, make the text harder to understand at times. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to demonstrate a higher level of grammatical control and accuracy, including very few, if any, spelling errors, and use a wider range of vocabulary with greater precision.	4.0	2.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
969	35239	2025-07-23 15:42:12.511158+00	A Place I Love	There are many wonderful places around the world such as Bangkok, Rome or Las Vegas, but my absolute favourite is Paris, France. Paris a major European city in France, renowed for its art, architecture, fashion and gastronomy. Paris is known as the "City of lights". Everytime I think about the time I was in Paris I think about the wonderful architecture and how they light up the city.<br><br><br>One of the reasons why I love Paris is because of its history and architecture. Paris has so many jaw dropping angelical buildings like the Louvre, the Eiffle tower or the palace of Vesaille. Each of these buildings are like a reflection of history, they blend the old with the new in a way that makes us feel like we're walking back in time. Alongside this, Paris is the fashion capital of the world, home to many fashion houses like Chanel, Louis Vuitton and Dior. Which are all my all time favourite designer brands. Which is why Paris stands out to me. Parisians are also very fashionable people, whether if its Paris fashion week or just an ordinary day.<br><br><br>In conclusion, Paris is a wonderful place to visit, with so much you can do, so many places and landmarks you can visit I urge you to go visit. Its more magical than you could ever imagine<br><br><br>	224	16.0	Ana, you wrote a wonderful article about Paris, a city you truly love. You beautifully described its famous art, architecture, fashion, and food, giving excellent examples like the Louvre and various fashion houses. Your writing is engaging and you clearly explained why Paris is so special to you. Your essay was a bit longer than the suggested word count, which is fine, but when you write more, it's very important to use separate paragraphs for your different ideas. This will make your fantastic writing even easier for readers to follow. You can also work on a few small grammar points to make your sentences flow perfectly.	Ana, you clearly introduced Paris as your favorite place and successfully supported this with its renowned art, architecture, fashion, and gastronomy. Your specific examples, like the Louvre, Eiffel Tower, and famous fashion houses, were excellent and helped explain your personal connection to them. You did a great job of informing the reader about why Paris holds such a special place for you. However, you could make some improvements.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Paris a major European city in France, renowed for its art, architecture, fashion and gastronomy. Paris is known as the "City of lights". Everytime I think about the time I was in Paris I think about the wonderful architecture and how they light up the city."\n    Problem: This sentence is a bit long and combines several facts about Paris.\n    How to fix it: You could break this into slightly shorter sentences to make it easier to read. For example, "Paris, a major European city in France, is renowned for its art, architecture, fashion, and gastronomy. It's also famously known as the "City of Lights." Every time I recall my visit to Paris, I remember the wonderful architecture and how beautifully the city is illuminated at night."\n\n*   Example: In your conclusion, you said, "with so much you can do, so many places and landmarks you can visit I urge you to go visit."\n    Problem: This part is a bit general and repeats the idea of "so much to do/see." While it's true, it could be phrased more powerfully.\n    How to fix it: To make your conclusion even stronger, you could be more specific or more evocative. For instance, "In conclusion, Paris is a truly wonderful place, offering countless enchanting experiences and iconic landmarks. I strongly encourage you to visit; its magic is beyond imagination."	You effectively communicated your ideas about Paris and did a good job of holding the reader's attention with engaging language, especially when you described the "City of lights" and the "jaw dropping angelical buildings." Your style was appropriate for a travel article, and while there are minor issues with sentence structure, the overall message is clear and persuasive.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Each of these buildings are like a reflection of history, they blend the old with the new in a way that makes us feel like we're walking back in time."\n    Problem: The phrasing "Each of these buildings *are* like" has a small agreement error (should be "is"). Also, "like a reflection of history" could be slightly more impactful.\n    How to fix it: Pay attention to subject-verb agreement. You could say, "Each of these buildings *is* a reflection of history, blending the old with the new in a way that truly transports us back in time."\n\n*   Example: You used "Which are all my all time favourite designer brands. Which is why Paris stands out to me."\n    Problem: Starting sentences with "Which" can sometimes create sentence fragments or sound a bit informal, even if it creates a "percussive effect" for emphasis.\n    How to fix it: While this can be a stylistic choice, it's often safer in formal writing to connect these ideas differently. You could combine the sentences: "These are all my all-time favorite designer brands, which is precisely why Paris stands out to me." or "These are all my all-time favorite designer brands. This is a key reason why Paris stands out to me."	You used a clear three-paragraph structure with an introduction, a body, and a conclusion. You also used a good variety of linking words and phrases like "One of the reasons why" and "Alongside this" to connect your ideas. This made your essay generally well-organized and coherent. Your essay was a bit longer than the suggested word count, which is fine, but when you write more, it's very important to divide your ideas into separate paragraphs to help the reader follow along more easily.\n\n*   Example: Your second paragraph is quite long and covers two distinct main ideas: history/architecture and fashion.\n    Problem: While the ideas are related to Paris, combining them into one very long paragraph can make it harder for the reader to digest all the information.\n    How to fix it: When your essay is longer, consider breaking down your main body into more focused paragraphs. For example, you could have one paragraph dedicated to the history and architecture, and then start a new paragraph for the topic of fashion. This creates clearer sections for the reader.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Parisians are also very fashionable people, whether if its Paris fashion week or just an ordinary day."\n    Problem: This sentence introduces a slightly different idea (Parisians' fashion) without a smooth connection to the previous point about fashion houses.\n    How to fix it: You could either integrate this more smoothly into the fashion paragraph or consider it a minor detail. If you keep it, you could connect it by saying, "It's not just the fashion houses that make Paris stand out; Parisians themselves are incredibly fashionable, whether during Paris Fashion Week or on an ordinary day."	You used a good range of vocabulary, including some more advanced words like "renowned," "gastronomy," and "angelical," which were used appropriately. You also attempted to use a range of simple and complex grammatical forms, showing good control and flexibility in many parts of your writing. While there were occasional errors, they generally did not stop the reader from understanding your message.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "renowed," "Eiffle tower," "palace of Vesaille"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled. They should be "renowned," "Eiffel Tower," and "Palace of Versailles."\n    How to fix it: Always carefully proofread your work for spelling errors. Pay special attention to proper nouns, as they are often unique.\n\n*   Grammar - Word Choice and Missing Words:\n    Example: "Paris a major European city" (missing "is"), "Each of these buildings are like" (agreement error), "whether if its Paris fashion week" ("its" vs "it's").\n    Problem: You are missing a verb in the first example, have a subject-verb agreement error in the second, and a common confusion between "its" (possessive) and "it's" (it is) in the third.\n    How to fix it: Pay attention to basic sentence structure (Subject + Verb). Ensure your verb agrees with your subject (singular subject needs a singular verb, plural subject needs a plural verb). Remember that "its" shows possession (e.g., "The city and *its* lights"), while "it's" is a contraction for "it is" (e.g., "It's a beautiful day").	4.0	4.0	4.0	4.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2+	\N	\N	\N	B2+	2026
