p your ideas flow better. Words like "firstly," "additionally," "however," "on the other hand," and "in conclusion" can make a big difference!<br>✔️ **Proofread carefully for common errors:** After you finish writing, read through your work slowly, specifically looking for common spelling mistakes and checking that your sentences make complete sense. Reading aloud can often help you spot these errors.	You've done a good job of starting to discuss the advantages and disadvantages of having pets, Kun, and you've clearly stated your opinion, which is a key part of writing an article! For example, you mentioned "being a friend" and "house keeping" as advantages. To really get a score of 5 here, you could try to explain these points more fully. What do you mean by "house keeping"? Does a pet help keep the house tidy, or does it mean something else? 😊 Also, when you talk about "the right places," it would be helpful to give more details. For instance, you could say, "If you live in a small apartment, a large dog might not be comfortable, whereas a cat would be a good fit." This helps your reader understand your points completely and makes your argument much stronger.	You've made a great effort to connect with your readers right from the beginning, Kun, which is perfect for an article! Starting with "I'll tell you my opinions which, maybe will help you decide" is a friendly way to invite them in. To make your article even more engaging and effective for a teenage magazine, try to ensure your introduction clearly sets the stage for what's to come, and your conclusion wraps up your thoughts. For instance, you could have a strong opening sentence that grabs attention, like "Ever wondered if a furry friend is right for you?" 🤔 And for your conclusion, aim to summarise your main points and offer a final thought or recommendation, rather than adding a new, unrelated sentence like "how to organize everthing." Thinking about your target audience – teenagers – and how to keep them interested with clear, well-organised ideas will help you communicate even more effectively.	You've used paragraph breaks, Kun, which is a good start for organising your thoughts! To improve this area and work towards a higher score, try to make sure each paragraph focuses on one main idea and that these ideas are logically connected. For example, your introduction is a bit separated from your main opinion. You could combine these, and then have distinct paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages. ✍️ Also, using a wider variety of linking words would help your ideas flow much more smoothly. Instead of jumping directly from "advantages" to "disadvantages," you could use phrases like "On the other hand," or "However, it's also important to consider the drawbacks." This makes your writing much easier to follow for your reader. Sometimes, in Thai, ideas can be linked more implicitly, but in English, using explicit linking words helps create a clear path for the reader.	You're using everyday words to talk about pets, Kun, which is appropriate for this article! To really elevate your writing in this area, let's focus on a few key improvements. First, watch out for common spelling errors like "disavantages" (it should be 'disadvantages'), "equ- ipment" (it should be 'equipment'), and "chep" (it should be 'cheap'). Always double-check your spelling! 🧐 Second, let's work on getting your verb agreements right. For example, you wrote "Having pets have," but it should be "Having pets **has**" because "having pets" is treated as a singular idea. Similarly, "Two stan out" should be "Two **stand** out." Lastly, when you talk about "having a pets," remember that "a" is for singular, so it should be "having **a pet**" or "having **pets**." Thai grammar structures can sometimes influence these choices, so focusing on singular and plural forms in English will be a great step forward for you! Keep practising, and these improvements will make your writing much clearer and more natural.