ting with my friends or my old friends. And just talking and just open the songs that very cool for me.<br><br><br>KATI: Um, I think it's not important because if we celebrate, uh, this, the event we don't need to celebrate, uh, too much big. Uh, like my birthday, I usually just, uh, eating on my home with my family, just buy some food or mini cake, mini cake to eat together. It's not important to me.	326	13.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	Term 1 Finals	PET	Your voice is clear and very easy to understand. Your intonation generally sounds natural and your sentence and word stress are usually correct. These strengths make your speech easy to follow. There are a few small pronunciation points to check, for example ensuring final /s/ sounds are distinct in words like "musics" (when referring to music in general the correct word is "music"). Practising listening and repeating target sounds will help your clarity even more.	Kati, you have a lovely clear voice and it's easy to understand you, which is a big plus for communication! Keep up the good work in making your points clearly. While you're good at sharing your ideas, focusing a little more on how you connect your sentences and thoughts will really help your English flow even better. If you can do this, your speaking will become much more natural.<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Practice verb tenses: Pay close attention to using the correct past tense forms when talking about past events (for example, "I went" instead of "I have went").<br>✔️ Expand sentence connectors: Try using a wider variety of words to link your ideas, such as "however," "therefore," or "in addition," to make your speech more organised.<br>✔️ Engage more in discussions: When talking with a partner, try not only to agree but also to ask them questions or build on their ideas with phrases like "What do you think about...?" or "That's a good point, and I'd also add..."	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/