961	28984	2025-07-23 14:47:22.165763+00	A Place I Love	There are rumors going around say dude I went to one place in thailand that so amazing. It have a very beatiful beach and sea. The sea was so clear that I can see the animals. People will reply like wow where is it, what does it called? and the answer was Khou island. It located in side of thailand. Which took 5 hours to go there. I went there on my last holiday and it was a the most fantatist place I ever went. I stay at Tinkerbell resort. Let me introduce some hotel it was name by disney character first is Tinkerbell, peterpan, wendy as you can see it. It come from a disney cartoon called "Peter Pan".<br><br><br>Well Tinkerbell resort have a great service. Their have no limt food like whenever you hungry you can just go to cofertia living room. I went there and I so shock cause the food. I have to eat a lot sometime I to eat it taste really good. But I too full. Not just that I went to waterfall to play around it after that unfortunaly I have to go home so I spent all day getting sun tan and it work well. So the next day I ride a boat and went to beach side since Khou island is surrounded by sea.<br><br><br>Afterwards I just want more attention to Khou island, more tourist. What I try to say is Khou island deserve more attention. The Reason was it have clear sea and have no horse poop. Which is really good. So that is the mainly topic why I write word for. Love and hope you guys visit Khao island more	281	12.0	Platwo, you wrote about Khou Island and shared some things you like about it, like the beautiful beach and the clear sea. You also talked about a resort you stayed at. You did a good job of introducing the place. To make your writing even better, you can work on making your article sound more suitable for a travel website and making your ideas connect more clearly. You can also improve your spelling, grammar, and punctuation so your message is always easy to understand. Keep practicing, and you'll see great improvement	Platwo, you introduced Khou Island and mentioned a few things about it like the beautiful beach, the clear sea, and a resort you stayed at. You also talked about wanting more attention for the island. However, you didn't fully explain *why* these things make it a place you truly love, and some parts felt a little disconnected from the main idea of loving the place.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "It have a very beatiful beach and sea. The sea was so clear that I can see the animals."\n    Problem: You tell us it's beautiful and clear, but you don't really show *why* you love it personally. The connection to your feelings about the place isn't very strong.\n    How to fix it: You could add more details about *how* you felt when you saw the beautiful beach or the clear sea. For instance, you could say, "The sea was so clear that I could see the colourful fish swimming right at my feet, and it made me feel so peaceful and happy." This helps the reader understand your personal connection.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "So that is the mainly topic why I write word for. Love and hope you guys visit Khao island more"\n    Problem: The ending feels a bit sudden and doesn't fully bring together all your reasons for loving the island. Also, the point about "no horse poop" in the previous sentence feels out of place and doesn't clearly support why you love the island.\n    How to fix it: Your conclusion should summarize why you love the place and invite the reader to visit in a more complete way. For example, "Khou Island is truly a special place for me because of its stunning natural beauty and welcoming resorts. I hope you get to experience its magic and see why I love it so much!" This makes the ending stronger and more focused on your main point.	You tried to write an article for a travel website, but the way you wrote it sometimes sounded more like a personal message to a friend rather than a formal article. An article for a website needs to be interesting and clear for a wide audience.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "There are rumors going around say dude I went to one place in thailand that so amazing. It have a very beatiful beach and sea. The sea was so clear that I can see the animals. People will reply like wow where is it, what does it called?"\n    Problem: This opening is very informal and sounds like spoken English ("say dude," "wow where is it, what does it called?"). This style isn't usually suitable for a written article on a travel website.\n    How to fix it: Start your article with a more inviting and formal tone that would draw in readers. You could say, "Have you ever dreamed of finding a truly amazing place to relax and enjoy nature's beauty? I recently visited an incredible island in Thailand that I'd love to tell you about." This is more appropriate for a website.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Their have no limt food like whenever you hungry you can just go to cofertia living room. I went there and I so shock cause the food."\n    Problem: The language here is very conversational and uses phrases that are too casual for an article, like "no limt food" and "I so shock cause the food."\n    How to fix it: Use more formal language to describe the resort and food. For example, "The Tinkerbell resort offers excellent service, including unlimited food available in their comfortable living room. I was truly surprised by the amazing variety and quality of the dishes they offered."	Your essay has three paragraphs, which is good for an article. However, some of your ideas jump around a bit, and the way you connect your sentences and paragraphs could be clearer. This makes it a little hard for the reader to follow your thoughts sometimes.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I stay at Tinkerbell resort. Let me introduce some hotel it was name by disney character first is Tinkerbell, peterpan, wendy as you can see it. It come from a disney cartoon called "Peter Pan"."\n    Problem: This part about the hotel names feels a bit like a list and breaks the flow of describing your experience at the resort.\n    How to fix it: You could weave this information into your description more smoothly. For example, "I stayed at the wonderful Tinkerbell resort, which I discovered was named after a Disney character, just like some of their other lovely accommodations such as Peterpan and Wendy." This integrates the information without interrupting the main idea.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I went to waterfall to play around it after that unfortunaly I have to go home so I spent all day getting sun tan and it work well. So the next day I ride a boat and went to beach side since Khou island is surrounded by sea."\n    Problem: The connection between playing at the waterfall, getting a tan because you had to go home, and then going to the beach the next day is a bit confusing. The "so" and "since" are not used correctly here to link the ideas.\n    How to fix it: Make the sequence of events clearer. For example, "After visiting the beautiful waterfall, I spent the rest of the day getting a lovely sun tan by the beach, enjoying my last hours on the island before heading home. The next morning, I took a boat trip around the island to fully appreciate its stunning coastline, which is surrounded by crystal-clear waters."	You used some simple words to describe your trip, but there were many mistakes with spelling, punctuation, and grammar throughout your essay. These mistakes often made your sentences difficult to understand.\n\n*   Spelling and Capitalisation:\n    Example: "It have a very beatiful beach and sea. The sea was so clear that I can see the animals. People will reply like wow where is it, what does it called? and the answer was Khou island. It located in side of thailand. Which took 5 hours to go there. I went there on my last holiday and it was a the most fantatist place I ever went. I stay at Tinkerbell resort."\n    Problem: Words like "beatiful," "fantatist," and "cofertia" are misspelled. Proper nouns like "Thailand" and "Khou Island" are not always capitalized correctly, and sentences don't always start with a capital letter.\n    How to fix it: Always check your spelling carefully. Remember to capitalize the first word of every sentence and all proper nouns (names of specific places, people, or things). So, "beautiful," "fantastic," "cafeteria," "Thailand," and "Khou Island" would be correct.\n\n*   Grammar and Word Choice:\n    Example: "It have a very beatiful beach and sea." and "Their have no limt food like whenever you hungry you can just go to cofertia living room."\n    Problem: You used "have" instead of "has" for "It" and "Their" instead of "They." Also, "no limt food" should be "unlimited food" or "food with no limit."\n    How to fix it: Pay attention to subject-verb agreement (e.g., "It **has**," not "It have"). Use the correct pronouns (e.g., "They **have**," not "Their have"). Also, choose words that express your meaning clearly and are appropriate for an article.\n\n*   Punctuation:\n    Example: "People will reply like wow where is it, what does it called?"\n    Problem: This sentence runs on, and the questions are embedded without proper question marks or sentence separation.\n    How to fix it: Break down long sentences and use correct punctuation. For example, "People will reply, 'Wow! Where is it? What is it called?'" This makes the questions clear and easier to read.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
967	28841	2025-07-23 15:27:16.168595+00	A Place I Love	Japan is a country that located in the middle the ocean as a Island which have almost 2 millions tourists visiting there and it's famous for its cultures, dresses, beautiful places to travel, and nature but why is Japan so famous? What things that attract people from around the world to visit this country for once in their lives? I'm going to answear these interesting questions and give more of information for you.<br><br><br>As you know, Japan is very famous and almost the most popular choice for those who want to travel for first time to another country to see something new or different from where they have lived since they were born. That's the point, it's because people want to see something new and to have a good experience, especially with their friends, family, and more which Japan can answear their wants. Also, there are a lot of trees that called "Cherry Blossoms." It's a tree with very breath taking pink flowers all around the tree during the spring and there is a whole garden filled with Cherry Blossoms which you can go to picnic there too having relax time from all hard working, or taking some photos with these trees so you can keep it as a part of your great time memories. However, I didn't use to vist the Cherry Blossom garden before but if you want a place to relax and full of adorable pastel pink, this place always welcomes you. Another place that's like the iconic of Japan is "Mountain Fuji", in short "Mt. Fuji". Mountain Fuji looks lik a very massive blue mountain with snow cover on top of its. However, Mt. Fuji isn't a mountain but an active volcano, despite its cute looking. Somehow, people still want to visit their for its great scenary and beautiful nature there. Last year, I went to Mt. Fuji with my family and it was very up there but the view was worth it, so if you want to go to the Mt. Fuji, you should bring a thick coat with you so you won't get cold or ill.<br><br><br>In the end, there are still much more things and facts that I didn't write like its history, anime, traditional clothes, and more but if you get a chance to travel to another country next time, why not Japan so you can get to see more than just in this writing?	403	12.0	Ie Ie, you wrote a very detailed article about Japan, a country you clearly love. You included interesting information about its cultures, beautiful places like Cherry Blossoms and Mt. Fuji, and even shared your own experience visiting Mt. Fuji. You did a good job of giving many reasons why Japan is special. To make your writing even better, you can focus on organizing your ideas with clearer paragraphs, especially since your essay is quite long. This will make it easier for readers to follow all the great information you want to share. You can also work on some small grammar, spelling, and punctuation mistakes to make your writing even clearer.	Ie Ie, you did a great job of introducing Japan and giving many good reasons why it's a place you love. You talked about its cultures, beautiful places, and nature, and gave specific examples like Cherry Blossoms and Mt. Fuji. You also shared a personal experience from your trip. You covered all the important parts of the task very well.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Japan is a country that located in the middle the ocean as a Island which have almost 2 millions tourists visiting there and it's famous for its cultures, dresses, beautiful places to travel, and nature but why is Japan so famous?"\n    Problem: This introduction is packed with a lot of information and questions. While it covers many points, it could be a bit smoother.\n    How to fix it: You could slightly refine the opening to be more inviting and flow better for a travel article. For example, "Japan, an island nation located in the middle of the ocean, attracts almost 2 million tourists who come to experience its famous cultures, beautiful places, and stunning nature. But what truly makes Japan so popular?" This still asks questions but guides the reader more smoothly.\n\n*   Example: You mentioned many things you didn't write about in your conclusion, like history, anime, and traditional clothes.\n    Problem: While it's good to acknowledge what you didn't cover, listing many unmentioned points in the conclusion can make the reader feel like the essay is incomplete, rather than well-rounded.\n    How to fix it: Instead of listing many things you didn't write about, you could hint at the richness of Japan more generally, inviting the reader to discover it themselves. For example, "In the end, this article only scratches the surface of what makes Japan so captivating. If you get a chance to travel abroad next time, I highly recommend visiting Japan to experience its magic firsthand!"	You tried to answer questions and describe Japan in a way that would inform and engage readers, which is good for a travel article. You used descriptive language to make the places sound appealing. However, some of your sentences were very long and had a few awkward phrases, which made it harder to keep the reader's attention consistently throughout the essay.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Japan is a country that located in the middle the ocean as a Island which have almost 2 millions tourists visiting there and it's famous for its cultures, dresses, beautiful places to travel, and nature but why is Japan so famous? What things that attract people from around the world to visit this country for once in their lives?"\n    Problem: This is a very long and complex sentence structure that is a bit difficult to follow at the beginning of an article.\n    How to fix it: Break down long sentences into shorter, clearer ones. For instance, "Japan is an island nation located in the middle of the ocean, attracting almost 2 million tourists. It's famous for its unique cultures, beautiful clothes, stunning places, and rich nature. But what truly makes Japan so popular? What truly attracts people from all over the world to visit this country at least once?"\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I'm going to answear these interesting questions and give more of information for you."\n    Problem: This phrase is a bit too direct and sounds like you are talking to the reader, which can be less formal than an article usually needs to be.\n    How to fix it: Use more formal and objective language. For example, "This article will attempt to answer these interesting questions and provide more information for prospective travelers."	You have a clear introduction and conclusion, which helps structure your article. However, your main body is written as one very long paragraph. It's perfectly okay to write a longer essay if you have a lot to say, but you need to use paragraphs more effectively to organize your ideas. When there are many different points, putting them all in one long paragraph makes it hard for the reader to follow your thoughts.\n\n*   Example: Your entire second paragraph is extremely long and covers many different ideas: Japan's general popularity, people's desire for new experiences, Cherry Blossoms (with details about picnics and photos), and then Mt. Fuji (its appearance, being a volcano, scenery, and personal travel advice).\n    Problem: Putting all these distinct ideas into one very long paragraph makes the essay feel dense and difficult to read. The reader might get lost in the middle of the paragraph.\n    How to fix it: Break this long paragraph into smaller, more focused paragraphs. For instance, you could have:\n    *   A paragraph about why Japan is popular for new experiences.\n    *   A separate paragraph dedicated to Cherry Blossoms.\n    *   Another separate paragraph focused on Mt. Fuji.\n    This makes your points easier to find and understand.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "However, I didn't use to vist the Cherry Blossom garden before but if you want a place to relax and full of adorable pastel pink, this place always welcomes you. Another place that's like the iconic of Japan is "Mountain Fuji", in short "Mt. Fuji"."\n    Problem: The transition between talking about Cherry Blossoms (which you haven't visited) and then immediately jumping to Mt. Fuji is a bit abrupt.\n    How to fix it: Use clearer linking phrases or start a new paragraph for each major point. For instance, "Even though I haven't personally visited a Cherry Blossom garden, I know it's a welcoming place for relaxation, filled with adorable pastel pink. Beyond these seasonal delights, another iconic symbol of Japan is Mount Fuji..."	You used a good range of vocabulary to describe Japan, and your message was generally understandable. However, there were frequent errors in spelling, grammar (like verb forms and sentence structure), and punctuation. These mistakes were noticeable and sometimes made it difficult to fully understand your meaning.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "located in the middle **the** ocean," "answear," "breath taking," "veiw," "lik," "scenary," "vist," "their" (instead of "there")\n    Problem: Words like "located in the middle *of the* ocean," "answer," "breathtaking" (one word), "view," "like," "scenery," "visit," and "there" are misspelled or missing words.\n    How to fix it: Proofread your essay carefully for spelling and missing words. Pay attention to common compound words and homophones (words that sound the same but have different spellings and meanings, like "their," "there," and "they're").\n\n*   Grammar - Verb Forms and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "Japan is a country that **located**," "which **have** almost 2 millions tourists," "Japan can **answear** their wants," "It's a tree with very breath taking pink flowers," "Mountain Fuji looks **lik** a very massive blue mountain with snow cover on top of its."\n    Problem: You often miss auxiliary verbs (e.g., "is located," "which has"). There are also errors in verb forms ("answer" not "answear") and sentence structure, making some sentences awkward or ungrammatical.\n    How to fix it: Focus on using correct verb forms and ensuring your subjects and verbs agree (e.g., "a country *that is located*," "which *has*"). Practice building clear and concise sentences.\n\n*   Punctuation and Capitalization:\n    Example: "Mt. Fuji isn't a mountain but an active volcano, despite its cute looking. Somehow, people still want to visit their for its great scenary and beautiful nature there."\n    Problem: The punctuation could be clearer to separate ideas. "Somehow" feels like an informal transition. "Mountain Fuji" is sometimes capitalized, sometimes not, and "Island" is capitalized incorrectly.\n    How to fix it: Use punctuation like commas and periods to separate your ideas clearly. Maintain consistent capitalization for proper nouns (e.g., "Mount Fuji"). Make sure you are capitalizing words correctly, usually only proper nouns and the first word of a sentence.	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
