2941	33170	2025-12-14 09:35:16.712286+00	Term 2 Midterm M2A Part 2	I looked out of the window and couldn't believe my eyes. It was two in the morning. There shouldn't be any light out side. But there it is.<br><br>A bright harmful light, burning everything in it's way. Fire. I almost thought someone turn on the light, but the huge amount of smoke outside tells me the diffent story. My eyes quickly fixed at the deer my only way exit. I feel so strange, because I didn't remmeber closing it. My footsteps echo through the room, when I reached the door in 3 stirke. Then I twisted the door knob. A golden one, shine by the reflection of the fire outside. A muscle in my throat tighten and a chill ran down my spin as I twisted the door knob again. But it didn't show any sign opening. With no choice, I started kicking the door, pushing it, breaking it. But no matter how hard I've tried, it was useless. My heart beat drumed in my ears, so panic I feel it in my throat.	174	16.0	Hello Venice! 👋<br><br>This is an incredibly atmospheric and intense story! 🔥 The imagery of the 'bright harmful light' and the 'huge amount of smoke' created a very strong sense of danger. Your descriptions of trying to open the door were particularly gripping.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you make your writing even more powerful! 📋<br>1. <b>Ending!</b> ❓ The story ends very abruptly in the middle of a sentence ('so panic I feel it in my throat'). Make sure to finish your thought or the scene!<br>2. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 Watch out for words like 'remmeber' (remember), 'stirke' (strike), and 'spin' (spine).<br>3. <b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'The diffent story' should be 'the <b>different</b> story'.	You created a very vivid and urgent scenario, responding to the prompt with a compelling story about a house fire and an escape attempt. 🌟 The details about the locked door and the character's panic were very realistic.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Resolution!</b> 🏁 The story ends mid-sentence. What happened next? Did you break the door? Were you rescued? A clear ending is crucial.</li><li><b>The 'Deer'!</b> ❓ You mentioned 'My eyes quickly fixed at the deer my only way exit'. Did you mean 'door'? Clarifying this is important for the reader to follow the escape route.</li>	You successfully built a strong sense of suspense and desperation through your descriptive language and pacing. 😱 The sensory details make the reader feel the heat and panic.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Show, Don't Tell!</b> 🎬 You did well with 'a muscle in my throat tighten'. Continue to use these physical reactions to show emotion rather than just stating it.</li>	Your descriptions are very vivid, but the story is currently one long block of text after the initial prompt. 🧱<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Paragraphing!</b> 📝 Break your story into paragraphs when the scene or major action changes (e.g., one for the initial sighting, one for getting to the door, one for trying to escape).</li><li><b>Sentence Flow!</b> 🌊 Some sentences are very long and contain multiple clauses. Breaking them down into shorter, more focused sentences would improve clarity and dramatic impact.</li>	You used strong descriptive vocabulary like 'harmful', 'flickered', 'shattered', and 'suffocating'. 📚<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Remmeber' -> '<b>remember</b>', 'stirke' -> '<b>strike</b>', 'spin' -> '<b>spine</b>', 'diffent' -> '<b>different</b>'.</li><li><b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Deer' (animal) vs. '<b>door</b>' (exit).</li><li><b>Possessives!</b> 📝 'In it's way' -> 'in <b>its</b> way'.</li><li><b>Past Tense!</b> ⏳ 'My heart beat drumed' -> 'My heart <b>beat drummed</b>'.</li>	5.0	4.0	3.0	4.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
1824	34014	2025-09-14 10:47:38.783001+00	Term 1 Exam Question 2	Many of you might want to have pets. Maybe because you want to play with them or take care them in your free time. But at my point, as a teenage, I wouldn't suggest you having pets because having pets get many disadvantage and I will tell you about three major problem about having pets; time, money and distrubing.<br><br><br>The biggest problem about having pets is time. It's good if you have enough time to play with your pets and you will be happy. But if you don't have enough time to play with them, you shouldn't have pets. As teengers I know that we don't have that enough time, we spend time on homework or with friends and you won't have time to take care your pets.<br><br><br>Beside time, money is another problem about having pets. If you adopt pets, you need to spend a lot of money on food, drink, calcium or even with their illness. Many thing for pets are costs more than human. If your family have money that's good, but it not, that mean you are not ready for having pets.<br><br><br>And the final problem about having pets is distrubing. Pets distrub you all the time except when they sleep. Pets will make some noise or do something to get some attention. You might felt annoy with your pets and don't want to keep them anymore.<br><br><br>In conclusion, I would say that you can have pets if you really want and you think you could keep them. But if you think you aren't ready to have them, you can wait until you grow up, make some money and ready for having pets.	281	12.0	Hello Lookpear! 👋 It’s wonderful to see your clear and strong opinions on pet ownership in your article. You've certainly captured the voice of a teenager, and your points come across very clearly. The most significant area for you to focus on is making sure you address all parts of the essay question directly. You only presented the disadvantages of owning a pet, but the task also asked for advantages. Keep up the great work, and remember that with a bit more attention to fully answering the prompt, your writing will truly thrive! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Lookpear:**<br>   ✅ Before you start writing, underline or highlight all the specific requirements in the task, such as "advantages and disadvantages," and make sure you tick them off as you write.<br>   ✅ When you brainstorm ideas, try to think of both sides of an argument, even if you ultimately decide to focus more on one. This helps ensure you've considered the full scope of the question.<br>   ✅ After you've finished writing, read the question again and then read your article, specifically checking if every part of the prompt has been answered.	Lookpear, you made some really strong arguments about the difficulties of owning pets, and your passion on the subject is clear! 📝 However, the task asked for both the advantages and disadvantages of having pets, and your article focused only on the drawbacks. To get a higher score in content, it’s really important to address every part of the question. For example, if you mentioned the challenges, you could briefly introduce an advantage, even if you then argued against it, like saying, "Some people enjoy the companionship pets offer, but for teenagers, this joy often comes with significant responsibilities." By doing this, you show the reader you've thought about both sides, even if your main point is about one. Sometimes, Thai learners might focus intently on one aspect of a question, which is good for depth, but it’s crucial to remember to cover all the points requested in the instructions to ensure the reader is fully informed.	You've done a really good job of writing an article that sounds like it's for other teenagers, Lookpear! 👍 Your chatty and direct style makes it engaging and easy to read, and you held my attention throughout. You successfully used the kind of language and approach that would appeal to a teenage audience online, which is an important part of communicating well. To move closer to a perfect score, you could try to make your introduction even more inviting, perhaps with a question to the reader or a strong opening statement that hints at the two sides of the argument before you reveal your main stance. This would make your article even more impactful and show you are thinking deeply about how to keep your reader engaged from the very beginning.	Your article has a clear structure, Lookpear, which is great! You started with an introduction, then moved through your main points, and finished with a conclusion. This makes your ideas generally easy to follow. You used some basic linking words like "Beside time" and "And the final problem" to connect your paragraphs, which is a good start. To improve this area, you could try using a wider range of linking words and phrases to show clearer relationships between your ideas. For instance, instead of just "But at my point," you could try "However, from a teenager's perspective," or "In addition to time, money also presents a challenge." Thinking about how each paragraph flows into the next with more varied connectors will make your writing feel even more polished and sophisticated.	You used some good everyday vocabulary to talk about pets, Lookpear, such as "play," "take care," and "adopt," which is appropriate for your topic. While your message is generally understandable, there were a few areas with spelling and grammar that could be tidied up to make your writing even clearer. Let’s look at three important areas for improvement:<br>1.  <b>Spelling:</b> Words like "distrubing" should be "disturbing," "teengers" should be "teenagers," and "felt annoy" should be "felt annoyed." Always try to double-check your spelling, perhaps by using an online spell-checker or a dictionary, as this can really help your writing look more professional. 📚<br>2.  <b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> You wrote "having pets get many disadvantage" but because "having pets" acts as a singular subject here, the verb should be "gets." So, it should be "having pets gets many disadvantages." Similarly, "Many thing for pets are costs more than human" should be "Many things for pets cost more than humans." This is about making sure your verbs match your subjects, whether they are singular or plural.<br>3.  <b>Prepositions and Articles:</b> Phrases like "take care them" should be "take care *of* them," and "distrub you all the time except when they sleep" is missing an article, so it should be "disturb you all the time except when they sleep." Also, "make some money and ready for having pets" should be "make some money and *be* ready for having pets." Getting these small words right makes a big difference to how natural and correct your English sounds.<br><br>Many Thai learners find these specific grammar points a bit challenging because English has very strict rules about how verbs change, how nouns are made plural, and when to use small linking words like prepositions, which can be quite different from the structure of Thai. Keep practicing these, and your writing will become much more accurate and easier to understand! 💪	2.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
