fectively to express emotion in your story. This kept me interested in your review of the plot and characters from beginning to end.<br><br><b>What to Watch Out For:</b> The most significant area for improvement is syllable stress /ˈsɪl.ə.bəl.stres/. You have a frequent tendency to overstress the final syllable of words, such as "Gunner" /ɡʌnˈɜːr/, "grandmother" /ɡrændˌmʌðˈɜːr/, and "finally" /faɪ.nəlˈliː/, which can be quite intrusive for a listener.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br>1. Practice the "schwa" sound /ə/ for final syllables to avoid overstressing them (e.g., Gunner /ˈɡʌn.ər/).<br>2. Listen to recordings of native speakers and notice how they often make the final sounds of words like "sleeping" /ˈsliː.pɪŋ/ or "secretly" /ˈsiː.krət.li/ shorter and softer.<br><br>Your energy is fantastic, Gunner, so keep working on that syllable stress! 🚀	Gunner, this was a successful book review where you clearly explained the plot and shared your personal thoughts on the characters! 🌟 You followed the requested structure and used an effective hook to grab the audience's attention. Your use of the past tense was generally accurate, and you used helpful transition words to move through the story's events. To reach a higher level, try to include all required details like the author's name and focus on speaking more smoothly without repeating words. Working on subject-verb agreement and singular versus plural nouns will also help polish your speaking. You are a brave speaker with a clear voice, so keep practicing your storytelling! 🎯	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/149T-QIza1cCqeNpsUNQzEvHxMwqCJN4X?usp=sharing	B2	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
