ll your interesting points much clearer for your readers. You should also work on making your language more formal for a website and correcting your grammar, spelling, and capitalisation so your message is always easy to understand.	Arther, you introduced Phuket as the best city you've ever visited and gave many reasons why you love it, such as the beautiful sea, activities, the old city, Phuket FantaSea, a unique landmark, and delicious food. You conveyed your excitement for the city. However, you tried to include too many different points, which meant you couldn't explain each one in enough detail. Your essay also ended abruptly, so the conclusion is missing, which affects the overall completeness of your article.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "They have a circus show in Phuket also old city, once im there i think i have travled back in time it is just old fashion i really want you to really give Phuket a try you will in love with this city."\n    Problem: You mention several different attractions (circus, old city) very quickly without much explanation about what makes them special or why you love them. The old city is described vaguely as "old fashion."\n    How to fix it: Choose one or two of your favorite attractions and describe them in more detail, explaining *why* they are amazing and how they made you feel. For example, instead of just "old city, once im there i think i have travled back in time it is just old fashion," you could say, "The historic Old Town of Phuket is like stepping back in time, with its beautifully preserved shophouses and charming local atmosphere that made me feel so connected to the past."\n\n*   Example: Your essay ended abruptly without a conclusion.\n    Problem: A complete article needs a conclusion to summarize your main points and give the reader a final thought or invitation. Ending suddenly leaves the reader feeling unfinished.\n    How to fix it: Always plan to write a full concluding paragraph. In it, you should briefly summarize why Phuket is a place you love and perhaps encourage readers to visit, reminding them of the main highlights.	You wanted to persuade the reader to visit Phuket, and you shared your strong positive feelings. However, your writing style was very informal and conversational ("i Say that because Phuket are just awsome," "you know?"). There were many errors and awkward phrases that made it very difficult to understand your ideas, and the article did not effectively hold the reader's attention.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "Phuket is the best city i ever been to, whole life with the beautiful tur.gouse sea fun activities to do in Phuket, i Say that because Phuket are just awsome, once i been there i think i wouldn't have return back because this city never sleep i begged my mom not to travel back to Bankok, They have a circus show in Phuket also old city,..."\n    Problem: This opening is one very long, informal sentence with many grammatical errors. Phrases like "tur.gouse," "i Say that because Phuket are just awsome," and "this city never sleep" are too casual and unclear for an article.\n    How to fix it: Start your article with a clear, inviting, and more formal introduction. Break down your ideas into shorter, clearer sentences. For instance, "Phuket is truly the best city I have ever visited. With its beautiful turquoise sea and endless fun activities, it's an amazing destination. Once I arrived, I never wanted to leave because this city is always lively."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Phuket, it is Awsome, Best city i've been to you know? this city got every thing in there many entertainment things"\n    Problem: This is very informal ("you know?") and repetitive ("Awsome, Best city"). It also has grammatical errors.\n    How to fix it: Use more formal language and vary your vocabulary. For example, "Phuket is an amazing city, truly one of the best I've experienced. It offers a wide variety of entertainment options."	You tried to include different aspects of Phuket, but your essay suffered from a severe lack of clear paragraph breaks and logical flow. Many different ideas were mixed together in very long sections, making it very difficult for the reader to follow your thoughts. This disorganized structure made your long essay much harder to read.\n\n*   Example: Your first paragraph covers your overall feeling about Phuket, why you wanted to stay, and then jumps to a circus show and the old city.\n    Problem: Too many unrelated ideas are crammed into one long paragraph without clear connections. The flow is very disjointed.\n    How to fix it: **Always use separate paragraphs for distinct ideas, especially in a longer essay.** Your first paragraph should be a clear introduction. Then, you could have a separate paragraph for entertainment (circus, FantaSea), another for historical/cultural sites (old city, specific landmarks), and another for food.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "i will show you what its look like. beautiful beaches, wonderful city i suggest you go to this placed it call Phuket Fanta Sea... i recommed you go to know tapo. relly amazing place with the signature nail it comes from the middle of island there a small clift it look like a nail stabbed into the island, it is really quite strange but it look unique and it is the one thing i can't really forget about are foods."\n    Problem: This is one extremely long, rambling section that jumps from general descriptions ("beautiful beaches, wonderful city") to specific attractions (FantaSea, the nail-like cliff) and then to food, all without proper paragraphing or smooth transitions. The sentence structure is very difficult to follow.\n    How to fix it: Break this down. For instance, you could have a paragraph about the natural beauty, including beaches and the unique "nail" cliff. Then, a separate paragraph about cultural shows like FantaSea. Finally, a new paragraph dedicated entirely to the delicious food.	You used very simple vocabulary, and there were many significant and frequent mistakes in spelling, capitalisation, grammar (including subject-verb agreement, verb forms, articles, and sentence structure), and punctuation. These errors made your writing extremely difficult to understand at times. Your tone was also consistently too informal for an article.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "tur.gouse," "awsome," "Bankok," "im," "travled," "plased," "vial," "Sens," "recommed," "tapo," "relly," "flavoury," "delicous," "ingredeants," "luxuros," "resturant," "that'se"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled or used incorrectly. They should be "turquoise," "awesome," "Bangkok," "I'm," "traveled," "place," "vibrant," "senses," "recommend," "Ko Tapu" (or James Bond Island), "really," "flavourful," "delicious," "ingredients," "luxurious," "restaurant," "that's."\n    How to fix it: You need to proofread your writing extremely carefully. Use a dictionary or a spell checker for every word you are unsure about. These frequent errors severely impact how well your message can be understood.\n\n*   Capitalisation:\n    Example: "Phuket is the best city i ever been to," "i Say that," "Bankok," "Phuket Fanta Sea," "Ocean," "Sens," "nail," "Dior" (used in another student's good example).\n    Problem: You often do not capitalize the first word of a sentence or proper nouns (like "Bangkok," "Phuket FantaSea," "Ko Tapu"). Sometimes words that shouldn't be capitalized are.\n    How to fix it: Always capitalize the first letter of every sentence. Capitalize all proper nouns (specific names of places, shows, or landmarks).\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement, Verb Forms, and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "Phuket **are** just awsome," "i wouldn't have **return** back," "i think i have **travled**," "it is just old fashion," "Phuket, it is Awsome, Best city i've been to you know? this city got every thing in there," "the food are so delicous"\n    Problem: You have consistent errors with subject-verb agreement ("Phuket *is*," not "are"). Verb forms are incorrect ("wouldn't have *returned*," "have *traveled*"). Many sentences are run-on or grammatically incomplete, making them very difficult to parse.\n    How to fix it: Focus on basic sentence structure and ensuring your verbs match your subjects. Practice using correct verb tenses and forms. Break down your complex thoughts into simpler, correct sentences.	3.0	2.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
