ud to yourself. This can help you catch awkward sentences or find places where words might be missing.<br>✅ Keep a small notebook of new vocabulary you learn and practice using these words in your own sentences.<br>✅ When you’re writing, pay close attention to how your sentences begin and end, and ensure your ideas flow smoothly from one to the next.	You did a great job including all the parts of the task – you talked about both the good things and the tricky things about having pets, and you shared your own thoughts on the topic. You even ended with a question for the reader, which is a lovely touch for an article designed to get teenagers thinking! 🐾 To take your content to the next level, remember to keep your examples and explanations tightly connected to your main argument. For example, the detailed story about the prisoner and the kitten, while interesting, was quite long and could have been made much shorter to support your point more directly and efficiently for an article of this length. Ensuring every detail helps your reader quickly grasp your main message will make your writing even stronger.	You successfully used the common features of an article, and your introduction and conclusion are quite welcoming for your readers. It’s clear you know how to start and finish an article! ✨ To make your article truly connect with teenagers, try to use language that feels a bit more natural and conversational for that age group. For instance, instead of "Firstly advantage," you could try saying "Let's start with the good things" or "One big plus is...". Thinking about how a friend might talk about these topics can help you choose more engaging words and phrases. Making your examples more relatable to a typical teenager's life might also help hold their attention even more effectively. Sometimes in Thai, direct and concise phrasing is common, but in English articles, adding a little more conversational flair can make a big difference for your target audience.	You’ve done a good job of structuring your article with separate paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages, which makes it easy to follow your main points. You also used some clear linking phrases like "Firstly advantage" and "To summarize." 📝 To make your writing flow even more smoothly, try varying your linking words and phrases. Instead of always starting with a similar phrase, you could use "Another benefit is..." or "On the other hand...". Also, consider how your sentences connect *within* each paragraph. Sometimes, adding a word like "Therefore," "Consequently," or "In addition" can help your ideas stick together better. For example, after stating an advantage, you could follow up with "This means that..." to elaborate on your point. In some languages, less explicit linking might be common, but English writing often relies on clear connecting words and phrases to guide the reader through the arguments.	You've used some appropriate everyday vocabulary related to pets and opinions, and you've shown you can construct simple sentences to convey your ideas. Keep building on this – your dedication will pay off! 📚 Let's focus on three important areas to make your language much clearer and more precise:<br>1. **Spelling:** I noticed a few words like "lonelynes" (should be 'loneliness'), "themselfs" (should be 'themselves'), "sciencist" (should be 'scientist'), "condeminium" (should be 'condominium'), and "chenale" (which seemed to be a misspelling of something like 'item' or 'valuable'). Getting these spellings right is really important for your reader to understand you without confusion. A great habit is to reread your work carefully, or even use an online spell checker if you're writing on a computer.<br>2. **Subject-Verb Agreement and Plurals:** You wrote "Having pet give you" which should be "Having **pets gives** you" or "A pet **gives** you." Similarly, "Some place do not allow pet" should be "Some **places do not allow pets**." In English, nouns and verbs often need to agree in number (singular with singular, plural with plural). Remember to use plural nouns like "advantages" and "disadvantages" consistently, and always make sure your verbs match your subjects.<br>3. **Sentence Structure and Verb Tense:** Some of your sentences were a little difficult to follow. For example, "sciencist gave a kitten to a prisoner let them spend time together" could be clearer as, "scientists gave a kitten to a prisoner, *allowing* them to spend time together." Also, "they broken an expensive chenale" should be "they *broke* an expensive *item*." Focusing on the correct verb tense (past tense for a past action like 'broke') and ensuring your sentences have a clear subject and verb will help a lot. In Thai, word forms often don't change for singular or plural, and verb conjugations are much simpler or non-existent compared to English. This can sometimes lead to mistakes in English with plural nouns and subject-verb agreement. Also, sentence structures can be more flexible in Thai, which might lead to sentences in English that feel a bit fragmented or run-on to a native speaker. Practicing these specific areas will really help your English writing become more precise.	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
