e a good effort in writing your essay and have clearly tried to express your thoughts on an interesting topic! You managed to discuss all the points from the question, which is a great start! Keep working on making your main argument clear from beginning to end, and your writing will become much more impactful. Remember that every time you write, it’s a chance to share your ideas with confidence!<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br> *   ✅ Before you start writing, make a quick plan of your main argument and stick to it all the way through your introduction, body paragraphs, and conclusion.<br> *   ✅ When you finish writing, read through your work and circle any words that look like they might be plurals or verbs, then double-check that they are formed correctly for the sentence.<br> *   ✅ Try to use a variety of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas smoothly, for example, instead of repeating "Frist of all," you could try "To begin with" or "Firstly."	You did well to include all the points from the question, Krewkehw, like talking about clothes and fashion, studying, and adding your own idea about activities. That shows you understood the task! However, an essay needs a very clear answer to the main question right from the start, and your essay was a little bit confusing about whether friends or parents have more influence. For example, in your introduction, you talked about both having an impact, and then in your conclusion, you said teenagers make choices "by them self," even though your paragraphs showed friends having a big effect. 😕 To improve, try making your main argument super clear at the beginning and then ensure every paragraph helps to prove that argument. It’s like telling a story: you want the reader to know what the story is about from the very first sentence!	You tried to express your opinion, which is a big part of communicative achievement. However, because your main message about friends versus parents wasn’t always clear, it was sometimes difficult for your reader to fully understand your point of view. 🤔 Also, essays for this exam usually need a more formal style of writing, and sometimes your tone felt a little casual. For instance, phrases like "Now you might experien your parent force you" could be rephrased more formally. Thinking about who you are writing for and what impression you want to make can help a lot. Practise keeping a consistent, slightly more formal tone throughout your whole essay; this will make your ideas sound more convincing and help you inform your reader better!	You did a good job of breaking your essay into paragraphs, Krewkehw, which is important for organisation! 👍 However, the way your paragraphs connect could be smoother. You used phrases like "Frist of all" and "Secondly," which are good starting points, but you could try a wider range of linking words to make your essay flow more naturally. For example, instead of "That come the impact of friend," you could say "This is where the influence of friends comes in." 🔗 Sometimes, sentences were quite long and had a lot of ideas in them, which made them a bit hard to follow. Thai learners sometimes use longer sentences because it's common in Thai writing, but in English, breaking them down into shorter, clearer sentences can make your writing much easier to read. Try to vary your sentence structures and use more sophisticated linking phrases to guide your reader smoothly from one idea to the next.	You used a lot of words to get your message across, Krewkehw, and it's great that you're not afraid to write! However, there were quite a few grammar and spelling mistakes that sometimes made your sentences difficult to understand. 😥 For example, with subject-verb agreement, you wrote "parent has impact" when it should be "parents have impact" because "parents" is plural. Another common issue was with plurals, like writing "many country" instead of "many countries." Also, some of your word choices made sentences a bit unclear, for instance, "friend some in siceen" or "experien your parent force you." These kinds of errors are common for Thai learners because English grammar, especially plurals and verb forms, can be very different from Thai. To improve, pay close attention to your verb endings and make sure your subjects and verbs agree. For instance, when you have a plural subject like "friends," the verb often doesn't need an 's' (e.g., "friends influence"). Also, when you finish writing, take a moment to proofread for spelling and think if there's a more precise word you could use. Even a few minutes checking for these common errors can make a big difference! ✨	3.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
1908	30221	2025-09-18 09:01:44.735572+00	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw somethin incredible! I was a little fox it was hiding in a bush. I was white and beautiful when we tried to touch it, it was scared and nervous. When we finally catch it and bring it home, I think it was sick and cold so we decided to drive along to the pet hospital when the doctor done everything we drive back home.	76	11.0	That's a lovely start to your story about seeing something incredible at school yesterday morning. You've clearly shown us a series of events, and it's easy to follow what happened. While your story has some good elements, there are a couple of areas we can focus on to help your writing progress even further. We want to ensure your stories are as clear and engaging as they can be, so let's aim for even greater accuracy and detail in your next piece.	You've done a good job of starting your story with the exact sentence provided, and describing the incredible sight of the fox was a delightful surprise. You took us through the sequence of events, from trying to touch the fox to taking it to the pet hospital and finally driving home. This shows you can develop a narrative. To improve, try to add a little more detail about how you and your friend felt throughout the experience. For instance, you could have written, 'We were so excited to see the fox, but also a bit worried when we saw it looked sick.' Adding these personal touches helps the reader connect with your story even more. Also, remember that for this task, the goal is to write around 100 words. Your story was a little shorter than that this time, so next time, try to add a few more descriptive sentences or some extra details about the vet visit to meet the word count.	Your story definitely communicates straightforward ideas, and the overall flow makes it easy to understand the main points. You've used the conventions of storytelling quite well, such as describing events in order. However, to make your stories truly captivating and hold the reader's attention throughout, consider adding a little more descriptive language and varying your sentence structures. For example, instead of just saying 'it was white and beautiful,' you could expand on that by saying, 'Its fur was as white as snow, and its tail was beautifully bushy.' This helps the reader imagine the scene more vividly. Think about how you can paint a picture with your words.	Your story is generally well-organised, and the events happen in a logical order, which is good. You've used some basic linking words like 'when' to connect your ideas. To achieve a higher score, try to introduce a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For instance, you could use 'After that,' 'Suddenly,' or 'Eventually' to show the passage of time or a change in events. Breaking your story into shorter paragraphs when there's a shift in focus, like when you decide to take the fox to the hospital, can also make it easier for the reader to follow along and make your writing appear more structured. This helps to group related ideas together.	You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, and despite some errors, the meaning of your story is generally clear, which is important. For example, you used words like 'hiding,' 'scared,' and 'sick,' which fit the context well. However, there are some noticeable grammatical errors that you can work on. A key area for improvement is using the correct pronouns. You wrote 'I was a little fox' and 'I was white and beautiful' when you meant to refer to the fox, which should be 'It was a little fox' and 'It was white and beautiful.' This is a common point for Thai learners to work on, as the structure for referring to things and animals can differ in Thai. Another area is the past tense; you wrote 'somethin' instead of 'something,' and 'when the doctor done everything we drive back home' should be 'when the doctor **had done** everything, we **drove** back home.' Focusing on these specific areas will really help your sentences become much more accurate and clear for your reader. Practising your past tense verbs and checking your pronouns carefully will make a significant difference to your writing.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
