eel the same sense of wonder that you did. Think about extending the 'incredible' moment before moving on to the next action. You touched on the man being 'kind,' but what made seeing him so 'incredible' at school? Developing this initial reaction will really elevate your storytelling. 🌟	You have a good understanding of how to tell a story with a beginning, middle, and end. The events in your story follow each other in a logical order, which helps the reader to understand what is happening. To make your story even more engaging, try to use more descriptive words that paint a clearer picture for your reader. For instance, instead of just saying 'cute,' you could describe *how* the kittens were cute—were they tiny, playful, or cuddly? Thinking about the emotions you want your reader to feel will help you choose words that grab their attention and make them want to read on. ✍️	Your story is easy to follow because you use words like 'and next' and 'Finally' to link your ideas together. This is a good start, Posh. To make your writing even better, you could try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of always saying 'and,' you could try 'then,' 'after that,' 'meanwhile,' or 'as a result.' Also, thinking about breaking your story into smaller paragraphs when a new idea or event begins can help your readers follow the flow of your narrative even more easily. This helps your ideas connect more smoothly, like putting together puzzle pieces. 🧩	You use everyday words that help to communicate your ideas. However, there are a few areas in your grammar that you can work on to make your writing clearer. For instance, you wrote 'my friend and i,' but the pronoun 'I' should always be a capital letter when referring to yourself. Also, remember to put a space after a comma and a full stop, like 'morning, we' should be 'morning, we.' Another important point to watch out for is when you are talking about one of something versus many. For example, you said 'five kitten' when it should be 'five kittens' (plural). Similarly, 'three cat' should be 'three cats' and 'raise animal' should be 'raise animals.' Many Thai learners sometimes miss these small but important plural endings, so it is a good idea to double-check these in your writing. Focusing on these small adjustments will help your writing become much more polished and professional. ✨	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
2010	29584	2025-09-21 16:45:43.876721+00	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! We noticed a poster on a school board about international English website for young people. You need to write an article about a festival that you and your family celebrate. We consulted together and decided to sign in. I am the one who fascinate in writing an article so it seems interesting! I went home and thought about what should I write for severals minutes, and I dicided to write about christmas festival! Me and my family always have a great time together. Like last year, we went to our grandparents' house. We went to shopping street, walked along stream's side, and enjoyed our meals together! It was a wonderful experience, may be I could write it down in an article	135	8.0	Hi Yaya, you've shown some creative thinking in your writing, and it's lovely to see your enthusiasm for festivals like Christmas. 🎉 To really make your stories pop, let's focus on sticking to the task and building your amazing ideas into a clear and exciting narrative. Keep up the great work. You have a lot of potential to write engaging stories.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   ✔️ Carefully read the task instructions to make sure your writing directly answers what's being asked.<br>*   ✔️ Practice using past tenses consistently to describe events that have already happened.<br>*   ✔️ Think about how you can use paragraphs to group your ideas and make your writing easier to read.	Yaya, you started your writing with the exact sentence you were given, which is an excellent start for a story. Well done! 😊 However, for a story, the "something incredible" needs to be an actual event that happens, not a poster about a writing competition. It seems like you then moved on to thinking about writing an article about Christmas, which isn't quite what the story task asked for. For example, if you see an incredible poster, you could imagine it showing a lost treasure map or a secret school event, and then your story would describe what happened next because of *that*. Always double-check what the task is asking for—is it a story about an event, or an article about a topic? This will help you make sure all your wonderful ideas fit the task perfectly.	You show that you can share ideas, but for a story, we want to make sure the reader is completely absorbed in the adventure from beginning to end. ✍️ Because your writing shifted from an "incredible" sighting to planning an article, it made it difficult for your reader to stay engaged in a single story. When you write a story, try to keep a clear narrative path. For instance, if you start with something incredible, continue to describe what happens because of that incredible thing, how you and your friend felt, and how it all concluded. Imagine you're telling a friend a really exciting piece of news—you'd want to keep them hooked until the very end.	You connected your ideas quite well, Yaya, and it's easy to follow your thought process from seeing the poster to thinking about Christmas. Good job! 👍 Even without using separate paragraphs, your ideas flowed in a clear sequence for the points you wanted to make. To help your reader even more, try using paragraphs to introduce new ideas or changes in your story. For example, you could have one paragraph for seeing the poster, another for deciding to write, and then another for your Christmas memories. This helps to group your thoughts and makes your writing look more organised on the page. In English writing, we often use new paragraphs to signal a new idea or a shift in time or place.	Yaya, you used many everyday words that were suitable for your topics, and it's wonderful that you tried to use more interesting sentence structures, like when you said, "the one who fascinate in writing." That shows you are thinking about how to connect ideas, which is excellent! 🌟 To make your writing even clearer, let's look at a few things.<br><br>First, when you wanted to say you were very interested in writing, "who fascinate" should be "who **is fascinated by** writing" or "who **loves** writing." This helps to express your feeling correctly. For example, instead of "I fascinate in writing," you could say, "I **am fascinated by** writing" or "I **really enjoy** writing."<br><br>Next, try to be consistent with your verb tenses, especially when you are telling a story about things that happened in the past. For example, you wrote, "I **dicided** to write about Christmas festival! ...Like last year, we **went**..." Here, "dicided" is a spelling mistake (it should be "decided"). When you talk about past events, use past tense verbs. So, it would be "I **decided** to write... Last year, we **went**..."<br><br>Also, a small point for formal writing: while "Me and my family" is common in everyday speech, for written tasks, it's generally better to say "My family and I." It's a subtle difference, but it shows strong command of English grammar in formal contexts.<br><br>Finally, be careful with small words like "a," "an," and "the." For example, you wrote "a school board about international English website." It would be better to say "**an** international English website" (because 'international' starts with a vowel sound) or "the international English website" if it's a specific one. Similarly, "shopping street" often needs "a" or "the" before it, like "we went to **the** shopping street." In Thai, you might not use these small words in the same way, so it's a common area for English learners to practice, and focusing on them will make a big difference in how clear your writing is.	1.0	1.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2	\N	\N	\N	A2	2026
1156	28758	2025-08-29 16:00:01.263008+00	Free time Activities	Great activities to doing their favorite activities in the time. So in every country like Thailand, teens in Thailand mostly like to play games in their free Time but there are some better activities to do, so these are two the best activities to do for Teenagers. There are paying sports and studying.<br><br><br>Let's start with this activity that are many admire for teens is playing sports, this activity is really wonderful because this activity let us to workout, to make us healty, not being sick esaliy. Example, for those sports that I recommed like badminton, football, swimming, basketball and other, acully every type of sport are good. I recommed this activity really much and this activity is popular because it's like doing excersie.<br><br><br>Beside, studying it's literly a really improtant activity, doing this activity in the free time it's really useful for the time that you had spend, it's will be really good for you to study in the class too. Because teens mostly study in middle school or high school. This activity seems hard but if you trying to do it, the solution would helpful a lot, so that why this activity is popular and many people recommed it.<br><br><br>In summary, I think not all the teens in Thailand like to do these activity but some of here are the better activity for teens in Thailand, instaded to play games,	232	12.0	<p>Dear Mymind<br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Free time Activities'. You wrote 232 words and achieved B2. You successfully identified two activities and discussed their popularity, which is a good start.</p>	<p>All content is relevant to the task, and you did identify two free time activities. You also explained why they are popular in Thailand and what they involve, which fully informs the reader. The essay is complete and meets the word count requirement. Studying is not typically a free time activity, but your justification for its importance makes it acceptable within the context of your essay.<br>To Improve: For a B2 level, try to develop your ideas even more. Instead of just stating facts, try to explore the deeper impact or benefits of these activities.<br>Weakness: "There are paying sports and studying." While these are valid points, the introduction could be more engaging and directly link to the essay's purpose.<br>Improved: "For many Thai teenagers, free time is a precious commodity, often filled with digital entertainment. However, beyond screen time, two profoundly beneficial activities stand out for their positive impact on youth: engaging in sports and dedicating time to academic pursuits."<br>Explanation: A more developed introduction sets a clearer context for your essay and immediately informs the reader of your main arguments, making the essay more cohesive and sophisticated.</p>	<p>You used an appropriate tone for an article and managed to communicate your straightforward ideas. However, there were some shifts between formal and informal language, which means the register wasn't always consistent. This can sometimes make it harder to keep the target reader fully engaged and clear about the essay's intended audience.<br>To Improve: Maintain a consistent register throughout your writing. Decide if you want to be more formal for a general audience or slightly more informal if addressing peers, and stick to it.<br>Weakness: "Let's start with this activity that are many admire for teens..." The use of "Let's start" is a bit informal for an essay.<br>Improved: "One highly admired activity among teenagers is playing sports..."<br>Explanation: Using more formal phrasing helps maintain a consistent register suitable for an essay and enhances the overall professionalism of your writing.</p>	<p>You organized your ideas into four paragraphs, which helps the reader follow your main points. However, the introductory paragraph didn't clearly state your main argument (thesis statement), and the topic sentences in the body paragraphs could be stronger and connect more explicitly to a central idea. Your conclusion was a bit brief and could have done more to summarise and restate your main points. While you used some basic linking words, expanding your use of cohesive devices would make the text more coherent for a B2 level.<br>To Improve: Start your introduction with a clear thesis statement. Ensure each body paragraph begins with a strong topic sentence that clearly relates to your thesis. In your conclusion, use a transition and thoroughly restate your thesis and main supporting ideas.<br>Weakness: "There are paying sports and studying." and "In summary, I think not all the teens in Thailand like to do these activity but some of here are the better activity for teens in Thailand, instaded to play games," The introduction and conclusion are a bit underdeveloped.<br>Improved: "These activities, particularly playing sports and engaging in studying, offer significant benefits that extend beyond mere leisure. They provide avenues for physical development and mental growth, making them superior alternatives to sedentary pastimes." (For thesis) and "In conclusion, while not every teenager may choose these paths, the profound advantages of sports and academic study present a compelling case for their integration into the daily lives of Thai youth, fostering well-rounded development." (For conclusion)<br>Explanation: A clear thesis statement in the introduction guides the reader, and a comprehensive conclusion effectively wraps up your arguments, making your essay feel complete and well-structured.</p>	<p>You used good vocabulary related to the topic, but there are several recurring grammar patterns that affect clarity.<br>Problem: You wrote: "it's will be really good"<br>Correction: "it will be really good"<br>Explanation: This combines "it is" (it's) with "will be," which is grammatically incorrect. In English, you use either 'it is' or 'it will be', but not both. This might happen because Thai grammar does not always explicitly distinguish between states (is) and future actions (will be) in the same way, leading to a blending of these forms in English.<br><br>Problem: You wrote: "but if you trying to do it"<br>Correction: "but if you are trying to do it"<br>Explanation: In English, for actions happening now or continuously, we use the structure 'be verb + -ing' (e.g., 'you are trying'). In Thai, verbs do not change form in this way, and an auxiliary verb like 'be' is not always required for continuous actions, which can lead to its omission in English.<br><br>Problem: You wrote: "to do these activity"<br>Correction: "to do these activities" or "to do this activity"<br>Explanation: "These" is used for plural nouns (e.g., these books), and "this" is for singular nouns (e.g., this book). "Activity" is singular, so it should be "this activity" or "these activities" if referring to more than one. In Thai, nouns do not have different singular and plural forms like in English, which causes confusion when choosing determiners like 'this' or 'these'.<br><br>YOUR ACTION PLAN<br>1. Complete all parts of writing tasks and develop each point fully<br>2. Maintain consistent formal register throughout your writing<br>3. Practice using different linking words to connect ideas<br>4. Review subject-verb agreement rules for present tense</p>	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
2379	28936	2025-11-28 10:04:25.755774+00	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. At first, after he pressed the button he need to choose the future or the passed that he want to go So he choose the passed.<br><br><br>After that, Sam thinking about to change the passed, So he went to the old house in the passed that he lived and he stayed there too many hours but he didn't know that "time travelling" have a big problem that is called "time limit" that if you live in the passed or the future in time travelling world. too many hours you will get stuck easily.<br><br><br>A little while later, Sam want to go back to the present So he grabbed a time travelling and pressed the button but it doesn't work So he very confused and pressed the button many times and then that time he realized that time travelling have a big problem again.<br><br><br>Sam need to fix it very quick but it doesn't work So he called his brother names Pea, who love to created something like him, help him fix it.<br><br><br>In the end, Sam and Pea finally finished and time travelling problem is gone! So what Sam thinking is he will tested it first before use it every time if he create the invention.	213	13.0	Hello Eng, 👋<br><br>Time travel is such a cool topic! ⏳ I really liked your idea about the 'time limit' and the danger of getting stuck. Bringing in Sam's brother, Pea, to help fix the machine was a nice touch that added a personal connection.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you polish your writing even further. 📋<br>1. <b>Past vs. Passed!</b> 📝 This is a common mistake! 'Passed' is a verb (he passed the ball). You meant 'the past' (history).<br>2. <b>Tenses!</b> 🔙 When you tell a story, use the past tense. 'He choose' -> 'He chose'.<br>3. <b>Feelings!</b> 🎭 Tell us how Sam felt! Was he scared? Relieved?	The plot about the time limit was very clever! 🧠 It gave the story real tension.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Details!</b> 👁️ You said he went to an 'old house'. Was it his house? What did it look like? Details help us see the story.<br>2. <b>Character!</b> 👤 Who is Pea? Is he older or younger? Adding a tiny detail about him would make him more real.	I understood the urgency of the problem perfectly. 🏃‍♂️<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Emotion!</b> 😢 Sam seemed very calm for someone stuck in time! Describe his panic to make it more exciting.<br>2. <b>Dialogue!</b> 🗣️ Maybe add a line of dialogue between Sam and Pea to show them working together.	You wrapped up the story nicely with 'In the end'. 🎬<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Connectors!</b> 🔗 You used 'So' a lot. Try other words like 'Therefore', 'Because of this', or 'As a result'.<br>2. <b>Paragraphing!</b> 🧱 Your paragraphs were logical. Keep it up!	You told a complete story, well done! 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections to help you:<br>1. <b>Word Choice!</b> 📝 'The passed' -> 'The <b>past</b>'.<br>2. <b>Verbs!</b> 🛠️ 'He choose' -> 'He <b>chose</b>'. 'Thinking about to change' -> 'Thinking about <b>changing</b>'.<br>3. <b>Infinitives!</b> 🔄 'Love to created' -> 'Loved to <b>create</b>'.<br>4. <b>Articles!</b> 🐜 'Grabbed a time travelling' -> 'Grabbed <b>the</b> time machine'.<br>5. <b>Tense!</b> 🔙 'Problem is gone' -> 'Problem <b>was</b> gone'.	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
2387	33957	2025-11-28 10:04:25.755774+00	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. Sam was a professor from a famous university in USA. He had been known as a person who knew the secret of time. At first, he was interested about space of time and he started to do some research about it. He did some research for the whole year and finally he created the time stop machine.<br><br><br>He thought that his invention was successful, but something went wrong. After he pressed the button, the inventor made a strange noise and then he saw that somethings in house were older.<br><br><br>A little while later, he opened the door and he knew that the time was row back to the World War 2. Many soldier tried to kill him, so he needed to hide from them. He realised that he should go back to his laboratory.<br><br><br>Finally, he found his laboratory and he tried to pressed the button to stop, but it didn't work. He was stressed and he though that he couldn't go back.<br><br><br>His friend, a professor name Flip, made a loud noise to make hi woke up. Suddenly, he knew that the all of the story was his dream. As a result, he learned that the time stop machine couldn't be build in real life.	196	16.0	Hello Peptine, 👋<br><br>What an intriguing story about the 'time stop machine'! ⏱️ I really liked the idea of the invention going wrong and sending Sam back to World War 2, only for it to be a dream. It shows great creativity in handling a classic sci-fi trope.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you polish your writing even further. 📋<br>1. <b>Verb Forms!</b> 📝 'Build' vs 'Built' can be tricky. 'Couldn't be build' -> 'Couldn't be <b>built</b>'.<br>2. <b>Word Choice!</b> 📖 'Row back' isn't correct here. 'Time rolled back' is what you mean. (Like a clock hand!).<br>3. <b>Show Feelings!</b> 😢 You said Sam was 'stressed'. How did he show that stress? A furrowed brow? Pacing? Add details!	The concept of a time-stop machine and the dream sequence was very creative and relevant! 💡<br>The World War 2 setting added an exciting element.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Elaborate!</b> 🌍 You mentioned 'many soldier tried to kill him'. Describe a quick scene – what did they look like? What weapons did they have? Make it more vivid.<br>2. <b>Pacing!</b> ⏱️ The transition into the dream felt a bit sudden. Maybe a sentence before he woke up to hint it wasn't real.	Your story was easy to follow, and the narrative flow was clear despite the time jumps. 🚀<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Engagement!</b> 🗣️ Try to make the reader feel more directly connected to Sam's emotions. Was he excited by his discovery?<br>2. <b>Impact!</b> 💥 The moment he realizes it's a dream could be made even more dramatic. How does Flip make a 'loud noise'?	You used good linking words like 'At first', 'A little while later', and 'Finally' to structure your narrative effectively. 🚧<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Variety!</b> 🔗 While your organisation is good, try to vary your linking words and sentence structures more. 'However' or 'Meanwhile' could add more flair.<br>2. <b>Paragraph Unity!</b> 🧱 Each paragraph should flow logically from the previous one, enhancing the overall cohesion of your story.	You have some great ideas in your writing! 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections to help you:<br>1. <b>Word Choice!</b> 📝 'Time was row back' -> 'Time was <b>rolled back</b>'.<br>2. <b>Verb Forms!</b> 🛠️ 'Couldn't be build' -> 'Couldn't be <b>built</b>'.<br>3. <b>Pronouns!</b> 🗣️ 'Make hi woke up' -> 'Make <b>him</b> wake up'.<br>4. <b>Articles!</b> 🐜 'Professor from a famous university' -> 'Professor from <b>a</b> famous university'.<br>5. <b>Consistency!</b> 📖 Ensure consistent spacing after punctuation and with hyphens (e.g., 'World War 2' not 'World War 2.').	5.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
