ounds, which were occasionally missing. I noticed this happening with the "n" in "imagine" (<b>/ɪˈmædʒ.ɪn/</b> → /ɪˈmædʒ.ɪ/), "l" in "school" (<b>/skuːl/</b>), and "f" in "life" (<b>/laɪf/</b>). I also noticed the "id" sound was dropped in "respected" (<b>/rɪˈspek.tɪd/</b> → /rɪˈspekt/). When these sounds are missing, it can sometimes change the meaning of your words.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Finish the Word:</b> Practice saying "imagine" (<b>/ɪˈmædʒ.ɪn/</b>) and "school" (<b>/skuːl/</b>) out loud, focusing on making the very last sound clearly audible.</li><li><b>Focus on -ed Endings:</b> When a word ends with "t" or "d" and we add -ed, like "respected" (<b>/rɪˈspek.tɪd/</b>), remember that we need to add an extra syllable.</li><li><b>Slow Down for Linking:</b> Try connecting words like "life is" or "school was" so you can practice the ending sounds while moving to the next word.</li></ul><br>You have a natural flow to your speech—keep focusing on those final sounds! 🎯	Peptine, you gave a fantastic review of Bleach that was very easy to follow! You used all the required parts of the structure, including the hook and the author's name, which made your talk complete. Your vocabulary was well-chosen for this topic, and you used clear transition words to tell the story of Ichigo. To improve, focus on your past tense endings like "named" and "written," and try to reduce word repetitions to make your speaking even smoother. You are a confident speaker who knows how to keep an audience interested! 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
963	28863	2025-07-23 15:00:49.43265+00	A Place I Love	The place that I love, called 'Salem'. Salem is one of the town in USA, which is unique because of it's history. People called it 'The town of witch hunt'. This town located next to the Attlantic Sea.<br><br><br>Let me explain more cleary for the reason that makes me love it. Salem is a charming town for it's beautiful nature. For examples the beautiful sea veiw, when I first saw it I'm feeling relax and calm. The forest and mountain veiw also! It was green everywhere, soothing waterfalls these things made me feel fresh. They reminded me that I choosed the right place to relax with my family that summer.<br><br><br>In conclusion, Salem is a charming town because of it's beautiful nature that attrach travellers to come to this town. It not just famous for it's beautiful nature, but it is famous for it's unqiue history too. Today Salem town is one of the best town in USA now	159	11.0	Namo T, you wrote about Salem and explained why you love it by talking about its history and beautiful nature. You shared how the sea, forest, and waterfalls made you feel relaxed. You did a good job of introducing the town and giving reasons. To make your writing even better, you can focus on making your sentences connect more smoothly and correcting some mistakes in spelling, punctuation, and grammar so your message is always very clear. Keep practicing, and you'll improve a lot!	Namo T, you introduced Salem as a place you love, mentioning its unique history and natural beauty. You gave examples like the sea view and waterfalls, explaining how they made you feel relaxed. You included the required points about the town and your feelings. However, the connection between the "witch hunt" history and *why* you love the place could be stronger. The essay mostly focuses on the nature aspect.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Salem is one of the town in USA, which is unique because of it's history. People called it 'The town of witch hunt'."\n    Problem: You mention the unique history, but you don't really explain *how* this history contributes to your love for the town. It feels like a separate fact rather than a reason for your affection.\n    How to fix it: You could expand on this by saying how the history makes the town feel, for example, "Salem is unique because of its fascinating history, often called 'The town of witch hunt.' This past gives the town a mysterious and interesting atmosphere that I really love to explore." This directly links the history to your feelings.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "In conclusion, Salem is a charming town because of it's beautiful nature that attrach travellers to come to this town. It not just famous for it's beautiful nature, but it is famous for it's unqiue history too. Today Salem town is one of the best town in USA now"\n    Problem: Your conclusion brings together nature and history, but the last sentence "Today Salem town is one of the best town in USA now" feels like a sudden, general statement that isn't fully supported by what you wrote before.\n    How to fix it: Make sure your conclusion summarizes your main points and connects back to your personal love for the place. You could say, "In conclusion, Salem's charming natural beauty, combined with its fascinating and unique history, makes it a truly special place that continues to attract visitors. It's a town I love because it offers both peace in nature and a captivating glimpse into the past."	You tried to write an article for a travel website, and you communicated straightforward ideas about Salem. You made an effort to hold the reader's attention by describing its beauty. However, some parts felt a bit simple or direct for an article, and the overall impression could be more engaging for a wider audience.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Let me explain more cleary for the reason that makes me love it."\n    Problem: This sentence is very direct and conversational, almost like you are speaking to the reader. It's a bit too informal for an article.\n    How to fix it: You can start this paragraph in a more descriptive way that doesn't sound like you are talking directly to the reader. For example, "Allow me to share the specific reasons that make Salem so special to me." or "My love for Salem stems from its captivating natural beauty."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "For examples the beautiful sea veiw, when I first saw it I'm feeling relax and calm. The forest and mountain veiw also!"\n    Problem: The phrasing "For examples" is incorrect, and "I'm feeling relax and calm" is a simple structure. "The forest and mountain veiw also!" feels a bit like an afterthought.\n    How to fix it: Use more precise language and combine ideas smoothly. For example, "For instance, the stunning sea view immediately made me feel relaxed and calm when I first saw it. The lush green forests and majestic mountain views also offered a sense of refreshing tranquility." This sounds more natural and descriptive.	Your essay is well-organized with a clear introduction, body, and conclusion, which helps the reader follow your ideas. You used some basic linking words, but you could use a wider range of words and phrases to connect your sentences and paragraphs more smoothly, making the flow of ideas more natural.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "This town located next to the Attlantic Sea. Let me explain more cleary for the reason that makes me love it."\n    Problem: The transition from the location of the town to explaining why you love it is a bit abrupt.\n    How to fix it: You could create a smoother link between these two ideas. For example, "This town is located next to the Atlantic Sea, and its coastal position is just one of the reasons that contribute to my deep affection for it."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "The forest and mountain veiw also! It was green everywhere, soothing waterfalls these things made me feel fresh."\n    Problem: The connection between "The forest and mountain view also!" and the following sentence is not very smooth. "These things" is a bit vague.\n    How to fix it: You could combine these thoughts with clearer transitions. For example, "Beyond the sea, the forest and mountain views are equally breathtaking. The vibrant greenery and soothing waterfalls truly made me feel refreshed and connected to nature."	You used everyday vocabulary, and your main message about Salem was understandable. However, there were many noticeable errors in spelling, punctuation, and grammar, which sometimes made your meaning unclear or difficult to read.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "it's history," "cleary," "veiw," "attrach," "unqiue," "Attlantic"\n    Problem: Words like "its" (possessive, no apostrophe), "clearly," "view," "attract," "unique," and "Atlantic" are misspelled.\n    How to fix it: Carefully proofread your writing for spelling errors. Use a dictionary or a spell checker to help you. Remember that "it's" means "it is," while "its" shows possession.\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement and Tense:\n    Example: "This town **located** next to the Attlantic Sea." and "I'm **feeling** relax and calm."\n    Problem: "Located" needs a "was" or "is" before it (e.g., "This town *is located*"). "I'm feeling" is continuous, but "I felt" or "I feel" might be more natural depending on if it's a current or past feeling. "Relax" should be an adjective "relaxed."\n    How to fix it: Pay close attention to verb forms and tenses. For example, "This town *is located* next to the Atlantic Sea." and "when I first saw it, I *felt relaxed* and calm."\n\n*   Punctuation and Capitalization:\n    Example: "The place that I love, called 'Salem'." and "The forest and mountain veiw also!"\n    Problem: The comma after "love" is not needed. The exclamation mark after "also!" is too informal for an article, and "forest" and "mountain" don't need to be capitalized.\n    How to fix it: Use commas only when necessary. Use periods at the end of most sentences in an article. Only capitalize proper nouns (like "Salem" and "USA") and the first word of a sentence.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
