yllables often have equal stress, unlike English which is stress-timed. Additionally, you sometimes confused the English /d/ sound with the Thai 'ต' sound. The English /d/ sound is made by tapping your tongue behind your top teeth without pushing it too far forward. Practising words with the /d/ sound and focusing on the natural rhythm of English words will help you sound even more like a native speaker. 👂	Hi Pea,<br><br>You did a really solid job in your speaking test! You communicated your ideas clearly and were a great partner in the discussions. You've got a good grasp of English, and with a bit more fine-tuning, you can make your speech even more fluent and natural. Keep up the excellent work! 🌟<br><br>📋 **Action Plan:**<br>*   Practise saying words with the English /d/ sound, making sure your tongue touches behind your top teeth.<br>*   Work on varying your intonation and stress, especially avoiding overstressing final syllables.<br>*   Focus on more complex grammatical structures to add variety to your sentences.<br>	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1yqGR5ip5XtVuoI2jPIboJbuDxmsnlN7a?usp=drive_link	B1	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
