of the most popular activities among Thai teenagers are playing computer games and surfing the internet because they are easy to access and help students relax."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved sentence is clearer: it uses correct word order, correct plural/singular forms and gives a brief reason which helps the reader understand why these activities are popular.<br><br>	The frequent very simple errors and unclear phrasing make it difficult for a reader to follow your ideas consistently.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Aim for clear, full sentences and avoid short fragments; keep a neutral formal tone for an essay (no text-message abbreviations).<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "I will have exam that it can change my life in m 4-6."<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "I will have an important exam in M.4–6 that could affect my future, so I am studying science and maths very hard."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved sentence is grammatically complete and more formal. It makes the purpose of the sentence clear and fits the essay tone expected in school assessments.<br><br>	You used paragraphing, but the essay does not meet the required four-paragraph structure and does not have a clear concluding paragraph. Topic sentences are weak or missing at the start of body paragraphs.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Use four paragraphs: (1) introduction with a clear thesis sentence, (2–3) two body paragraphs each starting with a clear topic sentence, (4) conclusion that restates the thesis and main supporting points.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> The essay ends with an unfinished sentence: "but it can make effect in"<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "In conclusion, playing games and surfing the internet are common ways for Thai teenagers to relax after studying, but it is important to use them in moderation to avoid negative effects."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This concluding sentence restates the thesis and summarises the supporting ideas, giving the reader a clear ending and improving cohesion.<br><br>	You show some useful vocabulary (names of games and apps), but very frequent spelling and grammar errors make the text hard to read. The essay also lacks the relative clauses and passive structures expected at higher levels. The main challenges are with a few key grammar rules.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "There have many computer games that we plays, such as Valorant, Roblox and ROV."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "There are many computer games that we play, such as Valorant, Roblox and ROV."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai verbs are not changed for person or number, so Thai learners often add or miss verb endings in English (subject–verb agreement). In English we say "we play" (not "we plays") and use "there are" for plural subjects. (Pattern reference: learner difficulty with verb agreement and plural marking.)<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "A Second successfal activities is sulfing internet, such as IG, Fackbook and Tiktok."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "A second successful activity is surfing the internet, such as Instagram, Facebook and TikTok."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai has no articles and different plural rules, so learners often use "a" with plural nouns or write incorrect plural/singular forms. Also spelling and word formation (e.g., "sulfing" → "surfing", "Fackbook" → "Facebook") need attention. (Pattern reference: articles and plural/word-form interference.)<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "It famous by eazy to downlode, eazy to play and can play with friend."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "They are famous because they are easy to download, easy to play, and can be played with friends."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai allows long clauses and often does not mark auxiliaries or passive forms the same way as English. This can lead to missing verbs/auxiliaries and run-on or fragmented sentences in English. Practise using full clauses (subject + verb) and correct passive/intransitive patterns. (Pattern reference: run-on clauses and word-form/auxiliary omission.)<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Every day this week: correct and rewrite 5 sentences from your essay focusing on verb agreement, article use, and correct plural/singular forms (use a notebook).<br>2. Spelling drill: choose 10 commonly-misspelt words from this essay (e.g., system, different, surfing, successful, Facebook, download, easy, teenage, friends, play) and write each word 5 times with its correct sentence.<br>3. Weekly writing practice: write one 140–160 word essay following the 4-paragraph plan; include at least one relative clause and one passive sentence. Ask a teacher to check for the three error types above and correct them.<br><br>	3.0	1.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
