essay style, but register and clarity vary across the essay.  <br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Use clear academic/essay phrasing in the introduction and avoid short, fragmented questions as opening lines.  <br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "Do you want to know? What's activities Thai teenagers do in a free time."  <br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "This essay explains what activities Thai teenagers do in their free time and why they enjoy them."  <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved version matches an essay register and clearly states the purpose; it avoids informal question openings and helps the reader know what to expect next.<br><br>	You have paragraph breaks, and each body paragraph is about one activity, which is good. However the introduction lacks a clear thesis and the conclusion is incomplete, so the whole essay does not feel finished.  <br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> End the introduction with one clear thesis sentence that states the two activities you will discuss; ensure the conclusion restates the thesis and summarizes the reasons.  <br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> The introduction does not end in a clear thesis (e.g., the ideas are vague and not signposted).  <br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "In this essay I will discuss two popular free-time activities among Thai teenagers—playing computer games and taking part in sports—and explain why many choose them."  <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This model thesis sentence tells the reader exactly what the essay will cover and links directly to the two body paragraphs, creating clear organisation and flow.<br><br>	You have one clear accurate sentence, but there are many errors with articles, verb forms, plural/singular agreement, and word choice. The main challenges are with forming correct noun phrases and verb agreement compared with Thai patterns.<br><br>\n\n❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "What's activities Thai teenagers do in a free time."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "What activities do Thai teenagers do in their free time?"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai learners often transfer Thai word order into English. In English, questions need the auxiliary verb "do" and the plural noun does not use "a". This matches the common Thai→English interference pattern of incorrect question structure and article/number usage.<br><br>\n\n❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Narrowing to Thailand, thai teens was really sophisticated in feelling."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Focusing on Thailand, Thai teenagers are often sophisticated in their feelings."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This shows errors in subject-verb agreement and spelling. Thai does not mark verb tense/agreement the same way as English, so learners sometimes use the wrong verb form ("was" instead of "are") and misspell words ("feelling" → "feeling").<br><br>\n\n❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "In they family were have a difference activities to do in free time."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "In their families, there are different activities to do in free time."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai does not use articles and plural markers like English, so learners often use wrong pronouns ("they" vs "their"), incorrect verb constructions ("were have"), and wrong use of "a" with plural nouns. This maps to the common Thai interference pattern for articles and plural marking.<br><br>\n\n❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "It's have another famous game more than these too."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "There are other famous games besides these."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The student used "It's have" (wrong subject and verb). Thai often omits auxiliary verbs or uses different subjects, causing incorrect English auxiliaries and subject-verb forms.<br><br>\n\n❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "like to play a computer games, which is a FPS games, except Valorant." <br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "like to play computer games, such as FPS games, except Valorant." <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> English does not use "a" with plural "games" and needs consistent number agreement. Thai L1 speakers sometimes misunderstand singular/plural marking and article use.<br><br>\n\n❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Because it really funny you can call team when you play."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Because it is really fun; you can call a team when you play."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This contains a missing verb ("is") and the wrong adjective form ("funny" vs "fun"). Thai learners often translate adjectives directly and may omit the verb "to be," reflecting Thai sentence patterns where a copula can be different or optional.<br><br>\n\n❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "In every sports have a rules and it help teens to be conformist in real life."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "In every sport there are rules, and they help teenagers to become more disciplined in real life." <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This shows errors in countability ("sports" vs "sport"), subject-verb agreement ("have" vs "there are") and word choice ("conformist" is not the correct collocation). Thai influence often causes mismatches in plurality and verb forms.<br><br>\n\n<b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Focus practice on articles and plural forms: write 10 short sentences describing activities and check each noun for correct article/plural use. (10 minutes daily for 5 days)<br>2. Practice subject-verb agreement and auxiliaries: transform 10 simple statements into questions (e.g., "They play games." → "Do they play games?") and check with a grammar key. (15 minutes, three times this week)<br>3. Edit your essay: rewrite the introduction and conclusion using the model sentences above; then underline every verb and noun to check agreement and article use. (One full revision session)<br><br>	4.0	3.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
1804	28936	2025-09-14 09:02:53.196118+00	Term 1 Exam Question 2	Hi, this announcement in an international online magazine is very interesting! Its about Having pets good or bad idea, So let me answer or give an opinion on having pets. I think having pet could be a great idea but to some people i think 50%.<br><br><br>I mean 50% 50% some people maybe good idea or bad idea but i think its a good idea because when i'm alone I can pick my dog and go for a walk with it every time when i'm alone but in a bad idea I surely that it could be about a time that you don't have with it to feed, to play with it and sometime if you let a dog or another pet to go inside the house and you that have a personal room for pet<br><br><br>I think it's very bad idea too because if a dog don't have a sand or something that a pet need to be a toilet room your house could be dirty. So you need a time for a pet it could be good idea.<br><br><br>In conclusion, good or bad idea its up to you may be if you want to have a pet you need to have time like my grandfather, who always, play with a pet everyday for me its good to have a pet so you have a friend when you are alone like me. When Its a holiday i'm always play with my cat outside the house.<br><br><br>But remember that if you want to have a pet you need to have time with it because if you don't time maybe your pet will don't any food or something. So remember it bye for now	283	8.0	Hello Eng! It's clear that you've put a good effort into thinking about the topic of having pets. You've made a great start at expressing your ideas, and it's wonderful to see you sharing your thoughts. Keep working on making your sentences a little clearer, and I am certain your writing will continue to develop well. 📋 Here are three things you can do to help your writing improve:<br>✅ Carefully re-read your work to check that every sentence clearly expresses one idea.<br>✅ Focus on using linking words like 'however', 'therefore', or 'in addition' to connect your thoughts smoothly between sentences and paragraphs.<br>✅ Practice building different kinds of sentences so your writing is more varied and interesting to read.	Eng, you did a good job of addressing the task by talking about both the good and not-so-good aspects of having pets. It's helpful that you included the idea of companionship, like taking your dog for a walk when you're alone, and playing with your cat on holiday. This shows you understand the positive side of pet ownership. 😊 To make this section even stronger, try to develop your ideas a little more evenly. For instance, you discussed the advantages of having a pet, but the disadvantages were mainly about the owner's time and potential mess. You could think of other challenges, such as the cost of food and vet visits, or how pets might affect travel plans. Thai learners sometimes focus heavily on one aspect, so try to balance your arguments more fully, giving equal attention to both sides of the discussion.	You've tried to write in the style of an article for a magazine, which is a good step. You've communicated some straightforward ideas, like the need for time if you own a pet. 👍 To truly hold a reader's attention in a magazine article, you'll want to make sure your introduction grabs them right away and that your points flow logically. For example, instead of starting with 'Hi, this announcement in an international online magazine is very interesting!', you could try an engaging question or a strong opening statement about pets. Think about how a good newspaper article starts. Thai learners sometimes use informal greetings in more formal contexts, so practicing opening lines for different types of writing can be very helpful.	You've clearly tried to structure your thoughts with an introduction and a conclusion, and that's a positive step. You've also made an effort to connect some ideas, for example, by bringing up 'good idea' and 'bad idea.' However, sometimes the connections between your sentences and ideas are a little tricky to follow. 🤔 For instance, you could use more connecting words to show how one idea leads to the next. Instead of starting a new thought abruptly, try phrases like 'Furthermore, another advantage is...' or 'On the other hand, a challenge arises when...'. This helps the reader follow your argument more easily. Thai learners sometimes translate directly from their first language, which can sometimes make English sentences feel a little disconnected. Focusing on clear linking phrases will help a lot.	You've used some basic vocabulary that is suitable for the topic, such as 'pick my dog' and 'play with my cat.' That's a good foundation. However, there are quite a few grammatical errors that sometimes make your message difficult to understand. For example, sentences like 'I surely that it could be about a time that you don't have with it to feed, to play with it' or 'if a dog don't have a sand or something that a pet need to be a toilet room your house could be dirty' contain several errors. Let's look at a few common areas:<br>1.  **Subject-verb agreement:** You wrote 'if a dog don't have,' but it should be 'if a dog doesn't have' because 'dog' is singular. Remember that with 'he,' 'she,' and 'it,' we use 'does not' or the 's' ending on verbs.<br>2.  **Sentence structure and clarity:** Some of your sentences are quite long and combine many ideas, which makes them hard to follow. Try to break complex ideas into shorter, clearer sentences. For instance, the sentence 'But remember that if you want to have a pet you need to have time with it because if you don't time maybe your pet will don't any food or something' could be rephrased as: 'Remember that if you want a pet, you need to have time for it. If you don't have enough time, your pet might not get food or attention.'<br>3.  **Word choice and prepositions:** Phrases like 'personal room for pet' or 'have a sand' could be improved. You might say 'a designated space for your pet' or 'a litter box.' Thai learners sometimes have difficulty with prepositions and articles (a/an/the) and direct translations, so focusing on these areas will greatly improve your accuracy.<br>Keep practicing, Eng! Paying attention to these areas will make your writing much clearer and more precise. 😊	3.0	2.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
