 Thank you.	353	10.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	FIRST	<p>Tatar, sometimes your pronunciation made it a little tricky to understand everything you said. Your voice was quite flat, which can make it harder for listeners to follow along. Try to vary the 'tune' of your voice (intonation) more, making some parts higher and some lower, just like music! 🎶</p><br><p>Also, you tended to separate words too much. For instance, "was a good student" sounded like individual words instead of flowing smoothly together. English often links words, especially short ones like "was" and "a," so practicing connecting them will make your speech sound much more natural.</p><br><p>It's common for English learners to overstress the last part of words, like in "England" /ˈɪŋ.ɡlənd/ or "information" /ɪn.fəˈmeɪ.ʃən/. In English, one part of a word usually sounds stronger than the others.</p><br><p>We noticed a few specific sounds that could use some extra practice! For "discover," the vowel sound in the middle should be like the 'u' in 'up' /dɪsˈkʌvər/, rather than an 'aw' sound. Also, a common habit is to drop the ends of words, especially in past tense verbs like "started" (make sure to say the /ɪd/ sound clearly!). Sometimes, you also missed the very last sound of words, like the /f/ in "life" or the /z/ in "was." Practicing these tricky sounds and really focusing on saying every sound at the end of words will help a lot. Listening carefully to English speakers and trying to copy exactly how they say things can be a fun way to improve! 🗣️👂</p>	<p>Hello Tatar! 👋 You did a really good job introducing Sir Isaac Newton and setting out what you were going to talk about. It's clear you put thought into structuring your presentation, and you covered most of the important points about your hero! ✨ That's fantastic effort!</p><br><p>To help you get even better, let's focus on a few things. You can make your sentences smoother by paying close attention to grammar, and by trying out new words to make your descriptions even more exciting! Also, practicing how you say words, especially endings, will really help everyone understand your great ideas even more clearly. Keep practicing, and you'll see amazing progress! 👍</p>	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
