casionally dropped final consonant clusters, such as the /st/ in "first" /fɜːst/. Your voice was also extremely quiet, so it was hard to hear in some places.<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b><br><ul><li><b>Practice Sentence Stress:</b> Try to identify the most important words in a sentence and give them more "life" by slightly increasing your volume and pitch on them.</li><li><b>Smooth Out Hesitations:</b> Practice reading short paragraphs aloud multiple times to build "muscle memory" for common phrases, which will help reduce pauses.</li><li><b>Focus on Final Consonants:</b> Slow down slightly when you reach the end of a word to ensure sounds like /t/, /d/, or /st/ are fully voiced.</li></ul><br>Keep practicing your projection and intonation, and you will become a much more confident speaker! 🌟	Tatar, you gave a clear introduction and used a very creative hook to start your review! You used good vocabulary to describe the action and the setting in South Korea. To improve, remember to include the author's name and spend more time explaining the most exciting parts of the plot. Focus on your subject-verb agreement and making sure plural words end with an "s" sound. Speaking for a bit longer and adding more detail to your opinion will help you reach a higher B1 level! 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
