ormal. Try to rephrase these thoughts to maintain a more consistent academic style, ensuring your ideas are communicated with clarity and conviction for your target reader.	Your essay has a clear beginning, middle, and end, which is a great foundation, Grace. You've used some simple linking words to connect your sentences, like "Because" and "In conclusion," which helps the reader follow your main points. 👍 To improve this further, think about using a wider range of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just "And," you could use "Furthermore" or "In addition" to introduce new points, making your writing more varied and sophisticated. Also, consider how each sentence and paragraph flows into the next. Ensuring every transition is smooth will help your whole essay feel more interconnected and easier to read. Thai learners sometimes use simpler linking words, and expanding this range can really elevate your writing.	You use many everyday words appropriately, and it's easy to understand what you are trying to say, Grace. You've tried to use some more complex sentence structures, which is a good step forward. However, there are a few areas where focusing on grammar could make your writing much clearer and more precise. For example, I noticed a few instances of subject-verb agreement, such as "no one are there" instead of "no one is there," and "pets species are" instead of "pet species is" or "many pet species are." 🧐 Also, keep an eye out for correct verb forms like "walk forward to you and sitting" which should be "walk forward to you and sit." And do a final check for spelling, like "fasinating" instead of "fascinating," and "apperiance" instead of "appearance." These small adjustments will significantly refine your communication. Often, Thai learners might transfer grammatical structures from their first language, so it's a good idea to pay extra attention to English-specific rules like subject-verb agreement and plural forms.	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Ter