ts readability and dramatic impact.	You crafted a very relevant and genuinely frightening narrative with strong imagery and a clever news report twist at the end. 🌟 The concept of the 'Honey man' legend coming true was very compelling.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Sensory Details!</b> 👁️ You described his hook hand. What did the 'red lique' look like? Was it sticky? Fresh? What did 'Honey man' smell like? Adding more sensory details would heighten the horror.</li><li><b>The News Report!</b> 📰 The details in the news report are grim. Could you add one more detail, perhaps about the victim's age or how the 'Honey man' might have entered the house?</li>	You successfully conveyed a strong horror tone, building suspense and culminating in a shocking, tragic ending. 😱 The shift to the news reporter's perspective was a bold and effective choice.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Clarity in Perspective!</b> 🧩 The switch to the news reporter is powerful, but ensure the transition is as smooth as possible.</li>	Your story follows a clear chronological progression of events, from the initial sighting to the hiding, the death, and the news report. ⏳<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Paragraphing is CRITICAL!</b> 🧱 This is the single biggest area for improvement. Splitting your long text into logical paragraphs would dramatically enhance readability, dramatic effect, and overall structure. Each major shift in time or action should get a new paragraph.</li><li><b>Sentence Structure!</b> 🏗️ Some sentences are very long and contain multiple ideas. Breaking these into shorter, distinct sentences would make your story easier to follow.</li>	You used evocative vocabulary like 'creepy', 'disbelief', 'legend', and 'rapidly'. 📚<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Creapy' -> '<b>creepy</b>', 'disapear' -> '<b>disappear</b>', 'suptonse' -> '<b>substance</b>', 'comture' -> '<b>captured</b>', 'avanturelly' -> '<b>eventually</b>'.</li><li><b>Grammar!</b> 🛠️ 'Could been die' -> 'could <b>have died</b>'. 'His hook hand, which have a red lique' -> 'which <b>had</b> a red <b>liquid</b>'.</li><li><b>Verb Forms!</b> ⏳ 'His name is 'Honey man' a name sapose to be sweet but No, not at all. He give me a creapy smile. His hook hand, which have a red lique drop rapidly, as he stand.' -> 'His name is 'Honey man', a name <b>supposed</b> to be sweet, but no, not at all. He <b>gave</b> me a creepy smile. His hook hand, which <b>had</b> red <b>liquid dropping</b> rapidly, as he <b>stood</b>.'</li>	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
