y. ✍️	Your essay is generally well-organised, Fergie, with a clear introduction, separate paragraphs for each of your ideas, and a conclusion. You've used paragraph breaks to separate your points, which helps the reader follow along. To make your writing flow even more smoothly, try to vary the ways you link your sentences and paragraphs. For instance, instead of starting paragraphs with "Firstly," "Besides," and "Lastly," you could try phrases like "To begin with," "Another important aspect is," or "Finally, consider how..." Using a wider range of linking words makes your writing more sophisticated and easier to read. ↔️	This is an area where you can make significant improvements very quickly, Fergie. The most noticeable issue in your writing is the lack of full stops at the end of many sentences, which means your essay reads like one very long sentence in places. This makes it quite difficult for the reader to understand where one idea finishes and another begins. For example, you wrote: "Friends mostly affect teenagers choices more than parents for example Like clothes and fashion studying and what to eat." This is actually several ideas strung together. You could split this into two or three clear sentences with full stops.<br><br>Another area to watch is subject-verb agreement. Often, when you were talking about "friends" (plural), you used "effects" instead of "affect," like in "friends really effects." Remember that "friends affect" (no 's' on the verb) and "a friend affects" (with an 's' on the verb). Many Thai learners sometimes find verb endings tricky because the 's' for plural nouns and third-person singular verbs isn't used in the same way in Thai.<br><br>Finally, try to expand your vocabulary beyond "effect" and "influence." You used these words quite a lot. For example, instead of "It effect are influences," you could say, "These are influences." Or, in your conclusion, instead of "friends really effects on teenagers choices ever More than their own p