here you or your friend felt surprised or tried to get closer. Think about how the visit unfolded moment by moment, like a short film playing out for your reader. 🎬	You've managed to share your main ideas effectively, and we understand what happened. That's a great start. However, to really grab and keep your reader's attention throughout your story, try to think about how a storyteller guides their audience. You can do this by varying your sentence beginnings, making sure your conclusion feels like a natural wrap-up rather than an abrupt ending, and perhaps including a short, impactful sentence to start each new point. Imagine you're telling your story to a friend. You might pause or change your voice to highlight different parts. Your writing can do the same. 😊	Your ideas are all connected, which is a good foundation. However, presenting your entire story in one continuous block makes it a bit challenging for the reader to follow the different parts of your experience. Imagine a book without any paragraph breaks—it would be very hard to read. Each time you introduce a new idea, such as *why* Emma Watson was invited or *what* activities she did, it's a good opportunity to start a new paragraph. This helps your reader see the structure of your thoughts and makes your writing much clearer and more pleasant to read. Using linking words like "First," "Next," or "After that" can also help guide your reader from one event to the next. 📝	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit your story well, like "actor" and "film." It's clear you're building a strong vocabulary. We've noticed a few areas where we can really make your writing sparkle. Firstly, with **capitalisation**, remember that names like "Hermione Granger" and titles of films like "Harry Potter Film" always start with a capital letter, as do the first words of every sentence. Also, words like "school" generally don't need a capital letter unless they are part of a specific name (e.g., "Hogwarts School"). Secondly, **spelling** is an area where a little extra care will make a big difference. For instance, words like "surprisingly" (not "surprisely"), "speech" (not "speece"), and "autograph" (not "auto ghrape") are common words that you can easily check. Many Thai learners sometimes face challenges with capitalisation and spelling because the rules in English can be quite different from Thai writing conventions, where capital letters aren't used in the same way. Keeping a small notebook of commonly misspelled words or using a dictionary when you're unsure will really help you improve. Lastly, watch out for grammatical structures like "actor to play"; a better way to say this would be "actor *who played*." Keep practicing, and your language will grow even stronger. 💪	4.0	2.0	2.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
