nse consistency. Keep reflecting on your experiences; your voice is interesting, and with a little more practice, your writing will become even more impactful.	Racing, you effectively address the topic of fitting in by drawing on your personal experiences and identifying reasons for your past difficulties, such as impulsive behavior and a different way of thinking. You use examples involving your friends to illustrate these points. However, your essay significantly exceeds the recommended word count of 140-190 words for this task (your essay is 280 words). In a timed exam setting, this would likely mean you have included too much detail or some content that is less central to fully informing the target reader within the expected length. Practicing writing concisely within word limits is important for exam success.	You use the style of a personal reflection to share your experiences with fitting in. You successfully communicate straightforward ideas about your feelings and challenges. However, at times, the clarity is affected by phrasing that could be more precise, which might make it harder for the reader to fully grasp your meaning or hold their attention consistently. For instance, the description of "voices talking overlapping each other" is a bit unclear and could be expressed more straightforwardly to ensure the target reader understands the intended idea without difficulty.	You structure your essay around your personal journey with fitting in, moving from past struggles to present understanding. The ideas are generally connected, but the organization could be improved with clearer paragraph breaks to separate distinct points. For example, creating new paragraphs for each main reason you struggled to fit in or for each example you provide would make the text easier to follow. Using a greater variety and more accurate application of linking words and phrases would also help create a smoother flow between your sentences and ideas.	You use everyday vocabulary appropriate for discussing personal experiences and social interactions. You demonstrate the ability to use a range of simple and some more complex grammatical structures. While there are a number of errors, they generally do not prevent the reader from understanding your meaning. For example, in the sentence "I often act before I think which makes a lot of people around me felt a bit annoyed by it," there is a tense inconsistency; since you are describing past experiences, it would be more grammatically correct to use the past tense, such as "which **made** a lot of people around me **feel** a bit annoyed by it." Another instance is "if I was lend an opportunity," which should be "if I was lent an opportunity" or "if I were offered an opportunity." Focusing on improving control over verb tenses and maintaining consistent tense throughout your narrative will significantly enhance the accuracy and fluency of your writing.	3.0	2.0	2.0	3.0	Writing	Formative	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
