icantly impacts its coherence and makes it challenging for the reader to follow the progression of ideas. For instance, the first two large paragraphs cover multiple distinct ideas (general features, specific highlights, personal connection, past vs. present) without clear separation. To achieve a score of 5, you should organise your text into well-structured, distinct paragraphs, using a wider variety of cohesive devices and organisational patterns to create a more fluent, logical, and easily digestible piece of writing.	You use everyday vocabulary generally appropriately for the topic of describing a city, such as \\"small city,\\" \\"temples,\\" \\"markets,\\" \\"bird park,\\" \\"pomelo,\\" \\"food,\\" \\"holidays,\\" \\"grandparent,\\" \\"local shop,\\" \\"air pollution,\\" and \\"traffic pollution.\\" However, there is pervasive evidence of very basic grammatical forms used with limited control, and numerous errors that often impede communication. Examples include: \\"city name Chainat, where is\\" (city named Chainat, which is), \\"It have many thing\\" (It has many things), \\"most bigest\\" (biggest), \\"So this make I know. What is it.\\" (This is why I know what it is), \\"pomelo that don't same in other city\\" (pomelo that isn't the same as in other cities), \\"pize of food is lower\\" (price of food is lower), \\"but It is same food\\" (but the food is the same), \\"In the fusture life\\" (In the future, life), \\"it have only\\" (it had only), \\"many thing, change\\" (many things have changed), \\"KFC open in it\\" (KFC has opened there), \\"Lotus that come and open in it\\" (Lotus that has come and opened there). These errors are very noticeable and frequently make the meaning unclear or require significant effort from the reader to understand. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to use a range of vocabulary and grammatical forms with control and flexibility, with only occasional errors that do not impede communication. For a score of 1, meaning 