with clear examples, just like you did for clothes and studying.	You did a good job of covering all the points in the question, Rome, and you even came up with your own interesting idea about "life style" for the third part! That shows you're really thinking about the prompt. 😊 Just remember to develop all your points fully. Your third point felt a little unfinished, and giving a clearer example there would really strengthen your argument. For instance, when you talk about how friends influence studying, think about providing a detailed example of *what* a friend teaches or *how* they help, rather than just that they do. Many students, especially here in Thailand, might find it tricky to fully develop their "own idea" point within a tight word limit, so it’s something we can practise together.	You've made a good start at writing in a formal way for an academic essay, which is exactly what’s needed for this type of task. 👍 Your essay’s purpose is quite clear. To really improve here, try to use more formal language and sentence structures throughout. For example, instead of saying "a lot of teenagers have interest," you could say "many teenagers are interested" or "a significant number of teenagers are interested." Practising more formal ways to express your ideas will help your essay sound more sophisticated and suitable for an academic audience. Sometimes, we get used to speaking informally with our friends, and it’s a natural habit to bring that into writing, but for essays, we want to try for a more academic tone.	You've made a great start by dividing your essay into clear paragraphs for each main point, which makes it easy for the reader to follow your argument. You’ve also used linking words like "secondly" to connect your ideas, which is super helpful! 🙌 To make your essay flow even better, try to vary your linking words a bit more and ensure your ideas transition smoothly from one to the next within each paragraph. Think about how each sentence builds on the last. For example, you could use words like "consequently," "furthermore," or "in addition" to add variety. Sometimes, in Thai, sentences can be a bit more direct in their linking, so it’s a good challenge to work on those smoother connections in English.	You’ve used a good range of everyday words to express your ideas, and your overall message comes across well. That’s a real positive! 👍 However, there are a few areas in your grammar that we can work on to make your writing even clearer and more accurate.<br>\nFirstly, watch out for **subject-verb agreement**. For example, when you wrote "friend is more effective," because "friend" is singular, the verb "is" matches. But if you’re talking about "friends" (plural), the verb needs to match too, so it should be "friends *are* more effective." This is a common one for many learners, as Thai grammar doesn't always have this kind of agreement.<br>\nSecondly, let's pay close attention to **articles** (a, an, the). Sometimes you left them out when they were needed, or used them in a way that felt a little off. For instance, you wrote "in choices of clothes and fashion" – it would be more natural to say "in *the* choices of clothes and fashion" or simply "in choices regarding clothes and fashion." Getting those little words right can make a big difference to how natural your English sounds!<br>\nFinally, try to make sure your **sentences are complete and fully developed**. Your final paragraph started a good point, but it was cut short. Making sure each sentence fully expresses an idea and connects clearly to the next one will really strengthen your writing. Sometimes, we might have an idea in our head but not quite get it all down on paper, so taking an extra moment to complete your thoughts will help.	3.0	2.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
