 follow!<br>- Check Your Basics: After writing, read through your work carefully. Look for common mistakes like verb agreements (e.g., "teenager don't" should be "teenagers don't" or "a teenager doesn't"), simple past tense forms, and making sure your sentences connect smoothly.	You had some interesting thoughts about how parents and teenagers interact, especially with clothes and studying. It's clear you're thinking about important family topics! 😊 To score even higher, it's really important to directly answer the exact question the exam asks. The question was about whether friends or parents have more effect on choices, and your essay focused more on problems between parents and teenagers. For example, when you wrote about clothes, you could have discussed how a teenager might choose clothes based on what their friends wear versus what their parents allow. Next time, try to keep the main question in your mind for every point you make!	You shared your opinions, which is a good start! However, for an essay in an exam like this, we need to try and sound a bit more formal and objective, like you're presenting a balanced argument, rather than just talking about personal feelings. 🤔 It felt like you were telling off parents a little, which made it less like an essay. For instance, instead of saying "I advice many parents to don't busy on their assessary," you could say, "It could be suggested that parents might consider giving teenagers more freedom with their choices." Many Thai learners find it tricky to shift from informal chatting to more formal academic writing, so practising different ways to express your ideas will be very helpful.	You did a good job of using paragraphs to separate your ideas, and you had a clear introduction and conclusion! That's a solid foundation. 👏 To improve, think about how each sentence and idea flows into the next within a paragraph, and how paragraphs connect to each other. For example, using words like "However," "Therefore," or "In contrast" can help link your ideas more smoothly. When you're talking about "our parents" and then "their parents," it can be a little confusing, so try to keep your pronouns consistent. Often, Thai learners sometimes translate ideas directly, which can make the connections feel a little less natural in English.	You used some good everyday words to express your points, which is fantastic! Keep building your vocabulary. 😄 However, there were quite a few grammar and spelling mistakes that made your message a bit difficult to understand sometimes. For instance, "more of teenager don't have a good relation ship to our parents" could be "many teenagers don't have a good relationship with their parents." Also, "she was anoryed because her mom told that she worm a short trousers" should be "she was annoyed because her mom told her she wore short trousers." Thai learners often face challenges with verb agreement, prepositions, and choosing the correct tense, so really focusing on these areas will make a huge difference to your score and how clearly you can express yourself. Remember to proofread for these common errors! ✨	2.0	1.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
