1148	29045	2025-08-29 09:26:19.213963+00	Free time Activities	Nowadays, Thai teens always on thier phone, but in my childhood we used to do lots of things. Before heading to childhood memory I want to show the importance of "Free time activities for teens especially in Thailand". Back then when the smart phone came out we didn't care much so, we used that free time without phone to do some activities.<br><br><br>Firstly, when it comes to free time activities For Thai teens, mostly for boys we used to played soccer. A lot of time when the san almost set we would play soccer outside with friends. It's popular especially for boys because it's fun and make us stronger, back then we all want to make it to pro.<br><br><br>lastly, chess. Chess, one of the most boring games for teens these days, it was one of the activities that I did when I was 10 until now. It's not really popular because for some people it's too hard or boring, but it make us to use our brain to predict the opponent's.<br><br><br>In conclusion, nowadays parents make their kids to do some activities in their free time to avoid being to much on the phone, which is one of the most teens problems we've seen. There are tons of interesting activities to cure your boredom - instead of being on the phone all days so, why don't you try something new to make your life better.	236	13.0	Well done: you chose two different activities (soccer and chess) and used personal examples that make the essay engaging. You wrote 236 words and achieved B2.	You identify two activities (soccer and chess) and give reasons for each, so the reader is informed. However, the introduction does not finish with a clear thesis that names both activities, and some reasons are short or repeated.\n👉 To Improve: End the introduction with a clear thesis naming both activities and add one specific detail or example for each activity.\n❌ Weakness: "Back then when the smart phone came out we didn't care much so, we used that free time without phone to do some activities."\n✨ Improved: "Back then, before smartphones became common, I spent my free time playing soccer with friends and learning chess at home because these activities kept me active and helped me think more carefully."\n💡 Explanation: A clear thesis listing both activities helps the reader know what to expect. Developing each activity with a detail (when/where/with whom) makes your reasons stronger.	Your tone is mostly appropriate for an essay but sometimes slips into conversational language, which weakens the formal/neutral register expected in a First exam.\n👉 To Improve: Keep a steady semi-formal tone and avoid fragments or very colloquial phrases.\n❌ Weakness: "lastly, chess. Chess, one of the most boring games for teens these days..."\n✨ Improved: "Finally, chess, which many teens find boring, is an activity I have enjoyed since I was ten because it trains concentration and planning."\n💡 Explanation: A consistent essay register (not too chatty) helps the examiner follow and respect your argument.	You used four paragraphs, which meets the form requirement. But the introduction lacks a clear thesis, the chess paragraph begins weakly, and topic sentences should link more clearly to the thesis.\n👉 To Improve: Make the introduction end with a thesis that names both activities; start each body paragraph with a clear topic sentence; begin the conclusion with a transition and restate the thesis and main reasons.\n❌ Weakness: Intro ends without explicit thesis; second body begins: "lastly, chess."\n✨ Improved intro ending: "In this essay I will describe two free-time activities I enjoy—playing soccer and playing chess—and explain why I chose them."\n✨ Improved topic sentence for chess paragraph: "Another activity I enjoy is chess, because it improves my thinking and planning skills."\n💡 Explanation: Clear topic sentences and a concluding restatement make your essay easier to follow and show good essay organisation.	You use everyday vocabulary well, but there are recurring grammar, spelling and form errors that affect clarity. Also, the task asked for relative clauses and passive structures; these are largely missing. Below are the three most urgent language problems with Thai-specific explanations and corrections.\n\n1) Problem (quote): "Nowadays, Thai teens always on thier phone,"\nWhy (Thai interference): Thai often omits the verb "to be" and does not mark plural with -s. Learners commonly transfer this pattern into English and omit "are" or the plural -s. Spelling errors (thier) are also frequent because English letter order differs from Thai sound mapping.\nCorrection: "Nowadays, Thai teens are always on their phones."\n\n2) Problem (quote): "we used to played soccer."\nWhy (Thai interference): Thai verbs don't change form for tense in the same way as English. After "used to" English requires the base verb, but Thai learners often add past -ed, producing incorrect verb forms.\nCorrection: "we used to play soccer."\n\n3) Problem (quote): "it make us to use our brain to predict the opponent's."\nWhy (Thai interference): Thai does not mark subject–verb agreement in the same way, and infinitive/particle usage differs. This causes errors with verb agreement (make → makes), unnecessary "to" before a base verb, and incorrect possessive/plural forms.\nCorrection: "it makes us use our brains to predict our opponents' moves."\n\nNotes on required structures:\n- Relative clauses: I did not find clear relative clauses (which/that/who) used to add information. Try adding one or two (e.g., "a game that improves concentration").\n- Passive structures: No passive sentences were used. At B2, include 1–2 passive forms where natural (e.g., "many activities are organised by schools").	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
