ese final sounds are very important for clarity.</p><p>Another area to focus on is word stress. English has variable stress, unlike Thai. I noticed you sometimes overstressed final syllables, for example, in the word "activity" /ækˈtɪv.ɪ.ti/, the stress should be on the second syllable, not the last.</p><p>To improve your pronunciation, focus on clearly articulating final consonants. You can practice this by exaggerating the final sounds of words. Also, pay attention to English word stress patterns; try listening to native speakers and mimicking their pronunciation. Learning about stress-timed rhythm in English will also help you link sounds more naturally. Keep practicing, and you will see improvement! ✨</p>	<p>Hello Nam! 👋 Great job on your presentation about Yok Thanalop! It's clear you put thought into choosing a hero who means a lot to you. You shared some really interesting details about her activism and the challenges she faced. Keep up the good work!</p><p>To make your next presentation even stronger, focus on making sure your sentences flow smoothly and that you pronounce all the sounds at the ends of words. Don't worry, with a little practice, you'll see a big improvement! 💪</p>	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
