ke who admire fame or talent, he is honored and gave one life to protect the people. He became a brave person that show a true hero.<br><br><br>Okay, and I will... it's time to conclusion. I will wrap up all my talk today. Uh... I talk about who is he. Next, I discuss what he did, and finally, why he is a hero. So Worachit Non Siwan is a military soldier that was killed from the... from the shrapnel while he doing his job at frontline area. So the most importantly, I give you a very strong message that the man who protect the nation. Teenager should follow and interested this because this and news because they are help us... it benefit to our daily life and all these are help up to grow up well with knowledge. Thank you.	403	11.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	FIRST	<p>Prize, your pronunciation is mostly intelligible, which is great! However, you frequently overstressed syllables in words like "family" /'fæmɪli/, "many" /'mɛni/, and "hero" /'hɪərəʊ/. In English, stress usually falls on one main syllable in a word, and overstressing other syllables can sometimes make it harder for your listener to understand. This is a common pattern for Thai speakers because Thai is a syllable-timed language, meaning each syllable tends to have equal stress, unlike English which has variable stress patterns.</p><br><p>You also tended to drop final consonant sounds, for instance, in words like "life" /laɪf/, "five" /faɪv/, and "killed" /kɪld/. This can make it difficult for listeners to distinguish words and link sounds together in connected speech. To improve, practice emphasizing the correct syllables in English words, and make sure to clearly pronounce those final consonant sounds. Try focusing on the words you mentioned, ensuring the final /f/, /v/, and /d/ sounds are audible. This will significantly improve your overall clarity and help you achieve a higher score!</p>	<p>Hello Prize! 👋 It was great to hear your presentation about Worachit Non Siwan. You did a good job of