es and replace everyday language with more formal vocabulary to suit an academic essay.<br>✅ After you finish writing, read through specifically for common grammar slips like subject-verb agreement and missing articles.	You've done a really commendable job, Arty, by covering all the points in the prompt and even adding your own interesting idea about gaming! It's clear that you understood the question and presented a relevant argument. Each of your points was explored with good supporting details. Just be mindful of the word count for future essays; while it's great to have lots to say, keeping within the suggested length helps you practise being concise and focused, making your arguments even stronger. For instance, you could try summarising your main point in each paragraph and then choosing just one or two strong examples to back it up, rather than adding too many details.	You've definitely attempted to use a formal tone in your essay, Arty, which is super important for academic writing like this! 🎉 However, there were a few spots where the language became a little more conversational than an essay requires. Remember, an academic essay aims to present ideas in a serious and objective way. For example, phrases like "super important" might be better expressed as "critically important" in a formal context. Many Thai learners sometimes find it tricky to switch completely to a formal register because spoken English often blends with academic writing in their learning. A good tip is to imagine you're writing for a teacher or examiner who expects very proper language; this can help you pick more formal words and sentence structures.	Your essay is really well-organised, Arty! You've used clear paragraphs for each of your main points, and it's easy for the reader to follow your argument from the introduction right through to your conclusion. 👍 This logical flow makes your ideas much easier to understand. You've also used a good variety of linking words and phrases such as