plain <em>what</em> the activities are in more detail.<br>❌ Weakness: The introduction is very long and uses up many words before getting to the main points. Also, the explanations for <em>why</em> the activities are popular could be deeper.<br>✨ Improved: "For instance, teens in Thailand often engage in two main free time activities: listening to music and playing sports. These activities are not only popular but also offer significant personal benefits."<br>💡 Explanation: A more direct and shorter introduction helps to focus your essay and allows you more space to develop your main ideas. Developing your points with more specific reasons or examples will make your essay stronger.</p>	<p>You used an appropriate tone for an article, but there are some informalities and repetitive sentence structures that can make it harder for the reader to stay interested.<br>👉 To Improve: Try to use a wider range of sentence structures to keep your writing engaging and maintain a consistently appropriate level of formality for an essay.<br>❌ Weakness: You wrote "Doing activities in their free time are common for teenagers." This is a bit informal and wordy.<br>✨ Improved: "Free time activities are a common aspect of teenage life."<br>💡 Explanation: Using more varied and formal sentence structures makes your writing more sophisticated and engaging for a B2 level essay.</p>	<p>You organized your ideas into paragraphs, which helps the reader follow your main points, but the connections between ideas could be stronger with better linking words and varied sentence structures. The introduction is also quite long, which affects the balance of your essay.<br>👉 To Improve: Use a wider variety of linking words and phrases to show clearer relationships between your ideas (e.g., "consequently," "furthermore," "in contrast"). Also, make sure your introduction is not too long compared to your body paragraphs.<br>❌ Weakness: You sometimes use simple linking words repeatedly, and the ideas within paragraphs sometimes jump without clear transitions.<br>✨ Improved: "While listening to music is a deeply personal activity, playing sports, conversely, offers a communal experience that fosters teamwork and social connections among Thai teenagers."<br>💡 Explanation: Using a variety of linking words like "while" and "conversely" helps to show more complex relationships between your ideas and makes your essay flow more smoothly. Balancing the length of your paragraphs also improves overall organisation.</p>	<p>You used good vocabulary related to the topic, but there are several recurring grammar patterns that affect clarity.<br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "each teenager use their free time differently."<br>✨ Correction: "each teenager uses their free time differently."<br>💡 Explanation: In Thai, verbs don't change form for different subjects. However, in English, we need to add '-s' to the verb for singular subjects like 'each teenager' in the present simple tense. This is a common challenge for Thai learners.<br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "help us makes friends."<br>✨ Correction: "help us make friends."<br>💡 Explanation: After the verb 'help,' the following verb is usually in its base form (without 's'). In Thai, this distinction is not always present, leading to errors in English verb forms.<br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "one of the most favorite thing to do"<br>✨ Correction: "one of my most favorite things to do"<br>💡 Explanation: The phrase "one of the" should always be followed by a plural noun. This is because you are talking about one item from a group of many. Thai grammar does not always require plural markers in the same way, which can lead to this error for Thai speakers.</p>	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
2383	31545	2025-11-28 10:04:25.755774+00	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. Sam was a normal person who liked to watch animes. One day, he decided to create an amazing invention. He created it basement to let no-one know about his invention. He used many months to create his invention. Finally, he finished.<br><br><br>When he pressed the button, the whole room was covering by hologram. That invention is about the hologram that show up a setting from his favorite animes. At first, he thought it was interesting but something was coming out of that invention. Sound of invention was so loud and torn it boom! Every things explored!<br><br><br>His invention was broken and he stuck in the anime. He felt panic and didn't know what to do. When he felt calm, he trying to find some equipments to fixed his invention. He tryed to survived in the horrible world.<br><br><br>However, he can fixed some equipments but he about to died so he decided to use his last breath to fix it. Finally, he can escaped from the terrible moment. He back to live normally. He kept his invention at basement.<br><br><br>Nevertheless, he used to live normally. The invention still at basement. But, something strange. The invention still open but nothing show up. So, it mean his invention is not fix but it still show hologram and he still stuck in that world.	227	13.0	Hello Mew, 👋<br><br>Wow, a story about being trapped in an anime world! 📺 Your idea of Sam's invention going wrong and pulling him into his favorite anime was very creative. The spooky twist that he's still stuck there at the end was a great touch!<br><br>I have a few tips to help you polish your writing even further. 📋<br>1. <b>Word Choice!</b> 💥 'Explored' means to look around. You meant 'Exploded' (like a bomb!).<br>2. <b>Vocabulary!</b> 📖 'Equipments' is uncountable, so it has no 's'.<br>3. <b>Show Emotions!</b> 😨 You said he 'felt panic'. Try to describe *how* he felt it - was his heart pounding?	The concept of an invention that brings an anime world to life is brilliant and relevant! 💡<br>The spooky twist at the end makes the reader think about what happens next.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Elaborate!</b> 🌍 You said 'horrible world'. What made it horrible? Describe the anime world more to immerse the reader.<br>2. <b>Resolution!</b> 🤔 The ending is good, but try to explain *why* the invention was still open and what it means for Sam.	I could clearly follow Sam's journey from inventor to being trapped in the anime world. 🏃‍♂️<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Clarity!</b> 🗣️ Some phrases, like 'torn it boom!', are a bit unclear. Try to describe the sound more precisely.<br>2. <b>Engagement!</b> 🤩 Try to use stronger verbs and more descriptive adjectives to make the action sequences more vivid.	You used good linking words like 'At first', 'However', and 'Finally' to structure your narrative. 🚧<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Flow!</b> 🌊 The phrase 'But, something strange' felt a bit abrupt. Try to link it more smoothly to the previous sentence.<br>2. <b>Paragraph Unity!</b> 🧱 Ensure each paragraph focuses on one main idea or event to maintain clarity.	You have some creative ideas in your writing! 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections to help you:<br>1. <b>Word Choice!</b> 📝 'Every things explored!' -> 'Everything <b>exploded</b>!'. 'Equipments' -> '<b>Equipment</b>'.<br>2. <b>Verb Forms!</b> 🛠️ 'He can fixed' -> 'He can <b>fix</b>'. 'He tryed to survived' -> 'He <b>tried to survive</b>'.<br>3. <b>Tense!</b> 🔙 'Room was covering' -> 'Room was <b>covered</b>'.<br>4. <b>Articles!</b> 🐜 'Created it basement' -> 'Created <b>in a</b> basement'.<br>5. <b>Prepositions!</b> 📍 'Pressed the button to start it' -> 'Pressed the button <b>to start</b> it'.	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
1095	34936	2025-08-22 11:00:08.198357+00	Social Issue	In my opinion, That mobile phones in classrooms is not good. First of all, Some students play game on it when techer say "You can use your mobile phones such for work" Like my class mate how use his mobile phones for playing game. Second reason, Students can't consitering on their study. One of my friend is not consitering when in class because he want to play mobile phones. For the third reason. When the techer giev a free time everyone got a phones when the time need to put the phones back some students don't do it. Like my classroom yes it happen all the time. For these three reasons, I feel That mobile phones in classrooms is not good.	108	11.0	<b>📝 Dear Richter</b><br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Social Issue'. You wrote 108 words and achieved A2+. You expressed your opinion clearly with good supporting reasons, which is a great start. Keep up the good work. ✨<br><br>	You clearly stated your opinion and provided three reasons to support it. 👍<br><b>Problem:</b> You used personal examples that sounded informal, such as "Like my class mate how use his mobile phones for playing game."<br><b>Correction:</b> You could try to make your examples more general or refer to observations, for example, "Some students in classrooms use their mobile phones for playing games."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In academic writing, it is often better to use more general observations or evidence rather than just personal experiences to make your points stronger.	You communicated your ideas clearly, but the tone was a bit too casual for an academic paragraph. 😊<br><b>Problem:</b> Your writing had an informal tone, for example, "What's up?" or "how use his mobile phones."<br><b>Correction:</b> To make it more formal, you could say: "It is my opinion that mobile phones in classrooms are not beneficial" or "Some students misuse their mobile phones by playing games."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Formal writing requires a more serious and objective tone, avoiding casual language often used in speaking or informal messages.	You used basic linking words like "First of all," "Second reason," and "For the third reason" to connect your ideas. 👍<br><b>Problem:</b> Some of your sentences had a repetitive structure and could be linked more smoothly, for instance, starting new sentences with "Like my class mate" or "One of my friend."<br><b>Correction:</b> You could try: "Firstly, some students play games on their phones when teachers allow their use for work. For example, my classmate uses his mobile phone for playing games. Secondly, students may struggle to concentrate on their studies..."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Using a variety of sentence structures and more advanced linking words helps your writing flow better and makes it more engaging for the reader. It also helps to avoid starting sentences with phrases like "Like my...""	You used everyday words, but there were several mistakes with grammar and spelling. Keep practicing. 💪<br><b>Problem:</b> You often forgot to add 's' to verbs when talking about 'he' or 'it', for example, "he want" or "time need."<br><b>Correction:</b> It should be "he wants" and "time needs."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, verbs do not change for different subjects. In English, we add 's' to verbs when the subject is singular (he, she, it) in the present tense.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
