anguage instead of conversational phrases	You included some interesting thoughts about fashion, studying, and mindset, which shows you're thinking about teenage life. However, the essay asked you to compare whether friends or parents have more influence on teenagers' choices, and you didn't directly answer this question. Next time, make sure you address exactly what the prompt asks - for example, you could discuss how friends influence fashion choices while parents might influence study habits. Thai students sometimes struggle with staying precisely on topic because they want to share all their ideas, but focusing on the specific question will really help your scores improve!	You attempted an essay format, which is good, but your writing sounds more like a casual conversation than a formal academic piece. An essay should use more formal language and present balanced arguments. Try using phrases like 'On one hand...' and 'On the other hand...' to compare different viewpoints. Many Thai learners find formal register challenging because everyday English exposure is often informal, but practicing academic phrases will make a big difference in your writing quality.	This is where we need the most work - your essay was written as one long paragraph without any breaks. Essays must have clear paragraph structure: an introduction that states your position, body paragraphs that develop your arguments, and a conclusion that summarizes your main points. Start by always writing at least 3 paragraphs, and use transition words like 'Firstly,' 'Furthermore,' and 'In conclusion' to guide your reader. Paragraphing is especially important for Thai students because Thai writing conventions are different, but mastering this will dramatically improve your scores!	You used some good vocabulary like 'massively' and 'mindset,' which shows you're trying to expand your word choices. However, there were several grammar errors that made your meaning unclear at times - for example, 'teenagers are chenge' should be 'teenagers are changing,' and 'many thing' should be 'many things.' The three most important areas to focus on are: subject-verb agreement (making sure verbs match their subjects), using correct articles ('a/an/the'), and proper sentence endings with periods. These are common challenges for Thai English learners, but practicing these specific grammar points will help your writing become much clearer and more accurate.	2.0	2.0	1.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
