2074	35231	2025-09-23 09:08:21.496465+00	Term 1 Writing Task 2	Hello everyone today I have a story that I will tell you can you have a little bit of the time please?<br><br>Lets begin this story. Tomorrow is the first day of my new school it's call Assumption College Thonburi. I have my news friends very fast because we friends in the first day of summer her name is Pink, she is very beautiful and very kind to everyone, but as my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! It's was the giants christmas tree in front of the school Pink and I was shocked because we don't know why this giant christmas tree here. So when we in the M.2/4 classroom that's my class I run to the old student the they heres before Pink and I came I asked her why the giant christmas tree is in the front of the school she answer me the in next week its will have the Christmas Fair day in school and she says the things that surprise is we can invite our friends from others school to come in this day. In evening I run so fast to get the phone and asked my old friends and they will come to the day. I'm very happy I can't wait for that's day.	217	12.0	Baimhon, your story about the giant Christmas tree at your new school is a delightful read, filled with enthusiasm and a very clear sequence of events. You have a wonderful imagination and a strong desire to tell interesting stories, which is a fantastic foundation for becoming an even stronger writer. To make your writing even more impactful and clear, focusing on keeping your sentences flowing smoothly and checking for those little grammar details will make a significant difference.<br><br>📝 **Action Plan:**<br> *   ✅ Practice identifying and correcting common grammatical errors, especially with verb tenses (e.g., past simple for narrative events) and subject-verb agreement.<br> *   ✅ When writing a story, jump straight into the action using the given opening sentence to keep your reader immediately engaged.<br> *   ✅ Experiment with breaking longer paragraphs into smaller ones, each focusing on a main idea, to make your story easier to follow.	Baimhon, your story is full of great ideas, and you did a splendid job using the exact starting sentence, which is really important for this task. You clearly described the "incredible" Christmas tree and told us all about finding out about the Christmas Fair. You also shared your feelings about being shocked and then very happy, which adds a lovely personal touch to your narrative. You certainly gave your readers a clear picture of what happened. Your story included all the important parts of the task, which is excellent. One thing to think about for next time is keeping your story within the suggested word count (around 100 words). While you included a lot of interesting details, sometimes writing a bit too much can make it harder for the reader to follow the main points quickly. For instance, the opening sentences before "As my friend and I arrived..." were a friendly greeting, but for a story task, it's best to dive straight into the adventure. Imagine you're writing a short film script; every sentence counts. Focusing on the core events and feelings will help you craft a concise and powerful story.	You certainly tried to make your story engaging, Baimhon, especially by sharing your excitement about the Christmas Fair. You also showed a good understanding of how a story unfolds by giving us a sequence of events and including your emotional reactions. This helps the reader connect with your experience. To make your storytelling even more effective, consider how you begin and end your narrative. The conversational opening ("Hello everyone today I have a story that I will tell you...") felt a bit like a chat with friends rather than the start of a story for a competition. For example, you could immediately begin with the given sentence and then let your narrative flow from there. Also, try to keep your writing focused purely on the story's events once you've started. This will help you keep the reader's attention from beginning to end, ensuring your fantastic ideas are communicated clearly and powerfully. Sometimes, in Thai, direct conversational openings are common, but in English stories, we often jump right into the narrative.	Your story has a clear order of events, Baimhon, which makes it generally easy to follow what happened. You moved from arriving at school to seeing the tree, finding out about the fair, and then being happy about inviting old friends. This logical flow is a great strength. To make your story even clearer and more enjoyable to read, try to break your writing into shorter paragraphs. For instance, after describing seeing the Christmas tree, you could start a new paragraph when you go to the classroom and ask the old student. Another paragraph could begin when you run home in the evening. This helps the reader take a pause and process new information. Think of paragraphs as mini-sections for different parts of your story. Using a few more linking words and phrases, like "After that," "Later," or "As a result," would also help connect your ideas even more smoothly, guiding your reader through your adventure.	You used a good amount of everyday vocabulary to tell your story, Baimhon, which helped us understand the events and your feelings clearly. Words like "new school," "friends," "beautiful," "kind," "shocked," and "happy" were all used appropriately to convey your message. To improve your language, let's focus on a few key areas. Firstly, pay close attention to verb tenses, especially when telling a story about past events. For example, you wrote "I run to the old student" and "I run so fast to get the phone," but because these actions happened "yesterday morning" and "in evening" (which refers to the past), you should use the past simple tense, which is "I **ran**." 🏃‍♀️ Secondly, remember subject-verb agreement. You wrote "it's call Assumption College Thonburi" – it should be "it's **called**." Also, for "Pink and I was shocked," since there are two people, the verb should be plural: "Pink and I **were** shocked." 🤯 Finally, some smaller issues like "giants christmas tree" should be "giant Christmas tree" (adjective before noun, and 'Christmas' is a proper noun, so it needs a capital letter). Many Thai learners sometimes face challenges with consistent past tenses and subject-verb agreement because the grammatical structures in Thai are different, often not requiring explicit tense marking or subject-verb matching. Practising these small but important grammar points will make your writing much more accurate and easier to understand.	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
