ns I can understand what you're saying! Your individual sounds are quite clear. However, the speed of your speech is very slow, which can sometimes make it harder to follow your ideas. You also tend to put too much stress on the final syllables of words, like in "eat**ing**," "fam**i**ly," and "peo**ple**," instead of letting the natural rhythm of English guide you. English is a stress-timed language, meaning some syllables are longer and louder than others. Thai, on the other hand, is syllable-timed, where syllables tend to have more equal length, which is why many Thai learners stress words in a way that sounds a little different to a native English speaker. Focusing on sentence stress and a more consistent pace will make your speech sound much more natural. You might try thinking of words in a sentence as flowing together, rather than as separate units. For instance, in "eating," the stress is usually on the first syllable /'iːtɪŋ/, not the last. And for "family," it's /'fæməli/, not /fə'mɪli/. Keep practising! 📣	All right, Preaw, here's your speaking report for your Term 1 Finals! You did a good job expressing your ideas clearly during the exam, and it was lovely to hear you talk about your favourite foods and family time. To really show what you know and move up to the next level, a great area to focus on will be using more varied sentence structures and keeping your speech flowing a bit more smoothly. Keep up the good work!<br><br>📋 Action Plan<br>*   ✔️ Practice using 'because', 'although', 'if', and 'when' to join your sentences together.<br>*   ✔️ Record yourself speaking for 60 seconds on a familiar topic, then listen back to see where you can speak a little faster and link your words.<br>*   ✔️ Pay attention to which words are stressed in English sentences – usually the important ones – and try not to stress every syllable equally.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1yqGR5ip5XtVuoI2jPIboJbuDxmsnlN7a?usp=drive_link	A2+	\N	\N	\N	