on is the final sounds of your words, which were sometimes missing. I noticed you dropped the "t" in "about" (<b>/əˈbaʊt/</b> → /əˈbaʊ/) and "ghost" (<b>/ɡəʊst/</b>), as well as the "d" in "named" (<b>/neɪmd/</b>) and "found" (<b>/faʊnd/</b>). You also tended to overstress the final syllables in words like "story" (<b>/ˈstɔː.ri/</b> → /stɔː<b>ˈriː</b>/), "temple" (<b>/ˈtem.pəl/</b>), and "sister" (<b>/ˈsɪs.tər/</b>).<br><br><b>How to Get Even Better:</b> 🚀<br><ul><li><b>Emphasize Final Consonants:</b> Practice saying "found" (<b>/faʊnd/</b>) and "ghost" (<b>/ɡəʊst/</b>) slowly, making sure you reach that final "d" or "t" clearly.</li><li><b>Practice First-Syllable Stress:</b> Try saying words like <b>/ˈstɔː.ri/</b>, <b>/ˈtem.pəl/</b>, and <b>/ˈsɪs.tər/</b> while clapping on the first syllable to help your brain learn the right rhythm.</li><li><b>Slow Down Slightly:</b> Try to speak about 5% slower to give your mouth more time to finish the sounds at the end of each word.</li></ul><br>You are a very clear and confident speaker, Pea—just polish those final sounds and you will be even stronger! 🎯	Pea, you gave a clear and exciting review of a famous horror story! You used past tense well and included many plot details that kept the audience interested. To reach the next level, try to be more careful with "he" and "she" and work on your verb endings like "died." You should also try to speak for the full two minutes by adding more details about your opinions and the characters. Your choice of vocabulary was very suitable for the topic, and your use of connectors helped the story flow logically. 🌟	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
