 minutes each day; use a spell-checker before you submit.	You cover both advantages (comfort, relaxation, learning kindness) and disadvantages (cost, time, problems with holidays, lifespan differences), so the reader is mostly informed. 😊 To improve, develop one or two points with a specific example — for example, say “a dog needs daily walks, so you must plan time every day” — and give a short detail or example for each main idea. Some sentences are vague or repeated, so try to expand one idea per sentence and delete repeated phrases. Thai learners sometimes write short, general sentences because classroom tasks often reward correct meaning over development; practising adding one specific detail to each point will help.	Your article uses the appropriate style for an online article and places advantages and disadvantages in separate paragraphs, so the reader can follow your purpose. 📝 To make it more engaging for an online audience, try opening with a short question or a concrete image — for instance, “Imagine coming home to a wagging tail after a long day.” Also vary sentence openings so the reader stays interested. Mistakes in grammar and spelling sometimes distract readers; focusing on clarity with shorter, correct sentences will help. Thai learners often aim for politeness and completeness, which is good, but practising a livelier opening and varied sentence starts will strengthen communicative effect.	You used paragraphs and a conclusion, which is the right idea, but links between sentences are weak and some ideas jump around. 🔗 For example, begin each paragraph with a clear topic sentence like “One advantage is that pets provide comfort,” then follow with two supporting sentences and one short example. Use simple linking phrases where needed — for instance, “however,” “for example,” and “therefore” — to connect ideas. Thai students may not have had much practice with paragraph structure in English, so practicing the pattern topic sentence → support → example will build stronger organisation.	The three most important errors are: (1) articles and determiners, (2) verb forms and subject–verb agreement, and (3) spelling/word-formation and prepositions. ✍️<br>1) Articles/determiners: You wrote phrases like “a bunny, bird or some exotic pets example snake.” Correct forms: “a bunny, a bird or some exotic pets, for example a snake” or “an exotic pet such as a snake.” For practice, write ten short noun phrases and add a/an/the where needed (for instance: __dog → a dog; __love → love (no article)). Thai has no articles, so learners must practise choosing them in context.<br>2) Verb forms / agreement: Examples in the text: “Having pets can teaches us” → “Having pets can teach us”; “Having pets makes your outcome increest” → unclear; aim for simple accurate verbs: “Having pets can teach us” or “Pets make your life more expensive.” Drill: make short sentences with plural subjects and verbs (e.g., “Dogs need”, “A cat likes”). Thai verbs do not change form with number, so focused practice on English verb agreement helps.<br>3) Spelling, word-formation and prepositions: Errors such as “take careing” → “taking care”, “some body” → “somebody”, “pets hotel” → “pet hotel”, “ayes” → probably “age(s)”. Use a quick habit: after writing a draft, run a spell-check and correct any red-underlined words, and keep a small personal list of repeated mistakes to review. Thai orthography and sound systems differ from English, which makes spelling and word endings tricky; regular checking reduces these errors.<br>Final note about length and task fulfilment<br>- You answered the task directly and included both pros and cons, so Content is not penalised for omission. Your text is 199 words, slightly above the recommended 140–190 for this exam; you will not be penalised for writing a bit over, but aim to stay within the range in future drafts so you practise concise development and meet exam expectations.	3.0	3.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
