at on a segregated bus in Montgomery. This event launched him into the national spotlight as an advocate for racial equality.<br><br>So, Martin Luther King did many good things and important things, but the most important thing that he did is lead the civil rights movement. So Martin Luther King led the civil rights movement from 1955 up until his assassination in 1968. Uh, using non-violent resistance inspired by Mahatma Gandhi, he organized peaceful protests against racial segregation, which helped raise awareness to the black community... He then went on to receive multiple awards, one of which being the infamous Nobel Peace Prize in 1964 for his leadership. He was only 35 at the time making him the youngest recipient at that time.<br><br>With success, always comes criticisms and problems. He faced many obstacles in his lifetime. Um, the most intense one being imprisoned 30 times for civil disobedience. And you see Martin Luther King did many good things, but people still disagreed with him. Most people in America viewed him as a militant figure especially among white Americans. And even the media, they labeled him as an extremist and labeled his protest as useless.<br><br>And you might ask, why is Martin Luther King a hero? So here's my take. So I think that what Martin Luther King did really made a huge impact on the world. He brought peace and equality into the world and helped the black community because without Martin Luther King, the black community would still most likely face racial segregation and he helped bring peace to them.<br><br>So to summarize, Martin Luther King was a great civil rights leader who led the civil rights movement for many, many years and helped to bring peace into the world, peace and equality. And the message that I want to leave you with is the time is always right to do the right thing. Like I said before, this means that it is never really truly a right time to do the right thing. And one major lesson that we can learn from Mart