 her because oh where did she come from? Like suddenly she appeared, like you know? Just like that. So have a lot of pressure. Yeah, pressure.<br><br><br>Okay. So let's move on to the last topic, which is why she is a hero. So first, she protects Nakhon Ratchasima and she's inspiring people to be like a brave person or something some kind of that. And the last one, she is symbol of bravery. As you can see that this monument, uh, is a Ya Mo monument. Uh, nowadays, uh, this monument is at Nakhon Ratchasima. Okay.<br><br><br>And this is all I want to introduce about Ya Mo, a woman who protects Nakhon Ratchasima. Thank you very much.	480	13.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	FIRST	<p>Your intonation is excellent, Kris! 🗣️ This really helps make your speech engaging and easy to follow. You generally articulate individual sounds clearly, making you highly intelligible.<br><br>To improve further and aim for a 5, pay close attention to final consonant sounds, especially for past tense verbs and plural nouns. For example, you said "marry" instead of "married" /mærid/ and "soldier" instead of "soldiers" /səʊldʒərz/. Thai speakers often drop final consonants, so practicing these sounds will significantly enhance your clarity. You can try focusing on words ending in consonant clusters and ensure that all the sounds are clearly pronounced. For instance, when saying "protects" /prəˈtɛkts/, make sure the /t/ and /s/ are both audible.</p>	<p>Hi Kris! 👋 You delivered a really interesting presentation about Ya Mo, and it's clear you put a lot of thought into explaining why she is a hero. Your introduction and conclusion were clear, and you managed to produce a good amount of language. Keep working on making your grammar even more precise, and remember to articulate those past tense and plural sounds to make your speech even clearer. You're doing great! Keep up the good work! 🚀</p>	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?u