1776	28863	2025-09-13 11:17:44.396135+00	Term 1 Exam Question 1	Have you ever wonder? Who has more effect on the choices teenagers make - friends or parents? And there are many ways to know, for examples clothes and fashion, studying and hobbies.<br><br><br>The first way, clothes and fashion.<br><br><br>Most teenagers prefer to dress the same fashion as their friends. Because, their ages are relatable.<br><br><br>The second way, studying. Teenagers choosed to do well in studying to make the grade reports pleasant. So their parents will be happy and get them some rewards, maybe new phone or bicycle.<br><br><br>And the last way, hobbies. Mostly teenagers would like to do some activities with their friends in their freetime. One of the reasons is their ages are relatable. Sometime it's because teenagers don't like to stay boring at their home so they go to their friend's house when it's weekend.<br><br><br>In short, teenagers mostly prefer friends because they spend a lot of time together at school.	148	10.0	Hello Namo T, it's lovely to see your effort in tackling this essay question. You've clearly thought about the different ways friends and parents can influence teenagers, and you've included some good ideas. While your essay has a clear structure and you’ve managed to put your points across, let's focus on building a more formal and detailed approach to your writing so that your ideas can truly blossom and impress your readers. Keep up the good work, and with a little focus, your writing will develop even further.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Namo T:**<br>*   ✔️ Practice writing clear and complete topic sentences that introduce the main idea of each paragraph, ensuring they connect smoothly to the previous one.<br>*   ✔️ Work on expanding your vocabulary, especially by finding different ways to express similar ideas, like using alternatives to "relatable."<br>*   ✔️ Review verb tenses and subject-verb agreement to make sure your sentences are grammatically accurate and polished.	You've done well to cover all the points in the essay question, including your own idea about hobbies, which is a relevant and thoughtful addition. 👍 Your reader is generally informed about your perspective. To make your content even stronger, try to develop your ideas with more detail and examples. For instance, when you discuss how friends influence fashion, you could describe *how* they do this—perhaps by sharing clothes or going shopping together. This will help you move beyond simpler statements and create a more convincing argument. Sometimes, learners focus a lot on making sure all points are there, which is a good start, but forget to add enough specific details to make their writing really stand out.	You've made a good attempt to write an essay, which is fantastic. However, essays for this level usually require a more formal tone. Phrases like "Have you ever wonder?" or starting paragraphs with "The first way" are a bit too casual for academic writing. Think about how you would speak to a teacher in a formal setting and try to match that in your writing. You want to make sure your essay holds the reader's attention by presenting ideas in a structured and sophisticated way. For example, instead of "And there are many ways to know," you could try, "Several aspects reveal the extent of influence friends and parents have." Thai learners sometimes translate directly from their spoken language, which can result in a more informal style in English.	Your essay has a clear beginning, middle, and end, which is a solid foundation. You’ve used basic linking words like "The first way" to guide the reader through your points. However, the transitions between your paragraphs could be smoother and more detailed. For instance, "The first way, clothes and fashion" is a bit abrupt. Instead, you could write something like, "Regarding choices in clothes and fashion, there is a clear distinction in influence." This helps to introduce the topic more smoothly and makes the connection between ideas more explicit. Building stronger topic sentences will greatly improve how your essay flows, making it easier and more engaging for your reader to follow your ideas. 🤔 Many students, not just Thai learners, sometimes rush their topic sentences, which makes the essay feel a little less connected.	You've used everyday vocabulary quite well, and your meaning is generally clear. However, expanding your vocabulary will certainly help you express your ideas with more precision and variety. For example, instead of using "relatable" multiple times, you could explore words like "similar age group," "shared experiences," or "mutual understanding." There were a few grammatical slips, such as "Have you ever wonder?" which should be "Have you ever wondered?" and "teenagers choosed" which should be "teenagers chose." Also, "Sometime it's" should be "Sometimes it's." Paying close attention to verb tenses and making sure your subjects and verbs agree will make your writing much more accurate. 😊 These types of errors are common, and with practice, you will find yourself using more complex structures accurately.	3.0	2.0	2.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
