525	28771	2025-07-11 03:11:31.578071+00	A notable city	[00:00:307] Yiwa: Good morning, everyone. Today, we're going to talk about Osaka, the city of comedy in Japan. Our main message is Osaka is a fun and exciting city to visit. Yiwa [00:18:117]: I'm Yiwa and this is Mymind. Yiwa [00:21:407]: I will talk about two topics, a special place to visit and landmarks, and Mymind will talk about the great outdoors, where to eat, and drink. Yiwa [00:33:487]: First topic that I'm going to talk about is a special place to visit. This is Universal Studios Japan. Um, this is a big... Yiwa [00:51:77]: This is a big theme park in Osaka that is have a lot of fun rides and a lot of shows that tourists want to visit. And... Yiwa [01:02:647]: Second, Osaka Aquarium Kaiyukan. Uh, this place have a lot of animals like, uh, fish, a lot of fish. Yeah. And you can go with your family and your friends. Uh, this is... Yodobashi Umeda. This is a mall that you can buy a lot of things and if you like shopping, you like this place. And you go shopping with your friends. Yiwa [01:42:617]: Second topics is landmarks. This is Osaka Castle. This place has a long history to learn. And... Yiwa [02:03:197]: Umeda Sky Building. This is a tall building that you can see a lot of city and you can take a picture with beautiful city. And, Yasaka Shrine... Yiwa [02:20:477]: Yasaka Shrine is a peaceful and traditional shrine in Japan.	\N	13.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	FIRST	Your pronunciation was excellent, Yiwa, characterized by lovely intonation and clear sounds, which made your speech engaging and easy to understand. While there were minor instances of mispronunciation, such as "Japan" and "Osaka" with overstressed final syllables, these did not significantly impede overall intelligibility. To maintain a score of 5, continue to practice these proper nouns and ensure consistent stress patterns. Your strong overall phonological control, especially your clear vowel and consonant sounds, is a significant strength.	Hello Yiwa, your presentation on Osaka was delivered with excellent body language, which strongly reinforced your points, and you demonstrated lovely fluency, intonation, and clear sounds, making your speech very engaging. Your contribution, however, was a bit short for the task. To further enhance your performance, focus on consistent pronunciation of proper nouns, ensuring you engage with the entire audience, and expanding your ideas to produce more extended discourse. Keep up the excellent work!	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
975	34059	2025-07-24 13:01:05.27581+00	A Place I Love	Hong-Kong, the finance center of Asia, is a city of light.<br><br><br>Hong-Kong is a island that is a part of China. There are many place that you must visit. I will give you my favorite place in Hong-Kong. Frist is Tsim Sha Tsui, where is the shopping road for tuorists, is the most bustling place in Hong-Kong. When I was there, I was very straving and I remember that I brought many food in addition: Tang hulu, Noodle, Xiao long bao. The Secound place is Victoria Habour, where is the port in the past, is the most iconic place in Hong-Kong. There are the largest light and fireworks in Asia. They will starting about 8 p.m. everday. It was very beutiful and unforgetable.<br><br><br>Overall, Hong-Kong is the place that you should visit once in your life. There have many place that full of culture history tecnology and art that waiting for you. Their traditional food are very delicious and the perspetive above the island is really gorgeous. If I have a chance I want to go back to Hong-Kong again.	180	10.0	Namunn, you wrote about Hong Kong, calling it the "finance center of Asia" and "City of Light." You shared your favorite places like Tsim Sha Tsui and Victoria Harbour, and talked about delicious food. You did a good job of introducing the city and giving specific examples of why you love it. To make your writing even better, you need to focus on correcting your spelling mistakes and making your language more precise. This will make your article much clearer and more professional for readers.	Namunn, you introduced Hong Kong very well, calling it the "finance center of Asia" and the "City of Light." You gave good reasons for loving it, like its famous places such as Tsim Sha Tsui and Victoria Harbour, and also its delicious food. You included personal memories, like being "straving" and trying many foods. Your conclusion brought together your main points effectively, and you stayed within the word limit, which is great.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I will give you my favorite place in Hong-Kong. Frist is Tsim Sha Tsui, where is the shopping road for tuorists, is the most bustling place in Hong-Kong."\n    Problem: The phrase "where is the shopping road for tourists" is a little awkward and makes the sentence very long.\n    How to fix it: You could refine the sentence to be smoother and more direct. For example, "First, I want to tell you about Tsim Sha Tsui, which is a bustling shopping district in Hong Kong popular with tourists."\n\n*   Example: You mentioned, "There are the largest light and fireworks in Asia. They will starting about 8 p.m. everday. It was very beutiful and unforgetable."\n    Problem: The timing of the lights and fireworks is a good detail, but the sentence "They will starting about 8 p.m. everday" has a small grammatical error and "everday" is misspelled.\n    How to fix it: You could phrase it as: "There is the largest light and fireworks show in Asia, which starts around 8 p.m. every day. It was truly beautiful and unforgettable."	You tried to clearly communicate your ideas about Hong Kong and persuade readers to visit. You picked good examples to describe. However, the many basic spelling and grammar mistakes made your writing hard to read and understand sometimes, which stopped it from effectively holding the reader's attention and communicating as smoothly as it could. The frequent errors can be quite distracting for the reader.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Hong-Kong is a island that is a part of China."\n    Problem: The missing "an" before "island" is a basic grammatical error.\n    How to fix it: Remember to use the correct articles ("a," "an," "the"). It should be "Hong Kong is **an** island that is a part of China."\n\n*   Example: You used "Frist" instead of "First," "Secound" instead of "Second," "tuorists" instead of "tourists," "straving" instead of "starving," "Habour" instead of "Harbour," "everday" instead of "every day," "beutiful" instead of "beautiful," and "unforgetable" instead of "unforgettable."\n    Problem: These are very common and basic spelling mistakes that appear throughout your essay. They make your writing look less professional and can make it harder for the reader to understand quickly.\n    How to fix it: **You must proofread your writing very, very carefully for spelling errors.** Use a dictionary or a spell-checker to help you. Learning common spellings is crucial for clear communication in English.	Your essay has a clear structure with an introduction, a body paragraph (even though it's quite long), and a conclusion. You used some basic linking words like "Frist" and "Secound place," and "Overall" for your conclusion. This helps to organize your ideas. However, the second paragraph is quite long and packs in many different details, which makes it feel a bit crowded.\n\n*   Example: Your second paragraph covers Tsim Sha Tsui (shopping, food) and then immediately shifts to Victoria Harbour (port, light show).\n    Problem: While "Frist" and "Secound place" help, putting all these details about two major attractions into one long paragraph can make it hard for the reader to process all the information.\n    How to fix it: Consider breaking the second paragraph into two separate ones. One paragraph could focus on Tsim Sha Tsui and its vibrant shopping and food scene. A second paragraph could then be dedicated to Victoria Harbour and its iconic light show, allowing you to describe each place in more detail without overwhelming the reader.\n\n*   Example: You used "Overall" to start your conclusion.\n    Problem: While "Overall" can be used, it's a very simple transition for a conclusion.\n    How to fix it: Use a more varied or classic concluding phrase. For example, "In conclusion," "To summarize," or "All in all," would be more suitable for an article.	You used some everyday vocabulary to describe Hong Kong and its attractions. You also attempted to use some more descriptive phrases like "bustling place" and "iconic place." However, there were very many errors in basic spelling, grammar (like missing articles and incorrect verb forms), and punctuation. These frequent mistakes made your writing quite difficult to understand clearly at times.\n\n*   Spelling: (As noted in Communicative Achievement, these are critical)\n    Example: "tecnology," "perspetive"\n    Problem: These are misspellings for "technology" and "perspective."\n    How to fix it: Practice spelling common and topic-specific words carefully. Consistently reviewing your work for these kinds of errors will greatly improve your writing.\n\n*   Grammar - Articles and Subject-Verb Agreement:\n    Example: "Hong-Kong is **a** island" (missing "an"), "is **the** most bustling place," "There have many place" (should be "There are many places").\n    Problem: You often miss or misuse articles ("a," "an," "the"). "There have" is a common error for "there are." "Place" should be plural for "many."\n    How to fix it: Remember to use "an" before words starting with a vowel sound (like "island"). Pay close attention to subject-verb agreement (e.g., "There **are** many places," not "There have many place").\n\n*   Punctuation and Capitalization:\n    Example: "Hong-Kong, the finance center of Asia, is a city of light. Hong-Kong is a island..."\n    Problem: You used a comma instead of a period between "city of light" and "Hong-Kong is a island," creating a run-on sentence. Capitalization for "Hong-Kong" is inconsistent (sometimes "Hong-Kong," sometimes "Hong Kong").\n    How to fix it: Always use a period at the end of a complete sentence. Remember to consistently capitalize proper nouns like "Hong Kong."	4.0	2.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
