1197	28997	2025-09-03 09:36:35.016707+00	My Hero	So, hello everybody. Good morning. So today I'm going to... uh, tell you all about Sueb Nakhasathien. So, uh, you may already known him. So, he is a conservationist that really want to protect Thai forest and concert animals. So he famous quote is, "I would like to speak on behalf of wildlife because they cannot speak for themselves." So this meaning is he said that he want humans or humans to say for them because animals can't talk. He want us to be their mouth, be their sound to like protect them.<br><br><br>Okay, so... and now I'm going to tell you all topic that I'm going to talk. So, first one is, who is he? And the second one is, what did he do? And the... and we are going to move on to the last one is, why he is a hero.<br><br><br>So, this is his early life. So first, he was born in Prachinburi in 1949 and his back story is his family was like to hunt and animals and they didn't love nature at all. So, and then... when he grew up, he grew that he... uh, loved nature and he realized that the hunting is wrong. So his perspective was changed. He become and he become like love the animals and the nature more.<br><br><br>And the last... and his biggest influence is his direct experience in wildlife con... conservation is... and conservation because he saw by himself that animals was tortured by a humans and the... the other environment like the trees and... and the trees, atmosphere was changed because of humans like cut the trees and, uh, like burn the trees.<br><br><br>And his achievement is he had a research wildlife research and he researched it complete that what, uh, I think... what he want to know. And the next is Sueb Nakhasathien Foundation. So, this was happened after he died. So, this foundation is go to the, uh, government that is about environment, about animals. And yeah, and he was made UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1991. So, he really want to protect and want them to get an award, so he tried to protect them and then UNESCO give the award.<br><br><br>But the problem is he can't stop the powerful people because powerful people still continue damaging forest and wild animal. He can't stop them because they have like money, they have powerful, like you know. So, they're too high for him to stop them.<br><br><br>And why is he a real hero? So, he purchase... he purchase through to this life. So he shot his... he shot... he shot himself because he really want to let the government know and many people know that he really want to protect and want to stop hunting animals. And he fought against illegal logging, poaching, and the absent-minded government because the government is really sucks. You know, like they don't care. They kind of don't care the environment. So, when they got the money, they let them... they let them cut the trees, they let them do anything what they want.<br><br><br>So, lastly, I want to remind you that I want to remind you about his quote is, "I would like to speak on behalf of wildlife because they cannot speak for themselves." So, I really want you guys to love, uh, uh, the nature and animals. So, we need to be their sound, we need to be their mouth. Thank you.	412	12.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Speaking	CA	FIRST	<p>Grace, your intonation was generally appropriate, which means the rise and fall of your voice helped listeners understand your meaning. That's a good strength! However, you would benefit from improving your linking of words and slowing down a bit. Speaking at a slightly slower pace would allow you to emphasize important words and make your speech clearer for your audience.</p><p>A common pattern in Thai English is difficulty with consonant clusters, and this was observed in your speech (e.g., "wants" might be pronounced as "wan"). Thai does not allow many consonant clusters, which can lead to extra vowel sounds being inserted or clusters being simplified. To improve, practice saying words with consonant clusters slowly and clearly, making sure all the sounds are present. For example, for "wants," ensure you pronounce both the /n/ and /ts/ sounds clearly. You can also work on your /l/ and /r/ sounds, as Thai speakers often confuse these; the /l/ sound requires your tongue to touch the roof of your mouth, while for /r/ your tongue curls back slightly. Another area to focus on is the 'th' sounds. English 'th' sounds like in "thing" /θ/ and "this" /ð/ require you to place your tongue between your teeth and blow air, which is a sound not present in Thai. Practicing these sounds will really help make your pronunciation shine! ✨</p>	<p>Hi Grace! 👋 You did a great job talking about Sueb Nakhasathien as your hero. Your presentation was well-structured, and you explained why he is so important. Keep practicing your grammar and pronunciation to make your English even clearer. You're doing really well, and with a little more practice, your speaking will be fantastic! 😊</p>	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1by6UKGd4IRmb0pAHXaTn1cuU1wgGUPUF?usp=drive_link	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
