stions about bringing a charger and water 💧, and explained why these were good ideas. This shows you were thinking carefully about the practicalities of the day. One small area to consider for next time is when the task asks you to disagree with an idea and suggest another. In this email, you ended up agreeing with the film, even though the instruction was to disagree and prefer building a robot. It’s a good idea to double-check these instructions to make sure you're always giving the exact information that's asked for.	You've created an email that definitely sounds like you're talking to a friend, which is perfect for this kind of task. Starting with "Hello" and ending with "Bye" is a friendly and appropriate way to begin and end your message 👍. You successfully shared your ideas in a way that Corey would understand. However, there were a couple of spots where the message could have been even clearer, especially when you were talking about the activities. Sometimes, when we write quickly, we might unintentionally contradict ourselves a little, which can make the reader pause. Just a tiny bit more focus on making sure every sentence directly supports your main point will help keep your reader completely engaged.	Your email is generally well-organised, and you've used paragraph breaks effectively to separate your different ideas, which makes your message easy to read 📝. It's like you're guiding Corey through your thoughts step by step. You used linking words like "because" and "but" to connect your sentences, which helps the email flow. To improve, you could try to use a wider variety of these connecting words to make your writing even more sophisticated and to ensure a very smooth transition between all your points. For instance, instead of just "and," you could sometimes use "also" or "in addition."	You used a good range of everyday words that are appropriate for talking to a friend, which is excellent. Words like "lucky," "interrupt," and "hydrated" fit perfectly into your email. Your grammar is generally good for simple sentences, and even with some errors, Corey would still understand what you mean. For example, you wrote "didn't ran out" when it should be "didn't run out," and "inturrupt" instead of "interrupt." Also, remember that the first-person pronoun "I" is always a capital letter in English, so "i think" should always be "I think" 👍. These are common areas where Thai learners sometimes make mistakes because the rules in Thai are different. Paying close attention to spelling and verb forms will really help your writing become more polished.	3.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
