970	34319	2025-07-23 15:52:33.930375+00	A Place I Love	1. I will start with "A place I love" a place I love is beach in Phuket the sea is so beatiful. I like to go with my family or my friends because when I go with my friends or my family is so perfect like such as funny, peaful and have many active. The water is so clear and blue sometimes I go to beach I love to swimming or build some sand castles the smell of the sea, the waves, the sonset I love it so much.<br><br><br>2. And I want to give you sure story wen I was tan in holyday in last year I go to Phuket. And go to the beach sume where in Phuket the water is so clear and blue I see a fish also I can remmember when I sleep on the sond is so soft and have a shell. When I go to at there I so relaxed and peaful. I think it has so many actives such as swimming in sea, play voraball I like to read a books or lie down on the sand and watch the sunset or sometime I sleep with sound of waves.<br><br><br>3. In conclusion in Phuket have so many beach there are so intersenting and peaful is a place like can makes you forget something bad and you can do a actives so much brink sume sanchs or drinks in there. If you go to Phuket I hope you be go to the beach is so beatiful and I hope you enjoy.	258	8.0	Mirin, you wrote about the beaches in Phuket and shared what you love about them, like the clear water, sunsets, and activities. You also included a personal story about your holiday there. You did a good job of introducing the place and giving some reasons. To make your writing even better, you need to remember that for your English exams, you should never use numbered paragraphs or very informal language. Focusing on clearer organization, more varied reasons, and correcting spelling and grammar mistakes will help your writing be much stronger and clearer for a travel article.	Mirin, you introduced the beaches in Phuket as a place you love and gave reasons like their beauty, clear water, and activities such as swimming and building sandcastles. You shared a personal experience from a holiday. You covered the main requirements of the task. However, your descriptions were a bit repetitive, and you could have given more varied reasons for loving the place beyond just its visual appeal and basic activities.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "the sea is so beatiful. ... The water is so clear and blue sometimes I go to beach I love to swimming or build some sand castles the smell of the sea, the waves, the sonset I love it so much." and later, "the water is so clear and blue I see a fish... When I go to at there I so relaxed and peaful."\n    Problem: You repeat similar ideas about the sea being beautiful, clear, and blue, and how it makes you feel relaxed. While these are good points, repeating them so much makes the essay less interesting.\n    How to fix it: Once you mention the clear blue water, try to describe *different* aspects of its beauty or *other unique experiences* it offers. For example, instead of just "clear and blue," you could say, "The water was so clear it was like swimming in a giant aquarium, with colourful fish darting around my feet."\n\n*   Example: You mentioned "many active" and "so many actives" several times.\n    Problem: This is vague and repetitive. The task asked for reasons and examples.\n    How to fix it: Instead of just saying "many actives," list more specific and varied activities beyond just swimming and sandcastles. For example, "There are so many exciting activities available, such as snorkeling to see the colourful coral reefs, trying delicious local seafood by the shore, or taking a boat trip to nearby islands."	You tried to engage the reader and explain why they should visit Phuket's beaches. However, the way you organized your paragraphs with numbers is **never appropriate for formal writing like an essay or article in an English exam, and it will really lower your score**. The language was also very informal, and the many errors made it difficult for your ideas to be communicated clearly and effectively. The article did not consistently hold the reader's attention due to these issues.\n\n*   Example: You started your paragraphs with "1.", "2.", and "3."\n    Problem: Using numbered paragraphs is for lists or instructions, not for essays or articles. This style is completely inappropriate for this type of writing task in an English exam and makes your writing look informal and unorganized.\n    How to fix it: **Always** use unnumbered paragraphs for essays and articles. Start each new paragraph with a clear topic sentence and indent the first line, or leave a blank line between paragraphs.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I will start with 'A place I love' a place I love is beach in Phuket" and "And I want to give you sure story wen I was tan in holyday in last year I go to Phuket."\n    Problem: This language is very informal and conversational ("I will start with," "I want to give you sure story," "wen I was tan"). It's not suitable for a travel website article.\n    How to fix it: Use more formal and descriptive language. For example, instead of "I will start with 'A place I love' a place I love is beach in Phuket," you could say, "My favorite place is the stunning beaches of Phuket." Instead of "And I want to give you sure story," you could write, "Let me share a memorable experience from my holiday there last year."	While you separated your text into three parts, using numbered paragraphs is a very weak way to organize an essay or article and is not appropriate for formal writing. This made your essay seem disorganized. The ideas within your paragraphs were also sometimes repetitive and didn't flow smoothly, making it hard for the reader to follow your thoughts.\n\n*   Example: Your first paragraph combines your general love for the beach, who you go with, and then jumps to water clarity, activities, and senses like smell and sunset.\n    Problem: Too many different ideas are packed into one section. Also, phrases like "so perfect like such as funny, peaful and have many active" are unclear and make the paragraph hard to follow.\n    How to fix it: Think about one main idea for each paragraph. Your introduction should introduce the place and state your overall feeling. Then, your body paragraphs should each focus on a specific aspect, like "Activities I enjoy" or "The relaxing atmosphere."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I see a fish also I can remmember when I sleep on the sond is so soft and have a shell. When I go to at there I so relaxed and peaful."\n    Problem: The ideas here jump quickly from seeing fish to remembering sleeping on the sand, and the connections are not smooth.\n    How to fix it: Use clear linking words to connect your sentences. For instance, "The water was so clear and blue that I could even see fish swimming below. I also remember how incredibly soft the sand felt when I lay down, noticing the beautiful shells, which made me feel so relaxed and peaceful."	You used very simple words and phrases to describe the beaches. There were many very noticeable and frequent mistakes in spelling, grammar (like verb forms, sentence structure, and subject-verb agreement), and punctuation. These errors often made it very difficult to understand what you were trying to say.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "beatiful," "peaful," "active," "sure story," "wen," "tan in holyday," "sume," "remmember," "sond," "voraball," "intersenting," "brink," "sanchs," "onece"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled or used incorrectly. They should be "beautiful," "peaceful," "activities," "true story," "when," "on holiday," "some," "remember," "sand," "volleyball," "interesting," "bring," "snacks," "once."\n    How to fix it: You need to proofread your writing extremely carefully. Use a dictionary or a spell checker for every word you are unsure about. Many common words are misspelled here.\n\n*   Grammar - Verb Forms and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "a place I love is beach in Phuket the sea is so beatiful." (missing "the"), "when I go with my friends or my family is so perfect like such as funny, peaful and have many active." (run-on sentence, awkward phrasing), "wen I was tan in holyday" ("was tan" is incorrect), "I go to Phuket" (should be past tense "went"), "I was saw cos player" (incorrect verb form).\n    Problem: These examples show consistent errors with missing articles, run-on sentences, incorrect verb tenses ("was tan," "was saw"), and general awkward sentence structures that make your meaning unclear.\n    How to fix it: Focus on building clear, simple sentences first. Practice using the correct verb tenses for past events (e.g., "I *went* to Phuket," not "I go"). Ensure your sentences are complete thoughts and use conjunctions or periods to separate them.\n\n*   Punctuation and Capitalization:\n    Example: "1. I will start with "A place I love" a place I love is beach in Phuket the sea is so beatiful." and "The water is so clear and blue sometimes I go to beach I love to swimming or build some sand castles the smell of the sea, the waves, the sonset I love it so much."\n    Problem: Besides the numbered paragraphs, there is very little proper punctuation. Sentences run together without periods or commas, making them very difficult to read. Capitalization is inconsistent.\n    How to fix it: **Always** use periods at the end of sentences. Use commas to separate items in a list. Capitalize the first word of every sentence and all proper nouns (e.g., "Phuket").	3.0	2.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
