er all parts of the question directly and help you express your thoughts with even greater clarity and accuracy in your language. Keep up the effort, and you'll see your writing grow stronger with each practice.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br>*   ✅ When you get an essay question, take a moment to circle the key parts of the question to make sure you address everything the prompt asks for. For example, if it asks you to compare two things, make sure you discuss both, even if you have a stronger opinion about one.<br>*   ✅ After writing, try reading your essay aloud. This can often help you catch sentences that sound a bit awkward or places where you've missed a word.<br>*   ✅ Practice identifying the main subject and verb in your sentences to make sure they always agree with each other. This is a common area for improvement that can make a big difference to your score.	You've done a good job of covering the points mentioned in the essay question: clothes and fashion, studying, and you even added your own interesting idea about relationships. That's a great start and shows you understand how to brainstorm relevant points. 👍 However, the question specifically asked "Who has more effect on the choices teenagers make - friends or parents?" and your essay focused mostly on the influence of parents. To score even higher, try to discuss both sides of the comparison. For instance, you could have talked about how friends might influence fashion choices or study habits, even if you feel parents have a stronger overall impact. Thai learners sometimes focus so much on their main idea that they forget to give enough attention to all aspects of the question.	You've certainly communicated your main point clearly: you believe parents have a very strong influence on teenagers' choices. That's good. However, an academic essay for this level usually requires a more formal and balanced tone, even when you have a strong opinion. Sometimes your writing sounded a little like a personal complaint rather than a formal discussion. To improve this, try to introduce your points with more neutral language and save your strong opinions for the conclusion, or present them as supported arguments. For example, instead of saying "Parents always think that They know the best," you could try something like, "It is often perceived by parents that they know what is best for their children." This helps maintain a formal tone.	Your essay has a clear structure with an introduction, separate paragraphs for each of your points, and a conclusion, which is a good foundation. 👏 This helps the reader follow your ideas. To make your organisation even stronger, think about how your paragraphs connect to each other. You used phrases like "And last but not least," which are simple connectors. To get a higher score, try to use a wider range of linking words and phrases that show more complex relationships between your ideas, like "Furthermore," "In contrast," or "Consequently." This makes the flow of your essay much smoother. For example, when moving from discussing clothes to studying, you could have used a phrase like "Beyond personal style, parental influence also extends to academic choices." Thai learners can sometimes use simple connectors and benefit from expanding their range.	You've used everyday vocabulary, which is appropriate for the task. However, there are quite a few noticeable errors in your language that sometimes make it a little harder to understand your message fully. Let's look at three important areas:<br><br>Firstly, **spelling**: Words like "thier" (should be "their"), "stlye" (should be "style"), "conclution" (should be "conclusion"), and "disapointed" (should be "disappointed") appeared several times. Taking a moment to double-check your spelling can significantly improve your score.<br>Secondly, **subject-verb agreement**: You wrote "Every teenager usually or always think" when it should be "Every teenager usually or always *thinks*," and "Parent study think" instead of "Parents *think*." Remember that when the subject is singular (like "teenager" or "parent"), the verb often needs an 's' at the end in the present tense.<br>Thirdly, **verb choice and form**: You often used "effect" when "affect" was needed, for example, "parents have effected" should be "parents have *affected*," and "it's effect everything" should be "it *affects* everything." "Affect" is typically a verb (to influence), and "effect" is usually a noun (the result). Also, be careful with contractions like "it's" which means "it is," versus "its" which shows possession. Many Thai learners find these small grammar points tricky, but paying attention to them can really elevate your writing. Keep practising, and you'll see a big difference.	3.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
