ar and transitions, your writing will continue to develop.	Kaohom C, you have included relevant information about Hua Hin and a specific hotel, "The Rain." The reader gets a sense of the location and your personal experience. To achieve a 5, you could provide more details about *why* Hua Hin is a favorite province or what makes "The Rain" a good hotel, expanding on the "beautiful village" aspect or offering sensory details.	You generally use the conventions of the task to communicate straightforward ideas. The essay is engaging and maintains the reader's interest, particularly with the anecdote about the café. The tone is personal and conversational. To reach a score of 5, you could incorporate more complex ideas or a more sophisticated tone, perhaps reflecting on the broader appeal of Hua Hin.	You have organised your text into paragraphs, and it generally follows a logical progression, describing Hua Hin, "The Rain," and the surrounding area. However, transitions between ideas could be smoother and more effective. Using transitions such as these will help to create a more cohesive flow. To achieve a 5, use more varied and effective linking words and cohesive devices.	Kaohom C, you demonstrate a range of everyday vocabulary, and there are attempts at more descriptive language. However, there are noticeable errors that impact the clarity of your writing. Examples include "Hua-Hin is the one of province that very famous" (should be "Hua Hin is one of the provinces that *is* very famous"), "some of landmark you could see" (should be "some of the landmarks you can see"), "I used to went to one mansion" (should be "I used to go to one mansion"), "friendly staffs" (should be "friendly staff"), and numerous instances of dropped verbs. Correcting these errors and using more consistent plural forms would significantly improve your score. Also, the use of uppercase for emphasis is generally avoided in formal writing.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N