guage for academic essays and connecting your ideas more smoothly between sentences and paragraphs. Keep up the excellent work in expressing your thoughts so thoughtfully.<br><br>📝 Action Plan:<br>✅ Practice using a more formal tone when writing essays by avoiding casual phrases.<br>✅ Work on varying your linking words and phrases to create a smoother flow between your sentences.<br>✅ Carefully check your grammar for common errors like subject-verb agreement and correct word forms.	Kathi, you’ve done a brilliant job of addressing every part of the essay question. You clearly stated your opinion that friends have more influence than parents and provided excellent examples related to clothes and fashion, studying, and music. Every point you made was relevant and well-explained, so anyone reading your essay would be fully informed about your perspective. That's a fantastic start, demonstrating a strong understanding of the task. Keep up this careful approach to covering all the required points. 🎉	You were successful in getting your main ideas across, and your reader could definitely understand your points about friends' influence. You managed to hold my attention with your examples. To make your essays even more effective, particularly for an academic setting like an exam, try to use a more formal way of writing. Instead of saying "it’s pretty fun" or "I’m barely saw," think about more academic phrases like "it is enjoyable" or "I rarely observed." Imagine you're writing for a teacher or a university lecturer. This helps your essay sound more serious and persuasive. For instance, instead of starting with "In my perspective the people affected me the most is my friends," you could try "From my perspective, friends exert the most significant influence on teenagers..." This makes your argument more impactful. You can also work on avoiding contractions like "it's" and "I'm" in formal writing, opting for "it is" and "I am" instead. 📚	You've made a good start at organising your essay with clear paragraphs for your introduction, each main point, and your conclusion. This structure helps the reader follow your argument easily. You also used clear signposting words like "First," "Second," and "Finally" to introduce each new idea. To make your writing flow even more smoothly, try to vary the way you link your sentences and paragraphs. Instead of using "And this is the most affected topic" you might try something like "Furthermore, this topic is particularly significant for my age group." Experiment with a wider range of cohesive devices. For instance, when you want to add more information, you could use "in addition," "moreover," or "furthermore." If you want to show a contrast, words like "however" or "on the other hand" are very useful. This will make your essay more sophisticated and easier to read. For example, in your first body paragraph, you could transition from the general idea of fitting in to your specific example by saying, "This desire to conform is clearly seen, for instance, when teenagers match their outfits..." ✍️	You used everyday vocabulary that was generally appropriate for discussing the topic of teenage choices, and your meaning was understandable most of the time. You successfully conveyed your points about fashion, studying, and music. Let's focus on three key areas to make your grammar even stronger. Firstly, pay close attention to **subject-verb agreement**. For example, you wrote "the people affected me the most is my friends." Since "people" is plural, the verb should be "are," so it should be "the people who affect me the most *are* my friends." Similarly, "it’s make we understandable" should be "it *makes* us understandable." A quick check of your subject and verb will help here. Secondly, be careful with your **word forms**, especially between nouns, verbs, and adjectives. You frequently used "effected" when you meant "affected." "Effect" is usually a noun (e.g., "the effect of friends"), while "affect" is usually a verb (e.g., "friends affect choices"). So, "the most affected topic" and "it *affects* teenagers' choices" would be correct. Thirdly, work on your **prepositions and articles** (a, an, the). For instance, "in my age" should be "at my age," and "in another hand" should be "on the other hand." Also, "many country" should be "many *countries*." These small words are important for sounding natural and precise in English. In Thai, sometimes the distinctions between word forms and the use of articles are less strict than in English, which might lead to these kinds of errors. By consciously reviewing these grammar points, you will find your writing becomes much clearer and more accurate. Keep practicing, and you will see great improvement. 💪	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
