l be well on your way to even higher scores! ✨<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Racing:<br>✅ When you get an essay question asking you to compare, underline or highlight the comparison words (like "who has more effect" in this prompt) to make sure you keep it in mind throughout your writing.<br>✅ For each point you discuss, try to dedicate a sentence or two to *how* friends influence, and then *how* parents influence, making the differences or similarities very clear.<br>✅ Take some time to review your work for common grammar slips, especially making sure your verbs match your subjects, and expand your formal vocabulary.	You did a good job making sure you wrote about clothes and fashion, studying, and even included your own idea about personal interests! That’s exactly what the task asked for. 👍 To make your content even stronger, try to be really clear about *who* influences teenagers more – friends or parents – for each of your points. For instance, when you talked about clothes and fashion, you mentioned friends introduce trends, which is great! But then you could add a sentence directly comparing that to how parents influence fashion choices, perhaps by talking about what they approve of or buy for you. Similarly, when discussing studying, make sure to explicitly show *how* parents influence academic choices versus *how* friends might. Thai learners often do well at describing different aspects, but sometimes we need to remember to explicitly compare and contrast when the question asks for it, rather than just implying the difference.	You successfully got your message across, and it's clear what you wanted to say! You also tried to write in a formal style, which is super important for this type of essay. However, some of your sentences felt a little less formal or a bit awkward, which can make it harder for the reader to understand your point quickly. For example, instead of "Most of their personality is from influence of others," you could say "Teenagers' personalities are largely shaped by the influence of others." 🤔 A good way to improve this is to read more academic essays or articles in English to get a better feel for the formal tone, and practice using a wider variety of formal connecting words like "furthermore," "consequently," or "however" to guide your reader smoothly through your arguments. Sometimes, directly translating ideas from Thai into English can unintentionally make the English sound a little less formal, so thinking about how an English speaker would naturally phrase something can really help!	Your essay is really well-structured, Racing! You have a clear introduction and conclusion, and each of your ideas is in its own paragraph, which makes it very easy to follow your thoughts. You also used paragraph breaks nicely, which helps the reader move through your essay. You’ve used some good connecting words too, which ties your sentences and paragraphs together. To make it even better, you could try introducing each paragraph with a really strong "topic sentence" that clearly states what that paragraph will be about. Keep up the great work with your clear organisation! 🥳	You used a good range of words in your essay, and generally, your grammar was accurate, which is excellent! There were a few places, though, where the language could be tightened up to make your writing even more precise and formal. Let's look at three areas that could help you shine even brighter:<br><br>Firstly, watch out for your vocabulary choices. For example, you wrote "preception" which should be "perception" – an easy spelling mistake to fix! Also, using words like "things" when you could use more specific terms like "aspects" or "areas" can make your writing sound more academic. For instance, instead of "studying is another important things," you could say "studying is another important *aspect*." Many Thai learners sometimes choose more general words, so try to think about the most precise word you can use in a formal context. 🧐<br><br>