ive up her seat on a segregated bus in Montgomery. This event launched him into the national spotlight as an advocate for racial equality.<br><br>So, Martin Luther King did many good things and important things, but the most important things that he did is lead the civil rights movement. So, Martin Luther King led the civil rights movement from 1955 up until his assassination in 1968. Using non-violent resistance inspired by Mahatma Gandhi, he organized peaceful protests against racial segregation, which helped raise awareness to the black community...<br><br>He then went on to receive multiple awards, one of which being the infamous Nobel Peace Prize in 1964 for his leadership. He was only 35 at the time, making him the youngest recipient at that time.<br><br>But with success always comes criticisms and problems. He faced many obstacles in his lifetime. Most intense one being imprisoned 30 times for civil disobedience. And you see, Martin Luther King did many good things, but still people still disagreed with him. Most people in America viewed him as a militant figure, especially among white Americans. And even the media, they labeled him as an extremist and labeled his protests as useless.<br><br>And you might ask, why is Martin Luther King a hero? So here''s my take. So I think that what Martin Luther King did really made a huge impact on the world. He brought peace and equality into the world and helped the black community because without Martin Luther King, the black community would still most likely face racial segregation and he helped bring peace to them.<br><br>So, to summarize, Martin Luther King was a great civil rights leader who led the civil rights movement for many, many years and helped to bring peace into the world, peace and equality. And the message that I want to leave you with is the time is always right to do the right thing. Like I said before, this means there is never really truly a right time to do the right thing. And one major lesson that we can learn from Martin Luther King is to always stand up for what you believe in. Thank you.	700	20.0	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	speaking	CA	FIRST	Your pronunciation was excellent, Ana! You were very clear and easy to understand throughout your talk. At the very beginning, your pronunciation was a bit flat, but your intonation, which is how your voice goes up and down when you speak, got much better as you continued. Your word stress (where you put emphasis on syllables within words) and sentence stress (which words you emphasise in a sentence) were generally spot-on. Thai speakers sometimes struggle with the ''th'' sounds, replacing /θ/ (as in ''think'') with /t/ or /d/, or /ð/ (as in ''this'') with /d/. However, you navigated these sounds with great clarity, which is a common challenge for Thai learners due to the lack of these dental fricatives in Thai. You also handled consonant clusters well, which can be tricky for Thai speakers who sometimes add extra vowels (like ''samoke'' instead of ''smoke''). Your overall intelligibility was fantastic! 🗣️	Hi Ana! What an impressive presentation on Martin Luther King! You spoke with great confidence and showed a really strong understanding of your hero. While there are a few small things we can work on to make your English even smoother, your ability to express complex ideas clearly and at length is truly outstanding! Keep up the fantastic work! ✨	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1by6UKGd4IRmb0pAHXaTn1cuU1wgGUPUF?usp=drive_link	B2+	\N	\N	\N	C1	2026
