e a lot of problem to there class."<br><b>Correction:</b> "It is important to acknowledge that every school has students who may cause disruptions in the classroom."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, direct address and a conversational tone are common. In English academic writing, a more objective and impersonal style is preferred. Avoid using "you" when referring to a general audience."	<b>Problem:</b> You frequently use "there have" instead of "there are" or "there is," which is a common error for Thai speakers due to direct translation. For example, "there always have some students" or "there have good side too."<br><b>Correction:</b> "there are always some students" and "there is a good side too."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, we use "there is" for singular and "there are" for plural to indicate existence. Thai uses 'มี' (mee) which covers both, leading to this common transfer error."	<b>Problem:</b> You often used "didn't" with a base verb in a way that sounds unnatural or grammatically incorrect in certain contexts, particularly when expressing a state or general truth rather than a past action. For example, "Because the phone didn't get allowed doesn't mean they want cause the problem in classroom."<br><b>Correction:</b> "Because forbidding phones does not mean they will necessarily cause problems in the classroom."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Thai sentence structure can be very direct, and the concept of "not doing" something (like 'ไม่ได้') might be directly translated, leading to an overuse of "didn't." English often requires more nuanced verb choices to convey general truths or states."	5.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
