nd help you "relax and calm." That personal touch is really lovely! You also gave some good examples of the challenges, like needing to find someone to care for them when you're away. However, it looks like you accidentally started answering a completely different question in the middle of your article, talking about Lucy and fundraising. 😅 It's super important to read the question carefully and make sure all your writing is focused on just one task. For instance, if the task is about pets, make sure every sentence is related to pets! If you had stuck to just the pet article, you would have scored much higher here.	You certainly communicated some straightforward ideas about your pets, which is a good start! However, when you're writing an article for an online magazine aimed at teenagers, we want to make sure it looks and feels like an article. Think about how magazines present information – they usually have a clear title and separate paragraphs for different ideas. Yours was all in one big block, and then it suddenly changed to a different topic. 😬 To make your writing more engaging, imagine you're talking directly to your readers. For example, you could start with a catchy sentence like, "Do you have pets? If so, you'll know how wonderful they are!" This helps to grab their attention from the beginning.	You've got some interesting ideas here, Mirin, but putting them all into one big paragraph makes it a bit tricky for your reader to follow. Imagine trying to read a whole book with no paragraph breaks! It would be really hard, wouldn't it? 😉 To make your writing much clearer and more coherent, try to group your ideas. For example, you could have one paragraph introducing your opinion on pets, another for the advantages, a third for the disadvantages, and a final one for your conclusion. Using simple linking words to connect your sentences, like "also," "however," or "in conclusion," will help your ideas flow smoothly from one to the next.	You've used some everyday words to talk about your pets, which is good! However, Mirin, there are quite a few errors that make it hard to understand exactly what you mean sometimes. For example, you wrote "haveing pets" instead of "having pets" and "I leaveing alone" instead of "I'm feeling lonely" or "when I'm alone." Also, when you talk about many pets, remember to add an 's' to the end of the noun, like "pets" and "cats," not "a pets." These kinds of mistakes, especially with verb forms and plurals, are common for Thai learners as English grammar has different rules for these. A great way to improve is to practice changing verbs to the correct "-ing" form after prepositions (like "of having") and making sure your nouns match whether you mean one or many things. Try to double-check your grammar for things like subject-verb agreement and plural forms after you finish writing. It really helps your message to be understood clearly! 👍	2.0	1.0	1.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
