running, playing basketball, or other sports is beneficial for both children and teenagers, as it offers a more productive alternative to spending excessive time on video games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Your improved statement clearly introduces the essay's focus on beneficial physical activities and sets up a contrast with less productive options, making your main idea more explicit from the start.<br><br>	You communicated your straightforward ideas, but the overall tone was quite informal and some phrases were repetitive, which affected how clearly your message came across.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Work on maintaining a consistent and more formal register suitable for an essay. Try to vary your sentence structures and vocabulary to avoid repetition.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> You wrote: "to not wasting time to play like something to play game"<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "to avoid wasting time on activities such as video games"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In academic writing, it's important to use more formal and precise language. Phrases like "like something" are often too casual. Using more specific vocabulary and formal structures makes your writing more appropriate for an essay.<br><br>	You used paragraphs to structure your essay, which is a good starting point. However, the internal structure of these paragraphs and the flow between them needs strengthening.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Focus on writing a clear thesis statement in your introduction. Each body paragraph should begin with a strong topic sentence that directly supports your thesis. Also, avoid introducing new ideas in your conclusion; instead, use it to summarise your main points.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> Your first body paragraph was a long, run-on sentence that began: "First, I think kid can play sports more than like to play video games like if you play sport everyday you can improve youself like to be a superstar and make a strong body more than play game everyday and make you better eye very bad."<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Firstly, engaging in sports offers significant advantages for children and teenagers over spending excessive time on video games. Regular participation in sports helps improve physical fitness and can even inspire aspirations of becoming a professional athlete, whereas prolonged gaming can negatively impact eyesight."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> A clear topic sentence at the start of a paragraph helps the reader understand its main point immediately. Breaking down long, complex ideas into shorter, focused sentences improves clarity and flow, making your argument easier to follow.<br><br>	You used everyday vocabulary, which is good. The main challenges are with a few key grammar rules, spelling, and sentence structure.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "activities for kid"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "activities for kids"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai often uses a single form for both singular and plural nouns. In English, however, you must add an '-s' to most countable nouns to show that there is more than one.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "I think kid can play sports more than like to play video games like if you play sport everyday you can improve youself like to be a superstar and make a strong body more than play game everyday and make you better eye very bad."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "I believe children can benefit more from playing sports than video games. If you play sports every day, you can improve yourself, potentially become a superstar, and build a strong body, whereas playing games every day can significantly harm your eyesight."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, it's common to connect many ideas in a single long sentence. In English, we use conjunctions (like 'because', 'whereas') or separate sentences to make ideas clearer and prevent 'run-on' sentences, which can be hard for the reader to follow.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "do in freetime"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "do in free time"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Many compound words in English are written as two separate words, like 'free time', especially when referring to the time itself. 'Everyday' is an adjective (e.g., 'everyday clothes'), but when you mean 'each day,' you should use the two words 'every day'.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Review the use of singular and plural nouns in English, paying close attention to adding '-s' for plural countable nouns.<br>2. Practice writing clear thesis statements for introductions and strong topic sentences for each body paragraph to improve essay structure and flow.<br>3. Work on breaking down long sentences into shorter, clearer ones, using appropriate conjunctions or full stops to separate distinct ideas and avoid run-on sentences.<br><br>	3.0	3.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
