 have a lot of potential to improve! 👍<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Before you start writing, make a quick plan of how you'll answer the *main question* in every paragraph, not just the introduction.<br>✔️ After you write, read your work aloud to catch any sentences that sound a bit awkward or any words that might be missing their 's' at the end.<br>✔️ Try to connect your paragraphs more smoothly using linking words like 'Furthermore', 'In addition', or 'Consequently' to guide your reader.	Kaohom L, you did a good job of bringing up different aspects of teenage life like clothes, fashion, studying, and hobbies! You definitely understood the points the question asked you to cover, and you even added your own idea, which is excellent. However, an essay needs to keep its main point in focus from beginning to end. While you mentioned in your introduction that friends are the most influential, the body of your essay then talked about teenage activities without always linking them back to *how* friends or parents influence these choices. For example, when you discuss studying, you could say something like, 'Teenagers often feel pressure to study hard, and this can come from parents who have high expectations, or from friends who encourage them to achieve similar goals.' Remember, every paragraph should help answer the central question! 🤔	You've made a good effort to write in an essay style, which is super! However, to really grab and keep your reader's attention in an academic essay, it's important to maintain a consistent tone. Sometimes your writing sounded quite formal, but then it slipped into more casual language, like saying 'Nowaday' or 'they are very stress.' To make your writing more effective and suitable for an essay, try to use more formal expressions consistently. For instance, instead of 'really care about they education,' you could write, 'teenagers are deeply committed to their education.' Choosing words carefully helps your essay sound polished and convincing. ✍️	Your essay is structured quite clearly, Kaohom L, with good use of paragraphs for each new idea. That’s a really important step! You also used some helpful phrases like 'The first thing is' and 'Besides' to introduce new sections. To make your writing flow even more smoothly, think about how each paragraph connects to the one before it and to your main argument. Sometimes, repeating phrases like 'in this age' can make the writing feel a little repetitive. You could try using synonyms or slightly different sentence structures to keep your reader engaged. For example, instead of repeating 'in this age,' you could say 'during adolescence' or 'at this stage of life.' This helps your ideas link together more elegantly. ✨	It's clear you have a good range of everyday words, which is brilliant! However, there are a few areas in grammar that we can work on to make your writing much clearer and more precise. For example, sometimes your verbs don't quite match your subjects, like saying 'friends are the most effect' instead of 'friends have the most effect.' Also, words like 'they' can sometimes be confused with 'their' or 'them,' so we need to be careful with those possessive pronouns—for instance, 'their friends' not 'they friends.' Finally, do a quick check for spelling, like 'Nowaday' (should be 'Nowadays') or 'preature' (should be 'pressure'). Many Thai learners sometimes face similar challenges with subject-verb agreement and pronoun usage because the rules in English can be quite different from Thai. With a bit of practice, you’ll master these! Keep trying to use more complex sentences, and with careful checking, your grammar will become much stronger. 💪	3.0	2.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
