instead of "make-friends") and making sure your spelling is correct.	You mentioned "clothes and fashion" and "studying," which were in the prompt, and even added your own idea about "games"! That's a good start. However, the essay asked "Who has more effect on the choices teenagers make - friends or parents?", but you wrote about ways to make friends instead. For example, when the question asks "Who has more effect?", it wants you to compare friends and parents and explain which group influences choices more. Because the main question wasn't answered directly, it was difficult for the reader to understand your main point. Thai learners sometimes misinterpret essay questions, so a great tip is to underline the most important words in the question before you begin writing to ensure you are answering exactly what is asked.	You tried to share your thoughts and put forward some ideas about how teenagers connect, which is good! You managed to inform the reader about your opinion on making friends. To improve, remember that for a formal essay like this, it's important to have a very clear main purpose that directly answers the question. Also, using a more formal tone, like "It is evident that..." rather than starting with "I think," can make your essay feel more academic. Continuing to practice answering different types of questions will help you express your ideas more clearly and achieve your writing goals.	You've done well in separating your ideas into different paragraphs, with an introduction and a conclusion, which helps to organise your thoughts! 😊 This is a good foundation. To make your essay even clearer, try to have a strong "thesis statement" right at the beginning of your introduction that clearly tells the reader what your essay will be about and directly answers the question. For instance, you could say, "This essay will argue that friends have a greater influence on teenagers' choices regarding fashion and studying than parents do." Also, try to use a wider range of linking words like "furthermore," "in addition," or "on the other hand" to connect your paragraphs more smoothly, instead of just "First way" or "Beside." Sometimes, learners focus more on individual sentences than the overall flow, so thinking about how each paragraph leads to the next can be very helpful.	You used many everyday words correctly, and the reader could generally follow your main message! That’s a real positive. However, there were quite a few grammar and spelling points that we can improve to make your writing even clearer and more accurate. For example, you frequently wrote "make-friends," but in English, we usually write "make friends" (two separate words as a verb phrase) or "making friends" (as a gerund or present participle). Another common point was using "other" when "others" would be more appropriate, such as "make friends with others easily." Also, a spelling check would catch words like "knowlegde," which should be "knowledge." These types of mistakes are quite common for Thai learners as English often uses compound nouns or phrases differently, and it’s easy to mix up singular and plural forms or omit articles. Focusing on these common errors will make a big difference in the clarity and precision of your writing! Keep practising, and you'll see great improvements.	1.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
