><li><b>Answering your own question!</b> 💬 You asked 'Do you think it's good to read a lot.' Instead of just repeating the prompt exactly, try to lead into your answer more smoothly, like: 'Many people wonder if reading a lot is really worth it, and I definitely think so!'</li><li><b>Engagement!</b> 🤝 Try to end with a recommendation for the reader to try a comic themselves to make the ending more 'article-like'.</li>	Your article is easy to follow because you used linking words like 'because', 'so', and 'also'. 🌊<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Variety!</b> 🔗 You used 'Reading also help' twice. To make your writing more advanced, try changing the second one to 'Furthermore, it improves...' or 'Another benefit is...'.</li><li><b>Paragraphs!</b> 📑 Try to start a new paragraph when you move from talking about comics to talking about the benefits of reading in general.</li><li><b>Transitions!</b> 🚀 Use a linking word to start your conclusion, like 'To sum up' or 'In conclusion', to show the reader the article is finishing.</li>	You used good everyday vocabulary like 'emotions', 'creative', and 'relax'. 👏<br><br>Here are some corrections to help you polish your writing:<br><li><b>Subject-Verb Agreement!</b> ⚙️ 'My favorite stories <b>is</b> comic' -> 'My favorite stories <b>are</b> comics' (Plural subjects need plural verbs). Also, 'the story <b>feel</b>' -> 'the story <b>feels</b>'.</li><li><b>Third Person 's'!</b> 🛠️ 'Reading also <b>help</b>' -> 'Reading also <b>helps</b>'. When 'Reading' is the subject, the verb needs an 's'.</li><li><b>Spelling!</b> ✏️ 'I <b>thing</b> reading' -> 'I <b>think</b> reading'.</li><li><b>Punctuation!</b> 📍 'Do you think it's good to read a lot.' -> 'Do you think it's good to read a lot<b>?</b>'. Questions always need a question mark! Also, check the very end: 'enjoy story more,' -> 'enjoy <b>stories</b> more<b>.</b>'</li>	5.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
