ork! 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br><ul><li>✔️ When you're brainstorming your ideas for an essay, think about how you can present your "own idea" point in a distinct way that adds a new dimension to the topic, rather than just another example of the main theme.</li><li>✔️ Practise writing in a more formal style by reading examples of academic essays or articles. Pay attention to how the sentences are structured and the vocabulary used, and try to incorporate those elements into your own writing.</li><li>✔️ After you write your essay, always read it aloud to yourself. This helps you catch awkward sentences and grammar mistakes that you might miss when reading silently!</li></ul>	You've done a good job of addressing the main points of the essay question, Namunn, which is fantastic! You clearly discussed how friends influence choices in fashion and studying. However, when you presented "hobby" as your own idea, it felt a little bit like another example of friends' influence, rather than a fresh, different perspective that an academic essay usually looks for. 🤔 For instance, you could have explored how friends influence teenagers' *future career choices* or *financial decisions* as your own idea, which would show a broader understanding of the prompt. This will help you demonstrate a deeper engagement with the topic and really get that higher score for content!	You've tried to present your ideas in an essay format, which is a great start! However, for a formal essay, we need to make sure the tone is consistent and sounds academic. Sometimes, your writing felt a little bit like you were chatting with a friend rather than writing for a teacher or examiner. For example, phrases like "really care about how to shine in the society?" are a bit too casual. 😊 To improve this, try to use more formal language and sentence structures. Instead of asking a question like that, you could say, "Teenagers are often highly concerned with their social image and how to gain acceptance from others." This small change makes a big difference in how formal your writing sounds!	Your essay is well-organised into paragraphs, with each one tackling a different aspect of your argument, which is a really good habit to get into! That's a solid foundation. 👍 However, sometimes the way you link your ideas together could be a bit smoother and more varied. Phrases like "Beside fasion" or "lastly hobby" are a bit simple and can make your essay feel a little choppy. For example, instead of just starting a new paragraph with "Beside fasion," you could say, "Beyond the realm of fashion, academic pursuits are also significantly shaped by peer influence." Adding these kinds of connecting phrases will help your essay flow much more naturally and impress your reader! 🌊	You have lots of ideas, Namunn, and that's wonderful! However, some of the grammar and word choices make it a bit difficult for the reader to understand your message clearly at times. For example, phrases like "teenagers are really care" should be "teenagers really care," and "a lots of impacts" should be "a lot of impacts" or "many impacts." Also, "many people may not no that who you really are" should be "many people may not *know* who they really are." 🧐 These kinds of errors are very common for Thai learners because the structure of English sentences, especially with verbs and plurals, can be quite different from Thai. To improve, try to pay close attention to verb agreement (making sure your verbs match your subjects, like "teenagers *care*," not "teenagers *are care*"), and be careful with your spelling, especially with words that sound similar but have different meanings, like "no" and "know." A good trick is to re-read your sentences slowly to catch these little slips. You've got this! 💪	3.0	2.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
