ed. For example, you could describe how the costumes were displayed, what your friends said, or even a funny encounter you had. Making sure your story has a clear ending, even a short one, helps your reader feel satisfied and fully informed. 👍	You certainly tried to create a narrative that would grab the reader's attention from the start, which is commendable. You’ve used the correct opening, and the idea of Halloween costumes at school is quite engaging. However, because the story is incomplete and ends rather abruptly ('but when I walked'), the overall goal of communicating a full, straightforward story isn't quite met. To improve this, think about your target reader. They want to know what happened next and how everything concluded. For instance, after seeing the costumes, you could have described a conversation with your friend about them, or how a teacher explained the event, leading to a small adventure or a funny moment. A complete narrative flow, with a middle and an end, will make your story much more satisfying for anyone reading it. 📚	Your story has a basic flow, following a sequence of events as you and your friend arrived at school and walked down the hallway, which is good. You used a basic linking word like 'Then' to move from one idea to the next. To elevate your score here, consider organising your ideas into slightly clearer sections or paragraphs. Even within a short story, beginning a new paragraph when a new main idea or shift in action occurs helps the reader follow along more easily. For example, you could have a short paragraph for your arrival and seeing the costumes, another for your reactions and your friend's, and then a final one for the resolution. This helps to make your story feel more polished and coherent, using a variety of ways to connect your ideas. ✍️	Frame, you've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, which helps convey your story. The words you chose for 'frightening' and 'amazing' are good examples of this. However, there are some grammatical points that we can work on to make your writing even clearer and more accurate. For instance, when you wrote 'The school was organized something,' it would be more natural to say 'The school had organised something' or 'The school was organising something.' Also, phrases like 'Me and my friend walked' are very common in spoken English, but in writing, it's better to say 'My friend and I walked.' This small change makes a big difference in formal writing. Additionally, do remember to double-check your spelling, as 'loughed' should be 'laughed.' These kinds of errors are very common for Thai learners, as sometimes the way words are pronounced doesn't always translate directly to how they are spelled or how sentence structures work in English. Practising subject-verb agreement and pronoun usage will really strengthen your writing. Keep an eye on these details, and your language control will surely improve. ✨	2.0	2.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
