de a good effort to pronounce the endings of words, which is super important for clear English! Keep practicing those final sounds, as sometimes they can still be a little quiet, making words like "played" sound like "play." For example, ensuring you clearly pronounce the /d/ at the end of "played" or the /s/ at the end of "games" helps your listener know if you are talking about one thing or many, or if something happened in the past. This is an area where Thai speakers sometimes find it tricky, as final consonants are often not pronounced as strongly in Thai. Your hard work on this will make a big difference! 🗣️	Hello Pheem! It was lovely to listen to your speaking test. You put in a great effort, and it’s clear you’re working hard on your English! 👍 To help you get even better, let’s focus on building longer, more detailed sentences and confidently linking your ideas. Keep up your positive attitude, and you’ll find yourself expressing your thoughts more smoothly and accurately. Keep practicing, and you'll see great progress! 🚀<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>• ✅ Practice Past Tenses: When talking about things that happened in the past (like "last week"), try to remember to use past tense verbs like "played" or "went". A good way to remember is to think, "If it's yesterday, the verb usually ends in '-ed' or changes form!"<br>• ✅ Expand Your Answers: Challenge yourself to add one or two more sentences to your responses. For instance, instead of just saying "My father is...", you could add, "My father is a kind person, and he works very hard." This helps you share more of your thoughts!<br>• ✅ Listen and Respond to Others: In conversations, try to connect your ideas to what your partner has just said. You could start with phrases like "That's a good idea, and I also think..." or "I agree with you, and another point is..." This shows you’re actively listening and can help the conversation flow more naturally.	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/