s and sentences. Also, your speech has a slightly 'staccato' effect due to minimal linking between words. In English, we often link words together, for example, "what she did" can flow more smoothly like /wɒtʃi dɪd/. To improve, focus on sentence stress and intonation patterns. Practicing linking words and reducing the stress on unstressed syllables can make your speech sound more natural and fluent. For example, try to reduce vowels in unstressed syllables to a schwa /ə/ sound.</p>	<p>Hi Ant! 👋 It's great to see your effort in talking about Lady Mo as your hero. You did a good job of introducing your hero and outlining your talk, which is a great start! However, to really shine in your presentations, you'll need to work on expanding your ideas and making sure your sentences are grammatically correct. Keep practicing, and you'll definitely improve! ✨</p>	a2e775c4-e795-45e6-b042-7e908b05f577	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
