 Finn was beautifully tragic, and the twist ending with Finn joining Sam in real life was brilliant. You truly showed great imagination.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you polish your writing even further. 📋<br>1. <b>Irregular Verbs!</b> ⚔️ 'Shoot' becomes 'shot' in the past tense, not 'shotted'.<br>2. <b>Reflexive Pronouns!</b> 🪞 It's 'himself' (one word) not 'him self'.<br>3. <b>Word Forms!</b> 📝 Remember that verbs and nouns can have different forms (e.g., 'eliminate' vs 'eliminated').	The concept of being pulled into a realistic zombie game is fantastic and relevant! 💡<br>The emotional depth of Sam's friendship with Finn was very strong.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Show, Don't Tell!</b> 🎭 You said Sam 'shocked'. How did he show his shock? Eyes wide? Jaw dropped? Show us his reaction.<br>2. <b>Pacing!</b> ⏱️ The final twist felt a little fast. Maybe add a few more sentences of Sam's reaction when Finn introduces himself in college.	Your story was very engaging and held my attention completely, especially the dramatic ending. 😭<br>The emotional impact of Finn's sacrifice was powerfully conveyed.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Clarity!</b> 🗣️ Some phrases, like 'giving his body', could be rephrased to be clearer (e.g., 'sacrificing himself').<br>2. <b>Sustained Impact!</b> 💥 Try to maintain the intense emotional impact all the way to the very last sentence.	The structure of your story, moving from the real world to the game and back, was well-handled. 🌐<br>You used linking words to guide the reader through the plot.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Variety!</b> 🔗 While your organisation is good, try to vary your linking words and sentence structures more to enhance complexity.<br>2. <b>Paragraph Cohesion!</b> 🧱 Ensure each paragraph flows seamlessly into the next, especially in fast-paced action sequences.	Your story uses good vocabulary to create a vivid game world! 🎮<br><br>Here are a few corrections to help you:<br>1. <b>Irregular Verbs!</b> 📝 'Shotted' -> '<b>Shot</b