 organised and flowing logically. <br>✔️ After writing, read your article aloud to yourself. This helps you catch awkward sentences or missing words that you might not notice when reading silently. <br>✔️ Pay close attention to small but important grammar points like making sure your verbs match your subjects (e.g., "pets are" not "pets is") and using words like "a," "an," and "the" correctly.	You've done a good job of addressing the task by discussing both the advantages and disadvantages of having pets, and you've included a conclusion, which is exactly what was asked for! It's clear that you have a personal opinion on the topic, especially since you mentioned having pets yourself. To make your content even more impactful and help your reader feel fully informed, try to elaborate a bit more on your points with specific examples. For instance, when you talk about pets helping with loneliness, you could give a brief example of how playing with your pet makes you feel less alone. Similarly, when discussing the "waste" of money, you could mention specific costs like vet visits or special food, which would add more detail and support to your arguments. Sometimes in Thai, ideas are expressed implicitly, but in English, being explicit with examples really helps the reader.	You’ve made a good start at writing an article for teenagers, sharing your personal connection with pet ownership. You tried to make it engaging by asking a question at the beginning, which is a great technique! To make your articles even more captivating for your audience and to communicate both straightforward and more complex ideas effectively, think about structuring your paragraphs with clear topic sentences. For example, you could start a paragraph about the good things with a sentence like, "Pets bring so much happiness into our lives because..." and then give your examples. Similarly, a paragraph about the downsides could begin with, "However, it's also important to remember the responsibilities that come with owning a pet." This helps to keep your readers interested and makes your ideas easier to follow. Your current paragraph breaks are a good start, but ensure each new idea gets its own dedicated space to help the reader understand your flow better.	Your article shows a clear attempt at organisation, with separate paragraphs for the advantages and disadvantages of owning pets, which is a good foundation. You also used some basic linking words like "First thing is" and "But in the other hand" to connect your ideas. To improve your score in this area, you could focus on using a wider variety of cohesive devices to make your text flow more naturally and clearly. For instance, instead of just "next bad thing," you could use phrases like "Furthermore," "In addition," or "Another drawback is." Also, ensure that each paragraph focuses on a single main idea and that your sentences within those paragraphs are well-connected. This will make your article much smoother and easier for your teenage readers to follow. Perhaps in Thai, longer sentences without explicit connectors are more common, but in English, a clearer breakdown of ideas with connecting words is generally preferred.	You use a range of everyday words that are appropriate for talking about pets, which is great! However, there are some areas where your grammar could be a bit more controlled and precise. For example, you often say "pets is" instead of "pets are," which is a common mistake related to subject-verb agreement. Also, remember to use articles like "a," "an," and "the" more consistently, for instance, "the basic question" or "a positive side." Spelling words like "basically," "positive," and "waste" (instead of "basicly," "possitive," and "waest") will also make your writing clearer. Finally, try to break up very long sentences into shorter, more manageable ones to avoid run-on sentences and improve readability. For example, instead of "First bad thing of pets is it extramely waest your money because you need to buy it house, buy it food and buy it snack in every month it really waest your money and time," you could say: "Firstly, pets can be extremely expensive. You need to buy them a house, food, and snacks every month, which truly wastes both your money and time." These small adjustments will make a big difference!	3.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
