1885	29561	2025-09-16 21:31:09.780247+00	Term 1 Email	Hello<br><br>the tickets you got is great so you will pick me up on the way best for me is pick me up on the way. No i don't think t film would be interesting yeah we should bring somthing else like phone or more money also we should go build ro bot i' think it would be better than film.	62	6.0	Hello Mathew! 👋 It's lovely to see your effort in writing this email. You've made a good start at communicating your ideas, and it's clear you understood the main points of Corey's email. Keep up the good work! However, a really important area for you to focus on right now is making sure your sentences are clear and easy to read, especially by using punctuation and paragraphs. Once you master that, your writing will flow so much better! But remember, you've got lots of excellent ideas, and with a bit more practice, you'll be expressing them even more effectively. Keep practising, and you'll definitely see improvement! <br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:** <br>• ✅ When you finish a thought or sentence, always put a full stop (.). Think of it like taking a breath! <br>• ✅ Remember to use capital letters at the beginning of every new sentence and for proper nouns like names (e.g., Corey). <br>• ✅ Break your email into different paragraphs for each new idea. This makes your writing much easier to read and understand.	You've done a good job of touching on most of the things Corey mentioned, like saying the tickets are great and choosing to be picked up. You also let him know you'd prefer to build a robot rather than watch the film, which is excellent! 👍 You even suggested bringing a phone or more money. To get an even better score here, try to add a short reason for your suggestions. For instance, if you suggest bringing money, you could say 'because we might want to buy a snack.' Also, it's really important to try and write a bit more! Your email was shorter than we hoped for, so aiming for around 100 words will help you add those extra details. Sometimes, Thai learners are a little too brief, so adding a sentence or two to explain your points can really help!	You started your email with a friendly 'Hello,' which is a great start for an informal email to a friend! 😊 To make your email even better, remember to also include a friendly closing, like 'See you soon,' and your name at the end. Another thing that makes emails easier to read is using paragraphs. Imagine your email is a short letter; each new idea should usually get its own paragraph. For instance, you could have one paragraph about the tickets and another about what activity you'd like to do. This helps your reader follow your thoughts much more easily! Sometimes, students forget that the way an email looks is just as important as what it says.	This is an area where you can make some really big improvements, Mathew! 🤗 Right now, your email is written as one long message, which can be tricky for your reader to understand. Think of your email like building blocks: each new idea or piece of information should be its own block, or paragraph. For instance, when you talk about the tickets, that could be one small paragraph. Then, when you discuss being picked up, that could be another. Using full stops at the end of each sentence and starting new sentences with capital letters will also help a lot with organising your thoughts. Thai learners often focus on getting all their ideas down, but remembering to separate them into clear sentences and paragraphs makes a huge difference!	You've used some good everyday words that fit the topic, like 'tickets,' 'film,' and 'robot.' That's a solid foundation! However, there are quite a few places where your grammar and punctuation made it a bit difficult to understand your message clearly. 🚧 For example, remember that when you're excited, you can say 'The tickets are great!' instead of 'the tickets you got is great.' Also, always put a full stop at the end of a sentence, and start a new sentence with a capital letter. For instance, instead of 'No i don't think t film would be interesting,' you could write 'No, I don't think the film would be interesting.' Many Thai learners sometimes forget these small but powerful grammar rules, like using articles (a/an/the) and making sure their verbs match their subjects, but focusing on these will make your writing much clearer and more confident!	2.0	2.0	1.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A1+	2026
