1085	29604	2025-08-21 13:01:23.407527+00	Social Issue	I believe that mobile phones should be banned in every class. First of all, when students got a chance to research information, they learn nothing. They didn't use it for learning or work; they just secretly play on their phone. Those phones do not help the students learn at all. They're just reading and writing the information, which mean they're not learning. And most of the kind teachers will neglecting this specific part just because they want their students to get a lot of score. Studies show that many student got lower grades in their exam because teacher let the students find all the information on their own. Thought they'll behave and learn but its the opposite. Said by some determined students. Furthermore, students always end up playing games or scrolling through social media in class. As soon as those students found their ways to get their phone they would immediately play their phone even though the teacher said the phones are unprohibited in class. And because of that the student will start to turn against them. Then start to bring two phone to class, hide their phone from teachers and behave badly. From my experience teachers not really checking the phones in class. So it's easy for students to tricked the teacher, which it's a serious situation. The last reason, We students don't really need phone in class. Everyone knows that mobile phones are the easiest way to do everything. But we also can learn in traditional ways too. Its make the students learn better too. Students actully learn better by listen and write instead of read and write. Teachers can describe the lessons better than some random stranger anyway. Everythings that phones can replace by some real technolgy for education. For these three reasons, I believe that mobile phone should be banned in every class. before the students be buried with phones.	314	15.0	Dear Yayar<br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Social Issue'. You wrote 314 words and achieved B1. You presented a very clear opinion and supported it with several reasons, which is a great start.	You did a great job explaining why you believe mobile phones should be banned. Your ideas were clear and relevant to the topic.<br><b>Problem:</b> You mentioned "Studies show that many student got lower grades" but didn't give specific details about these studies.<br><b>Correction:</b> For example, you could say: "Studies by educational researchers, such as the one conducted by Dr. Smith in 2023, show that many students achieved lower grades in exams when they were allowed to use phones for research."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In academic writing, it's good to give more specific evidence, like where the study came from, to make your points stronger. Even if you don't have exact facts, try to make it sound more like a general observation than a definite "study."	You successfully communicated your main point and kept the reader interested. Your essay was easy to follow, and your opinion came across clearly.<br><b>Problem:</b> You used phrases like "Said by some determined students" which sounds a bit informal for academic writing.<br><b>Correction:</b> Instead, you could write: "This view is shared by some determined students."<br><b>Explanation:</b> While it's good to include voices, in formal writing, we usually rephrase direct quotes or informal statements to fit a more academic tone. This helps maintain a consistent formal register throughout your essay.	You organized your essay with clear introductory phrases like "First of all," "Furthermore," and "The last reason," which helped guide the reader. Each section also focused on a different reason.<br><b>Problem:</b> Sometimes, the connection between sentences felt a bit sudden, for example: "They didn't use it for learning or work; they just secretly play on their phone. Those phones do not help the students learn at all. They're just reading and writing the information, which mean they're not learning."<br><b>Correction:</b> You could combine or link these ideas more smoothly: "They didn't use it for learning or work; instead, they just secretly played on their phone. Consequently, these phones did not help the students learn at all, as they were merely reading and writing information without true engagement."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Thai language often allows for sentences to be placed next to each other without strong linking words. In English, using connecting words like "instead," "consequently," or "therefore" helps ideas flow more logically and smoothly for the reader.	You used a good range of everyday words and tried to use different types of sentences. Most of your sentences were clear, even with some small errors.<br><b>Problem:</b> You often missed the plural '-s' on nouns, for example, "many student" and "two phone."<br><b>Correction:</b> It should be "many students" and "two phones."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, we don't always change words to show there is more than one. In English, when we talk about more than one person or thing, we usually add an '-s' to the end of the noun, like 'dogs' or 'books.' This is a very common difference between Thai and English grammar.	4.0	4.0	3.0	4.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
