entioned the book 'open your hypothetical'. I think you meant it 'opens your <b>imagination</b>'. Using the right word helps the reader understand your point better.</li>	Your article has a clear beginning and end, and it flows in a logical way from the plot summary to your recommendation. 🌊<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Use Linking Words!</b> 🔗 While you used 'Also' and 'At the end', try adding words like 'However' or 'Because' to connect your sentences. For example: 'He grew up in a bad atmosphere, <b>but</b> he eventually became very powerful.'</li><li><b>Sentence Structure!</b> 🛠️ Some sentences are missing verbs. For example, 'Harry Potter a book that...' should be 'Harry Potter <b>is</b> a book that...'.</li>	You used some good vocabulary like 'atmosphere' and 'powerful'! 👏<br><br>Here are some corrections to help you polish your English:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> ✏️ Check the spelling of character names and common words: 'Voldemour' -> <b>Voldemort</b>, 'atmogpheve' -> <b>atmosphere</b>, and 'wizowd' -> <b>wizard</b>.</li><li><b>Verb Tenses!</b> ⏳ For the past tense of teach, use 'taught' instead of 'thought'. Also, the past of beat is 'beat' (not 'beated'). So: 'who <b>beat</b> Voldemort'.</li><li><b>Plurals!</b> 👥 'an opportunities' should be 'an <b>opportunity</b>' (singular) or '<b>the</b> opportunities' (plural).</li><li><b>Possessives!</b> 🔑 'Voldemour power' should be '<b>Voldemort's</b> power'.</li>	2.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	A2	2026
