..." or "Moreover, it's worth considering..." This helps your ideas flow more smoothly and makes your writing more sophisticated. Sometimes, writers from a Thai background focus on conveying individual ideas clearly, and might benefit from consciously thinking about how each paragraph seamlessly transitions into the next.	You've used everyday words quite well, and your meaning is generally clear, which is important. However, I've noticed a few repeated grammatical patterns that we can work on. For instance, ensure that your verbs agree with your subjects. If you write "Human is a living things," remember that "things" is plural, so it should be "Human *are* living things." Similarly, "Pets is always loyal" should be "Pets *are* always loyal." Another area to focus on is the correct use of pronouns and articles. These kinds of grammatical inconsistencies are common for Thai learners as the structure of English grammar can be quite different from Thai, which often relies less on explicit subject-verb agreement or articles. Paying close attention to these details will make your writing much more accurate and polished.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
