the reader, which is exactly what we want to see!	You’ve made a good start at writing an article for teenagers, sharing your personal connection with Noah. To make your articles even more engaging for your audience, think about how you can use clear headings or topic sentences for each paragraph. For instance, you could start a paragraph about the advantages with a sentence like, 'Pets bring so much happiness into our lives because...' and then give your examples. Similarly, a paragraph about the disadvantages could begin with, 'However, it's also important to remember the responsibilities that come with owning a pet.' This helps to keep your readers interested and makes your ideas easier to follow. Your current paragraph breaks are a good start, but ensure each new idea gets its own dedicated space to help the reader understand your flow better.	Your article has a basic structure, which is a good foundation! You have a clear introduction and a conclusion that sums up your feelings. To make your writing even smoother, try to use more varied linking words and phrases to guide your reader from one idea to the next. For instance, after talking about the joy Noah brings, you could introduce the responsibilities by saying, 'On the other hand, owning a pet also comes with certain duties...' Or, 'Despite these joys, we must also consider the practical side...' This helps your ideas flow more logically and makes it easier for the reader to understand your points. Sometimes, in Thai, ideas are connected more implicitly, but in English, using explicit linking words helps to create a clear and coherent argument.	You’ve used everyday vocabulary appropriately, which is excellent! However, there are a number of grammatical areas we can work on to make your meaning even clearer. For example, in the phrase 'sometime I want to play,' you should use 'sometimes' to indicate frequency. Also, 'they will studying' should be 'they are studying' or 'they will be studying' to correctly form the future continuous tense. Another common area for improvement is subject-verb agreement, like when you wrote 'He like to stole my food' – it should be 'He likes to steal my food.' Remember to use 'itself' for things rather than 'them self,' and 'our friends' instead of 'ours friends.' Focusing on these areas will help your sentences become more accurate and your communication more precise. Keep an eye on your verb endings and make sure they match the subject of your sentence! 👀	5.0	2.0	3.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
