 made a good start at writing an article for young people, sharing your personal connection to Christmas. To make your articles even more captivating and to really keep your readers' attention, think about how you can structure your writing with a clear opening, a main part, and a satisfying ending. For example, you could start with a catchy sentence to introduce Christmas, then describe the events, and finally, summarise why it's so important to you. Ensuring your language is always welcoming and not too formal will also help your international audience feel more connected to your writing. You've got some good straightforward ideas here; the next step is to make them even more engaging. ✨	Your article has a clear flow, Saint, and the reader can definitely follow your ideas about Christmas. You connect your thoughts using words like 'Because' and 'And,' which is a good start. To make your writing even smoother and more sophisticated, try using a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For instance, instead of always starting a new sentence with 'And,' you could use words like 'Also,' 'Furthermore,' or 'In addition' to introduce new details. To show a result, you might use 'Therefore' or 'As a result.' Thinking about how each sentence leads to the next will help your paragraphs feel even more connected and logical, guiding your reader through your lovely descriptions effortlessly. 🔗	You use many everyday words correctly to talk about Christmas, and the meaning of your writing is clear despite a few noticeable errors. To really elevate your language, Saint, let's focus on a few key areas. Firstly, pay close attention to plural forms, for example, you wrote 'new years' when it should be 'new year's' or 'new year' and 'fire works' when it should be 'fireworks'. Second, review verb tenses, such as 'I will got present' which should be 'I will get presents' or 'I get presents,' as this can sometimes make your meaning a little less precise. In Thai, plurals are often 