noticeable problem with consonant clusters, specifically the 'tr' sound, as in "travel." Sometimes, the /r/ sound was missing or replaced with a /w/ or /v/ sound. English has many consonant clusters that are not common in Thai. To improve, practice saying words like 'train' /treɪn/, 'tree' /triː/, and 'strong' /strɒŋ/ slowly, making sure to articulate both consonants clearly without adding an extra vowel sound in between.</li><li><b>Dropped final consonants:</b> You frequently dropped consonant endings, for example, in the word "main." In English, final consonants are very important for meaning and clarity. Thai often emits or weakens final consonants, which can make it hard for English listeners to understand you. Practice exaggerating the final consonant sound in words like 'cat' /kæt/, 'book' /bʊk/, and 'main' /meɪn/ until it feels natural.</li></ul><p>Focusing on these specific areas will greatly enhance how clearly you speak English!</p>	<p>Hello ThatThat! 👋 You did a great job organizing your presentation about Pho Khun Ramkhamhaeng and sharing lots of interesting information about why he is your hero. Your talk followed a clear structure, which made it easy to follow your ideas. Keep working on making your sentences more accurate and clearer, especially with your grammar, and pay special attention to pronouncing all your sounds clearly. You have a lot of potential, and with consistent practice, your English will shine even more! ✨</p>	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
