 and has a spelling error.<br><b>Correction:</b> "First, students who use their phones a lot in the classroom are not focused on the teacher."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In academic English, use formal transition words like 'Firstly,' or 'First,' to introduce your points, and ensure correct spelling.	Your paragraph was well-structured with a clear beginning, middle, and end. You used basic linking words to connect your ideas. 👍<br><b>Problem:</b> You used ". Second reason," to start a new point. While clear, using a full stop and then starting a new sentence with "Second reason" makes the text a little less smooth.<br><b>Correction:</b> "Second, mobile phones offer many ways to find exam answers."<br><b>Explanation:</b> For better flow in English, transition words like "Second," or "Secondly," are typically followed by a comma, and the sentence starts with a capital letter.	You used everyday words, but there were frequent mistakes in grammar and spelling that sometimes made your meaning difficult to understand. This shows you are still developing control over basic sentence structures.<br><b>Problem:</b> You wrote "I believe that mobile phone should be banned in classroom".<br><b>Correction:</b> "I believe that mobile phones should be banned in the classroom."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, when talking about things in general, we use plural nouns (like 'phones') and articles (like 'the') for specific places (like 'the classroom'). Thai doesn't always use these, so it's a common area to practice for Thai speakers.	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
