968	28836	2025-07-23 15:32:06.871454+00	A Place I Love	A place I love is Kanchanaburi. Kanchanaburi, is a town with wonderful nature. I'm going tell you why it is. Firstly Kanchanaburi is a one out of 77 town in Thailand. It's mostly known by their faublous nature. From my perspective I think this is really true, Why? Because I've went there so many times That I can't remembered.<br><br><br>Now let's moved on to the most important part. Kanchanaburi, which is a town from Thailand that I endure the most. It's famous because of their wondeful nature. Mosty their waterfalls that includes Erawan waterfall, which is the most famouse and attract many tourist aroud the world. It have many levels of the waterfal, which had 7 levels. If you want play the waterfall at 7th level. You need to walk for 3,000+ meters, which is 3 kilometers. I usually have a whopping visits tourist of 40,000 per month. Let me give some interesting story from me. So for about 3-4 months ago I went to Kanchanaburi, which I was talking about. I went there with my family which induded me, my mother, my father and lastly my grand mother. We went there because my school is closed for awhile. We had so much fun there. We did some recomended activities such as Long Kang, Zipline across the emeral lake and much more. It was so thrilling that I want to go try it again<br><br><br>So, In conclusion, Kanchanaburi is a place worth to visit onece in your life. It's a town that is truely make for people who likes to explore nature and do a thrilling activities. I really recommended you should went there during rainy season or during summer. Remember it's worth to try once in your life,	289	10.0	Namo K, you wrote about Kanchanaburi and explained why you love it because of its wonderful nature, especially Erawan Waterfall. You shared your personal experiences there with your family and mentioned thrilling activities. You did a good job of introducing the town and giving reasons. To make your writing even better, you can focus on making your article sound more formal for a travel website, organizing your ideas with clearer paragraphs, and correcting mistakes in spelling, grammar, and punctuation so your message is always very clear and easy to understand.	Namo K, you introduced Kanchanaburi as a place you love and focused on its wonderful nature, especially Erawan Waterfall. You gave reasons by sharing your personal experiences and recommending activities. You did a good job of covering the prompt. However, you included a lot of details about the waterfall and visitor numbers that, while interesting, might have taken up too much space for a general travel article and distracted from other reasons you love the *town*.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "It have many levels of the waterfal, which had 7 levels. If you want play the waterfall at 7th level. You need to walk for 3,000+ meters, which is 3 kilometers. I usually have a whopping visits tourist of 40,000 per month."\n    Problem: This section gives very specific facts and numbers about the waterfall. While informative, it reads a bit like a factual report rather than a personal article about a place you *love*. It also takes up a lot of space for details that might be better summarized or presented differently.\n    How to fix it: Instead of just listing facts and numbers, connect them more to your personal experience and why you love it. For example, "Erawan Waterfall is truly magnificent, with its seven levels offering a refreshing challenge for visitors. It's so popular that it attracts thousands of tourists each month, and walking the 3 kilometers to the seventh level is a rewarding journey with breathtaking views."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Remember it's worth to try once in your life," at the very end.\n    Problem: This phrase feels a bit like a last-minute addition and doesn't fully bring a strong end to your article.\n    How to fix it: Your conclusion should summarize why the place is special and leave a strong, inviting message for the reader. You could say, "So, if you're looking for an unforgettable adventure in nature, Kanchanaburi truly is a place worth visiting. I hope you get the chance to experience its natural wonders and thrilling activities for yourself!"	You tried to explain why Kanchanaburi is a good place to visit and to get the reader interested. You communicated your ideas, but the informal language and conversational style ("I'm going tell you," "Let me give some interesting story from me") were not always suitable for an article on a travel website. This made it harder to keep the reader's attention consistently.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "A place I love is Kanchanaburi. Kanchanaburi, is a town with wonderful nature. I'm going tell you why it is."\n    Problem: The opening is quite direct and sounds like spoken English. "I'm going tell you why it is" is too informal.\n    How to fix it: Start your article with a more formal and engaging opening suitable for a travel website. For example, "Kanchanaburi, a town blessed with wonderful nature, holds a special place in my heart. Allow me to explain why it is such a remarkable destination."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Now let's moved on to the most important part."\n    Problem: This phrase is very conversational and breaks the flow of a written article. It's an unnecessary instruction to the reader.\n    How to fix it: You can simply start the next paragraph with a clear topic sentence. For example, "The most captivating aspect of Kanchanaburi is its wonderful nature, particularly its waterfalls."	You have a clear beginning and end to your essay. However, your main body is written as one very long paragraph that tries to cover too many different points at once. This makes it difficult for the reader to follow your ideas smoothly, even though you have some linking words. For a long essay like this, it's really important to break it into smaller, more focused paragraphs.\n\n*   Example: Your second paragraph is very long and talks about Erawan Waterfall's levels and visitors, then suddenly shifts to a personal story about your family trip, and then lists recommended activities.\n    Problem: Mixing so many different ideas (facts about the waterfall, visitor numbers, a personal anecdote, and activity recommendations) in one paragraph makes it hard to read and understand the main focus of that section.\n    How to fix it: Break this long paragraph into smaller, separate paragraphs for each main idea. For example:\n    *   One paragraph about the famous Erawan Waterfall and its features.\n    *   Another paragraph about your personal trip and what you did.\n    *   Perhaps another paragraph about other thrilling activities available in the area.\n    This will make your essay much clearer and easier to follow.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "We did some recomended activities such as Long Kang, Zipline across the emeral lake and much more. It was so thrilling that I want to go try it again"\n    Problem: The transition between listing activities and saying "It was so thrilling" is a bit abrupt.\n    How to fix it: Connect these ideas more smoothly. For instance, "We enjoyed several recommended activities, such as Long Kang and ziplining across the emerald lake, among others. These experiences were so thrilling that I am eager to try them again!"	You used some everyday vocabulary to describe Kanchanaburi. You also tried to use some more complex sentence structures and relative clauses, which shows you are trying to expand your language skills. However, there were many mistakes in spelling, grammar (like verb forms, subject-verb agreement, and sentence structure), and punctuation. These errors were very noticeable and often made it difficult to understand your intended meaning.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "faublous," "wondeful," "famouse," "aroud," "waterfal," "onece," "truely," "recomended," "emeral," "induded," "rememered," "althought"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled or used incorrectly. They should be "fabulous," "wonderful," "famous," "around," "waterfall," "once," "truly," "recommended," "emerald," "included," "remembered."\n    How to fix it: Proofread your writing very carefully for spelling errors. Use a dictionary or a spell checker to help you learn the correct spellings.\n\n*   Grammar - Verb Forms and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "Kanchanaburi, is a town with wonderful nature. I'm going **tell** you why it is." and "Kanchanaburi, which is a town from Thailand that I **endure** the most." and "It **have** many levels of the waterfal." and "I've **went** there so many times That I can't **remembered**." and "You should **went** there"\n    Problem: You often miss "to" after "going" ("going *to tell*"). "Endure" is the wrong word; perhaps you mean "love" or "enjoy." "It have" should be "It *has*." "I've went" should be "I've *been*," and "can't remembered" should be "can't *remember*." "Should went" should be "should *go*." These errors make your sentences grammatically incorrect and hard to understand.\n    How to fix it: Pay close attention to verb forms and subject-verb agreement. Practice using auxiliary verbs correctly (e.g., "is," "has," "have"). Learn common irregular past participles (e.g., "gone," "been"). When using modal verbs like "should," always follow them with the base form of the verb.\n\n*   Punctuation and Capitalization:\n    Example: "Kanchanaburi, is a town with wonderful nature." and "Why? Because I've went there..." and "Now let's moved on to the most important part. Kanchanaburi, which is a town from Thailand that I endure the most."\n    Problem: The comma after "Kanchanaburi" in the first sentence is unnecessary. The question "Why?" is out of place. There are many sentences that run together without proper punctuation. Capitalization for "Thailand" is sometimes missing.\n    How to fix it: Use commas and periods to clearly separate your ideas and sentences. Only use question marks for direct questions. Make sure you capitalize proper nouns consistently.	3.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
