ct verb forms.	Kao, you've made a good attempt at addressing two of the topics – clothes and studying – and shared your opinion, which is a great start! 👍 To improve this score, remember to include *all* parts of the question. For example, the prompt asked for "your own idea," and including this would demonstrate a more complete response. Also, try to provide more detailed reasons and examples for your points. If you say friends influence fashion more, you could explain *how* they do this, perhaps by mentioning social media trends or group activities. Many Thai learners sometimes focus on just stating their main point and might not add enough supporting details or an extra point when it's requested. Thinking about the "who, what, when, where, why, and how" for each point can help you develop your ideas. 💡	You've effectively communicated your main points, Kao, which is an important aspect of any essay! Your opinions are clear regarding who influences teenagers more in fashion and studying. To strengthen your writing and make it more appropriate for an academic essay, work on using more formal language. Phrases like "something like this" or "That make friends" are common in spoken English, but in an essay, we aim for a more formal style. For instance, instead of "That make friends," you could write "This suggests friends have a greater influence." Practising with more formal vocabulary and sentence structures will help your essays sound more sophisticated and convincing. Sometimes, Thai learners might use informal language due to comfort with it in daily conversations, but academic writing requires a different approach. ✍️	You've made a good effort to organise your essay by using paragraphs for different ideas and including an introduction and a conclusion. This provides a clear structure! To improve in this area, consider how you connect your sentences and paragraphs. You've used words like "Because" and "And," which are functional, but try to incorporate a wider range of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just "And," you could use "Furthermore," "Moreover," or "In addition." Additionally, try to expand your conclusion slightly to summarise your main arguments more thoroughly. A stronger conclusion helps to bring all your ideas together effectively. Many learners find it helpful to learn a list of linking words to ensure their writing flows more smoothly. 🔗	Kao, your writing is generally understandable, and you're using simple English sentences effectively, which is commendable! To make your language even more accurate, let's focus on a few key areas. For example, remember to use plural forms correctly (like "friends" instead of "friend" when referring to multiple people) and ensure your verbs agree with your subjects (e.g., "parents always *say*" instead of "parents always say like you need"). Also, pay close attention to spelling – words like "alot" should be "a lot," and "genaration" should be "generation." These small corrections will significantly improve the clarity and correctness of your writing. It's common for Thai learners to sometimes mix up plurals or verb tenses, as these grammar rules can differ from Thai. Regularly reviewing these grammar points and carefully proofreading your work will help you identify and correct these types of errors! 🎯	2.0	2.0	2.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
