nder if 1+2 is equal to 3 or not"<br><b>Correction:</b> When you might wonder if 1+2 equals 3 or not, you will be able to ask ChatGPT, Gemini, or other assistants.<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, it's common to use more direct or conversational language in many situations. However, in English academic writing, we usually try to keep a more formal or neutral tone to sound professional.	Your paragraph was well-organised and easy to follow. You used good linking words to introduce each of your reasons.<br><b>Problem:</b> While your linking words were good, you could vary them more to make the flow even smoother.<br><b>Correction:</b> Firstly, mobile phones can be quite useful for finding information. <em>Furthermore</em>, concentration can be augmented by mobile phones, as they have the ability to transform tasks from mind-numbing to breathtaking.<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, it's common to repeat linking phrases, but in English, using a variety of words like 'furthermore', 'moreover', or 'in addition' helps your writing sound more sophisticated and connected, making it flow better for the reader.	You used everyday vocabulary well, and your simple grammar was mostly correct. While there were some noticeable errors, the meaning of your writing was always clear.<br><b>Problem:</b> There were a few spelling mistakes. For example, "asistunts" and "alsolutely".<br><b>Correction:</b> You will be able to ask ChatGPT, Gemini, or other <em>assistants</em>. Mobile phones will <em>absolutely</em> be useful if they are allowed to use.<br><b>Explanation:</b> Thai spelling is quite phonetic (you spell words mostly as they sound), but English spelling can be less consistent. This often leads to common spelling errors for Thai learners, so it's a great area to focus on. Another example is "augmonted" which should be "augmented".	5.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
