iting at B1 often benefits from presenting ideas that feel more universally applicable or using observations as examples rather than direct personal experience as primary evidence.	You communicated your main ideas clearly, and the way you wrote was generally suitable for your audience.<br><b>Problem:</b> Sometimes, your language was a bit informal, like when you asked 'Why would I know about this?' This makes your writing less academic.<br><b>Correction:</b> Try to keep your tone more formal and direct. For instance, instead of 'Why would I know about this?', you could say, 'This is evident from observations,' or 'My own experience supports this.'<br><b>Explanation:</b> In formal writing, we usually avoid direct questions to the reader or very personal interjections. It helps to keep your writing objective and focused on the topic, which is common in English academic texts.	Your paragraph has a clear beginning, middle, and end, using basic linking words to connect ideas.<br><b>Problem:</b> You used simple linking words like 'Firstly' and 'The second reason,' but sometimes your sentences ran together without clear connections.<br><b>Correction:</b> To make your ideas flow more smoothly, try using a wider variety of linking words and making sure each sentence clearly leads to the next. For example, 'The secound reason, It's can make us didn't understand in the class' could be 'Secondly, mobile phones can prevent us from understanding the lesson because we are distracted.'<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, long sentences with many clauses are common. In English, it's often better to break up long ideas into more distinct sentences or use stronger connecting words to show how ideas relate to each other, improving coherence.	You used everyday words well, and your simple sentences were mostly correct, even though there were some errors.<br><b>Problem:</b> You made some errors with plural forms, like '2 week' instead of '2 weeks', and verb forms, such as 'my friend aren't learned shes playing game'.<br><b>Correction:</b> Remember to add '-s' to nouns when there is more than one, like 'weeks' for '2 week'. Also, pay attention to correct verb forms and subject-verb agreement. 'My friend wasn't learning; she was playing a game' would be correct.<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, we don't always change words for 'many' or specific tenses in the same way English does. In English, it's very important to add '-s' for plurals and to use the correct verb forms (like 'is' instead of 'are' for 'friend', and 'learning' instead of 'learned' for ongoing actions) to make your meaning clear.	4.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3649	29832	2026-02-17 13:22:35.531227+00	Term 2 Listening Final Exam	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Listening	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	19	17	Hi Nene! I am so happy to share your results for the Cambridge B1 Listening test. You achieved a score of 19 out of 25, which is a fantastic result and shows you have a solid B1 level. You should be very proud of your hard work! 🌟<br><br>You did an amazing job in Part 1, where you had to identify specific information from short recordings. You only missed one question there, which shows you are very good at hearing details like times and objects. Even more impressive was your perfect score in Part 4! 🎧 You clearly have a great talent for understanding long interviews and following the speakers' opinions and attitudes. This is a very advanced skill, Nene.<br><br>I noticed that Part 2 was a little bit more challenging for you this time. This section asks you to understand the general idea or the feelings of the speakers in short dialogues. Sometimes the speakers use different words than the ones on the page, which can be tricky. To improve here, I recommend listening to short English podcasts and trying to decide if the speakers are happy, worried, or in agreement. 💪<br><br>You also did well in the gap-fill section, though there were a couple of small slips. Keep practicing your spelling and listening for those specific numbers or dates. Overall, you are a very strong listener, and I am confident you will continue to improve. Keep up the wonderful effort! 📚<br><br>All the best, Ed	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
1118	33952	2025-08-27 15:37:06.350416+00	Free time Activities	They have enough activety for teen on free time in Thailand. Although most of teens are do homework and learn a lot in class That was by the fact, which can make some of teens feel stress and nervous with the work and that, why teen want to have a stly of activity for relact and rest on free time.<br><br><br>The first common activity but it popular for everyon is game, that people choose in first choic is played the favourite game. They made the a lot stlye that can make teen feel less stress from do a work. for example is the roblox, which have a lot of stlye that was the map call "Steal Brainrot" was to steal another brainrot to make a lot of money and that was a one of the most intriguing map on Roblox. or you can play the Application call "FC Mobile" that you can experiment be a manager of football, which you can buy a player to make our team be strength for battle with friend on online.<br><br><br>The finally of choic that you can choose is Badminton, which everyone can exercise with family and make meomery when you exhaust and you want encourage from your family or friend that is the one way that you can rest and relax with it. This only have 2 thing that you choose bring were badminton stick with the shoes that was the important to use during play it.<br><br><br>To summarize, which of 2 activity were Game and Badminton that you can relax, when you have stress for doing something. In the world have a lot of activity but you choose the activity that you like to do and the speacial one was to make a memory to do with another.	250	11.0	<b>📝 Dear Ken</b><br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Free time Activities'. You wrote 250 words and achieved B1+.<br>You've done a good job attempting to address the essay prompt and include two activities.	You successfully identified two free time activities (gaming and badminton) and provided some details about each. Your descriptions show you have ideas about these activities. To improve, make sure your reasons for choosing each activity are very clear and directly stated. For example, explain more explicitly *why* you chose "gaming" as a relaxing activity, and *why* badminton is specifically chosen by *you* for relaxation and memory-making. Think about adding more personal examples or clearer connections to your own experience.<br>👉 <b>To reach a 5/5:</b> Clearly and fully explain *why* you have chosen each activity, providing specific personal details or examples to support your choices and make them more engaging for the reader.<br><br>	You managed to communicate your ideas, but sometimes the essay felt a little informal or unclear, which made it harder for the reader to follow. Remember that an essay for FIRST (B2) should generally maintain a more formal tone than spoken Thai, and your explanations need to be very clear to keep the reader's attention.<br><br>	You followed the requested four-paragraph structure, which is a great start! You also used some linking words to connect your ideas. To make your essay even stronger, focus on ensuring your introduction has a very clear statement of your main point (a thesis statement). Also, make sure the first sentence of each body paragraph (your topic sentence) clearly tells the reader what that paragraph will be about and connects back to your main point.<br><br>	You showed good effort by trying to use different sentence structures, and you used some linking words effectively. However, the main challenges are with a few key grammar rules that often cause difficulty for Thai speakers.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "They have enough activety for teen on free time in Thailand."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "There are enough activities for teens in Thailand in their free time."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, we often use "there is/are" to introduce that something exists. Also, "activity" needs to be plural ("activities") when referring to many. Thai doesn't always mark plurals explicitly.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Although most of teens are do homework and learn a lot in class That was by the fact, which can make some of teens feel stress and nervous with the work and that, why teen want to have a stly of activity for relact and rest on free time."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Although most teens do a lot of homework and learn a lot in class, this can make some teens feel stressed and nervous, which is why they want to have a style of activity for relaxing and resting in their free time."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This sentence was quite long and hard to follow. English prefers shorter, clearer clauses than Thai. We also need to be careful with verb forms (e.g., "are doing" or "do" for general truth, "relaxing" not "relact"). "Teens" can be used without "of the" if it's general, or "most of the teens."<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "The first common activity but it popular for everyon is game, that people choose in first choic is played the favourite game."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "The first common activity, which is popular for everyone, is gaming, and it's often people's first choice to play their favourite games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> You are missing the "is" verb after "it popular" (it *is* popular). Also, using "which" can help connect ideas more smoothly, and "gaming" is a common noun for the activity itself. Thai sentence structure can sometimes omit "to be" verbs in ways English cannot.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "This only have 2 thing that you choose bring were badminton stick with the shoes that was the important to use during play it."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "For this, you only need to bring two things: a badminton racket and appropriate shoes, which are important to use when playing."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> You need "have" to agree with "This" (This *has* or *you* only need). Also, "things" should be plural. "Badminton stick" is usually called a "badminton racket". And using "which are important" clarifies the relationship. Thai does not have the same strict subject-verb agreement or pluralization rules as English.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. **Review Thai L1 Patterns:** Focus on plurals (-s), correct use of articles (a/an/the), and ensuring every sentence has a clear subject and verb. Look at the "Missing Plural Markers" feedback from the report.<br>2. **Sentence Structure:** Practice writing shorter, clearer sentences. Break down long sentences into two or three simpler ones to avoid run-on sentences and ensure your meaning is always clear.<br>3. **Word Forms & Spelling:** Pay close attention to word forms (e.g., activity vs. activety, stressed vs. stress, relaxing vs. relact) and check your spelling carefully.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
