ls, and with more practice, your confidence with longer recordings will definitely grow. I am excited to see your progress in our next lessons. Keep practicing and keep smiling! ✨	\N	\N	A2	\N	\N	\N	A2	2026
1954	29769	2025-09-19 08:37:17.607328+00	Term 1 Email	From : Posh<br><br><br>Subject: Science festival<br><br><br>Hello corey<br><br><br>I've heard that you get our tickets, thanks you for that i didn't have time to pick it. I think we could meet at the front entrance is the best for me since my mom said she will drive me to science festival and my house is far from the science festival. I think i want to build a robot more than watch the flim so i think we should try build a robot and i have knowledge about build a robot too. I already got a pocket money but we should get more because we might don't get enough money and i think we should bring food snack and drink too.<br><br><br>bye,<br><br><br>Posh	113	13.0	Your email to Corey is clear and easy to understand, which is a great starting point. To make your emails even better, focusing on how you arrange your ideas within paragraphs will help your messages become even more polished and easy for your readers to follow. You have a solid grasp of writing informal emails, and with a bit of practice, your communication will be even more effective.	You did a really good job of covering most of the points Corey mentioned in his email. You were clear about wanting to build a robot and why, and you remembered to suggest bringing snacks and drinks. That shows you are paying close attention to the details of the task. 🎉 Remember that the task asked for one *additional item* to suggest bringing, separate from the pocket money discussion, and a reason for it. For example, you could have said, "I think we should bring some snacks, like fruit or biscuits, because we will be hungry after all the activities." This would clearly meet that specific requirement. Keep an eye out for those smaller details in the instructions next time.	Your email definitely got the message across to Corey, and you used a friendly tone that is perfect for writing to a friend. You included a "Hello Corey" and a "bye, Posh," which is a great start for an email. 😃 To make your emails even more engaging, try to use different sentences to introduce new ideas or questions. For instance, you could have a short paragraph about the tickets, another about meeting arrangements, and then one about the about activities. This helps guide the reader through your thoughts more smoothly. Think about how a reader might feel when they see a wall of text.	Your email is connected and your ideas follow a logical path, making it easy to understand what you want to communicate to Corey. You connect your thoughts with simple linking words, which is helpful. 👍 However, to make your email even more organised, think about using paragraph breaks to separate your main points. For example, dedicate one paragraph to discussing the tickets and meeting place, another to talking about the robot activity, and a third to mentioning food and pocket money. This makes your email much more organised and pleasant to read. Currently, your email is one long paragraph. Breaking it up into smaller paragraphs would make it easier for the reader to digest each piece of information. Thai learners sometimes write very directly, and they might not always think about how paragraphing helps the reader separate different ideas, but it is an important part of good email etiquette in English.	You used a good range of everyday words that are appropriate for talking to a friend about a science festival. Your sentences are generally clear, and I can understand what you mean despite some small errors. Your vocabulary is suitable for this type of informal email. ✨ Here are three key areas to focus on for improvement:<br>1. **Subject-Verb Agreement and Tense:** You wrote "you get our tickets," but it should be "you *got* our tickets" (past tense) or "you *have got* our tickets" (present perfect). A quick check of your verb tenses will help ensure they match the time of the action.<br>2. **Pronoun Usage:** You wrote "I didn't have time to pick *it*," but when referring to multiple tickets, it should be "I didn't have time to pick *them* up." Remember that "tickets" is a plural noun, so you need a plural pronoun.<br>3. **Article Usage:** You wrote "the front entrance *is the best* for me," which could be improved to "the front entrance *is best* for me" or "the front entrance *is the best option* for me." Sometimes a definite article "the" is not needed, or adding a specific noun like "option" can clarify the meaning. Many Thai learners sometimes use "the" more often than needed, or they might omit it when it is required, so a focus on article usage would be beneficial.<br>Practising identifying subjects and their corresponding verbs, and paying close attention to whether nouns are singular or plural, will really help you.	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
