e on what "etc" means or give an example of another cost, like vet visits or toys. Your article was quite short, and making sure you write around 140-190 words will give you the space to explore your ideas more fully, making sure your reader is completely informed. 🧐 Sometimes, we might rush to get our ideas down, but taking a moment to think about how you can explain each point a little further will make a big difference.	You've captured a really heartfelt and personal tone in your article, which makes it engaging for the reader. ❤️ The way you express the strong bond people have with their pets and the sadness of loss shows a deep understanding. You used some effective phrasing to convey your feelings, which is a great skill for an article. To make your article even more effective, consider the broader audience of a school magazine and think about how you can consistently hold their attention from start to finish. For example, you could start with a captivating question or a strong opening statement to immediately draw the reader in. While your ideas are clear, expanding on them will give your thoughtful points more room to resonate with your audience.	Your article has a clear beginning and you introduce your main points, which is a good start. You've used some helpful linking words like "On the other hand" and "Nonetheless" to show the different sides of your argument, which helps the reader follow your thoughts. 😊 To make your writing even more organised and flow better, try to use a wider range of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just "On the other hand," you could use "Conversely" or "In contrast." Also, ensure that each new idea starts a new sentence, and perhaps even a new paragraph if it's a significant shift in topic. Thai learners sometimes write in a way that feels quite direct, and adding more explicit connections between sentences can help create a smoother reading experience for a wider audience.	You've used some good everyday vocabulary in your article, and words like "awful," "prepared," and "recover" show that you're building a solid vocabulary. That's fantastic. 👍 However, there are a few areas where your grammar could use a little more attention. For example, you wrote "That's mean" instead of "That means," and "it's food" and "it's lost" where "its" (without the apostrophe) would be correct for possession. Also, "you'll probably needed" should be "you'll probably need." These small errors can sometimes make it a little harder for the reader to understand your message clearly. My advice would be to carefully re-read your work specifically looking for these common errors, especially apostrophes and making sure your verbs agree with your subjects. Many Thai learners find apostrophes for possession and contractions a bit tricky, so focusing on this area will really help your writing shine. ✨	2.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
