ader feel part of the adventure. You even included a fun ending with all the candy! To reach a score of 5, consider making sure every part of your story links directly to the main incredible event. For example, the part about the "candy class" was a little bit separate from the teddy bear event. Perhaps you could have linked it by saying something like, "And it turned out the teddy bear was part of a special school event, where we could use the candy we collected to 'pay' for snacks in a fun new class." This way, all your ideas work together to build one amazing event for the reader.	You certainly held my attention with your exciting story, Mark, and you communicated your straightforward ideas well. It was easy to understand the main events you described. To make your writing even stronger and hold the reader's attention throughout, try to make sure each event flows naturally into the next without any sudden shifts. For instance, after discovering the teddy bear and the candy, you could have continued with how you felt about *that specific event* before introducing the "candy class." This helps the reader follow the incredible situation you initially set up, making your narrative feel more complete and polished.	Your story has a clear beginning, middle, and end, which is a good foundation for any narrative. You used some basic linking words to connect your ideas, such as "and" and "but." To help your story feel even more organised and flow beautifully, think about how you can group related sentences into paragraphs. For example, the description of finding the teddy bear and its initial movements could be one paragraph, your reactions and the candy dropping could be another, and then the part about the class and going home could be a third. This helps your reader follow the sequence of events easily and makes your writing very clear. Using a variety of linking words like "then," "suddenly," or "after that" could also help make your story feel more connected and less like a list of events.	You’ve used a good amount of everyday vocabulary to tell your story, Mark, which is fantastic for describing your exciting experience. Words like "giant teddy bear costume," "exciting," and "scary" really help paint a picture for the reader. While I could understand your meaning quite well, there were a few places where your grammar could be a little more precise, especially with verb tenses and sentence structure. For example, you wrote "it's starting to moves towards us" (which should be "it started to move towards us") and "the guy whose inside the costume" (which would be clearer as "the guy who was inside the costume"). Also, the sentence "and also theres a class where if you bring candy you can pay candy too. to allow yourself to eat candy in class" is a little tricky to understand. You could simplify this for clarity, perhaps by saying, "It turned out that there was a special class where we could use the candy we collected to enjoy snacks during the lesson." Focusing on correct verb forms and making sure each sentence has a clear subject and verb will help your writing become even more accurate and easier to read. Sometimes, in Thai, sentences can be less rigid about specifying subjects and tenses, but in English, these details are very important for clarity when telling a story about past events.	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
