ake your article more engaging for young readers around the world.	You've done a good job of communicating straightforward ideas about your festival. Your description of the activities is clear and easy to understand. To improve this area, think about how you can structure your article more like a typical article you might read online. This means having a clear opening sentence to introduce Songkran, and maybe a concluding sentence that summarises why it's important to you. Using paragraphs for different ideas, like one for the temple visit and another for the water play, would also help keep your readers interested and make your article even more polished. Remember, you're writing for an international audience, so imagine someone who has never heard of Songkran before. 🤩	You've used some helpful linking words like "At first," "secondly," and "after that," which really help your readers follow the order of events during Songkran. That's a smart way to connect your ideas. To reach a higher score here, focus on using paragraph breaks. Each time you introduce a new main idea, like a different activity, try starting a new paragraph. For example, your description of going to your grandfather's house and praying could be one paragraph, and then the party and water fight could be another. This makes your article much easier to read and helps your ideas stand out. Think of paragraphs as mini-sections that organise your thoughts. ✍️	You've used some good everyday words to talk about your festival, such as "festival," "temple," and "party." This helps your readers understand what you're describing. However, there are a few areas where we can tidy up your grammar to make your sentences super clear. For example, when you wrote "A festival my family was celebrate," it should be "A festival my family *celebrates*." Also, "they was going" should be "they *went*" (or "they *go*" if it's a regular event). And "in a mid night" should be "at midnight." Lastly, "played by shooting another people in the city by a water" would be clearer as "play by shooting water at other people in the city." Thai learners sometimes make these kinds of tense mistakes because the concept of specific verb tenses in English doesn't always have a direct equivalent in Thai. Focusing on using the correct past or present tense, depending on whether you're talking about a specific past event or a regular celebration, will make a big difference to your clarity. Keep practising these verb forms, and your English will become even more precise. 💪	2.0	3.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2	\N	\N	\N	A2	2026
