rove:<br>1. <b>Pacing!</b> ⏱️ Slow down the transformation scene. Describe how it felt to change.<br>2. <b>Clarity!</b> 👓 'Bore a resemblance' is a great phrase, but make sure it fits the action.	You used amazing linking words like 'Subsequently' and 'Ultimately'! 👏<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Sentence Variety!</b> 📝 Try to mix long and short sentences. Too many short ones can sound choppy.<br>2. <b>Repetition!</b> 🔄 You repeated 'Unknowingly he had created a disaster'. Remove one!	You used advanced vocabulary like 'uncontrollably' and 'furnished'. Great job! 🌟<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br>1. <b>Verbs!</b> 🛠️ 'He in fact' -> 'He *was* in fact'.<br>2. <b>Nouns vs Verbs!</b> 🔄 'His live' -> 'His *life*'.<br>3. <b>Word Forms!</b> 📝 'Required you to touch' is good!<br>4. <b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ 'Does instant babies' -> 'creates instant babies'.<br>5. <b>Spelling!</b> ✅ Your spelling is generally very good!	5.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
1884	35309	2025-09-16 21:28:02.186171+00	Term 1 Email	Hi<br><br><br>Great news! I'm so excited, this Saturday is going to be a blast<br><br><br>About that, both of my parents are absent for this whole month they're doing work in a difference City, so it would be so nice for your cousin to come and pick me up.<br><br><br>Both of that activities sound great but I'm more excited about buildings the robots, It's once in a blue moon chance to do so since they're quite expensive and I've watch alot of flims about the discovery of dinosaurs.<br><br><br>I won't forget to bring some money! I suggest we should bring some polaroid camera it would be nice to take some picture with nostalgic effects.<br><br><br>Bye,<br><br><br>Atom	109	17.0	Hello Atom! 👋 It's wonderful to see your enthusiasm and clear communication in this email. You did a fantastic job of including all the information Corey needed and your message came across very clearly. Keep up the great work in expressing your ideas thoughtfully.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan:**<br>*   ☑️ Check your past tense verbs: Always remember to add '-ed' to regular verbs when you're talking about something that happened in the past. For example, "I watched" or "we played."<br>*   ☑️ Use 'a lot' as two words: Make sure to separate 'a' and 'lot' when you write it. It's always 'a lot' not 'alot'.<br>*   ☑️ Pay attention to plurals: When you're talking about more than one of something, like "films" or "pictures," make sure to add an 's' to the end of the word.	Atom, you've really nailed the content of this email. You remembered to include every single detail Corey asked for, from saying how excited you are about the tickets to suggesting a polaroid camera for photos, which is a lovely touch 📸. It's clear you understood the task completely, and Corey will be fully informed and ready for the science festival. Well done.	Your email is perfectly set up, Atom. You started with a friendly "Hi" and ended with a clear "Bye," just as you would with a friend. Using separate paragraphs for different ideas made your message very easy to read and understand, which is exactly what we want to see in an email to a friend. You did a great job of holding the reader's attention throughout! 👍	Your email is well-organised, Atom, and your ideas flow quite logically, making it easy to follow your thoughts. You used phrases like "About that" and "Both of that activities sound great but" to help guide the reader from one point to the next. To make your writing even smoother, try to think of a few more ways to connect your sentences and paragraphs. For instance, you could use words like "Also," "However," or "Therefore" to create a stronger link between your ideas. Thai learners sometimes focus a lot on getting the main points across, and can benefit from practicing how to make those points connect even more gracefully. 🔗	You've used a good range of everyday words in your email, Atom, and it's great to see you trying out phrases like "going to be a blast" and "once in a blue moon," which really add character to your writing 🎉. While your message is certainly understandable, there were a few places where we could make the grammar even more precise. For example, remember that when you're talking about two things, we say "both of *those* activities" instead of "both of *that* activities." Also, when you talk about past actions, the verb usually needs an "-ed" ending, so "I've *watched* a lot of films" would be correct instead of "I've watch." And when you mention a quantity, like "a lot of films" or "some pictures," make sure the noun is plural if there's more than one. Focusing on these small adjustments will help your writing become even clearer and more accurate. Thai learners sometimes translate directly from their first language, which can lead to these sorts of small but important differences in English grammar.	5.0	5.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
