d bad points, or advantages and disadvantages.<br>   ✅ When you're writing, pay close attention to your spelling. If you're unsure how a word is spelled, it's a good idea to quickly look it up.<br>   ✅ Read your sentences slowly after you've written them to check if the verbs match the subjects (for example, "they want" instead of "they wants").	Mew, you've done a good job of discussing some of the positive aspects of having pets, like how they can protect your home and offer companionship. You've also given some helpful advice on how to care for them, which shows you've thought about the responsibilities involved. 🐾 To improve this area, remember that the task asked for both the advantages and disadvantages of owning pets. Next time, try to spend a little time thinking about the challenges or less positive aspects of pet ownership too. For example, you could mention the cost of food and vet visits, or the time commitment required. Including a balanced perspective will make your article more complete and informative for your readers.	You've made a good effort to write an article, Mew, by starting with a question and organising your thoughts into paragraphs. It's good that you tried to engage the reader from the start. ✍️ To make your articles even more effective at holding the reader's attention, consider using a wider range of sentence structures. For instance, instead of many short sentences, you could combine some ideas using linking words like "although" or "however" to create more varied and interesting sentences. Also, ensure that all parts of the article directly address the main question, so your reader is consistently informed about your opinion on both the good and bad aspects of pet ownership.	Your article has a clear beginning, middle, and end, and your ideas are generally grouped into paragraphs, which makes it easier to read. You've used some basic linking words to connect your sentences, like "However" and "First." 🔗 To strengthen your organisation, think about how each paragraph flows into the next. For example, when you move from discussing the benefits of pets to explaining how to care for them, you could use a clearer transition phrase like "Beyond the joys they bring, it's important to remember that caring for pets comes with responsibilities." This helps your reader follow your ideas smoothly. Many Thai learners sometimes make these transitions too quickly, but taking a moment to link your thoughts carefully will greatly assist your readers.	You've used some basic vocabulary related to pets that was appropriate, like "family," "house," and "play." This is a good starting point for discussing the topic. However, there were quite a few errors in your spelling and grammar that sometimes made it difficult to fully understand your meaning. Let's look at three important areas for improvement:<br>1.  **Word Form and Choice:** You wrote phrases like "It really normally now" and "they decent have anything to you." Instead, you should aim for "It's quite normal now" and "they don't demand anything from you." Choosing the correct form of words (e.g., adverb vs. adjective) and the right prepositions makes a big difference. 📖<br>2.  **Subject-Verb Agreement and Verb Forms:** You wrote "Many animals wants to stay..." but because "animals" is plural, the verb should be "want." So, it should be "Many animals want to stay..." Also, "Are you feel lonely?" should be "Are you feeling lonely?" or "Do you feel lonely?". Paying close attention to whether your subject is singular or plural and using the correct verb tense will help you choose the right verb form.<br>3.  **Spelling:** Words like "happyness" should be "happiness," "rutine" should be "routine," "enyoyng" should be "enjoying," and "defense" should be "defend." A simple trick is to always use a dictionary or an online spell-check tool when you are unsure of how to spell a word. Keep a list of words you often misspell and review them regularly. 📚<br><br>Many Thai learners find these areas challenging because English spelling is not always phonetic, and grammatical rules like subject-verb agreement and precise word choice are very different from Thai. Keep practising, and these improvements will make a big difference to your writing! 💪	3.0	3.0	3.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
