h him, which meant you didn't quite meet that specific part of the task. Remember, it's really important to read every instruction carefully to make sure you include all the necessary information and opinions. A good way to check this is to highlight each point in the question and tick it off as you write about it.	You managed to communicate your ideas to Corey, which is the main goal of any message. However, the way your email is presented makes it a little challenging to read. Emails, even informal ones to friends, usually start with a friendly greeting like "Hi Corey" and end with a clear sign-off such as "See you on Saturday" or "Best wishes." Also, breaking your email into paragraphs helps to separate different ideas, making it much easier for the reader to follow along. Imagine you're talking to your friend and pausing after each topic – those pauses are like your paragraph breaks. 📧 Thai learners sometimes focus so much on getting the words right that they forget about the overall structure of the message. Taking a moment to think about how your message looks and flows will make a big difference.	Your email has a logical flow, and your ideas are connected, which helps Corey understand your message. For instance, you move from expressing excitement to discussing travel plans, then activities, and finally what to bring. This shows good sequencing. 😊 To make your organisation even stronger, try using a wider variety of linking words to smoothly connect your sentences and ideas. For example, instead of just starting a new sentence, you could use phrases like "Regarding the activities," or "As for what to bring." Also, as mentioned earlier, using paragraph breaks for each new topic would significantly improve the clarity and readability of your email, guiding the reader through your thoughts more easily.	You used everyday words that are appropriate for talking to a friend about a festival, such as "science festival," "pick me up," and "snacks and water." This is a solid foundation. You also showed good control over simple sentence structures. However, there were some small language points that could be refined to make your writing more precise and natural. For example, you wrote "sending me email corey" when it should be "sending me *an* email, Corey." Also, "I'd enjoy to watch movie" could be improved to "I'd enjoy *watching a movie*." There was also a slightly informal phrase "if we wanna build a robot" and a question that was a little unclear "or we are too lazy?". Paying attention to these smaller grammatical details, such as using articles ("a," "an," "the") correctly and choosing the right verb forms (like using a gerund after "enjoy"), will really help your English sound more polished. Many Thai learners sometimes miss these smaller grammatical elements, so don't worry, it's a common area for improvement. Keep practising, and you'll see a great improvement. 👍	3.0	2.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
