o build a robot, and thoughtfully suggested bringing notes and a pen with a clear justification. You left the reader fully informed on all points, demonstrating a strong ability to follow instructions and incorporate details effectively. This comprehensive response is exactly what is needed for a top score in Content.	You've shown a solid understanding of how to write an informal email, Yayar. Your email began with a polite greeting ("Hello") and concluded with a friendly closing ("Bye, Yayar"), and you used paragraphs to separate your different ideas, which makes your email easy to navigate. This generally appropriate use of email conventions helps to hold the reader's attention and communicate your straightforward ideas effectively. To strengthen this area, consider focusing on making every sentence flow as smoothly as possible. While your ideas are clear, some minor grammatical inaccuracies occasionally create slightly awkward phrasing, which could be polished further to make your communication even more natural.	Your email is well-structured and easy to follow, Yayar, demonstrating a good sense of organisation! You clearly grouped related ideas into separate paragraphs, which is an effective way to present information in an email. You also used some basic linking words, such as "Because" and "But," to connect your thoughts. This logical progression of ideas helps the reader move through your message without difficulty. To make your organisation even better, try to ensure that every linking word perfectly fits the context and doesn't create a run-on sentence. Sometimes, combining too many ideas in one long sentence can make it a little harder for the reader to keep up, so breaking those down can be helpful.	Yayar, you've used a good range of everyday words that are appropriate for the topic of your email, which is commendable. However, there are some areas where more control over simple grammatical forms would enhance your clarity. For example, when you say "I will enjoy build a robot," it should be "I will enjoy **building** a robot" because "enjoy" is often followed by the -ing form of a verb. Similarly, "I rather to took the rough path" should be "I **would** rather **take** the rough path." Also, a word like "definitely" (you wrote "definately") needs careful spelling. Many Thai learners find prepositions and verb forms challenging, but focusing on these details, perhaps by keeping a grammar notebook, will make your English much more accurate and fluent.	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
