he reader understand exactly what you mean.	Your email has a friendly start with 'Hi' and a polite closing, 'See you at the science festival,' which is just right for writing to a friend. 😊 This helps the reader feel connected to your message. To make your email even easier to read and more engaging, try to break your ideas into separate paragraphs. This helps to guide the reader through your thoughts more smoothly. Think of each new idea as needing its own paragraph to make it stand out.	Your email has a clear order, moving from one point to the next, which makes it generally understandable. This shows you are thinking about how to present your ideas. However, some of your sentences are quite long and combine many different ideas without enough punctuation, making them a little tricky to follow. For example, when you listed all the things to bring, it became one very long sentence. Try to use full stops more often to break up these longer sentences into smaller, easier-to-read parts. This will help your ideas flow more clearly and smoothly for Corey. Thai learners sometimes connect many ideas with 'and' or 'so,' but in English, we often use shorter sentences for better clarity.	You use many everyday words correctly, and your message is understandable, which is important. For example, 'I have to feed my dog' is very clear. However, there are a few places where the grammar and sentence structure could be stronger. The biggest thing to work on is breaking up those very long sentences. When you write something like, 'We should bring a map of science festival so we will not get lost or maybe we should bring a medicine for emergency, a bag for pack stuff home, a water for sometimes it don't have water to drink or buy, and some snack to eat while we have a break,' it's very hard for the reader to keep track of all the different ideas. Try to use full stops to separate these ideas into shorter sentences. Also, little corrections like saying 'that's great' instead of 'that 