oon," followed by your name. Even when you're writing quickly, including both a start and an end makes your email feel complete and polite. Think of it like saying hello and goodbye when you meet someone.	You began your email in a way that makes sense, and your first two ideas are connected. When writing emails, it's helpful to group related sentences into short paragraphs. This makes your message easier for the reader to follow. For example, if you're talking about plans, put all those sentences together. If you're discussing activities, put those in another group. Using linking words like "also," "because," or "however" can help your ideas flow smoothly from one sentence to the next. Thai learners sometimes write very directly, but English often benefits from these connecting words to show how ideas relate to each other.	You used simple, everyday words that Corey can easily understand, like "sounds great" and "closer." That's a solid foundation! However, to score higher in language, try to include a wider variety of words and sentence structures. For instance, instead of just saying "it's closer and easier," you could say "it's more convenient for us to meet at the entrance" to show more vocabulary. Also, pay close attention to making sure your sentences are grammatically complete and correct. For example, you wrote, "If it's better for you two to meet me," but the sentence seems to trail off. You could add something like, "let me know," or "I'm flexible." Practising different ways to phrase your thoughts will help your English become more flexible and accurate.	1.0	2.0	1.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A1+	\N	\N	\N	A1+	2026
