se plot of being stuck in a movie and fighting for survival was very engaging.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Details!</b> 👁️ You mentioned 'some source'. What kind of source? Be specific! Was it wood? Steel?<br>2. <b>Pacing!</b> ⏱️ The action in the house with the murderer could be slowed down a little bit to build more suspense before the final confrontation.	Your narrative was very easy to follow, and the action sequences were clear and engaging. 🏃‍♂️<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Emotional Impact!</b> 😢 You said Sam 'knew he will be killed'. Describe his fear and panic in that moment.<br>2. <b>Dialogue!</b> 🗣️ Sam called the police. What did he say? What did the police say back? Adding dialogue makes the scene more realistic.	You used good linking words like 'Initially', 'Subsequently', and 'Finally' to create a clear sequence of events. 🚧<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Variety!</b> 🔗 Try using a wider range of cohesive devices to connect sentences and paragraphs. Words like 'Despite this' or 'As a consequence' can add variety.<br>2. <b>Sentence Structure!</b> ✍️ While clear, vary your sentence beginnings to keep the reader engaged.	You have a very active writing style that suits an action story! 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections to help you:<br>1. <b>Irregular Verbs!</b> 📝 'Shooted' -> '<b>Shot</b>' (past tense).<br>2. <b>Word Choice!</b> 📖 'Murder' (crime) -> '<b>Murderer</b>' (person). 'Normal live' -> 'Normal <b>life</b>'.<br>3. <b>Tense!</b> 🔙 'He will be killed' -> 'He <b>would</b> be killed'. 'Sam needed to found' -> 'Sam needed to <b>find</b>'.<br>4. <b>Articles!</b> 🐜 'Bought some source' -> 'Bought some <b>resources</b>'.<br>5. <b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ 'He didn't look carefully when he came up' -> 'He wasn't looking carefully as he approached'.	5.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cFmq4K2oHYkE4yIkPRNskT3opJFRSJ--?usp=sharing	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
