2013	34927	2025-09-21 17:02:49.016037+00	Term 1 Writing Task 2	There was a festival a few months ago and that festival is called "Song Kran". I celebrated Song Kran with my family. Every year we celebrate Song Kran in different countries every year. for example last Song Kran I went to china to celebrate. My family stayed there for about 6 days 5 nights. My favorite part was when I got to go shopping for my favorite blind boxes, my favorite blind boxes are mostly Sanrio because some of the figures are so cute and I think the color pallet of each figures are cute. I mostly like pastel colors. Oh and my second favorite place was on the highest moutain in China, I think. I don't remember its name because I went to 2 moutains. The first is "Ta gu ping suan" and "da lo nee" so I'm not sure wich one is it. The reason that it's my second favorite place is that up there it has lots of snow and it is very cold up there. Not like in Thailand, Thailand is always hot.<br><br>	177	12.0	Hello Eri, you've done a wonderful job telling us about the "Song Kran" festival and sharing some lovely personal experiences. Your writing is clear, and it's easy to understand what you enjoy about your family's celebrations. Keep up the good work in expressing your thoughts so clearly.<br><br>To make your articles even more captivating, let's focus on really describing the festival itself and how your experiences connect to it. This will help your readers fully understand the unique traditions you're sharing with them.<br><br>You have a friendly and engaging way of writing, which is a real strength. With a little more focus on explaining the festival's details and ensuring your sentences flow smoothly, your writing will truly bloom.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement**<br>*   ✅ When describing a festival, remember to explain *when* it happens more precisely and detail at least *two specific activities* that are part of the celebration.<br>*   ✅ Practice connecting your ideas and sentences more smoothly by using a variety of linking words and phrases.<br>*   ✅ Pay close attention to grammar, especially verb tenses and making sure your nouns are singular or plural as needed, to make your writing even more accurate.	Eri, you've made a great start by clearly telling us about "Song Kran" and how your family celebrates it. It's lovely that you shared your personal enjoyment of shopping for blind boxes. To make your article even more informative and engaging for an international audience, try to include more specific details about the festival itself. For example, you could mention a particular date or time of year when Song Kran is celebrated, and describe at least two or three traditional activities that happen during it, like splashing water or visiting temples. You mentioned celebrating in different countries, which is interesting, but remember to bring it back to Song Kran and describe what *happens* during the festival itself. This helps your readers picture the celebration clearly and learn more about your culture.	You have a friendly and accessible writing style, Eri, which is perfect for an article aimed at young people. Your ability to communicate straightforward ideas is clear. To make your article even more effective, think about how you can explicitly connect your personal experiences to the Song Kran festival. For instance, when you talk about shopping or visiting mountains, you could explain how these activities fit into *your family's unique way of celebrating Song Kran*, or if they were separate experiences during a trip. Making these connections clearer will help hold your reader's attention and ensure they fully understand your message about the festival. Consider explaining any cultural references or traditions to an international audience to make it even more accessible.	Your article has a basic structure, Eri, and your ideas are generally connected. You use linking words like "and," "for example," and "because," which is good. To help your readers follow your thoughts even more easily, try to organise your ideas into clearer paragraphs. For example, you could have one paragraph introducing Song Kran and its general timing, another detailing specific activities, and then a separate one for your personal favourite parts. Using more varied linking phrases at the beginning of sentences or paragraphs (such as "In addition," "Furthermore," or "Despite this") can create a smoother flow between your ideas. Sometimes, in Thai, it's common to connect many ideas with "and," but in English, varying your linking words can make your writing more sophisticated and easier to read.	Eri, you use everyday words very well, which helps your article be clear and easy to understand. Words like "festival," "family," "shopping," and "cute" are used appropriately. You also show good control over simple sentence structures. To make your language even more precise, let's focus on a few areas. Firstly, be careful with verb tenses; for general facts about Song Kran, "is" would be more appropriate than "was." For example, "Song Kran is called..." rather than "Song Kran was called...". Secondly, pay attention to singular and plural forms; you used "moutain" when you meant "mountains." Lastly, "color pallet" should be "colour palette," and "wich" should be "which." These small adjustments will make your writing even more accurate and impressive. Remember, even native English speakers make small mistakes, so keep practicing, and your accuracy will naturally improve.	3.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
