s is a special way we honour those who came before us.' Adding these little explanations can make your article even more informative and interesting for someone who might not know about your culture.	You've successfully communicated straightforward ideas about Chinese New Year, which is a great achievement. However, to really capture and keep your reader's attention in an article, it's helpful to have a clear beginning and end. Think of an article as having a friendly chat with your reader. You start by saying hello and introducing your topic, you talk about the main points, and then you offer a nice closing thought. Your current ending, 'And that all!', is a little bit sudden. Next time, try to wrap up your article with a sentence that leaves the reader with a good feeling or a final reflection, like 'I really enjoy Chinese New Year because it brings us all closer together' or 'It's a wonderful festival that I look forward to every year.' This will make your article much more satisfying to read. 🌟	Your article has a basic structure, which is good, especially how you've organised the events over the 'first day,' 'second day,' and 'last day.' This helps the reader follow the timeline of the festival. To make your writing even clearer and more organised, consider using more varied linking words and phrases. Instead of just 'first day,' 'second day,' 'last day,' you could try phrases like 'initially,' 'afterward,' 'finally,' or 'to conclude.' Also, think about starting a new paragraph for each main idea. For instance, you could have one paragraph introducing the festival and why it's important to your family, another describing the activities of the first two days, and a third for the final day's celebration and your personal enjoyment. This makes your writing flow better and is easier for the reader to digest. 📝	You've used everyday vocabulary effectively to describe your festival, and your meaning is generally clear, which is fantastic. To make your language even more precise and accurate, let's focus on a few grammar points. For example, when you talk about your 'family,' which is a singular group, the verb that follows should also be singular, like 'My family *likes* to celebrate' instead of 'My family like.' Also, pay close attention to plurals; for instance, 'every years' should be 'every year,' 'ancestor' should be 'ancestors,' and 'resterant' should be 'restaurant.' Another area to watch is making sure you use the correct form of nouns, such as 'Chinese New Year' and 'Chinese restaurant.' These small changes will really help your sentences sound natural and correct to an English speaker. Sometimes in Thai, you might not change the form of a word for plurals or subject-verb agreement in the same way, so it's a common point to practise in English. Keep an eye out for these little details, and your English will grow stronger. 💪	4.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
