er than a stage you already knew about. Try to connect those ideas more directly for your reader. Also, when you mentioned "almost risk my life," it might be a bit strong for the context of a school show. Perhaps you could say "I felt incredibly nervous" or "it felt like a huge challenge" instead, which would make the story even more believable. Keep working on making every detail count and linking each part of your narrative clearly.	Your story has a good flow, and you certainly captured the reader's attention with your excitement about the performance. You showed a good understanding of how to tell a story with a clear beginning, middle, and end. To get a higher score, think about how your emotional language helps the reader understand the situation. For instance, instead of saying "I'm really panic," you could say "I felt a real wave of panic" or "My heart was pounding with nerves" to describe your feelings more precisely. This helps your reader connect with your experience without being confused by how strong the feeling is. Keep practising how to describe emotions so your readers can truly feel what your characters are going through.	Your story is generally well-organised, and the events follow a clear timeline from arriving at school to the end of the show. You used basic linking words to move the story forward, which is a good foundation. To make your story even better, try to use a wider range of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just moving from one sentence to the next, you could use phrases like "In the blink of an eye," or "Meanwhile," to show how events are connected or happening at the same time. Also, breaking your text into short paragraphs when you introduce a new idea or a change in setting, like going from seeing the stage to preparing for the show, can make your story even easier for the reader to follow. This will give your writing a more sophisticated feel.	You use everyday vocabulary well, and your simple sentences are general