king sure your reader was fully informed about everything that happened. Well done. 🎉	You’ve done a good job of holding the reader's attention with your engaging story, Yayar. The way you showed the class's excitement and determination really makes the reader want to know what happens next. To make your stories even more captivating for your readers, think about varying your sentence structures a little more. For instance, instead of mostly using simple sentences, you could combine two related ideas into one longer sentence with words like 'because' or 'when.' This helps to keep your readers interested and makes your ideas flow even more smoothly, communicating both straightforward and more complex thoughts effectively. Keep connecting with your readers through your clear and heartfelt stories. 💬	Your story is well-organised, Yayar, and it flows in a clear, logical way from beginning to end. You’ve used basic linking words like 'and' and 'because' effectively to connect your ideas, which helps the reader follow the sequence of events easily. For example, 'We jump and immediately to call the others' clearly shows what happened next. To enhance the organisation of your writing further, consider trying out some more varied linking words. Words like 'then,' 'afterwards,' 'as a result,' or 'consequently' can add more sophisticated connections between your sentences and paragraphs. This will make your story feel even more polished and coherent, making it a little easier for your reader to follow all your ideas. 🔗	Yayar, you use a good range of everyday words that fit your story perfectly, and it's clear you're building your vocabulary well. It's great to see you attempting more complex structures, such as relative clauses like 'which is room one,' demonstrating good control. To make your language even stronger, focus on consistent verb tenses, especially when telling a story about past events. For instance, you wrote 'We jump and immietdately to call the others,' but sinc