xciting. You also did well in showing what happened next, like running to tell your teacher, and how everyone reacted. Your story had a clear beginning, middle, and end, which is great for a narrative. To make your next story even stronger, try to develop the ending a little more. For instance, what was the very final outcome for the bug, or what was the long-term impact of its discovery on the school or yourself? Just a little more detail at the end can make it feel completely finished for your reader.	You certainly know how to keep a reader interested, Atom. Your story flowed well and kept me engaged from the moment you saw the bug until it was on the news. You showed a good understanding of how to tell a story by describing what happened and how you felt. The way you described the bug as "incredible with such compelling patterns" really helped to capture the excitement of your discovery. To push this score even higher, think about how you can vary your sentence structures a bit more. Sometimes, using a mix of shorter, punchy sentences and longer, more descriptive ones can make your writing even more dynamic and captivating for young readers.	Your story is nicely organised, Atom, and it’s easy to follow the events from start to finish. You used words like "As" and "after" to help link your ideas together and show the order of events. This makes your story coherent and logical. One area where you can make your writing even better is by using paragraph breaks. Right now, your entire story is in one long paragraph. Imagine your story as a journey; each new stage or important event could be a new paragraph. For example, you could have one paragraph for the discovery of the bug, another for telling your teacher, and a third for the news coverage. This helps your reader follow the different parts of your story more easily. In Thai writing, it's common to see longer blocks of text, but in English, new paragraphs help signal new ideas or shifts in time or topic.	Atom, you used everyday words effectively to tell your story, and it was clear what was happening. You also made a good effort to use past tenses, which is exactly right for a story about something that happened yesterday. However, there are a few areas where we can tidy up your language use. The most important one is capitalisation. Remember to always start a new sentence with a capital letter. For example, you wrote "so it happened yesterday morning, me and my friend saw a bug" – this should be "So it happened yesterday morning, my friend and I saw a bug." Also, pay close attention to spelling, like "preculiar" which should be "peculiar", and verb forms such as "grab" instead of "grabbed" and "occures" instead of "occurred". Thai students sometimes forget capitalisation in English because the rules can be different in Thai, where capital letters are not used in the same way. Practising these details will make your writing much more accurate and polished.	4.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
