1697	33954	2025-09-12 12:33:35.644143+00	Term 1 Exam Question 1	The effect on the choices teenagers make went to who? A lot of things about teen had been effected to others also, friends and parents so I will express 3 things about choices, fashion, studying and hanging out.<br><br><br>Firstly, their clothes and fashion. It has changed because of their community or who that keeping company which is also changed the fashion to what is popular that time and that would be a lot of impact for teenagers. Friends are the one who maybe the person who got effected from this teen or the one who had been impacted to others.<br><br><br>Besides, study is very important for their grade which will can continue with their career so their parents are very got effected from how high and low the child's grade is so if it was high, they weren't going to be nervous but if it was low, they were going to be dissapointed.<br><br><br>Lastly, hanging out. For friends is no problem because teenagers always wanting to be with their friends in my opinion but with parents is not like that because teens have to ask for permission to go outside without their parent, and it would be so much effection if suddenly their child was dissapeared. The conclusion is some of teenagers' active would be a lot of effected to their parents but sometimes it went to their friends that worried about their company also the parents who wanted to take care of their child but about something aren't for protectiveness.	248	9.0	Hello Kaohom C! 👋 It was good to read your essay about the choices teenagers make. You've clearly put effort into thinking about the topic, and it's great that you included all three points from the question! Keep focusing on clearly answering the prompt and organising your thoughts, and you'll see great improvement. You're definitely on the right track! 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br><ul><li>✔️ Read the essay question carefully and underline the main question you need to answer. Then, make sure every paragraph helps answer that main question directly.</li><li>✔️ Before you start writing, jot down a quick plan for each paragraph. Think about the main idea and a couple of supporting sentences for it.</li><li>✔️ After you've finished writing, read through your essay specifically looking for words like "effect" and "affect," and check that your verbs match your subjects (e.g., "parents are," not "parents is").</li></ul>	You did a good job of discussing clothes and fashion, studying, and your own idea of hanging out, which covered all the points of the question. That’s an important part of a good essay! 👍 To improve, try to make sure you're always directly answering the main question asked in the prompt. For instance, the question asked "Who has more effect...?" so your essay needs to clearly state and argue whether it's friends or parents, or perhaps both, for each point. You also wrote quite a long essay, which shows you have a lot to say, but sometimes writing a little less can help you focus your message even more clearly. 🎯	You managed to get your ideas across, which is the main goal of writing! 😊 To make your writing clearer and more impactful for the reader, think about the overall tone and purpose. For a formal essay like this, it’s good to have a clear introduction that sets out your main argument, and a strong conclusion that summarises it. Try to avoid opening sentences like "The effect on the choices teenagers make went to who?" and instead aim for something more direct and academic. For example, you could start with a sentence that clearly states the debate: "It is often debated whether friends or parents have a greater influence on the choices teenagers make." This helps the reader immediately understand your focus.	You did well to use separate paragraphs for each of your main points, which helps to break up your writing and make it easier to read. 👏 However, sometimes the connections between your sentences and ideas felt a little loose, making it tricky for the reader to follow your argument smoothly. When you write, try to connect your sentences more clearly. For instance, instead of saying "Friends are the one who maybe the person who got effected from this teen or the one who had been impacted to others," you could try something like, "Friends often influence fashion choices, as teenagers look to their peers to see what is popular at the time." Thai learners sometimes make these kinds of mistakes because they are thinking in Thai and directly translating phrases, which doesn’t always work perfectly in English. Think about how your ideas link together, almost like building blocks that fit neatly. 🧱	You used some good everyday vocabulary, and the overall meaning of your essay was generally understandable, which is a great start! 😊 However, there were quite a few grammatical errors that sometimes made your sentences difficult to follow. Let's look at three important areas:<br><br>Firstly, watch out for the words 'effect' and 'affect'. 'Affect' is usually a verb (to influence), and 'effect' is usually a noun (the result). You often used phrases like "effected to others" when you probably meant "affected by others" or "had an effect on others." This is a common mix-up, and practising the difference will make your writing much clearer. 🤔<br><br>Secondly, pay close attention to verb tenses and how verbs agree with their subjects. For example, you wrote "teen had been effected" and "parents are very got effected." In English, we would usually say "teenagers are affected" or "parents are very affected." Also, be careful with modal verbs; "which will can continue" could be simply "which will continue."<br><br>Lastly, some of your sentence structures were a little confusing, for instance, "who that keeping company which is also changed the fashion." Try to simplify your sentences and ensure each part clearly relates to the others. Thai sentences often have a different structure, and sometimes learners try to use that same structure in English. Instead, focus on building clear, direct English sentences. A good trick is to read your sentences aloud to see if they sound natural and easy to understand. 🗣️<br><br>Keep up the hard work, Kaohom C! You're making progress, and with a bit more practice in these areas, your writing will become even stronger. 💪	3.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3706	34912	2026-02-20 12:50:40.299478+00	Question 2 (Article)	I found playing games hillarious because I can conduct some weird experiments. For an instance, yesterday, I found a glitch that caused a pig to fly while I was playing with my best friend, Chaokun, who I enjoy laughing with most. This is because I've known since I was a child like 7 years old.\n\nFrom my perspective, Laughing a lot is both benificial to your body and mind since it aids you to release stress, which makes you look younger and healthier. To illustrate, my best friend, whose name is Frame, laughs a lot, which makes him attractive and doesn't look sleepy or bored.	105	19.0	Hi Hero! This was an exceptional piece of writing. I loved the combination of your funny story about the flying pig and your thoughtful ideas about the health benefits of laughter. You are writing at a very high level! My main tip for next time is to just double-check the spelling of those wonderful, complex words you use. You're a star writer! All the best, Ed	I found your article absolutely hilarious to read! I noticed you mentioned the glitch with the 'pig to fly' and your friends Chaokun and Frame. This is a perfect example of how to make an article interesting and personal. You've answered every part of the task with great detail.	You've used a very sophisticated and engaging tone here! I really liked the part about laughter being 'beneficial to your body and mind'. It makes you sound like a real expert. You've held the reader's attention from the very first sentence to the very last.	I noticed how beautifully you organized your ideas into two clear paragraphs. You've used excellent connecting words like 'For an instance', 'From my perspective', and 'To illustrate'. This makes your article flow perfectly and it's a real joy to read!	Your vocabulary is truly impressive! 🌟 I was so happy to see big words like 'conduct', 'experiments', and 'attractive' used so well. I did notice two tiny 'spelling hurdles' in your big words: 'hillarious' should be 'hilarious' (with one 'l') and 'benificial' should be 'beneficial' (with an 'e'). For your next piece, try to watch out for these small spelling patterns so your brilliant ideas look even more professional! 🚀	5.0	5.0	5.0	4.0	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
2470	28823	2025-12-02 09:53:40.087331+00	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. His invention started 17000 years ago in his mothers basement, where he kept his secret away. His life goal is to make an invention that will steal other people space. He packed the button and activated it. Although the machine was working perfectly fine but he still wasn't satisfied what he created. He turned around to grab some drinks but when he turned back the machine was right infront of him, it started juking him and broke his ankles. Sam was watching it go up to the living room and his mom shouting "Not again FAHHH!". While he reattached his ankle he noticed that a BIG RED BUTTON still glowing. He picked it up and put it in his tiny little pocket. When he stood up he followed his invention track leading outside in the neighbor hood. He saw everyone hiking their spines ripped out by a machine and soon he would be next.<br><br><br>As machine got closer to him, he threw everything he could find such as Mountain, galaxy, The black hole and the Sun but it still not enough. When it got closer to him it started ripping out all of his bones one by one as a result of this, he turned into slime.<br><br><br>At first, he though it all over. He came up with an idea. He used this form to enter the machines body and started "Thick of it" song 10 hours loop, making machine die form cringe.<br><br><br>A little while later, he reclaims all of his bones and all of the stolen spine. He sold all the spines to black market, but he his point spine in black market having low price. In the end he learn that kidneys are more expensive than spine	298	15.0	Hello Rico, 👋<br><br>This is certainly the most unique story I have read! 🤪 Your imagination is wild, and the sequence of events—from the 'juking' machine to the black market ending—was very entertaining. It felt like a surreal cartoon or a meme come to life!<br><br>However, for a formal school assessment, I have some important tips. 📋<br><br>1. <b>Formal Tone!</b> 🎩 Slang like 'juking', 'broke his ankles', and 'cringe' is funny for friends, but try to avoid it in exam writing.<br>2. <b>Logic!</b> 🧠 The story jumps around a lot (galaxies, black holes, songs). Try to keep the plot a bit more grounded so the reader can follow it.<br>3. <b>Grammar!</b> 🛠️ Watch out for tense changes (switching between past and present).	You have a very vivid imagination! 🌟 The story definitely had a beginning, middle, and end.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Relevance!</b> 🎯 While creative, throwing mountains and galaxies is a bit *too* random. Try to make the events of the story fit the setting (the house/neighborhood) a bit better.</li><li><b>The Ending!</b> 🦴 The ending about kidney prices is funny, but it doesn't really resolve the story about the machine. A conclusion should wrap up the main problem.</li>	Your story holds attention because it is so unpredictable! 😲<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Audience Awareness!</b> 👥 In an exam, the 'target reader' is usually a teacher. Using internet memes (like the 'Thick of it' song) might confuse a reader who doesn't know them. Aim for a more neutral style.</li><li><b>Clarity!</b> 💡 'Steal other people space'—do you mean 'personal space' or 'outer space'? Be precise with your words.</li>	You used paragraphs to separate the different scenes, which is good. 📚<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Linking!</b> 🔗 You used 'As machine got closer' and 'A little while later'. Try to use more formal linkers like 'Suddenly', 'Consequently', or 'Eventually'.</li><li><b>Sentence Structure!</b> 🏗️ 'He his point spine in black market having low price.' This is hard to understand. Try: 'He found that spines had a low price on the black market.'</li>	You have a wide vocabulary ('galaxy', 'reclaim', 'neighborhood'). 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Neighbor hood' -> '<b>neighborhood</b>'. 'Infront' -> '<b>in front</b>'.</li><li><b>Verb Tenses!</b> ⏳ 'It still not enough' -> 'It <b>was</b> still not enough'. 'He learn that' -> 'He <b>learned</b> that'.</li><li><b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Hiking their spines' -> Maybe 'Hiking *with* their spines'? Or 'Having their spines ripped out'? Be careful with phrasing.</li>	5.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
974	33952	2025-07-24 09:06:37.59147+00	A Place I Love	The fist, that from my perspective is “Hua - Hin” that is locate at the gap of Prachuap and Phetchaburi. It usually be the most popular city of Prachuap about travelling and the landmark is call “Hua - Hin Beach”, That was the top 10 beautiful beach, That why it the place that I love to go.<br><br><br>Secondly, I will be introduce more about place, landmark and activity. Hua - Hin have a lot of place to take our memory. for the example, Had sai noi that was they another of turgoise sea and have the most beautiful wiew for take a photo, that side left of beach that have a lot of moss on the black rock, that so beautiful, that I can't explain it but also with the food on beach that have a lot of taste, like example the spicy noodle and juicy water from coconut. Next I will told you that have the biggest “Luang-bu-tuad” inside the temple call “Wat Huay - Mongkol” but actually it have a big of river that behind of temple. have a lot of fish that you can feed or let them be free and that was the another of land mark. The final that you should to go was the zipline, this activity wasn't dangerous too much because they hire a professtional of zipline to be care and protect you with your family, I can tell you that so funny because you will try hard to walk on the board wood that be alway swing and the price for each person didn't expensive to much but you have a good benefit to do this activity, I went this place that 3 months ago and this is all my place that I went it when I have, travel.<br><br><br>Finally, My conclusion that was Hua-Hin didn't only have 1 attraction but it have a lot of attraction, they have many place you could visit and rest it. Hua-Hin usually be the one of my young memory that I visited a lot. Suddenly I want you to go better than watch it, that can help you understand moreove about your thinking.	358	8.0	Ken, you wrote about Hua Hin as a place you love, talking about its beautiful beach, a special temple, and fun activities like ziplining. You shared many details about these places. To make your writing even better, you need to work on creating a clear introduction at the beginning of your article. Also, when you write a lot, it's very important to use separate paragraphs for each main idea. This will make all your interesting points much clearer for your readers. You should also work on correcting your spelling, grammar, and how you use connecting words so your message is always easy to understand.	Ken, you wrote about Hua Hin and gave many reasons why you love it, focusing on its nature like Hua Hin Beach and Had Sai Noi, as well as attractions like Wat Huay Mongkol and a zipline. You also mentioned food and your personal memories. You included a lot of information, which is good, but some details were not fully explained, and the sheer amount of information made it hard to keep your points concise for the type of article requested.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "The fist, that from my perspective is “Hua - Hin” that is locate at the gap of Prachuap and Phetchaburi. It usually be the most popular city of Prachuap about travelling and the landmark is call “Hua - Hin Beach”..."\n    Problem: This first paragraph is not a clear introduction to the city as a whole. It immediately jumps into a supporting idea (the beach) rather than setting the stage for why Hua Hin is a beloved place from your perspective.\n    How to fix it: Your first paragraph should be a true introduction. Start by broadly introducing Hua Hin and stating clearly why it's a place you love. For example, "From my point of view, Hua Hin, located between Prachuap and Phetchaburi, is a truly special place. This popular city, famous for its incredible attractions, holds many wonderful memories for me."\n\n*   Example: You talked about the zipline activity and said, "I can tell you that so funny because you will try hard to walk on the board wood that be alway swing and the price for each person didn't expensive to much but you have a good benefit to do this activity, I went this place that 3 months ago and this is all my place that I went it when I have, travel."\n    Problem: The description of the zipline is a bit unclear ("so funny because you will try hard to walk on the board wood that be alway swing") and mixes up past and present experiences. It also ends with a very general statement about your travels.\n    How to fix it: Describe the activity clearly and focus on your past experience. For example, "The zipline activity was truly exciting! It was so much fun trying to walk on the wooden board as it swung around. The price was quite reasonable, and it offers a great benefit for those who enjoy adventure. I experienced this thrilling activity three months ago, and it's certainly one of my favorite memories from my travels."	You tried to inform and persuade the reader about Hua Hin, but your language was consistently very informal and conversational ("The fist," "I will be introduce," "I will told you"). There were many grammatical errors and awkward ways of saying things that made it very difficult to understand your ideas clearly and kept the reader from staying interested in your article.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "The fist, that from my perspective is “Hua - Hin” that is locate at the gap of Prachuap and Phetchaburi. It usually be the most popular city of Prachuap about travelling and the landmark is call “Hua - Hin Beach”..."\n    Problem: This opening is full of informal language and grammatical issues. "The fist" is incorrect, and the sentence structure is very long and hard to follow.\n    How to fix it: Use more formal and appropriate language for an article. Break down your ideas into shorter, clearer sentences. For instance, "From my perspective, Hua Hin, located between Prachuap and Phetchaburi, is a truly captivating destination. It is often considered the most popular city in Prachuap for tourism, largely due to its famous landmark, Hua Hin Beach."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Secondly, I will be introduce more about place, landmark and activity. Hua - Hin have a lot of place to take our memory."\n    Problem: "I will be introduce" is grammatically incorrect, and "place, landmark and activity" is a bit too general. "take our memory" is an awkward phrase.\n    How to fix it: Use correct grammar and more descriptive language. For example, "Secondly, I would like to introduce you to more of Hua Hin's remarkable places, landmarks, and activities. Hua Hin offers many locations perfect for creating lasting memories."	You used three paragraphs with a clear beginning and end. However, your second paragraph is extremely long and tries to cover too many different ideas. **It's perfectly fine to write a lot, but if you go significantly over the word limit, you must divide your ideas into separate, focused paragraphs.** This will make your article much clearer and easier for the reader to follow. While you used basic linking words like "Secondly" and "Next," they weren't enough to make the many different points in your long paragraph flow smoothly.\n\n*   Example: Your second paragraph covers Had Sai Noi beach (description, food), then jumps to Luang-bu-tuad temple and feeding fish, and then to the zipline activity (description, price, personal experience).\n    Problem: All these distinct topics are crammed into one very long paragraph. This makes the essay dense and difficult to read, as the reader has to work hard to separate your different ideas.\n    How to fix it: **Break this long paragraph into multiple, shorter paragraphs.** For example:\n    *   One paragraph could focus on the beauty of Had Sai Noi beach and the food there.\n    *   Another paragraph could be about the Luang-bu-tuad temple and its unique features.\n    *   A third paragraph could be dedicated to the thrilling zipline activity and your personal experience.\n    This structure would greatly improve readability and coherence.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Next I will told you that have the biggest “Luang-bu-tuad” inside the temple call “Wat Huay - Mongkol” but actually it have a big of river that behind of temple."\n    Problem: The transition is simple, and the sentence tries to combine too many thoughts about the temple and the river without smooth connections.\n    How to fix it: Use more precise transitions and structure your sentences clearly. For instance, "Next, you must visit the impressive Luang-bu-tuad statue inside Wat Huay Mongkol temple. Behind the temple, there is also a large river where you can feed many fish or simply watch them swim freely."	You used simple vocabulary to describe Hua Hin. You attempted to use some transition words and more detailed descriptions, but there were very many noticeable and frequent mistakes in spelling, capitalisation, grammar (including verb forms, subject-verb agreement, and articles), and punctuation. These errors often made it impossible to understand what you were trying to say.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "fist," "locate," "faublous," "turgoise," "wiew," "professtional," "alway," "didn't expensive," "onece," "truely," "moreove"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled or used incorrectly. They should be "first," "located," "fabulous," "turquoise," "view," "professional," "always," "wasn't expensive," "once," "truly," "moreover."\n    How to fix it: You need to proofread your writing extremely carefully. Use a dictionary or a spell checker for every word you are unsure about. These frequent errors severely impact how well your message can be understood.\n\n*   Capitalisation:\n    Example: "The fist, that from my perspective is “Hua - Hin”," "That why it the place," "Had sai noi that was they another of turgoise sea," "Luang-bu-tuad," "Wat Huay - Mongkol"\n    Problem: You often do not capitalize the first word of a sentence or proper nouns (names of specific places, temples, statues). "That" is sometimes capitalized incorrectly in the middle of a sentence.\n    How to fix it: Always capitalize the first letter of every sentence. Capitalize all proper nouns (e.g., "Hua Hin," "Had Sai Noi," "Luang-bu-tuad," "Wat Huay Mongkol").\n\n*   Grammar - Verb Forms, Subject-Verb Agreement, and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "that **is locate**," "It usually **be**," "I've **went**," "I will be **introduce**," "Hua - Hin **have**," "I will **told** you," "it **have** a big of river," "that **was** the another of land mark," "you should **to go**," "you will try hard to walk on the board wood that **be** alway swing," "My conclusion that **was** Hua-Hin didn't only have 1 attraction but it **have** a lot of attraction," "you could visit and **rest it**."\n    Problem: These examples show pervasive errors with verb forms, subject-verb agreement, and complex sentence structures. Sentences are often ungrammatical and difficult to follow.\n    How to fix it: Focus on building clear, grammatically correct sentences. Pay very close attention to using correct verb forms and ensuring your verbs agree with your subjects (e.g., "it *is located*," "it *is*," "I've *been*," "I will *introduce*," "Hua Hin *has*," "I will *tell* you," "there *is*"). Also, improve clarity in phrasing (e.g., "rest it" should be "rest there").	3.0	2.0	2.0	1.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3707	34924	2026-02-20 12:50:40.299478+00	Question 2 (Article)	You see this announcement in your school English - language magazine.\n\nWhat make you laugh? First, what thing that make I laugh it would be me and my friend make a joke and play together we have fun because when we play roblox together that was a time to relax use time together we enjoy when you sad you will have friend we you. Laugh it good to for you think it funny and you enjoy with it. You don't have to stay quiet. My friend it person who i laugh with the most we tell our story or make a joke to look funny make feel confident when stay with...\n\nfor example yesterday it was Chinese new year me and my friend went to the mall to watch movie before we watch my friend and me tell about our life is it good or not we talk with each other for a long the movie was start the movie it so funny me and friend laugh durning movie end it so relax.	173	11.0	Hi Ping-Ping! It was so nice to read about your fun times playing Roblox and going to the movies for Chinese New Year. You have some really great stories to tell! My main tip for next time is to practice using full stops to break up your long sentences. This will make your wonderful ideas much easier for everyone to read. Keep up the good work! All the best, Ed	I noticed that you mentioned playing Roblox and celebrating Chinese New Year with your friends. This is exactly what the article asked for! You've told the reader what you find funny and why laughter is good. You did a great job including all the information.	You've shared some very sweet ideas about friendship! I noticed you wrote 'when you sad you will have friend'. It's a very kind thought. To make this even better, you could try to explain one specific joke or funny thing that happened while you were playing Roblox so the reader can laugh with you!	I noticed that you have a lot of long sentences without many full stops. For example, the part about Roblox and the movie feels like one big breath! For your next writing, try to use a full stop after each main idea. This will give the person reading your article a chance to pause and understand your points more easily.	You used some good words like 'confident' and 'relax'. I did notice some trouble with verbs, like 'What make you laugh' instead of 'What makes you laugh'. I also saw 'we talk with each other for a long', which just needs the word 'time' at the end. Practicing these common sentence endings will really help your writing!	5.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	A2+	2026
922	34014	2025-07-22 10:25:33.253599+00	A Place I Love	Hokkaido is the best place that I ever been to. Hokkaido, where snow covered, is the northernmost island of Japan, which is really huge island. I love Hokkaido atmosphere and people in Hokkaido. It's unique from any place I have been visited.<br><br><br>Hokkaido's vibes is full of happiness. Because we can relax in hotspring and listen to the nature sound. In my experience, people in Hokkaido are kind and friendly. I can speak little Japanese but they alway be kind and try to talk to me. Last time I went to Hokkaido, I met an old man, who live in Hokkaido for long time, He gave me two snowball makers for me and my little sister to play together. And when I went to a small restuarant, I met a woman who can't speak english. So, I used my Japanese language skill try to talked to her. Before I finished that lunch, She gave me a soft served - ice cream for free. I constantly feel great until now.<br><br><br>Hokkaido give me good feelings and I want to visit there again So, if anyone ask me about my favorite place, I will answer Hokkaido for sure! I recommend everyone to go Hokkaido, being relax and get the best experience which you can't get from anywhere else.	216	13.0	Lookpear, you've done a great job describing why Hokkaido is a special place for you, highlighting its atmosphere, friendly people, and unique experiences. To make your writing even stronger, work on clarifying some of your sentences and making sure your grammar is always accurate. Also, remember to stay within the word count in an exam setting.	Lookpear, you have successfully addressed the task by describing Hokkaido as a place you love and providing clear reasons for this. You discuss its atmosphere, the kindness of its people, and specific memorable experiences like meeting the old man and the woman at the restaurant. The essay provides good details to inform the reader. While your essay is 216 words, which is slightly over the expected 140-190 word range, it generally stays focused on the topic. To achieve a score of 5, you could aim for more conciseness and refine some of the details to fit more strictly within the word count while still fully informing the target reader.	You communicate straightforward ideas effectively, and your enthusiasm for Hokkaido is clear. The essay's purpose, to recommend Hokkaido, is also well-conveyed. The overall tone is friendly and inviting. However, some awkward phrasing and grammatical errors occasionally distract the reader and slightly hinder the flow of ideas. For example, \\"It's unique from any place I have been visited\\" could be clearer. To improve to a 5, you would need to communicate ideas with more precision and flair, using a wider range of linguistic resources to maintain consistent reader engagement.	The text is generally well-organised with a clear introduction, body, and conclusion. Ideas are mostly connected, and you use some linking words and phrases such as \\"Because,\\" \\"Last time,\\" \\"And when,\\" and \\"So.\\" However, the body paragraph is quite long and could benefit from being broken into smaller, more distinct paragraphs to improve overall coherence and make the text easier to follow. For example, separating the descriptions of \\"vibes\\" from the personal experiences with people would improve the flow. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to use a wider variety of cohesive devices and organisational patterns more effectively to ensure a seamless flow of ideas within and between paragraphs.	You use a range of everyday vocabulary appropriately, such as \\"atmosphere,\\" \\"friendly,\\" \\"hotspring,\\" \\"snowball makers,\\" and \\"ice cream.\\" You also attempt some less common lexis like \\"northernmost island\\" and \\"constantly feel great.\\" You use a range of simple and some complex grammatical forms with a good degree of control. However, there are occasional errors that, while not impeding communication, are noticeable. Examples include: \\"Hokkaido is the best place that I ever been to\\" (should be \\"I have ever been to\\"), \\"where snow covered\\" (should be \\"which is snow-covered\\"), \\"It's unique from any place I have been visited\\" (should be \\"It's different from any place I have visited\\"), \\"Hokkaido's vibes is full of happiness\\" (should be \\"Hokkaidohas a really happy vibe\\"), \\"alway be kind\\" (should be \\"always are kind\\"), \\"try to talked to her\\" (should be \\"try to talk to her\\"), \\"soft served - ice cream\\" (should be \\"soft-serve ice cream\\"), and \\"being relax\\" (should be \\"to relax\\"). To achieve a score of 5, you would need to use vocabulary with more precision and greater control and flexibility over complex grammatical forms, with only minimal and non-impeding errors.	4.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
