ur ideas, it can feel a little bit repetitive. For your next story, try using words like 'then' or 'after that' to vary your writing.	I noticed you used paragraphs and some basic connecting words like 'So' and 'When'. This is a good way to show the order of events! Some of your sentences are very long and have many ideas connected together. Breaking these up into smaller sentences will make your story even easier for the reader to follow.	You used some good everyday vocabulary like 'vacation' and 'resort'. 🌴 I did notice that many of your sentences are very long because you use the word 'so' many times to join them together, like in '...around 8 p.m. so Jo talks... so his friend agree...'. 🔗 We call these 'stringy sentences' because they are like a long piece of string with too many knots! For your next story, try to use a full stop instead of 'so' when you finish an idea. This gives your reader a chance to breathe and makes your writing sound much stronger! 🚀	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3711	35309	2026-02-20 12:50:40.299478+00	Question 3 (Story)	Jo looked at the map and decided to go left. Jo a young pirate on a journey to become the king of the pirates. He began his journey on a small fishing boat with barley any foods. In a miracle Jo had found a beautiful land.\n\nWhile sailing there something hit his boat. Crank! a sound of a bottle hitting his boat. Out of curiosity he picked it up, and soon found out that there's a map on the inside. After checking the map he realised the treasure is in the island up ahead.\n\nAfter arriving at the island, Jo has fallen for the beauty of a flower. Turned out it was a carnivorous plant! despretly Jo managed to survived. Following the map has lead him to a tough spot. He has to pick to go left or right. On the bright side Jo already knew that some of the plants were carnivorous. So he decided to go left.\n\nHe found a chest on the "x" marking spot. He opened it, and found a preculiar fruit, and ofcourse he took a bite. It tasted so bad, Jo shouted. Because of that Jo has gained the power of fire. He happily ruled the world after.	204	14.0	Hi Atom! That was an absolutely fantastic pirate story. I really felt like I was on the journey with Jo! You have a real talent for using descriptive words. My main advice for your next piece is to explain your ending a little more clearly and to vary how you start your sentences. You are a very strong writer! All the best, Ed	What an exciting pirate adventure! 🏴‍☠️ I noticed how you used the 'Crank!' sound to make the story feel real. You told a full story, but I was a little bit confused at the very end! 🤔 Because we didn't know the fruit was magical, it was a big surprise when Jo got fire powers and ruled the world! For your next story, try to give the reader a little 'hint' earlier on so the ending makes perfect sense. 📝	You've used the conventions of storytelling really well to hold my attention! I especially liked the 'Crank!' sound of the bottle. It makes the story feel very alive. 🌊 While the story is exciting, the jump from eating a fruit to ruling the world happens very quickly. To keep your reader fully engaged, try to explain *how* his new powers helped him become a king! 👑	I noticed that you used great paragraphing and clear time words like 'After checking the map' to show the order of your story. 🗺️ This makes it very easy for the reader to follow Jo's journey! One thing to watch out for is that almost every sentence starts with 'He' or 'Jo'. This can make the writing feel a little bit like a list of actions. For your next piece, try starting some sentences with an '-ing' word, like 'Searching the island, Jo found a chest...' to make the story flow even more naturally! 🌊	Your vocabulary is very impressive! I loved seeing words like 'miracle', 'carnivorous', and 'peculiar'. 🌟 I did notice a few small 'hurdles' with your verbs. For example, you wrote 'Jo a young pirate' (which needs 'is' or 'was') and 'managed to survived'. Since you used 'to', the next word should just be 'survive'. Keeping an eye on these small rules will help your adventurous language shine! ✨	4.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
982	28855	2025-08-01 15:13:27.790032+00	My Favorite Place	So, a city I want to recommend is Tokyo which is a capital city of Japan locate in Kanto reagion on Honshu island.<br><br><br>Tokyo is one of the greatest city I ever visited. It have a beautiful view, nice people, beautiful culture and more!<br><br><br>Lets talk about places in Tokyo.<br><br><br>First, if you are finding a place to shopping especially if you're interested in fashion I recommend you to visit Shibuya and Harajuku. At Shibuya there are a tall cylinder building calls 'Shibuya 109' which have many clothing shops, you can find whatever style you are wearing! And at Harajuku there are a main street with many clothing shops along the way.<br><br><br>Harajuku is popular known as a places for J-fashion enjoiners. You can find people with many styles of fashion here, for example: 'Emo', Decora, Jirai kei, Gyaru and one of the most popular J-Fashion with sweet and girly look, like they're a high-class lady from UK in 19th centuary, and these call 'Lolita' a fashion that look like a girl's doll. If I keep explain more about J-fashion well must can't move on to another topic, so you can find more information on internets.<br><br><br>And if you want some places feels traditional you must visit Asakusa temple, which famous with the big red lantern at the gate. And there are a market at the front were you can find some souvenier and street foods!<br><br><br>In conclution, Tokyo is an interesting place to visit, so don't forget to	249	11.0	Nam, you wrote about Tokyo as your favorite place and included some interesting details about Shibuya, Harajuku, and Asakusa. You did a good job of introducing different areas of the city. To make your writing even stronger, you could focus on explaining *why* these places are special to you and make sure your paragraphs flow smoothly from one idea to the next. Keep up the good work!	Nam, you introduced Tokyo and mentioned key aspects like shopping areas (Shibuya, Harajuku) and a traditional site (Asakusa Temple). You also highlighted general positive points like "beautiful view, nice people, beautiful culture." However, the essay is about "My Favorite Place," and you didn't fully explain *why* these aspects make Tokyo your favorite or share enough personal experiences and feelings. The essay sometimes feels more like a tourist guide than a personal reflection. There is also an abrupt ending that leaves the reader wanting more information.\n\n*   **Example:** You wrote, "Tokyo is one of the greatest city I ever visited. It have a beautiful view, nice people, beautiful culture and more!"\n*   **Problem:** You state that Tokyo is a "greatest city" with a "beautiful view, nice people, beautiful culture," but you don't explain *why* you think these things make it great or why you love them. You're telling the reader your opinion without showing them.\n*   **How to fix it:** Add more specific details and personal feelings. For example, you could say: "Tokyo is one of the greatest cities I've ever visited because when I stood at the top of the Tokyo Skytree, the view of the city lights stretching for miles was truly breathtaking, and it made me feel excited to explore every corner."\n\n*   **Example:** Your essay ends abruptly with, "In conclution, Tokyo is an interesting place to visit, so don't forget to"\n*   **Problem:** The conclusion is incomplete, and it feels like you ran out of time or ideas. This leaves the reader without a proper summary of your main points or a strong final thought.\n*   **How to fix it:** Make sure you always finish your essay with a complete conclusion. This paragraph should summarize your main ideas and leave the reader with a clear final impression. For example, you could say: "In conclusion, Tokyo is an interesting place to visit, and it is my favorite because of its unique blend of modern fashion, rich history, and delicious food. I hope you get a chance to experience its magic too!"	You used a good informal and friendly tone, which is suitable for recommending a place to others. You tried to get the reader's attention with phrases like "Lets talk about places in Tokyo." However, some ideas are communicated simply, and the essay sometimes feels more like a list than a piece of writing that keeps the reader fully engaged. The sudden end also makes it hard to feel like the message is complete.\n\n*   **Example:** You wrote, "If I keep explain more about J-fashion well must can't move on to another topic, so you can find more information on internets."\n*   **Problem:** While it's good to show you have more to say, this sentence breaks the flow of your writing and makes it sound like you are speaking directly to the reader in an informal chat, which isn't always best for a written essay.\n*   **How to fix it:** You can hint at more details without stopping the flow. Instead of saying you "can't move on," you could briefly mention the depth of the topic and then transition smoothly. For example, you could say: "J-fashion is a vast and fascinating world, with many styles to discover in Harajuku. To learn more, you can easily find plenty of information online. Now, let's explore some other attractions in Tokyo."	You tried to organize your essay with an introduction, separate paragraphs for shopping and traditional places, and a conclusion. You used some connecting words like "First" and "And." However, as you noted, the paragraphing is sometimes disjointed, and the ideas don't always flow smoothly from one to the next. Each paragraph needs more development to fully explain its main idea before moving on.\n\n*   **Example:** You start a new paragraph with "Lets talk about places in Tokyo." right after saying Tokyo "have a beautiful view, nice people, beautiful culture and more!"\n*   **Problem:** This sentence could easily be part of the previous paragraph or integrated into your introduction. Starting a new, very short paragraph for a simple transition makes your writing feel choppy and less connected.\n*   **How to fix it:** Try to combine short, related ideas into fuller paragraphs. You could add "Let's talk about places in Tokyo" to the end of your introductory paragraph or even integrate it into the start of your first main body paragraph. For example: "Tokyo is one of the greatest cities I ever visited. It has a beautiful view, nice people, beautiful culture, and much more to explore. Let's talk about some of these amazing places."\n\n*   **Example:** You have a long paragraph about Harajuku and J-fashion, ending with "If I keep explain more about J-fashion well must can't move on to another topic, so you can find more information on internets." Then you start a new, short paragraph about Asakusa: "And if you want some places feels traditional you must visit Asakusa temple, which famous with the big red lantern at the gate. And there are a market at the front were you can find some souvenier and street foods!"\n*   **Problem:** The transition between the detailed J-fashion section and the brief Asakusa section feels very sudden and not fully developed. The Asakusa paragraph is also quite short and could use more information to be well-rounded.\n*   **How to fix it:** Develop each paragraph more fully. For instance, after concluding your point about J-fashion, you could have a clear transition sentence that introduces the next theme. Then, expand on Asakusa, perhaps describing a personal experience or more details about the temple's significance or the market's atmosphere. For example: "While Harajuku offers a glimpse into modern Tokyo's unique fashion scene, the city also holds deep historical roots. For those seeking a taste of traditional Japan, you must visit Asakusa Temple..." You could then add more details about the temple itself, not just the market.	You used some common words correctly, but there were many spelling, grammar, and punctuation mistakes throughout your essay. These mistakes sometimes made your meaning hard to understand or made your writing sound less formal than it should be for an essay.\n\n*   **Spelling and Capitalisation:**\n    *   **Example:** You wrote, "capital city of Japan locate in Kanto reagion on Honshu island."\n    *   **Problem:** Words like "locate" should be "located," "reagion" should be "region." Also, proper nouns like "Kanto" and "Honshu" should be capitalized.\n    *   **How to fix it:** Always proofread your work for spelling errors. Remember to capitalize all proper nouns (names of specific places, people, or things).\n\n*   **Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement/Pronoun Use:**\n    *   **Example:** You wrote, "Tokyo is one of the greatest city I ever visited. It have a beautiful view..."\n    *   **Problem:** "city" should be "cities" when you say "one of the greatest." Also, "It have" should be "It has" because "it" is singular.\n    *   **How to fix it:** Pay attention to whether your subjects are singular or plural and make sure your verbs match. Remember that "it" takes "has."\n\n*   **Sentence Structure and Word Choice:**\n    *   **Example:** You wrote, "Harajuku is popular known as a places for J-fashion enjoiners."\n    *   **Problem:** "popular known as" should be "popularly known as." "a places" should be "a place." "enjoiners" is not the correct word here; you likely mean "enthusiasts" or "lovers."\n    *   **How to fix it:** Read your sentences aloud to catch awkward phrasing. Be careful with adverbs (like "popularly") and check the meaning of words to make sure you're using the best one for your idea.\n\n*   **Informal Language:**\n    *   **Example:** You wrote, "Lets talk about places in Tokyo."\n    *   **Problem:** "Lets" should be "Let's" (with an apostrophe). This phrase is also very conversational and can be too informal for an essay.\n    *   **How to fix it:** For an essay, try to use slightly more formal language. You could rephrase this as "Let's explore some of Tokyo's attractions" or integrate it into the topic sentence of your paragraph.\n\n*   **Missing Punctuation:**\n    *   **Example:** You wrote, "And at Harajuku there are a main street with many clothing shops along the way."\n    *   **Problem:** This sentence is missing a period at the end.\n    *   **How to fix it:** Always end your sentences with appropriate punctuation, usually a period.	3.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
