1792	33961	2025-09-13 15:41:27.361424+00	Term 1 Exam Question 2	Nowadays, a lot of people owns a pet. The major reason why people think pets are good for them are because it makes the environment around the family look warm and cozy, gives motivation and affects our emotions in the best way possible.<br><br><br>How does pets make family feel warm, cozy and comfortable? Pets are always moving or walking around the house and make family members look active. You have and enjoy playing with them and it make houses feels like home.<br><br><br>Apart from making good environment around families is giving motivation. When ever you are extremely tired from work or school but then you came back home and open the frontdoor saw your pet waiting for you with a precious smile is so adorable and released all the stresses you have all day.<br><br><br>Last but not least which is that it helps you and affects your emotions and mentalities. Pets make you enjoy your life with them, makes you happy and laugh, help releasing your stressed and doesn't make you feel lonely.<br><br><br>In conclusion, pets are something that is good to have, because it makes the family feel warm and cozy, gives motivation and released stresses from work and school, doesn't make you feel lonely. Otherwise, having pets need lots of responsibility, so make sure to love and take care of them.	223	10.0	Hello Gut-Jung, I'm so pleased to see you've tackled the challenge of writing an article about pets. You clearly have some lovely ideas about why pets are a joy to have. 🎉 To make your writing even stronger, you'll want to ensure you always cover all parts of the question in a balanced way, just like an experienced journalist would. Keep up the great effort, I know you can refine these skills. 🌟<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Before you start writing, make a quick list of all the points the question asks you to cover. This helps ensure you don't miss anything.<br>✔️ After you write, read through your work and focus specifically on subject-verb agreement. For example, check if "people owns" should be "people own."<br>✔️ Practise using a wider range of linking words to connect your ideas smoothly between sentences and paragraphs.	Gut-Jung, you've shared some wonderful thoughts about the joy pets bring to a family, making a home feel warm and helping us feel motivated and happy. That's a great start. 😊 However, the question asked you to discuss both the *advantages* and *disadvantages* of having pets. You've focused almost entirely on the good points, with only a very brief mention of responsibility at the very end. To get a higher score, you need to make sure you give equal weight to all parts of the task. For instance, you could have dedicated a paragraph to the challenges of pet ownership, such as the cost of food and vet bills, or the time required for training and walks. This would show your reader that you've thought deeply about the whole topic.	You've made a good attempt at writing an article, Gut-Jung, which is exactly what the task asked for. The overall message is clear, and your ideas are easy to follow. 👍 To really capture your reader's attention and communicate more complex ideas, think about varying your sentence structures and using a slightly more engaging tone throughout. For example, instead of simply stating "How does pets make family feel warm, cozy and comfortable?", you could try "Ever wondered how pets weave their way into our hearts, making our homes feel warm and cozy?" This helps to draw the reader in. Thai learners sometimes focus a lot on getting their message across, which is excellent, but adding a little flair can make a big difference in an article.	Your article has a clear structure with an introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion, which is fantastic! You also use some basic linking words like "Apart from" and "In conclusion" to guide the reader. 📝 To make your writing even more cohesive, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases to show the connections between your ideas. For instance, you could use "furthermore," "in addition," "however," or "consequently." Sometimes, simply starting a new paragraph makes sense, but other times, a good linking word will make the relationship between the ideas even clearer. Thai learners often use simple transitions, and expanding this range will really help your writing flow beautifully.	You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, which is great for communicating your ideas clearly. 😊 However, there are a few recurring grammatical errors that sometimes make it a little harder to understand your message. For example, take a close look at your subject-verb agreement; "a lot of people owns a pet" should be "a lot of people **own** a pet," and "it make houses feels like home" should be "it **makes** houses **feel** like home." Also, be mindful of your verb tenses, such as in "When ever you are extremely tired... then you came back home and open the frontdoor saw your pet waiting for you" – this mixes past and present tenses. A good tip is to read your sentences aloud to yourself to catch these kinds of errors. Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai, which can lead to these kinds of grammatical constructions, so focusing on these specific points will be very helpful for your progress. Keep practising, Gut-Jung, and you'll see great improvement! ✨	2.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
