3720	29034	2026-02-23 09:08:53.941124+00	Question 1	Hello, Mrs Hallam\r\n\r\nI beleive that organising a school talent competition is a great idea! It gives a chance for students to show off their skills and provides great entertainment for everyone. This event will also be great for showing off our school's most talented students and gives them a chance to shine.\r\n\r\nI reccomen that the event be for all ages due to the fact that talant can manifest at any age. I have seen many kids, who are in grade 4, sing better than some of my freinds. So I think that anybody should have the chance to participate.\r\n\r\nI disagree because some parents can be unfair in judgment when it comes to their child.\r\n\r\nThe winners should get a medal or a trophy, something for them to look at and remember.\r\n\r\nYour student, Aanoh	137	16.0	Hi Anu! You did a fantastic job responding to Mrs Hallam's email, and your arguments about why parents shouldn't be judges were very persuasive. Your writing is clear, polite, and very well-organized. My main 'homework' for you is to keep a little list of words you often find tricky to spell, like 'friends' and 'believe', so you can master them for next time. All the best, Ed	It was great to see how you included every single point from the notes, especially when you gave a clear reason why the show should be for all ages by saying 'talent can manifest at any age'. This thoroughness ensures the reader has no unanswered questions and feels completely confident in your suggestions. For your next piece, keep this habit of providing a 'why' for every 'what' you suggest! 🌟	Your choice of 'Your student' as a closing and the polite opening 'Hello, Mrs Hallam' perfectly matches the teacher-student relationship. 📝 This respectful tone keeps the reader engaged and makes your ideas feel well-considered. To reach the top level, try to make the transition between your opinions feel a bit more like a natural conversation and less like a list of responses. 🚀	I