ese:<br><li><b>Agreement!</b> ❌ "I am not agree" -> "I <b>disagree</b>" or "I <b>don't</b> agree". (Agree is a verb!).</li><li><b>Word Forms!</b> ⚙️ "more relax" -> "more <b>relaxed</b>".</li>\n	5.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
2947	29929	2025-12-14 09:36:22.436217+00	Term 2 Midterm M2A Part 2	I look out of the window and couldn't believe my eyes. I was the student in the high-school every day there would have my friend talking about the story of this high-school, it was really creepy. He told me that have many student in this school did at this building, someone hanging, someone drowning and someone fell from the balcony. He told me every day but still didn't believe him. But in one day I went to school like every day, I learn math subject and it's really boring, so I take a look at the window but when I focus on the floor I saw something that I can't believe my eyes. I saw a courp on the floor. I told the teacher that I would go to toilet to saw a courp but when I check it it hold my hand really fast and it said "come~ with~ me~" I was screemved very loud before I woke up and I believe now that I was dreamming.	168	15.0	Hello Tiw! 👋<br><br>This was a very creepy and effective ghost story set in a high school! 👻 The rumors about the school's past and the sudden appearance of the 'courp' really built the suspense. The twist ending where you realize it was a dream was a great way to resolve the tension!<br><br>I have a few tips to help you make your writing even more spine-chilling! 📋<br>1. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 Watch out for words like 'courp' (corpse), 'did' (died), and 'dreamming' (dreaming).<br>2. <b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Screemved' isn't a word—it should be '<b>screamed</b>'.<br>3. <b>Dialogue!</b> 💬 When writing dialogue, put each speaker's words on a new line and use quotation marks.	You started your story perfectly with the given sentence and created a very compelling horror narrative within a school setting! 🌟 The details of the school rumors and the math class made the story very relatable before the scary event.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>The Corpse's Appearance!</b> 🧟 You saw a 'courp'. Could you describe it a little bit? Was it pale? Did it have dark eyes? A small detail could make it much scarier.</li><li><b>Teacher's Reaction!</b> 👩‍🏫 You told the teacher you were going to the toilet. What was the teacher's reaction? Did they look suspicious?</li>	You successfully built a sense of unease and horror, especially with the ghost's words, "come~ with~ me~". 😱<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Show, Don't Tell!</b> 🎬 Instead of saying 'it was really creepy', describe *why* it was creepy. What specific sights or sounds contributed to that feeling?</li><li><b>Vary Your Language!</b> 🗣️ Try to avoid repetition. For instance, instead of 'I saw a courp on the floor... I told the teacher that I would go to toilet to saw a courp', use a pronoun or rephrase.</li>	Your story has a clear chronological progression, from the school rumors to the classroom incident and the dream ending. 🧱 Your paragraph breaks are generally effective.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Dialogue Formatting!</b> 💬 When a character speaks, it's best practice to put their speech on a new line and enclose it in quotation marks. This makes dialogue much easier to read.</li>	You used everyday vocabulary, along with some horror-related terms like 'creepy' and 'balcony'. 📚<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Courp' -> '<b>corpse</b>', 'screemved' -> '<b>screamed</b>', 'dreamming' -> '<b>dreaming</b>'.</li><li><b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Did at this building' -> '<b>died</b> in this building'.</li><li><b>Past Tense!</b> ⏳ 'I look out' -> 'I <b>looked</b> out'. 'I was the student' -> 'I <b>am a</b> student'.</li><li><b>Verbs!</b> 🛠️ 'Saw a courp' (the first time) -> '<b>see</b> a corpse' (when thinking about going to the toilet).</li>	5.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
2949	28858	2025-12-14 09:36:23.074573+00	Term 2 Midterm M2A Part 2	I looked out of the window and couldn't believe my eyes, Sam a handsome kid who loves to play basketball. Sam has a dream to become the best basketball player in the world. Sam mother didn't want him to play basketball and focus on studying. But his dad kept support him and train him.  One day sam was playing games in his house but suddenly he heard a man sound outside his house calling his name he looked out of the window and couldn't believe his eyes the coach from his dream team that he always wanted to meet is there. He quickly go up to him. The coach said that he had watched his game play in youtube and saw potential in his and he decided to come and invite him to the team. He was so confused and happy at the same time about the youtube the coach watched.  when he went inside. He asked his dad about the youtube. and found out that it was his dad who made the youtube and trust him and supported him all the time.  Finally he learned that always keep P trying and you will achieve his dream.	197	16.0	Hello Gunner! 👋<br><br>This is such a heartwarming and inspiring story about chasing your dreams! 🏀 I loved the twist about your supportive dad secretly being behind the YouTube channel and inviting the coach. It's a fantastic message about perseverance and trust. Keep that dream alive!<br><br>I have a few tips to help you make your story even more impactful! 📋<br>1. <b>Apostrophes!</b> 📝 'Sam mother' should be 'Sam<b>'s</b> mother' (possessive).<br>2. <b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Keep P trying' is a bit unclear. Did you mean 'keep <b>on</b> trying' or 'keep <b>persevering</b>'?<br>3. <b>Clarity of Dialogue!</b> 💬 Make sure to use quotation marks for direct speech (e.g., when the coach speaks).	You created a very relevant and emotionally resonant story about a young basketball player's journey. 🌟 The build-up of Sam's dream and his dad's secret support was excellently handled.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>The Coach's Words!</b> 💬 The coach saw 'potential in his'. What specific potential did he see? Describing that would add more depth to Sam's talent.</li><li><b>Sam's Game Play!</b> ⛹️‍♂️ You mentioned the coach watched 'his game play in youtube'. Briefly describing one exciting move Sam made in his game would make the coach's interest more tangible.</li>	You successfully conveyed an inspirational tone, highlighting the importance of dreams and family support. 💖 The repetition of 'couldn't believe his eyes' at key moments was effective in building excitement.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Dialogue Punctuation!</b> 💬 Using quotation marks around direct speech makes it much clearer who is saying what.</li>	Your story has a clear narrative arc: the dream, the struggle, the opportunity, and the revelation. 🧱 Your use of linking words like 'One day', 'suddenly', and 'Finally' helps guide the reader.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Paragraphing!</b> 📝 While you use some paragraph breaks, consider breaking the longer paragraph when the coach speaks, to give that important moment more emphasis.</li>	You used good vocabulary like 'potential', 'support', 'invite', and 'achieve'. 📚<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Possessives!</b> 📝 'Sam mother' -> 'Sam<b>'s</b> mother'.</li><li><b>Verb Forms!</b> 🛠️ 'Kept support him' -> 'kept <b>supporting</b> him'. 'He quickly go up' -> 'He quickly <b>went</b> up'.</li><li><b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Made the youtube' -> '<b>created the YouTube channel</b>'.</li><li><b>Phrasing!</b> 🔄 'Always keep P trying' -> 'always keep <b>on trying</b>'.</li>	5.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
1888	34931	2025-09-16 21:45:26.729885+00	Term 1 Email	Dear Corey,<br><br><br>Thanks for organizing a tickets for the science festival for me.<br><br><br>I'd be pleasure, if you can pick me up at my home. Because my parents won't have a time to take me to the science festival. They probably working on Saturday.<br><br><br>I think build a robot should be more interesting, because I don't really like Dinosaurs, so it would be boring. Build a robot seem fun and we can learn for our science project in the next term.<br><br><br>I think we should bring a camera to take a pictures and selfie to collect the amazing moments. And don't worry, I'll bring a pocket for sure.<br><br><br>Goodbye,<br><br><br>Frame	104	13.0	Hi Frame! It was lovely to read your email to Corey. You did a really good job of responding to all of his questions and making your preferences clear. To make your writing even clearer and more natural, you could focus on making sure your sentences are grammatically correct. Keep practicing, and you will see wonderful progress. 📋 Here are three steps you can take to improve your writing:<br>✔️ Check verb forms carefully to ensure they match the subject and tense of your sentences.<br>✔️ Pay close attention to articles (a, an, the) when you use nouns.<br>✔️ Practice using common phrases for expressing feelings and opinions so they sound more natural.	You've done well to include most of the important information Corey needed to know for the science festival. For example, it was great that you clearly told Corey you'd prefer to be picked up and gave a good reason why your parents couldn't take you. You also made it clear you'd rather build a robot than watch a film, and explained why the film sounded a bit boring to you. Suggesting a camera was a clever idea too, and you said why you wanted to bring it. However, to get an even better score, try to make your excitement about the tickets more obvious at the beginning. Also, when Corey asked about "pocket money," it seems you thought he meant "a pocket" instead. This minor misunderstanding meant you didn't quite address that point fully. Thai learners sometimes make these small slips when a word has a similar sound or form to another.	Your email to Corey was very well-structured and easy to follow. You started with a friendly "Dear Corey" and ended with a clear "Goodbye, Frame," which is exactly what we expect in an email to a friend. 🥳 You also did a fantastic job of organising your thoughts into different paragraphs, which makes your message very clear and helps the reader understand all your ideas without any trouble. This shows you know how to write an email that keeps the reader engaged and gets your message across effectively.	Your email was generally well-organised and coherent, with your ideas presented in a sensible order. You used some basic linking words, like "Because" and "so," which helped to connect your sentences and make the text flow. This is a good start. To improve, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For instance, instead of just "Because," you could use "since" or "as a result" to show different connections between your ideas. Thinking about how one sentence leads to the next will help you explore more ways to link your thoughts smoothly. Thai learners often focus on getting their main points across, and sometimes forget to add these small words that really help the reader follow their train of thought.	You used everyday vocabulary quite well, and I could generally understand what you wanted to say, which is excellent. However, there were a few noticeable grammatical errors that, while not completely stopping communication, did make some sentences a little less clear. For example, you wrote "organizing a tickets" instead of "organising the tickets," and "I'd be pleasure" should be "I'd be pleased" or "it would be a pleasure." Also, "won't have a time" should be "won't have time," and "They probably working" should be "They are probably working." To improve, try to pay close attention to articles like "a," "an," and "the," and make sure your verbs are in the correct tense and form. For instance, after "I think," you usually need a full sentence, like "I think **that building** a robot would be more interesting." Thai learners sometimes struggle with articles and verb agreement because these grammatical features work differently, or are not as prominent, in Thai.	3.0	5.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
2951	28841	2025-12-14 09:36:23.749581+00	Term 2 Midterm M2A Part 2	I looked out the window and couldn't believe my eyes. I saw a dragon standing calmly in my garden! Its icy blue eyes were staring me like it was waiting for me a long time already. I blinked so many times but the large dragon was still there no matter what.  Out of curiosity, I walked out from my house and slowly approached the dragon yet cautiously. The dragon quickly back away and lowly growled at me like it was scared of me. So I also stepped back so it didn't suddenly attack me. However I saw that its right wing was bleeding for some reasons. The dragon seemed to be in so much pain that I couldn't help but decided to help it.  Then I went back into my house to the first aid bag. After I had grabbed my first aid bag, I immediately headed to the injured dragon and carefully healed its wing with things inside my first aid bag then wrapped its wound with a bandage! Although the dragon seemed to be cautious at first, it later cooperated with me when I promised to heal its wound.  "You are a very kind person human" The voice suddenly came out from the dragon's mouth. I was so surprised that the dragon could talk but it later explained to me that this was just a trial and it didn't really hurt, just pretended to be. After that the dragon grabbed on my shirt collar with its mouth carefully and placed me down on its back. "As a thank for you a very kind-hearted human I will let you ride me to see the sky for once" and prepared to take off.  The dragon spreaded its beautiful wings widely before everything would turn black. In the end I woke up on my bed and it turned out it all was just a dream! I was a little bit disappointed for waking up before getting to see the sky but it was very inspiring that I immediately wrote what I remembered on my notebook so I could use this for my next novel idea after being burn out for a long time as a famous writer.	366	20.0	Hello Ie Ie! 👋<br><br>This is an absolutely breathtaking and imaginative story! 🐉 Your dragon encounter, from its icy blue eyes to the thrilling flight, was incredibly vivid. The twist about using the memory for your next novel was brilliant and very meta! You have a true gift for storytelling!<br><br>I have a few tiny tips to help you perfect this masterpiece! 📋<br>1. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 Watch out for 'spreaded' (spread) and 'burn out' (burnout).<br>2. <b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Staring me' should be 'staring <b>at</b> me'.<br>3. <b>Refine Dialogue!</b> 💬 Your dragon dialogue is strong, but ensure you punctuate it clearly.	You crafted an exceptionally relevant and richly detailed narrative, exceeding expectations for the prompt! 🌟 The emotional arc of healing the dragon, the magical flight, and the meta-narrative twist of it being a dream for a writer's novel idea were all perfectly executed. All content contributed significantly to the story's depth and enjoyment.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Dragon's Pain!</b> 💔 You said the dragon 'seemed to be in so much pain'. Describing *how* it showed its pain (e.g., whimpering, labored breathing) would heighten the reader's empathy.</li><li><b>Writer's Block!</b> ✍️ You mentioned 'being burn out'. Briefly explaining *what kind* of novel you write or what caused the burnout would add even more depth to your character as a writer.</li>	This is an outstanding piece of engaging and sophisticated narrative writing! 🤩 You skillfully use descriptive language, emotional cues, and a clever plot device to captivate the reader. The dialogue with the dragon is particularly effective.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Show, Don't Tell!</b> 🎬 You excelled at this! Continue to focus on describing actions and sensory details rather than just stating emotions (e.g., 'icy blue eyes were staring me').</li>	Your story is exceptionally well-organized, with clear paragraph breaks that logically guide the reader through each stage of the encounter. 🧱 Your use of linking words like 'Out of curiosity', 'Although', and 'In the end' creates a seamless flow.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Nothing!</b> 🌟 Your organization is superb. This story demonstrates excellent structural control.</li>	You display an excellent command of language, using a wide range of sophisticated vocabulary such as 'calmly', 'cautiously', 'cooperated', 'kind-hearted', 'inspiring', and 'burnout'. 📚 Your use of complex sentences is highly effective.<br><br>Here are a few minor polishes:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Spreaded' -> '<b>spread</b>'.</li><li><b>Prepositions!</b> 📍 'Staring me' -> 'staring <b>at</b> me'. 'Thank for you' -> 'thank <b>you for being</b> a very kind-hearted human'.</li><li><b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ 'Couldn't help but decided to help it' -> 'couldn't help but <b>decide</b> to help it'.</li><li><b>Capitalization!</b> 🅰️ 'I saw a dragon standing calmly in my garden! Its icy blue eyes were staring me like it was waiting for me a long time already. I blinked so many times but the large dragon was still there no matter what. Out of curiosity, I walked out from my house and slowly approached the dragon yet cautiously.' -> 'I saw a dragon standing calmly in my garden! Its icy blue eyes were staring at me like it had been waiting for a long time. I blinked many times, but the large dragon was still there. Out of curiosity, I walked out of my house and slowly but cautiously approached the dragon.'</li>	5.0	5.0	5.0	5.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
2955	28836	2025-12-14 09:36:25.031119+00	Term 2 Midterm M2A Part 2	Question 3  I looked out of the window and couldn't belive my eyes. I, who was a high-schooler that lives in USA and belive that aliens are true I get bullied because of what I belive that aliens are real.  One day, while I was walking home from school. I saw something strange. I looked up at the sky and saw a strange green light on the sky. At first I didnt care much of it. I thought it was just a plane flying by. So I continved to walk home.  After I arrived at my home I began to do my homework till midnight. As I was about to went to sleep I saw the green light again but now somethings different there was a strange mysterious sound, And I looked out of the window and couldn't belive my eyes. I saw a flying UFO! I was so scared and joyful at the same time.  The next day when I went to school with happyness until I heard a bully talking on the walkway about how humorus my face look yesterday. Turns out, it was the bully pronked all along from the first time that I saw the green light to when I saw the UFO. It was all fake.  I felt like my dreams were shater and broke apart.	221	15.0	Hello Namo K! 👋<br><br>This is a super intriguing story about a possible alien sighting turning out to be a bully's prank! 👽 You captured the feeling of being an outsider who believes in something unusual, and the disappointment when your dreams are 'shater and broke apart'.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you make your writing even more polished! 📋<br>1. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 Watch out for words like 'belive' (believe), 'continved' (continued), and 'pronked' (pranked).<br>2. <b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ 'Humorus my face look' is a bit unclear. Did you mean 'humorous how my face looked'?<br>3. <b>Show, Don't Tell!</b> 🎬 Instead of saying 'I was so scared and joyful', describe your physical reactions or internal thoughts.	You started your story perfectly with the given sentence and developed a very relatable plot about a belief in aliens and a disappointing prank. 🌟 The contrast between your character's belief and the reality of the prank was well-conveyed.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Bully's Motivation!</b> 🤔 Why did the bully target you specifically? Was it because of your belief in aliens? A small detail could add more depth to this aspect of the story.</li><li><b>The 'Joyful' Part!</b> 🎢 You mentioned being 'scared and joyful at the same time' about the UFO. What was the source of that joy? Was it the validation of your belief?</li>	You successfully conveyed the emotional journey of a high-schooler facing both belief and bullying. 💬<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Vary Emotional Expression!</b> 🎭 You repeated 'couldn't belive my eyes'. Try using synonyms or different phrases to express surprise or disbelief.</li>	Your story has a clear chronological progression, moving from your initial belief to the sightings and the eventual prank reveal. 🧱 Your paragraph breaks are generally effective.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Sentence Boundaries!</b> 🛑 Ensure that each sentence is a complete thought, especially when connecting multiple ideas.</li><li><b>Linking Words!</b> 🔗 While you use words like 'One day' and 'After', try to incorporate more varied transitions (e.g., 'however', 'consequently', 'despite this') to create a smoother flow.</li>	You used everyday vocabulary related to school, bullying, and space, like 'high-schooler', 'bullied', 'strange', and 'UFO'. 📚<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Belive' -> '<b>believe</b>' (multiple times), 'continved' -> '<b>continued</b>', 'pronked' -> '<b>pranked</b>', 'shater' -> '<b>shattered</b>', 'happyness' -> '<b>happiness</b>', 'humorus' -> '<b>humorous</b>'.</li><li><b>Verb Forms!</b> 🛠️ 'I get bullied' -> 'I <b>got</b> bullied'. 'Was about to went' -> 'was about to <b>go</b>'.</li><li><b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ 'Humorus my face look' -> 'how humorous my face <b>looked</b>'.</li>	5.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
3744	33953	2026-02-23 09:59:15.665585+00	Question 1	Dear Mrs Hallam,\n\nA school talent competition is very good idea because it make a space for all students to show they talent and it will make them be confident and proud of them self.\n\nI think the talent show could be for students of all ages to let them show their abilities for everyone who want not just for students over 15 only.\n\nI agree that we can let some parents judge the competition but I think we also have to let the students do the vote because this is a talent show for students so we have to let them vote.\n\nTo sumup, I think the winners of this competition should receive a certificate and some exclusive merchaindise.\n\nThank you\n\nSalila Lookasemsukwong	124	15.0	Hi Kaohom L! You’ve done a wonderful job responding to all of Mrs Hallam's questions, and your suggestion about letting students vote was very thoughtful. Your vocabulary is quite sophisticated for this level, especially with words like 'merchandise' and 'confident.' My main 'homework' for you is to focus on your 'subject-verb agreement' (like 'it makes' or 'everyone wants') to ensure your grammar is as strong as your great ideas. All the best, Ed	It was great to see how you included 'all students to show they talent' as a reason for supporting the show, as this provides the teacher with a very clear justification for your opinion. By answering every part of Mrs Hallam's email—including the age range, the judges, and the prizes—you have ensured the reader is fully informed and can move forward with the planning. To keep this high standard, continue to provide a specific 'why' for every suggestion you make, just as you did here! 🌟	Your choice of 'I agree that we can let some parents judge' really helps maintain a polite and respectful tone suitable for writing to a teacher. 🚀 To reach the top level, try to make the transition between your opinions feel a bit more like a natural conversation and less like a sequential list of responses to the prompt. 🌟	I see that you used clear paragraphs for each new topic, which acts like a helpful roadmap for your reader! 📝 However, each paragraph feels a little bit like an answer to a list of questions rather than a smooth, connected message. To help your writing flow even better next time, try using more 'internal' connectors between and within your paragraphs, like 'Additionally' or 'Furthermore', to make the flow even more natural. 🌟	When you wrote 'exclusive merchaindise', you showed a really impressive vocabulary range that goes beyond basic B1 levels. However, small slips in grammar like 'it make' instead of 'it makes' or 'they talent' instead of 'their talent' act as little speed bumps that slow the reader down slightly. For your next piece, try to have a quick 'grammar check' at the end specifically for subject-verb agreement and possessive pronouns to make your writing even smoother!	5.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3745	33954	2026-02-23 09:59:15.665585+00	Question 1	Hi Mrs Hallam,\n\nAfter I've read, I think this is a good idea! Because some of a students are so talented of musical instrument or another way.\n\nIn my opinion, I prefer that we should allow all ages of students because students are have their own ability which is want to show , perform or having competition to compare themselves to others.\n\nI disagree because I think the parents would bias to a students who are not their son or daughter.\n\nI think the prizes would be money because it's worth to their talent.\n\nStudent	95	14.0	Hi Kaohom C! You did a wonderful job responding to all of Mrs Hallam's questions, especially with your thoughtful reason for why parents shouldn't be judges! Your ideas are very clear, and you've used some great B1-level vocabulary like 'bias' and 'ability'. My main piece of 'homework' for you is to watch out for those extra 'be' verbs (like 'are') before other verbs, as this will make your sentences much stronger and smoother. All the best, Ed	It was great to see you start with 'I think this is a good idea!' because it immediately shows you have addressed the first part of the teacher's email. By answering every single question Mrs Hallam asked, including your thoughts on the age group, the judges, and the prizes, you've made sure the reader is fully informed. For your next piece, try to add one more tiny detail to your suggestions, like naming a specific musical instrument, to make your writing even more vivid! 🌟	Your choice of 'I prefer that we should allow all ages of students' clearly communicates your opinion in a polite and straightforward way that is perfect for a teacher. This clear expression of your preference makes it very easy for the reader to understand your point of view without any confusion. To make your email sound even more natural, you could start with a friendly opening like 'Thanks for your email!' to build a warmer connection with the reader. 🚀	I see that you included a new paragraph for each point, starting with clear markers like 'In my opinion' and 'I disagree', which acts like a map for the reader. This logical structure helps the reader follow your arguments one by one without getting lost in the text. One way to level up is to use 'linkers' like 'Additionally' or 'Finally' to connect these paragraphs so the email feels like one continuous, flowing conversation. 📝	When you wrote 'students are have their own ability', I could understand your meaning, but the extra 'are' makes the sentence feel a bit 'bumpy' for the reader. Using two verbs together like this is a common hurdle, and removing the 'are' would give the sentence a much stronger heartbeat. My tip for next time is to double-check your sentences for 'double verbs'—usually, you only need the main action verb to make your point clearly! ✨	5.0	3.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
2962	29045	2025-12-14 15:55:00.305219+00	Term 2 Midterms 2025-6	Me too! I am so excited for this trip.<br><br><br>Well, I rather not bother your dad so, can we take the train on Saturday morning instead?<br><br><br>I see your point, but I don't think sausages are going to fill stomach, why don't we just make a sandwich instead, it is better doesn't it?<br><br><br>In my opinion, I think we should bring like a hoodie or just a sleeping bag which one do you prefer? I prefer a hoodie because a sleeping bag is not really comfortable.<br><br><br>See you soon, In-sun	90	15.0	Hello In-Sun! 👋<br><br>This is a very considerate email! Staying empathetic to Alex's dad ("I rather not bother your dad") was really sweet. 🥰 You wrote very naturally.<br><br>Here are 3 tips for you: 📋<br>1. <b>Preference!</b> 🩷 "I rather not" -> "I <b>would</b> rather not".<br>2. <b>Question Tags!</b> 🏷️ "it is better doesn't it?" -> "it is better, <b>isn't it</b>?".<br>3. <b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ "fill stomach" -> "fill <b>our</b> stomachs".\n	You hit all the points nicely. ✅<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Interaction!</b> 💭 Asking "which one do you prefer?" about the hoodie/sleeping bag was a great way to involve Alex!</li>\n	Your tone is excellent - very B1/B2 level. 🌟<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Opening!</b> 👋 You forgot to say "Hi Alex" at the start. Don't forget the greeting!</li>\n	Your structure using `<br><br>` makes it very easy to read. 📝<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Linking!</b> 🔗 "In my opinion" is good. Try "Regarding warmth," or "As for the cold," to introduce the last point.</li>\n	"Comfortable" and "bother" are good words! 👏<br><br>Fix these:<br><li><b>Tags!</b> 🏷️ If the sentence is positive ("It is"), the tag is negative ("isn't it?"). If the sentence is negative ("It isn't"), the tag is positive ("is it?").</li><li><b>Grammar!</b> ⚙️ "I rather" -> "I'd rather" (I would rather).</li>\n	5.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
2961	28887	2025-12-14 15:55:00.166688+00	Term 2 Midterms 2025-6	Hi<br><br>Me too! This will fun and we should bring some games to play and don't forget bring something to light the camp fire or bring some food too.<br><br>That's a great idea but I don't like sausages and I think we should bring something more like snacks, crakers, pork or beef stew.<br><br>A sleep bag would do and you can bring more jackets and blankets. to or you can use the camp fire to make to bed warm.<br><br>I prefer your dads car I don't want to wake up early plus It's cheaper and we can watch the views along the way.<br><br>See you, Léo	106	13.0	Hi Leo! 👋<br><br>Nice email! Suggesting "beef stew" was a very specific and tasty idea! 🍲 Also, thinking about the cost ("It's cheaper") was a very mature point.<br><br>Here are 3 tips for you: 📋<br>1. <b>Verbs!</b> ⚙️ "This will fun" -> "This will <b>be</b> fun".<br>2. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 "crakers" -> "crackers".<br>3. <b>Possessives!</b> ✍️ "dads car" -> "dad's car".\n	You answered everything! ✅<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Warmth!</b> 💭 You mentioned "make to bed warm". Did you mean use the fire to warm stones for the bed? That's an advanced camping trick! Explain it clearly.</li>\n	Your tone is friendly. 🌟<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Clarity!</b> 🗣️ "don't forget bring" -> "don't forget <b>to</b> bring". Small words make big differences in flow!</li>\n	You used paragraphs well. 📝<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Transitions!</b> 🔗 Your sentences are a bit jumpy. Use "However," before disagreeing about sausages.</li>\n	Good idea with "stew"! 👏<br><br>Watch these errors:<br><li><b>To Be!</b> 🐝 "This will fun" -> "This will <b>be</b> fun" (Fun is an adjective).</li><li><b>Articles!</b> 🅰️ "A sleep bag" -> "A <b>sleeping</b> bag".</li>\n	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
3746	33955	2026-02-23 09:59:15.665585+00	Question 1	Dear Mrs Hallam,\n\nThat is a wonderful idea! It will make students show off their skills and boost their confidence. Taking part in the competition will also make them feel excited.\n\nThe talent show should be for students of all ages so that every one can join and no one is left out.\n\nI think parents should not be the judge. It is because parents will make their child win and I think it is unfair for other students,\n\nFinally for the prizes, I think we should let the winner of the competition ask for what they want. This will make students want to join the talent show. Moreover, it will make them practice to improve their skills.	118	18.0	Hi Arty! 🌟 This was a brilliant piece of writing. You addressed every point Mrs. Hallam asked for, and your vocabulary choices like 'boost their confidence' really stood out to me. You have a very clear way of explaining your ideas! My main 'homework' for you is to watch out for small plural errors—when talking about a group like 'parents,' make sure the noun 'judges' is also plural. Keep up the amazing work! 🚀 All the best, Ed	It was great to see you start with 'That is a wonderful idea!' because it immediately shows your enthusiasm and ticks off the first requirement of the task. This makes the reader feel your positive energy right from the start and confirms you've understood the teacher's plan. To make your response even stronger, you could briefly mention one specific talent, like singing or dancing, to connect even more closely with the email's details.	Your explanation that 'parents will make their child win' is a very clear and logical way to argue your point about the judges. 🚀 This helps the reader understand your perspective perfectly. To reach the top level, try to include a friendly sign-off like 'Best wishes' or 'See you soon' at the end to make it feel like a real, complete email! 🌟	Using 'Finally for the prizes' works like a clear map for the reader, showing exactly when you are moving to the last topic. 📝 Your use of paragraphs and connecting words like 'Moreover' makes your writing very easy to follow. For your next piece, try using even more varied connectors like 'Furthermore' or 'Additionally' to link your paragraphs even more tightly! 🌟	Your choice of phrases like 'boost their confidence' and 'show off' is fantastic because it shows you have a wide range of natural-sounding vocabulary! 🌟 Your control of complex sentences is excellent. Just keep an eye on plural agreement, such as saying 'parents should not be the judges' instead of 'the judge,' since you are talking about a group of people. ✨	5.0	4.0	4.0	5.0	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
