u used the rules for writing an essay in a good way to share your ideas clearly. You start your essay well and your ideas follow each other nicely. You share your thoughts, and the main goal of your essay is clear. It is quite easy to understand, but some small mistakes make it a little less clear sometimes.	You have put your ideas into clear paragraphs. Each paragraph talks about a different part of the movie, like the actors, the story, and what could be better. You use simple words like "first" and "Second" to connect your ideas. This helps your text to be connected and easy to follow.	You use many basic English words that are right for talking about movies. But you make many mistakes with grammar, spelling, and using capital letters. Even with these mistakes, we can still understand what you mean most of the time.\n\n**To help you improve, here are some things to practice:**\n\n*   **Capital Letters:** This is very important! Remember to use a capital letter for the start of *every new sentence*. Also, always use a capital letter for names of specific things (called "proper nouns") like:\n    *   **Names of people:** like "Jac Lu Si" (your text: "Jac lu si")\n    *   **Specific places/countries/languages:** like "Chinese" (your text: "chinese series", "chinese people", "lern chinese"), "Thai" (your text: "thai people", "thai translate")\n    *   **Brand names:** like "Netflix" (your text: "netflix")\n    *   **The start of a sentence:** For example, "A story is about" (your text: "A story is about"), "First, a story probably..." (your text: "first, a story probably..."), "Second, this series..." (your text: "Second, this series...")\n\n*   **Grammar (Verbs and Sentences):** Work on how words change and how to put sentences together correctly.\n    *   Instead of "One of the most that I like is," you could say "One of the most **popular** series I like is..."\n    *   Instead of "A main charactors in this series work by Jac lu si," try "The main **characters** in this series **are played** by Jac Lu Si."\n    *   Instead of "She like him since day one meet," you could say "She **has liked** him since the first day **they met**." (This is for actions that started in the past and continue to now).\n    *   Look at how you use verbs, for example, "they alway work hard together" should be "they **always** work hard together."\n    *   Also, try to use "there is" or "there are" correctly. For example, "It's not much things" should be "There are not many things."\n    *   "she's being a girlfriends" should be "she **becomes** his girlfriend."\n    *   "we can lern chinese when we watching" should be "we can **learn** Chinese when we **are watching**."\n    *   "it's have many movies" should be "there **are** many movies."\n\nPracticing these points will help make your writing much clearer and easier for others to understand.	5.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
