opinion, explain *why* friends or parents have more effect in each specific area, using clear examples that directly support your main argument. Sometimes your examples felt a little brief, and expanding on them would help your readers understand your points more deeply. 😊	You've made a good effort to write an essay, Nut, and your main point about friends having more influence is clear! However, to score a 5, you need to remember that an essay for this exam should sound a bit more formal, like something you'd read in a school textbook or a serious article. Sometimes your language felt a little too much like a chat with a friend (for example, "Hello, today I will tell you all about my opinion" or "Hng."). Try to use more formal vocabulary and avoid overly casual phrases or direct address to the reader to make your writing sound more academic. This will help your essay make a stronger impression on your reader! 👍	You've done well to separate your ideas into different paragraphs, Nut, and using words like "First," "Next," and "Finally" is a good start to organising your thoughts. However, to truly excel here and score a 5, your essay needs to flow much more smoothly from one idea to the next. Right now, the transitions between your paragraphs feel a bit like sudden stops and starts, almost like they are fragments rather than full connections. For example, when you wrote "First, clothes and fashion," this is a sentence fragment. A stronger transition would be a complete sentence that clearly introduces your point, such as, "Firstly, friends have a much bigger influence on fashion choices than parents." Think of your essay as a journey for your reader; you want to guide them gently from one point to the next with clear, complete sentences, not suddenly drop them into a new topic. Try using a wider range of linking phrases or even a short sentence to bridge the gap between your paragraphs. Many Thai learners sometimes focus on getting all the ideas down, and don't spend enough time thinking about how those ideas connect for the reader. Practising how to make your paragraphs hold hands, so to speak, will make your writing much clearer and easier to follow! 🤝	You have a good range of vocabulary to express your ideas, Nut, and that's a fantastic foundation! You're trying to use different sentence structures, which is also excellent. To improve your language score to a 5, let's focus on accuracy, especially with a few common areas. Firstly, **subject-verb agreement** is important; for example, you wrote "friends has more effect" when it should be "friends *have* more effect." Secondly, pay close attention to **pronouns**, making sure they match the nouns they refer to, like "their parents" instead of "they parents" or "our parents" instead of "ours parents". Lastly, be careful with **articles** (a, an, the, or no article), as these can be tricky. For instance, "in nowadays" should be "nowadays" or "in this day and age", and "many difference type of fashion" should be "many different types of fashion." Thai learners often find these aspects of English grammar challenging because Thai doesn't have the same grammatical rules, so it takes extra practice to get them right. Keep practising these details, and your writing will become much more precise! ✨	3.0	3.0	2.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
