sed a slightly informal phrase in your paragraph: "It makes students are happier when they are at school and make the class unserious."<br><b>Correction:</b> "This makes students happier when they are at school and can make the class feel less formal."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In academic writing, we aim for a more formal and objective tone. Also, "unserious" is not a common word to describe a class; "less formal" or "more relaxed" would be better.	[Single sentence performance summary]Your paragraph is very well-organised and easy to follow. You use linking words effectively to connect your ideas.<br><b>Strength:</b> You use clear linking words like "Firstly," "Secondly," and "The final reason" to introduce each of your main points, which makes your paragraph very logical and easy to read.	[Single sentence performance summary]You use a good range of everyday words and try to use more complex sentences with some success, but you have some errors in spelling and basic grammar.<br><b>Problem:</b> There were several spelling errors in your writing, for example: "thier phones" and "freetime".<br><b>Correction:</b> "their phones" and "free time".<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, some common words are often misspelled, and compound nouns like "free time" are usually written as two separate words. Thai students sometimes misspell these due to direct translation or phonetics.	5.0	3.0	5.0	3.0	Writing	CA	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
3794	29045	2026-02-23 13:32:08.183797+00	Question 3 (Story)	Lois smiled as she put the tickets in her pocket and walked out of her house. Not long after that the uber's finally arrived, she quickly get into the seat at the back of the driver, she was heading to the cinema near her school. Lois, cute five-feet five-inches tall with with her black-leather bag and pink hoodie, is going on a date with excitement. When the uber's arrived at the cinema, she quickly handed a one-hundred dollar bill to her driver and stormed out of the car and slowly walk to Aerion, blond six-feet tall with tattoo behind his neck, he is also a captain of shool basket ball team and Aerion is the boy who's Lois going to cinema with. From eleven AM to six PM, things go really well they have already kissed and Aerion decided to drive to her home to meets her parents but before they could arrive something happened. "Lois! Lois! Lois!" her mom shouted, Lois was just dreaming about her crush but in reality Aerion doesn't even know her.	188	16.0	Hi In-Sun! I really enjoyed the 'dream' twist you added to your story—it was a very creative way to use the prompt! Your descriptions made Lois and Aerion feel like real people. My main 'homework' for you is to focus on 'tense consistency'; try to pick one tense (usually the past for stories) and stick with it throughout the whole piece. This will make your writing much smoother. All the best, Ed	It was great to see how you used the required starting sentence to jump straight into a vivid scene. Your choice to end with 'Lois was just dreaming about her crush' was a clever way to wrap up the narrative, ensuring the reader is fully informed and entertained by the 'twist' ending. For your next story, try to keep the word count closer to the 100-word mark to practice being even more concise with your storytelling! 🌟	The way you included specific details like the 'black-leather bag and pink hoodie' really helped to paint a clear picture of the characters in my mind. These descriptive choices hold the reader's attention well because they make the world feel real. To reach a 5, try to ensure your tone stays consistent; for example, 'stormed out' is very dramatic, so make sure the reason for the character's speed matches that level of emotion! 🚀	I see that you used time-based phrases like 'Not long after that' and 'From eleven AM to six PM' to move the story forward logically. This helps the reader follow the timeline of the date without getting lost. To make your organization even stronger, try using a new paragraph when the 'reality' hits at the end, as this visual break acts like a signal to the reader that the scene has changed. 📝	Your use of descriptive vocabulary like 'stormed out' and 'crush' shows a wonderful range of expression. However, when you wrote 'she quickly get' and 'slowly walk', the mix of present and past tenses made the story's timeline feel a bit bumpy. A great tip is to check that all your verbs are in the past tense (like 'got' and 'walked') if your story starts in the past, as this keeps the 'heartbeat' of your writing steady and professional. 🛠️	5.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
