es, yes. I can do it, I can do it !”' really brings the characters to life and helps hold the reader's attention throughout the emotional shift of the story. This adds a nice personal layer to the narrative, though the reader has to slow down slightly to navigate a few tense slips. A great tip for your next piece is to read your work aloud to ensure the 'voice' of the story stays consistently in the past tense. 🚀	When you wrote 'When the time passed and passed until Sunday', you successfully showed the reader the transition of time. However, because the story is written as one large block of text, it's harder for the reader to see where one scene ends and the next begins. Next time, try to use separate paragraphs for each part of the story—like the booking, the concert day, and the hospital visit—to act as clear visual maps for your reader! 🗺️	I see that you used 'she needed to went', which is a very common hurdle when you are focused on telling a fast-paced and exciting story. This makes the sentence feel a bit inconsistent because the grammar rule for the infinitive requires the base form of the verb. My advice for your next piece is to remember that after 'to' or modal verbs like 'can', we always use the simplest form of the action, such as 'she needed to go' or 'she can book'. 📝	5.0	4.0	3.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	A2+	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
