5/5:</b> Make sure you complete all aspects of the task, providing thorough explanations and examples for every point, leaving no ideas unfinished.<br><br>	You attempted to communicate your ideas clearly, but the overall message was sometimes difficult to follow due to a number of language errors. Remember that written English, especially in an essay, requires a more formal and precise register than everyday spoken Thai. Be mindful of maintaining a consistent tone throughout your writing.<br><br>	You organized your essay into paragraphs, which is a good start. However, for a B2 level, your introduction needs a clear thesis statement that states your main argument. Your conclusion also needs to effectively summarise your main points and restate your thesis, rather than ending abruptly. Work on using clear topic sentences at the beginning of your body paragraphs to guide the reader.<br><br>	You used a good range of everyday vocabulary and attempted to use some complex sentences, which is great! The main challenges in your writing are with a few key grammar rules, common for Thai learners.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Activities help us by relive some stress in our life, and lots people in thailand have a similars activities" <br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Activities help us to relieve some stress in our lives, and many people in Thailand have similar activities" <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, we don't always change words for plurals, but in English, you need to add '-s' to plural countable nouns (like 'lives', 'activities'). Also, 'similar' is an adjective, it doesn't need an '-s'. 'Relieve' (meaning to reduce) is often confused with 'relive' (meaning to experience again).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "will shares a simple active" <br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "will share a simple activity" <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> After 'modal verbs' like 'will', 'can', or 'should', the next verb must always be in its basic form (e.g., 'share', not 'shares'). Additionally, 'active' is an adjective; you need the noun form 'activity'.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Fist Activites we will talking" <br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "The first activity we will talk about" <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Be careful with spelling words that sound similar; 'Fist' (a closed hand) is different from 'First' (indicating the beginning). Also, when introducing a topic, we often say "talk about" or "discuss".<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Game can be good for you but the bad side have more than good side." <br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Playing games can be good for you, but the negative aspects often outweigh the benefits." <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai often connects many ideas in one long sentence. In English, it's important to use clear connecting words (conjunctions) and sometimes commas to make complex sentences easier to understand, especially when comparing or contrasting ideas.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. **Master Plural Forms:** Practice adding '-s' to countable nouns and using quantifiers like 'many' or 'a lot of'. Review the use of articles ('a', 'an', 'the').<br>2. **Subject-Verb Agreement & Modals:** Pay close attention to making sure your verb matches your subject (e.g., 'he plays', 'they play') and use the base form of verbs after modal verbs.<br>3. **Essay Structure Practice:** Focus on writing clear thesis statements for your introductions and strong concluding sentences that summarise your main points.<br><br>	3.0	2.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
