related to free time activities quite well, and you attempted to use some more complex sentence structures, like relative clauses. However, there are frequent grammatical errors, and very few sentences are completely error-free. Incorrect capitalization is also a recurring problem, which affects the overall clarity and presentation of your writing.</p><br><p>❌ Problem: You wrote: "For Thai's teens, they have quite less freetime"</p><br><p>✨ Correction: "For Thai teens, they have quite less free time"</p><br><p>💡 Explanation: In English, "Thai" is an adjective and doesn't usually take an apostrophe 's' when referring to people in general. Also, "freetime" should be two words, "free time". Thai learners often combine words that are separate in English.</p><br><p>❌ Problem: You wrote: "many teenagers really love to do this even though there're not on their freetime."</p><br><p>✨ Correction: "many teenagers really love to do this even when they do not have free time."</p><br><p>💡 Explanation: The contraction "there're" (there are) is incorrect here. You need "they are" or "they're". Also, "do not have free time" is better as "not on their free time".</p><br><p>❌ Problem: You wrote: "When they finishing the homework or studying, they would grab their own instrument and note and then play it."</p><br><p>✨ Correction: "When they are finishing their homework or studying, they would get their instrument and notes and then play it."</p><br><p>💡 Explanation: You missed the auxiliary verb "are" before "finishing". In Thai, present continuous is often implied without a direct equivalent for 'to be' + -ing, which can lead to its omission in English. Also, "note" should be plural "notes" when referring to musical notation.</p><br><p>🎯 YOUR ACTION PLAN</p><br><p>1. Complete all parts of writing tasks and develop each point fully</p><br><p>2. Maintain consistent formal register throughout your writing</p><br><p>3. Practice using different linking words to connect ideas</p><br><p>4. 