Weakness: You wrote: "This activity helps about building more friendship, also, it allows them to relax and entertaining with friends." While true, this is a bit general.</p><br/><p>✨ Improved: "This activity is particularly popular because it not only provides a valuable opportunity for teenagers to relax and socialise, strengthening their friendships, but also offers a break from the demanding academic pressures common in Thailand."</p><br/><p>💡 Explanation: Developing your ideas more fully ensures the reader gets a deeper understanding and adds more weight to your arguments, which is expected at a B2 level.</p>	<p>You generally used an appropriate tone for an article, but there were some register shifts, and grammatical errors sometimes affected the clarity. The overall impression could be more consistently formal for a B2 article.</p><br/><p>👉 To Improve: Focus on maintaining a consistent, slightly more formal register throughout your writing. Also, ensure your sentences are grammatically correct to avoid confusing the reader.</p><br/><p>❌ Weakness: You wrote: "Free time is really important for Teens in Thailand. Because it allows us to focus about themself and it makes us relax." The start of the second sentence without proper connection and the word choice of "themself" are informal.</p><br/><p>✨ Improved: "Free time is exceptionally important for teenagers in Thailand because it allows them to focus on themselves and provides an opportunity for relaxation."</p><br/><p>💡 Explanation: Using appropriate linking words and correct grammar helps to maintain a consistent, more formal tone, making your writing clearer and more suitable for a general audience.</p>	<p>You organized your ideas into paragraphs, which helps the reader follow your main points, but the connections between ideas could be stronger. Your introductory paragraph could have a clearer thesis statement, and while you used some linking words, a wider variety would improve cohesion.</p><br/><p>👉 To Improve: Strengthen your thesis statement in the introduction. Use a wider variety of linking words and phrases to show clearer relationships between your ideas (e.g., however, therefore, as a result, in addition).</p><br/><p>❌ Weakness: You wrote: "To summarize, there are a lot of activities that teens like to do. Activities that I choose is a interesting one that easy to do." This conclusion feels a bit simple and doesn't fully restate your main ideas in a sophisticated way.</p><br/><p>✨ Improved: "In conclusion, despite the demanding academic schedules, free time activities such as hanging out with friends and watching series remain incredibly popular among Thai teenagers, offering essential opportunities for social connection and personal relaxation."</p><br/><p>💡 Explanation: A stronger concluding paragraph effectively summarises your main points and reinforces your thesis, providing a more satisfying close to your essay.</p>	<p>You used some good vocabulary related to the topic, but there are several recurring grammar patterns that affect clarity and the overall quality of your writing. Issues with capitalization, spelling, and verb forms are noticeable.</p><br/><p>❌ Problem: You wrote: "Free time is really important for Teens in Thailand. Because it allows us to focus about themself and it makes us relax."</p><br/><p>✨ Correction: "Free time is really important for teens in Thailand because it allows them to focus on themselves and it helps them relax."</p><br/><p>💡 Explanation: In English, sentences typically begin with a capital letter, but common nouns like 'teens' are not capitalised unless they start a sentence. Also, 'themself' is not a standard English pronoun; 'themselves' should be used when referring to 'teens'.</p><br/><p>❌ Problem: You wrote: "If they have an extra class, it would take more time. Maybe 10-12 hours in a day."</p><br/><p>✨ Correction: "If they have an extra class, it would take more time, perhaps 10–12 hours a day."</p><br/><p>💡 Explanation: In English, a comma is often used before a phrase that adds additional, non-essential information, like "perhaps 10-12 hours a day." Additionally, the phrase "in a day" is often simplified to "a day" when talking about duration.</p><br/><p>❌ Problem: You wrote: "Even it will not make you to not lonely, but it can entertaining you if it is a fascinating series or your favorite series."</p><br/><p>✨ Correction: "Even though it might not make you feel less lonely, it can be entertaining if it is a fascinating series or your favourite series."</p><br/><p>💡 Explanation: The structure "Even it will not make you to not lonely" is not grammatically correct. "Even though" is a more appropriate conjunction to introduce a contrasting idea. Also, modal verbs like "can" are followed by the base form of the verb (e.g., "can entertain" or "can be entertaining"), not the -ing form.</p>	4.0	3.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	CA	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
1786	33954	2025-09-13 15:10:55.183522+00	Term 1 Exam Question 2	A lot of articles are on my screen but I'll share mine too. Many pets could be mean a lots for some people such as, living alone or feeling lonely and that is a good idea for them to have a pets they like live with them. But everything might has its advantages and disadvantages so, I will continue with those.<br><br>First, the advantage of having pets are, not feeling lonely if the owner wasn't alone in a house or not. Pets can be friends with the owner who can be with them everytime also when they go outside, they can pick their pets up too. Pets foods aren't very expensive too and the owners can buy it easily. The owners can go adventure with their pets if they want to just pack their foods up.<br><br>Moving on to disadvantage, the problems are when they make a loud noise such as, dogs that bark at strangers or being clumsy and just hit anything maybe nonchalant but still made stuff broken. Also one thing about when those pets hurt someone so the owner might go opologize so fast and that was a headache one.<br><br>For conclusion, having pets is up to people who actually want a pet and it's their dicision to decide to have a pet.	213	14.0	Hello Kaohom C, what a thoughtful and interesting article you've written about pets! It's clear you've put a lot of consideration into the topic, and you've shared some really good points. Your effort to discuss both the good and not-so-good parts of having pets is commendable, and you've shown a great ability to connect with your readers. To make your writing even stronger, a key area to focus on is improving the accuracy of your grammar and the variety of your sentence structures. Keep up the fantastic work; you have a strong foundation, and with a little more attention to detail, your writing will truly flourish.<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✅ When you finish writing, take a moment to carefully read through your work, specifically looking for common grammar mistakes like subject-verb agreement and plural forms.<br>✅ Try to combine shorter sentences using conjunctions like "and," "but," or "because," or by using relative clauses (e.g., "who," "which," "that") to create more flow and sophistication in your writing.<br>✅ Practice using different vocabulary to express similar ideas, so your writing feels fresh and engaging for your readers.	Kaohom C, you've done a really good job of covering all the important parts of the task. You clearly presented both the benefits and challenges of having pets, and your article would certainly give your peers plenty to think about. It’s wonderful that you’ve managed to keep the reader well-informed about the topic. To take your content to the next level and achieve that perfect 5, you could try to develop some of your points with a little more detail or a personal anecdote. For example, when you mention pets not being expensive, you could briefly explain *why* or give an example of a pet that is affordable, or when you talk about adventures, you could suggest what kind of adventures. This adds depth and interest, making your article even more captivating for an online community. 🌟	You've made a really solid effort to write in a way that would appeal to other teenagers, which is exactly what an article for an online community needs! Your tone is appropriate, and you've managed to communicate your ideas clearly. It's great to see you adapting your style for your audience. To get to a 5 here, you might experiment with a wider range of rhetorical devices or more varied sentence openings to really grab and hold your reader's attention from start to finish. For instance, instead of starting a paragraph with "First, the advantage...," you could try something like, "One of the truly wonderful things about having a pet is..." This can make your introduction more engaging and persuasive. 💖	Your article has a clear structure, Kaohom C, with a good introduction, separate paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages, and a conclusion. This makes your writing easy to follow, and your use of basic linking words helps guide the reader through your ideas. However, sometimes within your paragraphs, the connections between your sentences could be a little smoother. To make your organisation even stronger, try to use a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For instance, instead of just using "also," you could use "in addition," "furthermore," or "moreover." Think about how each sentence relates to the one before it and the main idea of the paragraph. Thai learners sometimes focus on getting their ideas down, and then forget to use those little words and phrases that really tie everything together for the reader. 🔗	You've used everyday vocabulary well, and your general meaning is understandable, which is a great start. However, I noticed several grammatical errors that sometimes make your writing a bit difficult to follow. For example, phrases like "Many pets could be mean a lots for some people" or "everything might has its advantages" contain mistakes with plurals, articles, and verb forms. To improve your language score, focus on getting these basic grammatical structures right. For instance, "a lots" should be "a lot," and "might has" should be "might have." Another area to work on is sentence structure; try to vary the way you start your sentences and combine some of your shorter sentences to create more sophisticated expressions. Thai learners often struggle with English articles (a/an/the) and subject-verb agreement because these concepts work differently in their first language, so practising these areas will be very beneficial for you. Keep an eye out for these patterns when you review your work. 📝	4.0	4.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
