n help your writing flow beautifully.	You have done a good job of addressing the task by discussing both the joys and challenges of pet ownership. It is lovely how you shared your personal experiences with your cats, making the article feel very authentic and personal. 🥰 To make your content even stronger, try to develop both the advantages and disadvantages equally. For example, you could expand a bit more on the specific difficulties of owning a pet, perhaps by giving another quick example of when a pet might be 'naughty' or how much time it takes to care for them. Thai learners sometimes focus more on positive aspects, so remember to give equal weight to all parts of the question.	You have started to use some of the features of an article well, like sharing your opinions and using personal examples, which makes your writing feel friendly. 😊 To hold your reader's attention even more, think about how you introduce your ideas at the beginning of each section. For instance, instead of just stating an advantage, you could start a sentence with 'One wonderful thing about having pets is...' This helps to guide your reader through your thoughts. Your reader will enjoy your article even more if your ideas are clearly presented.	Your ideas are connected, and it is clear you have a main point you want to convey about pets. However, your article would be much easier to read if you used paragraph breaks. 📝 Imagine each new main idea or shift in your discussion as a fresh paragraph; it gives your reader a visual break and helps them follow your thoughts. For instance, you could have a paragraph for the advantages, and then a separate paragraph for the disadvantages. Also, try to use more linking words to connect your sentences smoothly. Words like 'firstly,' 'next,' or 'in conclusion' can help your ideas flow together. Thai learners sometimes omit these linking words, assuming the connection is obvious.	You use everyday words that are suitable for your topic, which is good. We can understand what you are trying to say. However, there are a few grammar points that we can work on to make your writing more accurate and clear. For example, remember that 'pets' is plural, so we say 'pets are a good idea,' not 'pets is a good idea.' Also, when you talk about 'having' something, it is often 'if you have your own pets' rather than 'if you having your own pets.' Another common point to watch out for is using 'a' or 'an' correctly before nouns. For instance, it's 'a good idea,' not 'good idea.' Many Thai learners find these small grammar points tricky, especially with articles and subject-verb agreement, but with practice, you will master them. 💪	3.0	3.0	2.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
1690	34061	2025-09-12 11:01:04.547464+00	Term 1 Exam Question 1	In my point of view, I think the people who has more effect on the choices that teenagers make is their parents. Because parents need to teach and take care the teenagers. such as when teenagers did something wrong, parents will tell them or teach them to do the right things. And three things that parents will have effect on the choices teenagers make are studying, clothes and fashion, and hobby.<br><br><br>First, studying is the important thing that teenagers need to do. because if teenagers don't study, the parents will get mad to teenagers and everyone will blame parents like why they didn't teach them or tell them to study.<br><br><br>Then, clothes and fashion can effect to the other people if they choose the wrong choices. because when teenager look like the bad person, other people can blame the parents too. so make sure you choose the right choices.<br><br><br>Finally, hobby is the staple things to represent the personallity	156	10.0	Great effort, Vegas! Your essay clearly shows you can express your thoughts, and you did a good job of covering all the points from the question. While there are some areas where your writing could be clearer and more formal, you've laid a solid foundation. Keep working on making your sentences flow smoothly and choosing just the right words, and you'll really see your writing flourish! ✨<br><br>📋 Action Plan<br>✔️ Read More: Try reading articles or short essays in English on topics you find interesting. Pay attention to how the authors connect their ideas and what kind of vocabulary they use.<br>✔️ Check Your Grammar: When you finish writing, read through your work carefully. Look for things like whether your verbs match your subjects (e.g., "people who *have*" instead of "people who *has*") and if you're using the correct prepositions (e.g., "angry *with* someone" or "an effect *on* something").<br>✔️ Plan Your Conclusion: Before you start writing, quickly jot down a sentence or two for a conclusion. This helps you remember to wrap up your thoughts neatly and make your essay feel complete.	You did a good job making sure you talked about clothes and fashion, studying, and your own idea, which was hobbies! It's great that you clearly stated your opinion that parents have a big effect on teenagers' choices. To make this part even stronger, try to give more detailed reasons or examples for each point. For instance, instead of just saying "parents will get mad," you could explain *how* parents' encouragement or expectations influence a teenager's study habits. Think about adding a bit more 'flesh' to your ideas to make your arguments more persuasive and interesting. 🧠	You clearly communicated your main message, which is fantastic! However, essays for the FIRST exam usually need a more formal style, a bit like writing for a school newspaper or a formal report. Sometimes your language felt a little too casual for this kind of writing. For example, phrases like "get mad to teenagers" or "look like the bad person" could be rephrased to sound more academic, such as "parents may become upset with teenagers" or "if a teenager presents an undesirable image." Also, try to avoid giving direct advice to the reader in your essay, as the purpose is to discuss and argue, not to instruct. Many Thai learners sometimes struggle with choosing the right level of formality in English, as direct translation from Thai can sometimes lead to expressions that sound informal in English. 💬	Your essay had a clear beginning, and you used 'First', 'Then', and 'Finally' to help the reader follow your ideas from one paragraph to the next, which is a very good start! To improve even further, remember to add a proper concluding paragraph at the end. This is where you can briefly summarise your main points and restate your opinion in a new way, which helps to give your essay a strong and complete finish. Think of your essay like a journey; the conclusion is like coming back to where you started, but with a new understanding! 🗺️	It's clear that you can get your ideas across, but there are some areas where we can make your language more precise and accurate. Let's look at a few examples: you wrote "people who has more effect" – remember that "people" is plural, so it should be "people who *have* more effect." Also, with prepositions, instead of "take care the teenagers," we say "take care *of* the teenagers," and "effect *on* the choices" rather than "effect *to* the choices." Finally, "important thing" needs an article, so "the important thing." These small changes make a big difference to how clear and correct your writing sounds. Many Thai learners often find prepositions and subject-verb agreement tricky because the rules are different from Thai grammar, but with practice, you'll get the hang of it! Keep an eye on your spelling too, like "personallity" should be "personality." Practice makes perfect! ✍️	3.0	2.0	3.0	2.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
