h more complete and effective! ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan:**<br>   ✅ When you start writing, make sure your first sentence clearly states your main argument or answer to the essay question.<br>   ✅ After writing a sentence, read it aloud to yourself. If you find yourself taking a very long breath or getting lost, it's a good sign that you might need to break it into two or three shorter sentences and add some punctuation like full stops or commas.<br>   ✅ Before you submit your writing, reread the essay question and then reread your essay to check if you have directly answered every part of the question.	Anu, you did a really good job of providing lots of positive points about having pets, like how they can help with mental health and create lovely memories. 🥰 You included many relevant ideas for the advantages part of the task. However, the question asked you to discuss both the *advantages and disadvantages* of having pets. It's like only telling half of a story, which means the reader doesn't get the full picture. Next time, try to brainstorm ideas for both sides of the argument before you start writing. For example, you could jot down "Advantages: mental health, free time, memories" and "Disadvantages: cost, responsibility, time commitment." This way, you make sure to cover all the points the question asks for. Remember, addressing every part of the task is super important for getting a higher score. It's a common thing for students to focus on what they feel most strongly about, but for an exam, we need to show we can tackle all aspects of the prompt.	You've made a good effort to write your response as an article, Anu, which is what the task required. You used a clear introduction, separate paragraphs for each main point, and a conclusion, all of which help to guide your reader through your ideas. 👍 Your writing is quite clear, and your points about pets are easy to follow. To make your article even more engaging and to reach a higher score, think about how you can make your ideas sound a little more natural and persuasive for an online audience. For instance, instead of just stating "Pets are good," you could try a more descriptive ending like, "Clearly, the joy and companionship pets bring make them a truly special part of our lives." This helps to hold the reader's attention and communicate your message more effectively. Sometimes, writers from Thailand focus more on getting their message across directly, and less on the subtle ways to make their writing sound more natural for a native English speaker.	Your article is generally very well organised, Anu, which makes it easy for the reader to follow your thoughts. You did a great job using clear paragraphs for each of your points, which helps to separate your ideas nicely. 📚 I also noticed you used some excellent linking words like "First," "Another," "Lastly," and "In conclusion" to connect your ideas smoothly. This shows you have a good grasp of how to structure your writing. To push towards a perfect score here, you could try to make your transitions between paragraphs even more varied. For example, instead of just "Another amazing advantage," you could try phrases like "Beyond their support for mental well-being, pets also offer fantastic opportunities for our leisure time." This adds a little more flair and sophistication to your connections. Keep up the good work on making your writing flow so well.	Anu, you've used a good variety of everyday words to talk about pets, and your sentences are generally clear enough for the reader to understand your main points. 😊 That's a solid foundation. However, there are a few areas where we can tidy up your language to make it even better. For instance, you wrote "releive" when it should be "relieve" (like "re**lie**f"), and "freetime" should be two words, "free time." Also, some phrases could be more natural, such as "Pets help develop yourself" which could be rephrased to "Pets help you develop as a person" or "Pets help you grow." A good tip is to read your work aloud; sometimes, you can hear these little slips more easily. Thai learners often focus on the core meaning, which is great, but sometimes miss small spelling details or natural-sounding phrases that native speakers use without thinking. Let's work on spotting these little errors so your writing becomes even more precise and polished. ✨	2.0	3.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B2	2026
