w sentence.	Dragon, you did a really fine job of responding to almost all of Corey's points in the email. You clearly told Corey you preferred being picked up, explained why, shared your choice for building a robot, and even suggested bringing water bottles with a good reason. 👍 Your reader was certainly informed about your preferences and suggestions. To get that top score of 5, make sure that when you acknowledge something, it's crystal clear what you're acknowledging. For example, your "That sounds great!" was a positive start, but it would be even better if you added "about the tickets!" to make it perfectly clear what you were excited about. Sometimes, Thai learners might assume the context is understood, but in English writing, being explicit from the beginning helps a lot.	You've shown a good grasp of writing an informal email to a friend, Dragon, and your meaning was always clear despite some small errors. Your vocabulary was appropriate for the topic, and you used simple sentences effectively. For instance, you said "I would be so dehydrated," which is good everyday language. However, there are a few areas where a little more precision with grammar would elevate your writing. Phrases like "I rather want" should be "I'd rather" or "I would prefer." Also, "water battle" should be "water bottle." 🤔 It's also worth looking at sentences that become quite long, such as the one about your brother and his car accident. Sometimes breaking these into two simpler sentences can make them much clearer and reduce the chance of small grammatical errors. For example, "My brother borrowed my car to go to work. He got into an accident which caused a big mess to his car." Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai sentence structures, which can lead to these longer, more complex English sentences. Practising simple sentence structures will help you gain even greater control over your grammar.	Your email was structured in a very logical way, Dragon, making it easy to follow your thoughts. You moved smoothly from one point to the next, just as Corey's email prompted you to. Using phrases like "For the main activity" helped guide the reader through your response. Your use of separate paragraphs for different ideas also helped your email feel coherent. 👏 To reach that excellent score of 5, consider using a wider variety of linking words or phrases to connect your sentences even more smoothly. For example, when you explain your reason for building a robot, you could say "Furthermore, I've never built a robot before..." or "In addition, I've never built a robot before..." This adds a bit more sophistication to how your ideas flow together.	You've used everyday language quite well in your email, Dragon, and your meaning was always clear despite some small errors. Your vocabulary was appropriate for the topic, and you used simple sentences effectively. For instance, you said "I would be so dehydrated," which is good everyday language. However, there are a few areas where a little more precision with grammar would elevate your writing. Phrases like "I rather want" should be "I'd rather" or "I would prefer." Also, "water battle" should be "water bottle." 🤔 It's also worth looking at sentences that become quite long, such as the one about your brother and his car accident. Sometimes breaking these into two simpler sentences can make them much clearer and reduce the chance of small grammatical errors. For example, "My brother borrowed my car to go to work. He got into an accident which caused a big mess to his car." Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai sentence structures, which can lead to these longer, more complex English sentences. Practising simple sentence structures will help you gain even greater control over your grammar.	4.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
