 so important for this kind of task. 👍 For instance, you clearly showed your excitement about the tickets, told Corey exactly where you wanted to meet, and explained why you'd prefer to build a robot rather than watch a film. You also gave some great suggestions for things to bring. To get that perfect score of 5, make sure you stick precisely to the number of suggestions asked for in the task, even if you have lots of good ideas, as the exam sometimes asks for a specific number. Keep up the great work here.	You've absolutely nailed the way you've written this email, Baimhon. It feels just like a friendly chat with Corey, which is exactly what you want for an informal email. 🥳 You started and ended your email in a lovely, natural way, and your message is easy to follow from beginning to end. This really helps to keep the reader interested and makes your communication very successful. You clearly understand how to write effectively for your audience.	Your ideas are all there, and they are generally connected, which is a good start. However, sometimes your sentences become quite long with many different ideas joined together. 🤔 For example, when you wrote about bringing phones, hats, and water, you put it all in one very long sentence. To make your writing even clearer, try to separate these ideas into shorter sentences or use linking words like "and," "also," or "in addition" more effectively to connect them. Thai learners sometimes create very long sentences that, in English, can make it a little harder for the reader to follow all the great points you want to make.	You've used some good everyday vocabulary, and your meaning usually comes across, which is important. However, there are a few areas in grammar and word choice that we can work on to make your writing more precise and natural. 🧐 For example, you wrote "alots activity" when it should be "a lot of activities" (remember those plurals!). Another example is "it might be bored for us." In English, when somethi