ord limit to ensure you can stay focused on the most important details during an exam.	When you wrote 'she yelled "I won!"' and used exclamation marks, you really brought the character's excitement to life for the reader. This use of direct speech acts like a spark that makes the scene feel immediate and engaging. To reach a perfect score, try to keep your tenses consistent, making sure the whole story stays in the past tense rather than jumping into the present.	I see that you included clear transitions like 'at first' and 'later on', which act like a map for the reader to follow the timeline of Lois's life. This logical flow makes it very easy to understand how her situation changed over time. To make your writing even smoother, you could use a few more 'joining' words like 'although' or 'however' to connect your sentences together.	Your use of 'she suddenly felt remorse by her wasteful thought' shows a fantastic range of vocabulary for this level, as words like 'remorse' and 'wasteful' add real emotion to your writing. While these strong words help the reader understand the meaning, there are several small slips with basic grammar, such as 'she have' instead of 'she had'. My tip is to spend a few minutes at the end checking that your subjects and verbs match up correctly.	5.0	4.0	4.0	3.0	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	\N	\N	\N	B1+	2026
