uctions. Also, when you mentioned your backpack and umbrella, the task asked for a suggestion for *them* to bring, not just for yourself. And asking for a ride with Corey's cousin wasn't part of the task either. Making sure you cover every instruction clearly and precisely will help you improve your score in this area.	You started your email with "Hi" and ended with "Bye, Dee," which are good ways to begin and finish an informal email 👋. You also communicated some simple ideas, like your excitement for the festival. However, because you went against a major instruction (about the film), and included a personal request that wasn't part of the task, your email didn't fully achieve its main purpose. For your communication to be most effective, try to stick strictly to the task's instructions and ensure all your points are directly related to the email's purpose.	You've connected your ideas using words like "because," which is good for showing reasons. However, presenting your entire email as one long paragraph makes it quite difficult for the reader to follow all your different thoughts 🧐. Breaking your email into shorter paragraphs, where each paragraph talks about one main idea (like one for the tickets, one for meeting, one for the activities, and one for what to bring), would make your writing much clearer and easier for Corey to read.	You've used some good everyday words that are appropriate for the topic, like "science festival," "front entrance," and "umbrella" ☂️. However, there were frequent errors in almost every sentence that made your message quite challenging to read and understand at times. A big area to focus on is capitalisation; remember to always use a capital "I" when you're talking about yourself, and start every new sentence with a capital letter. For example, instead of "it great! thank for in a vite me i really excited," it should be "It *is* great! *Thank* you for *inviting* me, *I* *am* really excited." Also, "how can dinosaur live" should be "