ed you're thinking realistically about the plans. It’s like you’ve thought through everything and are ready for a fun day out. Fantastic work!	Your email is super friendly and definitely sounds like you're writing to a friend, which is perfect for this task. The way you started with "Hi" and finished with "Bye, Praewa" sets just the right tone, and you've broken your ideas into paragraphs, making it very easy for Corey to read and understand. You're clearly able to communicate your ideas effectively, and your message is easy to follow, holding the reader's attention throughout! You've used the conventions of an email to a friend effectively to get your message across.	You've organised your email really well, Praewa. Your paragraphs help to separate your different ideas, which makes your email clear and logical. For instance, you talk about the tickets, then the meeting point, then the activities, and finally what to bring. This makes your message easy for Corey to follow. You're already using some linking words to connect your thoughts. To aim for a score of 5, you could try to introduce slightly more varied linking words to guide the reader even more smoothly from one idea to the next. Thinking about how one sentence leads into the next will help your writing flow beautifully.	You've used a good range of everyday words that are just right for talking about the science festival, like "front entrance" and "keep a memory." You're also doing well with your sentence structures. While there are a few little slips, like saying "It's a great news" instead of "It's great news" (because "news" is uncountable in English, like "water" or "information"), or "we never build it before" instead of "we haven't built it before," these don't stop Corey from understanding your message. To polish your language even further, you could pay close attention to singular and plural forms, and also think about verb tenses, especially when talking about past experiences or things that haven't 