<br>*   Check that you've addressed every part of the task question before you start writing. For example, if it asks for both good and bad points, make sure to include both.<br>*   After writing, read through your work and try to spot any words or phrases you've used a few times. Can you think of different ways to express the same idea?<br>*   Practice making sure your subjects and verbs agree. For instance, if you're talking about "pets" (plural), you'd say "pets help," not "pets is help."	You did a good job of presenting several advantages of having pets, like how they can be friends and help us relax. That's a strong start. 👍 However, the task specifically asked for both the advantages *and* disadvantages of pet ownership. Because you only focused on the positive aspects, the reader isn't fully informed about the whole picture. Next time, before you begin writing, quickly jot down a few points for both sides of the argument to make sure you cover everything the question asks for. For example, you could have thought about things like the cost of food, vet bills, or the time commitment involved. Sometimes, we get so excited to write about what we know that we forget to step back and look at the entire prompt.	You've managed to communicate your straightforward ideas about pets quite clearly, which is commendable. It's easy to understand what you're trying to say. 😊 To make your article more engaging for an online publication, think about how you can grab the reader's attention right from the start and keep them interested throughout. For instance, you could begin with a question or a surprising statement about pets. While your ideas are expressed simply, try to use a bit more variety in your sentence structures to create a more dynamic flow, rather than just stating facts.	Your article has a clear structure, which is a big plus. You've got an introduction, separate paragraphs for each of your points, and a conclusion, which helps guide the reader through your ideas. 💡 You also used some basic linking words like "First," "Secondly," and "Lastly," which is a good habit. To make your writing even more cohesive, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. Instead of just "And," you could use "Furthermore" or "Moreover" to connect your ideas. Also, aim to vary your sentence beginnings so that each paragraph feels fresh and distinct. Thai learners sometimes use simpler linking words, so practising a wider range can really make a difference.	You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, and your meaning is generally clear despite some small errors. That's a solid effort. Your command of simple grammatical forms is good, and you're getting your message across. However, there are a few areas where we can tidy things up to make your writing even clearer. For example, with subject-verb agreement, remember that "pets" (plural) takes "help" (no 's'), not "helps," so you would say "pets help us to feel relaxed." 📚 Also, pay close attention to articles like "a," "an," and "the." For instance, instead of "at less one pet," we would say "at least one pet," and "the most of all families" could be "most families" or "the majority of families." These small corrections will make a big impact on the overall accuracy of your writing. Many learners of English find articles and subject-verb agreement tricky because these rules can be quite different from their first language, so you're not alone. Keep practicing, and you'll master them.	2.0	2.0	2.0	3.0	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	B1	2026
