29799	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. "What is that" his voice shaken, amazed by such a magical beaches that when he finally looked around, everyone is gone. He surrounded by quiet and emthyness inside the plane that once full of people but then gone in a blink of an eye. The plane stopped on the beaches, Lou called out and shouting at captain door, No respond. He give up and got out of the plane, admiring the beaches, and griefting about not be able to go home. A lively sound of people getting louder, He turned behide and similar stangers walking toward him, They chatting and walk around the beaches like it's a normal day. Lou so confuse so he sit down and think about everything today. A stanger sat down beside him, and said "Lou, You have to go back! You aren't Our lou"", He wake up in a lively plane, He looked around and see that those stanger in the dream might be the same one on this plane. He look at the stanger next to him that sit with him the whole flight, "How was your sleep?, Lou" the stanger asked nervously looking down at the floor, "i think this is your passaport" the stanger said. How will Lou and this stanger story end? Nobody knows, because this is the end of the essay.	237	16.0	Ploy, your story is genuinely gripping. You took the opening sentence and built a complete world around it — a plane that empties mysteriously, a dream that blurs into reality, and a stranger who might know more than they let on. The moment when the stranger says <em>You aren't Our lou</em> is the kind of twist that makes a reader stop and reread. That's real storytelling instinct. To push even further, you can work on making every sentence as polished as your best ones. When the grammar is as strong as your imagination, your writing will be unstoppable. Let's look at each skill area now.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> You started with the given sentence and immediately created a vivid setting. The reader can picture the scene.</li>\n  <li><em>He surrounded by quiet and emthyness inside the plane that once full of people but then gone in a blink of an eye.</em> The contrast between a full plane and an empty one builds suspense effectively. The reader wants to know what happened.</li>\n  <li><em>How will Lou and this stanger story end? Nobody knows, because this is the end of the essay.</em> You ended with a playful, self-aware line that matches the mysterious tone of the whole story. It's clever and original.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer tells a story where every sentence feels natural and intentional, with no awkward phrasing that pulls the reader out of the world. You have a strong sense of suspense and pacing, so let's clean up a few spots where the language doesn't quite match the story's quality.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>griefting about not be able to go home</em> → <strong>grieving that he could not go home</strong>. 'Griefting' isn't a word, and 'about not be able' needs the full structure 'about not being able' or 'that he could not'.</li>\n  <li><em>He turned behide and similar stangers walking toward him</em> → <strong>He turned behind him and saw strangers who looked familiar walking toward him</strong>. 'Behide' is a spelling error, and 'similar stangers' is unclear — do you mean they looked like people he knew?</li>\n  <li><em>Lou so confuse so he sit down</em> → <strong>Lou was so confused that he sat down</strong>. The verb 'to be' is missing, and the past tense of 'sit' is 'sat'.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words and time expressions to guide the reader smoothly through the story. Your sequence of events is clear, and your paragraph breaks help, but you can add more variety to your transitions.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>when he finally looked around, everyone is gone</em> → <strong>when he finally looked around, everyone was gone</strong>. The shift from past to present tense here breaks the flow. Keep the tense consistent to help the reader stay in the story.</li>\n  <li><em>A lively sound of people getting louder, He turned behide</em> → <strong>Suddenly, a lively sound grew louder. He turned around</strong>. Adding 'suddenly' creates a stronger transition between the quiet emptiness and the new sound.</li>\n  <li><em>He wake up in a lively plane</em> → <strong>Then he woke up in a lively plane</strong>. Adding 'then' or 'suddenly' at this turning point would make the shift from dream to reality clearer.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer controls past tense consistently and uses correct verb forms, subject-verb agreement, and spelling throughout. Your vocabulary is good — words like <em>magical beaches</em> and <em>blink of an eye</em> show imagination. The main pattern holding you back is that you mix up the simple past and past continuous, and you sometimes forget the verb 'to be'. Let's fix that.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>amazed by such a magical beaches</em> → <strong>amazed by such magical beaches</strong>. 'Beaches' is plural, so 'a' doesn't belong here. Also, check spelling: 'beaches' not 'beaches'.</li>\n  <li><em>He surrounded by quiet and emthyness</em> → <strong>He was surrounded by quiet and emptiness</strong>. You need the verb 'was' here. 'Emthyness' should be 'emptiness'.</li>\n  <li><em>Lou so confuse so he sit down</em> → <strong>Lou was so confused that he sat down</strong>. You need 'was' before 'so confuse', and 'confuse' should be 'confused' (adjective). 'Sit' in past tense is 'sat'.</li>\n  <li><em>the stanger asked nervously looking down at the floor</em> → <strong>the stranger asked nervously, looking down at the floor</strong>. 'Stanger' should be 'stranger'. Also, 'passaport' should be 'passport'.</li>\n  <li><em>i think this is your passaport</em> → <strong>I think this is your passport</strong>. The pronoun 'I' must always be capitalised. This is a small but important rule for formal writing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check for next time: Read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Is the verb in the past tense? Did I include the verb 'to be' where needed? Are all my capital letters correct?'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29866	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I think I prefer Action flims because it was fun it make my heart beat quickly . I will fell very excited and I'll have fun with it like I'm in that inside the flims.<br><br>I prefer to watch movie at the cinema because the size of the TV was different in the cinema it was very big and if it is a action movie your chair will move follow the sound making it more realistic than watching at home. It have popcorn and refil water that you can buy it right there but it's bad that you can't paused while you're going to toilet and it have only one toilet in the cinema so it's hard and far to go it take many time the cinema always cold so if you wanted to watch the movie at the cinema don't forget to bring your jacket , hodd or blanhet.	157	15.0	Mawin, your article is genuinely exciting to read. You chose action films and made the reader feel the energy with phrases like <em>my heart beat quickly</em> and <em>like I'm in that inside the flims</em>. That personal connection is exactly what makes an article engaging. You also did a great job comparing the cinema experience to watching at home, giving specific reasons like the big screen, moving chairs, and the popcorn. To reach Band 5 across all areas, your next draft needs tighter organisation and stronger grammar control. Let's look at each part of your writing.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I think I prefer Action flims because it was fun it make my heart beat quickly .</em> You clearly stated your favourite genre and gave a personal, physical reason. The reader understands exactly why action films appeal to you.</li><li><em>if it is a action movie your chair will move follow the sound making it more realistic than watching at home.</em> You described a specific feature of the cinema (moving chairs) and connected it to the feeling of realism. This is detailed and convincing.</li><li><em>It have popcorn and refil water that you can buy it right there but it's bad that you can't paused while you're going to toilet</em> You balanced the advantages and disadvantages of the cinema, which shows you thought about both sides of the comparison.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the article focused and engaging without including details that distract the reader. Your writing is lively and personal, but the paragraph about the cinema includes a few extra points that slow down the energy.</p><ul><li><em>it have only one toilet in the cinema so it's hard and far to go it take many time</em> → <strong>the cinema only has one toilet, so it's far away and takes a long time to reach</strong>. This detail is relevant, but the repetition of 'it' and the run-on sentence make it feel less polished. Try combining the toilet point with the jacket point in one short sentence: 'The cinema is cold and has only one toilet, so bring a jacket.'</li><li><em>the cinema always cold so if you wanted to watch the movie at the cinema don't forget to bring your jacket , hodd or blanhet</em> → <strong>the cinema is always cold, so remember to bring a jacket, hoodie, or blanket</strong>. This advice is good, but it comes at the end of a long list of points. Try placing it earlier or cutting the list of items to just 'jacket or blanket' to keep the focus on your main comparison.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraph breaks and smooth transitions between ideas. You have two paragraphs, which is a good start, but the second paragraph lists advantages and disadvantages without clear signals for the reader.</p><ul><li><em>I prefer to watch movie at the cinema because the size of the TV was different in the cinema it was very big and if it is a action movie your chair will move follow the sound making it more realistic than watching at home.</em> → <strong>I prefer to watch movies at the cinema because the screen is huge, and the chairs move with the sound. This makes action films feel more realistic.</strong> Break this long sentence into two. Start with the advantage, then add 'However,' before introducing the disadvantages.</li><li><em>It have popcorn and refil water that you can buy it right there but it's bad that you can't paused while you're going to toilet and it have only one toilet in the cinema so it's hard and far to go it take many time</em> → <strong>You can buy popcorn and refillable water there. However, you can't pause the movie if you need the toilet, and there is only one toilet, which is far away.</strong> Use 'However' or 'On the other hand' to signal the shift from good points to bad points. This makes your argument much easier to follow.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>use the present simple tense for general facts and preferences, and make sure the subject and verb agree.</strong> You often use past tense or incorrect verb forms when talking about things that are always true.</p><ul><li><em>it make my heart beat quickly</em> → <strong>it makes my heart beat quickly</strong>. 'It' is singular, so the verb needs an '-s': 'makes'. This is a subject-verb agreement error. Every time you write 'it', check if the verb needs an 's'.</li><li><em>I will fell very excited</em> → <strong>I feel very excited</strong>. Use the present simple 'feel' for how you always feel during action films. 'Will fell' mixes future and past. Stick to 'feel' for general preferences.</li><li><em>you can't paused</em> → <strong>you can't pause</strong>. After 'can', use the base form of the verb (no '-ed'). This rule applies to 'can', 'will', 'must', and 'should'.</li><li><em>it have popcorn</em> → <strong>it has popcorn</strong>. Same subject-verb agreement: 'it' needs 'has', not 'have'.</li><li><em>the cinema always cold</em> → <strong>the cinema is always cold</strong>. You need the verb 'is' before 'always cold'. Without 'is', the sentence is missing its main verb.</li><li><em>if you wanted to watch the movie at the cinema don't forget to bring your jacket , hodd or blanhet</em> → <strong>if you want to watch a movie at the cinema, don't forget to bring your jacket, hoodie, or blanket</strong>. Use present tense 'want' for a general condition. Also check your spelling: 'hodd' → 'hoodie', 'blanhet' → 'blanket'.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30055	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. After a short time the plane landed, and Lou walked out to a giant sign "Welcome to Koh Samui". Koh Samui, a major economic island favored by tourist because of its beautiful native and scenery that is hard to beat, situated on the gulf of Thailand. This magnificent island is part of The Surat Thani province. Lou walked outside, a van was waiting for him at the terminal. He got on the van and started his journey to his resort, encountering breathtaking views along the way. Finally, he arrived at the resort, where he would enjoy a week long getaway in samui.	116	17.0	Jomtub, your story about Lou's arrival on Koh Samui is genuinely enjoyable to read. You built a clear, satisfying narrative arc from the plane landing all the way to the resort, and your descriptive vocabulary — like <em>giant sign</em>, <em>breathtaking views</em>, and <em>golden beaches</em> — brings the scene to life. The reader can picture everything clearly. To push your writing even further, think about weaving background information directly into the action rather than pausing the story to explain. For example, instead of stating that Koh Samui is a major economic island, you could show that through Lou's observations. Let's look at each area where you can strengthen your draft.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> This opening immediately places the reader in the scene and connects to the prompt. Strong start.</li>\n  <li><em>He got on the van and started his journey to his resort, encountering breathtaking views along the way.</em> You move the story forward naturally, and the reader understands exactly what happens between the airport and the resort.</li>\n  <li><em>Finally, he arrived at the resort, where he would enjoy a week long getaway in samui.</em> A clear ending that completes the journey. The target reader is fully informed about Lou's entire arrival experience.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the narrative flowing smoothly without breaking the reader's immersion. Your story is engaging and well-paced, but the sentence about Koh Samui's economy and geography interrupts the action. Here is how to integrate that information more naturally.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Koh Samui, a major economic island favored by tourist because of its beautiful native and scenery that is hard to beat, situated on the gulf of Thailand. This magnificent island is part of The Surat Thani province.</em> → <strong>Lou stepped off the plane and immediately understood why Koh Samui was famous — tourists crowded the terminal, drawn by beaches that postcards could not capture.</strong> This keeps Lou as the main character and shows the island's appeal through his eyes rather than listing facts.</li>\n  <li><em>Lou walked outside, a van was waiting for him at the terminal.</em> → <strong>When Lou walked outside, a van was waiting for him at the terminal.</strong> Adding <em>When</em> at the start connects this sentence to the previous action and keeps the story moving without a pause.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every sentence so the story flows without any bumpy transitions. Your sequence of events is logical, and you use good linking words like <em>After a short time</em> and <em>Finally</em>. The main issue is that the factual sentence about Koh Samui feels dropped into the middle of the narrative rather than connected to what comes before and after.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>After a short time the plane landed, and Lou walked out to a giant sign "Welcome to Koh Samui". Koh Samui, a major economic island favored by tourist...</em> → <strong>After a short time the plane landed, and Lou walked out to a giant sign "Welcome to Koh Samui." The island was busier than he expected — a major economic hub where tourists came for beaches that postcards could not capture.</strong> This version keeps the description attached to Lou's experience, so the paragraph stays unified.</li>\n  <li><em>He got on the van and started his journey to his resort, encountering breathtaking views along the way. Finally, he arrived at the resort...</em> These two sentences connect well. The word <em>Finally</em> signals the end of the journey clearly.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammar with very few errors. Your vocabulary is strong — <em>breathtaking views</em>, <em>giant sign</em>, <em>getaway</em> — and your meaning is always clear. The main pattern that needs attention is noun agreement and article use with countable nouns. Let's work through your specific examples.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>favored by tourist</em> → <strong>favored by tourists</strong>. The word <em>tourist</em> is a countable noun. When you mean tourists in general, use the plural form. Think: one tourist, two tourists, tourists in general.</li>\n  <li><em>beautiful native and scenery</em> → <strong>beautiful nature and scenery</strong>. The word <em>native</em> is an adjective, not a noun meaning 'nature'. You likely meant <em>nature</em>, which fits perfectly with <em>scenery</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>a week long getaway in samui</em> → <strong>a week-long getaway in Samui</strong>. Two small fixes: add a hyphen in <em>week-long</em> when it acts as an adjective before a noun, and capitalise <em>Samui</em> because it is a proper noun (the name of an island).</li>\n  <li><em>part of The Surat Thani province</em> → <strong>part of Surat Thani province</strong>. The word <em>The</em> should not be capitalised in the middle of a sentence unless it is part of an official name. Here, just write <em>Surat Thani province</em>.</li>\n  <li>One more capitalisation check: <em>the gulf of Thailand</em> → <strong>the Gulf of Thailand</strong>. Geographic names like this are proper nouns and need capital letters on the main words.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30082	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. The enormous landscape that fully cover with life and the gigantic shallow of the plane flew across the sky. Well, that used to be the first time when I finally descent to Thailand. Firstly, I'm the big fans of travelling and our desination is Phuket. The first thing I've noticed is people here. They're always smile and cheerful with others people and foods here is extremly delicious, the flavour of sour and sweet is perfectly intercept. Foods here is easy to find and it also surprisingly cheap, so that was the best trip ever!	111	9.0	Porsch, you started your story with the given sentence exactly as required, and you included some vivid descriptions of the island and the food. Phrases like <em>the golden beaches of the island below</em> and <em>the flavour of sour and sweet is perfectly intercept</em> show that you are trying to create a picture for the reader. To reach Band 5, your story needs to stay consistent from beginning to end. Right now, it begins with Lou seeing the island, but then it switches to <em>I</em> and becomes a personal travel diary. A story should follow one character and have a clear beginning, middle, and end. Let's look at each part of your writing to see exactly what to improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer keeps the same character and builds a complete story with a beginning, middle, and end. Your story starts with Lou, but then it becomes about your own trip to Phuket, which confuses the reader.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> → This is a strong opening. Keep Lou as the main character. Instead of switching to <em>I</em>, continue with Lou: <strong>Lou saw the golden beaches below and felt a rush of excitement. This was his first time in Thailand.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>Well, that used to be the first time when I finally descent to Thailand.</em> → This shifts the story to you. A Band 5 writer would stay with Lou: <strong>It was Lou's first time in Thailand, and he could not wait to explore.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>Foods here is easy to find and it also surprisingly cheap, so that was the best trip ever!</em> → This sounds like a diary entry. A story needs an event or a small problem. For example: <strong>Lou tried the local food, which was sour and sweet, and he smiled at how friendly everyone was. He knew this would be a trip he would never forget.</strong></li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent register and keeps the same point of view throughout the story. Your story starts in third person (Lou) and then moves to first person (I), which makes it hard for the reader to follow.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> → This is a good story opening in third person. Keep this point of view: <strong>Lou saw the golden beaches and felt excited. He had never seen anything so beautiful.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>Well, that used to be the first time when I finally descent to Thailand.</em> → The shift to <em>I</em> is confusing. A Band 5 writer would stay with Lou: <strong>It was Lou's first time in Thailand, and everything felt new and exciting.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>Firstly, I'm the big fans of travelling and our desination is Phuket.</em> → This sounds like a travel blog, not a story. To stay in story mode, write: <strong>Lou loved travelling, and his destination was Phuket.</strong></li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the story flows from one sentence to the next. Your story uses <em>Firstly</em> and <em>The first thing</em>, but the jump from Lou to <em>I</em> makes the sequence feel broken.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. The enormous landscape that fully cover with life...</em> → The second sentence does not follow naturally from the first. A Band 5 writer would connect them: <strong>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. The enormous landscape was covered with life, and he could see the shallow water as the plane flew across the sky.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>Well, that used to be the first time when I finally descent to Thailand. Firstly, I'm the big fans of travelling...</em> → The word <em>Well</em> and the switch to <em>Firstly</em> feel disconnected. Instead, keep the time sequence clear: <strong>It was Lou's first time in Thailand, and he was a big fan of travelling. The first thing he noticed was the people.</strong></li>\n  <li>You used <em>and</em> and <em>so</em> to connect ideas, which is good. To improve, add time words like <em>then</em>, <em>next</em>, or <em>after that</em> to show the order of events: <strong>After the plane landed, Lou stepped out and felt the warm air. Then he saw the friendly people and tasted the delicious food.</strong></li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling so the reader can follow easily. Your vocabulary is good — words like <em>landscape</em>, <em>destination</em>, <em>cheerful</em>, <em>delicious</em>, <em>flavour</em>, <em>sour</em>, and <em>sweet</em> show a nice range. The main grammar rule that needs attention is subject-verb agreement: the verb must match the subject in number (singular or plural). Let's fix the most common errors.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The enormous landscape that fully cover with life</em> → <strong>The enormous landscape that was fully covered with life</strong>. <em>Landscape</em> is singular, so the verb should be <em>was covered</em>, not <em>cover</em>. Also, you need the passive form here.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm the big fans of travelling</em> → <strong>I'm a big fan of travelling</strong>. <em>Fan</em> is singular because <em>I</em> is one person. Also, use <em>a</em> instead of <em>the</em> when talking about something for the first time.</li>\n  <li><em>our desination is Phuket</em> → <strong>our destination was Phuket</strong>. Check your spelling of <em>destination</em>, and keep the past tense consistent since the story happened in the past.</li>\n  <li><em>They're always smile and cheerful with others people and foods here is extremly delicious</em> → <strong>They always smile and are cheerful with other people, and the food here is extremely delicious</strong>. After <em>are</em>, you need the base form <em>smile</em>, not <em>smile</em> as a noun. <em>Others people</em> should be <em>other people</em>. <em>Foods</em> is usually uncountable — use <em>food</em>. And check the spelling of <em>extremely</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>Foods here is easy to find and it also surprisingly cheap</em> → <strong>Food here is easy to find, and it is also surprisingly cheap</strong>. <em>Food</em> is uncountable, so use <em>is</em> (correct) but drop the <em>s</em>. Add <em>is</em> before <em>surprisingly</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can apply to any sentence: read the subject and the verb together. Ask yourself, 'Is the subject one thing or many things? Does the verb match?' For example, <em>the food</em> (one thing) → <em>is</em>. <em>The people</em> (many things) → <em>are</em>. Practise this with every sentence you write.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30858	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. The sky was grey. Lou looked at his watch and saw It was alread past six, as the plane was landing, Lou saw a strange sight. There were things that looked like people, but something felt off. They had empty eyes and they were sitting in a circle, they wore leaves and animal skin, they were also chanting something in a unusual language. Lou could only spectate from afar not daring to step in, because he wasn't sure if he was considered a guest or supper. Never the less, Lou did find a lot of interesting carvings. Some in latin other's Roman, but they all looked Hundreds of years old, Lou was bewildered. He rushed to find a way home, but it was too late. They found him. After that he just dissapeared, Nobody talked about it after that and moved on with their lives.	161	18.0	Katie, your story is genuinely gripping. You took the prompt and built a complete narrative arc: Lou arrives, sees the strange people, investigates, and then vanishes. The ending <em>After that he just dissapeared, Nobody talked about it after that and moved on with their lives</em> leaves the reader with a haunting feeling, which is exactly what a good story should do. Your vocabulary choices like <em>spectate</em>, <em>carvings</em>, and <em>bewildered</em> show you are reaching for more interesting words. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you will need to tighten your sentence control and check your punctuation. Let me show you what is already working and where a few small changes will make your writing even stronger.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> You set the scene immediately and gave the reader a clear sense of place and time.</li><li><em>They had empty eyes and they were sitting in a circle, they wore leaves and animal skin, they were also chanting something in a unusual language.</em> You described the strange sight with specific, visual details that build suspense.</li><li><em>He rushed to find a way home, but it was too late. They found him. After that he just dissapeared</em> The story has a clear ending that follows logically from the events. The reader understands what happened to Lou.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>he wasn't sure if he was considered a guest or supper</em> This line is excellent. It adds dark humour and shows Lou's fear in a clever, memorable way.</li><li><em>empty eyes</em> and <em>chanting something in a unusual language</em> These vivid phrases create a tense, mysterious atmosphere that keeps the reader engaged.</li><li>The story holds the reader's attention from the first sentence to the last. You have a natural sense of how to build and release tension.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses punctuation to separate ideas clearly so the reader never has to pause and re-read. Your story has a logical sequence, but some sentences run together without proper breaks.</p><ul><li><em>They had empty eyes and they were sitting in a circle, they wore leaves and animal skin, they were also chanting something in a unusual language.</em> → <strong>They had empty eyes. They were sitting in a circle, wearing leaves and animal skin, and chanting something in an unusual language.</strong> Breaking this into shorter sentences makes each image stronger and easier to picture.</li><li><em>Some in latin other's Roman, but they all looked Hundreds of years old, Lou was bewildered.</em> → <strong>Some were in Latin, others in Roman, but they all looked hundreds of years old. Lou was bewildered.</strong> A full stop before <em>Lou was bewildered</em> gives that sentence the weight it deserves.</li><li><em>After that he just dissapeared, Nobody talked about it after that</em> → <strong>After that he just disappeared. Nobody talked about it after that.</strong> A comma cannot join two complete sentences. Use a full stop or a semicolon instead.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls grammar and spelling consistently, even when using more complex structures. Your main area to work on is articles: choosing <em>a</em> or <em>an</em> correctly before a noun.</p><ul><li><em>a unusual language</em> → <strong>an unusual language</strong>. Use <em>an</em> before words that start with a vowel sound. <em>Unusual</em> begins with a vowel sound, so it takes <em>an</em>.</li><li><em>alread past six</em> → <strong>already past six</strong>. <em>Already</em> is one word with a 'y' at the end. This is a common spelling slip, and now you know to check it.</li><li><em>other's Roman</em> → <strong>others in Roman</strong>. <em>Other's</em> means 'belonging to one other person'. You meant <em>others</em> (plural, no apostrophe) because there were multiple carvings.</li><li><em>Hundreds of years old</em> → <strong>hundreds of years old</strong>. <em>Hundreds</em> is not a proper noun, so it does not need a capital letter. Only the first word of a sentence and proper nouns (like Roman) get capitals.</li><li><em>dissapeared</em> → <strong>disappeared</strong>. The correct spelling is <em>disappeared</em> with two 'p's. A good trick: <em>dis</em> + <em>appear</em> + <em>ed</em>.</li></ul><p>Self-check for next time: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Does every noun that needs <em>a</em>, <em>an</em>, or <em>the</em> have one? Are all my common nouns in lowercase?'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34014	2025-08-17 09:40:27.262241+00	2026	Thai Food	Desserts aren't just sweets, they're full of feelings and emotion in each plate. Although every culture has own desserts, each culture stands out for its symbolic tastes and styles. In particular, many cultures have developed their own dessert for hundred of years, they use many kind of feelings to make or create a dessert. Especially, Thai desserts have absorbing iconic for each dessert. Three of more fascinatind desserts in Thailand are Thong Yod, Kanom Chan and Mango with sticky rice.<br><br><br>One of fascinating dessert in Thailand is Thong Yod, a golden drop, made by egg yolks, sugar, jasmine-scented and rice flour. There're 5 steps to make Thong Yod. First, beat the egg yolks until smooth and fluffy. Second, make syrup by boiled sugar with jasmine-water. Third, drop small spoonfuls of egg mixture into the syrup. Then, let them cook until firm and golden. Last, remove and cool it in water before serving. The word "thong" mean golden, which represents the wealth and successful. The rounded-shape symbolize perfection. Thong Yod often served at wedding and blessings ceremonis to wish good luck and a bright future.<br><br><br>A second amazing dessert in Thailand is Khanom Chan, Thai layered dessert that made by Tapioka flour, rice flour, coconut milk, sugar and Pandan juice. First we need to mix all flour, sugar and coconut-milk until smooth. Then, seperated them into bowls. Then, stream them each layer by layer until every layer smooth and streamed. Khanom Chan traditionally made by 9 layers. The layer represent step by step process and growth. Also, number 9 is lucky in Thai culture, symbolizing moving forward in life. Khanom Chan often given on promotions or new beginning to wish success.<br><br><br>A third absorbing dessert in Thailand is Mango with sticky rice. It made from Glutinous rice, Mango, coconut milk, sugar and salt. First, we streamed glutinous rice until it soft. Then, mixed rice with sugar, salt and coconut milk. Last, served with mango slices. Mango with sticky rice represents warmth sharing and harmony.<br><br><br>In conclusion, Thai desserts affect the overall of Thai traditional ceremonies. Moreover, Thai desserts has their own step and ingredients that can be found in Thailand. As a result Thai desserts are full of feelings and emotion, served on plate.	373	\N	You have a good understanding of the essay topic and provided relevant information about Thai desserts. You clearly describe the symbolic and cultural value of each dessert, which was a key requirement. The essay is generally well-structured with clear paragraphs for each dessert. Your main area for improvement is to focus on improving grammatical accuracy and sentence variety, especially through the consistent use of relative clauses and passive structures, and by varying transition words.	\N	Writing	Formative	FIRST	Part 2	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2025-08-17
31614	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.<br><br>Lou's trip in Hawaii has officially started. The first thing Lou do when he got out of the airport was calling a taxi to go to his hotel, so that he won't have to carry his suitcase everywhere. When he arrived to his hotel, a tropical themed hotel. He checked-in, but when he was about to pay he realized his wallet is gone. A chill ran down his spine. The taxi ride was 2 hours, and he had an expensive activity booked for the next 30 minutes. Suddenly, he remembered he put it in his suitcase after he pays the taxi driver. He quickly look for it and found it. He was relieves. He continued the next 3 weeks in Hawaii happily.	139	16.0	Lily, your story starts with a strong hook — <em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below</em> — and you build real tension when Lou realises his wallet is gone. That moment with <em>A chill ran down his spine</em> is exactly the kind of detail that makes a reader care. You also resolve the problem in a satisfying way, with Lou finding the wallet in his suitcase and continuing his trip happily. To reach Band 5 across all areas, the next step is to tighten your grammar and sentence control, especially with verb tenses and subject-verb agreement. Let's look at each subscale to see where you can improve.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> You used the given opening sentence perfectly and immediately set the scene.</li>\n  <li><em>Suddenly, he remembered he put it in his suitcase after he pays the taxi driver.</em> The moment of realisation is clear, and the reader understands exactly what happened.</li>\n  <li><em>He continued the next 3 weeks in Hawaii happily.</em> You give the story a proper ending, showing that Lou's holiday turned out well after all.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer tells a story where every sentence sounds natural and the reader never pauses to wonder what was meant. Your story has a clear beginning, middle, and end with good tension, but a few sentences feel slightly awkward.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>When he arrived to his hotel, a tropical themed hotel.</em> → <strong>When he arrived at his hotel, which was a tropical-themed hotel,</strong> The second part is a fragment — it needs to be attached to the main sentence.</li>\n  <li><em>He quickly look for it and found it.</em> → <strong>He quickly looked for it and found it.</strong> Keeping the past tense consistent makes the story flow more smoothly.</li>\n  <li><em>He was relieves.</em> → <strong>He was relieved.</strong> This small fix makes the sentence sound natural.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every sentence so the story flows without any bumps. Your story is well sequenced and uses good linking words like <em>so that</em>, <em>but when</em>, and <em>Suddenly</em>. The main issue is one sentence that is not a complete sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>When he arrived to his hotel, a tropical themed hotel.</em> → <strong>When he arrived at his hotel, which was a tropical-themed hotel, he checked in.</strong> Joining the description to the next action creates a smooth, complete sentence.</li>\n  <li>You used <em>Suddenly</em> to introduce the turning point, which works well to signal a change in the story. Keep using words like <em>then</em>, <em>after that</em>, and <em>finally</em> to guide the reader through the timeline.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls past tense and subject-verb agreement so the reader never notices errors. Your vocabulary is good — <em>a chill ran down his spine</em> is a strong phrase — but several verb forms need attention. The main rule to focus on is: <strong>when telling a story in the past, use the past tense for every main verb.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Lou do</em> → <strong>Lou did</strong>. After <em>Lou</em>, a third-person singular subject, the past tense of <em>do</em> is <em>did</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>he won't have</em> → <strong>he wouldn't have</strong>. In a past-tense story, change <em>will</em> to <em>would</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>after he pays</em> → <strong>after he paid</strong>. The paying happened in the past, so use <em>paid</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>He quickly look</em> → <strong>He quickly looked</strong>. Add <em>-ed</em> to <em>look</em> for the past tense.</li>\n  <li><em>He was relieves</em> → <strong>He was relieved</strong>. After <em>was</em>, use the past participle <em>relieved</em>, not the present tense verb.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can use: after you write each sentence, ask yourself — <em>Is this event happening now or in the past?</em> If it is in the past, make sure every main verb uses the past tense form.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
32217	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. Lou had arrive to Hawaii. He's super excited about the environment on this island. Lou checked-in at a hotel in Honolulu, feeling excited about his plan tomorrow. "Beep "Beep" "Beep" the sound of his alarm went off at 6 in the morning. Lou's getting ready for his first adventure of the day, surfing. He grabbed a cup of coffee and stormed off. Light breeze gently making its way pass his body, Lou was holding onto his board with a tight grip while walking right into the beach. The wave hit his foot calmly as he walking right into the bigger one. Water splashed, warm air pass through, Lou was having a really good time surfing. After a whole day at the beach, Lou can't wait to tell his family about his fantastic trip.	151	17.0	Poopup, you've written a story that feels alive. The moment when Lou hears <em>"Beep "Beep" "Beep"</em> and storms off for coffee immediately pulls the reader into his morning. Your choice to show Lou's excitement through action rather than just telling us works well. To reach Band 5 across all scales, we need to tighten your grammar control and smooth out a few transitions so your story flows as naturally as the waves Lou surfs. Let's look at each area.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> You start right inside the action, which hooks the reader immediately and sets the Hawaiian scene.</li><li><em>He grabbed a cup of coffee and stormed off.</em> This small detail shows Lou's eagerness and keeps the story moving forward without slowing down.</li><li><em>After a whole day at the beach, Lou can't wait to tell his family about his fantastic trip.</em> You close the story with a natural ending that completes the journey from arrival to reflection.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the register consistent throughout the story and avoids phrases that feel too casual for a written narrative. Your story has a strong sequence and descriptive moments, but <em>He's super excited</em> and <em>stormed off</em> sound more like spoken English than a written story.</p><ul><li><em>He's super excited about the environment on this island.</em> → <strong>He felt a rush of excitement at the island's beautiful surroundings.</strong> This keeps the tone of the story consistent with your descriptive style.</li><li><em>He grabbed a cup of coffee and stormed off.</em> → <strong>He grabbed a cup of coffee and hurried out.</strong> "Stormed off" suggests anger, but Lou is excited. "Hurried out" matches his mood.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses time markers and linking words so smoothly that the reader never has to pause and think about the order of events. Your sequence is logical, but the jump from the alarm to surfing happens too quickly.</p><ul><li><em>"Beep "Beep" "Beep" the sound of his alarm went off at 6 in the morning. Lou's getting ready for his first adventure of the day, surfing.</em> → <strong>"Beep, beep, beep!" The sound of his alarm went off at 6 in the morning. After a quick breakfast and a short walk, Lou reached the beach for his first adventure of the day: surfing.</strong> Adding a step between the alarm and the water makes the timeline feel complete.</li><li><em>After a whole day at the beach, Lou can't wait to tell his family about his fantastic trip.</em> → <strong>After a whole day at the beach, Lou could not wait to tell his family about his fantastic trip.</strong> Shifting "can't" to "could not" keeps the past tense consistent and makes the transition to the ending smoother.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls verb tenses and subject-verb agreement so the reader never stumbles. Your vocabulary is strong — words like <em>gripped</em>, <em>splashed</em>, and <em>fantastic trip</em> show range. The main rule that will lift your score is: <strong>after a past tense main verb, use past tense consistently throughout the sentence and paragraph.</strong> Let's fix the patterns.</p><ul><li><em>Lou had arrive to Hawaii.</em> → <strong>Lou had arrived in Hawaii.</strong> After "had" you need the past participle "arrived." Also use "in" for islands, not "to."</li><li><em>Light breeze gently making its way pass his body</em> → <strong>A light breeze gently made its way past his body.</strong> This sentence needs a main verb ("made") and "past" (direction) not "pass" (verb).</li><li><em>as he walking right into the bigger one</em> → <strong>as he walked right into the bigger one</strong> or <strong>as he was walking right into the bigger one</strong>. You need either the simple past or past continuous form of "walk."</li><li><em>warm air pass through</em> → <strong>warm air passed through</strong>. Keep the past tense matching the rest of the scene.</li></ul><p>Self-check for next time: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, "Is every verb in the past tense?" If you find a present tense verb in a past tense story, change it. This one habit will move you to Band 5.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33266	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Have you ever gotten bored doing something and decided to just watch a film?<br><br>A film is something that can cure your boredom whenever you're feeling a little less energetic than usual. Personally, I would watch action films or horror films so I can feel awake and enthusiastic after watching it.<br><br>When you talk about films, the first thing that comes to mind are cinemas, because it's so commonly found everywhere, Even though it's the first thing I think of every time someone says "film", I've actually never been to one before. When I decide I want to watch a film or a movie, I would just walk to the couch, find some blankets, and turn on the TV. It's much comfier when you're at home, and you can do whatever you want. I don't really like crowds either, so I don't think I'm ever going to watch a movie at the cinema. My TV and couch is enough for me to live with right now.	179	20.0	Taia, you've written an article that feels like a real conversation with the reader. Starting with a direct question — <em>Have you ever gotten bored doing something and decided to just watch a film?</em> — immediately pulls the reader in, and your personal voice stays strong throughout. The way you explain your preference for watching at home with specific reasons like comfort and dislike of crowds makes your opinion completely clear. To keep growing, you could experiment with even more varied sentence openings to add rhythm to your paragraphs. Here is how each area breaks down for your next draft.	B2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Personally, I would watch action films or horror films so I can feel awake and enthusiastic after watching it.</em> You named specific genres and gave a personal reason for choosing them. The reader understands exactly why those genres appeal to you.</li><li><em>It's much comfier when you're at home, and you can do whatever you want. I don't really like crowds either, so I don't think I'm ever going to watch a movie at the cinema.</em> You gave two clear, connected reasons for your preference, which fully informs the reader about your choice.</li><li><em>A film is something that can cure your boredom whenever you're feeling a little less energetic than usual.</em> This opening idea sets up the whole article by explaining the purpose films serve in your life. Every paragraph after this supports that idea.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Have you ever gotten bored doing something and decided to just watch a film?</em> Opening with a question is a classic article technique, and yours feels natural, not forced. It makes the reader want to keep reading.</li><li><em>When you talk about films, the first thing that comes to mind are cinemas, because it's so commonly found everywhere, Even though it's the first thing I think of every time someone says "film", I've actually never been to one before.</em> You set up an expectation and then surprise the reader with your personal experience. This is exactly the kind of engaging writing that holds a reader's attention.</li><li>The conversational tone — <em>My TV and couch is enough for me to live with right now</em> — fits the article genre perfectly. It sounds like you are talking directly to someone who shares your interest in films.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Personally</em> and <em>Even though</em> help you move smoothly from the general idea of films to your specific preferences. The reader never gets lost.</li><li><em>When I decide I want to watch a film or a movie, I would just walk to the couch, find some blankets, and turn on the TV.</em> The sequence of actions creates a clear, easy-to-follow picture of your routine.</li><li><em>I don't really like crowds either, so I don't think I'm ever going to watch a movie at the cinema.</em> The word <em>either</em> connects this reason back to your earlier point about comfort at home, creating a logical chain of reasoning.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>cure your boredom</em> and <em>enthusiastic</em> and <em>commonly found</em> show you can use vocabulary that goes beyond basic words. This makes your writing more precise and interesting to read.</li><li><em>so I can feel awake</em> and <em>Even though it's the first thing</em> and <em>so I don't think I'm ever going to watch</em> show you can use subordinate clauses to express cause, contrast, and result. These structures give your sentences variety and depth.</li><li><em>My TV and couch is enough</em> — this is a subject-verb agreement point. The subject is two items (TV and couch), so the verb should be <strong>are enough</strong>. This is a very minor slip in an otherwise strong piece of writing. When you have a compound subject joined by <em>and</em>, check whether the verb matches the plural form.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33919	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy to watching romantic and horror films because it very funny and it have mystery that we need to solve () and not serious so it make me more relax and from my school works. I prefer to watching films at the cinema because it have a big screen and more quiet than my home so I like to watching flim at cinema and the most I like about the cinema is popcorn. It have a lot of flavor like cheese, spicy, sour cream and it taste better that popcorn that I make at home.	95	13.0	Fuji, your article is genuinely enjoyable to read. You covered every part of the task — your film preferences, why you like them, why you prefer the cinema, and even a specific detail about cinema popcorn. That's exactly what a Band 5 writer does: the reader finishes your article knowing everything they need to. Now let's look at how to lift your language and structure so your good ideas get the presentation they deserve.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy to watching romantic and horror films</em> — You clearly named your two favourite genres right at the start. The reader knows exactly what you're talking about.</li>\n  <li><em>it have a lot of flavor like cheese, spicy, sour cream</em> — This specific detail about popcorn flavours makes your article feel personal and real. A reader can picture exactly what you mean.</li>\n  <li><em>it taste better that popcorn that I make at home</em> — You compared cinema popcorn with homemade popcorn, which is a thoughtful way to explain your preference. Every part of the task is fully covered.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer opens and closes an article clearly so the reader knows what to expect and what to remember. Your article jumps straight into your film preferences without an introduction, and it ends suddenly after the popcorn detail. The informal register is fine for an article, but adding a short opening sentence and a closing sentence will make it feel complete.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy to watching romantic and horror films</em> → <strong>When I have free time, I enjoy watching two very different kinds of films: romantic films and horror films.</strong> This opening tells the reader what your article is about in a friendly, inviting way.</li>\n  <li>Your article ends with <em>it taste better that popcorn that I make at home</em>. → <strong>Add a closing sentence like: For all these reasons, going to the cinema is my favourite way to watch a film.</strong> This wraps up your ideas and leaves the reader satisfied.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer groups related ideas into clear paragraphs and uses a variety of linking words. Right now, your whole article is one long paragraph, and you rely mostly on 'because', 'so', and 'and'. Breaking it into two or three paragraphs will make your ideas much easier to follow.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your first few sentences are about film genres and why you like them. <strong>Make this your first paragraph.</strong> Start it with your new opening sentence, then explain your preferences.</li>\n  <li>Your sentences about the cinema and popcorn belong together. <strong>Make this your second paragraph.</strong> You can connect the two paragraphs with a transition like: <em>Not only do I love these film genres, but I also love watching them at the cinema.</em></li>\n  <li>Try using <strong>also</strong>, <strong>another reason</strong>, or <strong>in addition</strong> instead of always using 'and' and 'so'. For example: <em>Another reason I like horror films is the mystery.</em></li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at a 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar most of the time, even when trying new vocabulary. The main pattern that causes most of your errors is this: after <em>enjoy</em>, <em>prefer</em>, and <em>like</em>, you need the <strong>-ing form</strong> (gerund), not <em>to + verb</em>. Also, <em>it</em> (singular) needs <strong>has</strong>, not <em>have</em>. Let's fix these patterns together.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy to watching</em> → <strong>I enjoy watching</strong>. After 'enjoy', use the -ing form directly. No 'to'.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to watching</em> → <strong>I prefer watching</strong>. Same rule: 'prefer' + -ing.</li>\n  <li><em>I like to watching</em> → <strong>I like watching</strong>. 'Like' also takes the -ing form for general preferences.</li>\n  <li><em>it have a big screen</em> → <strong>it has a big screen</strong>. 'It' is singular, so the verb must be 'has', not 'have'. Also: <em>it have mystery</em> → <strong>it has mystery</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>it taste better that popcorn</em> → <strong>it tastes better than popcorn</strong>. Two fixes here: 'it' needs 'tastes' (third person singular), and 'that' should be 'than' when comparing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After you revise, check every sentence for these two rules: (1) Did I use the -ing form after enjoy/prefer/like? (2) Did I use 'has' and 'tastes' with 'it'?</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34174	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Hello I'm Krabi this article is about flim.<br><br>I love action and sci-fi flims so much because action flims are easy and fun to watch and Also the story of action flims are not complicated but sci-fi is different I like it because when you watch it you will have alot of question about the flim and some times it's so strange thats what I like about it. I prefer to watch at home because it's more convinent and don't have to travel to watch a movies. Also watching movies at home makes me feel more comfortable.<br><br>So, after all I love action and sci-fi movies and I like to watch movies at home because it's easy and comfortable.	131	15.0	Krabi, your article is full of genuine enthusiasm for action and sci-fi films, and that energy makes it enjoyable to read. You clearly covered every point the task asked for: you named your favourite genres, explained why you like each one, and gave solid reasons for preferring to watch at home. Your personal voice comes through in lines like <em>some times it's so strange thats what I like about it</em>, which shows you're thinking about why a genre appeals to you. To take this article to the next level, we need to work on making your sentences flow more smoothly and fixing some spelling and grammar patterns. Let's break it down by the four scoring areas.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I love action and sci-fi flims so much because action flims are easy and fun to watch</em>. You named your preferred genres and gave a clear reason for liking action films, which directly responds to the task.</li>\n  <li><em>sci-fi is different I like it because when you watch it you will have alot of question about the flim and some times it's so strange thats what I like about it</em>. You explained why sci-fi appeals to you in a thoughtful, personal way. The reader understands your perspective completely.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to watch at home because it's more convinent and don't have to travel to watch a movies</em>. You stated your preference and gave two reasons for it, fully informing the reader.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would make their article feel more like a polished piece for a magazine or website, with a stronger opening hook and a more consistently engaging tone throughout.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hello I'm Krabi this article is about flim</em> → <strong>Are you someone who loves the thrill of an explosion and the mystery of outer space? Then you've come to the right place.</strong> Your current opening is a bit like a school introduction. A stronger article opening grabs the reader's attention with a question or an interesting statement.</li>\n  <li>Your middle paragraph is lively and personal, which is great. To keep the reader engaged, try varying your sentence lengths. For example, after <em>action flims are easy and fun to watch</em>, you could add a short punchy sentence like: <strong>But sci-fi? That's a different world.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words and structures to create a smooth, logical flow from one idea to the next.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I love action and sci-fi flims so much because action flims are easy and fun to watch and Also the story of action flims are not complicated but sci-fi is different</em>. You rely heavily on <em>and</em> and <em>but</em> to connect ideas. Try replacing some of them with other connectors. For example: <strong>I love action and sci-fi films. Action films are easy and fun to watch because the story is not complicated. Sci-fi, on the other hand, is completely different.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>Also watching movies at home makes me feel more comfortable</em>. <em>Also</em> is useful, but using it at the start of a sentence too often can feel repetitive. Try <strong>Furthermore, watching movies at home makes me feel more comfortable</strong> or <strong>What's more, watching at home is simply more comfortable.</strong></li>\n  <li>Your paragraphs are well-divided, which is good. To improve the sequence of ideas, consider adding a short final sentence to your first paragraph that leads into the second. For example, after explaining why you like both genres, you could write: <strong>But where do I prefer to watch these films? At home, of course.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls everyday vocabulary and simple grammar with few errors. The main pattern holding you back is spelling: you have several misspellings of key words, especially <em>film</em> and <em>convenient</em>. Let's fix those and a couple of other patterns.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>flim</em> → <strong>film</strong>. This word appears four times in your article. The correct spelling is <em>film</em>, not <em>flim</em>. Say it aloud: f-i-l-m. Practice writing it correctly five times.</li>\n  <li><em>convinent</em> → <strong>convenient</strong>. The correct spelling is c-o-n-v-e-n-i-e-n-t. Remember it has an <em>e</em> after the <em>v</em> and before the <em>n</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>alot</em> → <strong>a lot</strong>. This is two words, not one. Write it as <em>a lot</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>some times</em> → <strong>sometimes</strong>. This is one word. Write it as <em>sometimes</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>don't have to travel to watch a movies</em> → <strong>don't have to travel to watch movies</strong>. When talking about movies in general, use the plural without <em>a</em>. You wrote <em>a movies</em>, which mixes a singular article with a plural noun. Choose one: <em>a movie</em> or <em>movies</em>.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
28836	2025-08-15 08:42:58.64346+00	2026	Thai Food	You cant survive without food. Although all cultures have their own unique food. In Thailand, there are regional food the most famous is Issan, which is Thailand north east. Three of the most famous Issan foods are Prala, Somtum, and Soy ju.<br><br><br>One of the most popular Issan food is Prala which is known by its strong smell. It's made from fish and salt. The way to make Prala is. First step, you need a big bowl and put fish in the bowl. Secondly, you put salt in the bowl. And now, you need to let it merinate. It's a really fascinating Issan food that represent Thailand. Because Prala, develope when Thai people don't have refrigerator. It's a very interest way to keep food eatable and preserve.<br><br><br>Besides of Prala, the second popular Issan food is Somtam, which mostly known or called papaya salad. The basic ingredients of Somtam are papaya, tomato, lime, chillies, Prala, and seasoning such as salt, sugar, and msg. With all the ingredients, you can make a Somtum. How to make it is very easy but you need some tools. The tools are mortar and pestle, which will play a very important role in this dish. First step, you need to put all the ingredients in mortar and add seasoning. Lastly, you need to smash it with the pestle. This is one of the most well-know dish in Thailand. Because Somtum really represent Thailand in many ways. First is the flavour, Somtum uses chillies, which made this dish flavourish and memorable.<br><br><br>Finally, the dish that represents Thailand regional food from Issan is Soy ju, a very simple meal to make. This dish include any raw meat and Kao Kuea, a grinded rice as ingredients. The ways to cook is very easy just mix Kao Kua with raw meat. Then dipped it in any sauce you desire. It really represent Thailand by the way they use sauce to bring flavour to the dish<br><br><br>To summarize, Thailand regional food are very unique an interesting. Example like Prala, Somtam, and Soy ju Pralas or merinate fish with salt. It extend food expiration. Somtum, which use natural ingredients and spice to make the food flavourish, and memorable. Soy ju, a dangerous meal which may contain parasites. Although it's dagerous to eat because the parasites. People still eat it because of its flavour. This shows that every culture food is unique and good the way their are.	406	\N	You have written an interesting essay about Thai food, specifically focusing on Issan regional dishes. Your essay clearly introduces the three foods you want to discuss and provides details about each one. You successfully explain the cultural value of the food in each paragraph, which was a special requirement for this task. Your use of relative clauses helps to add some variety to your sentences. To improve, focus on correcting common grammar mistakes such as spelling, subject-verb agreement, and ensuring you use the correct form for passive structures.	\N	Writing	Formative	FIRST	Part 2	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2025-08-15
35330	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. Lou and her father arrived at their destination St'Mary's on the Isle's of Scilly. The perfect summer vacation, they were visiting cousins on St'Agnes, So they had to get on a boat to St'Agnes, during the boat trip Lou's father was telling Lou about the island's, while warm blue water was splashing everywhere. They got to the pier of st'Agnes, and the cousin's were waiting by the cottage near the pier, They greeted Lou and her father, then they all walked to aunt Rosie's. After Lou and her father had settle in, Aunt Rosie and the cousin's were preparing dinner.	125	17.0	Elenna, your story has a real sense of place and journey. You took the reader from the plane descending over golden beaches all the way to a warm dinner at Aunt Rosie's cottage. The way you included sensory details like the splashing water and the cousins waiting by the pier shows you were thinking carefully about what makes a scene feel real. To push your writing even further, you can focus on polishing your grammar and punctuation so that your strong ideas are delivered as cleanly as they deserve. Let's look at each area of your score in detail.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> This opening immediately sets the scene and draws the reader into the story. You gave us a clear location and a moment of discovery.</li><li><em>They got to the pier of st'Agnes, and the cousin's were waiting by the cottage near the pier</em> You introduced the characters naturally and showed their connection to the setting. The reader can picture the scene.</li><li><em>After Lou and her father had settle in, Aunt Rosie and the cousin's were preparing dinner.</em> You ended with a warm, domestic moment that gives the story a satisfying sense of closure. The narrative arc is complete.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would maintain the storytelling register consistently throughout and end with a stronger sense of closure. Your story is engaging and easy to follow, but the final sentence feels a bit flat compared to the vivid opening.</p><ul><li><em>while warm blue water was splashing everywhere</em> → <strong>while warm blue water splashed around the boat</strong>. This detail is lovely and descriptive. The original uses 'was splashing,' which is grammatically fine, but the simpler past tense keeps the action moving forward.</li><li><em>After Lou and her father had settle in, Aunt Rosie and the cousin's were preparing dinner.</em> → <strong>After Lou and her father had settled in, Aunt Rosie and the cousins were preparing a hearty dinner.</strong> Adding a small adjective like 'hearty' gives the ending more warmth and texture, matching the energy of your earlier descriptions.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would use a wider variety of linking words and avoid small punctuation breaks that interrupt the flow. Your paragraph breaks are logical, and the sequence of events is clear.</p><ul><li><em>So they had to get on a boat to St'Agnes, during the boat trip Lou's father was telling Lou about the island's</em> → <strong>They took a boat to St. Agnes. During the trip, Lou's father told her about the islands.</strong> Starting a sentence with 'So' feels a little informal for a story. Dropping it makes the sentence stronger.</li><li><em>They greeted Lou and her father, then they all walked to aunt Rosie's.</em> → <strong>They greeted Lou and her father. Then they all walked to Aunt Rosie's cottage.</strong> Using 'Then' at the start of a new sentence gives each action its own moment and improves the rhythm.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control apostrophes for possession and contractions, and use the past perfect consistently. One main rule will help you fix most of your errors: an apostrophe before 's' shows possession (one island → the island's beaches), while an apostrophe after 's' shows plural possession (many cousins → the cousins' cottage). A contraction like 'St.' uses a period, not an apostrophe.</p><ul><li><em>the Isle's of Scilly</em> → <strong>the Isles of Scilly</strong>. 'Isles' is a plural name, so no apostrophe is needed.</li><li><em>the island's</em> → <strong>the islands</strong>. You meant multiple islands, not something belonging to one island.</li><li><em>the cousin's were waiting</em> → <strong>the cousins were waiting</strong>. 'Cousins' is a plural subject, not a possessive. No apostrophe needed.</li><li><em>had settle in</em> → <strong>had settled in</strong>. After 'had' you need the past participle form of the verb: settle → settled.</li><li><em>St'Mary's</em> and <em>st'Agnes</em> → <strong>St. Mary's</strong> and <strong>St. Agnes</strong>. Place names use a period after 'St' (short for Saint), not an apostrophe. Also, 'St' should be capitalised.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: Look at every word ending in 's'. Ask yourself: Is this a plural (more than one)? A possessive (belonging to someone)? Or a contraction (short for 'is' or 'has')? If it is a plural, no apostrophe. If it is a possessive, add the apostrophe in the correct place. If it is a contraction, use the apostrophe where letters are missing.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29561	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi, Sandy\n\nI'm also excited because I could stay with you for a week.\n\nOn my first evening at your home which I prefer to relax at home because we could play a lot of games more than watching blah blah blah rock concert also I don't like concert and rock music and if I'm at rock music concert I would like be Nah I'm out of here right now also shall we go climbing in the mountains well No sorry because, My legs are hurting right now so No we shouldn't go climbing in the mountain, any questions?\n\nWell can I have a lot of food pizza drink junk food soda coke kfc Mcdonal slurpee BBQ ok?\n\nSee you soon\n\nMatthew	127	9.0	Mathew, you've written a friendly email that clearly shows you're excited about the visit. You included all the main points from the task: you mentioned the concert, the climbing, and you asked about food. That's a strong start. The challenge now is to remove the parts that don't belong in an email to a friend's parents, like 'blah blah blah' and 'Nah I'm out of here right now.' Those phrases confuse the reader and make your message less clear. Let's look at each part of your writing so you can reach Band 5.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer covers every point from the task clearly and adds only relevant details. You did include all the main ideas, but some of your comments don't help the reader understand your message.</p><ul><li><em>blah blah blah rock concert</em> → <strong>the rock concert</strong>. The words 'blah blah blah' don't tell the reader anything useful. Just name the concert directly.</li><li><em>Nah I'm out of here right now</em> → <strong>I wouldn't enjoy it at all</strong>. This is a clearer way to say you don't like rock concerts. The reader understands your feeling without confusion.</li><li><em>Well can I have a lot of food pizza drink junk food soda coke kfc Mcdonal slurpee BBQ ok?</em> → <strong>Could you tell me what food you usually have? I like pizza and soft drinks, but anything is fine.</strong> Your question was too broad and informal. A polite, specific question works better.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent, appropriate tone throughout the email. You started well with 'Hi, Sandy' and ended with 'See you soon,' but the middle of your email shifts between friendly and too casual.</p><ul><li><em>blah blah blah</em> and <em>Nah I'm out of here right now</em> → These phrases are too informal for an email to a friend's parents. Use <strong>I'm not really a fan of rock music, so I'd prefer to stay home</strong> instead.</li><li><em>ok?</em> at the end of your food question → This sounds like you're demanding an answer. Try <strong>Could you let me know what food you'll have?</strong> to keep the tone polite.</li><li>The overall tone should be friendly but respectful. Imagine you are writing to your friend's mum or dad. You can be warm without being too casual.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas clearly so the reader can follow the flow easily. Your email is one long paragraph, and the ideas jump from topic to topic without smooth transitions.</p><ul><li><em>On my first evening at your home which I prefer to relax at home because we could play a lot of games more than watching blah blah blah rock concert</em> → This sentence tries to say too many things at once. Break it into two sentences: <strong>On my first evening at your home, I would prefer to relax. We could play games together instead of going to a rock concert.</strong></li><li><em>also</em> and <em>well</em> are the only words connecting your ideas. Use <strong>however</strong>, <strong>because</strong>, or <strong>also</strong> more carefully. For example: <strong>However, my legs are hurting right now, so I don't think we should go climbing.</strong></li><li>Your email has a clear start and end, but the middle needs paragraph breaks. Use a new paragraph for each main topic: first the concert, then the climbing, then the food question.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar and appropriate vocabulary most of the time. Your meaning is usually clear, but there are several errors that can be fixed with one main rule: after 'would like' and 'prefer', use 'to' + the base verb.</p><ul><li><em>I would like be</em> → <strong>I would like to be</strong>. After 'would like', always add 'to' before the verb. This is a fixed pattern.</li><li><em>which I prefer to relax at home</em> → <strong>I prefer to relax at home</strong>. The word 'which' is not needed here. 'Prefer to' is correct, but the sentence structure was confused.</li><li><em>I could stay with you for a week</em> → <strong>I am going to stay with you for a week</strong>. Since the visit is already planned, use the present continuous or 'going to' future, not 'could'.</li><li><em>My legs are hurting right now so No we shouldn't go climbing</em> → <strong>My legs are hurting right now, so I don't think we should go climbing</strong>. Capitalise 'No' only at the start of a sentence. Use 'I don't think' instead of 'No' for a smoother sentence.</li><li>Watch your capitalisation: <em>My legs</em> starts with a capital M in the middle of a sentence. Only capitalise proper names and the first word of a sentence. Also, <em>Mcdonal</em> should be <strong>McDonald's</strong>.</li></ul><p>Self-check for every sentence: If you use 'would like' or 'prefer', does the next word have 'to' before the verb? If not, add it.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29579	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy<br><br>Me too! That's exciting to stay together for the first time.<br><br>As for activity, I prefer rock concert because I love music, and I hadn't ever joined the concert before. That would be fun! If you would like to relax at home, you can tell me. I can do whatever you want, but I can't go climbing in the mountain because it's too risky, and I scared of the high height. I want to tell you. I would like to petsua you to come to my new ice-cream shop. I have many flavour of ice-cream that you should try. That way, our holiday would be amazing!<br><br>Do you have any activity to do more? Let me know your idea.<br><br>See you soon<br><br>Ka-nhom	142	13.0	Ka-nhom, your email to Sandy is full of genuine enthusiasm, and that makes it a pleasure to read. You clearly covered all three required points: you expressed excitement about the visit, chose the rock concert and explained why, and gave a reason for not climbing. You also ended with a relevant question for Sandy. The extra idea about the ice-cream shop shows you are thinking creatively, but it is not part of the task and takes attention away from the main purpose. To reach Band 5, you need to stay focused on exactly what the task asks and make sure every sentence serves that purpose. Now let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer covers all the required points completely and does not add unrelated ideas. You did cover all three notes, but the ice-cream shop paragraph is extra content the task did not ask for.</p><ul><li><em>As for activity, I prefer rock concert because I love music, and I hadn't ever joined the concert before.</em> This is perfect. You stated your preference and gave a clear reason. The reader understands your choice completely.</li><li><em>I can't go climbing in the mountain because it's too risky, and I scared of the high height.</em> You explained why you cannot climb, which is exactly what the task required. The reason is personal and honest.</li><li><em>I would like to petsua you to come to my new ice-cream shop.</em> This idea is not part of the task. The task asks you to suggest an activity and explain your choice, not to introduce a new, unrelated plan. Removing this sentence would bring your content to a 5/5.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an email to a friend consistently and naturally, keeping the tone appropriate throughout. Your opening and closing are friendly, but two phrases feel slightly off.</p><ul><li><em>I want to tell you. I would like to petsua you to come to my new ice-cream shop.</em> The phrase 'I want to tell you' sounds like you are announcing something rather than continuing a natural conversation. Try merging the ideas: <strong>I'd love to persuade you to visit my new ice-cream shop</strong>. Also, 'petsua' looks like a typo for 'persuade'. A friendly email flows more smoothly when you connect your thoughts without announcing them.</li><li><em>Me too! That's exciting to stay together for the first time.</em> This is a warm, natural opening. It sets the right tone immediately. Keep this kind of direct, friendly language throughout.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never feels lost. Your email has a logical order, but the jump to the ice-cream shop feels sudden and unconnected.</p><ul><li><em>I can't go climbing in the mountain because it's too risky, and I scared of the high height. I want to tell you. I would like to petsua you to come to my new ice-cream shop.</em> The move from 'I can't climb' to 'I want to tell you about my shop' is abrupt. A linking sentence would help: <strong>Since we can't go climbing, I have another idea. I would love to persuade you to visit my new ice-cream shop</strong>. That way, the reader sees the connection between the two ideas.</li><li><em>As for activity, I prefer rock concert...</em> You used 'As for' to introduce the first activity, which works well. Try using similar signposts for each new topic to guide the reader.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls everyday vocabulary and simple grammar with few errors. Your vocabulary is good, but three grammar patterns need attention: verb tense after 'hadn't', the missing verb 'am' before adjectives, and the plural form 'flavours'. Let's fix them one at a time.</p><ul><li><em>I hadn't ever joined the concert before.</em> → <strong>I have never been to a rock concert before</strong>. When you talk about an experience up to now, use 'have never' + past participle, not 'hadn't ever'. 'Hadn't' is for a time before another past event, which is not what you mean here.</li><li><em>I scared of the high height.</em> → <strong>I am scared of heights</strong>. After 'I', you need the verb 'am' before an adjective like 'scared'. Also, 'heights' (plural) is the natural word for a fear of high places, not 'the high height'.</li><li><em>I have many flavour of ice-cream.</em> → <strong>I have many flavours of ice-cream</strong>. After 'many', the noun must be plural: 'flavours'. Also, 'of ice-cream' is correct here, but check your capitalisation: 'Ice-cream' does not need a capital letter in the middle of a sentence.</li><li><em>I would like to petsua you...</em> → <strong>I would like to persuade you</strong>. 'Petsua' is a typo for 'persuade'. This is a useful word to know for future writing.</li><li>Self-check: After every sentence, ask yourself: 'Did I put a verb after I? Did I make the noun plural after many?' This will catch most of your errors.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29589	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy,<br><br>Thanks for your email Sandy! - I'm so excited to stay with you for 2 week. On first evening we could relax at home because I think when I arrive to your house, I would get tried. Maybe we can have cook in your kitchen to relax. Also, climbing in the mountains I would say yes! It's my favourite hobbies.. While we go for climbing in the mountains could we have pizza as for lunch. That way we can have more power to climb! Also, wanna race? It would be fun if we did!<br><br>Let me know if you okay with it.<br><br>See you.<br><br>Elle	115	11.0	Elle, you've written a friendly email that really sounds like you're talking to a friend. The way you opened with <em>Thanks for your email Sandy!</em> and closed with <em>See you</em> shows you understand how an email should feel. You also gave a clear reason for wanting to relax at home: <em>because I think when I arrive to your house, I would get tried</em>. That's exactly the kind of personal detail the task wanted. To reach Band 5, you need to carefully read each note in the task and respond to every single one correctly. The climbing note asked you to say 'no', not 'yes', and the third note asked you to ask a question, which you missed. Let's look at each area so you know exactly what to fix.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer covers all the notes in the task with no mistakes. You handled the first note well by saying you want to relax and giving a reason. But the climbing note said you <em>don't</em> enjoy climbing, so saying <em>I would say yes!</em> was the opposite of what the task required. And the third note asked you to ask a question, but you didn't include one.</p><ul><li><em>On first evening we could relax at home because I think when I arrive to your house, I would get tried.</em> This is perfect for the first note. You stated your preference and explained why.</li><li><em>climbing in the mountains I would say yes!</em> → <strong>I would prefer not to go climbing because I get tired easily.</strong> The task said you don't enjoy climbing, so you needed to say no and give a reason.</li><li><em>Let me know if you okay with it.</em> This is close to a question, but the task wanted a specific question about the visit. Try: <strong>Would you like me to bring anything for our time together?</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent friendly tone and follows all task instructions. Your email sounds warm and personal with <em>Hi Sandy</em> and <em>See you</em>. But the phrase <em>wanna race?</em> is too casual for this email, and saying yes to climbing when the task said no will confuse the reader.</p><ul><li><em>wanna race?</em> → <strong>Would you like to race?</strong> 'Wanna' is very informal. Use the full form 'want to' or 'would you like to' to keep a friendly but appropriate tone.</li><li><em>I would say yes!</em> → <strong>I would prefer not to climb.</strong> The reader will be confused because the task clearly says you don't enjoy climbing. Fixing this one change makes your whole email make sense.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow easily. You used paragraphs for each topic and helpful linking words like <em>because</em> and <em>also</em>. However, the jump from climbing to pizza to racing feels rushed and a little confusing.</p><ul><li><em>While we go for climbing in the mountains could we have pizza as for lunch. That way we can have more power to climb! Also, wanna race?</em> → Try: <strong>If we go climbing in the mountains, could we have pizza for lunch? That would give us energy. After climbing, maybe we could have a race too.</strong> Adding 'after climbing' helps the reader see the order of events.</li><li>You used <em>also</em> twice in a row. Try replacing the second one with <strong>another thing</strong> or <strong>in addition</strong> to vary your linking words.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and spelling so the reader never has to guess the meaning. Your vocabulary is good for A2 level — words like <em>excited</em>, <em>relax</em>, <em>cook</em>, <em>power</em>, and <em>race</em> work well. The main rule that explains most of your errors is about <strong>plural nouns and subject-verb agreement</strong>. Let's fix those patterns.</p><ul><li><em>for 2 week</em> → <strong>for 2 weeks</strong>. When you have a number like 2, 3, or more, the noun must be plural. Always add -s. Check: 'for 1 week' but 'for 2 weeks'.</li><li><em>It's my favourite hobbies..</em> → <strong>It's my favourite hobby.</strong> 'It' is singular, so 'hobby' must be singular too. If you want to say more than one, write 'They are my favourite hobbies.'</li><li><em>if you okay with it</em> → <strong>if you are okay with it</strong>. Every sentence needs a verb. 'Are' is the missing verb here. Think: 'you are', 'he is', 'they are'.</li><li><em>I would get tried</em> → <strong>I would get tired</strong>. 'Tried' means you attempted something. 'Tired' means you need rest. These sound similar but have different spellings and meanings.</li><li><em>On first evening</em> → <strong>On the first evening</strong>. Use 'the' before ordinal numbers like first, second, third. Self-check: read every sentence aloud and ask 'Is the verb there? Is the -s on the plural noun?'</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29599	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy!<br><br>I'm so excited too! can't wait to go there!<br><br>Your idea is so fantastic! but I perfer us to stay at home to relax, so we can play some video games together, and I'll buy some snack for us to eat while we're playing video games.<br><br>I don't think I can go climbing with you because, last month I we climbing with my dad and I fell so hard it broke my leg, and that still tramatizing me until today.<br><br>And do you have a ps5 at your house? if you don't have it I can bring mine, so we can play it together.<br><br>see you soon<br>Praewwan	127	15.0	Praewwan, your email to Sandy is full of energy and personal detail. You did a great job covering every part of the task: you showed excitement, explained your preference to stay home with a clear reason, told the story about your broken leg, and asked a thoughtful question about the PS5. That's exactly what a Band 5 writer does. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll want to strengthen your grammar and sentence connections. Let's look at each part of your writing so you can revise your draft.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I'm so excited too! can't wait to go there!</em> You clearly showed your feelings about the visit, which matches the task perfectly.</li><li><em>I perfer us to stay at home to relax, so we can play some video games together</em> You gave a clear preference with a reason and a fun plan. The reader understands exactly what you want.</li><li><em>I don't think I can go climbing with you because, last month I we climbing with my dad and I fell so hard it broke my leg</em> You explained why you can't climb with a personal, honest story. The target reader feels for you.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone consistent and natural throughout the whole email, matching a friendly but polished style. Your opening and closing are warm, and the reader can follow everything easily. To reach Band 5, watch the level of formality.</p><ul><li><em>can't wait to go there!</em> → <strong>I can't wait to go there!</strong> This is fine for a friend, but adding the subject 'I' makes it slightly more complete and natural in writing.</li><li><em>Your idea is so fantastic! but I perfer us to stay at home to relax</em> → <strong>Your idea sounds fantastic, but I'd prefer for us to stay at home and relax.</strong> The exclamation mark before 'but' breaks the flow. Using a comma keeps the sentence smooth.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words and structures to connect ideas smoothly. Your email has a clear sequence: excitement → preference → climbing explanation → question. That's good. But you rely heavily on 'and', 'so', 'but', and 'because'. Try using a wider range of connectors.</p><ul><li><em>I'm so excited too! can't wait to go there! Your idea is so fantastic! but I perfer us to stay at home to relax</em> → <strong>I'm so excited too, and I can't wait to go there! Your idea sounds fantastic, but I'd prefer for us to stay at home and relax.</strong> Joining the first two short sentences with 'and' creates a smoother start.</li><li><em>I don't think I can go climbing with you because, last month I we climbing with my dad and I fell so hard it broke my leg, and that still tramatizing me until today.</em> → <strong>I don't think I can go climbing with you because last month I went climbing with my dad and fell so hard that I broke my leg. It still feels traumatic.</strong> Breaking this long sentence into two shorter ones makes the story clearer and more dramatic.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and vocabulary with few errors, so the reader never has to guess. You have a good range of everyday words like 'excited', 'fantastic', 'relax', 'video games', and 'climbing'. Your main grammar challenge is subject-verb agreement and verb forms. Let's fix the key patterns.</p><ul><li><em>I perfer us to stay</em> → <strong>I prefer for us to stay</strong> or <strong>I'd prefer to stay</strong>. The verb 'prefer' needs the correct spelling and structure. Good effort using a preference phrase.</li><li><em>last month I we climbing with my dad</em> → <strong>last month I went climbing with my dad</strong>. After a subject like 'I', you need a main verb. 'We' is a subject, not a verb. Try saying the sentence aloud: 'I went climbing' will sound right.</li><li><em>that still tramatizing me until today</em> → <strong>that still traumatises me</strong> or <strong>I still find it traumatic</strong>. The verb needs a form: 'is traumatising' or 'traumatises'. Also check spelling: 'tramatizing' → 'traumatising' or 'traumatic'.</li><li><em>can't wait to go there!</em> → <strong>I can't wait to go there!</strong> Every sentence needs a subject. 'Can't' is a verb, but who can't? Add 'I' at the start. This is a quick fix you can apply to every sentence you write.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29604	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi, I felt the same! My heart is pounding with excitement. I'm looking forward to stay over at your house!<br><br>I prefer stay at home to relax. I think we would have a great time talking and watching movies rather than go to the concert with loud and inappropriate lyrics. As for the climbing I will have to decline that idea. I would love to have a peace and comfortable atmosphere than go adventure outside. I want to ask if you have some board games at your house? If not, I can bring some so we could have some activities to do while we at it.<br><br>Can't wait to see you<br><br>Yayar	120	18.0	Yayar, your email is genuinely warm and personal. The opening line <em>My heart is pounding with excitement</em> immediately makes the reader feel your energy, and your reasons for declining the concert and climbing are thoughtful and specific. You've done an excellent job of addressing every part of the task. To reach Band 5 across all areas, the next step is to tighten your grammar and make a few small adjustments to how you connect your ideas. Here is how you can strengthen each part of your writing.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>My heart is pounding with excitement. I'm looking forward to stay over at your house!</em> You express your feelings about the visit clearly and enthusiastically, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer stay at home to relax. I think we would have a great time talking and watching movies rather than go to the concert with loud and inappropriate lyrics.</em> You give a personal, well-reasoned preference and explain why the concert does not appeal to you. The reader fully understands your choice.</li>\n  <li><em>I want to ask if you have some board games at your house? If not, I can bring some so we could have some activities to do while we at it.</em> You ask a relevant question and offer a practical solution, showing you have thought about how to spend your time together.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hi, I felt the same! My heart is pounding with excitement.</em> Your opening feels like a real conversation with a friend. It grabs attention and sets a friendly tone.</li>\n  <li><em>Can't wait to see you</em> Your closing is natural and matches the warm, informal register you used throughout the email.</li>\n  <li>The whole email reads like a genuine message between friends. You balance enthusiasm with polite refusals, and your personality comes through clearly.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas so smoothly that the reader never has to pause and think about how one idea relates to the next. Your two paragraphs are clear and logical, but the jump from climbing to board games feels a little abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I would love to have a peace and comfortable atmosphere than go adventure outside. I want to ask if you have some board games at your house?</em> → <strong>I would love to have a peaceful and comfortable atmosphere instead of going on an outdoor adventure. While we are at home, I want to ask if you have any board games.</strong> Adding a short linking phrase like 'While we are at home' makes the transition from the climbing refusal to the board game question feel more natural.</li>\n  <li><em>As for the climbing I will have to decline that idea.</em> This is a good use of 'as for' to introduce a new topic. You could strengthen it by adding a comma after 'climbing': <strong>As for the climbing, I will have to decline that idea.</strong></li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammatical forms with only occasional, minor errors. You have strong vocabulary and good sentence variety, but a small pattern of errors keeps you from the top band. The main rule to focus on is: after prepositions like 'to' and 'of', and after modal verbs like 'would' and 'can', use the base form of the verb (no '-ing' or other changes).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your vocabulary is a real strength. <em>pounding with excitement</em>, <em>loud and inappropriate lyrics</em>, and <em>peace and comfortable atmosphere</em> show you can choose words that add colour and precision to your writing.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm looking forward to stay over at your house!</em> → <strong>I'm looking forward to staying over at your house!</strong> After the phrase 'looking forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so the next verb needs '-ing'.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer stay at home to relax.</em> → <strong>I prefer to stay at home to relax.</strong> or <strong>I prefer staying at home to relax.</strong> After 'prefer', you need either 'to + verb' or 'verb + -ing'.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we would have a great time talking and watching movies rather than go to the concert</em> → <strong>I think we would have a great time talking and watching movies rather than going to the concert</strong> After 'rather than', use the '-ing' form of the verb.</li>\n  <li><em>I would love to have a peace and comfortable atmosphere than go adventure outside.</em> → <strong>I would love to have a peaceful and comfortable atmosphere instead of going on an adventure outside.</strong> Use 'peaceful' (adjective) to match 'comfortable', and use 'instead of + verb-ing' to compare two options.</li>\n  <li><em>while we at it</em> → <strong>while we are at it</strong> You need the verb 'are' here. This is a small missing word that is easy to fix when you check each sentence.</li>\n  <li>Self-check: After every 'to', 'of', 'for', 'rather than', and 'instead of', ask yourself: 'Does the next word need to change form?' If you are unsure, try saying the sentence aloud and listen for what sounds natural.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29623	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi,<br><br>Me too! I also excited to see you. I asked my mother a permision for this activity and finally she's accepted. I really glad about it. I can't wait to see you, and I know it's going to be fantastic!<br><br>Well, I would love to stay at home insted go to the concert, because I reckon home is a special place and we can do anything we wanted! as for concert I don't really like to go to places where's crowed, I prefer to stay at home and relaxed.<br><br>No, because I don't really like to go to the mountain 'cause it have a lot of insect.<br><br>Yea I had one question in my mind, well you going to shared bed with me or let me sleep in other room insted.<br><br>Can't wait to see you!<br><br>Siri	157	15.0	Siri, your email is full of genuine excitement and personality, and you've addressed every single point the task asked for. I can tell you really imagined writing to a close friend. Your opening line <em>I also excited to see you</em> immediately creates a warm, eager tone, and your question about sleeping arrangements shows you're thinking ahead. To reach Band 5, you'll need to focus on two things: making your grammar more accurate, especially with the verb 'to be', and smoothing out the flow between your ideas so the reader doesn't feel a jump. Let's look at each area.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I also excited to see you.</em> You clearly express your excitement about the visit, which perfectly addresses the first note.</li><li><em>I would love to stay at home insted go to the concert, because I reckon home is a special place and we can do anything we wanted!</em> You state your preference and give two clear reasons, fully informing the reader.</li><li><em>No, because I don't really like to go to the mountain 'cause it have a lot of insect.</em> You explain why you don't want to go climbing with a specific reason, and then you ask a relevant question about sleeping arrangements. The reader understands everything.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer would keep the friendly, enthusiastic tone throughout without any informal language that might confuse the reader. Your email starts and ends warmly, and the reader can follow your ideas easily. To reach Band 5, replace the informal <em>Yea</em> with something like 'Yes' or 'Actually,' and keep the contractions like <em>I can't wait</em> — those are perfect for a friend. Your tone is already very close to Band 5.</p>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer would connect each paragraph to the previous one so the reader never feels a jump. You've used paragraphs well, and words like <em>Well</em> and <em>as for</em> help, but the transition from climbing to your question feels sudden. Here's how to make it smoother:</p><ul><li><em>No, because I don't really like to go to the mountain 'cause it have a lot of insect.</em> → <strong>No, I'd rather not go to the mountain because it has a lot of insects. By the way, I had one question about where I'll sleep.</strong> Adding 'By the way' or 'Speaking of the plan' connects the two ideas.</li><li><em>Well, I would love to stay at home insted go to the concert</em> → <strong>Well, actually, I would love to stay at home instead of going to the concert</strong>. Adding 'actually' after 'Well' makes the contrast clearer.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer would control simple grammar forms like the verb 'to be' and subject-verb agreement with few errors. Your vocabulary is strong — words like <em>fantastic</em>, <em>permission</em>, and <em>crowded</em> are excellent. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: the verb 'to be' (am, is, are) must always appear in sentences like 'I am excited' or 'it is crowded.' You often leave it out. Let's fix that:</p><ul><li><em>I also excited to see you.</em> → <strong>I am also excited to see you.</strong> You need the verb 'am' after 'I'.</li><li><em>I really glad about it.</em> → <strong>I am really glad about it.</strong> Same rule: 'I am glad', not 'I glad'.</li><li><em>where's crowed</em> → <strong>where it is crowded</strong>. 'Crowded' is an adjective, so it needs 'is' before it. Also check spelling: crowded, not crowed.</li><li><em>it have a lot of insect</em> → <strong>it has a lot of insects</strong>. 'It' is singular, so the verb is 'has', not 'have'. Also 'insects' needs an 's' for plural.</li><li><em>you going to shared bed with me</em> → <strong>are you going to share a bed with me</strong>. After 'going to', use the base form of the verb: 'share', not 'shared'. And don't forget 'are' before 'you'.</li></ul><p>Here's a self-check you can use on any sentence: read it aloud and listen for the verb 'to be'. If you wrote 'I excited', ask yourself: 'Am I missing the word am?' If you wrote 'it have', ask: 'Should this be it has?' Try this with your next draft and you will see a big improvement.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29665	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hello, Sandy<br><br>Me too! I am so ready for this.<br><br>A rock concert? I think I'll pass. I prefer relaxing at home because rock concerts are way too loud, I really can't stand the loud speakers.<br><br>No, because I am really scared of going to high places. Everytime I look down from high places I would immediately start sweating, feeling dizzy. I really can't imagine what would happen if we go climbing.<br><br>And, by the way, instead of doing those activities you recommended, can we just watch movies at home? I just wanna relax, and have fun.<br><br>See you soon, Sandy<br><br>Peace	105	17.0	Peace, your email to Sandy covers every point from the task clearly, and your reasons are specific and natural. To reach a higher band, work on varying how you connect your ideas so the writing flows more smoothly rather than feeling like a list.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Me too! I am so ready for this.</em> You express excitement about the visit right away, which directly addresses the first note.</li>\n  <li><em>I think I'll pass. I prefer relaxing at home because rock concerts are way too loud.</em> You clearly state your preference and explain why, fully informing the reader.</li>\n  <li><em>No, because I am really scared of going to high places.</em> You give a personal, honest reason for not climbing, and then ask <em>can we just watch movies at home?</em> — a relevant question that shows you've thought about alternatives.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hello, Sandy</em> and <em>See you soon, Sandy</em> — your opening and closing are perfectly suited for an email to a friend.</li>\n  <li><em>Me too! I am so ready for this.</em> The exclamation and enthusiasm make the tone feel natural and engaging.</li>\n  <li><em>I really can't imagine what would happen if we go climbing.</em> This sentence sounds like something you'd actually say to a friend, keeping the register consistent throughout.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas in a way that feels natural, not like a list. Your email has good paragraph breaks, but each paragraph follows a very similar pattern: statement → reason.</p><ul><li><em>A rock concert? I think I'll pass. I prefer relaxing at home because rock concerts are way too loud.</em> → <strong>I think I'll pass on the concert — rock is way too loud for me, so I'd rather relax at home.</strong> Combining the decision with the reason in one flowing sentence feels less list-like.</li><li><em>No, because I am really scared of going to high places.</em> → <strong>I'm going to say no to climbing — heights really scare me, and even looking down from high places makes me dizzy.</strong> Try using dashes or colons to join your ideas rather than always starting new sentences.</li><li>Self-check: After you write each paragraph, read it aloud. If every sentence starts the same way (decision → because), try rewriting some sentences so the reason comes first or is embedded in the middle.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer would control small spelling and grammar details consistently. Your vocabulary is strong and your meaning is always clear, but there are two patterns to fix: the spelling of <em>every time</em> and the way you use conditional sentences.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Everytime I look down from high places I would immediately start sweating</em> → <strong>Every time I look down from high places, I start sweating immediately.</strong> The word <em>every time</em> is two words, not one. Also, when you describe a habit or repeated action, use the simple present (<em>I start</em>) instead of <em>would start</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>I really can't imagine what would happen if we go climbing.</em> → <strong>I really can't imagine what would happen if we went climbing.</strong> After <em>if</em> in a hypothetical situation, use the past tense (<em>went</em>), not the present (<em>go</em>). This is called the second conditional.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer relaxing at home because rock concerts are way too loud, I really can't stand the loud speakers.</em> → <strong>I prefer relaxing at home because rock concerts are way too loud. I really can't stand the loud speakers.</strong> These are two complete sentences. Join them with a period or a connecting word like <em>and</em>, not just a comma.</li>\n  <li><em>I just wanna relax, and have fun.</em> → <strong>I just want to relax and have fun.</strong> <em>Wanna</em> is fine in speech, but in writing, <em>want to</em> is more standard for this task. Also, no comma is needed before <em>and</em> here.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29720	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hello Sandy!<br><br>I hope this email finds you well.<br><br>Same here! I'm really honored and excited to stay with you for a whole week!<br><br>For me, I would prefer to relax at home. Maybe we could watch movies and eat some popcorns because there's a lot of people at the concert and we would be late because of the traffic jams.<br><br>That sounds great! But I wouldn't be able to climb the mountains because my left knee is injured. So maybe next time we could climb the mountains together.<br><br>Also let me know if you want something to eat. I could buy some at the supermarket.<br><br>See you soon,<br>Dragon	126	18.0	Dragon, your email to Sandy is warm, clear, and full of personality. You made every part of the task your own: you expressed real excitement about the visit, you gave a thoughtful reason for preferring to stay home (the crowds and traffic), you explained your knee injury honestly, and you ended with a practical question about food. The reader finishes the email feeling like they know you. To reach Band 5 in Organisation and Language, you just need to tighten a few small things — your paragraph flow and a couple of word choices. Let's look at each area.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm really honored and excited to stay with you for a whole week!</em> You showed genuine enthusiasm, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I would prefer to relax at home. Maybe we could watch movies and eat some popcorns because there's a lot of people at the concert and we would be late because of the traffic jams.</em> You gave a clear preference and backed it up with two specific, personal reasons. The reader fully understands your choice.</li>\n  <li><em>I wouldn't be able to climb the mountains because my left knee is injured. So maybe next time we could climb the mountains together.</em> You explained your limitation honestly and offered a kind alternative for the future. That shows thoughtfulness.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hello Sandy!</em> and <em>See you soon, Dragon</em> — your opening and closing are perfectly friendly and natural for an email to a friend.</li>\n  <li><em>Same here!</em> and <em>That sounds great!</em> — these short, excited responses make the email feel like a real conversation. The reader can hear your voice.</li>\n  <li><em>Also let me know if you want something to eat. I could buy some at the supermarket.</em> You asked a question naturally, which keeps the dialogue going and shows you care about Sandy's needs.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects their ideas so smoothly that the reader never has to pause. Your email is logical, but the jump from 'I can't climb' to 'Also let me know if you want something to eat' feels a little sudden.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>So maybe next time we could climb the mountains together.</em> → <strong>So maybe next time we could climb the mountains together. While we're at home, also let me know if you want something to eat.</strong> Adding a short linking phrase like 'While we're at home' bridges the two ideas naturally.</li>\n  <li><em>Also let me know if you want something to eat.</em> → <strong>Also, let me know if you want something to eat.</strong> Adding a comma after 'Also' at the start of a sentence is a small change that makes the flow cleaner.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls everyday vocabulary and grammar with almost no errors. Your range is excellent — 'honored', 'excited', 'traffic jams', 'injured' — but two small patterns need attention.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>some popcorns</em> → <strong>some popcorn</strong>. 'Popcorn' is an uncountable noun in English, so it has no plural form. You don't need the 's'.</li>\n  <li><em>there's a lot of people</em> → <strong>there are a lot of people</strong>. 'People' is plural, so the verb should be 'are', not 'is'. This is a common small error, and fixing it will make your grammar sound more polished.</li>\n  <li>Your use of conditionals and modals is strong: <em>I wouldn't be able to climb</em> and <em>we could climb the mountains together</em> are exactly right for talking about possibility and polite suggestions. Keep using these structures.</li>\n  <li>Self-check: after you write a sentence, ask yourself — 'Is the noun countable or uncountable?' and 'Is the subject singular or plural?' This will catch the two patterns above every time.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29832	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi, Sandy!<br><br>I'm glad that you still remember me and I'm so excited as well too!<br><br>For that, I prefer to go to the concert because I want to hangout with you too, not just relax at home. We can have more fun example for shopping, go to the cafe or else.<br><br>But I didn't agree with you to go climbing, that too dangerous! You didn't hear the news? that some people falling to death while climbing that why I would to say no.<br><br>And last, I want to ask that after the concert what should we do next?<br><br>Can't wait to see you!<br><br>Nene	110	15.0	Hi Nene! Your email to Sandy is full of energy and personality. You did a fantastic job addressing all three parts of the task: you shared your excitement, chose the concert and explained why, gave a clear reason for skipping climbing, and ended with a thoughtful question. The way you wrote <em>Can't wait to see you!</em> makes the email feel warm and personal, exactly right for writing to a friend. To reach Band 5, we need to smooth out a few grammar trouble spots and make your sentences flow more naturally from one idea to the next. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm glad that you still remember me and I'm so excited as well too!</em> You clearly show your feelings about the visit, which is exactly what the first note asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to go to the concert because I want to hangout with you too, not just relax at home.</em> You stated your preference and gave a reason. The reader fully understands why you chose the concert.</li>\n  <li><em>I want to ask that after the concert what should we do next?</em> You included a relevant question about the plan, covering the third note completely.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone friendly and natural throughout, without any phrases that sound too direct or blunt. Your email is warm and enthusiastic, which is great for a friend. The only small issue is <em>I didn't agree with you to go climbing</em> — this sounds a little stiff. A Band 5 writer would soften it.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I didn't agree with you to go climbing</em> → <strong>I don't think climbing is a good idea for us</strong>. This keeps the disagreement friendly and natural between friends.</li>\n  <li><em>that too dangerous! You didn't hear the news?</em> → <strong>It's too dangerous! Haven't you heard the news?</strong>. Using a question here is fine, but the grammar needs to be correct so the tone stays smooth.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never feels a jump between paragraphs. You have clear paragraphs for each point, which is good. But the move from the concert to climbing to your question feels a bit sudden, like each paragraph starts a new topic without a bridge.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>For that, I prefer to go to the concert...</em> → <strong>For that reason, I'd prefer to go to the concert...</strong> Adding 'reason' makes the link to your earlier excitement clearer.</li>\n  <li><em>But I didn't agree with you to go climbing...</em> → <strong>But I don't agree about going climbing...</strong> A smoother transition word like 'However' or 'As for climbing' would help the reader follow your change of topic.</li>\n  <li><em>And last, I want to ask that after the concert what should we do next?</em> → <strong>Finally, I'd like to ask what we should do after the concert.</strong> 'Finally' is a more natural connector than 'And last' for the final point.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple and some complex grammatical forms with few errors. Your vocabulary is good — words like <em>excited</em>, <em>hangout</em>, <em>dangerous</em>, and <em>falling to death</em> show you have a strong range. The main grammar pattern that needs work is how you form negative statements and questions with 'to' after verbs. Let's fix the key errors.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm so excited as well too</em> → <strong>I'm so excited too</strong> or <strong>I'm excited as well</strong>. 'As well' and 'too' mean the same thing — using both is redundant. Pick one.</li>\n  <li><em>I didn't agree with you to go climbing, that too dangerous!</em> → <strong>I don't agree with going climbing — it's too dangerous!</strong> After 'agree', we don't use 'to' + verb. Also, every sentence needs a subject and a verb: 'that too dangerous' is missing 'is'.</li>\n  <li><em>some people falling to death while climbing that why I would to say no</em> → <strong>some people have fallen to their death while climbing, and that's why I would say no</strong>. After 'would', use the base verb (say), not 'to say'. Also, 'that why' needs a verb: 'that is why' or 'that's why'.</li>\n  <li><em>I want to ask that after the concert what should we do next?</em> → <strong>I want to ask what we should do after the concert</strong>. When you embed a question inside a sentence, the word order changes: 'what should we do' becomes 'what we should do'.</li>\n  <li>Check your capital letters: <em>that some people falling to death while climbing that why I would to say no</em> — this sentence starts with a lowercase 't'. Every new sentence must begin with a capital letter.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29886	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi!<br><br>Me too! I'm so excited. I can't wait to come<br><br>Well, that's a great idea. For me I would prefer to go to the concert rather than staying at home. because I want to listen to the live music and I know you wanna do too! Also I rather say no to the climbing because I'm not good at it. beside that im also afraid of height so that's a big No for me.<br><br>I'm curious, what's for the dinner, can we order a Pizza or maybe a fried chicken.<br><br>See you later.<br><br>Captain.	111	15.0	Captain, your email is full of energy and personality. You did a great job addressing every part of the task: you showed excitement about the visit, chose the concert over relaxing at home, explained why you can't go climbing, and asked about dinner plans. The reader feels like they're talking to a real friend. To reach Band 5 on your next draft, you'll want to polish a few small things in how you connect your ideas and how you use grammar. Let's look at each part of your writing.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Me too! I'm so excited. I can't wait to come</em> You clearly showed your enthusiasm for the visit, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I would prefer to go to the concert rather than staying at home</em> You made your choice clear and gave a reason, so the reader understands your preference.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm curious, what's for the dinner, can we order a Pizza or maybe a fried chicken.</em> You asked a relevant question about dinner plans, which shows you've thought about the practical side of the visit.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone friendly but uses slightly more natural email language throughout. Your email feels warm and real, which is great. Two small spots could be more polished.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I know you wanna do too!</em> → <strong>I know you want to do that too!</strong> 'Wanna' is fine in speech, but in writing for this task, the full form sounds more careful and still friendly.</li>\n  <li><em>so that's a big No for me.</em> → <strong>so that's a no for me.</strong> Capitalising 'No' makes it look like a title or a sign. A lowercase 'no' keeps the tone natural.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects each idea smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. Your email has a clear order, but two spots could flow better.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>beside that im also afraid of height so that's a big No for me.</em> → <strong>Besides that, I'm also afraid of heights, so that's a no for me.</strong> 'Beside' means 'next to', but 'besides' means 'in addition'. Also, 'heights' is plural when talking about the fear.</li>\n  <li><em>so that's a big No for me. I'm curious, what's for the dinner</em> The jump from refusing climbing to asking about dinner is abrupt. Try adding a short bridge like: <strong>Anyway, I'm curious — what's for dinner?</strong> This signals a new topic smoothly.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and capitalisation so that errors don't distract the reader. You have good vocabulary and mostly correct tenses. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>Every sentence must start with a capital letter, and common nouns (like 'pizza', 'dinner', 'height') should not be capitalised mid-sentence.</strong> Let's fix the patterns together.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>because I want to listen to the live music</em> → <strong>because I want to listen to live music</strong> You started this sentence with a lowercase 'b'. Also, 'the live music' sounds like a specific concert, but you mean live music in general. No 'the' needed here.</li>\n  <li><em>beside that im also afraid of height</em> → <strong>Besides that, I'm also afraid of heights</strong> 'Im' needs an apostrophe ('I'm'), and 'height' should be 'heights' (the fear of heights is always plural).</li>\n  <li><em>what's for the dinner, can we order a Pizza or maybe a fried chicken.</em> → <strong>What's for dinner? Can we order pizza or maybe fried chicken?</strong> 'Dinner' doesn't need 'the' when talking about a meal in general. 'Pizza' and 'fried chicken' are general foods, so no capital letters. Also, this is two questions, so use a question mark after each.</li>\n  <li>One small grammar fix: <em>I rather say no</em> → <strong>I'd rather say no</strong> or <strong>I would rather say no</strong>. 'Rather' needs a helper verb ('would' or 'd') to sound natural.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
31416	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi, Sandy<br><br>Me too! I really miss you we haven't met for 3 month.<br><br>On my first evening I would like to go to the concert. I want to watch concert with you just like my childhood dream. Imagine us sing and enjoy together, that will be a great memories, if you want to relax at home we can also do that for next evening because I've stay for a whole week. Don't forget to match our cloth!<br><br>I don't want to go climbing in the mountian, because I'm so scare of hight. Last year I went with my sister and it very scary.<br><br>Would you want to go shopping at the mall? I've heard that have new cloth shop open nearby there, or would you want to visit anywhere, let me know.<br><br>Can't wait to see you!<br><br>Tonnam	158	15.0	Tonnam, your email to Sandy is full of genuine warmth and personality. You did a great job covering every part of the task: you responded to her excitement, stated your preference for the concert, explained why you don't want to climb, and asked a thoughtful question about shopping. The way you connected the concert to your childhood dream made your reason feel real and personal. To reach the next level, focus on making your grammar more accurate and your paragraph transitions smoother. Your ideas are strong — now it's about polishing how you express them.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>On my first evening I would like to go to the concert. I want to watch concert with you just like my childhood dream.</em> You clearly stated your preference and gave a personal, meaningful reason. The reader understands exactly what you want and why it matters to you.</li><li><em>I don't want to go climbing in the mountian, because I'm so scare of hight. Last year I went with my sister and it very scary.</em> You explained why you can't climb with a specific personal experience. The reader fully understands your decision.</li><li><em>Would you want to go shopping at the mall? I've heard that have new cloth shop open nearby there, or would you want to visit anywhere, let me know.</em> You asked a relevant question that keeps the conversation going. This shows you've thought about what Sandy might enjoy doing together.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer would keep the friendly, enthusiastic tone consistent throughout without any unclear phrases. Your email sounds like a real conversation between friends, which is excellent. The only thing holding you back is the phrase <em>match our cloth</em> — a native speaker would not understand what you mean here.</p><ul><li><em>Don't forget to match our cloth!</em> → <strong>Don't forget to coordinate our clothes!</strong> or <strong>Don't forget to dress alike!</strong> This is the clearest way to say you want to wear matching outfits.</li><li>Your opening and closing are perfect for an email to a friend. <em>Hi, Sandy</em> and <em>Can't wait to see you!</em> set exactly the right warm, excited tone.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer would connect their ideas so smoothly that the reader never feels a jump between topics. You used paragraphs well to separate the concert, climbing, and shopping ideas. However, the move from the concert to relaxing at home to matching clothes feels like several thoughts packed together, and the jump from climbing to shopping is abrupt.</p><ul><li><em>Imagine us sing and enjoy together, that will be a great memories, if you want to relax at home we can also do that for next evening because I've stay for a whole week. Don't forget to match our cloth!</em> → Try: <strong>Imagine us singing and enjoying the concert together — it would be a great memory. If you prefer to relax at home, we can do that another evening since I'm staying for a whole week. And don't forget to coordinate our clothes!</strong> This keeps the concert idea as the main focus and makes the relaxation option feel like an alternative, not a new topic.</li><li><em>I don't want to go climbing in the mountian, because I'm so scare of hight. Last year I went with my sister and it very scary.</em> → Then: <strong>Instead, would you want to go shopping at the mall?</strong> Adding a transition word like <em>instead</em> or <em>how about</em> would make the switch from climbing to shopping feel natural.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer would control basic grammar consistently so errors don't distract the reader. Your vocabulary is good — words like <em>childhood dream</em>, <em>memories</em>, and <em>coordinate</em> show you have strong ideas. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>after a form of 'be' (am/is/are/was/were), use an adjective, not a noun or verb.</strong></p><ul><li><em>I'm so scare of hight</em> → <strong>I'm so scared of heights</strong>. After 'am' (a form of 'be'), you need the adjective <em>scared</em>, not the verb <em>scare</em>. Also, 'height' is spelled with an 'e' before the 'i'.</li><li><em>it very scary</em> → <strong>it was very scary</strong>. You need the verb 'was' here. Every sentence needs a main verb. 'Very scary' is an adjective phrase, not a complete verb.</li><li><em>we haven't met for 3 month</em> → <strong>we haven't met for 3 months</strong>. After a number (3, 10, etc.), use the plural form of the noun. Also, capitalise the start of each sentence — <em>Me too!</em> is fine, but check that every new sentence begins with a capital letter.</li><li><em>I've stay for a whole week</em> → <strong>I've stayed for a whole week</strong> or <strong>I'm staying for a whole week</strong>. After 'have' (I've), use the past participle: <em>stayed</em>. If you mean right now, use present continuous: <em>I'm staying</em>.</li><li><em>have new cloth shop open</em> → <strong>there's a new clothes shop that has opened</strong>. The word 'there' is needed to introduce the idea. Also, 'cloth' is fabric; 'clothes' is what you wear.</li></ul><p>Self-check for every sentence: Can I find the main verb? Is it in the correct form? If the sentence has 'am/is/are/was/were', is the word after it an adjective (scared, happy, tall)?</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33896	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy,<br><br>I'm also excited for coming to your home! I really miss you so much! On my first evening, I would like to go to the rock concert because I really love rock music! As for climbing, I would like to stay at home for relax instead of go climbing because I reckon I'll tired after the concert. Can I bring my cat to your home? I want to look after him carefully because his leg is hurting.<br><br>Can't wait!<br><br>Kati	85	18.0	Kati, your email to Sandy is full of energy and warmth. You made every single detail from the task feel personal and real. The way you said <em>I really miss you so much!</em> and then connected your love of rock music to your choice of the concert shows that you were thinking like a real friend writing a real email. That is exactly what this task asks for. To reach a perfect score in Organisation and Language, you need to tighten a few small things. Let's look at each area one by one.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I would like to go to the rock concert because I really love rock music!</em> You clearly chose the concert and gave a personal reason. The reader understands your preference completely.</li><li><em>I would like to stay at home for relax instead of go climbing because I reckon I'll tired after the concert.</em> You explained why you don't want to climb, connecting it to being tired from the concert. That is logical and thoughtful.</li><li><em>Can I bring my cat to your home? I want to look after him carefully because his leg is hurting.</em> You asked a relevant question and gave a kind reason. The reader knows exactly what you need.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Hi Sandy,</em> and <em>Can't wait!</em> Your opening and closing are perfect for a friendly email. They set a warm, excited tone from the start.</li><li><em>I really miss you so much!</em> This sounds like a real friend talking. It makes the email feel personal and genuine.</li><li><em>I reckon I'll tired after the concert.</em> Even with the grammar slip, the informal word 'reckon' fits the friendly register you are using. Your tone is consistent throughout.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects each idea smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. Your email has a clear order: excitement, concert, climbing, cat question. The jump from the concert to climbing is a little sudden. Let's make it flow better.</p><ul><li><em>I would like to go to the rock concert because I really love rock music! As for climbing, I would like to stay at home...</em> → <strong>I would like to go to the rock concert because I really love rock music! After the concert, I will probably be tired, so as for climbing, I would like to stay at home...</strong> Adding 'After the concert, I will probably be tired' connects the two ideas naturally.</li><li><em>Can I bring my cat to your home?</em> → <strong>By the way, can I bring my cat to your home?</strong> Adding 'By the way' makes the question feel like a natural afterthought instead of a sudden topic change.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of grammar and vocabulary with only very minor slips. Your vocabulary is strong (<em>excited</em>, <em>rock concert</em>, <em>climbing</em>, <em>look after</em>, <em>leg is hurting</em>). The main rule that will help you reach Band 5 is: after prepositions (like 'for', 'of', 'to', 'in', 'on', 'at'), use the <strong>-ing form</strong> of the verb, not the base form or infinitive. Also, after 'will' or 'would', use the base form of the verb.</p><ul><li><em>I'm also excited for coming to your home!</em> → <strong>I'm also excited about coming to your home!</strong> 'Excited about' is the correct preposition. You used 'for coming' correctly (preposition + -ing), so you already know this rule in one place.</li><li><em>I would like to stay at home for relax</em> → <strong>I would like to stay at home to relax</strong> or <strong>for relaxing</strong>. After 'for', use the -ing form: 'for relaxing'. Or use 'to relax' to show purpose.</li><li><em>I reckon I'll tired</em> → <strong>I reckon I'll be tired</strong>. After 'will' (or 'I'll'), you need the base form of the verb. 'Tired' is an adjective, so you need 'be' before it: 'I'll be tired'.</li><li><em>instead of go climbing</em> → <strong>instead of going climbing</strong>. After 'of', use the -ing form: 'of going'.</li><li><strong>Self-check:</strong> Before you finish any sentence, ask yourself: 'Did I use the -ing form after a preposition? Did I use the base form after will/would?' If you check those two spots, most of your small errors will disappear.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34912	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy,<br><br>Thanks for your invitation - it is fabulous! First, I'd prefer us to relax at home since it's on the evening, so I think I'll be a bit exhausted. As for climbing in the mountains, I suppose we shall not do so. This is because it's quite risky. For instance, we could get injuries like broken arms or legs. Ultimately, I have few questions. Do you want me to bring anything? Also, do you mind if my friend come with me? And the last question, can we play football or badminton? That way we can play without having risk our lives by climbing mountains.<br><br>Can't wait<br><br>See you soon<br><br>Here	124	18.0	Hero, your email to Sandy is warm and personal from the very first line. The way you opened with <em>Thanks for your invitation - it is fabulous!</em> immediately creates a friendly, excited tone that makes the reader want to keep reading. You covered every point the task asked for: you stated your preference to relax, explained why climbing is not for you, and asked several thoughtful questions. That is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. To push even higher, focus on polishing a few small grammar spots and tightening the flow between your ideas. Let me walk through each skill area so you know exactly what to adjust for your next draft.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I'd prefer us to relax at home since it's on the evening, so I think I'll be a bit exhausted.</em> You clearly stated your preference and gave a personal reason. The reader understands why you want a quiet first evening.</li><li><em>This is because it's quite risky. For instance, we could get injuries like broken arms or legs.</em> You explained your objection to climbing with a concrete example. The reader is fully informed about your concern.</li><li><em>Do you want me to bring anything? Also, do you mind if my friend come with me? And the last question, can we play football or badminton?</em> You asked three relevant questions that show you are thinking about the practical side of the visit. This is exactly what a good email to a friend does.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Hi Sandy,</em> and <em>Can't wait</em> / <em>See you soon</em> — Your opening and closing are warm and appropriate for a friend. The reader immediately knows this is a personal email.</li><li><em>fabulous</em> and <em>Can't wait</em> — These casual, enthusiastic words create a friendly tone that matches the task perfectly. You sound like a real person writing to a real friend.</li><li><em>Do you mind if my friend come with me?</em> — Even with a small grammar slip, this question engages the reader naturally. You are inviting Sandy into a conversation, which is the heart of a good email.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every idea with smooth transitions so the reader never feels a jump. Your email flows well through the main points, but the final paragraph tries to do too many things at once.</p><ul><li><em>Ultimately, I have few questions. Do you want me to bring anything? Also, do you mind if my friend come with me? And the last question, can we play football or badminton? That way we can play without having risk our lives by climbing mountains.</em> → <strong>I also have a few questions. First, do you want me to bring anything? Second, do you mind if my friend comes with me? And finally, can we play football or badminton? That way we can still have fun without risking our lives climbing mountains.</strong> By numbering your questions with <em>First, Second, Finally</em>, you give the reader a clear path through your list. The closing thought about playing safely then connects naturally to the last question.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammar with very few errors. Your vocabulary is strong — <em>fabulous, exhausted, risky, injuries</em> — and your sentences are mostly clear. One pattern keeps appearing: prepositions and verb forms after certain words need attention.</p><ul><li><em>it's on the evening</em> → <strong>it's in the evening</strong>. With parts of the day, use <em>in</em>: <em>in the morning, in the afternoon, in the evening</em>. Use <em>on</em> only with specific days: <em>on Monday, on the evening of July 5th</em>.</li><li><em>my friend come with me</em> → <strong>my friend comes with me</strong>. After <em>Do you mind if</em>, the verb must match the subject. Since <em>my friend</em> is singular (one friend), the verb needs an <em>-s</em>: <em>comes</em>. Say it aloud: <em>Do you mind if my friend comes?</em> — that sounds right.</li><li><em>without having risk our lives</em> → <strong>without risking our lives</strong>. After <em>without</em>, use the <em>-ing</em> form of the verb. Think of it this way: <em>without + verb-ing</em> is a fixed pattern. <em>without running, without eating, without risking</em>.</li><li>Your capitalisation is mostly correct, but check one spot: <em>Can't wait</em> followed by <em>See you soon</em> and then <em>Here</em> — <em>Here</em> is not a standard closing for an email. You probably meant <em>Yours</em> or <em>Best wishes</em>. A small fix, but it matters for a polished finish.</li><li>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: ask yourself, 'What word comes before the verb? Is it <em>without</em>? Then I need <em>-ing</em>. Is it <em>Do you mind if</em>? Then the subject and verb must agree.'</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34924	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy,<br><br>I'm so excited too. I can't wait to meet you for a week!<br><br>I would prefer to go to the concert because we can have fun with the music by the way and we also relax to. At concert it's look excited move relax at home. Also we could go climbing in the mountains. After we leave concert we will have move activities to do. So before we go climbing we should to change clothes for our climbing time. Should we or we take the clothes to when we go concert. Let me know what you prefer, and see you next week!<br><br>See you Sandy<br><br>Ping Ping	120	8.0	Ping-Ping, you wrote a friendly email to Sandy that shows you understand the situation. Your opening line <em>I'm so excited too. I can't wait to meet you for a week!</em> sounds natural and warm, like a real message to a friend. That's a strong start. To reach Band 5, you need to make sure every part of the task is answered clearly. The task asked you to respond to three notes: agree about being excited, say which activity you prefer and why, and ask a question. Your preference for the concert is clear, but the climbing part and the question need more work. Let's look at each area so you know exactly what to improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer responds to every note in the task completely and clearly, without leaving the reader confused. You handled the first note well, but the climbing note and the question note need attention.</p><ul><li><em>I would prefer to go to the concert because we can have fun with the music by the way and we also relax to.</em> This tells Sandy you prefer the concert, but you didn't give a clear reason for <strong>not</strong> wanting to climb. Try: <strong>I would prefer to go to the concert because I love music and it helps me relax. I don't think climbing is a good idea because I am not very strong.</strong></li><li><em>Should we or we take the clothes to when we go concert.</em> This is meant to be your question, but it is hard to understand. A clear question would be: <strong>Should we bring extra clothes to the concert, or should we go home first to change?</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone for an email to a friend and makes every idea easy to follow. Your salutation <em>Hi Sandy</em> and closing <em>See you Sandy</em> are appropriate, but some parts of the email are confusing.</p><ul><li><em>At concert it's look excited move relax at home.</em> This sentence is unclear. The reader has to guess what you mean. Try: <strong>At the concert, we can move around and have fun, and later we can relax at home.</strong></li><li><em>After we leave concert we will have move activities to do.</em> The word 'move' here is confusing. Do you mean 'more' activities? A clearer version: <strong>After we leave the concert, we will have more activities to do.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges ideas in a logical order and uses linking words to connect them smoothly. You used paragraph breaks, which is good, but the flow from the concert to climbing is sudden and confusing.</p><ul><li><em>I would prefer to go to the concert because we can have fun with the music by the way and we also relax to. At concert it's look excited move relax at home. Also we could go climbing in the mountains.</em> You jump from the concert to climbing without a clear transition. Try adding a sentence like: <strong>After the concert, we could also go climbing in the mountains.</strong> This connects the two ideas naturally.</li><li><em>So before we go climbing we should to change clothes for our climbing time. Should we or we take the clothes to when we go concert.</em> The question at the end is hard to follow because the order of ideas is mixed. Put the question in its own sentence: <strong>Should we bring our climbing clothes to the concert so we can change there?</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar and vocabulary most of the time, even with simple sentences. Your main challenge is with verb forms and word choice. Let's work on a few patterns.</p><ul><li><em>we also relax to</em> → <strong>we can also relax</strong>. The word 'to' at the end is not needed here. In English, 'relax' does not need a preposition after it unless you add an object, like 'relax to music'.</li><li><em>it's look excited move relax at home</em> → <strong>it looks exciting to move around and relax at home</strong>. You used 'look' as a verb, but it needs the '-s' ending for 'it'. Also, 'excited' describes how a person feels, while 'exciting' describes the thing that causes the feeling. The concert is exciting, not excited.</li><li><em>we should to change</em> → <strong>we should change</strong>. After 'should', we do not use 'to'. The pattern is: should + base verb (should go, should eat, should change).</li><li><em>we take the clothes to when we go concert</em> → <strong>we take the clothes when we go to the concert</strong>. The word 'to' belongs with 'go to the concert', not at the end of the sentence. Also, 'concert' needs an article: 'the concert'.</li></ul><p>A good self-check: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Does this sound like something a native speaker would say?' If it feels strange, try breaking it into shorter sentences.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
