34926	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi,<br><br>I'm also super excited to stay with you too!<br><br>I'd prefer us to relax at home. Because I heard that your home go a play station 5 and I really wanted to play it. As for food, I'll bring 3 tray of pizzas and a bucket of KFC. I'll also bring some popcorns just incase we want to watch some movies together.<br><br>No, because I don't really like climbing and I'm afraid of heights.<br><br>Is there a swimming pool in your house? if so I'll bring my swimsuit.<br><br>See you soon<br>Chopin	111	15.0	Chopin, your email is lively and clear. You answered every part of the task with enthusiasm, and your reader would feel genuinely invited into your plans. The way you connected your preference to relax at home with the PlayStation 5 and the food you'll bring shows you thought carefully about what makes a fun visit. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll need to polish your grammar and vary how you link your ideas. Let's look at each subscale now.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I'm also super excited to stay with you too!</em> You expressed excitement clearly and warmly, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>I'd prefer us to relax at home. Because I heard that your home go a play station 5 and I really wanted to play it.</em> You stated your preference and gave a specific, personal reason. The reader understands your choice completely.</li><li><em>Is there a swimming pool in your house? if so I'll bring my swimsuit.</em> You asked a relevant question that shows you've thought about the visit and prepared for it. All three notes are fully addressed.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistently natural and appropriate tone throughout the email, with no awkward phrases that might distract the reader. Your opening and closing are friendly, and your enthusiasm comes through well.</p><ul><li><em>I heard that your home go a play station 5</em> → <strong>I heard that your home has a PlayStation 5</strong>. The original phrase 'go a' is a bit awkward and might confuse the reader. Using 'has' makes the sentence sound natural and clear.</li><li><em>See you soon</em> → <strong>See you soon!</strong> Adding an exclamation mark keeps the tone consistent with your earlier excitement. This small change helps the closing feel as energetic as the rest of the email.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a range of linking words and cohesive devices to connect ideas smoothly, not just simple connectors like 'because' and 'as for'. Your email follows a logical order, which is good, but the connections between sentences could be more varied.</p><ul><li><em>I'd prefer us to relax at home. Because I heard that your home go a play station 5...</em> → <strong>I'd prefer us to relax at home, because I heard that your home has a PlayStation 5...</strong> Starting a sentence with 'Because' is grammatically incomplete. Joining the two ideas with a comma makes the connection tighter and more natural.</li><li><em>As for food, I'll bring 3 tray of pizzas and a bucket of KFC. I'll also bring some popcorns just incase we want to watch some movies together.</em> → <strong>For food, I'll bring three trays of pizzas and a bucket of KFC. I'll also bring some popcorn in case we want to watch movies together.</strong> Using 'as for' is fine, but varying your transitions with 'for' or 'regarding' would show more range. The word 'also' appears twice in the email; you could replace the second one with 'besides that' or 'in addition'.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple and some complex grammatical forms with few errors, and uses a range of everyday vocabulary accurately. The main rule that will help you improve is subject-verb agreement and noun countability. Let's look at your specific examples.</p><ul><li><em>your home go a play station 5</em> → <strong>your home has a PlayStation 5</strong>. The verb 'go' doesn't work here. 'Has' (from 'have') is the correct verb for possession. Remember: a home 'has' things, it doesn't 'go' things.</li><li><em>3 tray of pizzas</em> → <strong>three trays of pizza</strong>. 'Tray' is a countable noun, so you need the plural 'trays'. Also, 'pizza' is usually uncountable when talking about the food in general, so 'pizza' (not 'pizzas') is more natural here.</li><li><em>some popcorns</em> → <strong>some popcorn</strong>. 'Popcorn' is uncountable in English, so it does not take an 's'. You can say 'some popcorn' or 'a bag of popcorn', but never 'popcorns'.</li><li><em>just incase</em> → <strong>just in case</strong>. 'In case' is two words. This is a common fixed expression. Write it as two separate words every time.</li><li><em>if so I'll bring my swimsuit</em> → <strong>If so, I'll bring my swimsuit</strong>. When you start a sentence with 'if so', you need a comma after it. Also, check your capitalisation: the word 'if' at the start of a sentence should be capitalised.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After you write a sentence, read it aloud and ask yourself: 'Does the subject match the verb? Is this noun countable or uncountable?' This will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34931	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hello,<br><br>I'm feeling so excited too. I always wait for your email. Thanks for invited me<br>I can't wait anymore!<br><br>In the evening, you told me there's a rock concert right? so why would I stay at home?<br>Which I prefer concert and I heard that they will be giving free band poster in the concert.<br><br>Also, I don't think we should go climbing in the mountains. Lately I've got a trouble on my<br>legs which is pretty annoying, so I don't think we should go climbing because I'm unable to<br>use my fully strength.<br><br>By the way, I heard that our town has a new restaurant, Sea Food Chili's. Shall we have a<br>dinner there? I heard that the favors are brilliant.<br><br>See you soon<br>Frame	151	15.0	Frame, your email to a friend is genuinely engaging. You covered every point from the task clearly: your excitement, your preference for the concert with a reason, your honest explanation about not climbing, and a thoughtful question about the new restaurant. The reader feels like they're talking to a real person. You have strong instincts for content. To reach Band 5 across all areas, the next step is to polish your grammar and connect your ideas more smoothly. Let's look at each part of the scoring guide.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm feeling so excited too. I always wait for your email.</em> You opened with genuine enthusiasm, which immediately makes the reader feel welcome and sets a friendly tone.</li>\n  <li><em>Which I prefer concert and I heard that they will be giving free band poster in the concert.</em> You clearly stated your choice (the concert) and gave a specific reason (the free poster). The task's first note is fully addressed.</li>\n  <li><em>Lately I've got a trouble on my legs which is pretty annoying, so I don't think we should go climbing because I'm unable to use my fully strength.</em> You explained why you can't climb with a personal, honest reason. The reader understands your situation completely.</li>\n  <li><em>Shall we have a dinner there? I heard that the favors are brilliant.</em> You asked a relevant question about the new restaurant, which shows you are thinking about the visit and keeping the conversation going.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the register perfectly consistent throughout the email. Your tone is friendly and enthusiastic, which is exactly right for writing to a friend. One small thing pulls the register slightly off balance.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>so why would I stay at home?</em> → <strong>so there's no way I'd stay at home!</strong> The rhetorical question feels a bit too casual and could confuse the reader. A clear statement keeps the tone warm but controlled.</li>\n  <li><em>Thanks for invited me</em> → <strong>Thanks for inviting me</strong> This error in form affects the reader's impression of your language control. Fixing it helps the whole email feel more polished.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words to create smooth, natural transitions between ideas. Your email has a logical sequence, but the connections between your points feel a bit abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I can't wait anymore!<br><br>In the evening, you told me there's a rock concert right?</em> → <strong>I can't wait anymore! Speaking of which, you mentioned there's a rock concert in the evening, right?</strong> Adding a phrase like 'Speaking of which' connects your excitement directly to the concert topic.</li>\n  <li><em>Also, I don't think we should go climbing in the mountains.</em> → <strong>As for climbing, I don't think we should go to the mountains.</strong> Using 'As for' or 'Regarding' makes the topic shift feel more deliberate and less sudden.</li>\n  <li><em>By the way, I heard that our town has a new restaurant...</em> This is a good connector, but you used only 'Also' and 'By the way' to move between topics. Try adding one more variety, like 'On another note' or 'Speaking of food'.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently, even when trying new ideas. The main rule you need to practise is: after a preposition (like 'for') or a verb that acts like a preposition (like 'thanks for'), use the -ing form of the next verb. Also, watch your word choice for body parts and nouns.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thanks for invited me</em> → <strong>Thanks for inviting me</strong>. After 'for', the verb must end in -ing. This is a fixed pattern.</li>\n  <li><em>I've got a trouble on my legs</em> → <strong>I've got trouble with my legs</strong> or <strong>I have a problem with my legs</strong>. 'Trouble' is uncountable here, so no 'a'. Also, we say 'trouble with' a body part, not 'on'.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm unable to use my fully strength</em> → <strong>I'm unable to use my full strength</strong>. 'Full' is an adjective modifying 'strength'; 'fully' is an adverb and doesn't work before a noun.</li>\n  <li><em>the favors are brilliant</em> → <strong>the flavours are brilliant</strong>. 'Favors' means things you do to help someone. 'Flavours' means tastes. These are easy to confuse but change the meaning completely.</li>\n  <li><em>Which I prefer concert</em> → <strong>I prefer the concert</strong>. 'Which' at the start of a sentence often creates a fragment. Also, 'concert' needs an article here ('the concert').</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check: Before you send an email, read each sentence aloud. Ask yourself: 'Did I use -ing after for? Did I use the right noun? Does every sentence have a main subject and verb?'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34936	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy,<br><br>Me too! Sandy I can't wait for this week. As for this evening I would prefer being relax at home. Mountian climbing? well No Sandy because we will be tired and sometime will get in danger while climbing up mountain. I have a question for you Sandy if there a roald near your place.<br><br>See you now Sandy	65	9.0	Richter, your email has a friendly, personal feel, and you clearly addressed all three parts of the task: you showed excitement, stated your preference, gave a reason, and asked a question. That's a strong start. The phrase <em>Me too! Sandy I can't wait for this week</em> captures real enthusiasm, and your reason for not climbing — <em>we will be tired and sometime will get in danger</em> — shows you thought about the situation. To reach Band 5, your writing needs to be clearer for the reader. Some sentences are missing words or have spelling errors that make the meaning hard to follow. The question at the end, for example, is difficult to understand. Let's look at each part of your writing to see exactly how to improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer covers all three notes fully and clearly so the reader understands everything. You did cover all three notes, but the question at the end is unclear.</p><ul><li><em>if there a roald near your place</em> → <strong>if there is a road near your place.</strong> The reader cannot tell what you are asking about. Adding the verb 'is' and spelling 'road' correctly makes your question complete.</li><li><em>I would prefer being relax at home</em> → <strong>I would prefer to relax at home.</strong> This is a clear preference, but the grammar makes it slightly confusing. Fixing it makes your meaning immediate.</li><li><em>we will be tired and sometime will get in danger while climbing up mountain</em> → <strong>we will be tired and might get into danger while climbing up the mountain.</strong> Your reason is good, but small word choices ('sometime' → 'sometimes', 'in danger' → 'into danger', 'mountain' → 'the mountain') make the idea much clearer.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent, appropriate register throughout the email. Your opening is friendly, but the closing and some phrases feel abrupt.</p><ul><li><em>See you now Sandy</em> → <strong>See you soon, Sandy</strong> or <strong>Talk to you later, Sandy.</strong> 'See you now' sounds like you are ending a conversation in person, not an email. A warmer closing matches your friendly opening.</li><li><em>Mountian climbing? well No Sandy because...</em> → <strong>Mountain climbing? Well, no, Sandy, because...</strong> The capital 'N' in 'No' and the missing comma after 'well' make the tone feel choppy. Smoother punctuation helps the reader hear your friendly voice.</li><li>The overall tone is good — you use 'Sandy' directly and show excitement. Just make sure every sentence sounds like you are talking to a friend, not rushing through the message.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow the flow easily. Your email moves from excitement to evening plans to climbing to a question, but the jumps are sudden.</p><ul><li><em>Me too! Sandy I can't wait for this week. As for this evening I would prefer being relax at home.</em> → <strong>Me too! I can't wait for this week. As for this evening, I would prefer to relax at home.</strong> The transition from excitement to evening plans works, but adding a comma after 'evening' and fixing the grammar makes the connection feel natural.</li><li><em>Mountian climbing? well No Sandy because we will be tired...</em> → <strong>Mountain climbing? Well, no, Sandy, because we will be tired...</strong> You used a question to introduce the topic, which is good. Adding a comma after 'well' and using a lowercase 'n' for 'no' (since it's part of the sentence) makes the sequence smoother.</li><li>Try using linking words like 'also' or 'another thing' to connect your question to the rest of the email. For example: <strong>Also, I have a question for you: is there a road near your place?</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and spelling so the reader never has to guess. Your vocabulary is good for the task — 'excited', 'relax', 'tired', 'danger' — but errors in spelling and verb forms are frequent. The main rule to focus on is: after 'prefer', use 'to + verb' (prefer to relax), not 'being'. Also, check every sentence for a main verb.</p><ul><li><em>I would prefer being relax at home</em> → <strong>I would prefer to relax at home.</strong> After 'prefer', use 'to' + the base verb. 'Being relax' is incorrect because 'being' is not the right form here.</li><li><em>sometime will get in danger</em> → <strong>sometimes we might get into danger.</strong> 'Sometime' means 'at an unspecified time'. 'Sometimes' means 'occasionally'. Also, 'in danger' is not the right phrase — we say 'get into danger' or 'be in danger'.</li><li><em>if there a roald near your place</em> → <strong>if there is a road near your place.</strong> Every sentence needs a main verb. 'There a road' is missing the verb 'is'. Also, 'roald' should be 'road'.</li><li><em>Mountian</em> → <strong>Mountain.</strong> This is a spelling error. The word has an 'ou' sound, not 'ui'.</li><li>Check your capital letters: 'Sandy' is a name and should be capitalised, but 'No' in the middle of a sentence should be lowercase. Also, 'well' at the start of a sentence is fine, but 'Well' with a capital W is correct. A good self-check: read your email aloud and ask yourself, 'Does every sentence have a verb? Are all common words spelled correctly?'</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35309	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi,<br><br>I'm so excited to stay with you for the week!<br><br>Rock concert would be nice, however I'm not a big fan of rocks, and it's a long way from my place to yours, so I'd rather relax and spend some time with you, and as for rock climbing, I think it's a wonderful idea, and let's bring your dog Sandy too! that way he can enjoy the trees and I've heard that he loves birds. I'll bring some treats for him if that's okay, my dog loves it. I'm sure Sandy would too!<br><br>See you soon<br><br>Atom	108	11.0	Atom, you've written a friendly email that sounds like a real message to a friend, and that's a great start. I like how you opened with <em>I'm so excited to stay with you for the week!</em> — that immediately sets the right tone. You also included a thoughtful detail about bringing treats for Sandy, which shows you were thinking about your friend's dog. To reach Band 5, the most important thing is to read the task instructions very carefully and respond to every single note exactly as asked. Let's go through each part of your writing so you can see exactly what to fix.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer responds to all four notes in the task completely and correctly, without adding ideas that go against the instructions. Your email addresses the first note (excitement) well, but you misunderstood the other notes.</p><ul><li><em>Rock concert would be nice, however I'm not a big fan of rocks, and it's a long way from my place to yours, so I'd rather relax and spend some time with you</em> → <strong>I'm excited about the rock concert, but it's far from my place. Would you prefer to go to the concert or relax at home?</strong>. The task asked you to choose between concert and relaxing at home, not to reject the concert and choose relaxing.</li><li><em>as for rock climbing, I think it's a wonderful idea</em> → <strong>As for rock climbing, I'm sorry but I can't join you. I have weak knees and it's not safe for me.</strong>. The third note asked you to say no to rock climbing, but you said yes.</li><li>The fourth note asked you to ask Sandy something. You didn't include any question for Sandy. A Band 5 writer would add something like <strong>Do you want to bring anything special for our trip?</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses email conventions correctly and makes the purpose of each part clear to the reader. Your email has a friendly tone and a greeting, which is good.</p><ul><li><em>Hi,</em> and <em>See you soon</em> — these are perfect for an email to a friend. You've got the opening and closing right.</li><li><em>I'm so excited to stay with you for the week!</em> — this shows enthusiasm and matches the task's first note. Well done.</li><li>However, when you wrote <em>I think it's a wonderful idea</em> about rock climbing, the reader gets confused because the task said you should refuse. To reach Band 5, every part of your email must clearly match what the task asks you to do. Try: <strong>I'm sorry, but rock climbing isn't for me. My knees are weak, so I'll have to say no.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer links ideas smoothly and uses clear paragraph breaks for each new topic. Your email has good paragraph breaks using line spaces, which helps the reader follow your ideas.</p><ul><li>You used <em>however</em> to connect the concert idea to your preference — that's a good linking word. But the sentence is very long. Try breaking it: <strong>Rock concert would be nice. However, I'm not a big fan of rock music, and it's a long way from my place to yours. So I'd rather relax and spend some time with you.</strong></li><li>The transition from relaxing to rock climbing is too sudden. You wrote <em>so I'd rather relax and spend some time with you, and as for rock climbing</em>. A Band 5 writer would start a new paragraph: <strong>Now, about rock climbing — I'm sorry, but I can't join you.</strong></li><li>Your last paragraph mixes the dog idea with the treats idea. Try separating them: <strong>Let's bring your dog Sandy too! That way he can enjoy the trees. I've heard he loves birds. I'll bring some treats for him if that's okay. My dog loves treats, so I'm sure Sandy would too!</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. You used some good everyday words like <em>excited</em>, <em>relax</em>, and <em>treats</em>. Let's work on a few things to push you higher.</p><ul><li><em>I'm not a big fan of rocks</em> → <strong>I'm not a big fan of rock music</strong>. You meant the music genre, not actual rocks. This is a small word choice error that changes the meaning.</li><li><em>that way he can enjoy the trees and I've heard that he loves birds.</em> → <strong>That way he can enjoy the trees. I've heard that he loves birds.</strong> You started a new sentence without a capital letter. Always capitalise the first word of a new sentence. Also, check your capitalisation of <em>I</em> — you wrote <em>I've</em> correctly here, which is good.</li><li><em>I'll bring some treats for him if that's okay, my dog loves it.</em> → <strong>I'll bring some treats for him if that's okay. My dog loves treats.</strong> This is a comma splice — two complete sentences joined with only a comma. Use a period instead. Also, <em>it</em> is unclear; <em>treats</em> is clearer.</li><li><em>I'm sure Sandy would too!</em> — This is correct and natural. Good job with the auxiliary verb <em>would</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>Self-check tip:</strong> After you write each sentence, read it aloud. Ask yourself: 'Did I start with a capital letter? Did I end with a period? Did I use the right word for what I mean?' This will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29561	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	what kind of films do I enjoy? well I don't know but I kinda like the one that was horror and relaxing and animation what I prefer watching them at the cinema or at home well I prefer cinema because it was more quite and the animation at the screen was more clean and the food wasn't limit if we ate all of our popcorn we could go out and buy it again and also the movie that I was watching its name was 8 noon warrior Thai somthing I already forgot it but when it release at the movie when I was watching it at the cinema it was perfect the sound the screen and more that all.	121	10.0	Mathew, you've done a great job sharing your personal opinions about films and why you prefer the cinema. I can tell you really thought about your experience watching "8 noon warrior" and how the sound and screen made it perfect. That personal story is exactly the kind of detail that makes an article interesting to read. To reach Band 5, your next draft needs to focus on organising your ideas into clear paragraphs and using more precise grammar and vocabulary. Let's look at each area in detail.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer includes all the required information clearly and keeps every sentence focused on the task. You answered the main questions about film types and cinema preference, but some details like forgetting the film name don't help the reader.</p><ul><li><em>what kind of films do I enjoy? well I don't know but I kinda like the one that was horror and relaxing and animation</em> → <strong>What kind of films do I enjoy? I like horror, relaxing movies, and animation.</strong> You answered the question, but "I don't know" and "kinda" make it sound uncertain. State your preference directly.</li><li><em>the movie that I was watching its name was 8 noon warrior Thai somthing I already forgot it</em> → <strong>One movie I watched was called "8 Noon Warrior."</strong> Telling the reader you forgot the name doesn't add useful information. Either give the full name or leave it out.</li><li><em>when it release at the movie when I was watching it at the cinema it was perfect the sound the screen and more that all</em> → <strong>When I watched it at the cinema, the sound and screen were perfect.</strong> This is your strongest content point. Keep it and cut the extra words.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format naturally, with a clear voice and consistent informal tone throughout. You used a question to start and shared personal opinions, which is good for an article. The informal register works, but some phrases are too vague.</p><ul><li><em>what kind of films do I enjoy? well I don't know</em> → <strong>What kind of films do I enjoy? I enjoy several types.</strong> Starting with a question is a strong article technique. But "well I don't know" sounds like you're unsure. An article should show confidence.</li><li><em>I kinda like the one that was horror</em> → <strong>I like horror films.</strong> "Kinda" is too casual even for an informal article. Use "kind of" or just state your opinion directly.</li><li><em>and more that all</em> → <strong>and more.</strong> This ending feels unfinished. A stronger closing sentence would leave the reader satisfied.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer groups related ideas into clear paragraphs and uses linking words to show how ideas connect. Right now your article is one long block of text connected mainly by "and" and "well."</p><ul><li><em>what kind of films do I enjoy? well I don't know but I kinda like the one that was horror and relaxing and animation what I prefer watching them at the cinema or at home well I prefer cinema because...</em> → <strong>What kind of films do I enjoy? I like horror, relaxing movies, and animation. Do I prefer watching them at the cinema or at home? I prefer the cinema because...</strong> Use a new sentence or paragraph when you move to a new topic. Your question about cinema vs. home is a separate idea from the film types.</li><li><em>the food wasn't limit if we ate all of our popcorn we could go out and buy it again and also the movie that I was watching...</em> → <strong>The food wasn't limited. If we ate all of our popcorn, we could buy more. Also, the movie I was watching...</strong> Each new reason (food, then the movie) deserves its own sentence. Using "and" too many times makes the ideas blur together.</li><li>Try this structure for your next draft: Paragraph 1: types of films you like. Paragraph 2: cinema vs. home and reasons. Paragraph 3: a specific movie experience. This will help the reader follow your thinking.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. The main rule to focus on is <strong>subject-verb agreement and past tense</strong>. Many of your errors happen when the subject and verb don't match, or when you use the wrong tense.</p><ul><li><em>it was more quite</em> → <strong>it was more quiet</strong>. "Quite" means "very" or "fairly." "Quiet" means silent or calm. These are easy to mix up.</li><li><em>the food wasn't limit</em> → <strong>the food wasn't limited</strong>. After "wasn't" you need a past participle (limited) or an adjective, not the base verb.</li><li><em>when it release at the movie</em> → <strong>when it was released at the cinema</strong>. For a past event, use "was released" (passive voice). Also, "at the movie" is unclear. Use "at the cinema" or "in theatres."</li><li><em>I kinda like the one that was horror</em> → <strong>I like horror films</strong>. "The one that was horror" is awkward. Use the noun directly: "horror films" or "horror movies."</li><li>Check your capitalisation: <em>what kind of films do I enjoy?</em> should start with a capital W. Every sentence in English begins with a capital letter. Read your draft aloud and check each sentence start.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29031	2025-08-14 13:42:46.323372+00	2026	Thai Food	Food is an important aspect in every culture. Although all food gives you energy, they all vary in different cultures. In particular, desserts exists in all cultures, specifically Thailand. The sweet taste has been relevant since the old period. Three of the most popular desserts are Mango Sticky Rice, Bua loi and Kanom chan.<br><br><br>One of the most known desserts in Thailand is Mango Sticky Rice. This cuisine consists of mango and sticky rice, usually paired with Kathi (coconut milk). The simplest method is by peeling the outer mango skin and steam the rice with flour in order to make sticky rice. Mango Sticky Rice is extremely popular around the world. This symbolizes how much impact it had in the food industry due to its easy recipe and local ingredients. This reflects Thailand's civilians of how they behave towards certain things by trying to make the best use of things around their area, instead of buying globalized food.<br><br><br>Besides Mango Sticky Rice, Bua Loi is another beloved dish. There are various reciepes and different flavours, but using corn strach and Kathi (coconut milk) is recommended. You can start off by boiling the corn strach then add the food coloring into the pot to add the taste and color. Finally, shape them into a mold then add them to Kathi. Bua loi displays how creativity can be shown through cooking, such as, shaping the mold into a star shape. This dessert freely express one's creativity, especially for people in Thailand.<br><br><br>Finally, Kanom Chan is another popular dessert among Thai individuals. It too has a simple recipe, inculding Baitocy and gelatin. Firstly, stir the Baitocy and then add some additional ingredients or accessory up to one's personal preference. Then add the gelatin in. A lot of classic reciepes mostly make them into a squared shape, so it all could stack up, hence the name, Kanom Chan. This dessert overall expresses how Thais', since from the old times, had always make up the most and best use of the enviroment around them.<br><br><br>To summarize, desserts in Thailand all have unique reciepes and taste, but one thing they all have in common is their local ingredients that is easily found in their own homes. In addition, Thai people is delicate about using their abudant enviroment to their best potential, specifically through desserts.	388	\N	You have written a well-structured essay about Thai desserts, demonstrating a good understanding of the topic and effectively communicating your ideas. You successfully commented on the symbolic and cultural value of the food in your body paragraphs, which was a key requirement. To improve further, focus on refining your spelling and consistently using relative clauses and passive structures to add variety to your writing. Your vocabulary use is appropriate and shows flexibility.	\N	Writing	Formative	FIRST	Part 2	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2025-08-14
29579	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Had you ever watched film with your family? I love to watch film with my family so much.<br><br>I enjoy romantic -comedy films because I love when the characters have fun together ,and there are a lot of emotion to feel when I watched. I prefer watching film at home.	52	10.0	Ka-nhom, your article already has the right idea — you're telling readers about the films you enjoy and where you like to watch them. That's exactly what the task asked for. I especially like how you started with a question to the reader: <em>Had you ever watched film with your family?</em> That's a smart way to pull someone into your article. To reach Band 5, you'll need to develop your ideas more fully. Give the reader more reasons, more details, and a stronger sense of why these things matter to you. Let's look at each area where you can grow.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer gives the reader enough detail to fully understand their ideas. You told us you like romantic-comedy films and prefer watching at home, but you didn't explain <em>why</em> for either point.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy romantic-comedy films because I love when the characters have fun together</em> → <strong>I enjoy romantic-comedy films because I love watching the characters have fun together, and the mix of laughter and romance always makes me smile.</strong> Adding one more reason helps the reader understand your taste more clearly.</li><li><em>I prefer watching film at home</em> → <strong>I prefer watching films at home because I can relax on the sofa with my family and pause the movie whenever we want.</strong> You told us <em>where</em> you watch, but not <em>why</em> it's better than the cinema. That's the missing piece.</li><li><em>Had you ever watched film with your family?</em> → <strong>Have you ever watched a film with your family?</strong> This is a strong opening question. Keep it, but fix the grammar so it works perfectly.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer makes the reader feel interested and connected throughout the whole article. You started well with a direct question, but the rest of the article is quite short and doesn't build on that connection.</p><ul><li><em>Had you ever watched film with your family? I love to watch film with my family so much.</em> → <strong>Have you ever watched a film with your family? I love watching films with my family because it's our special time together.</strong> The question draws the reader in, but then you need to keep them interested by explaining <em>why</em> this matters to you.</li><li><em>I enjoy romantic-comedy films because I love when the characters have fun together</em> → <strong>I enjoy romantic-comedy films because I love watching the characters laugh and fall in love — it always lifts my mood.</strong> Adding a personal feeling makes the article warmer and more engaging.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects their ideas smoothly so the reader can follow easily. You have two paragraphs, which is good, but the connections between your sentences are very simple.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy romantic-comedy films because I love when the characters have fun together ,and there are a lot of emotion to feel when I watched. I prefer watching film at home.</em> → <strong>I enjoy romantic-comedy films because I love watching the characters have fun together, and there are so many emotions to feel when I watch them. That's also why I prefer watching films at home — I can enjoy every feeling without distractions.</strong> The word <em>that's also why</em> connects your love of emotions to your choice of watching at home. It makes the paragraph flow.</li><li>You used <em>because</em> and <em>and</em> to link your ideas. Try using <strong>also, another reason, or that's why</strong> to vary your linking words and show how your ideas relate to each other.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and vocabulary so the reader never has to guess the meaning. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>use the present simple tense for general truths and habits, and add articles (a/an/the) before singular countable nouns.</strong></p><ul><li><em>Had you ever watched film with your family?</em> → <strong>Have you ever watched a film with your family?</strong> You used the past perfect (<em>had watched</em>), but this question is about a general experience up to now. The present perfect (<em>have watched</em>) is the right choice here. Also, <em>film</em> needs an article: <strong>a film</strong>.</li><li><em>there are a lot of emotion to feel when I watched</em> → <strong>there are a lot of emotions to feel when I watch</strong>. <em>Emotion</em> is countable here (many different feelings), so it needs an <strong>s</strong>. And since you're describing a general feeling you always have, use the present simple <strong>watch</strong>, not the past <strong>watched</strong>.</li><li><em>I prefer watching film at home</em> → <strong>I prefer watching films at home</strong> or <strong>I prefer watching a film at home</strong>. <em>Film</em> is a countable noun, so it needs either a plural <strong>s</strong> or an article like <strong>a</strong>.</li><li>Check your capitalisation: <em>romantic -comedy films</em> has a space before the hyphen. Write it as <strong>romantic-comedy films</strong> (no space before the hyphen).</li><li>After you finish your draft, read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Is this a general habit or a one-time event?' If it's a habit, use present simple. Then check every noun — does it need <strong>a, an, the,</strong> or an <strong>s</strong>?</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29589	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	My favorite and enjoy of films is mysteries. I'd love to eat popcornd while I watching because it's so fun. The best mystery films is Sherlock holmes. It so mystery and make me confuse at the same time. I perfer watching them at home so I can have any snacks that I want to eat.<br><br>Also, It hard to find kind of mystery films at the cinema. Maybe it have but the story was boring. I want everyone to try Enola's Holmes 2. The story, detail, and intro were fun and it makes you confuse the answer all the time.	105	14.0	Elle, your article on mystery films is genuinely enjoyable to read. You covered every part of the task: you named your favourite genre, explained why you prefer watching at home, and gave a specific recommendation with <em>Enola Holmes 2</em>. That is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. What I want to help you with next is shaping your ideas into a proper article structure and polishing the grammar so your strong content gets the presentation it deserves. Let me walk through each area.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>My favorite and enjoy of films is mysteries.</em> You clearly stated your preferred genre right at the start, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I perfer watching them at home so I can have any snacks that I want to eat.</em> You gave a personal reason for your preference, and the reader understands your choice completely.</li>\n  <li><em>I want everyone to try Enola's Holmes 2.</em> You ended with a specific recommendation, which is a strong way to finish an article about films.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an article fully: a title, an introduction that hooks the reader, a body with opinions, and a conclusion that wraps things up. Your article has good opinions, but it jumps straight into your preference without an opening paragraph, and it ends without a proper closing sentence.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Start with a short introduction. For example: <em>Do you love a good mystery? I do, and here is why.</em> This tells the reader what the article is about and makes them want to keep reading.</li>\n  <li>End with a conclusion. After recommending <em>Enola Holmes 2</em>, add something like: <strong>If you enjoy puzzles and surprises, this is the film for you.</strong> That finishes the article neatly.</li>\n  <li>The register is mostly friendly and personal, which suits an article. Just watch out for small grammar slips like <em>while I watching</em> that break the flow. Keep your sentences complete and your verbs in the right form.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly using a variety of linking words and organises the sequence so the reader never feels lost. Your two paragraphs separate home viewing from cinema problems, which is good. But the ideas jump a little, and you rely mostly on <em>so</em>, <em>also</em>, and <em>and</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I perfer watching them at home so I can have any snacks that I want to eat. Also, It hard to find kind of mystery films at the cinema.</em> → <strong>I prefer watching them at home so I can have any snacks I want. However, it is hard to find good mystery films at the cinema.</strong> Using <em>however</em> instead of <em>also</em> shows contrast and makes the connection clearer.</li>\n  <li>Add a topic sentence to each paragraph. For paragraph 2, try: <strong>Watching mysteries at the cinema is not always easy.</strong> Then list your reasons. This tells the reader what the paragraph is about before you give details.</li>\n  <li>Use <em>because</em>, <em>although</em>, or <em>for example</em> to vary your sentence openings. This makes your writing feel less repetitive and more connected.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and vocabulary well, with only small slips. Your vocabulary choices are good — <em>mysteries</em>, <em>snacks</em>, <em>cinema</em>, <em>detail</em>, <em>intro</em> — but there is one main grammar pattern that needs attention: subject-verb agreement and missing verbs (especially <em>is</em>/<em>are</em> and <em>be</em>).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>My favorite and enjoy of films is mysteries.</em> → <strong>My favourite type of film is mysteries.</strong> The word <em>enjoy</em> is a verb, not a noun. Use a noun like <em>type</em> or <em>genre</em> here.</li>\n  <li><em>It so mystery and make me confuse at the same time.</em> → <strong>It is so mysterious and makes me confused at the same time.</strong> Every sentence needs a main verb. <em>It so mystery</em> is missing the verb <em>is</em>. Also, <em>mystery</em> is a noun; use the adjective <em>mysterious</em>. And <em>confuse</em> should be <em>confused</em> (I feel confused).</li>\n  <li><em>It hard to find kind of mystery films at the cinema.</em> → <strong>It is hard to find mystery films at the cinema.</strong> Add <em>is</em> before <em>hard</em>. Remove <em>kind of</em> because it is not needed here.</li>\n  <li><em>Maybe it have but the story was boring.</em> → <strong>Maybe there are some, but the story was boring.</strong> Use <em>there are</em> to talk about existence. <em>It have</em> is not correct in this meaning.</li>\n  <li>Check capitalisation: <em>It hard</em> starts a new sentence, so the <em>I</em> in <em>It</em> should be capitalised. Also <em>Sherlock holmes</em> should be <em>Sherlock Holmes</em> (capital H for the surname). After every period, the next word must start with a capital letter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can use: Read each sentence aloud. Ask yourself, 'Is there a main verb? Does the subject match the verb?' If you find a sentence missing <em>is</em> or <em>are</em>, add it. This one fix will make your writing much stronger.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29599	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy watching horror film because it's not boring like the other kinds of film and I love the way I can't guess what is going on the next sence, it is so fun to watch!<br><br>I perfer watching horror films at the cinema because it's scarier	50	12.0	Praewwan, you've made a solid start on this article. I can see you clearly answered both parts of the task — you told us you enjoy horror films, and you said you prefer watching them at the cinema. Your sentence about loving the way you can't guess what happens next shows real personal feeling, which is exactly what an article needs. To reach Band 5, you'll want to give the reader more to think about. Why is the cinema scarier? What happens to you when you watch a horror film there? Adding those details will turn a good answer into a memorable one. Let's look at each area now.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer develops every idea fully so the reader feels they know the writer's thoughts inside and out. Your two main points are clear, but each one needs more detail.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy watching horror film because it's not boring like the other kinds of film</em> → <strong>I enjoy watching horror films because they keep me guessing, unlike other kinds of film that feel predictable.</strong> You could add one more sentence here: describe a specific horror film you love and what makes it exciting.</li><li><em>I perfer watching horror films at the cinema because it's scarier</em> → <strong>I prefer watching horror films at the cinema because the big screen and loud sound make every jump scare feel real.</strong> Explaining <em>why</em> the cinema is scarier gives the reader a clear picture and shows deeper thinking.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format to connect with the reader and make them feel involved. Your personal opinions come through well, but the article feels short and doesn't invite the reader to share their own thoughts.</p><ul><li><em>I love the way I can't guess what is going on the next sence</em> → <strong>I love the way I can't guess what will happen in the next scene — it keeps me on the edge of my seat.</strong> Adding a phrase like this makes the article more vivid and engaging.</li><li>Try ending with a question to the reader, such as: <strong>Do you enjoy the same kind of thrill I do?</strong> This is a classic article technique that makes the reader feel part of the conversation.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear structure with varied linking words to guide the reader smoothly. You have two paragraphs, which is good, but the connections between ideas are simple.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy watching horror film because it's not boring... and I love the way I can't guess... it is so fun to watch!</em> → Try breaking this long sentence into two: <strong>I enjoy watching horror films because they are never boring. I also love the way I can't guess what will happen next.</strong> This makes each idea stand out.</li><li>Add a transition between your two paragraphs, like: <strong>Watching at home is fine, but the cinema is where horror films truly come alive.</strong> This connects your two main ideas smoothly.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling with few errors. Your vocabulary is good for everyday topics, but a few patterns need attention. Let's work on the main rule: plural nouns after 'film' and correct spelling of common words.</p><ul><li><em>horror film</em> → <strong>horror films</strong>. When you talk about the category in general, use the plural form. You used 'films' correctly later, so just be consistent.</li><li><em>sence</em> → <strong>scene</strong>. This is a common spelling mistake. A 'scene' is a part of a movie. Try writing the word three times to remember it: scene, scene, scene.</li><li><em>perfer</em> → <strong>prefer</strong>. The correct spelling has two 'e's: p-r-e-f-e-r. You used it correctly in your first sentence with 'prefer' — check your second paragraph.</li><li><em>what is going on the next sence</em> → <strong>what is going to happen in the next scene</strong>. After 'can't guess', you need a future idea: 'what will happen' or 'what is going to happen'.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29604	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy watching thriller, horror, and comedy! Romance, Sci-fi, and drama are absolutely not my thing. One key reason is, these kinds of movies such as thriller and comedy never bored me out. It make me felt excited to watch since I know it's going to be brilliant. And my judgements are never wrong. For example, last month I went to watch a movie at the cinema in the mall. It called "COLONY" a zombie movie made by Korean producer and it was the best movie I ever watched. It made me felt excited, scared, and many much more emotions while watching it.<br><br>I prefer watching movies at the cinema. because I would love to see them in big screen with a comfortable sofa instead of stay at home and watching it. It giving vibes and memories when you watching it in the cinema. And if the movies are great you can brag it to everyone that you already watching it in the cinema	168	18.0	Yayar, your article is genuinely enjoyable to read. You have a clear, confident voice and you know exactly what you like and why. The specific example of <em>COLONY</em> is excellent — it makes your preference concrete and memorable. To reach Band 5 across all areas, focus on tightening your grammar and connecting your ideas a little more smoothly. Your content is already strong, so the next step is polishing how you express it.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy watching thriller, horror, and comedy! Romance, Sci-fi, and drama are absolutely not my thing.</em> You clearly state your preferences and even name genres you dislike, which makes your opinion feel complete and honest.</li><li><em>It called "COLONY" a zombie movie made by Korean producer and it was the best movie I ever watched.</em> This specific example gives the reader a real sense of what you enjoy and why. It makes your article personal and convincing.</li><li><em>I prefer watching movies at the cinema. because I would love to see them in big screen with a comfortable sofa</em> You give clear, practical reasons for your cinema preference, and the reader understands your choice completely.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>One key reason is, these kinds of movies such as thriller and comedy never bored me out.</em> You use a confident, article-style opener to introduce your reasoning. It sounds like a real writer sharing an opinion.</li><li><em>It made me felt excited, scared, and many much more emotions while watching it.</em> Even with the grammar slip, your enthusiasm comes through clearly. The reader can feel your excitement.</li><li><em>And if the movies are great you can brag it to everyone that you already watching it in the cinema</em> This ending is playful and personal — it makes the reader smile and keeps the tone of a fun website article.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every paragraph smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. Your first paragraph covers genres and reasons, and your second covers cinema preference — that's clear. But the second paragraph starts with <em>I prefer watching movies at the cinema.</em> which feels a little separate from the COLONY story. Try linking them directly.</p><ul><li><em>I prefer watching movies at the cinema.</em> → <strong>After that amazing experience at the cinema, I now prefer watching all movies there.</strong> This connects the COLONY example to your general preference.</li><li><em>And if the movies are great you can brag it to everyone</em> → <strong>And if the movie is great, you can brag to everyone that you saw it first at the cinema.</strong> This keeps the playful tone but flows better from the previous sentence about vibes and memories.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently and makes fewer errors with verb forms and sentence structure. Your vocabulary is strong — <em>thriller</em>, <em>horror</em>, <em>absolutely not my thing</em>, <em>bored me out</em>, <em>brilliant</em>, <em>vibes</em>, <em>brag</em> — but some grammar patterns need attention. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: after <strong>make</strong> or <strong>help</strong>, use the base form of the verb (no past tense). Also, after <strong>enjoy</strong>, <strong>prefer</strong>, and <strong>instead of</strong>, use the -ing form.</p><ul><li><em>It make me felt excited</em> → <strong>It makes me feel excited</strong>. After <em>make</em>, use the base verb: <em>feel</em>, not <em>felt</em>.</li><li><em>It made me felt excited</em> → <strong>It made me feel excited</strong>. Even in past tense, after <em>made</em> use the base verb: <em>feel</em>.</li><li><em>It giving vibes and memories when you watching it</em> → <strong>It gives vibes and memories when you are watching it</strong>. Use the correct present tense form <em>gives</em>, and add <em>are</em> before <em>watching</em>.</li><li><em>instead of stay at home and watching it</em> → <strong>instead of staying at home and watching it</strong>. After <em>instead of</em>, use the -ing form: <em>staying</em>.</li><li><em>you already watching it</em> → <strong>you have already watched it</strong>. Use present perfect (<em>have watched</em>) to talk about an experience before now.</li></ul><p>Self-check for any sentence: if you see <em>make</em> or <em>made</em>, the next verb must be the base form (no -ed, no -ing). If you see <em>enjoy</em>, <em>prefer</em>, or <em>instead of</em>, the next verb must end in -ing.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29623	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Hi. my name is Siri. After I noticed this website. I quickly ran from my bed to my computer to shared the story about the films I loved. It's a story about the boy who's name Harry Potter. Oh, wait. You already noticed this story? Whatever, Harry lived in the house with his cousin, aunt and uncle, but everyone in this house don't like Harry and looked after Harry just like a slave. One day there are an envelope from somewhere and send by an owl. : Okay, for people who's never watched this fims I recommeted it. It's super fun this story is about magical, mystery and a boy tried to find who's killed his father and mother and kill it. I always watched this movies at home when I'm free. Well, you can watchs it in cinema but it's hard to find now. I hope you like the films I recommend. You going to love it if you like a mystical movies same as me.	174	10.0	Siri, your article has a fun, energetic voice that makes it feel like you're actually talking to someone who loves films. The way you jump straight into your excitement — <em>I quickly ran from my bed to my computer to shared the story</em> — shows real enthusiasm, and that's a great quality for a website article. Your closing line, <em>I hope you like the films I recommend</em>, is exactly the kind of friendly invitation a good article needs. To reach Band 5, you'll need to focus your ideas more tightly on the task questions and organise them so the reader can follow your thinking without getting lost. Let's look at each area where you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer answers every part of the task clearly and stays focused on the topic without wandering into extra details. You told us you love Harry Potter and prefer watching at home, which is good, but you spent a lot of space retelling the story instead of explaining <em>why</em> you love it and <em>why</em> home is better than the cinema.</p><ul><li><em>It's a story about the boy who's name Harry Potter. Oh, wait. You already noticed this story?</em> → <strong>I love the Harry Potter films because the story is full of magic and mystery, and the characters feel like real friends.</strong> This directly answers 'describe a film you enjoy' without assuming what the reader already knows.</li><li><em>I always watched this movies at home when I'm free. Well, you can watchs it in cinema but it's hard to find now.</em> → <strong>I prefer watching Harry Potter at home because I can pause it whenever I want and watch it again the next day.</strong> This gives a clear, personal reason for your preference.</li><li><em>You going to love it if you like a mystical movies same as me.</em> → <strong>You will love these films if you enjoy magical stories as much as I do.</strong> This keeps the recommendation clean and focused on the reader.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format consistently to connect with the reader, share opinions, and recommend something. You already do this well in parts — your opening feels personal and your closing directly addresses the reader. The main gap is that some of your sentences talk <em>at</em> the reader instead of <em>to</em> them.</p><ul><li><em>Oh, wait. You already noticed this story? Whatever, Harry lived in the house with his cousin...</em> → <strong>You might already know the story, but let me tell you why it is so special to me.</strong> This keeps the reader engaged instead of dismissing them with 'whatever.'</li><li><em>Okay, for people who's never watched this fims I recommeted it.</em> → <strong>If you have not watched these films yet, I strongly recommend them.</strong> This is clearer and more inviting for the reader.</li><li>The register is appropriately informal throughout, which suits a website article well. You have a natural voice that readers would enjoy.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges ideas in a logical order so the reader can follow easily from beginning to end. Your article has a clear opening and closing, but the middle section jumps between retelling the story, making a recommendation, and explaining your viewing habits without a clear path.</p><ul><li>Your opening paragraph introduces yourself and your purpose. Your closing paragraph invites the reader. These are strong. The problem is the middle: you tell the Harry Potter plot, then say 'Okay, for people who's never watched...', then go back to talking about watching at home. Try grouping your ideas: one paragraph about <em>what</em> the film is about (briefly), and one paragraph about <em>why</em> you love it and <em>where</em> you watch it.</li><li><em>One day there are an envelope from somewhere and send by an owl. : Okay, for people who's never watched this fims I recommeted it.</em> → The colon and 'Okay' create a sudden break. Instead, end the plot summary and start a new paragraph: <strong>If you have not watched these films, I strongly recommend them.</strong></li><li>Use simple linking words like 'first', 'also', and 'finally' to guide the reader through your points. For example: <strong>First, let me tell you about the story. Also, I prefer watching at home because...</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar and vocabulary accurately enough that errors do not distract the reader. Your vocabulary is good for A2 level — words like 'recommend', 'mystical', and 'magical' show you have a nice range. The main grammar rule that explains most of your errors is <strong>subject-verb agreement</strong>: the verb must match the subject in number (singular or plural).</p><ul><li><em>everyone in this house don't like Harry</em> → <strong>everyone in this house does not like Harry</strong>. 'Everyone' is singular, so it needs 'does not' (or 'doesn't'), not 'don't'.</li><li><em>I quickly ran ... to shared the story</em> → <strong>to share the story</strong>. After 'to', use the base form of the verb (infinitive), not the past tense.</li><li><em>you can watchs it in cinema</em> → <strong>you can watch it in a cinema</strong>. After 'can', use the base form 'watch', not 'watchs'. Also add 'a' before 'cinema'.</li><li><em>You going to love it</em> → <strong>You are going to love it</strong>. The verb 'are' is missing before 'going'.</li><li><em>if you like a mystical movies same as me</em> → <strong>if you like mystical movies as much as I do</strong>. 'Movies' is plural, so do not use 'a'. Use 'as much as I do' instead of 'same as me'.</li></ul><p>Self-check for any sentence you write: find the subject (who or what is doing the action), then check if the verb matches it. If the subject is 'he', 'she', 'it', or a singular noun, the verb usually needs an 's' at the end — unless you have a helper word like 'can' or 'will'.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29665	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	What kind of films do you enjoy?<br><br>Me, personally, I enjoy romantic films because of it's vibes. Romantic films gives off a really weird feeling of sadness and happiness at the same time, and that's what makes them special. Unlike horror films which is scary sci-fi, fantasy films, which are exciting, or comedy, which is funny and entertaining, I think romantic films includes every single type of feelings in just one film.<br><br>And, for the next question. Do you prefer watching them at the cinema or at home? Why? I actually prefer watching them at the cinema because watching films at the cinema is really fun, especially with your friends, partner. At the cinema you can buy snacks, souvenirs at the front cashier which our home doesn't have one, of course.	135	17.0	Peace, your article is genuinely enjoyable to read. You answered both questions from the notice with real detail — your explanation of why romantic films mix sadness and happiness together is thoughtful and personal. You also gave a clear reason for preferring the cinema, mentioning snacks and souvenirs. To reach Band 5 across all scales, you'll need to tighten your grammar control and make sure every sentence follows standard rules. Let's look at each area.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy romantic films because of it's vibes. Romantic films gives off a really weird feeling of sadness and happiness at the same time, and that's what makes them special.</em> You fully answered the first question with a personal, detailed reason. The reader understands exactly why you like romantic films.</li><li><em>I actually prefer watching them at the cinema because watching films at the cinema is really fun, especially with your friends, partner.</em> You clearly stated your preference and gave a reason, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>At the cinema you can buy snacks, souvenirs at the front cashier which our home doesn't have one, of course.</em> You added a specific, relatable detail about why the cinema experience is better than home. This makes your article feel complete and honest.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would use the article conventions consistently with a clear, engaging voice throughout. Your opening question and conversational tone are strong, but a few informal spots keep you from the top band.</p><ul><li><em>Me, personally, I enjoy romantic films because of it's vibes.</em> → <strong>Personally, I enjoy romantic films because of their atmosphere.</strong> Starting with 'Me, personally' is a bit too casual for an article. Dropping 'Me' keeps the personal voice while sounding more polished.</li><li><em>And, for the next question. Do you prefer watching them at the cinema or at home? Why?</em> → <strong>Now, for the next question: do you prefer watching at the cinema or at home? Why?</strong> The fragment 'And, for the next question' breaks the flow. Connecting it smoothly to the question keeps the reader engaged.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would connect ideas with a wider range of linking words and avoid abrupt transitions. Your paragraph breaks are correct, and your sequence is logical.</p><ul><li><em>And, for the next question. Do you prefer watching them at the cinema or at home?</em> → <strong>Now, for the next question: do you prefer watching at the cinema or at home?</strong> The transition between your two paragraphs is a little sudden. Using 'Now' instead of 'And' makes the shift smoother.</li><li><em>especially with your friends, partner.</em> → <strong>especially with your friends or a partner.</strong> Adding 'or' clarifies the list and makes the sentence flow better.</li><li><em>At the cinema you can buy snacks, souvenirs at the front cashier which our home doesn't have one, of course.</em> → <strong>At the cinema you can buy snacks and souvenirs at the front cashier, which our home doesn't have, of course.</strong> The comma before 'which' and removing 'one' makes the relative clause grammatically correct and easier to follow.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control both simple and complex grammar with few errors. Your vocabulary is good — words like 'vibes', 'souvenirs', and 'cashier' show range. The main pattern to fix is subject-verb agreement with plural and singular nouns.</p><ul><li><em>Romantic films gives off</em> → <strong>Romantic films give off</strong>. When the subject is plural ('films'), the verb does not take an '-s'. This is the most common error in your article.</li><li><em>horror films which is scary</em> → <strong>horror films, which are scary</strong>. 'Films' is plural, so the verb must be 'are', not 'is'. The same rule applies to '<em>fantasy films, which are exciting</em>' — you wrote that correctly, so you already know this rule. Apply it every time.</li><li><em>romantic films includes every single type of feelings</em> → <strong>romantic films include every single type of feeling</strong>. Plural subject needs a plural verb. Also, 'feelings' is fine, but 'feeling' works better as a general concept here.</li><li><em>which our home doesn't have one</em> → <strong>which our home doesn't have</strong>. The word 'one' is unnecessary because 'which' already refers to 'a front cashier'. You don't need both.</li><li><em>because of it's vibes</em> → <strong>because of its vibes</strong>. 'Its' (without an apostrophe) shows possession. 'It's' always means 'it is'. This is a small spelling fix that makes a big difference.</li></ul><p>Self-check: For every sentence, find the subject. Is it singular or plural? Then check if your verb matches. This one rule will fix most of the errors in your writing.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29720	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	The flim I enjoyed the most is horror flim because It's the only type of flim that I find thrilling the most.<br><br>I prefer watching them at the cinema because I always wanted to watch it with my friends because they likes horror Fllms just like me. So It would be very pleased to watch it with my own people. And also I can eat popcorns while watching the flim because I don't usually brought snacks to my house because I always forget.	87	12.0	Dragon, you've written a clear article about your love for horror films, and I can see you have strong opinions about watching them at the cinema. Your sentence <em>I prefer watching them at the cinema because I always wanted to watch it with my friends because they likes horror Fllms just like me</em> shows you understand how to connect ideas with 'because'. The reader gets a good sense of your personal taste. To reach Band 5, you'll want to develop your ideas more fully and fix some spelling and grammar patterns. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer covers every part of the task with enough detail so the reader feels fully informed. You covered the type of film (horror), why you enjoy it (thrilling), and where you watch it (cinema with friends), but the point about watching at home is too brief.</p><ul><li><em>I don't usually brought snacks to my house because I always forget.</em> → <strong>I don't usually bring snacks to my house because I always forget, so watching at home means I have nothing to eat.</strong> This connects the snack problem directly to why you prefer the cinema, making the contrast clearer.</li><li><em>It's the only type of flim that I find thrilling the most.</em> → <strong>Horror films are the only type I find truly thrilling.</strong> You could add a second reason, like <em>The suspense keeps me on the edge of my seat.</em> This would give the reader more insight into your taste.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses article conventions smoothly, with a clear personal voice and varied sentence openings. Your article shares opinions and uses an informal tone, which is correct for the genre, but the structure feels repetitive because almost every sentence starts with 'I'.</p><ul><li><em>I prefer watching them at the cinema because I always wanted to watch it with my friends because they likes horror Fllms just like me. So It would be very pleased to watch it with my own people.</em> → <strong>Watching horror films at the cinema is better because my friends love them too. There's nothing like sharing the scares with your own people.</strong> Varying the sentence subject makes the article feel more dynamic and less repetitive.</li><li><em>And also I can eat popcorns while watching the flim because I don't usually brought snacks to my house because I always forget.</em> → <strong>Plus, I can eat popcorn at the cinema, which solves my problem of always forgetting to buy snacks at home.</strong> This connects the snack idea more naturally to the cinema experience.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges ideas in a logical order without repetition, using a clear paragraph structure and varied linking words. You have two paragraphs separated by a blank line, which is good, but the sequence of ideas is repetitive and the final point about snacks feels disconnected.</p><ul><li><em>I prefer watching them at the cinema because I always wanted to watch it with my friends because they likes horror Fllms just like me.</em> → <strong>I prefer watching horror films at the cinema with my friends, who enjoy them as much as I do.</strong> This removes the double 'because' and keeps the idea flowing forward without backtracking.</li><li><em>And also I can eat popcorns while watching the flim because I don't usually brought snacks to my house because I always forget.</em> → <strong>Another reason I love the cinema is the popcorn. Since I always forget to buy snacks at home, the cinema solves that problem.</strong> This makes the snack point feel like a natural addition rather than an afterthought.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar accurately, with only occasional minor errors. The main rule that will help you is subject-verb agreement: a singular subject needs a singular verb, and a plural subject needs a plural verb. Let's work through your errors.</p><ul><li><em>the flim I enjoyed the most is horror flim</em> → <strong>the film I enjoy the most is horror films</strong>. The word 'film' is spelled with an 'i', not an 'i' before 'm'. Also, 'horror film' is a category, so you can say 'horror films' (plural) or 'a horror film' (singular).</li><li><em>they likes horror Fllms</em> → <strong>they like horror films</strong>. 'They' is plural, so the verb should be 'like' without an 's'. Also, 'Fllms' has a double 'l' and a random capital letter. The correct spelling is 'films'.</li><li><em>It would be very pleased</em> → <strong>I would be very pleased</strong>. 'It' cannot feel pleased because 'it' is not a person. Use 'I' to express your own feelings.</li><li><em>I don't usually brought snacks</em> → <strong>I don't usually bring snacks</strong>. After 'don't', use the base form of the verb, not the past tense. This is a common pattern: 'don't' + base verb (bring, eat, watch, go).</li><li><em>popcorns</em> → <strong>popcorn</strong>. Popcorn is uncountable, so it does not take an 's'. Similarly, 'snacks' is fine because snacks are countable, but 'popcorn' is not.</li></ul><p>Self-check for next time: Read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Is the subject singular or plural? Does my verb match it?' Also, check every word for spelling, especially 'film' and 'popcorn'.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29832	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. Lou is a business man, he came to work at American. While he travel to his home, he looks throught the windows. He shockl _What did I have saw:;_ He though. He looked arourds and it seem that nobody has _ saw it, only Lou can. While he though, the plane fall in to that island. About hour later he woke up and know that he was only one in the golden island. And where is everyobody, He though, In this island were full of dangger. 3 Year later, He find the ways back home. When he about to left, the island, he didn't forgot to bring the gold back. On the outside he missing only one year! he got back home and became the richest person in the world, but noone know why...	150	12.0	Nene, you have a wonderful imagination. Your story takes Lou from a plane crash to a golden island, then back home as the richest person in the world. That is a complete and interesting journey. You included a clear beginning, middle, and end, which is exactly what a good story needs. To reach Band 5, the next step is to make your sentences flow more smoothly and to fix the grammar errors that sometimes make the story hard to follow. Let me show you how in each area.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> You started the story right where the prompt left off, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>About hour later he woke up and know that he was only one in the golden island.</em> You showed the crash and its result clearly. The reader understands that Lou is stranded alone.</li>\n  <li><em>he got back home and became the richest person in the world, but noone know why...</em> You gave the story a satisfying ending with a small mystery. The target reader is fully informed about what happened.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right storytelling style consistently and makes the reader feel engaged from start to finish. Your story has the right idea, but some parts feel rushed or unclear.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>He shockl _What did I have saw:;_ He though.</em> → <strong>He was shocked. "What did I just see?" he thought.</strong> The reader can guess what you mean, but the punctuation and grammar make it confusing. Using quotation marks for thoughts makes the story easier to follow.</li>\n  <li><em>3 Year later, He find the ways back home.</em> → <strong>Three years later, he found his way back home.</strong> This jump in time is very sudden. Adding one sentence like "He survived on the island by eating fruit and drinking from a stream" would help the reader feel the passing of time.</li>\n  <li><em>On the outside he missing only one year!</em> This is an interesting idea, but it is not explained. A Band 5 writer would add a short explanation, such as <strong>"When he returned, only one year had passed in the outside world."</strong></li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never gets lost. Right now, your sentences are mostly short and the time jumps are abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>While he travel to his home, he looks throught the windows. He shockl</em> → <strong>While he was travelling home, he looked through the window. He was shocked.</strong> Using <em>while</em> is good, but the verb tenses need to match. Try using time words like <em>after</em>, <em>suddenly</em>, or <em>then</em> to show the order of events.</li>\n  <li><em>About hour later he woke up and know that he was only one in the golden island. And where is everyobody, He though, In this island were full of dangger. 3 Year later, He find the ways back home.</em> The jump from waking up to three years later is too fast. A Band 5 writer would add a bridge sentence like <strong>"For three years, Lou survived alone on the island."</strong></li>\n  <li>Your story uses <em>While</em>, <em>and</em>, <em>but</em>, and <em>When</em>, which are good basic connectors. To reach Band 5, try adding <em>after that</em>, <em>suddenly</em>, or <em>finally</em> to show the passage of time more clearly.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple grammar and vocabulary with few errors. Your main challenge is verb tenses. You mix present and past tense throughout the story. Let me show you the pattern.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Lou is a business man, he came to work at American.</em> → <strong>Lou was a businessman. He came to work in America.</strong> The story happened in the past, so all verbs should be past tense. Check every verb in your story and ask yourself: "Did this happen in the past?" If yes, use past tense.</li>\n  <li><em>While he travel to his home, he looks throught the windows.</em> → <strong>While he was travelling to his home, he looked through the window.</strong> <em>Travel</em> should be <em>travelled</em> or <em>was travelling</em>, and <em>looks</em> should be <em>looked</em>. The whole story needs past tense consistently.</li>\n  <li><em>He shockl _What did I have saw:;_ He though.</em> → <strong>He was shocked. "What did I just see?" he thought.</strong> <em>Shockl</em> is not a word. Use <em>was shocked</em>. <em>Though</em> should be <em>thought</em> (past tense of <em>think</em>).</li>\n  <li><em>the plane fall in to that island</em> → <strong>the plane fell onto that island</strong>. <em>Fall</em> is present tense; the past tense is <em>fell</em>. Also, <em>in to</em> should be <em>onto</em> because the plane lands on the island, not inside it.</li>\n  <li><em>He find the ways back home</em> → <strong>He found his way back home</strong>. <em>Find</em> is present tense; the past tense is <em>found</em>. Also, <em>the ways</em> should be <em>his way</em> (singular).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can use: After you write a sentence, read it aloud and ask yourself, "Is this happening now, or did it happen in the past?" If it happened in the past, change the verb to past tense. This one habit will fix most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29886	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Films is a another source of entertainment, everybody know what it is and many people are enjoy watching them, same as me! but films are not just a one movie with one gerne. they are different kind of films that gives your different kind of vibe and feeling such as Commedy makes people laugh, Drama makes people cry, Horror/Thriller makes people scared and thrill for my personal favorite I would go with the Thriller, Slasher vibe. because It's always keep you wonder when will the killer "come". or ever the chase scene it makee my heart skip a beat, and this type of movies will keep you guessing who is the real killer. Movie with this type of gerne are such as HALLOWEEN, Scream, etc. and If you want to experience in such a imersive experience you should go to the cinema the sound and light, the screen. It's making it more immersive, but for me I prefer staying at home because thats given me all comfort I need.	172	15.0	Captain, your article on thriller films has real personality. You opened with a strong general statement about films being a source of entertainment, then moved naturally into your specific genre preference. The line <em>"it's always keep you wonder when will the killer 'come'"</em> shows you understand how to create suspense for your reader. That personal touch makes your writing memorable. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll need to tighten your grammar and structure your ideas into clear paragraphs. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5! You covered everything the task asked for: your preferred genre (Thriller/Slasher), why you enjoy it, and your preference for watching at home over the cinema with reasons.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>for my personal favorite I would go with the Thriller, Slasher vibe.</em> You clearly stated your preferred genre, which is exactly what the task required.</li>\n  <li><em>It's always keep you wonder when will the killer 'come'</em> You gave a specific, vivid reason why you enjoy thrillers. The reader understands your excitement.</li>\n  <li><em>for me I prefer staying at home because thats given me all comfort I need.</em> You explained your viewing preference with a personal reason. The task is fully addressed.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer holds an article's informal tone consistently from start to finish, using engaging questions and personal examples throughout. You already do this well in parts, but your tone shifts unevenly in places.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>same as me!</em> The exclamation and direct address work well for an article. It pulls the reader in.</li>\n  <li><em>Movie with this type of gerne are such as HALLOWEEN, Scream, etc.</em> This sentence feels more like a list than a personal article. Try <strong>Two of my favourite films in this genre are Halloween and Scream.</strong> That keeps the personal voice strong.</li>\n  <li><em>If you want to experience in such a imersive experience you should go to the cinema</em> You address the reader directly here, which is excellent for an article. Keep this kind of engagement throughout.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer groups related ideas into clear paragraphs and uses a wider range of linking words. Your ideas flow logically, but everything runs together without paragraph breaks.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your article has three natural sections: general thoughts about films, your favourite genre and why, and where you prefer to watch. Each of these deserves its own paragraph. Start a new paragraph after <em>same as me!</em> and after <em>who is the real killer.</em></li>\n  <li>You use <em>but</em>, <em>because</em>, and <em>such as</em> well. To reach Band 5, add variety: <strong>however</strong>, <strong>in addition</strong>, <strong>for example</strong>, <strong>as a result</strong>. Try: <em>"Thrillers keep you guessing. As a result, my heart races during every chase scene."</em></li>\n  <li>The jump from describing chase scenes to listing film titles is abrupt. Add a transition: <strong>Two films that do this brilliantly are Halloween and Scream.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and noun plurals with few errors. Your vocabulary is strong and appropriate, but several grammar patterns need attention. The main rule to focus on is: <strong>every present-tense verb must match its subject</strong> (he/she/it adds -s; they/we/I do not).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your vocabulary choices show good range: <em>entertainment</em>, <em>vibe</em>, <em>immersive</em>, <em>chase scene</em>. These are effective and appropriate for an article.</li>\n  <li><em>Films is a another source</em> → <strong>Films are another source</strong>. 'Films' is plural, so the verb must be 'are'. Also, 'a another' should be 'another' (no 'a' before 'another').</li>\n  <li><em>many people are enjoy watching them</em> → <strong>many people enjoy watching them</strong>. After 'are', use the -ing form (are enjoying) or remove 'are' for a simple present statement. Here, simple present is correct.</li>\n  <li><em>It's always keep you wonder</em> → <strong>It always keeps you wondering</strong>. 'It' needs 'keeps' (he/she/it + -s), and after 'keep' we use the -ing form: 'keeps you wondering'.</li>\n  <li><em>it makee my heart skip a beat</em> → <strong>it makes my heart skip a beat</strong>. 'It' needs 'makes'. Check every sentence with 'it', 'he', or 'she' for the missing -s.</li>\n  <li>Capitalisation: <em>Commedy</em> should be <em>Comedy</em>, and <em>gerne</em> should be <em>genre</em>. Also, <em>HALLOWEEN</em> in all caps is unusual — write it as <em>Halloween</em> unless you mean to shout it. Mid-sentence capitals like <em>Commedy</em> and <em>Drama</em> should be lowercase unless they start a sentence.</li>\n  <li>Self-check: Read every sentence aloud and ask yourself: "Is the subject singular or plural? Does my verb match?" This one habit will fix most of your errors.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
31416	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy to watch horror films, because it make me excited with many sceense. I would like to watching them at home over the cinema. If you stay to watching at home you can eat whatever you want, not just popcorn and coke, sit or sleep you don't need to care who will judging. you and opean with low or loud sound it's af to you. Last two week I watch horror movie on netflix at home with my mom while enjoy eaiting snacks. I can't remember a name of this horror movie, but I after watching I scared for 2 day. It's was really fun. Watching at home only pay for app, example like netflix, disney hotstar but only 99 bath per mount ( I can't remember a price.)	134	14.0	Tonnam, you have written an engaging article about watching horror films at home. Your content is strong with good personal examples. To improve, focus on grammar control and proofreading for errors.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy to watch horror films, because it make me excited with many sceense.</em> You state your preference clearly and give a reason right away. The reader knows exactly what you think.</li><li><em>If you stay to watching at home you can eat whatever you want, not just popcorn and coke, sit or sleep you don't need to care who will judging.</em> You list several specific advantages of watching at home. This shows you have thought about the topic from different angles.</li><li><em>Last two week I watch horror movie on netflix at home with my mom while enjoy eaiting snacks. I can't remember a name of this horror movie, but I after watching I scared for 2 day. It's was really fun.</em> This personal example adds depth and makes your article feel real. The reader can picture the scene.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the reader engaged from start to finish with consistently natural and smooth language. Your ideas are clear, but some sentences are phrased in a way that pulls the reader out of the experience.</p><ul><li><em>you and opean with low or loud sound it's af to you.</em> → <strong>you can adjust the volume to low or loud. It's up to you.</strong> The original is hard to follow. The corrected version keeps your informal, friendly tone while being instantly clear.</li><li><em>I after watching I scared for 2 day.</em> → <strong>After watching it, I was scared for two days.</strong> The original repeats 'I' and uses the wrong tense. The revision flows naturally and keeps the reader in the story.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear structure where each paragraph has one main idea, and the last sentence connects smoothly to the next part. Your ideas are in a logical order, but the final sentence about price feels disconnected from the rest.</p><ul><li><em>Watching at home only pay for app, example like netflix, disney hotstar but only 99 bath per mount ( I can't remember a price.)</em> This sentence introduces a new point (cost) at the very end without linking it to your earlier reasons. Try adding a transition: <strong>Another reason I prefer watching at home is the cost. You only pay for the app, like Netflix or Disney+ Hotstar, which is about 99 baht per month.</strong></li><li><em>If you stay to watching at home you can eat whatever you want, not just popcorn and coke, sit or sleep you don't need to care who will judging.</em> This sentence lists several ideas without clear separation. Try breaking it into two sentences: <strong>If you watch at home, you can eat whatever you want, not just popcorn and coke. You can also sit or sleep without worrying about who is judging you.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar so the reader never has to guess the meaning. Your article has several patterns that need attention.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy to watch horror films</em> → <strong>I enjoy watching horror films</strong>. After 'enjoy', use the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.</li><li><em>it make me excited with many sceense</em> → <strong>it makes me excited with many scenes</strong>. With 'it', the verb needs an 's' (it makes, it scares). Also, 'sceense' is spelled <strong>scenes</strong>.</li><li><em>I scared for 2 day</em> → <strong>I was scared for 2 days</strong>. You need the verb 'was' before 'scared', and 'day' needs to be plural because there are two.</li><li><em>It's was really fun</em> → <strong>It was really fun</strong>. You used both 'It's' and 'was' — choose one. 'It's' means 'It is' (present), but you need past tense 'It was'.</li><li><em>99 bath per mount</em> → <strong>99 baht per month</strong>. Check spelling of these common words. Self-check: After you finish writing, read each sentence slowly and ask 'Is the verb in the right tense? Does the subject match the verb?'</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33896	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	In my opinion, I enjoy watching Horror film such as Mudborn, which is Taiwanese Horror film. Firstly, it makes me feel excited everytime I watch . It has jumpscares of the ghosts, so I always get shocking. Secondly, I like to look at the make up of the ghosts such as thier black contactlens, extremely white foundation. They're amazing and I respect thier acting.<br><br>In my view, I prefer watching Horror film at the cinema Initialy, Cinema gives you a High quality screen. It makes me feel like I'm in the film too!	96	15.0	Kati, you have a real talent for describing what makes horror films exciting. Your detail about the ghosts' <em>thier black contactlens, extremely white foundation</em> shows that you notice specific things and can share them with a reader. That is exactly the kind of personal observation that makes an article interesting. To reach Band 5 overall, you will need to work on two things: making your opening more engaging for the reader, and improving your grammar and sentence connections. Let me show you how each area can improve.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy watching Horror film such as Mudborn, which is Taiwanese Horror film.</em> You named a specific film and gave its origin, which makes your opinion feel real and informed.</li>\n  <li><em>it makes me feel excited everytime I watch.</em> You clearly stated your emotional reaction, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer watching Horror film at the cinema</em> and <em>It makes me feel like I'm in the film too!</em> You gave a clear preference with a vivid reason, leaving the reader fully informed.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer opens their article in a way that immediately grabs the reader's attention and maintains an engaging tone throughout. Your current opening, <em>In my opinion, I enjoy watching Horror film such as Mudborn</em>, is clear but a bit flat for an article.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>In my opinion, I enjoy watching Horror film such as Mudborn</em> → <strong>Have you ever felt your heart race during a horror film? That is exactly how I feel watching Mudborn, a Taiwanese horror film that still gives me chills.</strong> Asking a question pulls the reader in and makes them curious.</li>\n  <li><em>In my view, I prefer watching Horror film at the cinema</em> → <strong>But there is one place where horror films truly come alive: the cinema.</strong> A short, punchy transition like this keeps the energy up and feels more like a real article.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words and builds ideas within paragraphs instead of just listing them. You have used <em>Firstly</em> and <em>Secondly</em> to list your points, which is a good start, but the paragraphs feel like a simple list rather than a connected flow of ideas.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Firstly, it makes me feel excited everytime I watch. It has jumpscares of the ghosts, so I always get shocking. Secondly, I like to look at the make up of the ghosts...</em> → Try connecting the two ideas: <strong>Firstly, horror films make me feel excited because of the jumpscares. But what I enjoy even more is looking at the makeup of the ghosts.</strong> This shows a relationship between your points instead of just listing them.</li>\n  <li><em>In my view, I prefer watching Horror film at the cinema Initialy, Cinema gives you a High quality screen.</em> The word <em>Initialy</em> (which should be <em>Initially</em>) is used to start a new paragraph, but it does not connect back to your previous paragraph. Try: <strong>Watching at home is fine, but I prefer the cinema because of the high quality screen.</strong> This creates a contrast with your earlier paragraph about enjoying horror films in general.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently and uses a wider range of vocabulary. The main pattern holding you back is <strong>noun agreement</strong>: when you talk about more than one thing, you need to make the noun plural. Let me show you how this affects your writing.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Horror film</em> → <strong>horror films</strong>. Since you are talking about the genre in general, use the plural form. Example: <em>I enjoy watching horror films such as Mudborn.</em></li>\n  <li><em>thier black contactlens, extremely white foundation</em> → <strong>their black contact lenses and extremely white foundation</strong>. 'Contact lenses' is always plural when referring to a pair, and 'their' is the correct spelling of the possessive form.</li>\n  <li><em>I always get shocking</em> → <strong>I always get shocked</strong>. 'Shocking' describes something that causes surprise (e.g., 'a shocking scene'), but when you feel the surprise, you are 'shocked'.</li>\n  <li><em>Initialy, Cinema gives you a High quality screen.</em> → <strong>Initially, the cinema gives you a high quality screen.</strong> Three fixes here: 'Initially' needs the correct spelling, 'Cinema' should not be capitalised mid-sentence (only proper nouns get capitals), and 'High quality' should not be capitalised either.</li>\n  <li><em>everytime I watch</em> → <strong>every time I watch</strong>. 'Every time' is two words. This is a common spelling mistake, but it is easy to fix once you know the rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: <strong>Am I talking about one thing or more than one? If more than one, does the noun end in 's'?</strong> Checking this will fix most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34912	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Have you ever wondered what does people back then watch instead of Youtube or scrolling on Instagram and Tiktok?<br><br>Well, it is film, and yes. Today, I'd like to discuss what kind of films I enjoy and whether I prefer watching them at the cinema or at home.<br><br>Firstly, I love to watch Sci-Fi movie as known as science fiction movie, especially watching it with my family at home. This is because I'd like to spend time with my family, and I also want to be frugal. For instance, yesterday, I watched the movie called Dragon warriors with my little brother. It was excellent! The villain was atrocious, and the plot was thrilled.<br><br>Overall, I like Sci-Fi movie best when watching it at home with my family.	135	18.0	Hero, your article is genuinely engaging. You opened with a clever question that pulls the reader in, and you gave a specific example from your own life — watching *Dragon warriors* with your little brother — which made your writing feel personal and real. You also showed strong vocabulary with words like *frugal* and *atrocious*. To reach Band 5 across all areas, focus on polishing your grammar and making the transitions between your ideas feel smoother. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I love to watch Sci-Fi movie as known as science fiction movie</em> You clearly stated your preferred genre and defined it for the reader. The task asked for this, and you delivered it directly.</li><li><em>I'd like to spend time with my family, and I also want to be frugal</em> You gave two clear, personal reasons for watching at home. The reader understands your choice completely.</li><li><em>yesterday, I watched the movie called Dragon warriors with my little brother. It was excellent!</em> A specific example makes your article memorable and convincing. This is exactly what a Band 5 writer does.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Have you ever wondered what does people back then watch instead of Youtube or scrolling on Instagram and Tiktok?</em> Your opening question grabs the reader's attention immediately. That is the mark of a strong article writer.</li><li><em>Well, it is film, and yes.</em> You answered your own question in a playful, confident way. The reader feels like you are talking directly to them.</li><li><em>Overall, I like Sci-Fi movie best when watching it at home with my family.</em> Your conclusion wraps up both parts of the prompt neatly. The reader finishes the article feeling satisfied.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every sentence so smoothly that the reader never has to pause and think about how the ideas fit together. Your paragraphs are well placed, but the jump from your opening question to your introduction feels a bit abrupt.</p><ul><li><em>Have you ever wondered what does people back then watch... Well, it is film, and yes.</em> → <strong>Have you ever wondered what people watched before YouTube and social media? The answer is film, and I think it is still one of the best ways to enjoy a story.</strong> This creates a smoother bridge from the question to your main topic.</li><li><em>Firstly, I love to watch Sci-Fi movie...</em> Your use of 'Firstly' is good. A Band 5 writer might also add a short linking sentence after the introduction, such as <strong>Let me explain what I enjoy most.</strong></li><li><em>Overall, I like Sci-Fi movie best when watching it at home with my family.</em> Your conclusion is clear. To strengthen it, you could echo your opening: <strong>So next time you are scrolling through your phone, consider watching a Sci-Fi film with your family instead.</strong></li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammar with very few errors. Your vocabulary is strong — words like <em>frugal</em>, <em>atrocious</em>, and <em>villain</em> show you are reaching for interesting language. The main grammar rule that will lift you to Band 5 is: <strong>after a question word (what, where, why, how), the subject comes before the verb — do not invert the subject and verb.</strong></p><ul><li><em>what does people back then watch</em> → <strong>what people back then watched</strong>. After 'what', keep the normal subject-verb order: what + people + watched. Also, 'people' is plural, so use 'do' not 'does' if you need an auxiliary.</li><li><em>Sci-Fi movie as known as science fiction movie</em> → <strong>Sci-Fi movies, also known as science fiction movies</strong>. 'As known as' is not correct. Use 'also known as' or 'also called'. Also, since you are talking about the genre in general, use the plural 'movies'.</li><li><em>the plot was thrilled</em> → <strong>the plot was thrilling</strong>. 'Thrilled' describes how a person feels (I was thrilled). 'Thrilling' describes the thing that causes the feeling (the plot was thrilling).</li><li><em>I love to watch Sci-Fi movie</em> → <strong>I love watching Sci-Fi movies</strong> or <strong>I love to watch Sci-Fi movies</strong>. Both are fine, but remember to make 'movie' plural when talking about the genre in general.</li><li><strong>Self-check for your next draft:</strong> Every time you write a question, check that the subject comes before the verb. For example, 'what did they watch' not 'what did watch they'. This one rule will fix most of your grammar errors.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
