34924	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I think my type of films that make me enjoy it was kind of horror movies. Horror movies for me it was scared but it have move exciting than romance. that to much love scence or drama. Horror movie the story it make me feel exciting for a long, what the story tell we about. And I prefer to watch horror movie at cinema only it because when we go to cinema it feeling good than watch at home. At home we can watch anytime we want to watch, but cinema friend can watch it together that have move feeling.	100	11.0	Ping-Ping, you made a strong choice by writing about horror movies. You told the reader what you prefer, why you like them, and where you like to watch them. That is exactly what an article needs. The sentence <em>I think my type of films that make me enjoy it was kind of horror movies</em> shows you know how to start with your personal opinion. To reach Band 5, you can develop your reasons more deeply and connect your ideas more smoothly. Let's look at each part of your writing so you can see exactly what to improve.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes every required detail and develops each reason with specific examples or explanations. You gave three key ideas — your favourite type, why you like it, and where you watch — but each one could go a little deeper.</p><ul><li><em>it have move exciting than romance. that to much love scence or drama.</em> You compared horror to romance, which is a good start. To reach Band 5, add a specific example: <strong>Horror movies are more exciting than romance because they keep me guessing what will happen next. In a romance, I already know the ending.</strong></li><li><em>what the story tell we about.</em> You hinted at the plot, but you did not explain what makes a horror story interesting. Try: <strong>I love trying to figure out what the story is telling us, and horror movies always have a mystery to solve.</strong></li><li><em>cinema friend can watch it together that have move feeling.</em> You mentioned watching with friends, which is a good detail. Strengthen it: <strong>Watching with friends at the cinema makes the scares more fun because we can scream together and talk about it after.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an article fully, including a clear introduction and conclusion that frame the topic. You addressed the reader with <em>I think</em> and <em>we</em>, which fits an article, but your text starts and ends abruptly.</p><ul><li><em>I think my type of films that make me enjoy it was kind of horror movies.</em> This is a good personal opening. To make it stronger, add a title or a question to grab the reader: <strong>Why do I love horror movies? Let me tell you.</strong> Then follow with your opinion.</li><li>Your article ends with <em>that have move feeling</em>. A Band 5 article needs a closing sentence that wraps up your main idea. Try: <strong>For me, nothing beats watching a scary movie with friends at the cinema.</strong></li><li>You used <em>we</em> to include the reader, which is excellent for an article. Keep doing that, and add one or two more direct addresses like <em>you might think horror is too scary, but...</em></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas clearly using a variety of linking words and arranges them in a logical sequence. Right now, your ideas jump between topics without clear connections.</p><ul><li><em>it have move exciting than romance. that to much love scence or drama.</em> The second sentence seems to explain why romance is less exciting, but the link is unclear. Connect them: <strong>Horror movies are more exciting than romance because romance has too much love scenes or drama.</strong></li><li><em>Horror movie the story it make me feel exciting for a long, what the story tell we about.</em> These two ideas — feeling excited and wondering about the story — belong together. Use <em>because</em> or <em>and</em> to join them: <strong>The story makes me feel excited for a long time because I want to know what the story tells us.</strong></li><li><em>At home we can watch anytime we want to watch, but cinema friend can watch it together that have move feeling.</em> You used <em>but</em> correctly here to show contrast. This is your strongest link. Build on it by adding a third option or a conclusion.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and a range of vocabulary. The main rule that will help you is <strong>subject-verb agreement</strong>: the subject and verb must match in number (singular subject → singular verb; plural subject → plural verb). Let's fix your errors using this rule.</p><ul><li><em>it have move exciting</em> → <strong>it has more exciting moments</strong>. The subject <em>it</em> is singular, so the verb must be <em>has</em>, not <em>have</em>. Also, <em>move</em> should be <em>more</em>.</li><li><em>that to much love scence</em> → <strong>that has too much love scenes</strong>. The subject <em>that</em> is singular, so use <em>has</em>. Also, <em>to</em> should be <em>too</em> (meaning 'excessively'), and <em>scence</em> should be <em>scenes</em>.</li><li><em>what the story tell we about</em> → <strong>what the story tells us about</strong>. The subject <em>story</em> is singular, so the verb is <em>tells</em>. Also, the object should be <em>us</em>, not <em>we</em>.</li><li><em>it feeling good</em> → <strong>it feels good</strong>. The subject <em>it</em> needs the verb <em>feels</em>, not <em>feeling</em>. <em>Feeling</em> is a noun or a gerund, not a main verb.</li><li><em>that have move feeling</em> → <strong>that has more feeling</strong>. Again, <em>that</em> is singular, so use <em>has</em>. <em>Move</em> should be <em>more</em>.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on every sentence: Find the subject. Is it singular or plural? Then check if the verb matches. For example, <em>the cinema</em> is singular, so it needs <em>has</em>, not <em>have</em>. <em>Friends</em> is plural, so it needs <em>have</em>, not <em>has</em>. Practice this on your next draft and you will see big improvements.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34926	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.<br>In the summer Lou wanted to go on a vacation with his family.<br>So, he booked a flight to Hawaii next week with his family. After that he surprised everyone in the family with the ticket to Hawaii. Everyone was so happy except for his sister Katie she couldn't go to hawaii with him because she had to go to new york for education. Everyone feel super dramatic.<br><br>After all that, we went on the plane and when we arrived we looked at the golden beach. It was gorgeous. We were so happy there. I wish that I could be there for the rest of my life!	126	9.0	Chopin, you opened your story with a strong line from the prompt and created a clear family vacation idea. The moment when Lou surprises everyone with the tickets is a good narrative beat. To reach Band 5, your story needs a consistent point of view and a clear sequence of events from beginning to end. Let's look at each area one by one.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer keeps the story focused on one clear timeline and resolves every subplot they introduce. Your story starts in the present with the plane flying lower, then jumps to booking a flight 'next week,' which confuses the reader about when things happen.</p><ul><li><em>So, he booked a flight to Hawaii next week with his family.</em> → <strong>So, he booked a flight to Hawaii for the following week.</strong> This keeps the timeline clear and avoids mixing present and future time references.</li><li><em>Everyone feel super dramatic.</em> → <strong>Everyone felt very dramatic about it.</strong> The sister's conflict is introduced but never resolved. A Band 5 writer would show what happened next with Katie. Did she stay home? Did she change her mind?</li><li><em>we went on the plane and when we arrived we looked at the golden beach.</em> The shift from 'Lou' to 'we' is confusing. A Band 5 writer keeps the same main character throughout the story so the reader always knows whose story this is.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer follows story conventions consistently and keeps the reader engaged from start to finish. Your story starts with Lou in third person, then switches to 'we' and 'I' in the final paragraph, which breaks the narrative frame.</p><ul><li><em>Lou saw the golden beaches...</em> → <strong>Lou saw the golden beaches... (keep Lou as the main character throughout)</strong> The reader expects to follow Lou's story. When you switch to 'we' and 'I', the reader has to guess who is speaking now.</li><li><em>Everyone feel super dramatic.</em> This tells us the family's emotion but doesn't show it. A Band 5 writer would show the drama through actions or dialogue, like <strong>Katie crossed her arms and said, 'I can't believe you're all going without me.'</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges events in a clear order and uses linking words to guide the reader smoothly from one event to the next. Your story jumps from booking a flight to surprising the family, then to the sister's conflict, and then suddenly to arrival.</p><ul><li><em>So, he booked a flight... After that he surprised everyone... After all that, we went on the plane...</em> These linking words are a good start, but the jump from 'After all that' to 'we went on the plane' skips over important events like packing, going to the airport, and boarding. A Band 5 writer would add one or two steps in between, like <strong>After all that, the family packed their bags. The next morning, they took a taxi to the airport and boarded the plane to Hawaii.</strong></li><li>The story has three paragraphs (separated by <br><br>), which is good. A Band 5 writer would use each paragraph for one clear stage: paragraph 1 = the problem or setup, paragraph 2 = the conflict or middle, paragraph 3 = the resolution or ending.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls simple grammar and uses everyday vocabulary accurately. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>keep the same verb tense throughout your story</strong>. Your story starts in past tense ('saw', 'wanted', 'booked'), so all verbs should stay in past tense.</p><ul><li><em>Everyone feel super dramatic.</em> → <strong>Everyone felt very dramatic.</strong> 'Feel' is present tense. Since your story is in past tense, use 'felt'.</li><li><em>she couldn't go to hawaii with him because she had to go to new york for education.</em> → <strong>she couldn't go to Hawaii with him because she had to go to New York for her education.</strong> Country and city names are proper nouns and must be capitalised: Hawaii, New York.</li><li><em>we went on the plane and when we arrived we looked at the golden beach.</em> This sentence is grammatically correct, but you can make it more vivid. A Band 5 writer might say: <strong>We boarded the plane, and when we arrived, we stood at the edge of the golden beach and stared at the sparkling water.</strong></li><li>Self-check for your next draft: read every sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Is every verb in the same tense?' If you find a present tense verb in a past tense story, change it to past tense.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34931	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy watch films kind of horror, thriller and mystery. They are very fun and frightening to watch, but I like it. I like the way it shows some mysterious scenes and jumpscare, even if it's so terrify.<br><br>For example, the movie name's "Saw". This movie is extraordinary terrify at first when I watched, I almost cried out when I saw a jumpscare with loud sound and the horror atmosphere.<br><br>I'd recommend to watch in the cinema, because you will hear and see all the sounds, lights, and a pretty large screen, and the atmosphere between watch at home and cinema is really different, so I prefer to watch at cinema.	118	15.0	Frame, your article shows a genuine enthusiasm for horror and thriller films, and that energy comes through clearly. I especially liked how you described the experience of watching 'Saw' — the detail about almost crying out at a jumpscare tells the reader exactly how intense the film was. You also did a great job explaining why the cinema is better than watching at home, comparing the sound, lights, screen, and atmosphere. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll want to make your opening sentence grab the reader's attention more strongly and use a wider variety of connecting words to link your ideas. Let's look at each subscale in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy watch films kind of horror, thriller and mystery.</em> You clearly stated your favourite genres right at the start, which tells the reader exactly what the article is about.</li>\n  <li><em>I almost cried out when I saw a jumpscare with loud sound and the horror atmosphere.</em> You gave a specific, personal example from 'Saw' that makes your writing vivid and memorable.</li>\n  <li><em>the atmosphere between watch at home and cinema is really different, so I prefer to watch at cinema.</em> You explained your preference with a clear comparison, fully informing the reader of your reasoning.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would open the article with a question or a bold statement that immediately hooks the reader. Your current opening, <em>I enjoy watch films kind of horror, thriller and mystery</em>, is clear but a bit flat for a website article where readers expect to be drawn in.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy watch films kind of horror, thriller and mystery.</em> → <strong>Are you the kind of person who loves a good scare? Then horror, thriller, and mystery films are for you.</strong> Starting with a question makes the reader feel included and eager to continue.</li>\n  <li><em>I'd recommend to watch in the cinema</em> → <strong>I'd recommend watching in the cinema</strong>. This small grammar fix keeps your register natural and consistent throughout the article.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear introduction, body, and conclusion, and links ideas with a variety of connecting words. You have three paragraphs, which is a good start, but your ideas repeat a bit and you rely on the same few connectors like 'and', 'but', and 'because'.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>They are very fun and frightening to watch, but I like it. I like the way it shows some mysterious scenes and jumpscare...</em> → <strong>They are very fun and frightening to watch, which is exactly why I enjoy them. I especially like the mysterious scenes and jumpscares...</strong> Using 'which is exactly why' and 'especially' adds variety and makes the sequence of ideas feel less repetitive.</li>\n  <li><em>I'd recommend to watch in the cinema, because you will hear and see all the sounds, lights, and a pretty large screen, and the atmosphere between watch at home and cinema is really different, so I prefer to watch at cinema.</em> → <strong>I'd recommend watching in the cinema because you can hear every sound and see every light on a large screen. The atmosphere at the cinema is completely different from watching at home, so I prefer it.</strong> Breaking this long sentence into two shorter ones makes your argument clearer and easier to follow.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of grammatical forms with good control, and only makes minor errors. The main pattern holding you back is using the base form of verbs where you need the '-ing' form (gerund) or the correct verb tense. Let's fix that.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy watch films kind of horror...</em> → <strong>I enjoy watching horror, thriller, and mystery films.</strong> After the verb 'enjoy', you need the '-ing' form. This same rule applies to 'recommend' — you wrote <em>I'd recommend to watch</em> but it should be <strong>I'd recommend watching</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>even if it's so terrify</em> → <strong>even if it's so terrifying</strong>. You need the adjective form here, not the verb. 'Terrify' is a verb; 'terrifying' is the adjective that describes the film.</li>\n  <li><em>the movie name's Saw</em> → <strong>the movie is called Saw</strong> or <strong>the movie's name is Saw</strong>. 'Name's' is a contraction of 'name is', but 'movie name's Saw' sounds unnatural. Use 'is called' or 'is named' instead.</li>\n  <li><em>extraordinary terrify</em> → <strong>extraordinarily terrifying</strong>. You need the adverb 'extraordinarily' to modify the adjective 'terrifying'. This is a common pattern: adverb + adjective.</li>\n  <li><em>the atmosphere between watch at home and cinema is really different</em> → <strong>the atmosphere between watching at home and at the cinema is really different</strong>. After 'between', use the '-ing' form of the verb. Also, add 'the' before 'cinema' for consistency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here's a self-check you can apply to any sentence: after verbs like 'enjoy', 'like', 'recommend', and 'prefer', ask yourself — should the next word end in '-ing'? If yes, use the gerund form.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34936	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	What kind of films do I enjoy? Well, I alway enjoy a lot of kind of films but I will tell you what I relly relly enjoy. It is fantasy sci-fi: historic like china. thai or engshil historic comdy animation mistical and alite bit of romans. That all my favorite. some thire like to watch horror telrr meder or drma one. I makey a films lover, but no I just want to watch so that why a watch a lot of films and moives a lot of kind.<br><br>Do I preter watching them at the cinema or at home? wow! this is relly hard for me to choas. So we slla of cinema firt in weekend esply in sub day. well sunday is the day I have exther class in a small and my mom usally take me to see the movie there I luv the food there is chases in sied d sos eguas that was delig wile watching the movie. At home well My mom watch the TV and say come and watch the moive but I say no some time the moive wesrit inthereing for me or sometime we have watch in the cinema but one exsp tion when I miss the moives at the cinema or I want to watch it agine. So yoh hard chose	223	11.0	Richter, you have a real passion for films and it comes through clearly in your article. I love how you listed so many genres you enjoy — from fantasy sci-fi to animation to a little bit of romance. You also gave a personal reason for watching at the cinema: the food there is delicious. That's the kind of specific detail that makes an article interesting to read. To reach Band 5, you'll need to make your sentences clearer so the reader can follow every idea without guessing. Let's look at each part of your writing.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all required information in a way the reader can understand easily. You answered both questions — what films you like and where you prefer to watch them — but some of your ideas are hard to follow because of language errors.</p><ul><li><em>I alway enjoy a lot of kind of films but I will tell you what I relly relly enjoy. It is fantasy sci-fi: historic like china. thai or engshil historic comdy animation mistical and alite bit of romans.</em> → <strong>I always enjoy many kinds of films, but I will tell you what I really really enjoy. It is fantasy, sci-fi, historical (like Chinese, Thai, or English history), comedy, animation, mystical, and a little bit of romance.</strong> The reader can see your list of genres, but the spelling and missing punctuation make it confusing.</li><li><em>That all my favorite.</em> → <strong>Those are all my favourites.</strong> A small fix makes your meaning clear.</li><li><em>So we slla of cinema firt in weekend esply in sub day.</em> → <strong>So we usually go to the cinema first on the weekend, especially on Sunday.</strong> Your idea about Sunday trips with your mom is a good personal detail, but the spelling makes it hard to understand.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an article smoothly and keeps the reader engaged throughout. You used an informal, personal tone with phrases like 'Well' and 'wow!' which suits a website article. Your two sections clearly answer the two questions. However, some sentences are so unclear that the reader has to stop and guess.</p><ul><li><em>I makey a films lover, but no I just want to watch so that why a watch a lot of films and moives a lot of kind.</em> → <strong>I am a film lover, but I just want to watch, so that is why I watch a lot of films and movies of many kinds.</strong> The reader can sense your enthusiasm, but the errors make the sentence confusing.</li><li><em>one exsp tion when I miss the moives at the cinema or I want to watch it agine.</em> → <strong>One exception is when I miss the movies at the cinema or I want to watch them again.</strong> This is a good idea — explaining when you do watch at home — but the spelling distracts from it.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas clearly within paragraphs, not just between them. You used a good structure: two paragraphs for two questions, with linking words like 'Well', 'but', 'So', and 'At home'. The reader can see the overall plan. However, the sequence of ideas inside each paragraph is sometimes confusing.</p><ul><li><em>So we slla of cinema firt in weekend esply in sub day. well sunday is the day I have exther class in a small and my mom usally take me to see the movie there I luv the food there is chases in sied d sos eguas that was delig wile watching the movie.</em> You jump from 'we go on Sunday' to 'I have extra class' to 'my mom takes me' to 'the food is delicious'. Try putting one idea per sentence: <strong>We usually go to the cinema on Sunday. On Sunday I have an extra class in the morning, and my mom takes me to see a movie afterwards. I love the food there — the cheese and sauce are delicious while watching the movie.</strong></li><li><em>At home well My mom watch the TV and say come and watch the moive but I say no some time the moive wesrit inthereing for me or sometime we have watch in the cinema but one exsp tion when I miss the moives at the cinema or I want to watch it agine.</em> This is one very long sentence. Break it into shorter parts: <strong>At home, my mom watches TV and says, 'Come and watch the movie.' Sometimes I say no because the movie is not interesting for me. But one exception is when I miss the movies at the cinema or I want to watch them again.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses basic vocabulary and grammar correctly, with few errors. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>every sentence needs a subject and a verb that agree.</strong> Many of your errors come from missing subjects, wrong verb forms, or missing 'to be' verbs.</p><ul><li><em>I alway enjoy a lot of kind of films</em> → <strong>I always enjoy a lot of kinds of films.</strong> Add 's' to 'kind' to show there are many kinds. Also check your spelling of 'always'.</li><li><em>That all my favorite.</em> → <strong>Those are all my favourites.</strong> You need the verb 'are' here. 'That' is singular, but you are talking about many genres, so use 'those'.</li><li><em>I makey a films lover</em> → <strong>I am a film lover.</strong> You need the verb 'am' (to be) here. 'Makey' is not a word — use 'am' to describe yourself.</li><li><em>my mom usally take me to see the movie</em> → <strong>my mom usually takes me to see the movie.</strong> When the subject is 'my mom' (he/she/it), the verb needs an 's' at the end: 'takes'.</li><li><em>the moive wesrit inthereing for me</em> → <strong>the movie was not interesting for me.</strong> The word you want is 'was not interesting'. Check your spelling of 'movie' and 'interesting'.</li></ul><p>A good self-check: after you write a sentence, read it aloud and ask yourself, 'Does this sentence have a subject and a verb? Do they match?' This will catch many of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35309	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.<br><br>Lou has arrived at an island called “Lilly Larry’s island” legend says whoever desire love, shall be granted on this island. Lou has been all alone, and she desired love. She searched for days, and stop upon a website.<br><br>The website is an advertisement on Lilly Larry’s island, going back to Lilly, after she arrives she was greeted by a local, his name is Lei. Lilly fell inlove after their first conversation, and after they went tanning on the beach together, they ate together, they laughed, they enjoyed the view. The sun is setting, and that is when Lei confessed his feelings, and she reciprocated.<br><br>They lived together, happily ever after.	133	13.0	Atom, your story has a strong romantic arc and a clear destination for the characters. The opening line about Lou seeing the golden beaches is a great way to start. You also used the legend of the island to create a reason for Lou's search, which is a smart way to build a plot. To reach Band 5, focus on keeping the main character's name consistent and on slowing down the key moments so the reader can follow the journey step by step. Let's look at each part of the rubric to see exactly how to improve.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer includes all the main points from the task and develops them in a way that feels complete. You have a clear beginning and ending, but the story jumps from Lou searching for love to finding it very quickly.</p><ul><li><em>She searched for days, and stop upon a website.</em> → <strong>She searched for days and stopped upon a website.</strong> This is the only step between wanting love and finding it. Add one or two sentences about what she hoped the website would offer, or what she thought about before clicking the link. This would make the search feel more real.</li><li><em>Lilly fell inlove after their first conversation, and after they went tanning on the beach together, they ate together, they laughed, they enjoyed the view.</em> → <strong>Lilly fell in love after their first conversation. They went tanning on the beach, ate together, laughed, and enjoyed the view.</strong> You list several events in one long sentence. A Band 5 writer would choose one or two of these moments and describe them in more detail, so the reader can picture the scene.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer tells a story that the reader can follow easily from start to finish. Your story has a beginning, middle, and end, but the change from Lou to Lilly confuses the reader.</p><ul><li><em>Lou has arrived at an island called 'Lilly Larry's island'</em> → <strong>Lou has arrived at an island called 'Lilly Larry's Island'.</strong> The name of the island has 'Lilly' in it, which makes the reader think the main character's name is Lilly. Then later you write <em>Lilly fell inlove</em>, which feels like a different person. Keep the main character as Lou throughout the whole story. If the island is named after someone else, make that clear. For example: 'Lou arrived at an island called Lilly Larry's Island, named after a famous explorer.'</li><li><em>after she arrives she was greeted by a local, his name is Lei.</em> → <strong>After she arrived, she was greeted by a local whose name was Lei.</strong> The word 'she' here refers to Lou, but because you just mentioned 'Lilly' in the island name, the reader has to pause and guess who 'she' is. Using the character's name here would remove the confusion.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects events so the sequence is clear and logical. You use paragraphs and some linking words, but the jump from the website to Lilly's arrival on the island is confusing.</p><ul><li><em>stop upon a website. The website is an advertisement on Lilly Larry's island, going back to Lilly, after she arrives she was greeted by a local</em> → <strong>stopped upon a website. The website was an advertisement for Lilly Larry's Island. Lou decided to go there, and after she arrived, she was greeted by a local.</strong> The phrase 'going back to Lilly' is unclear. The reader does not know who is going back or why. Add a clear step: she sees the ad, she decides to travel, she arrives. That makes the sequence easy to follow.</li><li><em>after they went tanning on the beach together, they ate together, they laughed, they enjoyed the view.</em> → <strong>After they went tanning on the beach, they ate together. They laughed and enjoyed the view.</strong> A list of actions without 'and' before the last item can feel rushed. Use 'and' before the final action, or break the list into shorter sentences to give each moment its own space.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses past tense consistently and controls verb forms with few errors. Your vocabulary choices are good, but the main grammar rule you need to practise is <strong>past tense consistency</strong>. Several verbs are in present tense or have irregular past forms that need attention.</p><ul><li><em>stop upon a website</em> → <strong>stopped upon a website</strong>. 'Stop' is present tense. The story is in the past, so use 'stopped'.</li><li><em>after she arrives she was greeted</em> → <strong>after she arrived, she was greeted</strong>. 'Arrives' is present tense. Keep 'arrived' to match the rest of the story.</li><li><em>The sun is setting</em> → <strong>The sun was setting</strong>. This is a beautiful image, but 'is' is present tense. Change it to 'was' to stay in the past.</li><li><em>fell inlove</em> → <strong>fell in love</strong>. 'In love' is two words. This is a common spelling error, but it matters for Band 5.</li><li><em>They lived together, happily ever after.</em> This is a strong ending. You used the past tense correctly here. Keep this pattern in mind: every time you write a verb, ask yourself, 'Is this story happening now, or did it happen in the past?' If it happened in the past, use the past tense form.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29508	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy!<br><br>Staying with you for a week sounds incredible. I'm very excited!<br><br>On my first evening there, I would like to relax at home first. I think I need time to adapt and get comfortable with your family. If you want to go to the concert instead, please let me know about it.<br><br>As for activity, I think that we could go climbing in the mountains. I'd love to expirience new thing. I never climb the mountain before, but I always want to do it.<br><br>Thank you for the email.<br><br>can't wait<br><br>Satang	109	11.0	Satang, you did a great job showing your excitement in this email. Phrases like <em>Staying with you for a week sounds incredible</em> and <em>I'm very excited!</em> make your friend feel welcome and happy. You also explained your reasons clearly when you said you wanted to relax at home first. To reach Band 5, you need to follow every instruction in the task notes carefully. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how you can improve.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer addresses all four notes in the task completely and correctly. You did well on the first two notes — your excitement and your preference to relax at home are clear. However, the third note says 'No, because...' but you wrote <em>I think that we could go climbing in the mountains</em>, which says yes instead of no. Also, the fourth note asks you to include a question, but you didn't ask one.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think that we could go climbing in the mountains</em> → <strong>I don't think climbing is a good idea because I've never done it before.</strong> The task note told you to say no and give a reason. Your job is to follow that instruction exactly.</li>\n  <li>You need a question for your friend. For example: <strong>Would you like to do something else instead, like watching a movie at home?</strong> This shows you're thinking about your friend's plans too.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent, natural tone throughout the email. Your opening <em>Hi Sandy!</em> and closing <em>can't wait</em> feel friendly and appropriate for a friend. But <em>Thank you for the email</em> sounds a bit formal, like a business letter. The target reader can follow your ideas, but the tone shifts slightly.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thank you for the email</em> → <strong>Thanks for your email!</strong> This keeps the friendly, casual tone you used at the start.</li>\n  <li>Your closing <em>can't wait</em> is good, but it would be stronger with a full sentence: <strong>Can't wait to see you!</strong> It feels more complete and personal.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly with a variety of linking words and a clear structure. You used paragraph breaks for each topic, which is excellent. Your phrases <em>On my first evening there</em> and <em>As for activity</em> help the reader follow your thoughts. The sequence is logical, but the transition from the concert to climbing could be smoother.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As for activity, I think that we could go climbing...</em> → <strong>As for the activity, I think we could go climbing...</strong> Adding 'the' before 'activity' makes it sound more natural. Also, consider a linking phrase like <strong>For our main activity,</strong> to connect better with the previous paragraph about the first evening.</li>\n  <li>Your email flows well from excitement → first evening → activity → closing. Keep this structure, but make sure each paragraph clearly answers one note from the task.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. Your vocabulary is appropriate for the task — words like <em>excited</em>, <em>relax</em>, <em>adapt</em>, and <em>comfortable</em> show you have good basic words. The main grammar rule that will help you is: use the present perfect tense to talk about experiences before now (e.g., 'I have never climbed...'). Let's fix the errors.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'd love to expirience new thing</em> → <strong>I'd love to experience new things.</strong> 'Expirience' is a spelling error — the correct spelling is 'experience'. Also, 'thing' should be plural because you're talking about new experiences in general.</li>\n  <li><em>I never climb the mountain before</em> → <strong>I have never climbed a mountain before.</strong> Use 'have' + past participle (climbed) for experiences before now. Also, 'the mountain' sounds like one specific mountain — use 'a mountain' for a general idea.</li>\n  <li><em>I always want to do it</em> → <strong>I have always wanted to do it.</strong> Again, use present perfect: 'have always wanted' because this is a feeling that started in the past and continues now.</li>\n  <li>Check your capitalisation: <em>can't wait</em> at the start of a sentence should be <strong>Can't wait</strong>. Also, in <em>I'm very excited!</em> you used correct capitalisation — keep doing that for every sentence.</li>\n  <li><strong>Self-check for any sentence:</strong> Ask yourself, 'Is this about an experience before now? If yes, use have/has + past participle (e.g., have climbed, have wanted, have done).'</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29576	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hello Sandy.<br><br>I'm so happy and excited to coming and stay with you for a week!<br><br>On our first evening, I prefer to go to a concert and then we will come back to relax at home because I want to visit the concert but I don't want to stay at the rock concert all day I want to relax at home after that.<br><br>I don't want to go climbing because it's very dansev. I think we should play a football or relax at home it might be better.<br><br>I have a two question First, what should I do before I stay with you? and the last question is can I invite my friend to join? He is very kind and friendly his name is Mark.	138	15.0	Dee, your email is full of genuine enthusiasm and it's clear you understood every part of the task. You made sure to address all four notes from the prompt: your excitement about the visit, your preference for the concert and relaxing at home, your reason for not wanting to go climbing, and two thoughtful questions at the end. That's exactly what a Band 5 writer does. Your tone is warm and friendly throughout, which suits an email to a friend perfectly. To move even closer to Band 5 across all areas, let's focus on making your grammar more consistent and your paragraph transitions smoother. Here's a breakdown of each area.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm so happy and excited to coming and stay with you for a week!</em> You clearly expressed your excitement about the visit, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to go to a concert and then we will come back to relax at home because I want to visit the concert but I don't want to stay at the rock concert all day</em> You stated your preference and gave a clear reason, fully informing the reader.</li>\n  <li><em>I don't want to go climbing because it's very dansev.</em> You explained why you don't want to climb, and your two questions at the end show you've thought about practical details. All content points are covered.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an email to a friend consistently and naturally, with a tone that stays friendly and appropriate throughout. Your opening and closing are warm, and the reader can follow your ideas without any trouble. To reach Band 5, pay attention to the phrasing of your questions and a few slightly informal word choices.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I have a two question First, what should I do before I stay with you? and the last question is can I invite my friend to join?</em> → <strong>I have two questions. First, what should I do before I stay with you? And my second question is, can I invite my friend to join?</strong> Breaking the questions into separate sentences makes them clearer and more natural for an email.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should play a football or relax at home it might be better.</em> → <strong>I think we should play football or relax at home. That might be better.</strong> Splitting this into two sentences makes your suggestion easier to read.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly using a range of linking words, and the sequence of information flows naturally from one point to the next. Your email has a logical order, but some transitions feel a bit sudden and the way you link ideas could be smoother.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I don't want to go climbing because it's very dansev. I think we should play a football or relax at home it might be better.</em> → <strong>I don't want to go climbing because it's very dangerous. Instead, I think we should play football or relax at home. That might be better.</strong> Adding 'Instead' creates a smoother transition from your refusal to your suggestion.</li>\n  <li><em>I have a two question First, what should I do before I stay with you? and the last question is can I invite my friend to join?</em> → <strong>I have two questions. First, what should I do before I stay with you? Second, can I invite my friend to join?</strong> Using 'First' and 'Second' to introduce each question makes the structure clearer and more organised.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls a range of grammatical forms with only minor errors, and vocabulary is used precisely. Your vocabulary is good for this level, and you use present and future tenses mostly correctly. The main pattern that needs attention is how you form phrases after verbs like 'to' and how you handle countable nouns like 'question'.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your vocabulary choices are strong: <em>excited, concert, relax, dangerous, football, invite, kind and friendly</em>. These are all appropriate and effective words for an email to a friend.</li>\n  <li><em>to coming and stay</em> → <strong>to come and stay</strong>. After 'to', use the base form of the verb (the infinitive), not the -ing form. So 'to come' not 'to coming', and 'to stay' not 'to stay' (you had 'stay' correct here, but 'coming' needs fixing).</li>\n  <li><em>I have a two question</em> → <strong>I have two questions</strong>. 'Question' is a countable noun. When you say 'two', you need the plural form 'questions'. Also, don't use 'a' before a number like 'two'.</li>\n  <li><em>it's very dansev</em> → <strong>it's very dangerous</strong>. Check the spelling of 'dangerous'. It's a common word for this topic, so practising it will help.</li>\n  <li>Capitalisation: <em>First</em> should start a new sentence: <strong>I have two questions. First, what should I do...</strong> Also, <em>He is very kind and friendly his name is Mark.</em> → <strong>He is very kind and friendly. His name is Mark.</strong> Start a new sentence after 'friendly'.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29584	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi,<br><br>Thanks for emailing me, I'm also excited! Can't believe we get to spend a week together.<br>I love an idea of going to the rock concert, I'm fond of it. It's my favorite music<br>genre, so I'm going with you.<br><br>For mount - climbing, I don't really want to go. The weather has been hot lately, and<br>that way we could get heat stroke. <br><br>Also, I heard that there's a new movie coming out next week, it is called<br>"The Backroom". I'm wondering if you want to watch with me? or you want to do<br>something else.<br><br>Another thing, do you want me to get anything for the sleepover? Please let me know!<br>Take care,<br>Yaya	139	14.0	Yaya, your email to Sandy is warm and personal. You did a great job showing your excitement with <em>Can't believe we get to spend a week together</em>, and you clearly stated your preference for the rock concert. The question about the sleepover at the end is exactly the kind of practical detail the task wanted. To reach Band 5, you need to make sure every part of the task is covered. The task asked you to ask Sandy a question related to the visit, like what to bring or what activities to do. Your question about watching a movie is nice, but it doesn't connect to the visit itself. Also, some of your sentences could be smoother, and your paragraphs could flow more naturally from one idea to the next. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer covers every point the task asks for completely. You expressed excitement, chose the rock concert, and explained why you don't want to climb. That's strong. The missing piece is your question to Sandy. The task said 'Ask Sandy about something related to the visit.' Your question about watching a movie is friendly, but it's not about the visit itself.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm wondering if you want to watch with me? or you want to do something else.</em> → <strong>I'm wondering if you'd like to bring any snacks for the sleepover?</strong> This directly asks about the visit, which is what the task required.</li>\n  <li><em>Another thing, do you want me to get anything for the sleepover?</em> This is perfect. You asked a relevant question about the visit. Keep this kind of question in mind for future tasks.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the email format naturally and keeps the tone consistent throughout. Your opening and closing are friendly and appropriate. The main issue is one awkward phrase that breaks the smooth flow.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I love an idea of going to the rock concert</em> → <strong>I love the idea of going to the rock concert</strong>. 'An idea' is not specific enough. Use 'the idea' because you are referring to Sandy's specific suggestion.</li>\n  <li>Your closing <em>Take care, Yaya</em> is warm and fits the email to a friend. Well done.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader moves easily from one topic to the next. You used paragraphs and basic linking words like 'also' and 'another thing', which is good. However, the jump from climbing to the movie feels sudden, and the reader might wonder how the topics are related.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>For mount - climbing, I don't really want to go. The weather has been hot lately, and that way we could get heat stroke. Also, I heard that there's a new movie coming out next week...</em> → <strong>For mount - climbing, I don't really want to go. The weather has been hot lately, and we could get heat stroke. After that, maybe we could watch a new movie called 'The Backroom' if you're interested.</strong> This connects the two activities with a time sequence, making the paragraph flow naturally.</li>\n  <li>Your question about the sleepover at the end works well as a separate paragraph. It's clear and easy to follow.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. Your everyday vocabulary is good: <em>excited, rock concert, favorite music genre, heat stroke, sleepover</em>. The main grammar issue is with articles (a, an, the) and prepositions. Let's fix those.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I love an idea of going to the rock concert</em> → <strong>I love the idea of going to the rock concert</strong>. Use 'the' because you are talking about a specific idea that Sandy mentioned. 'An' is for general, non-specific things.</li>\n  <li><em>For mount - climbing</em> → <strong>For mountain climbing</strong> or <strong>As for mountain climbing</strong>. 'Mount' is a title for a specific mountain (e.g., Mount Everest). The activity is 'mountain climbing'. Also, avoid the hyphen here.</li>\n  <li><em>that way we could get heat stroke</em> → <strong>that way we might get heat stroke</strong> or <strong>that way we could get heatstroke</strong>. 'Could' works, but 'might' sounds more natural for a possibility. 'Heatstroke' is usually written as one word.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm wondering if you want to watch with me? or you want to do something else.</em> → <strong>I'm wondering if you want to watch it with me, or if you want to do something else.</strong> When you connect two options, use a comma and 'if' again for parallel structure. Also, do not capitalise 'or' after a question mark.</li>\n  <li>Check your capitalisation: <em>I'm wondering if you want to watch with me? or you want to do something else.</em> The word 'or' should not be capitalised. Also, <em>it is called "The Backroom"</em> is correct, but make sure movie titles are consistently capitalised.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29615	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy!<br><br>I'm so excited too! It was my first time that I stay with my friend for a week! On my First evening, I think I would like to relax at home because it have an interesting TV program that evening, so that why. And for the ideas about go climbing in the mountains while I'm here, I think it's not a great ideas because I afraid of highly place, and it, looks danger, but I have a better ideas! Why shouldn't we go to zoo instead. It's way be better than climbing in the mountains, or maybe we should go to the cinema to watch some interesting films. And I have some questions to ask you. Would you mind if I went to sleep lates because of my personal work? please tell me.<br><br>I can't wait!<br><br>Punch	151	15.0	Punch, your email to Sandy is full of energy and personality. You did a great job covering every single point the task asked for: you showed excitement, explained why you don't want to climb, suggested a zoo or cinema instead, and even asked a thoughtful question about sleeping late. That means Sandy knows exactly what's happening, which is the main job of an email. To reach Band 5 in all areas, you need to focus on making your grammar more accurate and your sentences flow more smoothly. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm so excited too!</em> You expressed genuine enthusiasm, which matches the friendly tone of the task perfectly.</li>\n  <li><em>I think I would like to relax at home because it have an interesting TV program that evening, so that why.</em> You clearly stated your preference for the first evening and gave a reason, meeting the first note completely.</li>\n  <li><em>Why shouldn't we go to zoo instead.</em> You offered an alternative activity, which is exactly what the task wanted after refusing the climbing suggestion.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistently friendly and natural tone throughout, without any awkward phrases that slow the reader down. Your email starts and ends warmly, but a few expressions make the reader pause.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>so that why</em> → <strong>so that's why</strong>. This phrase needs the verb 'is' to be complete. A small fix makes it sound natural.</li>\n  <li><em>It's way be better</em> → <strong>It would be way better</strong> or <strong>It's much better</strong>. 'Way be' is not grammatical. Use 'would be' or 'is much' instead.</li>\n  <li>Your closing <em>please tell me</em> is a little direct. Try <strong>Let me know what you think!</strong> to keep the friendly, conversational feel.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. Your email has a logical order, but the move from refusing climbing to suggesting the zoo feels sudden.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think it's not a great ideas because I afraid of highly place, and it, looks danger, but I have a better ideas! Why shouldn't we go to zoo instead.</em> → <strong>I think it's not a great idea because I'm afraid of high places and it looks dangerous. But I have a better idea! Why don't we go to the zoo instead?</strong> Adding 'But' and changing 'Why shouldn't we' to 'Why don't we' makes the transition smoother and more natural.</li>\n  <li>Your question about sleeping late comes after the alternative suggestion without a clear link. Try adding <strong>Also,</strong> or <strong>By the way,</strong> before <em>I have some questions to ask you.</em> to show you're moving to a new topic.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and vocabulary with few errors, and the reader never has to guess the meaning. Your vocabulary is good, but several grammar patterns need attention. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>every sentence needs a subject + a correctly conjugated verb</strong>. Let's fix the most important ones.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your vocabulary choices are strong. Words like <em>excited</em>, <em>relax</em>, <em>climbing</em>, <em>zoo</em>, <em>cinema</em>, and <em>films</em> are all perfect for this task.</li>\n  <li><em>It was my first time that I stay</em> → <strong>It's my first time staying</strong> or <strong>It was my first time staying</strong>. After 'first time', use the -ing form of the verb, not 'that I + present tense'.</li>\n  <li><em>it have an interesting TV program</em> → <strong>it has an interesting TV program</strong>. 'It' is singular, so the verb must be 'has', not 'have'. This is a subject-verb agreement rule.</li>\n  <li><em>I afraid of highly place</em> → <strong>I'm afraid of high places</strong>. You need the verb 'am' (I'm). Also, 'highly' is an adverb; use the adjective 'high'. And 'place' should be plural 'places' because you mean heights in general.</li>\n  <li><em>it, looks danger</em> → <strong>it looks dangerous</strong>. No comma needed between the subject and verb. 'Danger' is a noun; use the adjective 'dangerous'.</li>\n  <li><em>Why shouldn't we go to zoo</em> → <strong>Why don't we go to the zoo</strong>. 'Zoo' needs the article 'the'. 'Why shouldn't we' sounds like you're asking for permission not to go; 'Why don't we' is a natural suggestion.</li>\n  <li><em>to sleep lates</em> → <strong>to sleep late</strong>. 'Late' is already an adverb; no 's' needed. Also check capitalisation: <em>First evening</em> should be <strong>first evening</strong> (no capital), and <em>I have some questions to ask you. please tell me.</em> should be <strong>I have some questions to ask you. Please tell me.</strong> (capital after a period).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check for next time: Read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Do I have a subject? Is my verb in the right form?' If you do this for every sentence, your grammar will improve quickly.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29710	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy,<br><br>Me too! I really excited to stay with you this week. For the rock concert I really want to go because I hope we will have a good time together, but if the ticket is sold out we can relax at home. But I can not go to climbing in the mountains because I don't know how to climb, and last month I broke my leg so this is a big problem I can not run, jump or play sport to much. How about picnic we can have picnic at the park near your house or we can shopping together. I have 1 question who will come to show at rock concert.<br><br>Can't wait<br><br>PingPing	119	10.0	PingPing, your email to Sandy is full of warmth and personality. You did a great job responding to all three notes: you said you're excited, you chose the concert, and you gave a clear reason for not climbing. Phrases like <em>I hope we will have a good time together</em> and <em>last month I broke my leg so this is a big problem</em> show that you can express your feelings and explain your situation in a way the reader can understand. To reach Band 5, the next step is to focus on two things: keeping your ideas tightly connected to the task, and improving your grammar so your sentences are consistently correct. Let's look at each area.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer covers all three task notes clearly and adds only relevant details. You answered all three notes, which is good, but some of your extra ideas, like suggesting a picnic and shopping, are not connected to the task. The task asks you to plan activities for your stay, not to suggest new ones that replace the concert or climbing.</p><ul><li><em>How about picnic we can have picnic at the park near your house or we can shopping together.</em> → <strong>If the concert tickets are sold out, maybe we could have a picnic at the park near your house instead.</strong> This keeps the picnic as a backup plan related to the concert, not a separate suggestion.</li><li><em>I have 1 question who will come to show at rock concert.</em> → <strong>I have one question: who will come to the rock concert with us?</strong> This question is fine, but make sure it connects back to the concert activity rather than standing alone.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the email format naturally and keeps a friendly but clear tone throughout. Your email has a good opening and closing, and the tone is warm. The main issue is that one phrase is a bit unclear.</p><ul><li><em>How about picnic</em> → <strong>How about a picnic</strong> or <strong>What about having a picnic</strong>. Adding the article 'a' makes the suggestion clear and natural.</li><li>The rest of your email, like <em>Me too!</em> and <em>Can't wait</em>, sounds exactly right for an email to a friend. Keep that friendly voice.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow without jumping. Your email has a clear beginning, middle, and end, but the jump from climbing to picnic feels sudden, and the question at the end seems disconnected.</p><ul><li><em>...so this is a big problem I can not run, jump or play sport to much. How about picnic...</em> → <strong>...so this is a big problem. I cannot run, jump, or play sport too much. If the concert tickets are sold out, how about a picnic at the park near your house?</strong> The word 'if' links the picnic to the concert, making the flow smoother.</li><li><em>I have 1 question who will come to show at rock concert.</em> → <strong>I have one question: who will come to the rock concert with us?</strong> Placing this question right after you talk about the concert keeps the topic connected.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar and a range of vocabulary with few errors. Your vocabulary is good for the task, and some sentences are correct, like <em>I hope we will have a good time together</em>. The main pattern that needs attention is missing verbs (especially 'am' and 'to be') and missing prepositions/articles. Let's fix these one by one.</p><ul><li><em>I really excited to stay with you this week.</em> → <strong>I am really excited to stay with you this week.</strong> After 'I', you need the verb 'am' before an adjective like 'excited'. This is a common rule: subject + be verb + adjective.</li><li><em>I can not go to climbing in the mountains</em> → <strong>I cannot go climbing in the mountains</strong> or <strong>I cannot go to climb in the mountains</strong>. After 'go', use the gerund (climbing) or 'to + verb' (to climb), not 'to + gerund'.</li><li><em>...play sport to much</em> → <strong>...play sport too much</strong>. 'Too' means 'more than enough'. 'To' is a preposition. Remember: too much / too many = excessive.</li><li><em>I have 1 question who will come to show at rock concert.</em> → <strong>I have one question: who will come to the show at the rock concert?</strong> Add 'the' before 'show' and 'rock concert' because you are talking about a specific event. Also, write 'one' instead of '1' in formal writing.</li><li>Check your capitalisation: words like 'Me' at the start of a sentence are correct, but make sure every sentence starts with a capital letter. Your sentences are fine here, but keep an eye on this in future drafts.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29727	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi! Sandy,<br><br>I can't wait to stay with you! As for the concert, I prefer going to the concert, that way we could enjoy some nice rock-style music, and perhaps get some drinks on the way! However, for the mountain climbing. I really don't like it very well because I used to fell down and went into the hospital for 2 year or so. Also, I was just about to ask you on what I should bring? perhaps, some drinks or food? that way we don't have to buy stuff very often,, and also because I'm kinda short on money right now. I hope you don't mind that.<br><br>Can't wait!<br><br>Mark	118	15.0	Mark, your email to Sandy is genuinely enjoyable to read. You did an excellent job covering all three notes from the task: you expressed excitement about the visit, chose the concert over climbing, explained your reason clearly, and asked a practical question about what to bring. The reader finishes your email feeling fully informed and ready to reply. That is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you will need to polish your grammar and tighten your paragraph structure. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I can't wait to stay with you!</em> You opened with genuine excitement, which perfectly matches the task's first note about expressing feelings about the visit.</li>\n  <li><em>As for the concert, I prefer going to the concert, that way we could enjoy some nice rock-style music</em> You clearly stated your preference and gave a specific reason. The reader understands exactly why you chose the concert.</li>\n  <li><em>I really don't like it very well because I used to fell down and went into the hospital for 2 year or so</em> You explained your past accident honestly. Even with some grammar issues, the meaning is clear and the reader feels your personal reason.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses email conventions smoothly and keeps the tone consistent throughout, so the reader never has to pause to understand. Your opening and closing salutations are perfect, and your friendly tone is warm and natural. The only issue is one sentence that is hard to follow.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I used to fell down and went into the hospital for 2 year or so</em> → <strong>I fell down and ended up in the hospital for about two years.</strong> The phrase 'used to fell down' is confusing because 'used to' describes a past habit, not a single event. Your reader might pause here. Changing it to a simple past tense makes the meaning instant.</li>\n  <li><em>I was just about to ask you on what I should bring?</em> → <strong>I was just about to ask you what I should bring.</strong> The preposition 'on' is unnecessary, and the question mark inside a statement confuses the reader. A declarative sentence works better here.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas so smoothly that the reader never feels a jump. Your email has a logical sequence — excitement, concert choice, climbing refusal, question — but the transitions between these ideas feel a little abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>However, for the mountain climbing. I really don't like it very well</em> → <strong>However, I really don't like mountain climbing because...</strong> The period after 'climbing' creates a sentence fragment. Joining these into one sentence makes the contrast flow naturally.</li>\n  <li><em>Also, I was just about to ask you on what I should bring? perhaps, some drinks or food? that way we don't have to buy stuff very often</em> → <strong>Also, I was just about to ask what I should bring — perhaps some drinks or food? That way we won't have to buy things often.</strong> The jump from the climbing topic to the question feels sudden. Adding a short bridge like 'Anyway' or 'By the way' would help the reader follow your shift in topic.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls past tense forms and sentence structure so that errors never distract the reader. Your vocabulary is good ('rock-style music', 'short on money'), and your meaning is always clear. The main grammar rule that explains most of your errors is the simple past tense for completed actions, not 'used to' + base verb.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I used to fell down and went into the hospital for 2 year or so</em> → <strong>I fell down and went to the hospital for about two years.</strong> 'Used to' describes a repeated past habit (e.g., 'I used to climb every weekend'). For a single event that happened once, use the simple past: 'I fell down'. Also, 'year' needs to be plural when the number is more than one.</li>\n  <li><em>on what I should bring</em> → <strong>what I should bring</strong>. After 'ask', we do not need the preposition 'on'. Just say 'ask you what I should bring'.</li>\n  <li><em>that way we don't have to buy stuff very often</em> → <strong>that way we won't have to buy things very often</strong>. 'Stuff' is informal but acceptable in an email to a friend. The present tense 'don't' is fine here, but 'won't' (future) matches the plan you are describing more naturally.</li>\n  <li><em>I can't wait to stay with you!</em> This sentence is correct and natural. You used the present tense 'can't' perfectly here to express future excitement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Self-check for your next draft:</strong> Read each sentence aloud. If you used 'used to', ask yourself: 'Did this happen many times, or just once?' If it happened once, change to simple past. Also check every noun after a number — if the number is 2 or more, add -s.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29745	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hey, Sandy!<br><br>Me too! I can't wait to spend time with you for a week!<br><br>I think we should go to the concert, since we both love music and it doesn't costs a lot of money too! As for the climbing, I think we shouldn't go without any professional climber or any teacher to help or guide us; but if there are some that is available, text me later! And I wanted to suggest that we should get some food while I stay there, your house is not far from the convenient store right?<br><br>Best wishes!<br>Pheem	107	18.0	Pheem, your email to Sandy is full of energy and personal warmth. You did an excellent job covering every part of the task: you showed excitement, chose the concert over climbing with a clear reason, explained why climbing is risky without a guide, and asked about food. The reader feels like they are actually talking to you. Your enthusiasm makes the email enjoyable to read. To reach Band 5 across all areas, we just need to tighten a few grammar points and make the flow between ideas a little smoother. Let's look at each part of your writing so you can see exactly what to adjust.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think we should go to the concert, since we both love music and it doesn't costs a lot of money too!</em> You clearly stated your preference and gave a personal reason that connects to Sandy's interests. This is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we shouldn't go without any professional climber or any teacher to help or guide us</em> You explained why climbing is not a good idea with a thoughtful, safety-focused reason. The reader fully understands your concern.</li>\n  <li><em>I wanted to suggest that we should get some food while I stay there, your house is not far from the convenient store right?</em> You asked a relevant question about food availability, which shows you have thought about the practical side of the visit.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hey, Sandy!</em> and <em>Best wishes! Pheem</em> Your opening and closing are perfectly suited for a friendly email. They set a warm, casual tone from the start.</li>\n  <li><em>Me too! I can't wait to spend time with you for a week!</em> You responded to Sandy's excitement with your own, which makes the conversation feel real and connected.</li>\n  <li><em>text me later!</em> and <em>right?</em> These phrases keep the tone conversational and engaging, as if you are actually talking to a friend.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects their ideas so smoothly that the reader never pauses to wonder how one thought leads to the next. Your paragraphs are logical, but the jump from climbing to the food suggestion feels a little sudden.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>...text me later! And I wanted to suggest that we should get some food while I stay there...</em> → <strong>...text me later! Also, I wanted to suggest that we get some food while I stay there.</strong> Changing 'And' to 'Also' makes the transition feel more natural, like a new thought rather than a rushed addition.</li>\n  <li><em>...your house is not far from the convenient store right?</em> → <strong>...your house is not far from the convenience store, right?</strong> Adding a comma before 'right?' helps the question flow more clearly. This small punctuation change improves the rhythm of your sentence.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently and uses a wider range of vocabulary accurately. Your vocabulary is strong — <em>professional climber</em> and <em>convenient store</em> show good effort — but a small grammar rule keeps appearing: after <strong>doesn't</strong> and <strong>don't</strong>, the main verb must be in its base form (no -s, no -es, no -ed).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>it doesn't costs a lot of money</em> → <strong>it doesn't cost a lot of money</strong>. After 'doesn't', use the base verb 'cost' without 's'.</li>\n  <li><em>if there are some that is available</em> → <strong>if there are some that are available</strong>. 'Some' refers to multiple things (climbers or teachers), so the verb should be 'are', not 'is'.</li>\n  <li><em>convenient store</em> → <strong>convenience store</strong>. This is a fixed compound noun. The first word is 'convenience', not 'convenient'.</li>\n  <li><em>your house is not far from the convenient store right?</em> → <strong>your house is not far from the convenience store, right?</strong> Also, remember to capitalise the start of every sentence: 'Your house' instead of 'your house' after a period.</li>\n  <li>Self-check: Read each sentence aloud. If you see <strong>doesn't</strong> or <strong>don't</strong>, check that the next verb has no -s, -es, or -ed ending. This one rule will fix most of your grammar mistakes.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29749	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hey Sandy! <br><br>I'm also excited that I get to stay with you for a week. In the evening, yeah lets go to your rock concert in your town. We could have alot of fun there, we could sing and dance along the crowd, or maybe befriend someone who has the same favorite band. I think we shouldn't go mountain climbing, Because im pretty much scared of falling over and highly injuring myself. that's why i don't want to go mountain climbing. Could i ask you something if you have a gym or a sports field could we maybe go there while im still with you. <br><br>See you soon! <br>Pipe	117	15.0	Pipe, your email to Sandy is full of energy and personality. You showed real enthusiasm for the rock concert with details like singing, dancing, and befriending someone who shares your favorite band. That's the kind of specific thinking that makes your writing come alive. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll need to organise your ideas into clear paragraphs and polish a few grammar patterns. Let's look at each part of your writing.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>yeah lets go to your rock concert in your town. We could have alot of fun there, we could sing and dance along the crowd, or maybe befriend someone who has the same favorite band.</em> You fully addressed the first note about what to do in the evening, and you added vivid, personal reasons that make the reader feel your excitement.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we shouldn't go mountain climbing, Because im pretty much scared of falling over and highly injuring myself.</em> You clearly explained why you don't want to climb, giving a honest, personal reason. The reader understands your fear completely.</li>\n  <li><em>Could i ask you something if you have a gym or a sports field could we maybe go there while im still with you.</em> You asked a relevant question about an alternative activity, which shows you've thought about what you would enjoy doing instead.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistently appropriate tone throughout the email and keeps the register natural for a friend without slipping into overly casual language. Your opening and closing are friendly and suitable, but the phrase <em>yeah lets go</em> is a bit too informal for this task.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>yeah lets go</em> → <strong>Yes, let's go</strong>. This small change keeps the enthusiasm but sounds more polished for an email.</li>\n  <li><em>Could i ask you something if you have a gym or a sports field could we maybe go there</em> → <strong>Could I ask you something? If you have a gym or a sports field, could we go there?</strong> Breaking this long question into two shorter sentences makes it easier for the reader to follow and sounds more natural.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer groups related ideas into clear paragraphs and uses a variety of linking words to guide the reader smoothly from one point to the next. Your email is one long paragraph, and the jump from climbing to your question feels sudden.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Try splitting your email into three short paragraphs: one for the concert, one for climbing, and one for your question about the gym. This helps the reader see each idea clearly.</li>\n  <li><em>that's why i don't want to go mountain climbing. Could i ask you something</em> → After explaining why you don't want to climb, add a transition like <strong>Instead, could I ask you something?</strong> This makes the shift feel natural.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling with few errors. Your vocabulary is good — words like <em>rock concert</em>, <em>befriend</em>, and <em>favorite band</em> are well chosen. The main pattern that needs attention is capitalisation: you sometimes capitalise mid-sentence or leave proper nouns lowercase.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Because im pretty much scared</em> → <strong>because I'm pretty much scared</strong>. The word 'because' does not need a capital letter in the middle of a sentence. Also, 'I' is always capitalised.</li>\n  <li><em>Could i ask you something</em> → <strong>Could I ask you something</strong>. The pronoun 'I' must be capitalised every time.</li>\n  <li><em>alot</em> → <strong>a lot</strong>. This is two words, not one.</li>\n  <li><em>highly injuring myself</em> → <strong>seriously injuring myself</strong> or <strong>getting seriously hurt</strong>. 'Highly' is not the natural word here; 'seriously' or 'badly' works better with 'injuring'.</li>\n  <li><em>while im still with you</em> → <strong>while I'm still with you</strong>. Remember to capitalise 'I' and use the apostrophe in 'I'm'.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check: Read every sentence and ask yourself — did I capitalise 'I'? Did I start a word with a capital letter only at the beginning of a sentence? This one habit will fix most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29769	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hey, <br><br>I'm so excited too, to do alot of activitys with you Sandy.<br><br>I would like to go to the rock concert in town, I had been trying to go in one of these for a long time<br><br>No, because if we fall down we might be dead and the mountains near your house was atleast three hour.<br><br>I got a question, where do I sleep. You don't need to make me sleep in bed or anything, I can sleep anywhere even on the floor<br><br>See you soon.<br><br>Posh	103	15.0	Posh, your email is full of energy and personality. You clearly expressed your excitement, your preference for the rock concert, your reasons for not wanting to climb, and a practical question about sleeping arrangements. The reader understands exactly what you want to say. That is a strong start. To reach Band 5 on your next draft, you will need to polish your grammar and spelling, and connect your ideas more smoothly. Let me show you how.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I'm so excited too, to do alot of activitys with you Sandy.</em> You immediately show shared excitement, which matches the task perfectly.</li><li><em>I would like to go to the rock concert in town</em> and <em>No, because if we fall down we might be dead and the mountains near your house was atleast three hour.</em> You clearly state your preference and give two solid reasons: fear of falling and the distance. The reader is fully informed.</li><li><em>I got a question, where do I sleep. You don't need to make me sleep in bed or anything, I can sleep anywhere even on the floor.</em> You ask a relevant question and add a thoughtful detail, which shows you have considered the practical side of the visit.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would keep the informal, friendly tone consistent throughout without any phrases that feel slightly too dramatic for the situation. Your email is warm and enthusiastic, and the opening and closing are appropriate. The phrase <em>we might be dead</em> is a bit strong for a friendly email about a weekend trip. Try something like <em>we could get hurt</em> or <em>it could be dangerous</em>. This keeps the same honest feeling without sounding alarming. Your tone is otherwise excellent, so this is a small adjustment.</p>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would use a wider range of linking words and smoother transitions between ideas. Your email has clear paragraphs for each point, which is good. However, the jump from the concert to climbing feels abrupt. Try adding a transition like <em>As for climbing,</em> or <em>About the climbing trip,</em> at the start of that paragraph. Also, instead of just <em>because</em> and <em>and</em>, use words like <em>since</em>, <em>also</em>, <em>another reason is that</em>, or <em>in addition</em>. This will make your writing flow more naturally.</p><ul><li><em>I would like to go to the rock concert in town, I had been trying to go in one of these for a long time</em> → <strong>I would like to go to the rock concert in town. I have been trying to go to one for a long time.</strong> A period or a linking word like <em>because</em> would connect these two ideas more clearly.</li><li><em>No, because if we fall down we might be dead and the mountains near your house was atleast three hour.</em> → <strong>No, because if we fall, we could get hurt. Also, the mountains near your house are at least three hours away.</strong> Breaking this long sentence into two and adding <em>also</em> makes it easier to follow.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control everyday vocabulary and simple grammatical forms with few errors. Your vocabulary is good, and your meaning is always clear. The main issue is spelling and a few grammar points. Let's fix these.</p><ul><li><em>alot of activitys</em> → <strong>a lot of activities</strong>. <em>A lot</em> is always two words. The plural of <em>activity</em> is <em>activities</em> (change the y to i and add es).</li><li><em>I had been trying to go in one of these</em> → <strong>I have been trying to go to one of these</strong>. Use the present perfect <em>have been trying</em> because you still want to go. Also, you go <em>to</em> a concert, not <em>in</em> a concert.</li><li><em>the mountains near your house was atleast three hour</em> → <strong>the mountains near your house were at least three hours away</strong>. <em>Mountains</em> is plural, so use <em>were</em>. <em>At least</em> is two words. Add <em>away</em> to show distance.</li><li><em>You don't need to make me sleep in bed</em> → <strong>You don't need to give me a bed to sleep in</strong>. The original is understandable, but this version sounds more natural in English.</li><li><strong>Capitalisation:</strong> Remember to capitalise the first word of every sentence. You wrote <em>I got a question, where do I sleep.</em> This should be <strong>I got a question. Where do I sleep?</strong> Also, <em>See you soon.</em> is correct, but check that every sentence starts with a capital letter.</li></ul><p>After you write a sentence, read it aloud and ask yourself: 'Is the spelling correct? Is the verb in the right tense? Did I capitalise the first word?' This habit will help you catch most of these small errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29793	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hey Sandy!<br><br>Me too! I'm also excited, It's going to be an epic week!<br><br>As for the first evening, I prefer going to the rock concert rather than relaxing at home. That way, we can spend more time together outside. I also think it's a better option that staying at home.<br><br>No, I suggest we don't go climbing in the mountains because my left ankle is injured. I think we should go to the beach instead. That way, we can build a sandcastle, go for a swim and watch the sunset together.<br><br>Do you want me to bring snacks over to your house? That way we can watch movies and eat snacks before bed-time.<br><br>Best wishes,<br><br>Charles	138	18.0	Charles, your email to Sandy is lively and personal. You did a great job of showing your excitement with <em>Me too! I'm also excited, It's going to be an epic week!</em> and you handled every part of the task: you chose the rock concert, explained your ankle injury, suggested the beach, and asked about snacks. The reader feels like a real friend is writing to them. To reach Band 5 in every area, you just need to polish a few small things in how you connect your ideas and how you handle grammar and punctuation. Let's look at each subscale.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Me too! I'm also excited, It's going to be an epic week!</em> You immediately show shared enthusiasm, which makes the email feel genuine and friendly.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer going to the rock concert rather than relaxing at home.</em> You clearly state your choice and give a reason, fully covering the task point.</li>\n  <li><em>I suggest we don't go climbing in the mountains because my left ankle is injured. I think we should go to the beach instead.</em> You explain why you can't climb and offer a specific alternative, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>Do you want me to bring snacks over to your house?</em> You ask a relevant question that shows you're thinking ahead about the plan.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hey Sandy!</em> and <em>Best wishes,</em> You use the correct email opening and closing for a friend, which sets the right tone.</li>\n  <li><em>That way, we can spend more time together outside.</em> and <em>That way, we can build a sandcastle, go for a swim and watch the sunset together.</em> Your friendly, enthusiastic voice keeps the reader engaged throughout.</li>\n  <li>Every sentence sounds like a real person talking to a friend, not like a formal letter. That is exactly the right register for this task.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every paragraph so the reader moves smoothly from one idea to the next. You already have a clear structure with one paragraph per task point, which is strong. The only gap is that your final paragraph about snacks feels a little separate from the beach plan.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Do you want me to bring snacks over to your house?</em> → <strong>After we go to the beach, do you want me to bring snacks over to your house?</strong> Adding a time link like "After we go to the beach" ties the snack question directly to the previous paragraph about the beach.</li>\n  <li><em>As for the first evening</em> and <em>No, I suggest we don't go climbing</em> You already use good linking phrases. Keep using phrases like <em>As for</em> and <em>instead</em> to guide the reader.</li>\n  <li>Your paragraphs are in a logical order: excitement → evening plan → next day plan → snack question. That is clear and easy to follow.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple and complex grammar with only very minor slips. Your vocabulary is strong and appropriate, and you attempt good complex sentences. The main area to improve is the rule for <strong>comparatives with 'than'</strong> and <strong>capitalisation after commas</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>a better option that staying at home</em> → <strong>a better option than staying at home</strong>. After a comparative word like <em>better</em>, use <em>than</em> (not <em>that</em>) to introduce the thing you are comparing it to.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm also excited, It's going to be an epic week!</em> → <strong>I'm also excited. It's going to be an epic week!</strong> or <strong>I'm also excited — it's going to be an epic week!</strong> When a comma joins two complete sentences, it creates a comma splice. Use a period or a semicolon instead. Also, after a comma, do not capitalise the next word unless it is a proper noun.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer going to the rock concert rather than relaxing at home.</em> and <em>I suggest we don't go climbing because my left ankle is injured.</em> These are excellent complex sentences with clear cause and effect. Keep building sentences like these.</li>\n  <li>Your vocabulary is varied and precise: <em>epic week, rock concert, relaxing at home, spend more time together, injured, build a sandcastle, go for a swim, watch the sunset, snacks, before bed-time.</em> These are all natural and appropriate for the task.</li>\n  <li>Self-check: After every comma you write, ask yourself: "Is this a full sentence on both sides?" If yes, use a period instead. Also, check every comparative word (better, bigger, more) and make sure the next word is <em>than</em>.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29798	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy!<br><br>I'm also excited about our appointment. Thanks for texting me.<br><br>I would prefer to relax at home because we can have some video game together, and we'll have a lot more time to do another things. That way we'll relax after we finished our work.<br><br>As for the climbing in the mountains, I would not go because climbing is not my cup of tea, and I afraid that I could fall from the mountains.<br><br>Do you want me to buy you some food, or snack, or any thing when I go to stay with you?<br><br>Can't wait!<br><br>See you soon,<br><br>Yaya K	108	18.0	Yaya, your email to Sandy is warm and natural. You made every part of the task feel personal, especially when you wrote <em>climbing is not my cup of tea</em> and <em>Can't wait!</em>. These small touches make your writing sound like a real conversation with a friend. To push your writing even further, focus on polishing your grammar so your strong ideas shine without small mistakes getting in the way. Here is how each area of your writing can move to Band 5.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I would prefer to relax at home because we can have some video game together, and we'll have a lot more time to do another things.</em> You clearly chose the relaxing option and gave two reasons. The reader understands exactly why you prefer this plan.</li><li><em>As for the climbing in the mountains, I would not go because climbing is not my cup of tea, and I afraid that I could fall from the mountains.</em> You explained why you refuse the climbing activity with a personal opinion and a specific fear. That is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>Do you want me to buy you some food, or snack, or any thing when I go to stay with you?</em> You asked a relevant question about bringing supplies, which shows you are thinking ahead about the visit. All three notes are fully covered.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Hi Sandy!</em> and <em>See you soon, Yaya K</em>. Your email opens and closes like a real message to a friend. The tone is friendly from the first word.</li><li><em>Can't wait!</em> This short exclamation adds enthusiasm and makes the reader feel your excitement. It is exactly the kind of language a friend would use.</li><li><em>Thanks for texting me.</em> You acknowledge the previous conversation, which makes the email feel connected and natural. The register stays consistent throughout.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every paragraph so smoothly that the reader never feels a jump between topics. Your email is well structured with one idea per paragraph, but the move from climbing to the food question could be smoother.</p><ul><li><em>As for the climbing in the mountains...</em> → <strong>As for the climbing, I would rather skip it because it is not my cup of tea. By the way, do you want me to buy you some food or snacks when I come?</strong>. Adding a short phrase like 'By the way' or 'Also' before your question makes the transition feel natural instead of sudden.</li><li><em>That way we'll relax after we finished our work.</em> → <strong>That way we can relax after we finish our work.</strong> This sentence already connects your first paragraph well. The logic is clear: playing video games leads to relaxation. Just fix the verb tense and it will be even stronger.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammar with very few errors. Your vocabulary is strong and appropriate, but a small grammar pattern keeps appearing. The main issue is missing verbs and incorrect forms after certain words. Let us fix the most common errors.</p><ul><li><em>I afraid that I could fall from the mountains</em> → <strong>I am afraid that I could fall from the mountains.</strong> After the subject 'I', you need the verb 'am' before 'afraid'. This is a fixed pattern: I am + adjective (afraid, excited, tired, happy).</li><li><em>we'll have a lot more time to do another things</em> → <strong>we'll have a lot more time to do other things.</strong> Use 'other' (not 'another') before plural nouns. 'Another' is for singular nouns only (another thing, another day).</li><li><em>after we finished our work</em> → <strong>after we finish our work.</strong> When talking about a future plan, use present tense after 'after', 'when', 'as soon as', and 'before'. Even though the meaning is future, the verb stays in present simple: after we finish, when I arrive, before you leave.</li><li><em>or snack, or any thing</em> → <strong>or snacks, or anything.</strong> 'Anything' is one word. Also, 'snack' should be plural here because you are talking about food in general, not one specific snack.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself 'Is every subject followed by a verb?' If you see 'I afraid' or 'I excited', add 'am'. If you see 'another' before a plural word, change it to 'other'. These two checks will catch most of your remaining errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30221	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hey, Sandy!<br><br>Thank you for letting me know, it's a great idea!<br><br>I think on our first day I arived, we should stay home and relax, because on my first day I might be very tired! We can play some games and do some activities. What do you think?<br><br>I don't think climbing in the mountains is a good idea, becan it's too risky and it's not worth it, However we can go watch a football match. We can also go ride a gokart too, do you agree with that? I guess that the day that I arived would be so fun!<br><br>See you at your home!<br><br>Best wish<br><br>Tata	126	15.0	Tata, your email to Sandy is genuinely warm and personal. You covered every part of the task completely, and your reader can picture exactly what you want to do together. The way you wrote <em>because on my first day I might be very tired</em> shows you thought about Sandy's perspective, not just your own. To move from Band 4 to Band 5 in the other areas, you'll need to polish your grammar and tighten the connections between your ideas. Let's look at each part of your writing.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thank you for letting me know, it's a great idea!</em> You start by showing excitement about the visit, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I think on our first day I arived, we should stay home and relax, because on my first day I might be very tired!</em> You clearly state your preference for the first evening and give a personal reason. The reader understands your choice completely.</li>\n  <li><em>What do you think?</em> and <em>do you agree with that?</em> You ask Sandy questions, which is a natural way to involve your friend in the planning. The task required you to ask a question, and you did it well.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses email conventions smoothly from start to finish, with a consistent friendly tone and no awkward phrases. Your opening <em>Hey, Sandy!</em> and closing <em>See you at your home!</em> are perfect for a friend. The only thing holding you back is the sign-off <em>Best wish</em>, which should be <strong>Best wishes</strong>. This is a fixed expression in English, so it always uses the plural form. Change that one phrase, and your email will feel polished from the first line to the last.</p>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects each idea to the next so the reader never feels a jump. Your email has a logical order: first evening → climbing refusal → alternative activities. But the jump from <em>it's too risky and it's not worth it</em> straight to <em>However we can go watch a football match</em> feels sudden. The reader wonders: why a football match instead of climbing? Add a bridge sentence like <strong>Instead, I have another idea</strong> or <strong>How about something safer?</strong> Also, the final sentence <em>I guess that the day that I arived would be so fun!</em> belongs with your opening excitement, not at the end. Move it to the first paragraph, or remove it.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>it's too risky and it's not worth it, However we can go watch a football match</em> → <strong>it's too risky and it's not worth it. Instead, how about we go watch a football match?</strong> This creates a clear contrast and a smoother transition.</li>\n  <li><em>I guess that the day that I arived would be so fun!</em> → <strong>I think the day I arrive will be so much fun!</strong> Place this sentence in the first paragraph after your excitement, not at the end of the email.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling so the reader never has to guess the meaning. The main rule that explains most of your errors is <strong>verb tense consistency</strong>: when you write about a future plan, use the future or present tense throughout, not the past tense. You wrote <em>I arived</em> twice, but you are talking about a day that hasn't happened yet. It should be <strong>I arrive</strong> or <strong>I will arrive</strong>. Also, <em>becan</em> is a spelling error for <strong>because</strong>. Let's fix the key patterns.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>on our first day I arived</em> → <strong>on our first day I arrive</strong>. Since the visit is in the future, use present simple for scheduled events.</li>\n  <li><em>the day that I arived would be so fun</em> → <strong>the day I arrive will be so much fun</strong>. Keep the future meaning consistent.</li>\n  <li><em>becan it's too risky</em> → <strong>because it's too risky</strong>. This is a spelling error, not a grammar error. Practise writing <em>because</em> until it feels automatic.</li>\n  <li><em>go ride a gokart</em> → <strong>go riding a go-kart</strong> or <strong>go for a go-kart ride</strong>. After <em>go</em>, use the -ing form of the verb.</li>\n  <li><em>Best wish</em> → <strong>Best wishes</strong>. This is a fixed expression in English, always plural.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After you revise, read your email aloud and check: does every sentence use the correct tense for the time I'm talking about? That one self-check will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29045	2025-09-13 16:19:55.437351+00	2026	Term 1 Exam Question 2	Having pets is a good or bad idea, well from my perspective pets are joyful and cute such as cats and dogs. Overall, having pets is a good idea and there are several reasnes for that.<br><br>Most importantly, pets can heal your mental health because when we play or interact with them they will show us their cuteness and they're very cute especially when you come back from work or school.<br><br>Secondly, they're like our child because they see us as their parents because we feed them and take care their health and make sure they're okay, therefore they'll interact with us a lot and you'll never be lonely.<br><br>However, having pets can be annoying sometimes like when you need to clean their messes, poop, pee whatever and sometimes they'll mess your room up by jumping around and scream and also their claws and teeth are very sharp.<br><br>In conclusion, having pets is good in my opinion because they help with our mental health and we'll be less lonely with them, but it can be worse if you are not ready to take these responsibility like clean up their messes and buy them foods to eat.	192	16.0	Hello In-Sun, that was a thoughtful article you wrote about pets. You did a great job of sharing your thoughts and exploring both the good and sometimes tricky sides of having animals in our lives. The main area to focus on for improvement is your grammar and the precision of your word choices, which will make your ideas even clearer for your readers. Keep up the excellent work in expressing your opinions.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br>✔️ When you finish writing, read your work aloud. This can often help you hear where a sentence might sound a little bit off, like "take care their health" instead of "take care *of* their health."<br>✔️ Pay close attention to word endings, especially for plural nouns and verb forms. For example, make sure you write "reasons" with an 's' and think about whether a word needs to be an '-ing' form, like "screaming" instead of "scream."<br>✔️ Practise using phrases that show cause and effect or contrast. This will help your article flow even better, just like you started to do with "However" and "therefore."	B2+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	5.0	You did a fantastic job of directly answering the question, In-Sun. You presented a clear opinion right from the start and then carefully explained both the benefits and the downsides of having pets. You covered all the important points, discussing how pets can help with mental well-being and companionship, as well as the responsibilities involved like cleaning up. This means your reader is completely informed about your viewpoint, which is exactly what a high-scoring article does. Your article showed that you understood the task perfectly.	4.0	You successfully managed to hold the reader's attention throughout your article, In-Sun. The tone was appropriate for an article sharing opinions, and you communicated your ideas in a way that was easy to understand. For instance, when you talked about pets healing mental health or being like children, you used language that made your points clear. To move towards a score of 5, you could try to develop some of your ideas a little more deeply, perhaps by giving a specific small example or elaborating on a feeling, which shows you can handle more complex ideas with confidence.	4.0	Your article was well-structured, In-Sun, which made it easy to follow your ideas. You started with a clear introduction, moved into separate paragraphs for the advantages, then discussed the disadvantages, and wrapped it up with a good conclusion. You used some helpful linking words like "Most importantly," "Secondly," "However," and "In conclusion," which guide the reader smoothly from one idea to the next. To make your organisation even stronger, try to add a few more phrases that connect the ideas within your paragraphs, ensuring every sentence feels naturally joined to the one before it. For example, instead of just starting a new sentence, you could use a phrase like "In addition to this..." or "Despite these benefits..." when you introduce a new point.	3.0	You used a good range of everyday words to talk about pets, In-Sun, and your meaning was generally clear despite some errors. However, there were a few places where the grammar could be more precise. For example, you wrote "reasnes" instead of "reasons" for plurals. Another area to watch is prepositional phrases, like "take care their health" which should be "take care *of* their health." Also, when you mentioned how pets "scream," it might be more natural to say "screaming" if you are describing a general behaviour. Finally, phrases like "take these responsibility" should be "take *this* responsibility" or "take *these* responsibilities" to match the number correctly. Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from their first language, or might overlook small grammatical endings that are very important in English for showing plurals or specific relationships between words. Focusing on these small but important details will really help your writing become more accurate and polished.	\N	\N	2025-09-13
30942	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy,<br><br>I'm also excited that you're letting me to stay with you!<br>Absolutely the concert. You know that I love rock music so much<br>I can not miss this concert.<br><br>It's good though, but I think it would be tired, because we will<br>go to the concert too.<br><br>I just realised that I have something to ask you. What cloth<br>you will wear at the concert? As for my clothes, I will wear<br>all black style. Maybe we could match together!<br><br>See you soon<br>Praewa	103	11.0	Praewa, you have written a friendly email that shows real enthusiasm for the concert. Your opening line <em>I'm also excited that you're letting me to stay with you!</em> sounds warm and natural, like a real message to a friend. You also did a good job of answering the first question about which activity you prefer by writing <em>Absolutely the concert</em>. To reach Band 5, you will need to make sure every part of the task is fully addressed. The climbing suggestion needs a clearer answer, and your question to Sandy should be more relevant to the original email. Let me show you exactly how to improve each area.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer addresses every part of the task completely and clearly. You expressed your preference for the concert well, but your response to the climbing suggestion is confusing.</p><ul><li><em>It's good though, but I think it would be tired, because we will go to the concert too.</em> → <strong>I don't think climbing is a good idea. I might be too tired after the concert.</strong> This clearly says no and gives a real reason.</li><li><em>What cloth you will wear at the concert?</em> → <strong>What should we bring to the concert? Do we need anything else?</strong> Your question about clothes is okay, but a question about what to bring or what time to meet would fit the email better.</li><li><em>Absolutely the concert.</em> You stated your preference strongly. That part is perfect for Band 5.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer makes their intention completely clear to the reader. Your email has a friendly tone with a greeting and closing, which is correct for an email to a friend. However, the climbing part confuses the reader.</p><ul><li><em>It's good though, but I think it would be tired, because we will go to the concert too.</em> → <strong>I think climbing sounds fun, but I'd rather just go to the concert. We can relax and enjoy the music together.</strong> Now the reader understands your choice clearly.</li><li><em>I'm also excited that you're letting me to stay with you!</em> This shows the right friendly energy. Keep this kind of clear emotion.</li><li><em>See you soon</em> and <em>Hi Sandy,</em> Your opening and closing are correct for an email to a friend. Good work.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects their ideas smoothly so the reader can follow easily. You used paragraphs for each main point, which is good. The jump from the concert to the climbing topic feels sudden.</p><ul><li><em>Absolutely the concert... It's good though, but I think it would be tired...</em> → <strong>Absolutely the concert! As for the climbing idea, I think I'd rather skip it.</strong> Adding a transition like <em>As for the climbing idea</em> makes the shift feel natural.</li><li><em>I just realised that I have something to ask you.</em> This is a good way to introduce a new topic. Keep using phrases like this to guide the reader.</li><li>Your paragraphs each focus on one idea: the concert, the climbing, and the question. That structure is clear and helpful for the reader.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and vocabulary so the reader never has to guess the meaning. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: after certain verbs like <em>let</em>, we use the base form of the verb (no <em>to</em>). Also, in questions, the subject comes after the helping verb. Let's fix these patterns.</p><ul><li><em>you're letting me to stay</em> → <strong>you're letting me stay</strong>. After <em>let</em>, we do not use <em>to</em>. The pattern is: let + person + base verb. For example: <em>She let me borrow her book.</em></li><li><em>What cloth you will wear at the concert?</em> → <strong>What clothes will you wear at the concert?</strong> In questions, the helping verb (<em>will</em>) comes before the subject (<em>you</em>). Also, <em>cloth</em> is a piece of fabric; <em>clothes</em> means what you wear. This is a common mistake.</li><li><em>it would be tired</em> → <strong>I would be tired</strong>. <em>Tired</em> describes how a person feels. <em>Tiring</em> describes an activity. You are saying the person will feel tired, so use <em>I would be tired</em>. For example: <em>The concert was tiring, so I was tired afterwards.</em></li><li><em>I can not miss</em> → <strong>I cannot miss</strong> or <strong>I can't miss</strong>. Both are correct. <em>Cannot</em> is one word in standard English.</li><li><em>I will wear all black style.</em> → <strong>I will wear all black.</strong> <em>Style</em> is not needed here. <em>All black</em> already describes the outfit clearly.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After you write a sentence with <em>let</em>, check that the next verb has no <em>to</em>. After you write a question, check that the helping verb comes before the subject. These two checks will fix most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34034	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hey, Sandy!<br><br>Yes! Am so excited too! I'm so glad that my parents allowed me to.<br><br>I would prefer to go to the concert. Not a bad idea, right? Plus that I think it would be nice to go to a concert with friend. I mean relax at home is a good idea, but concert is better for me.<br><br>I think it's a pretty good idea to go mountain climbing and also a bad one. I think too dangerous without a professional to guide us or help us up there. So, I don't think we should go. As for the question. I do have one actually, is there any store that near your house? Like 7-eleven or Big C will be nice. Alright, that's all of what I'll say See you there.<br><br>Take care.<br>Singto.	150	15.0	Singto, your email to Sandy is full of energy and real personality. You answered every part of the task completely: you showed excitement, chose the concert, explained why climbing is risky, and asked a smart question about nearby stores. The reader knows exactly how you feel and what you want. To reach Band 5 in all areas, the next step is to make your sentences flow more smoothly and to check a few grammar patterns. Let's look at each part of your writing so you can see exactly what to revise.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Am so excited too! I'm so glad that my parents allowed me to.</em> You showed real enthusiasm right from the start, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>I would prefer to go to the concert.</em> You stated your choice clearly and gave a reason, so the reader understands your thinking.</li><li><em>is there any store that near your house? Like 7-eleven or Big C will be nice.</em> You asked a relevant, specific question that shows you've thought about what you need during the visit.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone friendly but controlled, so every sentence sounds natural without being too casual. You have a warm, enthusiastic voice, which is great. The two phrases that pull the register down are <em>Not a bad idea, right?</em> and <em>Alright, that's all of what I'll say</em>. These sound like spoken conversation, not written email. Try replacing them with something like <strong>That sounds like a great plan, doesn't it?</strong> and <strong>Well, that's everything I wanted to share.</strong> Your closing <em>Take care. Singto.</em> is perfect — warm and appropriate.</p>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas so the reader never feels a jump. Your email moves logically from excitement to concert, then climbing, then your question. But two spots feel abrupt. First, <em>As for the question.</em> is a fragment — it needs to connect to the next sentence. Try <strong>As for your question about what I'd like to know, I do have one.</strong> Second, the final sentence <em>Alright, that's all of what I'll say See you there</em> is missing a full stop and feels rushed. Try <strong>That's all for now. See you there!</strong> Also, <em>Plus that I think</em> is not a standard connector. Use <strong>Plus, I think</strong> or <strong>Also, I think</strong>.</p>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar so errors don't distract the reader. Your vocabulary is good — <em>excited, concert, dangerous, professional, guide</em> — and your meaning is always clear. The main pattern to fix is missing subjects and verbs in sentences. Let's look at three examples.</p><ul><li><em>Am so excited too!</em> → <strong>I am so excited too!</strong> Every sentence needs a subject. You remembered <em>I'm</em> later, so just check every sentence.</li><li><em>Plus that I think it would be nice to go to a concert with friend</em> → <strong>Plus, I think it would be nice to go to a concert with a friend.</strong> Drop <em>that</em> after <em>Plus</em>, and add <em>a</em> before <em>friend</em>.</li><li><em>I think too dangerous without a professional to guide us</em> → <strong>I think it is too dangerous without a professional to guide us.</strong> You need <em>it is</em> before <em>too dangerous</em>.</li><li><em>is there any store that near your house?</em> → <strong>Is there any store that is near your house?</strong> Add <em>is</em> after <em>that</em>. Also, capitalise the first word of a question.</li><li><em>Alright, that's all of what I'll say See you there.</em> → <strong>Alright, that's all I'll say. See you there.</strong> Drop <em>of what</em> and add a full stop before <em>See</em>.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: 'Does every sentence have a subject (I, you, it, we, they, he, she) and a verb (is, am, are, go, think, have)?' If one is missing, add it. You are very close to Band 5 in Language — this one habit will get you there.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34927	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hello!<br><br>Thanks for inviting me to stay with you for a week. We will have so much fun! We can do lots of activities together.<br><br>I prefer that we stay at home and relax together instead because I am not a fan for loud music, it's just not my cup of tea. But we can still have fun.<br><br>for the moutain climbing, I think I'll skip this activity. I have a fear of heights and I get tired very easily when do these activities.<br><br>However, I do have a few questions to ask you. Firstly, can I get your address? So I can get there easily. And is the food there expensive? So I can withdraw some money on the way there. That's all the questions I have for you.<br><br>Can't wait!<br><br>Eri	149	18.0	Eri, your email is warm and personal, and you've done an excellent job covering every part of the task. I especially like how you used <em>I prefer that we stay at home and relax together instead</em> to clearly state your choice, and <em>Can't wait!</em> at the end keeps the friendly tone going. To move from a 4 to a 5 in Organisation and Language, we just need to smooth out a few transitions and fix some small grammar spots. Let's look at each area.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I prefer that we stay at home and relax together instead because I am not a fan for loud music</em> You clearly stated your preference and gave a reason, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>I have a fear of heights and I get tired very easily when do these activities</em> You explained why you can't climb with a personal, honest reason. The reader understands completely.</li><li><em>can I get your address? So I can get there easily. And is the food there expensive? So I can withdraw some money on the way there</em> You asked two relevant questions that show you've thought about the practical side of the visit.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>We will have so much fun!</em> This exclamation creates a cheerful, friendly tone that suits an email to a friend perfectly.</li><li><em>Can't wait!</em> Your closing is natural and enthusiastic, just like a real message to a friend.</li><li><em>Thanks for inviting me to stay with you for a week</em> You opened with gratitude, which sets a warm and polite tone from the start.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects each paragraph smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. Your paragraphs each focus on one topic, which is great, but the shift from climbing to questions feels a little sudden.</p><ul><li><em>However, I do have a few questions to ask you. Firstly, can I get your address?</em> → <strong>However, I do have a few questions to ask you. Firstly, can I get your address so I can get there easily?</strong> The word <em>However</em> works well here, but you can make the transition even smoother by connecting the questions directly to the previous idea about climbing. For example: <strong>Anyway, I do have a few questions. Firstly, can I get your address so I can get there easily?</strong></li><li><em>for the moutain climbing, I think I'll skip this activity.</em> → <strong>As for the mountain climbing, I think I'll skip this activity.</strong> Starting the paragraph with <em>for</em> feels a bit abrupt. Using <strong>As for</strong> or <strong>Regarding</strong> would make the topic introduction clearer.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammar with few errors. Your vocabulary is good, but there's one main rule that needs attention: after <em>when</em>, <em>because</em>, or <em>if</em>, you need a subject before the verb. Also, watch prepositions like <em>fan of</em> and capital letters at the start of sentences.</p><ul><li><em>I am not a fan for loud music</em> → <strong>I am not a fan of loud music</strong>. The correct preposition is <em>of</em>, not <em>for</em>.</li><li><em>when do these activities</em> → <strong>when I do these activities</strong>. After <em>when</em>, you need a subject like <em>I</em> before the verb. This pattern also applies to <em>because</em> and <em>if</em>.</li><li><em>for the moutain climbing</em> → <strong>For the mountain climbing</strong> or <strong>As for the mountain climbing</strong>. You started a new paragraph with a lowercase <em>f</em>. Every sentence must begin with a capital letter. Also, <em>moutain</em> is a spelling error — it should be <strong>mountain</strong>.</li><li><em>So I can get there easily. And is the food there expensive? So I can withdraw some money</em> → <strong>so I can get there easily, and is the food there expensive so I can withdraw some money?</strong> When you begin a sentence with <em>So</em> or <em>And</em> in a question, it's better to connect it to the previous sentence with a comma. This makes the flow more natural.</li><li>Self-check tip: After every <em>when</em> or <em>because</em>, ask yourself: 'Who is doing the action?' Then add that person (I, you, he, she, it, we, they) before the verb.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34929	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi<br><br>Yea, me too. I'm so excited it's going to be very fun and exciting.<br><br>I prefer to relax at home, because I don't like a place with alot of people and that way we can rest and play some games at home too.<br><br>No, because I don't like to do that kind of activity, it's hard and I'm scared of height too. Climbing a mountains would be very scary and use alot of strenght too thinking that way it would be pretty tiring right.<br><br>What about instead of climbing a mountains maybe we go to the water park or go watch some movies<br><br>alk later<br><br>Saint	125	15.0	Saint, your email is warm and personal — you clearly know how to write to a friend. The way you said <em>I'm so excited it's going to be very fun and exciting</em> captures real enthusiasm, and your reason for not wanting to climb — <em>I'm scared of height too</em> — is honest and specific. You covered every point the task asked for, and you even suggested alternative activities at the end. That's strong work. To push your writing to the next level, focus on making your sentences flow more smoothly from one idea to the next, and clean up a few spelling and grammar patterns. Here's how each area looks:	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm so excited it's going to be very fun and exciting.</em> You expressed your feelings about the visit clearly, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to relax at home, because I don't like a place with alot of people and that way we can rest and play some games at home too.</em> You gave a clear preference with a personal reason, and the reader fully understands your choice.</li>\n  <li><em>What about instead of climbing a mountains maybe we go to the water park or go watch some movies</em> You asked a relevant question and suggested alternatives, showing you've thought about the visit from both sides.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer maintains a consistent friendly tone from the first word to the last, with a proper closing that matches the opening. Your email sounds like a real conversation with a friend — <em>Hi</em> and <em>Yea, me too</em> feel natural and informal. The closing <em>alk later</em> is a small slip that breaks the friendly tone; it looks like a typing error. To reach Band 5, write the closing as <strong>Talk later</strong> to match the warmth of your opening. Also, your final suggestion paragraph starts a bit suddenly — adding a short transition like <strong>That sounds fun, but what about...</strong> would make the shift feel smoother.</p>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas clearly between paragraphs and ends with a sentence that wraps up the email naturally. You've separated each point into its own paragraph, which helps the reader follow your thinking. The linking words you used — <em>because</em>, <em>and</em>, <em>too</em>, <em>instead of</em> — work well inside paragraphs. However, the jump from your reason for not climbing to your suggestion feels abrupt. Try adding a sentence like <strong>Since climbing doesn't work for me, I have another idea</strong> before your suggestion. Also, the email ends very suddenly after your question. A closing sentence like <strong>Let me know what you think</strong> would give the reader a clear sense of completion.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Climbing a mountains would be very scary and use alot of strenght too thinking that way it would be pretty tiring right.</em> → <strong>Climbing a mountain would be very scary and use a lot of strength. Thinking about it that way, it would be pretty tiring, right?</strong> Breaking this long sentence into two shorter ones makes your reasoning easier to follow.</li>\n  <li><em>What about instead of climbing a mountains maybe we go to the water park or go watch some movies</em> → <strong>What about instead of climbing a mountain, we go to the water park or watch some movies?</strong> Adding a comma after 'mountain' and a question mark at the end clarifies the sentence structure.</li>\n  <li><em>alk later</em> → <strong>Talk later</strong> A complete closing phrase signals the end of the email properly.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling consistently, with only minor slips. Your vocabulary is good — words like <em>excited</em>, <em>relax</em>, <em>scared</em>, and <em>tiring</em> are all used naturally. The main pattern holding you back is noun agreement (singular vs. plural) and spelling of common words. Let's work on those.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Climbing a mountains</em> → <strong>Climbing a mountain</strong>. When you use 'a' before a noun, that noun must be singular. 'Mountains' is plural, so it needs 'a mountain' or 'the mountains'.</li>\n  <li><em>alot of people</em> → <strong>a lot of people</strong>. 'Alot' is not a word in English. It is always written as two words: 'a lot'.</li>\n  <li><em>use alot of strenght</em> → <strong>use a lot of strength</strong>. Same spelling fix for 'a lot', and 'strength' is spelled with an 'g' and 'th' at the end — not 'strenght'.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm scared of height too</em> → <strong>I'm scared of heights too</strong>. When talking about the general fear, native speakers say 'heights' (plural) because it refers to the concept of being high up, not one specific height.</li>\n  <li><em>thinking that way it would be pretty tiring right</em> → <strong>Thinking about it that way, it would be pretty tiring, right?</strong> Add 'about it' to make the connection clear, and use a comma before 'right' when it's a tag question.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check tip: Before you finish, read your email aloud slowly. Listen for any missing words, and check every noun with 'a' or 'an' — is it singular? Also, double-check common words like 'a lot' and 'strength' in your mind. These small fixes will push your Language score up quickly.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34932	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hello Sandy,<br><br>I'm also excited that I could stay in your house for a week.<br><br>On our first evening here I would rather stay at home than going to concert because I don't like to be in crowded places.<br><br>I wouldn't go climbing in the mountains because I want to stay at home so we wouldn't get any injuries from climbing mountains.<br><br>I don't really have any question because I have already prepared myself enough.<br><br>See you soon,<br><br>Natee.	84	13.0	Natee, your email to Sandy is warm and clear. You did a great job covering every point the task asked for: you showed excitement about the visit, explained why you prefer staying home over the concert, gave a reason for not climbing, and even mentioned that you have no questions. The phrase <em>I'm also excited that I could stay in your house for a week</em> sounds natural and friendly, exactly right for writing to a friend. To move from Band 3 to Band 5, you need to make your language more flexible and your tone more consistently warm. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer covers every task point completely and naturally, without any gaps or abrupt endings. You included all four points, but the last one feels a little short.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I don't really have any question because I have already prepared myself enough.</em> This answers the task note, but it sounds like you are closing the door to conversation. A Band 5 version would keep the friendly tone open: <strong>I don't have any questions at the moment, but if I think of something I'll let you know.</strong> This shows you are still open to talking.</li>\n  <li><em>I wouldn't go climbing in the mountains because I want to stay at home so we wouldn't get any injuries from climbing mountains.</em> You gave a clear reason, which is good. To reach Band 5, you could make it more personal: <strong>I'd rather not go climbing because I'm worried about getting injured. I'd prefer to stay home and relax.</strong> This sounds more like a real conversation.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm also excited that I could stay in your house for a week.</em> This is a strong opening. You clearly stated your feeling and connected it to the visit. Well done.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone warm and friendly throughout the whole email, even when saying no or giving information. Your email starts well but becomes a bit formal at the end.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I don't really have any question because I have already prepared myself enough.</em> This sounds like you are ending the conversation, and the word 'prepared' feels quite formal. A Band 5 writer would stay friendly: <strong>I don't have any questions right now, but I'm sure we'll figure everything out when I get there!</strong></li>\n  <li><em>I'm also excited that I could stay in your house for a week.</em> This is a perfect tone for an email to a friend. It sounds happy and personal. Keep writing openings like this.</li>\n  <li><em>I would rather stay at home than going to concert because I don't like to be in crowded places.</em> You explained your preference clearly and gave a reason. To sound more like a friend, you could add a softener: <strong>I'd actually prefer to stay home, if that's okay, because crowded places aren't really my thing.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraphs and connects them with a variety of linking words so the email flows smoothly. Your email has one paragraph per point, which is good, but the transitions between paragraphs are very basic.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Each paragraph starts with a new topic, but there are no connecting phrases between them. For example, after <em>On our first evening here I would rather stay at home...</em> you jump to <em>I wouldn't go climbing...</em> without any link. Try using <strong>As for the next day,</strong> or <strong>Regarding the climbing trip,</strong> to show the reader you are moving to a new point.</li>\n  <li>You used 'because' and 'so' correctly to connect ideas inside your sentences. That is a good start. To reach Band 5, add one or two more linking words like 'however', 'also', or 'actually' to vary your sentence openings.</li>\n  <li>The overall order is logical: excitement, first evening, climbing, questions. The reader can follow your thinking easily. That is a strength.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and some complex grammar with few errors, and uses a range of everyday vocabulary naturally. Your main grammar rule to focus on is <strong>articles (a, an, the) and plural nouns</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>First, a strength: <em>I'm also excited that I could stay in your house for a week.</em> This sentence is grammatically correct and uses 'could' naturally. Well done.</li>\n  <li><em>than going to concert</em> → <strong>than going to the concert</strong>. The word 'concert' is a countable noun, so it needs an article. This pattern appears more than once in your email.</li>\n  <li><em>any question</em> → <strong>any questions</strong>. After 'any', use a plural noun when you mean 'one or more'. This is a small fix that makes a big difference.</li>\n  <li><em>we wouldn't get any injuries from climbing mountains</em> → <strong>we wouldn't get injured from climbing</strong>. The original is understandable but slightly awkward. Using 'get injured' is simpler and more natural. Also, 'climbing mountains' is fine, but since you already mentioned mountains earlier, just 'climbing' is enough.</li>\n  <li>Self-check for next time: Read each sentence and ask yourself — 'Is this noun countable? Does it need a, an, or the? Should it be plural?' This will catch most of your errors.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34933	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi, Sandy,<br><br>I'm so excited to stay with you for week too!<br><br>I have to tell you that I don't like rock music ,so I prefer to stay at home and play some game as well.<br><br>Next I have to reject to climbing ,because I fear a high and I don't know how to climbing too.<br><br>And I want to ask some question, like 3 question. Do you have some some warm blanket, I don't like the cold wether. Next ,Can your family prepare the food without milk,pls ,I amegie to milk. Last, can I take my redio to open the song, I like relax song.<br><br>Tell me what you think.<br><br>See ya.	118	13.0	Grace, your email has so many thoughtful details that make it feel like a real message to a friend. You clearly explained your excitement, your preference to stay home, and your fear of heights, and you asked three very practical questions about the blanket, food, and radio. That is exactly what the task asked for. To reach Band 5 in the other areas, you will need to focus on making your language sound more natural and on organising your ideas more smoothly. Let me walk you through each part.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I'm so excited to stay with you for week too!</em> You expressed your excitement clearly and personally, which is exactly what the task required.</li><li><em>I prefer to stay at home and play some game as well.</em> You stated your preference and gave a reason, fully addressing the concert note.</li><li><em>I fear a high and I don't know how to climbing too.</em> Even though the grammar needs work, you clearly explained why you cannot go climbing, so the reader understands completely.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent, natural register throughout the email so the reader feels like they are reading a real message from a friend. Your opening and closing are friendly, but some phrases sound awkward and break the natural flow.</p><ul><li><em>I have to reject to climbing</em> → <strong>I have to say no to climbing</strong> or <strong>I can't go climbing</strong>. 'Reject to' is not how a native speaker would express this. Using a simpler, more natural phrase keeps the friendly tone.</li><li><em>I want to ask some question, like 3 question</em> → <strong>I have a few questions for you, actually three.</strong> The phrase 'like 3 question' sounds like you are guessing. Stating it directly sounds more confident and natural.</li><li><em>I amegie to milk</em> → <strong>I am allergic to milk</strong>. The reader can guess what you mean, but the correct word 'allergic' would make the message clear without any effort.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer links ideas smoothly so the reader never feels a jump between topics. You have used paragraphs and basic connectors like 'Next' and 'And', but the list of questions feels abrupt and disconnected from the climbing topic.</p><ul><li><em>Next I have to reject to climbing ... And I want to ask some question, like 3 question.</em> → <strong>Next, I have to say no to climbing. Anyway, I have three questions for you about my stay.</strong> Adding a word like 'Anyway' or 'By the way' helps the reader see that you are changing the topic naturally.</li><li><em>Next ,Can your family prepare the food without milk,pls ,I amegie to milk. Last, can I take my redio to open the song, I like relax song.</em> → <strong>Next, can your family prepare food without milk? I am allergic to milk. Last, can I bring my radio to play relaxing songs?</strong> Each question should be a complete sentence. Using a full stop or question mark between ideas makes the list easier to follow.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and vocabulary with few errors. You have some good sentences, but there is one main issue: you often use the wrong form after verbs, especially after 'to' and after 'can'. Let me show you.</p><ul><li><em>I'm so excited to stay with you for week too!</em> This is a strong sentence. You used 'to stay' correctly after 'excited'. Keep using this pattern.</li><li><em>I don't know how to climbing too</em> → <strong>I don't know how to climb</strong>. After 'to', use the base form of the verb, not the '-ing' form. So 'to climb', not 'to climbing'.</li><li><em>can I take my redio to open the song</em> → <strong>can I bring my radio to play songs?</strong> After 'can', use the base form: 'can I bring', not 'can I take'. Also, 'open the song' is not correct in English. You 'play' a song or 'listen to' a song.</li><li><em>I want to ask some question, like 3 question</em> → <strong>I want to ask some questions, about three questions</strong>. 'Question' is countable, so you need 'questions' for plural. Also, 'like' here is confusing; 'about' or 'around' works better for an approximate number.</li><li><em>I don't like the cold wether</em> → <strong>I don't like the cold weather</strong>. 'Wether' is a different word (a castrated sheep). 'Weather' is the climate. Also, check your capitalisation: 'Next ,Can' should be 'Next, can' (no capital after a comma). 'pls' is too informal for this level; write 'please'. After you finish editing, read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Is the verb after 'to' in base form? Is the verb after 'can' in base form? Are my nouns plural when they should be?'</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
37120	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi,<br><br>Thanks for your email , I'm so excited too!<br><br>I think I will prefer us to go to the rock concert in your town that would be a lots of fun. I really want to watch it..<br><br>No, because I think the concert is alredy enough for me it's okay, I think I will tired after the concert, I will relax at home instead.<br><br>What about we go play some badminton and, after that we can watch a movie and eat some snacks?<br><br>Can't wait!<br><br>Nadia	89	15.0	Nadia, your email is genuinely warm and personal. You answered every part of the task clearly: you showed excitement, chose the rock concert, explained why climbing is not for you, and asked a thoughtful question about what to do instead. The reader feels like they are talking to a real friend. To reach Band 5 in every area, you will need to tighten your grammar and make your sentences flow more smoothly. Let's look at each skill in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I'm so excited too!</em> You matched your friend's enthusiasm right away, which makes the email feel connected.</li><li><em>I think I will prefer us to go to the rock concert in your town</em> You clearly stated your choice and connected it to the task.</li><li><em>I think I will tired after the concert, I will relax at home instead.</em> You gave a logical reason for not climbing, which fully informs the reader.</li><li><em>What about we go play some badminton and, after that we can watch a movie and eat some snacks?</em> You asked a relevant question that keeps the conversation going.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses natural, fluent email language that sounds like a real friend writing. Your tone is friendly and enthusiastic, which is great. The main gap is that a few phrases sound slightly stiff or unnatural.</p><ul><li><em>I think I will prefer us to go to the rock concert</em> → <strong>I'd prefer to go to the rock concert</strong>. This is more direct and natural for an email to a friend.</li><li><em>What about we go play some badminton</em> → <strong>What about playing some badminton?</strong> After 'What about', use the -ing form. This is a common and natural way to make suggestions.</li><li><em>I think I will tired after the concert</em> → <strong>I think I'll be tired after the concert</strong>. You need the verb 'be' before 'tired'. This small fix makes the sentence sound complete and natural.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. You use basic linking words like 'Thanks for your email' and 'No, because', which is good. The problem is that the jump from refusing climbing to suggesting badminton feels sudden.</p><ul><li><em>No, because I think the concert is alredy enough for me it's okay, I think I will tired after the concert, I will relax at home instead. What about we go play some badminton...</em> → <strong>No, because the concert is enough for me. I think I'll be tired after it, so I'll relax at home instead. But what about playing some badminton another day?</strong> Adding 'But' or 'Another day' shows the reader that you are moving from a refusal to a new suggestion. This small linking word makes the whole paragraph flow.</li><li>Your paragraph breaks are correct. Each main idea has its own paragraph, which helps the reader follow your thoughts.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and vocabulary with very few errors. Your vocabulary is good ('excited', 'rock concert', 'fun', 'relax at home', 'badminton', 'movie', 'snacks'). The main grammar rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>after the verb 'will', always use the base form of the verb, and remember to include the verb 'be' before adjectives like 'tired'.</strong></p><ul><li><em>I think I will tired after the concert</em> → <strong>I think I will be tired after the concert</strong>. After 'will', you need the base verb 'be' before the adjective 'tired'. This is a very common pattern in English.</li><li><em>a lots of fun</em> → <strong>a lot of fun</strong>. 'A lot' is always singular. There is no 's' on 'lot'.</li><li><em>alredy enough</em> → <strong>already enough</strong>. Check the spelling of 'already' — it has an 'a' after the 'l', not an 'e'.</li><li><em>I really want to watch it..</em> → <strong>I really want to watch it.</strong> Use only one period at the end of a sentence. Two periods is a typo.</li><li><strong>Self-check:</strong> After every sentence with 'will', ask yourself: 'Is the next word a base verb? If I use an adjective like tired, hungry, or happy, did I put 'be' before it?' This will catch most of your errors.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29508	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Films are one of the great source of entertainment. I do love films, It helps me feel better when I'm stress out.<br><br>Films I mostly enjoy are Romance, Comedy, Action, and sometimes Thriller. Romance films may sounds weird, but I'll tell you, the moment that you click enter you cannot leave the site! As a teenage girl with no love experience I guarantee you that romance films are wonderful. I also enjoy comedy, action, and thriller too. These types of film genres are easy to watch, and funny except thriller.<br><br>If I need to choose between watching film at the cinema or at home I may choose cinema. Nothing can beat a combination of Thriller film, popcorn, and a bunch of friends. I usually go to the cinema with my friends, they are hilarious, and very dumb. I really enjoy it.	147	16.0	Satang, your article has a real spark to it. You chose four film genres and gave each one a personal touch — the way you described romance films with <em>the moment that you click enter you cannot leave the site</em> is vivid and memorable. You also made your preference for the cinema clear with a strong closing image of popcorn and friends. To reach Band 5 across the board, you'll need to tighten your grammar and polish a few informal phrases. Let's look at each area in detail.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Films I mostly enjoy are Romance, Comedy, Action, and sometimes Thriller.</em> You listed specific genres and gave reasons for each, which fully meets the task requirements.</li>\n  <li><em>Nothing can beat a combination of Thriller film, popcorn, and a bunch of friends.</em> You clearly stated your preference for cinema and supported it with concrete details.</li>\n  <li><em>As a teenage girl with no love experience I guarantee you that romance films are wonderful.</em> This personal perspective makes your article engaging and informative for the reader.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the register consistent throughout the article, matching the informal tone of a magazine without slipping into language that feels too casual for the task. Your conversational voice works well — <em>I'll tell you</em> and <em>I guarantee you</em> draw the reader in.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>they are hilarious, and very dumb</em> → <strong>they are hilarious, and a little silly at times</strong>. The word 'dumb' is too informal for a magazine article. Try a lighter word like 'silly' or 'goofy' to keep the friendly tone without losing polish.</li>\n  <li><em>Romance films may sounds weird</em> → <strong>Romance films may sound strange to some people, but I find them fascinating</strong>. 'Weird' is acceptable in speech but feels slightly off in writing. 'Strange' or 'unexpected' keeps the same idea with a more appropriate register.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas within paragraphs so each sentence flows naturally into the next. Your paragraph structure is solid — one paragraph for general thoughts, one for genres, one for cinema vs. home.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I also enjoy comedy, action, and thriller too. These types of film genres are easy to watch, and funny except thriller.</em> The shift from 'easy to watch' to 'except thriller' feels abrupt. Try: <strong>I also enjoy comedy, action, and thriller. Comedy and action are easy to watch and funny, though thriller keeps me on the edge of my seat.</strong> This way the contrast is clear and the sentence reads smoothly.</li>\n  <li><em>If I need to choose between watching film at the cinema or at home I may choose cinema.</em> → <strong>If I need to choose between watching a film at the cinema or at home, I would choose the cinema.</strong> Adding 'a' before 'film' and a comma after 'home' makes the sentence easier to follow.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and basic sentence structure so the reader never has to pause and re-read. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>the verb must match the subject in number and form</strong>. Let's look at your examples.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Films are one of the great source of entertainment.</em> → <strong>Films are one of the great sources of entertainment.</strong> After 'one of the', the noun should be plural because you're talking about one item from a group of many.</li>\n  <li><em>when I'm stress out</em> → <strong>when I'm stressed out</strong>. 'Stressed' is the adjective form. 'Stress' is a noun or verb. You need the -ed ending here.</li>\n  <li><em>Romance films may sounds weird</em> → <strong>Romance films may sound weird</strong>. After 'may', use the base form of the verb, not the third-person singular -s. 'May sound', not 'may sounds'.</li>\n  <li><em>the moment that you click enter you cannot leave the site</em> → <strong>the moment that you click enter, you cannot leave the site</strong>. This is a run-on sentence. Add a comma after 'enter' to separate the two clauses.</li>\n  <li><em>If I need to choose between watching film at the cinema or at home I may choose cinema.</em> → <strong>If I need to choose between watching a film at the cinema or at home, I would choose the cinema.</strong> Add 'a' before 'film', 'the' before 'cinema' the second time, and a comma after 'home'. Also, 'would' sounds more natural than 'may' in this conditional sentence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check: After you write a sentence, read it aloud and ask yourself: <em>Does the verb match the subject? Is every noun after 'one of the' plural? Did I add a comma between two complete thoughts?</em> Practising this will move you to Band 5 quickly.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29576	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I rewlly enjoy with Horror movies, because I'm really excited when I watching these movies. I prefer to watching them at home, because I can do what ever I want but at the cinema is not a bad idea. In the cinema if you want to go to a toilet you can't pause the movies, and	58	9.0	Dee, you've chosen a great topic — horror movies are fun to write about, and I can see you have strong opinions about them. You clearly stated that you prefer watching at home and gave a reason about being able to do what you want. That's a solid start. To reach Band 5, you'll need to develop your ideas more fully and make sure your writing feels complete. The examiner noticed your article was too short and didn't fully explore the comparison between cinema and home. Let's look at each area so you know exactly what to improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer covers all parts of the task with enough detail for the reader to feel fully informed. You told us you prefer horror movies and why, and you started comparing cinema and home, but you stopped too early. The reader is left wondering what you think about the cinema experience.</p><ul><li><em>I rewlly enjoy with Horror movies, because I'm really excited when I watching these movies.</em> You gave a reason for liking horror movies, which is good. To reach Band 5, add one more sentence about what makes horror movies exciting — for example, the suspense or the jump scares.</li><li><em>I prefer to watching them at home, because I can do what ever I want but at the cinema is not a bad idea.</em> You started the comparison well. A Band 5 writer would explain both sides: what you like about home AND what you like about the cinema. Try adding one sentence about the big screen or the sound at the cinema.</li><li>Your article ends with <em>and</em> — this tells the reader you were not finished. A Band 5 writer always completes their final thought. Make sure your last sentence has a full stop and a finished idea.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer writes an article that feels like an article — it grabs the reader's attention, has a clear structure with an introduction and conclusion, and uses a friendly but polished tone. Right now your text reads like a quick personal note, not an article for a website.</p><ul><li><em>I rewlly enjoy with Horror movies</em> → <strong>Do you love the thrill of a good horror movie?</strong> An article should hook the reader with a question or an interesting opening. Try starting with something that makes the reader want to keep reading.</li><li>Your article has no introduction or conclusion. A Band 5 writer would add a short opening paragraph (like 'I'm going to tell you why I love horror movies and where I prefer to watch them') and a closing sentence (like 'For me, home is the best place to enjoy a scary film').</li><li>The phrase <em>but at the cinema is not a bad idea</em> sounds a bit flat. A Band 5 writer would make the tone more engaging — try 'But the cinema has its own magic too, with the big screen and surround sound.'</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly using a variety of linking words, and the text is broken into clear paragraphs. Your ideas are in a logical order, but the connections are repetitive and you have no paragraph breaks.</p><ul><li><em>because</em> appears twice in a short text. A Band 5 writer would vary their connectors — try <strong>since</strong>, <strong>as</strong>, or <strong>the reason is that</strong> instead of repeating 'because'.</li><li>Your text is one block with no paragraph breaks. Even though you used line breaks in your original, the examiner expects clear paragraphs. Try splitting your article: Paragraph 1 — why you love horror movies. Paragraph 2 — home vs. cinema. Paragraph 3 — your final opinion.</li><li>The sentence <em>I can do what ever I want but at the cinema is not a bad idea</em> jumps from one idea to another without a smooth link. A Band 5 writer would separate these: 'At home, I can do whatever I want. However, the cinema is still a good option because...'</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and a range of vocabulary appropriate for the task. Your meaning is clear, but there are several errors that hold you back. The main pattern is difficulty with prepositions and verb forms after certain words.</p><ul><li><em>I rewlly enjoy with Horror movies</em> → <strong>I really enjoy horror movies.</strong> The verb 'enjoy' does not take 'with'. You just say 'enjoy + something'. Also, 'rewlly' is a spelling error — the correct word is 'really'.</li><li><em>when I watching these movies</em> → <strong>when I watch these movies</strong> or <strong>when I am watching these movies</strong>. After 'when', you need a subject and a verb. 'I watching' is missing the auxiliary verb 'am' if you want the continuous form, or you can use the simple present 'I watch'.</li><li><em>I prefer to watching them</em> → <strong>I prefer to watch them</strong> or <strong>I prefer watching them</strong>. After 'prefer to', use the base form of the verb (watch). After 'prefer' without 'to', you can use the -ing form.</li><li><em>what ever I want</em> → <strong>whatever I want</strong>. 'Whatever' is one word, not two.</li><li><em>you can't pause the movies, and</em> — this sentence is incomplete. A Band 5 writer always finishes their sentences. Also, check capitalisation: 'Horror movies' should not have a capital H in the middle of a sentence unless it's the start of the sentence. Here's a self-check you can use: after every sentence, read it aloud and ask yourself — does this sound like something a native speaker would say? If it feels strange, look up the correct pattern.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29584	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	What do you like to do on your spare time? For me, I like to watch movies.\n\nI watch them everytime when I'm bored.\n\nPersonally, my favorite film genre is probably a thriller. It's about criminal, police solving cases, and some is about murdering. Reasons I like them are as follows.\n\nOne key reason is because it's mostly about solving mystery, and it looks complicate.\n\nFurthermore, it teaches you how to handle some difficult situation wisely.\n\nI like to watch at home. It's is relax and I have some privacy.	94	13.0	Yaya, your article has a clear personal voice and you've covered all the main points the task asked for. You told us your favourite genre, explained why you like it, and stated where you prefer to watch. That's solid work. The opening question is a good way to pull a reader in. To move from 4/5 to 5/5 on your next draft, try adding a specific film example and developing your reasons a little more. A concrete title would also make your article feel complete. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all required points with specific development that makes the reader feel informed and engaged.</p><ul><li><em>Personally, my favorite film genre is probably a thriller.</em> You named the genre clearly. To reach Band 5, add one specific film title, like <strong>I especially enjoyed the film <em>Sherlock Holmes</em> because the mystery kept me guessing until the end.</strong></li><li><em>One key reason is because it's mostly about solving mystery, and it looks complicate.</em> You gave a reason. Strengthen it with a concrete example of a skill you learned, such as <strong>Watching thrillers taught me to look for small clues before making a decision.</strong></li><li><em>I like to watch at home. It's is relax and I have some privacy.</em> You stated your preference. Add a short reason why privacy matters to you, like <strong>I can pause the movie whenever I need to think about the plot.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an article fully, including a strong introduction, developed body paragraphs, and a clear concluding statement.</p><ul><li><em>What do you like to do on your spare time?</em> This is a good article opening. You engaged the reader with a question. That works well.</li><li><em>Reasons I like them are as follows.</em> This signals that reasons are coming, which is effective. Your register stays informal and consistent throughout, which suits an article.</li><li>Your article ends with <em>I like to watch at home. It's is relax and I have some privacy.</em> This feels abrupt. Add a concluding sentence that wraps up your main idea, such as <strong>For me, nothing beats a good thriller at home where I can enjoy the mystery in peace.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraph breaks and a logical sequence of ideas, with varied linking words to connect sentences smoothly.</p><ul><li>You used paragraph breaks to separate genre preference, reasons, and location preference. That is a good structure. The reader can follow your main ideas.</li><li><em>Furthermore, it teaches you how to handle some difficult situation wisely.</em> 'Furthermore' is a good linking word. Try using a wider variety, such as 'in addition', 'another reason', or 'for example'.</li><li>In the second paragraph, the sequence jumps. You wrote <em>Reasons I like them are as follows.</em> then immediately <em>One key reason is because...</em> This is fine, but you could add a transition like <strong>There are two main reasons I enjoy thrillers.</strong> before listing them.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls simple and some complex grammatical forms with few errors, and uses a range of appropriate vocabulary. The main rule that will help you is: after a subject, use the correct form of the verb 'to be' (is/are) without adding an extra verb.</p><ul><li><em>It's is relax.</em> → <strong>It is relaxing.</strong> You wrote 'It's is' which has two verbs. 'It's' already means 'It is'. Choose one: either <strong>It is relaxing</strong> or <strong>It's relaxing</strong>. Also, 'relax' is a verb; the adjective is 'relaxing'.</li><li><em>it looks complicate.</em> → <strong>it looks complicated.</strong> 'Complicate' is a verb. After 'looks', use the adjective form 'complicated'.</li><li><em>on your spare time</em> → <strong>in your spare time.</strong> We say 'in your spare time', not 'on'.</li><li><em>everytime</em> → <strong>every time.</strong> This is two words. Also check capitalisation: you wrote 'I' correctly throughout, which is good, but 'Reasons' at the start of a sentence is fine. Watch for capital letters on common nouns like 'mystery' — it does not need a capital.</li><li><em>some is about murdering</em> → <strong>some are about murder.</strong> 'Some' refers to 'films' (plural), so use 'are'. 'Murdering' is a verb; use the noun 'murder' here.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After every sentence, ask yourself — did I use the right form of 'be' (is/are), and did I use an adjective (like 'complicated', 'relaxing') after 'looks' or 'is'?</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29615	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	The kind of films that I enjoy with it, it's going to be a musical films, the reason why I enjoy this ^types of films because I like to listened to music while I watching the films, and another key reason is I like the way they telling us a story by the lyrics in the songs. I prefer to watching them at the cinema because at the cinema the volume is way better than at home. And actually watch at the cinema it got more feelings than at home. For example, many years ago I went to the cinema to watch Frozen 2 and it feel a lot better than watch at home.	115	15.0	Punch, you've written a clear and personal article about why you love musical films and prefer watching them at the cinema. Your specific example of watching <em>Frozen 2</em> at the cinema really helps the reader understand what you mean by 'more feelings' — that's a strong choice. To reach Band 5 in every area, you'll want to polish your grammar and vary your sentence openings. Let's look at each subscale to see exactly how.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>musical films</em> — You chose a specific film type and stayed focused on it throughout the article. The task asked for your preference, and you delivered clearly.</li>\n  <li><em>I like the way they telling us a story by the lyrics in the songs</em> — This is a personal, thoughtful reason that goes beyond a simple 'I like music.' You connected storytelling to lyrics, which shows real insight.</li>\n  <li><em>For example, many years ago I went to the cinema to watch Frozen 2</em> — You gave a concrete, personal example that supports your preference. The reader understands exactly why the cinema experience matters to you.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer opens an article with a confident, engaging sentence that hooks the reader. Your opening phrase <em>The kind of films that I enjoy with it, it's going to be a musical films</em> is a bit tangled and doesn't grab attention the way an article should.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The kind of films that I enjoy with it, it's going to be a musical films</em> → <strong>My favourite type of film is the musical.</strong> This is direct, confident, and makes the reader want to know why.</li>\n  <li><em>And actually watch at the cinema it got more feelings than at home</em> → <strong>Watching at the cinema actually gives me stronger feelings than watching at home.</strong> This keeps your personal voice while being grammatically clear.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a range of linking words and structures to connect ideas smoothly. Your ideas are in a logical order — film type, reasons, preference, example — but you rely heavily on <em>because</em> and <em>and</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>the reason why I enjoy this types of films because I like to listened to music</em> → <strong>I enjoy these films because I like listening to music while I watch.</strong> Using <strong>because</strong> once is enough. Avoid repeating 'the reason why' and 'because' together.</li>\n  <li>Try using <strong>also</strong> or <strong>another reason is</strong> to introduce your second point instead of <em>and another key reason is</em>. For example: <strong>I also love how the lyrics tell the story.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently, especially verb forms after 'to' and after subjects. The main pattern to fix is using the wrong verb form after <em>to</em> and after <em>I</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your vocabulary is strong — <em>musical films, lyrics, volume, feelings</em> — these are the right words for this topic.</li>\n  <li><em>I like to listened to music</em> → <strong>I like to listen to music.</strong> After <em>to</em>, use the base form of the verb: <em>to listen</em>, not <em>to listened</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to watching them</em> → <strong>I prefer to watch them.</strong> Same rule: after <em>to</em>, use the base form <em>to watch</em>, not <em>to watching</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>I like the way they telling us a story</em> → <strong>I like the way they tell us a story.</strong> After <em>they</em>, use the base form of the verb: <em>they tell</em>, not <em>they telling</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>it feel a lot better</em> → <strong>it feels a lot better.</strong> After <em>it</em>, add <em>-s</em> to the verb for present tense: <em>it feels</em>, not <em>it feel</em>. Self-check: ask yourself 'Is the subject he/she/it?' If yes, add <em>-s</em> to the verb.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29710	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I really like romantic comedy films because it very funny and it also has a lot of emotional. For example Chinese romantic comedy films name Hidden love and Falling in to you smile. I like to watch the films on Netflix and iQYi at home because I don't like to sit in the cinema I always felt uncomfortable and it also has a lot of people. I recommend you to watch the films at home for the first reason we don't need to pay the ticket we can safe a lot of money. Second reason is on webside it has a lot of movie and films we can watch anytime.	112	15.0	PingPing, your article shows a clear love for romantic comedies, and you gave strong reasons for your viewing preferences. I could see your personality coming through when you explained why you prefer watching at home. To reach Band 5, focus on making your opening sentence grab the reader's attention and vary your connecting words. Let's look at each area.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I really like romantic comedy films because it very funny and it also has a lot of emotional.</em> You introduced your favourite genre clearly and gave two reasons why you enjoy it.</li><li><em>For example Chinese romantic comedy films name Hidden love and Falling in to you smile.</em> You provided specific titles, which makes your article personal and convincing.</li><li><em>I recommend you to watch the films at home for the first reason we don't need to pay the ticket we can safe a lot of money. Second reason is on webside it has a lot of movie and films we can watch anytime.</em> You fully explained your preference with two clear reasons, leaving the reader well informed.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses an engaging opening that immediately draws the reader in. Your article starts with a simple statement, but you can make it more inviting.</p><ul><li><em>I really like romantic comedy films...</em> → <strong>Are you a fan of films that make you laugh and feel warm inside? I am, and my favourite type is romantic comedy.</strong> Starting with a question or a personal invitation hooks the reader right away.</li><li><em>I recommend you to watch the films at home...</em> → <strong>I recommend watching films at home.</strong> The phrase 'recommend you to watch' is not quite natural in English. 'Recommend watching' or 'recommend that you watch' sounds more like a native speaker.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words to connect ideas smoothly. Your article uses 'because', 'for example', 'for the first reason', and 'second reason', but these are repeated and feel mechanical.</p><ul><li><em>I really like romantic comedy films because it very funny and it also has a lot of emotional. For example Chinese romantic comedy films name Hidden love and Falling in to you smile.</em> → <strong>I really like romantic comedy films because they are very funny and emotional. Two of my favourites are Chinese dramas called Hidden Love and Falling into Your Smile.</strong> You can combine the genre reason and the example into one smoother sentence.</li><li><em>I recommend you to watch the films at home for the first reason we don't need to pay the ticket we can safe a lot of money. Second reason is on webside it has a lot of movie and films we can watch anytime.</em> → <strong>I recommend watching films at home. First, you don't need to buy a ticket, so you save a lot of money. Second, streaming websites have many films and shows that you can watch anytime.</strong> Using 'first' and 'second' without 'reason' each time, and adding 'so' or 'because', makes the flow more natural.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and vocabulary with few errors. Your main grammar rule to learn is: <strong>every sentence needs a subject and a verb that agree</strong>. For example, 'it' is singular, so 'it is funny', not 'it very funny'. Also, nouns after 'a lot of' need to be plural when they are countable.</p><ul><li><em>because it very funny and it also has a lot of emotional</em> → <strong>because it is very funny and it also has a lot of emotion.</strong> You need the verb 'is' after 'it'. Also, 'emotional' is an adjective; the noun form is 'emotion'.</li><li><em>Falling in to you smile</em> → <strong>Falling into Your Smile</strong> This is a title, so every main word starts with a capital letter. Also, 'into' is one word, and 'your' is the possessive form.</li><li><em>we can safe a lot of money</em> → <strong>we can save a lot of money</strong> 'Safe' is an adjective (a safe place). 'Save' is the verb meaning to keep money.</li><li><em>on webside it has a lot of movie and films</em> → <strong>on the website, there are a lot of movies and films</strong> 'Webside' is a misspelling of 'website'. You need 'the' before 'website'. 'Movie' is countable, so it should be 'movies'.</li><li>Check your capitalisation: <em>iQYi</em> should be <strong>iQiyi</strong> (the brand uses a lowercase 'i' and capital 'Q'), and <em>Netflix</em> is correct. Always capitalise the first word of a sentence.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29727	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Ever since I was very young, I really enjoy the intensity of action films. I have always loved action films 'cause it's making me feel entertained, and it's always best to enjoy it with a snack, like chocolate, popcorn and sweets that way you can watch the film while being energized by your choice of snack. My favorite film is Doctor Strange, Spider-man (No way home) just because of the impact it has create in my imaginary senses and helps me entertained with a higher creativity, brain processing has gotten more advanced since then. As for me, my personal favorite place to watch films is at the cinema. Although prices are expensive, they are really entertaining to watch. So, sit back and relax and enjoy your favorite movie with all your freedom, whether it's the location, the snack, the movies. It's all up to you to decide.	152	18.0	Mark, your article on action films is genuinely engaging to read. You did an excellent job answering both questions from the notice — you explained why you love action films and clearly stated your preference for watching at the cinema with specific reasons. The way you wove in details like your favorite snacks and the films <em>Doctor Strange</em> and <em>Spider-Man (No way home)</em> made the writing feel personal and real. To push your writing even further, focus on tightening your grammar so your strong ideas are delivered with equal precision. Let's look at each area of the marking scale to see exactly where you can improve.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I have always loved action films 'cause it's making me feel entertained</em> — You directly addressed the task by explaining your enjoyment of the genre, which is exactly what the notice asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>As for me, my personal favorite place to watch films is at the cinema. Although prices are expensive, they are really entertaining to watch.</em> — You answered the second question about where you prefer to watch and gave a clear reason, fully informing the reader.</li>\n  <li><em>So, sit back and relax and enjoy your favorite movie with all your freedom, whether it's the location, the snack, the movies. It's all up to you to decide.</em> — This closing line shows you understood the article format and gave the reader a satisfying, personal ending.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>So, sit back and relax</em> — This direct address to the reader is a classic article technique that makes the reader feel included and engaged.</li>\n  <li><em>As for me, my personal favorite place to watch films is at the cinema.</em> — You maintained a consistent personal tone throughout, which is exactly right for an article about your own preferences.</li>\n  <li><em>It's all up to you to decide.</em> — The register stays appropriately informal and inviting from start to finish, never slipping into overly casual or formal language.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer would use a wider variety of linking words and avoid repeating the same connector. Your ideas flow logically — personal preference, examples, reasons for cinema, and a closing invitation — but you rely on 'and' quite heavily, which makes some sentences feel long and run-on.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>...like chocolate, popcorn and sweets that way you can watch the film while being energized by your choice of snack.</em> → <strong>...like chocolate, popcorn, and sweets. That way, you can watch the film while being energized by your choice of snack.</strong> Breaking this into two sentences improves clarity and gives the reader a natural pause.</li>\n  <li><em>My favorite film is Doctor Strange, Spider-man (No way home) just because of the impact it has create...</em> → <strong>My favorite films are Doctor Strange and Spider-Man: No Way Home, just because of the impact they have created...</strong> Using a comma between two film titles can confuse the reader. A clearer structure helps the reader follow your ideas.</li>\n  <li><em>So, sit back and relax and enjoy your favorite movie with all your freedom, whether it's the location, the snack, the movies.</em> → <strong>So, sit back, relax, and enjoy your favorite movie with all your freedom — whether it's the location, the snack, or the movies themselves.</strong> Using 'and' three times in one sentence makes it feel rushed. Varying your connectors keeps the reader engaged.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer would control verb tenses and forms consistently. Your vocabulary is strong — words like <em>intensity</em>, <em>impact</em>, and <em>creativity</em> show you are reaching for less common lexis. The main grammar rule to focus on is using the correct verb form after helping verbs like <em>has</em>, <em>have</em>, and <em>helps</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>it has create</em> → <strong>it has created</strong>. After <em>has</em> or <em>have</em>, you need the past participle form of the verb. <em>Create</em> becomes <em>created</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>helps me entertained</em> → <strong>helps me feel entertained</strong> or <strong>helps entertain me</strong>. After <em>helps</em>, you need either the base verb (helps entertain) or an infinitive (helps me to feel entertained). <em>Entertained</em> alone doesn't work here.</li>\n  <li><em>Ever since I was very young, I really enjoy</em> → <strong>Ever since I was very young, I have really enjoyed</strong>. When you use <em>ever since</em> to talk about something that started in the past and continues now, use the present perfect tense (<em>have enjoyed</em>), not the simple present.</li>\n  <li>Capitalisation: <em>Doctor Strange, Spider-man (No way home)</em> → <strong>Doctor Strange, Spider-Man (No Way Home)</strong>. Film titles use capital letters for all main words. Also, <em>Spider-Man</em> always has a capital M.</li>\n  <li>One more small point: <em>'cause it's making me feel entertained</em> → <strong>because they make me feel entertained</strong>. <em>'Cause</em> is fine for an article's informal tone, but <em>it's making</em> is singular while <em>action films</em> is plural. Keep your subject and verb in agreement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can apply to any sentence: after <em>has</em>, <em>have</em>, or <em>had</em>, ask yourself — is the next word a past participle (like <em>created</em>, <em>made</em>, <em>seen</em>)? After <em>helps</em>, ask yourself — is the next word a base verb (like <em>feel</em>, <em>entertain</em>)? This small check will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33954	2025-08-30 13:36:51.174328+00	2026	Free time Activities	Activities for teens in Thailand absolutely mostly of them is using their phone or playing game in mobile-phone but they still have their own hobbies which is make a good relax moment in free time without internet or use it less than when just addicted with it. So, the activities teens usually do are hang out with friends and call with friends and talking any topic they like.<br><br><br>In the begining, if there was no any plan to do, hang out with friends is a good choice. To keep a good and fun memories we have to keep each other company going outside together whenever we like. For teenagers, we have to ask for permission from parents to go outside and even planing to friends well enough for a permission. Going outside, having fun, I think that was enough for teenager's life.<br><br><br>As well as calling with friends and talking whatever we want. This can be in real life to, sit then talk but this we can talk even long distance between friends. Calling isn't just for talking, playing games together or playing some challenges and more. All of this is for kill the time that passed.<br><br><br>All in all, all of teen's activities are time for go have a good childhood for life ness with any people they love, friends, parents, sibling or lover if they had a highschool sweetheart. Keeping memory of this life age is the best thing forever in life, keep it as much as we can so, we can remember it until we forgot this life age and wondering how was that life age we've been. Wish we could have a good memories.	278	11.0	<p>Here is your writing feedback for 'Free time Activities'. You wrote 278 words and achieved B1+. You organized your essay into paragraphs, which is a good way to structure your ideas.</p>	B1+	Writing	CA	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	<p>You attempted to address the task by mentioning free time activities, but the essay's ending paragraph becomes quite abstract and less focused on "free time activities" and more on a general reflection on childhood memories, which slightly detracts from fully informing the target reader about the topic. You also have some unclear phrasing in your introduction.<br>👉 To Improve: Make sure to finish your essay and cover all parts of the question clearly. Ensure your introduction clearly states your main points and your conclusion effectively summarizes them, staying focused on the topic.<br>❌ Weakness: Your conclusion talks about "time for go have a good childhood for life ness" and "Keeping memory of this life age is the best thing forever in life," which is a bit general for the topic of "free time activities."<br>✨ Improved: "In conclusion, free time activities like hanging out and calling friends are essential for Thai teenagers. They provide valuable opportunities to build strong friendships, share experiences, and create lasting memories that contribute positively to their social development and overall well-being."<br>💡 Explanation: Completing the essay and developing your ideas fully ensures the reader gets all the information they need and a clear understanding of your points. A strong conclusion summarises your main ideas and directly relates back to the essay topic.</p>	3.0	<p>You used an appropriate tone for an article, but the register shifts between formal and informal language, which can confuse the reader about the intended audience. Your grammatical errors also affect clarity and overall impact.<br>👉 To Improve: Maintain a consistent register throughout your writing - either formal for a general audience or consistently informal for peers. For an essay, a semi-formal to formal tone is usually best.<br>❌ Weakness: Phrases like "going outside together whenever we like" lean towards informal language, while other parts of your essay attempt a more formal approach.<br>✨ Improved: "Thai teenagers commonly engage in social activities, such as meeting friends in person or communicating with them by phone, to relax and strengthen their bonds."<br>💡 Explanation: Keeping a consistent formal tone helps establish credibility and makes your writing more professional for a general audience. Clear and accurate grammar also significantly improves how effectively your message is understood.</p>	3.0	<p>You organized your ideas into paragraphs, which helps the reader follow your main points, but the connections between ideas could be stronger with better linking words. The thesis statement in your introduction isn't explicitly clear, and your concluding paragraph becomes quite abstract rather than clearly summarizing your points.<br>👉 To Improve: Use a wider variety of linking words and phrases to show clearer relationships between your ideas (e.g., however, therefore, as a result, in addition). Make sure your introduction has a clear thesis statement, and your conclusion restates it and summarizes your main points.<br>❌ Weakness: Your introduction starts with "Activities for teens in Thailand absolutely mostly of them is using their phone... but they still have their own hobbies which is make a good relax moment..." which makes it difficult to immediately identify your main argument or the essay's focus.<br>✨ Improved: "While many Thai teenagers use their phones for leisure, they also engage in valuable social activities like meeting and calling friends, which are essential for relaxation and personal development."<br>💡 Explanation: Using specific linking words helps show the relationship between ideas more clearly and makes your writing flow better. A clear thesis guides your entire essay, and a strong conclusion brings all your ideas together effectively.</p>	2.0	<p>You used some good vocabulary related to the topic, but there are several recurring grammar patterns and sentence structure issues that affect clarity, and there are no error-free sentences.<br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "a good memories"<br>✨ Correction: "good memories"<br>💡 Explanation: In English, the word "memories" is a plural count noun, and you would not use "a" before it when talking about multiple memories. This is similar to how Thai handles plurals without a specific marker like 's' in English, which can be a common challenge for Thai learners.<br><br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "mobile-phone but they still have their own hobbies"<br>✨ Correction: "mobile-phone, but they still have their own hobbies"<br>💡 Explanation: In English, when you connect two complete sentences with a conjunction like "but," you usually need a comma before the conjunction. Thai sentences often connect ideas without these explicit markers, leading to 'run-on' sentences or comma splices in English.<br><br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "All of this is for kill the time"<br>✨ Correction: "All of this is to kill the time"<br>💡 Explanation: The choice of preposition can be tricky in English. In this context, "to" is used to show purpose or intention. Thai learners sometimes choose "for" because it can also indicate purpose, but the grammatical structure required here is "to" + base verb (infinitive).</p>	\N	\N	2025-08-30
29745	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	The kind of films I do enjoy watching is Romantic-Comedy, and Drama. To begin with, I have two personalities in my mind, one is the joyful, enthusiasm, and carefree one, while the other is the quiet and a little sad one. The moods I feel is the movies I watch. For example, last week, I've got a lot of homeworks todo - and I feel like my life is buried in deep pool of homeworks, so, I decide to watch the movie called "The Evil Lawyer" that mirroring real life of laws in Thailand, and it get me feel into it and stuck with the characters for whole week. To sum up, I would strongly prefer watching any kind of movie in the cinema - since I will often go there after an exam or when it's long holidays without any works to deal with, and that's why I'll always get an amazing memory whenever I have got a plan to go	165	17.0	Pheem, your article is genuinely engaging. You didn't just list film genres — you connected them to your own personality and moods, which is exactly what a good article does. The personal story about watching "The Evil Lawyer" after feeling buried in homework is specific, honest, and makes the reader want to keep reading. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll need to tighten your grammar and polish a few sentences so your strong ideas are delivered with equal strength in language. Let's look at each subscale.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The kind of films I do enjoy watching is Romantic-Comedy, and Drama.</em> You introduced your two favourite genres clearly, which sets up the whole article.</li>\n  <li><em>I have two personalities in my mind, one is the joyful, enthusiasm, and carefree one, while the other is the quiet and a little sad one. The moods I feel is the movies I watch.</em> This is a thoughtful, personal insight that connects your emotions to your film choices. The reader understands your system.</li>\n  <li><em>I would strongly prefer watching any kind of movie in the cinema - since I will often go there after an exam or when it's long holidays without any works to deal with</em> You gave a clear, personal reason for your cinema preference, which completes the task requirements fully.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the register consistent throughout — every sentence should feel like it belongs in the same article. You have a strong, personal voice, but a few phrases slip into a more casual tone.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I've got a lot of homeworks todo</em> → <strong>I had a lot of homework to do</strong>. The original sounds like rushed speech. The corrected version keeps your personal voice while sounding more polished.</li>\n  <li><em>it get me feel into it and stuck with the characters for whole week</em> → <strong>it pulled me into the story, and I stayed connected to the characters for an entire week</strong>. This keeps your enthusiasm but uses more controlled language.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every paragraph smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. Your structure is logical — genres, moods, example, conclusion — but the move from your example to your final paragraph feels abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>...stuck with the characters for whole week. To sum up, I would strongly prefer...</em> → Add a bridging sentence like <strong>That experience reminded me why I love watching films at the cinema.</strong> This connects your personal story to your final preference.</li>\n  <li>Your use of <em>To begin with</em>, <em>For example</em>, and <em>To sum up</em> gives clear signposts. Keep these, and add one more linking phrase between the example and the conclusion.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and noun forms consistently. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>singular subjects need singular verbs, and plural subjects need plural verbs</strong>. Also, some nouns (like "homework" and "work") are uncountable and never take an 's'. Let's fix the patterns.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The moods I feel is the movies I watch</em> → <strong>The moods I feel are the movies I watch</strong>. "Moods" is plural, so the verb must be "are".</li>\n  <li><em>I've got a lot of homeworks todo</em> → <strong>I had a lot of homework to do</strong>. "Homework" is uncountable — no 's'. Also, "todo" should be two words: "to do".</li>\n  <li><em>it get me feel into it</em> → <strong>it got me feeling involved in it</strong> or <strong>it pulled me into it</strong>. The verb "get" needs to match the past tense of the story, and "feel into it" is not a standard expression.</li>\n  <li><em>mirroring real life of laws in Thailand</em> → <strong>which mirrored real-life laws in Thailand</strong>. After "movie called...", you need a relative clause with a past tense verb.</li>\n  <li><em>when it's long holidays without any works to deal with</em> → <strong>when it's the long holidays, without any work to deal with</strong>. "Work" is uncountable (no 's'), and "holidays" needs an article here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check for next time: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself — is the subject singular or plural? Does the verb match? Is this noun countable or uncountable?</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29749	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I would definitly prefer films like Child's Play . Where the Villain is an evil little doll that kill's people that dolls name is Chucky, Chucky even kills kids he is very Strong too since he is Possessed by a real serial killer. I would prefer watching at the cinema because it has better quality	56	9.0	Pipe, you've made a solid start on your article about films. I can see you have a clear preference for horror movies, and you gave a specific example with <em>Child's Play</em>. You also explained that Chucky is a doll possessed by a serial killer, which shows you understand the story. To reach Band 5, you'll need to develop your ideas more fully and structure your writing like a proper article. Let's look at each area in detail.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer fully answers both parts of the task with developed ideas. You stated your film preference and gave a reason for the cinema, but the second question needs more depth.</p><ul><li><em>I would definitly prefer films like Child's Play.</em> → <strong>I would definitely prefer horror films like Child's Play.</strong> You named the film, which is good. Now add a sentence explaining <em>why</em> you like horror films specifically, not just describing the villain.</li><li><em>I would prefer watching at the cinema because it has better quality</em> → <strong>I would prefer watching at the cinema because the screen and sound quality are much better than at home.</strong> You gave one reason. A Band 5 writer would add a second reason, like the atmosphere or the shared experience with other viewers.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer writes a complete article with an engaging introduction and a clear conclusion. Your text reads like a short spoken answer, not a published article.</p><ul><li>Your article needs an opening sentence that grabs the reader's attention. Try starting with a question: <strong>Do you love being scared? Then horror films are for you.</strong></li><li>Your article also needs a closing sentence that wraps up your opinion. Try: <strong>For me, the cinema is the only place to watch a horror film.</strong></li><li>Your register is informal throughout, which is fine for an article, but avoid capitalising random words like <em>Villain</em> and <em>Strong</em>. Articles use standard capitalisation.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas clearly so the reader can follow the argument. Your text jumps from describing Chucky to stating your cinema preference without a smooth link.</p><ul><li>You describe Chucky in detail, then suddenly say <em>I would prefer watching at the cinema</em>. Add a transition: <strong>After watching this film, I realised that horror movies are best experienced on a big screen.</strong></li><li>Your article is one block of text. Break it into two paragraphs: one about the film you like, and one about why you prefer the cinema. Use a blank line between them.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct spelling, punctuation, and capitalisation. The main rule to learn here is: common nouns (like <em>villain</em>, <em>strong</em>, <em>possessed</em>) should not be capitalised mid-sentence. Only proper nouns like <em>Chucky</em> need capitals.</p><ul><li><em>definitly</em> → <strong>definitely</strong>. This is a common spelling error. Remember: it has an 'i' after 'defin' and ends with 'ely'.</li><li><em>doll that kill's people</em> → <strong>doll that kills people</strong>. Apostrophes show possession (the doll's name) or contractions (it's = it is). They are not used for plural verbs. Use <em>kills</em> for he/she/it.</li><li><em>he is very Strong too since he is Possessed</em> → <strong>he is very strong too since he is possessed</strong>. Words like <em>strong</em> and <em>possessed</em> are common adjectives, not proper nouns. Do not capitalise them.</li><li><em>Where the Villain is an evil little doll</em> → <strong>where the villain is an evil little doll</strong>. The word <em>Villain</em> should be lowercase. Only capitalise it if it is part of a title, like in a film poster.</li><li>Good use of the word <em>quality</em> to describe the cinema experience. Try adding <em>sound</em> or <em>atmosphere</em> for more precise vocabulary.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29769	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy science-fiction films, there this one movie called Project hail mary it my favorite science-fiction films, it was a films about something in space was eating light and can multiply alot so they were eating light in all galaxy so they need to find a teacher from univesity to go to the space and find the cause and a way to solve. To me this was a really good film that everyone can watch. <br><br> I prefer watching them at cinema because at cinema they got a very high quality speaker and screen, so the morte will have good quality and sounds like it is happen in real life.	113	13.0	Posh, your article shows a genuine love for science-fiction, and I can tell you were excited to write about 'Project Hail Mary.' You gave a detailed summary of the plot and clearly stated why you prefer the cinema experience. That is exactly what an article needs. To reach Band 5, the next step is to polish how you present those ideas so the reader feels guided through your thoughts, not rushed. Let's look at each area.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy science-fiction films</em> You opened with a clear, personal statement about your taste, which immediately connects with the reader.</li><li><em>To me this was a really good film that everyone can watch</em> You gave a personal recommendation, which is a strong way to end a paragraph in an article.</li><li><em>I prefer watching them at cinema because at cinema they got a very high quality speaker and screen</em> You directly answered the second task question with a specific reason, and the reader understands your choice completely.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format consistently with a clear introduction, body, and conclusion, and keeps the register appropriate throughout. Your ideas are good, but the text reads like a spoken story rather than a written article.</p><ul><li><em>there this one movie called Project hail mary it my favorite science-fiction films</em> → <strong>There is a movie called Project Hail Mary. It is my favourite science-fiction film.</strong> Breaking this into shorter, complete sentences makes the article feel more controlled and polished.</li><li><em>so they were eating light in all galaxy so they need to find a teacher from univesity to go to the space</em> → <strong>They were eating light across the galaxy, so the characters needed to find a scientist from a university to go into space.</strong> Using more precise nouns like 'scientist' instead of 'teacher' keeps the register appropriate for an article.</li><li>Your article would benefit from a short introduction that hooks the reader, such as 'Do you love being pulled into another world? I do, and science-fiction films do that best.' A closing sentence like 'For me, the cinema is the only way to truly experience a film like this' would also give your article a stronger finish.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly within paragraphs using a variety of linking words, and each paragraph has a clear focus. You have two paragraphs, which is good, but the first paragraph is one long sentence that jumps between ideas.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy science-fiction films, there this one movie called Project hail mary it my favorite science-fiction films, it was a films about something in space was eating light and can multiply alot so they were eating light in all galaxy so they need to find a teacher from univesity to go to the space and find the cause and a way to solve.</em> → <strong>I enjoy science-fiction films. One of my favourites is Project Hail Mary. It is about something in space that eats light and can multiply quickly. The characters need to find a scientist from a university to go into space and solve the problem.</strong> Breaking this into separate sentences with 'One of my favourites' and 'It is about' creates a logical flow.</li><li><em>To me this was a really good film that everyone can watch.</em> This sentence works well as a conclusion to your first paragraph. Good job.</li><li>In the second paragraph, you could add a linking word like 'Firstly' before 'because at cinema they got a very high quality speaker and screen' to signal the start of your reason. For example: <strong>I prefer watching them at the cinema. Firstly, the cinema has very high quality speakers and a large screen.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. The main pattern that needs attention is missing verbs in your sentences. Many of your sentences are missing the verb 'to be' (is, are, was, were). Let's fix that.</p><ul><li><em>there this one movie called Project hail mary</em> → <strong>There is this one movie called Project Hail Mary.</strong> Every sentence needs a main verb. 'There is' adds the missing verb.</li><li><em>it my favorite science-fiction films</em> → <strong>It is my favourite science-fiction film.</strong> 'It is' adds the verb, and 'film' is singular because you are talking about one film.</li><li><em>it was a films</em> → <strong>It was a film</strong> or <strong>It was a science-fiction film.</strong> 'A' is used with singular countable nouns, so 'a films' is incorrect. Use 'a film' or 'a science-fiction film'.</li><li><em>like it is happen in real life</em> → <strong>like it is happening in real life</strong> or <strong>as if it is happening in real life.</strong> After 'is', use the '-ing' form of the verb (happening), not the base form (happen).</li><li>Check your spelling: 'alot' should be <strong>a lot</strong> (two words), 'univesity' should be <strong>university</strong>, and 'morte' is unclear. Did you mean <strong>movie</strong>? Also, capitalise film titles: <strong>Project Hail Mary</strong>.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33169	2025-08-14 13:21:27.053501+00	2026	Thai Food	Food is one of the most important thing in all cultures. Although, all culture have food, each culture delelops its own styles of their food. In particular, street food in Thailand. Three of the most popular street foods in Thailand are moo-ping, pad thai, and pad ka praow.<br><br><br>The most well known street food is moo-ping, the ingredients are chopped pork, a wooden stick, and some soy sauce, which is made by stab the wooden stick into the pork and grill it on the barbecue stove then apply the sauce that you like or soy sauce on the pork as you grilling it. And when its cooked, you can eat it with rice or sticky rice. Moo-ping is the food that represent Thai people life style in the past where they want a food that have simple ingredients, not expensive and can fill their stomach. This make moo-ping one of the most beloved Thai street food and commonly enjoyed with sticky rice as a snack or breakfast.<br><br><br>Beside enjoying moo-ping, pad thai is also another great choice of street food. Pad thai is known for its perfect balance of sweet, sour, salty and umami flavors. It is a stir fried noodle dish which includes rice noodles, tofu or shrimp, egg, bean sprouts and the based sauce. Pad thai is often prepare right in front of customer, using high heat and rapidly cooking techniques to create the signature smoky aroma and colorful looks, made it a must try Thai dish. And this represent how Thai people used local ingrediants to create an unique and one of the most poplur dish in the wrold.<br><br><br>Finally, pad ka proaw, stir fried basil with meal, is a staple meal for many Thais who want a quick and delicious meal. The ingredients are as simple as its name, pork or chicken, garlic, basil leaves and soy sauce. Firstly, you chop the garlic and stir them on the hot pan with some oil, then add the meat and soy sauce, finally, add basil leaves and stir fried them well. Its commonly serve with rice and fried egg, its only take small amout of time to make. Pad ka proaw can be seen in every resturant not only in street food. Because of its comforting taste makes everyone loved it. This shows that Thai people like food that have simple ingredients, delicious yet affordable.<br><br><br>To sum up, Thai street food are mostly made by local ingredients that the locals can find such as moo-ping, pad th and pad ka praow which also made by the local ingredients because thai people don't really like to wake their food, yet its still flavorful and popular till today.	448	\N	Your essay on Thai street food is well-structured and clearly conveys your ideas about moo-ping, pad thai, and pad ka praow. You have successfully introduced the cultural significance of these dishes, which was a special requirement for this essay. Your use of vocabulary is good, and you attempt to use more complex sentence structures. To improve, focus on refining your grammar and spelling, particularly with articles and hyphenated words, and ensure consistent use of relative clauses and passive structures.	\N	Writing	Formative	FIRST	Part 2	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2025-08-14
29793	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy watching Sci-Fi films such as Spider-Man, X-man or Marvels since i was a kid, it's my favourite type of films because it brings me a lot of emotions while watching, thrilling, exciting and sometimes even sad, for me i think Spider-Man No way Home is one of the best movies I've ever watched in my entire life because it brings all the emotion in one movie. I prefer watching films at home rather than watching them at the cinema. That way, I have more privacy and i rather not spend money on movie tickets and overpriced popcorns. As for watching films at the cinema, I think it doesn't beat watching films at home. For me, i think watching films at the cinema is overrated because the ticket prices are too expensive and sometimes the seat is full and the theater is packed. Watching films at home brings me a better experience.	160	15.0	Charles, you've written an article that feels genuinely personal. Your choice of <em>Spider-Man: No Way Home</em> as an example and your honest reasons for preferring home viewing — <em>privacy</em>, <em>overpriced popcorns</em>, <em>the seat is full and the theater is packed</em> — show a real awareness of what makes an article interesting to read. You've covered every point the task asked for with clear opinions and specific details. To reach Band 5 across all areas, the next step is to give your writing more structure and polish the grammar. Let's look at each part.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy watching Sci-Fi films such as Spider-Man, X-man or Marvels since i was a kid</em> You introduced your genre and gave specific examples right away. The reader knows exactly what you like and why.</li><li><em>for me i think Spider-Man No way Home is one of the best movies I've ever watched in my entire life because it brings all the emotion in one movie</em> You named your favourite film and explained what makes it special. This personal touch makes the article engaging.</li><li><em>I prefer watching films at home rather than watching them at the cinema. That way, I have more privacy and i rather not spend money on movie tickets and overpriced popcorns.</em> You gave a clear preference with two concrete reasons. The reader is fully informed.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer hooks the reader from the very first sentence with a question, a surprising statement, or a vivid image. Your article is clear and personal, but it starts with <em>I enjoy watching Sci-Fi films...</em> which is a bit flat for an article opening.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy watching Sci-Fi films such as Spider-Man, X-man or Marvels since i was a kid</em> → <strong>Have you ever watched a film that made you feel everything at once? For me, that film is Spider-Man: No Way Home.</strong> A question pulls the reader in immediately.</li><li><em>As for watching films at the cinema, I think it doesn't beat watching films at home.</em> → <strong>But is the cinema really the best place to watch? I don't think so.</strong> Using a rhetorical question keeps the article conversational and engaging.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraph breaks and a variety of linking words to guide the reader smoothly from one idea to the next. Your ideas are in a logical order — genre, favourite film, home vs. cinema — but everything is in one block of text, and you repeat <em>I think</em> and <em>for me</em> several times.</p><ul><li>Break your article into three paragraphs: one for your favourite genre and film, one for why you prefer home viewing, and one for why the cinema is overrated. Each paragraph should focus on one main idea.</li><li><em>for me i think Spider-Man No way Home is one of the best movies...</em> → <strong>In my opinion, Spider-Man: No Way Home is one of the best movies...</strong> Vary your opinion phrases. Try <em>In my view</em>, <em>I believe</em>, or <em>It seems to me that</em> instead of repeating <em>I think</em>.</li><li><em>I prefer watching films at home rather than watching them at the cinema. That way, I have more privacy...</em> → <strong>I prefer watching films at home for two reasons. First, I have more privacy. Second, I don't have to spend money on expensive tickets and popcorn.</strong> Using <em>First</em> and <em>Second</em> makes your reasons clearer.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer controls capital letters and verb tenses consistently so the reader never has to pause. The main rule to focus on is: <strong>Always capitalise the pronoun 'I' and the first word of every sentence.</strong> Most of your errors come from forgetting this rule. There are also a few verb tense and word form issues.</p><ul><li><em>since i was a kid</em> → <strong>since I was a kid</strong>. The pronoun 'I' must always be capitalised, even in the middle of a sentence.</li><li><em>for me i think</em> → <strong>For me, I think</strong>. Capitalise 'I' and add a comma after <em>For me</em>.</li><li><em>i rather not spend</em> → <strong>I would rather not spend</strong>. The structure is <em>would rather + base verb</em>. You need <em>would</em> before <em>rather</em>.</li><li><em>it's my favourite type of films</em> → <strong>they are my favourite type of films</strong> or <strong>it is my favourite type of film</strong>. <em>Films</em> is plural, so <em>they are</em> matches, or use the singular <em>film</em> to match <em>it</em>.</li><li><em>since i was a kid</em> — this is a time expression that usually pairs with present perfect (<em>I have enjoyed... since I was a kid</em>), but your present tense is acceptable here. Focus on capitalisation first.</li></ul><p><strong>Self-check for every sentence:</strong> Read each sentence aloud. Did you capitalise 'I'? Did you start with a capital letter? Did you end with a period? If yes, your writing will look much more polished.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29798	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy to watch romantic comedy films, because this kind of film - doesn't make me feel uncomfortable, or being nervous. I always feel relax, and I don't need to think too much about the film's story.<br><br>When I watch this type of film, I thought about my mom, and my dad when they are meet each others for the first time, and how their love happened. I prefer watching them at home because I can bring some snack, and beverage to eat during watching. If I watch it at home, I can stay more concentrate because it's nothing to interrupts me.<br><br>For example, when I preparing the sofa, and my food to watch the film, I will close the curtain, and pause the highest volume button. So I can focusing on my film session, and stay with myself.	146	16.0	Yaya, you've written a genuinely engaging article about romantic comedies. The way you connect the genre to your parents' love story is a personal, thoughtful touch that makes your writing stand out. Your article is already strong, and with a few adjustments to your language accuracy, you can make it even more polished. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how to reach Band 5 everywhere.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy to watch romantic comedy films, because this kind of film - doesn't make me feel uncomfortable, or being nervous.</em> You clearly state your preferred genre and give two specific reasons. The reader knows exactly why you like romantic comedies.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer watching them at home because I can bring some snack, and beverage to eat during watching.</em> You explain your preference for home viewing with a practical reason, which fully addresses the second question from the notice.</li>\n  <li><em>For example, when I preparing the sofa, and my food to watch the film, I will close the curtain, and pause the highest volume button.</em> You give a detailed personal example that shows the reader your routine. The target reader is fully informed about your habits and preferences.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would keep every part of the article focused on the main topic, so the reader never feels a shift away from the core message. Your article is engaging and well-structured, but the example about your parents, while charming, pulls the focus slightly away from your own film-watching preferences.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>When I watch this type of film, I thought about my mom, and my dad when they are meet each others for the first time, and how their love happened.</em> → <strong>When I watch this type of film, I think about how my parents met and fell in love.</strong> This keeps the connection to romantic comedies but stays focused on your personal reaction, not their story.</li>\n  <li><em>So I can focusing on my film session, and stay with myself.</em> → <strong>So I can focus completely on the film and enjoy my own company.</strong> This ending is stronger because it ties back to the idea of relaxation you introduced at the start.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraph breaks and smooth transitions so the reader moves naturally from one idea to the next. You have good paragraph structure, but the jump from your parents to your home-viewing routine could be smoother.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>When I watch this type of film, I thought about my mom, and my dad... I prefer watching them at home because...</em> → <strong>When I watch this type of film, I think about my parents. Watching at home makes this even better because...</strong> The word 'this' connects the two ideas clearly.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer watching them at home because I can bring some snack, and beverage to eat during watching.</em> → <strong>I prefer watching them at home because I can bring snacks and a drink.</strong> Removing 'during watching' and using 'snacks' (plural) makes the sentence flow more naturally.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls verb patterns and tenses accurately so the reader never hesitates. Your main area for improvement is using the correct form after verbs like 'enjoy', 'focus', and 'prepare'. Let's fix the most common patterns.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I enjoy to watch</em> → <strong>I enjoy watching</strong>. After 'enjoy', use the -ing form, not 'to' + verb. This is the most important rule to remember.</li>\n  <li><em>I can stay more concentrate</em> → <strong>I can concentrate more</strong>. 'Concentrate' is a verb, not an adjective. Use 'more' before the verb, or say 'I can stay more focused' if you want an adjective.</li>\n  <li><em>when I preparing</em> → <strong>when I prepare</strong> or <strong>when I am preparing</strong>. After 'when', use either the simple present (for habits) or present continuous (for actions in progress). Don't drop the verb 'am'.</li>\n  <li><em>it's nothing to interrupts me</em> → <strong>nothing interrupts me</strong>. After 'nothing', use the base verb without 'to'. Also, check your capitalisation: 'I' is always capital, and 'romantic comedy films' doesn't need a capital R unless it starts a sentence.</li>\n  <li><em>So I can focusing on my film session</em> → <strong>So I can focus on my film</strong>. After 'can', always use the base form of the verb. No -ing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check for your next draft: after every verb like <em>enjoy</em>, <em>like</em>, <em>love</em>, <em>prefer</em>, and <em>finish</em>, ask yourself: is the next word an -ing form? If the verb is <em>can</em>, <em>will</em>, or <em>must</em>, ask: is the next word the base form (no -s, no -ing)?</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30221	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	In my opinion, I like action movies. It's a very exciting kind of movies. I like it because every time I watch it, there will be an unexpected moment that jump in every time. The second reason is it was a really intresting genre in the world! It's interesting because every time I watch it my heart is pounding! It's a very strange feelings!<br><br>I prefer watching in the cinema. When I watch it at home, I'll be like "Meh..." like, it's okay but at the cinema is better. When I went to the cinema it feels like I transport in to the film! Believe me at the cinema is better.	118	15.0	Tata, your article is genuinely exciting to read. You chose action movies as your topic and made the reader feel your energy right away with lines like <em>every time I watch it my heart is pounding!</em> That kind of personal enthusiasm is exactly what makes an article engaging. You also gave two clear reasons for liking action movies and then moved smoothly into explaining why the cinema is better than home. Your main idea is strong, and the reader finishes knowing exactly what you think and why. To reach Band 5, you'll need to polish your grammar and build a tighter structure so every sentence hits with full force. Let's look at each area.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>In my opinion, I like action movies.</em> You stated your preferred genre clearly and directly, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I like it because every time I watch it, there will be an unexpected moment that jump in every time.</em> You gave a specific, personal reason that helps the reader understand why action movies excite you.</li>\n  <li><em>When I went to the cinema it feels like I transport in to the film!</em> You explained why the cinema experience is better with a vivid image that makes your opinion memorable.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer holds the reader's attention from start to finish with a clear, well-developed opinion and a confident article voice. Your enthusiasm comes through strongly, but the structure within the first paragraph repeats the same idea a little too often.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>It's a very exciting kind of movies. I like it because every time I watch it, there will be an unexpected moment that jump in every time. The second reason is it was a really intresting genre in the world!</em> You have two good reasons here, but they both say 'action movies are exciting.' Try making the second reason different, like: <strong>The second reason is that action movies always surprise me. I never know what will happen next, and that keeps me glued to the screen.</strong> This way each sentence adds something new.</li>\n  <li><em>Believe me at the cinema is better.</em> Using direct address like 'Believe me' is a great article technique. To make it even stronger, add a comma: <strong>Believe me, at the cinema is better.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly within and between paragraphs so the reader never feels lost. You have two clear paragraphs, but the first one repeats itself and the jump to the second paragraph is sudden.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>It's a very exciting kind of movies. I like it because every time I watch it, there will be an unexpected moment that jump in every time. The second reason is it was a really intresting genre in the world! It's interesting because every time I watch it my heart is pounding!</em> You say 'exciting' or 'interesting' four times in a row. Try grouping your ideas: one sentence for the unexpected moments, one sentence for the pounding heart, and drop the repeated 'interesting' statement. A tighter sequence would be: <strong>I like action movies because they always surprise me. Every time I watch one, my heart starts pounding during the unexpected moments.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>It's a very strange feelings! I prefer watching in the cinema.</em> The transition from 'strange feelings' to 'cinema' is abrupt. Add a linking sentence: <strong>All that excitement is best enjoyed on a big screen. That is why I prefer watching in the cinema.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling so the reader never has to guess the meaning. Your vocabulary is good, but you have a few patterns that need attention.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>a very exciting kind of movies</em> → <strong>a very exciting kind of movie</strong>. After 'kind of', use the singular form. You can also say <strong>a very exciting type of movie</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>unexpected moment that jump in every time</em> → <strong>unexpected moments that jump out every time</strong>. 'Moment' needs to be plural because you are talking about many moments. Also, 'jump in' should be 'jump out' because the surprise comes at you.</li>\n  <li><em>it was a really intresting genre</em> → <strong>it is a really interesting genre</strong>. Use present tense here because you are describing your current opinion. Check your spelling: 'interesting' has an 'e' after the 'r'.</li>\n  <li><em>a very strange feelings</em> → <strong>a very strange feeling</strong>. After 'a', use the singular form of the noun. If you want to use the plural, say <strong>very strange feelings</strong> without 'a'.</li>\n  <li><em>I transport in to the film</em> → <strong>I am transported into the film</strong>. This is a passive construction: the film pulls you in. Also 'in to' should be one word 'into' when it means moving inside something.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can use: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Is my noun singular or plural? Does my verb match it?' That will catch most of these errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30942	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	The genre of films that I enjoy the most is Romantic-Comedy. I've liked it since I watched 10 Things I hate about you. It's a popular movie. Mostly, I watch movies at home because I can do everything while I'm watching it. However, watching movies at home makes me have less concentration with the movie. In addition, watching at the cinema would help you have better concentration. The reason is because you just have to focus on one thing. The phone or the loud sound from talking is banned, because it interrupts other people. So, I prefer watching them at the cinema, because I could understand the movie more than watching at home.	117	18.0	Praewa, your article is a pleasure to read. You chose a clear genre, gave a specific film example, and explained your preference for the cinema with solid reasoning. The sentence <em>watching at the cinema would help you have better concentration</em> shows you can connect an idea to a practical benefit for the reader. To reach Band 5 across all areas, focus on polishing your grammar and making your transitions between ideas feel effortless. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The genre of films that I enjoy the most is Romantic-Comedy.</em> You clearly stated your favourite genre right at the start, which answers the first question from the notice.</li>\n  <li><em>I've liked it since I watched 10 Things I hate about you.</em> You gave a specific, well-known example that makes your writing personal and credible.</li>\n  <li><em>watching at the cinema would help you have better concentration. The reason is because you just have to focus on one thing.</em> You explained why the cinema is better with a clear, logical reason. The reader understands your preference completely.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The genre of films that I enjoy the most is Romantic-Comedy.</em> You opened with a direct, engaging statement that hooks the reader and introduces your topic.</li>\n  <li><em>However, watching movies at home makes me have less concentration with the movie.</em> You used a contrast to show you've thought about both sides, which keeps the article interesting.</li>\n  <li><em>So, I prefer watching them at the cinema, because I could understand the movie more than watching at home.</em> Your conclusion states a clear personal preference and ties back to your earlier points, leaving the reader satisfied.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer links each paragraph so smoothly that the reader never has to pause to follow the logic. Your ideas are in a good order, but the jump from your film example to the home-versus-cinema comparison could be more natural.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I've liked it since I watched 10 Things I hate about you. It's a popular movie. Mostly, I watch movies at home...</em> → <strong>I've liked it since I watched 10 Things I Hate About You, a popular movie. When I watch movies, I usually do so at home...</strong> Connecting the example to your viewing habit with a short phrase like "When I watch movies" makes the transition feel intentional rather than sudden.</li>\n  <li><em>The reason is because...</em> → <strong>The reason is that...</strong> or <strong>This is because...</strong> Using "reason is because" is redundant. Choosing one or the other tightens your sentence.</li>\n  <li>Your use of <em>However</em> and <em>In addition</em> works well to guide the reader. Keep using these signposts, and add one more linking phrase like <em>For example</em> or <em>As a result</em> to vary your transitions further.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammar with very few errors, and their vocabulary sounds natural and precise. Your vocabulary is strong, and most of your sentences are correct. The main area to improve is capitalisation of titles and a few unnatural word choices.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>10 Things I hate about you</em> → <strong>10 Things I Hate About You</strong>. In English, every important word in a film title starts with a capital letter. The words <em>I</em> and <em>About</em> should be capitalised. Check any title you write in future.</li>\n  <li><em>makes me have less concentration</em> → <strong>makes it harder for me to concentrate</strong> or <strong>reduces my concentration</strong>. The phrase "have less concentration" is understandable but sounds slightly unnatural. Using <em>concentrate</em> as a verb or <em>reduces my concentration</em> is more natural.</li>\n  <li><em>The reason is because</em> → <strong>The reason is that</strong> or <strong>This is because</strong>. You do not need both <em>reason</em> and <em>because</em> in the same sentence. Choose one.</li>\n  <li><em>I could understand the movie more than watching at home</em> → <strong>I can understand the movie better than I can at home</strong>. Using <em>could</em> suggests past ability, but you are talking about a general preference. <em>Can</em> is the correct choice here. Also, <em>more than watching</em> is a little unclear; <em>better than I can at home</em> is clearer.</li>\n  <li>Your vocabulary is a strength: <em>genre</em>, <em>concentration</em>, <em>interrupts</em> are all effective choices. Keep using specific words like these. A self-check for your next draft: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, "Could I say this more simply?" If yes, try the simpler version first.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34034	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	What kind of films do I enjoy? As for the genre that I enjoy is fantasy, horror, comedy, science fictions, thriller, fighting, actions, and adventure! That's a lot, I could say that I like pretty much everything! Except romantic or a love story.<br><br>I'd pretty much say that I prefer watching it at home than at the cinema.<br>My first point is that it's free. You can watch anything you want, at anytime, but you'll have wait a couples of weeks before you can. And my second point is that you can do anything! You can pause the movie to go to the toilet or what ever you want to do. And lastly, you'll have you own privacy. You don't want to here someone baby crying during a movie or someone playing phones and interrupt the movie.<br><br>That's all I wanna share with yall.	156	18.0	Singto, your article is genuinely enjoyable to read. You answered both parts of the task completely — listing your favourite genres with real enthusiasm and giving three clear, personal reasons for preferring to watch at home. The line <em>I could say that I like pretty much everything! Except romantic or a love story</em> shows confidence and a clear opinion, which is exactly what a good article needs. To reach Band 5 across all areas, the next step is to polish your grammar and sentence control so your strong ideas come through without small errors distracting the reader. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>What kind of films do I enjoy? As for the genre that I enjoy is fantasy, horror, comedy, science fictions, thriller, fighting, actions, and adventure!</em> You listed many specific genres, which tells the reader you know your films well. The question mark at the start also draws the reader in.</li>\n  <li><em>My first point is that it's free. You can watch anything you want, at anytime, but you'll have wait a couples of weeks before you can.</em> You gave a clear reason with a realistic limitation, which makes your argument feel honest and thoughtful.</li>\n  <li><em>You don't want to here someone baby crying during a movie or someone playing phones and interrupt the movie.</em> This is a vivid, relatable example that makes your preference for privacy feel real to the reader.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>What kind of films do I enjoy?</em> Opening with a direct question to the reader is a classic article technique, and it works well here to create engagement.</li>\n  <li><em>That's a lot, I could say that I like pretty much everything!</em> This casual, opinionated tone suits a website article perfectly. It sounds like you're talking to a friend who loves films too.</li>\n  <li><em>That's all I wanna share with yall.</em> The informal register ('wanna', 'yall') is appropriate for the audience and gives the article a personal, friendly ending.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking devices and paragraph structures that feel natural, not like a numbered list. Your reasons are clear and easy to follow, but the structure relies on <em>My first point</em>, <em>And my second point</em>, and <em>And lastly</em>, which makes the organisation feel a bit mechanical.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>My first point is that it's free.</em> → <strong>First of all, watching at home is free.</strong> Using 'First of all' instead of 'My first point is' sounds more natural in an article.</li>\n  <li><em>And my second point is that you can do anything!</em> → <strong>Another advantage is that you can do anything you want.</strong> Varying your sentence openings keeps the reader interested.</li>\n  <li><em>And lastly, you'll have you own privacy.</em> → <strong>Finally, you have complete privacy.</strong> 'Finally' is a smoother transition than 'And lastly' for the last point in a series.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammar accurately, with only very minor slips. Your vocabulary is strong — words like <em>genre</em>, <em>fantasy</em>, <em>thriller</em>, and <em>privacy</em> show good range. The main pattern to work on is noun forms: countable nouns like 'science fiction' and 'couple' do not add 's' when they are used as modifiers or in fixed expressions.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>science fictions</em> → <strong>science fiction</strong>. When 'science fiction' is used as a genre name, it stays singular. The same applies to 'romantic' — you wrote <em>Except romantic or a love story</em>, which should be <strong>Except romantic films or love stories</strong>.</li>\n  <li><em>a couples of weeks</em> → <strong>a couple of weeks</strong>. 'Couple' is already singular. No 's' needed.</li>\n  <li><em>you'll have you own privacy</em> → <strong>you'll have your own privacy</strong>. 'Your' is the possessive form. 'You' is the subject form. This is a common mix-up that is easy to fix once you check each sentence.</li>\n  <li><em>to here someone baby crying</em> → <strong>to hear someone's baby crying</strong>. 'Here' is a place. 'Hear' is the verb for listening. Also, 'someone baby' needs a possessive apostrophe: 'someone's baby'.</li>\n  <li><em>what ever you want</em> → <strong>whatever you want</strong>. 'Whatever' is one word. Also, <em>wanna share with yall</em> is fine for an informal article, but in more formal writing use <strong>want to share with you all</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can apply to every sentence: Read your sentence aloud slowly. Ask yourself — is every noun in the right form (singular or plural)? Is every 'your' written as 'your' and not 'you'? Are 'here' and 'hear' the correct word? If you check these three things, most of your errors will disappear.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34319	2025-09-13 11:06:07.268193+00	2026	Term 1 Exam Question 1	Clothes and fashion. My friends has a influence on me in fashion she always dresses beatifully and has a own stlye. When I see her wears somethings beatiful I feel inspic because of her it make me cares more about what I wear today and make me confident. Studying. My Mother is a influence a lot of study of me. She tells me work hards is a keys to scuvess and when I feel lazy sometimes I will thinking about my nom adviced and it push me to feel better. Sports and health My friends is influence me of sports. My friends always invivite me to exercies after school. But frist I don't like to doing exercies so much I think is good and make our body healthy but it really lazy. But now I think I like to doing some exercies with my friends it make me feel stord and healty.	152	7.0	Hello Mirin, it's really good to see your effort in tackling this essay question. You've clearly given some thought to the influences on teenagers, which is a commendable start. Your writing would become so much more effective and understandable if you focused on organising your ideas into clear paragraphs and paid close attention to spelling and grammar. Keep working on these areas, and your ability to express yourself clearly will certainly develop further. 😊<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Mirin**<br>✔️ Before you begin writing, plan out your essay's structure, deciding where each new idea will start a new paragraph.<br>✔️ When writing, make sure to use paragraph breaks to separate your different ideas and help the reader follow your thoughts.<br>✔️ Take your time to carefully check your spelling and grammar after you have finished writing, focusing on common errors like subject-verb agreement and word forms.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	Mirin, you did a good job of identifying three areas where teenagers are influenced: clothes and fashion, studying, and your own idea of sports and health. It's great that you included your own thought, which shows you can think broadly about the topic. 👍 To achieve a higher score in content, remember that the essay question asks you to consider *who has more effect* – friends or parents. Your response describes influences, but it doesn't clearly state your opinion on which group has the stronger impact, nor does it compare their effects directly. For example, after discussing friends' influence on fashion, you could have explicitly contrasted it with parental influence, or lack thereof. Make sure to directly address every part of the prompt in your next essay.	3.0	You've shown a good basic ability to communicate straightforward ideas, Mirin, by describing different influences. 😊 However, for an essay at this level, the goal is to develop a clear argument that keeps the reader engaged. Because the central question about *who has more effect* wasn't explicitly answered, the essay doesn't fully achieve its communicative purpose of presenting a well-supported opinion. Think about starting with a clear statement of your main point or opinion in an introductory sentence. This will help you guide your reader and make your overall message much more impactful.	0.0	Mirin, you've shown you have distinct ideas, signalled by your labels like "Clothes and fashion," but presenting your entire response as one long, continuous block of text makes it very difficult for the reader to understand your organisation. There are no paragraph breaks at all, which means the text lacks basic coherence and makes it a challenge to follow your different points. 😔 Imagine reading a book without any spaces between the chapters. To improve this significantly, you must use paragraph breaks to separate each main idea. For instance, when you move from talking about "Clothes and fashion" to "Studying," start a brand-new paragraph. This is a fundamental step in making your writing clear and easy to read. Thai learners sometimes present ideas continuously, but in English academic writing, clear paragraphing is essential.	1.0	You've used some everyday words that relate to the topics, Mirin, and it's clear you have ideas you want to share. However, the frequent spelling and grammatical errors make it quite difficult for the reader to fully grasp what you are trying to say. 😟 For instance, "My friends has a influence" should be "My friends **have an** influence," and "she always dresses beatifully" needs to be "she always dresses **beautifully**." Another example is "it make me cares more about what I wear today," which should correctly be "it **makes** me **care** more about what I wear today." You also had several spelling mistakes like "scuvess" (success), "invivite" (invite), "exercies" (exercises), "frist" (first), "stord" (strong), and "healty" (healthy). These types of errors are common for Thai learners who might sometimes translate ideas directly or not yet be familiar with all English spelling and grammar rules. To improve, focus on checking your sentences for correct verb forms and subject-verb agreement, and always proofread for spelling before you finish writing.	\N	\N	2025-09-13
34927	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy watching Zootopia 1 and Zootopia 2, but mainly I watch Zootopia 2 more often because the quality is much more better and has much more creativity than the first movie. So that leads to where I prefer to watch these films. The answer is I like to watch then at cinima and at home! Because sometimes going out just to watch a movie with other people it just makes me so uncomfortable, but the good part is that in the cinima it has a better quality of the film it self and the sound. As for watch at home. I think you get more privacy and you can watch it where ever you want!	116	15.0	Eri, your article on Zootopia 2 shows you have strong opinions and you know how to support them. You gave clear reasons for preferring the sequel over the first film, and you balanced both sides of the viewing location question. That is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you will need to polish your grammar and sentence structure. Let's look at each subscale so you can see exactly what to improve.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy watching Zootopia 1 and Zootopia 2, but mainly I watch Zootopia 2 more often because the quality is much more better and has much more creativity than the first movie.</em> You clearly stated your preferred film and gave two specific reasons. The reader knows exactly why you choose Zootopia 2.</li><li><em>I like to watch then at cinima and at home!</em> You addressed both viewing locations, which the task asked for. You did not leave the reader guessing.</li><li><em>the good part is that in the cinima it has a better quality of the film it self and the sound</em> and <em>I think you get more privacy and you can watch it where ever you want!</em> You gave balanced reasons for each location. The reader is fully informed about your thinking.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses article conventions smoothly so the reader feels guided, not confused. Your introduction and balanced discussion work well, but one sentence is slightly awkward.</p><ul><li><em>So that leads to where I prefer to watch these films.</em> This sentence tells the reader you are moving to a new topic, but it sounds a little stiff. Try <strong>This brings me to where I prefer to watch them</strong> or <strong>So where do I prefer to watch these films?</strong> The second option is more engaging for an article.</li><li>Your closing sentence could be stronger. <em>As for watch at home. I think you get more privacy and you can watch it where ever you want!</em> This is two fragments, not a complete sentence. Try <strong>As for watching at home, I think you get more privacy and you can watch it wherever you want!</strong> This flows naturally and ends the article cleanly.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses linking words and complete sentences to create smooth transitions between ideas. Your article has a logical order, but two issues slow the reader down.</p><ul><li><em>As for watch at home.</em> This is an incomplete sentence. It should be <strong>As for watching at home,</strong> as part of the next sentence. A fragment like this stops the flow and confuses the reader.</li><li>The transition from cinema to home could be smoother. You wrote <em>the good part is that in the cinima it has a better quality of the film it self and the sound. As for watch at home.</em> Try <strong>On the other hand, watching at home has its own advantages.</strong> This creates a clear contrast and helps the reader follow your thinking.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple and some complex grammar with few errors. Your vocabulary is good, but a few patterns need attention. The main rule to focus on is: after prepositions like 'as for' and 'at', use the -ing form of the verb, not the base form.</p><ul><li><em>As for watch at home</em> → <strong>As for watching at home</strong>. After 'as for', the verb must be in -ing form. This is a fixed pattern.</li><li><em>watch then at cinima</em> → <strong>watch them at the cinema</strong>. 'Then' means 'next' or 'after that'. You meant 'them' (the films). Also, 'cinema' is spelled with an 'e', not an 'i'.</li><li><em>much more better</em> → <strong>much better</strong>. 'Better' is already the comparative form. You do not need 'more' in front of it. 'Much better' is correct.</li><li><em>the film it self</em> → <strong>the film itself</strong>. 'Itself' is one word, not two.</li><li><em>where ever</em> → <strong>wherever</strong>. This is one word, not two.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can apply to any sentence: If you are unsure whether a word is one or two, look it up. For verb forms after prepositions, ask yourself: 'Is there a preposition before this verb?' If yes, use the -ing form.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34929	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I enjoy action films, because it's very fun and exciting to watch, but it may contains some parts that are not for kid for example shooting, fighting and murdering. So you should have parents around you if you want to watch action films, that way little kids won't follow or copy what they saw. I prefer watching at the cinema because they have larger screen and no one would interrupt me, because sometimes when I'm watching a movie at home my mom or my dad will calls me and that really ruined the vibes, and if I go watch the movies at the cinema if I make a mess I don't have to clean it myself, because we can just left after the movies end, and the janiter will clean them because it's their jobs.	140	15.0	Saint, your article shows a real love for action films, and you've given the reader a clear picture of why you prefer the cinema. I especially like how you included a practical warning about age-appropriate content — that shows you were thinking about your audience. The personal detail about your parents calling you at home is exactly the kind of specific example that makes an article feel genuine. To reach Band 5, you'll want to vary your sentence structures and use a wider range of linking words. Let's look at each area one by one.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy action films, because it's very fun and exciting to watch, but it may contains some parts that are not for kid for example shooting, fighting and murdering.</em> You covered both the positive and negative sides of action films, which makes your opinion balanced and complete.</li><li><em>I prefer watching at the cinema because they have larger screen and no one would interrupt me</em> You clearly stated your preference and gave a reason, exactly as the task required.</li><li><em>if I make a mess I don't have to clean it myself, because we can just left after the movies end, and the janiter will clean them because it's their jobs.</em> You added a thoughtful, personal reason that makes the reader understand your choice fully.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would open the article with a hook that grabs the reader's attention immediately. Your first sentence starts a bit flat — it tells us you enjoy action films, but it doesn't invite the reader in.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy action films, because it's very fun and exciting to watch</em> → <strong>Are action films your favourite? Here's why I can't get enough of them.</strong> A question or a bold statement at the start makes the reader want to keep reading.</li><li><em>So you should have parents around you if you want to watch action films</em> → <strong>If you're younger, here's a tip: watch with a parent nearby.</strong> This keeps the informal, helpful tone but sounds more like advice from a friend.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words to connect ideas smoothly. Right now, you rely heavily on 'because' and 'and', which makes the article feel like one long sentence.</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy action films, because it's very fun and exciting to watch, but it may contains some parts that are not for kid for example shooting, fighting and murdering. So you should have parents around you if you want to watch action films</em> → <strong>I enjoy action films because they're exciting. However, some scenes include shooting, fighting, or murdering. For that reason, I recommend watching with a parent nearby.</strong> Using 'however' and 'for that reason' breaks the ideas into clear steps.</li><li><em>I prefer watching at the cinema because they have larger screen and no one would interrupt me, because sometimes when I'm watching a movie at home my mom or my dad will calls me and that really ruined the vibes</em> → <strong>I prefer the cinema for two reasons. First, the screen is larger. Second, no one interrupts me. At home, my parents often call me, which ruins the mood.</strong> 'First' and 'second' help the reader follow your logic.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and uses correct verb forms after modal verbs. The main pattern I see is that after modals like 'may', 'will', 'can', and 'should', the next verb should be in base form, not past or third-person.</p><ul><li><em>it may contains</em> → <strong>it may contain</strong>. After 'may', use the base verb: 'contain', not 'contains'.</li><li><em>will calls me</em> → <strong>will call me</strong>. After 'will', use the base verb: 'call', not 'calls'.</li><li><em>we can just left</em> → <strong>we can just leave</strong>. After 'can', use the base verb: 'leave', not 'left'.</li><li><em>not for kid</em> → <strong>not for kids</strong>. When talking about children in general, use the plural form 'kids'.</li><li><em>the janiter will clean them because it's their jobs</em> → <strong>the janitor will clean it because it's their job</strong>. 'Janitor' is spelled with an 'o', and 'their job' is singular because each janitor has one job.</li></ul><p>Here's a self-check you can use for any sentence: Find the main verb. If there is a modal word like 'may', 'will', 'can', 'should', or 'must' before it, make sure the verb is in base form — no 's', no 'ed', no 'ing'.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34932	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	My favorite films that I enjoy is horror, because I really like when the monster in the movie has scary costumes and when the main character has a survival instinct of a kindergart kid. The thing I mostly like in the horror movie was the moment that the innocent characters get killed by the monster because the monster will be happy and that just make me smile. I prefer watching the movie at home because most of the people at the cinema screams a lot when they saw scary scenes and it's really disappointing when people just shout.	99	15.0	Natee, your article on horror films is genuinely enjoyable to read. You have a clear point of view and you back it up with specific reasons, like the monster's costumes and the way innocent characters get killed. That personal voice is exactly what an article needs. To reach Band 5, you will want to add paragraph breaks so your ideas are easier to follow, and polish a few grammar patterns that can make your writing sound more natural. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>My favorite films that I enjoy is horror</em> You clearly state your preferred genre right at the start. The reader knows exactly what this article is about.</li><li><em>I really like when the monster in the movie has scary costumes</em> You give a specific, visual reason for liking horror. This helps the reader picture what you mean.</li><li><em>I prefer watching the movie at home because most of the people at the cinema screams a lot</em> You explain your viewing preference with a clear, relatable reason. The reader is fully informed.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer opens an article with a hook that grabs the reader's attention immediately. Your opening sentence states your preference, but it does not invite the reader in or create curiosity.</p><ul><li><em>My favorite films that I enjoy is horror</em> → <strong>If you love a good scare, horror is the genre for me.</strong> This version speaks directly to the reader and makes them feel included from the first line.</li><li><em>I really like when the monster in the movie has scary costumes</em> → <strong>There is something thrilling about a monster in a terrifying costume.</strong> Using 'there is something' adds a reflective, article-like tone that engages the reader more than a simple 'I like' statement.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraph breaks to separate different ideas and connects them with a variety of linking words. Your text is one block, and you rely mostly on 'because' and 'and'.</p><ul><li><em>My favorite films that I enjoy is horror, because I really like when the monster... The thing I mostly like in the horror movie was the moment...</em> → Split this into two paragraphs. Paragraph 1: your favourite genre and why you like the monsters. Paragraph 2: the moment innocent characters get killed and why that makes you smile. Each paragraph has one main idea.</li><li><em>I prefer watching the movie at home because most of the people at the cinema screams a lot when they saw scary scenes</em> → This is a new idea (where you watch), so it deserves its own paragraph. Start it with <strong>When it comes to where I watch, I prefer staying home.</strong> This transition signals a shift in topic.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and tense consistency. The main rule to focus on is: the subject and the verb must agree in number. A singular subject takes a singular verb; a plural subject takes a plural verb. Also, keep your tenses consistent within a sentence.</p><ul><li><em>My favorite films that I enjoy is horror</em> → <strong>My favorite film that I enjoy is horror</strong> or <strong>My favorite films that I enjoy are horror</strong>. If 'films' is plural, the verb must be 'are'. If you mean one favourite, use 'film' with 'is'. Decide and be consistent.</li><li><em>the monster will be happy and that just make me smile</em> → <strong>the monster will be happy and that just makes me smile</strong>. The subject 'that' is singular, so the verb needs an -s: 'makes'.</li><li><em>most of the people at the cinema screams a lot when they saw scary scenes</em> → <strong>most of the people at the cinema scream a lot when they see scary scenes</strong>. 'People' is plural, so 'scream' (no -s). Also, keep the tense consistent: 'scream' and 'see' are both present tense.</li><li><em>survival instinct of a kindergart kid</em> → <strong>survival instinct of a kindergarten kid</strong>. 'Kindergarten' is the correct word. This is a small vocabulary fix.</li><li><em>I really like when</em> and <em>The thing I mostly like in the horror movie was</em> → You switch from present tense 'like' to past tense 'was'. Keep it present: <strong>The thing I mostly like in horror movies is the moment...</strong> This keeps your article in a consistent time frame.</li></ul><p>Self-check: Read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Is my subject singular or plural? Does my verb match?' This will catch most agreement errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34933	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	About genre of films I prefer to watch is mystery and fantasy because the mystery it makes me exited everytime I watch it, like Sherlock Hlomes or who kills Alice ,and for fantacy I enjoy to watch because I like to watch something that unnormal, like Harry potter or Howl's moving castal. I prefer to watch them at home more than cinema, because in cinema is to expensive to watch the film just 1-2 hours! but sometim I prefer to watch at cinema tocc. but at home is more comfy because we watch with family ^.	97	15.0	Grace, your article shows a genuine love for films, and you've chosen two specific genres to write about. Your examples — <em>Sherlock Holmes, Harry Potter, Howl's Moving Castle</em> — are excellent because they give the reader a real sense of your taste. You also clearly explained why you prefer watching at home over the cinema, which is exactly what the task asked for. To reach Band 5 in every area, you'll need to focus on organising your ideas into clear paragraphs and polishing your grammar. Let's look at each part of your writing.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>About genre of films I prefer to watch is mystery and fantasy</em> — You clearly stated your two favourite genres right at the start, which gives the article a strong focus.</li>\n  <li><em>like Sherlock Hlomes or who kills Alice</em> — Naming specific films and characters makes your writing personal and interesting. The reader understands exactly what kind of mystery you enjoy.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to watch them at home more than cinema, because in cinema is to expensive</em> — You gave a clear reason for your preference, and you even added a second reason (watching with family). This fully informs the reader.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format to engage the reader from start to finish, with a clear opening and a satisfying closing. Your article shares opinions well, but it begins with <em>About genre of films I prefer to watch</em>, which sounds like a sentence that was cut off. Try a stronger opening that hooks the reader.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>About genre of films I prefer to watch is mystery and fantasy</em> → <strong>My favourite film genres are mystery and fantasy.</strong> This is a cleaner, more confident opening sentence for an article.</li>\n  <li><em>but at home is more comfy because we watch with family ^</em> → <strong>But at home, it's more comfortable because we watch with family.</strong> Ending with a full sentence and a clear thought makes the article feel complete. The caret symbol (^) is not standard punctuation in English articles.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs to separate different ideas and uses a variety of linking words to show how ideas connect. Right now, your whole article is one long paragraph. You already have natural breaks in your thinking: first you talk about genres, then you talk about where to watch. Those are two separate paragraphs.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>About genre of films I prefer to watch is mystery and fantasy... like Harry potter or Howl's moving castal.</em> → <strong>My favourite film genres are mystery and fantasy. I love mystery because it makes me excited every time I watch it, like in Sherlock Holmes. I also enjoy fantasy because I like watching something that is unusual, like Harry Potter or Howl's Moving Castle.</strong> This is your first paragraph — all about genres.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to watch them at home more than cinema... but at home is more comfy because we watch with family ^</em> → <strong>I prefer to watch films at home rather than at the cinema. The cinema is too expensive for just one or two hours of entertainment. At home, it is more comfortable because I can watch with my family.</strong> This is your second paragraph — all about where you watch.</li>\n  <li>You used <em>because</em>, <em>but</em>, and <em>sometim</em> to connect ideas. Try adding <em>however</em>, <em>also</em>, or <em>for example</em> to make your writing flow more smoothly.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and uses a range of vocabulary accurately. Your main grammar issue is that you often leave out the subject or use the wrong structure after <em>because</em>. Let's fix this pattern.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>the mystery it makes me exited</em> → <strong>mystery makes me excited</strong>. You don't need both <em>the mystery</em> and <em>it</em>. Choose one subject. Also, <em>exited</em> means 'left a place' — you want <em>excited</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>something that unnormal</em> → <strong>something that is unusual</strong> or <strong>something unusual</strong>. After <em>that</em>, you need a verb like <em>is</em>. The word <em>unnormal</em> is not standard English; use <em>unusual</em> or <em>not normal</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>in cinema is to expensive</em> → <strong>the cinema is too expensive</strong>. You need the article <em>the</em> before <em>cinema</em>, and <em>to</em> should be <em>too</em> (meaning 'excessively').</li>\n  <li><em>sometim I prefer to watch at cinema tocc</em> → <strong>sometimes I prefer to watch at the cinema too</strong>. <em>Sometim</em> needs an <em>s</em> at the end (<em>sometimes</em>), and <em>tocc</em> should be <em>too</em>.</li>\n  <li>Capitalisation: <em>fantacy</em> should be <em>fantasy</em>, <em>Hlomes</em> should be <em>Holmes</em>, <em>potter</em> should be <em>Potter</em> (proper nouns need capitals). Also, <em>I</em> is always capitalised in English. Check every sentence for these small fixes.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
37120	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I'm enjoying to comedy films. I think it's very funny if you watch it with your friends or your family even you watch it when you're alone. It's give me a positive feelings.<br><br>I prefer to watching them at cinema because, I think when I watch it in big screen it's will be more funny and, I can have delicians popcorn too. I can go to watching them with your family or with your friends, I think it's a good activities to do with someone that love or want to enjoy with.<br><br>When you're watch the movie at the cinema after that you can have a dinner with someone that you watch with.	124	15.0	Nadia, your article on comedy films is genuinely enjoyable to read. You covered every part of the task: you explained why you like comedy films, where you prefer to watch them, and what makes the cinema experience special. Your personal voice comes through clearly, especially when you describe watching with someone you love. To reach Band 5, you'll need to polish your grammar and tighten your sentence structure. The examiner has pointed out specific areas where small changes will make a big difference. Let me walk you through each part of your score.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think it's very funny if you watch it with your friends or your family even you watch it when you're alone.</em> You covered both social and solo viewing, which shows you thought about different situations.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to watching them at cinema because, I think when I watch it in big screen it's will be more funny and, I can have delicians popcorn too.</em> You gave clear reasons for your preference: the big screen, the popcorn, and the experience.</li>\n  <li><em>When you're watch the movie at the cinema after that you can have a dinner with someone that you watch with.</em> You added a practical detail about the social activity after the film, which makes the article feel complete.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer opens with a hook that grabs the reader's attention immediately. Your article is clear and personal, but the first sentence could be stronger.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm enjoying to comedy films.</em> → <strong>What makes comedy films so enjoyable for me is watching them with the right people.</strong> This opening invites the reader in and sets up your main idea more effectively.</li>\n  <li><em>I think it's very funny if you watch it with your friends or your family even you watch it when you're alone.</em> → <strong>Whether I'm with friends, family, or even by myself, a comedy film always lifts my mood.</strong> This version is more direct and confident in tone.</li>\n  <li><em>I can go to watching them with your family or with your friends, I think it's a good activities to do with someone that love or want to enjoy with.</em> → <strong>Going to the cinema with family or friends is a great way to share a laugh together.</strong> This is cleaner and keeps the reader engaged.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraph breaks and connects ideas smoothly without run-on sentences. You do have paragraphs, but some sentences are too long and need better linking.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm enjoying to comedy films. I think it's very funny if you watch it with your friends or your family even you watch it when you're alone.</em> → <strong>I enjoy comedy films. I think they are very funny, whether I watch them with friends, family, or even alone.</strong> Breaking this into two shorter sentences makes it easier to follow.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to watching them at cinema because, I think when I watch it in big screen it's will be more funny and, I can have delicians popcorn too.</em> → <strong>I prefer watching them at the cinema because the big screen makes them funnier, and I can also have delicious popcorn.</strong> Remove the extra commas and keep the sentence flowing naturally.</li>\n  <li><em>When you're watch the movie at the cinema after that you can have a dinner with someone that you watch with.</em> → <strong>After watching a movie at the cinema, you can have dinner with the person you watched it with.</strong> This version is clearer and avoids the repetition of 'watch'.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and vocabulary with few errors. Your main grammar pattern is about verb forms after certain verbs and prepositions. Let's fix that.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your vocabulary choices are good. <em>comedy films</em>, <em>big screen</em>, <em>popcorn</em>, and <em>dinner</em> are all appropriate and clear.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm enjoying to comedy films.</em> → <strong>I enjoy comedy films.</strong> After 'enjoy', use the gerund (enjoy + -ing) or a noun directly. You wrote 'to comedy films' which is incorrect. The correct form is 'I enjoy watching comedy films' or 'I enjoy comedy films'.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to watching them at cinema</em> → <strong>I prefer watching them at the cinema</strong> or <strong>I prefer to watch them at the cinema.</strong> After 'prefer', you can use either 'prefer + -ing' or 'prefer + to + verb', but NOT 'prefer to + -ing'. Also, 'at cinema' needs the article: 'at the cinema'.</li>\n  <li><em>It's give me a positive feelings</em> → <strong>It gives me a positive feeling.</strong> 'It's' means 'it is' or 'it has', but here you need the simple present 'it gives'. Also, 'feelings' should be singular 'feeling' after 'a'.</li>\n  <li><em>it's will be more funny</em> → <strong>it will be funnier.</strong> 'It's will' is incorrect because 'it's' already contains the verb 'is'. Just use 'it will'. Also, 'more funny' should be 'funnier' for one-syllable adjectives.</li>\n  <li><em>delicians popcorn</em> → <strong>delicious popcorn.</strong> 'Delicians' is not a word. The correct spelling is 'delicious'.</li>\n  <li><em>with someone that love</em> → <strong>with someone who loves.</strong> Use 'who' for people, not 'that'. Also, 'love' needs an 's' because 'someone' is third person singular.</li>\n  <li><strong>Self-check:</strong> After every sentence, ask yourself: 'Is the verb form correct? Did I use the right preposition? Did I add 's' for he/she/it?'</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34091	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy!!<br><br>I'm also excited! really looking forward to see you!!<br><br>On my first evening there I would prefer to relax at home I don't like loud music it give me migrants.<br><br>No, I don't like moving alot I just want to hangout and relax with you at home.<br><br>Also what time should I arrive and should I have breakfast first ? Let me know<br><br>looking forward to see you<br>Tanya	71	13.0	Tanya, your email to Sandy is full of genuine excitement and warmth. I can hear your voice clearly in lines like <em>I'm also excited! really looking forward to see you!!</em> and <em>I just want to hangout and relax with you at home</em>. You answered all three parts of the task, which is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. To reach that top score, focus on two things: making your grammar more accurate, especially with verb forms and word choice, and smoothing out the transitions between your ideas so the email flows naturally from one topic to the next. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all required information clearly and completely. You covered every point from the task — excitement, preference for relaxing at home, reason for not climbing, and a question about arrival time and breakfast. That's strong. The only gap is that your reason for not climbing is slightly unclear.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I don't like moving alot</em> → <strong>I don't enjoy moving around very much</strong>. The reader might wonder what you mean by 'moving alot.' Try a clearer phrase like 'I prefer to stay in one place' or 'I'm not very active.'</li>\n  <li><em>it give me migrants</em> → <strong>it gives me migraines</strong>. This is a vocabulary issue (see Language section), but it also affects content clarity. The reader can guess your meaning, but a Band 5 writer makes every idea crystal clear.</li>\n  <li>Your question <em>Also what time should I arrive and should I have breakfast first ?</em> is excellent. It shows you're thinking about the practical details of the visit, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone and register consistently throughout the email. Your email to Sandy has a friendly, enthusiastic feel with exclamation marks and informal language like <em>hangout</em> and <em>Let me know</em>. The opening and closing are suitable for a friend. However, the register slips in a few places.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>it give me migrants</em> → <strong>it gives me migraines</strong>. This phrase is confusing for the reader. Even in an informal email to a friend, your words need to be clear. A Band 5 writer would choose a word the reader can understand immediately.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm also excited! really looking forward to see you!!</em> → <strong>I'm also excited! I'm really looking forward to seeing you!</strong> The second part is a sentence fragment. In an email to a friend, fragments can work for emphasis, but here it reads like an unfinished thought. Joining it to the first sentence or completing it makes your meaning stronger.</li>\n  <li>Your closing <em>looking forward to see you</em> is friendly, but adding your name after it (as you did with 'Tanya') is perfect for an informal email. Keep that.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never feels a jump between topics. Your email has a logical sequence: excitement, first evening preference, climbing refusal, question. That's a good start. But the transitions between these ideas are abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>No, I don't like moving alot I just want to hangout and relax with you at home.</em> → <strong>No, I don't enjoy moving around much. I'd rather just hang out and relax with you at home.</strong> Starting with 'No' works for a friend, but the jump from 'climbing refusal' to 'question' in the next paragraph is sudden. Add a short bridge like 'By the way,' or 'Also,' before your question to make the shift feel natural.</li>\n  <li><em>Also what time should I arrive and should I have breakfast first ?</em> The word 'Also' is doing good work as a connector here. For a Band 5, you could add one more linking word earlier in the email, like 'Actually' or 'To be honest,' before stating your preference for relaxing at home.</li>\n  <li>Your paragraphs are separated with line breaks, which helps the reader. Keep that structure.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and vocabulary with errors that don't distract the reader. You use good everyday words and your meaning is always clear. A few patterns need attention.</p><ul><li><em>it give me migrants</em> → <strong>it gives me migraines</strong>. With 'it', the verb needs an 's' (it gives, it hurts). Also 'migrants' is very close to the word you want, which is 'migraines' — a type of headache.</li><li><em>moving alot</em> → <strong>moving a lot</strong>. 'Alot' is two words: 'a lot'. This is a common spelling mistake — write it as two words every time.</li><li><em>looking forward to see you</em> → <strong>looking forward to seeing you</strong>. After 'to' in 'look forward to', the verb takes -ing form: to seeing, to meeting, to hearing.</li><li>Self-check: After you finish writing, read every sentence aloud. If a sentence sounds incomplete or missing a word (like 'really looking forward'), add the missing subject ('I'm'). This will fix most of your sentence fragments.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34091	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	I love action film move specific the one with cool gadget like Kingmans a Mission Impossible. I find those film very interesting with bunch of action scene and using very cool gadget. Between watching film at a cinema and at home I would choose at home while it's true that watching at the cinema are more realistic with big screen and speaker all around us, but I prefer the comfyness at home with fun drink and snack I can choose not just popcorn, and more importantly the spot I can watch anywhere wherever. It's my bed in the bedroom or on the sofa in the living room.	111	14.0	Tanya, your article shows you know exactly what you want to say. You picked a clear favourite genre — action films with cool gadgets — and you gave real examples like <em>Kingsman</em> and <em>Mission: Impossible</em> that make your opinion concrete. Your comparison between cinema and home is thoughtful, especially the point about being able to choose your own snacks instead of just popcorn. That personal detail makes your writing stand out. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll need to tighten your grammar and make your sentences complete and correct. The ideas are already strong. Now it's about polishing the language so every sentence is as clear as your thinking.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I love action film move specific the one with cool gadget like Kingmans a Mission Impossible.</em> You named two real films as examples, which shows the reader exactly what kind of action movies you mean. This is excellent for content.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer the comfyness at home with fun drink and snack I can choose not just popcorn.</em> You gave a specific, personal reason for your preference. The reader understands your choice completely.</li>\n  <li><em>more importantly the spot I can watch anywhere wherever. It's my bed in the bedroom or on the sofa in the living room.</em> You added a final, relatable detail about where you actually watch. This fully informs the reader about your experience.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an article to engage the reader from start to finish. Your article has a clear opinion and addresses the reader, but the opening could grab attention more strongly, and the ending could feel more complete.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I love action film move specific the one with cool gadget like Kingmans a Mission Impossible.</em> → <strong>Do you love action films? I do — especially the ones with cool gadgets like Kingsman and Mission: Impossible.</strong> Starting with a question pulls the reader in and makes them feel part of the conversation.</li>\n  <li><em>It's my bed in the bedroom or on the sofa in the living room.</em> → <strong>That spot might be my bed, or the sofa in the living room — anywhere comfortable works.</strong> A complete final sentence gives the article a proper ending rather than trailing off.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly from beginning to end, with a clear introduction, body, and conclusion. Your ideas are in a logical order — genre first, then cinema vs. home — but the last sentence is incomplete, which makes the ending feel abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>It's my bed in the bedroom or on the sofa in the living room.</em> This sentence has no main verb that connects it to the previous idea. → <strong>I can watch in my bed, on the sofa, or anywhere else I feel comfortable.</strong> Now the sentence is complete and links back to your point about flexibility.</li>\n  <li>Your article moves from genre preference to the cinema-vs-home comparison, which is a clear sequence. To strengthen the organisation further, add a short concluding sentence that wraps up your overall feeling, such as <strong>For me, home is the best place to enjoy an action film.</strong></li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar so the reader never has to pause and guess the meaning. Your errors are fairly frequent and sometimes make sentences hard to follow. The main patterns to fix are: missing plural -s, missing articles (a, an, the), and subject-verb agreement.</p><ul><li><em>action film move specific the one with cool gadget</em> → <strong>action films, especially the ones with cool gadgets</strong>. Both 'film' and 'gadget' need plural -s. 'Move specific' is not a phrase — you meant 'especially'.</li><li><em>those film</em> → <strong>those films</strong>. After 'those', the noun must be plural. This is a fixed rule.</li><li><em>with bunch of action scene</em> → <strong>with a bunch of action scenes</strong>. Missing 'a' before 'bunch', and 'scene' needs plural -s.</li><li><em>watching at the cinema are more realistic</em> → <strong>watching at the cinema is more realistic</strong>. The subject 'watching' is singular, so the verb must be 'is', not 'are'.</li><li><em>the comfyness at home</em> → <strong>the comfort at home</strong>. 'Comfyness' is not a standard English word. Use 'comfort'.</li><li><em>the spot I can watch anywhere wherever</em> → <strong>I can watch anywhere I want</strong>. Using both 'anywhere' and 'wherever' together is redundant. Choose one.</li><li>Self-check: For every noun in your next draft, ask yourself: does this need 'a/an/the', a plural -s, or 'my/this/those'? If you cannot answer yes, the sentence is probably missing something.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29844	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hello, Sandy!<br><br>Not only you that were excited, me too!<br><br>For me, I prefer to go on a concert, so we can't hang out together and enjoy the show. And after the concert i think we should go grab something to feed our stomach before we arrived home.<br><br>About the mountains activity, I rather choose to stay at home because my both legs haven't recovered yet, and I'm scared of climbing, so can we get another activity please?<br><br>If you don't mind, can we change from climbing in the mountains to playing games or try watching movies together?<br><br>Can't wait!	101	15.0	Ome, your email to Sandy is full of energy and genuine excitement. You covered every part of the task thoroughly, and your enthusiasm comes through clearly in lines like <em>Can't wait!</em> and <em>Hello, Sandy!</em>. The reader can feel how much you're looking forward to this visit. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll want to polish your grammar and sentence structure so your great ideas are delivered as cleanly as possible. Let's look at each skill area now.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Not only you that were excited, me too!</em> You immediately show Sandy that you share her excitement, which is a warm and personal way to begin.</li>\n  <li><em>I prefer to go on a concert</em> and <em>I rather choose to stay at home because my both legs haven't recovered yet, and I'm scared of climbing</em> You clearly state your preference and give two honest reasons, fully informing the reader.</li>\n  <li><em>can we change from climbing in the mountains to playing games or try watching movies together?</em> You suggest two specific alternatives, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would use consistently natural email language throughout, with no awkward phrasing that slows the reader down. Your tone is friendly and your opening and closing are perfect, but a few sentences feel slightly off.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Not only you that were excited, me too!</em> → <strong>I wasn't the only one who was excited — me too!</strong> or <strong>You're not the only one who's excited — I am too!</strong>. The word order was a little confusing, but your meaning is clear.</li>\n  <li><em>so we can't hang out together and enjoy the show</em> → <strong>so we can hang out together and enjoy the show</strong>. You wrote 'can't' but your meaning is 'can' — you want to go to the concert with Sandy, not avoid her.</li>\n  <li><em>I rather choose to stay at home</em> → <strong>I would rather stay at home</strong>. 'Rather' needs 'would' before it in this structure.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would use a wider variety of linking words and clearer paragraph structure to guide the reader smoothly. Your email has a logical sequence, but it relies heavily on 'so', 'and', and 'because'.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>For me, I prefer to go on a concert, so we can't hang out together and enjoy the show. And after the concert i think we should go grab something to feed our stomach before we arrived home.</em> → Try breaking this into two sentences: <strong>For me, I prefer to go to the concert, so we can hang out together and enjoy the show. After the concert, I think we should grab something to eat before we go home.</strong> Using 'after' as a time linker works well.</li>\n  <li><em>About the mountains activity, I rather choose to stay at home because my both legs haven't recovered yet, and I'm scared of climbing, so can we get another activity please?</em> → This sentence has three ideas. Try: <strong>About the mountain activity, I would rather stay at home because my legs haven't recovered yet and I'm scared of climbing. So can we do another activity instead?</strong> Separating the reason from the request makes it easier to read.</li>\n  <li>Your paragraphs are well-organised: each one covers one main idea (excitement, concert plan, climbing problem, alternative suggestion). That's a strength to keep.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control basic grammar structures consistently and use a wider range of vocabulary accurately. Your vocabulary is good ('recovered', 'scared of climbing', 'playing games', 'watching movies'), but several grammar patterns need attention. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>after 'before' and 'after', use the base form of the verb, not past tense, when talking about future plans.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>before we arrived home</em> → <strong>before we go home</strong>. You're talking about a future plan, so use present tense after 'before'.</li>\n  <li><em>my both legs haven't recovered yet</em> → <strong>both my legs haven't recovered yet</strong>. 'Both' comes before 'my' in English.</li>\n  <li><em>I rather choose to stay at home</em> → <strong>I would rather stay at home</strong> or <strong>I prefer to stay at home</strong>. 'Rather' needs 'would', and 'choose' is unnecessary with 'rather'.</li>\n  <li><em>Not only you that were excited, me too!</em> → <strong>I wasn't the only one who was excited — me too!</strong> The 'not only...but also' structure needs a full clause. Your version skipped the subject and verb.</li>\n  <li>Also check capitalisation: <em>i think</em> should be <strong>I think</strong>. The pronoun 'I' is always capitalised.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check for any sentence: If you use 'before' or 'after' to talk about a future plan, ask yourself 'Is this a past event or a future plan?' If it's future, use present tense after the time word.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29844	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		I usually enjoyed the Sci-fi films than the another, because sci-fi films always give me a knowledge about space or science, which is sometime I even use these knowledge to fix my problem in daily life.<br><br>I prefer watching films at the home than the cinema, because it more cheap than the cinema and the best part is you can choose where to sit or even pause the films or be able to use mobile devices for myself, and I can set the quality of the films I want or choose the films I want to watch, even set the volume for myself.	102	15.0	Ome, your article shows you have a genuine interest in sci-fi films, and you used that personal knowledge to write a convincing response. Your reasons for preferring home viewing are practical and easy to understand. To reach Band 5, you'll need to tighten your grammar and organise your ideas more clearly so each point stands out. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>sci-fi films always give me a knowledge about space or science</em> You directly answered the first question from the notice by naming your preferred genre and explaining why it interests you.</li><li><em>I prefer watching films at the home than the cinema, because it more cheap than the cinema</em> You clearly stated your preference for watching at home and gave a practical reason. The reader understands your position completely.</li><li><em>you can choose where to sit or even pause the films</em> You added extra details about home viewing that make your argument convincing. The target reader is fully informed.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would open their article with a hook that grabs the reader's attention and makes them want to keep reading. Your first sentence starts with your preference, which is clear but not especially engaging.</p><ul><li><em>I usually enjoyed the Sci-fi films than the another</em> → <strong>If you ask me, nothing beats a good sci-fi film.</strong> A stronger opening like this makes the reader curious about your opinion.</li><li><em>I prefer watching films at the home than the cinema</em> You successfully communicated your second point, and the reader follows your reasoning. This is good article-style writing.</li><li><em>the best part is you can choose where to sit or even pause the films</em> You used a conversational tone that suits an article well. Keep using phrases like 'the best part is' to sound natural.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would break their second paragraph into smaller chunks so each reason gets its own moment to shine. Right now, your second paragraph lists five reasons in one long sentence, which makes it hard for the reader to remember each point.</p><ul><li><em>because it more cheap than the cinema and the best part is you can choose where to sit or even pause the films or be able to use mobile devices for myself, and I can set the quality of the films I want or choose the films I want to watch, even set the volume for myself.</em> → <strong>First, it is cheaper than the cinema. Second, you can choose where to sit or pause the film. Third, you can use your mobile devices and set the volume yourself.</strong> Using 'First, Second, Third' helps the reader follow each reason clearly.</li><li>Your two-paragraph structure is correct: one paragraph for genre preference and one for viewing location. Keep this clear separation.</li><li>The word 'even' appears twice in your second paragraph. Try using 'also' or 'in addition' for variety.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control present tense consistently and use correct comparative forms. Your main grammar challenge is mixing past tense with present tense when describing your general preferences.</p><ul><li><em>I usually enjoyed the Sci-fi films</em> → <strong>I usually enjoy sci-fi films.</strong> When you talk about something you generally like, use present simple 'enjoy', not past tense 'enjoyed'.</li><li><em>sci-fi films always give me a knowledge</em> → <strong>sci-fi films always give me knowledge.</strong> 'Knowledge' is uncountable, so do not use 'a' before it. Also, 'knowledge' is always singular.</li><li><em>it more cheap than the cinema</em> → <strong>it is cheaper than the cinema.</strong> For one-syllable adjectives like 'cheap', add '-er' to form the comparative. You also need the verb 'is'.</li><li><em>be able to use mobile devices for myself</em> → <strong>use my mobile devices.</strong> 'For myself' is unnecessary here. The meaning is clear without it.</li><li>Capitalisation: 'Sci-fi' should be 'sci-fi' (lowercase s) in the middle of a sentence. Only proper nouns like 'Sci-Fi' as a genre name at the start of a sentence need a capital.</li></ul><p>Self-check: Before you finish a sentence, ask yourself 'Is this a general fact about my preferences?' If yes, use present simple tense. Then check if you need 'a' before the noun — uncountable nouns like 'knowledge', 'information', 'advice' never take 'a'.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30321	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi, Corey<br><br>That is great to go to the science festival this Saturday! because I think that is interesting . OMG I want to go now! What do you think!<br><br>You can come to pick me because I never go there, so I think if we meet at the front entrance I dont know how to go that way, but if you cant come to pick me that time tell me I will tell my mom to pick me to go thant<br><br>Sorry, but I want to build a robot because I think it's will be fun . Oh, I forget I think we should bring some candy too when do something<br><br>See you later, bye . From Alin	125	11.0	Alin, your email to Corey is full of energy and real excitement. You wrote <em>OMG I want to go now!</em> and that enthusiasm makes the reader feel like you truly can't wait for the science festival. You also did a great job covering almost everything the task asked for: you expressed excitement, asked to be picked up, disagreed with the film idea by saying you want to build a robot, and suggested bringing candy. To reach Band 5 next time, you'll need to organise your ideas more clearly so each paragraph has one main point, and you'll need to check your grammar more carefully. Let's look at each part of your writing to see exactly how to improve.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes every required point clearly and completely. You covered excitement, transport, the activity, and candy, but you didn't clearly state whether you prefer to meet at the front entrance or be picked up. Your request to be picked up implies a choice, but the reader has to guess.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>You can come to pick me because I never go there, so I think if we meet at the front entrance I dont know how to go that way</em> → <strong>I would prefer if you could pick me up, because I don't know how to get to the front entrance by myself.</strong> This makes your choice clear and gives a reason.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should bring some candy too when do something</em> → <strong>I think we should also bring some candy to share while we explore.</strong> Adding a clear reason or detail makes the suggestion complete.</li>\n  <li>You included everything else perfectly. Just make your choice about transport explicit next time.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone and format for an email to a friend without confusing the reader. Your opening <em>Hi, Corey</em> and closing <em>See you later, bye . From Alin</em> are exactly right for a friend. The tone is friendly and excited. However, some sentences are phrased in a way that makes the reader pause to understand your meaning.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Sorry, but I want to build a robot</em> → <strong>Actually, I'd rather build a robot.</strong> 'Sorry, but' sounds like you're apologising for disagreeing, which is fine, but 'Actually, I'd rather' keeps the friendly tone while being clearer.</li>\n  <li><em>I dont know how to go that way, but if you cant come to pick me that time tell me I will tell my mom to pick me to go thant</em> → <strong>If you can't pick me up, please let me know and I'll ask my mom to take me there instead.</strong> This is easier for the reader to follow because the ideas are connected logically.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer organises ideas into clear paragraphs, each with one main point, and uses linking words to connect them smoothly. Your email has paragraphs, but the second paragraph mixes two ideas: being picked up and meeting at the entrance. The third paragraph jumps from building a robot to bringing candy without a transition.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>You can come to pick me because I never go there, so I think if we meet at the front entrance I dont know how to go that way, but if you cant come to pick me that time tell me I will tell my mom to pick me to go thant</em> → <strong>Could you pick me up? I don't know how to get to the festival by myself. If you can't, please tell me and I'll ask my mom to take me.</strong> This separates the request from the alternative plan into clear, short sentences.</li>\n  <li><em>Sorry, but I want to build a robot because I think it's will be fun . Oh, I forget I think we should bring some candy too when do something</em> → <strong>I'd like to build a robot instead. I think it will be fun! Also, should we bring some candy to share?</strong> Use 'Also' or 'Another idea' to move from one topic to the next.</li>\n  <li>You used 'because' and 'but' well. Next time, try 'First', 'Also', 'Finally' to guide the reader through your email.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar and vocabulary correctly, with few errors. Your vocabulary is fine for the task, but there is one main grammar rule that explains most of your errors: <strong>modal verbs like 'will' and 'can' are followed by the base form of the verb, not 'to' + verb or 'is' + verb.</strong> Let's fix that pattern and a few other small issues.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think it's will be fun</em> → <strong>I think it will be fun.</strong> After 'will', use the base verb (be). 'It's' means 'it is', so 'it's will' is like saying 'it is will'. Just say 'it will'.</li>\n  <li><em>I dont know</em> and <em>you cant</em> → <strong>I don't know</strong> and <strong>you can't</strong>. The apostrophe (') shows where letters are missing. In 'don't', the apostrophe replaces the missing 'o' from 'do not'. Always add the apostrophe in negative contractions.</li>\n  <li><em>pick me to go thant</em> → <strong>pick me up to go there.</strong> 'Thant' is likely a typo for 'there'. 'Pick me' needs 'up' to mean 'collect someone in a car'.</li>\n  <li><em>when do something</em> → <strong>while we do something</strong> or <strong>while we explore.</strong> 'When' is for a specific time; 'while' means 'during the activity'.</li>\n  <li>Capitalisation check: You correctly capitalised 'I', but 'OMG' is fine in informal writing. Watch for random capitals on common nouns like 'Science' in the task — only proper nouns need capitals. A good self-check: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Does the verb after 'will' have 'to' or 'is' in front of it?' If yes, remove them.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30399	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hey!<br><br>That's great! I'm looking forward to it<br><br>It would've been nice if we could meet at the front entrance, because I have things to take care of before entering the festival.<br><br>I think for the two main activities we should build a robot, it's far more interesting than watching a film. Think about it. We could see the robot that we created comes to life, wouldn't that be better than watching a film?<br><br>Yeah, I will be sure to bring money. As for food, I would recommend to bring your own, why? Because it's much more expensive there, and the line must've been long too.<br><br>Bye.	114	18.0	Mick, your email to Corey about the festival is excellent. You handled every part of the task with confidence and a natural, friendly voice. Your opening line <em>I'm looking forward to it</em> immediately sets a positive tone, and your argument for building a robot instead of watching a film is genuinely persuasive. To reach Band 5 in Organisation and Language, you can tighten a few small things. Let's look at each area.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I'm looking forward to it</em> You express excitement about the festival clearly and naturally.</li><li><em>It would've been nice if we could meet at the front entrance, because I have things to take care of before entering the festival.</em> You state your preference for a meeting point and give a clear reason.</li><li><em>I think for the two main activities we should build a robot, it's far more interesting than watching a film.</em> You disagree with Corey's film idea and offer a strong alternative with a reason.</li><li><em>As for food, I would recommend to bring your own, why? Because it's much more expensive there</em> You suggest bringing food and explain why, covering the final note completely.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Hey!</em> and <em>Bye.</em> Your opening and closing are perfectly casual for a friend.</li><li><em>Think about it. We could see the robot that we created comes to life, wouldn't that be better than watching a film?</em> You use a rhetorical question to engage Corey directly, which makes the email feel like a real conversation.</li><li>The overall tone is enthusiastic and personal throughout, exactly right for an email to a friend.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer connects their ideas with a wider variety of linking words and smoother transitions. Your email is logical and each paragraph covers one point, but you can make it flow even better.</p><ul><li><em>I think for the two main activities we should build a robot, it's far more interesting than watching a film.</em> → <strong>I think for the two main activities, we should build a robot. It's far more interesting than watching a film.</strong> Splitting this into two sentences makes your argument clearer and easier to follow.</li><li><em>Yeah, I will be sure to bring money. As for food, I would recommend to bring your own, why? Because it's much more expensive there, and the line must've been long too.</em> → <strong>I'll be sure to bring money. As for food, I'd recommend bringing your own, because it's much more expensive there and the lines are probably long too.</strong> Using <em>because</em> instead of the question-answer structure keeps the paragraph flowing naturally.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer uses consistent grammar and a wider range of sentence structures with few errors. Your vocabulary is strong, and you attempt complex forms. A small pattern to fix involves verb forms after certain expressions.</p><ul><li><em>We could see the robot that we created comes to life</em> → <strong>We could see the robot that we created come to life.</strong> After verbs like <em>see</em>, <em>watch</em>, or <em>hear</em>, use the base form of the verb (without 's').</li><li><em>I would recommend to bring your own</em> → <strong>I would recommend bringing your own.</strong> After <em>recommend</em>, use the -ing form of the verb, not 'to + verb'.</li><li><em>the line must've been long too</em> → <strong>the lines are probably long too</strong> or <strong>the line is probably long too</strong>. <em>Must have been</em> is for guessing about a past event, but here you are talking about a general situation at the festival. Use <em>probably</em> + present tense for a more natural prediction.</li><li>Your vocabulary choices are excellent: <em>looking forward to</em>, <em>take care of</em>, <em>comes to life</em>, <em>expensive</em>. These show a good range of everyday words used correctly.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30444	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>Great job about the ticket, it's the festival I've planned to go for a very long time.<br><br>And your cousin can pick me up on the way there. I'll be waiting at the shopping mall, so your cousin won't need to drive back and forth.<br><br>For the activity, I think building a robot is more interesting. Watching the film looks fun, but it's uninteractive, so it can get boring.<br><br>Okay, I'll won't forget the money. About the things we should bring, we should bring some shacks or chocolate bar, or even lunch pack.<br><br>See you very soon!<br><br>Arlong	94	15.0	Arlong, your email to Corey is genuinely warm and clear. You addressed every single note from the task, which is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. I especially like how you gave a specific meeting point at the shopping mall to save your cousin from driving back and forth. That kind of thoughtful detail makes your writing stand out. To reach Band 5 across all areas, we need to polish your grammar and make your paragraphs flow more naturally from one idea to the next. Let me show you exactly how.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Great job about the ticket, it's the festival I've planned to go for a very long time.</em> You expressed a positive reaction and showed how much this event means to you. The reader fully understands your excitement.</li><li><em>I'll be waiting at the shopping mall, so your cousin won't need to drive back and forth.</em> You stated your preference for being picked up and gave a specific, helpful location. This is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>I think building a robot is more interesting. Watching the film looks fun, but it's uninteractive, so it can get boring.</em> You disagreed with Corey's suggestion and gave a clear reason. The reader knows your opinion and why you hold it.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses consistently natural, idiomatic language that a friend would actually write in an email. Your tone is friendly and enthusiastic, which is great, but a few word choices sound slightly off. Let's polish those.</p><ul><li><em>it's uninteractive</em> → <strong>it's not very interactive</strong>. 'Uninteractive' is not a common word in English. Saying 'not very interactive' sounds more natural and still makes your point clearly.</li><li><em>Great job about the ticket</em> → <strong>Great news about the ticket</strong>. 'Great job' is for praising someone's effort. 'Great news' is what you say when you receive good news from a friend. This small change makes your opening sound exactly right.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer links their paragraphs so the reader glides smoothly from one idea to the next. Right now, each paragraph starts with a new point, but the connections between them feel a bit mechanical. Let's add some smoother transitions.</p><ul><li><em>Hi Corey! Great job about the ticket... And your cousin can pick me up...</em> → <strong>Hi Corey! Great news about the ticket... Also, your cousin can pick me up...</strong> Using 'Also' instead of 'And' at the start of a paragraph creates a stronger link back to the previous idea.</li><li><em>For the activity, I think building a robot is more interesting.</em> → <strong>As for the activity, I think building a robot is more interesting.</strong> Adding 'As for' signals a clear topic shift while keeping the flow natural.</li><li><em>Okay, I'll won't forget the money. About the things we should bring...</em> → <strong>Don't worry, I won't forget the money. About what we should bring...</strong> 'Don't worry' connects back to Corey's concern more naturally than 'Okay', and 'About what we should bring' sounds smoother than 'About the things we should bring'.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls grammar and vocabulary so well that errors do not distract the reader. You have a strong base, but a few patterns need attention. Let's focus on the main rule: <strong>never use two modals (like 'will' and 'won't') together in one verb phrase</strong>.</p><ul><li><em>I'll won't forget</em> → <strong>I won't forget</strong>. 'Will' and 'won't' are both modal verbs. You can only use one per verb phrase. 'I'll' is short for 'I will', so 'I'll won't' is like saying 'I will will not'. Just say 'I won't forget' and you're correct.</li><li><em>shacks</em> → <strong>snacks</strong>. This is a spelling error. 'Shacks' are small buildings. 'Snacks' are small foods. Read your writing aloud before submitting to catch these.</li><li><em>lunch pack</em> → <strong>packed lunch</strong> or <strong>lunchbox</strong>. In English, we say 'packed lunch' (a lunch you prepare at home and bring) or 'lunchbox' (the container). 'Lunch pack' is not a standard phrase.</li><li><em>chocolate bar</em> → <strong>chocolate bars</strong>. You used 'some' before it, which means you need a plural noun. 'Some chocolate bars' is correct.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: <strong>Read your sentence aloud. If you hear two helping verbs like 'will' and 'won't' together, remove one. If a noun follows 'some' or 'a few', make sure it is plural.</strong></p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30570	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>Thanks for your email and thank you for inviting me.<br><br>I think that we can meet at the front entrance, and we should build a robot because it is more interesting, you can do anything with the robot, but the film is kinda boring for me. But I can do anything we can just watch the film if you want.<br><br>I won't forget my pocket money. I think we should bring some food, snacks and drinks because there's no restaurant in the festival. I will bring it for you since you get the ticket for me.<br><br>Bye,<br>Atan	111	15.0	Atan, your email to Corey is clear, friendly, and covers every single point the task asked for. You thanked him for the invitation, suggested a meeting place, gave your opinion on the film versus building a robot, and even thought about bringing food and pocket money. That is excellent planning. The one area where you can grow is in making your sentences flow more smoothly and using a wider range of language. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how you can push every section to Band 5.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thanks for your email and thank you for inviting me.</em> You immediately showed a positive reaction and acknowledged the invitation, which is exactly what the task required.</li>\n  <li><em>I think that we can meet at the front entrance, and we should build a robot because it is more interesting</em> You clearly stated your disagreement with Corey's film idea and gave a reason, fully informing the reader.</li>\n  <li><em>I won't forget my pocket money. I think we should bring some food, snacks and drinks because there's no restaurant in the festival.</em> You addressed what to bring and explained why, showing you thought about the practical needs of the day.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistently appropriate and natural tone throughout the whole email, without any slips into language that feels too casual or slightly off for the situation.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>kinda boring</em> → <strong>a bit boring</strong> or <strong>not very interesting to me</strong>. 'Kinda' is very informal and can sound a little too casual for a school email, even to a friend. Choosing 'a bit' or 'not very' keeps your friendly tone while sounding more polished.</li>\n  <li><em>But I can do anything we can just watch the film if you want.</em> → <strong>But we can just watch the film if you prefer.</strong> This sentence is a bit confusing because 'I can do anything' doesn't fit naturally here. Simplifying it makes your meaning clearer and the tone more natural.</li>\n  <li><em>I will bring it for you since you get the ticket for me.</em> → <strong>I'll bring some for you since you got the ticket for me.</strong> Using 'got' (past tense) and 'some' (instead of 'it') makes the sentence sound more natural and friendly in an email.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words and phrases to connect ideas smoothly, not just 'and', 'but', and 'because'. Your email is logically ordered, but the connections between sentences can feel repetitive.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think that we can meet at the front entrance, and we should build a robot because it is more interesting, you can do anything with the robot, but the film is kinda boring for me. But I can do anything we can just watch the film if you want.</em> → <strong>I think we should meet at the front entrance. As for what to do, I'd rather build a robot because it's more interesting. You can do so many things with a robot. The film sounds a bit boring to me, but we can watch it if you prefer.</strong> Breaking this long sentence into shorter ones and using phrases like 'as for what to do' and 'sounds' adds variety and makes the email easier to read.</li>\n  <li>You started two sentences in a row with 'But'. Try using 'However' or 'On the other hand' for the second one to show contrast in a more sophisticated way.</li>\n  <li>Your email moves from greeting to meeting point to activity choice to what to bring. This is a logical sequence. To make it feel even more connected, you could add a short transition like 'After we decide what to do, let's talk about what to bring.'</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls a range of grammar and vocabulary accurately, with only very minor slips. Your vocabulary is good ('front entrance', 'build a robot', 'pocket money'), and your basic grammar is mostly correct. The main rule to focus on is <strong>subject-verb agreement with 'there is/are' and choosing the correct verb tense (present vs. past) when talking about completed actions.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>there's no restaurant in the festival</em> → <strong>there are no restaurants in the festival</strong> or <strong>there isn't a restaurant in the festival</strong>. 'Festival' is a place, not a single building, so it's better to use the plural 'restaurants' or 'a restaurant'. This is a common pattern: with 'there is/are', match the verb to the noun that follows.</li>\n  <li><em>since you get the ticket for me</em> → <strong>since you got the ticket for me</strong> or <strong>since you bought the ticket for me</strong>. Corey already got the ticket, so use the past tense 'got' or 'bought'. This is a key rule: use past tense for actions that are already finished.</li>\n  <li><em>I will bring it for you</em> → <strong>I will bring some for you</strong>. 'It' refers to one thing, but you mentioned 'food, snacks and drinks'. Use 'some' to refer to the group of items you listed.</li>\n  <li><em>you can do anything with the robot</em> → <strong>you can do a lot of things with a robot</strong> or <strong>you can do so many things with a robot</strong>. 'Anything' means 'any single thing', which sounds a bit extreme here. 'A lot of things' or 'so many things' is more natural for expressing possibilities.</li>\n  <li>Self-check for next time: After you write a sentence with 'there is/are', check if the noun after it is singular or plural. After you write about something that happened before the email, check that your verb is in the past tense.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30584	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hello<br><br>Thanks for your email, and I wish you doing well. I think that's a good idea to go to the science festival with you.<br><br>We should meet at the front entrance because my mom will take me to the science festival by car, so you can go with your cousin.<br><br>I think build a robot is more interesting for me. Because I want to improve my robot skill, but watch the Discovering Dinosaurs is a great idea too. If you want to go to watch the Discovering Dinosaurs, I can go with you.<br><br>We should bring hat or water, because I worry about the weather will be hot or rain when we go.<br><br>Mita,	126	15.0	Mita, your email to Corey is genuinely effective. You addressed every single point from the task with clear, personal reasons: you agreed to go, chose the front entrance because your mom is driving, disagreed about the film because you want to build a robot, and suggested practical items to bring. The reader finishes your email knowing exactly what you think and why. To move from Band 4 to Band 5 on the other scales, you need to polish a few grammar patterns and tighten how your ideas connect between paragraphs. Let's look at each area.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think that's a good idea to go to the science festival with you.</em> You showed a positive reaction right away, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>We should meet at the front entrance because my mom will take me to the science festival by car, so you can go with your cousin.</em> You stated a clear preference, gave a reason, and even thought about your friend's situation. That is thoughtful writing.</li>\n  <li><em>I think build a robot is more interesting for me.</em> You disagreed politely and explained why. The reader fully understands your position.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistently natural, friendly tone throughout the email without any awkward phrases. Your opening and closing are warm and appropriate, but one phrase sounds slightly off.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I wish you doing well</em> → <strong>I hope you are doing well</strong>. 'Wish' is used for things that are not true or are unlikely. For asking about someone's current state, use 'hope' plus a subject and verb.</li>\n  <li><em>I think build a robot is more interesting for me.</em> → <strong>I think building a robot is more interesting for me.</strong> Using the gerund ('building') as the subject sounds more natural and matches the friendly register you have established.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly between paragraphs so the reader never feels a jump. You used paragraphs well and basic linking words like 'because' and 'but', but the transition from your third paragraph to the final one feels abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I can go with you. We should bring hat or water...</em> → <strong>I can go with you. By the way, we should bring a hat or water...</strong> Adding 'By the way' or 'Also' signals to the reader that you are moving to a new topic, which makes the email flow more naturally.</li>\n  <li><em>Because I want to improve my robot skill, but watch the Discovering Dinosaurs is a great idea too.</em> Starting a sentence with 'Because' is acceptable, but here it creates a fragment. Try: <strong>I think building a robot is more interesting for me because I want to improve my robot skills, but watching the Discovering Dinosaurs is a great idea too.</strong> This keeps the contrast inside one smooth sentence.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently, even when trying slightly more complex ideas. Your vocabulary is good ('science festival', 'front entrance', 'robot skill', 'hat', 'water'), but a few grammar patterns repeat. The main rule to focus on is: after verbs like 'think', 'hope', and 'worry', you need a full clause with a subject and a verb.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I wish you doing well</em> → <strong>I hope you are doing well</strong>. After 'hope', use a subject ('you') and a verb ('are doing'). 'Wish' is for unreal situations, not for checking on a friend.</li>\n  <li><em>I think build a robot is more interesting</em> → <strong>I think building a robot is more interesting</strong>. When an action is the subject of a sentence, use the -ing form (gerund).</li>\n  <li><em>I worry about the weather will be hot</em> → <strong>I worry the weather will be hot</strong> or <strong>I worry about the weather being hot</strong>. You can use 'worry' + a clause (no 'about') or 'worry about' + a noun/gerund. Mixing both creates an error.</li>\n  <li><em>my robot skill</em> → <strong>my robot skills</strong>. 'Skill' is countable here because you are talking about multiple abilities related to robots. Use the plural form.</li>\n  <li><em>bring hat or water</em> → <strong>bring a hat or water</strong>. 'Hat' is a countable noun, so it needs an article ('a') before it. 'Water' is uncountable, so it can stay without an article.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: After verbs like 'think', 'hope', 'worry', 'believe', or 'know', ask yourself — does the next word have a subject and a verb? If yes, you are probably correct. If not, add them.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
32376	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Really I want to go to the science festival on this Saturday<br><br>It would be great if you can pick me up because I don't think my parents is free on Saturday. It would be awesome if you tell me a time when you would arrive at my house<br><br>It really hard to decide, but we can go to the festival and then decide which activities are we going to do, but I would like to build a robots because it would be more fun if we make robots.<br><br>I think we should bring a mini fan, but if it cool in festival don't bring it.<br><br>Bye,<br>Boss	119	14.0	Boss, you have a great instinct for making your email sound friendly and excited. Phrases like <em>It would be awesome</em> and <em>It would be great if you can pick me up</em> make the reader feel your enthusiasm. You also addressed every single point from the task: you said yes to the festival, asked to be picked up, disagreed with the film idea and suggested building a robot, and recommended bringing a mini fan. That is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. To reach that level in the other areas, you will need to pay closer attention to grammar and the flow of your ideas. Let us look at each part now.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Really I want to go to the science festival on this Saturday</em>. You clearly expressed your positive reaction to the festival, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>It would be great if you can pick me up because I don't think my parents is free on Saturday.</em> You asked to be picked up and gave a reason. The reader understands your situation completely.</li><li><em>I think we should bring a mini fan, but if it cool in festival don't bring it.</em> You suggested a practical item and added a condition, showing you thought about the weather.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone and structure for an email to a friend, making the reader feel like they are having a real conversation. Your tone is friendly, but the email feels a little rushed and lacks a greeting at the start.</p><ul><li><em>Really I want to go to the science festival on this Saturday</em> → <strong>I really want to go to the science festival this Saturday.</strong> Moving 'really' after 'I' makes the sentence sound more natural and polite.</li><li>You started the email without a salutation like 'Dear [friend's name],' or 'Hi [friend's name],'. Adding a greeting at the beginning would make it feel more like a real email to a friend.</li><li><em>It really hard to decide, but we can go to the festival and then decide which activities are we going to do</em> → <strong>It is really hard to decide, but we can go to the festival and then decide which activities we are going to do.</strong> Adding the verb 'is' and fixing the word order in the question makes the sentence flow more naturally.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow the story without stopping. You used paragraphs for each point, which is good, but the jumps between ideas feel a little sudden.</p><ul><li><em>It would be awesome if you tell me a time when you would arrive at my house</em> → <strong>It would be awesome if you could tell me what time you will arrive at my house.</strong> Adding 'could' and changing 'a time when' to 'what time' makes the sentence flow more naturally into the next paragraph.</li><li>After you ask about the pick-up time, you jump straight to <em>It really hard to decide</em> about activities. A simple transition like 'As for what we will do there,' would help the reader follow your thinking.</li><li>You used 'but' to connect ideas in the third paragraph, but the contrast is not strong. Try using 'and' or 'so' instead: <em>I would like to build a robot because it would be more fun if we make robots.</em></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar so well that errors do not distract the reader. The main rule you need to work on is subject-verb agreement: a singular subject needs a singular verb, and a plural subject needs a plural verb.</p><ul><li><em>my parents is free</em> → <strong>my parents are free</strong>. 'Parents' is plural, so the verb must be 'are', not 'is'.</li><li><em>build a robots</em> → <strong>build a robot</strong> or <strong>build robots</strong>. If you use 'a', the noun must be singular. If you want to talk about more than one, drop 'a' and use 'robots'.</li><li><em>if it cool</em> → <strong>if it is cool</strong>. Every sentence needs a main verb. 'Cool' is an adjective, so you need the verb 'is' before it.</li><li><em>It really hard</em> → <strong>It is really hard</strong>. Same rule: 'hard' is an adjective, so you need the verb 'is'.</li><li>Check your capitalisation: <em>on this Saturday</em> should be <strong>on Saturday</strong> (no 'this' needed, and days of the week are capitalised). Also, <em>in festival</em> should be <strong>at the festival</strong>.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33262	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey,<br><br>I've recived the email. It's a fantastic news.<br><br>I prefer we could meet at the front entrance. I think it's easier for us. Do you think so?<br><br>For the activities, I agree with you to watch the film, because I really like dinosaurs. It would be very interesting. For another activity, we could do it on next Saturday.<br><br>Thanks for reminding me I almost forgot to bring it. And I think we should bring some snark too - if you want to. Becase we might be hungry during the day. I'm exciting to go there.<br><br>See you soon,<br>Plearn	115	11.0	Plearn, your email to Corey is full of enthusiasm and it's clear you're excited about the trip. You did a great job covering most of the points from the task: you reacted positively to the news, suggested meeting at the front entrance, and even thought about bringing snacks. Your friendly tone, with phrases like 'Do you think so?' and 'Thanks for reminding me,' makes it sound like a real conversation between friends. To reach Band 5, the main thing to work on is making sure your answers match the task instructions exactly. The task asked you to disagree with Corey about the film, but you wrote 'I agree with you to watch the film.' Let's fix that and a few other details so your reader gets the full picture.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer addresses every part of the task clearly so the reader is fully informed. You handled most points well, but one key instruction was missed.</p><ul><li><em>I agree with you to watch the film</em> → <strong>I don't really agree with you about watching the film.</strong> The task said to disagree with Corey about the film activity. Even if you like dinosaurs, you needed to show disagreement first, then suggest something else.</li><li><em>For another activity, we could do it on next Saturday</em> → <strong>I think we should do something else instead. How about going to the science museum?</strong> You mentioned another activity but didn't say what it was. The reader needs a clear suggestion.</li><li><em>I prefer we could meet at the front entrance.</em> This is clear and works well. The reader knows exactly where to go.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses email conventions smoothly so the reader never feels confused. Your email has a friendly, natural feel, but the confusion about the film makes the reader pause.</p><ul><li><em>I agree with you to watch the film</em> → <strong>I'm not sure I agree with you about the film.</strong> This one sentence contradicts the task, which asks you to disagree. The reader wonders whether you actually agree or not.</li><li><em>Thanks for reminding me I almost forgot to bring it.</em> This is a nice, natural email moment. The reader understands the relationship between you and Corey.</li><li>Your opening ('Hi Corey') and closing ('See you soon') are exactly right for an email to a friend. Keep doing that.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the email flows from one point to the next. You used paragraphs and basic linking words, but some transitions feel sudden.</p><ul><li><em>For the activities, I agree with you to watch the film... For another activity, we could do it on next Saturday.</em> The jump from the film to 'next Saturday' is abrupt. Add a linking sentence: <strong>If we don't watch the film, we could do something else on Saturday instead.</strong></li><li><em>Thanks for reminding me I almost forgot to bring it.</em> The reader might wonder what 'it' refers to. Connect this to the previous idea: <strong>Thanks for reminding me about the snacks. I almost forgot to bring them.</strong></li><li>Your paragraph breaks are good. Each paragraph focuses on one topic: the meeting point, the activities, and the snacks.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses basic vocabulary and grammar accurately enough that errors don't distract the reader. You have a good range of ideas, but several errors make the meaning harder to follow. The main pattern to fix is using the correct structure after verbs like 'agree' and 'prefer'.</p><ul><li><em>I prefer we could meet</em> → <strong>I prefer to meet</strong> or <strong>I would prefer to meet</strong>. After 'prefer,' use 'to + verb' (infinitive), not a full clause with 'could'.</li><li><em>I agree with you to watch the film</em> → <strong>I don't agree with you about watching the film</strong> or <strong>I disagree about watching the film</strong>. After 'agree with someone,' use 'about + gerund' (about watching), not 'to + infinitive'.</li><li><em>It's a fantastic news</em> → <strong>It's fantastic news</strong>. 'News' is uncountable, so we don't use 'a' before it. Just say 'fantastic news'.</li><li><em>Becase</em> → <strong>Because</strong>. Check your spelling of common words. Also <em>recived</em> → <strong>received</strong> and <em>snark</em> → <strong>snacks</strong>.</li><li><em>I'm exciting to go there</em> → <strong>I'm excited to go there</strong>. Use 'excited' (how you feel) not 'exciting' (how something makes you feel).</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: After verbs like 'prefer,' 'agree,' 'want,' and 'like,' ask yourself: do I need 'to + verb' or 'about + verb-ing'? This will fix many of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34317	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi<br><br>Thank you for the science festival ticket! I love it!<br><br>Your cousin and you pick me up is a good chose. Because my, mom and dad is very busy on saturday, so there can't sent me to festival.<br><br>Sorry, but I think build a robot is more interesting than Discovering Dinosaurs flim, but after festival we can go t cinema to watch some Dinsaurs flim.<br><br>Oh! thank you if you not tell me I will forget it!<br><br>I think only money and my since is enogh.<br><br>Bye,<br><br>Khan	102	11.0	Khan, your email shows you really understood the task. You responded to all three notes: you said you loved the ticket, you asked for a pickup, you disagreed about the film and suggested building a robot instead, and you gave your idea for what to bring. That is exactly what the task asked for. Your enthusiasm comes through clearly with phrases like 'I love it!' and 'Oh! thank you'. To reach Band 5, the main areas to work on are making your sentences grammatically correct and connecting your ideas more smoothly. Let me show you how each part can improve.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer covers every part of the task clearly and completely. You did cover all three notes, which is great. The only small issue is that your suggestion for what to bring is a little unclear.</p><ul><li><em>I think only money and my since is enogh.</em> You meant 'my sense of humor'. The reader can guess this, but writing it clearly would make your meaning certain. Try: <strong>I think we only need money and a sense of humor.</strong></li><li><em>I love it!</em> This works perfectly to show your positive reaction to the tickets. The reader knows you are excited.</li><li><em>Sorry, but I think build a robot is more interesting than Discovering Dinosaurs flim</em> You clearly disagreed and gave a new suggestion, which is exactly what the second note asked for.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone for an email to a friend consistently, without awkward phrasing. Your friendly tone is good, but some sentences sound strange and break the natural flow.</p><ul><li><em>Your cousin and you pick me up is a good chose.</em> This sentence structure is confusing. A natural way to say this: <strong>It's a good idea for your cousin and you to pick me up.</strong> This sounds like a real person writing to a friend.</li><li><em>Because my, mom and dad is very busy on saturday, so there can't sent me to festival.</em> The comma after 'my' and the grammar errors make this hard to read. Try: <strong>My mom and dad are very busy on Saturday, so they can't send me to the festival.</strong> This keeps your friendly tone but is clear.</li><li><em>Oh! thank you if you not tell me I will forget it!</em> This is enthusiastic and friendly, which is good. To make it clearer: <strong>Oh, thank you! If you hadn't told me, I would have forgotten!</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly with a variety of linking words, and the paragraphs flow naturally from one to the next. Right now, your paragraphs jump between ideas without clear connections.</p><ul><li><em>Your cousin and you pick me up is a good chose. Because my, mom and dad is very busy on saturday, so there can't sent me to festival.</em> You start a new paragraph with 'Because', which is a fragment. Instead, combine the ideas: <strong>Your cousin and you picking me up is a good choice because my mom and dad are very busy on Saturday and can't send me.</strong></li><li><em>Sorry, but I think build a robot is more interesting than Discovering Dinosaurs flim, but after festival we can go t cinema to watch some Dinsaurs flim.</em> You use 'but' twice in one sentence. Try using 'and' or 'so' for the second connection: <strong>Sorry, but I think building a robot is more interesting than watching a Discovering Dinosaurs film. After the festival, we can go to the cinema to watch a dinosaur film instead.</strong></li><li>The final paragraph <em>I think only money and my since is enogh.</em> is very short and feels disconnected. Add a linking word like 'Also' or 'Finally' to connect it to the previous idea: <strong>Also, I think we only need money and a sense of humor.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. Your main challenge is subject-verb agreement and verb forms. Let me show you the pattern.</p><ul><li><em>my, mom and dad is very busy</em> → <strong>my mom and dad are very busy</strong>. When the subject is plural (mom and dad = two people), use 'are', not 'is'. This is the most common error in your writing.</li><li><em>there can't sent me</em> → <strong>they can't send me</strong>. After 'can', 'must', 'will', use the base form of the verb (send), not the past tense (sent). Also, 'there' is for places; use 'they' for people.</li><li><em>build a robot is more interesting</em> → <strong>building a robot is more interesting</strong>. When an action is the subject of a sentence, use the -ing form (gerund). 'Build' is the base verb; 'building' is the correct subject form.</li><li><em>flim</em> and <em>Dinsaurs</em> → <strong>film</strong> and <strong>Dinosaurs</strong>. These are spelling errors. 'Film' has an 'i', not an 'l'. 'Dinosaur' has an 'o' after 'D', not an 's'. Try writing new words three times when you learn them.</li><li>Capitalisation: <em>saturday</em> should be <strong>Saturday</strong> (days of the week are always capitalised). Also, <em>my, mom</em> has a comma that should not be there. Write <strong>my mom</strong> without a comma.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on every sentence: Ask yourself 'Is my subject singular or plural? Does my verb match? Am I using the right form after words like can, will, and must?'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35993	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi corey<br><br>Thanks corey The festival will be great, and could you please pick me up that day? For the activities well I'm gonna choose the film, and dinosaurs are more interesting than the robot. In my opinion we should bring some snacks because in the festival the food got to be expensive.<br><br>Bye,<br><br>Gimji	56	10.0	Gimji, your email to Corey is warm and friendly. I can see you understood the situation well when you wrote <em>the festival will be great</em> and suggested bringing snacks. You also remembered to close with <em>Bye</em>. That's a good start. To reach Band 5, you need to read the task instructions more carefully. The task asked you to disagree with Corey's opinion about the film, but you agreed instead. Next time, underline the key instruction words in the task before you start writing. Now let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer fully addresses every part of the task without missing or misinterpreting anything. Your email covers the pick-up request and the snack suggestion, but you missed the instruction to disagree with Corey's opinion about the film.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>well I'm gonna choose the film</em> → <strong>Actually, I don't agree with you about the film. I think the dinosaur movie sounds more interesting.</strong> The task wanted you to politely disagree with Corey, not just state your own choice.</li>\n  <li><em>dinosaurs are more interesting than the robot</em> → <strong>I think dinosaurs are more interesting than the robot show.</strong> This part is good because you gave a reason. You just needed to start by disagreeing with Corey first.</li>\n  <li>You remembered to ask for a pick-up and suggest snacks, which shows you read most of the task. Well done with those two points.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone and style for an email to a friend from start to finish. Your opening <em>Hi corey</em> and closing <em>Bye</em> are friendly, which is perfect for a friend.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thanks corey</em> → <strong>Thanks, Corey</strong>. This is a good friendly expression, but remember the comma before the name.</li>\n  <li><em>well I'm gonna choose the film</em> → <strong>Well, I think I'll choose the film.</strong> The word 'gonna' is too casual for this task. Use 'going to' or 'will' instead.</li>\n  <li>Your overall tone is warm and natural. The reader can follow your ideas easily. Just be careful to keep the language slightly more formal than a text message.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas clearly with linking words and organises the email into logical parts. Right now your email is one long paragraph with ideas that jump around.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>You wrote everything in one paragraph. Try splitting it: one paragraph for the pick-up request, one for the film choice, and one for the snacks. This makes it easier to read.</li>\n  <li><em>For the activities well I'm gonna choose the film</em> → <strong>For the activities, I think I'll choose the film.</strong> The word 'well' here doesn't connect your ideas. Use 'so' or 'as for' to show you're moving to a new topic.</li>\n  <li><em>In my opinion we should bring some snacks because in the festival the food got to be expensive.</em> → <strong>In my opinion, we should bring some snacks because the food at the festival will be expensive.</strong> You used 'because' here, which is good. Try adding 'also' or 'another thing' at the start of this sentence to show it's a new point.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses correct punctuation, capitalisation, and simple grammar with few errors. The main rule to focus on is capitalising names and using commas correctly.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hi corey</em> → <strong>Hi Corey</strong>. Corey is a name, so it must start with a capital letter. This happens twice in your email.</li>\n  <li><em>Thanks corey</em> → <strong>Thanks, Corey</strong>. When you say thank you to someone, put a comma before their name.</li>\n  <li><em>In my opinion we should bring some snacks</em> → <strong>In my opinion, we should bring some snacks.</strong> After phrases like 'In my opinion' at the start of a sentence, add a comma.</li>\n  <li><em>the food got to be expensive</em> → <strong>the food will be expensive</strong>. 'Got to be' is not correct here. Use 'will be' to talk about the future.</li>\n  <li>Your vocabulary choices like <em>great</em>, <em>interesting</em>, and <em>expensive</em> are simple but clear. Try adding one more descriptive word, like <em>really interesting</em> or <em>too expensive</em>, to make your writing stronger.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35994	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>Thank you for bought the Science festival tickets. That way,the place to meet me,I think you have to meet at the front of entrance because may be we have to use the tickets for enter,so you can't meet in the Sciense festival because I don't have the tickets. As for the main activities,I think you have to go build robot because we can watching the Dinosaurs film from the TV at home. As for a pocket, I think we have to bring the phone for may be we will miss in the festival.<br><br>Bye,<br>Science festival	95	10.0	Tee, thank you for sharing your email about the Science festival. You did a great job responding to all three notes from the prompt: you thanked Corey, suggested a meeting place, and shared your opinion about the activities. I especially liked how you thought about the practical problem of needing tickets to enter the festival. That shows real thinking. To reach Band 5, you will need to make your ideas clearer and connect them more smoothly. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer makes sure every part of their response is clear and directly answers the question. You covered all three notes, but some responses need to be more direct.</p><ul><li><em>Thank you for bought the Science festival tickets.</em> This implies you are happy, but the task asked for a clear positive reaction. Try <strong>Great, thank you for buying the Science festival tickets!</strong> This shows your excitement right away.</li><li><em>I think you have to meet at the front of entrance because may be we have to use the tickets for enter</em> You gave a reason for the meeting place, which is good. To make it clearer, try <strong>Let's meet at the front entrance because we will need the tickets to enter.</strong></li><li><em>As for a pocket, I think we have to bring the phone for may be we will miss in the festival.</em> The word 'pocket' is confusing here. The prompt asked what else to bring. Try <strong>As for what else to bring, I think we should bring a phone in case we get lost in the festival.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the email friendly and easy to follow from start to finish. Your email has a good opening and closing, but some sentences are awkward.</p><ul><li><em>That way,the place to meet me,I think you have to meet at the front of entrance</em> This sentence is hard to follow. A clearer version: <strong>As for where to meet, I think we should meet at the front entrance.</strong></li><li><em>we can watching the Dinosaurs film from the TV at home</em> Your reason for building a robot instead of watching the film is good. To make it sound more natural, try <strong>we can watch the dinosaur film on TV at home anytime.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraph breaks and a logical sequence of ideas. Your email uses 'As for' twice, which makes the structure feel repetitive.</p><ul><li><em>As for the main activities... As for a pocket</em> Using 'As for' twice in a short email feels mechanical. Try using different phrases to introduce new topics, like <strong>For the main activities...</strong> and <strong>About what else to bring...</strong></li><li>The ideas jump from meeting place to activities to what to bring without clear separation. Try putting each main idea in its own paragraph. First paragraph: thank you and meeting place. Second paragraph: activities. Third paragraph: what else to bring.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct basic grammar and clear vocabulary. The main rule that will help you is: after prepositions like 'for' and 'to', use the base form of the verb or the -ing form, not the past tense.</p><ul><li><em>Thank you for bought</em> → <strong>Thank you for buying</strong>. After 'for', use the -ing form of the verb.</li><li><em>use the tickets for enter</em> → <strong>use the tickets to enter</strong>. After 'to', use the base form of the verb.</li><li><em>we can watching</em> → <strong>we can watch</strong>. After 'can', use the base form of the verb without -ing.</li><li><em>may be we have to use</em> → <strong>maybe we have to use</strong>. 'Maybe' is one word when it means 'perhaps'. 'May be' is two words when it means 'might be'.</li><li>Check your capitalisation: <em>Sciense festival</em> should be <strong>Science festival</strong>, and <em>Dinosaurs</em> should be <strong>dinosaur</strong> (no capital unless it is a proper name).</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35995	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi,<br><br>That's great, Corey You've got a science festival ticket for c that's incredible the science festival must be fun!<br>I think we should meet at the front entrance, that wil be the best for me.<br>No i think building a robot is more fun than discoverin Dinosaurs film, building a robot is more interesting than Dinosaur films because Dinosaur films might be longer because of the people lining up for dinosaur films.<br>I suggest that we should bring water Incase If we get thirsty inside the festival and Incase If the festival doesn't have people giving water there inside the festival.<br><br>Sincerely,<br>Porsche	116	15.0	Porsche, your email to Corey is lively and full of personality. You did a great job addressing every single point from the task: you reacted positively, chose a meeting spot, disagreed about the film, and suggested bringing water. The reader gets a clear picture of your plans. To reach Band 5, focus on polishing your grammar and making your sentences flow more smoothly. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>That's great, Corey You've got a science festival ticket for c that's incredible the science festival must be fun!</em> You showed genuine excitement and directly responded to Corey's news. This is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>I think we should meet at the front entrance, that wil be the best for me.</em> You clearly stated your preference for the meeting point and gave a reason. The reader knows exactly where to go.</li><li><em>I suggest that we should bring water Incase If we get thirsty inside the festival and Incase If the festival doesn't have people giving water there inside the festival.</em> You gave a practical suggestion and explained why it matters. All task points are fully covered.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would use a consistently informal, friendly tone throughout the entire email. Your opening is warm and excited, but the closing word <em>Sincerely</em> is a bit too formal for a message to a friend.</p><ul><li><em>Sincerely,</em> → <strong>Best,</strong> or <strong>Talk soon,</strong>. For a friend, a warmer sign-off keeps the tone consistent.</li><li><em>That's great, Corey You've got a science festival ticket</em> → <strong>That's great, Corey! You've got a science festival ticket</strong>. Adding an exclamation mark after 'Corey' helps show your enthusiasm more naturally.</li><li><em>No i think building a robot is more fun than discoverin Dinosaurs film</em> → <strong>No, I think building a robot is more fun than watching a dinosaur film.</strong> Adding 'watching' makes the comparison clearer and keeps the friendly disagreement tone.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would connect ideas logically within each paragraph and avoid repetition. Your paragraphs are clearly separated, which is good, but the third paragraph repeats the same point several times.</p><ul><li><em>building a robot is more interesting than Dinosaur films because Dinosaur films might be longer because of the people lining up for dinosaur films.</em> → <strong>Building a robot is more interesting than watching a dinosaur film. The film might be longer because of the long lines.</strong> This version states your preference once and gives one clear reason, making the paragraph easier to follow.</li><li><em>I suggest that we should bring water Incase If we get thirsty inside the festival and Incase If the festival doesn't have people giving water there inside the festival.</em> → <strong>I suggest we bring water in case we get thirsty and the festival doesn't have free water.</strong> Combining the two 'incase' ideas into one sentence removes repetition and keeps the meaning clear.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control spelling, capitalisation, and word forms with few errors. Your vocabulary is good, but a few patterns need attention.</p><ul><li><em>that wil be</em> → <strong>that will be</strong>. 'Wil' is a spelling error. Remember: 'will' has two L's.</li><li><em>discoverin</em> → <strong>discovering</strong>. When a verb ends with 'ing', you need the 'g' at the end. This is called the '-ing' form.</li><li><em>Incase If we get thirsty</em> → <strong>in case we get thirsty</strong>. 'In case' is two words, and it already means 'if something happens'. You don't need both 'in case' and 'if' together. Choose one.</li><li><em>No i think</em> → <strong>No, I think</strong>. The pronoun 'I' must always be capitalised. Also, add a comma after 'No' to separate it from the rest of the sentence.</li><li><em>Dinosaur films</em> → <strong>dinosaur films</strong>. 'Dinosaur' is a common noun here, not a proper name, so it should not be capitalised. Only capitalise proper nouns like 'Thailand' or 'Corey'.</li></ul><p>Self-check: Before you finish writing, read each sentence aloud and check: Is every 'I' capitalised? Does every word have the correct spelling? Did I use 'in case' or 'if' but not both?</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36000	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>It amazing that you got a ticket for the sciene festival.<br><br>About how we get there for me I prefet your cousin to pick us up.Because I think it easier for us that way we don't need to waste time finding another at the entrance.<br><br>For the activities I agree with you to watch the Discovering Dinosaurs. I think it more interesting.<br><br>I thinks that is enough money is all it take<br><br>See you at the festival Corey.	82	11.0	Tul, your email to Corey has a really friendly, natural tone. Opening with <em>Hi Corey!</em> and ending with <em>See you at the festival Corey</em> makes it feel like a real message between friends. You also did a great job addressing each part of the prompt in order: the ticket, how to get there, and the activity choice. To reach Band 5, you'll need to cover every note in the prompt completely and make sure your sentences are clear enough for the reader to understand without guessing. Let's look at each area in detail.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer addresses every note in the prompt fully and clearly. You covered the positive reaction, how to get there, and the film choice, but the last note about what to bring was not answered.</p><ul><li><em>It amazing that you got a ticket for the sciene festival.</em> This is a good start. You showed excitement about the ticket. To make it stronger, add a reason why you're excited, like <strong>It's amazing that you got a ticket for the science festival. I've been wanting to go all year!</strong></li><li><em>About how we get there for me I prefet your cousin to pick us up.</em> You clearly chose to be picked up. Well done. The phrase <em>I prefet your cousin to pick us up</em> is a little unclear. Try <strong>I prefer your cousin to pick us up</strong> for a clearer meaning.</li><li><em>I thinks that is enough money is all it take</em> This sentence is very unclear. The reader cannot tell what you are suggesting to bring. A Band 5 writer would write something like <strong>I think that is enough money for the tickets. Should I bring some snacks for us?</strong> This answers the note about what to bring.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a friendly, informal tone throughout while keeping the grammar clear enough for the reader to follow easily. Your email has a warm, personal feel, but some unclear sentences make the reader work harder than they should.</p><ul><li><em>Hi Corey!</em> and <em>See you at the festival Corey.</em> These are perfect for an email to a friend. The opening and closing feel natural and warm.</li><li><em>Because I think it easier for us that way we don't need to waste time finding another at the entrance.</em> This sentence shows you are explaining your reasoning, which is great. To make it clearer, try <strong>Because I think it is easier for us that way. We don't need to waste time finding another way at the entrance.</strong> Breaking it into two sentences helps the reader follow your logic.</li><li><em>I thinks that is enough money is all it take</em> This sentence is confusing. The reader cannot tell if you are agreeing about money or suggesting something else. A Band 5 writer would write <strong>I think that is enough money. Is that all it takes?</strong> to clearly communicate their idea.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly with a variety of linking words and keeps paragraphs balanced. You used a new paragraph for each point, which is excellent. The sequence is logical, but your final paragraph is too short and unclear.</p><ul><li><em>About how we get there for me I prefet your cousin to pick us up.</em> You used <em>About</em> to introduce a new topic, which works well. To make it even smoother, try <strong>About how we get there, I prefer your cousin to pick us up.</strong> The comma helps the reader pause and understand the structure.</li><li><em>For the activities I agree with you to watch the Discovering Dinosaurs.</em> Using <em>For the activities</em> is a good way to transition to the next point. To improve, add a connector like <strong>For the activities, I agree with you about watching Discovering Dinosaurs.</strong></li><li>The final paragraph is only one sentence: <em>I thinks that is enough money is all it take</em>. A Band 5 writer would expand this into a full paragraph with a clear idea, such as <strong>About what to bring, I think that is enough money. Is there anything else you want me to bring?</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses basic vocabulary correctly and keeps grammar errors to a minimum. Your vocabulary is good for the task, but there is one main grammar rule that keeps tripping you up: after <em>I</em>, the verb must match. You wrote <em>I prefet</em>, <em>I think</em>, and <em>I thinks</em>. The correct form is always <strong>I think</strong> (no 's'). Let's fix this and a few other patterns.</p><ul><li><em>It amazing that you got a ticket</em> → <strong>It is amazing that you got a ticket</strong>. Every sentence needs a verb. After <em>It</em>, you need <em>is</em> before an adjective like <em>amazing</em>.</li><li><em>I prefet your cousin to pick us up</em> → <strong>I prefer your cousin to pick us up</strong>. The verb <em>prefer</em> is spelled with an 'e' after the 'r'. Also, after <em>I</em>, the verb has no 's'.</li><li><em>I think it easier for us</em> → <strong>I think it is easier for us</strong>. Again, the verb <em>is</em> is missing. When you compare two things, use <em>is</em> + <em>easier</em>.</li><li><em>I thinks that is enough money is all it take</em> → <strong>I think that is enough money. Is that all it takes?</strong> After <em>I</em>, use <em>think</em> (no 's'). The second part is a question, so it needs a question mark and the verb <em>takes</em> (with an 's' because the subject is <em>it</em>).</li><li>Check your spelling: <em>sciene</em> → <strong>science</strong>. This is a common word for this topic. Try to memorise the spelling.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can apply to any sentence you write: After the word <em>I</em>, does the verb have an 's'? If yes, remove it. After <em>he</em>, <em>she</em>, or <em>it</em>, does the verb have an 's'? If not, add it. This one rule will fix most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36001	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>My mom will give me some cash to hangout with friends this weekend! I am so happy now I have some money to spend with friends.<br><br>Do you want to go watch that new movie? I can pay for your ticket if you don't have any money. Meet me at Sushiro restaurant near the toy she I will take the bus to go there you can come too if you want to.<br><br>But if you don't want to go watch some movies I can take you to Disneyland and play some rollercoaster and ghost house. Disneyland have many things to do too. After trip I want to buy some gift for my family<br><br>Bye,<br><br>Poch	119	6.0	Poch, your email has a really friendly, excited tone. I can tell you're happy about having money to spend with friends, and that feeling comes through clearly in your writing. The greeting and closing are correct, which shows you know the basic shape of an email. Now, the task asked you to write about a science festival with tickets, transportation, building a robot, watching a film, and bringing items. Your email talks about a movie and Disneyland instead. To reach Band 5, you need to read the task instructions carefully and answer every part. Let's look at each area so you can see exactly what to change in your next draft.	A1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	1.0	<p>You're at 1/5 for content. A Band 5 writer covers all the points in the task and adds relevant details. Your email is about a movie and Disneyland, but the task asked about a science festival with tickets, transportation, building a robot, watching a film, and bringing items.</p><ul><li><em>Do you want to go watch that new movie? I can pay for your ticket if you don't have any money.</em> → <strong>Do you want to go to the science festival with me? I already bought two tickets.</strong> This directly answers the task about tickets.</li><li><em>I will take the bus to go there you can come too if you want to.</em> → <strong>I will take the bus to the science festival. You can come with me if you want.</strong> Now the transportation matches the task.</li><li><em>I can take you to Disneyland and play some rollercoaster and ghost house.</em> → <strong>At the festival, we can build a robot together and watch a science film.</strong> This covers two task points instead of one irrelevant place.</li></ul>	1.0	<p>You're at 1/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone for an email to a friend and completes the task fully. Your greeting and closing are good, but the content does not match what the task asked for, so the reader cannot follow the intended response.</p><ul><li><em>Hi Corey!</em> and <em>Bye,</em> are correct for an email to a friend. Keep doing this.</li><li><em>My mom will give me some cash to hangout with friends this weekend!</em> The tone is friendly and excited, which works well. Now apply that same friendly tone to the science festival topic.</li><li><em>But if you don't want to go watch some movies I can take you to Disneyland</em> → <strong>But if you don't want to build a robot, we can watch a science film instead.</strong> This keeps the friendly choice-making tone but uses the correct task content.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas clearly so the reader can follow the story easily. You have separate paragraphs and you used 'But if you don't want to', which is good. However, the ideas jump from movie to Disneyland without a clear connection to the task.</p><ul><li><em>Meet me at Sushiro restaurant near the toy she I will take the bus to go there you can come too if you want to.</em> This is a long sentence with too many ideas. → <strong>Meet me at the bus stop near the shopping centre. I will take the bus to the festival. You can come too if you want to.</strong> Breaking it into shorter sentences makes it easier to read.</li><li><em>But if you don't want to go watch some movies I can take you to Disneyland</em> You used 'But' to show a choice, which is good. Keep using words like 'but', 'also', and 'because' to connect your ideas.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar and vocabulary most of the time. You have some good basic words like 'money', 'bus', and 'ticket', but there are several errors we need to fix. The main rule to learn is: every sentence needs a subject and a verb, and two complete sentences cannot be joined with just a comma or nothing at all.</p><ul><li><em>hangout</em> → <strong>hang out</strong>. 'Hang out' is two words when it means spending time with friends. 'Hangout' is a noun, like a place where people meet.</li><li><em>Meet me at Sushiro restaurant near the toy she I will take the bus to go there you can come too if you want to.</em> This is a run-on sentence with three ideas pushed together. → <strong>Meet me at Sushiro restaurant near the toy shop. I will take the bus to go there. You can come too if you want to.</strong> Each idea gets its own sentence.</li><li><em>play some rollercoaster</em> → <strong>go on a rollercoaster</strong> or <strong>ride a rollercoaster</strong>. In English, we don't 'play' a rollercoaster. We ride it or go on it.</li><li><em>Disneyland have many things to do too.</em> → <strong>Disneyland has many things to do too.</strong> 'Disneyland' is singular, so the verb needs an 's': has, not have.</li><li><em>After trip I want to buy some gift for my family</em> → <strong>After the trip, I want to buy some gifts for my family.</strong> Add 'the' before 'trip', add a comma after 'trip', and change 'gift' to 'gifts' because you are buying more than one.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36018	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>The science festival is a brilliant idea! and thank you for getting me a ticket.<br><br>My car got towed yesterday, So I can't use it untill next week. I want your cousin to pick me up on the way there.<br><br>I disagree with you. I think building a robot is more interesting and fun. So I would prefer building a robot other than watching the film.<br><br>Understood, I will bring some pocket money. And I suggest you bring some snacks, drinks and some soda since we'll be there all day.<br><br>Reply back soon!<br>Pooh.	90	15.0	Pooh, your email to Corey is full of energy and personality. You did a great job addressing every single point from the task: you thanked Corey for the ticket, explained your transport problem, disagreed about the film, and suggested snacks. That's exactly what a Band 5 writer does. Your enthusiasm comes through clearly in phrases like 'brilliant idea!' and 'Reply back soon!' To reach Band 5 across all scales, you'll need to polish a few grammar and punctuation habits and add more variety to how you connect your ideas. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The science festival is a brilliant idea! and thank you for getting me a ticket.</em> You covered the first task point perfectly by showing a positive reaction and thanking Corey.</li>\n  <li><em>My car got towed yesterday, So I can't use it untill next week. I want your cousin to pick me up on the way there.</em> You clearly explained why you need a ride and made a specific request. The reader knows exactly what you need.</li>\n  <li><em>I disagree with you. I think building a robot is more interesting and fun. So I would prefer building a robot other than watching the film.</em> You stated your disagreement and gave a clear reason, which fully addresses the third task point.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses email conventions smoothly from start to finish, with no small mistakes that distract the reader. You have a friendly, enthusiastic tone and a good opening and closing, but two small issues stop you from reaching Band 5.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>brilliant idea! and thank you</em> → <strong>brilliant idea, and thank you</strong>. After 'idea' you used an exclamation mark, but then you started the next word with a lowercase 'a'. The exclamation mark is fine, but the word after it should start with a capital letter: 'And'. Or you could use a comma: 'brilliant idea, and thank you'.</li>\n  <li><em>Understood, I will bring some pocket money.</em> → <strong>Understood. I will bring some pocket money.</strong> The word 'Understood' here sounds a little abrupt for a friendly email. A full sentence like 'I understand, and I will bring some pocket money' would sound more natural and warm.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas using a variety of linking words and avoids repeating the same pattern. You do a good job putting each idea in its own paragraph, but your linking is limited to 'and', 'So', and 'And' at the start of sentences.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>So I can't use it untill next week. I want your cousin to pick me up on the way there.</em> → <strong>So I can't use it until next week. Could your cousin pick me up on the way there instead?</strong> Turning the second sentence into a question makes the connection between the problem and the request clearer and more natural.</li>\n  <li><em>And I suggest you bring some snacks, drinks and some soda since we'll be there all day.</em> → <strong>I also suggest you bring some snacks, drinks, and soda since we'll be there all day.</strong> Starting a sentence with 'And' is fine in casual writing, but using it too often makes your writing feel repetitive. Try 'also' or 'in addition' for variety.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls everyday vocabulary and simple grammar with very few errors. Your vocabulary is good ('brilliant idea', 'towed', 'interesting', 'fun'), but a few spelling and grammar mistakes hold you back. The main rule to learn is: after a comma, do NOT capitalise the next word unless it is a proper noun.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>untill</em> → <strong>until</strong>. This is a common spelling mistake. 'Until' has only one 'l'.</li>\n  <li><em>other than watching the film</em> → <strong>rather than watching the film</strong>. 'Other than' means 'except for', which doesn't fit here. 'Rather than' shows you prefer one thing over another.</li>\n  <li><em>My car got towed yesterday, So I can't use it</em> → <strong>My car got towed yesterday, so I can't use it</strong>. After a comma, the next word should not be capitalised unless it is a name. The same rule applies to 'brilliant idea! And' and 'interesting and fun. So'.</li>\n  <li><em>snacks, drinks and some soda</em> → <strong>snacks, drinks, and soda</strong>. You wrote 'snacks, drinks and some soda'. The word 'some' is unnecessary here because 'soda' is already a general item. Also, in a list of three or more items, add a comma before 'and' (this is called the Oxford comma).</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36047	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey<br><br>I've got your email. I'm really exciting for the science festival. It would be great if I go with you. You can pick me up at my house. For the two main activity. I'm reall love both of it, but I think watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film is more interesting than build a robot. I will bring a bit of my pocket money. We'll be there all day, I think we should bring some hat and wear sun screen. That's all. I'll not forget everything I can't wait till this Saturday. It's gonna be very fun to go with you<br><br>Bye<br><br>Disney	101	11.0	Disney, your email to Corey shows real enthusiasm for the science festival. You clearly told him you're excited, agreed to be picked up at home, and even suggested bringing a hat and sunscreen. That's exactly what the task asked you to do. The energy in your writing makes it feel like a real message between friends. To reach Band 5, you'll need to check your grammar more carefully, especially with adjectives like 'excited' vs 'exciting', and make sure your sentences connect more smoothly instead of jumping from one idea to the next.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer covers every part of the task completely and clearly. You did well on most points, but your response to the film choice was a little confusing.</p><ul><li><em>I'm reall love both of it, but I think watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film is more interesting than build a robot.</em> You tried to disagree with Corey, but saying you love both makes it unclear whether you actually prefer the dinosaur film. A clearer version: <strong>I think the Discovering Dinosaurs film sounds more interesting than building a robot, so I'd prefer that one.</strong> This shows your choice without the mixed message.</li><li><em>I will bring a bit of my pocket money.</em> This is a good detail that shows you're thinking about the day. The task didn't ask about money, but it fits naturally with the plan.</li><li><em>We'll be there all day, I think we should bring some hat and wear sun screen.</em> You gave a practical suggestion for the outing. Just remember to make 'hat' plural: <strong>some hats</strong>.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone and register for an email to a friend without awkward phrases. Your email feels friendly, but a few expressions sound unnatural.</p><ul><li><em>I'm reall love both of it</em> → <strong>I really love both of them</strong>. The word 'reall' is a spelling error, and 'both of it' should be 'both of them' because you're talking about two activities.</li><li><em>That's all.</em> This sounds a bit abrupt for the end of a message. Try <strong>That's all for now. See you on Saturday!</strong> This keeps the friendly tone and adds a natural closing.</li><li><em>It's gonna be very fun</em> → <strong>It's going to be a lot of fun</strong>. 'Gonna' is too informal for writing, even in an email to a friend. Use the full form in writing.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly and uses a clear structure. Your email jumps between topics without linking them, and the sentences feel choppy.</p><ul><li><em>I will bring a bit of my pocket money. We'll be there all day, I think we should bring some hat and wear sun screen.</em> These two ideas are related, but they feel disconnected. Try: <strong>I'll bring some pocket money, and since we'll be there all day, we should also bring hats and sunscreen.</strong> This connects the money and the sun protection to the same reason (being there all day).</li><li><em>For the two main activity. I'm reall love both of it, but I think watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film is more interesting than build a robot.</em> The sentence starting with 'For the two main activity' is incomplete. Combine it: <strong>For the two main activities, I really love both of them, but I think watching the Discovering Dinosaurs film is more interesting than building a robot.</strong></li><li>Your email has no paragraphs. You can group related ideas together: first paragraph about the plan and pick-up, second paragraph about the film choice and what to bring, third paragraph for the closing. This makes it much easier to read.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. The main rule that explains most of your mistakes is: <strong>after verbs like 'think' and 'love', use the gerund (-ing form) or infinitive correctly, not the base verb.</strong> Let's look at your specific errors.</p><ul><li><em>I'm really exciting for the science festival.</em> → <strong>I'm really excited about the science festival.</strong> 'Exciting' describes something that makes you feel excitement, like 'The festival is exciting.' 'Excited' describes how you feel. Use 'excited' when talking about your own feelings.</li><li><em>I'm reall love both of it</em> → <strong>I really love both of them</strong>. You wrote 'reall' instead of 'really', and 'it' instead of 'them' (because 'both' refers to two things). Also, 'I'm love' is incorrect because 'I'm' = 'I am', and you don't need 'am' before 'love'. Just say 'I love'.</li><li><em>I think watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film is more interesting than build a robot.</em> → <strong>I think watching the Discovering Dinosaurs film is more interesting than building a robot.</strong> After 'think', use the gerund (-ing form) when comparing activities. 'Watch' becomes 'watching', and 'build' becomes 'building'.</li><li><em>some hat</em> → <strong>some hats</strong>. 'Hat' is a countable noun, so when you say 'some', use the plural form: 'some hats'. Same rule applies to 'some sunscreen' (uncountable, so no change needed).</li><li><em>I'll not forget everything</em> → <strong>I won't forget anything</strong>. 'I'll not forget everything' means you will remember some things but forget others, which is the opposite of what you meant. Use 'anything' with 'not' to mean 'nothing'.</li></ul><p>Here's a self-check you can use on any sentence: <strong>Ask yourself: Is this how a native speaker would write it in a school email? If you're unsure, try saying the sentence out loud. If it sounds strange, look for the pattern we practiced.</strong></p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36052	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>Thank you for letting me know! I'm so excited this Saturday we can meet in the entrance because my mom could sent me to the festival and it will be easy for both of us. The two main activities also sound nice! I love to watch movie and I think watching together would be more fun too. So I think I'll choose watching films too. And also thank you for reminding me about the pocket money I almost forgot it. I will bring a backpack to for the stuff. In my opinion I think we should bring some food and water but not that much you know because we will need to stuff our new thing in the festival to and I think we need to bring because maybe the food would be expensive there too. I'm so excited I'll meet you there!<br><br>Bye!	144	10.0	Benz, you did a great job showing your excitement about the festival. Your opening line <em>I'm so excited this Saturday</em> really makes the reader feel your energy. You also remembered to answer most of the points from Corey's email, like where to meet and what to bring. That shows you understand the task. To reach Band 5, you need to pay closer attention to the task instructions. The email asked you to disagree with Corey about the film choice, but you agreed instead. Also, your email would be much easier to read if you broke it into shorter paragraphs. Let's look at each area in detail.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer responds to every note exactly as the task asks. You covered most points, but one instruction was not followed correctly.</p><ul><li><em>I love to watch movie and I think watching together would be more fun too. So I think I'll choose watching films too.</em> → <strong>I prefer watching films, but I think the rock climbing activity sounds more exciting. What do you think?</strong> The task asked you to disagree with Corey about the film choice. By agreeing, you missed the instruction. Try politely stating your different opinion instead.</li><li><em>I will bring a backpack to for the stuff.</em> → <strong>I will bring a backpack for our things.</strong> The idea of bringing something is there, but the sentence is unclear. Make sure the reader can easily understand what you mean.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone for an email to a friend and makes the purpose of each part clear. Your email sounds friendly and excited, which is perfect for writing to Corey.</p><ul><li><em>Thank you for letting me know!</em> This is a warm and natural opening for a friend. It works well.</li><li><em>In my opinion I think we should bring some food and water but not that much you know because we will need to stuff our new thing in the festival to and I think we need to bring because maybe the food would be expensive there too.</em> This sentence is long and confusing. The reader cannot easily follow your reasoning. Try shorter sentences: <strong>I think we should bring some food and water. The food at the festival might be expensive. What do you think?</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs and linking words to create a clear, easy-to-follow flow. Right now, your email is one long block of text that jumps between ideas.</p><ul><li>Your email has no paragraph breaks. Try grouping related ideas: one paragraph for meeting arrangements, one for the film choice, and one for what to bring. This helps the reader follow your thoughts.</li><li><em>And also thank you for reminding me about the pocket money I almost forgot it. I will bring a backpack to for the stuff.</em> The word <em>also</em> connects these ideas, but they are about different topics. Use a new sentence or paragraph when you change topics.</li><li>At the end, you repeat the idea of bringing things: <em>we will need to stuff our new thing</em> and <em>I think we need to bring</em>. This makes the ending feel jumbled. Plan your points before writing so you don't repeat yourself.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar correctly most of the time. One main pattern keeps appearing: you use the wrong verb form after modal verbs like 'can' and 'could'. Let's fix that.</p><ul><li><em>my mom could sent me to the festival</em> → <strong>my mom could send me to the festival</strong>. After 'could', use the base form of the verb (send, not sent). This rule also applies to 'can', 'will', 'should', and 'must'. For example: <em>I can go</em>, not <em>I can went</em>.</li><li><em>I love to watch movie</em> → <strong>I love to watch movies</strong>. 'Movie' is a countable noun, so it needs an article ('a movie') or a plural form ('movies'). Check every noun in your writing: does it need 'a', 'an', 'the', or an 's'?</li><li><em>I will bring a backpack to for the stuff</em> → <strong>I will bring a backpack for our stuff</strong>. The word 'to' is not needed here. 'For' shows the purpose of the backpack.</li><li><em>our new thing</em> → <strong>our new things</strong> or <strong>our souvenirs</strong>. 'Thing' is very vague. Try to use a more specific word like 'souvenirs', 'items', or 'belongings'.</li><li><em>to and</em> This looks like a typing mistake. Always read your writing aloud or slowly to yourself before submitting. This helps you catch small errors.</li></ul><p><strong>Self-check for your next draft:</strong> After every sentence, ask yourself: 'Did I use the right form after can/could/will/should?' and 'Does every noun need an article or plural s?'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36111	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi<br><br>That great!<br><br>I think we should go with your cousin because it will be a long walk to there so your cousin is coming to pick us up.<br><br>That a good ided i alway want to see that film and i think the film is more interesting<br><br>I bring alot of pocket money don't worry I suggest we bring a phone and a bag for storage.<br><br>Bye<br><br>Corey	82	9.0	Wan, you wrote a real email to Corey, and that's a great start. You answered every note in the task: you reacted to the plan, chose to go with the cousin, talked about the film, and suggested what to bring. That shows you understood the task. The biggest thing holding you back is that you agreed with Corey about the film when the task asked you to disagree. Read each note carefully before you write. Also, your email is very short and direct. A Band 5 email would feel more like a natural conversation, with longer sentences and a warmer tone. Let's look at each part of your writing to see exactly how to get there.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer responds to every note clearly and correctly so the reader has no questions. You answered all three notes, but one answer was wrong.</p><ul><li><em>That a good ided i alway want to see that film and i think the film is more interesting</em> → <strong>That's a good idea. I always want to see that film, but I think the book is more interesting.</strong> The task said Corey wants to see the film and you need to disagree. Saying you also want to see the film means you agree. To disagree, say you prefer the book instead.</li><li><em>I think we should go with your cousin because it will be a long walk to there so your cousin is coming to pick us up.</em> You chose the cousin and gave a clear reason. This is exactly what the task asked for. Well done.</li><li><em>I suggest we bring a phone and a bag for storage.</em> You suggested two items and explained why. The reader knows exactly what you mean. Good work.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer's email feels like a real message between friends, with a natural tone and enough detail to keep the reader interested. Your email has the right parts (Hi, Bye, Corey) but it is very short and the language is too simple.</p><ul><li><em>That great!</em> → <strong>That sounds great!</strong> Adding 'sounds' makes it feel like you are actually talking to your friend. Short, excited reactions are fine, but one or two extra words make the email feel more real.</li><li><em>I bring alot of pocket money don't worry</em> → <strong>I'm bringing a lot of pocket money, so don't worry.</strong> Your sentence is two ideas pushed together. A comma and a small word like 'so' make it flow like a real conversation.</li><li>Your email ends with 'Bye' and then 'Corey'. An email from a friend would usually end with a warmer closing, like 'See you soon!' or 'Can't wait!' before the name. Try adding one more sentence to say goodbye properly.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow without stopping. You separated each note into its own paragraph, which is a good start. But the connection between your ideas is weak.</p><ul><li><em>I think we should go with your cousin because it will be a long walk to there so your cousin is coming to pick us up.</em> You used 'because' and 'so' to connect reasons. That works well. Try using 'since' or 'as' sometimes to add variety.</li><li><em>That a good ided i alway want to see that film and i think the film is more interesting</em> → <strong>That's a good idea. I always want to see that film, but I think the book is more interesting.</strong> You used 'and' to connect two ideas that actually disagree. Using 'but' instead would make the contrast clear.</li><li>You start every paragraph with 'I think' or 'I suggest'. Try starting a paragraph with a different word, like 'Actually,' or 'Also,' or 'About the film,'. This makes your writing feel less repetitive.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar correctly most of the time and spells common words right. Your main rule to learn is: every sentence needs a verb. You are also missing the verb 'to be' in several places, and you have spelling errors.</p><ul><li><em>That great!</em> → <strong>That's great!</strong> You need the verb 'is' here. 'That' + 'is' = 'That's'. This is a very common pattern in English. Every time you write 'That' + an adjective, check if you need 'is'.</li><li><em>That a good ided</em> → <strong>That's a good idea.</strong> Same rule: 'That' + 'is'. Also, 'ided' is a spelling error. The word is 'idea'. Practise writing 'idea' three times: i-d-e-a.</li><li><em>i alway want to see that film</em> → <strong>I always want to see that film.</strong> Two things: 'I' must always be capitalised, and 'alway' needs an 's' to become 'always'.</li><li><em>I bring alot of pocket money</em> → <strong>I'm bringing a lot of pocket money</strong> or <strong>I brought a lot of pocket money.</strong> The plan is for the future, so 'I'm bringing' (present continuous for future plans) or 'I will bring' works. 'Alot' is not a word. It is two words: 'a lot'.</li><li><strong>Self-check for every sentence you write:</strong> 1) Does my sentence have a verb? 2) Is 'I' capitalised? 3) Did I spell common words like 'always', 'a lot', and 'idea' correctly? If you check these three things, half your errors will disappear.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36122	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi<br><br>Thank you for tickets for the science festival this Saturday. I'm very excited I can't wait to see you there!<br><br>I think we could meet at the front of entrance that would be great! Because I think I will get a taxi we could just meet there.<br><br>For the activities, I think build a robot is more interested than watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film because build a robot sounds more fun. And it's giving alot of experience - don't you agree?<br><br>I will bring a bit of pocket money. And I think you should bring a fan and a small umbrella because it's very hot. For that it will be good for us!<br><br>See you soon!<br><br>Yada	134	15.0	Yada, your email to Corey is full of genuine energy. You did a great job addressing every single point from the task: you showed excitement about the tickets, suggested a meeting place, disagreed about the film, and gave practical advice on what to bring. The reader feels like they are really talking to you. To reach Band 5 across all areas, focus on making your grammar as strong as your ideas. The examiner noticed a few errors that can be fixed with a simple rule. Let's look at each part of your writing.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thank you for tickets for the science festival this Saturday. I'm very excited I can't wait to see you there!</em> You clearly showed your reaction to the tickets, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we could meet at the front of entrance that would be great!</em> You suggested a specific meeting place and gave a reason, so the reader knows exactly where to go.</li>\n  <li><em>I think build a robot is more interested than watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film because build a robot sounds more fun.</em> You stated your preference and explained why, fully informing Corey about your choice.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a friendly, natural tone throughout without any run-on sentences or awkward phrases. Your email feels warm and personal, but one sentence needs a small fix to sound fully natural.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm very excited I can't wait to see you there!</em> → <strong>I'm very excited, and I can't wait to see you there!</strong> Adding a comma and 'and' makes it flow like natural speech.</li>\n  <li><em>For that it will be good for us!</em> → <strong>That will be good for us!</strong> The phrase 'For that' is a little unclear here. Dropping it makes the sentence sound more like something a friend would say.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear structure where each paragraph flows naturally into the next, without repeating the same opening pattern. Your email has good paragraph breaks for each idea, but you start three sentences with 'I think', which makes the writing feel repetitive.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think we could meet at the front of entrance that would be great! Because I think I will get a taxi we could just meet there.</em> → <strong>We could meet at the front entrance. I'll take a taxi, so we can just meet there.</strong> Dropping 'I think' makes the suggestion sound more direct and confident.</li>\n  <li><em>For the activities, I think build a robot is more interested...</em> → <strong>For the activities, building a robot sounds more interesting than watching the Discovering Dinosaurs film.</strong> Starting with 'For the activities' is fine, but you can avoid another 'I think' by stating your opinion directly.</li>\n  <li>The final sentence <em>For that it will be good for us!</em> feels disconnected from the previous idea about bringing a fan and umbrella. → <strong>That way, we'll be ready for the weather!</strong> This connects the advice to the reason more smoothly.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple and some complex grammatical forms accurately. Your vocabulary is good, but one main grammar rule explains most of your errors: after verbs like 'enjoy', 'like', 'suggest', and 'think about', you need a gerund (verb + ing), not the base form. Let's fix this together.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think build a robot is more interested...</em> → <strong>I think building a robot is more interesting...</strong> After 'think' in this structure, use the gerund 'building'. Also, 'interested' describes how a person feels ('I am interested'), while 'interesting' describes the thing itself ('the robot is interesting').</li>\n  <li><em>...than watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film</em> → <strong>...than watching the Discovering Dinosaurs film</strong> Keep the same gerund form to match 'building'.</li>\n  <li><em>alot</em> → <strong>a lot</strong> This is a common spelling error. 'A lot' is always two words.</li>\n  <li>Capitalisation: You correctly capitalise 'Saturday', 'I', and the start of sentences. But check <em>front of entrance</em> — it should be <strong>front entrance</strong> (no 'of'). Also, 'Discovering Dinosaurs' is a film title, so capitalising it is correct. Well done there.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36127	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi<br><br>Thanks you for invite me to the Science Festival this Saturday. I'm so excited to meet your cousin<br><br>We meet at the front entrance would be best for me<br><br>No sorry, I'm don't interested in dinosaur that much, How about we split. I'll go build a robot with your cousin he likes robot didn't he? Then after the dinosaurs film end we meet again at the toy shop near the giant fossil. sure I will bring some money<br><br>I think we should bring some camera to take the picture of this amazing time. Bye<br><br>Pang	101	13.0	Pang Pang, your email to your friend shows real energy and a clear understanding of the task. You addressed every point the task asked for: you reacted to the festival, chose a meeting spot, disagreed about the film and suggested splitting up, and remembered to bring a camera. That's strong work for a B1 writer. To reach Band 5, the next step is to make your grammar and tone more consistent. Your ideas are good, but the language needs to match the friendly, natural feel you're aiming for. Let's look at each area.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I'm so excited to meet your cousin</em> – You expressed your reaction to the festival with genuine enthusiasm, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>We meet at the front entrance would be best for me</em> – You clearly stated your preference for the meeting point, and the reader understands your choice.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should bring some camera to take the picture of this amazing time</em> – You suggested bringing a camera, which covers the final note and shows you're thinking about the whole day.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent friendly tone throughout the email, with natural openings and closings that match the relationship. Your email starts and ends well, but the register jumps between informal and slightly awkward. Let's smooth that out.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thanks you for invite me</em> → <strong>Thank you for inviting me</strong>. This is a standard friendly opening. 'Thanks you' is not correct English, and 'invite' needs to be 'inviting' after 'for'.</li>\n  <li><em>No sorry, I'm don't interested in dinosaur that much</em> → <strong>No, sorry, I'm not that interested in dinosaurs.</strong> 'No sorry' sounds too abrupt for an email to a friend. A comma after 'No' makes it more natural, and 'I'm not' replaces 'I'm don't'.</li>\n  <li><em>How about we split</em> – This is a good, natural suggestion. Keep this tone, but make sure the sentences around it are equally smooth.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraph breaks and smooth transitions between ideas so the reader never feels lost. Your email has a logical order, but the jump from the film idea to the camera suggestion is a bit sudden.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Then after the dinosaurs film end we meet again at the toy shop near the giant fossil. sure I will bring some money</em> → <strong>Then after the dinosaurs film ends, we can meet again at the toy shop near the giant fossil. Sure, I will bring some money.</strong> Adding a period or a new paragraph before 'Sure' helps separate the plan from the confirmation.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should bring some camera to take the picture of this amazing time</em> – This idea comes right after the plan to meet at the toy shop. Try starting a new paragraph here, like: <strong>Also, I think we should bring a camera to take pictures of this amazing time.</strong></li>\n  <li>The sequence of excitement → meeting point → disagreement → camera is clear overall. Just add paragraph breaks to give each idea its own space.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at a 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. The main pattern that needs work is subject-verb agreement and verb forms after prepositions or auxiliary verbs. Let's fix the key errors.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thanks you for invite me</em> → <strong>Thank you for inviting me</strong>. After 'for', the verb must be in the -ing form. Also, 'Thanks you' should be 'Thank you'.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm don't interested</em> → <strong>I'm not interested</strong>. The negative of 'I am' is 'I am not', not 'I am don't'. Think of it this way: 'I'm' is short for 'I am', so the negative is 'I'm not'.</li>\n  <li><em>he likes robot didn't he?</em> → <strong>he likes robots, doesn't he?</strong> The question tag for 'he likes' is 'doesn't he', not 'didn't he'. Also, 'robot' needs to be plural here because you're talking about robots in general.</li>\n  <li><em>take the picture</em> → <strong>take pictures</strong>. You're talking about taking photos throughout the day, so the plural 'pictures' is more natural. 'The picture' suggests just one specific photo.</li>\n  <li>Capitalisation: <em>How about we split. I'll go build a robot... Then after the dinosaurs film end we meet again... sure I will bring some money</em> – The word 'sure' should be capitalised as <strong>Sure</strong> because it starts a new sentence. Also, check that every sentence begins with a capital letter.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34929	2025-09-21 17:44:40.320694+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	A festival That I like and celebrate is christmas. Because we will leave this year as a memory and start a new years with fresh feeling. It's take place at my hous Or. sometimes my little cousins house. every one will start to go crazy shooting fire works and all the sky is full of colors because of the fire work. The best part that I like is 00:00 o'clock or midnight because people will start shooting firewor at the same time and it's very beautiful to watch and my birth day is on 1st January so it will be my birthday with christmas. And I will got present from my parents. And thats why my favourite festival is christmas	120	14.0	Hello Saint, what a lovely article you've written about Christmas. It's clear you have lots of good ideas about this special time of year, and your enthusiasm really comes through. There are some wonderful parts in your writing, and with a little focus on how you connect your ideas and choose your words, your writing will become even clearer and more enjoyable for your readers. Keep up the great work. <br> 📋 Action Plan for Improvement: <br> ✔️ Practise using different connecting words to link your sentences and paragraphs smoothly. <br> ✔️ Pay close attention to spelling common words and ensuring you use the plural form when needed. <br> ✔️ Read through your writing to check for common grammar patterns like verb tenses and word order.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	5.0	You've done a fantastic job, Saint, covering all the points asked for in the task. You clearly told us that Christmas is a festival you celebrate, where it takes place, and exactly what happens, like the exciting fireworks that fill the sky with colour. It's lovely to hear that midnight is your favourite part and how special it is because of your birthday on January 1st, meaning you get presents. You truly gave the reader a full picture of your Christmas celebrations. Keep describing your experiences with such clear and enjoyable detail. 🌟	3.0	You've made a good start at writing an article for young people, sharing your personal connection to Christmas. To make your articles even more captivating and to really keep your readers' attention, think about how you can structure your writing with a clear opening, a main part, and a satisfying ending. For example, you could start with a catchy sentence to introduce Christmas, then describe the events, and finally, summarise why it's so important to you. Ensuring your language is always welcoming and not too formal will also help your international audience feel more connected to your writing. You've got some good straightforward ideas here; the next step is to make them even more engaging. ✨	3.0	Your article has a clear flow, Saint, and the reader can definitely follow your ideas about Christmas. You connect your thoughts using words like 'Because' and 'And,' which is a good start. To make your writing even smoother and more sophisticated, try using a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For instance, instead of always starting a new sentence with 'And,' you could use words like 'Also,' 'Furthermore,' or 'In addition' to introduce new details. To show a result, you might use 'Therefore' or 'As a result.' Thinking about how each sentence leads to the next will help your paragraphs feel even more connected and logical, guiding your reader through your lovely descriptions effortlessly. 🔗	3.0	You use many everyday words correctly to talk about Christmas, and the meaning of your writing is clear despite a few noticeable errors. To really elevate your language, Saint, let's focus on a few key areas. Firstly, pay close attention to plural forms, for example, you wrote 'new years' when it should be 'new year's' or 'new year' and 'fire works' when it should be 'fireworks'. Second, review verb tenses, such as 'I will got present' which should be 'I will get presents' or 'I get presents,' as this can sometimes make your meaning a little less precise. In Thai, plurals are often inferred from context or through the use of quantifiers, which can lead to English learners sometimes omitting the 's' for plurals. Practising these small but important changes will make your writing much more accurate and polished. ✍️	\N	\N	2025-09-21
30321	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		I go to school with my friends, and we saw something incredible! It is have a some car drive, and fall to the water like a pool. And it have so many people there, because there try to help people in the water, so there try to help so long time. I see there can poh him up but he didn't wake up, so I don't how he is good or not. Oh I forgot my friends try to help him to because she want to be doctor, so she know how to help.	96	9.0		A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	The story continues from the given prompt and describes an event (a car falling into water) with a beginning, middle, and end. However, the resolution is unclear ('I don't how he is good or not'), and the target reader is only partially informed about the outcome. Minor irrelevances are present (e.g., the friend wanting to be a doctor).	2.0	The text attempts to follow story conventions but the register is inconsistent and the narrative is difficult to follow at times. The target reader has to make an effort to understand the sequence of events due to unclear pronoun references and abrupt shifts.	2.0	The text shows some organisation with basic linking words ('and', 'because', 'so'). However, the sequence breaks down in places (e.g., 'Oh I forgot' introduces a new idea without clear connection), and the overall coherence is weak.	2.0	Basic vocabulary is used for the task ('car', 'water', 'help'). Simple grammatical forms are attempted but errors are frequent: subject-verb agreement ('It is have'), pronoun errors ('there' for 'they'), and missing words ('I don't how'). Meaning is generally clear but errors distract.	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30399	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027			0	0.0	Mick, I see that you did not submit any writing for this task. That is okay. Every writer starts somewhere, and you have a chance to build your skills from the ground up. The key is to begin with small steps. For your next attempt, try writing just two or three sentences that answer the question directly. Even a short response shows the reader that you understand the task. Let's look at each area where you can grow.	NA	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	0.0	<p>You're at 0/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all the information the task asks for, so the reader understands everything clearly. Since you did not write anything, the target reader has no information at all.</p><ul><li>Start by reading the task carefully. Ask yourself: What does the task want me to do? Write down two or three key points you need to include.</li><li>Then write one sentence for each key point. For example, if the task asks you to describe a place you like, write: <em>I like the park near my house because it has a big playground and many trees.</em> That one sentence already gives the reader useful information.</li></ul>	0.0	<p>You're at 0/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone and style for the task, so the reader feels the writer is speaking directly to them. Without any text, there is no communication happening.</p><ul><li>Think about who will read your writing. Is it a friend, a teacher, or someone you do not know well? For an email to a friend, you can use friendly words like <em>Hi</em> and <em>Thanks</em>. For a story, you need descriptive words that paint a picture.</li><li>Try writing a short email to a friend. Start with <strong>Hi [friend's name],</strong> then write one sentence about your weekend. That small step already shows you are trying to communicate.</li></ul>	0.0	<p>You're at 0/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges ideas in a clear order, with a beginning, middle, and end. Since there is no text, there is no structure to evaluate.</p><ul><li>Every piece of writing needs a simple structure. For an email, use a greeting, a body with your main points, and a closing line. For a story, start with what happens first, then what happens next, and finally how it ends.</li><li>Practice with one paragraph. Write a topic sentence, two supporting sentences, and a concluding sentence. For example: <em>Yesterday I went to the beach. I swam in the sea and built a sandcastle. It was a fun day.</em> That is a complete paragraph with a clear order.</li></ul>	0.0	<p>You're at 0/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar and vocabulary so the reader understands every sentence easily. Without any text, there is no language to assess.</p><ul><li>Start by learning the basic sentence pattern: Subject + Verb + Object. For example: <em>I eat rice.</em> <em>She reads a book.</em> This pattern works for most simple sentences.</li><li>Practice writing three simple sentences about your daily life. Use the present simple tense. For example: <em>I wake up at 6 a.m.</em> <em>I go to school by bus.</em> <em>I like English class.</em></li><li>Check each sentence for capital letters at the start and a period at the end. This habit will help you avoid small mistakes as you write longer texts.</li><li>Self-check: After you write a sentence, ask yourself: Does this sentence have a subject? Does it have a verb? Does it make sense? If the answer is yes to all three, you are on the right track.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30444	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Christmas Festival<br><br>Hi everyone! Me and my family are going to host a christmas Festival for this year. And it's going to be super fun!<br><br>Where will the festival going to take place is in discussion for now, so it's either San Jose park or the river promenade. There're going to be a booth selling some decorations and outfits, also a firework when the clock hits 12AM in Christmas day. We'll maybe have Santa at the festival, but it costs a lot, so it's still in discussion too. Also, who dress the best match with the theme, will receive some rewards. I'll post again once we have confirmed some infos. Hoping to see everyone in the festival!	127	12.0	Arlong, you have a clear idea of what makes a festival exciting, and you shared those details with real enthusiasm. Your reader can picture the fireworks at midnight, the decorations, and even the possibility of Santa showing up. That is a strong start. To reach Band 5, the next step is to shift your focus from planning a festival to describing a festival you personally enjoy. An article in a school magazine should make the reader feel like they are experiencing the festival through your eyes, not reading an invitation to an event that is still being organised. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer describes a festival they enjoy with specific, personal details that fully match the task. Your article tells us about a festival you are planning, but the task asks you to describe a festival you like. That is the main gap to close.</p><ul><li><em>Me and my family are going to host a christmas Festival for this year.</em> → <strong>My family and I always celebrate Christmas with a big festival in our neighbourhood.</strong> This small change shifts the focus from planning to describing a tradition you already enjoy.</li><li><em>Where will the festival going to take place is in discussion for now</em> → <strong>The festival takes place at San Jose park, which is decorated with colourful lights every year.</strong> Instead of telling the reader about discussions, describe the location as a real, vivid place.</li><li><em>We'll maybe have Santa at the festival, but it costs a lot, so it's still in discussion too.</em> → <strong>Last year, Santa arrived on a small float and handed out candy canes to everyone.</strong> Replacing uncertainty with a past memory makes the article feel personal and complete.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an article to share a personal experience, not to invite people to an event. Your friendly tone is good for a school magazine, but the register sounds like an invitation rather than a description.</p><ul><li><em>Hi everyone! Me and my family are going to host a christmas Festival for this year.</em> → <strong>Hi everyone! Every year, my family and I celebrate Christmas with a festival that I really look forward to.</strong> This keeps the friendly greeting but starts describing a tradition instead of announcing a plan.</li><li><em>Hoping to see everyone in the festival!</em> → <strong>I hope you all get to experience a festival like this someday!</strong> A closing that invites the reader to imagine the experience is more appropriate for an article than an invitation to attend.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear paragraph structure with varied linking words to guide the reader smoothly. Your ideas are in a logical order, but the first paragraph is very short and the connectors are basic.</p><ul><li><em>Hi everyone! Me and my family are going to host a christmas Festival for this year. And it's going to be super fun!</em> → Combine this into a single opening paragraph: <strong>Hi everyone! Every year, my family and I celebrate Christmas with a festival, and it is always super fun!</strong> This makes your introduction feel complete.</li><li><em>There're going to be a booth selling some decorations and outfits, also a firework when the clock hits 12AM in Christmas day.</em> → <strong>There will be a booth selling decorations and outfits, and a firework display at midnight on Christmas Day.</strong> Using 'and' instead of 'also' creates a smoother connection between the two ideas.</li><li>Try using 'however' or 'although' to show contrast. For example: <strong>The decorations and fireworks are confirmed. However, we are still deciding about Santa.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls everyday vocabulary and simple grammar accurately enough that errors rarely appear. Your vocabulary is good — words like <em>promenade</em>, <em>booth</em>, <em>decorations</em>, and <em>rewards</em> show a range that is well above basic. Your simple grammar (present tense, future plans, modal verbs) is mostly correct and meaning is always clear. A few patterns need attention to reach Band 4 or 5.</p><ul><li><em>Me and my family are going to host</em> → <strong>My family and I are going to host</strong>. 'I' is the subject form (the one doing the action), while 'me' is the object form (the one receiving the action). A simple test: remove 'and my family' — would you say 'Me am going to host' or 'I am going to host'? The answer tells you which form to use.</li><li><em>Where will the festival going to take place</em> → <strong>Where will the festival take place</strong> or <strong>Where is the festival going to take place</strong>. You combined two future structures in one verb phrase. Pick one: 'will + base verb' for simple future, or 'be + going to + verb' for plans.</li><li><em>There're going to be a booth</em> → <strong>There is going to be a booth</strong>. 'There're' is short for 'there are', which needs a plural noun. Since 'a booth' is singular, use 'there is' (there's).</li><li><em>infos</em> → <strong>information</strong>. 'Information' is uncountable in English — no 's'. Say 'some information' or 'more details'.</li><li><em>christmas</em> → <strong>Christmas</strong>. Days, months, holidays, and names all need capital letters.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30570	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday moring, we saw something incredible!<br><br>In the moring me and my friend saw big music festival in the school, so we walked in and there's is so many famous musician playing there song, and there's a music instrument store, it was a big one in that stare<br>I saw guitar, vialin, drum, piano, electric guitar and speakers. Some of them got signed by famous musician, so the price goes up. After that me and my friend went to our music teacher who taught us how to play guitar. We asked him which one we should buy, and he show us 2 guitars it was black and white and it is really cheap only 2000 baht so we bought and play together.	135	12.0	Atan, your story starts with real energy. You opened with the required sentence and took us through a clear sequence of events: arriving at school, discovering the music festival, exploring instruments, and getting help from your teacher. That's strong storytelling structure, and it shows you understood exactly what the task asked for. To reach Band 5, you'll want to polish your language control and add a bit more detail at the end so the story feels complete. Let's look at each area one by one.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all required information and develops the story with enough detail that nothing feels rushed or incomplete. You have a strong sequence of events, but the ending could use more development.</p><ul><li><em>so we bought and play together.</em> → <strong>so we bought them and played together all afternoon.</strong> Adding a sentence about how the experience felt or what happened next would give the reader a more satisfying finish.</li><li><em>we saw something incredible!</em> This is a great hook. You kept the reader curious about what was coming next.</li><li><em>it was a big one in that stare</em> → <strong>it was a big one inside the festival area.</strong> The reader understands the general idea, but a clearer description of where the store was located would make the scene easier to picture.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent storytelling register throughout and makes sure every phrase is clear to the reader. Your story has a clear beginning, middle, and end, which is good, but some phrases feel informal or confusing.</p><ul><li><em>so we walked in</em> → <strong>so we walked into the festival</strong>. This is a bit too casual for a written story. Adding 'the festival' makes it clearer and more formal.</li><li><em>in that stare</em> → <strong>in that store</strong>. This is a spelling issue, but it also makes the reader pause and wonder what you mean. A clear phrase keeps the story flowing.</li><li><em>he show us 2 guitars it was black and white</em> → <strong>he showed us two guitars, one black and one white.</strong> This small change makes the description much easier to follow.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs to separate different parts of the story and connects ideas with a variety of linking words. Your story is one long paragraph, and some sentences run together.</p><ul><li><em>After that me and my friend went to our music teacher who taught us how to play guitar. We asked him which one we should buy, and he show us 2 guitars it was black and white and it is really cheap only 2000 baht so we bought and play together.</em> This is a very long sentence that tries to say too much at once. Try breaking it into shorter sentences: <strong>After that, my friend and I went to our music teacher. He taught us how to play guitar. We asked him which one we should buy. He showed us two guitars, one black and one white. They were really cheap, only 2000 baht, so we bought them and played together.</strong></li><li>You used 'so', 'and', and 'after that' to connect ideas. That works, but a Band 5 writer would also use words like 'then', 'next', 'when', or 'while' to add variety.</li><li>Try splitting your story into at least two paragraphs: one for arriving and seeing the festival, and another for exploring the store and buying the guitar. This helps the reader follow the timeline more easily.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling consistently so errors do not distract the reader. The main rule to focus on is subject-verb agreement in the past tense. Many of your errors happen because you mix present and past tense in the same sentence.</p><ul><li><em>there's is so many famous musician playing there song</em> → <strong>there were so many famous musicians playing their songs</strong>. 'There's' is present tense and singular. Since the story is in the past, use 'there were'. Also, 'musician' needs to be plural, and 'there' should be 'their' to show possession.</li><li><em>he show us 2 guitars</em> → <strong>he showed us two guitars</strong>. The past tense of 'show' is 'showed'. This is a common pattern: for regular verbs, add -ed. Check every verb in your story to make sure it matches the past tense.</li><li><em>moring</em> → <strong>morning</strong>. This is a spelling error that appears twice. Try writing the word slowly: m-o-r-n-i-n-g. A quick self-check: read your story out loud and listen for words that sound wrong.</li><li><em>playing there song</em> → <strong>playing their songs</strong>. 'There' is a place (over there). 'Their' shows something belongs to them (their songs). This is a common mix-up, but it matters for clarity.</li><li><em>so we bought and play together</em> → <strong>so we bought them and played together</strong>. You started with past tense 'bought' but switched to present tense 'play'. Keep the same tense throughout the sentence. A good self-check: after you finish a sentence, ask yourself, 'Is every verb in the same tense?'</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30584	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		I love sonkran. Last year me and my brother went to siam to played sonkran with our friends on 13-15 febuary and we bought a water gun and went to played with another people. In festival will have concert like, hiphop or thai remix. That make me want to dance. I giggling every time when I saw people dance because thai people will have a strang dance. And we met many people in festival, and it make me so happy. Some people when there saw pretty girls or boys. They will said " Can I have your instagram?". That why I like this festival, and my friend has boyfriend because of this festival. And the horrible story is have a lot of people died because have troble in festival or a road is wet when people ride a motorbike. It will always have an accident.	145	10.0	Mita, you chose a fantastic topic for your article. Your love for Songkran comes through clearly when you describe the dancing, the concert, and meeting new people. The reader can tell this is a real experience you care about. To reach Band 5, you will need to focus your writing more tightly on the task. The task asks you to explain why you enjoy the festival, so every detail you include should support that main idea. Some parts of your article, like the story about your friend finding a boyfriend and the horrible accident report, take the reader away from your enjoyment. Keep those personal, happy reasons at the center, and your article will be much stronger. Let us look at each area now.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You are at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes only details that directly support the main purpose of the task. Your article is about why you love Songkran, so every sentence should help the reader understand your happiness.</p><ul><li><em>That why I like this festival, and my friend has boyfriend because of this festival.</em> Your friend finding a boyfriend is not a reason you enjoy the festival. Replace this with another reason you personally love Songkran, such as: <strong>I love this festival because I get to spend time with my closest friends and make new memories together.</strong></li><li><em>And the horrible story is have a lot of people died because have troble in festival or a road is wet when people ride a motorbike.</em> This is a serious, sad topic that does not belong in an article about why you enjoy the festival. Remove it entirely. Your article should stay focused on the positive, fun reasons you love Songkran.</li><li><em>In festival will have concert like, hiphop or thai remix. That make me want to dance.</em> This is a good, relevant reason. You could make it even stronger by adding a personal detail: <strong>I love the concerts because the hip-hop and Thai remix music makes me want to dance with my friends.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You are at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format with a clear beginning, middle, and end. Your article shares personal feelings, which is correct, but it starts too suddenly and ends without a proper closing.</p><ul><li>Your article begins with <em>I love sonkran.</em> This is a good start, but an article usually has a short introduction to grab the reader's attention. Try: <strong>Songkran is my favourite festival of the year, and here is why.</strong></li><li>Your article ends with the accident story, which leaves the reader with a negative feeling. A Band 5 article ends by wrapping up the main idea. Try ending with: <strong>For all these reasons, Songkran is a festival I look forward to every year. It is full of joy, music, and wonderful people.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You are at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow the story easily. Your article jumps between topics without clear transitions, and you use 'and' too many times.</p><ul><li><em>And we met many people in festival, and it make me so happy. Some people when there saw pretty girls or boys.</em> The jump from meeting people to people asking for Instagram is abrupt. Use a transition word to connect these ideas: <strong>We met many people at the festival, which made me so happy. For example, some people asked pretty girls or boys for their Instagram.</strong></li><li><em>In festival will have concert like, hiphop or thai remix. That make me want to dance. I giggling every time when I saw people dance because thai people will have a strang dance.</em> These sentences are all connected with 'and' or just placed next to each other. Try grouping them into one smooth paragraph: <strong>There are concerts with hip-hop and Thai remix music, and that makes me want to dance. I always giggle when I see people dance because Thai people have a strange but funny dance style.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You are at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar most of the time and checks for simple errors. The main rule that will help you is: after 'to', always use the base form of the verb (infinitive without 'to'). Also, always capitalise proper nouns like festival names, months, and place names.</p><ul><li><em>went to siam to played sonkran</em> → <strong>went to Siam to play Songkran</strong>. After 'to', use the base verb 'play', not 'played'. Also, 'Siam' and 'Songkran' must be capitalised.</li><li><em>In festival will have concert</em> → <strong>At the festival, there are concerts</strong>. Use 'there are' or 'there will be' to say something exists. 'Will have' is not correct here.</li><li><em>I giggling every time when I saw people dance</em> → <strong>I giggle every time I see people dance</strong>. Use the present simple tense 'giggle' and 'see' because this happens every year. 'Giggling' needs a helper verb like 'am'.</li><li><em>13-15 febuary</em> → <strong>13-15 February</strong>. Months and days are always capitalised in English. Also check your spelling: 'February' has an 'r' after 'Feb'.</li><li><em>Some people when there saw pretty girls or boys. They will said</em> → <strong>Some people, when they see pretty girls or boys, will say</strong>. The word order was confusing. Keep the subject 'they' close to the verb 'see'. Use 'will say' (future) or 'say' (present), not 'will said'.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
32376	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Our family doesn't like to celebrate, but it have 1 celebrate that we do every years. It is happy new years.<br><br>The festival is around my house. The reasons why we play or do a festival around my house because it had a lots of space and can do a lots of thing around here such as: playing football or golf and swimming. I spend a lots of time on bike because on new years I love to bike around home to watch a fire work that really beatiful, and another thing that I really like about it is I am free and I can sleep whenever I want because my parents a prove that I can play all might.	123	10.0	Boss, you chose a great topic — New Year celebrations at home — and you shared some personal reasons why you enjoy it, like watching fireworks and sleeping late. That personal touch is exactly what makes an article interesting to read. To reach Band 5, your article needs to give the reader a clearer picture of when the festival happens and connect all your ideas more directly to the celebration. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all the key information from the task and adds specific, relevant details that fully inform the reader. You have the right idea but need to be more specific and stay focused on the festival.</p><ul><li><em>It is happy new years.</em> → <strong>It is the New Year festival.</strong> Also tell the reader when it happens: <strong>It takes place on December 31st and January 1st.</strong> This answers the question 'when' clearly.</li><li><em>playing football or golf and swimming</em> These activities are mentioned but not clearly connected to the festival. → <strong>On New Year's Day, my family plays football and swims in our backyard to celebrate together.</strong> Now the reader understands how these activities are part of the celebration.</li><li><em>I am free and I can sleep whenever I want</em> This is a nice personal detail. To make it stronger, add a specific reason: <strong>Because there is no school, I can sleep until 10 a.m. and stay up late watching fireworks.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format naturally, with a clear introduction and conclusion that engage the reader. Your informal tone is good for a school magazine, but the opening needs work.</p><ul><li><em>Our family doesn't like to celebrate, but it have 1 celebrate that we do every years.</em> This opening is a bit confusing. → <strong>My family doesn't usually celebrate festivals, but there is one we look forward to every year: New Year.</strong> This is a clearer, more inviting start for the reader.</li><li>Your article ends suddenly after <em>I can play all might.</em> Add a concluding sentence like: <strong>That is why New Year is my favourite time of the year.</strong> This wraps up the article and leaves the reader satisfied.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer organises ideas into clear paragraphs and uses a variety of linking words to create a smooth flow. Your ideas jump around a bit, and the text is one big block.</p><ul><li>You have two main ideas: (1) why you celebrate New Year at home, and (2) what you do during the festival. Split these into two paragraphs. Start a new paragraph at <em>The festival is around my house.</em> This gives the reader a clear break between topics.</li><li><em>because it had a lots of space and can do a lots of thing around here such as: playing football or golf and swimming.</em> This sentence tries to list too many things. → <strong>because there is a lot of space. We can play football, golf, and even swim.</strong> Short, clear sentences are easier to follow.</li><li>Use linking words like <strong>also</strong>, <strong>another reason</strong>, and <strong>finally</strong> to connect your ideas. For example: <strong>Another thing I love about New Year is the fireworks. I ride my bike around the neighbourhood to watch them.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with only minor errors. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>use 'there is' or 'there are' to talk about existence, and make sure your subject and verb agree.</strong> Let's fix the most important errors.</p><ul><li><em>it have 1 celebrate that we do every years</em> → <strong>there is one celebration that we do every year</strong>. 'It have' is wrong because 'it' is singular and needs 'has'. Also, 'every years' should be 'every year' (no 's' after 'every').</li><li><em>because it had a lots of space</em> → <strong>because there is a lot of space</strong>. 'It had' is past tense, but you are describing a general fact. Use 'there is' for present. Also 'a lots' should be 'a lot'.</li><li><em>a fire work that really beatiful</em> → <strong>fireworks that are really beautiful</strong>. 'Firework' is usually plural 'fireworks'. Add 'are' before 'beautiful' because you need a verb. 'Beatiful' is spelled 'beautiful'.</li><li><em>my parents a prove that I can play all might</em> → <strong>my parents prove that I can play all night</strong>. Remove the extra 'a' before 'prove'. 'All might' should be 'all night' — these are different words with different meanings. 'Might' means possibility, 'night' means the dark hours.</li><li><strong>Capitalisation:</strong> You wrote <em>happy new years</em> and <em>new years</em> with lowercase letters. 'New Year' is a proper noun and should always be capitalised: <strong>New Year</strong>.</li></ul><p>After you revise, read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Does the subject match the verb? Do I need 'there is' or 'there are'?' This self-check will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33262	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Festivals<br><br>The festival that our family like the most is New year. It's starts on 31st of December and ends on 1st of January. We have a very great time on New year. We celebrated by watching firework at midnight and eating a special food like pizza or pasta. The reason that I really like it, because I love to spend time with my family and another reason is I can sleep late to watched firework at midnight. I think New year is a very happy festival but it's matter with who do you celebrate with. Although you celebrated alone it's still a very fantastic festival. Overall New year is a very heartwarming festival.	117	13.0	Plearn, your article clearly explains why you enjoy New Year, especially the family time, fireworks, and special food. To reach Band 5, the main things to work on are covering all the required points — the task also asks you to say where the festival takes place — and making the writing feel more like a magazine article with an engaging title and a less report-like tone.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer covers every point the task asks for. You told us when the festival happens and why you like it, but the task also asks you to say <strong>where</strong> it takes place.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>You wrote <em>It's starts on 31st of December and ends on 1st of January.</em> That is a clear 'when'.</li>\n  <li>You explained <em>I love to spend time with my family</em> and <em>I can sleep late</em> as reasons, which covers 'why'.</li>\n  <li>But you never said <strong>where</strong> the celebration happens. Is it at home? At a temple? In the city centre? Add a sentence like: <strong>We celebrate at home with our family, watching fireworks from our balcony.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of a magazine article to hold the reader's attention — a catchy title, an engaging opening, and a personal voice throughout. Your article communicates your ideas clearly, but it reads more like a factual report than a magazine piece.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your title is <em>Festivals</em>, which is very generic. Try something more engaging like <strong>Why New Year Is My Favourite Time of Year</strong> — this tells the reader exactly what your article is about and creates curiosity.</li>\n  <li><em>It's starts on 31st of December and ends on 1st of January.</em> This is a factual statement. A magazine article could express the same idea more engagingly: <strong>New Year begins on 31st December and carries all the way through to 1st January.</strong></li>\n  <li>Your closing <em>Overall New year is a very heartwarming festival</em> works well as a conclusion. Keep that personal touch throughout the article and you will reach Band 4 or 5.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer connects each idea to the one before it so the reader never feels a jump. Your article has a clear order, but one transition could be smoother.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>The reason that I really like it, because I love to spend time with my family and another reason is I can sleep late to watched firework at midnight.</em> → <strong>The reason I really like it is that I love to spend time with my family. Another reason is that I can sleep late to watch fireworks at midnight.</strong> Breaking this into two sentences makes each reason stand out clearly.</li>\n  <li>Your use of <em>because</em>, <em>although</em>, and <em>overall</em> shows good variety. Keep using these linking words to guide the reader.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar so errors don't distract the reader. The main pattern holding you back is subject-verb agreement and verb tense after modal verbs. Let's fix that.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>It's starts</em> → <strong>It starts</strong>. The verb <em>starts</em> already has the 's' for third person. Adding 's' to <em>It</em> creates a double subject. Write <em>It starts</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>I can sleep late to watched firework at midnight</em> → <strong>I can sleep late to watch fireworks at midnight</strong>. After <em>to</em> (infinitive), use the base form <em>watch</em>, not past tense <em>watched</em>. Also, <em>firework</em> should be plural <em>fireworks</em> because there are many.</li>\n  <li><em>it's matter with who do you celebrate with</em> → <strong>what matters is who you celebrate with</strong>. This sentence has two problems: <em>it's matter</em> should be <em>what matters</em>, and <em>who do you celebrate with</em> should be <em>who you celebrate with</em> (no auxiliary verb <em>do</em> in a statement).</li>\n  <li><em>We celebrated by watching firework</em> → <strong>We celebrate by watching fireworks</strong>. Since the festival happens every year, present tense works better here. Also, <em>firework</em> → <em>fireworks</em>.</li>\n  <li>Self-check: Read each sentence aloud. If you see <em>it's</em> before a verb, ask yourself: 'Do I mean <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>?' If not, remove the apostrophe and write <em>It starts</em>.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
34317	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Hello<br><br>I went to festival with my friend and his cousin lost saturday. The festiva is really amazing!<br><br>We have build a robot. I really like it! It's feel like I am Eron Mark, but my friend Corey is not interesting with it. Because he want to watch Discovering Dinosauns flim than build a robot. I'm sorry Corey. However, build a robot is the best!<br><br>I wish I'm the best articles answer.<br><br>Thank you,<br>Khan	87	10.0	Khan, your article has some really enjoyable moments. I can tell you had fun writing about the festival and building a robot with your friend Corey. The personal details you included, like your friend wanting to watch a dinosaur film instead of building a robot, make the story feel real and lively. To reach Band 5, you will need to make sure your article answers all parts of the task clearly: it should tell the reader when and where the festival takes place, and explain why you like it in more detail. Let's look at each area of your writing to see how you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all the information the task asks for and stays focused on the topic. Your article mentions a festival and an activity, but it leaves out where the festival happens and only gives a vague time.</p><ul><li><em>lost saturday</em> → <strong>last Saturday</strong>. This tells the reader when, but the task needs a clearer time. Try: <strong>Last Saturday, I went to the Chiang Mai Science Festival at the convention centre.</strong></li><li>You did not say where the festival took place. The reader needs to know the location. Add a sentence like: <strong>The festival was held at the city park near my school.</strong></li><li><em>I really like it!</em> You express enjoyment, but a Band 5 writer develops the reason. Instead of just saying you like building a robot, explain why: <strong>I really like building robots because I love solving problems and seeing my ideas come to life.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone and style for an article throughout. Your writing has a personal voice, which is good for an article, but the tone shifts in a way that confuses the reader.</p><ul><li><em>I'm sorry Corey.</em> This sounds like you are apologising directly to a friend, which is too conversational for a magazine article. Instead, keep the focus on your own opinion: <strong>Corey wanted to watch a film about dinosaurs, but I preferred building the robot.</strong></li><li><em>I wish I'm the best articles answer.</em> This sentence does not fit an article at all. It sounds like you are talking to a teacher about your test. Remove it and end with a strong closing thought about the festival, such as: <strong>Building a robot at the festival was an unforgettable experience, and I cannot wait to go again next year.</strong></li><li>Your opening is abrupt. Start with an engaging sentence that grabs the reader's attention, like: <strong>Have you ever dreamed of building your own robot? Last Saturday, I got to do exactly that at a festival!</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly and uses a clear structure. Your paragraphs are short and the ideas jump around without good links between them.</p><ul><li><em>We have build a robot. I really like it! It's feel like I am Eron Mark, but my friend Corey is not interesting with it. Because he want to watch Discovering Dinosauns flim than build a robot.</em> These sentences feel disconnected. Use a linking word to show contrast: <strong>We built a robot, and it felt amazing, like I was Elon Musk. However, my friend Corey was not interested because he preferred watching a film about dinosaurs.</strong></li><li>The final sentence about being the best articles answer does not connect to the festival. Every sentence should support your main topic. Remove it or replace it with a sentence that wraps up your experience.</li><li>Use a wider range of connectors. You use 'and', 'but', 'because', and 'however'. Try adding 'first', 'then', 'after that', or 'in the end' to show the order of events: <strong>First, we arrived at the festival. Then, we found the robot-building area. After that, we spent an hour creating our robot.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar and spelling most of the time, with a good range of vocabulary. The main rule that explains most of your errors is <strong>past simple tense</strong>. You are writing about something that happened last Saturday, so you need to use past tense verbs consistently.</p><ul><li><em>We have build a robot</em> → <strong>We built a robot</strong>. For completed actions in the past, use the past simple form 'built', not 'have build'.</li><li><em>It's feel like</em> → <strong>It felt like</strong>. After 'it', use 'felt' for past tense. Also, 'it's' means 'it is', which is present tense. You need past tense here.</li><li><em>my friend Corey is not interesting with it</em> → <strong>my friend Corey was not interested in it</strong>. 'Interested in' is the correct phrase, and use 'was' for past tense.</li><li><em>flim</em> → <strong>film</strong>. Check your spelling. 'Flim' is not a word. Also, <em>Eron Mark</em> → <strong>Elon Musk</strong>. Names need correct capitalisation and spelling.</li><li><em>I'm the best articles answer</em> → <strong>I have the best article answer</strong> or better, remove this sentence entirely as it does not fit the article. The grammar here is confused: 'I'm' means 'I am', which does not work with 'answer'.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can apply to any sentence: Ask yourself, 'Did this happen in the past?' If yes, change the verb to past simple (e.g., go → went, build → built, feel → felt, is → was).</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35993	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		“Loy Katong Festival”<br><br>This take place by the river next to the temple. In this festival people come by the river and put a "katong" on the surface of the water because old people thought that this is a way to respect the mother of water. The things that I like the most about The Loy Katong Festival is that you can light up the sky with fireworks and this festival only happens during one night in November so the coolness of the wind makes this festival even better.	89	15.0	Gimji, your article about Loy Krathong has a clear strength: you included all the key information a reader would want to know. You told us where the festival happens, when it takes place, and why it matters to people. The detail about the fireworks and the cool wind gives the reader a real sense of being there. To reach Band 5, your next draft needs two things: a more exciting opening that pulls the reader in, and a clearer structure with separate paragraphs. Let me show you exactly how.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>This take place by the river next to the temple.</em> You gave a clear location that helps the reader picture the scene.</li>\n  <li><em>this festival only happens during one night in November</em> You included the exact time, which is important information for an article.</li>\n  <li><em>you can light up the sky with fireworks</em> This is a vivid, specific detail that makes the festival come alive for the reader.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer grabs the reader's attention from the very first sentence and keeps them engaged throughout. Your article shares good information, but the opening could be stronger.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>This take place by the river next to the temple.</em> → <strong>Imagine a river glowing with hundreds of tiny lights, each one carrying a wish.</strong> Starting with a vivid image pulls the reader into the scene immediately.</li>\n  <li><em>The things that I like the most about The Loy Katong Festival is that you can light up the sky with fireworks</em> → <strong>What I love most about Loy Krathong is lighting up the night sky with fireworks.</strong> This version is more direct and personal, which suits an article well.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer divides their article into clear paragraphs and arranges ideas in a logical order. Your text is one long paragraph, and the ideas jump between description and personal reasons without a clear path.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>This take place by the river next to the temple. In this festival people come by the river and put a "katong" on the surface of the water because old people thought that this is a way to respect the mother of water.</em> → <strong>Start a new paragraph here.</strong> The first paragraph could describe the festival's location and traditions. The second paragraph could explain why you personally enjoy it.</li>\n  <li><em>The things that I like the most about The Loy Katong Festival is that you can light up the sky with fireworks and this festival only happens during one night in November so the coolness of the wind makes this festival even better.</em> → <strong>Separate this into two sentences.</strong> Try: <strong>What I like most is lighting up the sky with fireworks. Since the festival only happens one night in November, the cool wind makes it even more special.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and capitalisation consistently. Your main pattern of errors involves the verb 'take' not matching the subject 'this', and some capitalisation choices that confuse the reader.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>This take place</em> → <strong>This takes place</strong>. The subject 'This' is singular, so the verb needs an 's'. This is the most important rule to check in every sentence you write.</li>\n  <li><em>old people thought that this is a way to respect the mother of water</em> → <strong>people believed that this was a way to respect the goddess of water</strong>. 'Old people thought' sounds awkward in English. 'People believed' is clearer and more natural.</li>\n  <li><em>put a "katong"</em> → <strong>put a krathong</strong>. The festival is spelled 'Loy Krathong', so the floating basket should be 'krathong'. Check your spelling against the festival name.</li>\n  <li><em>The things that I like the most about The Loy Katong Festival</em> → Notice the capital 'T' on 'The' in the middle of the sentence. In English, only proper nouns and the first word of a sentence get capital letters. <strong>the things that I like the most about the Loy Krathong Festival</strong> is the correct form.</li>\n  <li>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: find the subject and the verb. If the subject is singular (he, she, it, this, a festival), does the verb have an 's'? If the subject is plural (they, people, fireworks), does the verb NOT have an 's'? Apply this check to every sentence in your next draft.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35994	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		The festival that my family will go is the "Delta force festival", so I'll go there for know what the next season will be. For example next season will have a new map or new charactors, so that make me really interesting and I can go to try the best gun that I don't have money to buy it in the game or may be best ammo for that gun. This festival will have somes model of the charactor for buy, and I like it because Delta force is my favorite game since I have played it. I've already bought the ticket, so I will go to Delta force festival next mouth absolutly.	116	11.0	Tee, you have a clear love for the Delta Force game, and that energy comes through in your article. You did a great job explaining why the festival excites you, especially when you wrote about trying the best gun you cannot afford in the game. That personal reason makes your writing feel honest. For your next draft, think about whether the festival you describe is one a reader could actually attend. The task asked for a real festival, so choosing a real event will help you hit the target more accurately. Let me walk you through each area so you can push your score higher.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer describes a real festival with clear details about when, where, and why it matters. Your article describes a video game event, which is not quite what the task asked for, and you left out the specific time and place.</p><ul><li><em>The festival that my family will go is the "Delta force festival"</em> → <strong>The festival my family will go to is the Songkran Festival in Chiang Mai.</strong> Choosing a real festival immediately makes your article match the task.</li><li><em>I will go to Delta force festival next mouth</em> → <strong>I will go to the festival in April.</strong> Adding the month gives the reader the specific time they need.</li><li><em>so I'll go there for know what the next season will be</em> → <strong>I will go there to find out what the next season brings.</strong> Explaining why the festival is important to you works well, but make sure the reason connects to a real festival.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer opens an article in a way that grabs the reader's attention and keeps a consistent informal but polished tone throughout. Your article is easy to follow, but your opening could be stronger.</p><ul><li><em>The festival that my family will go is the "Delta force festival"</em> → <strong>Have you ever wondered what it feels like to step inside your favourite video game? That is exactly what the Delta Force Festival offers.</strong> A question or a vivid image at the start pulls the reader in.</li><li><em>I like it because Delta force is my favorite game since I have played it</em> → <strong>I love it because Delta Force has been my favourite game since I started playing.</strong> Your personal opinion is good, but polishing the grammar makes it sound more natural for a school magazine.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear sequence and a variety of linking words without repeating the same idea. Your article flows logically, but you mention 'Delta force festival' many times, which feels repetitive.</p><ul><li><em>Delta force festival ... Delta force festival ... Delta force festival</em> → <strong>Try replacing some repetitions with 'the event', 'this festival', or 'it'.</strong> For example: 'I've already bought the ticket, so I will go to this event next month absolutely.'</li><li><em>so I'll go there ... so that make me ... so I will go</em> → <strong>Try using 'therefore', 'as a result', or 'that is why' instead of 'so' every time.</strong> This gives your writing more variety.</li><li><em>absolutly</em> at the end works as a closing statement. To make it stronger, add a final thought: <strong>I cannot wait to be there, absolutely!</strong> This feels more complete.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling well enough that errors do not distract the reader. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: after a preposition like 'for' or 'to', use the base form of the verb (not 'buy' after 'for'). Also, after 'since', use a past tense verb. Let's fix these patterns.</p><ul><li><em>for know</em> → <strong>to find out</strong>. After 'for', you need a noun or an -ing form, not a base verb. Use 'to + verb' to show purpose: 'I go there to find out.'</li><li><em>since I have played it</em> → <strong>since I started playing it</strong>. 'Since' marks the beginning of an action in the past, so use the past simple after it: 'since I started'.</li><li><em>so that make me really interesting</em> → <strong>that makes me really interested</strong>. 'That' is singular, so use 'makes'. Also, 'interesting' describes the thing; 'interested' describes how you feel. You feel interested, not interesting.</li><li><em>charactors</em> → <strong>characters</strong>. <em>mouth</em> → <strong>month</strong>. <em>absolutly</em> → <strong>absolutely</strong>. <em>somes</em> → <strong>some</strong>. These spelling errors are small but noticeable. Try reading your article out loud slowly to catch them.</li><li><em>for buy</em> → <strong>to buy</strong>. After 'for', use an -ing form: 'for buying'. Or use 'to + verb' for purpose: 'to buy'.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After every sentence, ask yourself: 'Did I use the right verb form after for, to, and since?' If you check those three words, you will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35995	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		A festival that me and my family celebrate is New Year.<br><br>This festival takes place in December at the last day of the calendar and when it reaches midnight people will shoots fireworks to the sky to celebrate. What i like about this festival is that how beautiful the fireworks is in the sky even it might be annoying for someone sleeping but staying up late was worth it the firework will last until January because people still celebrates New year all around the world with fireworks still launching to the sky and at the last day of December there will be a New year countdown that is the best moment I have ever been.	117	14.0	Porsche, your article about the New Year festival is genuinely enjoyable to read. You gave the reader a clear picture of when the festival happens, what people do, and why you love it. Your description of the fireworks and the countdown makes the celebration feel real and exciting. To reach Band 5, your next draft needs stronger structure and more consistent grammar. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>A festival that me and my family celebrate is New Year.</em> You introduced the festival clearly and stated who celebrates it, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>This festival takes place in December at the last day of the calendar and when it reaches midnight people will shoots fireworks to the sky to celebrate.</em> You gave specific details about when and how the festival happens, so the reader fully understands the event.</li><li><em>at the last day of December there will be a New year countdown that is the best moment I have ever been.</em> You shared a personal feeling about the best part of the festival, which makes the article engaging and honest.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format with a clear introduction, body, and conclusion, and keeps the tone consistent throughout. Your article starts and ends abruptly, and some phrases sound too casual for a written article.</p><ul><li><em>even it might be annoying for someone sleeping</em> → <strong>even though it might disturb someone who is sleeping</strong>. 'Even it' is grammatically incomplete, and 'annoying for someone sleeping' sounds like spoken language. Use 'even though' to connect the idea properly, and choose a more formal word like 'disturb'.</li><li><em>A festival that me and my family celebrate is New Year.</em> → <strong>One festival that my family and I celebrate is New Year's Eve.</strong> Start with a stronger opening sentence that introduces the topic clearly. 'Me and my family' is too casual for an article.</li><li>The article ends without a closing thought. Add a final sentence like <strong>For me, this is the most magical night of the year, and I look forward to it every December.</strong> This gives the reader a satisfying finish.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer breaks their article into clear paragraphs and uses a variety of linking words to guide the reader. Your text is one long paragraph with mostly basic connectors like 'and' and 'but'.</p><ul><li><em>A festival that me and my family celebrate is New Year. This festival takes place in December...</em> → Split this into two paragraphs. One paragraph could introduce the festival and when it happens. A second paragraph could describe what happens during the celebration and why you like it. This helps the reader follow your ideas more easily.</li><li><em>staying up late was worth it the firework will last until January</em> → <strong>staying up late was worth it because the fireworks will last until January</strong>. You have a run-on sentence here. Add a conjunction like 'because' or use a period to separate the two ideas.</li><li>Use a wider range of linking words. Instead of only 'and' and 'but', try 'however', 'in addition', or 'as a result' to connect your ideas more smoothly.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement, capitalisation, and sentence structure with few errors. Your main grammar rule to focus on is <strong>subject-verb agreement</strong>: a singular subject takes a singular verb, and a plural subject takes a plural verb. Let's fix the key errors.</p><ul><li><em>people will shoots fireworks</em> → <strong>people will shoot fireworks</strong>. After 'will', always use the base form of the verb. 'Shoots' is the third-person singular form, but 'people' is plural.</li><li><em>how beautiful the fireworks is</em> → <strong>how beautiful the fireworks are</strong>. 'Fireworks' is plural, so the verb must be 'are', not 'is'.</li><li><em>people still celebrates New year</em> → <strong>people still celebrate New Year</strong>. 'People' is plural, so the verb is 'celebrate' without the 's'.</li><li><em>What i like</em> → <strong>What I like</strong>. The pronoun 'I' must always be capitalised in English. Also check 'New year' → <strong>New Year</strong> (capitalise both words as a proper noun).</li><li><em>that is the best moment I have ever been</em> → <strong>that is the best moment I have ever experienced</strong>. 'Been' needs a complement like 'been to' or 'been in'. Use 'experienced' or 'had' to complete the meaning.</li></ul><p>After you revise, read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: Is my subject singular or plural? Does my verb match it? This self-check will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36000	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		The festival I want to talk about is my friend birth day party.<br><br>This festival took place at my friend house about 2 month ago. We have a lot of fun together such as playing in a swimming pool, play basket ball, play some relax games and the part that I like the most is playing music with my friend. Another thing that I like is having camp fire and play chill music talk about our life our band and our dream. It was the best times with my friend.<br><br>I would to have times like this every year.	103	11.0	Tul, your article about your friend's birthday party is a warm and personal piece of writing. You did a great job choosing a specific event and explaining why it was special to you, especially the part about playing music and having a camp fire. That personal touch is exactly what makes an article engaging. To reach Band 5, you'll need to add more detail about the festival itself, use more precise vocabulary, and make sure your grammar is accurate. Let's look at each area where you can improve.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer gives enough specific details so the reader can picture the event clearly. You described the activities, but you didn't name the type of festival or give the reader a strong sense of what made it special.</p><ul><li><em>my friend birth day party</em> → <strong>my friend's birthday party</strong>. Also, consider adding a sentence that tells the reader what kind of festival or celebration this was, like 'a small birthday party with close friends.'</li><li><em>playing in a swimming pool, play basket ball, play some relax games</em> → <strong>swimming in the pool, playing basketball, and playing relaxing games</strong>. You listed activities, but a Band 5 writer would describe one or two in detail. For example: 'We splashed in the swimming pool and laughed until our stomachs hurt.'</li><li><em>It was the best times with my friend</em> → <strong>It was the best time with my friends</strong>. You could add a specific memory, like 'I will never forget the moment we all sang together around the camp fire.'</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an article in a way that feels natural and engaging for the reader. Your article has a clear beginning and ending, but the tone is quite simple and could be more lively.</p><ul><li><em>The festival I want to talk about is my friend birth day party.</em> → <strong>The festival I want to tell you about is my friend's birthday party.</strong> This is a good start. A Band 5 writer might add a hook to grab the reader's attention, like 'Have you ever been to a birthday party that felt like a festival? I have.'</li><li><em>I would to have times like this every year.</em> → <strong>I would like to have times like this every year.</strong> Your conclusion expresses your feeling, which is good. To make it stronger, you could add a final thought: 'I hope every birthday can be this memorable.'</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer organises ideas in a logical sequence that is easy to follow. You used paragraphs and some linking words, but the order of your ideas is a bit repetitive.</p><ul><li><em>We have a lot of fun together such as playing in a swimming pool, play basket ball, play some relax games and the part that I like the most is playing music with my friend.</em> → <strong>We had a lot of fun together. We played in the swimming pool, played basketball, and played relaxing games. But the part I liked the most was playing music with my friend.</strong> Try grouping similar activities together and using 'but' or 'however' to introduce your favourite part.</li><li><em>Another thing that I like is having camp fire and play chill music talk about our life our band and our dream.</em> → <strong>Another thing I liked was having a camp fire and playing chill music while we talked about our lives, our band, and our dreams.</strong> This sentence connects well to the previous one, but a Band 5 writer might put this before the swimming pool activities to build a stronger story.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling so the reader never has to guess the meaning. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>use the past tense consistently</strong> when writing about a past event. Also, remember to use apostrophes for possession and check your spelling of common words.</p><ul><li><em>my friend birth day party</em> → <strong>my friend's birthday party</strong>. 'Birthday' is one word, not two. Also, 'friend' needs an apostrophe to show the party belongs to your friend.</li><li><em>my friend house</em> → <strong>my friend's house</strong>. Same rule: add 's to show possession.</li><li><em>We have a lot of fun together</em> → <strong>We had a lot of fun together</strong>. The party was in the past, so use 'had' instead of 'have'. Check every verb in your article to make sure it is in the past tense.</li><li><em>play basket ball</em> → <strong>played basketball</strong>. 'Basketball' is one word. Also, keep the past tense: 'played' matches the rest of the sentence.</li><li><em>I would to have times like this every year</em> → <strong>I would like to have times like this every year</strong>. The phrase is 'would like to', not 'would to'.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After you finish writing, read each sentence and ask yourself: 'Is this verb in the past tense? Did I spell this word correctly? Did I use an apostrophe for possession?'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36001	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		A festival that my family and other Thai people celebrate in Thailand are Songkran. It is a festival that people splash water to each other and take a flour to apply on each other face. This festival is so fun when Songkran is started many people will drive a truck to play Songkran and have a big tank full of water in their trunk and someone need to stand there too then people on the trunk start splashing water to other car or people it is like a war. Same place will open concert and invite a famous singer to come on stage then people will go crazy because the music is too good combine with loud music. I like the vibe when celebrating this festival	126	15.0	Poch, your article about Songkran is full of energy and personal feeling. You did a great job choosing details that make the reader feel like they are there: the trucks with big tanks, the flour on faces, the concerts with loud music. That is exactly what a good article does. To reach Band 5, you need to work on two things: organising your ideas into clear paragraphs and fixing some grammar patterns. Let me show you how.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>A festival that my family and other Thai people celebrate in Thailand are Songkran.</em> You introduced the festival clearly and told the reader who celebrates it. That is a strong start.</li>\n  <li><em>people splash water to each other and take a flour to apply on each other face</em> You explained the main activity of Songkran in a way that anyone can picture. Good job.</li>\n  <li><em>Same place will open concert and invite a famous singer to come on stage then people will go crazy because the music is too good combine with loud music.</em> You added a specific detail about concerts, which makes the article feel personal and lively. The reader learns something real about your experience.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer opens an article with a direct, engaging sentence that pulls the reader in immediately. Your first sentence is a bit long and general. Let's sharpen it.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>A festival that my family and other Thai people celebrate in Thailand are Songkran.</em> → <strong>Every year, my family and I celebrate Songkran, the most exciting festival in Thailand.</strong> This version starts with a clear subject and adds excitement right away. The reader knows exactly what the article is about and wants to keep reading.</li>\n  <li><em>I like the vibe when celebrating this festival</em> This is a nice personal opinion, and it fits an article well. Next time, try putting a sentence like this near the beginning to hook the reader earlier.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer breaks an article into clear paragraphs, each with one main idea. Your whole text is one long paragraph, and some sentences run together without stops. Let's fix that.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>This festival is so fun when Songkran is started many people will drive a truck to play Songkran and have a big tank full of water in their trunk and someone need to stand there too then people on the trunk start splashing water to other car or people it is like a war.</em> → Break this into two or three shorter sentences. For example: <strong>This festival is so fun. When Songkran starts, many people drive a truck with a big tank full of water in the back. Someone stands in the truck and splashes water at other cars and people. It is like a water war.</strong> Shorter sentences are easier for the reader to follow.</li>\n  <li>Try using paragraphs: one paragraph for the introduction, one for the water splashing, one for the concerts, and one for your personal feeling. That structure will help your reader move through the article smoothly.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and article use consistently. One main pattern keeps appearing: your subjects and verbs do not always match in number. Let's fix that, along with a few other common errors.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>A festival that my family and other Thai people celebrate in Thailand are Songkran.</em> → <strong>A festival that my family and other Thai people celebrate in Thailand is Songkran.</strong> The subject is <em>a festival</em> (singular), so the verb must be <em>is</em>, not <em>are</em>. The words between the subject and verb do not change the rule.</li>\n  <li><em>take a flour to apply on each other face</em> → <strong>take flour to apply on each other's faces</strong>. <em>Flour</em> is uncountable, so we do not use <em>a</em>. Also, <em>each other</em> needs an apostrophe + s: <em>each other's faces</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>when Songkran is started many people will drive</em> → <strong>when Songkran starts, many people drive</strong>. Use the simple present tense for general facts and annual events: <em>Songkran starts</em>, not <em>is started</em>. Also, add a comma after the time clause.</li>\n  <li><em>someone need to stand there</em> → <strong>someone needs to stand there</strong>. <em>Someone</em> is singular, so the verb needs an -s: <em>needs</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>the music is too good combine with loud music</em> → <strong>the music is so good, combined with the loud music</strong>. Use <em>so</em> instead of <em>too</em> for positive meaning, and <em>combined</em> (past participle) instead of <em>combine</em> (base verb).</li>\n</ul><p>Self-check tip: After you write a sentence, find the main subject and ask yourself: is it singular or plural? Then check if the verb matches. This will fix most of your agreement errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36018	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Hello everbody!<br><br>If anyone has a passion or likes Basketball I recommand you to register for our Basketball festival. It has alot of athletic players and competitions.<br><br>It take place at 2 o'clock on 1st of August 2026 at Assumption College Thanburi. What I like about it is We can spend time with our family playing sports rather than phones. My dad and I celebrate this festival once a year.<br><br>See you soon!<br><br>Poh.	83	11.0	Pooh, your article about the Basketball festival is a great start. You did a wonderful job including all the key information the task asked for: the name of the festival, the time and place, and a personal reason why you like it. Your opening line, 'Hello everbody!', immediately catches the reader's attention, and ending with 'See you soon!' gives it a friendly, inviting feel. To reach Band 5, you can expand your ideas a little more and check your spelling and grammar carefully. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how you can make it even stronger.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer includes all the required information and develops each idea with enough detail to fully inform the reader. Your article has the key facts, but it is a bit short and the description could be richer.</p><ul><li><em>It has alot of athletic players and competitions.</em> → <strong>It has a lot of athletic players and exciting competitions, including a three-point contest and a slam dunk show.</strong> Adding specific examples makes the festival feel real and engaging.</li><li><em>We can spend time with our family playing sports rather than phones.</em> → <strong>We can spend time with our family playing sports together rather than looking at our phones.</strong> This is a nice idea. You could add one more sentence about why that time together matters to you.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an article in a fully effective way, making the text feel lively and complete. Your opening and closing are good, and your informal tone is perfect for a school magazine.</p><ul><li><em>Hello everbody!</em> → <strong>Hello everybody!</strong> Just a small spelling fix. This greeting works well to grab attention.</li><li><em>If anyone has a passion or likes Basketball I recommand you to register for our Basketball festival.</em> → <strong>If you have a passion for basketball or just enjoy playing, I recommend you register for our Basketball Festival.</strong> This version flows more naturally and sounds more like a real recommendation.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear structure with a variety of linking words to connect ideas smoothly. Your article has a logical sequence, but it could use more connectors to make the ideas flow.</p><ul><li><em>It take place at 2 o'clock on 1st of August 2026 at Assumption College Thanburi. What I like about it is We can spend time...</em> → <strong>It takes place at 2 o'clock on 1st August 2026 at Assumption College Thanburi. What I like about it is that we can spend time...</strong> Adding 'that' after 'is' connects the two parts more clearly.</li><li>Try using words like 'also', 'because', or 'for example' to link your ideas. For instance, after saying there are competitions, you could add: 'For example, there is a three-point contest that is always very exciting.'</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with good control, and errors are rare. Your main challenge is spelling and subject-verb agreement. Let's fix these patterns.</p><ul><li><em>everbody</em> → <strong>everybody</strong>. This is a common spelling mistake. Remember: every + body = everybody.</li><li><em>recommand</em> → <strong>recommend</strong>. The correct spelling has two 'm's: re-comm-end.</li><li><em>alot</em> → <strong>a lot</strong>. This is two words, not one. Write 'a lot' with a space.</li><li><em>It take place</em> → <strong>It takes place</strong>. With 'it' (third person singular), the verb needs an 's'. Say 'it takes', 'it has', 'it celebrates'. This is called subject-verb agreement.</li><li><em>We can spend time with our family playing sports rather than phones</em> → <strong>We can spend time with our family playing sports rather than on our phones</strong>. You need the preposition 'on' before 'phones' to make the comparison complete.</li></ul><p>After you revise, read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Does the verb match the subject? Are all the words spelled correctly?' This self-check will help you catch these small but important errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36047	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Hi<br><br>I've seen your notice on an international English website for young people. I want to telling you about my best festival that I have celebrate. It's like a club but don't have alcohol and it's have light sound color light like club, and it allow for the young people. I went to it like 3 times in my life. For the first time I'm really scare it's look like a real club, but around me is a young people, but then I order friedrice and water, It's very delicious. I went with my family because that day is my birthday. I like it because music is very good and the light make it good mood. I think young people should go it it very interesting. I'm very like it. Thank you for reading my email.	144	9.0	Disney, you've written about a festival you clearly enjoy, and I can feel your excitement in lines like <em>the light make it good mood</em> and <em>I like it because music is very good</em>. That personal energy is exactly what draws a reader in. One thing that will help you reach Band 5 is making sure your writing matches the task. This task asked for an article for a school magazine, but you wrote an email reply to a notice. An article should sound like you're sharing an experience with classmates, not responding to someone. Let's look at each part of your writing and see how to push it to the next level.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer makes sure every sentence fits the task exactly. You described a festival you enjoy, which is good, but the task asked for an article, not an email.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I've seen your notice on an international English website for young people.</em> → <strong>Have you ever been to a festival that you'll never forget? Let me tell you about mine.</strong> This opening sounds like an article for a school magazine, not a reply.</li>\n  <li><em>Thank you for reading my email.</em> → <strong>I hope you get a chance to visit this festival someday.</strong> An article ends with an invitation or a thought, not an email closing.</li>\n  <li>You gave good details about the music, lights, and food. Keep those! Just present them as an article writer, not an email writer.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right format and keeps the same tone throughout. Your writing mixes article and email styles, and some sentences are hard to follow.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hi</em> → <strong>No greeting needed.</strong> Articles start with a title or a question, not a greeting like a letter.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm really scare</em> → <strong>I was really scared.</strong> The reader understands, but using the correct past tense makes your meaning clearer.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm very like it</em> → <strong>I really like it.</strong> Small grammar fixes like this help the reader trust your English more.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow easily. Your text has a clear start and end, but the middle jumps around.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I went to it like 3 times in my life. For the first time I'm really scare ... but then I order friedrice and water, It's very delicious. I went with my family because that day is my birthday.</em> → Try grouping your ideas: first paragraph about what the festival is like, second paragraph about your first visit, third paragraph about why you love it. Use <strong>Firstly, Secondly, Finally</strong> to guide the reader.</li>\n  <li><em>and</em> and <em>but</em> appear many times. Try replacing some with <strong>because</strong>, <strong>so</strong>, or <strong>however</strong> to show how ideas connect.</li>\n  <li>Your last sentence <em>I think young people should go it it very interesting</em> feels rushed. End with a clear final thought, like <strong>It's an experience every young person should try.</strong></li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses correct basic grammar and checks their sentences for mistakes. The main pattern to fix is verb forms after <em>want to</em> and <em>like</em>, and subject-verb agreement.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I want to telling you</em> → <strong>I want to tell you</strong>. After <em>want to</em>, use the base form of the verb, not <em>-ing</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>I have celebrate</em> → <strong>I have celebrated</strong> or <strong>I celebrated</strong>. After <em>have</em>, use the past participle form.</li>\n  <li><em>it's have light sound color light like club</em> → <strong>it has lights, sound, and colors like a club</strong>. Use <em>has</em> for possession, not <em>it's have</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm really scare</em> → <strong>I was really scared</strong>. Use past tense for past events, and <em>scared</em> is the adjective form.</li>\n  <li>Check capitalisation: <em>It's</em> should start with a capital letter at the beginning of a sentence. <em>I</em> is always capitalised. <em>friedrice</em> should be <strong>fried rice</strong> (two words).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check: After every sentence, ask yourself: <em>Is this verb in the right tense? Is the subject matching the verb? Did I capitalise the first word and 'I'?</em></p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36052	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! Me and my friend sally usally see a dog a stray dog lying there in front of the school yard. So me and my friend always bring her some dog food and water. We name her lily because she has a pure white fur and her eyes are blue like an ocean. He alway tought her to shakehands or do some new trioles. She also looks abit old. So let me continue the story so as me and my friend arrived at school yestory we saw the bully in our class Tom hitting and drawing in lily skin. Me and my friend was so angry knowing she was ill. Sally walle to Tom and push him and say "Dont do that to her Tom!" Tom shocked because sally is actually a quite girl I was shocked too. Tom ending us saying sorry but lily fainted and didn't wakee up Sally hold lily up and take her to the hospital with me and Tom follow. At the end dacter say lily have an disease and she will die soon. Tom face torn pole and he cried we never see he cried before he said he actually didn't do lily his little brother did first ne don't belive but when sady remember the situation she say that actually ture so to make it up for lily Tom decided to take her as his pet and love her so much. We were shocked the bully in the class weren't an bad person he really kind so nember dont jde people on how they look. In the end lily didn't die she live so long until how and we always visit her In Tom house he became a good friend too.	306	11.0	Benz, you've written a story that genuinely surprised me. The moment where Tom cries and reveals his little brother was the real culprit is a good twist, and you ended with a clear moral. You also correctly used the required opening sentence. The biggest thing holding this story back is the lack of paragraph breaks — over 300 words in one block makes it hard for the reader to follow the timeline. Breaking it into paragraphs will immediately make your story easier and more enjoyable to read.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer includes every important detail the reader needs, with nothing confusing or missing. Your story has all the right events — the dog Lily, the conflict with Tom, the hospital, the apology, the moral — but a few moments need more explanation so the reader doesn't get lost.</p><ul><li><em>Tom ending us saying sorry but lily fainted and didn't wakee up</em> → <strong>Tom ended up saying sorry, but then Lily fainted and didn't wake up.</strong> The reader needs to know that Tom apologised first, then Lily collapsed. Right now the two events blur together.</li><li><em>Tom face torn pole and he cried we never see he cried before he said he actually didn't do lily his little brother did first ne don't belive but when sady remember the situation she say that actually ture</em> → <strong>Tom's face turned pale and he cried. We had never seen him cry before. He said he didn't actually hurt Lily — his little brother did. We didn't believe him at first, but when Sally remembered the situation, she said it was true.</strong> This is the most important moment in the story, but the rush of events makes it hard to follow. Slow down and let each piece of information land.</li><li><em>In the end lily didn't die she live so long until how and we always visit her In Tom house</em> → <strong>In the end, Lily didn't die. She lived a long life, and we always visit her at Tom's house.</strong> The happy ending is clear, but the reader needs to know where 'how' came from. A small revision makes the ending satisfying.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer tells a story in a consistent, natural storytelling voice from start to finish. Your story has a clear beginning, middle, and end, but the voice shifts in a way that pulls the reader out of the world you built.</p><ul><li><em>So let me continue the story</em> → remove this sentence entirely. When you say 'let me continue', you remind the reader that this is a school assignment, not a real story. A Band 5 storyteller just tells the story without announcing the next part.</li><li><em>We name her lily because she has a pure white fur and her eyes are blue like an ocean.</em> This is a lovely, natural description. You sound like a real storyteller here. Keep this voice throughout.</li><li><em>so nember dont jde people on how they look</em> → <strong>so remember: don't judge people by how they look.</strong> The moral is a good idea, but the spelling makes it feel rushed. A clear, correctly spelled moral will leave the reader with the same warm feeling you intended.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer breaks their story into paragraphs and uses clear transitions so the reader never has to stop and figure out what is happening. Your story has one problem that makes it much harder to read than it needs to be: <strong>it is a single block of text with no paragraph breaks.</strong></p><ul><li>In 300+ words with no paragraph breaks, the reader has to work very hard to follow the timeline. Break the story into at least three paragraphs: one for meeting Lily, one for the conflict with Tom, and one for the resolution. Each paragraph should contain events that happen in one scene or time period.</li><li><em>Tom ending us saying sorry but lily fainted and didn't wakee up Sally hold lily up and take her to the hospital with me and Tom follow.</em> → <strong>Tom ended up saying sorry. But then Lily fainted. Sally picked her up and took her to the hospital, with me and Tom following.</strong> Using full stops instead of 'and' or 'but' helps the reader see each event as a separate step in time.</li><li><em>We were shocked the bully in the class weren't an bad person he really kind so nember dont jde people on how they look. In the end lily didn't die she live so long until how and we always visit her In Tom house he became a good friend too.</em> → Split this into sentences: <strong>We were shocked. The bully wasn't a bad person. He was really kind. In the end, Lily didn't die. She lived a long life, and we always visit her at Tom's house. He became a good friend too.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar (past tense, subject-verb agreement, articles, prepositions) so that errors never get in the way of the story. The main rule you need to practise is: <strong>use the past tense consistently for every action in a story that happened yesterday.</strong> Most of your errors come from switching between past and present, or from missing past tense endings. Let's fix the most important ones.</p><ul><li><em>Me and my friend sally usally see a dog a stray dog lying there in front of the school yard.</em> → <strong>My friend Sally and I usually saw a stray dog lying there in front of the school yard.</strong> Two fixes: 'My friend Sally and I' is the correct subject form (not 'Me and my friend'), and 'saw' is the past tense of 'see'. Also, capitalise Sally — it's a name.</li><li><em>We name her lily because she has a pure white fur and her eyes are blue like an ocean.</em> → <strong>We named her Lily because she had pure white fur and her eyes were blue like the ocean.</strong> The whole story is about yesterday, so 'named', 'had', and 'were' must all be past tense. 'A pure white fur' should be 'pure white fur' (no article needed for uncountable nouns).</li><li><em>He alway tought her to shakehands or do some new trioles.</em> → <strong>He always taught her to shake hands or do some new tricks.</strong> 'Always' needs to be spelled correctly. 'Tought' should be 'taught' (past tense of teach). 'Trioles' should be 'tricks'.</li><li><em>dacter say lily have an disease and she will die soon.</em> → <strong>The doctor said Lily had a disease and she would die soon.</strong> 'The doctor' needs the article 'the'. 'Say' should be 'said' (past tense). 'Have' should be 'had' (past tense). 'Will' should be 'would' (past tense for reported speech). 'An disease' should be 'a disease' (use 'a' before consonant sounds).</li><li><em>Tom face torn pole and he cried we never see he cried before</em> → <strong>Tom's face turned pale and he cried. We had never seen him cry before.</strong> 'Tom face' needs an apostrophe: 'Tom's face'. 'Torn pole' should be 'turned pale'. 'We never see he cried' should be 'We had never seen him cry' (past perfect for something that happened before another past event).</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on every sentence: <strong>Is this story about yesterday? If yes, is every main verb in the past tense?</strong> Read your story aloud and listen for verbs that sound present — 'see', 'name', 'say', 'have', 'will'. Change them all to past tense. That single habit will move you from 2/5 to 4/5 in Language.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36111	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! the thing I saw was the school hold a sport festival and me and my friend were surprise, because it was very big and very crazy, a lot of energy they even a sell food and give out prize, the one who play in sport festival, And they even give out shirt, it you play the game and did the mission they even have cocktail practise it you wanna try making one, it you make one you can eat it, and last me and my friend and go, cheer football and cheer badminton.	107	9.0	Wan, you started your story with a strong opening sentence: <em>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible!</em> That is exactly how a story should begin — it makes the reader curious and ready to read more. You also included many interesting details about the sports festival, like the food, the prizes, and the shirts. To reach Band 5, you will need to organise those details into clear, separate sentences so the reader can follow the sequence of events easily. Let me show you how each part of your writing can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer includes all the key details from the task clearly, so the reader understands exactly what happened without guessing. Your story has the right ideas, but some parts are confusing.</p><ul><li><em>they even have cocktail practise it you wanna try making one</em> → The reader does not know what 'cocktail practise' means in a school sports festival. Try: <strong>There was even a fruit cocktail station where you could make your own drink.</strong> This makes the detail clear and believable.</li><li><em>the thing I saw was the school hold a sport festival</em> → You already told us you saw something incredible. You can skip this phrase and go straight to describing the festival. A Band 5 writer trusts the reader to remember what came before.</li><li><em>and last me and my friend and go, cheer football and cheer badminton</em> → The ending is unclear. Did you and your friend cheer for the football and badminton teams? Try: <strong>At the end, my friend and I cheered for the football team and then the badminton team.</strong> This gives the story a clear finish.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer tells a story in a way that the reader can follow easily from beginning to end, with a consistent style throughout. Your story has a good start, but the reader has to work hard to understand what happens next.</p><ul><li><em>it you play the game and did the mission they even have cocktail practise</em> → The reader does not know what 'it' refers to. Try: <strong>If you played the games and completed the missions, you could try the fruit cocktail station.</strong> This makes the condition clear and keeps the story moving smoothly.</li><li><em>me and my friend were surprise, because it was very big and very crazy, a lot of energy</em> → This feels like you are telling the reader your feelings instead of showing them through the story. Try: <strong>My friend and I were surprised. The festival was huge and full of energy.</strong> Short, clear sentences help the reader feel the excitement with you.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses separate sentences and simple time words to guide the reader through the story step by step. Right now, your story is mostly one long sentence, which makes it hard to follow.</p><ul><li><em>...and me and my friend were surprise, because it was very big and very crazy, a lot of energy they even a sell food...</em> → This is too many ideas in one sentence. Break it into smaller parts. Try: <strong>My friend and I were surprised. The festival was very big and very crazy. There was a lot of energy. They even sold food and gave out prizes.</strong> Each sentence gives the reader one clear piece of information.</li><li><em>...it you play the game and did the mission they even have cocktail practise it you wanna try making one, it you make one you can eat it</em> → The word 'it' is used to connect everything, but it creates confusion. Try using 'if' and 'then' to show the order: <strong>If you played the games and finished the missions, you could try the cocktail station. If you made a drink, you could eat it.</strong> This makes the sequence clear.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar correctly most of the time, so the reader can understand every sentence without guessing. The main rule that will help you is: every sentence needs a clear subject and a verb that matches it. Let's look at your sentences.</p><ul><li><em>the thing I saw was the school hold a sport festival</em> → <strong>The thing I saw was the school holding a sports festival.</strong> After 'was', use the '-ing' form of the verb. This is a common pattern in English: 'was holding', 'was playing', 'was selling'. Also, 'sport festival' should be <strong>sports festival</strong>.</li><li><em>me and my friend were surprise</em> → <strong>my friend and I were surprised</strong>. 'Surprised' is the correct adjective form here. Also, put yourself second: 'my friend and I' sounds more natural than 'me and my friend'.</li><li><em>it you play the game</em> → <strong>if you played the game</strong>. The word 'it' is not the same as 'if'. 'If' introduces a condition. This error appears three times in your story, so practise using 'if' for conditions.</li><li><em>they even a sell food and give out prize</em> → <strong>they even sold food and gave out prizes</strong>. After 'they', you need a verb in the past tense: 'sold', 'gave'. Also, 'prize' should be plural 'prizes' because they gave out more than one.</li><li><em>the thing I saw was the school hold a sport festival</em> → Notice the capital T in 'the'. In the middle of a sentence, do not capitalise words unless they are proper nouns. Check your whole story for random capitals. A good self-check: read each sentence from the first word to the period. If a word is capitalised but is not the first word of the sentence, ask yourself: is it a person's name or a place name? If not, use a lowercase letter.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36122	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		The festival that me and my family celebrate is Christmas day! It's on December, we've celebrate at our house. Alot of my cousin will come and celebrate together. Before that day my grandma will ask what we want on Christmas day I'm very excited because, I know that I will certainly get a present! I prepare my clothes for that day only. Last year I wear red shirt with jeans and a green socks. What I like the most is that we'll play alot of games together. Hide and seek, music chair, tags and other alot of games. That's make me very fun and become my favorite festival!	113	14.0	Yada, your article about Christmas Day has a warm, personal feel — the reader can tell this is a celebration you truly love. You covered all the task requirements: when, where, and why. To reach Band 5, the main change is to make your writing feel like a magazine article rather than a diary entry. That means adding a title, a more engaging opening, and a tone that addresses your readers directly.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>The festival that me and my family celebrate is Christmas day!</em> You introduced the topic directly and made the reader curious to learn more.</li><li><em>It's on December, we've celebrate at our house.</em> You clearly stated the time and place, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>What I like the most is that we'll play alot of games together. Hide and seek, music chair, tags and other alot of games.</em> You gave specific examples of the games, so the reader can picture the fun.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of a magazine article to engage the reader — a catchy title, an interesting opening, and a tone that feels like sharing with classmates rather than writing in a diary. Your article shares your personal experience, which is good, but it reads more like a personal diary entry than a school magazine piece.</p><ul><li>Your article has no title. A magazine article needs a title that grabs attention, like <strong>Why Christmas Is My Favourite Day of the Year</strong>. This tells the reader what to expect and makes them curious.</li><li><em>The festival that me and my family celebrate is Christmas day!</em> → <strong>Every December, my family and I celebrate Christmas Day — my favourite festival of the year.</strong> This opening is more direct and sounds like the start of a magazine article, not a casual diary entry.</li><li><em>That's make me very fun and become my favorite festival!</em> → <strong>That is why Christmas is my favourite festival!</strong> A strong closing sentence wraps up your article and leaves the reader with a clear impression of your feelings.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer groups related ideas into clear paragraphs and uses a variety of linking words to show the order of events. Your article is one long paragraph, which makes it harder for the reader to follow the sequence. Let's fix that.</p><ul><li><em>Before that day my grandma will ask what we want on Christmas day I'm very excited because, I know that I will certainly get a present!</em> → Break this into two sentences: <strong>Before that day, my grandma asks what we want for Christmas. I'm very excited because I know I will certainly get a present!</strong> One idea per sentence makes your writing clearer.</li><li><em>I prepare my clothes for that day only. Last year I wear red shirt with jeans and a green socks.</em> → <strong>I prepare my clothes just for that day. Last year, I wore a red shirt with jeans and green socks.</strong> Adding 'last year' as a time marker at the start of the second sentence helps the reader see the shift from general habit to a specific memory.</li><li><em>Hide and seek, music chair, tags and other alot of games.</em> → <strong>We play hide and seek, musical chairs, tag, and many other games.</strong> This turns a list into a complete sentence and connects it to the previous sentence about playing games.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic tenses and subject-verb agreement with only occasional small mistakes. Your article has several errors that follow a pattern: you sometimes use the wrong verb form after helping verbs like 'have' and 'do', and you sometimes use the present tense when the past tense is needed. Let's work on that.</p><ul><li><em>we've celebrate</em> → <strong>we celebrate</strong>. The contraction 'we've' means 'we have', so the next verb should be 'celebrated' (past participle). But since you're describing a general habit, use simple present: 'we celebrate'.</li><li><em>Last year I wear</em> → <strong>Last year I wore</strong>. 'Last year' is a past time marker, so the verb must be in past tense.</li><li><em>a green socks</em> → <strong>green socks</strong>. 'Socks' is plural, so you don't need 'a'. Use 'a' only with singular countable nouns.</li><li><em>other alot of games</em> → <strong>many other games</strong>. 'Alot' is not a word; the correct form is 'a lot'. But 'a lot of other games' is a bit repetitive. 'Many other games' sounds more natural in this context.</li><li><em>That's make me very fun</em> → <strong>That makes it very fun</strong>. 'That's' means 'that is', so the next verb should be 'making' or you should use 'that makes'. Also, 'make me fun' means 'make me funny', but you mean 'make the festival fun'. Use 'it' instead of 'me'.</li></ul><p>Here's a self-check you can use on any sentence: Ask yourself, 'Does this sentence describe something that happens every year (present tense) or something that happened one time in the past (past tense)?' If it's past, change the verb to past tense. If it's present, make sure the verb matches the subject (I celebrate, we celebrate, she celebrates).</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36127	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		The secrat festival\nAs my friend and I arrived at School yesterday morning,\nWe saw somthing incredible! We saw our gym are\nfills with sciend project a giant sign that writ Sciend\nFestivals Who every win the sciend Project will recive a\n50,000 bath you only one day to make it the best\nsciend project.	56	8.0	Pang Pang, you started your story with the exact sentence from the task, which shows you understood the assignment. The idea of a science festival with a competition is a creative direction, and your excitement comes through when you describe the gym filled with projects. To reach Band 5, your story needs to be longer and have a clear beginning, middle, and end. A story is not just a description of one moment; it needs events that happen one after another, leading to a conclusion. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how you can build this out.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes enough details to tell a complete story with a clear start, middle, and end. Your story introduces the festival and the competition, but it stops there.</p><ul><li><em>As my friend and I arrived at School yesterday morning, We saw somthing incredible!</em> This is a good opening. Now you need to continue the story. What happened next? Did you and your friend decide to join the competition? What did you build? <strong>We decided to enter the competition together. We spent the whole day building a volcano model.</strong></li><li><em>We saw our gym are fills with sciend project a giant sign that writ Sciend Festivals</em> You describe the scene, but the story needs action. <strong>We saw the gym filled with science projects and a giant sign that said 'Science Festival'. My friend pointed at a poster about the competition.</strong></li><li><em>Who every win the sciend Project will recive a 50,000 bath you only one day to make it the best sciend project.</em> This explains the prize, but a story needs an ending. Did you win? Did you learn something? <strong>We worked all night and finished our project just in time. In the end, we didn't win the prize, but we had so much fun.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of a story consistently, so the reader can follow the narrative without effort. Your text reads more like a description than a story.</p><ul><li><em>We saw our gym are fills with sciend project a giant sign that writ Sciend Festivals</em> This is a description of what you saw. A story needs characters doing things. <strong>We walked into the gym and saw science projects everywhere. A giant sign said 'Science Festival'.</strong></li><li><em>Who every win the sciend Project will recive a 50,000 bath</em> The phrase 'Who every win' is unclear. Use 'whoever wins' to make the meaning clear. <strong>Whoever wins the science project competition will receive 50,000 baht.</strong></li><li>Your story has no dialogue or thoughts from the characters. Adding a line like <strong>'This is our chance!' my friend whispered excitedly.</strong> would make it feel more like a real story.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the story flows from one sentence to the next. Your sentences feel separate and the story ends suddenly.</p><ul><li><em>As my friend and I arrived at School yesterday morning, We saw somthing incredible!</em> Good start. But then you jump straight to describing the gym. Use a linking word. <strong>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible. When we went inside, the gym was filled with science projects.</strong></li><li><em>We saw our gym are fills with sciend project a giant sign that writ Sciend Festivals Who every win the sciend Project will recive a 50,000 bath</em> This is one long, run-on sentence. Break it into shorter sentences. <strong>We saw the gym filled with science projects. A giant sign said 'Science Festival'. Whoever wins the competition will receive 50,000 baht.</strong></li><li>Your story has no ending. A story needs a final sentence that wraps things up. <strong>It was the most exciting day of my life.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and spelling so the reader can understand the story easily. Your main challenge is subject-verb agreement and spelling. Let's fix these step by step.</p><ul><li><em>gym are fills</em> → <strong>gym was filled</strong>. 'Gym' is singular, so the verb must be 'was', not 'are'. 'Fills' should be 'filled' because it is a past tense story.</li><li><em>sciend</em> → <strong>science</strong>. This is a spelling error. 'Science' is spelled s-c-i-e-n-c-e. Practice writing it three times: science, science, science.</li><li><em>Who every win</em> → <strong>Whoever wins</strong>. 'Whoever' is one word, and after it we use 'wins' (third person singular).</li><li><em>recive</em> → <strong>receive</strong>. Remember the rule: 'i before e except after c'. Re-c-e-i-v-e.</li><li><em>bath</em> → <strong>baht</strong>. 'Bath' is what you take to get clean. 'Baht' is Thai money. They sound similar but are spelled differently.</li><li><em>you only one day</em> → <strong>you have only one day</strong>. This sentence is missing a verb. Every sentence needs a verb. 'Have' is the missing verb here.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After you write a sentence, read it aloud. Does every sentence have a verb? Is every word spelled correctly? This will help you catch errors before you finish.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29765	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Dear, Corey<br><br>I am writing to you, Thank you for kindness I'm excited to hear that!<br>But I'm think we can't go together because, In My Saturday morning I've to practice my voice for the next competition in next two months, If the festival in the afternoon Can you pick me up at my house?<br><br>Oh! Sorry I promise something, My family have booked dinner at hotel restaurant I want to go with you but I don't have enough Can be next time, So this ticket you can keep it to other people And don't forget to take some pictures for me when I have time you tell me how it was<br>Best regards<br>August	128	8.0	August, your email to Corey shows you understood the task was a reply to an invitation, and you included some important details like your Saturday morning practice and the dinner booking. Your opening line <em>Thank you for kindness I'm excited to hear that!</em> gets the friendly tone started, which is a good instinct. To reach Band 5, you need to respond to every note in the task and keep the message clear and complete. Let's look at each area where you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer responds to all the notes in the task clearly and does not add information that confuses the reader. You reacted to the news and mentioned transport, but you did not clearly disagree about the film choice or suggest what to bring. Instead, you declined the whole invitation, which was not asked for.</p><ul><li><em>But I'm think we can't go together because...</em> → <strong>I disagree about the film choice. I'd prefer something more exciting.</strong> The task asked you to react to the film choice, not to cancel the plan.</li><li><em>So this ticket you can keep it to other people</em> → <strong>I think we should bring some snacks and drinks.</strong> The task asked you to suggest what to bring, not to give away the ticket.</li><li><em>Can be next time</em> → <strong>I can bring my favourite board game.</strong> You don't need to postpone the whole event. Just answer what to bring.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent, polite register throughout an email and makes the message easy to follow. You started politely but then switched to an apologetic tone that confused the reader.</p><ul><li><em>Thank you for kindness</em> → <strong>Thank you for your kind invitation.</strong> This is a more natural and complete way to show gratitude in an email.</li><li><em>Oh! Sorry I promise something</em> → <strong>I'm sorry, but I already have plans.</strong> The word 'Oh!' and the broken promise make the email sound messy. A calm, clear apology works better.</li><li><em>you tell me how it was</em> → <strong>Please tell me how it was.</strong> Adding 'Please' keeps the polite tone you started with.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow the sequence without effort. You used paragraph breaks, which is good, but the ideas jumped from declining to asking for pictures without clear links.</p><ul><li><em>But I'm think we can't go together because... If the festival in the afternoon Can you pick me up at my house?</em> → <strong>But I don't think we can go together because I have practice. However, if the festival is in the afternoon, could you pick me up at my house?</strong> Adding 'However' shows the shift from problem to possible solution.</li><li><em>Oh! Sorry I promise something... So this ticket you can keep it to other people And don't forget to take some pictures for me</em> → <strong>I'm sorry, but my family has booked dinner. Could you give the ticket to someone else? Also, please take some pictures for me.</strong> Using 'Also' connects the two requests clearly.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar correctly and chooses vocabulary that fits the task. Your main challenge is verb forms: you often use the base verb when you need a different tense or an '-ing' form. Let's fix that.</p><ul><li><em>I'm think</em> → <strong>I think</strong> or <strong>I don't think</strong>. After 'I'm' you need an adjective or an '-ing' verb, not a base verb. 'I'm excited' is correct because 'excited' is an adjective. 'I think' is correct because there is no 'am'.</li><li><em>I've to practice</em> → <strong>I have to practice</strong> or <strong>I've got to practice</strong>. 'I've' is short for 'I have', but 'have to' is the fixed phrase for obligation. 'I've to' is not standard.</li><li><em>In My Saturday morning</em> → <strong>On Saturday morning</strong>. Use 'on' for days, not 'in'. Also, 'my' is not needed here because the day is clear.</li><li><em>My family have booked dinner at hotel restaurant</em> → <strong>My family has booked dinner at a hotel restaurant.</strong> 'Family' is singular in this context, so use 'has'. Also add 'a' before 'hotel restaurant'.</li><li><em>I don't have enough</em> → <strong>I don't have enough time</strong> or <strong>I don't have enough money</strong>. The sentence is incomplete. Always finish the thought so the reader knows what you don't have enough of.</li></ul><p>Self-check: Before you write a verb, ask yourself 'Is this after 'am' or 'is' or 'are'? If yes, the verb needs '-ing'. If not, use the base form or add 'to' for obligation.'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30334	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hello<br><br>Thanks For ask me to come wonderful Festival, It looks amazing in the review. You can pick me at front entrance. It can be easy there. Activities that I recommend is build a robot because you can learn how to build it by your self. That way it will be more fun together. So I afraid I will disagree with you. As for item that we will need of course money to my gifeshop and phone because some shops you can only pay in apps. Also watch is important to so we can cheeck the time there.<br><br>Can't wait!<br><br>Pran	108	15.0	Pran, your email has real energy and a clear personality. You did an excellent job addressing every single point from the task: you thanked Corey, stated your preference for pickup, disagreed about the film and suggested building a robot instead, and listed the items you think you should bring. That is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. The reader finishes your email knowing exactly what you want and why. To reach Band 5 across all areas, we just need to polish the structure and grammar so your good ideas come through more clearly.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Thanks For ask me to come wonderful Festival</em> — You immediately showed a positive reaction, which the task asked for. The reader knows you are excited.</li><li><em>You can pick me at front entrance</em> — You clearly stated your preference for where to be picked up. Simple and direct.</li><li><em>Activities that I recommend is build a robot because you can learn how to build it by your self</em> — You disagreed with Corey's film idea and offered a different activity with a reason. This fully addresses the task point.</li><li><em>Also watch is important to so we can cheeck the time there</em> — You suggested an extra item beyond money and phone, showing you thought beyond the basic requirements.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an email to a friend consistently, with a clear salutation and a tone that stays friendly and natural throughout. Your email is enthusiastic and the reader can follow it easily, but it starts a bit abruptly with just 'Hello' and then jumps straight into the content. Adding a proper greeting like 'Dear Corey,' or 'Hi Corey,' would make it feel more like a real email. Also, some phrases like 'So I afraid I will disagree with you' sound a little stiff. A friend would say something more natural like 'I'm afraid I disagree with you' or 'I actually disagree with you.' Your closing 'Can't wait!' and your name are perfect for the friendly tone.</p><ul><li><em>Hello</em> → <strong>Hi Corey,</strong> or <strong>Dear Corey,</strong>. A salutation with the friend's name makes the email feel personal from the start.</li><li><em>So I afraid I will disagree with you</em> → <strong>So I'm afraid I disagree with you</strong>. Adding 'am' after 'I' fixes the grammar and keeps the friendly, polite tone.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs to separate different ideas and a wider range of linking words to show connections between sentences. Your email is one long paragraph, which makes it harder for the reader to see where one topic ends and another begins. You used 'because', 'that way', 'as for', and 'also', which are good basic connectors, but you can add more variety to make your writing flow more smoothly.</p><ul><li><em>Thanks For ask me to come wonderful Festival... You can pick me at front entrance... Activities that I recommend is build a robot... As for item that we will need of course money...</em> → Break your email into 3 short paragraphs: 1) greeting and reaction, 2) transport and activity, 3) items to bring. Each paragraph should focus on one main idea.</li><li><em>That way it will be more fun together. So I afraid I will disagree with you.</em> → <strong>That way it will be more fun together. However, I'm afraid I disagree with you about the film.</strong> Using 'however' instead of 'so' shows a contrast more clearly.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple and some complex grammatical forms with few errors. The main pattern that needs attention is subject-verb agreement and missing verbs (especially 'am', 'is', 'are'). Let's look at your sentences and fix them step by step.</p><ul><li><em>Thanks For ask me to come wonderful Festival</em> → <strong>Thanks for asking me to come to the wonderful festival.</strong> After 'for', use the -ing form of the verb. Also, 'Festival' is a common noun here, so it should be lowercase unless it's part of a name.</li><li><em>Activities that I recommend is build a robot</em> → <strong>The activity that I recommend is building a robot</strong> or <strong>One activity I recommend is to build a robot.</strong> 'Activities' is plural, so the verb should be 'are', but the sentence works better with a singular subject. Also, after 'is', use 'building' (gerund) or 'to build' (infinitive).</li><li><em>So I afraid I will disagree with you</em> → <strong>So I'm afraid I disagree with you.</strong> You need the verb 'am' after 'I' to make 'I am afraid'.</li><li><em>Also watch is important to so we can cheeck the time there</em> → <strong>Also, a watch is important so we can check the time there.</strong> Add 'a' before 'watch', remove 'to' before 'so', and spell 'check' correctly.</li><li><em>Thanks For ask me to come wonderful Festival</em> — Notice the capital 'F' in 'For' and 'Festival'. In the middle of a sentence, only proper nouns and the word 'I' should be capitalised. Check every sentence for random capitals.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After every sentence, ask yourself: 'Do I have a main verb? Is my subject singular or plural? Does my verb match?' This will catch most of these errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30335	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>Thanks for your last email, that's great we can hangout in science festival this Saturday. I'll take the bus so we can meet at the front entrance. And for activites, I'd like to build a robot, because build a robot seem fun for me and I've already watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film. <3 As for food I think we can bring our lunch box. That way we'll eat things we want. And I'd like to suggest that we should bring rain coat. The reason is in 12 o'clock it might rain, umbrella is too heavy to carry around. Don't forget to meet me at front entrance.<br><br>Can't wait!<br><br>Prin R	127	15.0	Prin R, your email to Corey is full of energy and clear purpose. You did an excellent job addressing every single point from the task: you reacted positively to the plan, chose a specific activity, explained your food suggestion, and even added a thoughtful weather-related recommendation. The way you connected your ideas with phrases like <em>That way we'll eat things we want</em> shows you were thinking about Corey's experience, not just your own. To reach even higher, focus on polishing your grammar so your strong ideas come across without any confusion. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>that's great we can hangout in science festival this Saturday.</em> You clearly showed a positive reaction to the plan, which the task required.</li><li><em>I'd like to build a robot, because build a robot seem fun for me and I've already watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film.</em> You disagreed with Corey's film suggestion and gave a personal reason. The reader understands your choice completely.</li><li><em>I'd like to suggest that we should bring rain coat. The reason is in 12 o'clock it might rain, umbrella is too heavy to carry around.</em> You suggested a practical item and explained why. This fully informs Corey about your thinking.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistently appropriate and natural friendly tone throughout, without any phrases that feel slightly off for an email to a friend. Your greeting, closing, and enthusiasm are all strong.</p><ul><li><em>hangout in science festival</em> → <strong>hang out at the science festival</strong>. 'Hang out' is fine between friends, but 'hangout' is a noun (a place to relax). Using 'at' instead of 'in' also sounds more natural for a specific event.</li><li><em>And for activites</em> → <strong>As for activities</strong>. You used 'and' here, but 'as for' is a smoother way to shift topics in an email. You actually used 'as for' correctly later with food, so try to use it consistently.</li><li>Your closing <em>Can't wait!</em> is perfect for a friend. Keep that energy.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear, logical structure without any repeated or confusing sentences. Your email moves from greeting to transport, activities, food, and a suggestion, which is a good order.</p><ul><li><em>Don't forget to meet me at front entrance.</em> You already said <em>we can meet at the front entrance</em> earlier. Repeating the same idea at the end makes the email feel less organised. Remove the last sentence or replace it with a new detail, like <strong>See you at the front entrance!</strong> as a quick reminder, not a full repeat.</li><li><em>And for activites</em> → <strong>As for activities</strong>. Starting a sentence with 'And' is fine in informal writing, but using 'As for' creates a clearer topic shift and shows better paragraph flow.</li><li>Your use of <em>That way</em> and <em>The reason is</em> helps connect your ideas logically. Keep using these kinds of linking phrases.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently and avoids errors that make the reader pause. Your vocabulary is good, but several grammar patterns need attention. The main rule to focus on is: <strong>after 'to' or a modal verb (can, will, should, would), use the base form of the verb, not a past tense or an -ing form.</strong> Also, remember to use 'a' or 'an' before singular countable nouns.</p><ul><li><em>I've already watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film</em> → <strong>I've already watched the Discovering Dinosaurs film</strong>. After 'have' (in present perfect), you need the past participle 'watched', not the base form 'watch'.</li><li><em>build a robot seem fun</em> → <strong>building a robot seems fun</strong>. When an action is the subject of a sentence, use the -ing form ('building'). Also, 'seem' needs an 's' because 'building a robot' is singular.</li><li><em>in 12 o'clock it might rain</em> → <strong>at 12 o'clock it might rain</strong>. Use 'at' for specific times, not 'in'.</li><li><em>umbrella is too heavy to carry around</em> → <strong>an umbrella is too heavy to carry around</strong>. 'Umbrella' is a singular countable noun, so it needs 'a' or 'an' before it. Same issue with <em>rain coat</em> → <strong>a rain coat</strong> and <em>lunch box</em> → <strong>a lunch box</strong>.</li><li><em>for activites</em> → <strong>for activities</strong>. Watch your spelling of 'activities'. A quick self-check: read your email aloud slowly. If a word sounds wrong, look it up.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30365	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi, Corey<br><br>Great, I know that you have a tickets for the science festival and thats a good idea.<br><br>The idea that you say" we could meet at the front entrance" thats good but, I prefer you're pick me up.<br><br>Yeab, but I like to see how to build a robot more than the discovering dinosaurs film.<br><br>Ok, I'll not forget the pocket money because I'll be there all day. I think we should something like snacks and the book about the robot or the discovering dinosaurs film too because it have a detail about these two things.	109	12.0	Anpan, you did a great job covering all the main points the task asked for. You told Corey you were happy about the festival, said you preferred to be picked up, disagreed about the film, and suggested bringing snacks and a book. That's exactly what an email to a friend needs. The part where you wrote <em>I'll not forget the pocket money because I'll be there all day</em> shows you're thinking like a real writer, connecting your ideas with <em>because</em>. To reach Band 5, you'll need to polish your grammar and make your sentences flow more smoothly. Let's look at each area one by one.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for content. A Band 5 writer covers every point from the task completely and clearly. You included all the required ideas, but one suggestion was a little unclear.</p><ul><li><em>I think we should something like snacks and the book about the robot or the discovering dinosaurs film too because it have a detail about these two things.</em> → <strong>I think we should bring snacks and a book about robots or dinosaurs, because it has details about both.</strong> The reader can guess your meaning, but the missing verb <em>bring</em> and the unclear <em>it</em> make the sentence confusing. A small fix makes your idea crystal clear.</li><li><em>I like to see how to build a robot more than the discovering dinosaurs film.</em> → <strong>I'd like to see how to build a robot more than the dinosaur film.</strong> You stated your preference clearly. Just a small grammar adjustment makes it sound natural.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a friendly, consistent tone throughout and writes every phrase in a way a friend would understand easily. Your opening <em>Hi, Corey</em> and the overall friendly tone are good, but a few phrases feel awkward.</p><ul><li><em>Yeab, but I like to see how to build a robot more than the discovering dinosaurs film.</em> → <strong>Actually, I'd rather see how to build a robot than watch the dinosaur film.</strong> <em>Yeab</em> is not a real word in English. Using <em>Actually</em> keeps the friendly disagreement tone while sounding natural.</li><li><em>thats good but, I prefer you're pick me up.</em> → <strong>That's good, but I'd prefer you to pick me up.</strong> The comma should come before <em>but</em>, and <em>you're</em> (you are) is not the same as <em>your</em> or the structure needed here. A friend would understand, but the grammar distracts from your message.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects paragraphs smoothly so the reader feels a natural flow from one idea to the next. You separated each main point into its own paragraph, which is good. But the jumps between paragraphs feel sudden.</p><ul><li><em>Great, I know that you have a tickets for the science festival and thats a good idea.</em> → <strong>Great, I know you have tickets for the science festival, and that's a good idea.</strong> Your first paragraph responds to Corey's news. The next paragraph starts with <em>The idea that you say...</em> which repeats information. Try starting with <em>About meeting at the front entrance...</em> to move the conversation forward.</li><li>Your paragraphs follow a logical order: reaction, meeting plan, film choice, what to bring. That's strong. To reach Band 5, add a short linking phrase at the start of paragraphs 3 and 4, like <em>About the film</em> or <em>As for what to bring</em>.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar most of the time and chooses vocabulary that fits the task. You have good ideas and some useful words like <em>tickets</em>, <em>science festival</em>, and <em>pocket money</em>. The main pattern causing most of your errors is subject-verb agreement and missing verbs. Let's fix that.</p><ul><li><em>I know that you have a tickets</em> → <strong>I know that you have tickets</strong>. <em>Tickets</em> is plural, so it does not need <em>a</em>. Use <em>a</em> only before singular countable nouns: <em>a ticket</em> (one) or <em>tickets</em> (more than one).</li><li><em>I prefer you're pick me up</em> → <strong>I prefer you to pick me up</strong>. <em>You're</em> means <em>you are</em>. Here you need the structure <em>prefer + someone + to + verb</em>. Say it aloud: <em>I prefer you to pick me up</em>.</li><li><em>I think we should something like snacks</em> → <strong>I think we should bring something like snacks</strong>. After <em>should</em>, you need a main verb. <em>Should bring</em>, <em>should take</em>, <em>should buy</em>. The verb cannot be missing.</li><li><em>it have a detail about these two things</em> → <strong>it has details about these two things</strong>. <em>It</em> is third person singular, so the verb must be <em>has</em>, not <em>have</em>. Also <em>a detail</em> sounds like only one fact. Use <em>details</em> (plural) because a book has many details.</li><li>Check your capital letters. <em>thats</em> should be <em>that's</em> (with an apostrophe and a capital T at the start of a sentence). The same applies to <em>yeab</em> and <em>ok</em> — when they start a sentence, capitalise them. A good self-check: read your email aloud and ask yourself, 'Does every sentence start with a capital letter?'</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30378	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey<br><br>That great about the tickets!<br><br>We can meet at the front of entrance or near at entrance. That are good for me<br><br>I think build the robot. it more interesting that Discovering Dinosaurs film because build the robot it need to use the brian that I like it. But Discovering Dinosaurs flim for me I think it bored and I like to play more watch the flim.<br><br>We can bring some little camera and some notebook to write something. And I will bring some 600 $/ I think that good for the trip	102	11.0	Ti, your email to Corey shows you really understood the task. You said yes to the tickets, chose a meeting point, disagreed about the film, and suggested what to bring. That is exactly what the prompt asked for, and your idea to build a robot instead of watching the dinosaur film is creative and personal. To reach Band 5, you will need to make your grammar more accurate and connect your ideas more smoothly so the reader can follow your thinking without any confusion. Let's look at each area in detail.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer covers every required point completely and clearly. You did cover all the main points, but the suggestion about pocket money was already in the prompt, so that part is a little less original.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That great about the tickets!</em> You reacted positively to the tickets, which is exactly right. To make it even stronger, add a reason: <strong>That is great about the tickets! I am really excited to go with you.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>We can meet at the front of entrance or near at entrance.</em> You gave a clear preference for the meeting point. This is well done.</li>\n  <li><em>I think build the robot.</em> You clearly disagreed about the film and suggested an alternative. That is a good response to the task.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone and register consistent throughout the whole email. Your opening and closing are friendly, which is good, but some phrases are too informal or unclear for a reader to follow easily.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That great about the tickets!</em> → <strong>That is great about the tickets!</strong> Adding the verb 'is' makes the sentence complete and more natural for an email to a friend.</li>\n  <li><em>That are good for me</em> → <strong>That is good for me</strong> or <strong>Those are good for me</strong>. 'That' is singular, so it needs 'is'. If you mean multiple options, use 'Those are'.</li>\n  <li><em>I like to play more watch the flim</em> → <strong>I would rather play than watch the film</strong>. The meaning is a bit unclear here. Using 'would rather' or 'prefer' makes your opinion much clearer.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses linking words and clear paragraph structure so each idea flows naturally into the next. You did separate each topic into its own paragraph, which is good, but the connections between your ideas are weak, especially in the third paragraph about the robot and the film.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think build the robot. it more interesting that Discovering Dinosaurs film because build the robot it need to use the brian that I like it. But Discovering Dinosaurs flim for me I think it bored and I like to play more watch the flim.</em> This paragraph jumps between ideas. Try this structure: <strong>I think we should build a robot instead of watching the Discovering Dinosaurs film. Building a robot is more interesting because you have to use your brain, and I like that. The film seems boring to me, and I would rather play than watch it.</strong> Using 'instead of', 'because', and 'rather than' helps the reader follow your reasoning.</li>\n  <li>You used 'But' to contrast, which is fine, but try using 'However' or 'On the other hand' for variety. For example: <strong>However, the Discovering Dinosaurs film seems boring to me.</strong></li>\n  <li>You repeated 'I think' and 'it' many times. Try replacing some with phrases like 'In my opinion' or 'For me' to make your writing more varied.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with few errors. Your basic vocabulary is good for the task, but there are frequent grammar mistakes that make some sentences hard to understand. The main rule to focus on is that every sentence needs a main verb (is, are, have, build, etc.) — you often leave it out.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That great about the tickets!</em> → <strong>That is great about the tickets!</strong> You need the verb 'is' after 'That'. Every sentence needs a verb.</li>\n  <li><em>I think build the robot</em> → <strong>I think we should build the robot</strong>. After 'think', you need a full clause with a subject ('we') and a verb ('should build').</li>\n  <li><em>it more interesting that Discovering Dinosaurs film</em> → <strong>It is more interesting than the Discovering Dinosaurs film</strong>. You need 'is' for the verb, and 'than' for comparison, not 'that'. Also, capitalise 'It' at the start of a sentence.</li>\n  <li><em>build the robot it need to use the brian that I like it</em> → <strong>Building the robot needs you to use your brain, and I like that</strong>. 'Brian' is a name; 'brain' is the organ you think with. Also, avoid repeating 'it' after the subject.</li>\n  <li><em>I will bring some 600 $</em> → <strong>I will bring about 600 dollars</strong>. The dollar sign goes before the number ($600), and 'some' is not needed with a specific amount. Use 'about' or 'around' for an estimate.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check: Read each sentence aloud. Does it have a main verb (is, are, have, can, will, etc.)? If not, add one. Also check that every sentence starts with a capital letter.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30489	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hello!<br><br>Thanks for texting me! I am very excited after reading the text you sent. I am very happy that you got the tickets for me too. Also thankyou for going to pick me up we can meet at the front entrance as you said it'll be easy for both of us. As for the activities we are going to it kinda hard to choose because I want to do both of the activities why did they need to happen at the same time? Maybe it's bad luck but I will choose to build a robot I think this one is a bit more interesting for me. About the pocket money I thinks that I will bring it too but I don't know how much should I take there 100,500,1000? Please informed me. Also don't forget to bring your student id there I heard if we gave them the card we can get into health exhibition that we normally need to pay for free.<br><br>I am looking forward to seeing you!<br><br>Mil	178	15.0	Mil, your email is full of energy and genuine excitement. The way you wrote <em>I am very excited after reading the text you sent</em> and <em>I am very happy that you got the tickets for me too</em> makes your reader feel your enthusiasm right away. You addressed every point from the task: the tickets, meeting at the front entrance, choosing an activity, pocket money, and the student ID. That is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you need to tighten your grammar and make your sentences flow more smoothly. Let me show you how.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thanks for texting me! I am very excited after reading the text you sent.</em> You opened warmly and showed your feelings clearly. The reader knows you are happy.</li>\n  <li><em>I will choose to build a robot I think this one is a bit more interesting for me.</em> You stated your choice and gave a personal reason. That is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>Also don't forget to bring your student id there I heard if we gave them the card we can get into health exhibition that we normally need to pay for free.</em> You included a helpful suggestion about the student ID, which shows you thought about the practical side of the outing.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone friendly but uses consistently appropriate informal English. Your email sounds like a real message to a friend, which is great. The phrase <em>it kinda hard to choose</em> is a bit too casual. Try <strong>it is kind of hard to choose</strong> or <strong>it is a bit hard to choose</strong>. Also, <em>why did they need to happen at the same time?</em> sounds a little dramatic for an email to a friend. A smoother version would be <strong>I wish they were not at the same time</strong> or <strong>it is a shame they are at the same time</strong>. Keep your friendly tone, but choose words that feel natural without being too slangy.</p>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. You used paragraph breaks, which is good, but the shift from <em>I will choose to build a robot</em> to <em>About the pocket money</em> feels sudden. Try adding a linking sentence like <strong>Now, about the pocket money</strong> or <strong>While we are planning, I also wanted to ask about pocket money</strong>. You used <em>also</em>, <em>as for</em>, and <em>about</em>, which help, but varying your connectors will make the email flow better. For example, instead of starting every new idea with <em>also</em>, try <em>next</em>, <em>another thing</em>, or <em>by the way</em>.</p>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling with few errors. The main pattern holding you back is missing or incorrect verb forms. Let's fix that.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>thankyou for going to pick me up</em> → <strong>thank you for picking me up</strong>. 'Thank you' is two words, and after 'thank you for', use the -ing form of the verb.</li>\n  <li><em>it kinda hard to choose</em> → <strong>it is kind of hard to choose</strong>. You need the verb 'is' after 'it'. 'Kinda' is very informal; 'kind of' is better for this task.</li>\n  <li><em>I thinks that I will bring it too</em> → <strong>I think that I will bring it too</strong>. With 'I', use 'think' (no 's'). The -s ending is only for he/she/it.</li>\n  <li><em>Please informed me</em> → <strong>Please inform me</strong>. After 'please', use the base form of the verb (no -ed, no -s).</li>\n  <li>Check your capitalisation: <em>student id</em> should be <strong>student ID</strong>, and <em>health exhibition</em> should be <strong>Health Exhibition</strong> if it is a specific event. Also, <em>there</em> in <em>there 100,500,1000?</em> should be <strong>there? 100, 500, or 1000?</strong> (add a question mark and commas between numbers).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After you write a sentence, ask yourself: 'Did I put the right verb form? Did I add 'is' or 'are' where needed?' That self-check will catch most of these errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30490	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hello,<br><br>That sounds awesome! It will be nice to go to that festival, I love the idea that we could meet at the front entrance because I don't want to bothered your cousin. As for the activity, I agree with you about Discovering Dinosaur. I'd like to choose it because it sounds a lot more entertain and fun than building robot. For the pocket money, I think Five hundred Baht will be enough for us if we don't waste it on some toys at the festival and use it for foods and festival tour only. As for the stuff we should bring, I suggest that sunglasses, hat and umbrella will be very important in this type of weather.<br><br>I'm looking forward to seeing you there!<br><br>Mile.	130	15.0	Mile, you've written a warm and enthusiastic email that makes your friend feel genuinely excited about the festival. Your opening line <em>That sounds awesome!</em> sets a perfect friendly tone, and you've covered every required point from the task. The way you explained your choice of meeting place and your budget plan shows you thought carefully about the practical details. To push your writing to Band 5 across all areas, focus on making your grammar more accurate and organising your ideas so each paragraph flows naturally into the next. Let me show you exactly how.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That sounds awesome! It will be nice to go to that festival</em> — You expressed genuine enthusiasm right from the start, which is exactly what the task asked for. The reader feels your excitement.</li>\n  <li><em>I love the idea that we could meet at the front entrance because I don't want to bothered your cousin</em> — You clearly stated your preference and gave a personal reason. The target reader understands your choice completely.</li>\n  <li><em>I suggest that sunglasses, hat and umbrella will be very important in this type of weather</em> — You offered practical suggestions that fit the festival context, showing you thought beyond the basic requirements.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses consistently appropriate language for an email to a friend, keeping the tone natural and error-free throughout. Your email feels friendly and personal, but a few small issues keep it from reaching the top band.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I don't want to bothered your cousin</em> → <strong>I don't want to bother your cousin</strong>. This phrase needs the base form of the verb after 'want to'. The meaning is still clear, but correcting this makes your writing sound more natural.</li>\n  <li><em>As for the activity, I agree with you about Discovering Dinosaur</em> → <strong>As for the activity, I agree with you — let's go with Discovering Dinosaur</strong>. You wrote 'I agree with you', which matches Corey's suggestion, but the task asked you to disagree. A clearer way to handle this would be to say something like 'I know you suggested the other film, but I'd really like to see Discovering Dinosaur'. This keeps the friendly tone while showing your own preference.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never has to pause and re-read. Your email has two clear paragraphs, but the transitions between topics feel a bit repetitive and choppy.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As for the activity... As for the stuff we should bring</em> → <strong>About the activity... For the things we should bring</strong>. Starting two sentences with 'As for' makes your writing sound repetitive. Varying your transition words — like 'About', 'Regarding', or 'When it comes to' — creates a smoother flow.</li>\n  <li><em>I think Five hundred Baht will be enough for us if we don't waste it on some toys at the festival and use it for foods and festival tour only</em> → <strong>I think five hundred baht will be enough if we spend it only on food and the festival tour, not on toys</strong>. This sentence is quite long and tries to explain too many ideas at once. Breaking it into two shorter sentences or using a clearer structure helps the reader follow your reasoning.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently and avoids errors that make the reader pause. You have good vocabulary and some strong sentences, but a few patterns of error keep appearing. The main rule to focus on is <strong>verb forms after modal verbs and 'want to'</strong> — after these, always use the base form of the verb (no -ed, no -s).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I don't want to bothered your cousin</em> → <strong>I don't want to bother your cousin</strong>. After 'want to', use the base verb. This is a very common pattern in English: want to + base verb.</li>\n  <li><em>it sounds a lot more entertain and fun than building robot</em> → <strong>it sounds a lot more entertaining and fun than building a robot</strong>. 'Entertain' is a verb; you need the adjective 'entertaining'. Also, 'robot' is a countable noun and needs an article: 'a robot'.</li>\n  <li><em>use it for foods and festival tour only</em> → <strong>use it for food and the festival tour only</strong>. 'Food' is uncountable in this context, so no 's'. 'Festival tour' needs 'the' because you're referring to a specific tour at this festival.</li>\n  <li><em>I think Five hundred Baht will be enough</em> → <strong>I think five hundred baht will be enough</strong>. In English, currency amounts are written in lowercase unless they start a sentence. 'Baht' is a unit, not a proper noun here.</li>\n  <li><strong>Self-check for any sentence you write:</strong> Read your sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Did I use the base form of the verb after want to, can, will, should, and must?' If the answer is no, change it. This one rule will fix many of your errors.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30847	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey<br><br>I've got the tickets for the Songkran festival at Mon do you want to come to gayler I think the be best for you?<br><br>We've got to playing a water if we want to go to the Songkran festival and watch the worldcup watch a match because they're happening at the same time I think the worldcup be more interesting - don't you?<br><br>Don't forget to buy a tickets to watch a worldcup and bring a spick anything else?<br><br>Bye<br>Corey	96	4.0	Tonnam, you've written a friendly email to Corey with a greeting and a closing, which is exactly the right format for this task. You also used some good vocabulary like 'tickets', 'festival', and 'interesting'. The challenge is that the task asked you to reply to an email about a science festival, but instead you wrote about Songkran and the World Cup. For your next draft, read the task instructions very carefully and answer each part directly. Let's look at each area in detail.	A1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	1.0	<p>You're at 1/5 for content. A Band 5 writer responds to all parts of the task and stays on topic. Your email is about Songkran and the World Cup, but the task asked about a science festival. You didn't react to the science festival, say when to meet, disagree about a film, or suggest what to bring.</p><ul><li><em>I've got the tickets for the Songkran festival at Mon</em> → <strong>I've got the tickets for the science festival on Monday.</strong> The task was about a science festival, not Songkran. Change the topic to match the task.</li><li><em>We've got to playing a water if we want to go to the Songkran festival and watch the worldcup</em> → <strong>If we want to go to the science festival, we should meet at 10 a.m.</strong> The task asked you to reply about meeting arrangements. You didn't suggest a time or place.</li><li><em>Don't forget to buy a tickets to watch a worldcup and bring a spick anything else?</em> → <strong>Don't forget to bring some snacks. Is there anything else I should bring?</strong> The task asked you to suggest what to bring. 'A spick' is unclear. Try 'snacks' or 'a water bottle'.</li></ul>	1.0	<p>You're at 1/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right register for an email to a friend and makes the purpose clear. Your greeting and closing are good, but the content doesn't match the task, so the reader would be confused.</p><ul><li><em>Hi Corey</em> and <em>Bye</em> are a good start for an informal email. Keep this friendly tone.</li><li><em>I think the be best for you?</em> → <strong>I think it would be best for you.</strong> The grammar here makes it hard to understand your meaning. Try 'I think it would be best for you to come to the science festival.'</li><li><em>Don't forget to buy a tickets to watch a worldcup and bring a spick anything else?</em> The purpose of this sentence is unclear. Are you reminding Corey, or asking a question? Try: <strong>Don't forget to buy tickets and bring something to eat. Is there anything else you want to bring?</strong></li></ul>	1.0	<p>You're at 1/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas in a logical order and uses linking words. Your email has a greeting and closing, which is good, but the ideas jump from Songkran to the World Cup without a clear connection.</p><ul><li><em>I've got the tickets for the Songkran festival at Mon do you want to come to gayler</em> → <strong>I've got the tickets for the science festival on Monday. Do you want to come to the gallery?</strong> Separate your ideas into clear sentences. Use a period or question mark between them.</li><li><em>We've got to playing a water if we want to go to the Songkran festival and watch the worldcup watch a match because they're happening at the same time</em> → <strong>We need to decide what to do because the science festival and the film are at the same time.</strong> Use 'because' to explain the reason, then write one clear idea per sentence.</li></ul>	1.0	<p>You're at 1/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar and vocabulary most of the time. You have some good words like 'tickets', 'festival', and 'interesting', but there is one main rule that will help you: after modal verbs like 'can', 'must', 'should', 'will', and 'do', use the base form of the verb (no 'to', no '-ing'). Let's fix that.</p><ul><li><em>We've got to playing a water</em> → <strong>We have to play in the water.</strong> After 'have to', use the base form 'play', not 'playing'.</li><li><em>I think the be best for you</em> → <strong>I think it would be best for you.</strong> After 'think', you need a subject ('it') and a verb ('would be').</li><li><em>Don't forget to buy a tickets to watch a worldcup</em> → <strong>Don't forget to buy tickets to watch the World Cup.</strong> Use 'tickets' (plural) because 'a' is for singular. Also, 'worldcup' should be 'World Cup' with capital letters.</li><li><em>bring a spick anything else?</em> → <strong>bring something else?</strong> 'A spick' is not a real word. Try 'something' or 'snacks'.</li><li>Check your capital letters: 'Mon' should be 'Monday', 'gayler' should be 'gallery', and 'worldcup' should be 'World Cup'. In English, days of the week, place names, and proper nouns start with a capital letter.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After every 'can', 'must', 'should', 'will', 'do', or 'have to', ask yourself: 'Is the next word the base form (no -ing, no to)?' If not, fix it.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33157	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hey Corey!<br><br>Really? That's amazing! and you are going to visit me too? You are the best!<br><br>And I'd prefer for you to pick me up.. haha, my parents are kind of busy so that'll be the best choice<br><br>I think building a robot is better! Watching a film is boring! beside we can watch it anydays anyways and not like anyone want to watch that film you're like the only one!<br><br>And ofc I won't forget and if I'd I can just borrow you anyways you won't say no right? anyways. We should bring something to eat the food there really isn't my type that way we don't have to buys it from there that's all<br><br>bye can't wait to see you!<br><br>milin	135	15.0	Milin, your email to Corey is full of energy and personality. You did an excellent job addressing every single point from the task: you expressed excitement about the festival, stated your preference for being picked up, disagreed with Corey about the film and suggested building a robot instead, and brought up bringing food. The reader gets a clear picture of your plans and your friendship. To reach Band 5, you'll want to polish your grammar and make your writing flow more smoothly. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Really? That's amazing!</em> You show genuine excitement about the festival, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I think building a robot is better!</em> You clearly state your disagreement with Corey's film idea and offer a creative alternative.</li>\n  <li><em>We should bring something to eat</em> You suggest bringing food, covering the final point from the task completely.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps a consistent, friendly email tone throughout without abrupt shifts or overly casual phrases that break the flow. Your enthusiasm is great, but phrases like <em>you're like the only one</em> and <em>anyways</em> used too often make the tone feel a bit scattered.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>you're like the only one</em> → <strong>you're the only one</strong>. Dropping 'like' makes the sentence sound more natural and mature in writing.</li>\n  <li><em>anyways</em> appears three times in the email. Try replacing two of them with <strong>anyway</strong> (the standard form) or with <strong>besides</strong> or <strong>plus</strong> to vary your transitions.</li>\n  <li><em>And ofc I won't forget</em> → <strong>And of course I won't forget</strong>. 'Ofc' is too casual for a PET email. Writing 'of course' shows you can use full forms in a friendly but correct way.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words and keeps the flow smooth from start to finish. You have good paragraph breaks, but the word <em>anyways</em> is repeated three times, and the final paragraph feels a bit rushed and jumbled.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>anyways</em> appears in three different spots. Replace the second and third uses with <strong>anyway</strong> or <strong>plus</strong> or <strong>so</strong> to avoid repetition.</li>\n  <li><em>the food there really isn't my type that way we don't have to buys it from there that's all</em> → <strong>The food there really isn't my type, so that way we don't have to buy it from there.</strong> Adding 'so' creates a clear cause-and-effect link, and removing 'that's all' makes the ending feel more complete.</li>\n  <li>Your ideas are in a logical order: greeting, excitement, pickup preference, robot idea, food suggestion, goodbye. That's good. Just work on connecting them more smoothly.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple and some complex grammatical forms with few errors, and uses everyday vocabulary accurately. You have some strong phrases, but there is one main pattern that affects many sentences: subject-verb agreement and missing subjects.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>not like anyone want to watch that film</em> → <strong>it's not like anyone wants to watch that film</strong>. The subject 'it' was missing, and 'anyone' takes 'wants' (third person singular).</li>\n  <li><em>if I'd I can just borrow you</em> → <strong>if I do, I can just borrow it from you</strong>. 'I'd' is a contraction of 'I would', but here you need 'if I do'. Also, 'borrow you' should be 'borrow it from you' because you borrow something from someone.</li>\n  <li><em>we don't have to buys it from there</em> → <strong>we don't have to buy it from there</strong>. After 'don't have to', use the base form of the verb: 'buy', not 'buys'.</li>\n  <li><em>anydays anyways</em> → <strong>any day anyway</strong>. 'Anydays' is not a word. 'Any day' is two words, and 'anyway' is one word (not 'anyways').</li>\n  <li>Check capitalisation: <em>and you are going to visit me too?</em> should start with a capital letter: <strong>And you are going to visit me too?</strong> In English, every new sentence starts with a capital letter, even after an exclamation like 'Really? That's amazing!'</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33185	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi, Corey<br><br>Thank for the ticket I was excited to go with you now. I think you could pick me up because my parents go outside for preparing something my house is on the way we go usually it's the best way for me. When we arrive there we should go discover first but I'm so sorry I think we should go build a robot for the main activity because we can help each other and I think watch the discovering dinosaurs is boring for me.<br><br>I bring some of money for buy a food or drink and taxi in there we should bring the battery for charge the phone because we in there all day.<br><br>See you soon!<br><br>Auto	122	13.0	Auto, you did an excellent job addressing every single point from the task. You told Corey you were excited about the ticket, asked to be picked up, disagreed about the film and suggested building a robot instead, and even thought ahead about bringing a battery charger. That is exactly what a Band 5 writer does — the reader knows everything they need to. To reach Band 5 in the other areas, you will need to focus on writing clearer sentences and organising your ideas more logically. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thank for the ticket I was excited to go with you now.</em> You expressed your excitement clearly and thanked Corey — both required by the task.</li>\n  <li><em>I think you could pick me up because my parents go outside for preparing something my house is on the way we go usually it's the best way for me.</em> You stated your preference for being picked up and gave a reason, which fully informs the reader.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should go build a robot for the main activity because we can help each other and I think watch the discovering dinosaurs is boring for me.</em> You disagreed with Corey about the film and offered a clear alternative, exactly as the task asked.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent friendly tone throughout the email and expresses every idea in a way that sounds natural and clear to a friend. Some of your sentences are a bit confusing, which makes the reader stop and guess what you mean.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thank for the ticket</em> → <strong>Thank you for the ticket</strong>. Adding 'you' makes it sound complete and natural in an email to a friend.</li>\n  <li><em>I bring some of money for buy a food or drink and taxi in there</em> → <strong>I will bring some money to buy food, drinks, and a taxi when we are there.</strong> This version is clearer and keeps the friendly tone while being grammatically correct.</li>\n  <li><em>we in there all day</em> → <strong>we will be there all day</strong>. Adding 'will be' makes the sentence complete and natural.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges ideas in a logical order so the reader can follow the story easily. Your email has two paragraphs, which is good, but the first paragraph jumps between ideas without a clear sequence.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I was excited to go with you now. I think you could pick me up because my parents go outside for preparing something my house is on the way we go usually it's the best way for me. When we arrive there we should go discover first but I'm so sorry I think we should go build a robot...</em> Try grouping your ideas: first thank Corey, then talk about the pickup, then discuss the activity. A simple order like 'thank you → pickup → activity' helps the reader follow your thoughts.</li>\n  <li>Your second paragraph talks about bringing money and a battery charger. These are both about what to bring, so they fit well together. Adding a linking word like 'also' before the battery charger would make the connection clearer: <strong>I will bring some money for food and drinks. I will also bring a battery charger for my phone.</strong></li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and vocabulary, with only small mistakes. Your writing has several frequent errors that make it harder to understand. The main rule that will help you is: every sentence needs a main verb with a subject, and you need to use the correct verb form (like 'will bring' instead of 'bring' for future plans).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thank for the ticket</em> → <strong>Thank you for the ticket</strong>. The verb 'thank' needs an object ('you') to be complete.</li>\n  <li><em>my parents go outside for preparing something</em> → <strong>my parents are going outside to prepare something</strong>. Use 'are going' (present continuous) for an action happening now, and 'to prepare' (infinitive) to show purpose.</li>\n  <li><em>I bring some of money for buy a food or drink</em> → <strong>I will bring some money to buy food and drinks</strong>. For future plans, use 'will bring'. Also, 'money' does not need 'some of', and 'food' and 'drink' are usually uncountable here.</li>\n  <li><em>we in there all day</em> → <strong>we will be there all day</strong>. Every sentence needs a main verb. 'Will be' is the verb here.</li>\n  <li>Check your capitalisation: 'Hi, Corey' is correct, but 'my parents' and 'my house' should start with a lowercase letter (they are not proper nouns). Also, 'I' is always capitalised.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35996	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		From: Fah<br><br>Subject: Science festival<br><br>Hi, Corey<br><br>That's a great news! So you got two ticket, right? And it's on the weekends, how great!<br><br>Thanks your cousin for offering to pick me up with you, but I think we should meet at the front entrance, because I want to drive there by myself. And about the activities, I don't agree that film is more interesting, of coures! Building a robot is difinetely more interesting<br><br>I won't forget to bring my pocket money with we, and why don't we bring a camera to take some photos while staying in the festival.<br><br>Do you think so?<br><br>I can't wait to seeing you, Corey<br><br>Fah	112	15.0	Fah, your email to Corey is lively and personal. You did a great job covering every point from the task: you reacted positively to the tickets, stated your preference to meet at the front entrance, disagreed about the film with a clear reason, and suggested bringing a camera. The reader gets a full picture of your plans. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll need to polish your grammar and spelling, and add a bit more variety to how you connect your ideas. Let's look at each part of your writing.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That's a great news!</em> You showed a positive reaction to the tickets right away, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should meet at the front entrance, because I want to drive there by myself.</em> You clearly stated your preference and gave a personal reason, so the reader understands your choice.</li>\n  <li><em>I don't agree that film is more interesting, of coures! Building a robot is difinetely more interesting</em> You disagreed with Corey and explained why, which fully addresses the note about the film.</li>\n  <li><em>why don't we bring a camera to take some photos while staying in the festival.</em> You suggested bringing a camera, which is a thoughtful idea that fits the context perfectly.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would use the friendly email tone consistently without any slips that sound too informal or ungrammatical. Your email feels warm and natural, but a few small errors make it sound less polished.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That's a great news!</em> → <strong>That's great news!</strong> 'News' is uncountable, so we don't say 'a news'.</li>\n  <li><em>how great!</em> → <strong>How great!</strong> Capitalise the start of a sentence, even after a comma.</li>\n  <li><em>Thanks your cousin</em> → <strong>Thanks to your cousin</strong> or <strong>Thank your cousin</strong>. You need a preposition or a different verb structure.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would use a wider range of linking words and smoother transitions between ideas. Your email uses paragraphs well to separate topics, but the connections between sentences are very simple.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That's a great news! So you got two ticket, right? And it's on the weekends, how great!</em> → <strong>That's great news! You got two tickets, and it's on the weekend — how exciting!</strong> Try combining short exclamations into one flowing sentence.</li>\n  <li><em>And about the activities, I don't agree that film is more interesting, of coures! Building a robot is difinetely more interesting</em> → <strong>As for the activities, I don't agree that the film is more interesting. Building a robot is definitely more interesting!</strong> Use 'as for' or 'regarding' instead of 'and about' for a more natural transition.</li>\n  <li><em>I won't forget to bring my pocket money with we, and why don't we bring a camera to take some photos while staying in the festival.</em> → <strong>I won't forget to bring my pocket money, and why don't we bring a camera to take photos while we're at the festival?</strong> Adding 'we're' makes the connection between the two ideas clearer.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control basic grammar and spelling with very few errors. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>countable vs. uncountable nouns and subject-verb agreement</strong>. Many of your mistakes come from treating uncountable nouns as countable or from forgetting to match singular/plural forms.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That's a great news!</em> → <strong>That's great news!</strong> 'News' is an uncountable noun in English. We never say 'a news'. Think of it like 'information' or 'advice' — no 'a' before it.</li>\n  <li><em>you got two ticket</em> → <strong>you got two tickets</strong>. When you have a number (two, three, etc.), the noun must be plural. 'Ticket' becomes 'tickets'.</li>\n  <li><em>Thanks your cousin</em> → <strong>Thanks to your cousin</strong>. The verb 'thank' needs a preposition: 'thank someone for something' or 'thanks to someone'.</li>\n  <li><em>with we</em> → <strong>with us</strong>. After a preposition like 'with', use the object pronoun 'us', not the subject pronoun 'we'.</li>\n  <li><em>I can't wait to seeing you</em> → <strong>I can't wait to see you</strong>. After 'can't wait to', use the base form of the verb (infinitive without 'to'), not the '-ing' form.</li>\n  <li>Spelling check: <em>of coures</em> → <strong>of course</strong>; <em>difinetely</em> → <strong>definitely</strong>. These are common spelling errors. Try saying the word slowly in your head as you write it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check: After you write a sentence, read it aloud and ask yourself: 'Is every noun with a number plural? Did I use 'a' before an uncountable noun? Did I use the right pronoun after a preposition?' This will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36006	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hello<br><br>You just got our tickets for the science festival, that's great! Thank you Corey. You can pick me up on the way there it would be quick and easy. If we met at the front entrance I think we will get lost because we never been there. For me, build a robot is more interesting because the Discovering Dinosaurs film we can watch together on youtube but we can't build a robot at my house or your house. So I think we should go build a robot. I think we should bring a phone just in case that we're lost, a water bottle so we will not get thirsty, a small bag for carry some stuff or snacks. I think that's it.<br><br>B yes,<br>Corey	135	15.0	Indy, you have written a very clear and friendly email to Corey. You responded to every part of the task with genuine enthusiasm, and your reasons for each choice feel personal and thoughtful. For example, explaining that you can watch the dinosaur film on YouTube but cannot build a robot at home shows real critical thinking. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you will need to work on organising your ideas into paragraphs and polishing your grammar. Let's look at each subscale in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>You just got our tickets for the science festival, that's great! Thank you Corey.</em> You started with a warm, personal reaction that shows you are genuinely excited about the tickets.</li>\n  <li><em>You can pick me up on the way there it would be quick and easy.</em> You clearly stated your preference for being picked up and gave a sensible reason, which fully informs the reader.</li>\n  <li><em>For me, build a robot is more interesting because the Discovering Dinosaurs film we can watch together on youtube but we can't build a robot at my house or your house.</em> You disagreed with Corey's film idea and explained why building a robot is better, using a creative and logical reason.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should bring a phone just in case that we're lost, a water bottle so we will not get thirsty, a small bag for carry some stuff or snacks.</em> You suggested three specific items to bring, each with a reason, which shows you thought carefully about the practical needs of the day.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an email to a friend consistently, including a proper closing. Your email feels friendly and natural, and the reader can follow your ideas easily. The only thing holding you back is the closing <em>B yes,</em> which is a creative attempt but does not match standard email conventions.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>B yes,</em> → <strong>Best,</strong> or <strong>Bye,</strong>. A clear, standard closing helps the reader feel the email is complete and professional.</li>\n  <li>Your opening <em>Hello</em> is perfect for a friend, and your use of <em>Thank you Corey</em> keeps the tone warm. Keep that friendly voice throughout.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer groups related ideas into paragraphs and uses a range of linking words to guide the reader smoothly. Your email is one long paragraph, which makes it harder for the reader to follow your different points. The ideas are logical, but the structure can be improved.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Group your ideas into paragraphs. For example, one paragraph for the pick-up plan, one for the activity choice, and one for what to bring. This helps the reader see the structure clearly.</li>\n  <li>You used <em>because</em> and <em>so</em> well, but try adding <em>also</em>, <em>however</em>, or <em>in addition</em> to connect ideas more smoothly. For instance: <em>I think we should bring a phone. Also, a water bottle would be useful.</em></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls a range of grammatical forms and uses vocabulary accurately. Your everyday vocabulary is good, but there are some noticeable errors. The main pattern is that you sometimes use the base form of a verb where a different form is needed (infinitive, gerund, or past participle). Let's fix that.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>For me, build a robot is more interesting</em> → <strong>For me, building a robot is more interesting</strong>. After a preposition like 'for', or when a verb is the subject of a sentence, use the -ing form (gerund).</li>\n  <li><em>a small bag for carry some stuff</em> → <strong>a small bag for carrying some stuff</strong>. Again, after 'for', use the -ing form.</li>\n  <li><em>we never been there</em> → <strong>we have never been there</strong>. With 'never' to talk about experience, use the present perfect: have/has + past participle.</li>\n  <li><em>just in case that we're lost</em> → <strong>just in case we get lost</strong>. After 'just in case', use a simple present or present tense, not 'that'.</li>\n  <li>You wrote <em>youtube</em> with a lowercase 'y'. Proper nouns like YouTube should be capitalised. Also, check that you capitalise the start of each sentence consistently.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36013	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Cory!<br><br>Thank you for the tickets for the science festival this saturday. That great! I think we could meet at the front entrance because I can go with my dad. As a food I think we should build a robot because in my opinon it more interesting then watching a film. In the others hand we should bring some food or drink because I think at the science festival will be very expensive and don't forget to bring your mobile phone in case that you are lost. lastly bring your jacket because inside it very cold.<br><br>See you soon at the festival!<br><br>Bye<br><br>Prin A	107	15.0	Prin A, your email to Corey is full of personality and shows you understood every part of the task. I especially liked how you thanked him for the tickets and gave a clear reason for meeting at the front entrance: <em>I can go with my dad</em>. That is exactly the kind of personal detail that makes an email feel real. You also covered all four notes from the task, which is excellent. To reach Band 5 on your next draft, you will need to focus on two things: making your grammar more accurate and polishing your transitions between ideas. Let me show you exactly how to do that.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Thank you for the tickets for the science festival this saturday.</em> You expressed your gratitude clearly and identified the event, which is exactly what the task required.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we could meet at the front entrance because I can go with my dad.</em> You stated a preference, gave a reason, and connected it to your personal situation. The reader understands your choice completely.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should build a robot because in my opinon it more interesting then watching a film.</em> You disagreed with Corey and offered an alternative activity with a reason. This shows you thought about what would be fun for both of you.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the friendly email tone consistent from start to finish, with every phrase sounding natural and clear. You have a strong start and a warm closing, but two phrases need polishing so the reader never has to pause and wonder what you mean.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As a food I think we should build a robot</em> → <strong>As for food, I think we should build a robot</strong>. The phrase <em>As a food</em> is confusing. You probably meant to introduce a new topic. Try <em>As for food</em> or <em>As for what to do</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>In the others hand</em> → <strong>On the other hand</strong>. This is a fixed phrase in English. Practice saying it aloud: <em>On the other hand</em>. It will help you remember the correct words.</li>\n  <li><em>lastly bring your jacket</em> → <strong>Lastly, bring your jacket</strong>. Adding a comma after <em>lastly</em> makes the sentence flow more naturally, like the friendly tone you use elsewhere.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects their ideas smoothly so the reader never feels a jump between topics. Your email has a logical order: meeting point, then activity, then what to bring. But the move from building a robot to bringing food feels sudden, and one linking phrase needs fixing.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I think we should build a robot ... In the others hand we should bring some food or drink</em> → <strong>I think we should build a robot. On the other hand, we should also bring some food or drink</strong>. The word <em>also</em> helps the reader see that you are adding a new point, not contrasting it.</li>\n  <li><em>don't forget to bring your mobile phone ... lastly bring your jacket</em> → <strong>Don't forget to bring your mobile phone. Lastly, bring your jacket</strong>. You already use <em>lastly</em> well. Try using a smoother transition like <em>Also, bring your jacket because inside it is very cold.</em> to connect the two items.</li>\n  <li>Your opening and closing sentences are strong. The greeting <em>Hi Cory!</em> and the sign-off <em>See you soon at the festival!</em> give the email a clear beginning and end.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar so well that errors do not distract the reader. Your vocabulary choices are good — <em>science festival</em>, <em>front entrance</em>, <em>build a robot</em>, <em>mobile phone</em>, <em>jacket</em> — but several sentences have small grammar mistakes that add up. The main rule to focus on is: after <em>it is</em> (or <em>it's</em>), you need the full verb <em>is</em> or <em>was</em>. You cannot drop it.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That great!</em> → <strong>That's great!</strong> or <strong>That is great!</strong> You need the verb <em>is</em> after <em>That</em>. This pattern appears again in your email.</li>\n  <li><em>it more interesting then watching a film</em> → <strong>it is more interesting than watching a film</strong>. Two fixes here: add <em>is</em> after <em>it</em>, and change <em>then</em> to <em>than</em> when comparing things. <em>Then</em> is for time (first this, then that). <em>Than</em> is for comparisons.</li>\n  <li><em>inside it very cold</em> → <strong>inside it is very cold</strong>. Again, the verb <em>is</em> is missing. Try saying the sentence slowly: <em>Inside, it is very cold.</em></li>\n  <li><em>in case that you are lost</em> → <strong>in case you get lost</strong>. After <em>in case</em>, you do not need <em>that</em>. Also, <em>get lost</em> sounds more natural than <em>are lost</em> for this situation.</li>\n  <li><em>this saturday</em> → <strong>this Saturday</strong>. Days of the week are always capitalised in English. Check your writing for other words that need capitals, like the start of sentences — <em>lastly</em> should be <em>Lastly</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Self-check for your next draft:</strong> Read every sentence aloud and listen for the verb. If you have a subject like <em>it</em>, <em>that</em>, or <em>this</em>, make sure a verb like <em>is</em>, <em>was</em>, or <em>will be</em> follows it.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36023	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Casey!<br><br>That's brilliant, I was looking forward to going there!<br><br>I'd prefer that you guys picking me up instead of waiting at the front entrance because I get lost very easy, and it's hard to find you guys in the crowd. As for the activity, watching the film is nice. But for me building robots sounds way cooler than watching a film about dinosaurs because we can just watch the film at home. I think you guys should also bring an umbrella and bags incase it rains or if we buy too many things. I already planned to buy many merchandise. So it would be so kind of you guys if you bring extra bags with you!<br><br>Can't wait to go there!	123	18.0	Good, your email to Casey is full of energy and personality. You responded to every single point from the task with your own clear opinion and a reason, which is exactly what a Band 5 writer does. The reader finishes your email knowing exactly what you want and why. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you just need to polish a few grammar spots and tighten one or two sentences. Let's look at each subscale.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>That's brilliant, I was looking forward to going there!</em> You expressed a positive reaction to the festival right away, which covers the first task point perfectly.</li><li><em>I'd prefer that you guys picking me up instead of waiting at the front entrance because I get lost very easy</em> You stated your preference for being picked up and gave a personal reason. The reader understands your situation completely.</li><li><em>I think you guys should also bring an umbrella and bags incase it rains or if we buy too many things.</em> You suggested bringing items and explained why, covering the final task point. Every note from the task is addressed.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Hi Casey!</em> and <em>Can't wait to go there!</em> Your opening and closing are friendly and natural for an email to a friend. The tone is warm and excited.</li><li><em>But for me building robots sounds way cooler than watching a film about dinosaurs because we can just watch the film at home.</em> You disagreed with Corey in a polite, personal way. You gave a reason that makes sense, keeping the conversation engaging.</li><li><em>So it would be so kind of you guys if you bring extra bags with you!</em> You made a request politely, which holds the reader's attention and keeps the register consistent.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer connects every sentence so the whole email flows smoothly from one idea to the next. Your paragraphs are clear and each one covers a different task point, which is great. The only small issue is the final sentence about bags feels slightly disconnected from the sentence before it. You wrote <em>I already planned to buy many merchandise. So it would be so kind of you guys if you bring extra bags with you!</em> The word 'so' works, but the jump from 'I planned to buy merchandise' to 'please bring bags' feels a little abrupt. Try linking them more directly.</p><ul><li><em>I already planned to buy many merchandise.</em> → <strong>I already plan to buy a lot of merchandise, so it would be kind of you to bring extra bags.</strong> This connects the two thoughts in one smooth sentence.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5. A Band 5 writer controls grammar and vocabulary so that errors are rare. You have good vocabulary and you try complex sentences, which is excellent. The main rule that will help you is: after <em>would prefer that</em>, use the base form of the verb (no -ing). Also, after <em>plan to</em>, use the base form. Let's fix the key errors.</p><ul><li><em>I'd prefer that you guys picking me up</em> → <strong>I'd prefer that you guys pick me up</strong>. After 'prefer that', the verb stays in the base form. No -ing.</li><li><em>I get lost very easy</em> → <strong>I get lost very easily</strong>. 'Easy' is an adjective. You need 'easily', the adverb, to describe how you get lost.</li><li><em>incase</em> → <strong>in case</strong>. 'In case' is two words. It means 'to prepare for the possibility that'.</li><li><em>I already planned to buy many merchandise</em> → <strong>I already plan to buy a lot of merchandise</strong>. 'Merchandise' is uncountable, so you can't say 'many merchandise'. Use 'a lot of' or 'much'. Also, 'planned to' is fine, but 'plan to' (present tense) sounds more natural here since you're talking about your current intention.</li><li>Check your capitalisation: <em>I already planned to buy many merchandise. So it would be...</em> → <strong>I already plan to buy a lot of merchandise, so it would be...</strong> When 'so' connects two clauses in the middle of a sentence, use a comma, not a capital letter.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36027	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi, Thanks for the tickets corey! i really like science festival.<br><br>I think we could meet at the front entrance because my mom has to go to work near there too, it would be best for me.<br><br>For two main activities, my opinion i want to build a robot because i like to creative my own robot and we can watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film in next time but if you want to watch the film it ok, i can build a robot in next chance.<br><br>I will bring a bit of pocket money and i think we should bring a gap and the hand fan because in that day will be hot.<br><br>Bye, I can't wait!<br><br>Bluebell	119	15.0	Bluebell, your email to Corey is full of enthusiasm and real personality. You addressed every single note in the task, from thanking Corey for the tickets to suggesting what to bring, and each point is supported with a clear reason. The reader finishes your email knowing exactly what you think and why. That is strong content work. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you will need to polish your grammar and make your sentences flow more smoothly. Let's look at each skill area to see exactly how to get there.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Hi, Thanks for the tickets corey! i really like science festival.</em> You opened with a positive reaction to the gift, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>I think we could meet at the front entrance because my mom has to go to work near there too, it would be best for me.</em> You suggested a meeting point and gave a personal reason. The reader understands your situation completely.</li><li><em>I will bring a bit of pocket money and i think we should bring a gap and the hand fan because in that day will be hot.</em> You addressed the final note about what to bring, and you connected it logically to the weather. Every part of the task is covered.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone friendly and consistent throughout the whole email, without any informal shortcuts that might confuse the reader. Your email is warm and enthusiastic, which is perfect for a friend, but a few small phrases could be more polished.</p><ul><li><em>it ok</em> → <strong>that's okay</strong>. Adding 'that's' makes the sentence complete and sounds more natural in writing.</li><li><em>my opinion i want to build a robot</em> → <strong>in my opinion, I want to build a robot</strong> or <strong>I think I want to build a robot</strong>. The phrase 'my opinion' needs a preposition like 'in' to connect it to the rest of the sentence.</li><li><em>i really like science festival</em> → <strong>I really like the science festival</strong>. Adding 'the' before 'science festival' makes the reference specific and clear to the reader.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs to separate ideas and connects those ideas with smooth transitions so the reader never feels a jump. You have good paragraph breaks, which is a strong start, but the link between the robot/film discussion and the suggestion about what to bring could be smoother.</p><ul><li><em>For two main activities, my opinion i want to build a robot because i like to creative my own robot and we can watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film in next time but if you want to watch the film it ok, i can build a robot in next chance.</em> → <strong>For the two main activities, I want to build a robot because I like to create my own robot. We can watch the Discovering Dinosaurs film another time. If you want to watch the film, that's okay. I can build a robot another time.</strong> Breaking this long sentence into shorter ones makes your reasoning easier to follow.</li><li>Your third paragraph is about the activities, and your fourth paragraph is about what to bring. Adding a short connecting sentence like <strong>"Now, about what to bring..."</strong> would help the reader move smoothly from one topic to the next.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls simple grammar well and makes very few errors. The main pattern holding you back is missing or incorrect articles (a, an, the) and missing subjects in sentences. Let's fix these.</p><ul><li><em>i really like science festival</em> → <strong>I really like the science festival</strong>. 'Science festival' is a specific event, so it needs 'the' before it. Also, remember to capitalise 'I' every time.</li><li><em>my opinion i want to build a robot because i like to creative my own robot</em> → <strong>in my opinion, I want to build a robot because I like to create my own robot</strong>. 'Creative' is an adjective, but here you need the verb 'create'. Also add 'in' before 'my opinion'.</li><li><em>in that day will be hot</em> → <strong>it will be hot that day</strong>. Every English sentence needs a subject. 'In that day will be hot' has no subject. Adding 'it' fixes the sentence.</li><li><em>i think we should bring a gap</em> → <strong>I think we should bring a cap</strong>. 'Gap' means a space or opening. 'Cap' is a type of hat. Double-check spelling for words that sound similar.</li><li>Self-check: After you write a sentence, ask yourself: Does every sentence have a subject (a person or thing doing the action)? Is every 'I' capitalised? Do I need 'the' before a specific thing? These three checks will catch most of your errors.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36029	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey<br><br>That sound great! I want to go to Science festival for a longtime but I never go<br><br>I think we should meet at the front entrance, because I think it easier.<br><br>No, I thinks build a robot would be more interestingg As for film that about a dinosaurs it can watch anywhere. I think youtube have a film that about dinosaurs, but you can go watch a Discovering Dinosaurs film and I will go build a robot and we will meet at the front of a restauro<br><br>I think that we should bring a water and a phone, because if we don't have a phone we can't text or call each other and maybe we lost each other.<br><br>See you soon,<br>Ai	137	14.0	Ai, you've done a great job with this email to Corey. You covered every single point the task asked for: you showed excitement about the science festival, chose a meeting place, disagreed about the film and offered a better idea, and suggested what to bring. That's exactly what a Band 5 writer does for Content. Your email also has a warm, friendly tone that sounds like a real message to a friend. Now let's look at how you can make your grammar and organisation stronger so your ideas come across even more clearly.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That sound great! I want to go to Science festival for a longtime but I never go</em> You clearly expressed your excitement and explained why you've never been before. The reader understands your feelings completely.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should meet at the front entrance, because I think it easier.</em> You chose a specific meeting point and gave a reason. The task asked for a choice with explanation, and you delivered.</li>\n  <li><em>No, I thinks build a robot would be more interestingg As for film that about a dinosaurs it can watch anywhere. I think youtube have a film that about dinosaurs, but you can go watch a Discovering Dinosaurs film and I will go build a robot and we will meet at the front of a restauro</em> You disagreed politely, suggested an alternative activity (building a robot), and proposed a practical solution for doing different things. All the required content is there.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the tone friendly and the language smooth throughout, so the reader never has to pause to figure out what was meant. Your email opens and closes warmly, and the reader can follow your ideas without much trouble. To reach Band 5, work on making every sentence flow naturally.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That sound great!</em> → <strong>That sounds great!</strong> This small grammar fix keeps your enthusiastic opening clean and natural.</li>\n  <li><em>No, I thinks build a robot would be more interestingg As for film that about a dinosaurs it can watch anywhere.</em> → <strong>No, I think building a robot would be more interesting. As for the film about dinosaurs, you can watch it anywhere.</strong> Breaking this into two sentences and fixing the grammar makes your disagreement much clearer and more polite.</li>\n  <li><em>I think youtube have a film that about dinosaurs, but you can go watch a Discovering Dinosaurs film and I will go build a robot and we will meet at the front of a restauro</em> → <strong>I think YouTube has a film about dinosaurs, but you can go watch Discovering Dinosaurs and I will go build a robot. Then we can meet at the front of a restaurant.</strong> Adding a period before 'Then' creates a smoother, more natural rhythm.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraph breaks and linking words to guide the reader smoothly from one idea to the next. You did use paragraphs, which is good, but the connection between your robot suggestion and the film idea is a bit confusing.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>No, I thinks build a robot would be more interestingg As for film that about a dinosaurs it can watch anywhere.</em> → <strong>No, I think building a robot would be more interesting. As for the film about dinosaurs, you can watch it anywhere.</strong> Adding a period after 'interesting' separates your two ideas clearly. The reader can follow your reasoning step by step.</li>\n  <li><em>I think youtube have a film that about dinosaurs, but you can go watch a Discovering Dinosaurs film and I will go build a robot and we will meet at the front of a restauro</em> → <strong>I think YouTube has a film about dinosaurs, but you can go watch Discovering Dinosaurs and I will go build a robot. Then we will meet at the front of a restaurant.</strong> Using 'Then' to start the last part tells the reader the order of events. This small linking word makes a big difference.</li>\n  <li>Your final paragraph about bringing water and a phone is well-organised. You gave the items first, then explained why. That logical order helps the reader understand your thinking.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar correctly most of the time. The main rule to work on is subject-verb agreement: when the subject is 'I', the verb does not add an 's'. When the subject is 'it', 'he', or 'she', the verb adds an 's'. Many of your errors come from mixing this up.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>That sound great!</em> → <strong>That sounds great!</strong> 'That' is like 'it', so the verb needs an 's'.</li>\n  <li><em>I thinks build a robot would be more interestingg</em> → <strong>I think building a robot would be more interesting.</strong> 'I' does NOT take an 's'. Also, after 'think', use the '-ing' form 'building', not 'build'.</li>\n  <li><em>it easier</em> → <strong>it's easier</strong>. You need the verb 'is' (or the contraction ''s').</li>\n  <li><em>a water</em> → <strong>water</strong> or <strong>some water</strong>. 'Water' is uncountable, so we don't use 'a' with it.</li>\n  <li><em>a restauro</em> → <strong>a restaurant</strong>. Spelling fix: 'restaurant' is the correct word.</li>\n  <li><em>interestingg</em> → <strong>interesting</strong>. One 'g', not two.</li>\n  <li><em>we lost each other</em> → <strong>we might lose each other</strong>. Since you're talking about a possibility, not the past, use 'might lose' or 'could lose'.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check for your next draft: after every verb, ask yourself — 'Does this verb match its subject? If the subject is I/you/we/they, no 's' on the verb. If the subject is he/she/it, add 's'.'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36036	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey!<br><br>You got our tickets for the science festival this saturday? that's awesome! Please tell your cousin thank you so much, we can meet at the front entrance that would be much easier than your cousin meet me on the way there. I think for the two main activity both are very interesting but I suggest we could watch the Discovering Dinosaurs so I agree the flim would be more interesting than build a robot. Okay I won't forget to bring a bit of pocket money. I think that we should bring some raincoat too in case it has a storm coming.<br><br>See you there!<br><br>Yada	109	15.0	Yada, your email to Corey is lively and friendly. You did an excellent job of covering all the required points: you showed excitement about the tickets, suggested a meeting place, gave your opinion on the activities, and even added a thoughtful idea about bringing a raincoat. The reader can clearly picture the plan. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll want to make your meaning completely clear in a few spots and strengthen your sentence control. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>You got our tickets for the science festival this saturday? that's awesome!</em> You immediately show excitement and acknowledge the gift, which is exactly what the task asks for.</li>\n  <li><em>we can meet at the front entrance that would be much easier than your cousin meet me on the way there.</em> You choose a specific meeting point and explain why it's better, so the reader knows the plan clearly.</li>\n  <li><em>I think that we should bring some raincoat too in case it has a storm coming.</em> You add a practical suggestion that shows you've thought ahead, which makes the email feel complete and helpful.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer makes every idea easy to understand on the first reading, with no confusing moments. Your email has a warm, friendly tone and a great opening and closing, but the part about the film is a little unclear.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>both are very interesting but I suggest we could watch the Discovering Dinosaurs so I agree the flim would be more interesting than build a robot.</em> → <strong>Both activities look very interesting, but I suggest we watch Discovering Dinosaurs. I think the film would be more interesting than building a robot.</strong> The word 'so' makes it sound like you agree with something, when you are actually giving your own opinion. Separating the two ideas into two sentences makes your meaning clear.</li>\n  <li><em>You got our tickets for the science festival this saturday? that's awesome!</em> This is a perfect, natural opening for an email to a friend. Keep writing openings like this.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. Your email has a logical order overall, but the move from the film discussion to pocket money feels sudden.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I agree the flim would be more interesting than build a robot. Okay I won't forget to bring a bit of pocket money.</em> → <strong>I think the film would be more interesting than building a robot. By the way, I won't forget to bring some pocket money.</strong> Adding a small transition like 'By the way' or 'Also' helps the reader see that you are moving to a new topic.</li>\n  <li>You used <br><br> to separate your greeting, body, and closing, which is a good start. To reach Band 5, also use linking words like 'because', 'so', and 'but' inside your paragraphs to connect your sentences more tightly.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls everyday grammar and vocabulary with few errors, so the reader never has to stop and guess. Your vocabulary is good ('awesome', 'front entrance', 'pocket money', 'raincoat'), but some grammar patterns need attention. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>after a modal verb like 'can', 'should', 'will', or 'would', always use the base form of the verb (no -s, no -ed, no 'to').</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>we can meet at the front entrance that would be much easier than your cousin meet me</em> → <strong>we can meet at the front entrance. That would be much easier than your cousin meeting me.</strong> After 'than', you need a gerund (meeting) or a full clause (than for your cousin to meet me).</li>\n  <li><em>I suggest we could watch the Discovering Dinosaurs so I agree the flim would be more interesting than build a robot.</em> → <strong>I suggest we watch Discovering Dinosaurs. I think the film would be more interesting than building a robot.</strong> After 'suggest', use the base form (watch). After 'than', use a gerund (building) or a noun (a robot-building activity). Also, 'flim' is a spelling error — the correct word is 'film'.</li>\n  <li><em>I think that we should bring some raincoat too in case it has a storm coming.</em> → <strong>I think we should bring a raincoat too in case a storm comes.</strong> 'Raincoat' is a countable noun, so it needs 'a' or 'some raincoats'. Also, 'in case it has a storm coming' is not natural — use 'in case a storm comes' or 'in case there is a storm'.</li>\n  <li><em>this saturday</em> → <strong>this Saturday</strong>. Days of the week and months are always capitalised in English.</li>\n  <li><em>You got our tickets for the science festival this saturday? that's awesome!</em> → <strong>You got our tickets for the science festival this Saturday? That's awesome!</strong> The word after a question mark must start with a capital letter. Also, this sentence is a question, so it needs a question mark after 'Saturday'.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36039	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey! That sounds great! It would be great if you can pick me up on the way there. Because my parent's aren't at home this Saturday and they don't allow me to go somewhere by myself.<br><br>For the main activity, I think we should build a robot. The Discovering Dino-saur film is too boring. I don't like to watch about dinosaurs. Building a robot is more interesting and I like to do it.<br><br>I will bring a bit of pocket money with me so if we are going to do something else so we can pay for it. What about bringing our homework to do it together? Maths homework is really difficult so I want to do it with you. Do you agree?<br><br>I'm waiting to see you!<br>Beya	143	18.0	Beya, your email to Corey is lively and natural. You handled every part of the task with confidence: you reacted positively, arranged the pickup, disagreed about the film with a creative alternative, and suggested bringing homework. The way you wrote <em>That sounds great!</em> and then smoothly moved into the practical details shows you know how to write a friendly email that gets things done. To reach Band 5 in every area, you just need to tighten a few small things in grammar and organisation. Let's look at each part.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hi Corey! That sounds great!</em> You opened with enthusiasm and a clear positive reaction, which directly addresses the first note.</li>\n  <li><em>I think we should build a robot. The Discovering Dino-saur film is too boring.</em> You clearly disagreed about the film and offered a specific alternative, covering the second note fully.</li>\n  <li><em>What about bringing our homework to do it together?</em> You suggested a practical item to bring and connected it to the plan, which covers the third note naturally.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Hi Corey! ... I'm waiting to see you! Beya</em> Your opening and closing are perfect for a friend — warm and personal.</li>\n  <li><em>I don't like to watch about dinosaurs.</em> You expressed your opinion in a direct, honest way that a friend would understand and accept.</li>\n  <li><em>Do you agree?</em> You ended the body with a question, which keeps the conversation going and involves Corey. That's exactly what a good email does.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a range of linking words without repeating the same one too often, and connects ideas smoothly within each paragraph. You have a clear structure with one topic per paragraph, which is great. The main gap is that you use <em>so</em> three times, and some sentences could be linked more tightly.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>I will bring a bit of pocket money with me so if we are going to do something else so we can pay for it.</em> → <strong>I will bring a bit of pocket money so that if we do something else, we can pay for it.</strong> Using <em>so that</em> instead of <em>so</em> makes the purpose clearer and avoids the double <em>so</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>Maths homework is really difficult so I want to do it with you.</em> → <strong>Maths homework is really difficult, so I want to do it with you.</strong> Adding a comma before <em>so</em> helps the reader see the cause-and-effect connection more clearly.</li>\n  <li>You used <em>because</em> once, <em>so</em> three times, and <em>what about</em> once. Try using <em>since</em> or <em>as</em> for reasons, and <em>therefore</em> or <em>that's why</em> for results. This variety makes your writing sound more mature.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls simple grammar with no errors and attempts complex sentences with few mistakes. Your vocabulary is good — words like <em>pick me up</em>, <em>allow me</em>, and <em>pocket money</em> are natural and appropriate. The main grammar rule that explains most of your errors is <strong>apostrophe use for possession and contractions</strong>. Let's fix that.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>my parent's aren't at home</em> → <strong>my parents aren't at home</strong>. Here, <em>parents</em> is just plural (more than one parent), so no apostrophe is needed. The apostrophe in <em>aren't</em> is correct because it's a contraction of <em>are not</em>. Remember: apostrophes show possession (the parent's car) or contractions (aren't, don't, I'm), not simple plurals.</li>\n  <li><em>they don't allow me to go somewhere by myself</em> → <strong>they don't allow me to go anywhere by myself</strong>. In negative sentences, use <em>anywhere</em> instead of <em>somewhere</em>. This is a common pattern: <em>some-</em> words are for positive statements, <em>any-</em> words are for negatives and questions.</li>\n  <li><em>I don't like to watch about dinosaurs.</em> → <strong>I don't like watching films about dinosaurs.</strong> After <em>like</em>, use the <em>-ing</em> form when talking about general preferences. Also, <em>watch about</em> is not natural — you watch <em>a film</em> or you watch <em>something</em>. Add the object.</li>\n  <li><em>I will bring a bit of pocket money with me so if we are going to do something else so we can pay for it.</em> → <strong>I will bring some pocket money so that if we do something else, we can pay for it.</strong> The double <em>so</em> makes the sentence confusing. Use <em>so that</em> for the purpose, and remove the second <em>so</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: Read each sentence aloud. Ask yourself: (1) Did I use an apostrophe? Is it for possession or a contraction, not a plural? (2) Did I use <em>some-</em> or <em>any-</em> correctly? (3) Did I use <em>like</em> + <em>-ing</em> for general preferences? Apply these three checks and your grammar will be much stronger.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36171	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027		Hi Corey,<br><br>I heard you got a tickets for the science festival. That sound great! I really excited to go with you.<br><br>For me, I think you should met at my house and prepare some important thing first. At my house have many science book you can borrow it from me too. But I have one book that I want you to read the name of the book is "How do animals live with out people" its have so much interesting.<br><br>I think we should watch the film about the dinosaurs. You will like it very much. I know you're loves dinosaur so much.<br><br>And off-course I'll bring my money. But I think we should prepare some water and little bag to keep our thing. I will bring some camera to record to I think it's a great idea. Do you? I want you to bring some snack because you're always being hungry.<br><br>I can't wait to see you and be in science festival. It will be so good if we have time to spend more together. But never mind just see you soon! good luck<br><br>Tietie	187	12.0	Tietie, your email to Corey is full of genuine enthusiasm, and that energy makes it a pleasure to read. You clearly stated your excitement about the science festival, suggested meeting at your house, and even offered to share your book about animals. The reader can feel how much you want this day to be special. One area to sharpen is making sure your response to each note in the task is crystal clear. For example, when the task asked you to disagree with Corey's film choice, you wrote 'I think we should watch the film about the dinosaurs,' which sounds like you agree with him instead of disagreeing. A small shift in wording can fix that. Now let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer covers every point in the task clearly and completely. You covered most of the notes, but your response to the film suggestion was confusing.</p><ul><li><em>I think we should watch the film about the dinosaurs.</em> → <strong>Actually, I'd prefer to watch the film about the dinosaurs instead.</strong> The task said to disagree with Corey's film choice. Your original sentence sounds like you're agreeing. Adding 'instead' or 'actually' makes the disagreement clear.</li><li><em>I heard you got a tickets for the science festival.</em> You did a good job reacting positively to the tickets right away. That is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li>You suggested meeting at your house, bringing money, water, a bag, a camera, and snacks. All of these ideas are relevant. Next time, try to include a clear reason for each suggestion so the reader understands your thinking.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of an email to a friend naturally and keeps the tone consistent throughout. Your opening and closing are warm and appropriate, and your enthusiasm comes through clearly. The reader can follow your ideas easily. A few small grammar slips prevent it from being Band 5, but you are very close.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>At my house have many science book</em> → <strong>I have many science books at my house</strong>. This small grammar fix will make your email sound more natural and polished.</li>\n  <li><em>I heard you got a tickets for the science festival</em> → <strong>I heard you got tickets for the science festival</strong>. 'Tickets' is plural, so drop the 'a'.</li>\n  <li><em>off-course</em> → <strong>of course</strong>. This is a common spelling error. 'Of course' is two words, and 'of' is not spelled 'off'.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs and linking words smoothly so the email flows naturally from one idea to the next. You used paragraphs to separate different topics, which is good, but some transitions feel abrupt.</p><ul><li><em>For me, I think you should met at my house...</em> → <strong>First, let's meet at my house...</strong> 'For me' is a bit awkward as a transition. Using 'First' or 'To start' makes the sequence clearer.</li><li><em>And off-course I'll bring my money.</em> → <strong>Of course, I'll bring some money.</strong> Starting a sentence with 'And' is okay in casual emails, but using 'Of course' or 'Naturally' sounds more polished. Also, 'off-course' should be 'of course'.</li><li>You moved from talking about the film to bringing items without a connecting sentence. → <strong>After the film, I think we should prepare some things. I'll bring my money, a bag, and a camera.</strong> A short linking sentence helps the reader follow your plan.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses mostly correct grammar and a range of vocabulary. The main rule that will help you is: every sentence needs a subject before the verb. Many of your errors come from missing or doubling the subject. Let's fix that.</p><ul><li><em>I heard you got a tickets for the science festival.</em> → <strong>I heard you got tickets for the science festival.</strong> 'Tickets' is plural here, so no 'a' needed. Use 'a' only before singular countable nouns (a ticket, a book).</li><li><em>That sound great! I really excited to go with you.</em> → <strong>That sounds great! I am really excited to go with you.</strong> After 'That', use 'sounds' (third person -s). After 'I', you need the verb 'am' before 'excited'.</li><li><em>I think you should met at my house...</em> → <strong>I think you should meet at my house...</strong> After 'should', always use the base form of the verb (meet, not met).</li><li><em>At my house have many science book you can borrow it from me too.</em> → <strong>I have many science books at my house. You can borrow one from me.</strong> The subject 'I' was missing at the start. Also, 'book' should be 'books' (plural) and 'it' should be 'one' because there are many books.</li><li><em>I know you're loves dinosaur so much.</em> → <strong>I know you love dinosaurs so much.</strong> After 'you', use 'love' (no 's'). 'You're' means 'you are', which doesn't fit here. Also, 'dinosaur' should be 'dinosaurs' (plural, general category).</li></ul><p>Self-check: Before you finish a sentence, ask yourself — 'Who or what is doing the action?' If you can't find the subject, add one. This will fix most of your grammar errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29765	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		This festivals want to tell us about you or your family celebrate. When does the festival take place, It was amazing to hear that. I think I like about it but the best articles<br><br>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible. We saw a group of people are bullied the man that come from asia, So we went to stop that bullying and helping that man. Then he say thank you for help. And he asking us why are you help me. We say we don't like the person that make other people feel bad. So It was bad thing	110	6.0	August, your story has a strong moment of kindness at its heart. When you wrote <em>we don't like the person that make other people feel bad</em>, you showed that you understand what a meaningful story needs: a clear moral position. That is a real strength. Now, the challenge is to build the whole story around that moment. Your opening paragraph about festivals does not belong in a story about helping someone. The task asked you to write a story, not an article about festivals. Next time, read the task twice before you start writing. Every sentence you write should move the story forward. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how you can reach Band 5.	A1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	1.0	<p>You're at 1/5 for content. A Band 5 writer tells a complete story that matches the prompt from the first sentence to the last. Your story about helping the man is good, but the festival paragraph at the beginning does not belong there.</p><ul><li><em>This festivals want to tell us about you or your family celebrate. When does the festival take place, It was amazing to hear that. I think I like about it but the best articles</em> → Delete this entire paragraph. It is not part of your story. The reader is confused because the story starts, stops, and then starts again. Your real story begins with <strong>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning</strong>.</li><li><em>So It was bad thing</em> → This ending is too short. A Band 5 story gives the reader a sense of closure. Add one more sentence to show how the man felt or what you learned. For example: <strong>The man smiled and said he would never forget what we did. I felt proud that we had helped someone.</strong></li></ul>	1.0	<p>You're at 1/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of a story consistently: a clear setting, characters, a problem, and a resolution. Your story has these elements, but the festival opening breaks the reader's trust.</p><ul><li><em>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible.</em> This is a strong story opening. It sets the time, place, and characters. Keep this sentence exactly as it is.</li><li><em>This festivals want to tell us about you or your family celebrate.</em> This sounds like an article, not a story. Remove it entirely. Your story should start with the school arrival sentence and never look back.</li><li><em>So It was bad thing</em> → <strong>So it was a bad thing, but at least we helped.</strong> The word <em>It</em> is capitalised in the middle of a sentence, and <em>bad thing</em> needs the article <em>a</em>. More importantly, the ending feels flat. A Band 5 story ends with a feeling or a lesson, not just a label.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects events in a clear sequence from beginning to middle to end. You used <em>so</em>, <em>and</em>, and <em>then</em> to link ideas, which is good. But the festival paragraph breaks the flow, and the story ends too suddenly.</p><ul><li><em>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible. We saw a group of people are bullied the man that come from asia, So we went to stop that bullying and helping that man.</em> This sequence works well. First you arrived, then you saw the bullying, then you acted. The word <em>So</em> connects the action to the problem. Keep this structure.</li><li><em>Then he say thank you for help. And he asking us why are you help me. We say we don't like the person that make other people feel bad. So It was bad thing</em> → The word <em>Then</em> is good, but the ending needs a stronger final sentence. After <em>So It was bad thing</em>, add a closing thought: <strong>We walked away feeling that we had done the right thing.</strong> This gives the reader a finished feeling.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls simple grammar most of the time, even when mistakes appear. The main grammar rule you need to learn is: after <em>saw</em>, use the base form of the verb, not <em>are</em> + past participle. Also, after <em>he</em> and <em>we</em>, use the correct verb form for present or past tense consistently.</p><ul><li><em>We saw a group of people are bullied the man</em> → <strong>We saw a group of people bully the man.</strong> After <em>saw</em>, use the base form of the verb (<em>bully</em>), not <em>are bullied</em>. Think: I saw him <em>run</em>. I saw them <em>play</em>. I saw a group <em>bully</em>.</li><li><em>we went to stop that bullying and helping that man</em> → <strong>we went to stop the bullying and help the man.</strong> After <em>to stop</em>, use the base form <em>help</em>, not <em>helping</em>. The pattern is: to + base verb.</li><li><em>Then he say thank you for help. And he asking us why are you help me.</em> → <strong>Then he said thank you for the help. He asked us, 'Why did you help me?'</strong> When telling a past story, use past tense: <em>said</em> not <em>say</em>, <em>asked</em> not <em>asking</em>. Also, <em>help</em> needs an object: <em>help me</em>.</li><li><em>We say we don't like the person that make other people feel bad.</em> → <strong>We said we don't like people who make others feel bad.</strong> In a past story, use <em>said</em> for the main verb. The word <em>that</em> can be replaced with <em>who</em> for people. Also, <em>make</em> should be <em>makes</em> if the subject is singular (<em>the person who makes</em>), or change <em>person</em> to <em>people</em> to keep <em>make</em>.</li><li><em>So It was bad thing</em> → <strong>So it was a bad thing.</strong> Do not capitalise <em>It</em> in the middle of a sentence. Add the article <em>a</em> before <em>bad thing</em>. Check every sentence for random capital letters. Only capitalise the first word of a sentence and proper nouns like <em>Asia</em>.</li></ul><p>Self-check: After every sentence, ask yourself: 'Is this verb in the past tense? Did I capitalise a word in the middle for no reason?' If you check these two things, your language score will rise quickly.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30334	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Festival that I enjoy most is my birthday. Every one will give me and my twin a gife. And I like it because it never make me disappoint. Two months ago my family celebrated at Taiwan. It felt like a dream and I can't belived it was ture!<br><br>We travelled and enjoyed every minutes at there. My sister gave me cute note book that reminds me of her favorite animal. My mom and My dad gave me a ticket to go Taipei 101! When I come back to Thailand even all my memories are at Taiwan. My friend were celebrated me by drawing and colouring picture of me and my twin. It was beautiful. I'm very thankful in every present every one give and celebrate. Thanks for reading.	132	14.0	Pran, your article about your birthday festival in Taiwan is full of personal details that make the reader feel like they were there with you. You described the trip, the gifts from your family, and even what your friends did back in Thailand. That kind of specific storytelling is exactly what a magazine article needs. To reach Band 5, you will need to polish your grammar and connect your ideas more smoothly, so every sentence flows naturally into the next. Let's look at each area where you can improve.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Festival that I enjoy most is my birthday.</em> You introduced the topic clearly and made the reader curious about your favourite festival.</li>\n  <li><em>Two months ago my family celebrated at Taiwan.</em> You gave a specific time and place, which helps the reader picture the event.</li>\n  <li><em>My mom and My dad gave me a ticket to go Taipei 101!</em> You included a concrete detail about a famous landmark, making the story more interesting.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the tone and style of a magazine article consistently, with a clear sense of audience from start to finish. You already have a good start with your personal stories and a closing thanks to the reader, but a few phrases feel more like a diary entry than an article.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Festival that I enjoy most is my birthday.</em> → <strong>The festival I enjoy most is my birthday.</strong> Adding 'The' at the beginning makes it sound like a proper article opening, not a casual thought.</li>\n  <li><em>Thanks for reading.</em> This is a nice gesture, but a magazine article usually ends with a final thought or invitation. Try something like <strong>I hope you enjoyed hearing about my special birthday celebration.</strong></li>\n  <li><em>Every one will give me and my twin a gife.</em> This sentence sounds like a general statement, but the rest of your article is about one specific birthday. Changing it to <strong>Everyone gives my twin and me gifts on our birthday</strong> would match the personal tone of the rest of the article.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear sequence of ideas and a variety of linking words to guide the reader smoothly. You have paragraph breaks and basic connectors like 'and' and 'when', but the order of events could be clearer.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Two months ago my family celebrated at Taiwan. It felt like a dream and I can't belived it was ture! We travelled and enjoyed every minutes at there.</em> The timeline jumps from the celebration to the travel. Try: <strong>Two months ago, my family celebrated my birthday in Taiwan. We travelled around and enjoyed every minute there. It felt like a dream, and I couldn't believe it was true!</strong></li>\n  <li><em>When I come back to Thailand even all my memories are at Taiwan.</em> The word 'even' is confusing here. Use 'but' or 'yet' to show contrast: <strong>When I came back to Thailand, all my memories were still in Taiwan.</strong></li>\n  <li>You used 'and' many times. Try replacing some with 'also', 'then', or 'after that' to vary your sentence openings.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar and vocabulary correctly so the reader can understand every sentence without effort. The main rule to work on is spelling common words and using past tense consistently. Many of your errors are from trying to write beyond your current spelling ability.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>gife</em> → <strong>gift</strong>. This is a common PET-level word. Practise writing it until it becomes automatic.</li>\n  <li><em>belived</em> → <strong>believed</strong>. 'Believe' follows the 'i before e' rule.</li>\n  <li><em>ture</em> → <strong>true</strong>. Spelling: t-r-u-e.</li>\n  <li><em>enjoyed every minutes at there</em> → <strong>enjoyed every minute there</strong>. 'Minutes' should be singular ('every minute'), and 'at there' is not correct — just 'there'.</li>\n  <li><em>My friend were celebrated me</em> → <strong>My friends celebrated with me</strong>. The verb 'celebrated' is already past tense — you don't need 'were' before it. Also, 'friend' should be plural 'friends' if there were more than one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check for your next draft: after you write a word you are unsure about, stop and check the spelling. Make a list of the five words you miss most often (like 'gift', 'believed', 'true', 'minute', 'celebrate') and practise them before you write.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30335	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Hello everyone! Today I'll tell you about Halloween festival. Halloween is one of my favorite festival. It's take place on 31th October. In Halloween festival people dress them selves as a ghost and decorate their house in spooky theme. And people often play trick or treat. Trick or treat is a game which people will knock knock on the door and say trick or treat. If you choose trick people that knock your door will scare you in silly face until you give them candy. If you choose treat you must give a candy to them. That's why I love halloween because I'll get candy every year. My family doesn't agree with me because It make me fat.	123	15.0	Prin R, your article about Halloween is full of energy and personal voice. I can tell you genuinely enjoy this festival, and that feeling comes through in every sentence. The way you explained trick or treat with the two choices — scare or candy — shows you can describe a tradition in a way that makes sense to someone who has never heard of it. That is exactly what a good magazine article does. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you will need to tighten your grammar and connect your ideas with a wider variety of linking words. Let's look at each subscale to see exactly where to focus your revision.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Halloween is one of my favorite festival.</em> You clearly state the festival and why it matters to you. The reader understands the topic immediately.</li><li><em>Trick or treat is a game which people will knock knock on the door and say trick or treat. If you choose trick people that knock your door will scare you in silly face until you give them candy. If you choose treat you must give a candy to them.</em> This is a detailed, step-by-step explanation of the tradition. A reader who has never celebrated Halloween would understand exactly how it works after reading your article.</li><li><em>That's why I love halloween because I'll get candy every year.</em> You connect your personal enjoyment directly to the tradition you described. The article feels honest and personal.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would end the article with a closing that wraps up the main idea, rather than stopping abruptly after the family disagreement. Your opening is strong and personal, and the informal register is perfect for a school magazine. The reader can follow everything you say without confusion. To reach Band 5, add a final sentence that gives the article a sense of completion.</p><ul><li><em>Hello everyone! Today I'll tell you about Halloween festival.</em> This is a warm, direct opening that makes the reader feel included. Great choice for a magazine article.</li><li><em>My family doesn't agree with me because It make me fat.</em> This is an interesting personal detail, but the article ends too suddenly. After this sentence, add something like: <strong>But I think a little candy once a year is worth it. What about you — do you celebrate Halloween in your country?</strong> This gives the reader something to think about and closes the article naturally.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would use a wider variety of linking words and avoid run-on sentences. Your ideas are in a logical order — introduction, description of the festival, explanation of trick or treat, personal opinion — which is good. But you rely heavily on 'and' and 'because' to connect everything, which makes some sentences feel rushed.</p><ul><li><em>In Halloween festival people dress them selves as a ghost and decorate their house in spooky theme. And people often play trick or treat.</em> Starting a sentence with 'And' is fine in informal writing, but you can make the connection smoother. Try: <strong>During Halloween, people dress as ghosts and decorate their houses with a spooky theme. After that, they often play trick or treat.</strong></li><li><em>If you choose trick people that knock your door will scare you in silly face until you give them candy. If you choose treat you must give a candy to them.</em> These two sentences are clear, but you could combine them for better flow: <strong>If you choose 'trick,' the people at the door will make silly faces to scare you until you give them candy. If you choose 'treat,' you simply give them candy and they leave happily.</strong></li><li><em>That's why I love halloween because I'll get candy every year.</em> This sentence uses 'because' to explain the reason, which works, but you could add variety: <strong>That is why I love Halloween — I get candy every year.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control subject-verb agreement and noun plurals consistently. The main rule you need to practice is: <strong>every singular noun needs a determiner (a, an, the, my, this, etc.) or a plural ending (-s, -es)</strong>. For example, 'festival' is singular, so you need 'a festival' or 'the festival' or 'my favorite festival.' Also, every verb must agree with its subject: 'it takes place,' not 'it take place.' Let's fix the most important errors.</p><ul><li><em>Halloween is one of my favorite festival.</em> → <strong>Halloween is one of my favorite festivals.</strong> 'One of' always takes a plural noun because you are talking about a group.</li><li><em>It's take place on 31th October.</em> → <strong>It takes place on 31st October.</strong> 'It's' means 'it is' or 'it has,' but here you need the simple present verb 'takes.' Also, ordinal numbers: 31st, not 31th.</li><li><em>people dress them selves as a ghost</em> → <strong>people dress themselves as ghosts</strong> 'Themselves' is one word, not two. Also, 'people' is plural, so 'ghosts' should be plural too.</li><li><em>people will knock knock on the door</em> → <strong>people knock on the door</strong> 'Knock knock' is not standard English. Just use 'knock' once.</li><li><em>It make me fat</em> → <strong>It makes me fat</strong> 'It' is third person singular, so the verb needs an -s: 'makes.'</li></ul><p>Self-check for next time: After you write a sentence, read it aloud and ask yourself two questions: (1) Is every singular noun marked with a, an, the, or a possessive word? (2) Does every verb match its subject? If the subject is he, she, or it, the verb needs an -s.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29635	2025-12-13 05:19:44.791553+00	2026	M2-4A 2025-6 T2 Midterm Exam Task 1	Dear maya, I'm so happy to know your class gonna visit my school next month. If you need help about a good place to stay i can help. we have a hostel in front of a school, it a good hostel a price was maybe 400 Bath per night I will give you a location later. And a place you should go to the museum i don't exacly know the name but i can give you a location. It not to far from school, the museum is usually opens at 10 am. About a body language I think wave a hand is not exacly good I think you should use a translate app to talk with Thai people.	118	12.0	Hello Pheem! 👋<br><br>Good effort! You wrote enough to communicate your ideas. However, we need to fix one big habit immediately.<br><br>📋 <b>Tips for next time:</b><br>1. <b>Capital Letters!</b> 🔠 You often write 'i' instead of 'I' and 'maya' instead of 'Maya'. This is a big mistake in exams. Always capitalize names and 'I'!<br>2. <b>Details:</b> 🎯 Add more detail to your tips.	B1	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	Partial coverage. Missed time, tips were weak. ⚠️	3.0	Okay but informal because of the capitalization issues.	3.0	Bit messy. Needs clearer structure.	3.0	Capitalization is the main issue! ⚠️ 'i' must be 'I'.	\N	\N	2025-12-13
30365	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Hello everyone. Today I'll write about the festival that my family or me celebrate. The festival I choose it is halloween festival. It's have on October in every year. In the festival it have the word that everyone have to say it is "trick or treat". It mean you want to give a candy to me or you want to see me make a scary face. I like halloween because when have this festival after this festival finish I have a lot of candy in my house and the second festival is christmas and the third is new year festival<br><br>I think my article will be published next month.	113	10.0	Anpan, you chose Halloween as your festival and you gave the reader some real details about it. I can see you know the tradition of <em>trick or treat</em> and you explained what the words mean. That is exactly the kind of specific information that makes an article interesting. You also told us when the festival happens, which helps the reader understand. To reach Band 5, your article needs to stay focused on one festival the whole way through. When you mentioned Christmas and New Year at the end, the reader lost track of Halloween. A Band 5 article also has a strong ending that wraps up the main idea. Let us look at each part of your writing to see how you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all the key information the task asks for and does not add unrelated ideas. You gave good details about Halloween, but then you brought in Christmas and New Year, which confused the focus.</p><ul><li><em>I like halloween because when have this festival after this festival finish I have a lot of candy in my house</em> → <strong>I like Halloween because after the festival ends, I have a lot of candy in my house.</strong> This is a clear, personal reason. Keep this kind of specific detail.</li><li><em>and the second festival is christmas and the third is new year festival</em> → Remove this sentence. The task asks you to write about one festival. Adding two more festivals makes the article feel unfocused.</li><li><em>I think my article will be published next month.</em> → Remove this sentence. The task does not ask you to talk about the article itself. End with a final thought about Halloween instead, like <strong>Halloween is my favourite festival because of the candy and the fun traditions.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone for a magazine article and keeps the reader interested from start to finish. You started well by greeting the reader, but the ending felt sudden.</p><ul><li><em>Hello everyone. Today I'll write about the festival that my family or me celebrate.</em> This is a good opening for a school magazine article. You are talking directly to your classmates.</li><li><em>I like halloween because when have this festival after this festival finish I have a lot of candy in my house</em> → <strong>I like Halloween because after the festival ends, I have a lot of candy in my house.</strong> This shows your personal feeling. A Band 5 article keeps this personal voice throughout.</li><li>The ending needs work. Instead of <em>I think my article will be published next month</em>, try something like <strong>That is why Halloween is my favourite festival of the year.</strong> This gives the reader a satisfying finish.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly so the reader can follow easily. Right now your article jumps from Halloween to Christmas to New Year without any warning.</p><ul><li><em>I like halloween because... and the second festival is christmas and the third is new year festival</em> → <strong>I like Halloween because after the festival ends, I have a lot of candy in my house. Halloween is my favourite festival of the year.</strong> By removing the other festivals, your article stays on one clear topic from start to finish.</li><li>You used <em>and</em> and <em>because</em> to connect ideas, which is a good start. Try adding a word like <strong>also</strong> or <strong>another reason</strong> to show the reader you are adding a new point. For example: <strong>Another reason I love Halloween is the scary decorations.</strong></li><li>Your paragraph break (the blank line between the main article and the last sentence) was a good idea. In your next draft, put that last sentence's content inside the main paragraph so the article feels complete.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct grammar most of the time and chooses words that fit the topic. The main rule that will help you is: every sentence needs a subject and a verb that agree. Let us fix the errors together.</p><ul><li><em>It's have on October in every year</em> → <strong>It is in October every year.</strong> The word <em>have</em> is not the right verb here. Use <em>is</em> or <em>takes place</em>. Also, <em>in every year</em> is not natural English. Just say <em>every year</em>.</li><li><em>In the festival it have the word that everyone have to say</em> → <strong>In the festival, there is a word that everyone has to say.</strong> Use <em>there is</em> or <em>there are</em> to say something exists. Also, <em>everyone</em> is singular, so it needs <em>has</em>, not <em>have</em>.</li><li><em>It mean you want to give a candy to me</em> → <strong>It means you want to give candy to me.</strong> The subject <em>it</em> needs the verb <em>means</em> (with an <em>s</em>). Also, <em>candy</em> is usually uncountable, so do not say <em>a candy</em>.</li><li><em>The festival I choose it is halloween festival</em> → <strong>The festival I choose is Halloween.</strong> You do not need <em>it</em> after <em>choose</em>. Also, festival names like Halloween, Christmas, and New Year need capital letters.</li><li><strong>Self-check for your next draft:</strong> After you write each sentence, ask yourself: <em>Does my verb match my subject?</em> For example, <em>everyone has</em>, <em>it means</em>, <em>the festival is</em>. This one rule will fix most of your errors.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30378	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		See you on Saturday!<br><br>Bye,<br><br>Ti<br><br>Part 2	8	0.0	Ti, you wrote <em>See you on Saturday!</em> and <em>Bye, Ti</em>, which shows you know how to end a friendly message. That's a real skill for informal emails. However, the task asked you to write an article for a school magazine. An article needs a title, an introduction, and paragraphs with your ideas or opinions. For your next draft, start by reading the task instructions twice. Ask yourself: what kind of writing does this ask for? Then plan three short paragraphs before you write. Let's look at each part of the scoring guide to see exactly how to reach Band 5.	NA	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	0.0	<p>You're at 0/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer responds fully to the prompt with all required information. Your text says <em>See you on Saturday!</em> and <em>Bye, Ti</em>, which does not address the article task at all.</p><ul><li><em>See you on Saturday!</em> → <strong>"My Favourite Weekend Activity" by Ti</strong>. Start with a title that tells the reader what your article is about. Then write your first sentence introducing the topic.</li><li><em>Bye, Ti</em> → <strong>"I hope you enjoyed reading about my weekend. What do you like to do on Saturdays?"</strong>. End an article with a question to your readers, not a sign-off. This keeps them thinking.</li></ul>	0.0	<p>You're at 0/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right register and format for the task. Your text sounds like the end of a personal email, not an article for a school magazine.</p><ul><li><em>See you on Saturday!</em> → <strong>"Do you ever feel like weekends are too short? I do, and here is why Saturday is my favourite day."</strong>. Articles use a friendly but informative tone. They grab the reader's attention with a question or a statement.</li><li><em>Bye, Ti</em> → <strong>"Ti"</strong> (as a byline). In an article, your name goes at the top or bottom as the author, not as a goodbye. The reader should know who wrote it, but the text itself should not say goodbye.</li></ul>	0.0	<p>You're at 0/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs and logical flow. Your text has only one line and a sign-off, so there is no structure to follow.</p><ul><li><em>See you on Saturday!</em> → <strong>Start with an introduction paragraph (2-3 sentences). Then write a body paragraph with details. End with a conclusion paragraph.</strong>. Every article needs at least three parts: beginning, middle, and end. Use a blank line between each part.</li><li><em>Bye, Ti</em> → <strong>End with a concluding paragraph that wraps up your ideas, not a farewell.</strong>. The sign-off belongs in a letter or email, not an article. Replace it with a sentence like "That is why Saturday is the best day of the week for me."</li></ul>	0.0	<p>You're at 0/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar correctly. Your text is too short to show your language ability, but we can build from here.</p><ul><li><em>See you on Saturday!</em> This sentence is grammatically correct. It shows you can use the present tense for future arrangements. In your article, try expanding: <strong>"Every Saturday, I see my friends at the park."</strong> This uses the same verb but in a different context.</li><li><em>Bye, Ti</em> This is a sign-off, not a complete sentence. In an article, write full sentences: <strong>"I hope you enjoyed my article. Write back and tell me about your Saturday!"</strong></li><li>Your text has no capitalisation errors, which is good. When you write your article, remember: every sentence starts with a capital letter, and every proper noun (like Saturday) is capitalised. Check your work by reading each sentence from the beginning.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30489	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning we saw something incredible! We saw that one new student named Sarah rode an elephant to school.<br><br>We were both shocked and amazed by the thing we saw at the same time. We walked away while walking we kept on talking about the thing we just saw. In class Sarah she walkied to sit with us. We both had question in our head but when I am about to asked her my question the teacher walked in. At first I thought it would just be a normal boring morning math class, but instead of grabbing a book he grabbed a card instead. Me and my friends were both confused but then he performed a very great magic trick.<br><br>We were all laughing and clapping. Then it break times Sarah still followed us. We were both quiet and keep on looking at her she thinks that she maybe do something wrong. Then we both asked her the same question how did she ride an elephant. She was confused at first but she started laughing and asked us to follow her. She then taught us to ride an elephant I was scared at first, but the thought that it was scary didn't stay that long. After that I bought her a drink and said thankyo. I have never felt this fun in my whole life. It was both an incredible and unforgettable experience.	246	16.0	Mil, your story is genuinely engaging. You took the given sentence about arriving at school and turned it into a memorable adventure with Sarah and the elephant. The moment where the teacher pulls out a magic trick instead of a math book is a clever twist that keeps the reader interested. To reach Band 5, focus on making the sequence of events smoother and tightening your grammar so nothing distracts from your creative ideas. Let's look at each area in detail.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning we saw something incredible! We saw that one new student named Sarah rode an elephant to school.</em> You start with a strong hook that immediately grabs attention and sets up the story perfectly.</li><li><em>She then taught us to ride an elephant I was scared at first, but the thought that it was scary didn't stay that long.</em> You develop the story through a clear arc: seeing the elephant, the magic trick, break time, learning to ride, and buying a drink. The reader follows every event without confusion.</li><li><em>It was both an incredible and unforgettable experience.</em> You end with a satisfying conclusion that matches the tone of the story.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the reader fully engaged with a completely consistent narrative voice and clear sequence. Your story already holds attention well, but a few tense shifts and choppy sentences break the flow slightly.</p><ul><li><em>We walked away while walking we kept on talking about the thing we just saw.</em> → <strong>We walked away, still talking about what we had just seen.</strong> This version is smoother and keeps the past tense consistent.</li><li><em>Then it break times Sarah still followed us.</em> → <strong>Then at break time, Sarah followed us.</strong> This small fix makes the time transition clear and grammatically correct.</li><li><em>We were both quiet and keep on looking at her she thinks that she maybe do something wrong.</em> → <strong>We were both quiet and kept looking at her. She thought that she might have done something wrong.</strong> Keeping all verbs in past tense helps the reader stay in the story without confusion.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects events with clear transitions so the reader never feels lost. You use paragraph breaks well, but some jumps between events happen too quickly.</p><ul><li><em>he performed a very great magic trick. We were all laughing and clapping. Then it break times Sarah still followed us.</em> → <strong>he performed a very great magic trick. We were all laughing and clapping. When break time came, Sarah followed us.</strong> Adding a short time phrase like 'When break time came' bridges the gap between the magic trick and break time.</li><li><em>We both had question in our head but when I am about to asked her my question the teacher walked in.</em> The jump from 'teacher walked in' to 'magic trick' is abrupt. Try: <strong>We both had questions in our heads, but when I was about to ask her, the teacher walked in. I thought we would start math class, but instead he pulled out a card.</strong> This prepares the reader for the surprise.</li><li><em>She then taught us to ride an elephant I was scared at first, but the thought that it was scary didn't stay that long.</em> This is a run-on sentence. Try: <strong>She then taught us to ride an elephant. I was scared at first, but the thought that it was scary didn't stay that long.</strong> Short sentences can be powerful when used deliberately.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with only occasional slips. Your vocabulary is a real strength — words like <em>incredible, unforgettable, shocked, amazed, clapping</em> show you are comfortable with a wide range of everyday language. You also attempted some more complex sentence structures, which is excellent. A few small errors prevent Band 5, but you are very close.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>she walkied</em> → <strong>she walked</strong>. The past tense of 'walk' is 'walked' (no 'i').</li>\n  <li><em>thankyo</em> → <strong>thank you</strong>. This is two words, and it's a very common phrase you know.</li>\n  <li><em>when I am about to asked her</em> → <strong>when I was about to ask her</strong>. Keep the past tense consistent: 'was' not 'am', and 'ask' not 'asked' after 'to'.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check: after you finish writing, read through and look for inconsistent tenses. If your story is in past tense, make sure every verb stays in the past. This one check will clean up most errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30490	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		In my family, we celebrated christmas every years even though we're not christian. Normally we will celebrated on 22 december since we'll travel on 24 - 26 December. My grandparent's house is where the party take place. At the party every one will buy a gift and shuffle it. last year, I got a beautiful blanket! My aunt and uncle will cook for us. their foods are the best! Around 6 p.m. all the relatives will be around the house and we'll open some songs to dance too. That's not even the best part! We'll have a firework! It was very beautiful and I'm the one who sit the closest too! The party will end around 8 - 9 p.m. We all were full and fun at the end of the party. I wish there will be more day like this.	148	16.0	Mile, your article about your family's Christmas celebration is genuinely enjoyable to read. You gave the reader a clear picture of the event—the date, the location, the gift exchange, the food, the dancing, and the fireworks—all in a natural, personal voice. The line <em>That's not even the best part!</em> creates real energy and makes me want to keep reading. To reach Band 5 across all areas, you'll need to tighten your grammar and tense control. Your ideas are strong and well-organised, so the next step is polishing the language that carries them.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>we celebrated christmas every years even though we're not christian.</em> You immediately tell the reader when, where, and why this festival matters to your family. The detail about not being Christian adds a personal, honest touch.</li><li><em>last year, I got a beautiful blanket!</em> A specific, memorable gift makes the celebration feel real. The reader can picture it.</li><li><em>We'll have a firework! It was very beautiful and I'm the one who sit the closest too!</em> You describe a highlight of the evening with excitement, and the personal detail about where you sat makes the scene vivid.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the same tense throughout the article so the reader never feels confused about when events happen. Your article shifts between present, future, and past, which slightly breaks the flow.</p><ul><li><em>we celebrated christmas every years</em> → <strong>we celebrate Christmas every year</strong>. Since this is a tradition that happens repeatedly, the simple present tense is the right choice. Stick with it for the whole article.</li><li><em>last year, I got a beautiful blanket!</em> → <strong>Last year, I got a beautiful blanket!</strong> This is fine as a past-tense example, but you need a clear signal like 'Last year' to mark the shift. The surrounding sentences should stay in present tense.</li><li><em>We all were full and fun at the end of the party</em> → <strong>We all were full and had fun at the end of the party</strong>. Keep the tense consistent with the rest of the paragraph.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects short sentences into longer, smoother ones so the reader moves easily from one idea to the next. Your sequence is logical, but some sentences feel choppy.</p><ul><li><em>My grandparent's house is where the party take place. At the party every one will buy a gift and shuffle it.</em> → <strong>The party takes place at my grandparents' house, where everyone buys a gift and shuffles them.</strong> Combining these two sentences makes the information flow more naturally.</li><li><em>My aunt and uncle will cook for us. their foods are the best!</em> → <strong>My aunt and uncle cook for us, and their food is the best!</strong> Joining cause and effect with 'and' creates a tighter connection.</li><li><em>Around 6 p.m. all the relatives will be around the house and we'll open some songs to dance too. That's not even the best part! We'll have a firework!</em> The exclamation works well as a transition. Try linking the fireworks sentence more closely: <strong>Around 6 p.m. all the relatives gather at the house, and we play songs to dance to. That's not even the best part—we set off fireworks!</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls verb tenses and subject-verb agreement so the reader never has to pause and guess the meaning. The main pattern that needs attention is tense consistency: you mix present, future, and past without a clear system.</p><ul><li><em>we celebrated christmas every years</em> → <strong>we celebrate Christmas every year</strong>. For a tradition that happens annually, use the simple present tense. Also check: 'every years' should be 'every year' (singular).</li><li><em>we will celebrated on 22 december</em> → <strong>we celebrate on 22 December</strong>. After 'will', the verb must be in base form (no -ed). But since this is a repeated event, the simple present is cleaner: 'we celebrate on 22 December'.</li><li><em>the party take place</em> → <strong>the party takes place</strong>. The subject 'party' is singular, so the verb needs an -s. This is a subject-verb agreement rule you can check by asking yourself: 'Is the subject one thing or many things?'</li><li><em>their foods are the best!</em> → <strong>their food is the best!</strong> 'Food' is usually uncountable, so it takes a singular verb and no -s.</li><li><em>We all were full and fun</em> → <strong>We all were full and had fun</strong>. 'Fun' is a noun, not an adjective. Use 'had fun' or 'felt happy'.</li></ul><p>Self-check for any sentence: Find the main verb. Ask yourself: 'Is this a one-time event in the past, a repeated tradition, or something happening right now?' That will tell you which tense to use. Then check if the subject is singular or plural to match the verb correctly.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
30847	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Hi auto<br><br>when you visited thailand . I need your to try the this somtam and Padthai , somtam is a food every body like it somtam is a food have a test very spicy<br><br>Padthai it is same a noodles but in have vegetable bus it is very yummy and I need to come at this festival it is songkran festival because everyone playing the water and watch , dancing everyone all go very happenning drink the water play all of day very fun bye have a good day .	91	9.0	Tonnam, you chose a great topic for your article — Songkran is a wonderful festival to write about, and your excitement really comes through when you describe everyone playing with water and dancing. The reader can tell you enjoy this celebration. To reach Band 5, you will need to focus your writing more closely on the festival itself and organise your ideas so each paragraph has one clear topic. Let's look at each area where you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer includes only information that is directly relevant to the task and develops each point clearly. You started with food, which is not part of the festival description, and that distracts the reader from your main topic.</p><ul><li><em>I need your to try the this somtam and Padthai</em> → <strong>I want to tell you about my favourite festival, Songkran.</strong> Open your article with the festival itself so the reader knows your topic right away.</li><li><em>I need to come at this festival it is songkran festival</em> → <strong>I love the Songkran festival because everyone plays with water, watches performances, and dances.</strong> This keeps the focus on the festival and explains why you enjoy it.</li><li><em>everyone all go very happenning drink the water play all of day</em> → <strong>Everyone goes out, drinks water, and plays all day long. It is very exciting.</strong> You described the fun of Songkran well — just keep the food details for a different article.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the correct format for an article and keeps a consistent tone throughout. Your opening 'Hi auto' sounds like a personal message, not a magazine article.</p><ul><li><em>Hi auto</em> → <strong>My Favourite Festival: Songkran</strong> or <strong>Have you ever been to Songkran?</strong> Articles often start with a title or a question to catch the reader's attention.</li><li><em>bye have a good day</em> → <strong>I hope you can experience Songkran one day!</strong> End your article with a closing thought, not a goodbye like in a letter.</li><li>The middle part of your article shares your personal experience, which is good for an article. Keep that style, but make the beginning and ending match the article format.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraphs with logical order and good linking words. You have paragraph breaks, but the sequence is confusing because you start with food, then switch to the festival.</p><ul><li><em>when you visited thailand . I need your to try the this somtam and Padthai</em> → <strong>Songkran is a festival in Thailand that happens every April.</strong> Start your first paragraph by introducing the festival. Then use the second paragraph to describe what happens during Songkran.</li><li><em>everyone playing the water and watch , dancing everyone all go very happenning</em> → <strong>Everyone plays with water, watches performances, and dances. It is very exciting.</strong> Use linking words like 'also', 'then', and 'because' to connect your ideas smoothly.</li><li>Try this structure: Paragraph 1 — what Songkran is and when it happens. Paragraph 2 — what you do and why you love it. This order helps the reader follow your thoughts easily.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses correct sentence structure and basic grammar most of the time. The main rule that will help you is: every sentence needs a subject and a verb that match. Let's fix your sentences step by step.</p><ul><li><em>somtam is a food have a test very spicy</em> → <strong>Somtam is a food that has a very spicy taste.</strong> You need 'that' or 'which' to connect the two parts. Also, 'test' should be 'taste'.</li><li><em>Padthai it is same a noodles but in have vegetable bus</em> → <strong>Pad Thai is like noodles, but it has vegetables in it.</strong> Remove the extra 'it' after 'Padthai'. Use 'like' instead of 'same a'. 'Bus' should be 'but' or 'and'.</li><li><em>everyone playing the water and watch</em> → <strong>everyone plays with water and watches</strong> or <strong>everyone is playing with water and watching</strong>. Choose one tense and use it consistently. Here, present simple works well.</li><li><em>when you visited thailand</em> → <strong>When you visit Thailand</strong>. Capitalise 'Thailand'. Also, 'visited' is past tense, but your article is about a general festival, so present tense is better.</li><li><strong>Capitalisation check:</strong> 'thailand', 'songkran', and 'padthai' should all start with capital letters. Also, 'somtam' is usually written 'som tam' or 'Som Tam'. Always capitalise proper nouns.</li></ul><p>Self-check for every sentence you write: Does this sentence have a subject? Does the verb match the subject? Is the tense the same as the sentences around it? If you check these three things, your writing will become much clearer.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33157	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Hello! this is milin\n\nI'm going to write about a festival my family celebrate or I can say the whole world. yes I'm talkin about new year everyone I know celebrate like who shouldn't this festival take in any place even in your house you just have to have yourself or anyones and you can celebrate this.\n\nMostly they will say "happy new year" to each other or make a wish there's many things you can do. And there'll be fire work too! I love taking pic of these I got so many of them in my phone you won't belive it! It's my fav time of the years! I love making a promies to myself like "I'll change this years' lik you know" new your new me!".\n\nthis's all now thanks for reading!\n\nbye!	144	10.0	Milin, your article about New Year has a lot of energy, and I can tell you really enjoy celebrating this festival. You did a great job including specific details like fireworks, photos, and resolutions, which makes your writing personal and interesting. The way you talk directly to your reader with phrases like 'you won't believe it!' makes the article feel lively and engaging. To reach Band 5, you'll want to make sure every sentence stays on topic and that your ideas are organised into clear paragraphs. Let's look at each area to see how you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all the key information the task asks for and keeps every sentence relevant to the topic. Your article mentions New Year, where it happens, and why you like it, which is a strong start. However, a few sentences are confusing or off-topic.</p><ul><li><em>like who shouldn't this festival</em> → <strong>like, who wouldn't celebrate this festival?</strong> The original sentence is unclear and doesn't add useful information. Stick to clear, relevant ideas.</li><li><em>this festival take in any place even in your house you just have to have yourself or anyones</em> → <strong>this festival can take place anywhere, even in your house. You just need yourself or other people.</strong> This is a good point about where to celebrate, but the wording is confusing. A clearer version helps the reader understand your message.</li><li><em>new your new me!</em> → <strong>New year, new me!</strong> This is a fun phrase, but it comes at the end without a clear connection to what you said before. If you want to keep it, explain it briefly, like 'I like the idea of a fresh start.'</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right style for a magazine article from start to finish. Your article has a friendly, informal tone that works well for a school magazine, but the opening and closing feel more like a personal letter or diary entry.</p><ul><li><em>Hello! this is milin</em> → <strong>Have you ever wondered what makes New Year so special? Let me tell you about my favourite festival!</strong> Starting with a question grabs the reader's attention and feels like a real magazine article.</li><li><em>this's all now thanks for reading! bye!</em> → <strong>I hope you enjoyed reading about my New Year traditions. What about you? How do you celebrate?</strong> Ending with a question to the reader keeps the article style consistent and invites them to think.</li><li>The middle of your article uses direct address well, like <em>you won't believe it!</em> This is exactly the kind of language that makes a magazine article engaging. Keep doing this.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer organises ideas into clear paragraphs, each with one main topic, and uses linking words to show how ideas connect. Your article has some paragraph breaks, but the ideas jump around, and your linking words are very basic.</p><ul><li><em>Hello! this is milin I'm going to write about a festival my family celebrate or I can say the whole world. yes I'm talkin about new year</em> → <strong>Hello! I'm Milin, and I'm going to write about a festival my family celebrates. In fact, the whole world celebrates it: New Year.</strong> Start a new paragraph when you introduce the main topic. This first paragraph could just introduce the festival.</li><li><em>Mostly they will say 'happy new year' to each other or make a wish there's many things you can do. And there'll be fire work too!</em> → <strong>Mostly, people say 'Happy New Year' to each other and make wishes. There are many things you can do. For example, there are fireworks!</strong> Use 'for example' to show that fireworks are one of the activities, not a new random idea.</li><li>Your second paragraph covers activities, fireworks, photos, resolutions, and a personal motto all in one block. Try splitting it: one paragraph about activities and fireworks, and another about resolutions and what New Year means to you.</li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses basic grammar and vocabulary correctly, even if the sentences are simple. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>every sentence needs a clear subject and a verb that matches it.</strong> Let's look at your sentences.</p><ul><li><em>my family celebrate</em> → <strong>my family celebrates</strong>. 'Family' is a singular group, so the verb needs an 's'. Think: one family = one action.</li><li><em>this festival take in any place</em> → <strong>this festival takes place anywhere</strong>. 'This festival' is singular, so the verb is 'takes'. Also, 'take place' is the correct phrase, not 'take in place'.</li><li><em>I love taking pic of these I got so many of them in my phone you won't belive it!</em> → <strong>I love taking pictures of them. I have so many on my phone. You won't believe it!</strong> Short sentences are fine. Break long run-on sentences into separate sentences. Also, 'believe' is spelled with 'ie', not 'ei'.</li><li><em>I love making a promies to myself like 'I'll change this years' lik you know'</em> → <strong>I love making promises to myself, like 'I'll change this year, you know?'</strong> 'Promise' is spelled with one 's'. 'Years'' is confusing — you probably mean 'this year' (singular).</li><li>Capitalisation: <em>Hello! this is milin</em> should be <strong>Hello! This is Milin.</strong> Start every sentence with a capital letter, and capitalise your name. Also <em>new year</em> should be <strong>New Year</strong> when it's the name of the festival.</li></ul><p>Here's a self-check for any sentence you write: Read it aloud. Does it have a subject (who or what)? Does the verb match that subject? If the sentence feels too long, can you split it into two shorter sentences? Try this with your next draft.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33185	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		The festivals that me and my parents celebrate is the new year it's on 31 December that day we went to the Khao Yai the city is very quiet but that day everybody are very excited. We go eat dinner in restaurant and wait there for around 5 or 6 hours to countdown here in restaurant is so many people in there because the restaurant is open for waiting the countdown when that time come the celebration start the firework come out everyone celebrate and happy I am so excited in that time the firework is so lound on the sky and it's very beautiful I love the new year countdown.	113	14.0	Auto, you've written a lively description of your New Year celebration at Khao Yai. I can feel your excitement when you describe the countdown and the fireworks lighting up the sky. Your essay gives the reader a clear picture of where you were, what you did, and why this festival matters to you. To reach Band 5, you'll need to shape this into a proper magazine article with clear paragraphs and a more consistent tone. Let's look at each area of your score.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>the new year it's on 31 December that day we went to the Khao Yai</em> You told us exactly when and where the festival happens. The reader knows it's New Year on December 31st at Khao Yai.</li><li><em>when that time come the celebration start the firework come out everyone celebrate and happy</em> You explained why you enjoy this festival: the fireworks, the celebration, and the excitement. The reader fully understands your feelings.</li><li><em>the firework is so lound on the sky and it's very beautiful</em> You added a personal reaction to the fireworks, which makes your article feel genuine and specific.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of a magazine article consistently, with a tone that suits the reader throughout. Your text reads more like a personal story told to a friend than a polished article for a magazine audience.</p><ul><li><em>me and my parents</em> → <strong>my parents and I</strong>. In a magazine article, use the more formal subject form. This small change makes your writing sound more polished.</li><li><em>everybody are very excited</em> → <strong>everyone was very excited</strong>. Use 'everyone' with a singular verb. Also, since this happened in the past, use 'was' not 'are'.</li><li><em>here in restaurant is so many people</em> → <strong>the restaurant was full of people</strong>. Magazine articles use complete sentences with articles and past tense. 'Here' is too informal for this context.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer organises ideas into clear paragraphs and uses a variety of linking words to guide the reader. Your text is one long sentence without any paragraph breaks, which makes it hard to follow.</p><ul><li><em>The festivals that me and my parents celebrate is the new year it's on 31 December that day we went to the Khao Yai</em> → <strong>The festival my parents and I celebrate is New Year. It takes place on 31 December. That day, we went to Khao Yai.</strong> Break your ideas into separate sentences. Each new idea deserves its own sentence.</li><li><em>We go eat dinner in restaurant and wait there for around 5 or 6 hours to countdown here in restaurant is so many people</em> → <strong>We ate dinner at a restaurant and waited there for about five or six hours for the countdown. The restaurant was full of people.</strong> Use a full stop to separate the dinner scene from the crowd description. This creates a natural pause for the reader.</li><li><em>when that time come the celebration start the firework come out everyone celebrate and happy I am so excited in that time the firework is so lound on the sky and it's very beautiful</em> → <strong>When the time came, the celebration started. The fireworks came out, and everyone celebrated. I was so excited. The fireworks were loud in the sky and very beautiful.</strong> Use periods to separate the countdown moment, the fireworks, your feelings, and the description of the fireworks. Each part is a new step in the story.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls past tense consistently and uses correct subject-verb agreement. The main pattern that needs attention is subject-verb agreement: your subjects and verbs must match in number (singular/plural) and tense.</p><ul><li><em>the festivals that me and my parents celebrate is</em> → <strong>the festival my parents and I celebrate is</strong>. 'Festival' is singular, so 'is' is correct. But 'festivals' is plural, which would need 'are'. Decide if you mean one festival (New Year) or multiple, and match your verb.</li><li><em>everybody are very excited</em> → <strong>everyone was very excited</strong>. 'Everybody' and 'everyone' are singular words. They take 'is' or 'was', not 'are' or 'were'.</li><li><em>the firework come out</em> → <strong>the fireworks came out</strong>. 'Fireworks' is plural, so use 'came' (past tense). Also, 'firework' is usually written as 'fireworks' (plural) because they come in many pieces.</li><li><em>the celebration start</em> → <strong>the celebration started</strong>. This happened in the past, so use the past tense 'started'.</li><li><em>the firework is so lound on the sky</em> → <strong>the fireworks were loud in the sky</strong>. 'Lound' should be 'loud'. Use 'in the sky', not 'on the sky'.</li></ul><p>Self-check: Before you finish, read every sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Is the subject singular or plural? Is the verb in the right tense for when this happened?' This will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35996	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Every year, we have one festival that take place on 25th december. Of couse, it's Christmas festival, My family and I love this festival, so much! During Chrismas festival we actually wear green and red theme, In Thailand it's winter, so the weather is so cool, but doesn't cool enough for snowing TT. So on Christmas festival. Many shops sale warm cloths, socks, or gloves in red or green. And Many of malls decorate a tree around them places. That's one of the reasons that I like Christmas! I usually go to The mall when I feel bored. So during The Christmas Many malls are decorated so beautifolly! I really like it. And they have some events about Christmas, too! such as decorate a tree, drawing santa, and some staff wearing and red and green uniform!	140	13.0	Fah, your article about the Christmas festival is full of genuine enthusiasm, and that's exactly what a school magazine article needs. I can feel your excitement when you write about the decorations and the events. You've given your reader a clear sense of when the festival happens and why you love it. To reach Band 5, you'll want to organise those ideas into a clearer structure with a proper introduction and conclusion, and polish your grammar so your reader doesn't get distracted by errors. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer includes all the information the task asks for, leaving no gaps for the reader. You told us the festival is on 25th December in Thailand, and you explained why you like it with decorations, events, and the weather. That's strong. The only thing missing is a more specific location — 'in Thailand' is quite broad. Your reader might wonder: is it in Bangkok, Chiang Mai, or somewhere else?</p><ul><li><em>Every year, we have one festival that take place on 25th december. Of couse, it's Christmas festival</em> → <strong>Every year on 25th December, we celebrate Christmas in Bangkok, Thailand.</strong> This tells the reader exactly when and where, which is what the task requires.</li><li><em>That's one of the reasons that I like Christmas!</em> You gave several reasons — the weather, the decorations, the events — which is excellent. The reader understands your feelings clearly.</li><li><em>And they have some events about Christmas, too! such as decorate a tree, drawing santa</em> You listed specific events, which makes your article vivid and personal. Well done.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer structures their article with a clear opening that hooks the reader, a developed middle section, and a closing that wraps up the main idea. Your article has a friendly, informal tone that suits a school magazine, but it reads like a list of thoughts rather than a complete article. Let's give it a proper shape.</p><ul><li><em>Every year, we have one festival that take place on 25th december. Of couse, it's Christmas festival</em> → <strong>Have you ever wondered what Christmas feels like in a tropical country? Every year on 25th December, my family and I celebrate Christmas in Thailand.</strong> This opening draws the reader in with a question and sets up the topic clearly.</li><li><em>So on Christmas festival.</em> This is a sentence fragment. → <strong>During Christmas,</strong> then continue with your idea. A complete sentence keeps the reader engaged.</li><li><em>That's one of the reasons that I like Christmas! ... I really like it.</em> You repeat 'I like' twice. Try a concluding sentence like <strong>Christmas in Thailand is truly special to me, and I look forward to it every year.</strong> This gives your article a satisfying ending.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges ideas in a logical order so the reader can follow easily. You have used paragraph breaks, which is good, but the sequence jumps around — you start with the date, then the weather, then decorations, then events, then back to decorations. Let's group similar ideas together.</p><ul><li><em>During Chrismas festival we actually wear green and red theme, In Thailand it's winter, so the weather is so cool, but doesn't cool enough for snowing TT. So on Christmas festival. Many shops sale warm cloths, socks, or gloves in red or green.</em> You mix clothing, weather, and shopping in one paragraph. → <strong>Group them: first paragraph about weather and clothing, second paragraph about decorations and events.</strong> This makes your article easier to follow.</li><li><em>And Many of malls decorate a tree around them places. That's one of the reasons that I like Christmas! I usually go to The mall when I feel bored. So during The Christmas Many malls are decorated so beautifolly!</em> You mention decorations twice with different details. → <strong>Combine all decoration ideas into one paragraph.</strong> For example: <strong>Many malls decorate trees around them, and the decorations are so beautiful. That's one reason I love Christmas — I usually go to the mall when I feel bored, and during Christmas it's even more magical.</strong></li><li>Your article ends abruptly after 'uniform!' → <strong>Add a short concluding sentence.</strong> Something like: <strong>Christmas in Thailand may not have snow, but it has warmth, colour, and joy.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling so the reader never has to guess the meaning. Your vocabulary is good — 'festival', 'decorate', 'events', 'uniform' — and your meaning is always clear. The main rule that explains most of your errors is <strong>subject-verb agreement with third-person singular subjects (he/she/it)</strong>. Let's fix that and a few other patterns.</p><ul><li><em>that take place on 25th december</em> → <strong>that takes place on 25th December</strong>. The subject 'festival' is singular, so the verb needs an 's'. This is the most common error in your article — check every 'he/she/it' subject.</li><li><em>doesn't cool enough for snowing</em> → <strong>isn't cool enough for snow</strong>. After 'isn't' or 'doesn't', use the base form of the verb or an adjective. 'Cool' is an adjective, so use 'isn't'. 'Snowing' should be 'snow' (the noun).</li><li><em>Many shops sale warm cloths</em> → <strong>Many shops sell warm clothes</strong>. 'Sale' is a noun; 'sell' is the verb. Also, 'cloths' are pieces of fabric, but 'clothes' are what you wear.</li><li><em>I usually go to The mall</em> → <strong>I usually go to the mall</strong>. 'The' does not need a capital letter in the middle of a sentence unless it's part of a proper name like 'The Mall Bangkapi'.</li><li><em>Of couse</em> → <strong>Of course</strong>. Spelling check. Also <em>Chrismas</em> → <strong>Christmas</strong>, and <em>beautifolly</em> → <strong>beautifully</strong>. These are common spelling errors — try reading your writing aloud slowly to catch them.</li></ul><p>Self-check for next time: After you write a sentence with 'he', 'she', 'it', or a singular noun like 'festival', stop and ask yourself: 'Does the verb have an s?' If not, add one. This one habit will fix half your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36006	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		My family love to celebrate Songkran festival. It's a Thailand's new year, people will visit their parents or cousin. Some are going to the temple to make a wishes of them self and family. After all of that, we will celebrate. We celebrate every when in thailand. when it's 26 april. We will shoot water gun and splash water on each other. Everyone love to celebrate Songkran because it's fun and we can make a new friends there. Every years, I will buy a new water gun and go out to celebrate with my friends. Then the next day I will go on a vacation with my family. We love going to the mountain, forest or some time beach. I very love Songkran Festival.	128	15.0	Indy, your article about Songkran is genuinely enjoyable to read. You gave the reader a clear picture of what happens during the festival — from visiting the temple to shooting water guns to the family vacation afterward. The detail about buying a new water gun every year is a lovely personal touch that makes your writing feel real. To reach Band 5, your next draft needs stronger organisation and more consistent grammar. Let's look at each area.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>My family love to celebrate Songkran festival.</em> You introduced the topic clearly and gave the reader a reason to keep reading.</li><li><em>Some are going to the temple to make a wishes of them self and family.</em> This shows the cultural side of Songkran, which makes the article informative.</li><li><em>Then the next day I will go on a vacation with my family. We love going to the mountain, forest or some time beach.</em> You included your personal plans, which fits the article genre perfectly.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer opens a magazine article with a hook that immediately grabs the reader's attention. Your opening is friendly but a bit flat. Try starting with a question or a vivid image instead of a simple statement.</p><ul><li><em>My family love to celebrate Songkran festival.</em> → <strong>Have you ever been soaked from head to toe while laughing with strangers? That is Songkran, my favourite festival of the year.</strong> This opening pulls the reader in and makes them curious.</li><li><em>I very love Songkran Festival.</em> → <strong>Songkran is the festival I look forward to most every year.</strong> This sounds more natural and confident for a magazine article.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer groups related ideas into clear paragraphs and uses time words to guide the reader smoothly. Your paragraphs are separated, but the order of ideas jumps around a bit.</p><ul><li><em>My family love to celebrate Songkran festival. It's a Thailand's new year, people will visit their parents or cousin. Some are going to the temple to make a wishes of them self and family. After all of that, we will celebrate.</em> → <strong>Group the temple visit and family visit together in one paragraph, then start a new paragraph for the water celebration.</strong> This makes the sequence easier to follow.</li><li><em>We celebrate every when in thailand. when it's 26 april.</em> → <strong>We celebrate all over Thailand on 26 April.</strong> Put the date and location right after the introduction so the reader knows the basics first.</li><li><em>Every years, I will buy a new water gun and go out to celebrate with my friends. Then the next day I will go on a vacation with my family.</em> The transition from 'celebrate with friends' to 'vacation with family' is clear. Well done keeping that logical.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar consistently, especially subject-verb agreement, plural forms, and articles. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>every countable noun needs an article (a, an, the) or a determiner (my, some, every) unless it is plural and general.</strong> Let's work through your sentences.</p><ul><li><em>It's a Thailand's new year</em> → <strong>It is Thailand's New Year.</strong> You do not need 'a' before a possessive noun like 'Thailand's'. The possessive already acts like an article.</li><li><em>make a wishes of them self and family</em> → <strong>make wishes for themselves and their family.</strong> 'Wishes' is plural, so no 'a'. 'Themself' is not a word; use 'themselves'.</li><li><em>Every years</em> → <strong>Every year.</strong> 'Every' is always followed by a singular noun. This is a fixed rule.</li><li><em>some time beach</em> → <strong>sometimes the beach.</strong> 'Sometimes' is one word meaning 'occasionally'. Also, 'beach' needs 'the' because you are talking about a specific type of place.</li><li><em>I very love Songkran Festival</em> → <strong>I really love the Songkran Festival.</strong> In English, 'very' cannot modify a verb directly. Use 'really' or 'a lot'. Also, 'Songkran Festival' needs 'the' when you name the specific festival.</li></ul><p>Self-check: Before you submit, read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Does every singular countable noun have a, an, the, or my/your/his/her/its/our/their?' If not, add one.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36013	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		Songkran Festival<br><br>Songkran is a Thai new year that week people celebration all week people often go back home to live with there family<br><br>In my family we went to my grandpa horse at Nakron patrom we do lot of celabrate sphech water to people and ate lot of yummy food together as a family. On songkran day I went to siam with my friends and play songkran water and water gun we have a lot of fun there. In the last day of songkran people usually goes to the tempo in thailand to see the thai Buddha. lastly in songkron festival people in thailand will have a very fun time.	115	11.0	Prin A, your article about Songkran has a warm, personal feel that makes it enjoyable to read. I can tell you really know this festival from your own experience — mentioning your grandpa's house, going to Siam with friends, and the temple visit all show that you have plenty to share. To reach Band 5, you'll want to add more specific details, like the month Songkran happens and what makes each part of the celebration special to you. Also, checking your spelling and grammar will help your reader follow your ideas without any confusion. Let's look at each part of your writing in more detail.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes all the key information the task asks for and develops each point with specific, personal details. Your article tells us when and where Songkran happens and why you like it, but some important information is missing or too general.</p><ul><li><em>Songkran is a Thai new year that week people celebration all week</em> → <strong>Songkran is the Thai New Year, and people celebrate it for a whole week in April.</strong> You mentioned 'all week' but did not name the month. Adding 'in April' gives the reader the exact time.</li><li><em>we went to my grandpa horse at Nakron patrom</em> → <strong>we went to my grandpa's house in Nakhon Pathom.</strong> You told us the province, which is good. The reader can picture where the festival happens.</li><li><em>we do lot of celabrate sphech water to people and ate lot of yummy food together as a family</em> → <strong>we celebrate by splashing water on people and eating lots of delicious food together as a family.</strong> You described two activities — water play and food — but you could add one more sentence about why these traditions matter to you, like 'I love getting my grandparents wet because everyone laughs together.'</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the right tone for a magazine article — personal and engaging — and keeps it consistent throughout. Your article has a clear introduction and conclusion, and you share your own experiences well. However, some words are hard to understand, which breaks the reader's flow.</p><ul><li><em>sphech water</em> → <strong>splash water</strong>. This is a common Songkran activity. Using the correct word helps the reader picture the scene instantly.</li><li><em>tempo</em> → <strong>temple</strong>. This spelling mistake might confuse someone who does not know Thailand. Once corrected, the sentence is clear: 'people usually go to the temple to see the Thai Buddha.'</li><li><em>grandpa horse</em> → <strong>grandpa's house</strong>. 'Horse' and 'house' sound similar but mean very different things. A magazine reader would expect 'house' here. Fixing this keeps your article professional.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer connects ideas smoothly using a variety of linking words and arranges paragraphs in a logical, easy-to-follow order. Your article has clear paragraphs separated by line breaks, each focusing on one topic: family, friends, and the temple. That is a good start.</p><ul><li><em>In my family we went to my grandpa horse... On songkran day I went to siam... In the last day of songkran people usually goes to the tempo...</em> You used 'In my family', 'On songkran day', and 'In the last day' to move from one paragraph to the next. Try varying these phrases: <strong>First, my family and I went to my grandpa's house... Then, on Songkran day, I went to Siam with my friends... Finally, on the last day, people go to the temple...</strong> This makes the sequence feel more natural.</li><li>You used 'and' and 'but' inside sentences, which is fine. To reach Band 5, add one or two more connectors like 'because', 'so', or 'also'. For example: <em>we do lot of celabrate sphech water to people and ate lot of yummy food</em> → <strong>we celebrate by splashing water on people, and we also eat lots of delicious food together.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar and spelling so that every sentence is clear. Your vocabulary is good for this topic — words like 'celebration', 'family', 'friends', and 'fun' are all appropriate. The main issue is that errors in spelling, verb forms, and word choice happen in almost every sentence. Let's focus on the most common pattern: subject-verb agreement and verb tense.</p><ul><li><em>week people celebration</em> → <strong>people celebrate</strong>. 'Celebration' is a noun. You need the verb 'celebrate' here. The rule: after a subject like 'people', use the base form of the verb (no -tion).</li><li><em>people usually goes</em> → <strong>people usually go</strong>. 'People' is plural, so the verb should be 'go' without 's'. This is a common mistake. Remember: he/she/it + verb+s, but they/people + verb (no s).</li><li><em>celabrate</em> → <strong>celebrate</strong>. Spelling. The word has an 'e' after the 'l'. Practice writing it: c-e-l-e-b-r-a-t-e.</li><li><em>songkron</em> → <strong>Songkran</strong>. The festival name is spelled S-o-n-g-k-r-a-n. You used 'songkron' at the end, which is a different sound. Keep the spelling consistent throughout your article.</li><li>Capitalisation: You wrote 'thai new year', 'nakron patrom', 'siam', 'thailand', 'buddha' — all of these should start with capital letters because they are proper nouns. <strong>Thai New Year, Nakhon Pathom, Siam, Thailand, Buddha.</strong> Every time you write a name of a country, city, festival, or religion, use a capital letter.</li></ul><p>After you fix these, read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: 'Does the verb match the subject? Is every proper noun capitalised? Is the spelling correct?' This self-check will help you catch errors before you submit.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36023	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! When we were almost at the front entrance, I heard meowing. At first I thought I was hallucinating or maybe my friend was pranking me, but turns out it was a box containing four kittens and their mom! I felt bad for them because they looked skinny. So I slowly approached the cats cautiously and then gave them a sausage that was originally my breakfast leftovers. After I gave it to them, Me and my friend hurried to the morning assembly. So we won't be punished for arriving late. After school, Me and my friend are planning to adopt them and bring them home. We lived next to eachother. So we can help take care of them. I'm hoping no one else got there before us and took them. We both wanted a cat as a pet!	153	18.0	Good, your story is genuinely fun to read. You opened with the given sentence and built a real narrative around it — the discovery of the kittens, the cautious approach, the shared breakfast, the rush to assembly, and the adoption plan. That's a complete story arc, and it works. You also included your own reactions (thinking you were hallucinating, feeling bad for the cats) which makes the reader care about what happens. To push this to Band 5 everywhere, you'll want to polish a few grammar patterns and smooth out the ending. Let's look at each area.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible!</em> You started with the given sentence exactly and immediately created curiosity. The reader wants to know what the incredible thing is.</li>\n  <li><em>I felt bad for them because they looked skinny. So I slowly approached the cats cautiously and then gave them a sausage that was originally my breakfast leftovers.</em> You included a clear cause (they looked skinny) and effect (you gave them food). The detail about the sausage being your breakfast leftovers makes the moment feel real.</li>\n  <li><em>After school, Me and my friend are planning to adopt them and bring them home.</em> You gave the story a satisfying resolution. The reader knows exactly what happens next, and the final line about both wanting a cat ties everything together.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer makes the ending feel complete, not rushed. Your story has a strong beginning and middle, but the ending arrives quickly without much buildup.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>After school, Me and my friend are planning to adopt them and bring them home.</em> → <strong>After school, my friend and I plan to adopt them and bring them home.</strong> The shift from past tense ("arrived", "heard", "gave") to present tense ("are planning") is confusing. Keep the tense consistent throughout the story.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm hoping no one else got there before us and took them.</em> → <strong>I hope no one else gets there before us and takes them.</strong> This sentence adds tension, which is good, but it comes after the resolution. Try moving it before the adoption plan: <strong>I hope no one else gets there before us. After school, my friend and I plan to adopt them and bring them home.</strong> That way the worry builds, then the plan resolves it.</li>\n  <li>The story could use one more sentence at the end to show how you feel about the whole day. Something like: <strong>It was the best start to a school day I'd ever had.</strong></li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses transitions that guide the reader smoothly from one moment to the next. Your time references are good, but a few transitions feel abrupt.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>So I slowly approached the cats cautiously and then gave them a sausage...</em> → <strong>I slowly and cautiously approached the cats, then gave them a sausage...</strong> You used both "slowly" and "cautiously" which is repetitive. Pick one adverb, or combine them as shown. The word "so" at the start of this sentence is fine, but try <strong>Feeling sorry for them, I slowly approached...</strong> to create a smoother link.</li>\n  <li><em>After I gave it to them, Me and my friend hurried to the morning assembly. So we won't be punished for arriving late.</em> → <strong>After I gave it to them, my friend and I hurried to morning assembly so we wouldn't be punished for arriving late.</strong> Joining these two sentences makes the cause and effect clearer and removes the abrupt "So" at the start of the second sentence.</li>\n  <li><em>We lived next to eachother. So we can help take care of them.</em> → <strong>Since we live next to each other, we can help take care of them.</strong> Using "since" or "because" connects the two ideas naturally instead of leaving them as separate short sentences.</li>\n</ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>hallucinating; cautiously; leftovers; adopt</em> — You used a range of vocabulary beyond basic everyday words, and you used them correctly and naturally.</li>\n  <li><em>When we were almost at the front entrance, I heard meowing.</em> — You used a complex time clause to set the scene, which shows strong grammatical control.</li>\n  <li><em>At first I thought I was hallucinating or maybe my friend was pranking me</em> — You used past continuous naturally within a narrative, exactly what strong PET writers do.</li>\n  <li><em>I'm hoping no one else got there before us and took them</em> — You managed a complex sentence with two time references, showing you can handle sophisticated structures.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36027	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		The warming christmas festival!<br><br>My familys always celebrate in this festival!. Before christmas day we have to decorate a christmas tree and this we celebrate at grandparents home and in christmas day we alway wear the red or green shirt and sometimes we wear santa , santy costumes my grandma and my aunt have make a cookie and break the buns but i didn't help i only waiting to eat only but the most that i like is when we gave the present to each other and open it !, I like to gusse what in my box and every years i get the present more than 5 box!<br><br>that why i really like christmas festival because it was the warming times!.<br><br>I felt happy to spend time with my family and do fun game with they.	142	14.0	Bluebell, your article about the Christmas festival is full of personal details that make it a pleasure to read. You describe decorating the tree, wearing costumes, eating cookies, and opening presents with real warmth. The reader can picture your family celebration clearly. To reach Band 5, you'll need to polish your grammar and organise your ideas into a smoother sequence. Let's look at each area in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>we celebrate at grandparents home</em> You clearly state where the festival takes place, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>we always wear the red or green shirt and sometimes we wear santa, santy costumes</em> You describe the costumes with specific colours and types, making the scene vivid.</li><li><em>I like to gusse what in my box and every years i get the present more than 5 box</em> You share a personal feeling about the presents, which helps the reader understand why this festival matters to you.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer keeps the same tone throughout the article and uses exclamation marks sparingly. Your title and opening are engaging, but the informal language and too many exclamation marks make the writing feel uneven.</p><ul><li><em>i didn't help i only waiting to eat only</em> → <strong>I didn't help. I just waited to eat.</strong> This sentence sounds like spoken language. In an article, use full sentences and avoid repeating 'only'.</li><li><em>that why i really like christmas festival because it was the warming times!</em> → <strong>That is why I really like the Christmas festival. It is a warm time.</strong> Keep the tone consistent. 'Warming' is not the right word here; 'warm' works better.</li><li>You used 5 exclamation marks in a short article. In a magazine article, one or two exclamation marks can show excitement, but using them in every sentence makes the writing feel less controlled. Try saving them for the most exciting moment, like opening presents.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges events in a clear order so the reader can follow the timeline easily. Your article jumps from decorating to costumes to eating to presents, which can confuse the reader.</p><ul><li><em>Before christmas day we have to decorate a christmas tree and this we celebrate at grandparents home and in christmas day we alway wear the red or green shirt</em> → <strong>Before Christmas Day, we decorate the Christmas tree at my grandparents' home. On Christmas Day, we always wear red or green shirts.</strong> Putting the location with the decorating and starting a new sentence for Christmas Day makes the sequence clear.</li><li><em>my grandma and my aunt have make a cookie and break the buns but i didn't help i only waiting to eat only but the most that i like is when we gave the present</em> This sentence covers eating and presents in one long sentence. Try splitting it: <strong>My grandma and aunt make cookies and buns. I don't help. I just wait to eat. But the part I like most is giving presents.</strong></li><li>Your paragraphs are separated with blank lines, which is good. To reach Band 5, make sure each paragraph covers one main idea: paragraph 1 for before Christmas, paragraph 2 for Christmas Day activities, and paragraph 3 for your feelings.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses correct capitalisation, subject-verb agreement, and consistent verb tenses. Your vocabulary is good, but errors in capitalisation, plurals, and verb forms hold you back.</p><ul><li><em>My familys always celebrate</em> → <strong>My family always celebrates</strong>. 'Family' is a singular noun, so the verb needs an 's'. Also, the plural of 'family' is 'families', not 'familys'.</li><li><em>every years i get the present more than 5 box</em> → <strong>every year I get more than five presents</strong>. 'Year' is singular after 'every'. 'Present' needs an 's' for plural. And 'I' is always capitalised in English.</li><li><em>my grandma and my aunt have make</em> → <strong>my grandma and my aunt make</strong> or <strong>have made</strong>. Use either simple present (for a habit) or present perfect (for something that happened), but not a mix of both.</li><li><em>do fun game with they</em> → <strong>play fun games with them</strong>. 'They' changes to 'them' after a preposition like 'with'. Also, 'game' needs an 's' because you play more than one game.</li><li>Capitalisation: In your article, 'christmas', 'i', 'that', and 'the warming' all start with lowercase letters. In English, the first word of every sentence, the pronoun 'I', and proper nouns like 'Christmas' are always capitalised. Before you submit your next draft, read every sentence aloud and check the first word and the word 'I'.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36029	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! Me and my friend saw that every one is not wearing a school uniform. The whole school have just two people that wear a school uniform. Me and my friend don't want to go to anywhere, because everyone is look at me and my friend. We are so shy. Then we saw one people that wear a school uniform, that make me and my friend feel shy less than that one people. but today everyone is wearing a school uniform and me and my friend do not feel shy anymore.	105	14.0	Ai, your story starts with a fantastic hook: <em>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible!</em> That opening sentence pulls the reader right into the scene, and you've built a clear beginning, middle, and end around the surprising sight of no uniforms. You also gave your characters a believable emotional journey from shyness to relief. To push your writing to the next level, you'll want to polish your grammar and make your sentences flow more smoothly. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible!</em> You used the given sentence perfectly and immediately created curiosity about what the characters saw.</li>\n  <li><em>Me and my friend don't want to go to anywhere, because everyone is look at me and my friend. We are so shy.</em> You clearly showed the characters' reaction to the situation, which makes the story feel personal and real.</li>\n  <li><em>but today everyone is wearing a school uniform and me and my friend do not feel shy anymore.</em> You brought the story to a satisfying close by showing how the problem was resolved. The reader understands the full journey.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would use a consistent, appropriate register throughout the story and make the narrative feel like a polished piece of writing rather than a spoken account. Your story has a clear plot, but the repeated use of <em>me and my friend</em> makes it sound informal, like you're telling a friend what happened instead of writing a story.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Me and my friend saw that every one is not wearing a school uniform.</em> → <strong>My friend and I saw that everyone was not wearing a school uniform.</strong> In stories, use the subject form <em>my friend and I</em> instead of <em>me and my friend</em>. This small change makes your writing sound more mature and appropriate for a written story.</li>\n  <li><em>Me and my friend don't want to go to anywhere, because everyone is look at me and my friend.</em> → <strong>My friend and I did not want to go anywhere, because everyone was looking at us.</strong> Using <em>us</em> instead of repeating <em>me and my friend</em> keeps the writing smooth and avoids sounding like spoken language.</li>\n  <li><em>that make me and my friend feel shy less than that one people</em> → <strong>that made my friend and me feel less shy than that one person</strong>. When you need to use the object form, <em>my friend and me</em> is correct. Also, <em>people</em> is already plural, so <em>one person</em> is the right way to say it.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would connect their ideas with a variety of linking words and avoid repeating the same sentence openings. Your story has a clear sequence of events, but you use <em>me and my friend</em> to start most sentences, which makes the structure feel repetitive.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Me and my friend saw that every one is not wearing a school uniform. ... Me and my friend don't want to go to anywhere ... Me and my friend do not feel shy anymore.</em> → Try varying your sentence openings. For example: <strong>My friend and I saw that everyone was not wearing a school uniform. We did not want to go anywhere. ... Today, however, we do not feel shy anymore.</strong> Using <em>we</em> after introducing <em>my friend and I</em> keeps the story flowing naturally.</li>\n  <li><em>Then we saw one people that wear a school uniform, that make me and my friend feel shy less than that one people. but today everyone is wearing a school uniform</em> → Use a wider range of linking words. <strong>After a while, we saw one person wearing a school uniform. That made my friend and me feel less shy. However, today everyone is wearing a school uniform.</strong> Words like <em>after a while</em> and <em>however</em> add variety and show the passage of time more clearly.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control basic grammar consistently, especially subject-verb agreement, tense, and word forms. Your vocabulary choices are good (like <em>incredible</em> and <em>shy</em>), but several grammar patterns need attention. The main rule to focus on is: <strong>after <em>be</em> (am/is/are/was/were), use a present participle (-ing form) for an action, not the base verb.</strong> For example, <em>everyone is looking</em>, not <em>everyone is look</em>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>every one is not wearing</em> → <strong>everyone was not wearing</strong>. This is a good start with the -ing form! Just remember to match the tense to the story (past tense: <em>was</em>).</li>\n  <li><em>everyone is look at me</em> → <strong>everyone was looking at me</strong>. After <em>is/was</em>, the verb must end in -ing. This is the most common error in your story.</li>\n  <li><em>The whole school have just two people</em> → <strong>The whole school had just two people</strong>. <em>School</em> is singular, so use <em>has</em> (present) or <em>had</em> (past). Since your story is in the past, <em>had</em> is correct.</li>\n  <li><em>one people that wear</em> → <strong>one person who was wearing</strong>. <em>People</em> is plural, so for one person use <em>person</em>. Also, use <em>who</em> for people and match the tense to the story.</li>\n  <li><em>but today everyone is wearing</em> → <strong>But today everyone is wearing</strong>. Remember to capitalise the first word of a sentence. This is a small fix that makes your writing look more polished.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: <strong>If you see <em>is/am/are/was/were</em> followed by a verb, ask yourself: does that verb end in -ing?</strong> If not, add -ing or change the sentence structure. This one rule will fix most of the errors in your story.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36036	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		On 25 december 2026, I have a chrismas festival to cerabrate chrismas eve! It take place in a fancy private resturant called "The marias" it has so many deserts and snacks. When I first walked into the resturant I saw a bunch of chrismas decoration all over the resturant, I was so excited that I ran and bump into a huge chrismas tree. After that me and my family choose the seat and read the menu, I picked out tomato pasta and french fries. While I wait for my dinner to come suddenly everyone in the resturant stand up from there seat and sings chrismas song at the same time, then the firework came out of the grass and made the most beautiful scene in my whole life! that is the best experience I ever had! I hope that next year I came here again next year!	147	15.0	Yada, your article about the Christmas festival is full of wonderful, specific details that make the reader feel like they were there with you. You described the decorations, the food, the singing, and even the fireworks coming out of the grass, which is a truly memorable image. To reach Band 5, you can focus on making your article even more polished by checking your grammar and spelling, and by using a wider variety of sentence structures to connect your ideas.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>On 25 december 2026, I have a chrismas festival to cerabrate chrismas eve!</em> You clearly state the festival, the date, and the reason for the celebration, fully informing the reader from the very first sentence.</li><li><em>I picked out tomato pasta and french fries.</em> This specific detail about your food choice makes the experience feel real and personal.</li><li><em>the firework came out of the grass and made the most beautiful scene in my whole life!</em> You end with a strong, emotional impression that leaves the reader with a clear picture of why this festival is special to you.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would structure their article with a clear, engaging introduction that hooks the reader from the start. Your enthusiasm is wonderful, but the opening sentence could be rewritten to sound more like the beginning of a magazine article.</p><ul><li><em>On 25 december 2026, I have a chrismas festival to cerabrate chrismas eve!</em> → <strong>Have you ever celebrated Christmas Eve at a fancy private restaurant? On 25 December 2026, I did exactly that!</strong> This kind of question or bold statement grabs the reader's attention and sets the scene like a real article.</li><li><em>that is the best experience I ever had!</em> → <strong>That was the best experience I have ever had!</strong> The past tense 'was' and the present perfect 'have ever had' are more grammatically precise for this closing thought.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words and time phrases to connect ideas smoothly, not just 'When', 'After that', and 'then'. Your story is in a clear order, which is good, but you can make it flow even better.</p><ul><li><em>When I first walked into the resturant I saw a bunch of chrismas decoration all over the resturant, I was so excited that I ran and bump into a huge chrismas tree.</em> → <strong>When I first walked into the restaurant, I saw Christmas decorations everywhere. I was so excited that I ran straight into a huge Christmas tree!</strong> Breaking this long sentence into two shorter ones makes it easier to read and more dramatic.</li><li><em>While I wait for my dinner to come suddenly everyone in the resturant stand up from there seat and sings chrismas song at the same time, then the firework came out of the grass</em> → <strong>While I was waiting for my dinner, everyone in the restaurant suddenly stood up and sang a Christmas song. Then, fireworks came out of the grass!</strong> Using 'While I was waiting' and starting a new sentence before 'Then' creates a clearer sequence of events.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar (like past tense and subject-verb agreement) consistently and uses everyday vocabulary accurately. The main rule to focus on is <strong>past tense consistency</strong>: when you are telling a story about something that already happened, almost every verb should be in the past tense.</p><ul><li><em>I have a chrismas festival</em> → <strong>I had a Christmas festival</strong>. Since this happened in 2026, use the past tense 'had'.</li><li><em>It take place</em> → <strong>It took place</strong>. 'Take' is present tense; 'took' is the correct past form.</li><li><em>I ran and bump into</em> → <strong>I ran and bumped into</strong>. Both actions happened in the past, so both verbs need the -ed ending.</li><li><em>everyone stand up from there seat and sings chrismas song</em> → <strong>everyone stood up from their seat and sang a Christmas song</strong>. 'Stood' and 'sang' are the correct past tense forms. Also, 'there' means a place; 'their' shows possession.</li><li><em>I hope that next year I came here again next year!</em> → <strong>I hope that next year I can come here again!</strong> When you talk about a hope for the future, use 'can come' or 'will come', not the past tense 'came'. Also, remove the repeated 'next year' at the end.</li></ul><p>One more thing to check: capital letters for proper nouns. 'December', 'Christmas', and 'The Marias' should all start with capital letters. Try reading your article aloud and ask yourself, 'Is this verb in the past tense? Does it match the time of the story?'</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36039	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		My family always celebrate Christmas. We have lived in Finland with my grandmother and her new husband. He is from Finland so we celebrate Christmas every year. We celebrate it on 24th, 25th, 26th December. We celebrate it at my grandmothers house. My mom will make the cake for everyone , my dad will help to cook the food for everyone, my grandmother and her new husband will make the salad. We will random the name of everyone in our family and then we will buy the present for the person we got. We often sing a song, play games and watch movies together. Before eating, we will take some picture together. We do these things together every year and it's very fun. I felt happy when I spend my time with my family. If it could happen, I want to celebrate Christmas day with my family in Finland again this year.	152	17.0	Beya, your article about Christmas in Finland is genuinely enjoyable to read. You gave the reader a clear picture of the celebration — the dates, the location at your grandmother's house, and the specific family traditions like making the cake, drawing names for presents, and singing together. That level of detail is exactly what a magazine article needs. To push your writing even further, think about how you can make the ending feel more complete. Your final sentence is a wish, which is nice, but a stronger closing could leave the reader with a lasting impression of why this festival matters to you. Let's look at each part of your writing to see where you can reach Band 5.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>We celebrate it on 24th, 25th, 26th December. We celebrate it at my grandmothers house.</em> You clearly told the reader when and where the festival happens, which is exactly what the task required.</li><li><em>My mom will make the cake for everyone, my dad will help to cook the food for everyone, my grandmother and her new husband will make the salad.</em> You described the family roles in preparation, giving the article a warm, personal feel.</li><li><em>We often sing a song, play games and watch movies together.</em> You included specific activities that show why you enjoy this celebration, fully informing the reader about your experience.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would end the article with a conclusion that ties everything together and leaves the reader satisfied. Your article starts strong and builds nicely, but the last sentence <em>If it could happen, I want to celebrate Christmas day with my family in Finland again this year</em> feels a bit sudden, like the article just stops.</p><ul><li><em>We do these things together every year and it's very fun.</em> → <strong>Doing these things together every year fills the house with laughter and warmth.</strong> This gives the reader a stronger feeling of the atmosphere before you move to the ending.</li><li><em>If it could happen, I want to celebrate Christmas day with my family in Finland again this year.</em> → <strong>I hope we can celebrate Christmas in Finland again this year, because these traditions mean so much to me.</strong> This ending feels more complete and explains why the wish matters to you.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear introduction, body, and conclusion, with smooth transitions between ideas. Your article has a logical flow — you introduce the festival, describe the preparations, then the activities — but the shift from describing what you do to your feelings could be smoother.</p><ul><li><em>We do these things together every year and it's very fun. I felt happy when I spend my time with my family.</em> → <strong>We do these things together every year, and that is what makes it so special. I always feel happy when I spend time with my family during Christmas.</strong> Connecting the activities directly to your feelings creates a smoother bridge between description and reflection.</li><li>You used <em>so</em>, <em>then</em>, <em>before</em>, and <em>and</em> to connect your ideas. Try adding a transition like <em>after that</em> or <em>finally</em> to guide the reader through the sequence of events more clearly.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls both simple and complex grammar accurately, and uses a wider range of vocabulary. Your article communicates clearly, but there are a few patterns that need attention. The main rule to focus on is <strong>subject-verb agreement in the present simple tense</strong>. When the subject is singular (he, she, it), the verb needs an -s ending. When the subject is plural (we, they, my parents), the verb stays in base form.</p><ul><li><em>My family always celebrate Christmas.</em> → <strong>My family always celebrates Christmas.</strong> 'Family' is singular here (one family), so the verb needs -s.</li><li><em>We will random the name of everyone in our family</em> → <strong>We will draw the name of everyone in our family</strong>. 'Random' is not used as a verb in English. The correct verb is 'draw' or 'pick'.</li><li><em>I felt happy when I spend my time with my family.</em> → <strong>I feel happy when I spend time with my family.</strong> Keep the tense consistent. You are describing a general feeling, so present simple works best here.</li><li><em>my grandmothers house</em> → <strong>my grandmother's house</strong>. When something belongs to one person, add an apostrophe before the -s.</li><li>Check your capitalisation: <em>Christmas day</em> should be <strong>Christmas Day</strong>, and <em>December</em> is always capitalised as a proper noun.</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
36171	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027		New year Day<br><br>When it near the christmas day me and my family will prepare some trip to traveling on New year day. I'll be really exciting because some Newyear it not just me and my family it have my friends and their family too. I will go to some hotel in other place that I've never been their. The last newyear I went to Hauhin with my friends and family. I be their for 4 days. first thing I'vedone is go to the beach and played with my friends. I really enjoy in that time. When I played finish I go to ated dinner. I go to my aunt restaurant it have many belisious food and dessert. You should try it. Then I went back to my condo after this I played game with my friends till 9 p.m. I went down to the beach to watch the fireworks. It so beatifull my friends really like it after that I play only games in my condo and sleep. Thistrip is the most trip I like.<br><br>I know my friend for 9 years. No I mean my parent and my friend parent are being frien befor so when I was born about 3 mouth I just know every one. I'm so enjoy in last year trip but it make me tan. But whatever I still like it!!	236	10.0	Tietie, you have written about a New Year trip with your family and friends, and you included some lovely personal details like watching fireworks on the beach and eating at your aunt's restaurant. The reader can tell you truly enjoyed this trip. To reach Band 5, you need to stay focused on the festival experience itself and cut out information that does not directly relate to it. You also need to organise your ideas in a clearer order so the reader can follow the story easily. Let me show you how each part of your writing can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for content. A Band 5 writer includes only information that is directly about the festival experience and keeps every sentence relevant to the task. You have good details about the trip, but some parts take the reader away from the main topic.</p><ul><li><em>I know my friend for 9 years. No I mean my parent and my friend parent are being frien befor so when I was born about 3 mouth I just know every one.</em> This paragraph explains how your families became friends, but the task asks you to describe a festival experience. Your reader wants to know more about what you did during New Year, not how long you have known your friends. Try replacing this with a sentence about a New Year tradition or a special moment from the celebration.</li><li><em>I go to my aunt restaurant it have many belisious food and dessert. You should try it.</em> This detail about the restaurant is good because it shows a personal experience. However, the phrase <em>You should try it</em> sounds like an advertisement. A Band 5 writer would keep the focus on describing the food as part of the festival memory: <strong>My aunt's restaurant served delicious food and dessert, which made the New Year dinner even more special.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for communicative achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the article format fully, with a clear opening that catches the reader's interest and a closing that wraps up the experience. Your writing shares personal feelings well, which suits an article, but it starts and ends abruptly.</p><ul><li><em>New year Day</em> is your only title, and the first sentence jumps straight into planning. A Band 5 article would begin with an engaging opening that makes the reader want to continue. For example: <strong>Every New Year, my family does something special, but last year's trip to Hua Hin was unforgettable.</strong></li><li>Your ending <em>But whatever I still like it!!</em> feels like an afterthought. A stronger closing would reflect on why the trip mattered: <strong>Even though I got a tan, I would do it all again because spending New Year with my favourite people was the best gift.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for organisation. A Band 5 writer arranges events in a logical order and uses a variety of linking words to show the sequence clearly. Your paragraphs are separated, but the second paragraph goes off topic, and your transitions are very repetitive.</p><ul><li><em>first thing I'vedone is go to the beach and played with my friends. I really enjoy in that time. When I played finish I go to ated dinner.</em> You use <em>first</em> and then <em>when</em>, but the time order is confusing. A Band 5 writer would use clearer sequence words: <strong>First, I went to the beach and played with my friends. After that, I had dinner at my aunt's restaurant.</strong></li><li><em>Then I went back to my condo after this I played game with my friends till 9 p.m. I went down to the beach to watch the fireworks.</em> The word <em>then</em> and <em>after this</em> are used too often. Try varying your transitions: <strong>Later, I returned to the condo and played games with my friends until 9 p.m. Finally, we went down to the beach to watch the fireworks.</strong></li></ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for language. A Band 5 writer uses correct basic grammar and checks their spelling. The main rule that explains most of your errors is <strong>subject-verb agreement and verb tense</strong>. You often use the wrong verb form after the subject. Let me show you how to fix this.</p><ul><li><em>When it near the christmas day</em> → <strong>When it was near Christmas Day</strong>. The subject <em>it</em> needs a past tense verb. Also, <em>christmas</em> and <em>new year</em> are proper nouns and must be capitalised: <strong>Christmas Day</strong>, <strong>New Year</strong>. Check every holiday name in your writing.</li><li><em>I be their for 4 days</em> → <strong>I was there for 4 days</strong>. The verb <em>be</em> must change to <em>was</em> for past tense. <em>Their</em> means possession (their house), but <em>there</em> means a place. These are different words.</li><li><em>I'vedone</em> → <strong>I had done</strong> or <strong>I did</strong>. You need a space between <em>I've</em> and <em>done</em>, or use simple past. Also check <em>I go to ated dinner</em> → <strong>I went to eat dinner</strong>. You mixed present and past in the same sentence.</li><li><em>It so beatifull</em> → <strong>It was so beautiful</strong>. You need the verb <em>was</em> after <em>it</em>. Also, <em>beatifull</em> is spelled <strong>beautiful</strong> (one 'l' at the end).</li><li><em>Thistrip is the most trip I like</em> → <strong>This trip is the one I like the most</strong>. The phrase <em>the most trip</em> is not correct. Use <em>the one I like the most</em> or <em>my favourite trip</em>. A self-check you can use: after every sentence, ask yourself 'Does my verb match my subject? Is it past or present tense?'</li></ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29528	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hello,<br><br>I'm so excited too that I'm going to stay with you for a week, we would have so much fun things to do for the two of us.<br><br>I would prefer to relax at home and watching TV programs, because I'm too lazy for the concert things.<br><br>I'm not very good at climbing the mountians and I've hurt my hamstring so it's too difficult for me, sorry.<br><br>And I just wanting to know if there's any Doritos there?<br>See you. Tonnam.	98	15.0	Tonnam, your email to your friend is full of energy and personality. You did a great job covering every point the task asked for: you showed excitement, explained your preference for relaxing at home, gave a clear reason for not climbing, and even asked about Doritos. The reader can picture exactly what you mean. To reach Band 5 across the board, your next draft needs stronger grammar control and more varied connecting words. Let me walk you through each area.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	All content is relevant to the task. The target reader is fully informed about all points: the candidate expresses excitement about the visit, states a preference for relaxing at home, explains why they cannot go climbing (hurt hamstring), and asks about Doritos. All three notes are addressed.	4.0	The conventions of an email to a friend are generally used appropriately. The tone is friendly and informal, with a suitable opening and closing. The register is consistent, though the phrase 'I'm too lazy for the concert things' is slightly informal but still appropriate for the context. The target reader can follow without effort.	3.0	The text is connected and coherent. Basic linking words such as 'because' and 'and' are used. The sequence is logical, with each paragraph addressing a different point. However, the text relies on simple connectors and lacks variety in cohesive devices.	3.0	Everyday vocabulary is used generally appropriately ('excited'; 'relax at home'; 'watching TV programs'; 'climbing the mountains'; 'hamstring'; 'Doritos'). Simple grammatical forms are used with a good degree of control ('I'm so excited too'; 'I would prefer to relax at home'; 'I'm not very good at climbing'; 'it's too difficult for me'). Errors are noticeable but meaning can still be determined ('we would have so much fun things to do'; 'I'm too lazy for the concert things'; 'I just wanting to know').	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29530	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hello Sandy<br><br>Me too! I've been thinking about it for the past 3 days. I think we should stay home or goos to the mall or the supermarket to buy some meat or go grab some drinks at the new boba shop that it's just open at the mall last monday, Because it's a rock concert it might cause a lot of Caos.<br><br>I just read the news this morning the news reporter she said that the Sant. peters mountains just open a hiking trail for only 5 euro a person. Also the Sant. peters mountains it's the mountain I showed you a couple weeks ago. I thinks it's 10 miles away from our school. So what time will you arrived at the front of my lawn?	134	10.0	Army, your email to Sandy has a really friendly, natural tone. I can tell you were thinking about what to say, and the way you shared your excitement with "Me too! I've been thinking about it for the past 3 days" sounds exactly like how friends talk. You also did a good job covering all three notes from the task. To reach Band 5, you'll need to make sure every part of your answer is clear and complete, especially when the task asks you to say no to something. Let's look at each area of the marking scale to see how you can improve.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	Minor irrelevances and/or omissions may be present. Target reader is on the whole informed. The candidate responds to all three notes: expresses excitement ('Me too!'), states preference for staying home or going to the mall instead of the concert, gives a reason for not climbing (mentions a hiking trail but does not clearly say no or give a reason against climbing), and asks a question ('what time will you arrived at the front of my lawn?'). However, the response to the climbing note is ambiguous and does not clearly address the task requirement to say no and give a reason.	3.0	Uses the conventions of the communicative task in generally appropriate ways to communicate straightforward ideas. The email has an opening salutation and a friendly tone. The register is mostly consistent, though some phrases are awkward ('goos to the mall', 'Caos'). The target reader can follow the main ideas without effort.	2.0	Text shows some organisation but links are basic or repetitive. Sequence may break down in places. The email is structured as a single paragraph with some use of basic linking ('Because', 'Also', 'So'). However, the flow is disrupted by abrupt shifts between topics and unclear connections between ideas.	2.0	Uses basic vocabulary for the task ('concert'; 'mall'; 'supermarket'; 'drinks'; 'boba shop'; 'hiking trail'; 'euro'). Uses simple grammatical forms, but errors are fairly frequent. Meaning is generally clear. Errors include: 'goos' (go), 'it's just open' (just opened), 'Caos' (chaos), 'the news reporter she said' (redundant subject), 'Sant. peters mountains' (St. Peter's), 'I thinks' (I think), 'what time will you arrived' (will you arrive).	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29547	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy!<br><br>I'm excited about that too! It's my first time spending time with you all week.<br><br>On first evening, I think it would be better to having fun outside than stay at home. I think I'd choose to go to the concert over relaxing at home. As for the activily that you've suggest, I think climbing mountain isn't a good idea. You know I'm afraid of high, so lets better do the other activies. May be can drive around the town or we could have a little picnic at the park? Tell me which you prefer.<br><br>Oh, I have a little question before you go. Who would come for the show at the concert? It's better for me to prepare!<br><br>See you soon Sandy!<br><br>-Proud-	143	15.0	Proud, your email to Sandy shows you know exactly how to make a reader feel welcome. You chose the concert over staying home and gave a clear reason, you explained why climbing is not a good idea with a personal detail, and you asked a thoughtful question about who will come. That is strong content work. To reach Band 5 across the board, you will need to polish your grammar and make your sentences flow more smoothly. Let's look at each area.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	All content is relevant to the task. The target reader is fully informed about all points: the candidate expresses excitement about the visit, chooses the concert over relaxing at home, explains why climbing is not a good idea (fear of heights) and suggests alternative activities, and asks a question about who will come to the concert. All three notes are addressed completely.	4.0	The conventions of an email to a friend are generally used appropriately. The tone is friendly and enthusiastic, with suitable opening and closing. The register is mostly consistent, though the phrase 'May be can drive around the town' is slightly unclear. The target reader can follow without effort, and the email communicates straightforward ideas effectively.	3.0	The text is connected and coherent. Basic linking words such as 'too', 'as for', 'so', 'or', and 'oh' are used. Paragraph breaks are present and help separate ideas. However, the sequence from the climbing topic to the question about the concert could be smoother, and the transition is a bit abrupt.	3.0	Everyday vocabulary is used generally appropriately ('concert'; 'climbing'; 'picnic'; 'show'; 'prepare'). Simple grammatical forms are used with a good degree of control, though mainly in present and future tenses ('I'm excited'; 'I think it would be better'; 'I'd choose'; 'lets better do'). Errors are noticeable but meaning can still be determined ('to having fun'; 'the activily that you've suggest'; 'I'm afraid of high'; 'May be can drive around the town').	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29580	2026-07-10 01:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 1	Hi Sandy,<br><br>I'm so excited with you either. That would be fun.<br><br>In my suggession, I prefer to go to concert with you over relax at home because there're familler and having fresh air.<br><br>But I don't want to climbing in the moutains, because it would be dangerous if we tell down the hills. It we have nothing to do shall we go to aquarium?<br><br>See you!<br><br>Plern	75	10.0	Plern, you wrote a friendly email to Sandy, and I can tell you were thinking about how to respond to her excitement. Your opening line, <em>I'm so excited with you either. That would be fun.</em>, shows you understand the task is to reply to a friend. You also made a clear choice between the concert and relaxing at home, and you gave a reason for not wanting to climb mountains. That's strong work for a first draft. To reach Band 5, you need to make sure every part of your answer matches what the task asks. The task asked you to respond to Sandy's excitement, say which activity you prefer and why, explain why you don't want to do the other activity, and ask a question. You did three of those things well, but you added an idea about the aquarium that was not asked for, and you forgot to ask Sandy a question. Let's look at each part of your writing to see how to push every section to Band 5.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 1	3.0	Minor irrelevances and/or omissions may be present. Target reader is on the whole informed. The candidate responds to Sandy's excitement, expresses a preference for the concert, and gives a reason for not climbing. However, the candidate adds an irrelevant suggestion to go to the aquarium, which is not prompted. The note about asking a question is not addressed, so there is a minor omission.	3.0	Uses the conventions of the communicative task in generally appropriate ways to communicate straightforward ideas. The email has a friendly opening and closing, and the tone is appropriate for a friend. However, the register is inconsistent due to spelling errors and awkward phrasing, but the target reader can follow the main points.	2.0	Text shows some organisation but links are basic or repetitive. Sequence may break down in places. The email is divided into paragraphs, but the connection between ideas is weak. The transition from the concert preference to the climbing refusal is abrupt, and the suggestion about the aquarium is introduced without a clear link.	2.0	Uses basic vocabulary for the task ('excited'; 'concert'; 'relax'; 'climbing'; 'dangerous'; 'aquarium'). Uses simple grammatical forms, but errors are fairly frequent. Meaning is generally clear. Errors include 'with you either' (instead of 'too'), 'suggession' (suggestion), 'familler' (familiar), 'tell down' (fall down), 'It we have nothing' (If we have nothing).	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
