h breaks (via <br><br>) helps structure the email. However, the transition from the climbing topic to the zoo question could be smoother.	4.0	A range of everyday vocabulary is used appropriately ('wonderful time'; 'rock concert'; 'settle in'; 'long journey'; 'injured my leg'; 'falling down the stairs'; 'came across'; 'zoo'). A range of simple and some complex grammatical forms is used with good control, including present perfect ('I've heard'), conditional ('I'd prefer'), and past simple ('I injured'). Errors are minor and do not impede communication ('we're gonna' is informal but acceptable; 'any leg involving activities' is slightly awkward but clear).	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
35309	2025-08-21 15:44:29.390872+00	2026	Social Issue	in my view, I strongly agree that students should be able to use mobile phones in classrooms. Firstly, our technology is way ahead of what we had before. According to my dad, who's an engineer, cellular devices weren't a thing before my generation, and he also told me that it's quite challenging to even find how to draw a car without searching online. Next, kids need online assistant on quite lot of stuff such as reading, listening, information gathering and overall entertainment. I couldn't survive a day without my phone, it helps me research informations on those difficult subjects like history, science etc. and also with AI assistant peoples work are always in the best qaulity. Lastly, the statement which, I saw online. According to World's Technology Association (WTA), kids with the access of cellular devices or online, tend to have a better discipline than the one that doesn't, because of how the phone's algorithm taught us to be discipline using notifications and more. Ultimately I think kids should be able to access to mobile phones in classes so it would be more compelling to attend classes and to do works.	191	14.0	<b>📝 Dear Atom</b><br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Social Issue'. You wrote 191 words and achieved B1. You presented a clear opinion with supporting ideas, showing a good grasp of English grammar and an ambition to use more complex structures.<br><br>	B1	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	4.0	You presented a clear opinion and provided supporting ideas. The target reader is generally well-informed, but strengthening the direct connection between your reasons and your main opinion would make your arguments even more convincing.<br><b>Problem:</b> Your first supporting idea about your dad's generation and finding how to draw a car does not directly support why mobile phones should be allowed in classrooms.<br><b>Correction:</b> For example, you could write: "Firstly, the rapid advancement of technology means that mobile phones are now essential learning tools. My father, an engineer, often highlights how much more challenging it would have been to access information for his studies without modern cellular devices, demonstrating their value in today's educational environment."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Ensure your reasons directly answer or explain your main opinion. In English academic writing, each supporting point should clearly link back to your main argument.	3.0	You used the conventions of an academic paragraph to communicate your ideas. However, some of your supporting reasons didn't fully or clearly support your main opinion, which made your argument less impactful for the reader. This is a common point for B1 level writers to develop.<br><b>Problem:</b> In your second reason, you state, "Next, kids need online assistant on quite lot of stuff such as reading, listening, information gathering and overall entertainment. I couldn't survive a day without my phone, it helps me research informations on those difficult subjects like history, science etc." While it speaks to the usefulness of phones, the personal tone and the phrase "I couldn't survive a day without my phone" are too informal for an academic paragraph.<br><b>Correction:</b> You could revise this to be more formal and directly connected to classroom use: "Furthermore, mobile phones offer indispensable online assistance for students. They provide immediate access to resources for reading, listening, and information gathering, which is crucial for researching challenging subjects like history and science. The integration of AI assistants also ensures that students can produce work of the highest quality."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Academic writing in English typically requires a more formal and objective tone. Personal anecdotes and informal language can weaken the impact of your arguments. Focus on presenting information and arguments in a way that is suitable for a formal essay or report.	3.0	Your paragraph is connected and coherent, and you used basic linking words like "Firstly," "Next," and "Lastly" to guide the reader through your points. This is a good foundation. To achieve a higher score, try to use a wider variety of linking words and more complex cohesive devices to create an even smoother flow between your ideas.<br><b>Problem:</b> While you use good basic linking words, there are instances where sentences could be connected more smoothly for a richer flow, e.g., "Lastly, the statement which, I saw online. According to World's Technology Association (WTA)..."<br><b>Correction:</b> You could combine these thoughts with a more sophisticated linker: "Lastly, there is external validation for the benefits of mobile device access. For instance, according to the World's Technology Association (WTA), students with access to cellular devices or online resources tend to exhibit better discipline..."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, using a variety of cohesive devices (like conjunctions, transitional phrases, and pronouns) helps to create a more sophisticated and fluent text. It shows the relationship between ideas clearly and avoids a choppy feeling.	4.0	You used a range of everyday vocabulary appropriately, and you showed ambition in using both simple and some complex grammatical forms with good control. Your errors did not prevent the reader from understanding your message. This is a strong aspect of your writing. To further improve, pay close attention to minor errors, especially those common for Thai speakers.<br><b>Problem:</b> "in my view, I strongly agree that students should be able to use mobile phones in classrooms. Firstly, our technology is way ahead of what we had before." The sentence starts with a lowercase 'i'.<br><b>Correction:</b> "In my view, I strongly agree that students should be able to use mobile phones in classrooms. Firstly, our technology is way ahead of what we had before."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, every sentence must begin with a capital letter. This is a fundamental rule of punctuation.	\N	\N	2025-08-21
29528	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. And now, she has arrived at Bali, and she knows that this trip is going to be insane and she's going to have so much fun at Bali. The first thing after she got out from the airport is, she ran to the nearest taxi and she said "take me to the nearest beach around here". The Taxi driver just nodded yes and quickly drove to the beach. She was stunned by the clearness of the water and the sight she was seeing is magnificent. at first, she came here just for the vacation but now, she's in love with Bali now, and decided to not going back. since then she became a diver there and she lives with the ocean every day.	139	15.0	Tonnam, you have written a story that is genuinely satisfying to read. The moment when Lou is stunned by the clearness of the water and calls it magnificent shows that you know how to make a reader feel what the character feels. Your story has a clear beginning (arrival), middle (first impression of the beach), and end (decision to stay and become a diver), which means the reader never feels lost. To reach Band 5 across all areas, the next step is to pay closer attention to how you connect your sentences and how you use grammar. Let's look at each part of your writing in detail.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> You continued the prompt sentence perfectly, which immediately places the reader in the story.</li>\n  <li><em>She was stunned by the clearness of the water and the sight she was seeing is magnificent.</em> You gave a vivid, personal reaction from Lou that makes the beach feel real and important.</li>\n  <li><em>since then she became a diver there and she lives with the ocean every day.</em> You closed the story with a satisfying resolution that shows how the trip changed Lou's life.</li>\n</ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses storytelling conventions with full confidence, making the narrative feel natural and polished from the first sentence to the last. Your story communicates clearly, but the opening repeats the prompt sentence exactly, which is fine, and the register stays consistent throughout.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>she ran to the nearest taxi and she said "take me to the nearest beach around here".</em> This dialogue is a great choice for a story. To make it feel more natural, you could add a small action before the speech: <strong>she ran to the nearest taxi, jumped in, and said, "Take me to the nearest beach around here."</strong></li>\n  <li><em>at first, she came here just for the vacation but now, she's in love with Bali now</em> The repetition of "now" weakens the effect. Try: <strong>At first, she came just for a vacation, but now she's in love with Bali.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs to separate different parts of the story and connects ideas with a variety of linking words. Your story is written as one long paragraph, which makes it harder for the reader to see where one moment ends and the next begins.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Your story has three clear parts: the arrival, the trip to the beach, and the decision to stay. Each part deserves its own paragraph. For example, the sentence <em>She was stunned by the clearness of the water...</em> could start a new paragraph because it describes a new moment.</li>\n  <li>You use <em>and</em> and <em>but</em> to connect ideas. Try adding time words like <em>as soon as</em>, <em>the moment</em>, or <em>after that</em> to show the order of events more clearly. For instance: <strong>As soon as she got out of the airport, she ran to the nearest taxi.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls tenses consistently and forms sentences with few errors. The main rule that explains most of your errors is this: after a preposition like <em>of</em> or <em>from</em>, use the gerund (-ing form), and after <em>decided</em>, use the infinitive (to + verb).</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>decided to not going back</em> → <strong>decided not to go back</strong>. After <em>decided</em>, use the infinitive form of the verb: <em>decided to go</em>, <em>decided not to go</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>after she got out from the airport</em> → <strong>after she got out of the airport</strong>. The correct preposition after <em>got out</em> is <em>of</em>, not <em>from</em>.</li>\n  <li><em>the sight she was seeing is magnificent</em> → <strong>the sight she was seeing was magnificent</strong>. The whole story is in the past, so keep <em>was</em> consistent. A good self-check: read every verb in your story and ask yourself, "Is this in the same time as the sentence before it?"</li>\n  <li><em>at first</em> and <em>since then</em> need capital letters because they start sentences. Also, <em>Taxi driver</em> should be <em>taxi driver</em> (no capital unless it starts a sentence). Watch for random capitals on common nouns like <em>Taxi</em>.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29530	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. Lou saw a lovely couple holding hands while there were looking at the golden sand beach as the sun pour down on them. When she first landed Lou went and bought her self a pink lemon drink to help her self cool down from the extream heat from the sun. Then Lou book her self a cab to her hotel in the middle of the island. Her room had the best view of the island.	89	12.0	Army, your story has a strong start. You immediately put the reader in the scene with Lou looking down at the golden beaches from the plane. That's a great way to begin a narrative. You also kept the action moving forward with a clear sequence of events: landing, buying a drink, taking a cab, and arriving at the hotel. To reach Band 5, your story needs a complete arc. Right now it stops after Lou gets to her room. A Band 5 story would show what happens next. Does she explore the island? Does she meet someone? Does something unexpected happen? A strong ending gives the reader a sense of closure. Let's look at each part of your writing to see exactly how to push every area to 5/5.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer tells a complete story with a clear beginning, middle, and end. Your story has a beginning and middle, but it stops just as things get interesting. The reader wants to know what happens after Lou reaches her room.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Her room had the best view of the island.</em> This is where your story ends, but it could be the start of the next event. Add one or two more sentences to show what Lou does next. For example: <strong>She unpacked her bag, grabbed her camera, and headed straight for the beach.</strong> This gives the reader a satisfying sense of completion.</li>\n  <li>You described Lou's actions clearly: landing, buying a drink, booking a cab. That's good. Now add a small problem or a moment of surprise to make the story more interesting. Even a simple detail like <strong>Lou realised she had forgotten her sunscreen</strong> would add a small conflict that makes the story feel complete.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses the conventions of storytelling to create a complete narrative with a clear arc. Your story follows a logical order and the reader can follow easily, but it lacks a conclusion that wraps up the experience.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>When she first landed Lou went and bought her self a pink lemon drink to help her self cool down from the extream heat from the sun.</em> This sentence works well as a narrative moment. The reader understands why Lou buys the drink. To reach Band 5, add a final paragraph that gives the story a sense of resolution. For example: <strong>After a long day of travelling, Lou finally relaxed on her balcony, watching the sunset over the ocean. She knew this holiday was exactly what she needed.</strong></li>\n  <li>The register is simple but appropriate for a story. To make it more engaging, add one or two sentences that show Lou's feelings or thoughts. For instance, instead of just describing what she did, tell us how she felt: <strong>Lou felt a wave of relief as she stepped into the cool hotel lobby.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a variety of linking words and structures to connect ideas smoothly. Your story is easy to follow because you use 'when', 'then', and 'and' to show the order of events. That's a solid foundation.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. Lou saw a lovely couple holding hands while there were looking at the golden sand beach as the sun pour down on them.</em> These two sentences both start with 'Lou saw'. Try combining them or varying the sentence openings. For example: <strong>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches below. On the sand, a lovely couple held hands while watching the sun pour down on them.</strong> This avoids repetition and sounds more natural.</li>\n  <li>You use 'then' to move from one event to the next. That works, but you can also use words like 'after that', 'next', 'soon', or 'a few minutes later' to add variety. Try replacing one 'then' with a different time word.</li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses a range of vocabulary and grammar with good control. You have some strong phrases, like 'golden beaches', 'pink lemon drink', and 'the best view of the island'. Your past tense is mostly correct. The main rule to focus on is subject-verb agreement in the past continuous and past simple. Let's fix the key errors.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>while there were looking at the golden sand beach</em> → <strong>while they were looking at the golden sand beach</strong>. 'There' is for places. 'They' is for people. This is a common confusion. Remember: 'there' = a place, 'their' = belonging to them, 'they're' = they are.</li>\n  <li><em>as the sun pour down on them</em> → <strong>as the sun poured down on them</strong>. The story is in the past tense, so 'pour' needs to become 'poured'. Check every verb in your story to make sure it matches the past tense.</li>\n  <li><em>book her self a cab</em> → <strong>booked herself a cab</strong>. Two corrections here: 'book' needs '-ed' for past tense, and 'her self' is one word: 'herself'. The same applies to 'help her self' → 'help herself'. When you use 'self' with 'her', 'him', 'my', 'your', it is always one word.</li>\n  <li><em>extream heat</em> → <strong>extreme heat</strong>. The correct spelling is 'extreme'. This is a common spelling error. Try writing the word three times to remember the 'e' at the end.</li>\n  <li>Your capitalisation is correct throughout. Well done. After you finish a sentence, ask yourself: 'Is every verb in the past tense?' and 'Is every -self word written as one word?' This self-check will catch most of your errors.</li>\n</ul>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29547	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Everyone have the different types of films they enjoy, but have you<br>ever wondered how teenagers think about their favourite types of film? As a<br>teenager, I'll tell you about my opinion in this article.<br><br>In my opinion, I enjoy thriller films the most, and followed by<br>action films. What I see about thriller films is whenever we're watching<br>it, we would think more about how each character would do next. For<br>example when it's about police tries to arrest the murderer, we'd try<br>to imagine how the murderer is going to escape. It sometimes complicate<br>and you might be confuse about it, and you know it's common things you'll<br>see while people are watching this type of films.<br><br>As about choosing ways to watch films, I guess I'd prefer watching<br>in cinema over at home. The reasons is when I watch it at the<br>cinema, I can focus more on the film I'm watching at the time.<br>No one would shout or make loud noises in cinema. It's the rule<br>that everyone must know and follow.<br><br>All in all, these are only my opinions so please don't judge all<br>teenagers with my article. We all have different sight and opinions.	185	17.0	Proud, your article is genuinely engaging. You opened with a rhetorical question that pulls the reader in, and you gave a complete, thoughtful answer to every part of the task. The way you explained why thriller films make you think — <em>we would think more about how each character would do next</em> — shows real insight. To reach Band 5 in every area, focus on polishing your grammar so your strong ideas are expressed just as clearly as they are in your mind. Here is how you can strengthen each part of your writing.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>I enjoy thriller films the most, and followed by action films.</em> You clearly stated your two favourite film types, which is exactly what the task asked for.</li><li><em>when it's about police tries to arrest the murderer, we'd try to imagine how the murderer is going to escape.</em> You gave a specific, vivid example of why thrillers are exciting. The reader can picture the scene.</li><li><em>when I watch it at the cinema, I can focus more on the film I'm watching at the time. No one would shout or make loud noises in cinema.</em> You explained your preference for cinema over home with a clear, personal reason. The reader understands your choice completely.</li></ul>	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Everyone have the different types of films they enjoy, but have you ever wondered how teenagers think about their favourite types of film?</em> Your opening question directly addresses the reader and sets a friendly, magazine-style tone.</li><li><em>All in all, these are only my opinions so please don't judge all teenagers with my article.</em> This closing adds a personal, humble touch that makes the article feel genuine and thoughtful.</li><li>Throughout the article, you used a consistent first-person voice and a tone that is both informative and warm. The reader feels like they are hearing a real teenager's perspective.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses clear paragraphing and smooth transitions so the reader moves easily from one idea to the next. Your paragraphs are well separated, but the link between your first and second paragraph could be stronger, and <em>As about</em> is not a standard transition.</p><ul><li><em>As about choosing ways to watch films, I guess I'd prefer watching in cinema over at home.</em> → <strong>As for choosing how to watch films, I would prefer watching at the cinema over at home.</strong> <em>As for</em> is the correct phrase to introduce a new topic.</li><li>To connect your opening paragraph to your film preferences, try: <em>As a teenager, I'll tell you about my opinion in this article.</em> → <strong>As a teenager, I want to share my opinion. First, let me explain which types of film I enjoy most.</strong> This gives the reader a clear signal that you are moving from the introduction to your main point.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and noun plurals consistently. The main rule to focus on is: <strong>singular subjects need singular verbs, and plural subjects need plural verbs.</strong> Many of your errors happen because the subject and verb do not match in number. Let's fix the most important examples.</p><ul><li><em>Everyone have the different types of films they enjoy</em> → <strong>Everyone has different types of films they enjoy.</strong> <em>Everyone</em> is singular, so it takes <em>has</em>, not <em>have</em>.</li><li><em>The reasons is when I watch it at the cinema</em> → <strong>The reason is when I watch it at the cinema</strong> or <strong>The reasons are when I watch it at the cinema.</strong> If you use the singular <em>reason</em>, use <em>is</em>. If you use the plural <em>reasons</em>, use <em>are</em>.</li><li><em>It sometimes complicate and you might be confuse about it</em> → <strong>It sometimes gets complicated, and you might be confused about it.</strong> After <em>might be</em>, you need an adjective: <em>confused</em>, not <em>confuse</em>. Also, <em>complicate</em> is a verb; you need the adjective <em>complicated</em> or the verb <em>gets complicated</em>.</li><li><em>you know it's common things you'll see while people are watching this type of films</em> → <strong>you know these are common things you'll see while people are watching this type of film</strong> or <strong>these types of films.</strong> <em>Type of film</em> is singular, so use <em>this type of film</em>. If you mean multiple types, use <em>these types of films</em>.</li><li>One more pattern: check your capitalisation. In your article, <em>As about</em> starts a new paragraph, but <em>As</em> is correctly capitalised. However, in the middle of a sentence, make sure only proper nouns and the word <em>I</em> are capitalised. You did well with this overall, so just keep it in mind.</li></ul><p>After you revise, read each sentence aloud and ask yourself: <em>Is my subject singular or plural? Does my verb match?</em> This self-check will help you catch these errors before you submit.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29580	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	Last week Lou book the Japan ticket for traveling. When Lou arrive at airport and step into the plane Lou saw a group of tourist either but they were quite bossy so Lou ignore them and sit at Lou' seat. As the plane flew lower Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. Lou started to take his picture with his favorite camera, but Lou camera get broke for some resonable. Lou were sad, but after the flight Lou camera can use. Lou got confuce very much.	88	10.0	Plern, you've written a complete story with a clear beginning, middle, and end. The way you described Lou seeing the golden beaches from the plane creates a nice visual moment. To reach Band 5, your story needs clearer explanations of what happens and more varied sentence structures. Let me show you how each part of your writing can grow.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer makes sure every event in the story is clearly explained so the reader never feels confused. Your story has a good sequence, but the camera breaking and then working again without any reason leaves the reader wondering what happened.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Lou camera get broke for some resonable.</em> → <strong>Lou's camera broke for some reason.</strong> The word 'reasonable' doesn't fit here, and the reader needs to know why the camera broke. Even adding one detail like <strong>Lou's camera broke, perhaps because it hit the seat.</strong> would help.</li>\n  <li><em>after the flight Lou camera can use.</em> → <strong>After the flight, Lou's camera worked again.</strong> The reader needs to understand how it was fixed. Did Lou fix it? Did someone help? A short explanation like <strong>Lou asked a mechanic at the airport to look at it, and it worked again.</strong> would make the story complete.</li>\n  <li><em>Lou got confuce very much.</em> → <strong>Lou was very confused.</strong> This is a good moment to show Lou's feelings, but it needs to connect to the camera situation. Try: <strong>Lou was very confused about how the camera could break and then work again.</strong></li>\n</ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer uses a consistent storytelling tone and adds details that make the reader feel involved. Your story is easy to follow, but some words feel too casual for a written story, and the reader doesn't get enough sensory details.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>they were quite bossy so Lou ignore them</em> → <strong>they were quite loud and demanding, so Lou decided to ignore them.</strong> 'Bossy' and 'ignore them' sound like spoken English. In a written story, use more descriptive language to show the reader what happened.</li>\n  <li>You could add one more detail about the setting. For example, instead of just saying <em>Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below</em>, you could add: <strong>Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below, sparkling in the afternoon sunlight.</strong> This helps the reader picture the scene.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects events with varied linking words and makes the sequence of events clear. Your story uses 'and', 'but', and 'so' too many times, and the jump from the camera breaking to working again is confusing.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><em>Lou camera get broke for some resonable. Lou were sad, but after the flight Lou camera can use.</em> → <strong>Lou's camera broke for some reason. Lou was sad. However, after the flight, the camera worked again.</strong> Using 'however' instead of 'but' makes the contrast stronger and sounds more mature.</li>\n  <li>Try using time words to guide the reader. For example: <strong>First, Lou booked the ticket. Then, at the airport, Lou saw a group of tourists. Later, on the plane, Lou's camera broke.</strong> This helps the reader follow the timeline clearly.</li>\n  <li>The camera breaking and then working again needs a connecting sentence. Add something like: <strong>Lou did not understand how this happened.</strong> between the two events to show the reader that time passed and Lou was confused.</li>\n</ul>	2.0	<p>You're at 2/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer uses past tense consistently and checks for small grammar and spelling errors. The main rule that will help you is: <strong>use the simple past tense for actions that happened in the past, and add an apostrophe + s for possession.</strong> Let's look at your sentences.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>You did a good job using past tense for some verbs, like <em>saw</em> and <em>started</em>. This shows you understand the idea.</li>\n  <li><em>Lou book the Japan ticket</em> → <strong>Lou booked the Japan ticket.</strong> 'Book' needs to be 'booked' because the story is in the past.</li>\n  <li><em>Lou arrive at airport</em> → <strong>Lou arrived at the airport.</strong> 'Arrive' needs to be 'arrived', and 'airport' needs 'the' before it.</li>\n  <li><em>Lou camera get broke</em> → <strong>Lou's camera broke.</strong> 'Lou' needs an apostrophe to show the camera belongs to Lou. 'Get broke' should be 'broke' (simple past).</li>\n  <li><em>Lou were sad</em> → <strong>Lou was sad.</strong> 'Lou' is one person, so use 'was', not 'were'.</li>\n  <li><em>Lou got confuce very much</em> → <strong>Lou was very confused.</strong> 'Confuce' should be 'confused', and 'very much' is not natural here. Use 'very' before the adjective.</li>\n  <li>Check your capitalisation. Words like 'Japan' and 'Lou' are correct, but make sure every sentence starts with a capital letter. Your sentences do start correctly, so keep doing that.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-check: Read each sentence and ask yourself: 'Is this verb in past tense? Does the noun need 'the' or 'a' before it? Does the person need an apostrophe for possession?' This will catch most of your errors.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29591	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. It was beautiful. The light reflecting on water the color of golden sand. Lou wanted to go there. It look so magical she couldn't put her eyes away. She slowly be began to walk there, eyes glued to the beautiful color of the sand. When she arrived there she played with the sand and it was very soft. She loved it she spend hours playing there. When it was dark she went home with a smile on her face she wanted to come back again before she even reached home. And it was another beautiful and happy day for her.	115	15.0	Preme, you have a real gift for description. Your opening line — <em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below</em> — immediately pulls the reader into the scene. The way you layered sensory details like <em>golden beaches</em>, <em>light reflecting</em>, and <em>beautiful color of the sand</em> shows you understand how to make a story vivid. To push your writing even further, try varying your sentence openings and using a wider range of connecting words. Let's look at each area of your score to see exactly where you can grow.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> You started the story right from the prompt and immediately set the scene. The reader knows exactly where we are.</li><li><em>She played with the sand and it was very soft.</em> This small sensory detail makes Lou's experience feel real and personal. You didn't just say she went to the beach — you showed her interacting with it.</li><li><em>When it was dark she went home with a smile on her face she wanted to come back again before she even reached home.</em> You gave the story a complete emotional arc: arrival, discovery, reluctance to leave. The reader understands Lou's full journey.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would make the ending feel as complete and polished as the beginning. Your story has strong descriptive power and a clear emotional journey, but the final sentence feels rushed compared to the careful opening.</p><ul><li><em>It look so magical she couldn't put her eyes away.</em> → <strong>It looked so magical that she couldn't take her eyes off it.</strong> This is a creative way to express wonder. Fixing the grammar would make the reader feel Lou's amazement even more strongly.</li><li><em>she wanted to come back again before she even reached home. And it was another beautiful and happy day for her.</em> → <strong>She already wanted to return before she had even reached home. It had been another beautiful, happy day.</strong> The original ending has two ideas joined by 'And'. Splitting them and removing 'And' gives the story a more satisfying close.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer would use a wider variety of sentence openers and connecting words to keep the reader engaged. Your story has a clear sequence of events, but <em>she</em> starts most sentences, and <em>and</em> connects nearly every idea.</p><ul><li><em>Lou saw the golden beaches... Lou wanted to go there.</em> → <strong>Lou saw the golden beaches... She wanted to go there.</strong> Using 'she' after introducing Lou by name is fine, but try opening sentences with time words or descriptive phrases: <strong>From the window, Lou saw the golden beaches... Eager to explore, she walked toward the shore.</strong></li><li><em>She loved it she spend hours playing there. When it was dark she went home with a smile on her face she wanted to come back again</em> → <strong>She loved it and spent hours playing there. When it grew dark, she headed home with a smile, already planning to return.</strong> The original runs three ideas together without connectors. Adding 'and', 'when', and a comma creates rhythm and clarity.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer would control past tense consistently and use a wider range of vocabulary. Your word choices are strong — <em>golden</em>, <em>magical</em>, <em>soft</em>, <em>smile</em> — but several grammar errors stop the reader from enjoying them fully. The main pattern to fix is past tense: every action happened in the past, so every main verb needs the -ed ending or an irregular past form.</p><ul><li><em>It look so magical</em> → <strong>It looked so magical</strong>. 'Look' is a regular verb. Add -ed for past tense: <strong>looked</strong>.</li><li><em>she spend hours playing there</em> → <strong>she spent hours playing there</strong>. 'Spend' is irregular. Past tense: <strong>spent</strong>.</li><li><em>She slowly be began to walk there</em> → <strong>She slowly began to walk there</strong>. You started two verbs ('be began'). Choose one: <strong>began</strong> is correct here.</li><li><em>she couldn't put her eyes away</em> → <strong>she couldn't take her eyes off it</strong>. This is a fixed expression in English. 'Put away' means to store something. 'Take your eyes off' means to stop looking.</li><li><em>she loved it she spend hours</em> → <strong>she loved it and spent hours</strong>. When two past-tense actions happen in the same sentence, both verbs need past form. Also add a connector like 'and' or 'so' between them.</li></ul><p>Self-check for any sentence: Is the verb in past tense? If the action happened before now, use -ed (for regular verbs) or the correct irregular form (spent, began, took, went).</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
29613	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. The trip has begun. Lou and his friends has been working a lot lately so they decided to have a break at Phuket. Phuket is the famous beaches of having one of the most beautiful sea and sky. When their arrived at the beaches their saw the ocean and the blue sky just like in the movies.<br><br>Lou and his friends ran swim in the ocean and ride a speed boats after. "That is so fun, I will come back here again!" Lou said "Why not?, wanna grab some food?" They ended up eating somtum with fried chicken and watch the sunlight slowly going down as little by little untill its all dark and cold then they would went back to rest.	138	15.0	Tam, your story is genuinely enjoyable to read. You took the prompt about the plane landing and built a complete, satisfying narrative around it. The detail about Phuket being famous for its sea and sky, and the specific mention of <em>somtum with fried chicken</em>, shows you know how to ground a story in real, sensory details that make it come alive. To push your writing even further, the main work is in tightening your grammar and breaking your sentences into more manageable chunks. Let's look at each area the examiners scored.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>As the plane flew lower, Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> You started exactly where the prompt ended, which is the perfect way to continue a story. The reader is immediately in the scene.</li><li><em>Lou and his friends has been working a lot lately so they decided to have a break at Phuket.</em> You gave a clear reason for the trip, which makes the story feel realistic and complete.</li><li><em>They ended up eating somtum with fried chicken and watch the sunlight slowly going down...</em> You included a specific meal and a beautiful closing image. The reader knows exactly what happened and feels the peaceful ending.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at a 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer would make the ending feel as complete and satisfying as the beginning and middle. Your story has a strong arc and dialogue that works well, but the final sentence rushes through the ending in one long breath.</p><ul><li><em>"That is so fun, I will come back here again!" Lou said "Why not?, wanna grab some food?"</em> Your dialogue feels natural and gives the characters personality. This is exactly what a good storyteller does.</li><li><em>...watch the sunlight slowly going down as little by little untill its all dark and cold then they would went back to rest.</em> This is a lot of action packed into one sentence. Try breaking it into two: <strong>They watched the sunlight slowly going down, little by little, until it was all dark and cold. Then they went back to rest.</strong> This gives the ending room to breathe and feel complete.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses paragraphs to group related ideas and keeps sentences short enough to be clear. Your story has two paragraphs, which is a good start, but the second paragraph is one very long sentence that makes the sequence of events harder to follow.</p><ul><li><em>Lou and his friends ran swim in the ocean and ride a speed boats after.</em> This sentence works well as its own short paragraph about the beach activities. You could stop here and start a new paragraph for the dialogue.</li><li><em>"That is so fun, I will come back here again!" Lou said "Why not?, wanna grab some food?" They ended up eating somtum with fried chicken and watch the sunlight slowly going down as little by little untill its all dark and cold then they would went back to rest.</em> Try splitting this into three sentences: <strong>"That is so fun! I will come back here again!" Lou said. "Why not? Wanna grab some food?" They ended up eating somtum with fried chicken. They watched the sunlight slowly going down, little by little, until it was all dark and cold. Then they went back to rest.</strong> Each short sentence makes one moment clear.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at a 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls basic grammar so the reader never has to guess the meaning. Your vocabulary is strong and specific, but one main pattern keeps causing errors: you are mixing up verb forms after helping verbs like 'has', 'have', 'had', and 'would'. Let's fix that.</p><ul><li><em>The trip has begun.</em> This is correct. You used the past participle 'begun' after 'has'. Good work.</li><li><em>Lou and his friends has been working a lot lately</em> → <strong>Lou and his friends have been working a lot lately</strong>. The subject 'Lou and his friends' is plural, so the helping verb must be 'have', not 'has'.</li><li><em>their arrived</em> → <strong>they arrived</strong>. 'Their' is a possessive word (their bags, their house). For the subject of a verb, use 'they'. Also, <em>their saw</em> → <strong>they saw</strong>.</li><li><em>ran swim</em> → <strong>ran to swim</strong> or <strong>went swimming</strong>. After a verb of motion like 'ran', you need 'to' before another verb, or use a different structure like 'went swimming'.</li><li><em>untill its all dark</em> → <strong>until it was all dark</strong>. 'Until' has one L, and 'its' is possessive (its colour, its size). For 'it is' or 'it was', use 'it's' or 'it was'.</li><li><em>they would went back</em> → <strong>they would go back</strong>. After 'would', use the base form of the verb, not the past tense. 'Would go', 'would eat', 'would watch'.</li></ul><p>Here is a self-check you can use on any sentence: find the helping verb (has, have, had, would, will, can, could, should). The next verb must be in its base form (for would, will, can, could, should) or its past participle form (for has, have, had). If you remember this one rule, most of these errors will disappear.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
33961	2025-07-13 03:02:38.907763+00	2026	Fitting In	From my perspective I have many experience struggling to fit in, the reason are because it's pretty weird to be different in something that I think it will be bad if I didn't fit in and I also hate to be a topic of someone else conversations because I'm strange. For example; when I'm using a social platforms, especially Instagram. I would be weird if the profile picture and other photos are not in the same vibe or the same color tones. I think decorate profiles make people know a lot about your personality and what do you like, your music taste and many other stuff. That's the reason why you have to had a good profile pictures, photos and a few word for the profile bio.	127	7.0	Hi Gut-jung, thank you for sharing your experience about struggling to fit in. You wrote clearly about feeling strange when you are different and how that makes you feel. To improve your writing, focus on providing more specific examples and developing your ideas more fully, as the word count was a bit low. Keep practicing, and you will continue to improve!	A2+	Writing	Formative	FIRST	Part 2	2.0	Gut-jung, you addressed the topic of struggling to fit in by explaining that you feel weird being different and dislike being talked about. You gave an example related to social media profiles and wanting them to have the same vibe or color tones. However, your essay is under the expected word count (140-190 words), which means the content is not fully developed and the target reader is minimally informed. To improve, you need to expand on your ideas and provide more specific details or examples of your experiences.	1.5	You used generally appropriate conventions for expressing your personal perspective on the topic. You communicated the straightforward idea that being different feels weird and you want to avoid negative attention. However, the text does not fully hold the target reader's attention due to the limited development of ideas and the short length. To improve, try to elaborate on your points and provide more engaging details to keep the reader interested.	1.5	You connected your ideas using basic linking words like "because" and "For example". The text has a basic structure with an introduction to your feeling and then an example. However, the organisation could be improved by using a wider variety of cohesive devices and perhaps separating the ideas into clearer paragraphs. The current structure is quite simple.	2.0	You used everyday vocabulary to discuss your experience, such as "struggling to fit in," "weird," and "social platforms." There are some noticeable errors with plurals ("many experience" instead of "many experiences") and verb agreement ("the reason are" instead of "the reason is"). You used simple grammatical forms with some degree of control, but errors do occasionally impede the clarity of your meaning. To improve, focus on subject-verb agreement and correct use of plural nouns.	\N	\N	2025-07-13
28890	2025-09-13 10:58:41.122852+00	2026	Term 1 Exam Question 1	Teenager makes good and bad choices but who has more affect on the choices they make, friends or parents well, I'll be writting about this three choices that they make, first I'll writting about clothes and fashion then I'll be moving on to studying and lastly is about the job they going to do.<br><br><br>from my point of view, I think the clothes and fashion teenagers wear is more like their friends then their parents, which some time parents would be upset by the clothes they wear, but teenagers would do anything to be fit in.<br><br><br>Now let's move over to how friends and parents has effect on their studying, and oh boy this can go in two way, one is that parents has more effect on them, which is good, they would be- a lot more fogus in their studying, and the other one is that went their has more effect on there study, which isn't that bad but sometime they can't fogus and just play with their friends, but I think parents has more effect.	179	11.0	Kun, you've made a really solid start on tackling this essay question and have some interesting ideas about how friends and parents influence teenagers. Your writing could become even clearer and more persuasive by focusing on improving the accuracy of your grammar and spelling. This will help your good ideas come across even more strongly. Keep practicing expressing your thoughts, and you'll find your writing becoming more polished and impactful.<br><br>📋 Action Plan:<br>• ✅ Proofread carefully for spelling mistakes and common grammar errors like subject-verb agreement. For instance, read your writing aloud to yourself – sometimes your ears can catch mistakes your eyes might miss.<br>• ✅ Work on expanding your paragraphs with more detailed explanations and examples for each point. Try to give specific reasons why friends or parents might have a particular influence.<br>• ✅ Practice using a wider range of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas smoothly between sentences and paragraphs. Words like "furthermore," "however," or "consequently" can really help your essay flow.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	Kun, you have done a good job of addressing the essay question, discussing the influence of friends and parents on choices related to clothes, fashion, and studying. You even added your own thoughtful idea about career choices, which is excellent. 👍 To elevate your score, think about adding more depth to your arguments. For example, instead of just stating that parents might be upset by clothes, you could explain *why* they might feel that way, or give a specific scenario. Adding more specific details and examples would help the reader feel fully informed and truly understand your perspective.	3.0	You've made a good attempt to write an essay, which is the correct format for this task, and you've communicated your main ideas clearly. 😊 To make your writing even more effective, consider refining your tone to be consistently more formal. An essay at this level often benefits from a more academic style of language, rather than conversational phrases. For instance, instead of starting with "well, I'll be writing about...", you could use a phrase like "This essay will explore..." or "This report will examine..." This helps to set a more serious and appropriate tone for your reader.	3.0	Your essay has a clear structure, which is a great foundation. You have an introduction and separate paragraphs for each of your points, which helps the reader follow your arguments. 👏 To improve further, focus on making the connections between your ideas and paragraphs even smoother. While you use basic linking words like "first" and "then," try incorporating a greater variety of cohesive devices. For example, when moving from discussing fashion to studying, you could use a phrase such as "Turning our attention to academics..." or "In addition to fashion choices, another significant area of influence is..." This helps to guide the reader seamlessly through your essay. Sometimes, Thai learners might be a little too direct in their transitions, so thinking about how each paragraph relates to the previous one can be very helpful.	2.0	You use everyday words well, and your meaning generally comes across, which is very important. However, a key area for you to concentrate on is improving the accuracy of your grammar and spelling. For instance, you wrote "Teenager makes good and bad choices," but "Teenagers make" or "A teenager makes" would be grammatically correct. Also, be careful with common spelling mistakes like "writting" instead of "writing," and distinguishing between "affect" and "effect." Remember, "affect" is usually a verb (to influence), and "effect" is usually a noun (the result). Another common area to review is words that sound similar but have different spellings and meanings, like "their," "there," and "they're." For example, you wrote "went their has more effect on there study," which should be "when *friends* have more *effect* on *their* study." Many Thai learners sometimes make these kinds of errors because English pronunciation does not always clearly differentiate between homophones, so extra practice with spelling rules and common confusing words will be very beneficial. Keep working on these details, and your writing will become much more precise and impressive. ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-13
