ongly about how wonderful pets can be. It's great to see your enthusiasm, and with a few adjustments, your writing can become even clearer and more impactful. Keep working on those writing skills, and you will certainly see improvement. 📋 Your action plan for improving your writing includes:<br>•\t✅ Carefully reading the whole question to make sure you have covered every part of the task.<br>•\t✅ Taking some time to check your spelling and punctuation after you have finished writing.<br>•\t✅ Practising writing with different sentence structures to make your ideas flow smoothly.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	2.0	You've made a good start by sharing your strong opinion that owning a pet is a great idea, and your reasons for this are clear and interesting. However, the task asked you to consider both the advantages and disadvantages of having pets. You focused mostly on the positive aspects, which is understandable given your enthusiasm. Next time, try to remember that a balanced article for a magazine like this should explore both sides of the argument, even if you ultimately lean towards one. For example, you could have briefly mentioned things like the cost of food, the time needed for walks, or the responsibility of looking after them, even if it was just to say you think the advantages outweigh these things. This would ensure your article fully informs the reader, as mentioned in the B2 marking guide, "Minor irrelevances and/or omissions may be present. Target reader is on the whole informed."	3.0	You've certainly managed to keep the reader's attention, Fergie, especially with your wonderful story about Jean the golden retriever. It's a really touching and memorable part of your article, and those kinds of personal touches make your writing engaging and lively. 🎉 Your use of a question at the start also helps to draw the reader in. You've successfully communicated your ideas in a way that is easy to follow. To get an even higher score, you could work on refining the overall tone and style to make sure it consistently fits the expectations of an article for a school magazine. For instance, sometimes the language feels a little informal. The B2 marking guide notes, "Uses the conventions of the communicative task to hold the target reader's attention and communicate straightforward ideas."	3.0	Your article has a clear structure, which is a great foundation. You've included an introduction, body paragraphs to explain your points, and a conclusion, which helps the reader follow your arguments. You also used some linking words like "Firstly," "Besides," and "Lastly," which are helpful signposts for the reader. 👍 To improve, try to make sure your paragraphs are not too long, as very long paragraphs can sometimes make it harder for the reader to absorb your ideas. You could also experiment with a wider range of linking words and phrases to create an even smoother flow between your sentences and paragraphs. Thinking about how one idea naturally leads to the next will help make your article even more connected and coherent.	2.0	You use everyday vocabulary quite well to express your points, which is important for getting your message across. However, there are quite a few issues with spelling and grammar that sometimes make your ideas a little harder to understand. For instance, words like "pete" (which should be 'pet'), "Entertain" (should be 'entertain' and lowercase), and "hallarious" (should be 'hilarious') need a closer look. 🧐 It's really common for English learners, especially Thai learners, to sometimes misspell words that sound similar or to get their capitalisation a little mixed up, so you are not alone in this. Also, try to break down some of your longer sentences, as they can sometimes run on a bit, making them tricky to read. For example, instead of "all pets are a Great companion here's why because they will always keep you Entertain," you could write, "All pets are great companions because they will always keep you entertained." Pay close attention to your punctuation, too, as this helps to separate your thoughts and make your sentences clearer. A good tip is to read your work aloud; often, you can hear where a sentence needs a break or a word sounds a bit off. The B2 marking guide states that at this level, "While errors are noticeable, meaning can still be determined," but aiming to reduce these will definitely help your score.	\N	\N	2025-09-13
34080	2025-07-23 14:53:47.491618+00	2026	A Place I Love	A place I love is Beijing, a mega city, which is in north of China. There are many delicious food you can eat and many beautiful place you can go to visit. Also, Beijing is a city that full of history.<br><br><br>I had been to Beijing when I was young. There have many delicious such as Peking duck and Peking noodle. It taste really good. Second, there have many beautiful and interesting place such as The great wall of China and The forbidden city. You can see the old wall that is over 2,300 year and walk on the wall, which is the longest wall in the world. In the forbidden city you can know about the life style of the king in the past. There also have beautiful buliding, which is over 600 year that you can visit.<br><br><br>In conclusion, Beijing is a city that have famous foods and places. It is a really good city that you can go to visit.	163	12.0	ThatThat, you wrote a good article about Beijing as a place you love. You did well introducing the city and giving specific examples of delicious food and interesting places like the Great Wall and the Forbidden City. To make your writing even better, you can focus on making your sentences flow more smoothly and correcting some small grammar mistakes, especially with how you use "there have." Keep up the good work!	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	Part 1	4.0	ThatThat, you did a good job of telling us about Beijing and why it's a place you love. You mentioned delicious food, beautiful places, and its history, which directly answers the prompt. You gave good examples like Peking duck and the Great Wall, helping the reader understand why you find Beijing special.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "A place I love is Beijing, a mega city, which is in north of China. There are many delicious food you can eat and many beautiful place you can go to visit. Also, Beijing is a city that full of history."\n    Problem: While you introduce Beijing well, you could add a little more personal feeling right at the beginning to really show *why* you love it, rather than just stating facts.\n    How to fix it: You could add a sentence like, "From the moment I first visited Beijing, I knew it was a place I would always carry in my heart." This immediately tells the reader about your strong personal connection.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "In conclusion, Beijing is a city that have famous foods and places. It is a really good city that you can go to visit."\n    Problem: Your conclusion is clear, but it could be a bit more powerful or memorable. It repeats ideas you already shared.\n    How to fix it: To make your conclusion stronger, you could try to summarize your main points while also leaving the reader with a lasting impression or a clear call to action. For example, "In short, Beijing offers a perfect blend of ancient wonders and modern delights, making it a truly unforgettable city. I encourage everyone to experience its unique charm and discover why it holds such a special place in my heart."	3.0	You wrote an article that is mostly appropriate for a travel website, and you communicated your ideas clearly. You shared information about Beijing that would interest a reader. However, some phrases were a bit repetitive or informal for an article.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "There are many delicious food you can eat and many beautiful place you can go to visit." and later "There have many delicious such as Peking duck and Peking noodle. It taste really good. Second, there have many beautiful and interesting place such as The great wall of China and The forbidden city."\n    Problem: You repeat the phrases "many delicious food" and "many beautiful place," which makes the writing less engaging. Also, "It taste really good" is a bit simple for an article.\n    How to fix it: Vary your language to keep the reader interested. Instead of repeating "many delicious food," you could say "a wide variety of delectable dishes" or "an array of mouth-watering cuisine." For "It taste really good," you could use "Its flavour is truly exquisite" or "The taste is unforgettable."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "You can see the old wall that is over 2,300 year and walk on the wall, which is the longest wall in the world."\n    Problem: The phrasing "you can see... and walk on the wall" is a bit direct and less descriptive for an article that aims to evoke a sense of wonder.\n    How to fix it: You could make the language more vivid. For instance, "Imagine gazing upon the ancient stones of the Great Wall, a structure over 2,300 years old, and walking along its majestic length – the longest wall in the world." This helps the reader visualize and feel the experience.	3.0	Your article has a clear three-paragraph structure, with an introduction, a body, and a conclusion. This helps the reader follow your main points. You used some basic linking words, but you could use a greater variety to make your sentences and ideas connect more smoothly within your paragraphs.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "There have many delicious such as Peking duck and Peking noodle. It taste really good. Second, there have many beautiful and interesting place such as The great wall of China and The forbidden city."\n    Problem: The transition from food to places is a bit abrupt. While "Second" helps, it's a very simple connector.\n    How to fix it: You could use a stronger transition to smoothly move from one topic to the next. For example, "Beyond the culinary delights, Beijing also boasts a wealth of historical and beautiful places to explore." This makes the link between the two main points clearer.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "In the forbidden city you can know about the life style of the king in the past. There also have beautiful buliding, which is over 600 year that you can visit."\n    Problem: The sentence about knowing the king's lifestyle and then immediately talking about beautiful buildings feels a little disconnected.\n    How to fix it: You could combine these ideas more effectively or add a word that shows the connection. For instance, "Inside the Forbidden City, you can learn about the fascinating lifestyle of past emperors and admire the stunning architecture of its buildings, some of which are over 600 years old." This creates a more cohesive description.	2.0	You used everyday words to describe Beijing, and your message was generally understandable. However, there were a number of repeated grammar mistakes, especially with verbs, and some small spelling errors that made the writing less clear.\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement:\n    Example: "It **have** a very beatiful beach and sea." (from previous essay, similar error here "Beijing is a city that **have** famous foods") and "There **have** many delicious such as Peking duck..." and "There also **have** beautiful buliding..."\n    Problem: You consistently used "have" when you should use "has" (for singular subjects like "It" or "Beijing") or "are" (for "There" when talking about plural things).\n    How to fix it: Remember that for singular subjects like "it" or "Beijing," you should use "has" (e.g., "Beijing **has** famous foods"). When you talk about multiple things existing, use "there are" (e.g., "There **are** many delicious foods") or "there were" if you're talking about the past (e.g., "There **were** many delicious foods").\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "beautiful **place** you can go to visit." (should be "places") and "beautiful **buliding**" (should be "building")\n    Problem: You missed an "s" for plural nouns and misspelled "building."\n    How to fix it: Always proofread your work carefully for spelling mistakes. Think about whether you are talking about one thing or many things to decide if you need an "-s" at the end of a noun.\n\n*   Word Choice and Phrasing:\n    Example: "You can see the old wall that is over 2,300 year"\n    Problem: "2,300 year" should be "2,300 years old" or "2,300 years."\n    How to fix it: Pay attention to common phrases and how numbers are used with measurements of time. "Over 2,300 years old" is the correct way to say this.	\N	\N	2025-07-23
