nd also a bad idea on the time," you could try "Having pets can be both a good and a bad idea, depending on the situation." This makes the sentence clearer and more engaging for your reader.	3.0	Your article has a basic structure, which is a good foundation. You’ve included an introduction, separate sections for different points like time and money, and a conclusion. This helps to guide the reader through your thoughts. 👏 You've also used some basic linking words, such as "Firstly" and "Beside" (which should be "Besides"). To improve even further, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just starting a new paragraph, you could use phrases like "In addition to this," or "On the other hand," to show how your ideas are connected. This makes your writing more sophisticated and easier to follow. Thai learners sometimes focus on conveying information but can overlook these subtle connections that really make writing coherent.	1.0	Mymind, you've used some everyday words to talk about pets, which is a great start. However, this is an area where we can really make a lot of progress together. There are quite a few spelling errors, like "typecal" (should be typical), "quation" (question), "prepair" (prepare), "realax" (relax), and "excally" (exactly). I know English spelling can be tricky. 😬 Also, we need to work on making your sentences grammatically correct. For example, instead of "Do you have time to take care them," it should be "Do you have time to take care *of* them." Another example is "Beside, do you have enough money for petting them," which should be "Besides, do you have enough money *for* them" or "to *keep* them." We also see some issues with articles, like "on the time" instead of "at the time." These small changes will make a huge difference to how clear and precise your writing is. Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai, which can lead to these sorts of grammatical patterns that are not standard in E