n, body paragraphs, and a conclusion, which helps to communicate your straightforward ideas. This aligns with a Band 3, where the writing "uses the conventions of the communicative task in generally appropriate ways to communicate straightforward ideas." To make your writing even more engaging for your reader, try to vary your sentence structures a little more. Sometimes, repeating similar sentence patterns can make the text feel a bit drawn out. For instance, instead of always starting sentences with "Friends have...", you could try rephrasing to "The influence of friends often extends to..." or "One way friends impact choices is...". This helps to keep the reader interested from beginning to end.	3.0	Your essay was generally well-organised, Ie Ie, and it was easy to follow your points thanks to your clear paragraphing. You used basic linking words like "First," "Besides," and "Lastly" to connect your ideas, which helps create coherence in your writing. This is characteristic of a Band 3, where the "text is connected and coherent, using basic linking words and a limited number of cohesive devices." To make your writing flow even more smoothly, you could try using a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just "Besides," you might use "Furthermore," "In addition," or "Moreover." Also, some of your sentences were quite long. Breaking these down into two shorter sentences could sometimes make your ideas clearer and help the reader move through your text more easily. Thai learners sometimes focus heavily on getting all their ideas down, which can sometimes lead to longer sentences that could benefit from being broken up.	3.0	You used a good range of everyday vocabulary in your essay, Ie Ie, and mostly in an appropriate way. Your use of simple grammatical forms also showed a good degree of control. While there were some noticeable errors, the meaning of your message was still clear, which puts you at a Band 3 in Language: "Uses everyday vocabulary generally appropriately, while occasionally overusing certain lexis. Uses simple grammatical forms with a good degree of control. While errors are noticeable, meaning can still be determined." Let's look at three areas where you can make some excellent improvements. Firstly, be mindful of subject-verb agreement. For example, you wrote "friends has more effect," but since "friends" is plural, it should be "friends **have** more effect." Similarly, "Everyone have their own favorite hobbies" should be "Everyone **has** their own favorite hobbies" because "everyone" takes a singular verb. Secondly, pay close attention to word choice and common pairings of words. You used "many effect" which should be "much effect" or "many effects," and "adsorb" when you likely meant "absorb" (to take in). English has many specific word combinations, and learning these can make your writing sound more natural. Lastly, review your spelling and word forms, such as "frequnetly" which should be "frequently." Taking a moment to proofread for these common slips can make a big difference. These kinds of errors are sometimes common for Thai learners, as direct translations or a focus on conveying the main idea can sometimes lead to overlooking these smaller, but important, grammatical details.	\N	\N	2025-09-13
