as a clear flow with an introduction, body paragraphs, and a summary, which is a great start for keeping your ideas in order. You've used basic linking words like "First" and "Lastly" to guide the reader, which is helpful. To improve, try using a wider range of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas even more smoothly. For example, instead of just "Besides," you could use "Furthermore" or "In addition to this." Think about how each paragraph connects to the next and use transition words that show that connection clearly. Sometimes, Thai learners might use simpler linking words, so expanding this range will make your essay more sophisticated. 🔗	2.0	You've used everyday words quite well, and your meaning generally comes across, which is important. However, there are some areas in grammar and word choice that we can work on to make your writing clearer and more accurate. For instance, you wrote "friends has" when it should be "friends have" because "friends" is plural. Also, "I will gave you" should be "I will give you." Paying close attention to verb tenses, subject-verb agreement, and correct prepositions will really help. Many Thai learners sometimes struggle with these grammatical points, as the structures in English can be quite different from Thai. Reviewing these fundamentals will help your message be understood without any confusion. Keep practising, and you'll see a big difference! ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-13
