 make your writing flow more smoothly.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	2.0	It's wonderful that you focused on clothes and fashion and offered some interesting points about why teenagers care about how they look! 😊 For example, you talked about wanting to look good with friends and following trends, which are very relatable ideas. To get a higher score, you need to make sure you answer *all* parts of the question. In this essay, you were asked to write about "studying" and "your own idea" too, but these weren't included. Next time, try to brainstorm a few ideas for each point in the question before you start writing so you can cover everything. Thai learners sometimes focus very strongly on one good idea and forget to spread their attention across all the required parts of the task.	2.0	You made a good effort to express your ideas about friends and fashion, and your writing is generally easy to understand. Keep up the good work! 👍 To reach a higher score, think about the overall purpose of your essay. This task asked you to discuss who has *more effect* (friends or parents) on teenagers' choices across different areas, but because you only wrote about one area, you didn't fully achieve this purpose. Try to make sure your arguments are balanced by addressing both sides or all aspects of the question. Also, while your writing is clear, working on your grammar and vocabulary will help make your arguments even more convincing and suitable for a formal essay.	2.0	You did a good job of using clear paragraphs and introductory words like "Firstly," "Secondly," and "In conclusion" to guide the reader through your ideas. That's a great start! ✍️ To improve your score, try to develop each paragraph more fully. Since you only addressed one point of the question, the structure for a comparative essay wasn't fully developed. When you have multiple points to discuss, make sure each point gets its own well-developed paragraph with clear ideas and supporting details. Using a wider variety of linking words (like "however," "therefore," "in addition") can also make your writing flow even better between sentences and paragraphs.	2.0	You used some good everyday words to talk about clothes and fashion, like "trendy" and "boring t-shirt." Keep building your vocabulary! However, there were quite a few spelling and grammar mistakes that sometimes made it a little difficult to read. For example, you wrote "effected" instead of "affected," "especialy" instead of "especially," "Horrever" instead of "However," "wan't" instead of "want," and "tred" instead of "trend." Also, in some places, like "every on," it should be "everyone." 🧐 Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai or make common spelling errors because English spelling can be tricky! Try proofreading your work carefully, perhaps reading it out loud, to catch these kinds of errors. Focusing on common mistakes with articles (a, an, the) and verb agreements (like "friends has" should be "friends have") will also really help your writing become more accurate.	\N	\N	2025-09-12
