e time Activities'. You wrote 279 words and achieved B1. You have some good ideas about why free time activities are important for teenagers.<br><br>	B1+	Writing	CA	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	You successfully identified three free time activities (sport, games, and reading) and explained why they are important for teenagers. This shows you understood the task well.<br>👉 <b>To reach a 5/5:</b> Make sure to explicitly address all parts of the prompt. If asked for two activities, focus your detail on those two, providing more in-depth explanations and convincing examples for each.<br><br>	3.0	You communicated your ideas clearly, and the reader could understand your main points. However, the tone of your essay was quite informal for an academic essay.<br>To improve, remember that academic essays in English usually require a more formal tone. This means avoiding very casual language or expressions you might use when speaking to friends. Think about the difference between informal spoken English and more formal written English when you are writing.<br><br>	2.0	You used paragraphs to separate your ideas, which helps the reader follow along. However, to achieve a higher score, focus on making your introduction clearly present the main purpose of your essay. Also, each body paragraph should have one main idea that is clearly explained. Your conclusion should then summarise your main points and bring your essay to a clear end, avoiding repetition and informal language.<br><br>	2.0	You used a good range of vocabulary related to free time activities, and you attempted to use some more complex sentences. The main challenges are with a few key grammar rules, often influenced by how you would say things in Thai.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Thai culture **are** differance from other..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Thai culture **is** different from other..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, verbs don't change form based on the subject. In English, a singular subject like 'culture' needs a singular verb like 'is'.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "thai is made many amazing **book**"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Thais have written many amazing **books**"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, you don't always add a plural marker. In English, we usually add '-s' to count nouns when there is more than one.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Most of the teenager in the world not really use **they** free time widely."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Most of the teenagers in the world do not really use **their** free time widely."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, we use possessive adjectives like 'their' to show who something belongs to. In Thai, this is often expressed differently.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "most of the time **is drain** by school"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "most of their time **is drained** by school"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, when something *is done to* an object, we use the passive voice, which needs the 'be' verb + the past participle (e.g., 'is drained').<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Review subject-verb agreement rules, especially for 'he/she/it' and singular/plural nouns, and practice writing 5-7 sentences correctly using these rules.<br>2. Before writing, create a short outline for your essay, making sure your introduction clearly presents the main purpose of your essay and each body paragraph focuses on one main idea.<br>3. When writing, pay close attention to using possessive adjectives (my, your, his, her, its, our, their) correctly, and practice forming passive voice sentences (e.g., 'My time was drained by school').<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-27
