gher score here, try adding a little more detail about why the new facilities were "incredible" – perhaps describing what they looked like, or how you felt when you first saw them. For instance, instead of just saying "This swimming pool is so big," you could say, "The swimming pool was sparkling blue and looked enormous, much bigger than I expected!" This helps your reader really picture what you saw.	3.0	You've managed to tell a clear story with a beginning, middle, and end, which is a key part of writing a good narrative. You also communicated straightforward ideas well, making it easy for the reader to follow what was happening. To improve in this area, think about how you can make your story more engaging for the reader. While you mention being "exited," try to show that excitement through more descriptive words or actions. For example, instead of "We are exited to use it," you could write, "We were so excited we could barely stand still, imagining all the fun we'd have there." Adding these kinds of details helps to hold the reader's attention more strongly, making them feel like they are right there with you.	3.0	Your story has a clear structure, moving from arriving at school to seeing the new classroom and then the swimming pool, and you've used some linking words like "After the class" and "after that..." which is good. This helps the reader follow the sequence of events. To make your writing even more organised and coherent, try to use a wider range of linking words to connect your sentences and ideas more smoothly. For instance, when you talk about the new classroom and then the new swimming pool, you could use a phrase like "In addition to the classroom, we also saw..." or "Next, our attention turned to the new swimming pool." Also, ensure that each distinct idea gets its own paragraph to make the story easier to read and understand, allowing your ideas to flow more naturally from one to the next.	2.0	Ping Ping, you've used a good amount of everyday vocabulary to describe your school experience, and your simple sentences generally make your meaning clear. For example, "This swimming pool is so big" is easy to understand. However, there are a few areas where focusing on accuracy will make a significant improvement to your writing. Firstly, be careful with your spelling, especially common words. You wrote "swimmingpool," "exited," "studies," "to morrow," "classrom," and "to day" where they should be "swimming pool," "excited," "study," "tomorrow," "classroom," and "today." Remembering that words like "swimming pool" are two separate words, and "tomorrow" is one word, will help a lot. Secondly, pay attention to articles (like 'a' or 'the') and how you connect phrases. For example, "My school has new swimmingpool, and new classroom" would be clearer as "My school has a new swimming pool and a new classroom." Similarly, "teacher said This is our new classroom" would be better as "the teacher said, 'This is our new classroom.'" These small changes make your writing sound more natural and correct. Many Thai learners sometimes omit articles or struggle with combining sentences because it's different from how these concepts are expressed in Thai. Practising these small grammatical points will help your English become much more precise.	\N	\N	2025-09-21
