r draft.<br>- ✅ Keep a short list of words you misspell and check them with a dictionary before you hand in your work.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	Prize, you answered the task and you give pros and cons of having pets, and you name three types of pet which helps the reader know what you will talk about 😊; to reach a 5 you need to develop each point a bit more, for instance explain one clear advantage of dogs (for example: protection or companionship) and one clear disadvantage (for example: cost or time needed) with a short example; right now some ideas are only mentioned and not fully explained, so try writing one extra sentence for each point next time; Thai learners sometimes write the main idea but do not expand it because they stop after the first thought — practise adding one supporting sentence for each point.	2.0	You start like an article and you try to list benefits and problems, which shows you know the purpose of the task 😊; to improve, work on the tone for an online article by choosing simple, friendly sentences and a few linking phrases like “for example” or “however” to guide the reader; avoid sentences that are hard to follow and keep the reader in mind — imagine you are explaining to a classmate; Thai learners often translate directly from Thai and this can affect the natural flow and register in English, so practise short model sentences for the article style.	2.0	Your text has clear paragraphs for each pet and a conclusion, so there is basic structure, which is good 😊; to improve coherence, start each paragraph with a clear topic sentence (for example: “Dogs are loyal and can protect their owners.”) and use a linking phrase between paragraphs (for example: “Beside dogs, cats are different because…”); also check paragraph order so similar ideas are grouped together; Thai students often write every idea as a separate short paragraph — try grouping related sentences and using a clear opening sentence for each paragraph.	2.0	The three main error areas I noticed are (1) verb forms and subject–verb agreement, (2) article/determiner and pronoun use, and (3) spelling and sentence punctuation, and these are the best places to focus your practice 😊. For example, your sentence “Pets are ours bestfriends” should be “Pets are our best friends.” Fix: change “ours” to “our” and write “best friends” as two words. Another: “it heal self when we get a bad thing today” — better: “they make us feel better when we have a bad day.” Here you need plural pronouns and a clearer verb phrase. Also “they were wating you” should be “they are waiting for you.” For articles and determiners, “There are 3 pet I recomment which are dog, cat and hamster” → “There are three pets I recommend: a dog, a cat and a hamster.” For spelling and forms, “recomment” → “recommend,” “somtimes” → “sometimes,” “makesure” → “make sure.” Concrete steps: (1) every time you write, underline the subject and verb and check they agree; (2) use a short checklist for common words (a/an/the, my/your/our) and check them before you finish; (3) read each sentence aloud and pause at each punctuation mark — if the pause feels wrong, rewrite the sentence shorter. Thai learners often find English articles, verb endings and spelling difficult because those features do not exist in the same way in Thai, so focused practice on these three points will help quickly.<br><br>Final short note on length and style<br>Your piece is longer than the exam target (247 words). This is not penalised, but try to practise writing within 140–190 words because exam tasks expect that range; planning a short outline before you write will help you stay on target and make your paragraphs tighter.	\N	\N	2025-09-14
