ter, and you also thoughtfully discussed the costs, space requirements, and extra work involved. This means your article fully informed the reader, which is exactly what the task asked for. You didn't miss any parts of the question, and your ideas were well-chosen. That's a top score for content! 🎯	3.0	Peptine, you've successfully communicated your ideas about pets in an article format, which is a great achievement! 🌟 You’ve used an appropriate style for an article, and your main points come across clearly. To move closer to a perfect score, consider making your writing a little more engaging for your target audience, which is teenagers reading a magazine online. For example, you could try using more varied sentence structures or perhaps asking a rhetorical question to draw the reader in. While your ideas are straightforward and easy to understand, consistently holding the reader’s attention with a more dynamic and less direct style would elevate your article further. Thai learners sometimes lean towards a more direct style of writing, but experimenting with different ways to present your ideas can make your writing much more lively and captivating. Keep practising, and you'll soon find your voice becomes even more compelling! ✍️	3.0	Peptine, your article has a clear structure with an introduction, separate paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages, and a conclusion. That's fantastic! 📝 You've used linking words like 'Firstly,' 'Secondly,' 'In the other hand,' and 'In conclusion' to guide your reader through your ideas. To make your writing even smoother, try to vary your linking words a bit more. For instance, instead of always starting a new point with 'Secondly,' you could use 'Furthermore' or 'Moreover.' Also, think about how you can connect your sentences more tightly within each paragraph so that the ideas flow from one to the next without feeling too abrupt. For example, after mentioning pitbulls protecting us, you could link that idea more explicitly to why they are a good example. Thai learners sometimes focus more on getting the main points down and less on these finer points of connecting ideas, but practicing different transition words and phrases will help your article feel even more polished. Keep up the good work on your paragraphing! 🔗	2.0	Peptine, you've used a good range of everyday words to talk about pets, which is excellent! 😊 However, there were quite a few small grammar mistakes that made your sentences a little harder to follow sometimes. For example, when you wrote 'my brother are having,' it should be 'my brother is having' because 'brother' is singular. Also, when you talk about protecting your home from 'dangerous,' it would be clearer to say 'from danger' or 'from dangerous situations.' Another thing to watch out for is making sure you use the right form of words, like 'hard work' instead of 'hardworks,' as this is a very common countable/uncountable noun error. Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai or overlook these smaller grammatical points, so paying close attention to subject-verb agreement, plural forms, and correct word choice will really help your writing become more accurate and natural. Keep an eye on those small details, and you'll see a big improvement! 🚀	\N	\N	2025-09-14
