29689	2025-11-26 21:53:49.901149+00	2026	Creative Writing	Sam is the robot creator his job is making robot for presenting. The robot that Sam made was very expensive and many people want to buy it. One day Sam has a biggest project that will make a lot of money to him. That project is the machine that can help about pollution problem So he decided to making it. First Sam thought his invention will took a lot of time to made but when he finish he was very proud of his job. Untill he started using his machine. Something that Sam was not happy when he turned on the machine and then it started to glow but he wasn't want the glow machine So he fix it Untill he test it again this time the machine was destroyed every trash<br><br><br>Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. W	143	8.0	Hello Pun, 👋<br><br>You have a great idea about a robot that cleans up pollution. That is a very kind and useful invention! However, the story was a bit confusing because it didn't start with the correct sentence. The exam rules are very strict about this.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you improve. 📋<br>1. <b>Follow Instructions!</b> ⚠️ Always start with the exact sentence given ('Sam finished his invention...').<br>2. <b>Past Tense!</b> ⏳ Use 'decided to make' instead of 'decided to making'.<br>3. <b>Negatives!</b> ➖ Say 'he <b>didn't</b> want', not 'he wasn't want'.	A2	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	2.0	You wrote about a robot, which is good. 👍 But you lost marks for not following the first sentence rule.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Check First!</b> 🏁 Before you write, copy the starting sentence exactly.<br>2. <b>Ending!</b> 🏁 The story ends suddenly. Did the robot work forever?	2.0	I can see you want to tell a hero story. 🤖<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Sequence!</b> ➡️ Make sure the events are in order. Build -> Test -> Success.<br>2. <b>Clarity!</b> 💭 'Glow machine' is hard to understand. Maybe 'shiny machine'?	2.0	You used 'One day' and 'First' to help order the story. 📑<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Connections!</b> 🔗 Your sentences jump around. Use 'Then' or 'Next' to link them.<br>2. <b>Logic!</b> 🧠 Why did he fix it if it was just glowing? Explain the problem.	2.0	You used good words like 'pollution' and 'expensive'. 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br>1. <b>Verbs!</b> 🛠️ 'Decided to making' -> 'decided to *make*'.<br>2. <b>Negatives!</b> ➖ 'He wasn't want' -> 'he *didn't* want'.<br>3. <b>Articles!</b> 🅰️ 'A biggest project' -> '*the* biggest project'.<br>4. <b>Tenses!</b> ⏳ 'Will took' -> 'would take'.<br>5. <b>Prepositions!</b> 📍 'Help about' -> 'help *with*'.	\N	\N	2025-11-26
34931	2025-08-21 12:25:08.321921+00	2026	Social Issue	I strongly agree with students to bring their phones to the classes because phones are important to study. The first reason is that in higher grade the topics to study in each classes are getting harder and complicate, like math and science. Both classes main things is calculations, which is pretty messy to do in the paper, but easier to do in the phone or Ipad. The second reason is that why students are allowed to bring their phones to the classes is that most of all classes must have presentation when the teachers are finished theirs lessons so, it would be easier to make the presentation slide and open the presentation. The last reason is that why students are allowed to bring their phones to the classes is that a lot of students could be stressful from study too much without relaxing. Students should get some free time to relax their mind by play phones in the classes. For this three reason, I strongly agree students should be allowed to bring theirs phones to the classes because it's important.	154	15.0	<b>📝 Dear Frame</b><br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Social Issue'. You wrote 154 words and achieved B1. You did a good job expressing your opinion clearly with strong reasons. Your writing is clear and easy to understand, showing a good grasp of the topic. Keep up the great work. We are going to help you make it even better.<br><br>	B1+	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	5.0	You fully addressed the task by clearly stating your opinion and providing three strong reasons why students should be allowed to bring their phones to class. Your arguments are relevant and well-explained, ensuring the reader is fully informed.<br><b>Strength:</b> You clearly stated your opinion and provided three distinct supporting reasons, such as 'The first reason is that in higher grade the topics to study in each classes are getting harder and complicate, like math and science.'	4.0	You effectively communicated your ideas and kept the reader's attention. While your overall purpose was clear, some phrases like "pretty messy" and "play phones" are a bit informal for academic writing. Focusing on a more formal tone will help you improve in this area.<br><b>Problem:</b> You used informal language in a formal piece of writing: "which is pretty messy to do in the paper".<br><b>Correction:</b> "which is often complex to do manually on paper".<br><b>Explanation:</b> Thai learners often use direct, informal translations. In English academic writing, it is important to maintain a formal tone, so more precise and formal vocabulary should be used.	3.0	Your paragraph is well-connected and coherent, using clear linking phrases like "The first reason is that," "The second reason is that," and "The last reason is that" to guide the reader through your points. To improve, try to vary your sentence beginnings and linking words to make your writing flow even more smoothly.<br><b>Problem:</b> You repeated the sentence structure "The second reason is that why students are allowed to bring their phones to the classes is that".<br><b>Correction:</b> "The second reason students are allowed to bring their phones to class is that most classes require presentations".<br><b>Explanation:</b> Repeating sentence structures can make writing feel monotonous. In Thai, repetition is sometimes used for emphasis, but in English, varying sentence structure improves readability and sophistication.	3.0	You used everyday vocabulary appropriately and generally controlled simple grammatical forms well. There were some noticeable errors, particularly with plurals (e.g., "each classes" instead of "each class" or "all classes"), word form (e.g., "complicate" instead of "complicated"), and possessive pronouns (e.g., "theirs phones" instead of "their phones"). These errors did not stop the reader from understanding your meaning, but improving these areas will enhance your overall accuracy.<br><b>Problem:</b> You used the incorrect plural form: "each classes".<br><b>Correction:</b> "each class" or "all classes".<br><b>Explanation:</b> Thai nouns do not change form for plural, so learners often omit the -s ending in English plurals. Remember to add '-s' for plural countable nouns, especially when using quantifiers like 'each' or 'all'.	\N	\N	2025-08-21
