 every thing').	4.0	The text communicates the character's feelings and realization effectively ('He feel really happy with that moment'). To improve, ensure greater precision in language to avoid potentially confusing phrases.	4.0	The text is connected and coherent. Linking words are used to guide the narrative ('At the outset', 'After that', 'When', 'So'). To improve, ensure paragraphs flow smoothly, with clear transitions between ideas.	3.0	Vocabulary is appropriate ('divorced', 'memories', 'balance'). There are some errors ('falled down', 'lookd', 'devorce', 'happend', 'make he relise') but they do not impede communication. To improve, focus on correcting irregular verbs and ensuring correct subject-verb agreement.	\N	\N	2025-12-01
29045	2025-07-22 15:14:48.555047+00	2026	A Place I Love	One year ago, I've visited a city called the Bakersfield, USA. It's a city near Los Angeles by two hours and today I'm going to write about "A place I love". However my favorite city is LA not Bakersfield.<br><br><br>LA, as known as the city of angels and the home city of NBA teams. I love LA because I'm the Lakers fan and I've got a lots of good memories there. Firstly, the most important part of this story which was when I've met my mother in real life as a person for the first time in my entire life. In addition, I wasn't just met her but I've done a lots of things with her. Let me tell you a story, on the first day we went to watch the Lakers versus the Minesota Timberwolves, I still remember that moment Lakers center number five throw a beutiful lob to Lobron James and he dunked it down, it was fantastic! on the second day we went to the pismo beach and then we went to the bublegum restaurant, then we ate steaks, it was perfectly grilled, medium rare and juicy. then we went back to the hotel in LA. On the last day we went to nowhere because we want to spend our time as much as possible because it was the last day I've got time for my mother because after that I need to went back and lived with my hosted family in Bakersfield. On the other hand others will say that the last day was nothing much, well I agree, but for me the last day meant a lot to me.<br><br><br>In conclusion, Los Angeles is my favorite place of all places in the world because I love the LA Lakers and this place is where I've met my mom for the first time also. Hope you like my story	313	12.0	In-Sun, you've written a very personal and engaging essay about Los Angeles, focusing on a truly special memory. You did a great job conveying your emotions and the significance of your trip. To improve, focus on structuring your paragraphs more clearly to enhance readability and work on refining your grammatical accuracy, especially with verb forms and consistent use of capitalization.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	Part 1	4.0	In-Sun, you clearly state that Los Angeles is your favorite place and provide very personal and relevant content to support this. The central theme of meeting your mother for the first time and the associated memories (Lakers game, beach, food, and the emotional significance of the last day) are well-addressed. You effectively explain *why* LA is special to you. However, at 313 words, the essay significantly exceeds the expected word count of 140-190 words. While you were not penalised for this, in an exam context, it could impact how 'fully informed' the target reader is if the conciseness is not managed. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to convey this rich content more succinctly and efficiently, ensuring all key points are made within the appropriate word limit.	3.0	You communicate straightforward ideas clearly, especially your strong emotional connection to Los Angeles and the specific memories you have there. The essay has a personal, narrative tone that largely holds the target reader's attention due to the compelling story. You make an attempt at using essay conventions with an introduction and conclusion. However, the almost continuous body paragraph, despite internal markers like \\"Firstly,\\" \\"on the second day,\\" and \\"On the last day,\\" makes the text somewhat dense and can make it harder to follow the progression of your ideas smoothly. To improve to a 5, you would need to use a wider range of communicative strategies and structure, such as varying sentence structure and length, and using clear paragraphing, to better engage the reader with both straightforward and more nuanced ideas.	3.0	The essay has a logical chronological flow, describing your experiences over several days in Los Angeles. You use some cohesive devices to link events in time, such as \\"Firstly,\\" \\"on the second day,\\" and \\"On the last day,\\" which help connect your ideas. The introduction sets up your topic well, and the conclusion effectively reiterates your main reasons for loving LA. However, the entire body of your essay is presented as a single, long paragraph. This lack of clear paragraph breaks significantly impacts the text's overall organisation and coherence, making it harder for the reader to process the distinct events and ideas. To achieve a score of 5, you should organise your text into more distinct and coherent paragraphs, using a wider variety of linking words and organisational patterns to create a more fluid and structured piece.	2.0	In-Sun, you use everyday vocabulary generally appropriately for describing your travel experiences and interests, such as \\"city of angels,\\" \\"NBA teams,\\" \\"Lakers fan,\\" \\"memories,\\" \\"restaurant,\\" \\"steaks,\\" and \\"beach.\\" You also convey strong emotions with words like \\"fantastic!\\" and \\"meant a lot.\\" However, there are frequent and fundamental errors in basic grammatical forms, which are quite noticeable and sometimes hinder meaning. Most critically, you demonstrate **persistent issues with capitalization**, such as \\"minesota,\\" \\"loborn james,\\" \\"pismo beach,\\" and \\"bublegum restaurant,\\" which should be capitalized as proper nouns or parts of proper names. You also consistently miss capitalizing the first letter of sentences (e.g., \\"on the second day we went..."). Additionally, there are errors in **verb forms** (e.g., \\"Lakers center number five **throw** a beautiful lob\\" should be \\"threw\\"; \\"I need to **went** back\\" should be \\"I needed to **go** back\\"), and occasional issues with noun agreement (\\"a lots of good memories\\" should be \\"a lot of good memories\\"). While your overall meaning can generally still be determined, these recurring elementary errors, especially the consistent capitalization mistakes, indicate a limited control over simple grammatical forms. To achieve a score of 5, you need to significantly improve your accuracy in fundamental grammar, particularly in capitalization and verb forms, use a wider range of vocabulary with more precision, and demonstrate flexibility with more complex grammatical forms.	\N	\N	2025-07-22
33896	2025-09-16 18:40:55.602088+00	2026	Term 1 Email	Dear, corey<br><br><br>Thanks so much for get a ticket for me in the science festival on Saturday.<br><br><br>I would like to meet you guys at the front of the entrance. It would be the best way for me because in the morning I have to go to the hospital, where is next to my house.<br><br><br>I think we have watched a film of dinosaurs for about many times. It would be great if we decide to build a robot. That's will be educational!<br><br><br>I think it would be great if we bring a bottle of water. I've heard from my sister on last year that the water there is very expensive.<br><br><br>Bye,<br><br><br>Kati	107	13.0	It's clear that you've put a lot of thought into your email to Corey, and you've done a very good job of including all the information needed for the task. You've managed to communicate your ideas well, even with a few small errors, and with continued practice, your writing will develop even further.<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Carefully check your grammar for common errors, especially verb forms and prepositions.<br>✔️ Pay close attention to the small details of email conventions, like punctuation in salutations.<br>✔️ Try to use a wider range of linking words to connect your ideas smoothly.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	5.0	You've done an excellent job of responding to all parts of Corey's email. You clearly expressed your enthusiasm about the tickets, suggested meeting at the entrance with a good reason, and politely disagreed about the film, explaining why building a robot would be more educational for you. Your suggestion to bring a water bottle and the reason behind it were also very helpful. You made sure Corey was fully informed about all your preferences and suggestions, which is exactly what the task asked for.	3.0	You successfully used the format of an email to get your message across. You included an opening and a closing, and you organised your thoughts into clear paragraphs, which makes your email easy to read. However, remember to be very precise with your email conventions. For instance, in your greeting, "Dear, corey" should ideally be "Dear Corey," with a comma after the name and no comma after "Dear". Paying attention to these small details will make your emails even more natural and effective.	3.0	Your email is well-organised and easy to follow. You connected your ideas logically, moving from acknowledging the tickets to discussing meeting arrangements, then activity preferences, and finally, your suggestion for what to bring. You used basic linking words like "because" and "if" effectively to show the connections between your sentences. To make your writing flow even more smoothly, you could try using a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of always starting a new idea, you could use phrases like "Furthermore" or "In addition" to build on previous points. Thai learners sometimes focus more on conveying meaning than on explicitly showing how ideas are linked, so practising different linking words can really help.	2.0	You used everyday words that were appropriate for your email, and your meaning was generally clear. However, there were a few grammatical areas where you could improve. For example, you wrote "for get a ticket" when "for getting a ticket" or "to get a ticket" would be more accurate. Another example is "where is next to my house," which should be "which is next to my house." Also, "That's will be educational" should be "That will be educational," and "on last year" should simply be "last year." Many Thai learners find prepositions and auxiliary verbs a bit tricky, and it's a very common area for improvement. Practising these specific grammar points will help you gain more control and make your writing even clearer and more precise.	\N	\N	2025-09-16
