 great way to engage your audience! For example, starting with "Have you ever wanted a little cute creature..." is very inviting. To make your articles even more effective and hold your reader's attention throughout, think about breaking your text into clear paragraphs. Imagine your article as having an introduction, a section for advantages, a section for disadvantages, and a conclusion. Each of these parts would get its own paragraph. This helps your readers follow your ideas more easily and makes your writing feel more polished, just like a professional magazine article. Using distinct paragraphs helps to communicate both straightforward and more complex ideas much more clearly to your target audience.	3.0	You have a logical flow to your ideas, Aunna, starting with an introduction, moving to the advantages and disadvantages, and then concluding with a strong recommendation. You also use some useful linking words like "first of all," "on the other hand," and "but overall," which help to connect your thoughts. However, the biggest area for you to focus on here is using paragraphs. At the moment, your article is one long block of text. For instance, after you discuss the advantages of pets, you could start a new paragraph with "On the other hand, having a pet could be challenging for someone..." This creates a visual break and helps the reader understand that you are moving on to a new idea. In Thai, sometimes writers don't use as many paragraph breaks as we do in English, which can lead to a long text. In English writing, especially for articles, clear paragraphing is really important because it makes your text much easier to read and understand. Adding these breaks will make your writing feel much more organised and coherent! 📚	3.0	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit your topic well, Aunna, like "emotional support," "cheer you up," and "memorable moment." This shows you have a good vocabulary for discussing pets! However, there are a few places where your grammar could be a bit more precise to make your writing even clearer. For example, when you wrote "let you pet or being or having one," a clearer way to say this might be "let you pet them or have them as emotional support." Similarly, "another lives" should be "another life" or "other lives," and "livingthing" is usually written as "living thing" (two words). Another good example is "alot better," which should always be "a lot better." 😊 Sometimes, in Thai, the way we phrase things might be more direct, but in English, we need to pay close attention to plural forms, verb agreements, and compound words. Focusing on these small but important grammar points will make your sentences smoother and your meaning absolutely clear to your reader. Keep practicing those small details, and your language control will grow beautifully!	\N	\N	2025-09-22
