ng to focus on for next time is making sure your essay answers the exact question asked. It's like being asked to draw a cat, and you draw a wonderful dog instead! Both are lovely, but only one is what was requested. With a little more practice focusing on the question and tidying up your grammar, you'll be well on your way to showing off your amazing ideas more clearly! <br><br>📝 **Action Plan for Naenae:**<br>*   ✅ **Read the Question Twice:** Before you start writing, read the essay question very carefully, then read it again! Circle the key words to make sure you understand exactly what you need to write about.<br>*   ✅ **Plan Your Points:** Quickly jot down a few ideas for each part of the question (like for clothes, studying, and your own idea). Make sure each idea directly helps answer the main question.<br>*   ✅ **Check Your Sentences:** After you write a paragraph, read it aloud to yourself. Does it sound natural? Are there any small words missing, or words that don't quite fit? Paying attention to small details like 'a' and 'the' can make a big difference!	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	1.0	Naenae, you did a good job of giving examples of topics like clothes, studying, and games, which shows you have lots of ideas! 🎉 However, the essay question was all about who has *more effect* on teenagers' choices – their friends or their parents. Your essay focused more on *how to make friends* through these topics instead of talking about influence. For example, when you wrote about clothes, you could have talked about how friends might encourage you to try new fashion styles, or how parents might have rules about what you wear to school. To score higher, always make sure your writing directly answers the prompt. If the question asks 'who', you need to tell me if it's friends or parents, and why, for each point.	1.0	You certainly managed to get your ideas across, Naenae, which is a great start! 😊 But for this kind of essay, which is more formal, it’s important to make sure your writing style matches. An academic essay needs a slightly more formal tone, a bit like how you'd write an important letter or report, rather than a chat with a friend. For instance, instead of saying 'Nowadays have a lot of way,' you could say 'Nowadays, there are many ways.' Thinking about your reader and the purpose of your writing will help you choose the best way to say things, making sure your message is received exactly as you intend for a formal piece.	3.0	It's great that you've structured your essay with clear paragraphs, Naenae! Each one introduces a new idea, and that makes your writing easy to follow. 👍 You also tried to use linking words like 'Beside clothes' and 'Last one is' to connect your paragraphs, which is a good effort to guide the reader. To make your essay even better, you could try to have stronger topic sentences at the beginning of each paragraph that clearly state what that paragraph will be about in relation to the main essay question. Also, making sure your introduction clearly sets up your argument and your conclusion effectively summarises it would help you score higher. Remember, a well-organised essay is like a clear road map for your reader!	2.0	Naenae, you've used a good number of everyday words, and your reader can generally understand your main message, which is fantastic! ✨ Many Thai learners often mix up how to use articles like 'a', 'an', and 'the', and also struggle with making sure verbs agree with their subjects. For example, you wrote 'Nowadays have a lot of way' instead of 'Nowadays, there *are* many ways,' because 'ways' is plural. Another common error was with missing words or slightly incorrect phrases, such as 'improve you self' which should be 'improve *your* self,' or 'make friend with other' which should be 'make friend*s* with other *people*.' To improve, really focus on these common mistakes: first, check your subject-verb agreement (is it 'he goes' or 'he go'?); second, pay attention to articles (is it 'a book' or 'the book'?); and third, review basic sentence structures to make sure all necessary words are there and in the right order. Practising these will make your writing much clearer and more precise!	\N	\N	2025-09-12
