m'. *Self-Correction: Ensure I reference the correct text!*). <br><br>Let me re-read Ken's text properly.<br><i>Text: Som/Sam janitor at Mall. Smell of bathroom bad. 3D prototype fail. Find chip. Fix it. World class product.</i><br>Okay, correction made.<br><br>Hello Ken, 👋<br><br>You have written a very inspiring story about a janitor who solves a big problem! 🌟 I liked how you described the specific problem (the bad smell) and how he worked hard to fix his invention. The quote at the end is a great lesson!<br><br>I have a few tips to help you improve your writing. 📋<br><br>1. <b>Verb Forms!</b> 🛠️ Watch out for 'it have' -> 'it has'.<br>2. <b>Adjectives!</b> 🎨 'He suspicious' -> 'He was suspicious'.<br>3. <b>Clarity!</b> 💡 Some sentences are a bit long. Try to keep them simple so the reader can follow your great ideas.	B1+	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	5.0	You followed the narrative arc well! 📚 You had a clear character (Som the janitor), a problem (the invention failed), and a success at the end.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>The Beginning!</b> 🏁 The prompt asked to start with 'Sam finished his invention...'. You wrote 'Som', which is fine, but make sure to copy the starter sentence exactly next time!</li><li><b>Explain the Invention!</b> 🧪 You mentioned a 'chip' and a 'program'. It would be cool to describe <i>how</i> the chip fixed the smell problem. Was it magic? Was it chemistry?</li>	3.0	You have some great ideas, and the story is encouraging. 🌈 The ending quote shows you want to communicate a strong message.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ Some phrases like 'the challenge way that he had to face' are a bit confusing. It's better to say 'the challenge he faced'. Keep it simple!</li><li><b>Consistency!</b> 🔄 You switched between 'Som' and 'He'. Make sure the reader knows who you are talking about.</li>	4.0	Your story is organised into paragraphs, which is excellent! 👏<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Linking!</b> 🔗 You used 'After that', 'Then', and 'Finally