u said you really wanted to see it. It's important to make sure you follow all the instructions precisely. For example, if it says "disagree", you need to clearly state that you don't want to watch the film and explain why building a robot is better. Also, when it asked you to suggest something *they* should bring and a reason, you asked Corey to bring your coat instead. Think about what they might need at the festival, like "We should bring some extra water bottles because it might be hot." Thai learners sometimes focus so much on getting their ideas down that they forget to double-check if they've answered every part of the question. A quick reread of the instructions after you've written your email can help with this.	3.0	You've used the email format well, with a friendly greeting and closing to Corey, which is just right for writing to a friend 😊. Your message comes across clearly, and Corey would certainly understand what you're trying to say. To aim for a higher score, you could think about how to make your suggestions and opinions even more persuasive or engaging for your friend. For instance, when you disagree, you could say something like, "Actually, I think building a robot would be much more fun because it's a chance to be creative."	5.0	Your email is really well put together, Mark! The way you've structured your message makes it easy to read and understand 👍. You've linked your ideas nicely, and the use of paragraphs helps the email flow logically from one topic to the next. This shows you have a good grasp of how to organise your thoughts clearly in writing.	4.0	You've used a good range of everyday words that are perfect for talking about the science festival, like "pocket money" and "snacks" 🍬. Most of your sentences are clear, and you show good control of grammar. There were just a few small things that, if corrected, would make your writing even more polished. For instance, instead of "It's seems like you're doing great," we usually say "It seems lik