ns. To achieve a 5/5, consider explicitly linking the reasons for choosing these activities more directly to your *personal* preference, rather than just general observations about Thai teens.<br><br>	3.0	<b>COMMUNICATIVE ACHIEVEMENT: 3/5</b><br>You made a good effort to maintain an essay-appropriate tone, which is important for this assessment. However, sometimes your sentences were joined in a way that felt more like spoken Thai than formal written English, often using comma splices. This can make your writing less engaging and harder for the reader to follow. Remember that in English, separate complete thoughts usually need stronger connections than just a comma.<br><br>	3.0	<b>ORGANISATION: 3/5</b><br>You structured your essay clearly with an introduction, two body paragraphs, and a conclusion, which is a great start for an essay. Your paragraphs had clear topics. However, the abrupt ending of your conclusion meant your essay didn't feel fully wrapped up. Also, the frequent comma splices sometimes made the flow between ideas less smooth, affecting the overall coherence of your arguments.<br><br>	3.0	<b>LANGUAGE: 3/5</b><br>You used some excellent vocabulary, such as "inestimable value" and "digital creativity," which shows a good range of words! The main challenges in your writing were with grammar rules like subject-verb agreement, article use (a/an/the), plural nouns, and spelling ("exciment" instead of "excitement," "freetime" instead of "free time," "outgoing" instead of "going out"). Additionally, you often joined independent clauses with only a comma (comma splices), which is a common pattern in Thai when connecting ideas, but in English, this usually requires a conjunction (like 'and', 'but', 'so') or a stronger punctuation mark (like a semicolon or a full stop).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Freetime is a time where everyone usually do the things they like, although every country have freetime, but Thailand actually have less freetime than anyone else, so in Thailand freetime is inestimable value that Thai people yearn to have, three of the most famous and full of exciment activities that Thai people mostly choose are, travelling with friends and playing video games."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Free time is a period when everyone usually *does* the things they like. Although every country *has* free time, Thailand actually *has* less than many others, so in Thailand, free time is an inestimable value that Thai people yearn to have. Three of the most famous and exciting activities that Thai people mostly choose are travelling with friends and playing video games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, it's common to string many clauses together with commas. In English, each complete thought often needs its own sentence or a stronger connection (like a conjunction or semicolon). Also, we need subject-verb agreement (everyone *does*, country *has*) and correct articles (an *inestimable* value).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "One of the most successful hobbies that Thai teens often choose is travelling with friends, so you see, during the school time most Thai students are mostly concentrating in their school work or school project, which means that they don't usually talk together that much, so one of the first things to interact more with other friends is by outgoing with them, so to include interacting with friends and doing things teenager like during freetime, going out or travelling with friends would be the best option for it."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "One of the most popular hobbies that Thai teens often choose is travelling with friends. During school time, most Thai students are usually concentrating on their school work or school projects, which means they don't typically talk together much. Therefore, one of the best ways to interact more with friends and engage in activities teenagers enjoy during free time is by going out or travelling with them."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Again, the original sentence was a run-on. Breaking it into shorter, more distinct sentences improves clarity. "Outgoing" is not typically used as a verb in this context; "going out" or "socialising" would be more appropriate. "Teenager" should be pluralized if referring to a general group.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Video game is a game which is full of digital creativity and can fulfill most childhood time, same as in Thailand where people often on their electric devices such as Ipad, phone, computer which all of this devices are full of video games, so whenever they have their freetime one of the activities they always do is open their devices and play their video game."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Video games are a form of entertainment full of digital creativity and can enrich most childhoods. Similarly, in Thailand, people are often on their electronic devices, such as iPads, phones, and computers, all of which are full of video games. So, whenever they have free time, one of the activities they always do is open their devices and play their video games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> "Video game" should be plural if referring to the general concept, and "childhood time" is better as "childhoods." "Electric devices" is more accurately "electronic devices." The use of "this" with plural "devices" is incorrect; "these devices" or "all of which" is needed. The long sentence again demonstrates the comma splice pattern, which is clarified by breaking it into more manageable parts.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1.  **Practice Sentence Construction:** Focus on writing clear, complete sentences and use conjunctions (and, but, because, so, therefore) or semicolons to correctly join independent clauses, rather than just commas. Review how to avoid "run-on sentences" and "comma splices."<br>2.  **Review Subject-Verb Agreement & Plurals:** Pay close attention to making sure your verbs agree with your subjects (e.g., "everyone *does*", "country *has*") and correctly forming plural nouns (e.g., "activities," "devices," "teenagers").<br>3.  **Refine Word Choice:** Work on distinguishing between similar words (e.g., "electric" vs. "electronic," "outgoing" as an adjective vs. "going out" as a verb phrase). Make a list of new vocabulary and practice using it in different sentences.<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-27
