28813	2025-07-23 15:19:54.366077+00	2026	A Place I Love	A place I love is Hongkong and it's should be yours to because Hongkong is a amazing place that you can visit they have amazing food you must try and placese you must go. I Will give you my excample last summer break I went to Hongkong for the first time it's was insanely amazing I have a amazing mermory there. first I went to hotel and it's was so comfy and have a beatful vive. Hongkong Is so dirty but it's not effect my expereant at all I went to a mouten with train it's so beatiful it's have the vive that you can see the whole city and then I went to Disney land I having alot of fun & talked to the Disney Princess and Mascot they were so fun to talked to. and they had the rollercoaster that'se so seavy and Fun and the ticket is so cheap you must visit the Disneyland Hongkong Once in your life and then I visit the Ocean park. It's a theme park that's are even scarier more than Disney and the rollercoaster that's are one of the fastest and scariest rollercoaster in the world. I went to eat the food and it's so amazing the food isn't too salty often people migth think Hongkong people eat salty things but they are not it's mix of sweetness and Omami. I ate alot there<br><br><br>You migth think you need car to go where you wanted but it's not in Hongkong they have train that can go every where you can go To the beach on the other island of even Disneyland from a train it's actually not bad at all they are cheap and I alway use train to go every where	291	8.0	Boss, you wrote about why you love Hong Kong, mentioning its amazing food, beautiful places like the mountain view and theme parks, and convenient train system. You shared many personal memories from your trip. To make your writing even better, you can focus on choosing fewer main points to describe in more detail, make your article sound more formal for a travel website, organize your ideas with clearer paragraph breaks and transitions, and work on your spelling, capitalisation, and grammar so your message is always very clear.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	Part 1	3.0	Boss, you introduced Hong Kong as a place you love and gave several reasons, like its amazing food, places to visit (mountain view, Disneyland, Ocean Park), and good public transportation. You gave personal examples from your summer trip. You covered many points, but because you included so many, you couldn't explain each one in enough detail to fully inform the reader about *why* these aspects make Hong Kong a place you love.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I Will give you my excample last summer break I went to Hongkong for the first time it's was insanely amazing I have a amazing mermory there."\n    Problem: You start by saying you'll give an example, but then you list many different experiences in a very fast way without much detail about each. This makes it hard for the reader to really understand *why* each place or experience was "insanely amazing."\n    How to fix it: Instead of listing many places, choose one or two of your favorite experiences and describe them in more detail. For example, you could pick either Disneyland *or* Ocean Park and describe what made that experience so special and memorable for you, explaining your feelings and observations more deeply.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Hongkong Is so dirty but it's not effect my expereant at all"\n    Problem: This comment about Hong Kong being dirty feels a bit out of place given that the rest of your essay is very positive about the city. It's a sudden, negative comment that doesn't fit the "A Place I Love" theme.\n    How to fix it: When writing an article about a place you love, try to keep the tone positive and focused on the aspects that make it special *to you*. If you want to mention a challenge, you could rephrase it to show how you still found beauty or enjoyment despite it, or simply leave out details that don't support your main point of loving the place.	2.0	You wanted to tell the reader why they should visit Hong Kong, and you shared many ideas. However, your writing style was very informal and conversational, which is not suitable for an article on a travel website. The many errors also made it difficult for your ideas to be clearly understood and to keep the reader interested.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "A place I love is Hongkong and it's should be yours to because Hongkong is a amazing place that you can visit they have amazing food you must try and placese you must go."\n    Problem: This opening is very informal ("it's should be yours to") and includes many grammatical errors that make it hard to read. It sounds more like spoken English than a written article.\n    How to fix it: Start your article with a clear, inviting, and more formal tone that would capture a reader's attention. For example, "Hong Kong is a city I truly love, and I believe it should be on everyone's travel list! This amazing place offers incredible food and countless must-visit attractions."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I having alot of fun & talked to the Disney Princess and Mascot they were so fun to talked to."\n    Problem: The language is too casual ("I having," "&," "talked to," "fun to talked to") for an article, and the sentence structure is awkward.\n    How to fix it: Use more precise and formal language. For instance, "I had a lot of fun, and it was a joy to talk to the Disney Princesses and mascots; they were so entertaining." This makes your experience sound more appealing to a wider audience.	2.0	You presented many different points about Hong Kong, but your essay did not use clear paragraph breaks and the ideas jumped from one topic to another without smooth connections. This made your writing feel unorganized and hard to follow, especially since it was also much longer than the suggested word count.\n\n*   Example: Your first paragraph combines the introduction, your personal experience at a hotel, a comment about the city's cleanliness, a mountain trip, Disneyland, Ocean Park, and food.\n    Problem: This is a very long paragraph that includes too many different ideas without clear separation. This makes it difficult for the reader to process all the information.\n    How to fix it: Break your essay into clearer paragraphs. Your first paragraph should introduce the city. Then, you can have separate paragraphs for different themes, like food, attractions (theme parks), or transportation. For example, one paragraph could be about the fun attractions, and another about the delicious food.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "and then I went to Disney land I having alot of fun & talked to the Disney Princess and Mascot they were so fun to talked to. and they had the rollercoaster that'se so seavy and Fun and the ticket is so cheap you must visit the Disneyland Hongkong Once in your life and then I visit the Ocean park."\n    Problem: This is one very long sentence with many ideas strung together without proper punctuation or a clear link between the ideas. The transition from Disneyland to Ocean Park is very abrupt.\n    How to fix it: Use shorter sentences and clear connecting words. For instance, "Afterwards, I visited Disneyland, where I had a lot of fun talking to the Disney Princesses and mascots. Their rollercoasters were thrilling and exciting, and the tickets were quite affordable – you really must visit Disneyland Hong Kong! Following that, I explored Ocean Park, another theme park that was even more exhilarating than Disney, with some of the fastest and scariest rollercoasters in the world."	1.0	You used simple words, but there were many serious mistakes in spelling, capitalisation, grammar, and punctuation throughout your essay. These errors were very noticeable and often made it extremely difficult to understand what you were trying to say. Your vocabulary was also quite limited.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "Hongkong," "it's should be yours to," "amazinge," "placese," "excample," "mermory," "beatful vive," "mouten," "expereant," "that'se so seavy," "migth," "Omami," "alot," "chouce"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled or used incorrectly. They should be "Hong Kong," "it should be yours too," "amazing," "places," "example," "memory," "beautiful view," "mountain," "experience," "that's so scary/thrilling," "might," "umami," "a lot," "choice."\n    How to fix it: You need to proofread your writing very carefully. Use a dictionary or a spell checker to learn the correct spelling of common words. Pay attention to words that sound similar but are spelled differently.\n\n*   Capitalisation:\n    Example: "Hongkong Is so dirty," "Disney land," "Disney Princess and Mascot," "Ocean park"\n    Problem: Many proper nouns (names of specific places, parks, and characters) are not capitalized, and sometimes common nouns are capitalized incorrectly in the middle of a sentence.\n    How to fix it: Always capitalize the first letter of every sentence. Capitalize all proper nouns (e.g., "Hong Kong," "Disneyland," "Disney Princess," "Ocean Park").\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement, Tense, and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "it's should be yours to," "it's was insanely amazing," "I having alot of fun," "they had the rollercoaster that'se so seavy and Fun," "the food isn't too salty often people migth think Hongkong people eat salty things but they are not it's mix of sweetness and Omami."\n    Problem: You often use "it's" instead of "it is" or "its," and you mix up tenses (e.g., "I having" instead of "I had"). Sentence structures are very complex and contain multiple errors, making them ungrammatical and hard to follow.\n    How to fix it: Focus on basic sentence structure: subject + verb + object. Ensure your verbs match your subjects (e.g., "it *is*," not "it's"). Consistently use the correct past tense for past events. Practice breaking down long thoughts into shorter, clearer sentences.	\N	\N	2025-07-23
