ive kids'</li>	\N	\N	2025-12-14
29929	2025-07-23 15:14:50.933367+00	2026	A Place I Love	The place that I really like is in Japan, there was a really big toy centre in Tokyo. It is Akihabara this place is really popular in Tokyo because it's the biggest toy centre in Japan and it have a massively big toy store in every where even on the road.<br><br><br>I really like this place because I really love Japans toy like Gundam or figure, that mean this centre is really good for me. When I was fourteen I was traveled to and I went to Akihabara and it was really amazing. I saw many toy shop and toy store and sometime I was saw cos player too.<br><br><br>So When you go there What you can do there? First thing that you can do when you arrive there is, you can check in with the cosplayer at there because there are meny cos player at there. And another thing that you can do is shopping the toys, that told you Akihabara is the biggest toy centre in Japan that make you can buy the toy in every where that you go.<br><br><br>Why this place is really special, well this place is really special because this place is the biggest toy centre in the Tokyo and it is the biggest toy centre in Japan too, that make this place is so really special for Japanese.<br><br><br>In my conclusion I think this really special for me. I really love it becaus I really love Japans toy I love Gundam I love figure and I really love beautiful cosplayer too. It make this place is really special for me and I really love it more than meny place that I ever visit.	280	8.0	Tiw, you wrote about Akihabara in Tokyo, Japan, and shared your love for it because it's a big toy center. You told us about your trip there and seeing toy shops and cosplayers. To make your writing even better, you can try to add more variety to your reasons for loving the place, beyond just toys, and make your article sound more formal for a travel website. You can also work on connecting your ideas more smoothly and fixing mistakes in spelling, grammar, and sentence structure so your message is always very clear and easy for everyone to understand.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	Part 1	3.0	Tiw, you introduced Akihabara and told us it's a big toy center in Tokyo, which is why you love it. You shared your personal interest in Japanese toys like Gundam and figures, and mentioned seeing cosplayers. You did address the task by saying why you love the place. However, your essay repeated the idea of it being the "biggest toy center" many times, which meant you didn't explore other reasons why the *place* itself is special to you, beyond just the toys.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Akihabara this place is really popular in Tokyo because it's the biggest toy centre in Japan and it have a massively big toy store in every where even on the road." and then again, "Why this place is really special, well this place is really special because this place is the biggest toy centre in the Tokyo and it is the biggest toy centre in Japan too, that make this place is so really special for Japanese."\n    Problem: You repeat the idea of Akihabara being the "biggest toy center" many times. While it's a key point, repeating it so often makes the essay feel less developed and doesn't add new information.\n    How to fix it: Once you mention it's the biggest, you can then focus on *what that means for you* or *what the experience is like* because of its size. For example, "Akihabara is known as Japan's largest toy center, a fact that creates an incredible atmosphere for collectors and enthusiasts like me. The sheer number of toy stores on every street, even spilling onto the road, creates a unique energy."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "In my conclusion I think this really special for me. I really love it becaus I really love Japans toy I love Gundam I love figure and I really love beautiful cosplayer too."\n    Problem: Your conclusion mostly repeats the reasons you already gave about loving toys and cosplayers, without adding a new summary or leaving a lasting impression on the reader.\n    How to fix it: Your conclusion should summarize your main points without just listing them again, and leave the reader with a final thought about the place. For example, "In conclusion, Akihabara is truly a special place for me because it's a paradise for Japanese toy lovers. The chance to explore countless stores and even meet cosplayers makes it an unforgettable experience that I cherish more than many other places I've visited."	2.0	You tried to write an article about Akihabara, but the style was very informal and direct, which is not usually suitable for a travel website. Many errors and repetitive phrases made it hard for the article to clearly communicate all your ideas and keep the reader's attention.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "The place that I really like is in Japan, there was a really big toy centre in Tokyo. It is Akihabara this place is really popular in Tokyo because it's the biggest toy centre in Japan and it have a massively big toy store in every where even on the road."\n    Problem: The language is too conversational and includes run-on sentences. "It is Akihabara this place" is awkward.\n    How to fix it: Start your article with a clear, inviting, and more formal introduction. For example, "If you're looking for an unforgettable experience in Japan, let me tell you about Akihabara in Tokyo. This district is incredibly popular, known as Japan's largest toy center, with massive toy stores on every street, even overflowing onto the sidewalks."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "So When you go there What you can do there? First thing that you can do when you arrive there is, you can check in with the cosplayer at there because there are meny cos player at there. And another thing that you can do is shopping the toys, that told you Akihabara is the biggest toy centre in Japan that make you can buy the toy in every where that you go."\n    Problem: This section sounds like a checklist rather than a smooth description. The questions are informal, and the repetition of "at there," "meny cos player at there," and "every where that you go" is distracting.\n    How to fix it: Present activities in a more engaging way using varied language. For example, "When you visit, one exciting activity is interacting with the many talented cosplayers you'll find there. Another must-do is shopping for toys; since Akihabara is Japan's largest toy center, you'll discover fantastic toy stores around every corner."	2.0	You used paragraphs, but the way you organized your ideas often felt repetitive and sometimes illogical. There were not enough varied linking words, which made it hard for the reader to follow the flow of your thoughts, and the essay was much longer than required, showing difficulty in selecting and ordering information.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I really like this place because I really love Japans toy like Gundam or figure, that mean this centre is really good for me. When I was fourteen I was traveled to and I went to Akihabara and it was really amazing. I saw many toy shop and toy store and sometime I was saw cos player too."\n    Problem: The connection between your love for toys and then your travel experience and what you saw is a bit disjointed. The phrase "that mean this centre is really good for me" is a bit informal and simple as a connector.\n    How to fix it: Combine these ideas more smoothly. For example, "I genuinely love Akihabara because I'm a huge fan of Japanese toys like Gundam and various figures, making this center an ideal destination for me. When I traveled there at fourteen, my visit to Akihabara was truly amazing. I was thrilled to see countless toy shops and stores, and I even spotted many talented cosplayers."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Why this place is really special, well this place is really special because this place is the biggest toy centre in the Tokyo and it is the biggest toy centre in Japan too, that make this place is so really special for Japanese."\n    Problem: This whole paragraph is very repetitive and doesn't add much new information. It could have been integrated into earlier points or removed to make the essay more concise.\n    How to fix it: Avoid repeating information. Instead of a separate paragraph for "Why this place is really special," you could have explored the *impact* of it being a large toy center in your main body paragraph, discussing the atmosphere, the community, or specific unique aspects.	1.0	You used simple vocabulary to describe Akihabara, but there were many significant errors in spelling, grammar, and sentence structure. These mistakes were very noticeable and often made it difficult to understand what you were trying to say. Your vocabulary was also quite limited, and you repeated the same words often.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "centre," "every where," "cos player," "meny," "becaus," "Japans"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled or used incorrectly. They should be "center" (or "centre" if using British English consistently), "everywhere," "cosplayer" (one word), "many," "because," and "Japan's" (possessive).\n    How to fix it: Always double-check your spelling. Pay attention to compound words like "everywhere" and "cosplayer." Remember to use an apostrophe for possession (e.g., "Japan's toys").\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "it **have** a massively big toy store," "I was **traveled** to," "sometime I was **saw** cos player too," "there **are** meny cos player at there." (should be "there **were**" if past)\n    Problem: You used "have" instead of "has" for singular subjects ("it has"). Your past tense verbs are often incorrect (e.g., "I *traveled*" not "I was traveled," "I *saw*" not "I was saw"). Also, "cos player" should be plural "cosplayers" for "many."\n    How to fix it: Focus on correct subject-verb agreement (e.g., "it **has**"). Learn the correct past tense forms of verbs. When talking about more than one person or thing, remember to make the noun plural (e.g., "many cosplayers").\n\n*   Word Choice and Repetition:\n    Example: You used "really big" and "really special" and "I really love" many times.\n    Problem: Repeating these phrases makes your writing less interesting and shows a limited range of vocabulary.\n    How to fix it: Try to use different words to express similar ideas. Instead of "really big," you could say "enormous," "immense," or "vast." Instead of "really special," you could use "unique," "extraordinary," or "cherished." For "I really love," you could try "I adore," "I'm fascinated by," or "I'm passionate about."	\N	\N	2025-07-23
34924	2025-09-23 08:46:50.966159+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	I see an international English website for young people. Firstly, I think this website it's might be Articles wanted! to telling about a festival that we can go with our family celebrate. this website it was be website find a festival to celebrate with family to give then have time to stay longer. Secondly, It's will find a place, think did you like about it or what happens. website will tell you about the place, thing that will be happen and thing you like about. Lastly, the best articles answering these question will be published next month, it's will answer you question and find a good festival to give you celebrate with you family. This kind of this website might be like chat gpt or life coach.	127	2.0	Hello Ping-Ping,<br><br>It is wonderful to see you engaging with the writing task. You have approached the assignment with enthusiasm, and that is a great starting point for any writer. The most significant area for you to focus on for improvement is ensuring that you answer the specific questions in the task rather than writing about the task itself. If you can focus on writing directly about the topic, your writing will become much clearer and more engaging for your readers. Keep practising, and you will see your writing skills flourish.<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement:<br>✔️ Carefully read the task instructions and underline the key information you need to include in your writing.<br>✔️ Before you start writing, make a quick plan or a list of ideas that directly answer the task questions.<br>✔️ After writing, check if every sentence helps to answer the questions you underlined in the task.	A1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	0.0	Ping-Ping, it is important in writing tasks to make sure your article directly answers the questions asked in the prompt. In this piece, you wrote about what the website would do or what the "Articles wanted" section was asking for, rather than telling us about a specific festival that you or your family celebrate. For example, the task asked for you to name a festival, say when it happens, what activities take place, and what you like about it. To earn a higher score, you would need to choose a real festival, like Songkran or Christmas, and then describe it in detail. Think of it like this: if someone asked you about your favourite game, you would talk about the game itself, not about how to find a game on the internet. 🧐 Always try to bring your own experiences and knowledge into your writing to make it personal and interesting.	1.0	When you write an article, Ping-Ping, the goal is to inform and engage your readers about a specific topic. While you made an attempt to communicate some ideas about the website prompt, your writing didn't quite function as an article about a festival. To improve, imagine you are writing for another young person who knows nothing about the festival you are describing. You would want to grab their attention and keep them interested with a clear introduction, body paragraphs that explain things, and a conclusion. For instance, you could start with a catchy title and an opening sentence that makes the reader want to learn more about the festival. Always think about your target reader and how you can make your writing enjoyable and easy for them to understand. 😊	0.0	Organisation is about how you arrange your ideas so they flow smoothly from one to the next, Ping-Ping. In an article, we usually have a clear beginning, middle, and end, with each paragraph introducing a new idea or developing an existing one. For example, you might have one paragraph for when the festival takes place, another for the activities, and a final one for your personal feelings. Although you used words like "Firstly," "Secondly," and "Lastly," these were used to organise your thoughts about the prompt rather than the content of an article about a festival. To get a better score, try to plan out what each paragraph will focus on before you start writing about the festival itself. This will make your writing much clearer and easier for your reader to follow. ✨	1.0	Ping-Ping, your writing shows that you are beginning to use some everyday words appropriately, which is a positive start. For example, phrases like "international English website for young people" and "find a festival to celebrate with family" are understandable. However, there are many grammatical errors that make your sentences hard to follow at times. For instance, sentences like "this website it's might be Articles wanted! to telling about a festival" or "it was be website find a festival" are difficult to understand because of the way the words are put together. A common area for Thai learners to focus on is subject-verb agreement and using the correct verb tenses. In English, we usually say "this website is" or "it will be." Keep practising forming complete, grammatically correct sentences, and try to vary your sentence structures to make your writing more interesting. Focus on getting the basic sentence patterns right, and your communication will become much clearer. Practice using pronouns like "it" and "they" carefully to refer to the correct nouns. You are using some linking words, which is a good step towards more complex writing. Keep up the good effort. 🌟	\N	\N	2025-09-23
