ave a strong personal connection to the topic. Your writing shows a good understanding of how to put your ideas across, and with a little more focus on balancing your arguments and refining your word choices, your articles will be even clearer for your readers. Keep up the great effort, and remember that every piece of writing is a chance to grow your skills. <br> 📋 Action Plan for Improvement <br> Here are three concrete steps you can take to make your writing even stronger: <br> ✅ When you're planning your articles, try to list an equal number of points for and against an idea, so your discussion feels balanced for the reader. <br> ✅ After writing, read through your work specifically looking for words that might be plural or singular. For example, check if you mean 'a pet' or 'pets' in general, and make sure your verbs match. <br> ✅ Take some time to proofread your work carefully for common spelling mistakes. Reading your work aloud can sometimes help you catch these little slips.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	You did a good job of exploring the topic of pets and providing some thoughtful reasons why they are a good idea. You included some lovely personal touches, which made your writing feel genuine. 🥰 To develop your content further for an article, try to ensure that both sides of your argument – the advantages and disadvantages – are presented with equal detail and explanation. Sometimes, it felt like the positive aspects were discussed more fully than the challenges, so think about giving those 'troublesome' aspects a bit more elaboration too. For instance, when you talk about the cost of medicine, perhaps you could expand on how that impacts pet owners, or suggest how people manage these costs. Thai learners sometimes use personal anecdotes very effectively, but for an article, it's good to balance these with broader observations that apply to most readers.	3.0	You certainly managed to communicate your ideas clearly, and you kept me engaged as a reader. It's evident that you have a good grasp of expressing your views. 👍 To enhance your communicative achievement, think about the overall 'feel' of an article for a magazine. While personal stories are charming, an article often aims for a slightly more general and persuasive tone that would appeal to a wide audience. For example, instead of focusing heavily on just your own experience with an alarm clock pet, you could frame it as a common experience for many pet owners, making it more relatable for the target reader of a magazine. Practising writing for different purposes, like a formal report versus a friendly email, can help you adapt your writing style more easily.	3.0	Your article has a clear beginning where you introduce your perspective, and you've used some connecting words like 'furthermore' and 'in the other hand' to link your ideas. This helps the reader follow along. ✨ To make your organisation even better, you could work on varying the types of linking words and phrases you use. Instead of just 'but' or 'and,' explore words like 'however,' 'consequently,' or 'in addition.' Also, try to ensure that each paragraph has a very clear main idea, and that all the sentences within that paragraph directly support it. Sometimes, paragraphs began with a general point but then shifted quickly to a personal example. Making sure your paragraphs flow smoothly from one to the next, with clear topic sentences, will create a more unified text.	3.0	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit the topic well, and you generally control simple sentences effectively. I could understand your meaning, which is the most important thing. However, there are a few areas where we could strengthen your control over language. For example, paying close attention to whether a noun should be singular or plural will help your accuracy; you sometimes wrote 'having pet' when you meant 'having pets,' or 'Pets like cat or dog' instead of 'Pets like cats or dogs.' 🐾 Another common 