mily participates in. Your article focused mostly on a fun trip with friends, which is great, but it meant we didn't learn much about the New Year festival. Try to keep the main focus on the festival details mentioned in the prompt. Next time, try to clearly name the festival, explain when it happens, and describe two or more specific things people do during the celebration, giving some good details. This will help your readers understand the festival better. For instance, you could talk about special foods, decorations, or family gatherings specific to New Year's. Think about what someone who doesn't know about New Year's would need to learn from your writing.	2.0	You do a good job of communicating straightforward ideas, and it’s clear you had fun celebrating. To make your article even more effective, let's think about how an article is usually structured for young people reading an international website. An article about a festival should probably start by introducing the festival clearly, then describe its traditions, and finish with your personal feelings about it. Your article shifted quite a bit to your personal story with friends, which, while interesting, meant it didn't quite fit the expectations for an informative article about a festival. To help your readers stay engaged, try to keep your writing focused on the main topic throughout. You could try using a friendly, informative tone and making sure your content explains any cultural points that someone from another country might not know. Your reader will enjoy learning about the festival if you present it clearly. 😊	2.0	Your writing has some good connections between sentences, and you've used paragraph breaks, which is a solid start. To really improve the organisation of your article, consider how you can arrange your ideas so they flow logically from one point to the next. For example, you could have an opening paragraph that introduces the festival, a middle paragraph that talks about what people do during the festival, and a concluding paragraph that shares what you love most about it. Right now, your story about Atom and the sandcastle, while entertaining, took up a lot of space that could have been used to describe the New Year festival more fully. Using linking words more often, like "First," "Next," "Also," or "Finally," can help your reader move smoothly through your ideas. Think of it like a journey for your reader – you want to guide them clearly from the beginning of your article to the end. 🗺️	2.0	You’ve used some everyday vocabulary to talk about your experience, which is good. We also see some attempts at using simple grammatical forms. To make your writing clearer and more accurate, let's focus on a few common areas. Firstly, pay close attention to subject-verb agreement. For example, you wrote "me and my friends went" and "Atom get really mad." It should be "my friends and I went" and "Atom **gets** really mad." Secondly, be careful with plural forms, like "30 minute's" instead of "30 **minutes**," and "favarit festival" instead of "favourite festival." Also, practice using the correct verb tenses. For example, "we have so much fun playing" could be "we **had** so much fun playing" if you're talking about a past event, or "we **have** so much fun playing" if it's something you do regularly. Thai learners sometimes face challenges with these aspects because the Thai language doesn't always mark plurals or verb tenses in the same way English does. A good step for you would be to double-check your sentences for these kinds of errors after you've written your first draft. This will make your meaning much clearer for your readers. Keep practicing, and your language control will grow. ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-23
