ven more smoothly.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	You've done a really good job of addressing the task, Arty, by clearly discussing both the good things and the not-so-good things about having pets. You made sure to include everything the question asked for, and anyone reading your article would be fully informed about your opinions. That's a strong start. 👍 It is always important to make sure you answer all parts of the question, and you certainly did that here.	3.0	Your article successfully communicates your straightforward ideas, and it definitely holds the reader's attention. You've used the basic conventions of an article well, which is important for this type of writing. To make your writing even more engaging, try to develop your ideas a little more fully. For instance, instead of just saying pets are 'cute,' you could describe *how* they are cute or what specific actions make them endearing. This helps to bring your writing to life. ✨	3.0	The way you've organised your article is logical and clear, Arty. You've used some good basic linking words like 'Firstly' and 'In conclusion,' which help the reader follow your points. You have a clear introduction, body paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages, and a conclusion, which is an excellent structure. To improve even further, try to include a greater variety of linking words and phrases to connect your sentences and paragraphs. For example, instead of just starting a new paragraph, you could use phrases like 'On the other hand' or 'Furthermore' to build a stronger bridge between your ideas. Thai learners sometimes focus on getting the main points down, and can forget these little connectors that really help the reader. 🌉	3.0	Your control over simple grammatical forms is good, Arty, and even with a few errors, your message is very clear, which is the main goal. You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, but you did repeat phrases like 'lots of advantages' a few times. Trying to use different words like 'numerous benefits' or 'several drawbacks' can make your writing more interesting. For example, you wrote 'Pets make you feel relax,' and it would be more grammatically accurate to say 'Pets make you feel **relaxed**.' Another example is 'But having lots of disadvantages do not mean pets are bad,' which could be improved by saying 'But having lots of disadvantages **does** not mean **that** pets are bad.' These are common areas where learners sometimes make mistakes. A great way to improve is to actively look for different ways to express similar ideas and to pay close attention to verb endings when describing feelings or states. Keep practising, and your language will become even more precise. ✍️	\N	\N	2025-09-13
