d to catch any missing words or places where the verb doesn't quite match the subject.<br>*   ✅ Try to use a variety of linking words and phrases to show how your ideas connect, instead of repeating the same ones.<br>*   ✅ Practice using articles (a, an, the) and choose words carefully to make your meaning super clear and exact.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	5.0	Kris, you did a fantastic job here! 🎉 You understood the question perfectly and talked about clothes, studying, and even came up with your own idea about food. You gave clear reasons why you think friends have more influence than parents in these areas. It's really impressive how you covered all parts of the question so thoroughly and supported your opinions well. Thai students often shine when they understand the task clearly, and you certainly did!	3.0	You did a good job of getting your message across to the reader, Kris, and your ideas were generally easy to follow. 😊 To score a 5 in this area, you could work on making your writing sound a little more formal and academic throughout, especially in an essay. For example, instead of starting a paragraph with "First things that friends have more effect," you could try something like "Firstly, when considering the influence of friends, clothes and fashion are a key area." Thai learners sometimes find it tricky to consistently use formal language because in everyday spoken English, we use a mix of styles. Practising different ways to introduce your points will help your essay sound even more sophisticated.	3.0	Your essay was quite well-organised, Kris! Each paragraph had a clear main idea, which makes it easy to read. 👍 However, to reach a higher score, you could try using a wider variety of linking words and phrases to connect your sentences and paragraphs. For instance, instead of often using "Moreover" or simple phrases like "The second topic," you could explore words like "furthermore," "in addition," "consequently," or "on the other hand." This helps your essay flow more smoothly and shows a more complex understanding of how ideas relate to each other. Many Thai students start with basic linking, which is a great foundation, but expanding your range will make a big difference!	3.0	Kris, you used a good range of words and different sentence structures, which is great! 🌟 Your meaning was almost always clear. To improve, focus on accuracy with grammar, especially subject-verb agreement (e.g., "every teenager *has*" instead of "have"). Also, pay close attention to word choice – for example, "influence a massive consequence" could be rephrased for more natural English, perhaps "have a significant influence." Similarly, "most effect topic" should be "most effective topic" or "most influential topic." Finally, remember to check your use of articles like 'a', 'an', and 'the'. These are common areas where English grammar differs quite a bit from Thai, so many Thai learners find them challenging. Focusing on these details will make your writing much more precise and error-free!	\N	\N	2025-09-12
