It is lovely to hear about your family celebrating together and the joy you find in these moments 🥳. However, when writing for an international website, it is really important to include all the basic information so that everyone can understand. For example, you didn't mention *when* Songkran takes place. Imagine someone from another country reading your article; they would be curious if it happens in summer, winter, or a specific month. You could start your article by saying something like, "Songkran is the traditional Thai New Year's festival, which usually takes place in April each year," to give your readers a clear starting point. Also, try to add a few more general details about the festival itself, not just what your family does. Perhaps you could mention other common traditions like visiting temples or making merit, even if your family focuses on the water activities. This will help your readers feel completely informed about Songkran as a whole.	3.0	You have made a good effort to write an article that communicates your ideas clearly. Your personal voice comes through, which is lovely for this type of writing. To make your article even more appealing and easy to follow for young people from all over the world, try to think about how you introduce new ideas. For instance, you could start with a clear, engaging title that immediately tells the reader what your article is about, something like "My Favourite Festival: Songkran Fun!" 🎉. You have used paragraphs, which is a great start, but consider making each paragraph focus on one main idea, like an introduction, then what happens, and finally what you like about it. This structure helps guide your reader through your thoughts smoothly. Remember to make sure any cultural references are explained briefly so that everyone can understand without prior knowledge.	3.0	Your article is generally well-organised, and your ideas flow quite logically from one paragraph to the next. You use good linking words like "Nevertheless" and "In conclusion" which help connect your thoughts and signal to the reader when you are moving to a new point 🔗. To make your writing even stronger, try to introduce a wider variety of these linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just saying "But there's one the moment I like most," you could say, "However, my absolute favourite part is..." or "Yet, the highlight for me is...". Also, ensure that all your sentences are complete and fully developed so that your readers can follow your narrative without any interruptions. This will make your article much more cohesive and enjoyable to read.	4.0	Hero, your writing shows a good control of everyday vocabulary, which you use appropriately to describe your experiences at Songkran, and you attempt a range of sentence structures, which is commendable 👍. However, there are a few areas where we can focus to make your grammar even more precise. One common area is **subject-verb agreement**. For example, you wrote "There's so many things happen." A clearer way to say this would be "There *are* so many things *that* happen," because "things" is plural. Another area is **pronoun use**. You wrote "my favorite festival that me and my family have celebrated." In English, when you are part of the subject of a sentence, you should use "I," so "my family and *I* have celebrated" would be more accurate. Finally, pay close attention to **verb tense and aspect**. You wrote, "I feel like 'I was being a child again at that point." While we understand your meaning, a more natural way to express this feeling would be "I felt like I *was* a child again at that point" or "I felt like I *was* a child again *then*." Sometimes, in Thai, the way we express past actions or feelings can be a bit different, and English requires a more specific match between the action and the time it occurred. Keep up the good work with your vocabulary, and with a little more attention to these grammatical points, your writing will become even more clear and accurate 🎯.	\N	\N	2025-09-23
