d how to include it clearly, just as you did here. 🥳	3.0	You certainly used a friendly tone for your email, and your "Hi" and "Bye" at the beginning and end were perfect for writing to a friend. This helps the reader feel like they are having a proper conversation with you. However, to make your emails even clearer and more engaging, try to arrange your ideas into different paragraphs. For example, you could have one paragraph where you talk about the tickets and where to meet, and then a new paragraph for discussing the activities. This helps your reader follow your thoughts more easily and makes your email much more structured. Remember, new topic, new paragraph. 🤔	3.0	Your ideas in this email are connected and flow quite well, which is important for any piece of writing. You've used words like "Because" to link your sentences and explain your choices, which helps the reader understand your reasoning. To take your organisation to the next level, focus on using paragraphs to separate your different points. Imagine each main idea needs its own little "box" of text, and that's what a paragraph does. For instance, after you talk about meeting Corey, you could start a new paragraph to discuss the robot or film activity. This way, your writing will be even clearer and more enjoyable for your reader to follow, showing off your ideas in a really polished way. ✨	3.0	You've used many everyday words appropriately in your email, and it's easy to understand what you mean, which is the main goal of communication! You expressed your ideas clearly, even with some noticeable errors. To make your English even more precise, let's look at a few things. Firstly, remember to always start a new sentence with a capital letter, even after a short phrase like "No." or "okay." For example, "we can meet..." should be "We can meet..." and "Because it will be..." should be "Because it will be...". Secondly, be careful with your verb forms; you wrote "if we go build a robot" which should be "if we **build** a robot," and "we should brought a fan" which should be "we should **bring** a fan." Sometimes, in Thai, you might not change the verb form for tense, but in English, these changes are quite important. Lastly, pay attention to words like "a" and "all days." "Pocket money" is usually uncountable in English, so we just say "pocket money" without "a." Also, for duration, we often say "all **day**" instead of "all days." Focusing on these small but significant details will help your writing sound more natural and accurate. You're already communicating well, so these refinements will truly enhance your command of English. 🚀	\N	\N	2025-09-21
