consistent register, and the direct address of the reader in a conversational way (\\"you would met an interesting,\\" \\"your benefits are the hotel,\\" \\"you could be chill\\") detracts from effectively holding the target reader's attention in an academic context. To improve to a 5, you should consistently use conventions of an essay, maintaining a more formal and objective tone, and communicate both straightforward and complex ideas in a way that is consistently engaging and appropriate for the task.	3.0	You have attempted to organise your ideas into paragraphs, and there is a logical progression, starting with an introduction to Hua-Hin, moving to its attractions, and then to your personal reasons for loving it, with a concluding remark. You use some linking phrases like \\"From the first thing I've told,\\" \\"Now, I'll give you,\\" \\"At this point,\\" and \\"So.\\" However, the internal coherence within paragraphs could be improved, as some sentences feel loosely connected. For example, the long paragraph describing Hua-Hin's benefits and \\"The Rain\\" jumps between ideas. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to use a wider variety of cohesive devices and organisational patterns more effectively to create a well-organised and coherent text, ensuring smooth transitions and clear connections between all ideas.	3.0	You use a range of everyday vocabulary related to travel, places, and personal feelings (e.g., \\"lovely place,\\" \\"beautiful beach,\\" \\"interesting,\\" \\"adorable,\\" \\"seafood,\\" \\"tired,\\" \\"healed,\\" \\"released\\"). You also attempt some more descriptive words like \\"gorgeous.\\" You use simple grammatical forms with a good degree of control, particularly in describing your experiences. However, there are noticeable grammatical errors. For example, instead of \\"my lovely place to go for me is always be,\\" it should be \\"I always love going to ....". Instead of \\"you would met an interesting in those place,\\" it should be \\"you would find interesting things in those places.\\" The phrase \\"doesn't just a chill location\\" could be improved to \\"isn't just a relaxing location.\\" \\"which is will be more gorgeous\\" should be \\"which will be more gorgeous.\\" For \\"There're a lot of seafood,\\" it should be \\"There's a lot of seafood\\" or \\"There is a lot of seafood\\" as 'seafood' is generally uncountable. \\"everything that happend\\" should be \\"everything that happened.\\" There are also issues with spelling, such as \\"lisening\\" which should be \\"listening.\\" Additionally, informal contractions and phrasing like \\"I've told,\\" \\"you're at the beach,\\" and \\"I'm broke\\" should be avoided in formal essays (e.g., \\"I have told,\\" \\"you are at the beach,\\" \\"I do not have enough money\\"). While these errors generally do not impede communication, they are frequent enough to detract from overall accuracy and control. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to demonstrate a higher degree of control and flexibility across a wider range of grammatical forms, more precise vocabulary, and greater accuracy in spelling and punctuation.	\N	\N	2025-07-22
