d to work on. For example, in sentences like "Having pets have many disadvantages," the verb "have" needs to agree with "Having pets" (which is a singular idea), so it should be "Having pets *has* many disadvantages." Also, be careful with articles like "a," "an," and "the." For instance, "want to have a pets" should be "want to have pets" or "want to have a pet." Lastly, watch your verb tenses and forms; "that make me felt so good" should be "that *made* me *feel* so good." Thai learners often find these aspects of English grammar tricky because your language handles subject-verb agreement, articles, and verb tenses differently, so it's a very common hurdle. Regular practice and careful proofreading will really help you overcome these common errors. Keep a notebook of common errors you make and try to correct them in future writing. ✏️	\N	\N	2025-09-13
