amples could be integrated more smoothly into the argument to fully inform the reader.	2.0	You attempt to use the conventions of an essay to communicate your ideas. However, the consistent grammatical errors and awkward phrasing often impede the clarity and hold back the communicative effectiveness. To improve, focus on expressing your ideas more clearly and accurately to better hold the target reader's attention.	3.0	Your essay has a basic organisational pattern with an introduction, body, and conclusion. You use some basic linking words and phrases, which help to connect your ideas. However, the coherence could be further improved with a greater variety of cohesive devices and more precise use of linking words. Some sentences feel somewhat disjointed, and the flow of ideas is not always smooth.	2.0	You use everyday vocabulary generally appropriately, but there are instances of overuse of certain lexis and some inappropriate usage. There are also many noticeable errors in grammatical forms. While the meaning can usually be determined, these errors are frequent and significantly affect the quality of the language, impeding communication at times. A wider range of simple and some complex grammatical forms with better control would significantly improve your score.	\N	\N	2025-07-13
29720	2025-09-23 08:41:57.2827+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! It's a festival called Songkran so today a lot of people at my school splashes water at each other by using a bowl and some people even uses a water guns! It was very chaotic but my friend and I still joined them. It was full of joys and chaos. Everyone got wet and cold because of the wind and splashes but everyone still had fun! The main reason I liked about this festival is everyone had fun by splashing or even shoot water with guns at each others. It was a big mess. but we still enjoyed it.	113	14.0	Hello Dragon! 👋<br>It's clear from your story that you have a wonderful imagination and a knack for making your writing exciting. You've given us a vibrant picture of a lively event. To make your stories even more captivating, focusing on how you connect your opening sentence to the main event will be a super step forward. Keep up the great effort, as your energy for writing really comes through.<br><br>📝 **Action Plan for Next Time:**<br>*   Always re-read your first sentence and make sure the "incredible" moment clearly kicks off the main event of your story.<br>*   Break your story into short, clear paragraphs to help your reader follow your ideas more easily.<br>*   Before you finish, double-check your sentences for small grammar points like making sure your verbs match your subjects.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	4.0	You've done a really good job starting your story with the sentence provided, "As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible!" You then describe a fun and chaotic Songkran festival, which certainly sounds incredible. 🎉 To make your content even stronger for a story task, think about how the "something incredible" in the first sentence could be a unique, one-off event that kicks off a special adventure, rather than a known festival. For example, if you said "we saw a giant inflatable duck floating over the school," it would make the initial surprise truly unexpected and lead to different kinds of developments in your story. You did a great job of telling us what happened and how you and your friend reacted, showing that you understood what to write about.	4.0	You really managed to grab my attention with your descriptions of the water splashing and the general chaos, making your story very engaging. You've clearly communicated straightforward ideas about the festival and your enjoyment. Your words truly convey the excitement, and it feels like I'm right there in the middle of the water fight with you. To get a perfect score in this area, try to ensure that your opening sentence sets up a truly *unexpected* event that forms the core of your narrative, rather than a description of a regular (though exciting) event. This will help you fully use the "story" genre conventions.	3.0	Your story flows quite nicely, Dragon, and the ideas are connected well enough that I can follow what happened from beginning to end. However, presenting your entire story as one long paragraph can sometimes make it a little tricky for the reader to follow all your different thoughts and events. 📖 A good tip for improving your organisation is to use paragraph breaks when you introduce a new main idea or when there's a shift in the story. For instance, you could have one paragraph describing the initial sight of Songkran, another for when you and your friend decide to join, and a third for your reflections on why you enjoyed it so much. This helps to group related ideas together and makes your writing much clearer and easier to read.	3.0	You've used some great everyday words to describe the fun and excitement of Songkran, like "chaotic," "full of joys," and "big mess." 🥳 These words help me imagine the scene clearly. However, there were a few places where the grammar could be tightened up a bit. For example, you wrote, "a lot of people at my school splashes water." When you have "a lot of people," the verb should be "splash" (without the 'es') because "people" is plural. Similarly, you said, "some people even uses a water guns!" Here, "use" (without the 's') would be correct for "people," and "water guns" (plural) would also be more natural. Also, for "The main reason I liked about this festival is...", a smoother way to say this would be "The main reason I liked this festival is..." or "The main reason I liked **about** this festival **is that**...". These little corrections will make your sentences even more accurate and help you express yourself with greater control. Sometimes in Thai, the verb form doesn't change for singular or plural subjects as much as in English, so it's a common area to pay extra attention to. Keep practising, and you'll master these small but important details.	\N	\N	2025-09-23
