uced 'hobbies' but didn't quite get to explain it fully, and in an essay, we need to develop all our points properly. For instance, you could have added a sentence or two explaining *how* friends influence hobbies, just like you did for clothes.	3.0	You've made a good effort to get your message across, and your ideas are generally understandable. That's a solid foundation! 👏 To improve here, think about the person you are writing for. An essay for an exam usually needs a more formal tone, a bit like when you're talking to a teacher, rather than chatting with a friend. Try to use phrases that sound a bit more serious and less like everyday conversation. For example, instead of "our school is fill with cosplay anime," you could say "our school was filled with students cosplaying anime characters." This small change helps to maintain a consistent academic feel throughout your writing.	3.0	You've done well by dividing your essay into paragraphs, which helps the reader follow your arguments. Starting new paragraphs for each main idea, like 'clothes and fashion' and 'studying,' is exactly right! ✨ To make your writing flow even more smoothly, try using a wider variety of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas more clearly. For instance, instead of just "Next is studying," you could say, "Another significant area where friends influence choices is in academic pursuits," and then explain how it connects to your previous point. Many Thai learners sometimes use simple linking words, but expanding your range will make your essay much more sophisticated and easier for the reader to navigate.	2.0	You've used a good range of everyday words to explain your points, which is a positive start! 📚 However, there are quite a few small errors in grammar, spelling, and how words are used that can sometimes make your meaning a little unclear. For example, you wrote "friends has" instead of "friends *have*," and "effected by friends" when it should be "affected by friends." Also, "our school is fill with cosplay anime" needs a helping verb, like "our school *was filled* with cosplay anime." These types of errors, especially with subject-verb agreement (making sure the verb matches the noun) and distinguishing between similar-sounding words like 'affect' and 'effect,' are common for many learners. Keep an eye out for these, Leo; carefully checking your work for these kinds of errors will really help you to express yourself more accurately!	\N	\N	2025-09-12
