ou could provide more specific details and development for each point. The essay does cover the topic, but with limited depth.	3.0	You have communicated straightforward ideas about Tokyo, and the personal anecdotes help to hold the reader's attention. The tone is enthusiastic and engaging. However, the numerous errors in spelling and grammar detract from the overall effectiveness.	2.0	You've structured your essay with an introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion. However, the transitions between paragraphs could be smoother. The second paragraph feels a bit disjointed, jumping from the people to the colors and then to the clothing styles. To improve your organization, focus on these concrete steps: Create clearer topic sentences, Use transitions between paragraphs, Group related ideas together, Provide a stronger conclusion.	2.0	Florence, your language use is limited by frequent errors in spelling and grammar. These errors often impede communication. You also have trouble with plurals. Examples include: "live" (should be "life"), "torist" (should be "tourist"), "specilly" (should be "especially"), "a anime fan" (should be "an anime fan"), "There were full of color" (should be "It was full of color" or "There was a lot of color"), "store and mall were painted" (store and mall are singular, but "were" is plural), "cloths" (should be "clothes"), "peopel" (should be "people"). The phrase "This town will forever be the best town I have ever been to" is a bit repetitive and could be rephrased. To improve your language use, consider the following advice: Prioritize spelling accuracy, Focus on singular and plural forms, Review basic grammar rules, Vary your sentence structure, Proofread carefully.	\N	\N	2025-07-11
