ed information was included!	5.0	You've done a fantastic job of writing an email that is completely appropriate for a friend 👍. Your greeting "Hello Corey" and closing "bye Tanya" are just right, and your tone is friendly and natural throughout. The email flows well, and you've communicated all your straightforward ideas effectively. Corey would certainly find your email easy to understand and would feel like you're having a real chat with him.	3.0	Your email is coherent, and your ideas flow logically from one to the next, which is a good foundation. You used linking words like "And," "So," and "For the main activity" to connect your thoughts. However, presenting the entire body of your email as one long block of text makes it a little harder for the reader to quickly pick out individual points. Think about using paragraph breaks to separate each main idea. For instance, you could have one paragraph for acknowledging the tickets and meeting arrangements, another for the activity choice, and a third for what to bring. This would make your well-connected ideas even clearer for your reader.	4.0	You've used a good range of everyday vocabulary in your email that fits the context perfectly, such as "excited," "robot building," and "extra bags" 🛍️. Your grammar shows a good degree of control, and while there were a few small errors, they didn't stop Corey from understanding your message. For example, you wrote "buid" instead of "build," and "alot" instead of "a lot." Also, phrases like "if would be alot of interesting things" could be more accurately expressed as "if there would be a lot of interesting things," and "we should prepared for that" should be "we should *be* prepared for that." Lastly, "looking forward to meet you" should be "looking forward to *meeting* you." These are small adjustments that, with a little more practice, will make your English even more precise and accurate.	\N	\N	2025-09-17
