Part 1	4.0	Ana, you clearly introduced Paris as your favorite place and successfully supported this with its renowned art, architecture, fashion, and gastronomy. Your specific examples, like the Louvre, Eiffel Tower, and famous fashion houses, were excellent and helped explain your personal connection to them. You did a great job of informing the reader about why Paris holds such a special place for you. However, you could make some improvements.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Paris a major European city in France, renowed for its art, architecture, fashion and gastronomy. Paris is known as the "City of lights". Everytime I think about the time I was in Paris I think about the wonderful architecture and how they light up the city."\n    Problem: This sentence is a bit long and combines several facts about Paris.\n    How to fix it: You could break this into slightly shorter sentences to make it easier to read. For example, "Paris, a major European city in France, is renowned for its art, architecture, fashion, and gastronomy. It's also famously known as the "City of Lights." Every time I recall my visit to Paris, I remember the wonderful architecture and how beautifully the city is illuminated at night."\n\n*   Example: In your conclusion, you said, "with so much you can do, so many places and landmarks you can visit I urge you to go visit."\n    Problem: This part is a bit general and repeats the idea of "so much to do/see." While it's true, it could be phrased more powerfully.\n    How to fix it: To make your conclusion even stronger, you could be more specific or more evocative. For instance, "In conclusion, Paris is a truly wonderful place, offering countless enchanting experiences and iconic landmarks. I strongly encourage you to visit; its magic is beyond imagination."	4.0	You effectively communicated your ideas about Paris and did a good job of holding the reader's attention with engaging language, especially when you described the "City of lights" and the "jaw dropping angelical buildings." Your style was appropriate for a travel article, and while there are minor issues with sentence structure, the overall message is clear and persuasive.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Each of these buildings are like a reflection of history, they blend the old with the new in a way that makes us feel like we're walking back in time."\n    Problem: The phrasing "Each of these buildings *are* like" has a small agreement error (should be "is"). Also, "like a reflection of history" could be slightly more impactful.\n    How to fix it: Pay attention to subject-verb agreement. You could say, "Each of these buildings *is* a reflection of history, blending the old with the new in a way that truly transports us back in time."\n\n*   Example: You used "Which are all my all time favourite designer brands. Which is why Paris stands out to me."\n    Problem: Starting sentences with "Which" can sometimes create sentence fragments or sound a bit informal, even if it creates a "percussive effect" for emphasis.\n    How to fix it: While this can be a stylistic choice, it's often safer in formal writing to connect these ideas differently. You could combine the sentences: "These are all my all-time favorite designer brands, which is precisely why Paris stands out to me." or "These are all my all-time favorite designer brands. This is a key reason why Paris stands out to me."	4.0	You used a clear three-paragraph structure with an introduction, a body, and a conclusion. You also used a good variety of linking words and phrases like "One of the reasons why" and "Alongside this" to connect your ideas. This made your essay generally well-organized and coherent. Your essay was a bit longer than the suggested word count, which is fine, but when you write more, it's very important to divide your ideas into separate paragraphs to help the reader follow along more easily.\n\n*   Example: Your second paragraph is quite long and covers two distinct main ideas: history/architecture and fashion.\n    Problem: While the ideas are related to Paris, combining them into one very long paragraph can make it harder for the reader to digest all the information.\n    How to fix it: When your essay is longer, consider breaking down your main body into more focused paragraphs. For example, you could have one paragraph dedicated to the history and architecture, and then start a new paragraph for the topic of fashion. This creates clearer sections for the reader.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Parisians are also very fashionable people, whether if its Paris fashion week or just an ordinary day."\n    Problem: This sentence introduces a slightly different idea (Parisians' fashion) without a smooth connection to the previous point about fashion houses.\n    How to fix it: You could either integrate this more smoothly into the fashion paragraph or consider it a minor detail. If you keep it, you could connect it by saying, "It's not just the fashion houses that make Paris stand out; Parisians themselves are incredibly fashionable, whether during Paris Fashion Week or on an ordinary day."	4.0	You used a good range of vocabulary, including some more advanced words like "renowned," "gastronomy," and "angelical," which were used appropriately. You also attempted to use a range of simple and complex grammatical forms, showing good control and flexibility in many parts of your writing. While there were occasional errors, they generally did not stop the reader from understanding your message.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "renowed," "Eiffle tower," "palace of Vesaille"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled. They should be "renowned," "Eiffel Tower," and "Palace of Versailles."\n    How to fix it: Always carefully proofread your work for spelling errors. Pay special attention to proper nouns, as they are often unique.\n\n*   Grammar - Word Choice and Missing Words:\n    Example: "Paris a major European city" (missing "is"), "Each of these buildings are like" (agreement error), "whether if its Paris fashion week" ("its" vs "it's").\n    Problem: You are missing a verb in the first example, have a subject-verb agreement error in the second, and a common confusion between "its" (possessive) and "it's" (it is) in the third.\n    How to fix it: Pay attention to basic sentence structure (Subject + Verb). Ensure your verb agrees with your subject (singular subject needs a singular verb, plural subject needs a plural verb). Remember that "its" shows possession (e.g., "The city and *its* lights"), while "it's" is a contraction for "it is" (e.g., "It's a beautiful day").	\N	\N	2025-07-23
28890	2025-09-22 12:00:18.881706+00	2026	Term 1 Exam Question 2	Having pets have both advantages and disavantages, but is it a good idea to have pets?, I'll tell you my opinions which, maybe will help you decide.<br><br><br>Hi, there I'm kun and on today weekly articles I'll tell you about my opinions on having pets by talking about the disad vantages and advantages.<br><br><br>From my point of view, I think having a pets for being a friend, or for house keeping is a good thing to have but They can also be a, disavantages, if you didn't live in the right places, like the places is too small or so, but if you live in the right places I'll go for it.<br><br><br>the advantages of having pets, In my opinion having pets had a lot of advantages but I think Two stan out more then the other, having them as friend and for house keeping Now moving on to the disad vantages of having pets.<br><br><br>The dis advantages of having, the disadvantages of having pets is also a lot and Two of them also stans out one is that they cost a lot of money I have dogs my self so I know that some of the equ- ipment isn't chep, Two is that if it isn't the right places.<br><br><br>how to organize everthing	224	8.0	Hello Kun! 👋 It's fantastic to see you tackling the topic of pets with your writing. You've made a great start by giving your opinion and thinking about both the good and not-so-good things about having animal friends. To make your writing even clearer and more interesting, we'll focus on making your ideas flow smoothly and using your words with even more care. Keep up the wonderful effort! 📝<br><br>📋 Here are three steps you can take to improve your writing:<br>✔️ **Plan your ideas more thoroughly:** Before you start writing, make a quick plan or a mind map of all the points you want to include for each paragraph. This will help you ensure all your ideas are fully explained and connected.<br>✔️ **Connect your sentences and paragraphs:** Practise using linking words and phrases to help your ideas flow better. Words like "firstly," "additionally," "however," "on the other hand," and "in conclusion" can make a big difference!<br>✔️ **Proofread carefully for common errors:** After you finish writing, read through your work slowly, specifically looking for common spelling mistakes and checking that your sentences make complete sense. Reading aloud can often help you spot these errors.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	You've done a good job of starting to discuss the advantages and disadvantages of having pets, Kun, and you've clearly stated your opinion, which is a key part of writing an article! For example, you mentioned "being a friend" and "house keeping" as advantages. To really get a score of 5 here, you could try to explain these points more fully. What do you mean by "house keeping"? Does a pet help keep the house tidy, or does it mean something else? 😊 Also, when you talk about "the right places," it would be helpful to give more details. For instance, you could say, "If you live in a small apartment, a large dog might not be comfortable, whereas a cat would be a good fit." This helps your reader understand your points completely and makes your argument much stronger.	2.0	You've made a great effort to connect with your readers right from the beginning, Kun, which is perfect for an article! Starting with "I'll tell you my opinions which, maybe will help you decide" is a friendly way to invite them in. To make your article even more engaging and effective for a teenage magazine, try to ensure your introduction clearly sets the stage for what's to come, and your conclusion wraps up your thoughts. For instance, you could have a strong opening sentence that grabs attention, like "Ever wondered if a furry friend is right for you?" 🤔 And for your conclusion, aim to summarise your main points and offer a final thought or recommendation, rather than adding a new, unrelated sentence like "how to organize everthing." Thinking about your target audience – teenagers – and how to keep them interested with clear, well-organised ideas will help you communicate even more effectively.	1.0	You've used paragraph breaks, Kun, which is a good start for organising your thoughts! To improve this area and work towards a higher score, try to make sure each paragraph focuses on one main idea and that these ideas are logically connected. For example, your introduction is a bit separated from your main opinion. You could combine these, and then have distinct paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages. ✍️ Also, using a wider variety of linking words would help your ideas flow much more smoothly. Instead of jumping directly from "advantages" to "disadvantages," you could use phrases like "On the other hand," or "However, it's also important to consider the drawbacks." This makes your writing much easier to follow for your reader. Sometimes, in Thai, ideas can be linked more implicitly, but in English, using explicit linking words helps create a clear path for the reader.	2.0	You're using everyday words to talk about pets, Kun, which is appropriate for this article! To really elevate your writing in this area, let's focus on a few key improvements. First, watch out for common spelling errors like "disavantages" (it should be 'disadvantages'), "equ- ipment" (it should be 'equipment'), and "chep" (it should be 'cheap'). Always double-check your spelling! 🧐 Second, let's work on getting your verb agreements right. For example, you wrote "Having pets have," but it should be "Having pets **has**" because "having pets" is treated as a singular idea. Similarly, "Two stan out" should be "Two **stand** out." Lastly, when you talk about "having a pets," remember that "a" is for singular, so it should be "having **a pet**" or "having **pets**." Thai grammar structures can sometimes influence these choices, so focusing on singular and plural forms in English will be a great step forward for you! Keep practising, and these improvements will make your writing much clearer and more natural. 🚀	\N	\N	2025-09-22
28984	2025-07-23 14:47:22.165763+00	2026	A Place I Love	There are rumors going around say dude I went to one place in thailand that so amazing. It have a very beatiful beach and sea. The sea was so clear that I can see the animals. People will reply like wow where is it, what does it called? and the answer was Khou island. It located in side of thailand. Which took 5 hours to go there. I went there on my last holiday and it was a the most fantatist place I ever went. I stay at Tinkerbell resort. Let me introduce some hotel it was name by disney character first is Tinkerbell, peterpan, wendy as you can see it. It come from a disney cartoon called "Peter Pan".<br><br><br>Well Tinkerbell resort have a great service. Their have no limt food like whenever you hungry you can just go to cofertia living room. I went there and I so shock cause the food. I have to eat a lot sometime I to eat it taste really good. But I too full. Not just that I went to waterfall to play around it after that unfortunaly I have to go home so I spent all day getting sun tan and it work well. So the next day I ride a boat and went to beach side since Khou island is surrounded by sea.<br><br><br>Afterwards I just want more attention to Khou island, more tourist. What I try to say is Khou island deserve more attention. The Reason was it have clear sea and have no horse poop. Which is really good. So that is the mainly topic why I write word for. Love and hope you guys visit Khao island more	281	12.0	Platwo, you wrote about Khou Island and shared some things you like about it, like the beautiful beach and the clear sea. You also talked about a resort you stayed at. You did a good job of introducing the place. To make your writing even better, you can work on making your article sound more suitable for a travel website and making your ideas connect more clearly. You can also improve your spelling, grammar, and punctuation so your message is always easy to understand. Keep practicing, and you'll see great improvement	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	Part 1	3.0	Platwo, you introduced Khou Island and mentioned a few things about it like the beautiful beach, the clear sea, and a resort you stayed at. You also talked about wanting more attention for the island. However, you didn't fully explain *why* these things make it a place you truly love, and some parts felt a little disconnected from the main idea of loving the place.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "It have a very beatiful beach and sea. The sea was so clear that I can see the animals."\n    Problem: You tell us it's beautiful and clear, but you don't really show *why* you love it personally. The connection to your feelings about the place isn't very strong.\n    How to fix it: You could add more details about *how* you felt when you saw the beautiful beach or the clear sea. For instance, you could say, "The sea was so clear that I could see the colourful fish swimming right at my feet, and it made me feel so peaceful and happy." This helps the reader understand your personal connection.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "So that is the mainly topic why I write word for. Love and hope you guys visit Khao island more"\n    Problem: The ending feels a bit sudden and doesn't fully bring together all your reasons for loving the island. Also, the point about "no horse poop" in the previous sentence feels out of place and doesn't clearly support why you love the island.\n    How to fix it: Your conclusion should summarize why you love the place and invite the reader to visit in a more complete way. For example, "Khou Island is truly a special place for me because of its stunning natural beauty and welcoming resorts. I hope you get to experience its magic and see why I love it so much!" This makes the ending stronger and more focused on your main point.	3.0	You tried to write an article for a travel website, but the way you wrote it sometimes sounded more like a personal message to a friend rather than a formal article. An article for a website needs to be interesting and clear for a wide audience.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "There are rumors going around say dude I went to one place in thailand that so amazing. It have a very beatiful beach and sea. The sea was so clear that I can see the animals. People will reply like wow where is it, what does it called?"\n    Problem: This opening is very informal and sounds like spoken English ("say dude," "wow where is it, what does it called?"). This style isn't usually suitable for a written article on a travel website.\n    How to fix it: Start your article with a more inviting and formal tone that would draw in readers. You could say, "Have you ever dreamed of finding a truly amazing place to relax and enjoy nature's beauty? I recently visited an incredible island in Thailand that I'd love to tell you about." This is more appropriate for a website.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Their have no limt food like whenever you hungry you can just go to cofertia living room. I went there and I so shock cause the food."\n    Problem: The language here is very conversational and uses phrases that are too casual for an article, like "no limt food" and "I so shock cause the food."\n    How to fix it: Use more formal language to describe the resort and food. For example, "The Tinkerbell resort offers excellent service, including unlimited food available in their comfortable living room. I was truly surprised by the amazing variety and quality of the dishes they offered."	3.0	Your essay has three paragraphs, which is good for an article. However, some of your ideas jump around a bit, and the way you connect your sentences and paragraphs could be clearer. This makes it a little hard for the reader to follow your thoughts sometimes.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I stay at Tinkerbell resort. Let me introduce some hotel it was name by disney character first is Tinkerbell, peterpan, wendy as you can see it. It come from a disney cartoon called "Peter Pan"."\n    Problem: This part about the hotel names feels a bit like a list and breaks the flow of describing your experience at the resort.\n    How to fix it: You could weave this information into your description more smoothly. For example, "I stayed at the wonderful Tinkerbell resort, which I discovered was named after a Disney character, just like some of their other lovely accommodations such as Peterpan and Wendy." This integrates the information without interrupting the main idea.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "I went to waterfall to play around it after that unfortunaly I have to go home so I spent all day getting sun tan and it work well. So the next day I ride a boat and went to beach side since Khou island is surrounded by sea."\n    Problem: The connection between playing at the waterfall, getting a tan because you had to go home, and then going to the beach the next day is a bit confusing. The "so" and "since" are not used correctly here to link the ideas.\n    How to fix it: Make the sequence of events clearer. For example, "After visiting the beautiful waterfall, I spent the rest of the day getting a lovely sun tan by the beach, enjoying my last hours on the island before heading home. The next morning, I took a boat trip around the island to fully appreciate its stunning coastline, which is surrounded by crystal-clear waters."	2.0	You used some simple words to describe your trip, but there were many mistakes with spelling, punctuation, and grammar throughout your essay. These mistakes often made your sentences difficult to understand.\n\n*   Spelling and Capitalisation:\n    Example: "It have a very beatiful beach and sea. The sea was so clear that I can see the animals. People will reply like wow where is it, what does it called? and the answer was Khou island. It located in side of thailand. Which took 5 hours to go there. I went there on my last holiday and it was a the most fantatist place I ever went. I stay at Tinkerbell resort."\n    Problem: Words like "beatiful," "fantatist," and "cofertia" are misspelled. Proper nouns like "Thailand" and "Khou Island" are not always capitalized correctly, and sentences don't always start with a capital letter.\n    How to fix it: Always check your spelling carefully. Remember to capitalize the first word of every sentence and all proper nouns (names of specific places, people, or things). So, "beautiful," "fantastic," "cafeteria," "Thailand," and "Khou Island" would be correct.\n\n*   Grammar and Word Choice:\n    Example: "It have a very beatiful beach and sea." and "Their have no limt food like whenever you hungry you can just go to cofertia living room."\n    Problem: You used "have" instead of "has" for "It" and "Their" instead of "They." Also, "no limt food" should be "unlimited food" or "food with no limit."\n    How to fix it: Pay attention to subject-verb agreement (e.g., "It **has**," not "It have"). Use the correct pronouns (e.g., "They **have**," not "Their have"). Also, choose words that express your meaning clearly and are appropriate for an article.\n\n*   Punctuation:\n    Example: "People will reply like wow where is it, what does it called?"\n    Problem: This sentence runs on, and the questions are embedded without proper question marks or sentence separation.\n    How to fix it: Break down long sentences and use correct punctuation. For example, "People will reply, 'Wow! Where is it? What is it called?'" This makes the questions clear and easier to read.	\N	\N	2025-07-23
