all your interesting points much clearer for your readers. You should also work on making your language more formal for a website and correcting your grammar, spelling, and capitalisation so your message is always easy to understand.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	Part 1	3.0	Arther, you introduced Phuket as the best city you've ever visited and gave many reasons why you love it, such as the beautiful sea, activities, the old city, Phuket FantaSea, a unique landmark, and delicious food. You conveyed your excitement for the city. However, you tried to include too many different points, which meant you couldn't explain each one in enough detail. Your essay also ended abruptly, so the conclusion is missing, which affects the overall completeness of your article.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "They have a circus show in Phuket also old city, once im there i think i have travled back in time it is just old fashion i really want you to really give Phuket a try you will in love with this city."\n    Problem: You mention several different attractions (circus, old city) very quickly without much explanation about what makes them special or why you love them. The old city is described vaguely as "old fashion."\n    How to fix it: Choose one or two of your favorite attractions and describe them in more detail, explaining *why* they are amazing and how they made you feel. For example, instead of just "old city, once im there i think i have travled back in time it is just old fashion," you could say, "The historic Old Town of Phuket is like stepping back in time, with its beautifully preserved shophouses and charming local atmosphere that made me feel so connected to the past."\n\n*   Example: Your essay ended abruptly without a conclusion.\n    Problem: A complete article needs a conclusion to summarize your main points and give the reader a final thought or invitation. Ending suddenly leaves the reader feeling unfinished.\n    How to fix it: Always plan to write a full concluding paragraph. In it, you should briefly summarize why Phuket is a place you love and perhaps encourage readers to visit, reminding them of the main highlights.	2.0	You wanted to persuade the reader to visit Phuket, and you shared your strong positive feelings. However, your writing style was very informal and conversational ("i Say that because Phuket are just awsome," "you know?"). There were many errors and awkward phrases that made it very difficult to understand your ideas, and the article did not effectively hold the reader's attention.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "Phuket is the best city i ever been to, whole life with the beautiful tur.gouse sea fun activities to do in Phuket, i Say that because Phuket are just awsome, once i been there i think i wouldn't have return back because this city never sleep i begged my mom not to travel back to Bankok, They have a circus show in Phuket also old city,..."\n    Problem: This opening is one very long, informal sentence with many grammatical errors. Phrases like "tur.gouse," "i Say that because Phuket are just awsome," and "this city never sleep" are too casual and unclear for an article.\n    How to fix it: Start your article with a clear, inviting, and more formal introduction. Break down your ideas into shorter, clearer sentences. For instance, "Phuket is truly the best city I have ever visited. With its beautiful turquoise sea and endless fun activities, it's an amazing destination. Once I arrived, I never wanted to leave because this city is always lively."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "Phuket, it is Awsome, Best city i've been to you know? this city got every thing in there many entertainment things"\n    Problem: This is very informal ("you know?") and repetitive ("Awsome, Best city"). It also has grammatical errors.\n    How to fix it: Use more formal language and vary your vocabulary. For example, "Phuket is an amazing city, truly one of the best I've experienced. It offers a wide variety of entertainment options."	2.0	You tried to include different aspects of Phuket, but your essay suffered from a severe lack of clear paragraph breaks and logical flow. Many different ideas were mixed together in very long sections, making it very difficult for the reader to follow your thoughts. This disorganized structure made your long essay much harder to read.\n\n*   Example: Your first paragraph covers your overall feeling about Phuket, why you wanted to stay, and then jumps to a circus show and the old city.\n    Problem: Too many unrelated ideas are crammed into one long paragraph without clear connections. The flow is very disjointed.\n    How to fix it: **Always use separate paragraphs for distinct ideas, especially in a longer essay.** Your first paragraph should be a clear introduction. Then, you could have a separate paragraph for entertainment (circus, FantaSea), another for historical/cultural sites (old city, specific landmarks), and another for food.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "i will show you what its look like. beautiful beaches, wonderful city i suggest you go to this placed it call Phuket Fanta Sea... i recommed you go to know tapo. relly amazing place with the signature nail it comes from the middle of island there a small clift it look like a nail stabbed into the island, it is really quite strange but it look unique and it is the one thing i can't really forget about are foods."\n    Problem: This is one extremely long, rambling section that jumps from general descriptions ("beautiful beaches, wonderful city") to specific attractions (FantaSea, the nail-like cliff) and then to food, all without proper paragraphing or smooth transitions. The sentence structure is very difficult to follow.\n    How to fix it: Break this down. For instance, you could have a paragraph about the natural beauty, including beaches and the unique "nail" cliff. Then, a separate paragraph about cultural shows like FantaSea. Finally, a new paragraph dedicated entirely to the delicious food.	1.0	You used very simple vocabulary, and there were many significant and frequent mistakes in spelling, capitalisation, grammar (including subject-verb agreement, verb forms, articles, and sentence structure), and punctuation. These errors made your writing extremely difficult to understand at times. Your tone was also consistently too informal for an article.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "tur.gouse," "awsome," "Bankok," "im," "travled," "plased," "vial," "Sens," "recommed," "tapo," "relly," "flavoury," "delicous," "ingredeants," "luxuros," "resturant," "that'se"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled or used incorrectly. They should be "turquoise," "awesome," "Bangkok," "I'm," "traveled," "place," "vibrant," "senses," "recommend," "Ko Tapu" (or James Bond Island), "really," "flavourful," "delicious," "ingredients," "luxurious," "restaurant," "that's."\n    How to fix it: You need to proofread your writing extremely carefully. Use a dictionary or a spell checker for every word you are unsure about. These frequent errors severely impact how well your message can be understood.\n\n*   Capitalisation:\n    Example: "Phuket is the best city i ever been to," "i Say that," "Bankok," "Phuket Fanta Sea," "Ocean," "Sens," "nail," "Dior" (used in another student's good example).\n    Problem: You often do not capitalize the first word of a sentence or proper nouns (like "Bangkok," "Phuket FantaSea," "Ko Tapu"). Sometimes words that shouldn't be capitalized are.\n    How to fix it: Always capitalize the first letter of every sentence. Capitalize all proper nouns (specific names of places, shows, or landmarks).\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement, Verb Forms, and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "Phuket **are** just awsome," "i wouldn't have **return** back," "i think i have **travled**," "it is just old fashion," "Phuket, it is Awsome, Best city i've been to you know? this city got every thing in there," "the food are so delicous"\n    Problem: You have consistent errors with subject-verb agreement ("Phuket *is*," not "are"). Verb forms are incorrect ("wouldn't have *returned*," "have *traveled*"). Many sentences are run-on or grammatically incomplete, making them very difficult to parse.\n    How to fix it: Focus on basic sentence structure and ensuring your verbs match your subjects. Practice using correct verb tenses and forms. Break down your complex thoughts into simpler, correct sentences.	\N	\N	2025-07-24
28836	2025-09-22 10:50:35.285702+00	2026	Term 1 Exam Question 2	A lot of people around the world adopt a pet. Some think its good some don't think the same way. Today I'm going to tell you some advantages, and disadvantages.\r<br><br><br>Firstly advantage. Having pet give you a lot of advantages. First it help reducing your lonelynes. A large number of residents live by themselfs without a partner neither with their family. This made them to have something to play with or spending time with. Beside that pets are a great mental support too. Due to an experiment, sciencist gave a kitten to a prisoner let them spend time together. Prisoner end up having a great behavior following orders from the policemen. Because of that it proves having have advantages.\r<br><br><br>Beside advantage, there are some disadvantages too. First is some place do not allow pet. Some places like hotel, and condeminium don't allow pet owner because the can cause very serious trouble example like they broken an expensive chenale. Other than that pets owners care about their pet's safety. Because they spend time together oftenly they begun to creat a big bond with each other. Pet owners care their pets like their children.\r<br><br><br>To summarize, having pet is a good idea but there some advantages and disadvantages. Advantages are it help reliving lovelyness by spending time with each other. And some disadv-antages are you will worried about their safety when you went to work or a vacation. From what I have told you I wanted to know. Would you adopt a pet?	257	12.0	Namo K, your article about pets is quite thoughtful, and you've shared some interesting ideas with your readers. To make your writing even clearer and more engaging, focusing on precise language and careful sentence construction will be very helpful. Keep up your hard work; with a little practice, your articles will truly captivate your audience!<br><br>📋 Action Plan for Improvement<br>✅ After writing, try reading your article aloud to yourself. This can help you catch awkward sentences or find places where words might be missing.<br>✅ Keep a small notebook of new vocabulary you learn and practice using these words in your own sentences.<br>✅ When you’re writing, pay close attention to how your sentences begin and end, and ensure your ideas flow smoothly from one to the next.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	You did a great job including all the parts of the task – you talked about both the good things and the tricky things about having pets, and you shared your own thoughts on the topic. You even ended with a question for the reader, which is a lovely touch for an article designed to get teenagers thinking! 🐾 To take your content to the next level, remember to keep your examples and explanations tightly connected to your main argument. For example, the detailed story about the prisoner and the kitten, while interesting, was quite long and could have been made much shorter to support your point more directly and efficiently for an article of this length. Ensuring every detail helps your reader quickly grasp your main message will make your writing even stronger.	3.0	You successfully used the common features of an article, and your introduction and conclusion are quite welcoming for your readers. It’s clear you know how to start and finish an article! ✨ To make your article truly connect with teenagers, try to use language that feels a bit more natural and conversational for that age group. For instance, instead of "Firstly advantage," you could try saying "Let's start with the good things" or "One big plus is...". Thinking about how a friend might talk about these topics can help you choose more engaging words and phrases. Making your examples more relatable to a typical teenager's life might also help hold their attention even more effectively. Sometimes in Thai, direct and concise phrasing is common, but in English articles, adding a little more conversational flair can make a big difference for your target audience.	3.0	You’ve done a good job of structuring your article with separate paragraphs for advantages and disadvantages, which makes it easy to follow your main points. You also used some clear linking phrases like "Firstly advantage" and "To summarize." 📝 To make your writing flow even more smoothly, try varying your linking words and phrases. Instead of always starting with a similar phrase, you could use "Another benefit is..." or "On the other hand...". Also, consider how your sentences connect *within* each paragraph. Sometimes, adding a word like "Therefore," "Consequently," or "In addition" can help your ideas stick together better. For example, after stating an advantage, you could follow up with "This means that..." to elaborate on your point. In some languages, less explicit linking might be common, but English writing often relies on clear connecting words and phrases to guide the reader through the arguments.	2.0	You've used some appropriate everyday vocabulary related to pets and opinions, and you've shown you can construct simple sentences to convey your ideas. Keep building on this – your dedication will pay off! 📚 Let's focus on three important areas to make your language much clearer and more precise:<br>1. **Spelling:** I noticed a few words like "lonelynes" (should be 'loneliness'), "themselfs" (should be 'themselves'), "sciencist" (should be 'scientist'), "condeminium" (should be 'condominium'), and "chenale" (which seemed to be a misspelling of something like 'item' or 'valuable'). Getting these spellings right is really important for your reader to understand you without confusion. A great habit is to reread your work carefully, or even use an online spell checker if you're writing on a computer.<br>2. **Subject-Verb Agreement and Plurals:** You wrote "Having pet give you" which should be "Having **pets gives** you" or "A pet **gives** you." Similarly, "Some place do not allow pet" should be "Some **places do not allow pets**." In English, nouns and verbs often need to agree in number (singular with singular, plural with plural). Remember to use plural nouns like "advantages" and "disadvantages" consistently, and always make sure your verbs match your subjects.<br>3. **Sentence Structure and Verb Tense:** Some of your sentences were a little difficult to follow. For example, "sciencist gave a kitten to a prisoner let them spend time together" could be clearer as, "scientists gave a kitten to a prisoner, *allowing* them to spend time together." Also, "they broken an expensive chenale" should be "they *broke* an expensive *item*." Focusing on the correct verb tense (past tense for a past action like 'broke') and ensuring your sentences have a clear subject and verb will help a lot. In Thai, word forms often don't change for singular or plural, and verb conjugations are much simpler or non-existent compared to English. This can sometimes lead to mistakes in English with plural nouns and subject-verb agreement. Also, sentence structures can be more flexible in Thai, which might lead to sentences in English that feel a bit fragmented or run-on to a native speaker. Practicing these specific areas will really help your English writing become more precise.	\N	\N	2025-09-22
