usages is" -> "Sausages <b>are</b>".</li><li><b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ "do a steak" -> "<b>cook</b> a steak".</li>\n	\N	\N	2025-12-14
29034	2025-11-28 10:04:25.755774+00	2026	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. The apparition of a ghost appeared before him, slowly becoming... Solid or in other words tangible.<br><br><br>Sam was a 16 year old inventor in Texas. There is a saying in Texas "Everything's bigger in Texas", which could be used for Sam's brain. He was a smart person, able to create pretty much anything, like a laser gun or his newest invention "Ghost Detector". An intricate device that allows one to locate THE DEAD... However, there was a massive problem. Sam couldn't see or speak to ghosts, that gave him a brilliant idea. What if he made a machine to make ghosts visible? It initially seemed impossible but everything was at the start.<br><br><br>Three tiring months of work later, he did it. The "Tangibler", he called it was a projector that used electrical waves to turn ghosts into ghouls. All he needed was to test it at a haunted house. Good thing there was a random house down the street. The next day, Sam set off to the haunted house despite his mom's concern (mostly for his sanity).<br><br><br>Sam set up the Tangibler, made a few small adjustments and started it. It actually worked. The ghost now ghoul was strangely aggressive but nevertheless, it was a success. Sam decided to make more ghouls but it was a huge mistake.<br><br><br>The ghouls started to crave blood as they started to chase Sam down the halls, not giving him a chance to hide until Sam found an opportunity to hide in a closet. Unfortunately one of the ghouls found him and played A game where they do sexual stuff for 7 minutes in heaven in a closet. The other ghouls just decided to rip his limbs of one by one. Until he bled out to a slow death.<br><br><br>The lesson was learned the hard way. Leave ghosts alone.	311	18.0	Hello Anu, 👋<br><br>This is a genuinely chilling story! 👻 You did a fantastic job setting the scene with the 'Ghost Detector' and building up the suspense. The description of the ghosts becoming 'tangible' was excellent and very spooky. You clearly have a talent for horror writing.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you polish your writing even further. 📋<br>1. <b>Audience!</b> 🎭 Remember that for school assessments, we need to keep the content PG-13. Avoid graphic violence or sexual references.<br>2. <b>Tone!</b> ⚖️ The shift from scientific invention to extreme horror was quite sudden. Try to keep the tone consistent.<br>3. <b>Vocabulary!</b> 🌟 You used amazing words like 'intricate' and 'sanity'. Keep building that vocabulary!	B2	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	5.0	You answered the prompt perfectly with a very creative invention! 💡 The background in Texas added nice flavor.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Appropriateness!</b> 🚫 As mentioned, avoid graphic scenes in school writing. Focus more on the *feeling* of fear rather than the gory details.<br>2. <b>Pacing!</b> ⏱️ The ending felt a bit rushed compared to the detailed beginning. Spend a bit more time resolving the conflict.	5.0	You held my attention from the very first line! 😱 The 'Ghost Detector' concept is very engaging.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Consistency!</b> 🎭 The tone shifted from a fun sci-fi story to a dark horror very quickly. Try to hint at the danger earlier.<br>2. <b>Ending!</b> 🎬 'Leave ghosts alone' is a catchy lesson, but maybe show us Sam reacting to this lesson.	4.0	Your story is very well organized. 📚 The progression from idea to invention to disaster is logical.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Linking!</b> 🔗 You used 'However' well. Try adding more variety like 'Furthermore' or 'Despite this'.<br>2. <b>Paragraphs!</b> 🧱 Your paragraphs are solid. Just ensure the ending isn't too abrupt.	4.0	Your vocabulary is fantastic! 'Intricate', 'tangible', 'sanity' - wow! 🌟<br><br>Here are a few corrections to help you:<br>1. <b>Capitalization!</b> 🔡 'THE DEAD' doesn't need to be all caps. Italics work better for emphasis.<br>2. <b>Punctuation!</b> 🖊️ 'Sam couldn't see or speak to ghosts, that gave him...' -> This is a comma splice. Use a period or semi-colon: 'ghosts. That gave him...'.<br>3. <b>Articles!</b> 🐜 'Played A game' -> 'played <b>a</b> game'.<br>4. <b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ 'Rip his limbs of' -> 'Rip his limbs <b>off</b>'.	\N	\N	2025-11-28
29405	2025-12-14 15:55:07.098378+00	2026	Camping trip	Hi,<br><br>I'm so happy that we are going to camping this weekend too! I like the nature and animal. But the most important. I love to go to camping with you cause you are my best friends. And I thinks we can take the train on saturday morning. I think it will be more fun. We can enjoy much on this trip. But... i think just cook sausages it not good. I'm disagree about it. But we love to eat - & hot. I think I'll take sandwich and some fruit and the things to cook bar-b-q. And i thinks i should take the big warm pillow. And big sweater for us so we will feel didn't too cold.<br><br>I'm so happy. See you soon, Alex	126	13.0	Hello LinLin! 👋<br><br>Your email is so full of love! "Cause you are my best friends" - that is so sweet! 🥰 Suggesting a "big warm pillow" sounds like the ultimate comfort item! 🛌<br><br>Here are 3 tips for you: 📋<br>1. <b>Verbs!</b> ⚙️ "I thinks" -> "I <b>think</b>" (no 's' for I).<br>2. <b>Negatives!</b> ⛔ "I'm disagree" -> "I <b>disagree</b>".<br>3. <b>Adjectives!</b> 🎨 "feel didn't too cold" -> "feel <b>not</b> too cold" or "won't feel cold".\n	B1	Writing	Term 2 Midterm	PET	Part 1	5.0	You covered the points with emotion. ✅<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Explanation!</b> 💭 "just cook sausages it not good" -> Why? Are they boring? Unhealthy? Add a reason!</li>\n	4.0	Your affectionate tone is lovely. 🌟<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Starting sentences!</b> 🛑 You started many sentences with "But" or "And". Try not to do this too much.</li>\n	2.0	Your sentences were short and choppy. 📝<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Flow!</b> 🌊 Join sentences with "because" or "so".</li>\n	2.0	"Nature" and "pillow" are good words. 👏<br><br>Watch these basics:<br><li><b>Subject-Verb!</b> ⚙️ "I thinks" -> "I <b>think</b>". (He/She thinks).</li><li><b>Agreement!</b> ❌ "I'm disagree" -> "I <b>disagree</b>".</li>\n	\N	\N	2025-12-14
29576	2025-09-21 16:20:48.392137+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	The festival that my family celebrate is christmas day. christmas day is make me always be excited and enjoy in christmasday me and my family always have a christmas tree inside the house. this festival started on december month. We trust that the santa will ride an raindeer fly on the sky. the santa will come to give a present to the kids, when the christmas day is coming i always seen a lot of neon light around in the city and heard a christma song play in the restaurants and a christmas in the restaurant. I like about a christmas song and a christmas tree.	106	11.0	It's lovely to read your article about Christmas Day. You've done a good job of sharing some personal details about a festival that's special to you and your family. Your enthusiasm for Christmas really comes through. The most significant area for you to focus on for improvement is making sure your writing is clearly organised and using capital letters correctly, which will make your ideas much easier for everyone to understand. Keep up the good work and with a little more practice, your writing will become even clearer and more engaging.<br>📋 Action Plan for Dee:<br>✅ Practice writing in separate paragraphs, with each paragraph introducing a new idea.<br>✅ Pay close attention to capital letters for proper nouns (like names of festivals and people) and at the beginning of every sentence.<br>✅ Focus on using correct verb forms and plurals when describing actions and things.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	4.0	You've made a great start at telling us about Christmas Day, Dee. You clearly named the festival and explained that your family celebrates it in December. It was lovely to hear about having a Christmas tree inside your house and seeing all the bright neon lights around the city, and you also shared that you enjoy the Christmas songs and the tree itself. To make your articles even more informative for your readers, try to add a little more detail about what happens during the festival. For instance, when you mentioned Santa and reindeer, you could have described a bit more about how children get presents or what the atmosphere feels like when 'Christmas day is coming.' This would help your readers feel like they are right there with you during the celebrations.	3.0	You’ve done well to write in a friendly style that's suitable for other young people reading your article. You're sharing straightforward ideas about Christmas, which is just what we want to see. To really keep your readers interested and make your article flow even better, try to think about how you can arrange your information. For example, you could have started with a catchy title, and then perhaps a short introduction to Christmas, followed by details about when and what happens, and finally, what you enjoy most. This helps guide your reader through your thoughts and makes your writing more compelling.	2.0	Your ideas about Christmas are connected, which is a good foundation, Dee. However, your article is currently presented as one long block of text. To help your readers follow your ideas more easily, it's really helpful to break your writing into paragraphs. Imagine each new main idea gets its own paragraph. For instance, you could have one paragraph introducing Christmas, another describing the activities you do with your family, and a third about what you particularly like. Using linking words like 'Firstly,' 'Next,' or 'Finally' can also help your sentences and paragraphs connect smoothly. A good tip is to start a new paragraph whenever you introduce a new point or change the topic.	2.0	You've used some good everyday words to talk about Christmas, like 'excited' and 'enjoy,' which helps your reader understand your feelings. However, there are a few important areas where we can really tidy up your writing to make it clearer. One key area is capitalisation. Remember that proper nouns, like the name of a festival ('Christmas Day') and names of characters ('Santa'), always start with a capital letter. Also, every new sentence begins with a capital letter. For example, 'christmas day is make me always be excited' should be 'Christmas Day always makes me excited.' Another point to watch is your verb forms and plurals. For instance, 'christmas day is make me' should be 'Christmas Day makes me,' and 'a raindeer' should be 'a reindeer,' while 'christma song' should be 'Christmas song' or 'Christmas songs.' In Thai, nouns don't change form for plural, and capitalisation rules can be different, so it's a common area for learners to focus on in English. Paying close attention to these small but important details will make a big difference to how clear and correct your writing looks to your reader.	\N	\N	2025-09-21
34926	2025-11-26 21:53:49.901149+00	2026	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. Sam, who is an inventor, loves inventing weird things. He often invents something in his basement for multiple hours. Recently, he invented a revival machine. The revival machine that when you press the button, it would let you choose someone to revived and age, but the machine had 50% successfull rate if it failed it would revived a bad person. At first, Sam really missed his grand mother that passed away 1 year ago.<br><br><br>After he pressed the button. It failed and revived a bad guy in prisoner suit. The bad guy grabbed the machine and immediately ran away. The machine was stolen, however, Sam predicted that so he put a gps tracker on it before he pressed the button. Sam chased the gps and he hadn't ran far away. Sam immedietly got on his car and followed the gps. Sam found the prisoner. The prisoner refused to gave Sam back his machine but, the prisoner offered Sam. The offer was if he gave him back the machine Sam had to bought him an meal. Sam immedietly took the offer because, he spent so much time invented and built that machine.<br><br><br>After that, Sam bought him a meal and drove back home.<br><br><br>Finally, Sam set up the machine and pressed the button subsequently, his grand mother was revived with an age that he chose, which is 53 years. In the end, Sam was really happy because his grandma was revived by him.	250	16.0	Hello Chopin, 👋<br><br>This is a very creative story! The 'revival machine' is a fascinating idea, and the 50% success rate added real tension. The twist with the prisoner stealing it was exciting, and I'm glad Sam got his machine back!<br><br>I have a few tips to help you improve. 📋<br>1. <b>Infinitive Verbs!</b> 🛠️ After 'to', use the base verb: 'to revive', 'to give'.<br>2. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 Watch out for 'successfull' (successful) and 'immedietly' (immediately).<br>3. <b>Flow!</b> 🌊 Some sentences are a bit short. You can join them with 'because' or 'although'.	B1+	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	5.0	You answered the prompt fully with a very original plot! 🌟<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Motivation!</b> 💭 Tell us more about why he missed his grandmother. It would make the ending sweeter.<br>2. <b>The Villain!</b> 🦹‍♂️ Describe the prisoner more. Did he look scary? Was he big?	4.0	Your story was exciting to read! 🤩 The chase scene kept me interested.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Dialogue!</b> 🗣️ You could write the actual words the prisoner said when he offered the deal.<br>2. <b>Pacing!</b> ⏱️ The negotiation happened very fast. You could slow it down to make it more tense.	4.0	Your story is well-structured with a clear beginning, middle, and end. 📑<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Transitions!</b> 🔗 Use words like 'Consequently' or 'Moments later' to move between scenes.<br>2. <b>Paragraphs!</b> 🧱 The paragraph about the chase is good. Make sure the final resolution stands out.	3.0	You used great words like 'prisoner suit' and 'GPS tracker'! 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br>1. <b>Infinitives!</b> 🛠️ 'Refused to gave' -> 'refused to *give*'. 'Had to bought' -> 'had to *buy*'.<br>2. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Successfull' -> 'successful'. 'Immedietly' -> 'immediately'.<br>3. <b>Articles!</b> 🅰️ 'An meal' -> '*a* meal'.<br>4. <b>Adverbs!</b> 🏃‍♂️ 'Ran far away' -> 'run far'.<br>5. <b>Past Perfect!</b> ⏳ 'Grand mother that passed away' -> 'who *had* passed away'.	\N	\N	2025-11-26
34034	2025-07-11 02:39:44.573004+00	2026	The most interesting part of the article	In my opinion, paragraph D is the most interesting one Because we can know which gender of a humpbacks whale is the one singing the song. We can also know how long the song will last. in the arcticle, for example, the song last long for 30 minute, and the female humpback whale didn't respond or sing. The male singing because they let the other know that they are in their areas. The mystery that we still don't know why is the humpback whale singing, and why is there only the male humpback singing. Another interesting thing is why is it pointing it head down to the ocean floor. Is there something at the ocean floor. We can also know so many of the whale can sing last for 30 minutes. And why can they can only last long for 30 minutes. We don't have to be aware of a humpback whale because it will not harmed us.	154	14.0	Hi Singto, you've done a good job of identifying paragraph D as the most interesting and providing several reasons why. To improve, aim to organize your thoughts more clearly, avoid repetition, and refine your grammar. Proofreading your work carefully will help you catch errors and present a more polished piece of writing.	B1	Writing	Formative	PET	Part 2	4.0	Singto, you have effectively identified paragraph D and provided several reasons why you found it interesting. You mention the gender-specific singing, song duration, and the mystery surrounding the behavior. To achieve a score of 5, try to explore the reasons *behind* these facts further, or connect them to other themes from the article. For example, you could speculate why only the males sing, based on clues in the text.	4.0	You clearly communicate your ideas and maintain the reader's interest by posing questions and highlighting intriguing aspects of the whales' behavior. For example, the question "Is there something at the ocean floor?" engages the reader and indicates your curiosity. To improve, focus on presenting your ideas in a more structured way.	3.0	Your response presents several interesting points, but the organisation could be improved. For example, the text lacks clear transitions between ideas, and some points are repeated ("We can also know so many of the whale can sing last for 30 minutes. And why can they can only last long for 30 minutes."). To improve, try grouping related ideas together and using transition words such as "Furthermore," "Moreover," or "However" to guide the reader.	3.0	You demonstrate a reasonable command of vocabulary, but numerous grammatical errors and awkward sentence structures affect the clarity of your writing. For example, "which gender of a humpbacks whale is the one singing the song" and "We can also know so many of the whale can sing last for 30 minutes" are grammatically incorrect. Also "in the arcticle" is a spelling mistake and should be "in the article". It is important to focus on improving sentence structure and grammar. Careful proofreading and seeking feedback will be beneficial.	\N	\N	2025-07-11
28849	2025-09-12 13:08:42.418545+00	2026	Term 1 Exam Question 1	Every body knows that now a day teenagers don't really have much choice to make them self the impact of friend parents and friend has impact but also parent has impact on them self. This usually also happen in a many country including Thailand to, for me personally I think that that teenager follow a choice friend made than parent ricen that bad disition such as clothes and fashion, studying and activity.<br><br><br>Frist of all clothes and fashoin. Now you might experien your parent force you to wear a proper clothe that you clear not like it some might. That come the impact of friend, friend are really great fashion suggestion or in bad case, teenager when they wear a proper clothe often get bullying by other that the reason why some teenager wear unappropriate clothe. some might really like it Teenager are just want to fit in and don't have to worry about critisism.<br><br><br>Secondly studing. Do to thailand were really serious about study they are plantly of parent who really hard ageist they kids. That made teenager in thailand were sturggle to have some time for them self. That friend some in siceen teenager have to study a lots and really bored with that mad friend have many impact on there study disition some might follow they friend to the wrong path or even some just leave school go to there friend.<br><br><br>Now lastly, Ativity such as sport or hobby. This topic was quite optional siceen they from have impact bout so I think major beome choice teen made on this was only by them self either friend are not rally care much.<br><br><br>I conclusion, I think as for the thing I told the main reason I want to let you know is teenager have not really much effect on the friends or parents choises made Teen were clear can made up they choice by them self and nither what teenager choices is how bad it was The person who support you was still your parent. That will be it.	346	9.0	Krewkehw, you have made a good effort in writing your essay and have clearly tried to express your thoughts on an interesting topic! You managed to discuss all the points from the question, which is a great start! Keep working on making your main argument clear from beginning to end, and your writing will become much more impactful. Remember that every time you write, it’s a chance to share your ideas with confidence!<br><br>📋 **Action Plan**<br> *   ✅ Before you start writing, make a quick plan of your main argument and stick to it all the way through your introduction, body paragraphs, and conclusion.<br> *   ✅ When you finish writing, read through your work and circle any words that look like they might be plurals or verbs, then double-check that they are formed correctly for the sentence.<br> *   ✅ Try to use a variety of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas smoothly, for example, instead of repeating "Frist of all," you could try "To begin with" or "Firstly."	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	You did well to include all the points from the question, Krewkehw, like talking about clothes and fashion, studying, and adding your own idea about activities. That shows you understood the task! However, an essay needs a very clear answer to the main question right from the start, and your essay was a little bit confusing about whether friends or parents have more influence. For example, in your introduction, you talked about both having an impact, and then in your conclusion, you said teenagers make choices "by them self," even though your paragraphs showed friends having a big effect. 😕 To improve, try making your main argument super clear at the beginning and then ensure every paragraph helps to prove that argument. It’s like telling a story: you want the reader to know what the story is about from the very first sentence!	2.0	You tried to express your opinion, which is a big part of communicative achievement. However, because your main message about friends versus parents wasn’t always clear, it was sometimes difficult for your reader to fully understand your point of view. 🤔 Also, essays for this exam usually need a more formal style of writing, and sometimes your tone felt a little casual. For instance, phrases like "Now you might experien your parent force you" could be rephrased more formally. Thinking about who you are writing for and what impression you want to make can help a lot. Practise keeping a consistent, slightly more formal tone throughout your whole essay; this will make your ideas sound more convincing and help you inform your reader better!	2.0	You did a good job of breaking your essay into paragraphs, Krewkehw, which is important for organisation! 👍 However, the way your paragraphs connect could be smoother. You used phrases like "Frist of all" and "Secondly," which are good starting points, but you could try a wider range of linking words to make your essay flow more naturally. For example, instead of "That come the impact of friend," you could say "This is where the influence of friends comes in." 🔗 Sometimes, sentences were quite long and had a lot of ideas in them, which made them a bit hard to follow. Thai learners sometimes use longer sentences because it's common in Thai writing, but in English, breaking them down into shorter, clearer sentences can make your writing much easier to read. Try to vary your sentence structures and use more sophisticated linking phrases to guide your reader smoothly from one idea to the next.	2.0	You used a lot of words to get your message across, Krewkehw, and it's great that you're not afraid to write! However, there were quite a few grammar and spelling mistakes that sometimes made your sentences difficult to understand. 😥 For example, with subject-verb agreement, you wrote "parent has impact" when it should be "parents have impact" because "parents" is plural. Another common issue was with plurals, like writing "many country" instead of "many countries." Also, some of your word choices made sentences a bit unclear, for instance, "friend some in siceen" or "experien your parent force you." These kinds of errors are common for Thai learners because English grammar, especially plurals and verb forms, can be very different from Thai. To improve, pay close attention to your verb endings and make sure your subjects and verbs agree. For instance, when you have a plural subject like "friends," the verb often doesn't need an 's' (e.g., "friends influence"). Also, when you finish writing, take a moment to proofread for spelling and think if there's a more precise word you could use. Even a few minutes checking for these common errors can make a big difference! ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-12
30221	2025-09-18 09:01:44.735572+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw somethin incredible! I was a little fox it was hiding in a bush. I was white and beautiful when we tried to touch it, it was scared and nervous. When we finally catch it and bring it home, I think it was sick and cold so we decided to drive along to the pet hospital when the doctor done everything we drive back home.	76	11.0	That's a lovely start to your story about seeing something incredible at school yesterday morning. You've clearly shown us a series of events, and it's easy to follow what happened. While your story has some good elements, there are a couple of areas we can focus on to help your writing progress even further. We want to ensure your stories are as clear and engaging as they can be, so let's aim for even greater accuracy and detail in your next piece.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	3.0	You've done a good job of starting your story with the exact sentence provided, and describing the incredible sight of the fox was a delightful surprise. You took us through the sequence of events, from trying to touch the fox to taking it to the pet hospital and finally driving home. This shows you can develop a narrative. To improve, try to add a little more detail about how you and your friend felt throughout the experience. For instance, you could have written, 'We were so excited to see the fox, but also a bit worried when we saw it looked sick.' Adding these personal touches helps the reader connect with your story even more. Also, remember that for this task, the goal is to write around 100 words. Your story was a little shorter than that this time, so next time, try to add a few more descriptive sentences or some extra details about the vet visit to meet the word count.	3.0	Your story definitely communicates straightforward ideas, and the overall flow makes it easy to understand the main points. You've used the conventions of storytelling quite well, such as describing events in order. However, to make your stories truly captivating and hold the reader's attention throughout, consider adding a little more descriptive language and varying your sentence structures. For example, instead of just saying 'it was white and beautiful,' you could expand on that by saying, 'Its fur was as white as snow, and its tail was beautifully bushy.' This helps the reader imagine the scene more vividly. Think about how you can paint a picture with your words.	3.0	Your story is generally well-organised, and the events happen in a logical order, which is good. You've used some basic linking words like 'when' to connect your ideas. To achieve a higher score, try to introduce a greater variety of linking words and phrases. For instance, you could use 'After that,' 'Suddenly,' or 'Eventually' to show the passage of time or a change in events. Breaking your story into shorter paragraphs when there's a shift in focus, like when you decide to take the fox to the hospital, can also make it easier for the reader to follow along and make your writing appear more structured. This helps to group related ideas together.	2.0	You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, and despite some errors, the meaning of your story is generally clear, which is important. For example, you used words like 'hiding,' 'scared,' and 'sick,' which fit the context well. However, there are some noticeable grammatical errors that you can work on. A key area for improvement is using the correct pronouns. You wrote 'I was a little fox' and 'I was white and beautiful' when you meant to refer to the fox, which should be 'It was a little fox' and 'It was white and beautiful.' This is a common point for Thai learners to work on, as the structure for referring to things and animals can differ in Thai. Another area is the past tense; you wrote 'somethin' instead of 'something,' and 'when the doctor done everything we drive back home' should be 'when the doctor **had done** everything, we **drove** back home.' Focusing on these specific areas will really help your sentences become much more accurate and clear for your reader. Practising your past tense verbs and checking your pronouns carefully will make a significant difference to your writing.	\N	\N	2025-09-18
29769	2025-09-21 17:07:02.089364+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and i arrived at school yesterday morning,we saw something incredible!, We saw a man who raise five kitten and three cat .He was giving them food water and milk for them to eat, we think he is a very kind people to raise animal that much, and next we saw him putting them to sleep and he smile at them but soon one kitten ran away so we help him find the kitten after a long time we finally found to kitten. Finally we go to him and return it and ask him if we could play with them he say yes and they were so cute when we play with them.	115	11.0	It is really lovely to see you putting your imagination to work and crafting a story for your writing task. You have a clear sense of narrative, and it's wonderful to read about your helpful nature in finding the lost kitten. Keep up this spirit of storytelling. 👍 To help your stories truly come alive for your readers, you could focus on adding a little more descriptive detail and making sure your sentences flow smoothly from one to the next. That way, your readers will feel even more connected to your adventures. 🚀<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for your next writing task:**<br>*   Remember to use capital letters for the first letter of a new sentence and for proper nouns (like names).<br>*   Always leave a space after a comma or a full stop to make your writing clear and easy to read.<br>*   Try to think of different ways to connect your ideas instead of always using 'and.'	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	3.0	Posh, you did a great job starting your story with the sentence you were given, and it was lovely to read about the man with the kittens and cats. It's a sweet detail to describe the man giving them food and milk. To make your stories even more exciting, you could try to develop the 'something incredible' part a bit more. For example, when you first saw the man, what were your immediate thoughts or feelings? Were you surprised, curious, or excited? Adding these details helps the reader to feel the same sense of wonder that you did. Think about extending the 'incredible' moment before moving on to the next action. You touched on the man being 'kind,' but what made seeing him so 'incredible' at school? Developing this initial reaction will really elevate your storytelling. 🌟	3.0	You have a good understanding of how to tell a story with a beginning, middle, and end. The events in your story follow each other in a logical order, which helps the reader to understand what is happening. To make your story even more engaging, try to use more descriptive words that paint a clearer picture for your reader. For instance, instead of just saying 'cute,' you could describe *how* the kittens were cute—were they tiny, playful, or cuddly? Thinking about the emotions you want your reader to feel will help you choose words that grab their attention and make them want to read on. ✍️	3.0	Your story is easy to follow because you use words like 'and next' and 'Finally' to link your ideas together. This is a good start, Posh. To make your writing even better, you could try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of always saying 'and,' you could try 'then,' 'after that,' 'meanwhile,' or 'as a result.' Also, thinking about breaking your story into smaller paragraphs when a new idea or event begins can help your readers follow the flow of your narrative even more easily. This helps your ideas connect more smoothly, like putting together puzzle pieces. 🧩	2.0	You use everyday words that help to communicate your ideas. However, there are a few areas in your grammar that you can work on to make your writing clearer. For instance, you wrote 'my friend and i,' but the pronoun 'I' should always be a capital letter when referring to yourself. Also, remember to put a space after a comma and a full stop, like 'morning, we' should be 'morning, we.' Another important point to watch out for is when you are talking about one of something versus many. For example, you said 'five kitten' when it should be 'five kittens' (plural). Similarly, 'three cat' should be 'three cats' and 'raise animal' should be 'raise animals.' Many Thai learners sometimes miss these small but important plural endings, so it is a good idea to double-check these in your writing. Focusing on these small adjustments will help your writing become much more polished and professional. ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-21
29584	2025-09-21 16:45:43.876721+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! We noticed a poster on a school board about international English website for young people. You need to write an article about a festival that you and your family celebrate. We consulted together and decided to sign in. I am the one who fascinate in writing an article so it seems interesting! I went home and thought about what should I write for severals minutes, and I dicided to write about christmas festival! Me and my family always have a great time together. Like last year, we went to our grandparents' house. We went to shopping street, walked along stream's side, and enjoyed our meals together! It was a wonderful experience, may be I could write it down in an article	135	8.0	Hi Yaya, you've shown some creative thinking in your writing, and it's lovely to see your enthusiasm for festivals like Christmas. 🎉 To really make your stories pop, let's focus on sticking to the task and building your amazing ideas into a clear and exciting narrative. Keep up the great work. You have a lot of potential to write engaging stories.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   ✔️ Carefully read the task instructions to make sure your writing directly answers what's being asked.<br>*   ✔️ Practice using past tenses consistently to describe events that have already happened.<br>*   ✔️ Think about how you can use paragraphs to group your ideas and make your writing easier to read.	A2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	1.0	Yaya, you started your writing with the exact sentence you were given, which is an excellent start for a story. Well done! 😊 However, for a story, the "something incredible" needs to be an actual event that happens, not a poster about a writing competition. It seems like you then moved on to thinking about writing an article about Christmas, which isn't quite what the story task asked for. For example, if you see an incredible poster, you could imagine it showing a lost treasure map or a secret school event, and then your story would describe what happened next because of *that*. Always double-check what the task is asking for—is it a story about an event, or an article about a topic? This will help you make sure all your wonderful ideas fit the task perfectly.	1.0	You show that you can share ideas, but for a story, we want to make sure the reader is completely absorbed in the adventure from beginning to end. ✍️ Because your writing shifted from an "incredible" sighting to planning an article, it made it difficult for your reader to stay engaged in a single story. When you write a story, try to keep a clear narrative path. For instance, if you start with something incredible, continue to describe what happens because of that incredible thing, how you and your friend felt, and how it all concluded. Imagine you're telling a friend a really exciting piece of news—you'd want to keep them hooked until the very end.	3.0	You connected your ideas quite well, Yaya, and it's easy to follow your thought process from seeing the poster to thinking about Christmas. Good job! 👍 Even without using separate paragraphs, your ideas flowed in a clear sequence for the points you wanted to make. To help your reader even more, try using paragraphs to introduce new ideas or changes in your story. For example, you could have one paragraph for seeing the poster, another for deciding to write, and then another for your Christmas memories. This helps to group your thoughts and makes your writing look more organised on the page. In English writing, we often use new paragraphs to signal a new idea or a shift in time or place.	3.0	Yaya, you used many everyday words that were suitable for your topics, and it's wonderful that you tried to use more interesting sentence structures, like when you said, "the one who fascinate in writing." That shows you are thinking about how to connect ideas, which is excellent! 🌟 To make your writing even clearer, let's look at a few things.<br><br>First, when you wanted to say you were very interested in writing, "who fascinate" should be "who **is fascinated by** writing" or "who **loves** writing." This helps to express your feeling correctly. For example, instead of "I fascinate in writing," you could say, "I **am fascinated by** writing" or "I **really enjoy** writing."<br><br>Next, try to be consistent with your verb tenses, especially when you are telling a story about things that happened in the past. For example, you wrote, "I **dicided** to write about Christmas festival! ...Like last year, we **went**..." Here, "dicided" is a spelling mistake (it should be "decided"). When you talk about past events, use past tense verbs. So, it would be "I **decided** to write... Last year, we **went**..."<br><br>Also, a small point for formal writing: while "Me and my family" is common in everyday speech, for written tasks, it's generally better to say "My family and I." It's a subtle difference, but it shows strong command of English grammar in formal contexts.<br><br>Finally, be careful with small words like "a," "an," and "the." For example, you wrote "a school board about international English website." It would be better to say "**an** international English website" (because 'international' starts with a vowel sound) or "the international English website" if it's a specific one. Similarly, "shopping street" often needs "a" or "the" before it, like "we went to **the** shopping street." In Thai, you might not use these small words in the same way, so it's a common area for English learners to practice, and focusing on them will make a big difference in how clear your writing is.	\N	\N	2025-09-21
28758	2025-08-29 16:00:01.263008+00	2026	Free time Activities	Great activities to doing their favorite activities in the time. So in every country like Thailand, teens in Thailand mostly like to play games in their free Time but there are some better activities to do, so these are two the best activities to do for Teenagers. There are paying sports and studying.<br><br><br>Let's start with this activity that are many admire for teens is playing sports, this activity is really wonderful because this activity let us to workout, to make us healty, not being sick esaliy. Example, for those sports that I recommed like badminton, football, swimming, basketball and other, acully every type of sport are good. I recommed this activity really much and this activity is popular because it's like doing excersie.<br><br><br>Beside, studying it's literly a really improtant activity, doing this activity in the free time it's really useful for the time that you had spend, it's will be really good for you to study in the class too. Because teens mostly study in middle school or high school. This activity seems hard but if you trying to do it, the solution would helpful a lot, so that why this activity is popular and many people recommed it.<br><br><br>In summary, I think not all the teens in Thailand like to do these activity but some of here are the better activity for teens in Thailand, instaded to play games,	232	12.0	<p>Dear Mymind<br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Free time Activities'. You wrote 232 words and achieved B2. You successfully identified two activities and discussed their popularity, which is a good start.</p>	B2	Writing	CA	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	<p>All content is relevant to the task, and you did identify two free time activities. You also explained why they are popular in Thailand and what they involve, which fully informs the reader. The essay is complete and meets the word count requirement. Studying is not typically a free time activity, but your justification for its importance makes it acceptable within the context of your essay.<br>To Improve: For a B2 level, try to develop your ideas even more. Instead of just stating facts, try to explore the deeper impact or benefits of these activities.<br>Weakness: "There are paying sports and studying." While these are valid points, the introduction could be more engaging and directly link to the essay's purpose.<br>Improved: "For many Thai teenagers, free time is a precious commodity, often filled with digital entertainment. However, beyond screen time, two profoundly beneficial activities stand out for their positive impact on youth: engaging in sports and dedicating time to academic pursuits."<br>Explanation: A more developed introduction sets a clearer context for your essay and immediately informs the reader of your main arguments, making the essay more cohesive and sophisticated.</p>	3.0	<p>You used an appropriate tone for an article and managed to communicate your straightforward ideas. However, there were some shifts between formal and informal language, which means the register wasn't always consistent. This can sometimes make it harder to keep the target reader fully engaged and clear about the essay's intended audience.<br>To Improve: Maintain a consistent register throughout your writing. Decide if you want to be more formal for a general audience or slightly more informal if addressing peers, and stick to it.<br>Weakness: "Let's start with this activity that are many admire for teens..." The use of "Let's start" is a bit informal for an essay.<br>Improved: "One highly admired activity among teenagers is playing sports..."<br>Explanation: Using more formal phrasing helps maintain a consistent register suitable for an essay and enhances the overall professionalism of your writing.</p>	3.0	<p>You organized your ideas into four paragraphs, which helps the reader follow your main points. However, the introductory paragraph didn't clearly state your main argument (thesis statement), and the topic sentences in the body paragraphs could be stronger and connect more explicitly to a central idea. Your conclusion was a bit brief and could have done more to summarise and restate your main points. While you used some basic linking words, expanding your use of cohesive devices would make the text more coherent for a B2 level.<br>To Improve: Start your introduction with a clear thesis statement. Ensure each body paragraph begins with a strong topic sentence that clearly relates to your thesis. In your conclusion, use a transition and thoroughly restate your thesis and main supporting ideas.<br>Weakness: "There are paying sports and studying." and "In summary, I think not all the teens in Thailand like to do these activity but some of here are the better activity for teens in Thailand, instaded to play games," The introduction and conclusion are a bit underdeveloped.<br>Improved: "These activities, particularly playing sports and engaging in studying, offer significant benefits that extend beyond mere leisure. They provide avenues for physical development and mental growth, making them superior alternatives to sedentary pastimes." (For thesis) and "In conclusion, while not every teenager may choose these paths, the profound advantages of sports and academic study present a compelling case for their integration into the daily lives of Thai youth, fostering well-rounded development." (For conclusion)<br>Explanation: A clear thesis statement in the introduction guides the reader, and a comprehensive conclusion effectively wraps up your arguments, making your essay feel complete and well-structured.</p>	2.0	<p>You used good vocabulary related to the topic, but there are several recurring grammar patterns that affect clarity.<br>Problem: You wrote: "it's will be really good"<br>Correction: "it will be really good"<br>Explanation: This combines "it is" (it's) with "will be," which is grammatically incorrect. In English, you use either 'it is' or 'it will be', but not both. This might happen because Thai grammar does not always explicitly distinguish between states (is) and future actions (will be) in the same way, leading to a blending of these forms in English.<br><br>Problem: You wrote: "but if you trying to do it"<br>Correction: "but if you are trying to do it"<br>Explanation: In English, for actions happening now or continuously, we use the structure 'be verb + -ing' (e.g., 'you are trying'). In Thai, verbs do not change form in this way, and an auxiliary verb like 'be' is not always required for continuous actions, which can lead to its omission in English.<br><br>Problem: You wrote: "to do these activity"<br>Correction: "to do these activities" or "to do this activity"<br>Explanation: "These" is used for plural nouns (e.g., these books), and "this" is for singular nouns (e.g., this book). "Activity" is singular, so it should be "this activity" or "these activities" if referring to more than one. In Thai, nouns do not have different singular and plural forms like in English, which causes confusion when choosing determiners like 'this' or 'these'.<br><br>YOUR ACTION PLAN<br>1. Complete all parts of writing tasks and develop each point fully<br>2. Maintain consistent formal register throughout your writing<br>3. Practice using different linking words to connect ideas<br>4. Review subject-verb agreement rules for present tense</p>	\N	\N	2025-08-29
28936	2025-11-28 10:04:25.755774+00	2026	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. At first, after he pressed the button he need to choose the future or the passed that he want to go So he choose the passed.<br><br><br>After that, Sam thinking about to change the passed, So he went to the old house in the passed that he lived and he stayed there too many hours but he didn't know that "time travelling" have a big problem that is called "time limit" that if you live in the passed or the future in time travelling world. too many hours you will get stuck easily.<br><br><br>A little while later, Sam want to go back to the present So he grabbed a time travelling and pressed the button but it doesn't work So he very confused and pressed the button many times and then that time he realized that time travelling have a big problem again.<br><br><br>Sam need to fix it very quick but it doesn't work So he called his brother names Pea, who love to created something like him, help him fix it.<br><br><br>In the end, Sam and Pea finally finished and time travelling problem is gone! So what Sam thinking is he will tested it first before use it every time if he create the invention.	213	13.0	Hello Eng, 👋<br><br>Time travel is such a cool topic! ⏳ I really liked your idea about the 'time limit' and the danger of getting stuck. Bringing in Sam's brother, Pea, to help fix the machine was a nice touch that added a personal connection.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you polish your writing even further. 📋<br>1. <b>Past vs. Passed!</b> 📝 This is a common mistake! 'Passed' is a verb (he passed the ball). You meant 'the past' (history).<br>2. <b>Tenses!</b> 🔙 When you tell a story, use the past tense. 'He choose' -> 'He chose'.<br>3. <b>Feelings!</b> 🎭 Tell us how Sam felt! Was he scared? Relieved?	B1	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	5.0	The plot about the time limit was very clever! 🧠 It gave the story real tension.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Details!</b> 👁️ You said he went to an 'old house'. Was it his house? What did it look like? Details help us see the story.<br>2. <b>Character!</b> 👤 Who is Pea? Is he older or younger? Adding a tiny detail about him would make him more real.	3.0	I understood the urgency of the problem perfectly. 🏃‍♂️<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Emotion!</b> 😢 Sam seemed very calm for someone stuck in time! Describe his panic to make it more exciting.<br>2. <b>Dialogue!</b> 🗣️ Maybe add a line of dialogue between Sam and Pea to show them working together.	3.0	You wrapped up the story nicely with 'In the end'. 🎬<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Connectors!</b> 🔗 You used 'So' a lot. Try other words like 'Therefore', 'Because of this', or 'As a result'.<br>2. <b>Paragraphing!</b> 🧱 Your paragraphs were logical. Keep it up!	2.0	You told a complete story, well done! 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections to help you:<br>1. <b>Word Choice!</b> 📝 'The passed' -> 'The <b>past</b>'.<br>2. <b>Verbs!</b> 🛠️ 'He choose' -> 'He <b>chose</b>'. 'Thinking about to change' -> 'Thinking about <b>changing</b>'.<br>3. <b>Infinitives!</b> 🔄 'Love to created' -> 'Loved to <b>create</b>'.<br>4. <b>Articles!</b> 🐜 'Grabbed a time travelling' -> 'Grabbed <b>the</b> time machine'.<br>5. <b>Tense!</b> 🔙 'Problem is gone' -> 'Problem <b>was</b> gone'.	\N	\N	2025-11-28
33957	2025-11-28 10:04:25.755774+00	2026	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. Sam was a professor from a famous university in USA. He had been known as a person who knew the secret of time. At first, he was interested about space of time and he started to do some research about it. He did some research for the whole year and finally he created the time stop machine.<br><br><br>He thought that his invention was successful, but something went wrong. After he pressed the button, the inventor made a strange noise and then he saw that somethings in house were older.<br><br><br>A little while later, he opened the door and he knew that the time was row back to the World War 2. Many soldier tried to kill him, so he needed to hide from them. He realised that he should go back to his laboratory.<br><br><br>Finally, he found his laboratory and he tried to pressed the button to stop, but it didn't work. He was stressed and he though that he couldn't go back.<br><br><br>His friend, a professor name Flip, made a loud noise to make hi woke up. Suddenly, he knew that the all of the story was his dream. As a result, he learned that the time stop machine couldn't be build in real life.	196	16.0	Hello Peptine, 👋<br><br>What an intriguing story about the 'time stop machine'! ⏱️ I really liked the idea of the invention going wrong and sending Sam back to World War 2, only for it to be a dream. It shows great creativity in handling a classic sci-fi trope.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you polish your writing even further. 📋<br>1. <b>Verb Forms!</b> 📝 'Build' vs 'Built' can be tricky. 'Couldn't be build' -> 'Couldn't be <b>built</b>'.<br>2. <b>Word Choice!</b> 📖 'Row back' isn't correct here. 'Time rolled back' is what you mean. (Like a clock hand!).<br>3. <b>Show Feelings!</b> 😢 You said Sam was 'stressed'. How did he show that stress? A furrowed brow? Pacing? Add details!	B1+	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	5.0	The concept of a time-stop machine and the dream sequence was very creative and relevant! 💡<br>The World War 2 setting added an exciting element.<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Elaborate!</b> 🌍 You mentioned 'many soldier tried to kill him'. Describe a quick scene – what did they look like? What weapons did they have? Make it more vivid.<br>2. <b>Pacing!</b> ⏱️ The transition into the dream felt a bit sudden. Maybe a sentence before he woke up to hint it wasn't real.	4.0	Your story was easy to follow, and the narrative flow was clear despite the time jumps. 🚀<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Engagement!</b> 🗣️ Try to make the reader feel more directly connected to Sam's emotions. Was he excited by his discovery?<br>2. <b>Impact!</b> 💥 The moment he realizes it's a dream could be made even more dramatic. How does Flip make a 'loud noise'?	4.0	You used good linking words like 'At first', 'A little while later', and 'Finally' to structure your narrative effectively. 🚧<br><br>To improve:<br>1. <b>Variety!</b> 🔗 While your organisation is good, try to vary your linking words and sentence structures more. 'However' or 'Meanwhile' could add more flair.<br>2. <b>Paragraph Unity!</b> 🧱 Each paragraph should flow logically from the previous one, enhancing the overall cohesion of your story.	3.0	You have some great ideas in your writing! 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections to help you:<br>1. <b>Word Choice!</b> 📝 'Time was row back' -> 'Time was <b>rolled back</b>'.<br>2. <b>Verb Forms!</b> 🛠️ 'Couldn't be build' -> 'Couldn't be <b>built</b>'.<br>3. <b>Pronouns!</b> 🗣️ 'Make hi woke up' -> 'Make <b>him</b> wake up'.<br>4. <b>Articles!</b> 🐜 'Professor from a famous university' -> 'Professor from <b>a</b> famous university'.<br>5. <b>Consistency!</b> 📖 Ensure consistent spacing after punctuation and with hyphens (e.g., 'World War 2' not 'World War 2.').	\N	\N	2025-11-28
