, and I'm sure with a bit of practice, your writing will develop wonderfully.<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement**<br>✔️ Before you finish writing, read through your essay and pay close attention to the small grammar rules for plurals and verb agreements. For example, if you are talking about "many pets," remember they "are" sweet, not "is" sweet.<br>✔️ Try to think of a few different ways to connect your ideas between sentences and paragraphs using words like "consequently," "additionally," or "however," to make your writing flow more smoothly.<br>✔️ Proofread carefully for any spelling errors; it is a small step that can make a big difference to how polished your writing appears.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	You've done a really good job of addressing the exam question, Grace, and you've given some lovely reasons why people should have pets, which is exactly what the task asked for. You discussed how pets can be friends and understand feelings, which makes for a very informative read. 🐾 To make your content even stronger and reach that top score, you could try to develop each of your reasons a little more deeply, perhaps with more detailed examples or by exploring slightly different aspects of why they are comforting. For instance, when you mention cuteness, you could expand on how that specific appearance links to a sense of calm or happiness. Sometimes, Thai learners might focus on making a point quickly, but adding a little more detail can really make your arguments robust.	3.0	You've generally managed to get your message across well, and your aim to persuade the reader about the benefits of pets is quite clear. You've used the structure of an essay effectively to communicate your straightforward ideas. 😊 To lift this score, you might want to consider the overall tone and make sure it remains consistently semi-formal throughout, which is usually expected for this type of essay. Sometimes, phrases like "not really make sense but many people like to think like that" can sound a little less formal. Try to rephrase these thoughts to maintain a more consistent academic style, ensuring your ideas are communicated with clarity and conviction for your target reader.	3.0	Your essay has a clear beginning, middle, and end, which is a great foundation, Grace. You've used some simple linking words to connect your sentences, like "Because" and "In conclusion," which helps the reader follow your main points. 👍 To improve this further, think about using a wider range of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just "And," you could use "Furthermore" or "In addition" to introduce new points, making your writing more varied and sophisticated. Also, consider how each sentence and paragraph flows into the next. Ensuring every transition is smooth will help your whole essay feel more interconnected and easier to read. Thai learners sometimes use simpler linking words, and expanding this range can really elevate your writing.	3.0	You use many everyday words appropriately, and it's easy to understand what you are trying to say, Grace. You've tried to use some more complex sentence structures, which is a good step forward. However, there are a few areas where focusing on grammar could make your writing much clearer and more precise. For example, I noticed a few instances of subject-verb agreement, such as "no one are there" instead of "no one is there," and "pets species are" instead of "pet species is" or "many pet species are." 🧐 Also, keep an eye out for correct verb forms like "walk forward to you and sitting" which should be "walk forward to you and sit." And do a final check for spelling, like "fasinating" instead of "fascinating," and "apperiance" instead of "appearance." These small adjustments will significantly refine your communication. Often, Thai learners might transfer grammatical structures from their first language, so it's a good idea to pay extra attention to English-specific rules like subject-verb agreement and plural forms.	\N	\N	2025-09-13
