ions during the visit, perhaps a moment where you or your friend felt surprised or tried to get closer. Think about how the visit unfolded moment by moment, like a short film playing out for your reader. 🎬	2.0	You've managed to share your main ideas effectively, and we understand what happened. That's a great start. However, to really grab and keep your reader's attention throughout your story, try to think about how a storyteller guides their audience. You can do this by varying your sentence beginnings, making sure your conclusion feels like a natural wrap-up rather than an abrupt ending, and perhaps including a short, impactful sentence to start each new point. Imagine you're telling your story to a friend. You might pause or change your voice to highlight different parts. Your writing can do the same. 😊	2.0	Your ideas are all connected, which is a good foundation. However, presenting your entire story in one continuous block makes it a bit challenging for the reader to follow the different parts of your experience. Imagine a book without any paragraph breaks—it would be very hard to read. Each time you introduce a new idea, such as *why* Emma Watson was invited or *what* activities she did, it's a good opportunity to start a new paragraph. This helps your reader see the structure of your thoughts and makes your writing much clearer and more pleasant to read. Using linking words like "First," "Next," or "After that" can also help guide your reader from one event to the next. 📝	3.0	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit your story well, like "actor" and "film." It's clear you're building a strong vocabulary. We've noticed a few areas where we can really make your writing sparkle. Firstly, with **capitalisation**, remember that names like "Hermione Granger" and titles of films like "Harry Potter Film" always start with a capital letter, as do the first words of every sentence. Also, words like "school" generally don't need a capital letter unless they are part of a specific name (e.g., "Hogwarts School"). Secondly, **spelling** is an area where a little extra care will make a big difference. For instance, words like "surprisingly" (not "surprisely"), "speech" (not "speece"), and "autograph" (not "auto ghrape") are common words that you can easily check. Many Thai learners sometimes face challenges with capitalisation and spelling because the rules in English can be quite different from Thai writing conventions, where capital letters aren't used in the same way. Keeping a small notebook of commonly misspelled words or using a dictionary when you're unsure will really help you improve. Lastly, watch out for grammatical structures like "actor to play"; a better way to say this would be "actor *who played*." Keep practicing, and your language will grow even stronger. 💪	\N	\N	2025-09-21
29643	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, I saw the golden beaches of the island below. The enormous landscape that fully cover with life and the gigantic shadow of the plane flew across the sky. Well, that used to be the first time, when I finally descent to Thailand. Firstly, I'm the big fans of travelling and our destination is Phuket. The first thing I've noticed is people here. They're always smile and cheerful with others people and foods here is extremely delicious, the flavour of sour and sweet is perfectly intercept. Foods here is easy to find and it also surprisingly cheap, so that was the best trip ever.	111	12.0	Aat, you started your story with a strong opening image — <em>the golden beaches of the island below</em> — that immediately puts the reader in the scene with you. That's a good instinct for storytelling. To reach Band 5, your story needs a clear arc: a beginning that sets up a problem or goal, a middle with some action or change, and an ending that resolves it. Right now your story describes impressions of Thailand but doesn't really go anywhere. Let's look at each part of your writing so you can revise toward that Band 5.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Content. A Band 5 writer tells a complete story with a clear beginning, middle, and end, where every detail moves the plot forward. Your story starts well but then becomes a list of observations about people and food that don't build a storyline.</p><ul><li><em>As the plane flew lower, I saw the golden beaches of the island below.</em> This is a strong opening. You set the scene and the moment. To build a story from here, add what you felt or what you hoped would happen next. For example: <strong>As the plane flew lower, I saw the golden beaches of the island below, and my heart raced with excitement.</strong> That small addition starts a feeling that can drive the story.</li><li><em>People here is always smile and cheerful with others people and foods here is extremely delicious.</em> This describes the place, but it doesn't advance a story. Instead, turn it into an event: <strong>When I finally stepped onto the beach, a local vendor handed me a plate of spicy papaya salad. I took one bite and my mouth was on fire — but I couldn't stop eating.</strong> Now you have a small moment that a reader can follow.</li><li><em>so that was the best trip ever.</em> This is an ending, but it's too general. A Band 5 ending connects back to the beginning or shows how you changed. Try: <strong>As the plane lifted off again, I looked down at the golden beaches and knew I would come back someday.</strong> That closes the circle with your opening sentence.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer holds the reader's attention with a consistent storytelling voice from start to finish. Your voice shifts between descriptive narrative and informal comment, which breaks the story feeling.</p><ul><li><em>Well, that used to be the first time, when I finally descent to Thailand.</em> The word <em>Well</em> sounds like spoken conversation, not written storytelling. Remove it and rephrase: <strong>That was the first time I had ever descended into Thailand.</strong> Now the tone stays consistent with the descriptive opening.</li><li><em>Firstly, I'm the big fans of travelling and our destination is Phuket.</em> The word <em>Firstly</em> belongs in a list or an essay, not a story. In a story, you can just say: <strong>I've always been a huge fan of travelling, so when we landed in Phuket, I was ready for anything.</strong> This keeps the narrative moving forward.</li><li>Your story tells us what you saw and ate, but it doesn't make us feel anything about the experience. To hold a reader's attention, include one moment of surprise, a small problem, or a change in feeling. For example: <strong>I expected the food to be mild, but the first bite of tom yum soup burned my tongue. After that, I was hooked.</strong></li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer uses a clear sequence of events and varied linking words so the reader never gets lost. Your ideas are in a logical order — plane, arrival, people, food — but the connections between them are repetitive and mechanical.</p><ul><li><em>As the plane flew lower... Well, that used to be the first time... Firstly, I'm the big fans...</em> You use <em>Well</em> and <em>Firstly</em> to move between ideas, but these feel like filler words. Instead, use time words that build a timeline: <strong>As the plane flew lower... When I finally stepped off the plane... Later that day...</strong> This creates a clear before-and-after structure.</li><li>Your sentences are mostly short and connected with <em>and</em> or <em>so</em>. Try varying your sentence openings. Instead of <em>Foods here is easy to find and it also surprisingly cheap</em>, try: <strong>Not only was the food easy to find, but it was also surprisingly cheap.</strong> That one change makes the sentence feel more connected to what came before.</li><li>You used <code>&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;</code> to separate the opening from the rest, which counts as a paragraph break. For a story, consider breaking it into three paragraphs: one for the plane descent and arrival, one for the first impressions of Phuket, and one for the food experience and ending. That gives the reader a visual map of your story's three parts.</li></ul>	3.0	<p>You're at 3/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls subject-verb agreement and noun plurals with few errors. The main rule that explains most of your errors is: <strong>the verb must match the subject in number</strong> — singular subjects take singular verbs, plural subjects take plural verbs. Let's fix the patterns in your writing.</p><ul><li><em>The enormous landscape that fully cover with life</em> → <strong>The enormous landscape that was fully covered with life</strong>. <em>Landscape</em> is singular, so the verb should be <em>was covered</em>, not <em>cover</em>. Also, you need the past participle <em>covered</em> here because the landscape is being covered by life.</li><li><em>I'm the big fans of travelling</em> → <strong>I'm a big fan of travelling</strong>. <em>Fan</em> is singular because you are one person. <em>Fans</em> is plural. Also, <em>big fans</em> needs the article <em>a</em> before it.</li><li><em>people here is always smile</em> → <strong>people here are always smiling</strong>. <em>People</em> is plural, so the verb is <em>are</em>, not <em>is</em>. Also, after <em>are</em> you need the <em>-ing</em> form: <em>smiling</em>.</li><li><em>foods here is extremely delicious</em> → <strong>the food here is extremely delicious</strong>. <em>Food</em> is usually uncountable, so we don't add <em>-s</em>. And <em>food</em> is singular, so <em>is</em> is correct here — but the subject should be <em>the food</em>, not <em>foods</em>.</li><li><em>the flavour of sour and sweet is perfectly intercept</em> → <strong>the flavours of sour and sweet were perfectly balanced</strong>. <em>Intercept</em> is not the right word here. You probably mean <em>balanced</em> or <em>combined</em>. Also, <em>flavour</em> can be singular or plural depending on what you mean. Since you're talking about two flavours, use the plural <em>flavours</em> and the plural verb <em>were</em>.</li></ul><p>Here's a self-check you can apply to every sentence you write: <strong>Find the subject. Is it singular or plural? Now check the verb. Does it match?</strong> If you do this for every sentence, most of these errors will disappear.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
