ense consistency. Keep reflecting on your experiences; your voice is interesting, and with a little more practice, your writing will become even more impactful.	B1+	Writing	Formative	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	Racing, you effectively address the topic of fitting in by drawing on your personal experiences and identifying reasons for your past difficulties, such as impulsive behavior and a different way of thinking. You use examples involving your friends to illustrate these points. However, your essay significantly exceeds the recommended word count of 140-190 words for this task (your essay is 280 words). In a timed exam setting, this would likely mean you have included too much detail or some content that is less central to fully informing the target reader within the expected length. Practicing writing concisely within word limits is important for exam success.	2.0	You use the style of a personal reflection to share your experiences with fitting in. You successfully communicate straightforward ideas about your feelings and challenges. However, at times, the clarity is affected by phrasing that could be more precise, which might make it harder for the reader to fully grasp your meaning or hold their attention consistently. For instance, the description of "voices talking overlapping each other" is a bit unclear and could be expressed more straightforwardly to ensure the target reader understands the intended idea without difficulty.	2.0	You structure your essay around your personal journey with fitting in, moving from past struggles to present understanding. The ideas are generally connected, but the organization could be improved with clearer paragraph breaks to separate distinct points. For example, creating new paragraphs for each main reason you struggled to fit in or for each example you provide would make the text easier to follow. Using a greater variety and more accurate application of linking words and phrases would also help create a smoother flow between your sent