r><b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "the one who have the cloth will called E-mon"<br><b>Correction:</b> "the one who IS left holding the cloth will be called E-mon"<br><b>Explanation:</b> 👉 We dont know who is calling someone else "E-mon", so you have to use a passive structure. Also remember that "who" refers to one person, so we use "has" instead of "have."<br><b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "freetimes activities"<br><b>Correction:</b> "free time activities"<br><b>Explanation:</b> 👉 In English, we write "free time" as two separate words when it functions as a compound noun describing activities.<br><b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "the player that nearest E-mon"<br><b>Correction:</b> "the player who is nearest to E-mon"<br><b>Explanation:</b> 👉 We use "who" for people and need the verb "is" to complete the comparative structure "nearest to."<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-25
