hey play but not fully why it's a popular choice for their free time beyond having a phone.<br>✨ Improved: "This widespread popularity stems from the fact that nearly every teenager now owns a smartphone, making gaming an easily accessible and highly social way to unwind and connect with friends, fostering community even online."<br>💡 Explanation: Expanding on the 'why' makes your points more convincing and ensures the reader understands the cultural significance or personal relevance of your choices, making the essay feel complete.</p>	3.0	<p>You used an appropriate tone for an article, but the register shifts between formal and informal language in places, which can confuse the reader about the intended audience. For instance, using "most of teenagers" and "a lot of them" feels less formal than other parts of your essay.<br>👉 To Improve: Maintain a consistent register throughout your writing - aim for a semi-formal or formal tone, which is typical for an article targeting a general audience.<br>❌ Weakness: You wrote: "To begin, most of teenagers play games in their free times." The phrasing "most of teenagers" is slightly less formal.<br>✨ Improved: "Firstly, it is evident that a significant proportion of adolescents engage in gaming during their leisure time."<br>💡 Explanation: Keeping a consistent tone and register throughout your article helps your reader understand who you are writing for and makes your argument more authoritative.</p>	3.0	<p>You organized your ideas into paragraphs, which helps the reader follow your main points. You also used some good linking words like "In addition," "Furthermore," and "However" to connect your ideas. However, your conclusion started with "In short, teens who play games will have more connection with their friends. So, they won't feel depress but..." but then it stopped suddenly, leaving the reader without a clear summary or final thought.<br>👉 To Improve: Ensure all parts of your essay, especially the conclusion, are complete. A strong conclusion should summarise your main points and offer a final thought.<br>❌ Weakness: Your conclusion stopped suddenly: "In short, teens who play games will have more connection with their friends. So, they won't feel depress but..."<br>✨ Improved: "In short, while both gaming and studying are prominent free time activities for Thai teens, it is clear that balancing academic pursuits with social interactions, perhaps through engaging activities like gaming, is essential for their overall well-being and sense of community."<br>💡 Explanation: A complete conclusion, with a transition and a clear summary of your main points, helps to leave a lasting impression on the reader and makes your essay feel well-rounded and complete.</p>	3.0	<p>You used good vocabulary related to the topic, but there are some recurring grammar patterns that affect clarity, particularly with articles.<br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "most of teenagers"<br>✨ Correction: "most teenagers" or "most of the teenagers"<br>💡 Explanation: In English, when you use "most of" with a plural countable noun, you often need "the" (e.g., most of the students). However, you can also just use "most" directly before the plural noun (e.g., most students). Thai doesn't have articles like "the" or "a/an," so it's common for Thai learners to omit them or use them incorrectly.<br><br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "most of children"<br>✨ Correction: "most children" or "most of the children"<br>💡 Explanation: Similar to the previous example, "children" is a plural countable noun. You can say "most children" or "most of the children." Thai grammar does not use articles, which often leads to this type of error for Thai speakers learning English.<br><br>❌ Problem: You wrote: "to have connection with their friends"<br>✨ Correction: "to have a connection with their friends" or "to have connections with their friends"<br>💡 Explanation: "Connection" is a countable noun, and in this context, it needs an article ('a') or to be plural ('connections'). In Thai, nouns don't typically change form for countability in the same way, which can make it tricky to know when to use articles in English.</p>	\N	\N	2025-08-30
