raph. Think about how each sentence builds on the last. For example, you could use words like "consequently," "furthermore," or "in addition" to add variety. Sometimes, in Thai, sentences can be a bit more direct in their linking, so it’s a good challenge to work on those smoother connections in English.	2.0	You’ve used a good range of everyday words to express your ideas, and your overall message comes across well. That’s a real positive! 👍 However, there are a few areas in your grammar that we can work on to make your writing even clearer and more accurate.<br>\nFirstly, watch out for **subject-verb agreement**. For example, when you wrote "friend is more effective," because "friend" is singular, the verb "is" matches. But if you’re talking about "friends" (plural), the verb needs to match too, so it should be "friends *are* more effective." This is a common one for many learners, as Thai grammar doesn't always have this kind of agreement.<br>\nSecondly, let's pay close attention to **articles** (a, an, the). Sometimes you left them out when they were needed, or used them in a way that felt a little off. For instance, you wrote "in choices of clothes and fashion" – it would be more natural to say "in *the* choices of clothes and fashion" or simply "in choices regarding clothes and fashion." Getting those little words right can make a big difference to how natural your English sounds!<br>\nFinally, try to make sure your **sentences are complete and fully developed**. Your final paragraph started a good point, but it was cut short. Making sure each sentence fully expresses an idea and connects clearly to the next one will really strengthen your writing. Sometimes, we might have an idea in our head but not quite get it all down on paper, so taking an extra moment to complete your thoughts will help.	\N	\N	2025-09-21
