e positive and challenging sides of having pets, which is exactly what the task asked for! 👍 However, to really make your article stand out, try to explain your ideas a bit more. For example, when you talk about advantages, instead of saying "It have lots of things that be advantages," you could explain *what* those advantages are. Perhaps pets teach you responsibility because you have to feed them every day, or they give you comfort when you're feeling down. Similarly, for disadvantages, think about concrete examples. Instead of just saying you feel "boring" when your pet sleeps, you could explain that pets need a lot of your time, which might mean you have less time for other hobbies, or that looking after them can be expensive. Adding these kinds of details will make your article much more informative and engaging for your readers!	2.0	You've shown that you can put together an article with an introduction and conclusion, which is a great start! 😊 To truly connect with your teenage audience in an international magazine, think about making your language a little more varied and engaging. Sometimes, repeating phrases like "Having pets it" can make your writing sound a bit flat. You could try using different sentence structures or asking rhetorical questions to draw your reader in. For instance, instead of "Having pets it also can make you life feel funny and happy," you might say, "What's better than coming home to a wagging tail or a purring cat to make your day brighter?" Focusing on clear and accurate grammar will also help your readers stay focused on your wonderful ideas.	3.0	You’ve made a good effort to organise your article with clear paragraphs for your introduction, advantages, disadvantages, and conclusion. That's a solid structure! 👏 To improve even further, focus on making sure your ideas flow smoothly from one sentence to the next within each paragraph, and between paragraphs. Using a variety of linking words can really help with this. For example, after discussing advantages, you could start your next paragraph with "On the other hand," or "However," to introduce the disadvantages clearly. This helps the reader follow your arguments easily and shows a sophisticated way of connecting your thoughts.	2.0	You use some everyday vocabulary well, like "love," "happy," and "friend." However, there are quite a few grammatical points that need some attention, LinLin. For instance, you often say "very goods" instead of "very good," or "to learns" instead of "to learn." Also, words like "kindy" should be "kind," and "relaxs" should be "relaxed" or "relaxing." When you talk about disadvantages, "disadvanted i things" is tricky; you could say "disadvantages, I think" or "the disadvantages are." Many Thai learners sometimes struggle with plurals and verb forms in English because the structures are different in Thai. For example, in Thai, you might not change a noun to show it's plural or change a verb to show tense as explicitly as in English. Regularly checking your sentences for correct plurals (like "shops" instead of "shop" when there's more than one) and making sure your verbs match the subject (e.g., "pets *are* good" not "pets *is* good") will make a big difference in how clearly your message comes across. Keep practicing, and these errors will become less noticeable! 💪	\N	\N	2025-09-22
