b of covering all the points asked in the question, Sun! You talked about clothes, studying, and even added your own thoughtful idea, which is excellent. You clearly understood what the question was asking, and your answer was relevant. To make this part even stronger next time, try to make sure your 'own idea' is a brand new choice that teenagers make, rather than a conclusion or a piece of advice. For example, you might have written about choices related to hobbies or future careers. Sometimes, we can interpret a point broadly, but for an essay, it's best to introduce a distinct new topic. 😊	2.0	You certainly got your main message across, and a reader could understand what you were trying to say. However, for an essay, we aim for a more formal and academic style, and sometimes the number of language errors made it a bit tricky to keep that formal tone throughout. It means the reader had to work a little harder than we'd ideally like. To improve this, focus on building your confidence with clearer sentence structures and practicing those formal linking words. English academic writing often uses specific phrases, which can be different from how we speak every day. 🤓	3.0	Your essay was well-organised into paragraphs, which is a really important step, Sun! Each paragraph generally focused on a different part of the question, which helps the reader follow your ideas. You also used some basic connecting words like 'But when' and 'I strongly belief that' to link your thoughts. To improve your score here, try using a wider variety of linking words and phrases. Think about how your sentences flow together within each paragraph and how one paragraph leads to the next. For instance, words like 'however', 'therefore', or 'in addition' can add a lovely smoothness to your writing. Sometimes, we rely on simpler connectors, and thinking about how ideas logically connect can make a big difference. 📚	2.0	You managed to get your points across, which is the main goal of writing, Sun! However, there were quite a few places where the grammar made it a bit difficult to understand your meaning clearly. Let's look at three key areas for you to work on:<br>1.  **Subject-verb agreement:** This means making sure your subject (who or what is doing the action) matches your verb (the action word). For example, if you say 'Most teenager,' the correct verb is 'wear,' not 'wears,' because 'teenager' here is meant to be plural. Similarly, 'parent' needs 'has' rather than 'have' when talking about one parent. Many Thai learners find this tricky because subject-verb agreement isn't as strict in Thai. A good trick is to circle the subject and the verb in each sentence and ask yourself, 'Do they match?' ✍️<br>2.  **Pluralisation:** English uses '-s' at the end of many words when you are talking about more than one of something. You used 'a cloth' when you meant 'clothes' (many pieces of clothing) and 'many teenager' instead of 'many teenagers.' Remember to add that 's' when you're referring to multiple items or people! 🧐<br>3.  **Prepositions:** These little words like 'in,' 'on,' 'at,' and 'with' can be very tricky in English. You wrote 'at fashion' when 'in fashion' would be more natural, and 'help them on their work' when 'help them with their work' sounds more native. The best way to get better at prepositions is to read a lot in English and notice how native speakers use them in different contexts. They can be very specific! 📖	\N	\N	2025-09-12
