ng requires a more serious and objective tone, avoiding casual language often used in speaking or informal messages.	3.0	You used basic linking words like "First of all," "Second reason," and "For the third reason" to connect your ideas. 👍<br><b>Problem:</b> Some of your sentences had a repetitive structure and could be linked more smoothly, for instance, starting new sentences with "Like my class mate" or "One of my friend."<br><b>Correction:</b> You could try: "Firstly, some students play games on their phones when teachers allow their use for work. For example, my classmate uses his mobile phone for playing games. Secondly, students may struggle to concentrate on their studies..."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Using a variety of sentence structures and more advanced linking words helps your writing flow better and makes it more engaging for the reader. It also helps to avoid starting sentences with phrases like "Like my...""	2.0	You used everyday words, but there were several mistakes with grammar and spelling. Keep practicing. 💪<br><b>Problem:</b> You often forgot to add 's' to verbs when talking about 'he' or 'it', for example, "he want" or "time need."<br><b>Correction:</b> It should be "he wants" and "time needs."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, verbs do not change for different subjects. In English, we add 's' to verbs when the subject is singular (he, she, it) in the present tense.	\N	\N	2025-08-22
