ense.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>The Fire!</b> 🔥 You mentioned your neighbour's house caught fire. Describing the flames or smoke a little more vividly would increase the drama.</li><li><b>Parent's Reaction!</b> 👨‍👩‍👧‍👦 You said your parents called emergency services. Did they look scared? Were they rushing? Adding details to their actions would make the scene more dynamic.</li>	4.0	You created a strong sense of atmosphere and successfully built tension throughout your narrative. ⛈️ The dream ending provided a clever resolution.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Smoother Transitions!</b> 🌊 The jump from the middle of the chaotic night to 'The next morning started in my room and I was on my bed again' is a bit abrupt. A sentence bridging these two events could improve the flow.</li>	4.0	Your story follows a clear chronological progression, from waking up to discovering the fire and then the dream revelation. 🧱 Your paragraph breaks are generally effective.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Varying Linkers!</b> 🔗 You used 'So' and 'Then' effectively. Try incorporating a wider range of transitional words (e.g., 'Subsequently', 'Meanwhile', 'As a result') to enhance the flow and sophistication of your writing.</li>	3.0	You used good everyday vocabulary related to home and emergencies, like 'thunder', 'kitchen', 'curtains', and 'emergency service'. 📚<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Latenight' -> '<b>late night</b>', 'rainning' -> '<b>raining</b>', 'awfull' -> '<b>awful</b>'.</li><li><b>Phrasing!</b> 🗣️ 'It should on Sunday morning' -> 'it should be <b>on</b> Sunday morning'.</li><li><b>Past Tense!</b> ⏳ 'Bright light shine' -> 'bright light <b>shone</b>'. 'Tooked at me' -> '<b>looked</b> at me'.</li>	\N	\N	2025-12-14
