  ✅ Experiment with breaking longer paragraphs into smaller ones, each focusing on a main idea, to make your story easier to follow.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	4.0	Baimhon, your story is full of great ideas, and you did a splendid job using the exact starting sentence, which is really important for this task. You clearly described the "incredible" Christmas tree and told us all about finding out about the Christmas Fair. You also shared your feelings about being shocked and then very happy, which adds a lovely personal touch to your narrative. You certainly gave your readers a clear picture of what happened. Your story included all the important parts of the task, which is excellent. One thing to think about for next time is keeping your story within the suggested word count (around 100 words). While you included a lot of interesting details, sometimes writing a bit too much can make it harder for the reader to follow the main points quickly. For instance, the opening sentences before "As my friend and I arrived..." were a friendly greeting, but for a story task, it's best to dive straight into the adventure. Imagine you're writing a short film script; every sentence counts. Focusing on the core events and feelings will help you craft a concise and powerful story.	3.0	You certainly tried to make your story engaging, Baimhon, especially by sharing your excitement about the Christmas Fair. You also showed a good understanding of how a story unfolds by giving us a sequence of events and including your emotional reactions. This helps the reader connect with your experience. To make your storytelling even more effective, consider how you begin and end your narrative. The conversational opening ("Hello everyone today I have a story that I will tell you...") felt a bit like a chat with friends rather than the start of a story for a competition. For example, you could immediately begin with the given sentence and then let your narrative flow from there. Also, try to keep your writing focused purely on the story's events once you've started. This will help you keep the reader's attention from beginning to end, ensuring your fantastic ideas are communicated clearly and powerfully. Sometimes, in Thai, direct conversational openings are common, but in English stories, we often jump right into the narrative.	3.0	Your story has a clear order of events, Baimhon, which makes it generally easy to follow what happened. You moved from arriving at school to seeing the tree, finding out about the fair, and then being happy about inviting old friends. This logical flow is a great strength. To make your story even clearer and more enjoyable to read, try to break your writing into shorter paragraphs. For instance, after describing seeing the Christmas tree, you could start a new paragraph when you go to the classroom and ask the old student. Another paragraph could begin when you run home in the evening. This helps the reader take a pause and process new information. Think of paragraphs as mini-sections for different parts of your story. Using a few more linking words and phrases, like "After that," "Later," or "As a result," would also help connect your ideas even more smoothly, guiding your reader through your adventure.	2.0	You used a good amount of everyday vocabulary to tell your story, Baimhon, which helped us understand the events and your feelings clearly. Words like "new school," "friends," "beautiful," "kind," "shocked," and "happy" were all used appropriately to convey your message. To improve your language, let's focus on a few key areas. Firstly, pay close attention to verb tenses, especially when telling a story about past events. For example, you wrote "I run to the old student" and "I run so fast to get the phone," but because these actions happened "yesterday morning" and "in evening" (which refers to the past), you should use the past simple tense, which is "I **ran**." 🏃‍♀️ Secondly, remember subject-verb agreement. You wrote "it's call Assumption College Thonburi" – it should be "it's **called**." Also, for "Pink and I was shocked," since there are two people, the verb should be plural: "Pink and I **were** shocked." 🤯 Finally, some smaller issues like "giants christmas tree" should be "giant Christmas tree" (adjective before noun, and 'Christmas' is a proper noun, so it needs a capital letter). Many Thai learners sometimes face challenges with consistent past tenses and subject-verb agreement because the grammatical structures in Thai are different, often not requiring explicit tense marking or subject-verb matching. Practising these small but important grammar points will make your writing much more accurate and easier to understand.	\N	\N	2025-09-23
28997	2025-08-14 08:47:50.347381+00	2026	Thai Food	Food is a savior for humans although there are many subculture food around the world. Especially Thai street food. There are three of the most tasty street food are Somtam, Pad Thai, and Lab.<br><br><br>One of the food which is particularly captivating is Somtam. The ingredients are papaya the main ingredient of this dish. Tomato slice, Peanut, lime juice, chilli, fish sauce, carrot, and the last thing is Palm sugar. Firstly, we need to chop and slice all the vegetables. Secondly, put all ingredients into the Motar. Pound it and mix it all together. After that, put our food in the plate and eat it with sticky rice.<br><br><br>Besides Somtam, people really enjoy eating Pad Thai. It's a food that really popular among tourist in Thailand. This dish is made of noodle, egg, spring onion, fish sauce, soy sauce, and sugar. Or you can add prawn if you want more protein. Fisrt, you need to boil the noodle until it soft but not that soft. Then, prepare the ingredient that makes flavour. After that, put each ingredient into the pan and stir fried it all together. Finally, put it in the plate and serve. You can put a peanut or add more flavour you want.<br><br><br>Finally, Lab is a food that is sure to interest everyone. It has many flavour in one dish such as spicy, sour, salty, sweet. This dish is made of ingredients that pretty easy to find. For instance, Pork, lime juice, sugar, shallot, spring onion, fish sauce, Khao Kuo. Firstly, you need to boil a pork or any meat that you want to make. Secondly, chop all vegetables into a smaller piece. Lastly, put all ingredients into a bowl and mix it all together.<br><br><br>To summarize, all of this dish is a Thai street food that represent Thai local food that is really easy to find it.	311	\N	Overall, your essay is well-organized and clearly explains how to make three popular Thai street foods. You use some good vocabulary and your ideas are easy to follow. To improve your essay, 