ed to include.<br>   ✅ After writing, check your work to make sure all your sentences are about the same topic and directly answer the question you chose.	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	0.0	Bona, it's really important to remember that in the exam, you must choose **only one** task to write about. Unfortunately, in this piece of writing, you started to talk about pets, which was Task 2, but then you also began to answer Task 3, which was about fundraising. This means you didn't fully answer either question, and it showed that you misunderstood the instructions to pick just one. To get a better score in this section, you need to focus all your writing on one topic and make sure you include all the points asked for in that *single* question. Imagine you're writing a letter to a friend; you wouldn't suddenly start talking about a completely different topic halfway through, would you? Thai learners sometimes try to include everything they know, but for English exams, sticking to the topic and the task is key.	2.0	You've managed to communicate some straightforward ideas, which is a good foundation. When you were talking about pets, your ideas like "make me happy" came across clearly. However, because you tried to answer two different questions, the reader became quite confused, and it was hard to follow your main message. To improve this, think about who you are writing for (your "target reader") and make sure your writing style and purpose are consistent throughout. For example, if you're writing an article, try to keep the tone engaging and informative for someone reading a magazine. If you're writing a letter, ensure it sounds like a friendly message. Using varied sentence structures can also make your writing more interesting. Sometimes, Thai learners translate directly from Thai, which can make sentences sound a little less natural in English.	2.0	You have used some basic linking words like "and" and "because," which help to connect a few of your ideas. That's a good start. However, because you jumped between the topic of pets and fundraising, your writing wasn't very organised or easy for the reader to follow. It felt like two separate pieces of writing joined together. To improve your organisation, try to plan your writing with a clear introduction, body paragraphs for your main ideas, and a conclusion, all focused on one topic. Use linking words and phrases like "firstly," "in addition," or "finally" to guide your reader smoothly from one idea to the next. Thinking about how paragraphs logically connect will make a big difference. Thai students sometimes don't use enough connecting words, which can make their writing seem a bit choppy to English readers.	2.0	You used some everyday words that were appropriate for your topics, such as "friendly" for pets and "football" for sports. This shows you have some basic vocabulary. However, there were quite a few errors in your grammar and spelling that sometimes made it difficult to fully understand your meaning. Let's look at three important areas for improvement:<br>1.  <b>Spelling:</b> Words like "resterong" should be "restaurant" and "premort" should be "promote." It's very helpful to always use a dictionary or an online spell-check tool when you are unsure how to spell a word. You could also keep a special notebook for words you often misspell and review them often. 📚<br>2.  <b>Subject-Verb Agreement:</b> You wrote "many animal, which is can haveing pets, can make them..." but because "animal" is singular, and then "many" is used, the sentence structure became confusing. When you have a plural subject like "many animals," the verb should also be plural, such as "many animals *want*." Always double-check that your verbs match your subjects.<br>3.  <b>Word Form:</b> You wrote "haveing pets" where it should be "having pets." Also, "it is a good idea, In my opinions..." needs a full stop after "idea" and then "In my opinion," using the correct singular form. Paying attention to these smaller word forms and making sure your sentences are complete will help your writing become much clearer.<br><br>Many Thai learners find these areas challenging because English grammar rules can be very different from Thai, and sometimes word-for-word translation doesn't quite work. Keep practising these points, and your language will become much more accurate and natural. 💪	\N	\N	2025-09-14
