ons ask you to agree or disagree with an idea. For example, if the task says "Disagree," you could write something like, "Actually, I think building a robot sounds much more engaging than watching a film." Also, try to be a bit clearer with your reasons. For instance, instead of "when you hungry you can be some food in there," you could say, "We might get hungry, so having some extra money for food would be good." Thai learners sometimes focus on conveying information but might overlook specific instructions like disagreeing, or they might feel uncomfortable expressing a different opinion.	3.0	You structured your email well with a clear opening ("Hi Corey") and a friendly closing ("See you on saturday! Thank you! Ping Ping"), which is excellent for an email. You also used paragraphs, which helps organise your thoughts. 👍 To make your email even more engaging for your friend, try to make your tone consistently informal and natural. For instance, "I think let meet at the front entrance that would be best for me" could be phrased as, "I think it would be best if we met at the front entrance." This makes your message flow a little more smoothly and keeps the reader interested. Sometimes, when writing in English, learners might use slightly more formal phrasing, which can make informal communication sound a bit stiff.	3.0	Your email is generally connected and coherent, and you used paragraph breaks, which is a good way to separate your ideas. Keep up this good habit. 😊 However, the flow of ideas within your second paragraph could be smoother. Try to group similar thoughts together. For example, you talked about meeting Corey, then the film, then pocket money, and then other items to bring. You could try to use linking words to make these transitions clearer. For instance, after discussing meeting arrangements, you might say, "Regarding the activities,..." and then transition to what to bring with a phrase like, "As for what else to bring,..." This helps the reader follow your thoughts easily. It's common for learners to include all necessary information, but sometimes they focus less on making the connections between sentences and ideas explicit for the reader.	2.0	You used some good everyday words related to the festival, which is a great start. Keep building your vocabulary. However, there were a few grammatical points that made some sentences a little challenging to understand.<br>1.  When suggesting an action, remember to include a subject and the correct verb form. For example, "I think let meet at the front entrance" would be clearer as "I think **we should** meet at the front entrance" or "I think **let's** meet at the front entrance."<br>2.  When comparing things, pay attention to verb forms. "film was interesting more than build a robot" would be better as "the film **is** more interesting than **building** a robot." Remember to use the "-ing" form (gerund) after prepositions or when comparing activities.<br>3.  After "to," we always use the base form of the verb. So, "I have to brings it" should be "I have to **bring** it." Focusing on these kinds of verb forms will significantly improve your accuracy. These types of grammatical structures can be tricky for Thai learners because English grammar, particularly verb forms and how they change, is quite different from Thai, which tends to use more fixed verb forms.	\N	\N	2025-09-16
