ld a robot instead, which was a key part of the instruction. You also asked about paying for the festival, which wasn't relevant to Corey's email. To get a higher score, remember to include all the specific details asked for and make sure your suggestions are clear and have a reason. For instance, if you suggest a bottle of water, you could add, "because we'll be walking around a lot and it will be hot!" Your email was also a little short, so expanding on your ideas would help you meet the word count.	2.0	You've used a simple "Hello" and "Seeya" to start and end your email, which is appropriate for writing to a friend 👋. Your main ideas, like not wanting to watch the film, do come across. However, some of your sentences were a little unclear, which makes it harder for the reader to understand everything easily. For example, the sentence about paying for the festival was a bit unexpected in this context. Try to make sure your sentences are always clear and directly address the points in Corey's email so that your friend can follow your thoughts without any confusion.	2.0	Your email has some breaks between your sentences, which is a step towards good organisation. To improve this area, think about grouping sentences that talk about the same idea into their own paragraph 📝. For instance, all your thoughts about the film could be in one paragraph, and all your suggestions for what to bring in another. This will make your email more logical and much easier for Corey to read and understand. Adding simple connecting words like "Also" or "However" can help your ideas flow more smoothly from one sentence to the next.	3.0	You've used some good everyday words that fit the topic, like "Science festival," "front entrance," and "bottle of water" 💧. You also show some control over simple sentence structures. However, there were errors in almost every sentence that made your meaning a little harder to grasp at times. For example, you wrote "plase tell me" instead of "please tell me