hers," you could say "Teenagers' personalities are largely shaped by the influence of others." 🤔 A good way to improve this is to read more academic essays or articles in English to get a better feel for the formal tone, and practice using a wider variety of formal connecting words like "furthermore," "consequently," or "however" to guide your reader smoothly through your arguments. Sometimes, directly translating ideas from Thai into English can unintentionally make the English sound a little less formal, so thinking about how an English speaker would naturally phrase something can really help!	4.0	Your essay is really well-structured, Racing! You have a clear introduction and conclusion, and each of your ideas is in its own paragraph, which makes it very easy to follow your thoughts. You also used paragraph breaks nicely, which helps the reader move through your essay. You’ve used some good connecting words too, which ties your sentences and paragraphs together. To make it even better, you could try introducing each paragraph with a really strong "topic sentence" that clearly states what that paragraph will be about. Keep up the great work with your clear organisation! 🥳	3.0	You used a good range of words in your essay, and generally, your grammar was accurate, which is excellent! There were a few places, though, where the language could be tightened up to make your writing even more precise and formal. Let's look at three areas that could help you shine even brighter:<br><br>Firstly, watch out for your vocabulary choices. For example, you wrote "preception" which should be "perception" – an easy spelling mistake to fix! Also, using words like "things" when you could use more specific terms like "aspects" or "areas" can make your writing sound more academic. For instance, instead of "studying is another important things," you could say "studying is another important *aspect*." Many Thai learners sometimes choose more general words, so try to think about the most precise word you can use in a formal context. 🧐<br><br>Secondly, let’s focus on subject-verb agreement. In your conclusion, you wrote, "friends are often the people who has an influence..." but because "people" is plural, the verb should also be plural, so it should be "friends are often the people who *have* an influence." This is a very common error for many English learners, including those from Thailand, so always double-check that your verb matches your subject, especially when the subject is plural! ✨<br><br>Lastly, sometimes your sentences ran a little long, like "They want to make their parents proud of their academic where as it could be stressful if their parents put too much pressure." This sentence combines two ideas that could be clearer with slightly different punctuation or a more precise connecting word. "Whereas" is often used to show a direct contrast. Here, you could perhaps use "but" or rephrase it. Practicing how to combine clauses smoothly using a variety of conjunctions will help your sentences flow even better and sound more natural in English. Keep practicing those longer, more complex sentences, and you'll master them! 🚀	\N	\N	2025-09-12
