rd" is a good one, and you explained how pets can be friends when someone feels lonely. To make your article even more compelling, try to expand on your points with a little more detail or a quick example. For instance, when you mentioned pets "make more stuff on working around," you could add something like, "for example, you need to clean their cage or take them for walks." This helps your readers understand your ideas fully. Keep up the great work in addressing all parts of the question! 🐾	3.0	You've started well by using the format of an article, which includes an introduction, discussing different points, and then a conclusion. Asking a direct question like, "Is having a pet are good ideas?" is a good way to grab the reader's attention right from the start! To make your article even more appealing to teenagers, think about using a wider variety of ways to engage them. For instance, you could use more descriptive language or try a different kind of sentence to introduce an idea, making it flow even better. Your ideas are clear, but exploring more ways to present them will make your writing even more interesting to read. 😊	2.0	You've connected your ideas about pets, which is a great start! However, to help your readers follow your thoughts more easily, it's really important to use clear paragraphs. Think of each paragraph as a new mini-chapter for a different idea. For example, you could have one paragraph for your introduction and general opinion, another for the advantages, a third for the disadvantages, and a final one for your conclusion. This helps to group your ideas logically and makes your article much clearer to read. 📚 In Thai, paragraphing might not be as strictly defined as in English, where each distinct idea usually gets its own paragraph, so focusing on this will really improve the structure of your writing.	2.0	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit the topic of pets, such as "responsibility" and "lonely," which is great! However, some errors in grammar and word choice made your meaning a little harder to understand at times. For example, at the beginning, you wrote, "Is having a pet are good ideas?" which should be "Is having a pet a good idea?" or "Are pets a good idea?". Another example is "can't take care your pet," which should be "can't take care *of* your pet" or "can't care for your pet." And "pet will make more stuff on working around" could be clearer, perhaps "pets create more chores" or "pets require more work." Sometimes, in Thai, the verb "to be" isn't always used in the same way as in English, and prepositions like "of" can be tricky because their use might differ from how you express similar ideas in your first language. Focusing on these kinds of precise grammatical structures will make your writing much more accurate and easier for everyone to understand. Keep practising, and you'll see great progress! ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-22
