ets'), and making sure your verbs match your nouns (like 'pet *is*' not 'pet *are*').	B1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	2.0	You've done a lovely job sharing your personal opinion on why having a pet is a good idea, especially with your sweet story about Nub! It's clear how much she means to you, and that makes your writing very engaging. To get an even higher score in content, remember that an article like this usually asks you to look at *both* the good things and the more challenging parts of a topic. For instance, the question specifically asked about "advantages and disadvantages." Next time, after you write about the wonderful aspects of pet ownership, you could add a paragraph starting with a phrase like, "However, it's also good to consider that owning a pet can be a big responsibility..." and then mention things like needing to save money for food and vet visits, or how much time they need each day. Thinking about both sides of the argument shows a really complete understanding of the topic! 🤔	3.0	You do a good job of getting your main ideas across and your personal story about Nub certainly helps to keep the reader interested! You've communicated straightforward ideas effectively. To really grab and hold a teenager's attention in an international online magazine, think about how you can use the structure of an article to your advantage. For example, you could start with an engaging title, like "Paws-itively Perfect? The Truth About Pets!" and perhaps finish with a concluding sentence that leaves the reader with something to think about, like "So, are pets for everyone? It all depends on how 'ready' you truly are!" Adding a clear introduction and conclusion helps the reader feel like they've read a complete and well-thought-out piece. 😊	2.0	Your article has a natural flow of ideas, especially as you tell us about your journey with Nub. However, to make your writing even clearer and easier for readers to follow, try to break your text into separate paragraphs. Imagine each new main idea getting its own special space! For example, you could have one short paragraph for your introduction, another for the advantages of pets (like your personal experience), and if you had discussed them, another for the disadvantages. This helps the reader move smoothly from one idea to the next without feeling overwhelmed. Think of it like chapters in a book – each one helps organise the story! 📖	2.0	You use everyday words very well to describe your experiences with your dog, and we can definitely understand what you mean, which is the most important thing! For example, "my Mom and my dad think I need to have a dog to make me do some thing at free time" clearly explains the situation. To take your language skills to the next level, let's focus on a few things. Firstly, be careful with spelling, especially common words like "ready" (which you wrote as "rady"). Secondly, pay attention to plural forms; for instance, "pet" should often be "pets" when you're talking about them in general, and "sweet" should be "sweets" when referring to sweet foods. Thirdly, remember to use "a" or "an" correctly – it's "a good life," not "good live." Finally, be mindful of subject-verb agreement; you wrote "my dog is pet," but when talking about a general category, it's more natural to say "my dog is *a* pet," or "my dog *is* part of my family." In Thai, nouns don't change for plurals in the same way English ones do, and articles (a/an/the) are used differently, which can sometimes lead to these kinds of common mistakes. With a bit of practice, you'll be using these English structures more naturally! ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-22
