le, "when you have a pets" should be "when you have **pets**" or "when you have **a pet**." Another area is **word choice and form**, such as "relax alot" which should be "relax **a lot**" (two words) and "feel relax" which should be "feel **relaxed**" (using the adjective). Also, a phrase like "physique felthy no more energy" is a bit unclear and could be rephrased to something like "feel physically exhausted and without energy." Sometimes, in Thai, nouns don't change for singular or plural, and adjectives might be used differently, which can make these English patterns tricky. Don't worry, with practice, you'll master these! Remember to double-check your plural nouns and choose the correct form of words (like adjectives ending in -ed or -ing) to make your sentences sparkle!	\N	\N	2025-09-20
