ing more of the real story.\\n\\nTo get a score of 5, you need to give more details and make sure your ideas are clear. For example, you could describe what makes the movie a horror film, talk more about the actors' performances, and explain which parts of the real story should be shown.	3.0	You use the conventions of the communicative task in generally appropriate ways to communicate straightforward ideas. While some sentences may not be entirely clear, you are able to communicate the basic points of your review. To improve you could use more descriptive language to help the reader visualise key features of the film.	2.0	You used some paragraphs to organize your writing, but the ideas didn't always flow smoothly. For example, the connection between the actors and the story is not very clear. You also used 'Firstly,' but didn't include 'Secondly' or 'Finally' to continue the organization.\\n\\nTo get a score of 5, you need to make your writing well-organized and easy to follow. You can use different connecting words (like 'also,' 'however,' 'first,' 'next,' 'finally') to show how your ideas are linked.	3.0	Kati, you used some everyday words that fit the topic, like 'horror film,' 'actors,' and 'story.' You also tried to use some more difficult grammar, like relative clauses ('which is the movie that I really like'), but they are not always correct. There are also some other mistakes in grammar and spelling that make it harder to understand. For example, 'This film has used' should be 'This film used,' 'characther' should be 'character,' 'sicks' should be 'is sick,' and 'dissapear' should be 'disappears.'\\n\\nTo get a score of 5, you need to use more different everyday words correctly and make sure your grammar and spelling are more accurate. Try to practice using relative clauses correctly.	\N	\N	2025-07-21
