rents *have*" not "parents *has*." ✔️<br>*   **Proofread for small words:** Pay extra attention to little words like "their" and "theirs" – getting these right will make your writing much more accurate! 📝	B2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	Lookpear, you did a fantastic job of covering all the points in the question, including your own interesting idea about sports! That shows you thought carefully about the prompt. To get an even higher score, focus on making sure your final thoughts strongly support the ideas you've discussed earlier in your essay. For example, if you argue that friends have more influence throughout, your conclusion should clearly state that, rather than suggesting parents do. It seemed like you changed your mind a little at the very end, and that made your overall message a bit unclear. This can be tricky because sometimes we want to show both sides, but in an essay, it's important to pick a side and stick to it!	3.0	You made a good effort to write formally, which is important for an essay, and you clearly stated your position at the beginning. That's a great start! To score higher, ensure your argument is consistent throughout the entire essay, especially in the conclusion. For instance, if you want to persuade the reader that friends have more influence, then every part of your essay, including the final sentence, should reinforce that idea. The main challenge was that your essay's overall message became a little unclear because your conclusion didn't fully support the points you made earlier. Sometimes, students might be a little hesitant to strongly commit to one side of an argument, or they might try to summarise all points without explicitly reiterating their main stance, which can affect the essay's persuasive power for the reader.	3.0	You did a really good job of breaking your essay into paragraphs, with a clear introduction and separate paragraphs for each of your ideas. That makes it much easier to read! To improve, focus on making sure all your ideas flow smoothly from one to the next, especially ensuring your conclusion ties everything together without introducing new or conflicting ideas. For example, double-check that your linking words and pronouns ("their" vs. "theirs") are always correct. There were a few small errors in how you linked ideas, like using "theirs" instead of "their" when talking about friends' possessions or relationships. Also, the contradicting conclusion affected the overall flow. In English, strong connections between ideas help the reader follow your thoughts easily, and sometimes in Thai, the way ideas are linked and the use of pronouns can be different, so these small but important grammatical points can be tricky.	3.0	You used some good everyday vocabulary, and you tried to use different sentence structures, which is great! You got your message across clearly for the most part. To help your writing become even more accurate, let's focus on three key areas. Firstly, make sure your subjects and verbs always agree; for example, if you say "parents," the verb should be "have," not "has." Secondly, be careful with verb forms, like using "would follow" instead of "would followed" when talking about choices. Thirdly, pay close attention to little words like "their" and "theirs" – they are often confused! For instance, you wrote "theirs friends" when it should be "their friends." These little errors can add up, but they are definitely fixable with practice. English grammar, especially subject-verb agreement and specific verb forms, can be quite different from Thai, which doesn't have the same strict rules for agreement or tense markings in the same way. This is a common area for improvement for many learners!	\N	\N	2025-09-12
