ns between ideas smoother. 📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   ✅ Double-check your sentences for correct subject-verb agreement.<br>*   ✅ Practise using a wider variety of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas.<br>*   ✅ Proofread carefully for spelling and awkward repetitions in your sentences.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	Anu, you did a fantastic job of addressing all parts of the essay question! You clearly explained how friends influence choices in clothes and fashion, and studying, and you also thought of your own great idea about 'closeness' or 'togetherness' – that shows real engagement with the topic! You presented your arguments well and directly answered the question. One small thing to remember for next time is to keep an eye on the word count. While it's great to have lots to say, academic essays often have a specific length, and learning to express your ideas concisely is a valuable skill for your B2 level. Sometimes, when we have many ideas, we just want to get them all down, but for academic writing, choosing the most impactful words is key! 🎯	3.0	You made a good effort to write in a formal style, which is important for an essay like this. You successfully conveyed your main point that friends have a strong influence on teenagers. However, there were a few times where the language felt a little less formal or slightly less sophisticated than what's expected for a B2 essay. For example, phrases like "outdated mindset" could be rephrased to something more academic, such as "generational differences in perspectives" or "divergent viewpoints." Similarly, instead of "What was cool then could not be cool now," you could write, "Fashion preferences are subject to rapid evolution." Keeping a consistent formal tone throughout your essay helps your reader take your arguments seriously. It's common for Thai learners to sometimes mix formal and informal language because in everyday conversations, the lines between formality can be a bit more flexible, but in academic writing, clarity and consistent register are very important. 🗣️✍️	3.0	Your essay was quite well-organised, Anu, with each paragraph focusing on a different point, which is excellent! You also tried to use linking words to guide the reader through your ideas. To make your essay flow even better, try to use a wider range of linking words and phrases to connect your paragraphs and sentences. Sometimes, you used the same kinds of linking phrases repeatedly, or your sentences felt a little bit separate from each other, which made the reading a little choppy. Think of your linking words as signposts that smoothly lead your reader from one idea to the next. For instance, instead of just starting a new paragraph with "The second choice," you could try "Another significant influence from friends is..." or "In addition to fashion, friends also play a crucial role in..." Many Thai learners benefit from exploring a broader vocabulary for connecting ideas, as this really strengthens the overall structure of their writing. 🔄📚	3.0	You used a good range of everyday words in your essay, Anu, and you attempted some more complex sentences, which is great to see! However, there were quite a few grammatical errors that sometimes made your meaning a little unclear. For example,<br>1.  **Subject-Verb Agreement**: Sometimes, the verb didn't quite match the subject of your sentence, like in "Some choice that friends influene more... are clothes." Remember, if the subject is singular ("choice"), the verb should also be singular ("is"). Thai doesn't always have subject-verb agreement in the same way English does, so this is a common area for improvement for Thai learners.<br>2.  **Repetitive or Awkward Phrasing**: You had some phrases like "parents are parents are clothes" or "Teenagers are often closer to friends than their friend." These repetitions can confuse the reader. For instance, instead of "The reason being friends know...", you could write, "The primary reason is that friends are often more attuned..." Try to re-read your sentences to catch these moments and rephrase them for clarity and conciseness. This often happens when translating thoughts directly from Thai into English, where sentence structures can be very different.<br>3.  **Spelling**: There were a few spelling mistakes, like "influene" instead of "influence" and "in to" instead of "into." Taking a moment to proofread for spelling errors can make a big difference in how polished your writing looks! Spelling can be tricky when English sounds don't map perfectly to Thai phonetics, so careful checking is always a good idea. Keep practising, and these areas will definitely improve! 📝🧐	\N	\N	2025-09-12
