fashion, studying, and hobbies, which shows you understood the task well. However, to get an even better score for 'Content,' try to remember the exact wording of the question: 'Who has more effect on the choices teenagers make – friends or parents?' Your essay focused almost entirely on friends, which is great for showing their influence, but it didn't really compare them to parents. To fully answer the question, you need to discuss both sides and then explain *why* you think one has *more* effect than the other. For example, you could have acknowledged that parents also influence choices, perhaps in different ways, and then presented your arguments for why friends' influence might be stronger in certain areas. 🤔 Many learners, especially from Thailand, tend to present one strong argument rather than a balanced discussion, perhaps because of how persuasive writing is taught. To improve, always break down the question and make sure you're addressing every part of it, especially comparative elements like 'more effect'. Next time, try to dedicate a paragraph or two to discussing the role of parents before concluding why friends might have a greater overall impact. Keep up the excellent work in making your points clear! 👍	3.0	Peptine, you've done well to present your viewpoint clearly in the essay, which is a great start! Your main point about friends' influence comes across consistently. 😊 To really excel in Communicative Achievement and aim for a perfect score, let's work on two key things: register and engaging the reader. Firstly, an academic essay for the B2 level usually needs a more formal tone. Sometimes your writing feels a little informal, for example, when you use phrases like 'when I didn't have any ideas' or 'My friends and I talked together'. For a formal essay, try to use more objective language and avoid personal anecdotes or casual expressions. Think about how you would explain your ideas to a teacher or in a formal report. Instead of 'I would asked them first,' you could say 'teenagers often consult their peers first.' 🧐 Secondly, an engaging essay doesn't just present information; it draws the reader in and makes them think. While your essay is clear, it could benefit from more sophisticated ways of introducing points or developing arguments that make the reader actively consider your perspective. For instance, you could use rhetorical questions or more nuanced topic sentences. Thai learners sometimes struggle with maintaining a consistently formal register in academic writing, as everyday communication can be more direct. Practicing writing about abstract ideas without using personal examples will help you refine your academic voice. Keep aiming to make your writing sound sophisticated and appropriate for a formal audience! ✨	3.0	Peptine, your essay's organisation is quite good! You’ve done a commendable job of structuring your ideas into clear paragraphs, each with a distinct focus on clothes and fashion, studying, and hobbies. This makes your essay easy to follow. 😊 To make your writing even stronger and achieve a higher score, let's think about expanding your range of linking words and phrases. While 'The first thing is,' 'Beside,' and 'The last thing' are clear, using a greater variety of cohesive devices would make your essay flow more smoothly and sound more sophisticated. For example, instead of just 'Beside,' you could try 'Furthermore,' 'In addition to this,' or 'Moreover.' When introducing your conclusion, 'In short' is fine, but you could also consider 'To conclude,' 'In summary,' or 'All things considered.' Also, within your paragraphs, try to build stronger connections between your sentences. For instance, you could use phrases like 'consequently,' 'as a result,' or 'however' to show relationships between different ideas you're presenting. Thai learners sometimes stick to a limited set of linking words, so trying out new ones will definitely help you improve the overall coherence and sophisticated feel of your essays. 📝 Keep practicing, and your essays will become even more engaging! 🚀	3.0	Peptine, your language in this essay is generally clear, and you use everyday words well. However, to move towards a higher score, let's focus on three key areas. Firstly, pay close attention to subject-verb agreement. For example, you wrote 'most of choices that teenagers make come from' but 'choices' is plural, so 'come' is correct, but 'most of choices' is an awkward phrasing that could be 'most choices' or 'most of the choices'. Similarly, 'Beside clothes and fasion' should be 'Besides clothes and fashion'. Keep an eye on your plurals and singulars! 🧐 Secondly, try to use a wider variety of grammatical structures. While your sentences are understandable, sometimes they are a little simple. Experiment with more complex sentences using conjunctions like 'although', 'whereas', or 'therefore' to show more sophisticated relationships between your ideas. For instance, instead of two short sentences, you could combine them into one with a subordinating conjunction. Finally, review your use of articles (a/an/the) and prepositions. For example, you wrote 'most of choices that teenagers make come from the atmosphere' where 'atmosphere' might need to be more specific or perhaps 'their environment' would be a clearer choice. These small adjustments can make a big difference to how precise and natural your English sounds! ✍️ Many Thai learners sometimes translate directly from Thai sentence structures into English, which can lead to these kinds of grammatical slips, especially with articles and subject-verb agreement. By actively practicing different sentence structures and checking for these common errors, you'll see a real improvement. ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-20
