arning English.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Free time is a time that we can do thing How we wanted."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Free time is a time when we can do things as we want."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, we use "when" for time, and "things" needs to be plural. Also, "how we wanted" is a direct translation from Thai; a more natural English phrasing is "as we want" or "as we like."<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Besides Free time can waste our brain it also know as brainwashing."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Besides, free time can *also* be known as 'brainwashing' because it can make our brains feel wasted."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This sentence had several issues. "Besides" needs a comma after it when used as a transition. "It also know as" is a common Thai-to-English interference where the verb "to be" is often omitted, and the past participle is needed for passive voice ("is known as"). Also, "waste our brain" isn't a natural English phrase in this context; rephrasing helps clarify the meaning.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Some time brain washing also affect people."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Sometimes brainwashing also affects people."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> "Sometimes" (one word, plural 's') means "on some occasions." "Brainwashing" is usually one word. "Affects" needs an '-s' for third-person singular present tense, which is a common error for Thai speakers.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "You can find tiktok Thailand is the 2th people who which mostly show that in their free time is playing phone it getting more waste time I have suggest for you."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "You can find that TikTok users in Thailand are the second highest in the world, which mostly shows that people spend their free time playing on their phones. This is becoming a waste of time, so I have a suggestion for you."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This sentence has many issues with clarity and structure. "2th" should be "second." "Who which" is an incorrect use of relative clauses. "Playing phone" should be "playing on their phones." "I have suggest" should be "I have a suggestion" or "I suggest," as "suggest" is a verb. Thai speakers often omit articles and prepositions, leading to phrases like "playing phone."<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Practice writing full paragraphs for an essay, making sure each paragraph has a clear topic sentence and is not numbered.<br>2. Focus on using correct plural forms (e.g., "things," "times") and subject-verb agreement (e.g., "it affects").<br>3. Practice using the verb "to be" correctly in passive voice constructions (e.g., "is known as").<br>4. Work on forming clear and correct complex sentences, paying attention to relative clauses (who/which/that) and transitions.<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-27
