or example, you could add a paragraph starting with something like, 'Of course, having a pet isn't always easy, as they need lots of time and money,' and then explain those points. This way, your reader gets a complete picture, and you'll fully meet the task requirements. You also wrote quite a lot, which is fantastic that you have so many ideas, but for exam purposes, try to keep your articles within the suggested word count of 140-190 words to show you can be concise.	1.0	You’ve made a good start at writing an article for teenagers, expressing your personal connection and opinions about pets. The most important thing for an article is to keep your reader interested and make sure they understand your main points easily. While your opinion about pets comes through, some of your sentences are a bit long or unclear, which can make it hard for your teenage audience to follow. To make your articles even more engaging, try to use shorter, clearer sentences when you introduce new ideas. For instance, instead of a long sentence, you could break it into two: 'Having pets is a wonderful thing. My dog, Noah, is a good boy.' Also, think about using a friendly, direct style. You could ask a question like, 'Have you ever thought about getting a pet?' to draw your reader in! 🤩	2.0	You’ve organised your article into paragraphs, which is a really good start, Ken! Each of your main ideas – encouragement, fun, and memory – gets its own space, and that helps the reader see your structure. To make your writing flow even better, try to use more varied linking words and phrases to connect your ideas smoothly. For example, instead of always starting a new paragraph directly, you could say 'Firstly, one great advantage is...' or 'Another enjoyable aspect is...' or 'Finally, creating lasting memories is truly special.' ✨ Also, make sure that the ideas within each paragraph stick closely to just one topic. This will make your argument much clearer and easier for your readers to follow, helping them move from one point to the next effortlessly.	2.0	Ken, you use everyday words to talk about your feelings and experiences with pets, and it’s clear you have many ideas to share! However, to make your writing stronger, we need to focus on accuracy in grammar. I noticed a few areas where we can improve. For example, a common area for improvement is using the correct prepositions and articles (a/an/the). For instance, 'care our pet' should be 'care **for your** pet,' and 'main message of almost people' would be clearer as 'the main message that almost **all** people agree with.' Lastly, watch out for spelling mistakes like 'adventage' (should be **advantage**) and 'stlye' (should be **style**). Sometimes, in Thai, subject-verb agreement is less strict than in English, and articles might not be used in the same way, which can lead to these kinds of errors. Reading your work aloud, as suggested in your action plan, can really help you catch these small but important mistakes! Keep practising, and you'll see a big difference. 🚀	\N	\N	2025-09-22
