s about having pets!' or 'One of the biggest pluses of pet ownership is...' This makes your article more inviting and professional. Ensuring your language is clear and appropriate for your audience will help them connect with your message much more effectively.	3.0	You've clearly thought about how to organise your article, Krewkehw, with an introduction, sections for advantages and disadvantages, and a conclusion. This logical flow helps your reader follow your arguments. 👍 To make your writing even smoother and more engaging, think about using a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just repeating "and lastly" or "but," you could use phrases like "In addition," "Furthermore," "On the other hand," or "Despite these benefits." This helps connect your ideas more subtly and makes your writing feel more sophisticated. Practising starting new paragraphs when you introduce a completely new idea will also help your article look more organised and professional.	1.0	Your enthusiasm for the topic comes through in your writing, Krewkehw, and you use some everyday vocabulary well! However, there are quite a few grammatical and spelling errors that sometimes make it difficult for your reader to fully understand your message. Let's look at three key areas for improvement. First, spelling is very important. Words like "migth" (should be "might"), "opinions" (should be "opinion" or "opinions"), "evry" (should be "every"), "flor" (should be "flaw"), "ownner" (should be "owner"), "sicen" (should be "since"), "passitive" (should be "positive"), "reponsible" (should be "responsible"), "takeing" (should be "taking"), "brougth" (should be "brought"), "mountly" (should be "monthly"), "includ" (should be "include"), and "procuze" (should be "process") appear throughout your text. Regularly checking a dictionary or using a spell checker can really help with this! 📝<br><br>Second, subject-verb agreement and pluralisation need attention. For instance, you wrote "having pet are great" which should be "having pets *is* great" or "having *a* pet *is* great." Similarly, "every body" should be "everybody," and "lot's of amazing thing" should be "lots of amazing *things*." Thai learners sometimes make these kinds of mistakes because the concept of pluralisation and strict subject-verb agreement can be different in Thai. For example, in Thai, a single word can often refer to both singular and plural, and verbs don't change based on the subject in the same way English verbs do. Practising sentences where you focus on matching your verb to your subject will make a big difference! 🧠<br><br>Finally, sentence structure and word choice could be clearer. Phrases like "Talk about Advantage of having pets first" or "you have to give a hold money section all to your pet" are a bit awkward. Try to express your ideas in more direct and natural English sentences. For example, "First, let's discuss the advantages of having pets" or "You have to dedicate a significant portion of your money to your pet." Focusing on forming complete and grammatically correct sentences will make your writing much clearer and more pleasant to read. Keep practising these areas, and your language will become much stronger! 💪	\N	\N	2025-09-22
