r both the good and not-so-good points of having pets, which was the main idea of the article. You talked about pets being like friends and offering protection, which are certainly strong advantages. You also touched on the idea that pets can have a "dark side," which shows you're thinking about the challenges. For example, when you mentioned tigers as pets, it was an interesting thought, but sometimes those specific examples can take away from the general discussion about everyday pets. To get a higher score, try to make sure all your points directly link back to the main question and that your examples support your overall argument about pet ownership in general. 😊	2.0	You've made a good start by writing in the format of an article, which is what the task asked for. That shows you understand the task's purpose. However, to truly hold your reader's attention and communicate your thoughts clearly, working on making your ideas easier to follow will be beneficial. Sometimes the way your sentences are put together makes it a bit tricky for the reader to understand exactly what you mean. For instance, rather than saying "But not every pets I will tell you more," you could try "However, this isn't true for all pets, and I'll explain why." Practising writing clearer, simpler sentences will help your reader stay interested and understand your message better. 👍	2.0	It's clear you've tried to organise your thoughts by dividing your writing into paragraphs, which is an important step in structuring an article. You also used some basic linking to introduce advantages and disadvantages. However, the connections between your ideas could be much stronger to help the reader move from one point to the next more easily. For example, instead of just starting a new paragraph with "let begen by," you could use a phrase like "Let's begin by considering the advantages of having pets." Think about how each sentence and paragraph leads into the next. Thai learners sometimes focus on individual sentences, but linking them together with words like "firstly," "in addition," or "on the other hand" makes your writing much more coherent. 🔗	1.0	Your enthusiasm for the topic comes through, but there are quite a few areas in your grammar and vocabulary that need some attention to make your meaning clearer. For example, you often struggled with making sure your verbs agree with your subjects, like in "Having a pets is" (it should be "Having pets **is**" or "A pet **is**") and "Every pets always have" (it should be "Every pet always **has**"). Also, possessive pronouns like "ours" and "they" were sometimes used incorrectly; for instance, it should be "our friends" and "their dark side," not "ours friends" or "they darkside." Finally, articles (a, an, the) were sometimes missing or incorrect, like "Having a pets" instead of "Having pets" or "a tiger" instead of "tigers" when talking generally. These types of errors, which are common for Thai learners, can make your sentences difficult to understand. Practising these basics will make a big difference in how clearly you express your great ideas! Keep a special notebook for common errors you make and review it often. 📝	\N	\N	2025-09-13
