nd even added your own idea about activities with friends. It's clear you understood what the task was asking. 😊 To get an even better score, try to develop your points with more details and examples. For instance, instead of just saying 'Friends can helped you,' you could explain *how* they help or give a specific situation. Also, remember the question asked about 'friends *or parents*' – a great essay would discuss both sides a little, or at least acknowledge the contrast, to show a deeper understanding. Think about expanding your ideas a bit more next time. ✍️ Many Thai learners focus on getting all points covered, which is good, but often forget to add enough specific information to really make their arguments strong.	2.0	You managed to get your main message across, Bona, and it's clear you believe friends have a big impact! 👍 However, for an essay like this, the writing needs to be more formal. Phrases like 'Now I am going to tell you about' or 'this is the last part to tell you' are a bit too casual for an academic essay. Try to use more formal linking phrases and avoid directly addressing the reader so much. For example, instead of 'Now I am going to tell you about clothes and fashion,' you could write, 'Regarding clothes and fashion...' or 'Firstly, considering clothes and fashion...' 💡 Many Thai students find it challenging to shift between informal and formal registers, as spoken English often blur these lines, and they might not have had extensive exposure to formal written academic English.	2.0	Your essay has a clear structure, Bona, with an introduction, body paragraphs for each point, and a conclusion! That's a solid foundation. You used simple linking words to guide the reader, which is helpful. ✅ To make your writing flow even better, try to use a wider range of linking words and phrases to connect your ideas more smoothly. Instead of just 'Next one is,' you could try 'Furthermore,' 'In addition,' or 'Secondly.' Also, make sure each paragraph focuses