ood job covering all three points from the question - fashion, studying, and your own idea about feelings. 👍 However, the essay question asked you to compare whether friends OR parents have more influence, but you only wrote about friends. Next time, make sure to directly answer the comparison question by discussing both sides before giving your opinion. For example, you could mention how parents influence study habits while friends influence fashion choices, then explain which is stronger.	2.0	Your essay has some nice personal examples that make it engaging! 😊 However, academic essays need a more formal tone. Try to avoid conversational phrases like "you know what" and instead use more academic language like "it is evident that" or "research suggests." Also, remember to use complete sentences without contractions - "they are" instead of "they're." These small changes will make your writing sound much more professional.	2.0	I like how you used "Firstly, Secondly, Finally" to structure your points - that's a great start! 📝 To improve, try using more varied linking words like "Furthermore," "In addition," or "On the other hand" to connect your ideas more smoothly. Also, make sure each paragraph has a clear topic sentence that tells the reader what that paragraph will be about. Your conclusion should summarize your main argument about which has more influence.	3.0	You've used some good vocabulary like "mindset" and "relaxing," which shows you're trying to express complex ideas! 🌟 Watch out for these common errors: subject-verb agreement ("teenagers choice" should be "teenagers choose"), article usage ("the school" vs "to school"), and spelling ("differts" → "different," "subjuct" → "subject"). Thai learners often struggle with these because Thai doesn't have articles or verb conjugations like English. A good trick is to read your work aloud - you'll hear many of these mistakes!	\N	\N	2025-09-12
