een \\"ages and genders\\")\\" That''s a great reason!<br><br>👎 <b>Problem:</b> To get an even higher score, you could try to expand on your explanations with more specific examples. For instance, you mentioned that movies are popular because they are \\"related to their life story and emotional,\\" but you could give an example of <i>how</i> they are relatable or <i>what kind</i> of emotions they evoke.<br><br>💡 <b>Suggestion for improvement:</b> Think about adding a small detail or a mini-example. Instead of just \\"entertaining,\\" you could say \\"entertaining because of their exciting graphics and competitive nature.\\"<br><br>🌍 <b>Explanation:</b> Sometimes, when we''re writing, we focus on getting all the main points down, which you did perfectly! As you become more confident, you can start to add those extra details that make your writing even richer and more convincing.	4.0	You used the essay format well to get your message across, and your reader would definitely understand your main points!<br><br>👍 <b>Done well:</b> You maintained an appropriate tone for an essay and made sure the reader was informed about the topic. Your writing communicates your ideas in a straightforward way, like when you state, \\"One of the most popular free time activity is playing video games.\\"<br><br>👎 <b>Problem:</b> To really grab and hold the reader''s attention, you could try to vary your sentence beginnings and make your explanations a little more engaging or dynamic.<br><br>💡 <b>Suggestion for improvement:</b> Experiment with different sentence structures to make your writing flow more smoothly and interestingly. For example, instead of starting a sentence with \\"It''s popular because...\\", you could try \\"The popularity stems from...\\" or \\"A key reason for its widespread appeal is...\\"<br><br>🌍 <b>Explanation:</b> Sometimes in English, we use different ways to introduce information to keep the reader interested. Practising varied sentence structures will make your essays more sophisticated.	3.0	Your essay has a clear paragraph structure, which is a good foundation!<br><br>👍 <b>Done well:</b> You used four clear paragraphs, which is exactly what was asked for. You separated your ideas nicely, for example, dedicating one paragraph to video games and another to movies.<br><br>👎 <b>Problem:</b> Your introductory paragraph tells us what the essay will be about, but it doesn''t end with a clear thesis statement that presents your main argument or the <i>overall reason</i> for the popularity of these activities. Also, your conclusion repeated some of the exact reasons you gave in the body paragraphs without really summing up or giving a final thought. For instance, in your introduction, you write, \\"Free time activities that Thai teens usually do are playing video games and watching movies or series.\\" This is a list, not a thesis statement. Then, your conclusion repeats the same points.<br><br>💡 <b>Suggestion for improvement:</b> Work on crafting a strong thesis statement at the end of your introduction. It should summarize your main argument. For example, you could write: \\"These activities, playing video games and watching movies or series, are incredibly popular among Thai teenagers because they offer both engaging entertainment and relatable experiences.\\" Then, your body paragraphs would explain <i>why</i> they are engaging and relatable. For your conclusion, try to rephrase your main ideas and add a final concluding thought.<br><br>🌍 <b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, it''s common to state facts and then explain them. In English essays, especially at B2, a thesis statement acts like a mini-map for your reader, telling them your main point right away. Also, sometimes in Thai, we can connect ideas with \\'and\\' or simply list them. In English, we need a wider variety of linking words and phrases to show how our ideas are connected and to avoid repetition.	3.0	You used everyday vocabulary well, and your meaning was always clear, which is the most important thing!<br><br>👍 <b>Done well:</b> Your vocabulary is appropriate for the topic, and you used it generally well. For example, \\"entertaining\\" and \\"relatable\\" are good words to describe why these activities are popular.<br><br>👎 <b>Problem:</b> There were some small grammatical slips, especially with articles (`a`/`an`/`the`) and verb forms. For instance, you wrote \\"New things are need to learn by humans\\" which should be \\"New things <i>need to be learned</i> by humans\\" or \\"New things <i>are needed for humans to learn</i>.\\" Also, \\"most popular free time activity\\" should be \\"the most popular free time activity.\\" You were also asked to include relative clauses and passive structures, which were not consistently used.<br><br>💡 <b>Suggestion for improvement:</b><br>1.  <b>Articles:</b> Practice using \\"a,\\" \\"an,\\" and \\"the\\" correctly. A good rule is to think about whether you''re talking about a specific thing (\\"the book I read yesterday\\") or a general thing (\\"a book\\").<br>2.  <b>Passive Voice:</b> Practice forming sentences in the passive voice. Remember, it''s often used when the action is more important than who did it (e.g., \\"The ball was kicked\\" instead of \\"He kicked the ball\\").<br>3.  <b>Verb Forms:</b> Pay close attention to verb agreement and correct tenses.<br><br>🌍 <b>Explanation:</b><br>*   <b>Articles (a/an/the):</b> In Thai, there are no articles like \\'a\\', \\'an\\', or \\'the\\'. This is why many Thai learners find them tricky in English and might omit or misuse them.<br>*   <b>Passive Voice:</b> The way Thai structures sentences can sometimes lead to direct translations into English that aren''t grammatically correct for passive forms, as seen in \\"are need to learn.\\" English has specific rules for how to form the passive voice with \\"to be\\" + past participle.<br><br>✨ <b>Action Plan:</b><br>Here are three important steps you can take to improve your writing, Gut-Jung:<br><ul><li>✍️ <b>Craft Strong Thesis Statements:</b> Before you start writing your essay, clearly identify your main argument and write it as a single, clear sentence at the end of your introduction.</li><li>🧐 <b>Review Articles & Passive Voice:</b> Pay special attention to using \\"a,\\" \\"an,\\" and \\"the\\" correctly. Also, practice constructing sentences using the passive voice.</li><li>🚀 <b>Elaborate with Examples:</b> After stating a point, challenge yourself to add a specific example or a more detailed explanation to make your writing richer and more convincing.</li></ul><br>I hope this feedback helps you, Gut-Jung! You''re doing really well, and with a little more practice on these areas, your writing will become even stronger! Keep up the great work! ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-04
