b> Add one short, specific example or a short reason in each body paragraph (who, what, or how).<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "You can design your outfit in this game. Thier many unige games."<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "There are many unique games where you can design an outfit, such as Brookhaven."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved sentence combines the idea into one clear statement and gives a short example, which makes your point stronger and easier for the reader to follow.<br><br>	3.0	You generally use an appropriate essay style and you try to explain your reasons, but the register and phrasing are sometimes informal or unclear. Use more formal, complete sentences in an essay and avoid short fragments.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Use full sentences and a slightly more formal tone (avoid fragments and short notes). Start body paragraphs with a topic sentence that links to your thesis (e.g., "The first activity I enjoy is playing games because...").<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> "Games and Art work done by teenages in Thailand"<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Games and artwork are popular among teenagers in Thailand."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved version is a full sentence with a clear subject and verb, which fits the formal writing style of an essay and helps the reader immediately understand your point.<br><br>	2.0	You used four paragraphs, but the thesis and the topic sentences are not clear or grammatical, so the reader cannot easily see the structure you intended. The introduction should end with one clear thesis sentence; each body paragraph should start with a topic sentence that links to that thesis.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Rewrite your introduction so it ends with a clear thesis (one sentence stating both activities and why you like them). Begin each body paragraph with a topic sentence that refers to the thesis (e.g., "Firstly, I enjoy playing online games because...").<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> Introduction lacking a clear thesis: "Free time is benefit for teenages. Each country has many activities to do, Thailand has different activities. Games and Art work done by teenages in Thailand"<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Free time is beneficial for teenagers. In Thailand, two popular activities are playing online games and doing artwork; I enjoy them because they help me relax and connect with others."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved introduction ends with a clear thesis that names both activities and gives the reader the reason you will explain, so each paragraph can clearly relate back to this thesis.<br><br>	2.0	You use everyday vocabulary and you try to explain ideas, which is good. The main challenges are frequent spelling, agreement and form errors, and some sentence-structure problems. Below are examples from your essay with corrections and simple explanations that relate to common Thai-to-English interference patterns.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "Free time is benefit for teenages."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Free time is beneficial for teenagers."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (word form / plural) In Thai learners often use the base form of words where English needs a different form (adjective "beneficial" rather than noun "benefit") and plural marking. From the patterns: "Word form (noun/verb/adjective) — Thai uses flexible forms; English requires precise morphology." Using the correct form and plural makes the meaning grammatical and natural.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "Each country has many activities to do, Thailand has different activities."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Each country has many activities to do. Thailand has different activities."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (run-on / clause boundary) Thai often allows long clauses separated differently; English requires clear sentence boundaries or connectives. The run_ons pattern: "Thai allows long clauses; English needs clear clause boundaries." Use a full stop or a linking word (e.g., "and", "however") to join ideas correctly.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "Games and Art work done by teenages in Thailand"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Games and artwork are popular among teenagers in Thailand."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (passive/word form) The original is a fragment and misses a correct verb form. Thai often omits small grammatical words that English needs. Using "are popular" makes the sentence complete and natural in English.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "First of free time activities is play games."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "The first free-time activity is playing games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (word form) English frequently uses gerunds (playing) after "activity is". Thai-to-English transfer can produce noun/verb mismatches; check common collocations ("activity is + -ing").<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "Thier are many game that teenages usally play, but the most famouse is Roblox."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "There are many games that teenagers usually play, but the most famous is Roblox."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (plural / spelling) Multiple issues here: plural nouns ("games"), spelling ("teenagers", "usually", "famous"), and the word "There" (not "Thier"). Thai does not mark plural with -s in the same way, so learners sometimes forget it. See the plural_s and word_form patterns.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "Thier many unige games."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "There are many unique games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (spelling / word order) Spelling and the required verb "are" are missing. Thai sentence patterns allow subjects and verbs in different orders; English needs "There are ..." for this meaning.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "for example, Brook Haven which is a role play game, 99 night in the forest which is a surviver game."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "For example, Brookhaven, which is a role-play game, and 99 Night in the Forest, which is a survival game."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (relative clauses / word form) Use commas and "which" correctly for non-defining clauses; correct word forms ("role-play", "survival"). The relative_clauses pattern shows Thai learners may omit or misuse who/which/that and punctuation — add them for clarity.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "It become famous by many YouTuber play this game."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "It becomes famous because many YouTubers play this game."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (verb agreement / plural / clause linkers) English verbs need -s with third person singular ("becomes") and plural nouns ("YouTubers"). Also English prefers "because" or "when" to explain cause. Thai does not always mark third-person -s, so learners forget this ending (related to plural_s/word_form patterns).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "As well as play game is art work."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "In addition to playing games, artwork is another activity."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (structure / word form) English prefers "In addition to + -ing" for linking ideas. Thai sentence connectors differ, so students may produce sentences that are literal and ungrammatical in English; use standard linking phrases.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "Doodle Art is the easy one to do."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Doodle Art is easy to do."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (word form) English omits "the" in this structure and uses the adjective "easy" directly: "is easy to do". Thai learners sometimes keep extra determiners that English does not need (word_form pattern).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "It become famouse by many artist to Doodle Art and sold it which many money."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "It became famous because many artists created Doodle Art and sold it for a lot of money."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (tense / plural / preposition / word form) Multiple issues: past tense "became", plural "artists", and correct preposition "for". Thai does not always mark tense or plural the same way; this causes common errors (see word_form and plural_s patterns). Use simple past for completed past events and "sold it for a lot of money" for natural phrasing.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "All of this are made teenages be calm and relax."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "All of these help teenagers feel calm and relaxed."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (plural / verb choice / adjective form) "these" agrees with plural, "help ... feel" is the natural English pattern, and "relaxed" is the correct adjective. Thai speakers sometimes translate word-by-word and miss these collocations (plural_s and word_form patterns).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "It help teenages to communicate each other and have time with yourself."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "They help teenagers communicate with each other and have time for themselves."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (agreement / reflexive pronoun / preposition) Use plural agreement ("They help"), "communicate with each other", and "for themselves". Thai does not use these exact pronoun or preposition patterns, so they are often mistranslated into English (prepositions_time_place and plural_s patterns).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> "In my opinnion, these activities are important for teenages if they use it in correct way."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "In my opinion, these activities are important for teenagers if they are used in the correct way."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (spelling / passive structure / pronoun) "opinion" spelling, plural "teenagers", and passive "are used" make the sentence correct and clear. Thai often uses active short forms and omits articles, so using the passive here is clearer and matches English conventions (relative_clauses / word_form patterns).<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Focus on 10 minutes/day of spelling and form drills: correct common words you miss (teenagers, famous, usually, opinion, unique). Make a short list and test yourself twice this week.<br>2. Practice 5 model sentences using passive and relative clauses (e.g., "which is...", "that was...") — write one paragraph using at least one relative clause and one passive sentence.<br>3. Rewrite the essay: make the introduction end in one clear thesis sentence; start each body paragraph with a topic sentence and add one short specific example in each. Compare your rewritten sentences with the corrections above.<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-28
