l the content is relevant, and the target reader is fully informed. The slightly over word count does not detract from the content being fully addressed.	3.0	You have used the conventions of an essay in generally appropriate ways to communicate straightforward ideas. You clearly state your favorite movie and then move on to actors, the story, and your opinions. The essay is generally easy to understand. The tone is appropriate for informing the reader about your favorite movie.	3.0	You have organised your text into clear paragraphs, with each paragraph focusing on a specific aspect of the movie (introduction/actors, story, likes/improvements). You use basic linking words like "Next" and "because" to connect your ideas. This makes the text connected and coherent.	2.0	You have used basic vocabulary reasonably appropriately, such as "movie," "actor," "singer," "story," "human," "ghost," "grandmother," "sad," "song." However, there are noticeable errors that sometimes impede meaning. For instance, "I gonna tell you" should be "I'm going to tell you," "actor that important" should be "actors who are important," "in the same time" should be "at the same time," "He is as a singer and an actor as Bilkin" is unclear and should be something like "He is also a singer and an actor like Bilkin," "married together because of the ghost's grandmother" is a bit unclear, "made PP Krit died" should be "made PP Krit die," and "they didn't together" should be "they weren't together" or "they didn't end up together." These errors make the text hard to understand at times.	\N	\N	2025-07-21
29561	2025-09-16 21:31:09.780247+00	2026	Term 1 Email	Hello<br><br>the tickets you got is great so you will pick me up on the way best for me is pick me up on the way. No i don't think t film would be interesting yeah we should bring somthing else like phone or more money also we should go build ro bot i' think it would be better than film.	62	6.0	Hello Mathew! 👋 It's lovely to see your effort in writing this email. You've made a good start at communicating your ideas, and it's clear you understood the main points of Corey's email. Keep up the good work! However, a really important area for you to focus on right now is making sure your sentences are clear and easy to read, especially by using punctuation and paragraphs. Once you master that, your writing will flow so much better! But remember, you've got lots of excellent ideas, and with a bit more practice, you'll be expressing them even more effectively. Keep practising, and you'll definitely see improvement! <br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:** <br>• ✅ When you finish a thought or sentence, always put a full stop (.). Think of it like taking a breath! <br>• ✅ Remember to use capital letters at the beginning of every new sentence and for proper nouns like names (e.g., Corey). <br>• ✅ Break your email into different paragraphs for each new idea. This makes your writing much easier to read and understand.	A1+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	2.0	You've done a good job of touching on most of the things Corey mentioned, like saying the tickets are great and choosing to be picked up. You also let him know you'd prefer to build a robot rather than watch the film, which is excellent! 👍 You even suggested bringing a phone or more money. To get an even better score here, try to add a short reason for your suggestions. For instance, if you suggest bringing money, you could say 'because we might want to buy a snack.' Also, it's really important to try and write a bit more! Your email was shorter than we hoped for, so aiming for around 100 words will help you add those extra details. Sometimes, Thai learners are a little too brief, so adding a sentence or two to explain your points can really help!	2.0	You started your email with a friendly 'Hello,' which is a great start for an informal email to a friend! 😊 To make your email even better, remember to also include a friendly closing, like 'See you soon,' and your name at the end. Another thing that makes emails easier to read is using paragraphs. Imagine your email is a short letter; each new idea should usually get its own paragraph. For instance, you could have one paragraph about the tickets and another about what activity you'd like to do. This helps your reader follow your thoughts much more easily! Sometimes, students forget that the way an email looks is just as important as what it says.	1.0	This is an area where you can make some really big improvements, Mathew! 🤗 Right now, your email is written as one long message, which can be tricky for your reader to understand. Think of your email like building blocks: each new idea or piece of information should be its own block, or paragraph. For instance, when you talk about the tickets, that could be one small paragraph. Then, when you discuss being picked up, that could be another. Using full stops at the end of each sentence and starting new sentences with capital letters will also help a lot with organising your thoughts. Thai learners often focus on getting all their ideas down, but remembering to separate them into clear sentences and paragraphs makes a huge difference!	1.0	You've used some good everyday words that fit the topic, like 'tickets,' 'film,' and 'robot.' That's a solid foundation! However, there are quite a few places where your grammar and punctuation made it a bit difficult to understand your message clearly. 🚧 For example, remember that when you're excited, you can say 'The tickets are great!' instead of 'the tickets you got is great.' Also, always put a full stop at the end of a sentence, and start a new sentence with a capital letter. For instance, instead of 'No i don't think t film would be interesting,' you could write 'No, I don't think the film would be interesting.' Many Thai learners sometimes forget these small but powerful grammar rules, like using articles (a/an/the) and making sure their verbs match their subjects, but focusing on these will make your writing much clearer and more confident!	\N	\N	2025-09-16
28836	2025-08-27 10:16:11.565753+00	2026	Free time Activities	People need free time for they to relax and reduce stress. In Thailand people have a little amount of free times. But they have an interesting activities such as Lili Kao song, and Ma Kan Kluy.<br><br><br>Firstly, Lili Kao Son. It's a simple game that only use a small sheet of cloth. How to play it is sit in circle one of the kids will have a cloth and walk around the circle while singing the song. When the kid with the cloth dropped the cloth on one of the children. The children stood up and run after the kid around the circle. the kid that were running need to sit at the kids seat. It's popular because it only use a cloth to play and it improves children strength and stamina.<br><br><br>Besides Lili Kao San, Ma Kan Kluy is another graet free time activities in Thailand. It's the most famous and valuable activity from Thailand. You just need banana leaf then carve it into a shape of a horse. It was pretty boring but it's better than doing nothing. It's famous because it's really easy to make and play by just using banana leaf.<br><br><br>To summarize, Thailand freetime activities is much more thrilling than you thonk. Example like Lili Kao Song, and Ma Kan Kluy. Lili Kao San is a game that children sit in a circle shape and one kid that have a cloth will walk around the circle while singing when he dropped the cloth on one of the children back. The children ran after each other the childre need to sot on the another child spot. Unlike Ma Kan Kluy, a simple Thailand freetime activity, a great outdoor activity. How you can play it is very simple the steps include, cut some Bai tong, which is banana leaf in Thai. Then carve it into a horse shape and ride it. This shows that Thailand activities aren't boring.	321	13.0	Here is your writing feedback for 'Free time Activities'. You wrote 321 words and achieved B2. You try to use complex sentences, showing developing skills!	B2	Writing	CA	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	You successfully addressed all parts of the prompt by identifying two free time activities, explaining what they are, and giving reasons for your choices. Your ideas are clear and relevant to the task.<br>👉 <b>To reach a 5/5:</b> Focus on ensuring all your ideas are consistent throughout your essay, especially between your main points and your conclusion. Try to add fresh insights or a strong final thought in your conclusion instead of repeating earlier points.<br><br>	3.0	Your essay communicates straightforward ideas, but the register is notably uneven, sometimes feeling informative and other times more casual. English essays, especially at B2 level, often require a consistent, more formal tone, unlike spoken Thai which can be more flexible. Try to avoid very informal phrases and maintain a balanced, informative voice throughout your writing.<br><br>	3.0	You used a clear four-paragraph structure with an introduction, two body paragraphs, and a conclusion. However, developing a strong opening sentence (thesis statement) for your introduction and clear topic sentences for each body paragraph will make your arguments much easier to follow. Also, aim for a conclusion that synthesizes your ideas rather than just repeating them.<br><br>	3.0	You use a range of everyday vocabulary appropriately, which is great! The main challenges are with a few key grammar rules.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "In Thailand people have a little amount of free times. But they have an interesting activities such as Lili Kao song, and Ma Kan Kluy."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "In Thailand people have little free time, but they have interesting activities such as Lili Kao Song and Ma Kan Kluy."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai has no articles ('a', 'an', 'the') and handles plural forms differently than English. In English, 'activities' is a plural noun, so it should not be preceded by 'an'. Also, 'free time' is generally uncountable, so it doesn't usually take a plural 's'.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "It's a simple game that only use a small sheet of cloth."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "It's a simple game that only uses a small sheet of cloth."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, when the subject is 'it' (referring to 'the game'), the verb needs an '-s' at the end in the present simple tense (e.g., 'it uses', 'it makes'). This is a common point of confusion as Thai verbs do not change form in this way.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "People need free time for they to relax and reduce stress. In Thailand people have..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "People need free time to relax and reduce stress. In Thailand, people have..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, every new sentence must begin with a capital letter. Also, transition words or phrases like 'In Thailand' when starting a sentence, are often followed by a comma to improve flow.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Review and practice using articles ('a', 'an', 'the') and plural forms for countable and uncountable nouns. Pay attention to how Thai and English differ here.<br>2. Focus on consistent subject-verb agreement in the present simple tense (e.g., 'he/she/it + verb + -s').<br>3. Practice varying your sentence structures, particularly combining ideas with more accurate linking words and relative clauses, to improve coherence and avoid run-on sentences.<br>4. Before writing, decide on the appropriate register (formal/informal) for your essay and try to maintain it consistently throughout, avoiding overly casual language.	\N	\N	2025-08-27
28836	2025-12-02 09:53:40.087331+00	2026	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. Sam was a normal guy like everyone else. But, there was one thing that he was better than everyone else. He had a great talent in inventing. In his free time he liked to invent tools. One day he was inventing a special clock, an old style clock, that could stop time. <br><br><br> When he finished it, he used his special clock. For his own gain, he used it to prank others and steal people belongings. Until something he never expected happened, when he pressed the button to make the time flow again nothing, At first he thought that he hadn't pressed it hard enough, so he pressed it again harder and harder till eventually the device broke. <br><br><br> As a result, he have been staying alone in the world that everything frozen for ten years. Until he realized that he can make another device to make him turn back to normal, which he started his long journey to find the materials he needed. <br><br><br> Finally after 10 years stuck in the frozen world and 5 years finding materials. Sam created another special clock and came back to the normal world, which made him able inventing <br><br><br> and live his normal life with his family.	212	19.0	Hello Namo K, 👋<br><br>This is a fantastic story with a very strong lesson! 🕰️ The idea of getting stuck in frozen time for 10 years is actually quite terrifying, and it made for a great plot twist. I really liked how Sam worked hard to redeem himself.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you make your writing even better. 📋<br><br>1. <b>Grammar Polish!</b> 💎 You have a few small grammar mistakes like 'he have been' (he had been).<br>2. <b>Sentence Structure!</b> 🏗️ Be careful with sentence fragments (incomplete sentences).<br>3. <b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Able inventing' should be 'able <b>to invent</b>'.	B2	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	5.0	You have a perfect narrative arc! 🌟 You set up the character, introduced a conflict (breaking the clock), had a long struggle (10 years!), and a happy resolution.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Show the Pranks!</b> 🃏 You said he used it to 'prank others'. Giving one funny example of a prank he pulled would make the beginning more entertaining.</li><li><b>Describe the Frozen World!</b> ❄️ What did the frozen world look like? Was the water stopped in mid-air? Birds stuck in the sky? A little description here would be amazing.</li>	5.0	You told the story very clearly and I wanted to know what would happen to Sam. 🤩 The moral of the story is very strong.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Sentence Fragments!</b> 🧩 'Until something he never expected happened...' This sentence gets a bit lost. Try: 'He never expected what happened next: when he pressed the button...'</li><li><b>Transitions!</b> ➡️ The jump of '10 years' is huge! You could add a sentence about how lonely he felt during that time to make the reader feel more sympathy.</li>	5.0	Your story is well-organised with clear paragraphs. 📚<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Linking Words!</b> 🔗 You used 'As a result' and 'Finally' very well. Try to vary your sentence starters so they don't all sound the same.</li><li><b>Paragraph Breaks!</b> 🧱 You have a very short paragraph at the end ('and live his normal life'). This could be joined to the previous paragraph to make a strong conclusion.</li>	4.0	You used some great vocabulary like 'talent', 'eventually', and 'materials'. 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Verb Tenses!</b> ⚙️ 'He have been staying' -> 'He <b>had</b> been staying' (past perfect). 'He can make' -> 'He <b>could</b> make'.</li><li><b>Infinitives!</b> 🛠️ 'Made him able inventing' -> 'Made him able <b>to invent</b>'.</li><li><b>Prepositions!</b> 📍 'Steal people belongings' -> 'Steal people<b>'s</b> belongings'.</li>	\N	\N	2025-12-02
28863	2025-12-02 09:53:40.087331+00	2026	Creative Writing	Sam finished his invention and pressed the button to start it. This was Sam, who's a middle class guy whos not poor and not rich. His normal life is driving a truck. He had some knowledge about how to fix a truck, and he lived in a small country side house. One day Sam decided to make a machine that could make his truck untouchable but visible, so he could pass through the traffic jam easily. Although the machine worked, he was unhappy. Things went wrong when he started to tow a trailer and drive. The problem got bigger because the trailer sweep all the traffic cars. Because the machine only worked with his truck but not the trailer. Suddenly people in their cars were mad at Sam for causes<br><br><br>damages to their cars. As a result, Sam had to pay a lot for the damaged cars. After that Sam became a poor guy and his driving lisence got banned. In the end Sam was a truck mechanic and was saving up to build his new garage. After the bad days passed Sam tried to forget about the machine project and he has learned to test multiple times when make a project.	201	16.0	Hello Namo T, 👋<br><br>This is a very creative story! 🚛 I loved the idea of an 'untouchable' truck to beat traffic jams—we all wish we had one of those! The consequences of the trailer not being untouchable were funny but also a serious lesson for Sam. It was a great read.<br><br>I have a few tips to help you improve. 📋<br><br>1. <b>Spelling!</b> 📝 Watch out for 'lisence' (license) and 'whos' (who's).<br>2. <b>Verb Tenses!</b> ⏳ Make sure all your verbs are in the past tense (e.g., 'sweep' should be 'swept').<br>3. <b>Punctuation!</b> ✒️ Be careful not to split sentences in the wrong place (like the break after 'causes').	B1+	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	5.0	You have a clear and complete story structure! 🌟 The problem (the trailer wasn't invisible) was a clever twist.<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Show the Accident!</b> 💥 You said the trailer 'sweep all the traffic cars'. A little more description here (crunching metal, honking horns) would make the scene more exciting.</li><li><b>Sam's Reaction!</b> 😲 How did Sam feel when he realized what was happening? Was he panicked? Embarrassed? Adding feelings makes the character more real.</li>	4.0	Your story is easy to follow and the main idea is very clear. 👏<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Sentence Flow!</b> 🌊 'Suddenly people in their cars were mad at Sam for causes... damages'. This sentence is split awkwardly. Try: 'Suddenly, people were mad at Sam for causing damage to their cars.'</li><li><b>Word Choice!</b> 🗣️ 'Untouchable' is a cool word, but maybe 'Intangible' or 'Ghost Mode' would be more scientifically accurate for an invention?</li>	4.0	You used paragraphs to separate the beginning, middle, and end. Good job! 📚<br><br>To improve:<br><li><b>Linking Words!</b> 🔗 You used 'Although' and 'As a result'. Try adding words like 'Unfortunately', 'Consequently', or 'However' to make your writing sound more advanced.</li><li><b>Sentence Breaks!</b> 🛑 You have a paragraph break in the middle of a sentence ('causes... damages'). Make sure you finish the sentence before starting a new line!</li>	3.0	You used some good vocabulary like 'traffic jam', 'mechanic', and 'damages'. 👏<br><br>Here are a few corrections:<br><li><b>Spelling!</b> 📝 'Lisence' -> '<b>license</b>'. 'Whos' -> '<b>who was</b>' or '<b>who's</b>'.</li><li><b>Verb Forms!</b> ⚙️ 'Trailer sweep' -> 'Trailer <b>swept</b>' (past tense). 'He has learned' -> 'He <b>had</b> learned' or just 'He learned'.</li><li><b>Prepositions!</b> 📍 'Mad at Sam for causes' -> 'Mad at Sam for <b>causing</b>'.</li>	\N	\N	2025-12-02
28887	2025-07-22 15:19:59.245982+00	2026	A Place I Love	In my opinnion my favorite that I would love to go again is "Shang hai". It was a very good experience and it's first country that I ever been too I love that country.<br><br><br>Shang hai is a city in China that mixed with cultures and high technology that makes Shang hai is unique. The famouse food that is traditional Shang hai food is mala the flavor spicy and it's like a suace that people always ate. It's often use on hot pot, sha bu and griled pork it's very delicouse I like it. The is advace than Thai a little. The train is very fast that I remember it's faster than our trian in Thai land.<br><br><br>Overall this wha I like but there some weird things that people do. Like people ate some weird animals like rabbits, turtles, bats and more.... which was really disturbing but I did ate it though. So yeah that is just a facts. Shang hai is one of best experince that ever have. There are many amazing things that I never experience before and many weird things as well and I would love go there again.	192	9.0	Leo, you've shared an interesting and personal account of your experience in Shanghai, highlighting both its unique aspects and some surprising cultural differences. You did well to include details about food and technology. To improve your writing, focus on increasing your grammatical accuracy, especially with word choice, verb forms, and sentence structure. Also, consider how to connect your ideas more smoothly to enhance the flow for your reader.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	Part 1	3.0	Leo, you clearly identify Shanghai as your favorite place and provide relevant content about your experience. You mention specific aspects like its mix of cultures and high technology, traditional food (mala), and fast trains. You also include a personal reflection on \\"weird things\\" people eat. The essay is 192 words, which falls within the expected range of 140-190 words (just slightly over). However, some parts of the content could be more fully developed to inform the target reader more comprehensively. For example, while you mention \\"high technology,\\" you don't elaborate on it. The connection between \\"mala\\" being a sauce and \\"people always ate\\" it with hot pot is a bit unclear. To achieve a score of 5, you should ensure all aspects are thoroughly described and explained, providing a richer and more complete picture for the reader.	2.0	You communicate straightforward ideas about Shanghai and your personal experience there. The essay has a generally informal and personal tone, which is suitable for expressing a favorite place. However, the conventions of an essay are not used effectively to consistently hold the target reader's attention. The opening sentence is a bit awkward (\\"my favorite that I would love to go again is...\\") and some ideas are presented abruptly, affecting the flow. The concluding section feels somewhat disconnected. To improve to a 5, you would need to use essay conventions more effectively, employing clear paragraphing, varying sentence structures, and using more sophisticated language to communicate ideas clearly and keep the reader fully engaged.	2.0	The essay has a basic attempt at organisation with an introduction, a body discussing characteristics (culture, technology, food, trains), and a concluding section. You use some basic linking words and phrases such as \\"In addition,\\" \\"For example,\\" and \\"Overall.\\" However, the text is not generally well-organised or coherent. Paragraph breaks are inconsistent or lead to very short paragraphs that disrupt the flow. For instance, the final paragraph begins with \\"Overall this wha I like,\\" which feels abrupt and doesn't clearly transition from the previous point. The shift to \\"weird things\\" could be integrated more smoothly. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to structure your essay into distinct, well-linked paragraphs, using a wider variety of cohesive devices and organisational patterns to create a more fluid and logically structured piece.	2.0	Leo, you use some everyday vocabulary appropriate for describing a city and personal experiences, such as \\"culture,\\" \\"technology,\\" \\"food,\\" \\"train,\\" \\"spicy,\\" and \\"disturbing.\\" However, there are frequent and fundamental errors in basic grammatical forms, which are quite noticeable and often hinder clear communication. Examples include: \\"In my opinnion my favorite that I would love to go again is \\\\\\"Shang hai\\\\\\"". (should be \\"In my opinion, my favorite place that I would love to visit again is Shanghai.\\"), \\"it's first country that I ever been too\\" (should be \\"it was the first country I had ever been to\\"), \\"Shang hai is a city... that makes Shang hai is unique\\" (should be \\"makes Shanghai unique\\"), \\"The famouse food\\" (spelling), \\"it's like a suace\\" (spelling), \\"The is advace than Thai a little\\" (should be \\"It is more advanced than Thailand\\"), \\"our trian in Thai land\\" (spelling, capitalization), \\"this wha I like\\" (should be \\"this is what I like\\"), and \\"experince that ever have\\" (should be \\"experience that I have ever had\\"). The consistent errors in spelling, subject-verb agreement, articles, and verb forms demonstrate a limited control over simple grammatical forms, and some errors impede meaning. To achieve a score of 5, you need to significantly improve your accuracy in fundamental grammar, use a wider range of vocabulary with more precision, and demonstrate flexibility with more complex grammatical forms.	\N	\N	2025-07-22
29604	2025-08-21 13:01:23.407527+00	2026	Social Issue	I believe that mobile phones should be banned in every class. First of all, when students got a chance to research information, they learn nothing. They didn't use it for learning or work; they just secretly play on their phone. Those phones do not help the students learn at all. They're just reading and writing the information, which mean they're not learning. And most of the kind teachers will neglecting this specific part just because they want their students to get a lot of score. Studies show that many student got lower grades in their exam because teacher let the students find all the information on their own. Thought they'll behave and learn but its the opposite. Said by some determined students. Furthermore, students always end up playing games or scrolling through social media in class. As soon as those students found their ways to get their phone they would immediately play their phone even though the teacher said the phones are unprohibited in class. And because of that the student will start to turn against them. Then start to bring two phone to class, hide their phone from teachers and behave badly. From my experience teachers not really checking the phones in class. So it's easy for students to tricked the teacher, which it's a serious situation. The last reason, We students don't really need phone in class. Everyone knows that mobile phones are the easiest way to do everything. But we also can learn in traditional ways too. Its make the students learn better too. Students actully learn better by listen and write instead of read and write. Teachers can describe the lessons better than some random stranger anyway. Everythings that phones can replace by some real technolgy for education. For these three reasons, I believe that mobile phone should be banned in every class. before the students be buried with phones.	314	15.0	Dear Yayar<br>Here is your writing feedback for 'Social Issue'. You wrote 314 words and achieved B1. You presented a very clear opinion and supported it with several reasons, which is a great start.	B1+	Writing	CA	PET	Part 2	4.0	You did a great job explaining why you believe mobile phones should be banned. Your ideas were clear and relevant to the topic.<br><b>Problem:</b> You mentioned "Studies show that many student got lower grades" but didn't give specific details about these studies.<br><b>Correction:</b> For example, you could say: "Studies by educational researchers, such as the one conducted by Dr. Smith in 2023, show that many students achieved lower grades in exams when they were allowed to use phones for research."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In academic writing, it's good to give more specific evidence, like where the study came from, to make your points stronger. Even if you don't have exact facts, try to make it sound more like a general observation than a definite "study."	4.0	You successfully communicated your main point and kept the reader interested. Your essay was easy to follow, and your opinion came across clearly.<br><b>Problem:</b> You used phrases like "Said by some determined students" which sounds a bit informal for academic writing.<br><b>Correction:</b> Instead, you could write: "This view is shared by some determined students."<br><b>Explanation:</b> While it's good to include voices, in formal writing, we usually rephrase direct quotes or informal statements to fit a more academic tone. This helps maintain a consistent formal register throughout your essay.	3.0	You organized your essay with clear introductory phrases like "First of all," "Furthermore," and "The last reason," which helped guide the reader. Each section also focused on a different reason.<br><b>Problem:</b> Sometimes, the connection between sentences felt a bit sudden, for example: "They didn't use it for learning or work; they just secretly play on their phone. Those phones do not help the students learn at all. They're just reading and writing the information, which mean they're not learning."<br><b>Correction:</b> You could combine or link these ideas more smoothly: "They didn't use it for learning or work; instead, they just secretly played on their phone. Consequently, these phones did not help the students learn at all, as they were merely reading and writing information without true engagement."<br><b>Explanation:</b> Thai language often allows for sentences to be placed next to each other without strong linking words. In English, using connecting words like "instead," "consequently," or "therefore" helps ideas flow more logically and smoothly for the reader.	4.0	You used a good range of everyday words and tried to use different types of sentences. Most of your sentences were clear, even with some small errors.<br><b>Problem:</b> You often missed the plural '-s' on nouns, for example, "many student" and "two phone."<br><b>Correction:</b> It should be "many students" and "two phones."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, we don't always change words to show there is more than one. In English, when we talk about more than one person or thing, we usually add an '-s' to the end of the noun, like 'dogs' or 'books.' This is a very common difference between Thai and English grammar.	\N	\N	2025-08-21
