n points about pets, discussing how they can help with mental well-being and teach us responsibility. That's excellent! 👍 To make your article even more compelling, try to expand on your ideas with a few more specific details or examples. For instance, instead of just saying "spending times with your pets makes your feel happienes called dopamin," you could describe a particular activity, like "stroking a purring cat or playing fetch with a bouncy dog can release 'dopamine,' a feel-good chemical, helping us to feel less stressed." Thai learners sometimes focus on getting their ideas down quickly, but adding a little more detail can really make your writing come alive for the reader.	3.0	Your article generally gets your message across, and you've chosen a suitable, semi-formal style for your online audience, which is a big tick. ✅ You're communicating straightforward ideas well, but sometimes the article could hold the reader's attention a little more firmly. Think about how you can vary your sentence structures or introduce your points in slightly different ways to keep things interesting. For example, instead of repeating "having pets can give you more work and responsibility," you could use synonyms like "pet ownership comes with demands" or "it requires a commitment of time and effort." This helps to keep your reader engaged and shows a broader range of expression.	3.0	You've structured your article logically with an introduction, separate body paragraphs for your main points, and a conclusion, which is a solid foundation. 👏 You use basic linking words like "First," "Next," and "In conclusion" to guide the reader. To make your writing flow even better, try to use a wider range of connecting words and phrases. For instance, instead of just starting with "Next is responsibility," you could say something like, "Beyond the emotional benefits, pets also cultivate a sense of responsibility." This creates a smoother bridge between your ideas and makes the article feel more connected. Thai learners sometimes use simpler linking words, and expanding this range will really improve the cohesion of your writing.	3.0	You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, which is great, and your meaning is generally clear despite some errors. However, there are a few places where your grammar and spelling could be refined. For example, you wrote "happienes" instead of "happiness," "flufy" instead of "fluffy," "resopsebility" instead of "responsibility," and "there mess" instead of "their mess." Also, when you write "every pets has there own adorable ways," remember that "every" takes a singular verb ("has") and "their" is the correct possessive pronoun. It's very common for Thai learners to mix up "there," "their," and "they're," and to occasionally miss a plural '-s' or a correct verb ending, so don't worry too much about it. Just keep an eye out for these little details when you're checking your work. Reading your writing aloud can often help you catch these kinds of errors. 🧐	\N	\N	2025-09-13
