ime activities for teenagers.<br><br>	B1	Writing	CA	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	You wrote about two free time activities, cardio and drawing, and you explained what they are. To make your essay stronger, you need to explain more clearly *why* you chose these activities and *how* they help teenagers.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> When you explain an idea, give more details and examples. Make sure your conclusion only talks about ideas from the main part of your essay.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> You wrote: "Free time is make ppl calm and relax, so there are meny activities to do in free time. but for teens they are need the good activities for their body and wind because in this age all of teens need to grow up to helthy in the future..." This introduction is quite long but doesn't clearly tell the reader what your essay will be about or which activities you will talk about.<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Many teenagers today look for activities that help them become healthy adults. This essay will talk about two good free time activities: cardio exercise for the body, and drawing for a healthy mind and creativity."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> A good introduction tells the reader *right away* what your essay is about. This makes your writing easier to understand.<br><br>	2.0	You used paragraphs, which is a good step for an essay. However, sometimes your writing sounds too much like speaking, not writing. Words like "ppl" and "weny" are not good for an essay.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Try to use more formal language and make sure your introduction clearly tells the reader what your essay is about.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> You wrote: "Free time is make ppl calm and relax, so there are meny activities to do in free time." The word "ppl" is too informal for an essay.<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Free time allows individuals to unwind and relax, offering numerous activities. However, for teenagers, it is important to choose activities that foster both physical and mental well-being."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English essays, use full words like "people" instead of short forms like "ppl". This makes your writing more suitable for school work.<br><br>	2.0	You used four paragraphs, which helps organize your writing. But your ideas need to connect better. Sometimes it is hard to follow what you are saying because the paragraphs don't link together smoothly.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Start each new paragraph with a clear sentence that tells the reader the main idea of that paragraph. Also, make sure your conclusion reviews what you already said, not new ideas.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> Your first body paragraph begins: "The first activity is cadio. Cadio is the activity that will make teens have body helthy." This is a bit repetitive and doesn't clearly introduce the paragraph's main point.<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Firstly, doing cardio exercises can make teenagers' bodies very healthy."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> A good opening sentence for a paragraph tells the reader exactly what that paragraph will be about. This helps your essay flow better.<br><br>	2.0	You used some common vocabulary in your essay, which is a good foundation. The main challenges are with a few key grammar rules.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Free time is make ppl calm and relax..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Free time <b>makes</b> people feel calm and relaxed..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, when you talk about "Free time" (one thing), the verb "make" needs an '-s' to become "makes." In Thai, verbs don't change like this, so it's a common mistake for Thai speakers.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "but for teens they are need the good activities..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "but teens <b>need</b> good activities..." <br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, we say "teens need" not "teens are need" for a simple action. "Are" is used with verbs ending in -ing (e.g., "are needing" for a continuous action, which is not what you mean here). This is different from how verbs work in Thai.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b>