nce" but it was not very strong. For example, "For childrens' use mobile phones its easy to understand about work."<br><b>Correction:</b> For children, using mobile phones makes it easy to understand their work.<br><b>Explanation:</b> In English, we need clearer connections between ideas and more specific evidence to support them. Also, "childrens'" is usually "children's" (possessive) or "children" (plural).	3.0	You communicated your ideas well, but sometimes the formality of your writing changed. For academic writing, try to keep a consistent formal tone throughout.<br><b>Problem:</b> You used informal phrasing like "it didn't respect to teacher who are teached to other childrens" which is a bit casual for an academic paragraph.<br><b>Correction:</b> "...it showed a lack of respect for the teacher who was instructing other children."<br><b>Explanation:</b> In academic writing, we aim for more formal and precise language. "Didn't respect to teacher" is a direct translation from Thai, but in English, we use "showed a lack of respect for" or "disrespected the teacher."	3.0	Your paragraph had a clear beginning, middle, and end, and you used linking words like "First," "Second," and "A third reason." However, some parts of your writing were difficult to follow because the ideas weren't always connected smoothly.<br><b>Problem:</b> The sentences "And played mobile phones makes childrens relex being fun in class don't have to do many work a lot just give work in phones it can makes children have more searching and find a ideas." were very long and hard to understand.<br><b>Correction:</b> Additionally, using mobile phones can help children relax and have fun in class. If work is assigned on phones, it can encourage children to search for and discover more ideas.<br><b>Explanation:</b> This is an example of a "run-on sentence," which is common for Thai speakers because Thai allows for longer sentences without needing conjunctions or clear punctuation. In English, we need 