onal experiences, academic writing at B1 often benefits from presenting ideas that feel more universally applicable or using observations as examples rather than direct personal experience as primary evidence.	4.0	You communicated your main ideas clearly, and the way you wrote was generally suitable for your audience.<br><b>Problem:</b> Sometimes, your language was a bit informal, like when you asked 'Why would I know about this?' This makes your writing less academic.<br><b>Correction:</b> Try to keep your tone more formal and direct. For instance, instead of 'Why would I know about this?', you could say, 'This is evident from observations,' or 'My own experience supports this.'<br><b>Explanation:</b> In formal writing, we usually avoid direct questions to the reader or very personal interjections. It helps to keep your writing objective and focused on the topic, which is common in English academic texts.	3.0	Your paragraph has a clear beginning, middle, and end, using basic linking words to connect ideas.<br><b>Problem:</b> You used simple linking words like 'Firstly' and 'The second reason,' but sometimes your sentences ran together without clear connections.<br><b>Correction:</b> To make your ideas flow more smoothly, try using a wider variety of linking words and making sure each sentence clearly leads to the next. For example, 'The secound reason, It's can make us didn't understand in the class' could be 'Secondly, mobile phones can prevent us from understanding the lesson because we are distracted.'<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, long sentences with many clauses are common. In English, it's often better to break up long ideas into more distinct sentences or use stronger connecting words to show how ideas relate to each other, improving coherence.	3.0	You used everyday words well, and your simple sentences were mostly correct, even though there were some errors.<br><b>Problem:</b> You made some errors with plural forms, like '2 week' instead of '2 weeks', and verb forms, such as 'my friend aren't learned shes playing game'.<br><b>Correction:</b> Remember to add '-s' to nouns when there is more than one, like 'weeks' for '2 week'. Also, pay attention to correct verb forms and subject-verb agreement. 'My friend wasn't learning; she was playing a game' would be correct.<br><b>Explanation:</b> In Thai, we don't always change words for 'many' or specific tenses in the same way English does. In English, it's very important to add '-s' for plurals and to use the correct verb forms (like 'is' instead of 'are' for 'friend', and 'learning' instead of 'learned' for ongoing actions) to make your meaning clear.	\N	\N	2025-08-22
