 show you have ideas about these activities. To improve, make sure your reasons for choosing each activity are very clear and directly stated. For example, explain more explicitly *why* you chose "gaming" as a relaxing activity, and *why* badminton is specifically chosen by *you* for relaxation and memory-making. Think about adding more personal examples or clearer connections to your own experience.<br>👉 <b>To reach a 5/5:</b> Clearly and fully explain *why* you have chosen each activity, providing specific personal details or examples to support your choices and make them more engaging for the reader.<br><br>	3.0	You managed to communicate your ideas, but sometimes the essay felt a little informal or unclear, which made it harder for the reader to follow. Remember that an essay for FIRST (B2) should generally maintain a more formal tone than spoken Thai, and your explanations need to be very clear to keep the reader's attention.<br><br>	3.0	You followed the requested four-paragraph structure, which is a great start! You also used some linking words to connect your ideas. To make your essay even stronger, focus on ensuring your introduction has a very clear statement of your main point (a thesis statement). Also, make sure the first sentence of each body paragraph (your topic sentence) clearly tells the reader what that paragraph will be about and connects back to your main point.<br><br>	2.0	You showed good effort by trying to use different sentence structures, and you used some linking words effectively. However, the main challenges are with a few key grammar rules that often cause difficulty for Thai speakers.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "They have enough activety for teen on free time in Thailand."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "There are enough activities for teens in Thailand in their free time."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, we often use "there is/are" to introduce that something exists. Also, "activity" needs to be plural ("activities") when referring to many. Thai doesn't always mark plurals explicitly.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Although most of teens are do homework and learn a lot in class That was by the fact, which can make some of teens feel stress and nervous with the work and that, why teen want to have a stly of activity for relact and rest on free time."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Although most teens do a lot of homework and learn a lot in class, this can make some teens feel stressed and nervous, which is why they want to have a style of activity for relaxing and resting in their free time."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This sentence was quite long and hard to follow. English prefers shorter, clearer clauses than Thai. We also need to be careful with verb forms (e.g., "are doing" or "do" for general truth, "relaxing" not "relact"). "Teens" can be used without "of the" if it's general, or "most of the teens."<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "The first common activity but it popular for everyon is game, that people choose in first choic is played the favourite game."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "The first common activity, which is popular for everyone, is gaming, and it's often people's first choice to play their favourite games."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> You are missing the "is" verb after "it popular" (it *is* popular). Also, using "which" can help connect ideas more smoothly, and "gaming" is a common noun for the activity itself. Thai sentence structure can sometimes omit "to be" verbs in ways English cannot.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "This only have 2 thing that you choose bring were badminton stick with the shoes that was the important to use during play it."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "For this, you only need to bring two things: a badminton racket and appropriate shoes, which are important to use when playing."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> You need "have" to agree with "This" (This *has* or *you* only need). Also, "things" should be plural. "Badminton stick" is usually called a "badminton racket". And using "which are important" clarifies the relationship. Thai does not have the same strict subject-verb agreement or pluralization rules as English.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. **Review Thai L1 Patterns:** Focus on plurals (-s), correct use of articles (a/an/the), and ensuring every sentence has a clear subject and verb. Look at the "Missing Plural Markers" feedback from the report.<br>2. **Sentence Structure:** Practice writing shorter, clearer sentences. Break down long sentences into two or three simpler ones to avoid run-on sentences and ensure your meaning is always clear.<br>3. **Word Forms & Spelling:** Pay close attention to word forms (e.g., activity vs. activety, stressed vs. stress, relaxing vs. relact) and check your spelling carefully.	\N	\N	2025-08-27
