uage, but there are repeated mistakes with word choice, spacing, and prepositions. The main challenges are with a few key grammar and spelling points.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "freetime"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "free time"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, "free time" is written as two words. Thai learners sometimes run words together or separate them differently because Thai script and word boundaries work differently; this can cause confusion when writing English words that are actually two separate words.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "an use full skills in real life."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "useful skills in real life."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The correct form is the adjective "useful" (one word). Thai learners often transfer flexible word-formation from Thai (where one root can be used in different positions) and so they may separate or change word forms incorrectly in English. Remember to use the correct English word form (useful).<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Because, it can make them relax and joyful for doing some exercise."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "It can help them relax and feel happier when they do some exercise."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Thai learners often use sentence fragments starting with "Because" or omit prepositions. In English, we avoid starting a sentence with "Because" when it creates a fragment, and we use verbs and prepositions differently (e.g., "help someone to relax" or "help someone relax"). This is a common Thai → English interference pattern about sentence connectors and prepositions.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Rewrite the essay to 140–190 words with four paragraphs: intro (thesis), two body paragraphs (one activity each), conclusion. Aim for ~40 words per body paragraph for clear development.<br>2. Focus on three language targets: (a) write "free time" as two words, (b) use correct adjective forms (useful, relaxed), (c) avoid sentence fragments with "Because" — write full sentences and check 