ially for an exam like First (B2), it's important to use a consistent and more formal tone. Sometimes, your language felt a bit like spoken English, which is very natural in conversation but can be too informal for written English essays. For example, phrases like "calm down people even they are kids or aldults" or "some brake time" sound very casual.<br><br>	3.0	You structured your essay into paragraphs, with an introduction, body paragraphs, and a conclusion, which is a very good foundation. This helps the reader follow your ideas.<br>However, to improve, try to make your introduction end with a clearer thesis statement – this is one sentence that tells the reader your main argument or what your essay will be about. Also, start each body paragraph with a clear topic sentence that introduces the main idea of that paragraph. This will make your essay's structure even stronger and easier to follow.<br><br>	2.0	You used a good range of everyday vocabulary to express your ideas, and your overall message was understandable! The main challenges are with a few key grammar rules and spelling patterns.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "So Thai people's <b>hobbys</b> are not using long period of time..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "So Thai people's <b>hobbies</b> do not take up a lot of time..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, when a noun ends in a consonant + 'y' (like 'hobby'), we change the 'y' to 'i' and add '-es' to make it plural ('hobbies'). Thai does not have the same system for marking plural nouns, which can lead to this common error.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "How ever listening to music became <b>poppular</b> in Thailand..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "However, listening to music became <b>popular</b> in Thailand..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> This is a common spelling mistake. Reviewing common English spelling patterns can help catch these errors. Thai pronunciation doesn't always translate directly to English spelling.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "In short, Thai hobbies <b>aren't take</b> a long time..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "In short, Thai hobbies <b>don't take</b> a long time..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> For negative statements in the present simple tense, we use 'do not' (or 'don't') + the base form of the verb. 'Aren't' is a form of 'to be' and is not used with 'take' in this way. This often happens because Thai sentence structures might not require an auxiliary verb in the same way English does.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. **Master Plurals & Basic Verbs:** Practice forming plural nouns correctly (e.g., hobby -> hobbies) and using 'do/don't' and 'does/doesn't' accurately for negative statements and questions in the present simple.<br>2. **Proofread for Spelling:** Take extra time to carefully check your spelling. Use a dictionary or online tool if unsure. Pay attention to words like 'popular', 'busy', 'break', and 'adults'.<br>3. **Develop Essay Structure:** Practice writing clear thesis statements for your introductions and strong topic sentences for the beginning of each body paragraph. This will make your essays much more organised.<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-27
