em."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Adding specific details and reasons for why surfing the internet is a pleasure for teenagers makes your explanation much clearer and more convincing, fully addressing the prompt.<br><br>	3.0	You made a good attempt at using an appropriate essay style, but the incomplete nature of your response means your ideas are not fully communicated to the reader.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Ensure your essay is complete and all ideas are fully developed to effectively hold the reader's attention and convey your message.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> The unfinished nature of your essay means the reader does not receive all the information or a complete argument, which reduces the overall impact.<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "To effectively communicate your ideas, make sure to complete all paragraphs and fully explain each point. For example, after introducing surfing the internet, expand on its benefits and why teenagers enjoy it, similar to how you discussed playing sport."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In academic writing, it is crucial to present a complete argument. An unfinished essay leaves the reader with unanswered questions and an incomplete understanding of your points.<br><br>	3.0	You used paragraphs to structure your ideas, which is a good start for essay organisation.<br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Work on creating a clear thesis statement in your introduction and ensuring all body paragraphs are fully developed with clear topic sentences and linking words to create better flow.<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> Your introduction clearly states the topic but does not end with a specific thesis statement that outlines the main points of your essay. Additionally, the second body paragraph is not fully developed due to the essay's incompleteness.<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Freetime is essential for teenagers, especially in Thailand where they face significant academic pressure. Engaging in relaxing activities like playing sports and surfing the internet can effectively help them to refresh their minds and develop important life skills."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> A clear thesis statement at the end of your introduction tells the reader exactly what your essay will discuss, making your argument easier to follow and your essay more coherent from the start.<br><br>	2.0	You use everyday vocabulary in your essay. The main challenges are with a few key grammar rules.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "All teenager need their freetime..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "All <b>teenagers</b> need their freetime..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, the word "all" is a plural determiner and must be followed by a plural noun (e.g., "all students," "all books"). In Thai, nouns do not change form for plural, so it's a common interference to forget the '-s' for plural nouns in English.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "study for 8 hours per day make them weakly..."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "study for 8 hours per day make them <b>weak</b>..."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> "Weakly" is an adverb describing how an action is performed. Here, you need an adjective ("weak") to describe the state of the teenagers (how they *are*). Thai often uses flexible word forms where a single word can function as an adjective or adverb, leading to confusion in English where these forms are distinct.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "...from different school."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "...from a different school." or "...from different schools."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> In English, singular countable nouns often require an article (a/an/the) or a determiner before them. When referring to one of many schools, "a different school" is correct. If you mean multiple schools, "different schools" (plural) is needed. Thai does not use articles, so it's common for learners to omit them or use them incorrectly.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Complete a full essay following the word count and paragraph structure guidelines, focusing on developing all points of the prompt.<br>2. Review subject-verb agreement and plural noun forms, ensuring nouns match their determiners (e.g., "all students" not "all student").<br>3. Practice distinguishing between adjectives and adverbs to ensure you use the correct word form to describe nouns and verbs.<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-28
28763	2025-09-12 09:38:32.040313+00	2026	Term 1 Exam Question 1	Who has more effect on the choices teenagers make. In my way I stronger think that friends has effect on the choices teenagers make because friends are peolpe in same age and they do some thing same like playing sport, study or playing games. So first I will talk about clothes and fashion. Teenagers don't like their parents clothes or fashion because their think It look to old or I not cool for they. So their friends is the best way for them. They can wear clothes that have brand are look great for teenagers. Next I will talk about study. If teenagers have their parents that want them to be some thing when them grow up like doctor or something that teenagers don't want to be. It will has big affect to them, such as don't study or when them grow up them will don't do any work. But mostly now parents suport way that teenagers want to be. Parents will don't care about grade or study if you are good student don't do some thing bad. But the important thing that affect to study is your friends. If you have great comunity that study you will study alot to follow your friends. In my point of view I think friends have more effect with teenagers lives more than parents but parent will have affect will teenagers lives by suport money or suport your ideae.	236	7.0	Hello Best! 😊 Your essay shows a good effort in tackling the question about who influences teenagers more. You've clearly expressed your opinion and provided some interesting ideas. With a bit more attention to how you organise your thoughts and some key grammar points, your writing will truly improve! ✨	A2+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	You've made a good start by addressing the main question and talking about clothes and studying. That's a solid foundation! However, for an essay like this, it's really important to develop all parts of the question equally, and your "own idea" point wasn't quite as clear or detailed as the others. Sometimes we get so excited writing that we go a little over the word count, like you did here, which shows you have lots to say! To make your content even stronger, try spending a few minutes before you write to plan out each point, especially that third "own idea" section, making sure it's fully explained. For example, you could explicitly state your third idea at the beginning of its paragraph and then provide specific reasons or examples related to it. Many Thai learners sometimes focus so much on answering the direct questions that they don't quite get around to fully expanding on the 'extra' parts of the prompt, or they might write a lot without enough structure, so a little planning helps a lot! 🤔	2.0	You've successfully communicated your main argument, which is the most important thing! 👍 You stated your opinion directly, which is great. However, for a formal essay at the B2 level, we need to make sure our language sounds a little more academic and less like everyday chat. Phrases like "In my way I stronger think" are a bit too casual and not quite right grammatically for an essay. Think about who you're writing for – imagine you're presenting a serious argument to a teacher or an examiner. To improve, try to use more formal vocabulary and sentence structures. For instance, instead of "I stronger think," you could say "I strongly believe that..." or "My conviction is that..." Practice adapting your tone to suit different writing tasks; it’s a common challenge for many learners, especially when moving from speaking casually to writing formally. ✍️	1.0	This is an area where a small change can make a huge difference, Best! Your essay currently reads as one long block of text, which can make it quite hard for your reader to follow your ideas. 😟 Imagine you're building a house – you need clear rooms for different functions. In writing, paragraphs are like those rooms! For example, you should have one paragraph for your introduction, one for clothes and fashion, another for studying, one for your "own idea," and a final concluding paragraph. Each paragraph should have one main idea. Using simple linking words to connect your ideas, like "Firstly," "Secondly," "Furthermore," and "In conclusion," will also guide your reader smoothly through your essay. Many Thai learners sometimes write continuously without clear breaks, so getting into the habit of using paragraphs will really make your arguments much clearer and more effective! 🏠	1.0	You've managed to get your message across, which is the goal of language! 🎉 However, to move up to a B2 level, we need to tighten up some fundamental grammar points. Let's look at three important areas:<br><br>Firstly, **subject-verb agreement** is crucial. Remember, if your subject is plural (like "friends" or "teenagers"), your verb often needs to be plural too. For example, you wrote "friends has effect" – it should be "friends *have* effect." Also, "their think It look" should be "they think it *looks*." Paying close attention to whether your subject is singular or plural will help a lot. 👀<br><br>Secondly, let's focus on **verb tenses and forms**. You wrote "It will has big affect to them." Here, "has" is not the correct form after "will." It should be "it *will have* a big effect." Similarly, "when them grow up them will don't do any work" has a few issues; it should be more like "when they grow up, they *won't do* any work." Practising the correct forms of verbs, especially after modal verbs like "will," is a great next step. 🕰️<br><br>Finally, **pronoun usage and spelling** need a bit of a polish. You used "their" when you meant "they" (e.g., "because *they* think it looks to old"). "Their" shows possession, while "they" is a pronoun for people. Also, double-check your spelling for common words like "people" (you wrote "peolpe"), "support" (you wrote "supot"), and "ideas" (you wrote "ideae"). These small changes will make your writing much clearer and more professional. These are very common areas for improvement for Thai learners, as English grammar rules for agreements and tenses can be quite different from Thai, so regular practice with sentence construction is absolutely key to success! ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-12
