Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	Preaw, you've done a super job of tackling the essay question! You clearly stated your opinion on pets, and you included both the positives and negatives, which is exactly what the task asked for. You mentioned that pets can help you feel better and offer security, which are lovely points. You also gave good examples of the challenges, like the time and money involved. To get a perfect score here, try to make sure all your "advantages" are clearly presented as positives. For instance, when you talked about "protect our pet from other animals," it sounded more like a challenge or a risk rather than a benefit of having a pet. Perhaps you meant that having a pet makes *you* more protective, which is a sweet thought! Keep making sure your points are super clear for your reader. 💡	3.0	You've made a good start at writing an article for teenagers, sharing your personal connection with pets. To make your articles even more engaging for your audience, think about how you can use a catchy title or heading at the beginning – something that makes teenagers want to read more, like "Furry Friends: The Ups and Downs of Pet Ownership!" You've started with your opinion and ended with a conclusion, which is great, but try to keep your reader interested throughout. You could even use a question at the start to grab attention, like "Ever wondered if having a pet is for you?" This will help you communicate your ideas even more effectively to your target audience. ✍️	2.0	You've organised your article into paragraphs, which is a good step towards making your writing clear. You have an introduction, some points about advantages, then disadvantages, and a conclusion. That's a solid structure! However, to make your article flow even better, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases between your sentences and ideas. For example, instead of just "Second is time," you could say "Another important consideration is the time commitment involved." Or, to introduce a contrasting idea, you could use "On the other hand," or "However." Sometimes your ideas jump a little quickly from one to the next, especially in the first paragraph, where the "advantages" felt a bit mixed up. Giving each idea a little more space and a clear linking phrase will help your reader follow your thoughts easily. 🗺️	2.0	You've used a good range of everyday words that are appropriate for talking about pets. That's fantastic! Your sentences are generally understandable, which is the most important thing. However, to make your writing even stronger, let's focus on a few key areas. Firstly, pay close attention to **subject-verb agreement**. For example, you wrote "Having pet have" but it should be "Having **pets has**" or "Having **a pet has**" because "having" is singular when it's the subject. Another example is "them met" – it should be "they met" because "they" is the subject pronoun. This is a common area for Thai learners, as Thai grammar doesn't always require these changes. Secondly, try to vary your sentence structures a little more. You often use "it is" or "it will be," which is fine, but adding different ways to start your sentences will make your writing more interesting. For instance, instead of "it can security house protect people," you could say "pets can **provide security for the house and protect** family members." With a bit more practice on these points, your language control will really shine! ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-20
