s about Songkran, which is an important step. To really engage your readers and guide them through your article, think about using an article format. This means having a clear introduction, separate paragraphs for different ideas (like one for when it happens, another for what people do, and a final one for why you like it), and a concluding sentence. Right now, all your ideas are in one long paragraph, which can make it a little harder for the reader to follow. Imagine your article is a guided tour of Songkran; using paragraphs is like having clear signposts along the way. 🗺️	1.0	Your text uses some basic words like "because," "firstly," and "secondly" to connect your ideas, which is a good foundation. However, to make your writing truly well-organized and easy to read, try to use more paragraph breaks. Each new main idea should generally start a new paragraph. For instance, you could have one paragraph introducing Songkran, another describing the activities, and a third explaining why you like it. This helps your ideas flow more logically and makes your article much more coherent. Think of paragraphs as helping your reader breathe and digest one idea before moving on to the next. 🌬️	3.0	You've used everyday vocabulary effectively to talk about the festival, like "weather" and "water guns." That's a great start. Your sentences generally convey your meaning, even with a few small errors. For example, you wrote "most of the festival" when "most festivals" or "most of the festivals" would be more accurate. Also, "splash each others" would be better as "splash each other." Many Thai learners sometimes struggle with articles (like 'a' or 'the') and plural forms, as these work differently in Thai. Practising these will make your writing even clearer and more precise. Keep up the good work on using a range of simple sentences, and perhaps try to introduce a few slightly more complex ones to add variety. 🧐	\N	\N	2025-09-21
