 a general 'College day' or 'Sports day' that was going to happen, try to think about one specific, unexpected thing you and your friend saw right then and there. For example, if you saw a hot air balloon land in the school field 🎈, or a famous person unexpectedly walking through the gates ✨, that would be a specific incredible sight. Focusing on one clear event and then describing what happened because of it will make your story much clearer and more engaging for your reader.	2.0	You’ve shown a good understanding that this is a story by beginning with the provided sentence. However, the purpose of a story is to take your reader on a journey through a specific event, making them feel as if they were there! Because your story quickly moved from an "incredible" sight to a general description of a future sports day, it might have been a little hard for your reader to understand what the amazing surprise actually was. To really hold your reader's attention, make sure your story focuses on developing that one "incredible" idea. Imagine you're telling a friend about a truly shocking moment; what details would you include to make them gasp? Keeping your narrative focused on a single, surprising event will help your story achieve its purpose and truly connect with your audience. 🤩	2.0	Your writing shows an attempt to connect ideas, which is important for any text. You've used words like "since" and "Because" to link your sentences, which is a good step. To make your story even easier to follow and more coherent, think about organising your ideas into clearer sections, perhaps with short paragraphs. For instance, you could have one part for the amazing thing you saw, another for your reactions and what happened next, and a final part for how everything concluded. At the moment, your ideas tend to run together in one long section, which can make it a little tricky for the reader to follow the flow of events. Try to use linking words that show the order of things in a story, like "first," "next," "then," or "finally," to guide your reader through your exciting tale. 📖	3.0	You've used a good range of everyday words that fit your topic, and it's clear you're trying to use some more interesting sentence structures. That's fantastic progress. However, there are a few areas in your grammar that we can work on to make your sentences even clearer. For example, when you said "they have alot of Sports," it would be more accurate to say "there are a lot of sports" to show that many sports exist. Also, be careful with how you connect your sentences. Sometimes, sentences become very long and a bit hard to follow, like "Play it against your opponent's who's on the opposing team and play until which team color wins or lose." You could break this into shorter, clearer sentences, for instance: "You can choose to play against opponents on the opposing team. Then, you play until one team wins or loses." Shortening these longer thoughts and double-checking your subject-verb agreement will help your meaning come across much more smoothly. Sometimes, in Thai, long sentences are very common, but in English, breaking them down often helps with clarity. Keep practicing, and your language will become even more precise. ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-21
