he challenging parts. It's clear that your reader would be well-informed about your viewpoint! For example, you mentioned how pets can be friends when we're lonely, which is a lovely and relatable point. To get to a score of 5, try to expand a little more on your disadvantages. For instance, instead of just saying "buying them food," you could add something like, "The cost of special dietary food for pets can be surprisingly high each month," giving a more vivid picture. Also, when you write your conclusion, aim to summarise your main points and offer a final thoughtful reflection, rather than simply listing the advantages and disadvantages again. This will make your argument feel even stronger and more complete! 😊	3.0	Your article communicates your ideas in a clear and understandable way, which is a strong foundation for an online magazine for teenagers! You've used paragraphs to separate your thoughts, which is a great convention for an article. To help your reader stay even more interested, think about how you can introduce your article with a sentence that grabs their attention or use a rhetorical question. For example, you could start with something like, "Have you ever wondered if a furry friend is really for you?" 🤔 Also, your conclusion could offer a more satisfying sense of closure. Perhaps end with a forward-looking statement or a recommendation based on your balanced view, rather than simply expressing your personal desire for a pet. This would make your article feel more polished and impactful for your audience!	3.0	You've organised your article with clear paragraphs for your introduction, advantages, disadvantages, and conclusion, which helps your reader follow your ideas. You've also used some basic linking words like "such as" and "lastly" to connect your points. To really improve your organisation and aim for a higher score, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For instance, when you move from advantages to disadvantages, you coul