erfect for a story. Using descriptive phrases like 'something shocking, unexplainable' and 'investigate the unhonorable crime scene' kept me interested in what would happen next. To make this even better, try to ensure your tone stays consistent; while the story is quite dark, the ending felt a bit rushed compared to the detailed description of the house.	3.0	Your story follows a logical sequence, and I liked how you used 'Firstly' and 'Finally' to show the order of events. However, I have to give you a 3 here because the entire story is written as one large block of text. For your next piece of writing, try to use paragraph breaks—for example, start a new paragraph when Jo enters the house or when he finds the second note. This makes it much easier for your reader to follow the action.	3.0	I was impressed by your ambitious vocabulary, especially words like 'retribution' and 'intangibly.' These really added flavor to your writing. The reason for this score is that there are some repeated grammar errors that affect the flow. For instance, there are issues with subject-verb agreement like 'he realize' (should be 'he realized') and modal verbs like 'can finally went' (should be 'could finally go'). Also, remember to use articles like 'the' before nouns, such as 'the witch' instead of just 'witch'.	\N	\N	2026-02-18
