ere, make sure you focus carefully on what the question is asking for. For instance, if the question asks about a festival, you could think about Songkran or Loy Krathong, and tell us when it happens and what specific traditions are involved. Your article was also a little shorter than the suggested word count, which meant there wasn't much space to develop your ideas fully. Next time, try to expand on your descriptions and feelings a bit more to reach around 100 words.	1.0	You successfully shared a straightforward story about a family celebration, which shows you can communicate simple ideas. That's a great foundation. However, when writing an article for an international website for young people, we also need to think about how to make it engaging and easy to read for that specific audience. Your writing felt more like a personal story than an article. To improve, try adding a catchy title to your article, perhaps something like "My Mum's Special Japanese Birthday!" You could also try to break your writing into different paragraphs. For example, one paragraph could be about *why* you chose the restaurant, another about *what happened* during the dinner, and a final paragraph about *how everyone felt*. This helps to guide the reader through your ideas. Remember, articles often use a slightly more informative and structured approach than a personal narrative.	1.0	Pheem, your writing tells a clear story from beginning to end, which is a good way to organise events chronologically. You used words like "Recently," "Then," and "At the end of the day" to help the reader follow the timeline of the birthday celebration. That's a great start. However, for an article, we usually expect to see ideas grouped into separate paragraphs. Think of each paragraph as holding one main idea. For example, if you wanted to describe a festival, you might have one paragraph introducing the festival, another describing what people *do* during it, and a third explaining *why you love it*. Your current writing is one long block, and using paragraph breaks would make it much easier for your reader to understand your different points. Breaking your text into smaller, related sections will help your ideas flow even better. 📝	2.0	You used everyday vocabulary effectively to describe the birthday celebration, which helps your reader understand what happened. Words like "restaurant," "Japanese food," "surprise," and "necklace" are all appropriate and clear. That's excellent. However, there are a few areas where we can refine your grammar to make your writing even more precise. For instance, when you wrote "my mom love Japanese food," it would be more accurate to say "my mom *loves* Japanese food" because 'mom' is a singular subject. Also, you wrote "We celebrate at one restaurant," which could be improved to "We celebrate at *a* restaurant" or "We celebrate at *the* restaurant named 'Maguro'." In Thai, articles like 'a' or 'the' might not always be used in the same way, but in English, they are important for clarity. Practising subject-verb agreement and the correct use of articles will make your sentences sound much more natural and correct. Keep an eye on these small but important details as you write. 👀	\N	\N	2025-09-21
