d bad points, or advantages and disadvantages.<br>   ✅ When you're writing, pay close attention to your spelling. If you're unsure how a word is spelled, it's a good idea to quickly look it up.<br>   ✅ Read your sentences slowly after you've written them to check if the verbs match the subjects (for example, "they want" instead of "they wants").	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	Mew, you've done a good job of discussing some of the positive aspects of having pets, like how they can protect your home and offer companionship. You've also given some helpful advice on how to care for them, which shows you've thought about the responsibilities involved. 🐾 To improve this area, remember that the task asked for both the advantages and disadvantages of owning pets. Next time, try to spend a little time thinking about the challenges or less positive aspects of pet ownership too. For example, you could mention the cost of food and vet visits, or the time commitment required. Including a balanced perspective will make your article more complete and informative for your readers.	3.0	You've made a good effort to write an article, Mew, by starting with a question and organising your thoughts into paragraphs. It's good that you tried to engage the reader from the start. ✍️ To make your articles even more effective at holding the reader's attention, consider using a wider range of sentence structures. For instance, instead of many short sentences, you could combine some ideas using linking words like "although" or "however" to create more varied and interesting sentences. Also, ensure that all parts of the article directly address the main question, so your reader is consistently informed about your opinion on both the good and bad aspects of pet ownership.	3.0	Your article has a clear beginning, middle, and end, and your ideas are generally grouped into paragraphs, which makes it easier to read. You've used some basic linking words to connect your sentences, like "However