mple (for instance: “pets can be friends when you are home alone — my friend’s dog stays with her and helps her feel calmer”).<br>  ✅ Read your paragraph aloud and mark any places where you run out of breath; break those into two sentences.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	3.0	You did well to include both advantages and disadvantages and to state your opinion clearly — the reader is generally informed. To improve, try to develop each point with one concrete example or brief detail; for instance, when you say pets cost money, write a short example: “you must pay for food and vaccines, which can be about X per month” or “you need to buy a bed or lead.” Also avoid repeating the same point in different words; instead, add a new detail that explains why the point matters. Thai learners sometimes repeat ideas because they want to make sure the reader understands; instead, practise adding one example or a short reason after each main idea to strengthen your Content score.	3.0	Your tone fits an article and your ideas are straightforward, so a general online reader can follow you. To move closer to the next band, try opening with a short hook sentence (for example: “Have you noticed more cafés now welcome dogs?”) and use a clearer concluding sentence that answers the reader directly, such as “If you want a pet, think about time and money before you decide.” Use varied sentence lengths — one short question or sentence can interest the reader, then follow with a fuller explanation. Thai learners often translate directly and keep a similar sentence rhythm; practising short opening sentences and a final sentence that sums up your advice will help.	2.0	You have clear paragraphs, which is good, but many sentences are long and ideas sometimes run together. Work on one main idea per paragraph and begin each paragraph with a short topic sentence: for instance, “One advantage is companionship.” Then give one supporting sentence and, if helpful, a short example. Also use simple linking words to show contrast or result: “however,” “for example,” “because,” and “in conclusion.” A practical step is to underline where a sentence could be split into two; often a comma shows a place to stop and make a new sentence. Thai writing tends to link ideas in long lines rather than separate them into clear sentences, so training yourself to write shorter sentences will give your article clearer organisation.	2.0	Your meaning is usually recoverable, but frequent grammar and word-choice errors make some parts hard to read. Here are the three most important errors with examples and concrete fixes:<br>1) <b>Verb forms and infinitives/gerunds</b> — wrong: “choose to having pets” → correct: “choose to have pets” or “choose having pets” (but “choose to have” is usual here). Practise by writing the pattern “choose to + verb” and making three example sentences.<br>2) <b>Subject-verb agreement and verb forms</b> — wrong: “Having pets have a lot advantages” → correct: “Having pets has many advantages” or “Pets have many advantages.” Check the subject (having pets = singular idea; pets = plural) and match the verb. A quick trick: identify the subject and ask yourself, “Is this one thing or more than one?”<br>3) <b>Prepositions and collocations</b> — wrong: “spend money with food” → correct: “spend money on food”; wrong: “bring their pets go to with them” → correct: “bring their pets with them.” Make a short list of common verbs + prepositions (spend on, go to, bring with) and practise five sentences for each pair.<br>Why these happen for Thai learners: Thai does not change verbs for person or number and it has different ways to show relationships between words, so it is natural to translate directly. Focused practice on verb forms and common prepositions will reduce these errors quickly. Also, read your sentences slowly and check each verb + subject and each preposition.	\N	\N	2025-09-14
