ses on your wonderful trip to Japan rather than giving many details about the New Year festival itself. To get a higher score in content, it’s important to give your readers a clearer picture of the festival you are celebrating. For instance, you could describe a few more specific New Year traditions your family has, like special food, decorations, or activities, before talking about your travels. This helps your international audience understand the festival better.📝	3.0	You’ve done a good job of sharing a personal experience in a way that’s quite easy to read and follow. This shows you can communicate straightforward ideas well. To help your articles reach an even higher score, remember that an article for a website often benefits from clearly explaining the main topic to the audience. For example, if you’re writing about New Year, you might briefly explain its general significance before diving into your personal experiences. This ensures your readers, especially those from other cultures, are fully informed about the festival you are describing.🗣️	3.0	Your story about your trip to Japan flows quite well, and you use basic linking words like "because," "For example," and "On the first day" to connect your ideas. That's a solid foundation. To make your writing even clearer and easier to read, try to use more paragraphs. Think of each paragraph as a mini-section for a new idea. For instance, you could have one paragraph introducing New Year, another about your family's travel traditions, and a third specifically about your Japan trip. This helps organise your thoughts, making your writing more coherent and professional-looking.🔗	4.0	You use everyday words well, which helps to tell your story clearly. Your simple sentences are generally well-controlled, and you have included some more complex sentences, such as "My family always celebrate on Newyear because my parents' are free on these week." Your tenses are mostly correct, which is very helpful for a reader following your narrative. To make your English even more precise, pay close attention to prepositions, like saying "celebrate **New Year**" instead of "celebrate **on** Newyear." Also, remember to use plural forms correctly, for example, "buy **clothes**" instead of "a clothes" and "buy **things**" instead of "a thing". Sometimes, in Thai, the way you express singular and plural might be different, so it’s a good idea to double-check these in English. Keep practicing, and you'll find these small adjustments really help your writing progress.🌟	\N	\N	2025-09-21
