ample," and "because," which is good. To help your readers follow your thoughts even more easily, try to organise your ideas into clearer paragraphs. For example, you could have one paragraph introducing Song Kran and its general timing, another detailing specific activities, and then a separate one for your personal favourite parts. Using more varied linking phrases at the beginning of sentences or paragraphs (such as "In addition," "Furthermore," or "Despite this") can create a smoother flow between your ideas. Sometimes, in Thai, it's common to connect many ideas with "and," but in English, varying your linking words can make your writing more sophisticated and easier to read.	3.0	Eri, you use everyday words very well, which helps your article be clear and easy to understand. Words like "festival," "family," "shopping," and "cute" are used appropriately. You also show good control over simple sentence structures. To make your language even more precise, let's focus on a few areas. Firstly, be careful with verb tenses; for general facts about Song Kran, "is" would be more appropriate than "was." For example, "Song Kran is called..." rather than "Song Kran was called...". Secondly, pay attention to singular and plural forms; you used "moutain" when you meant "mountains." Lastly, "color pallet" should be "colour palette," and "wich" should be "which." These small adjustments will make your writing even more accurate and impressive. Remember, even native English speakers make small mistakes, so keep practicing, and your accuracy will naturally improve.	\N	\N	2025-09-21
