fights. You covered all the important points requested in the task, so your readers are well-informed. For example, you talked about using water to "feel cold and fresh," which paints a clear picture. To make your next article even stronger, try to be a little more precise with details, like making sure you write "pm" for afternoon times instead of "am" for early morning. Also, sometimes less is more; aiming for the suggested word count helps you practise expressing your ideas clearly and concisely, which is a valuable skill. 👍	2.0	Your article has a friendly and engaging tone, which is perfect for an international website for young people. You communicate your straightforward ideas effectively, and it feels like you're talking directly to your reader, which is a wonderful quality in an article. However, a significant part of writing an article is how it looks on the page, and your article could hold your reader's attention even more if you used paragraphs. Think of paragraphs like signposts for your reader, showing them where one idea ends and another begins. For instance, you could have a paragraph introducing Songkran, another describing the activities, and a final one sharing what you like best about it. This structure would make your article much more easier and more inviting for your audience to read. Try to imagine how your article would appear on a webpage; clear sections make it much more accessible. 💡	3.0	You've done a good job of connecting your ideas, Singto. Your thoughts generally flow in a logical order, and you use basic linking words like "because" to show relationships between your sentences. For instance, when you explain why you splash water, it makes sense. This helps your reader follow what you're saying. However, to make your writing even clearer and more organised, it's really important to use paragraphs. Each new main idea usually gets its own paragraph. For example, your current article could have started with an introduction, followed by a paragraph about the timing and cooling activities, then a separate one about the water fights and your favourite parts, and finally, a concluding paragraph about relaxation. This helps break up the text and makes it much more readable, allowing your fantastic ideas to be understood more easily. 🏗️	3.0	You use everyday vocabulary appropriately, Singto, and it helps your reader understand the topic well. Phrases like "feel cold and fresh" and "splash a big wave of water" are very effective. You generally use simple grammatical forms with good control, and your meaning is almost always clear, even with a few errors. To help your writing become even more polished, let's look at three areas. Firstly, make sure your verbs match your subjects. For example, instead of "Today am going to talk," you need to say "Today **I am** going to talk." And "There's a lot of festival" should be "There **are** a lot of **festivals**." Secondly, be careful with your spelling; words like "stard" should be "start," "inappopraite" should be "inappropriate," and "basicly" should be "basically." Finally, pay close attention to your punctuation, especially full stops and capital letters at the beginning of sentences. For instance, after "at him," you wrote "It's sounds funny." This should be "at him. **It** sounds funny." Practising these small but important details will make a big difference in how your writing is received. Many Thai learners sometimes face challenges with subject-verb agreement because Thai grammar doesn't typically change verbs based on the subject, so it's a great area to focus on for English. Keep practising, and you will see big improvements. ✍️	\N	\N	2025-09-21
