 that you received the tickets, clearly stated your preference for being picked up, and gave excellent reasons for wanting to build a robot instead of watching the dinosaur film. You also remembered to suggest bringing extra items and explained why, which is just what the task asked for. This means anyone reading your email would be fully informed and would understand your plans completely. You followed all the instructions carefully, and it really shows in the completeness of your message. 👏	4.0	Your email uses the typical style for writing to a friend, which is great, and you kept the reader interested. You started with "Hi" and ended with "Bye, Eri", which are perfect for an informal email. You also used paragraphs, which makes your email easy to read. However, when writing for an exam, it's generally best to avoid exclamation marks, even in informal writing, as they can sometimes be seen as a little too casual or emotional. Additionally, while "Hi" is a good start, adding the recipient's name, like "Hi Corey," makes the greeting a little more complete and personal, which is always a nice touch in an email. Thinking about these small details can help you achieve an even higher score next time. 📧	4.0	Your email is well-organised and easy to follow, Eri, with your ideas clearly separated into paragraphs. This makes it very simple for the person reading your email to understand your message. You used some good connecting phrases like "it's because" and "In my opinion," which help link your thoughts together. To make your writing even more polished, try to use a wider range of words and phrases to connect your sentences and paragraphs. For instance, instead of always starting a new idea directly, you could use phrases like "Furthermore," "On the other hand," or "Moving on to..." This adds more variety and makes your writing flow even more naturally. Sometimes in Thai, direct translations can lead to simpler sentence structures, so practicing these more varied linking words in English can be very helpful. ✍️	4.0	You used a good range of everyday words that are appropriate for the topic, Eri, and your grammar was mostly very good. Words like "Science festival," "pick me up," "build a robot," and "pocket money" were all used correctly. There were a few small errors, such as "cousic" instead of "cousin," "it fix it and clean it" instead of "to fix and clean it," and "activite" instead of "activity." These are minor points, and despite them, your message was perfectly clear. No one would struggle to understand what you meant, which is the most important thing. Focusing on careful proofreading for these small spelling and grammar points will truly elevate your writing. Thai speakers sometimes omit articles or have slight difficulties with verb agreement in English; being aware of these common areas can help you fine-tune your accuracy. ✨	\N	\N	2025-09-21
