 did a super job with the email format, Ka-nhom. Starting with "Hello, Corey," and ending with "see you, Ka-nhom," was perfect for writing to a friend. You managed to keep the reader's attention throughout your email, communicating all your ideas clearly and in a way that felt natural and friendly. This shows you know how to adapt your writing style for different purposes. 😊	4.0	Your email was well-organised, Ka-nhom, and flowed nicely from one idea to the next. You used paragraphs effectively to group your thoughts, which helps the reader follow your points easily. Using linking words like "because" and "but" also helped to connect your ideas smoothly. To make your organisation even stronger, think about how you can introduce each new piece of information with a clear topic sentence, perhaps starting a new paragraph for each main point from Corey's email. This will add more structure and make your writing even more coherent. 💡	3.0	You used everyday vocabulary appropriately, Ka-nhom, and your meaning was always clear, which is fantastic. You have a good handle on using simple grammatical forms. However, there were a few areas where more precision in grammar could really elevate your writing. For instance, you wrote: "I don't want you and your cousin wait for me." A more accurate way to say this would be "I don't want you and your cousin to wait for me," using the infinitive form. Another example was: "Watch the Discovering Dinosaurs is nice but build a robot would be more fun." Here, you could say "Watching the Discovering Dinosaurs is nice, but building a robot would be more fun," using the '-ing' form of the verb when it's the subject of a sentence. Finally, when talking about using phones for pictures, you said: "we will use phone to take a picture." It would be more accurate to say "we will use our phones to take pictures" (using the plural for both nouns). These small corrections will make your English even more natural and accurate. Sometimes, in Thai, the way