, and you gave a good reason for wanting to build a robot instead of watching the film. You also remembered to suggest bringing flasks and explained why, which is excellent. Keep this up, because covering all the points is super important for good communication. 👍	3.0	You've started well by using the email format, including a greeting and a closing, which is just right for writing to a friend. You also broke your email into paragraphs, which makes it easier to read. To make your communication even stronger, try to think about the natural flow of conversation in English. Sometimes, your phrasing was a little bit formal or not quite how a friend might chat, like 'Thanks ' for buying a ticket' could be simply 'Thanks for getting the ticket.' Practising common informal phrases and expressions will make your emails feel more natural and engaging for your reader. For instance, instead of 'you've asked what best for me,' you could say 'about your question regarding what's best for me.' This will help you hold your reader's attention even more effectively. 😊	4.0	Your email is generally well-organised, Hero, and it's clear you've thought about how to present your ideas. You did a great job of using paragraph breaks to separate your different thoughts, which makes your email easy to follow. You also used some good linking words like 'Well, you've asked' and 'This is because' to connect your sentences and help the reader move from one idea to the next. To make your writing flow even more seamlessly, you could try to introduce a wider range of these linking words and phrases. For example, instead of starting a new paragraph with 'Of the two choices you gave me,' you could try something like 'Regarding our activities' or 'As for the main attractions.' This will help your ideas connect even more smoothly and logically, making your writing even clearer. ✍️	3.0	You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately to talk about the festival, which is good. Your use of simple grammatical forms shows a good degree of control, and generally, I can understand what you want to say, even with some small errors. However, there are some noticeable mistakes in grammar and word choice that you could improve. For example, you wrote 'It is such a great news' where 'news' is uncountable, so it should be 'It is such great news.' Also, 'I've taken part in any exhibitions' sounds like you *have* participated, but your meaning seems to be that you *haven't* taken part in any, which would require 'I haven't taken part in any exhibitions' or 'I have never taken part in any exhibitions.' Another example is 'my mom also want me to go there either,' which should be 'my mom also wants me to go there too' because 'either' is typically used in negative sentences. Finally, 'more convinence' should be 'more convenient' because we need an adjective there, not a noun. In Thai, sometimes the structure of sentences and the use of singular/plural forms are a little different, which can lead to these kinds of English errors. Focusing on subject-verb agreement and the correct forms of words will help your writing become even more precise and natural. Keep an eye out for those small but mighty words and verb endings. 💪	\N	\N	2025-09-22
34926	2026-02-19 14:05:53.139105+00	2026	Question 1	Dear Mrs Lake,\n\nGreat!, I'd love to have a party to celebrate the end of the school year.\n\nI'd prefer have a party in the classroom. That's because, firstly we have an AC in our classroom. Secondly, we have a bunch of space in our room. Lastly, we have way more activity to do.\n\nFor activities or games. I reccommend us to play some challenging board games such as werewolf, explosion kitten or UNO. We can also connect our laptop to the Television and play a game that we need to help each other to complete a quest.\n\nIn my opinion, We should have a food that every body can eat, which is KFC.\n\nGood luck!\n\nChopin	117	17.0	Hi Chopin! I really enjoyed reading your ideas for the end-of-year party. You've done a wonderful job of responding to Mrs. Lake and making her feel included in your plans. Your suggestions for games like 'Werewolf' and 'UNO' sound like a lot of fun, and your reasons for staying in the classroom were very logical. My biggest tip for you is to make sure your closing greeting matches the person you are writing to, and to keep practicing those 'to' verbs. Keep up the great work! All the best, Ed	B2	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	Part 2	5.0	I noticed that you included every single point Mrs. Lake asked for, from your excitement about the party to your specific food suggestion of 'KFC'. This made me feel like Mrs. Lake would have all the information she needs to start planning! You did a great job following the instructions.	4.0	I loved your enthusiastic opening, 'Great!, I'd love to have a party'. It makes you sound very helpful and polite. I noticed you ended the email with 'Good luck!'. This felt a little bit funny to read because usually, a teacher says that to a student before an exam, rather than a student to a teacher about a party! Next time, you could try something like 'See you soon' to keep it feeling natural.	4.0	I noticed you used words like 'firstly', 'Secondly', and 'Lastly' to group your reasons for staying in the classroom. This made your ideas very clear and easy to follow! However, I saw that 'For activities or games.' was written as a very short, separate sentence. This felt a little bit like a 'stringy' start to a thought; next time, try connecting it to your suggestion to help the text flow even better.	4.0	You used some fantastic and specific words like 'challenging', 'quest', and 'bunch of space'. I noticed a small slip when you wrote 'I'd prefer have'; in English, we usually need to say 'I'd prefer to have'. Also, keep an eye on your spelling for 'reccommend', which has two 'm's. These are very small points, and your meaning was always very clear!	\N	\N	2026-02-19
29689	2025-09-23 09:23:18.41466+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	christmas is my favorite festival, when I was Yoong I very like christmas so much, were celebra have a dinner together and we are very so much fun but when, I was in this school on detember it has a big christmas tree	43	4.0	Hello Pun,<br><br>It's lovely to see your enthusiasm for writing about Christmas, your favourite festival. You've made a start on this article, and it's clear you enjoy thinking about this special time. With a little more focus on developing your ideas and connecting them for your reader, you can make your writing much clearer and more engaging. Remember, every piece of writing is a chance to share your thoughts and experiences with others, so let's work on making those thoughts easy to follow and enjoyable to read. ✨<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement**:<br>*   ✅ Aim to write at least 80 words for each task to fully develop your ideas.<br>*   ✅ Practice using linking words like 'and', 'but', 'because', and 'then' to connect your sentences and ideas smoothly.<br>*   ✅ Before you start writing, quickly jot down a few notes about *when* the festival happens, *what* you do, and *why* you like it, to ensure you cover everything.	A1	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	1.0	Pun, it's great that you chose Christmas and clearly stated it's your favourite festival. That's a strong start for an article like this. To help your readers truly understand and enjoy your writing, try to include more details. For example, when you say "on detember," you could add the exact date or mention if it's "every year in December" to give a clearer picture. You also mentioned having dinner together, which is a lovely detail, but can you think of two or three *other* things you do during Christmas? Maybe you decorate a tree, exchange gifts, or sing carols. Adding these kinds of specific activities will make your article much more interesting. Also, it's really important to explain *why* Christmas is your favourite. Is it the food, the family time, or the festive atmosphere that makes you happy? Telling your readers about your feelings and reasons will help them connect with your writing. Remember, the goal is to fully inform your reader, and that means giving them plenty of information. 😊	1.0	You're trying to share your experience of Christmas, which is wonderful. To make sure your article really grabs the reader's attention and keeps it, think about how you organise your thoughts. An article usually has an introduction, a main part where you describe things, and a conclusion where you share your feelings. For instance, you could start with a sentence that introduces Christmas, then have another sentence (or even a new paragraph) for *when* it happens, and then dedicate a section to *what* happens. Finishing with why you love it would tie everything together nicely. Breaking your writing into clearer sections helps the reader follow your story without getting lost. 🤔	1.0	Right now, Pun, your writing is all in one large sentence, and it's a bit tricky for the reader to follow. Imagine your article as a journey for your reader. You want to guide them smoothly from one idea to the next. Using simple linking words can be a great help here. For example, instead of "when I was Yoong I very like christmas so much, were celebra have a dinner together," you could say, "When I was young, I really liked Christmas **because** we celebrated by having dinner together." Also, using full stops to end sentences and starting new sentences with a capital letter makes your writing much easier to read. Try to group similar ideas together, perhaps in short paragraphs, so your readers can understand your message more easily. 🚦	1.0	You've used some everyday words like "Christmas" and "dinner," which is a good start. To make your writing even clearer and more correct, let's focus on a few things. Firstly, when you said "when I was Yoong," it should be "when I was **young**." Also, "were celebra have a dinner together" has a few things we can fix. It should be "we **celebrated** and **had** dinner together." The past tense "celebrated" and "had" helps the reader understand that this happened in the past. Another sentence, "we are very so much fun but when, I was in this school on detember it has a big christmas tree," is a little confusing. Perhaps you meant, "We **had** so much fun. However, when I was in this school, in **December**, it **had** a big Christmas tree." Thinking about when things happened (past or present) will help you choose the right word form. Keep practising your sentences, and you'll find they become much clearer. Don't worry, many Thai learners sometimes mix up verb tenses because Thai language doesn't always show tense in the same way English does, but with practice, you'll get the hang of it. 👍	\N	\N	2025-09-23
35309	2025-09-23 08:59:11.926138+00	2026	Term 1 Writing Task 2	As my friend and I arrived at school yesterday morning, we saw something incredible! so it happened yesterday morning, me and my friend saw a bug that thought to be extinct. As a bug nerd I almost cried it's so incredible with such a compelling patterns. It look so much better in person, after we took some photos we immediately ran to our favourite biology teacher Ms. Preawwan, we showed her the photo and she was shocked. she immediately grab a jar and ran to capture it, it was successful. After I got home I told all my parents about what occures and they were also shocked. It even got on the news! I saw Ms. Preawwan talked about the bug and she gave us credits too. I was so proud of my self for attending school on that preculiar day. It was the best day ever <br><br>	147	15.0	What a wonderfully imaginative story you have written. It's truly impressive how you took the starting sentence and developed it into such an exciting adventure about discovering an extinct bug. You have a real knack for storytelling, and it's clear you put a lot of thought into the narrative. Keep up this creative energy. The main area for you to focus on for improvement is consistently using capital letters and correct punctuation, which will make your fantastic stories even easier for everyone to enjoy. With a little practice, your writing will truly thrive.<br>📋 **Action Plan for Improvement:**<br>*   Remember to start every sentence with a capital letter.<br>*   Pay close attention to spelling as you write and then reread your work carefully to spot any errors.<br>*   Practice using paragraph breaks to separate different events or ideas in your story, making it clearer and more organised.	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	PET	Part 2	4.0	Atom, you did a really good job making sure your story included all the important parts of the task. You started with the exact sentence given, which is perfect, and you clearly described the "incredible" thing you saw – an extinct bug. How exciting. You also did well in showing what happened next, like running to tell your teacher, and how everyone reacted. Your story had a clear beginning, middle, and end, which is great for a narrative. To make your next story even stronger, try to develop the ending a little more. For instance, what was the very final outcome for the bug, or what was the long-term impact of its discovery on the school or yourself? Just a little more detail at the end can make it feel completely finished for your reader.	4.0	You certainly know how to keep a reader interested, Atom. Your story flowed well and kept me engaged from the moment you saw the bug until it was on the news. You showed a good understanding of how to tell a story by describing what happened and how you felt. The way you described the bug as "incredible with such compelling patterns" really helped to capture the excitement of your discovery. To push this score even higher, think about how you can vary your sentence structures a bit more. Sometimes, using a mix of shorter, punchy sentences and longer, more descriptive ones can make your writing even more dynamic and captivating for young readers.	4.0	Your story is nicely organised, Atom, and it’s easy to follow the events from start to finish. You used words like "As" and "after" to help link your ideas together and show the order of events. This makes your story coherent and logical. One area where you can make your writing even better is by using paragraph breaks. Right now, your entire story is in one long paragraph. Imagine your story as a journey; each new stage or important event could be a new paragraph. For example, you could have one paragraph for the discovery of the bug, another for telling your teacher, and a third for the news coverage. This helps your reader follow the different parts of your story more easily. In Thai writing, it's common to see longer blocks of text, but in English, new paragraphs help signal new ideas or shifts in time or topic.	3.0	Atom, you used everyday words effectively to tell your story, and it was clear what was happening. You also made a good effort to use past tenses, which is exactly right for a story about something that happened yesterday. However, there are a few areas where we can tidy up your language use. The most important one is capitalisation. Remember to always start a new sentence with a capital letter. For example, you wrote "so it happened yesterday morning, me and my friend saw a bug" – this should be "So it happened yesterday morning, my friend and I saw a bug." Also, pay close attention to spelling, like "preculiar" which should be "peculiar", and verb forms such as "grab" instead of "grabbed" and "occures" instead of "occurred". Thai students sometimes forget capitalisation in English because the rules can be different in Thai, where capital letters are not used in the same way. Practising these details will make your writing much more accurate and polished.	\N	\N	2025-09-23
