ammar and more varied sentence structures to express your ideas clearly and engage the reader more effectively. Keep practicing, Kaohom L!	B1+	Writing	CA	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	Kaohom L, your essay is relevant and offers a good amount of detail about Chiang Rai, fulfilling most aspects of the task. You've effectively covered traditional elements, nature, food (like "Khao soy"), and activities (like hiking or "sipline at Sing Park"). You also mention the people and museums like "Baan Dum museum." To reach a score of 5, you could further elaborate on *why* these elements are "beautiful" or "interesting," for instance, by describing the unique flavors of Khao Soy or the specific beauty of the "algriteeture."	3.0	You have communicated straightforward ideas about Chiang Rai, and the essay's purpose of introducing your favorite city is generally clear. However, the consistent flow and ability to fully hold the target reader's attention are occasionally hindered. For example, some sentences feel a bit abrupt, such as "There lot of activity you can do fore example." To improve to a 5, you could use more engaging introductory phrases or vary your sentence structure more. For instance, instead of "Most of cafe in Chiang Rai is mix of modern cafe style and nature," you could say, "Many cafes in Chiang Rai *offer* a unique blend of modern design and natural surroundings, allowing you to enjoy a drink *amidst* nature."	2.0	You have attempted to organize your essay into paragraphs, but the flow of ideas is not always smooth or logical. The connections between sentences and paragraphs are often weak, and there are some abrupt transitions. For instance, in your first body paragraph, the transition between discussing architecture and nature feels a bit sudden. Similarly, "There lot of activity you can do fore example" starts a new idea somewhat abruptly without a clear linking phrase. To improve to a 5, you should use a wider variety of cohesive devices, such as transition words and phrases like "In addition," "Furthermore," or "Moving on to..." to create smoother connections. Also, ensure clear topic sentences for each paragraph to guide the reader.	2.0	Kaohom L, your essay contains numerous errors in spelling, punctuation, and grammar that noticeably impede communication at times. For instance, common spelling errors like "whic" (which), "algriteeture" (architecture), "fore" (for), "can'try" (can try), "Ching" (Chiang), "mantain" (mountain), "throught" (through), "anveture" (adventure), "sipline" (zipline), "famouse" (famous), "their" (there), "recomened" (recommend), and "conclution" (conclusion) are frequent. Grammatically, phrases like "it a big area of mantain and forest" (it *is* a big area of mountains and forests) and "this city have an interesting art work" (this city *has* interesting art work) show consistent errors. To improve to a 5, you need to focus significantly on accuracy in spelling and basic grammar. A wider range of accurately used vocabulary and grammatical structures, including relative clauses, would also enhance your writing.	\N	\N	2025-07-11
