s attention consistently due to the informal register, lack of clear paragraphing, and repetitive sentence structures. To improve to a 5, you would need to consistently use a formal and appropriate register for an essay, employ a wider range of linguistic resources, and vary your sentence structures to effectively communicate both straightforward and more complex ideas, ensuring the target reader's attention is consistently held.	2.0	You attempt to organise the text with an introduction, sections on food and places, and a conclusion. You use basic linking words like \\"First,\\" \\"Second,\\" and \\"Conclusion.\\" However, the text lacks clear paragraphing, leading to very long blocks of text that are difficult to follow. This significantly impacts the essay's coherence and makes it challenging for the reader to track the progression of ideas. For example, the \\"Foods\\" section is a single, dense paragraph with many loosely connected ideas. The attempt to separate \\"foods\\" and \\"places\\" is clear, but the internal organisation within these sections is weak. To achieve a score of 5, you should organise your text into well-structured, distinct paragraphs, using a wider variety of cohesive devices and organisational patterns (e.g., topic sentences, transition phrases, referencing) to create a much more fluent, logical, and easily digestible piece of writing.	2.0	You use everyday vocabulary generally appropriately for the topic of travel, food, and history, such as \\"capital city,\\" \\"shopping malls,\\" \\"street foods,\\" \\"resterant\\" (restaurant), \\"sushi,\\" \\"museum,\\" \\"history,\\" and \\"Samurai.\\" However, there is noticeable overuse of certain simple lexis and repeated grammatical errors that impede communication. Examples include: \\"I going to talk\\" (I am going to talk), \\"capital city at Japan\\" (in Japan), \\"There have\\" (There are), \\"Hi-Tecnology\\" (High-Technology), \\"interest things\\" (interesting things), \\"high-level ingredients\\" (high-quality ingredients), \\"reserants\\" (restaurants), \\"macha\\" (matcha), \\"alot of varity\\" (a lot of variety), \\"you will go there should went\\" (you should go there), \\"at the eveing\\" (in the evening), \\"Edo-Museaum Tokyo\\" (Edo-Tokyo Museum), \\"Armor Samurai, which is make from irons\\" (made from iron), \\"learning any thing\\" (anything), \\"In there have alot\\" (There is a lot), \\"Is the history and Important story for me\\" (This is history and an important story for me), \\"visited Japan for you life it very pretty\\" (visit Japan in your life; it is very pretty), \\"Hope you enjoy you trips\\" (Hope you enjoy your trip). While the meaning can often be determined, the frequency and type of errors significantly affect the overall clarity, fluency, and control of language, suggesting a limited range of simple grammatical forms and an inconsistent grasp of appropriate vocabulary. To achieve a score of 5, you would need to use a range of vocabulary and grammatical forms with control and flexibility, with only occasional errors that do not impede communication.	\N	\N	2025-07-22
28823	2025-07-23 15:06:39.731732+00	2026	A Place I Love	A place I love, it will be LA for short or Los Angelis for full but you can call it what ever you want.<br><br><br>The reson of why i chouse this city for, it because one of place I alway want to been there since i was a child. The name of that place was Universal studio'. Fist time i've met this wonderful land was in the jurrsic park book. And when i grew up, i have an oppitinity to goes there. The story i will giving to you now it start last summers ago on day two, i was going with my mom and the fist thing I notice, it's a Harry potter theme Attraction. me and mom simply don't care the Warning about motion sickness, and after we realize it was too late. After we got out the ride. my mom have a feeling she can throw up any time but for me no reaction.<br><br><br>To summarise LA is veary interesting place for me be caus it have a lot of thing you can do but I only a little activity in there but what ever i finally fullfill my one of chilhood dream at last.	198	8.0	Rico, you chose Los Angeles as a place you love and shared your exciting story about visiting Universal Studios, especially the Harry Potter ride. You did a good job of telling us about your childhood dream coming true. To make your writing even better, you can focus on giving more reasons why you love the whole city of LA, not just one place within it. It will also help to use more formal language for a website article, connect your ideas more smoothly, and work on your spelling, grammar, and capitalisation so your writing is always clear and easy to read.	B1	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	FIRST	Part 1	3.0	Rico, you introduced Los Angeles and clearly showed why Universal Studios is important to you by sharing your childhood dream and the story of your visit. You focused on one key example (the Harry Potter ride). However, the task asked you to write about a "city or town" you love, and your essay mostly focused on just one specific attraction within LA, rather than the city as a whole. You could add more reasons why you love the entire city.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "The name of that place was Universal studio'. Fist time i've met this wonderful land was in the jurrsic park book. And when i grew up, i have an oppitinity to goes there."\n    Problem: This part is very detailed about Universal Studios, but it makes the reader wonder what else you love about LA. The essay feels more like it's about Universal Studios than the whole city.\n    How to fix it: While your Universal Studios story is interesting, try to connect it more broadly to why you love LA. You could say, "Universal Studios was a major reason for my visit, a place I dreamed of since childhood. But beyond its thrilling attractions, Los Angeles itself offers so much to love, from its diverse neighbourhoods to its vibrant art scene." This helps cover the whole city.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "To summarise LA is veary interesting place for me be caus it have a lot of thing you can do but I only a little activity in there but what ever i finally fullfill my one of chilhood dream at last."\n    Problem: In your conclusion, you mention that LA has "a lot of thing you can do," but your essay only talks about one activity. This creates a disconnect.\n    How to fix it: Make sure your conclusion matches what you've written in the body. If you want to mention other things in LA, include them in your main paragraphs with brief examples. Otherwise, focus your conclusion on the experiences you actually described. For instance, you could say, "To summarize, Los Angeles became truly special to me the moment my childhood dream of visiting Universal Studios came true. It was an unforgettable trip, and I look forward to exploring more of this exciting city in the future."	2.0	You tried to engage the reader with a personal story about your trip. However, the style you used was very informal and conversational, which is not usually suitable for an article on a travel website. Many errors also made your ideas hard to understand, so the article did not fully hold the reader's attention.\n\n*   Example: You started with, "A place I love, it will be LA for short or Los Angelis for full but you can call it what ever you want."\n    Problem: This opening is very informal ("for short," "for full," "what ever you want") and sounds like spoken English. It doesn't set the right tone for a travel article.\n    How to fix it: Start your article with a more formal and inviting tone. For example, "Los Angeles, often simply called LA, is a city that holds a special place in my heart." or "Of all the places I've visited, Los Angeles stands out as a city I truly love."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "me and mom simply don't care the Warning about motion sickness, and after we realize it was too late. After we got out the ride. my mom have a feeling she can throw up any time but for me no reaction."\n    Problem: This part is very informal ("me and mom," "simply don't care," "no reaction"). The sentences are also quite long and rambling, which makes them hard to read and understand.\n    How to fix it: Use more formal language and break down long sentences into shorter, clearer ones. For instance, "My mother and I decided to ignore the motion sickness warning, only to realize our mistake too late. After the ride, my mother felt quite unwell, but I did not experience any adverse reaction." This is clearer and more appropriate.	2.0	You structured your essay into three paragraphs, which is good. However, the ideas within your paragraphs sometimes jumped around, and you used very few linking words to connect your sentences and thoughts. This made your essay hard to follow in some places.\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "The reson of why i chouse this city for, it because one of place I alway want to been there since i was a child. The name of that place was Universal studio'. Fist time i've met this wonderful land was in the jurrsic park book. And when i grew up, i have an oppitinity to goes there."\n    Problem: The ideas here are a bit disconnected. You state the reason, then jump to the name of a place, then to a book, then to growing up. There are no smooth transitions.\n    How to fix it: Use connecting words to link your ideas. For example, "The main reason I chose Los Angeles is because it holds a place I always dreamed of visiting since childhood: Universal Studios. My fascination with this wonderful land began when I first encountered it in a Jurassic Park book. Later, as I grew up, I finally had the opportunity to go there."\n\n*   Example: You wrote, "After we got out the ride. my mom have a feeling she can throw up any time but for me no reaction."\n    Problem: This is two sentences run together without proper punctuation or a clear link between the ideas.\n    How to fix it: Separate sentences clearly and use linking words when needed. For instance, "After we got off the ride, my mom felt as if she might throw up at any moment, but surprisingly, I had no reaction."	1.0	You used simple vocabulary, but there were many significant errors in spelling, grammar, and punctuation throughout your essay. These mistakes made your writing very difficult to understand at times. Capitalisation was also a major issue.\n\n*   Spelling:\n    Example: "Angelis," "reson," "chouse," "alway," "oppitinity," "jurrsic," "fist," "summers," "veary," "caus," "fullfill," "chilhood"\n    Problem: These words are misspelled. They should be "Angeles," "reason," "chose," "always," "opportunity," "Jurassic," "first," "summer" (singular), "very," "cause," "fulfill," "childhood."\n    How to fix it: Proofread your work very carefully for spelling errors. Use a dictionary or a spell checker to help you.\n\n*   Capitalisation:\n    Example: "LA for short or Los Angelis for full," "i chouse this city," "i've met," "jurrsic park book," "harry potter theme Attraction," "me and mom," "Warning," "to summarise LA"\n    Problem: Many words that should be capitalized (like the start of sentences, proper nouns such as "Los Angeles," "Universal Studios," "Jurassic Park," "Harry Potter") are not. Also, words like "Warning" are capitalized incorrectly in the middle of a sentence.\n    How to fix it: Remember to always capitalize the first letter of every sentence. Capitalize proper nouns, which are specific names of places, people, books, or attractions (e.g., "Los Angeles," "Universal Studios," "Jurassic Park," "Harry Potter"). Do not capitalize common nouns unless they are at the beginning of a sentence.\n\n*   Grammar - Subject-Verb Agreement and Sentence Structure:\n    Example: "it will be LA," "it because one of place," "i have an oppitinity to goes there," "The story i will giving to you now it start last summers ago," "my mom have a feeling"\n    Problem: These sentences have grammatical errors, including incorrect verb forms ("goes" instead of "go"), missing words, and awkward phrasing. "my mom have a feeling" should be "my mom *had* a feeling" (past tense).\n    How to fix it: Practice forming clear and correct sentences. Pay attention to verb tense and subject-verb agreement. For example, "I had the opportunity to *go* there." and "My mom *had* a feeling."	\N	\N	2025-07-23
