 English, we don't capitalize words in the middle of sentences unless they are proper nouns. Thai doesn't have this capitalization rule, so this is a common challenge.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "activities that teen enjoy"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "activities that teens enjoy"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> Remember that "teen" needs to be plural ("teens") because you're talking about many teenagers. In Thai, the same word can be singular or plural, but English requires this change.<br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "the easyest ways teens can be fit in shape"<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "the easiest way teens can get fit and stay in shape"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The spelling rule for "easy" → "easiest" follows the "y to i" pattern. Also, we say "get fit" rather than "be fit in shape" - this is a common Thai-to-English translation issue.<br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Practice writing complete 4-paragraph essays within the 140-190 word range<br>2. Work on capitalization rules - only capitalize proper nouns and sentence beginnings<br>3. Review plural forms and subject-verb agreement, especially with words like "teen/teens"<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-27
