entences with a mix of simple and more complex linking words to improve the flow of your ideas.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	4.0	Mew, you did a really good job of tackling all parts of the essay question, which is super important! You talked about fashion, studying, and even came up with a great "own idea" about traveling, which shows good thinking. 👍 To get that top score, try to make sure your argument about whether friends or parents have *more* influence is super clear and consistent from start to finish. For example, if you state that parents have more influence, ensure each body paragraph consistently supports that claim, even if friends also play a role. Sometimes, it felt like the main argument got a little lost, especially in your conclusion. Keep practicing how to weave your main idea through every part of your writing!	3.0	You've made a good effort to write formally, which is exactly what an essay needs! Your essay generally conveys your message effectively. To move towards a higher score, focus on refining your language to ensure it's always appropriate for an academic essay. For instance, phrases like "People in the same ages as teenages" could be rephrased to sound more formal, perhaps "Peers in the same age group." 📝 Think about how you can consistently present your arguments with sophisticated phrasing, making sure your viewpoint on who has *more* influence comes across strongly and clearly without any informal slips.	3.0	Your essay has a good basic structure, Mew, with clear paragraphs for your introduction, main points, and conclusion. This is a great start! However, to make your writing flow even better, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases between your sentences and paragraphs. Instead of just "First," "In addition," and "Third," you could use phrases like "Furthermore," "Moreover," or "On the other hand" to connect your ideas more smoothly. 🔗 This will help your reader follow your thoughts easily and make your essay feel more cohesive. Many Thai learners sometimes stick to simpler connectors, but expanding your range will make a big difference!	2.0	This is an area where we can really make some progress together, Mew! I noticed a few recurring grammatical points that we can focus on. Firstly, **subject-verb agreement** is super important – for example, we say "teenagers *are* important" rather than "teenages *are* important" (and remember, "teenagers" is already plural, so no 's' needed after "teenage" unless it's possessive!). Secondly, watch out for **word choice** and **spelling**, especially words like "necessary" (you wrote "nessary") and "chose" (you wrote "choses" when you meant "choose" or "chosen"). For instance, instead of "Many parents was give pressure," we should say "Many parents *gave* pressure" or "Many parents *were giving* pressure." And instead of "bough by teenages," we'd use "bought by teenagers." Many English learners from Thailand sometimes struggle with these tricky verb forms and spelling, so you're not alone! Let's also look at using articles like "a" and "the" correctly; for example, "an excellent way" rather than "a excellent way." 📚 Focusing on these three areas will make your writing much clearer and more accurate. Keep a close eye on your verb forms and word endings!	\N	\N	2025-09-12
