 You wrote 323 words and achieved B2+. You give very clear, detailed descriptions of three popular video games and show good knowledge and enthusiasm for the topic — this makes your essay interesting and informative for the reader.<br><br>	B2+	Writing	CA	FIRST	Part 2	5.0	Your essay fully fulfils the task by describing free-time activities within the category of computer/video games and giving clear reasons why these games are chosen. You provide concrete details and examples for Roblox, Minecraft and ROV which fully inform the reader. <br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Consider briefly acknowledging an alternative type of free-time activity (for example, a sport or a creative hobby) in one sentence to show a slightly broader perspective. This is optional at B2 but can increase variety. <br><br>	4.0	You use the conventions of an essay well and generally keep an appropriate informative tone. Your examples and descriptions hold the reader’s interest. <br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Make the register a little more formal and precise in places (avoid conversational phrases). This will strengthen the essay’s academic tone. <br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> “You get put on a team and in order to win you have to destroy the other team's base.”<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "Players are assigned to teams and must cooperate to destroy the opposing team's base."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved version uses a more formal phrasing (passive “are assigned” and “must cooperate”) which is more suitable for an academic essay and makes the sentence clearer and more concise.<br><br>	3.0	You use paragraphs and there is a clear introduction and conclusion, which helps the reader follow the text. However, some paragraph topic sentences are weak or run-on, which reduces clarity about each paragraph’s main function. <br>👉 <b>To Improve:</b> Make each paragraph start with a clear topic sentence that directly links to your thesis. Also aim for four distinct paragraphs: introduction (thesis), body paragraph 1 (activity/category 1), body paragraph 2 (activity/category 2 or subcategory), conclusion (restate thesis + summary).<br>❌ <b>Weakness:</b> “The first popular game, Roblox is a platform that includes thousands of games "called "maps" that people can choose to play.”<br>✨ <b>Improved:</b> "One popular choice is Roblox, which is a platform offering thousands of user-created games, known as 'maps'"<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> The improved sentence gives a clear topic statement (one popular choice) that links to the thesis and uses a relative clause ("which is...") to add essential information without creating a long or confusing clause.<br><br>	4.0	You use a good range of everyday vocabulary and attempt complex structures. The main challenges are with a few key grammar and spelling points. <br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Freetime activities are the pinicle of relaxation."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Free-time activities are the pinnacle of relaxation."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (Thai interference — word form/word choice) Thai learners often transfer word forms directly from Thai where word endings and derivational changes are not marked the same way as in English. This can lead to incorrect word forms or spellings such as *pinicle* instead of *pinnacle*. Focusing on common noun spellings and word endings in English helps reduce this error. <br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Some of the most played video games are Roblox, Minecraft and ROV which are chosen due to it's creativity."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Some of the most played video games are Roblox, Minecraft and ROV, which are chosen for their creativity."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (Thai interference — possessive/grammar and relative clause punctuation) Thai has no articles or the same possessive spelling distinctions as English, so learners often confuse "it's" (it is) with the possessive "its." Also, relative clauses in English often need commas and clearer linking words. Putting a comma before "which" and using "their" for plural possession makes the sentence grammatically correct and clearer. <br><br>❌ <b>Problem:</b> You wrote: "Every teenager has freetime activites even in countries that give little to no freetime like Thailand, Thai teenagers have many freetime activites but the most popular one is playing videogames."<br>✨ <b>Correction:</b> "Every teenager has free-time activities, even in countries that give little leisure time such as Thailand. Thai teenagers, however, often enjoy playing video games the most."<br>👉 <b>Explanation:</b> (Thai interference — run-ons and clause boundary use) Thai allows long connected clauses without the same punctuation or linking conventions used in English. This can lead to comma splices and run-on sentences in English. Breaking the idea into two sentences and using linking words (however) gives clearer clause boundaries and improves readability. <br><br><b>📚 YOUR ACTION PLAN</b><br>1. Weekly task: Choose two short model paragraphs (one per week) and rewrite them, focusing on clear topic sentences and correct clause boundaries (15–20 minutes each).<br>2. Grammar focus: Spend 3 sessions practicing relative clauses and possessive forms (its vs it's) with 10 example sentences each; check and correct them.<br>3. Spelling drills: Keep a personal error log for 2 weeks of your frequent misspellings (e.g., pinnacle, activities, characters, strategize) and practice them with flashcards until you can spell them correctly 10/10.<br><br>	\N	\N	2025-08-28
