 body paragraph also lacked a clear topic sentence.\n✨ Improved: "While many Thai teenagers are burdened with extra classes and homework, I find immense value and relaxation in pursuing two distinct free-time activities: drawing and playing badminton. This essay will explain why these activities are essential for my well-being and personal development."\n💡 Explanation: Clearly stating your main argument (thesis) in the introduction and using topic sentences at the start of each body paragraph helps the reader understand your point in each section and how it supports your overall argument.	2.0	You used some vocabulary related to your topic, but there were several basic grammar and spelling mistakes that made it difficult to understand your ideas sometimes. You also didn't use relative clauses or passive structures, which are important for a B2 level.\n❌ Problem: You wrote: "teens might says"\n✨ Correction: "teens might say"\n💡 Explanation: In English, after a modal verb like 'might', 'can', 'should', etc., we always use the base form of the verb (the infinitive without 'to'). Verbs in Thai do not change form in this way, which is a common point of confusion.\n\n❌ Problem: You wrote: "every days"\n✨ Correction: "every day"\n💡 Explanation: The word "every" already indicates plurality for the phrase "every day," so "days" should be singular. In Thai, the concept of singular or plural is often understood from context or by using specific words, not by adding 's' to nouns.\n\n❌ Problem: You wrote: "it very famous"\n✨ Correction: "it is very famous"\n💡 Explanation: In English, a sentence needs a main verb. The verb 'to be' (is, am, are, was, were) is often used as a linking verb. In Thai, the equivalent of 'to be' can sometimes be left out, which is why learners might forget to include it in English.\n\n❌ Problem: You wrote: "This happed very often"\n✨ Correction: "This happened very often"\n💡 Explanation: When we talk about something that finished in the past, we usually add '-ed' to regular verbs in English to form the past simple.\n\n❌ Problem: You wrote: "work on their home works"\n✨ Correction: "work on their homework"\n💡 Explanation: The word "homework" is an uncountable noun in English, similar to "information" or "advice." This means it doesn't usually take a plural 's'. In Thai, nouns don't have distinct singular and plural forms like English does.\n\n🎯 YOUR ACTION PLAN\n1. Read the task carefully to ensure you address ALL parts of the question.\n2. Develop a clear thesis statement in your introduction and use topic sentences for each body paragraph.\n3. Maintain a consistent and appropriate tone (formal for an essay) throughout your writing.\n4. Review basic grammar rules for subject-verb agreement and the correct use of modal verbs.\n5. Pay attention to plural forms, especially for uncountable nouns like "homework."\n6. Practice using linking verbs ('to be') when necessary to form complete sentences.	\N	\N	2025-08-29
