n, which helps guide the reader through your ideas. 💡 You also used some basic linking words like "First," "Secondly," and "Lastly," which is a good habit. To make your writing even more cohesive, try to use a wider variety of linking words and phrases. Instead of just "And," you could use "Furthermore" or "Moreover" to connect your ideas. Also, aim to vary your sentence beginnings so that each paragraph feels fresh and distinct. Thai learners sometimes use simpler linking words, so practising a wider range can really make a difference.	3.0	You've used everyday vocabulary appropriately, and your meaning is generally clear despite some small errors. That's a solid effort. Your command of simple grammatical forms is good, and you're getting your message across. However, there are a few areas where we can tidy things up to make your writing even clearer. For example, with subject-verb agreement, remember that "pets" (plural) takes "help" (no 's'), not "helps," so you would say "pets help us to feel relaxed." 📚 Also, pay close attention to articles like "a," "an," and "the." For instance, instead of "at less one pet," we would say "at least one pet," and "the most of all families" could be "most families" or "the majority of families." These small corrections will make a big impact on the overall accuracy of your writing. Many learners of English find articles and subject-verb agreement tricky because these rules can be quite different from their first language, so you're not alone. Keep practicing, and you'll master them.	\N	\N	2025-09-13
