 on developing your ideas, and you'll see a real improvement in your clarity and overall expression!<br><br>📋 **Action Plan for Prize:**<br>*   ✅ When you start writing, read the question carefully and make sure your first paragraph tells the reader exactly what your essay will argue.<br>*   ✅ Before you finish, read your essay through and check that all your paragraphs clearly support your main argument.<br>*   ✅ Circle words like 'effect' and 'affect' in your writing and double-check you've used the correct one. For instance, "your choices will *affect* you" but "your choices will *have an effect* on you."	B1+	Writing	Term 1 Finals	FIRST	Part 2	2.0	Prize, you did a good job of writing about different aspects like fashion and studying, and you tried to connect them to teenagers' choices. You also managed to cover the topics given in the prompt. ✨ Sometimes, it was a little hard to see if you were talking about *who* influences choices (friends or parents) or *what happens* because of those choices. For example, in your introduction, you talked about what teenagers want to choose, but the essay question was asking about *who* has more influence. 🤔 Next time, try to make sure your first paragraph clearly states if you think friends or parents have more influence. Then, in each paragraph, clearly explain *how* that influence happens. For instance, if you're talking about fashion, you could write, "Parents often influence fashion choices by buying clothes for their children," or "Friends influence fashion by encouraging certain trends." This will help your reader understand your main point. Thai learners sometimes focus on the consequences or related ideas, rather than directly tackling the specific angle of the question.	2.0	Prize, you made a good effort to write an essay that has an introduction and paragraphs to talk about your ideas. You tried to make your point clear to the reader. 👍 Sometimes, the way you wrote felt a little like you were talking to a friend rather than writing an essay for school. Also, some of your sentences were a bit hard to understand, and this made your overall message less clear. For example, "Consequently not only parents will get effect but also some people get effect too" is a bit confusing. 😅 When you're writing an essay, imagine you're explaining your ideas to someone important, like a teacher, so your language should be a bit more formal. Try to use clear and complete sentences, and make sure each sentence helps your reader understand your ideas. For instance, instead of "So their will effect to their parents," you could say, "Consequently, their choices will have an impact on their parents." Focusing on clear, direct language helps your message come across strongly.	3.0	Prize, you did a nice job of splitting your essay into paragraphs, which makes it easier to read! You used words like "first," "beside fashion," and "finally" to guide the reader through your ideas, which is a good step towards organising your writing. 👏 While you used some connecting words, sometimes the ideas within your paragraphs, or between different paragraphs, didn't flow super smoothly. It was a little like reading individual thoughts rather than one continuous argument. 🔗 To make your writing even more connected, try using a wider variety of linking words and phrases. For example, instead of just "first," you could use "to begin with" or "firstly." To connect ideas between sentences, you might use words like "furthermore," "however," or "in addition." Also, ensure that each sentence logically follows the one before it, helping your reader move effortlessly from one idea to the next.	3.0	Prize, you used many everyday words, and your reader could usually understand what you meant, even with some errors. You also used different sentence structures to share your thoughts. ✨ I noticed a few repeated mistakes that made your essay a little tricky to follow in places. One big area to watch out for is confusing 'effect' and 'affect'. 'Affect' is usually a verb (an action), like "your choices *affect* your parents," and 'effect' is usually a noun (an article), like "their choices *had an effect* on their parents." Another common mistake was with verb forms after "will," like "will said" instead of "will say," and "will made" instead of "will make." Also, sometimes it was hard to tell if you meant "some teenager" or "some of the teenagers." 📝 Here are three concrete steps to improve your language:<br>1.  **Affect vs. Effect:** Practice using 'affect' and 'effect' correctly. Try writing sentences where 'affect' is a verb and 'effect' is a noun. For instance, "The new rule will *affect* everyone, and it will *have a big effect* on our daily lives."<br>2.  **Verb Forms with Modals:** Always remember that after 'will,' 'can,' 'should,' etc., you need to use the base form of the verb (e.g., *will go*, *can see*, *should study*).<br>3.  **Singular/Plural Nouns:** Pay close attention to whether you are talking about one teenager or many. When you mean many, use plural forms like "teenagers" or "some of the teenagers." These kinds of errors are very common for Thai learners as English grammar around articles, singular/plural, and verb forms can be quite different from Thai. Reading more English texts and practicing specific grammar exercises can help a lot!	\N	\N	2025-09-12
