etter, try to stick a bit closer to the 100-word guide, as this helps you keep your story tight and punchy! 🌟	4.0	When you wrote that the cold gave 'a chill down her spine,' it really helped me feel the spooky atmosphere of the dark alley and showed a great understanding of how to set a mood. Using such descriptive imagery holds the reader's attention well beyond a basic B1 level. Just keep an eye on some of your word choices like 'bending home,' which might confuse a reader; try using more common phrases like 'heading home' to keep the story flowing smoothly. 🚀	4.0	I see that you included helpful phrases like 'The next day' and 'when she arrived' to move the story along, which acts like a map for the reader so we never feel lost. These time markers create a very logical sequence of events from the house to the mysterious alley. For your next piece, try using a few more varied connectors like 'Despite the cold' or 'Eventually' to link your sentences even more naturally. 📝	4.0	I was very impressed by your ambitious use of language, especially phrases like 'chill down her spine' and 'case still mysterious'. Even though there are some spelling and grammar slips, you used a wide range of vocabulary to tell a complex and scary story that I could follow easily. ✨ For next time, try to double-check your past continuous forms (e.g., 'she was trying') to make your writing even more polished! 💡	\N	\N	2026-02-23
28813	2026-02-27 08:51:27.16672+00	2026	Question 2	\N	\N	16.0	Hi Boss! This is a wonderfully creative and suspenseful story with a really clever ending! You've shown a great ability to build atmosphere and engage the reader. Keep focusing on those spelling details and try using more paragraphing next time. All the best, Ed	B1	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	Part 2	5.0	You've done an excellent job continuing the story from the opening sentence! 🚀 Your plot about a young actress encountering a mysterious 'someone' in a haunted house is very creative and engaging. The ending with the 'The end' reveal provides a really clever meta-fictional twist. The reader is fully informed and kept on the edge of their seat! 🌟	4.0	I really liked how you built a sense of suspense and atmosphere in your story. 🌟 Using phrases like 'The wind passing her cold to her spine' and 'a growl coming from behind' really helps the reader imagine the scene. Your creative ending shows you're thinking about how to engage your audience in unique ways! ✨	4.0	Your story follows a very clear and logical sequence of events, from Lois leaving her house to the surprising conclusion. 📝 I noticed you used several good linking words like 'then,' 'when,' and 'but' to connect your ideas. To make it even better, try using paragraph breaks to separate different parts of the story, like the introduction, the journey, and the encounter in the house. 🚀	3.0	You've used some great everyday vocabulary and some more descriptive words like 'actress' and 'adjust.' ✨ I noticed a few spelling errors, like 'gasstation,' 'hunted' (instead of haunted), and 'vition' (instead of vision). If you keep an eye on these little details, your writing will be even more polished! You're doing a fantastic job—keep it up! 📝	\N	\N	2026-02-27
33961	2026-02-27 08:51:46.654796+00	2026	Question 2	\N	\N	16.0	Hi Gut-Jung! This is a brilliantly structured email where you've addressed every point with great clarity and tone. For your next task, try adding a bit more detail to your explanations. All the best, Ed	B1+	Writing	Term 2 Finals	PET	Part 2	5.0	You've done a fantastic job addressing all the points! 🚀 You clearly expressed your agreement, suggested a great age range, shared your thoughts on the judges, and gave a very practical suggestion for the prizes. The reader is fully informed because you provided clear reasons for each of your choices. Well done! 🌟	4.0	I love the warm and respectful tone you used in your email! 🌟 Your greeting and closing are perfectly suited for a teacher you like. You've communicated your ideas in a very clear and engaging way that holds the reader's attention perfectly. To make it even better, try to expand slightly on your reasons with another short sentence. Keep up the great communication! 📝	3.0	Your story has a clear beginning, middle, and end, and your ideas follow a logical sequence which is great to see! 📝 However, the entire story is written as one large block of text, which makes it a little bit difficult for the reader to follow the shifts in time and place. For your next story, try using paragraph breaks to separate the introduction, the main events, and the conclusion. This will make your wonderful story even clearer and more organized! ✨	4.0	You've used a wonderful range of everyday vocabulary, and I particularly liked phrases like 'show off their amazing talent' and 'attract the teenagers'. 📝 I noticed that many of your sentences are completely error-free, which is fantastic! Just remember to keep an eye on subject-verb agreement (for example, 'students who are' instead of 'is'). You have great control over your language—keep it up! ✨	\N	\N	2026-02-27
29713	2026-07-10 02:00:00+00	2027	Midterm Part 2	As the plane flew lower, LOU saw the golden beaches of the island below. And that's when he spotted a submerged sign that reads "Welcome to Sansiri". That made him realise that this island was owned by Joe Sleepys, his idol. Lou immediately decided to land the plane. After landing, Lou went straight to meet Joe. They talked for a bit then he heard loud noises. Both of them turned and see a crowd protesting about "Mansphere" and "Safety for woman". Then Lou realised, they were protesting for Joe to get out. Turns out Joe was posting Mansphere videos on Socmed. Later investigations found that, Joe was a psycho. He was sleeping in front of girl bedrooms and using AI to wake deepfake inappropriate images of girls. Joe's popularity was gone in just ad He became head lines of news. What he did was not okay.	148	17.0	PK, you've written a genuinely gripping story here. The moment Lou spots the submerged sign and realises the island belongs to his idol is a clever hook, and the twist where Joe turns out to be a criminal is bold and memorable. You took the prompt and built a complete narrative with a clear arc, which is exactly what a Band 5 story needs. To reach the top level, you'll want to polish a few small things in how you tell it. Let's look at each part of the scoring guide to see exactly where you can sharpen your writing.	B2	Writing	Term 1 Midterm	PET	Part 2	5.0	<p>You're at a 5/5!</p><ul><li><em>Lou saw the golden beaches of the island below. And that's when he spotted a submerged sign that reads "Welcome to Sansiri".</em> You immediately connect the setting to the character's personal world. The reader knows exactly why this island matters to Lou.</li><li><em>Both of them turned and see a crowd protesting about "Mansphere" and "Safety for woman".</em> You introduce conflict through a concrete, visual scene. The reader can picture the protest and understand the stakes.</li><li><em>He was sleeping in front of girl bedrooms and using AI to wake deepfake inappropriate images of girls.</em> You reveal Joe's dark secret with specific, disturbing details. The story feels complete and the twist lands.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Communicative Achievement. A Band 5 writer maintains a consistent storytelling register throughout and ends the narrative in a way that satisfies the reader. Your story has a strong arc and a clear twist, but the register shifts in a few places and the ending feels rushed.</p><ul><li><em>Turns out Joe was posting Mansphere videos on Socmed.</em> → <strong>It turned out that Joe had been posting Mansphere videos on social media.</strong> 'Turns out' is more casual spoken English. In a written story, 'It turned out that' keeps the tone consistent and polished.</li><li><em>What he did was not okay.</em> → <strong>What he had done was unacceptable.</strong> This final line sounds like a comment from a friend, not the conclusion of a story. A stronger ending would match the serious tone of the events you described.</li><li><em>Joe's popularity was gone in just ad He became head lines of news.</em> → <strong>Joe's popularity vanished overnight. He became the headline of every news story.</strong> The ending feels abrupt because you tell us what happened instead of showing a final image or thought from Lou. Try ending with Lou's reaction or a quiet, impactful sentence that closes the loop.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Organisation. A Band 5 writer connects every sentence smoothly so the reader never feels a jump. Your story flows well through most of the events, but the final sentence feels disconnected from what came before.</p><ul><li><em>Joe's popularity was gone in just ad He became head lines of news. What he did was not okay.</em> → <strong>Joe's popularity was gone in just an ad. He became the headline of every news story — and what he had done was unacceptable.</strong> The last two sentences feel like separate thoughts. Joining them or rewriting the final line to echo an earlier image would make the ending feel intentional.</li><li><em>They talked for a bit then he heard loud noises.</em> → <strong>They talked for a bit, and then Lou heard loud noises.</strong> A small comma and the name 'Lou' make the sequence clearer. The reader knows exactly who heard the noise.</li></ul>	4.0	<p>You're at 4/5 for Language. A Band 5 writer controls tense consistently and uses correct word forms and spelling. Your vocabulary is strong and you attempt complex sentences, which is great. The main pattern to fix is tense consistency: you shift between past and present within the same narrative.</p><ul><li><em>a submerged sign that reads "Welcome to Sansiri"</em> → <strong>a submerged sign that read "Welcome to Sansiri"</strong>. The story is in past tense, so the sign 'read' at that moment, not 'reads' now.</li><li><em>Both of them turned and see a crowd</em> → <strong>Both of them turned and saw a crowd</strong>. 'Turned' is past, so 'see' must become 'saw' to match.</li><li><em>using AI to wake deepfake inappropriate images</em> → <strong>using AI to create deepfake inappropriate images</strong>. 'Wake' is the wrong verb here. 'Create' or 'generate' is what you meant.</li><li><em>in just ad</em> → <strong>in just an ad</strong> or <strong>overnight</strong>. 'Ad' is missing 'an', and the phrase 'in just an ad' is unclear. 'Overnight' would be more natural.</li><li><em>head lines of news</em> → <strong>the headline of every news story</strong> or <strong>headline news</strong>. 'Head lines' is two words; it should be one word 'headline'. Also check your capitalisation: 'woman' should be 'women' (plural), and 'Socmed' is fine as a shortened form but in a story you might write 'social media' for clarity.</li></ul><p>A good self-check: read each sentence aloud and ask yourself, 'Is this happening now or in the past?' If the story is in the past, every main verb should be past tense.</p>	0.00	[]	2026-07-10
